const CHEF={"53400":{"lv":"EERREERRRRRR","w":{"2":"Gives the broth its golden color and earthy warmth; skipping makes the dish paler and a little flat.","3":"Julienned peel adds sweet-tart perfume that makes mastawa distinct; skipping gives a plain lamb-and-rice stew.","6":"Fried garlic stirred in at the end gives pungent savory lift against the yogurt; skipping leaves it mild.","8":"Dried dill is the classic herbal note with yogurt and lamb; skipping loses the fresh-green fragrance.","9":"1 tbsp gives a warm, peppery background; reduce to 1 tsp if you prefer less heat.","10":"Adds creamy bite and extra protein; skipping gives a smoother, more lamb-forward stew.","11":"Seasons the whole pot, including the rice; without it the yogurt and rice taste dull.","7":"Oil only starts the garlic and lamb; the lamb renders its own fat, so skipping it is fine"},"sub":{"7":{"x":"1 tbsp olive oil or 1 tbsp butter","d":"Butter is richer and sweeter with the garlic; olive oil adds a faint fruity note. Either works for the quick fry.","k":["olive oil","butter"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Skim the grey foam in the first 10 minutes and keep the pot at a bare simmer. A hard boil toughens the lamb and clouds the broth that the rice will absorb.","s":0},{"t":"Cut away as much white pith as you can and change the soaking water once or twice; pith is bitter and will otherwise leach into the stew.","s":1},{"t":"Temper the yogurt by whisking it with a ladle of hot broth before folding it in, then keep the heat low. Cold yogurt added to a boiling pot splits into grainy curds.","s":4}],"rl":"PXXXBMXFXZVZ"},"53403":{"lv":"EEER","w":{"3":"Seeds of about 10 to 12 pods (1 to 2 tsp) give the jam its signature perfume; 60 g is far too much. Skipping leaves a plain orange-pumpkin jam."},"tips":[{"t":"The long sugar maceration draws water out of the pumpkin, so the cubes firm up and stay whole during cooking. Do not shorten it or the pieces will collapse into mush.","s":4},{"t":"Simmer gently and stir only occasionally. The jam is ready when a spoonful on a chilled plate wrinkles when you push it; the syrup keeps thickening as it cools, so stop a little before it looks fully set.","s":5},{"t":"Use jars washed and dried in a 250F (120C) oven and fill them while the jam is still warm. Unless properly canned, store it in the fridge and use within a few weeks.","s":6}],"rl":"VVWX"},"53398":{"lv":"EEOERREREE","w":{"2":"A garnish at the end; fresh heat against the sweet squash. Skip or use a pinch of red pepper flakes.","3":"Used for frying the squash and onions; any neutral oil works, so you can sub it.","4":"Gives the golden color and earthy warmth to the sauce; skipping makes it paler and a little flat.","5":"Ground into the onion sauce for savory depth. A full bulb is about 10 cloves; 5 or 6 cloves is plenty.","7":"Balances the 7 tbsp sugar; without it the squash tastes cloying."},"sub":{"3":{"x":"Any neutral oil, such as sunflower, canola or corn oil, in the same amount","d":"Practically identical; avoid olive oil for deep frying as it burns sooner.","k":["sunflower oil","canola oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Fry the squash in a single layer at about 350F (175C) and drain on paper towels; crowding the pan steams it. It should be deep golden outside so it holds its shape in the sauce.","s":0},{"t":"Do not stir roughly once the squash is layered in the sauce; let the sugar slowly glaze it. Cook until the liquid is nearly gone so the squash caramelizes instead of stewing.","s":3},{"t":"Use thick strained yogurt, at room temperature, and stir a crushed garlic clove and a pinch of salt into it. Spoon it under and over the squash for the traditional contrast.","s":4}],"rl":"VXGFXXWZMG"},"53397":{"lv":"ERERRRRR","w":{"1":"Adds a milder, sweeter fruit and lighter color; regular raisins are fine in its place.","3":"Adds creamy richness and crunch; skipping leaves a fruit-only compote.","4":"Gives a firm, crisp-tender bite once peeled; skip if you have other nuts.","5":"Mostly adds color and mild sweetness; any other nut works.","6":"Tart cherries balance the sweet raisins; skipping makes it sweeter.","7":"Gives the floral fragrance of Afghan compote; skipping loses the aroma but the fruit still tastes good."},"sub":{"1":{"x":"50 g regular raisins","d":"Darker color and slightly deeper flavor; otherwise identical.","k":["raisins"]},"6":{"x":"50 g dried cranberries","d":"Similar tartness; slightly sweeter and a brighter red.","k":["dried cranberries"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Slip the skins off the nuts while they are still hot from the soak; cold nuts hold onto them. Peeled almonds and pistachios stay pale and sweet instead of tasting bitter.","s":1},{"t":"Rest it in the fridge a full two days or more. The dried fruit plumps and the soaking liquid turns into a lightly sweet, fruity syrup that is the best part of the dish.","s":2},{"t":"Add the rose water a little at a time and taste; brands vary widely and too much tastes soapy.","s":2}],"rl":"VVVVVVVX"},"53401":{"lv":"EEEERRE","w":{"4":"Scallions give the filling its sharp fresh bite; chives or finely sliced leek work as swaps. Skipped, it tastes like plain mash.","5":"Adds gentle warmth to the potato; reduce or skip without much change."},"sub":{"4":{"x":"1/2 cup chopped chives or 1/2 cup finely sliced leek","d":"Chives are milder; leek is sweeter and needs a quick sauté first.","k":["chives","leek"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Rest the dough at least 30 minutes, covered. Relaxed gluten rolls paper-thin without springing back or tearing.","s":1},{"t":"Steam the mashed potato dry over low heat for a minute and do not add milk or butter. A wet filling steams the dough from inside and bursts the seam.","s":2},{"t":"Fry in a thin film of oil over medium heat, pressing lightly so the bolani blisters. Too low and it soaks up oil, too high and the dough browns before the filling is hot.","s":5}],"rl":"SMZBXZF"},"53402":{"lv":"EEERERREEO","w":{"3":"Small amount of heat in the filling; skip or use a pinch of red pepper flakes.","5":"Adds warm spice to the lamb; 2 tbsp is generous, so reduce to 1 tbsp if you prefer milder.","9":"A garnish only; a little fresh coriander or parsley adds freshness against the yogurt.","6":"Fries the tomato paste for the sauce; skipped, the sauce is flatter but meat and yogurt still carry it"},"sub":{"6":{"x":"1 tbsp olive oil or 1 tbsp butter","d":"Butter gives a richer sauce; olive oil adds a slight fruity note. Both fry the tomato paste well.","k":["olive oil","butter"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Use lamb with about 20 percent fat and chop the onion very fine; the onion juices keep the small filling moist. Cook to 160F (71C); check by cutting one dumpling open.","s":1},{"t":"Pinch the corners together firmly and use a dab of water on the seams. Leaks almost always start at the folded corners.","s":3},{"t":"Oil the steamer tiers generously and leave gaps between the mantu or they will fuse. Stir a crushed garlic clove and pinch of salt into the yogurt so it clings to the sauce.","s":4}],"rl":"SMPXXZFAGG"},"53396":{"lv":"EEEEERREE","w":{"5":"Adds nutty flavor in the dough and the classic sprinkle on top; skipping gives a plainer pastry.","6":"Fragrant signature of Afghan sweets; use ground cardamom, about 10 g. Skipping leaves the dough bland.","3":"Frying medium for the pastry pieces; without it the dish cannot be cooked"},"sub":{"3":{"x":"75 ml (1/3 cup) sunflower or canola oil","d":"Same frying performance; use enough oil to float the pieces, as 75 ml is only the starting amount.","k":["sunflower oil","canola oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Rest the dough 20 minutes before rolling so it stretches thin without snapping back. A thin sheet gives the crisp, layered texture.","s":2},{"t":"Hold the oil at 340 to 350F (170 to 175C). Test with a scrap of dough; it should sizzle and rise in seconds. Too cool and the pastry is greasy, too hot and it browns before the middle cooks.","s":3},{"t":"Simmer the syrup 8 to 10 minutes until it lightly coats a spoon, then coat the baghlaw while both are still warm. Cold pastry in cold syrup gets soggy instead of glazed.","s":5}],"rl":"SSMFLGXWM"},"53399":{"lv":"EEEEROO","w":{"4":"Floral perfume that is typical of firni; skipping leaves a milder, more plain pudding.","5":"Topping for crunch and color only; skip if you have none.","6":"Topping for color only; skip or use extra almonds."},"sub":{"4":{"x":"1/2 tsp vanilla extract","d":"Warmer and less floral; still gives a pleasant aromatic finish.","k":["vanilla extract"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Cornstarch must reach a gentle boil to thicken fully. Whisk constantly as it heats, then keep stirring 3 to 5 minutes after it bubbles until it coats the back of a spoon.","s":2},{"t":"Always dissolve the cornstarch in cold milk first and add it in a thin stream; adding it to hot milk makes lumps. If lumps form, pass the mixture through a fine sieve.","s":0},{"t":"Press plastic wrap directly on the surface while it chills to prevent a skin. Serve cold, and garnish just before serving so the nuts stay crisp.","s":3}],"rl":"MTWXXGG"},"53413":{"lv":"EEEEEREEREER","w":{"4":"Stops the butter from burning under the grill and keeps the filling pourable; any neutral oil works.","5":"Lines 3 to 5, 6 to 8 and 9 to 10 look like three alternate filling versions; choose one dairy and use it only.","7":"Part of the second alternative filling; any neutral oil works.","8":"Alternative to yogurt or sour cream; use one version only. It gives a rich, cream-like tang.","10":"Alternative filling version; use only one version, not all three.","11":"Just greases the tray so the first layer lifts out; any fat works."},"sub":{"4":{"x":"200 ml sunflower or canola oil","d":"Same function; neutral flavor.","k":["sunflower oil","canola oil"]},"5":{"x":"3 tbsp sour cream","d":"Slightly sharper and thinner; fine in this batter.","k":["sour cream"]},"7":{"x":"200 ml sunflower or canola oil","d":"Same function; neutral flavor.","k":["sunflower oil","canola oil"]},"8":{"x":"3 tbsp sour cream","d":"Tangier and less rich than clotted cream.","k":["sour cream"]},"10":{"x":"500 ml greek yogurt thinned with 2 tbsp milk","d":"Thicker and tangier; thinning keeps the filling brushable.","k":["greek yogurt","milk"]},"11":{"x":"3 tbsp melted butter or vegetable oil","d":"Butter gives a nuttier bottom crust.","k":["butter","vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Make each layer paper-thin, about 2 mm. Flija is dozens of thin layers cooked one at a time; a thick layer stays raw and gummy inside.","s":5},{"t":"Cook the layers under a medium grill and brush the filling over each layer while it is still hot, so the layers fuse into a soft, buttery stack with a crisp top.","s":6},{"t":"Rest the finished flija in the pan 10 minutes, covered with a cloth, so the layers steam together. Cut with a sharp serrated knife and serve with yogurt or honey.","s":9}],"rl":"SMZFFMFFFFFF"},"53404":{"lv":"EREERERROEEEERO","w":{"1":"Browns the lamb alongside the oil; either fat works.","2":"Browns the lamb and adds a fruity note; vegetable oil or butter work as well.","4":"Classic aromatic with lamb and yogurt; skipping leaves it plainer.","6":"Gentle aroma in the lamb base; skip it if needed.","7":"Seasons the lamb during the long simmer; a little is needed so the rice does not taste flat.","8":"Light warmth; skip or use a small pinch.","13":"Seasons the yogurt custard, which tastes flat without it.","14":"Light warmth in the custard; skip."},"sub":{"1":{"x":"1 1/2 tbsp olive oil","d":"Loses the dairy richness but browns fine.","k":["olive oil"]},"2":{"x":"2 tbsp vegetable oil or 2 tbsp butter","d":"Butter gives a richer finish; vegetable oil is more neutral.","k":["vegetable oil","butter"]}},"tips":[{"t":"The butter-and-flour roux stabilizes the yogurt so it does not curdle in the oven. Cook it 2 minutes, cool slightly, and whisk it quickly into room-temperature yogurt and eggs.","s":6},{"t":"Pour the yogurt over a lamb-rice base that is hot but not boiling; a cold base slows setting and a boiling one scrambles the eggs. Keep it at 180C (350F) and move it to the middle shelf only when it starts to set.","s":9},{"t":"It is done when the custard is puffed, golden and just set with a slight wobble. Overbaking makes it watery and grainy; rest 5 to 10 minutes so it firms up.","s":11}],"rl":"PFFBXMXZZFTMSZZ"},"53410":{"lv":"EEREEROR","w":{"2":"Adds sweetness and aroma to the lamb base; skipping makes it less savory.","5":"Needed to season the rice during baking; it is bland without it.","6":"Light warmth; skip if you like.","4":"The liquid the rice bakes in; without it the rice stays raw and dry","7":"A splash to soften the leeks; skipped, the leeks still cook but with less flavor"},"sub":{"4":{"x":"1 l beef stock, or 1 l hot water with 1 beef bouillon cube","d":"Less lamb flavor but fine for the rice; adjust salt as bouillon is salty.","k":["beef stock","bouillon cube"]},"7":{"x":"1 to 2 tbsp vegetable oil or butter","d":"Butter browns the leeks nicely; oil is more neutral.","k":["vegetable oil","butter"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Slice the leeks lengthwise and rinse well; grit hides between the layers. Brown them properly in step 1, since the caramelized leek is the sweet backbone of the dish.","s":1},{"t":"Cook the ground lamb to at least 160F (71C) and brown it well so no raw pink remains before adding rice. The rice absorbs the fat, which gives the bake its flavor.","s":2},{"t":"Check at 45 minutes. If the rice is still firm but the liquid is gone, add 1/2 cup hot stock and cover with foil for the rest of the bake so the top does not dry out.","s":6}],"rl":"VPXBMZZF"},"53405":{"lv":"EEERERROOROR","w":{"3":"Gives a mild savory backbone; skipping makes the rice milder.","5":"Adds sweet color and mild peppery flavor to the rice; skipping makes it paler and plainer.","6":"Mediterranean note that suits peppers and leeks; skip if you have other herbs.","7":"Just a teaspoon of dried parsley; negligible. Use fresh to garnish instead.","8":"A small amount of basil adds a little fragrance; skip without notice.","9":"Seasons the rice as it absorbs the stock; needed for flavor.","10":"Light warmth; skip.","4":"The liquid that cooks the rice and leeks; without it the bake is dry and uncooked","11":"A splash to sweat the leeks; skipping it only slightly dulls flavor"},"sub":{"4":{"x":"1 l water with 1 vegetable bouillon cube","d":"Slightly less rounded; adjust the salt since bouillon is salty.","k":["vegetable bouillon","water"]},"11":{"x":"1 to 2 tbsp vegetable oil","d":"Neutral; less fruity.","k":["vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Cook the leeks and peppers until they begin to brown before adding liquid. Browning is the only source of savory depth in this meatless bake.","s":1},{"t":"Use a tray with at least 2 inches of depth and level the rice so it cooks evenly; thin corners dry out and stay crunchy.","s":5},{"t":"Cover the tray with foil for the first 40 minutes then uncover to finish. This keeps the top rice from drying while the liquid is absorbed.","s":7}],"rl":"VVBXMXXXXZZF"},"53407":{"lv":"EEEERR","w":{"4":"Greases the tray and gives the top a golden sheen; butter or any oil works.","5":"The filling tastes flat without it; a pinch is enough if you are short."},"sub":{"4":{"x":"1 tbsp melted butter or vegetable oil","d":"Butter browns the top more deeply; oil is neutral.","k":["butter","vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Grate the squash and squeeze out the liquid in a towel first. The batter should be thick and sticky; wet squash makes a gummy, steamed center.","s":1},{"t":"The batter has no salt in the list, so add a small pinch; the pie tastes flat without it.","s":1},{"t":"Rubbing oil over the top with a wet hand gives an even golden crust. Cool completely before slicing so the center sets and cuts cleanly into squares.","s":3}],"rl":"VSSLFZ"},"53408":{"lv":"EEEER","w":{"4":"Brushed on for color and a soft crust; butter or any oil works."},"sub":{"4":{"x":"1 tbsp melted butter or vegetable oil","d":"Butter gives a richer crust; oil is neutral.","k":["butter","vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Knead only until the dough comes together, a minute or two; this is a soda-style bread, and overworking toughens it. Keep the dough slightly tacky and use as little flour as possible.","s":3},{"t":"Use thick Greek yogurt as the acid; thin yogurt makes a slack dough that spreads instead of rising. The yogurt reacts with the leavener, so shape and bake right away.","s":2},{"t":"Press the patterns in deeply, almost halfway through. They let heat reach the 5 cm thick middle so it bakes through; knock on the bottom, it should sound hollow.","s":6}],"rl":"SMZLF"},"53411":{"lv":"EEEERRORORRE","w":{"4":"Gentle warmth in the filling; skipping hardly changes the result.","5":"Adds color and mild sweetness to the meat; skipping makes the filling plainer.","6":"Just 1 tsp of dried bouillon powder; adds savory depth but salt and paprika cover for it.","7":"Fresh herb that lifts the lamb and rice; skipping makes the filling heavy.","8":"Small amount of dried basil; negligible.","9":"Fries the onions; any neutral oil or butter works.","10":"Seasons the filling; needed, but adjust since pickled leaves and bouillon are already salty."},"sub":{"9":{"x":"1 to 2 tbsp vegetable oil","d":"Neutral; less fruity.","k":["vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Rinse the pickled leaves and soak them in hot water for 10 minutes to remove the brine, then taste before adding any salt to the filling, as the leaves are salty.","s":3},{"t":"Use only about 1 tablespoon of filling per parcel and roll fairly loosely. The rice swells as it cooks, and tight rolls split open.","s":4},{"t":"Pack the parcels seam-side down and weigh them with an inverted plate so they do not unroll. Simmer gently; a hard boil bursts them. Ground lamb should reach 160F (71C); a 1.5 hour simmer easily gets there.","s":6}],"rl":"VPXBZXXXXFZM"},"53412":{"lv":"EERRREORORE","w":{"2":"Gives the stew its red color and sweet pepper flavor; skipping makes it paler.","3":"Adds umami and a rounded tomato depth; skipping makes the broth thinner.","4":"Sweet pepper adds body and sweetness; skipping gives a more cabbage-forward stew.","6":"Just a pinch of heat; skip it if you prefer milder.","7":"Seasons the long-simmered broth; needed or the stew is flat.","8":"Light warmth; skip.","9":"A splash to start the stew; the meat releases fat so it still cooks"},"sub":{"9":{"x":"1 to 2 tbsp vegetable oil","d":"Neutral flavor; less fruity.","k":["vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Choose a stewing cut like chuck or shin and brown it well, so the broth gets savory depth. Lean cuts turn dry and stringy over a long simmer.","s":1},{"t":"Cook the paprika only 1 to 2 minutes in the hot fat and add liquid right after; scorched paprika turns the whole stew bitter.","s":2},{"t":"Keep the simmer very low. If you prefer cabbage with some texture, simmer the beef alone for about 1.5 hours, then add the cabbage for the last hour.","s":5}],"rl":"PVXAVXXZZFM"},"53406":{"lv":"EEEE","tips":[{"t":"Use ripe, soft figs; underripe ones stay hard and bland. Cook them gently so they soften and release their juices without bursting.","s":2},{"t":"Reduce the fig juices with the sugar until the syrup coats a spoon; it thickens further as it cools, so stop when it is still a little loose.","s":6},{"t":"Toast the walnut halves in a dry pan for a few minutes before pressing them in; this brings out their flavor and crunch against the soft fruit.","s":8}],"rl":"VGMW"},"53409":{"lv":"EEEERREEE","w":{"4":"Adds nutty flavor and a little crunch in the dense cake; skipping gives a plainer, honey-sweet cake.","5":"Gives the cake its honey flavor and moistness; skipping leaves it less aromatic but still sweet from the sugar."},"sub":{"5":{"x":"4 tbsp maple syrup","d":"Slightly less floral than honey, with a caramel note; the cake texture stays the same.","k":["maple syrup"]}},"tips":[{"t":"The dough is firm, almost like a thick cookie dough. Knead only until smooth and press it evenly into the tray; overworking makes it tough and uneven.","s":3},{"t":"Starting in a cold oven lets the cake rise slowly and set evenly before the crust hardens, so it stays tender inside with a dark crust.","s":6},{"t":"Pour hot syrup over a cooled cake, or cooled syrup over a hot cake, never both hot. The big temperature difference lets the cake soak up the syrup without turning soggy; a squeeze of lemon in the syrup stops crystals.","s":10}],"rl":"WSFSVWLWM"},"53414":{"lv":"EEEEEEROO","w":{"6":"Adds a soft background aroma to the cookies; skipping leaves them tasting buttery and plain.","7":"Egg wash gives color and a light shine; skip and they bake pale but taste the same.","8":"Only used to grease your hands for rolling; skip and use a little oil or flour."},"tips":[{"t":"The dough should be soft and just a bit sticky. Add flour a tablespoon at a time only until it holds a ball; extra flour makes dry, crumbly kurabie.","s":1},{"t":"Roll balls of equal size, roughly golf-ball size, and flatten slightly so they bake evenly. Space them 5 cm apart because they spread.","s":3},{"t":"Pull them out when they are light golden and still slightly soft in the middle. They firm up as they cool on the tray; baking until brown gives dry cookies.","s":5}],"rl":"SMSWFLXGF"},"53280":{"lv":"RRRRRROREE","w":{"0":"Creates a steamy, braising liquid in the roasting dish so the bird stays moist and gives you pan juices; skipping risks burnt drippings.","1":"Sweetens and flavors the pan juices as it roasts; skipping makes the sauce thinner.","2":"Coats the chicken and carries the garlic and spice; any other oil or butter will work.","3":"Adds tangy sweetness to the marinade; a swap with vinegar plus sugar works well.","4":"Emulsifies the marinade and adds tang; plain yellow mustard also works.","5":"Gives savory flavor to the marinade and juices; skipping makes the chicken blander.","6":"Light peppery warmth; skip it.","7":"Gives the signature heat of Algerian roast chicken; reduce to 1/2 tsp for milder."},"sub":{"2":{"x":"5 tbsp vegetable oil or 5 tbsp melted butter","d":"Butter gives richer browning; vegetable oil is neutral.","k":["vegetable oil","butter"]},"3":{"x":"2 tbsp red wine vinegar plus 1 tsp sugar","d":"Sharper and less syrupy than balsamic; the sugar mimics its sweetness.","k":["red wine vinegar","sugar"]},"4":{"x":"1 tbsp yellow mustard","d":"Milder and more vinegary, but it emulsifies the marinade just as well.","k":["mustard"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Pat the skin dry before it goes in the dish, and baste every 20 minutes with the pan juices. Dry skin crisps; wet skin steams.","s":1},{"t":"Roast to 165F (74C) in the thickest part of the thigh near the bone. The legs are done when juices run clear and the leg wiggles loosely.","s":2},{"t":"Rest the chicken 10 to 15 minutes and simmer the pan juices on the stove for 5 minutes to make a sauce; the onion and garlic melt into it.","s":2}],"rl":"MXFAXXZXZP"},"53287":{"lv":"ERRRRRROOROEOREEER","w":{"1":"Adds sweetness and savory depth to the sauce base; skipping makes it slightly flatter.","2":"Fresh, green herb flavor; skip it and the tagine will taste heavier and less lively.","3":"Fresh herb that balances the sweet fruit; skip or use more cilantro.","4":"Gives the sauce its golden color and earthy flavor; skipping makes it paler.","5":"Warm, earthy spice typical of North African braises; skipping makes it less aromatic.","6":"Adds color and mild sweetness; skip or use extra turmeric.","7":"A small warm bite; can be skipped.","8":"A small amount of heat; skip it for a milder dish.","9":"Seasons the sauce and chicken; needed or the tagine tastes flat.","10":"Light warmth; skip.","12":"Drizzled over the chicken to carry the spices; any oil or butter works.","13":"Sweet counterpoint to the spices; skip them or use more prunes.","14":"Braising liquid for the vegetables and chicken; water with bouillon also works.","17":"Adds sticky sweetness and richness to the sauce; skipping makes it more savory and less traditional."},"sub":{"12":{"x":"1 tbsp vegetable oil or butter","d":"Neutral oil or butter work fine; olive oil adds a faint fruitiness.","k":["vegetable oil","butter"]},"14":{"x":"1 cup water with 1/2 chicken bouillon cube","d":"Slightly less rounded flavor; adjust salt as bouillon is salty.","k":["water","chicken bouillon"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Use bone-in, skin-on thighs. Cook them until the meat is tender and reaches at least 165F (74C), though dark meat tastes better closer to 175F (80C) where it turns silky.","s":4},{"t":"Cut the sweet potato and carrots into large chunks. They cook faster than the chicken and turn to mush if cut small; add the dried fruit late so it plumps without dissolving.","s":3},{"t":"Soak the raisins in hot water first, as written, so they plump up and do not draw liquid out of the sauce. Finish with fresh cilantro for contrast.","s":1}],"rl":"XXXXXXXXXZZPFWMBVW"},"53288":{"lv":"ERRRROR","w":{"1":"Softens the onion and gives the salad a silky finish; vegetable oil also works.","2":"Adds mild sweetness and bite once softened; skipping gives a plainer pepper salad.","3":"Pungent depth that makes flafla savory; skipping makes it quite mild.","4":"Seasons the peppers; needed since roasted peppers are sweet and flat without salt.","5":"Light warmth; skip.","6":"Adds juice and acidity; skipping makes the salad drier and sweeter."},"sub":{"1":{"x":"1 tbsp vegetable oil","d":"Neutral flavor and a little less fruity.","k":["vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Roast the peppers until blackened in spots and collapsed, then cover them for 10 minutes. The steam loosens the skins so they peel off cleanly.","s":0},{"t":"Do not rinse the peppers when peeling; the water washes away the smoky juices that give flavor. Wipe off stray skin with your fingers.","s":1},{"t":"Simmer the peppers with the onion, garlic and tomato until the liquid reduces and it turns jammy. The salad tastes better after an hour or the next day, served warm or room temperature.","s":2}],"rl":"VFXXZZA"},"53281":{"lv":"ERRROEOE","w":{"1":"Gives savory depth to both meat and sauce; skipping makes the kefta bland.","2":"Adds moisture and sweetness to the meat and sauce; skipping makes the kefta a bit drier.","3":"Seasons the meat and sauce; needed or both taste flat.","4":"Light warmth; skip.","6":"A small pinch of herb; skip."},"tips":[{"t":"Use 80/20 ground beef and mix gently only until the garlic and onion are combined. Overworking makes tight, rubbery meatballs.","s":0},{"t":"Cook the kefta to 160F (71C) inside, not just brown outside. Cut one open to check that there is no pink, and finish in the tomato sauce if needed.","s":1},{"t":"Authentic kefta is usually spiced. Add 1 tsp cumin and 1/2 tsp paprika to the meat if you have them, and reduce the tomatoes until the sauce coats a spoon.","s":2}],"rl":"PXXZZVGM"},"53282":{"lv":"EEEROREROOR","w":{"3":"Seasons the carrots and brings out their sweetness; needed.","4":"Light warmth; skip.","5":"Adds a sweet, warm Algerian note; a small amount, but skipping loses a signature flavor.","7":"Gives savory depth to the oil; skipping makes it less pungent.","8":"Small herbal note; skip.","9":"A subtle background aroma; skip.","10":"Brightens the sweet carrots; vinegar also works."},"sub":{"10":{"x":"1 tsp white vinegar or 1 tsp lime juice","d":"Slightly sharper; a few drops is enough.","k":["white vinegar","lime juice"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Steam the carrots just until tender with a little bite, about 8 to 10 minutes. They keep cooking in the warm oil and turn mushy if overcooked.","s":0},{"t":"Bloom the spices and garlic in the oil on low heat for a minute. Cumin and cinnamon need the fat to bloom, but garlic burns fast and tastes bitter.","s":1},{"t":"Serve warm or at room temperature. The carrots are best after 30 minutes in the spiced oil, which they soak up; add the lemon juice last.","s":2}],"rl":"MVFZZXXXXXA"},"53284":{"lv":"EERRRREEERE","w":{"2":"Fries the garlic and jalapeno for the sauce; any oil works.","3":"Gives pungent savory depth to the sauce; skipping makes it flatter.","4":"Provides heat in the sauce; skipping makes it mild but still tasty.","5":"Seasons the sauce; roasted vegetables taste flat without it.","9":"Enriches the dough and makes it tender; melted butter works.","10":"Used to pan-fry each round; any neutral oil works."},"sub":{"2":{"x":"1 tbsp vegetable oil","d":"Neutral; less fruity.","k":["vegetable oil"]},"9":{"x":"4 tbsp melted butter","d":"Richer, more tender bread with a buttery flavor.","k":["butter"]},"10":{"x":"6 tbsp vegetable oil","d":"Neutral and a bit less flavorful; fine for frying.","k":["vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Broil the peppers and tomatoes until the skins blister and blacken. This char is the smoky flavor of the sauce; peel them after a few minutes of cooling.","s":0},{"t":"Rub the oil into the semolina well and add the water slowly. The dough should hold together when squeezed; rest it 15 minutes so the semolina hydrates and rolls without cracking.","s":2},{"t":"Fry each round over medium heat so the center cooks through before the outside darkens. Press it flat and flip once.","s":3}],"rl":"VVFXXZBZMFF"},"53286":{"lv":"EEREEEREREEEO","w":{"2":"Adds nutty crunch inside and on top of the loaf; skipping is fine but loses its signature topping.","6":"Enriches and softens the crumb; melted butter works as well.","8":"Yolk glaze gives the crust its glossy shine; skipping leaves it paler but the bread is the same.","12":"Just thins the egg yolk for brushing; skip it and use a little milk instead."},"sub":{"6":{"x":"1/4 cup melted butter","d":"Richer, slightly more tender crumb with a buttery flavor.","k":["butter"]}},"tips":[{"t":"The dough starts as a sticky batter. Let the first mix bubble until frothy, about an hour, so the yeast is active before the flour goes in.","s":1},{"t":"Resist adding much flour. A slightly sticky dough gives soft bread; too much flour makes it dense. Use flour on your hands only to shape it.","s":4},{"t":"Bake until golden and the loaf sounds hollow when tapped on the bottom, or about 200F (93C) inside. Cool before cutting so the crumb sets.","s":7}],"rl":"SSGWLZFSGMSSM"},"53290":{"lv":"RRROEEEE","w":{"0":"Binds the cheese filling and keeps it from leaking out; skipping makes the filling runnier.","1":"Fresh herb flavor that cuts the richness of the cheese; skipping makes the filling heavy.","2":"Savory kick for the filling; skipping makes it milder.","3":"Light heat in the filling; skip.","5":"Melts smoothly and adds nutty flavor; Swiss cheese or more gouda also works."},"sub":{"5":{"x":"6 oz Swiss cheese or 6 oz extra gouda","d":"Swiss is nearly identical; extra gouda is milder and creamier.","k":["swiss cheese","gouda"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Keep the phyllo covered with a damp towel while working. Exposed sheets dry in minutes and crack when folded.","s":1},{"t":"Brush butter lightly between each layer and fold the triangles snugly. Too much filling bursts the seam and the cheese leaks onto the tray.","s":1},{"t":"Check at 20 minutes. They are ready when they are deep golden and crisp; 30 minutes can be too long for thin phyllo, and burnt tips taste bitter.","s":3}],"rl":"TXXXPPBF"},"53283":{"lv":"EREEORREEEER","w":{"1":"Adds fresh chili heat to the dersa; skipping lowers heat unless you add more cayenne.","4":"Light warmth; skip.","5":"Adds heat; skip if the red chili supplies enough.","6":"Seasons the paste; needed or the stew is bland.","7":"Carries the spice paste and gives it a fruity base; any neutral oil works.","11":"Final seasoning; potatoes need salt to taste lively."},"sub":{"7":{"x":"2 tbsp vegetable oil","d":"Neutral and a little less fruity.","k":["vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Pound the garlic with the spices into a smooth paste before adding oil. This dersa is the flavor base, and grinding releases more flavor than chopping.","s":0},{"t":"Fry the dersa on medium heat just until fragrant, 2 to 4 minutes. Add the tomato paste and cook it for a minute before the water so it loses its raw taste.","s":1},{"t":"Simmer with just enough water to barely cover. Mash a few potato pieces into the sauce at the end to thicken it, then serve with bread.","s":1}],"rl":"XXXXZXZFBAMZ"},"53289":{"lv":"EEEORRRRRRRRERRRER","w":{"3":"Light warmth; skip.","4":"Adds color and mild sweetness to the soup; skipping makes it paler.","5":"A small warm note typical of chorba hamra; skipping makes it less distinctive.","6":"Seasons the whole pot; needed.","7":"Browns the lamb and onions and carries the spices; any neutral oil or butter works.","8":"Fresh herb finish; skipping makes the soup less lively.","9":"Cool brightness against the rich lamb; skip if you have none.","10":"Flavors the broth and is removed later; skip with a mild loss of savory depth.","11":"Adds protein and creamy bite; skipping makes it lighter.","13":"Vegetable bulk and sweetness; skip or add more potato.","14":"Adds sweetness and body; skip.","15":"Adds depth and red color to the broth; skipping makes it thinner.","17":"Thickens the soup and makes it hearty; skipping makes it thinner."},"sub":{"7":{"x":"3 tbsp olive oil or 3 tbsp butter","d":"Butter gives richness; olive oil adds a faint fruity flavor.","k":["olive oil","butter"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Rinse and soak the freekeh first, as written, to remove dust and soften it. Add it late so it stays chewy instead of turning to porridge.","s":0},{"t":"Simmer the lamb gently until tender before adding the vegetables; hard boiling toughens it. Add the potato and freekeh in the order given so each is just done together.","s":1},{"t":"Stir in the herbs at the end for fresh flavor and serve with a lemon wedge; the acid brightens the tomato and lamb.","s":4}],"rl":"BPXZXXZFXXXVMVVAAB"},"53285":{"lv":"EERREREOROEEEERRROO","w":{"2":"Prevents the syrup from crystallizing and adds a little brightness; skip if needed.","3":"Gives the syrup its perfume; skipping makes it plain sugar syrup.","5":"Makes the dough pliable and tender; melted butter works.","7":"A few drops that relax the dough; skipping is fine.","8":"Brings out the dough flavor; skipping makes it bland.","9":"Gives the dough a floral aroma; skipping is fine as the filling has it too.","14":"Rounds out the almond filling; skipping makes the filling less sweet-smelling.","15":"Lemon zest brightens the rich almond filling; skipping is a small loss.","16":"Gives the almond filling its floral aroma; skipping makes it plainer.","17":"Just for dusting the rolling surface; flour works.","18":"Garnish; skip."},"sub":{"5":{"x":"2 tbsp melted butter","d":"Slightly richer and more tender; the dough is a little softer.","k":["butter"]},"17":{"x":"1 to 2 tbsp flour","d":"Works as a dusting; use sparingly so it does not toughen the dough.","k":["flour"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Roll the dough very thin, 1 to 2 mm, and rest it first for 30 minutes so it does not shrink back. A pasta machine gives an even sheet.","s":4},{"t":"Grind the blanched, dried almonds fine but not to oil. The paste should be moist enough to hold together, so add a little egg white or water if crumbly.","s":3},{"t":"Soak the warm baked tarts in cooled syrup so they absorb it. Hot syrup on hot tarts turns them soggy; the syrup should be thick like thin honey.","s":5}],"rl":"WMAXSFSAZXBWTLXXXGG"},"53291":{"lv":"EERREREERERROR","w":{"2":"Slightly sweetens the pastry dough; skipping is fine.","3":"Warm note in the almond filling; skipping leaves it nuttier.","5":"Floral aroma that is typical of the filling; skipping makes it plainer.","8":"Brings out the pastry flavor; needed so the dough is not flat.","10":"Makes the dough tender; melted butter works.","11":"Gives the honey glaze its floral perfume; skipping makes the glaze plain honey.","12":"Garnish; skip.","13":"Glazes the warm horns; skip it and dust with powdered sugar instead."},"sub":{"10":{"x":"3 tbsp melted butter","d":"Richer and more tender dough with a buttery flavor.","k":["butter"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Dip them while still slightly warm.","s":8},{"t":"Roll the dough thin and wrap the almond logs snugly, curving them into crescents. Thick pastry bakes pale and heavy; thin pastry gives a crisp shell.","s":6},{"t":"Bake only until pale golden. If they brown, the almond filling dries out and the pastry loses its delicate, short texture.","s":7}],"rl":"BWWXTXSFZSFXGW"},"3253":{"lv":"EEEE","tips":[{"t":"Use the starter at its peak, when it is bubbly and has doubled.","s":0},{"t":"Wet your hands for the stretch and folds instead of adding flour. Each set builds gluten strength; the dough should feel smooth and billowy by the fourth set.","s":1},{"t":"The covered Dutch oven traps steam so the loaf springs fully before the crust sets. Remove the lid for the color; a finished loaf reads 205 to 210F (96 to 99C) inside.","s":5}],"rl":"LSZM"},"3254":{"lv":"EEEEER","w":{"0":"Softens the crumb and adds richness; water plus extra butter works but is slightly less tender.","5":"Tenderizes the crumb; oil also works."},"sub":{"0":{"x":"1 1/4 cups warm water plus 1 tbsp extra butter","d":"A bit less soft and golden crust; still a good sandwich loaf.","k":["water","butter"]},"5":{"x":"3 tbsp vegetable oil","d":"Slightly less buttery flavor; crumb stays soft.","k":["vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Warm the milk to 105 to 110F (40 to 43C). Hotter kills the yeast, and cold milk makes the foam very slow.","s":0},{"t":"Knead until the dough passes the windowpane test; a small piece should stretch thin enough to see light without tearing. This gives a tall, fine-crumbed loaf.","s":1},{"t":"Bake until the loaf reaches 190F (88C) in the center. Brushing butter on the hot loaf softens the crust, and wait 30 minutes before slicing or the crumb is gummy.","s":6}],"rl":"MLWSZF"},"3255":{"lv":"EREEERE","w":{"1":"Feeds the yeast and softens the whole wheat bitterness; brown sugar works.","5":"Keeps the whole wheat crumb softer; vegetable oil or butter works."},"sub":{"1":{"x":"3 tbsp brown sugar","d":"Close in sweetness; slightly less moist and floral.","k":["brown sugar"]},"5":{"x":"2 tbsp vegetable oil or 2 tbsp melted butter","d":"Neutral oil or butter work the same way; butter adds flavor.","k":["vegetable oil","butter"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Whole wheat is thirsty, so rest the dough 20 minutes after mixing to soften the bran before kneading.","s":0},{"t":"Do not add much extra flour; the dough should stay slightly tacky. Dry whole wheat dough makes a dense, crumbly loaf.","s":1},{"t":"Bake until it reads 200F (93C) in the middle and sounds hollow, then cool at least 30 minutes so the crumb finishes setting.","s":5}],"rl":"LWSSZFM"},"3256":{"lv":"EEEEEREERE","w":{"0":"Softens and enriches the crumb; water plus a little extra butter works but gives a slightly less tender loaf.","5":"Tenderizes the crumb; oil also works.","8":"The cinnamon-sugar swirl; skipping gives plain raisin bread with no ribbon of sweetness."},"sub":{"0":{"x":"1 cup warm water plus 1 tbsp butter","d":"Slightly less rich and soft; still a good loaf.","k":["water","butter"]},"5":{"x":"3 tbsp vegetable oil","d":"Slightly less buttery flavor; crumb stays soft.","k":["vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Soak the raisins in hot water for 10 minutes and pat them dry before kneading them in. Dry raisins pull moisture from the dough and burn on the surface.","s":1},{"t":"Brush only a thin film of water before the cinnamon sugar and roll tightly, pinching the seam. Too much wet filling leaves a gap under the swirl.","s":3},{"t":"Tent with foil when the top browns, as written. The sugar in the filling and dough colors the crust fast; bake until the center reads about 190F (88C).","s":5}],"rl":"MLWSZFSVWX"},"3257":{"lv":"EREEROE","w":{"1":"Feeds the yeast and adds a little color; plain sugar does the same job.","4":"Boiling with honey gives the crust a faint sweetness, shine and better browning; sugar works, skipping is fine.","5":"Gives a glossy, deeper-brown crust; skipping leaves the bagels matte but still good."},"sub":{"1":{"x":"1 tbsp sugar","d":"Same yeast food; loses the faint honey flavor.","k":["sugar"]},"4":{"x":"2 tbsp brown sugar","d":"Helps browning and shine just like honey.","k":["brown sugar"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Bagel dough is very stiff, so knead the full 10 minutes until smooth; it should feel much firmer than bread dough.","s":1},{"t":"Boil each bagel for 1 minute per side in gently bubbling water. The short boil gelatinizes the surface starch, which is what gives bagels their chewy, shiny crust.","s":5},{"t":"To test the proof, drop a shaped bagel into a bowl of cold water; if it floats it is ready to boil. Rest in the fridge overnight for better flavor.","s":4}],"rl":"LWSZWCM"},"3258":{"lv":"EREERREE","w":{"1":"Feeds the yeast and adds a little color; plain sugar does the same job.","4":"Boiling with honey gives the crust a faint sweetness, shine and better browning; sugar works.","5":"Acts as glue for the topping and gives deep color; skipping makes the seasoning fall off.","6":"The topping is the defining flavor of everything bagels; without it they are plain bagels"},"sub":{"1":{"x":"1 tbsp sugar","d":"Same yeast food; loses the faint honey flavor.","k":["sugar"]},"6":{"x":"3 tbsp mix of 1 tbsp sesame seeds, 1 tbsp poppy seeds, 2 tsp dried minced onion, 1 tsp garlic powder and 1/2 tsp coarse salt","d":"Close to store-bought; salt level is lower, so taste and adjust.","k":["sesame seeds","poppy seeds","dried onion","garlic powder"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Brush the egg on right after boiling and press the seasoning on straight away. The egg is the glue and sets as it bakes.","s":6},{"t":"Add the seasoning only to the top half and keep the pan clear; seeds that fall onto the pan burn and taste bitter.","s":6},{"t":"Do not crowd the boiling pot; two or three bagels at a time keep the water at a simmer. Drain them briefly on a rack so they do not stick to the pan.","s":5}],"rl":"LWSZWCGM"},"3260":{"lv":"EEEREEERER","w":{"3":"Sharpens the sweetness and flavor of the muffins; without it they taste flat.","4":"Keeps the crumb moist and tender; melted butter gives more flavor.","6":"Liquid for the batter; yogurt thinned with water also works.","7":"Adds a warm aroma that rounds out the berries; skip and the muffins taste plainer.","9":"Gives the crunchy sugar top; skipping loses the sparkle but not the flavor."},"sub":{"4":{"x":"1/2 cup melted butter","d":"More buttery flavor, slightly less moist the next day.","k":["butter"]},"6":{"x":"1/2 cup plain yogurt thinned with 2 tbsp water","d":"Slightly tangier and denser; still works with the baking powder.","k":["plain yogurt","water"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Start at 400F for 5 minutes, then lower the heat as written. The initial blast makes the batter rise fast and domes the tops before the crust sets.","s":5},{"t":"Fold only until the flour streaks disappear; lumps are fine. Overmixing develops gluten and gives tunnels and tough muffins.","s":3},{"t":"Toss the berries in 1 tbsp of the measured flour before folding them in so they stay suspended. Use frozen berries straight from the freezer so they do not bleed purple.","s":3}],"rl":"SWLZFSMXVG"},"3261":{"lv":"EEEEREERR","w":{"1":"Adds fat for a tender crumb; vegetable oil gives a similar texture.","4":"Adds aroma that deepens the banana flavor; skipping makes them plainer.","7":"Sharpens flavor and balances the sweetness; needed.","8":"Warm spice note with banana; skip without a big loss."},"sub":{"1":{"x":"1/2 cup vegetable oil","d":"Moister crumb that stays soft longer, with less buttery flavor.","k":["vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Use bananas that are heavily speckled or black. The starch has turned to sugar, so the muffins are sweeter and more fragrant, and the acidity activates the baking soda.","s":0},{"t":"Mix the baking soda in right before folding in the flour, then bake right away; its reaction with the banana begins immediately.","s":2},{"t":"Fold gently until the flour disappears. Overmixing makes the muffins tough and tunneled; bake until the tops spring back.","s":2}],"rl":"VFWSXSLZX"},"3262":{"lv":"EEERREEEERR","w":{"2":"Keeps the muffins moist; melted butter works.","3":"Gives molasses flavor and moistness; white sugar works but is less deep.","4":"Adds sweetness and moisture and helps browning; maple syrup works.","9":"Balances the sweetness and bran; needed.","10":"Sweet chewy bits that balance the bran; skip them if needed."},"sub":{"2":{"x":"1/4 cup melted butter","d":"Richer flavor and slightly firmer crumb.","k":["butter"]},"3":{"x":"1/3 cup white sugar","d":"Less molasses flavor and a paler muffin.","k":["sugar"]},"4":{"x":"1/4 cup maple syrup","d":"Slightly less floral but similar sweetness and moisture.","k":["maple syrup"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Do not skip the 10-minute bran soak. Dry bran steals moisture from the batter and produces gritty, dry muffins; soaked bran gives a tender, moist crumb.","s":1},{"t":"Combine the dry ingredients first and fold them in just until mixed. The batter will be thick and slightly wet, which is correct.","s":3},{"t":"Fill the cups only three quarters full so the muffins do not spill. They are done when they spring back lightly, since bran muffins stay darker and appear done before they are.","s":5}],"rl":"BMFWWSSLLZV"},"3263":{"lv":"EEROEEEER","w":{"2":"Seasons the batter; cheese is salty, but the muffins taste flat without it.","3":"A subtle savory note; skip and the cheese and chives still carry the flavor.","6":"Keeps the muffins moist; melted butter works.","8":"Gives fresh onion flavor and color; green onion works."},"sub":{"6":{"x":"1/3 cup melted butter","d":"Richer, more buttery flavor.","k":["butter"]},"8":{"x":"2 tbsp finely sliced green onion","d":"Slightly stronger onion flavor.","k":["green onion"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Shred a block of sharp cheddar yourself. Pre-shredded cheese has anti-caking starch that makes the crumb dry, and sharp cheese holds its flavor through baking.","s":1},{"t":"Stir until just combined. Overmixing makes cheese muffins tough and rubbery; the batter should look lumpy.","s":2},{"t":"Reserve some cheese for the top, as written. It melts into a golden, crisp crust, and the muffins are best eaten warm.","s":3}],"rl":"SLZXFMFSX"},"3264":{"lv":"EEEREEEEEERR","w":{"3":"Seasons the batter so the lemon tastes bright, not flat.","6":"Keeps the muffins tender; melted butter works.","10":"Makes the sweet-tart glaze; skipping leaves plain muffins.","11":"Makes the glaze; use more juice if too stiff."},"sub":{"6":{"x":"1/2 cup melted butter","d":"Richer and a little less moist after a day.","k":["butter"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Rub the zest into the sugar with your fingers for a minute before mixing. The oils release into the sugar and give a deeper lemon aroma.","s":2},{"t":"Stir only until the dry streaks vanish; overmixing toughens the crumb. Poppy seeds are distributed evenly if you whisk them into the flour first.","s":1},{"t":"Cool the muffins fully before glazing. If they are warm, the glaze melts and soaks in instead of forming a crisp, white drizzle.","s":5}],"rl":"SWLZXMFSAXGG"},"3265":{"lv":"EEEEEEEEEERRRO","w":{"10":"Gives the frosting its tang and creamy texture; skipping gives a plain sweet icing.","11":"Softens and enriches the cream cheese frosting; skip if you prefer a firmer frosting.","12":"Sweetens and thickens the frosting; needed for body.","13":"Rounds the frosting flavor; skip.","0":"The warm liquid that hydrates and activates the dough; it cannot form without liquid"},"sub":{"0":{"x":"1 cup warm water plus 1 tbsp butter","d":"Slightly less rich and tender, but the rolls are still soft.","k":["water","butter"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Cut the rolls with unflavored dental floss or a thread instead of a knife. A knife squashes the spiral; the floss slices cleanly.","s":4},{"t":"Roll the dough tightly from the long edge and pinch the seam. A loose roll unwinds in the oven and leaves a gap in the center.","s":4},{"t":"Frost while the rolls are still warm, not hot. The warmth melts the frosting into a glaze, while very hot rolls make it run off.","s":7}],"rl":"MLWSZFSWXFGGGX"},"3266":{"lv":"EEEEEEER","w":{"7":"Softens the crust and adds a buttery shine; skipping leaves the crust a little firmer.","0":"The warm liquid that hydrates and activates the dough; it cannot form without liquid"},"sub":{"0":{"x":"1 cup warm water plus 1 tbsp butter","d":"Slightly less soft and rich; still a fine roll.","k":["water","butter"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Shape each piece by cupping it on the counter and rolling in circles until the surface is tight. A taut surface gives round, tall rolls with an even crumb.","s":3},{"t":"Place them close together so they touch. They rise against each other and bake into soft, pull-apart sides.","s":3},{"t":"Brush with melted butter while still hot so it soaks into the crust; the bread softens and the shine is better.","s":6}],"rl":"MLWSZFSF"},"3275":{"lv":"EEEREEEERR","w":{"3":"Rounds out the sweetness and adds aroma; skipping makes the bread a little flat.","8":"Seasons the loaf and balances the sugar; needed.","9":"Warm spice that suits zucchini bread; skip for a plainer loaf.","1":"The main fat and moisture of the quick bread; without it the loaf is dry and crumbly"},"sub":{"1":{"x":"1 cup melted butter","d":"Richer flavor, slightly drier the next day than oil.","k":["butter"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Squeeze the shredded zucchini in a towel until no more water drips out. Extra moisture makes a gummy loaf that sinks in the middle.","s":1},{"t":"Do not peel the zucchini. The skin melts into the loaf and adds flecks of color.","s":1},{"t":"Bake until a toothpick comes out clean and the top is deep golden; cool in the pan 10 minutes so the loaf does not break when turned out.","s":4}],"rl":"SFWXVSLLZX"},"3276":{"lv":"EEEEREEERRO","w":{"8":"Seasons the bread and balances the sweetness; needed.","9":"Warm spice that defines pumpkin bread; skipping makes it plainer.","10":"A small amount of extra warmth; skip.","1":"The main fat and moisture of the quick bread; without it the loaf is dry and crumbly","4":"Adds caramel flavor and moisture; white sugar in the recipe still sweetens the loaf"},"sub":{"1":{"x":"1 cup melted butter","d":"Richer flavor, slightly drier crumb after a day.","k":["butter"]},"4":{"x":"1/2 cup white sugar","d":"Slightly less moist and less caramel flavor.","k":["sugar"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Use plain pumpkin puree, not pumpkin pie filling. Pie filling is already sweetened and spiced, so the bread would be too sweet and the balance off.","s":1},{"t":"If the top browns before the center is set, tent with foil. The loaf is dense, so check with a toothpick in the center.","s":4},{"t":"The flavor improves when the loaf rests wrapped overnight, so the spices mellow and the crumb becomes more moist.","s":5}],"rl":"VFSWWSLLZXX"},"3625":{"lv":"EEEEERO","w":{"3":"Makes the dough tender and adds flavor; vegetable oil or melted butter works.","4":"Hydrates the dough; water works but the crackers are slightly less tender.","5":"Adds toasty crunch and flavor on top; skip.","6":"Finishing crunch of salt on top; skip and use more salt in the dough."},"sub":{"3":{"x":"1/4 cup vegetable oil or 1/4 cup melted butter","d":"Butter gives richer flavor; both give crisp results.","k":["vegetable oil","butter"]},"4":{"x":"2/3 cup water","d":"Slightly less tender and rich, but still crisp.","k":["water"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Roll as thin as you can, about 1/16 inch, directly on parchment. Thick spots stay chewy while thin spots burn.","s":2},{"t":"Prick the dough all over with a fork so steam can escape. Without the holes the crackers puff into pillows instead of lying flat and crisp.","s":3},{"t":"The edges brown first, so pull any done crackers off and return the rest to the oven. They crisp further as they cool on the rack.","s":5}],"rl":"SZLFMGG"},"3627":{"lv":"EREERERRE","w":{"1":"Feeds the yeast and gives light sweetness; plain sugar works.","4":"Softens the crumb; oil works.","6":"Gives the dark, glossy crust and holds the salt on; skipping leaves it paler and duller.","7":"Gives pretzel-style salty crunch; skipping loses the classic topping."},"sub":{"1":{"x":"1 tbsp white sugar","d":"Same yeast food with less molasses flavor.","k":["sugar"]},"4":{"x":"3 tbsp vegetable oil","d":"Softer, less buttery rolls.","k":["vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"The baking soda bath turns the surface alkaline, which gives the deep brown, chewy pretzel crust. Add the soda slowly, as it foams, and use a non-aluminum pot because it discolors.","s":3},{"t":"Bake right after boiling, while the crust is wet. Add the coarse salt right after the egg wash so it sticks.","s":4},{"t":"Slash the tops with a sharp blade before baking so they open in a neat line instead of tearing.","s":4}],"rl":"LWSZFXCGM"},"120":{"lv":"EEEEEORR","w":{"3":"Thins the batter and hydrates the oats; water gives a slightly less rich pancake.","5":"A small sweet-warm note; skip.","6":"Greases the pan and gives butter flavor; oil works.","7":"For serving; sweetens the pancakes, which are only lightly sweet from the banana."},"sub":{"3":{"x":"1/2 cup water","d":"Slightly less rich and creamy, but the batter works the same.","k":["water"]},"6":{"x":"1 tbsp vegetable oil","d":"Neutral and a little less flavorful.","k":["vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Rest the blended batter 5 minutes. The oats absorb liquid and thicken it so the pancakes hold their shape.","s":0},{"t":"Cook on medium-low heat in small portions. Oat and banana pancakes are fragile and burn easily; flip only when the edges look set and bubbles appear.","s":1},{"t":"Use a ripe banana with brown spots for sweetness; a green banana makes bland, gummy pancakes.","s":0}],"rl":"BVSMLXFG"},"121":{"lv":"EEERRRRRR","w":{"3":"Warm spice that defines cinnamon French toast; skipping loses its identity.","4":"Rounds out the custard flavor; skip and it tastes plainer.","5":"Helps browning and a light sweet crust; skipping is fine.","7":"For serving; the toast has only a little sugar so syrup finishes it.","8":"Fresh, tart contrast to the sweet toast; skip or use other fruit.","2":"Forms the custard soak for the bread; without it there is no French toast","6":"Pan fat for browning; the toast still cooks with a little oil or a nonstick pan"},"sub":{"2":{"x":"3/4 cup half-and-half or 3/4 cup evaporated milk","d":"Richer, more custardy toast.","k":["half-and-half","evaporated milk"]},"6":{"x":"2 tbsp vegetable oil","d":"Browns fine but with less buttery flavor.","k":["vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Use thick, slightly stale bread. Fresh bread gets soggy and tears when dipped; stale bread absorbs the custard without collapsing.","s":1},{"t":"Cook over medium heat for a golden crust and a fully set center. If the toast browns before the middle sets, finish in a 350F oven for 5 minutes.","s":2},{"t":"Dip for only 5 seconds per side so the bread is coated but not saturated. Keep finished slices warm on a rack in a 200F oven.","s":1}],"rl":"BSMXXWFGG"},"122":{"lv":"EERREREO","w":{"2":"Adds sweetness, color and soft texture to the hash; skipping makes it more potato-and-sausage.","3":"Builds savory sweetness when browned with the sausage; skipping makes the hash less rounded.","4":"Crisps the potatoes; any neutral oil works.","5":"Adds smoky color and flavor to the potatoes; skipping makes them plainer.","7":"Fresh garnish at the end; skip it."},"sub":{"4":{"x":"2 tbsp vegetable oil","d":"Neutral and a bit less flavorful; crisps the potatoes just as well.","k":["vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"The microwave step cooks the potatoes through so the skillet only has to crisp them. Dry the cubes on a towel afterward; surface moisture is what keeps them from browning.","s":0},{"t":"Leave the potatoes untouched for 4 to 5 minutes between stirs. Constant stirring prevents the crust from forming and you get soft, steamed cubes instead.","s":0},{"t":"Cook the sausage until no pink remains, at least 160F (71C). Press wells into the hash and cover so the whites set while the yolks stay soft.","s":2}],"rl":"BPVXFXPG"},"123":{"lv":"EERRRE","w":{"2":"Adds creaminess and tang and thickens the oats; skipping gives a looser, less rich result.","3":"Sweetens the oats; honey works as well.","4":"Gives the apple-cinnamon flavor; skipping leaves the oats plainer.","1":"The liquid that softens the oats overnight; without it the oats stay dry"},"sub":{"1":{"x":"1 cup water","d":"Less creamy and rich; add an extra 2 tbsp yogurt to compensate.","k":["water"]},"3":{"x":"2 tbsp honey","d":"Slightly more floral; stir well so it dissolves in the cold milk.","k":["honey"],"wn":"Honey is not safe for children under 1 year"}},"tips":[{"t":"Use rolled oats, not instant or steel-cut. Rolled oats soften overnight but keep a chewy bite, while instant turns pasty and steel-cut stays hard.","s":0},{"t":"Add the diced apple in the morning, not the night before. Overnight in the liquid it goes soft and brown; fresh apple adds crunch against the creamy oats.","s":2},{"t":"Keep the jar tightly covered in the fridge and eat within 3 days. If it looks too thick in the morning, stir in a splash of milk.","s":1}],"rl":"BMTWXV"},"124":{"lv":"EERRO","w":{"2":"Melts into the eggs and gives savory richness; skipping makes the bites milder.","3":"Adds salty, smoky flavor; skip for a vegetarian version.","4":"Fresh onion bite and color; skip."},"sub":{"2":{"x":"1 cup shredded Monterey jack or Swiss","d":"Milder, creamier, and melts smoothly.","k":["monterey jack","swiss cheese"]},"3":{"x":"1/2 cup diced cooked ham","d":"Less smoky but still salty and savory.","k":["ham"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Blend the cottage cheese with the eggs until completely smooth. Any lumps stay as curds and ruin the silky texture of these bites.","s":0},{"t":"The water bath keeps the oven gentle and humid, so the eggs set to a custardy texture instead of turning rubbery. Do not skip the pan of hot water.","s":2},{"t":"They are done when the centers are set and jiggle only slightly, around 160F (71C) inside. Rest 5 minutes before loosening with a knife.","s":2}],"rl":"PPFXG"},"125":{"lv":"EEREERRRER","w":{"2":"Warm spice that suits the oats and berries; skipping makes it plainer.","5":"Sweetens and flavors the oats; honey works.","6":"Adds richness and keeps the oats moist; oil works.","7":"Rounds out the sweetness; skip and it still tastes good.","9":"Without it the baked oats taste flat and overly sweet."},"sub":{"5":{"x":"1/3 cup honey","d":"Slightly more floral and sticks more; otherwise the same.","k":["honey"]},"6":{"x":"2 tbsp vegetable oil or 2 tbsp coconut oil","d":"Slightly less buttery flavor.","k":["vegetable oil","coconut oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Baked oats without it taste flat and sweet.","s":0},{"t":"Sprinkle some blueberries on top rather than burying them all in the batter. The ones on top caramelize slightly, and frozen berries can go in straight from the freezer.","s":2},{"t":"Rest 10 minutes after baking so it sets enough to slice. It reheats well and keeps 4 days in the fridge.","s":3}],"rl":"BLXMSWFXVZ"},"126":{"lv":"EEERR","w":{"3":"Melts over the hot egg and holds the sandwich together; cheddar or any sliced cheese works.","4":"Fries the eggs with butter flavor; oil works."},"sub":{"3":{"x":"4 slices cheddar or provolone","d":"Sharper flavor and less creamy melt than American.","k":["cheddar","provolone"]},"4":{"x":"1 tbsp vegetable oil","d":"Neutral and less flavorful.","k":["vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Press the patties thin, a little wider than the muffin, since they shrink. Cook to 160F (71C), with no pink left inside.","s":0},{"t":"Place the cheese on the egg or sausage right as it comes off the pan so the residual heat melts it. Stack immediately and wrap the sandwich a minute to steam together.","s":2},{"t":"Split the muffins with a fork rather than a knife. The rough nooks and crannies toast crisper and hold melted butter.","s":2}],"rl":"BPPFF"},"128":{"lv":"EREREERRROR","w":{"1":"Adds light sweetness and browning; skipping makes the pancakes plainer.","3":"Brings out the flavor of the batter; without it the pancakes taste flat.","6":"Adds richness and tenderness; oil works.","7":"Gives aroma that lifts the pancakes; skip.","8":"Burst of fruit through the pancakes; skip for plain.","9":"Sweet topping; skip or swap with more berries.","10":"For serving; the pancakes are lightly sweet on their own."},"sub":{"6":{"x":"4 tbsp vegetable oil","d":"Less buttery flavor but equally tender.","k":["vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Line or well-grease the sheet pan, and preheat it for a few minutes if you can. A hot pan gives crisp golden edges and makes the pancakes release cleanly.","s":0},{"t":"Do not overmix; a few lumps are fine. Overmixed batter makes tough, rubbery pancakes with no rise.","s":1},{"t":"Press the toppings in lightly after scattering, so they do not slide off. Bake until the edges are golden and the center springs back, then cut into squares.","s":2}],"rl":"SWLZMSFXVGG"},"129":{"lv":"EEER","w":{"3":"Sweetens the berries and draws out their juices; maple syrup works too."},"sub":{"3":{"x":"2 tbsp maple syrup","d":"Slightly less floral, with caramel notes.","k":["maple syrup"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Toss the berries with honey and leave them 10 minutes. The sugar draws out their juice, making a light syrup that sweetens the plain yogurt.","s":0},{"t":"Use thick Greek yogurt so the layers hold and do not turn watery. Add the granola at the last minute, or just before serving, so it keeps crunch.","s":1},{"t":"Build layers with a spoon against the glass wall so the berries show. Put granola in the middle layers and a thin layer on top.","s":1}],"rl":"BGVW"},"3138":{"lv":"EROREERO","w":{"1":"Builds the crust and seasons the meat; steak is dull without it.","2":"Light peppery warmth; skip.","3":"Fries the eggs with butter flavor; oil works.","4":"Used for high-heat searing; any high-smoke-point oil works.","6":"Soaks up the runny yolk; skip.","7":"Fresh garnish; skip."},"sub":{"3":{"x":"1 tbsp vegetable oil","d":"Neutral and less rich.","k":["vegetable oil"]},"4":{"x":"1 tbsp canola oil or another high-heat neutral oil","d":"Avoid olive oil here as it smokes at searing heat.","k":["canola oil","vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Pat the steak very dry and let the pan get really hot before it goes in. Surface moisture causes steaming; a dry steak gets a deep brown crust.","s":0},{"t":"130F (54C) pulled off the heat rises to about 135F (57C) after resting, which is medium-rare. USDA guidance for steak is 145F (63C) with a rest, so cook to that if you prefer it safe.","s":1},{"t":"Slice against the grain to shorten the muscle fibers and keep each bite tender. Fry the eggs over medium heat so the whites set without crisping.","s":3}],"rl":"PZZFFPBG"},"3541":{"lv":"EEEEERROR","w":{"5":"Gives a gentle tang and savory depth to the custard; skipping is mild loss.","6":"Seasons the custard; sausage and cheese are salty, but without any it tastes bland.","7":"Light peppery warmth; skip.","8":"Just to grease the dish so it does not stick; oil or cooking spray works."},"sub":{"8":{"x":"1 tbsp vegetable oil or cooking spray","d":"Just prevents sticking; less buttery flavor on the bottom.","k":["vegetable oil","cooking spray"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Use day-old or lightly dried bread. Fresh bread dissolves into mush, but stale cubes absorb the custard and keep a structure.","s":2},{"t":"Drain the sausage well, or the casserole turns greasy. Overnight chilling lets the custard soak all the way through so it bakes evenly.","s":4},{"t":"Bake until the center reaches 160F (71C) and no longer jiggles. Resting 10 minutes sets the custard so squares hold together.","s":6}],"rl":"PBFSMXZZF"},"3542":{"lv":"EEERERRRRROROR","w":{"3":"Makes the custard richer and silkier; half-and-half works.","5":"Rounds the custard flavor; skipping makes it plainer.","6":"Warm spice that defines the flavor; skipping makes it plain.","7":"Brings out the sweetness and balances the dish; needed.","8":"Makes the streusel caramelly; white sugar works but is less deep.","9":"Gives the topping body and crumb; skipping makes it greasy.","10":"Warmth in the topping; skip.","11":"Makes the streusel crumbly and buttery; skip for no topping.","12":"Adds crunch to the topping; optional.","13":"Finishing sweetness; skip."},"sub":{"3":{"x":"1/2 cup half-and-half","d":"Slightly lighter but still custardy.","k":["half-and-half"]},"8":{"x":"1/2 cup white sugar","d":"Less caramel flavor and slightly crunchier topping.","k":["sugar"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Dry the bread cubes in a low oven first if they are fresh. Stale or dried bread absorbs the custard without turning to paste.","s":0},{"t":"Soak at least 1 hour or overnight and press the bread down. The center must absorb custard to bake into a creamy, set pudding.","s":2},{"t":"Keep the butter cold when making the streusel so it stays in small pieces and bakes crisp. Tent with foil if the top browns before the center sets at about 160F (71C).","s":3}],"rl":"BSMMWXXZWBXFGG"},"3543":{"lv":"EEEREREORO","w":{"1":"Salty meat layer; deli ham works.","3":"Helps the egg whites set neatly in the poaching water; skipping makes ragged whites.","5":"Brightens the rich sauce; white vinegar works.","7":"A small pinch of heat; skip.","8":"Seasons the hollandaise; needed.","9":"Fresh garnish; skip."},"sub":{"1":{"x":"4 slices deli ham","d":"Sweeter and less meaty; warm the slices as written.","k":["ham"]},"5":{"x":"2 tsp white vinegar","d":"Sharper and less fruity; start with 2 tsp as vinegar is stronger.","k":["white vinegar"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Keep the water barely simmering and the bowl above it, not touching. If the bowl gets too hot the yolks scramble. Whisk until thick, with ribbons, about 160F (71C).","s":0},{"t":"If the hollandaise splits, whisk in a teaspoon of hot water, or beat a fresh yolk in a clean bowl and drip the broken sauce in slowly.","s":1},{"t":"Use the freshest eggs for poaching and crack each into a cup first. Slide it into water that is barely trembling, not boiling, which tears the whites.","s":3}],"rl":"BPPATAFXZG"},"3544":{"lv":"EERERRORO","w":{"2":"Brightens the avocado and keeps it from browning; lime juice works.","4":"Helps the whites set neatly in the poaching water; skip with slightly more ragged whites.","5":"Brushing the toast with oil gives a crisp, flavorful surface and keeps it from getting soggy.","6":"Light heat on top; skip.","7":"Seasons the avocado; needed or it tastes flat.","8":"Light warmth; skip."},"sub":{"2":{"x":"1 tbsp lime juice","d":"Slightly more floral; works the same way.","k":["lime juice"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Crack each egg into a cup and slide it into the water so the white stays together. Strain off thin, watery white first for a neat shape.","s":3},{"t":"Toast the sourdough until dark golden and sturdy. A pale slice gets soggy under the avocado and runny yolk.","s":2},{"t":"Mash the avocado only partly and salt it well; creamy chunks give texture. Runny yolks are not recommended for pregnant women or the vulnerable; cook those longer.","s":1}],"rl":"BVAPAFXZZ"},"3545":{"lv":"EREERRROO","w":{"1":"Adds toasty crunch and richness; skipping makes the granola oat-heavy.","2":"Sweetens and binds the clusters; honey works.","3":"Crisps the oats and holds the clusters; vegetable oil works.","4":"Adds caramel flavor and crunch; skipping makes it slightly less sweet.","5":"Warm spice; skipping makes it plainer.","6":"Brings out sweetness; needed to avoid flat granola.","7":"Rounds out the sweetness; skip.","8":"Chewy sweet fruit added after baking; skip or use raisins."},"sub":{"2":{"x":"1/3 cup honey","d":"Binds just as well; slightly more floral and browns faster.","k":["honey"]},"3":{"x":"1/3 cup vegetable oil","d":"Neutral flavor, a bit less rich; still crisp.","k":["vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Press the granola down firmly into an even layer before baking and do not stir after cooling. The compact mass sets into big clusters as it cools.","s":3},{"t":"Bake at a low heat and stir only once. Watch the last 5 minutes; sugar and nuts go from golden to burned fast.","s":4},{"t":"Add the dried cranberries after baking. In the oven they dry into hard, bitter nuggets; mixing afterward keeps them chewy.","s":5}],"rl":"BGWFWXZXV"},"3546":{"lv":"EREEREEERO","w":{"1":"Helps browning and a light sweetness; skipping makes the waffles paler.","4":"Seasons the batter; needed.","6":"Gives the tangy flavor and activates the baking soda; use milk plus an acid, not plain milk.","7":"Gives richness and crisp edges; oil works.","8":"Rounds out the flavor; skip.","9":"For serving."},"sub":{"6":{"x":"1 3/4 cups milk stirred with 1 3/4 tbsp lemon juice or white vinegar, rested 5 minutes","d":"Slightly thinner and less tangy, but the acid still activates the baking soda.","k":["milk","lemon juice"]},"7":{"x":"6 tbsp vegetable oil","d":"Crisper and lighter, with less buttery flavor.","k":["vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Stop mixing while some small lumps remain. Overmixing develops gluten and makes tough, chewy waffles instead of crisp ones.","s":3},{"t":"Let the batter rest 5 minutes, as written. The baking soda starts reacting with the buttermilk and the flour hydrates, giving a lighter waffle.","s":4},{"t":"Do not stack the finished waffles. Keep them in a single layer on a rack in the oven; stacked waffles steam each other soggy. Wait for steam to slow before opening the iron.","s":5}],"rl":"SWLLZSMFXG"},"3547":{"lv":"EROOER","w":{"1":"Seasons the potatoes; without it they taste dull.","2":"Light warmth; skip.","3":"A savory background note; skip.","4":"Fries the potatoes; any neutral oil works.","5":"Adds butter flavor and browning; more oil works."},"sub":{"4":{"x":"3 tbsp sunflower or canola oil","d":"Neutral and similar frying performance.","k":["sunflower oil","canola oil"]},"5":{"x":"2 tbsp vegetable oil","d":"Less flavor and less browning, but crisp.","k":["vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Rinsing removes surface starch so the shreds crisp rather than glue together. Wring them out in a towel until nearly dry; moisture is what makes hash browns steam.","s":1},{"t":"Use russet potatoes. Their high starch and low moisture give a crisp crust; waxy potatoes stay soft.","s":0},{"t":"Press into a thin layer and do not move it for 5 minutes. Flip in sections once a deep golden crust has formed, or the shreds tear and turn greasy.","s":4}],"rl":"BZZXFF"},"3548":{"lv":"EREREORO","w":{"1":"Adds sweetness and moisture; without it the frittata is meatier and plainer.","3":"Makes the custard slightly more tender; water or skipping it works fine.","5":"Fresh onion bite and color on top; purely a garnish.","6":"Seasons the eggs; sausage and cheddar are already salty, so go light.","7":"Mild background heat; barely noticed."},"tips":[{"t":"Pour off all but about 1 tablespoon of rendered sausage fat before adding the eggs; extra fat pools under the frittata and makes the bottom greasy.","s":1},{"t":"Keep the stovetop stage short and the heat at medium; the oven sets the middle. It is done when the center no longer jiggles and reads 160F (71C).","s":4},{"t":"Run a rubber spatula around the edge as soon as it comes out, rest 5 minutes, and it slides out in clean wedges instead of sticking.","s":5}],"rl":"PXPMFGZZ"},"3549":{"lv":"EERROOOO","w":{"2":"Adds tang and creaminess; without it the base is icier and closer to sorbet.","4":"Crunch only; any cereal or nuts work, or skip it.","5":"Fresh fruit for contrast; skip if the frozen berries already give enough fruit.","6":"Adds a little fiber and texture, no real flavor.","7":"Extra sweetness for tart berries; skip if the banana is ripe.","3":"A little liquid to blend the bowl; the frozen fruit blends with less"},"sub":{"3":{"x":"1/4 cup almond, oat or soy milk","d":"Slightly less creamy and a touch nuttier; keep the amount small so the bowl stays thick.","k":["almond milk","oat milk","soy milk"],"wn":"Almond milk contains tree nuts; soy milk contains soy; check oat milk is gluten-free"}},"tips":[{"t":"Add liquid a tablespoon at a time and stop to scrape down the blender; too much milk turns this into a drinkable smoothie that will not hold toppings.","s":1},{"t":"Put the yogurt and milk in first and the frozen fruit on top so the blades catch it; use a tamper or pulse if the blades spin in an air pocket.","s":0},{"t":"Chill the bowls in the freezer for 10 minutes while you blend so the base stays thick while you decorate.","s":2}],"rl":"VVFMGGGW"},"3550":{"lv":"EEERORO","w":{"3":"Melts into the hot egg and holds the sandwich together; without it you have a plain bacon and egg bagel.","5":"Eggs and the bagel need a little; bacon is already salty, so add only a pinch.","6":"Mild bite; skip if you like.","4":"Buttering the bagel is minor; it can be toasted dry"},"sub":{"4":{"x":"1 tbsp reserved bacon drippings or neutral oil","d":"Bacon fat tastes more savory; oil is neutral. Bagels can be toasted dry instead of buttered.","k":["bacon fat","vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Bake the bacon on a rack so it stays flat and crisp as the fat drips away; line the pan with foil and cleanup is a fold and toss.","s":0},{"t":"Cook the eggs in a greased ring mold, or fold the edges into a square, so they match the bagel width; break the yolk if you want a firmer egg that will not run out.","s":3},{"t":"Cover the pan for the last 30 seconds after adding the cheese; trapped steam melts it faster than the burner does.","s":4}],"rl":"BPPFFZZ"},"3551":{"lv":"EREEERERO","w":{"1":"A little milk makes the eggs more tender; too much makes the omelet weep.","5":"Adds melty richness and salt; the omelet is drier without it.","7":"Seasons the eggs; ham is salty, so go light.","8":"Mild bite only.","6":"The cooking fat for the eggs; without it the omelet sticks and tears"},"sub":{"6":{"x":"2 tbsp olive or neutral vegetable oil","d":"Less rich and buttery; the omelet browns a little less.","k":["olive oil","vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Cook the pepper and onion until their moisture has evaporated; a wet filling makes the omelet soggy and tears it when you fold.","s":0},{"t":"Keep the heat at medium-low; a browned omelet is overcooked and rubbery. You want a pale yellow, just-set exterior with a tender center.","s":3},{"t":"Put the filling and cheese on one half only, then fold and slide onto the plate seam side down so the weight keeps it closed.","s":4}],"rl":"PMPVVFFZZ"},"3552":{"lv":"EEERRREROO","w":{"3":"Adds a little browning and sweetness; the pancake tastes slightly flat and eggy without it.","4":"Keeps the batter from tasting bland.","5":"Gives a bakery aroma; skip if you serve with plenty of lemon and sugar.","7":"The lemon juice cuts the richness and is the classic way to serve it.","8":"Dusting for sweetness and looks only.","9":"Optional fresh fruit topping."},"tips":[{"t":"The skillet has to be screaming hot, and the butter sizzling, when the batter goes in; that blast of heat is what makes the sides climb.","s":2},{"t":"Use room-temperature eggs and milk and blend a full 30 seconds; a smooth, aerated batter puffs higher than a lumpy one.","s":1},{"t":"Do not open the oven door for the first 15 minutes; escaping steam collapses the puff. Deflating at the table is normal.","s":4}],"rl":"LMSWZXFAGG"},"3553":{"lv":"EEERO","w":{"3":"Seasons the egg; bread and butter are mild, so a pinch matters.","4":"Mild bite only.","2":"The fat that fries the bread and egg; without it the toast does not crisp or release"},"sub":{"2":{"x":"2 tbsp olive or neutral vegetable oil","d":"Less buttery and rich; the bread still crisps and browns well.","k":["olive oil","vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Use thick-cut bread, which holds the egg without turning soggy, and cut the hole small enough that the white sets before it runs under the bread.","s":0},{"t":"Keep the heat at medium to medium-low; a hot pan browns the bread before the white sets. Cover for 30 seconds if the top of the white stays clear.","s":3},{"t":"Flip with a wide spatula in one confident motion and do not press down; for a runny yolk give it just 1 to 2 more minutes.","s":4}],"rl":"BPFZZ"},"52957":{"lv":"ERROERRO","w":{"1":"Sweetens the cream cheese filling. The quantity looks high for the amount of cream cheese; start with a few tablespoons of powdered sugar and taste.","2":"Rounds out the filling; plain sweetened cream cheese is still good without it.","3":"Only for dusting the work surface; any flour or none works.","5":"Fresh berries add tartness to balance the sweet filling; any berry works.","6":"Fresh berries add tartness; use whichever berries you have.","7":"Use any one or two berries; a third kind adds color only."},"tips":[{"t":"Thaw puff pastry in the fridge, not the counter, and keep it cold while you work; if it turns soft or sticky, slide the tray into the fridge for 10 minutes before baking so the butter layers stay intact.","s":0},{"t":"Keep the filling to 1 to 2 teaspoons in the center and pat the berries dry; extra filling or wet berries leak and glue the folds shut.","s":3},{"t":"Bake on the middle rack on parchment; if the tops brown before the bottoms look golden, move the tray lower for the last few minutes.","s":5}],"rl":"FWXGBVVV"},"52962":{"lv":"EREOEEREE","w":{"1":"A splash of vinegar helps the egg whites set quickly into a tidy shape; plain water also works.","3":"For buttering the muffins; skip it, since salmon and hollandaise are already rich.","6":"Acid in the hollandaise reduction; lemon juice still gives brightness"},"sub":{"6":{"x":"2 tsp extra lemon juice","d":"A bit more lemon and slightly less sharp; the sauce works the same.","k":["lemon juice"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Whisk the yolks over barely simmering water in a bowl that does not touch the water until thick and ribbony and 160F (71C), then drizzle in the melted butter very slowly. If it splits, whisk in a teaspoon of warm water.","s":0},{"t":"Use the freshest eggs and strain each through a sieve first to drain the watery white; swirl the water and poach about 3 minutes for a set white and runny yolk.","s":1},{"t":"Hollandaise breaks if hot and sets if cold; keep it in a warm bowl over off-heat water until the muffins are ready.","s":2}],"rl":"PABFPAATF"},"6":{"lv":"EEERREREEORO","w":{"3":"Savory base flavor in place of fresh garlic.","4":"Savory sweetness in place of fresh onion; skip if you add fresh.","9":"Fresh color on top.","10":"Needed for the beef and broth; add gradually and taste at the end.","11":"Mild bite.","1":"The fat for the roux that thickens the stroganoff sauce; without it the sauce stays thin","6":"Lightens the sauce; broth and sour cream already make it creamy","8":"The noodles the stroganoff is served on; without them the dish is just sauce"},"sub":{"1":{"x":"2 tbsp olive or vegetable oil","d":"Less rich and no buttery taste; the roux still thickens the sauce just as well.","k":["olive oil","vegetable oil"]},"6":{"x":"1/2 cup half-and-half, or 1/2 cup more beef broth","d":"Half-and-half is richer; extra broth makes it more savory and slightly thinner.","k":["half and half","beef broth"]},"8":{"x":"8 oz penne, rotini or other short pasta","d":"Cook to the package time; egg noodles are more tender and soak up sauce faster.","k":["penne","rotini","pasta"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Do not stir the sour cream into a boiling sauce; take the pot off the heat, let it stop bubbling, and stir in a ladle of hot sauce with the cream first so it does not curdle.","s":3},{"t":"Drain the beef fat down to about 2 tablespoons before making the roux; too much leaves a greasy sauce. Ground beef should reach 160F (71C).","s":1},{"t":"Cook the flour for a full minute in the butter before adding broth so it loses its raw taste, and pour the liquid in gradually to avoid lumps.","s":1}],"rl":"PFTXXMMFBGZZ"},"27":{"lv":"ERRRREERROOEREO","w":{"1":"Sweet, savory base of the filling; without it the sauce is flatter.","2":"Adds sweetness, color and body to the filling.","3":"Aromatic backbone of the filling.","4":"Savory depth; skip and the filling is milder.","8":"The classic herb note in a pot pie; skip and it tastes more generic.","9":"Redundant with the fresh garlic; skip.","10":"Redundant with the fresh onion; skip.","12":"Adds sweet pops and color; skip if you do not like peas.","14":"Gives the crust gloss and deeper browning; milk or melted butter also work.","7":"Adds richness to the filling; the broth and flour sauce still works"},"sub":{"7":{"x":"3/4 cup whole milk plus 1 tbsp butter melted in with the vegetables","d":"Less rich and slightly thinner; simmer 2 extra minutes to thicken before filling the dish.","k":["whole milk","butter"]}},"tips":[{"t":"The filling should be as thick as gravy before it goes in the dish; the vegetables and chicken release liquid in the oven, and a loose sauce makes the pastry soggy.","s":1},{"t":"Let the filling cool 10 minutes before topping with puff pastry; hot filling melts the butter layers and the crust will not puff.","s":2},{"t":"Set the dish on a rimmed baking sheet to catch drips, and cut the vent slits only after brushing with egg so they stay open.","s":3}],"rl":"FXVVXTMFXXXPVSG"},"28":{"lv":"ERRRERRORERO","w":{"1":"Savory aromatic in the meat layer; skip and it tastes less rounded.","2":"Concentrated umami that gives the gravy depth and color.","5":"Adds savory tang to the gravy.","6":"Classic herb note in shepherd's pie.","7":"Stands in for fresh onion; fine to skip.","8":"Sweetness, color and texture; frozen vegetables make this a complete meal.","10":"Helps the potatoes brown and taste richer under the broiler.","11":"Adds a salty, golden crust; skip it and the top will be paler.","3":"Thickens the gravy slightly; the filling is still fine slightly looser"},"sub":{"3":{"x":"1 1/2 tsp cornstarch stirred into the broth","d":"Thickens like flour without any raw taste; bring to a simmer for a minute to activate it.","k":["cornstarch"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Simmer until a spoon dragged through the filling leaves a clear trail; runny filling floods up through the mashed potatoes and ruins the crust.","s":1},{"t":"Spread warm potatoes from the edges inward to seal the filling in, then drag a fork across for ridges that brown and crisp under the broiler.","s":2},{"t":"Use a broiler-safe skillet without a plastic handle, keep it about 6 inches from the element, and watch it closely; it goes from golden to burnt in under a minute.","s":3}],"rl":"PXXTMXXXVBFG"},"29":{"lv":"ERREEROOERRO","w":{"1":"Cooks the garlic and adds richness; the cream already supplies plenty of fat.","2":"Savory aromatic note in the sauce.","5":"Adds sharpness that cuts the richness and helps keep the sauce smooth.","6":"Minor savory background.","7":"Subtle smoky color and flavor; a pinch for show.","11":"Fresh color on top only.","4":"The liquid of the cheese sauce; without it the sauce is a clumpy paste","9":"A second cheese for flavor; the other three cheeses still carry the dish","10":"A second cheese for melt and stretch; the other cheeses still carry the dish"},"sub":{"4":{"x":"1 cup half-and-half","d":"Slightly richer and a bit thicker than milk; the sauce is a little less lean.","k":["half and half"]},"9":{"x":"2 cups more shredded sharp cheddar","d":"Sharper and less smoky-mellow, but it melts and browns just as well.","k":["cheddar"]},"10":{"x":"2 cups shredded monterey jack","d":"Milder and creamier; you lose a little of the cheese pull.","k":["monterey jack"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Boil the shells 2 minutes under the package time; they keep absorbing sauce in the oven and turn mushy if fully cooked first.","s":0},{"t":"Shred the cheese yourself from blocks; pre-shredded cheese carries anti-caking starch that makes the sauce grainy. Add it off the heat in handfuls so the fat does not separate.","s":2},{"t":"The sauce should look looser than you think before baking, since the pasta and oven thicken it; tent with foil if the top browns before the center bubbles.","s":3}],"rl":"BFXFMXXXFFFG"},"30":{"lv":"EEERREROERO","w":{"3":"Sweetens and flavors the broth; skip and the stew tastes flatter.","4":"Adds color and a fresh vegetable note; the stew works without them.","6":"Adds savory depth; if skipped, add a pinch more salt.","7":"Subtle herbal background that is barely missed.","9":"Season the beef well at the start; an unsalted slow-cooker stew tastes flat.","10":"Mild bite.","8":"The stew needs liquid to cook in; without it the slow cooker scorches"},"sub":{"8":{"x":"1 cup beef broth","d":"Richer, more beefy gravy; go easy on added salt because broth is salted.","k":["beef broth"]}},"tips":[{"t":"A slow cooker never browns meat. For a deeper stew, brown the beef in a hot skillet for 5 minutes first and deglaze the pan with the water before adding it.","s":1},{"t":"Keep the lid on; every peek costs 15 to 20 minutes. The beef is ready when a cube shreds with a fork; if it is tough, it needs more time, not more heat.","s":2},{"t":"Frozen green beans turn very soft over 8 hours; stir them in for the last 45 minutes if you want a little bite.","s":2}],"rl":"PBVXVAXXMZZ"},"33":{"lv":"ERRRREERRE","w":{"2":"Sweet pepper flavor that is classic to cheesesteak; skip and it tastes plainer.","3":"Sweetness and body; without it the dish loses the classic cheesesteak flavor.","4":"Savory background note.","7":"Adds beefy tang to the broth.","1":"Cooking fat for the vegetables; steak releases some fat if it is skipped","8":"Adds richness to the sauce; the orzo and cheese still make it creamy","9":"The cheese that defines a Philly cheesesteak skillet; without it the dish loses its identity"},"sub":{"1":{"x":"2 tbsp olive or vegetable oil","d":"Slightly less rich but the vegetables brown the same.","k":["olive oil","vegetable oil"]},"8":{"x":"1/2 cup half-and-half or whole milk","d":"Less rich and a bit thinner; add at the very end off the heat so it does not curdle.","k":["half and half","whole milk"]},"9":{"x":"6 slices white American cheese or 1 1/2 cups shredded mozzarella","d":"American melts creamier, mozzarella is stringier; both are milder than provolone.","k":["american cheese","mozzarella"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Freeze the steak for 20 minutes if it is not pre-shaved so you can slice it paper thin; sear in two batches in a very hot pan, about 1 minute per side, because it finishes in the sauce.","s":0},{"t":"Stir the orzo every couple of minutes; it sticks like risotto. If the pan dries before it is tender, add a splash of broth.","s":2},{"t":"Add the cream off high heat and cover the pan to melt the cheese; boiling it with the beef can look greasy.","s":3}],"rl":"PFVVXBMXFF"},"58":{"lv":"ERREEERRER","w":{"1":"Sweetness and body for the sauce; without it the goulash is flatter.","2":"Savory aromatic note.","6":"Adds the herbal backbone of the sauce; oregano and basil together stand in.","7":"Gives color and mild sweet pepper flavor that defines the dish.","9":"Melted richness on top; the dish is fine without it."},"tips":[{"t":"Brown the beef without moving it for the first minute or two so it crusts, then drain all but about 1 tablespoon of fat before adding the tomatoes; ground beef should reach 160F (71C).","s":0},{"t":"Simmer the sauce for the full 15 minutes before adding macaroni so the canned tomato taste cooks off; a pinch of sugar fixes sharp tomatoes.","s":1},{"t":"Stir the pasta once or twice while it cooks covered; it sticks to the bottom. If the sauce runs dry before the macaroni is tender, add 1/2 cup water.","s":2}],"rl":"PXXAAMXXBG"},"64":{"lv":"ERREREREER","w":{"1":"Sweetness and body for the chili base.","2":"Savory aromatic note.","4":"Gives chili its earthy depth; chili powder carries most of the flavor if skipped.","6":"Adds protein and heartiness; skipping makes it more of a meat and pasta dish.","9":"Melty richness stirred in at the end; skip for a lighter dish."},"tips":[{"t":"Stir the chili powder and cumin into the hot beef fat for 30 seconds before adding liquid; blooming spices in fat deepens their flavor.","s":0},{"t":"Drain and rinse the canned beans so the starchy liquid does not turn the sauce cloudy and pasty.","s":1},{"t":"Stir the macaroni often while it simmers; it sticks. Take the pot off the heat before adding the cheese so it melts smoothly instead of going oily.","s":3}],"rl":"PXXXXABMBF"},"65":{"lv":"ERRRRRERRRR","w":{"2":"Melts into the juices and sweetens the gravy.","3":"Classic sweetness and body for the pot roast.","4":"Hearty starch that soaks up the juices.","5":"Mellows during the long cook and sweetens the gravy.","7":"Adds depth and a savory sweetness to the juices.","8":"Adds a savory tang to the gravy.","9":"Classic herb note of pot roast.","1":"Browns the roast; the roast still cooks if browned dry or skipped","10":"Thickens the gravy; the pot roast is fine with a thinner jus"},"sub":{"1":{"x":"2 tbsp vegetable oil or butter","d":"Same browning job; butter adds a slightly nutty flavor but can burn if the pan is too hot.","k":["vegetable oil","butter"]},"10":{"x":"1/4 cup all-purpose flour whisked with 1/2 cup cold water","d":"Makes a more opaque, classic gravy; simmer 3 to 4 minutes to cook out the raw flour taste.","k":["flour"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Sear the roast in a very hot pan without moving it until a dark crust forms, then deglaze the pan with a little broth and pour that fond into the cooker; the slow cooker cannot brown anything itself.","s":0},{"t":"Chuck roast needs low, long cooking to melt its collagen; pull it when it shreds easily. Cooking on high gives a tougher, stringier roast.","s":2},{"t":"Mix the cornstarch with cold water first and bring the juices to a boil; cornstarch only thickens once it boils. Skim the fat off the juices before thickening.","s":3}],"rl":"PFXVBXMXXXT"},"68":{"lv":"EEERRRRERR","w":{"3":"Sweetness and moisture in the filling.","4":"Savory aromatic note in the filling.","5":"Binds the filling so the rolls hold together when sliced; rice helps hold it too.","6":"Mild sweet pepper flavor in the filling.","8":"Adds texture and fresh tomato flavor to the sauce; extra tomato sauce works.","9":"Balances the acid in the tomatoes; skip and the sauce is a bit sharp."},"sub":{"9":{"x":"1 tbsp white sugar or honey","d":"Same balancing job; honey adds a faint floral note.","k":["sugar","honey"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Freeze the whole head of cabbage overnight and thaw it, or core it and microwave it 5 to 8 minutes; the leaves peel off tender without tearing and no boiling is needed.","s":0},{"t":"Shave the thick center rib flat with a knife so the leaf rolls tightly, then tuck the sides in and place seam side down.","s":2},{"t":"Bake covered until the filling reaches 160F (71C) because it contains raw ground beef; a leaf-lined pan keeps the bottoms from scorching.","s":3}],"rl":"VPBXXTXMAW"},"3127":{"lv":"EERRERR","w":{"2":"Sharp bite on the crust; skip and the steak is milder.","5":"Perfumes the butter; skip and the butter is simpler but still good.","6":"Herbal note in the butter; any other woody herb works.","3":"Sear oil; the garlic butter and beef fat also cook the steak"},"sub":{"3":{"x":"2 tbsp canola oil or 1 tbsp ghee","d":"Needs a high smoke point; ghee gives a nutty flavor, extra virgin olive oil will smoke.","k":["canola oil","ghee"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Dry the steak thoroughly and salt it well ahead; surface moisture steams the meat and keeps it from forming a dark crust.","s":0},{"t":"Lower the heat before adding the butter, garlic and thyme; butter and smashed garlic burn at searing temperature. Spoon the foaming butter over the top for 2 minutes.","s":3},{"t":"Take the steak off the heat about 5F below your target temperature since it keeps climbing during the 8-minute rest; slice against the grain.","s":4}],"rl":"PZZFFXX"},"3128":{"lv":"EERERO","w":{"2":"Gives bite to the crust; skip for a milder steak.","4":"Baste for a nutty, rich finish; the steak still tastes good without it but less luxurious.","5":"Subtle herbal aroma in the basting butter.","3":"The only high-heat fat to sear the strip; without it the steak sticks and browns unevenly"},"sub":{"3":{"x":"1 tbsp canola oil or ghee","d":"Needs a high smoke point; ghee gives a nutty flavor, extra virgin olive oil will smoke.","k":["canola oil","ghee"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Salt the steaks 20 minutes ahead or at the very start of resting and pat dry again before searing; that wet surface will not brown.","s":0},{"t":"Stand the steaks on their fat cap for the last minute to render it; the crisp, rendered fat is the best bite of a strip.","s":2},{"t":"Rest the steak on a rack or board rather than a plate so the crust does not steam, and slice across the grain.","s":5}],"rl":"PZZFFX"},"3129":{"lv":"EERERRO","w":{"2":"Gives the crust some bite; skip for a milder steak.","4":"Filet is very lean, so the basting butter provides its richness.","5":"Aromatic note in the butter.","6":"Subtle herbal aroma in the basting butter.","3":"The only high-heat fat to sear the lean filet; without it it sticks and browns poorly"},"sub":{"3":{"x":"1 tbsp canola oil or ghee","d":"Needs a high smoke point; ghee gives a nutty flavor, extra virgin olive oil will smoke.","k":["canola oil","ghee"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Filet has almost no fat, so do not skip the butter baste; it adds the richness the cut lacks. Letting it sit out 30 minutes keeps the center from staying cold.","s":0},{"t":"Hold the filets on their sides with tongs for 30 seconds each to sear the edges so the crust is even all around.","s":2},{"t":"The skillet handle will be 425F coming out of the oven; keep a towel or mitt on it so you do not forget. Pull at 125F for rare or 130F for medium-rare because it rises about 5F while resting.","s":4}],"rl":"PZZFFXX"},"3133":{"lv":"EERERRO","w":{"2":"Gives the crust some bite; added after the dry brine.","4":"Baste for a nutty finish; steak is great without it.","5":"Aromatic note in the basting butter.","6":"Subtle herbal aroma in the basting butter.","3":"The searing fat for the porterhouse; without it the crust is patchy"},"sub":{"3":{"x":"1 tbsp canola oil or ghee","d":"Needs a high smoke point; ghee gives a nutty flavor, extra virgin olive oil will smoke.","k":["canola oil","ghee"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Salting an hour or more uncovered on a rack dries the surface so it sears fast and seasons the meat deeply; do not wipe the salt off, but do pat the steak dry before searing.","s":0},{"t":"Slide the thermometer into the thickest part of the strip side, away from the bone, which reads hotter; the steak should be 110F before searing and about 130F after.","s":2},{"t":"Sear the fat edge of the strip too, and rest the steak before cutting the meat off the bone in one piece, then slice each section against the grain.","s":5}],"rl":"PZZFFXX"},"3135":{"lv":"EERREORRREEER","w":{"2":"Binds the patties so they hold their oval shape in the gravy.","3":"Adds savory depth to the patties and the gravy.","5":"Mild bite only.","7":"The classic mushroom gravy flavor; skip and the sauce is milder.","8":"Sweetness and body in the gravy.","12":"A little sweet-tang in the gravy; skip if you like it savory.","6":"Browns the patties; the beef fat renders if skipped","9":"The fat for the roux of the Salisbury gravy; without it the gravy has no base"},"sub":{"6":{"x":"1 tbsp canola oil or butter","d":"Same browning job; butter adds flavor but browns faster, so watch the heat.","k":["vegetable oil","butter"]},"9":{"x":"2 tbsp olive oil or bacon fat","d":"Gives the roux a different flavor but works the same; bacon fat adds smokiness.","k":["olive oil","bacon fat"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Mix the beef gently just until combined; overworked ground beef turns dense and bouncy. Wet hands keep it from sticking.","s":0},{"t":"Let the mushrooms release their water first and then brown for the full 6 to 8 minutes; the fond they pick up from the pan is the gravy's flavor.","s":2},{"t":"Patties are safe at 160F (71C); check by sliding the probe in from the side. Simmer the gravy until it coats a spoon.","s":4}],"rl":"PTTXZZFVVFTMX"},"3144":{"lv":"ERREREREORRO","w":{"1":"Adds sweetness and moisture; without it the loaf is meatier but drier.","2":"Savory aromatic note.","6":"Adds savory depth to the meat.","8":"Mild bite.","9":"Forms the tangy-sweet glaze that makes it classic meatloaf.","11":"Sharpens the glaze slightly; ketchup and sugar carry it.","4":"Moistens the breadcrumb panade; the loaf is a bit drier without it","10":"Sweetens the glaze; the ketchup still gives a glaze without it"},"sub":{"4":{"x":"1/2 cup beef broth or water","d":"Soaks the crumbs and keeps the loaf moist, but is less rich and tender than milk.","k":["beef broth","water"]},"10":{"x":"2 tbsp white sugar or honey","d":"Sweetens the glaze the same; brown sugar adds a little molasses depth that you lose.","k":["sugar","honey"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Soaking the breadcrumbs in milk first (a panade) traps moisture in the meat; it is why meatloaf turns out tender rather than dry.","s":0},{"t":"Bake it free-form on a lined sheet pan if you can: the sides brown and the fat runs off instead of simmering the loaf in a pan.","s":2},{"t":"Apply only half the glaze at the start; sugar scorches over 65 minutes. Check that the center reaches 160F (71C), then rest 10 minutes so it slices cleanly.","s":4}],"rl":"PVXTMTXZZXWX"},"3145":{"lv":"ERRERROOR","w":{"2":"Gives color and mild flavor to the rub.","4":"Gives a little heat and bite to the rub.","5":"Savory dimension in the rub.","6":"Minor savory note; skip.","7":"A touch of heat; optional.","8":"Glazes the ribs and adds sweet-tangy flavor at the end.","1":"Builds the sticky bark; the ribs are still good with the rest of the rub"},"sub":{"1":{"x":"3 tbsp white sugar plus 1 tsp molasses, or just 3 tbsp white sugar","d":"Drier and slightly less caramel-like but still builds a sticky bark.","k":["sugar","molasses"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Peel the membrane off the back by loosening a corner with a knife and gripping it with a paper towel; left on, it turns leathery and blocks the rub.","s":0},{"t":"Pork is safe at 145F (63C), but ribs need to cook to about 195F (90C) to melt the collagen; they are ready when the meat pulls back from the bones about 1/4 inch.","s":3},{"t":"Brush on the sauce only at the end and broil for a few minutes; the sugar scorches fast, so watch it.","s":4}],"rl":"PWXZZXXXX"},"3237":{"lv":"ERORRREEERROO","w":{"1":"Seasons the chicken; add gradually since parmesan and broth are salty.","2":"Mild bite.","3":"Color and mild warmth on the chicken.","5":"Savory base for the sauce.","9":"Thickens the sauce and adds salty richness.","10":"Herbal note in the sauce; basil and oregano can stand in.","11":"A little heat to balance the cream; skip for a milder sauce.","12":"Fresh finishing herb that brightens the dish.","4":"Fat for searing the chicken; a nonstick pan or a little butter covers it"},"sub":{"4":{"x":"2 tbsp vegetable oil or butter","d":"Same searing job; butter gives a richer sear but browns faster.","k":["vegetable oil","butter"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Slice the breasts into thin cutlets of even thickness so they cook through in the same time as the sauce simmers; the chicken must reach 165F (74C).","s":1},{"t":"Scrape up the browned bits when you add the broth; they season the sauce. Do not let the cream boil hard or it can split.","s":3},{"t":"Use the oil from the jar of sun-dried tomatoes in the pan if you have it; it carries the tomato flavor into the sauce.","s":2}],"rl":"PZZXFXXMFXXXG"},"3240":{"lv":"EERROREORRR","w":{"2":"Savory aromatic note in the herb butter.","4":"Fresh color and flavor; skip.","5":"Zest brightens the butter and the cavity perfumes the meat; skip for a plainer chicken.","7":"Mild bite.","8":"Sweetens the pan drippings and perfumes the cavity.","9":"Roast in the juices for a hearty side; skip if serving other sides.","10":"Roast in the juices for a hearty side; skip if serving other sides.","1":"The herb butter under the skin is the defining technique; without it the roast loses its flavor and moisture","3":"Adds herbal flavor; other herbs and lemon still season the chicken"},"sub":{"1":{"x":"4 tbsp olive oil","d":"Browns the skin well but will not hold as a paste under the skin as butter does; the herbs sink.","k":["olive oil"]},"3":{"x":"2 tsp dried thyme","d":"More earthy and less floral than fresh.","k":["dried thyme"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Dry the chicken as thoroughly as you can, and salting it uncovered in the fridge overnight dries the skin for a crisper result.","s":1},{"t":"Check the thickest part of the thigh and also the breast with a thermometer without touching bone; both must reach 165F (74C).","s":4},{"t":"Rest the bird 15 minutes before carving so the juices stay in the meat, and spoon the pan juices over it.","s":5}],"rl":"PFXXGAZZXBV"},"3580":{"lv":"EERRRRRRERRREORO","w":{"3":"Sweetness and body for the filling.","4":"Adds sweetness and color to the filling.","5":"Aromatic backbone of the filling.","6":"Savory aromatic note.","7":"Adds depth and savory sweetness to the gravy.","9":"Adds savory tang to the gravy.","10":"Classic herb note for beef pot pie.","11":"Sweet pops and color; skip if you do not like peas.","14":"Season at the start and adjust after simmering; the gravy tastes flat without it.","13":"Gloss and browning for the crust; milk or melted butter also work.","15":"Mild bite.","2":"Browns the beef; the meat browns dry or with its own fat"},"sub":{"2":{"x":"2 tbsp olive oil or butter","d":"Same browning job; butter browns faster, so watch the heat.","k":["olive oil","butter"],"wn":"Butter contains dairy"}},"tips":[{"t":"Brown the beef in batches; crowded meat steams gray instead of forming the crust that flavors the gravy.","s":1},{"t":"Simmer it at barely a bubble; hard boiling toughens the beef, and the flour on the meat thickens the gravy as it cooks.","s":3},{"t":"Cool the filling before the top crust goes on, then bake on a sheet pan and rest 10 minutes so the gravy sets enough to slice.","s":5}],"rl":"PTFXVXXXMXXVBGZZ"},"3581":{"lv":"EERREEROROR","w":{"2":"Sweetness and body in the filling.","3":"Savory note in the filling.","6":"Herbal backbone; oregano and basil together stand in.","7":"Adds a tiny bit of savory depth.","8":"Seasons the filling; skip and the peppers taste flat.","9":"Mild bite.","10":"Melted, stretchy topping; the dish is good without it."},"tips":[{"t":"Pick peppers that stand flat and trim a sliver off the base without cutting through; a muffin tin helps keep them upright.","s":3},{"t":"Cover the dish at first so the peppers soften and the filling reaches 160F (71C); uncovering at the end lets the cheese brown.","s":5},{"t":"Drain the fat after browning so the sauce stays tomatoey instead of greasy.","s":1}],"rl":"VPXXBMXXZZG"},"3582":{"lv":"EERREERRERRORR","w":{"2":"Savory depth and meaty texture; skip and the sauce is plainer.","3":"Sweetness and body in the sauce.","7":"Adds sharp, salty richness to the sauce.","9":"Pops of sweetness and color.","10":"Seasons the sauce; skip and the casserole tastes bland.","11":"Mild bite.","12":"Gives the crunchy topping; skip for a softer casserole.","13":"Browns the panko; extra butter or oil can stand in.","1":"The fat for the roux and vegetables; without it the casserole sauce lacks body","6":"Adds savory depth to the sauce; the milk and soup still make it creamy"},"sub":{"1":{"x":"4 tbsp olive oil","d":"Browns the vegetables just as well but the sauce is less rich.","k":["olive oil"]},"6":{"x":"1/2 cup more milk","d":"Slightly less savory but thickens the same.","k":["milk"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Drain the tuna very well, pressing the lid into the can, and fold it in last in large flakes; stirred hard it turns to paste and the casserole tastes fishy.","s":3},{"t":"Make the sauce thicker than seems right; the noodles and peas release water and the casserole bakes thinner than it looks.","s":2},{"t":"Toss the panko with the melted butter before sprinkling it on so it browns evenly; tent with foil if it darkens before the middle bubbles.","s":5}],"rl":"BFVXTMMFPVZZGG"},"3583":{"lv":"ERRRERROEERRO","w":{"2":"Sweetness and body in the broth.","3":"Savory aromatic note.","5":"Adds savory depth to the broth.","6":"Classic herb note for beef and noodles.","7":"Subtle herbal background that is barely missed.","10":"Adds a little richness to the gravy.","11":"Season at the start and adjust after thickening.","12":"Mild bite.","1":"Browns the beef; the beef fat renders if skipped","9":"The thickener for the gravy; without it the beef and noodles are in a watery broth"},"sub":{"1":{"x":"2 tbsp olive oil or butter","d":"Same browning job; butter browns faster, so watch the heat.","k":["olive oil","butter"]},"9":{"x":"1/4 cup flour whisked with 1/2 cup cold water","d":"Thickens into a more opaque gravy; simmer 3 to 4 minutes to cook out the raw flour taste.","k":["flour"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Keep the simmer at barely a bubble; a hard boil toughens the beef before the connective tissue has melted.","s":3},{"t":"Browning the beef in batches builds the deep flavor of the broth; crowded meat steams gray.","s":1},{"t":"The noodles soak up liquid; add a cup of hot water if the pot looks dry, and slide them in only after the beef is tender.","s":4}],"rl":"PFXXMXXXBTFZZ"},"3584":{"lv":"EEEEERRRRRRRRO","w":{"6":"Gives the sauce a creamy tang; skip and use more cream.","7":"Brightens the sauce and cuts the richness.","8":"The signature mild curry note of a divan; skip and it is a plain cheese sauce.","9":"Adds cheesy richness and browns on top.","10":"Gives the crunchy topping; skip for a softer top.","11":"Browns the panko; extra butter or oil can stand in.","12":"Seasons the sauce; skip and the casserole tastes flat.","13":"Mild bite.","2":"The fat for the roux; without it the divan sauce does not thicken","5":"Adds richness to the sauce; milk or broth can carry it"},"sub":{"2":{"x":"3 tbsp olive oil","d":"Makes the roux just as well but the sauce is less rich.","k":["olive oil"]},"5":{"x":"1/2 cup half-and-half or whole milk","d":"Less rich; the sauce is slightly thinner, so simmer a minute longer.","k":["half and half","whole milk"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Blanch the broccoli no more than 3 minutes and drain it well, even patting it dry; wet broccoli waters down the sauce in the dish.","s":0},{"t":"Whisk the mayonnaise in once the sauce is off the heat; boiling a mayo-based sauce can make it split.","s":2},{"t":"The sauce should coat a spoon thickly before it goes over; it thins in the oven as the broccoli releases liquid.","s":3}],"rl":"VPFTMFFAXFGGZZ"},"3585":{"lv":"EEREERRERRRRO","w":{"2":"Gives savory depth and meaty texture; skip and the sauce is plainer.","8":"Pops of sweetness and color.","9":"Adds salty richness to the sauce.","10":"Gives the crunchy topping; skip for a softer top.","11":"Seasons the sauce; skip and the casserole tastes flat.","12":"Mild bite.","1":"The fat for the roux and mushrooms; without it the tetrazzini sauce does not thicken","5":"Adds richness to the sauce; broth still makes the sauce","6":"Adds acidity and depth; broth and lemon cover it"},"sub":{"1":{"x":"4 tbsp olive oil","d":"Browns the mushrooms just as well but the sauce is less rich.","k":["olive oil"]},"5":{"x":"1/2 cup half-and-half or whole milk","d":"Less rich; the sauce is a little thinner, so simmer a minute longer.","k":["half and half","whole milk"]},"6":{"x":"1/4 cup more chicken broth plus 1 tsp lemon juice","d":"Gives acidity without the wine's depth; use white wine vinegar if you have no lemon.","k":["chicken broth","lemon juice"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Break the spaghetti in half and cook it 2 minutes short; it finishes in the oven and holds its shape.","s":0},{"t":"Let the mushrooms give up their water and then brown before adding flour; browned mushrooms give the sauce its depth.","s":1},{"t":"Mix the panko with the parmesan for the topping so it crisps and browns; if it browns before the middle bubbles, tent with foil.","s":4}],"rl":"BFVTMFMPVFGZZ"},"3586":{"lv":"EREEEREERROO","w":{"1":"Sweetness and body in the sauce.","5":"Adds cheesy richness; skip and the sauce is plainer.","8":"Adds a second vegetable and color; skip for a plain chicken and rice bake.","9":"Seasons the sauce and rice; skip and the casserole is bland.","10":"Mild bite.","11":"Minor savory note.","0":"The fat for the roux and onion; without it the casserole sauce has no thickener"},"sub":{"0":{"x":"2 tbsp olive oil","d":"Cooks the onion and makes the roux just as well but the sauce is less rich.","k":["olive oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Pour the sauce over the rice while it is still hot and stir so every grain is submerged; uncooked rice dries out where it pokes above the liquid.","s":3},{"t":"Cover tightly with foil; a loose seal lets steam escape and leaves the rice crunchy. The chicken must reach 165F (74C).","s":4},{"t":"Cut the chicken and broccoli small and even so they finish cooking at the same time as the rice.","s":3}],"rl":"FXTMMFBPVZZX"},"52812":{"lv":"EEERRROERRO","w":{"3":"Aromatic base of the pot sauce; skip and the braising liquid is less savory.","4":"Herbal note for the braise.","5":"Piney, resinous note that suits beef; skip for a simpler sauce.","6":"Subtle herbal background that is barely missed.","8":"Sweetness and color in the pot; skip for a simpler dish.","9":"Adds tang to the sauce.","10":"A side for serving; any starch works."},"tips":[{"t":"Sear fat side down first and render until it is deeply browned; that fat bastes the meat and the fond builds the sauce.","s":4},{"t":"Brisket is done when a fork twists in with little resistance, about 195 to 205F; time varies a lot, so test rather than trust the clock.","s":10},{"t":"Rest it tented 20 minutes and slice across the grain; with the grain, even tender brisket turns stringy. It is even better made a day ahead and sliced cold.","s":15}],"rl":"PZXXXXXMVXB"},"52829":{"lv":"EERRROOEEEEEOERRR","w":{"2":"Gives the cheese sauce a sharp tang.","3":"Savory background in the sauce.","4":"Seasons the sauce; skip and the mac tastes flat.","5":"Mild bite.","6":"A touch of heat; optional.","12":"Adds a savory note to the bread butter.","14":"Adds sharp cheesy flavor on the outside layers.","15":"Adds mild melty cheese in the sandwich.","9":"The cream for the cheese sauce that sets the mac filling; without it the filling is dry and does not bind","11":"The fat that toasts the bread outsides; without it the sandwich does not crisp","16":"A second portion of butter for the pan; the bread butter already browns the sandwich"},"sub":{"9":{"x":"1 cup half-and-half","d":"Slightly less rich but still sets firm once chilled.","k":["half and half"]},"11":{"x":"3 tbsp mayonnaise","d":"Spread thinly on the bread outsides; browns evenly and crisply, with a faint tang.","k":["mayonnaise"],"wn":"Contains egg"},"16":{"x":"3 tbsp mayonnaise","d":"Used for the pan; browns evenly and crisply, with a faint tang.","k":["mayonnaise"],"wn":"Contains egg"}},"tips":[{"t":"Chill the mac and cheese until fully firm, preferably overnight, so it cuts into clean slabs that stay together in the pan.","s":5},{"t":"Cook the sandwiches on medium-low and covered so the cheese melts before the bread browns too much.","s":11},{"t":"Boil the pasta until just al dente; it softens more when chilled and reheated in the sandwich.","s":1}],"rl":"BTXXZZXFMFFFXBFFF"},"52987":{"lv":"RRRREEEEE","w":{"1":"Adds a little sharpness to the spread.","2":"Gives the spread its lasagna-style herbal flavor.","3":"Seasons the spread; skip and the spread is bland.","5":"Must be fully cooked first; the steps do not cook the bacon, and a few minutes of toasting won't make raw bacon safe.","0":"Adds tang to the filling; ricotta or other cheese covers it","8":"The fat that toasts the bread; without it the sandwich does not crisp and brown"},"sub":{"0":{"x":"1/4 cup ricotta or plain Greek yogurt","d":"Ricotta tastes closer to lasagna; yogurt is tangier and a little looser.","k":["ricotta","greek yogurt"]},"8":{"x":"2 1/2 tbsp mayonnaise or olive oil","d":"Mayo gives a crisp, golden toast; oil is lighter and a little less rich.","k":["mayonnaise","olive oil"],"wn":"Mayonnaise contains egg"}},"tips":[{"t":"The bacon is not cooked in the steps; fry or bake it crisp first, since a few minutes in the sandwich will not render it.","s":0},{"t":"Pat the tomato slices dry before layering so the sandwich does not turn soggy.","s":0},{"t":"Toast on medium-low and cover for the last minute so the mozzarella melts before the bread burns.","s":1}],"rl":"FXXZBPVFF"},"52992":{"lv":"ERRRROEEORR","w":{"1":"Sweet side vegetable; skip if you have another side.","2":"Fresh onion bite on top.","3":"Seasons the mash and meatloaf.","5":"Minor savory note in the glaze.","9":"Sweetens the soy glaze.","10":"Richness in the mash.","4":"The panade moistening the meatloaf; the loaf is denser without it but works","8":"A tiny bit of oil for roasting carrots; the carrots roast fine without"},"sub":{"4":{"x":"3 tbsp plain breadcrumbs","d":"Same moisture-retaining paste when mixed with the water; use a little less water.","k":["breadcrumbs"]},"8":{"x":"1 tsp olive oil","d":"Same job for roasting carrots; olive oil adds a little flavor.","k":["olive oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Mash the bread with the water into a paste before adding the beef; the paste keeps the meatloaf moist and tender. Mix just until combined.","s":1},{"t":"Add the wasabi to the mash a little at a time; its heat varies, and 1/4 tsp is a gentle start.","s":7},{"t":"The meatloaf is done at 160F (71C); add the glaze only for the last 4 to 5 minutes so the sugar does not burn.","s":4}],"rl":"BVGXTXXPFWF"},"52993":{"lv":"ERREEERRRR","w":{"1":"Roasts crisp for a vegetable side; skip if you have another side.","2":"Flavors the garlic oil for the broccoli and the glaze.","7":"Gives richness to the pan sauce.","6":"Helps the glaze; honey and vinegar still make it","8":"Sears the chicken; a nonstick pan can do it with less","9":"Helps roast the vegetables; they still cook but less crisp"},"sub":{"6":{"x":"Same amount of water plus a pinch of salt","d":"Less savory; reduce the glaze a bit longer and add a splash more vinegar if it is flat.","k":["water","salt"]},"8":{"x":"A drizzle of olive oil","d":"Same searing job; slightly fruitier flavor.","k":["olive oil"]},"9":{"x":"A drizzle of vegetable oil","d":"Same roasting job; slightly less fruity.","k":["vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Cut the potato wedges the same thickness and give them a 5-minute head start on a hot sheet pan so they crisp rather than steam.","s":1},{"t":"Pat the chicken dry and cook until 165F (74C); slice thicker breasts in half horizontally so the center finishes before the outside dries out.","s":4},{"t":"Reduce the glaze until it coats a spoon; honey and balsamic burn quickly, so keep the heat at medium and stir.","s":5}],"rl":"BVXPAWMFFF"},"52994":{"lv":"EERRERRROR","w":{"2":"Savory note for the pan sauce.","3":"Brightens the sauce at the end; skip and the sauce is sweeter and heavier.","5":"Roasts alongside for a vegetable side.","6":"Moistens the pan sauce.","8":"Helps the apples brown a little; skip if they are sweet.","9":"Enriches the pan sauce, swirled in at the end.","7":"Sears the pork chops; the pan can manage with little fat"},"sub":{"7":{"x":"A drizzle of olive oil","d":"Same searing job; slightly fruitier flavor.","k":["olive oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Pork chops are done at 145F (63C) followed by a 3-minute rest; do not cook to gray, the lean cut dries out quickly.","s":3},{"t":"Cook the apples in the pan after the pork so they pick up the browned bits, and stop while they still hold their shape.","s":5},{"t":"Swirl in the butter and lemon off the heat so the pan sauce stays glossy and does not split.","s":6}],"rl":"BVXAPVMFWF"},"52995":{"lv":"ERRREEEORORRO","w":{"1":"Pickled for a sharp topping; skip and the sandwich is sweeter.","2":"Savory base for the meat.","3":"Makes the quick pickle brine and brightens the sandwich.","7":"A little heat to balance the sweet sauce.","8":"Adds tang and sweetness to the sauce.","9":"Balances the sauce and the pickled onion.","11":"Seasons the meat and the sauce.","12":"Mild bite.","10":"Cooks the pork and onion; the pork releases fat so the dish survives"},"sub":{"10":{"x":"A drizzle of olive oil","d":"Same cooking job; slightly fruitier flavor.","k":["olive oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Cook ground pork to 160F (71C), breaking it into small crumbles; larger lumps make the filling messy to eat.","s":3},{"t":"Simmer the sauce until it clings to the pork and no liquid pools; runny filling soaks the bun.","s":4},{"t":"Toast the buns until golden before filling; the toasted surface keeps the bun from going soggy.","s":5}],"rl":"BXXABPXGXWFZZ"},"52996":{"lv":"EREEEEORORRO","w":{"1":"Roasts sweet for a vegetable side; skip if you have another side.","7":"Enriches the mash.","8":"A pinch helps the onions caramelize; skip if you cook them longer.","10":"Seasons the chicken and mash.","11":"Mild bite.","6":"A small enrichment in the mash; the mash is fine without it","9":"Cooks the chicken and onions; skipping it makes them stick"},"sub":{"6":{"x":"2 tbsp milk plus 1 tsp butter","d":"Less tangy but just as creamy; the mash is a little milder.","k":["milk","butter"]},"9":{"x":"A drizzle of olive oil","d":"Same cooking job; slightly fruitier flavor.","k":["olive oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"The onions need 8 to 10 minutes to soften; for a deeper French onion flavor, go 20 to 25 minutes with a splash of water to keep them from scorching.","s":3},{"t":"Chicken breast must reach 165F (74C); pat it dry so it browns well.","s":4},{"t":"Melt the cheese under the broiler until bubbly, and watch it; it moves from melted to burned fast.","s":4}],"rl":"PVBVMGFFWFZZ"},"53016":{"lv":"EEERERRROROOEE","w":{"5":"Helps the coating brown and gives a faint sweetness.","6":"Gives color and a mild savory note.","7":"Seasons the coating; skip and it is bland.","8":"Mild bite.","9":"Savory note in the flour.","10":"Minor savory note.","11":"A little heat; optional.","3":"Egg-wash liquid for the coating; the eggs alone still bind it","12":"The frying oil for the chicken; without it the chicken cannot be fried"},"sub":{"3":{"x":"1/4 cup water or buttermilk","d":"Egg wash works the same; buttermilk adds slight tang.","k":["water","buttermilk"]},"12":{"x":"1 cup peanut or canola oil","d":"Better for frying than olive oil; has a higher smoke point.","k":["peanut oil","canola oil"],"wn":"Contains peanut; avoid with peanut allergy"}},"tips":[{"t":"Pickle juice flavors and tenderizes, but marinate only 30 to 60 minutes; longer and the acid turns the chicken mushy.","s":2},{"t":"Keep the oil at about 350F; cooler oil makes greasy chicken and hotter oil burns the coating before the center cooks. The chicken must reach 165F (74C).","s":6},{"t":"Drain on a wire rack rather than paper so the bottom stays crisp.","s":8}],"rl":"PATMTWXZZXXXFB"},"10":{"lv":"EREEORERRROR","w":{"1":"Seasons the salmon; skip and the glaze is sweeter.","4":"Minor savory note.","7":"Sweet pop and color.","8":"Cool crunch against the spicy salmon.","9":"Creamy, rich contrast.","10":"A squeeze for brightness.","11":"Makes the creamy spicy mayo that finishes the bowl.","5":"Coats the salmon; skipped, the fish still roasts but may stick"},"sub":{"5":{"x":"1 tbsp vegetable oil","d":"Same coating job; flavor is more neutral.","k":["vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Do not marinate the salmon long; the salt and sugar will start to cure it. Toss it right before cooking.","s":1},{"t":"Line the pan or air-fryer basket because the honey caramelizes and sticks; salmon is done at 145F (63C).","s":1},{"t":"Use sticky rice and season it lightly with a splash of rice vinegar so it holds its flavor under the sauces.","s":0}],"rl":"PXXWXFBVVVAF"},"81":{"lv":"EEEERO","w":{"4":"Cool, creamy balance to the hot sauce.","5":"Fresh onion bite on top.","1":"The roasting fat for the sweet potatoes; without it they dry out and do not brown"},"sub":{"1":{"x":"2 tbsp vegetable oil","d":"Same roasting job; flavor is more neutral.","k":["vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Roast on a single layer with space between pieces; crowded sweet potatoes steam instead of caramelizing.","s":0},{"t":"Warm the buffalo sauce before tossing with the chicken; cold sauce makes the dish lukewarm. Reheat cooked chicken to 165F (74C).","s":1},{"t":"Cut the sweet potatoes the same size so they cook evenly, and flip them once halfway through.","s":0}],"rl":"BFPXGG"},"84":{"lv":"EEERREERRO","w":{"3":"Savory aromatic note in the meatballs.","4":"Gives the meatballs their Mediterranean flavor.","7":"Cool crunch in the sauce and the filling.","8":"Fresh sweetness; skip if tomatoes are out of season.","9":"Sharp bite; skip if you do not like raw onion."},"tips":[{"t":"Ground turkey is lean and sticky; wet your hands and mix just until combined. The meatballs must reach 165F (74C).","s":0},{"t":"Grate the cucumber and squeeze it dry in a towel before mixing into the yogurt, or the sauce turns watery.","s":2},{"t":"Warm the pitas briefly in the oven or a dry pan so they bend without cracking.","s":3}],"rl":"PTTXXBGVVV"},"85":{"lv":"EEERORERR","w":{"3":"Adds salty richness and helps browning.","4":"Mild onion bite.","5":"Savory note in the fritter.","7":"Cool tangy dip.","8":"Brightens the yogurt dip.","6":"The frying fat for the fritters; without it they cannot be fried"},"sub":{"6":{"x":"3 tbsp vegetable oil","d":"Same frying job; flavor is more neutral.","k":["vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Squeeze the salted zucchini very dry in a towel; excess water makes the batter loose and the fritters limp.","s":0},{"t":"Keep the heat at medium and give each fritter 3 minutes per side; too hot and the outside burns before the center cooks.","s":2},{"t":"If the batter is loose, stir in 1 to 2 tablespoons of flour. Keep fritters warm on a rack in a 200F oven.","s":1}],"rl":"VTTXXXFGA"},"86":{"lv":"EEEREOO","w":{"3":"Savory note in the butter.","5":"Gives color and mild flavor.","6":"Fresh color on top.","2":"The lemon garlic butter sauce is the dish flavor; without it the cod is plain"},"sub":{"2":{"x":"4 tbsp olive oil","d":"Same job; the sauce is less rich but lighter.","k":["olive oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Cod is done when it turns opaque and flakes easily at 145F (63C); thin tail pieces overcook first, so fold the tails under to even out the thickness.","s":2},{"t":"Pencil-thin asparagus needs about 10 minutes and thick spears closer to 15; snap off the woody ends where the stalk bends naturally.","s":0},{"t":"Pat the fish dry before it goes on the pan so the butter sauce coats it instead of pooling with released water.","s":1}],"rl":"PVFXAXG"},"90":{"lv":"EERERRRRR","w":{"4":"Adds crunch and freshness.","5":"Sweet, juicy pops.","6":"Salty, creamy tang; skip for a lighter salad.","7":"Fresh herb brightness.","8":"Seasons the chicken and echoes the Mediterranean flavor.","2":"Dresses the salad; the lemon and chicken still season it but the salad is drier"},"sub":{"2":{"x":"3 tbsp vegetable oil","d":"Same job in the dressing; flavor is more neutral.","k":["vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Use a 1 to 1 1/4 ratio of water to couscous and cover tightly; fluff with a fork rather than stirring.","s":0},{"t":"Cook the chicken until 165F (74C), then let it rest before slicing against the grain.","s":1},{"t":"Dress the couscous while it is still warm so it absorbs the lemon and oil, then let it cool before adding the vegetables.","s":2}],"rl":"BPFAVVXGX"},"91":{"lv":"EERRREO","w":{"2":"Adds sharp tang.","3":"Adds crunch.","4":"Brightens the chickpeas.","6":"Crunch and freshness.","1":"The binder and moisture for smashed chickpeas; without it the filling is dry"},"sub":{"1":{"x":"3 tbsp plain Greek yogurt","d":"Tangier and lighter; slightly less rich.","k":["greek yogurt"],"wn":"Contains dairy"}},"tips":[{"t":"Drain and rinse the chickpeas and pat them dry before mashing; leftover canning liquid makes the filling runny and the bread soggy.","s":0},{"t":"Mash only about two thirds of the chickpeas and leave the rest whole; that mix of creamy and chunky is what makes it eat like a chicken-salad sandwich.","s":0},{"t":"Toast the bread and put the lettuce against it as a moisture barrier before piling on the filling.","s":2}],"rl":"PFXVABG"},"93":{"lv":"EEEREEERRO","w":{"3":"Seasons the coating.","8":"Brightens the slaw.","9":"Fresh herb on top.","4":"The frying fat for the crispy fish; without it the fish does not crisp","7":"Binds the slaw dressing; the slaw is lighter and drier without it"},"sub":{"4":{"x":"2 tbsp olive oil","d":"Same frying job; slightly fruitier flavor.","k":["olive oil"]},"7":{"x":"3 tbsp plain Greek yogurt","d":"Lighter and tangier dressing.","k":["greek yogurt"],"wn":"Contains dairy"}},"tips":[{"t":"Pat the fish dry before the egg dip so the panko sticks; press the crumbs on firmly.","s":0},{"t":"Fry at medium heat and let each side form a crust before flipping; cod is done at 145F (63C).","s":1},{"t":"Toss the slaw at the last minute so it stays crisp.","s":2}],"rl":"PTTXFBVFAG"},"3131":{"lv":"EERREEREERR","w":{"2":"Mild bite on the crust; skip for a milder steak.","6":"Gives the chimichurri a sharp, sweet bite; skip and it is more herby.","9":"Earthy note that is classic in chimichurri but minor next to parsley and garlic.","10":"Gives a little heat to cut the richness of the beef; skip for a milder sauce.","3":"Rubs the steak to help browning; the marinade and pan still work"},"sub":{"3":{"x":"1 tbsp vegetable oil","d":"Same job rubbing the steak so it browns; flavor is more neutral.","k":["vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Chop the parsley by hand rather than blending it; a blender bruises it into a dull paste, while hand-chopped chimichurri stays bright and textured.","s":0},{"t":"Flank is a thin, lean cut with long fibers; cook it hot and fast to 130F (54C) for medium-rare, since it turns tough and dry past medium.","s":3},{"t":"Slice thinly against the grain at an angle after resting; slicing with the fibers makes even a well-cooked flank steak chewy.","s":4}],"rl":"PZZFXXXFAXX"},"3140":{"lv":"ERORERREEER","w":{"1":"Seasons the steak and brings out its flavor; it tastes flat without.","2":"Mild bite on the crust.","5":"Sweet, juicy pops against the rich steak and cheese.","6":"Sharp crunch that cuts the richness of the steak and blue cheese.","10":"Emulsifies the dressing so it clings and adds a little tang.","3":"Sears the steak; the steak can sear with less fat"},"sub":{"3":{"x":"1 tbsp vegetable oil","d":"Same searing job; flavor is more neutral.","k":["vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Salt the steak and let it sit 15 minutes, then pat it dry; a dry surface sears to a crust instead of steaming in a salad-sized skillet.","s":0},{"t":"Rest the steak a full 8 minutes before slicing so the juices stay in the meat rather than wilting the greens.","s":1},{"t":"Whisk the dressing and toss half with the greens, then drizzle the rest over the steak and cheese at the end so the blue cheese stays distinct.","s":3}],"rl":"PZZFVVXGFAT"},"3152":{"lv":"EEEEERROEERR","w":{"5":"Sweet, juicy pops that cut the richness of the bacon and cheese.","6":"Salty tang that is a hallmark of a Cobb; skip for a milder, less funky salad.","7":"Mild onion note; skip.","10":"Emulsifies the dressing and adds tang.","11":"Seasons the dressing; skip and it tastes flat."},"tips":[{"t":"Lower the eggs into already boiling water and cook exactly 11 minutes, then move them straight to ice water; this makes yolks that are set but not gray and shells that peel cleanly.","s":0},{"t":"Cut every ingredient to the same small dice so each forkful has a bit of everything; keep the avocado until just before serving or squeeze lemon over it.","s":3},{"t":"Arrange in rows and dress at the table; dressing on the lettuce early wilts it and makes the bacon soft.","s":4}],"rl":"VPPPVVGGFAXZ"},"3601":{"lv":"EERRRRROORO","w":{"3":"Adds tang and savory depth to the dressing.","4":"Brightens and balances the mayonnaise.","5":"Gives crunch and a fresh, grassy note.","6":"Sweet, juicy pops that contrast the savory chicken.","7":"Mild onion note.","8":"Fresh color and flavor; skip.","9":"Seasons the salad; cold dulls flavors, so taste after chilling.","10":"Mild bite.","2":"Lightens the dressing; mayo and lemon still dress the salad"},"sub":{"2":{"x":"2 tbsp more mayonnaise","d":"Richer and less tangy; add an extra squeeze of lemon to compensate.","k":["mayonnaise"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Poach the chicken gently in salted water or broth just until it reaches 165F (74C) and cool it in the liquid; it stays juicy instead of stringy.","s":0},{"t":"Chill the dressing mixture before adding warm chicken or it can turn oily; cool the chicken fully first.","s":1},{"t":"Taste for salt after chilling; cold mutes seasoning, and the salad usually needs another pinch before serving.","s":4}],"rl":"PFFXAVVXGZZ"},"3603":{"lv":"EERRRRRORO","w":{"2":"Adds sharp tang; skip and it tastes plain.","3":"Brightens the creamy mayo.","4":"Crunch and freshness.","5":"Sharp bite and crunch.","6":"Tangy crunch; a splash of pickle brine can stand in.","7":"Fresh color.","8":"Seasons the salad; tuna and mayo are mild.","9":"Mild bite."},"tips":[{"t":"Drain the tuna thoroughly, pressing on the lid, or the salad turns watery; oil-packed tuna tastes richer, so use less mayo with it.","s":0},{"t":"Flake the tuna into chunks and fold gently; stirring hard turns it to paste.","s":1},{"t":"A spoonful of pickle brine in the dressing adds tang and moisture without extra mayonnaise.","s":2}],"rl":"PFXAVXXGZZ"},"3605":{"lv":"EEERRRRRRO","w":{"3":"Adds sharp tang and emulsifies the dressing.","4":"Balances the bitterness of the kale and the lemon.","5":"Salty, nutty richness.","6":"Crunch and nutty flavor.","7":"Sweet chew that balances the bitter kale.","8":"Seasons the kale; unsalted kale tastes bitter and flat.","9":"Mild bite."},"tips":[{"t":"Strip the stems, slice the kale into thin ribbons, and massage it with the dressing for the full 2 minutes; it should turn darker and tender instead of raw and tough.","s":2},{"t":"Toast the almonds in a dry skillet 3 to 4 minutes until golden; raw almonds taste flat in a salad this simple.","s":3},{"t":"Unlike lettuce, massaged kale holds up; dress it 10 minutes ahead and it improves, and leftovers keep a day in the fridge.","s":4}],"rl":"VFAXWGGVZZ"},"3607":{"lv":"EERREREORO","w":{"2":"Adds sweet juicy pops; skip for a more apple-forward salad.","3":"Gives the salad its classic crunch and nutty richness.","7":"Rounds off the tartness of the apple and lemon.","8":"Seasons the dressing; a pinch lifts the sweetness.","9":"Just for serving; the salad works in a bowl.","5":"Adds tang to the dressing; the other dressing still coats the salad"},"sub":{"5":{"x":"1/3 cup sour cream","d":"Slightly richer and tangier; use plain yogurt if sour cream is not on hand.","k":["sour cream","plain yogurt"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Use crisp, sweet-tart apples such as Honeycrisp, Fuji or Granny Smith; soft apples turn mealy in the dressing.","s":1},{"t":"Toss the diced apple with the lemon dressing immediately; the acid keeps it from browning for hours.","s":1},{"t":"Toast the walnuts in a dry pan for 4 minutes and cool them before adding so they stay crisp in the dressing.","s":2}],"rl":"VVVGFFAWZG"},"52794":{"lv":"EEEEEERE","w":{"6":"Adds a warm aroma that deepens the chocolate; skip and it tastes a bit flat.","0":"The flour that gives the cake structure and leavening; without it there is no cake","1":"The sweetener and structure of the cake; without it the cake is bitter and dry","5":"The liquid of the vegan batter; without it the batter will not mix"},"sub":{"0":{"x":"1 1/4 cups all-purpose flour plus 1 3/4 tsp baking powder and 1/4 tsp salt","d":"Behaves the same as self-rising; keep the extra 1 tsp baking powder in the recipe too.","k":["all-purpose flour","baking powder","salt"]},"1":{"x":"1/2 cup packed brown sugar","d":"Moister with a slightly stronger molasses flavor; coconut sugar is already a brown sugar swap.","k":["brown sugar"]},"5":{"x":"1/2 cup soy or oat milk","d":"Keeps the cake vegan; soy gives the best structure and neutral flavor.","k":["soy milk","oat milk"],"wn":"Soy milk contains soy; oat milk may contain gluten"}},"tips":[{"t":"Make the flax eggs first: 2 tablespoons ground flax stirred into 6 tablespoons water, then rest 5 minutes until gelled; they bind but do not lift, so the baking powder matters.","s":0},{"t":"The thin batter and boiling water are intentional; the hot water blooms the cacao and makes a moist crumb. Do not add more flour.","s":0},{"t":"Cool the cake completely before decorating, or the melted vegan chocolate will slide off. Test with a toothpick; it should come out with a few moist crumbs.","s":0}],"rl":"SWXLTMXM"},"52816":{"lv":"ERRRREOEROOERRO","w":{"1":"Sweetness and body in the lentil base.","2":"Aromatic base for the lentils.","3":"Sweet, savory base for the lentils.","4":"Savory depth in the lentils.","6":"Subtle herbal background that is barely missed.","8":"Seasons the lentils; add more at the end, unsalted lentils taste flat.","10":"Mild bite.","12":"Aromatic herb that roasts with the eggplant.","13":"Rich crunch and nutty flavor; the dish is softer without them.","14":"Fresh color on top.","0":"The roasting fat for the eggplant; without it the eggplant dries and does not brown","9":"A small bit of acid in the dressing; the lemon and tahini still brighten"},"sub":{"0":{"x":"2 tbsp vegetable oil","d":"Same sauteing job; flavor is more neutral.","k":["vegetable oil"]},"9":{"x":"2 tsp red wine vinegar","d":"Same brightening effect; a little sharper.","k":["red wine vinegar"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Score the eggplant deeply and roast it cut side down first so the flesh steams and softens, then flip; it is done when the flesh collapses and the skin wrinkles.","s":1},{"t":"Add the vinegar to the lentils after they finish cooking; acid early can keep lentils from softening, and brown lentils hold their shape best.","s":0},{"t":"Toast the pine nuts in oil over medium heat and watch them closely; they go from golden to burnt in seconds.","s":2}],"rl":"FVVVXPXMZAZVXGG"},"52817":{"lv":"EEREREER","w":{"4":"Sweet, juicy tomatoes balance the heat; skip and the dish is drier.","7":"Fresh herb brightness and color.","0":"The cooking fat for the eggplant; without it the eggplant sticks and burns","2":"Adds the harissa heat; other spices still flavor the dish"},"sub":{"0":{"x":"4 tbsp vegetable oil","d":"Same cooking job; flavor is more neutral.","k":["vegetable oil"]},"2":{"x":"1/2 tbsp chili powder plus 1/4 tsp ground cumin and 1/4 tsp ground coriander","d":"Gives a similar warm heat; harissa has more garlic and smokiness.","k":["chili powder","cumin","coriander"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Rotate the eggplants every few minutes until browned on all sides, then cover so they steam through; they should be completely soft, with no resistance.","s":0},{"t":"Toast the cumin in a dry pan for 20 seconds before mixing into the yogurt; it deepens the flavor and perfumes the sauce.","s":2},{"t":"Cook the tomatoes until they burst and break down so they form a light sauce; finish with extra virgin olive oil for fresh flavor.","s":1}],"rl":"FVXPVGXG"},"13":{"lv":"EEEEEERER","w":{"6":"Crisp, fresh crunch; skip and the wrap is mostly chicken and dressing.","8":"Adds salty richness.","4":"The frying fat for the crispy chicken; without it it will not crisp"},"sub":{"4":{"x":"2 tbsp olive oil","d":"Same frying job; slightly fruitier flavor.","k":["olive oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Shake off excess flour and press the panko on firmly; the coating stays crisp rather than falling off. The chicken must reach 165F (74C).","s":0},{"t":"Rest the cutlets on a wire rack for a few minutes; paper or a flat plate steams the bottom and softens the crust.","s":1},{"t":"Toss the lettuce with the dressing at the last moment and warm the tortillas first so they fold without cracking.","s":3}],"rl":"PSTSFBVFX"},"40":{"lv":"EEERRRREOORO","w":{"3":"Fresh juicy slices; seed them to keep the sandwich from getting soggy.","4":"Peppery crunch added at the end; skip and the sandwich is richer and heavier.","5":"Browns into a crisp, salty cheese crust on the outside.","8":"Brightens the spread.","9":"Savory note; skip.","11":"Sweet-sour finish; optional.","6":"Spread for the sandwich; the pesto and cheese still flavor it","10":"Helps crisp the bread; a dry pan will still toast it a little"},"sub":{"6":{"x":"2 tbsp plain Greek yogurt or sour cream","d":"Tangier and slightly less rich in the spread; it will not brown the same way.","k":["greek yogurt","sour cream"]},"10":{"x":"1 tbsp melted butter","d":"Browns faster and tastes richer; watch the heat so it does not burn.","k":["butter"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Cook on medium-low and press with a spatula; high heat burns the outside before the provolone melts. A lid for the last minute helps.","s":2},{"t":"Brush the oil and sprinkle the parmesan on the bread so it fries into a golden cheese crust.","s":2},{"t":"Add the arugula after cooking so it stays fresh instead of wilting inside the hot sandwich.","s":3}],"rl":"BPFVGXFXAXFG"},"41":{"lv":"ERREROOEEE","w":{"1":"Sharp bite that lifts the tuna; skip and it is richer and flatter.","2":"Tangy crunch; the splash of juice also brightens the mix.","4":"Adds sharp tang.","5":"Rounds off the tang with a little sweetness.","6":"A little heat; optional.","9":"The fat that toasts the bread of the melt; without it the sandwich is pale and dry"},"sub":{"9":{"x":"2 tbsp mayonnaise","d":"Spread thinly on the outsides, it browns evenly and crisply with a faint tang.","k":["mayonnaise"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Drain the tuna very well; excess liquid makes the filling soggy and the melt steams instead of crisping.","s":0},{"t":"Put cheese on both sides of the tuna so it seals the bread from moisture and holds the sandwich together.","s":1},{"t":"Cook over medium-low with the lid on so the cheese melts fully before the bread browns too dark.","s":2}],"rl":"PXAFXWXBFF"},"45":{"lv":"EERRERRRO","w":{"2":"Sweetens and flavors the juices.","3":"Savory depth that infuses the butter.","6":"Gives herbal flavor; skip and it still tastes of garlic butter.","7":"Seasons the steak and potatoes; skip and the packets taste flat.","8":"Mild bite.","5":"Coats the potatoes; the garlic butter also coats them"},"sub":{"5":{"x":"1 tbsp vegetable oil","d":"Same coating job; flavor is more neutral.","k":["vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Dice the steak and potatoes to the same small size so they finish together; the potatoes get a microwave head start because they take longer.","s":0},{"t":"Use heavy-duty foil or double up, and leave some room inside the packet so steam can circulate instead of squashing the food.","s":2},{"t":"Open the packets carefully, tilting them away from your face; the escaping steam is very hot. Rest 3 to 5 minutes so the juices thicken.","s":3}],"rl":"PBXXFFXZZ"},"72":{"lv":"EERERRR","w":{"2":"Smoky, salty crunch that makes it a club; without it you have a turkey-avocado sandwich that is milder and softer.","4":"Adds juice and acidity to cut the avocado and mayo; skipping makes the sandwich richer and drier.","5":"Gives a cool crisp layer and some structure; skipping loses crunch but nothing else changes.","6":"Moistens the toast and acts as glue; avocado is already creamy so a thin spread is enough."},"sub":{"6":{"x":"2 tbsp plain Greek yogurt","d":"Tangier and lighter; spread thin so it does not make the toast soggy.","k":["greek yogurt"],"wn":"Contains dairy"}},"tips":[{"t":"Let the toast cool for a minute on a rack before building. Hot toast steams the lettuce and softens the bread from the inside.","s":0},{"t":"Slice the avocado thin and salt the tomato lightly. Put lettuce against the bread as a moisture barrier, with tomato in the middle of the stack.","s":1},{"t":"Pin each quarter with a pick through the center, then cut with a serrated knife using a sawing motion so the layers do not squish out.","s":2}],"rl":"BPPVVVF"},"76":{"lv":"EERRRERROR","w":{"2":"Sweet pop and a little starch; skipping makes the bowl less hearty and less colorful.","3":"Fresh acidity and juice against the beans; skipping loses brightness, though salsa covers some of it.","4":"Cool creamy contrast to the warm beans; skip it and the bowl is drier, so add a little more salsa.","6":"Earthy warmth that makes the beans taste Tex-Mex rather than canned; skip and the beans are flat.","7":"Carries the lime dressing and adds richness; any neutral oil works.","8":"Fresh herbal lift on top; skipping barely changes the bowl.","9":"Adds salt, acid and moisture so the quinoa is not dry; skip and add extra lime and a pinch of salt."},"sub":{"7":{"x":"2 tbsp avocado oil or any neutral oil","d":"Slightly less fruity than olive oil; works fine in a lime dressing.","k":["vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Rinse the quinoa in a fine strainer first to remove the bitter saponin coating. Use 2 cups water, simmer covered 15 minutes, then rest 5 minutes off heat before fluffing.","s":0},{"t":"Warm the cumin in the bean liquid with a little of the oil for a minute so it blooms. Raw cumin sprinkled on tastes dusty.","s":1},{"t":"Toss the quinoa with lime, oil and a pinch of salt while it is still warm so it absorbs the dressing. Add the avocado last so it stays green and intact.","s":3}],"rl":"BPVVFAXFGX"},"77":{"lv":"EERRREO","w":{"2":"Binds and adds richness, but the avocado is already creamy; you can skip it for a lighter, greener spread.","3":"Sharp tang that keeps the avocado and yolk from tasting flat; skipping makes it blander.","4":"Brightens and slows avocado browning; without it the spread browns faster and tastes heavy.","6":"Salty, garlicky crunch; if you skip it, add a pinch of salt and pepper because the recipe has none."},"sub":{"4":{"x":"1 tbsp lime juice","d":"Same acid and anti-browning effect with a slightly more tropical note.","k":["lime"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Boiling 10 minutes gives a grey-green yolk ring. For creamier yolks bring the water to a boil, cover, turn off the heat and wait 10 to 11 minutes, then chill in ice water.","s":0},{"t":"Mash the avocado only partly so the texture stays chunky, and fold in the eggs gently. Taste before salting because everything bagel seasoning is salty.","s":1},{"t":"Assemble right before eating. If you have to wait, press plastic wrap directly onto the surface of the salad to keep air off the avocado.","s":2}],"rl":"PVFXABG"},"79":{"lv":"ERRREERRRRR","w":{"1":"Garlic rounds out the shrimp; skip and the shrimp tastes of paprika only.","2":"Cooking fat for the shrimp; butter or any neutral oil works.","3":"Smoky color and mild flavor on the shrimp; skipping leaves them pale and plainer.","6":"Cool crunch against the rich avocado and spicy sauce; skip it and the bowl is softer.","7":"Sweet pop and color; skipping makes the bowl less filling and sweet.","8":"Base of the spicy sauce; a yogurt version is lighter.","9":"The heat in the sauce; skip it and you get plain mayo, so add chili flakes or hot sauce instead.","10":"Acid to cut the rich avocado and sauce; do not skip unless you add vinegar."},"sub":{"2":{"x":"1 tbsp butter or 1 tbsp vegetable oil","d":"Butter gives a richer, slightly browned finish on the shrimp.","k":["butter","vegetable oil"]},"8":{"x":"2 tbsp plain Greek yogurt","d":"Tangier and lighter; stir in a pinch of salt as the yogurt is less seasoned.","k":["greek yogurt"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Pat the shrimp bone dry and have the pan hot before they go in. Pull them the moment they curl into a loose C; a tight O means overcooked and rubbery.","s":1},{"t":"Toss the frozen corn with the hot rice for the last few minutes of its rest. The steam thaws it without dirtying another pan.","s":0},{"t":"Rinse the rice until the water runs nearly clear, then use about 3 cups water for the 1.5 cups rice for separate, fluffy grains.","s":0}],"rl":"PXFXBVVVFXA"},"3139":{"lv":"EREROEE","w":{"3":"Seasons the beef and onion; the cheese and roll are mild, so skimping leaves the sandwich flat.","4":"A pinch of heat; skip it with little loss.","1":"High-heat cooking fat for the onions and beef; canola or another neutral oil is equivalent.","5":"Mild, creamy melt that defines this sandwich; white American melts even smoother."},"sub":{"1":{"x":"2 tbsp canola oil or other neutral oil","d":"Same function; olive oil works but smokes at the high heat used for the beef.","k":["vegetable oil","canola oil"]},"5":{"x":"8 slices white American cheese","d":"Melts smoother and saltier, a classic cheesesteak choice; provolone has more bite.","k":["american cheese"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Slice the semi-frozen beef across the grain, almost transparent, with your sharpest knife. Thin slices stay tender in a minute or two of cooking.","s":0},{"t":"Cook the beef on very high heat in two batches if needed. Crowding makes it steam grey and tough; stop cooking when the pink is just gone.","s":2},{"t":"Pile the beef and cheese into mounds, cover the pan for the cheese to melt, then press the open roll over a mound and flip it. The cheese fuses to the roll.","s":3}],"rl":"PFVZZFB"},"3146":{"lv":"EEEOERRO","w":{"3":"A background savory note; the buffalo sauce is bold enough that skipping barely shows.","5":"Butter mellows the vinegar heat and makes the sauce cling and glossy; skipping gives a sharper, thinner coating.","6":"Cool, creamy contrast to the heat; skip if you serve blue cheese or another dip.","7":"Crunch and a cooling bite; purely a serving extra."},"tips":[{"t":"Use aluminum-free baking powder, and for crispier skin let the seasoned wings sit uncovered on the rack in the fridge for an hour or more before baking. The baking powder raises the pH so the skin browns and blisters.","s":1},{"t":"Wings are safe at 165°F, but they are best at 175 to 185°F, where the collagen has melted. That is why the long bake works.","s":2},{"t":"Toss the hot wings in the warm sauce in a large bowl right before serving. Sauce that sits on them softens the crust within minutes.","s":4}],"rl":"PXZXXFGG"},"3150":{"lv":"EEEEERRE","w":{"5":"Gives the dressing its pink color and sweetness; chili sauce works as well.","6":"Adds tang and crunch to the dressing; skipping makes it plainer, so add a splash of pickle brine.","7":"Butters the outside for a golden crust; mayo does the same job."},"sub":{"7":{"x":"2 tbsp mayonnaise","d":"Spread thinly on the outside; browns evenly and crisps a little more than butter.","k":["mayonnaise"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Squeeze the sauerkraut in a towel until it stops dripping. Wet kraut turns the bread soggy and steams the sandwich.","s":2},{"t":"Put the cheese against the bread on both sides of the pile as a moisture barrier, with kraut and corned beef between.","s":2},{"t":"Cook on medium-low and cover the pan for the last minute so the cheese melts before the rye over-browns.","s":4}],"rl":"BPFVFXXF"},"3610":{"lv":"EERRRERREERER","w":{"2":"Adds crunch and mild heat to the olive salad; skip and the salad is more olive-forward and less tangy.","3":"Briny pops of salt and acid; skipping weakens the signature olive salad.","4":"Raw garlic punch that mellows as it marinates; skip for a milder spread.","6":"Acid that brightens the olive salad.","7":"Earthy herb that makes the olive salad taste Italian-American; skip with moderate loss.","10":"Mild, silky pork cold cut; extra ham or salami replaces it.","12":"Mellow, milky layer that balances the salty meats; Swiss or more provolone works."},"sub":{"6":{"x":"1 tbsp apple cider vinegar","d":"Slightly fruitier and sharper; white wine vinegar or lemon juice also work.","k":["apple cider vinegar"]},"10":{"x":"1/4 lb more ham or salami","d":"Less delicate; use the saltier salami if substituting ham.","k":["ham","salami"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Make the olive salad a day ahead if you can. The oil, garlic and brine mellow and the flavor roughly doubles in depth.","s":0},{"t":"Press with a heavy pan or cast-iron for the full 30 minutes, or up to a few hours in the fridge, so the oil soaks into the bread and the layers fuse.","s":4},{"t":"Slice with a serrated knife in a sawing motion. Serve at room temperature because chilled cheese and meats dull the flavor.","s":5}],"rl":"BXXXXFAXPPPPP"},"3611":{"lv":"EERROERRO","w":{"2":"Brightens the sweet lobster and cuts the mayo; skipping makes the salad heavy.","3":"Fresh crunch against the soft lobster; skipping makes the filling softer.","4":"Mild oniony note; skipping barely matters.","6":"Butter toasting gives the nutty crisp contrast to the cold salad; oil toasts but tastes less rich.","7":"Lobster and mayo are mild, so seasoning is needed to bring out the flavor.","8":"Slight bite; the dish tastes fine without it."},"tips":[{"t":"Pat the lobster dry before mixing. Excess water thins the mayo and washes out the dressing.","s":1},{"t":"Use just enough mayo to bind; the filling should read as lobster first. Keep it cold (below 40°F) until it goes into the buns.","s":0},{"t":"Toast the buns on their flat sides, where the crumb is soft and takes color evenly, then fill them while they are still warm.","s":3}],"rl":"PFAVXBFZZ"},"3612":{"lv":"EERRERRORO","w":{"2":"Melty, mild cheese that makes it a hot sandwich; skip it and you have a classic dip.","3":"Fat for browning the onion; olive oil works.","5":"Adds savory, tangy depth to the jus; skip and the broth tastes thin.","6":"Gives the jus a roasted-garlic note; skipping is a small loss.","7":"Subtle herbal note; the onion and Worcestershire carry most of it.","8":"Sweet, jammy flavor that makes the jus taste richer; skip and it is less layered.","9":"Slight bite; the broth and Worcestershire already season."},"sub":{"3":{"x":"1 tbsp olive oil","d":"Slightly less rich but browns the onion just as well.","k":["olive oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Simmer the jus until it tastes beefy before seasoning. Boxed broth is already salty and concentrating it can overshoot, so adjust at the end.","s":1},{"t":"Only warm the sliced beef in the jus for about 2 minutes. Longer turns thin slices grey and tough.","s":2},{"t":"Dunk only the cut side of the roll or serve the jus on the side, since a fully soaked roll falls apart. Watch the broiler closely because it takes seconds to scorch.","s":4}],"rl":"PBFFMXXXVZ"},"3613":{"lv":"EEEEERRRO","w":{"2":"Fat for browning the onions and crisping the bread.","5":"Adds tang and helps the bread brown on the inside; skipping makes it slightly drier.","6":"Deepens the beef; skipping makes the patty plainer.","7":"Beef and bread need seasoning; skipping gives a flat sandwich.","8":"Slight bite; the sandwich works without it."},"sub":{"2":{"x":"1 tbsp olive oil for the onions + 3 tbsp mayonnaise spread on the outside of the bread","d":"Mayo gives a very crisp golden crust, slightly tangier than butter.","k":["olive oil","mayonnaise"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Cook the onions on medium-low and stir rarely. Deep golden, jammy onions take the full 12 to 15 minutes, and a splash of water frees stuck bits.","s":0},{"t":"Handle the beef lightly and make the patties wider than the bread, since they shrink as they cook. Pull them at 160°F.","s":1},{"t":"Put cheese on both sides of the patty and onions so they glue the sandwich together. Griddle on medium so the cheese melts before the rye over-browns.","s":4}],"rl":"PVFBFFXZZ"},"3614":{"lv":"EERROOEORO","w":{"1":"Binder; plain Greek yogurt can replace it for a tangier, lighter salad.","2":"Adds sharp tang and some emulsifying; skipping makes the salad plainer.","3":"Crunch against soft eggs; skipping makes the texture uniform.","4":"Gentle onion note; purely a flavor accent.","5":"A little acid that brightens the yolks; mustard already helps, so skipping is fine.","7":"A crisp layer; skip with no real loss.","8":"Eggs are bland and need salt to taste like egg salad.","9":"Slight bite; optional."},"sub":{"1":{"x":"1/3 cup plain Greek yogurt","d":"Tangier and lighter; salt more and stir in an extra pinch of mustard.","k":["greek yogurt"],"wn":"Contains dairy"}},"tips":[{"t":"Eggs 7 to 10 days old peel far more easily than very fresh ones. After the ice bath, crack and peel under running water.","s":1},{"t":"Chop the yolks and whites separately and fold in the yolks first so the mayo makes a creamy base. This avoids both mush and dry chunks.","s":3},{"t":"Keep the salad refrigerated and use within 4 days. Do not leave the sandwiches out more than 2 hours.","s":4}],"rl":"PTXVXABGZZ"},"19":{"lv":"EREREEERER","w":{"1":"Sweetens and builds the soup base; skip and it is flatter.","3":"Adds savory background; onion and broth already carry a lot.","7":"Adds tang and silky body; skipping gives a thinner, less luscious soup.","9":"Potatoes are bland and need salt; add in stages and taste at the end."},"tips":[{"t":"Add the milk-roux and dairy only in the last 30 minutes on low. Long cooking or high heat can make the dairy grainy.","s":2},{"t":"Soften the cream cheese first and whisk it with a ladle of hot broth before stirring it in. Cold cream cheese stays in lumps.","s":2},{"t":"Mash only about a third of the potatoes so the soup is thick but still chunky. Shred the cheddar from a block because pre-shredded melts grainy.","s":3}],"rl":"BXMXFTMFFZ"},"20":{"lv":"ERRRRERROOEERRRO","w":{"1":"Cooking fat for the aromatics; butter or any oil works.","2":"Sweet base that rounds out the peppers and spices.","3":"Savory depth; skip and the chili tastes less rounded.","4":"Mild grassy heat that defines white chicken chili; canned green chiles are the usual stand-in.","6":"Adds mild sweetness and color; skip with small loss.","7":"Adds an earthy, slightly minty note that supports the cumin.","8":"A subtle citrusy note; skipping is barely noticeable.","9":"A small kick; skip it for a mild chili.","12":"Sweetness and texture against the spice; skipping makes it less colorful.","13":"Gives the creamy body; heavy cream is looser but works.","14":"Brightens the creamy chili; skipping makes it feel heavy.","15":"Fresh finish; purely optional."},"sub":{"1":{"x":"1 tbsp butter or vegetable oil","d":"Butter adds a little richness; either works.","k":["butter","vegetable oil"]},"4":{"x":"1 can (4 oz) diced green chiles","d":"Milder and tangier than fresh poblano; stir in with the broth and skip the 5-minute saute.","k":["green chiles"]},"13":{"x":"1/2 cup heavy cream, simmered 3 minutes","d":"Thinner and less tangy; mash a few extra beans to thicken.","k":["heavy cream"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Thighs stay juicy for the whole simmer; they are safe at 165°F but tender and shreddable once they reach about 175°F or more.","s":1},{"t":"Cube the cream cheese and whisk it with a ladle of hot broth off the boil before stirring in. It will not clump or break.","s":3},{"t":"Add the lime juice at the end because heat dulls it. Skim any chicken fat off the surface before adding the cream cheese.","s":3}],"rl":"PFXXVXXXXXMBVFAG"},"3616":{"lv":"EERRRRORRO","w":{"2":"Moisture so the chicken does not dry; plain water works.","3":"Balances the vinegar and adds caramel notes.","4":"Tang that cuts through the sweet sauce.","5":"Mild heat and earthiness; skip and the sauce is plain barbecue.","6":"Savory background; the sauce has plenty already.","7":"Smoky depth, standing in for the pit; skipping makes it plainer.","8":"Seasons the meat through the long cook; chicken breast is bland.","9":"Optional."},"sub":{"2":{"x":"1/2 cup water + 1/4 tsp salt","d":"Less savory but works; the barbecue sauce supplies most of the flavor.","k":["water"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Breasts dry out fast. Start checking at 2 1/2 hours on high or 4 hours on low, and pull as soon as they hit 165°F and shred.","s":2},{"t":"Shred while hot and return the meat to the juices for 10 minutes so it reabsorbs moisture. Do not lift the lid mid-cook.","s":3},{"t":"Chill the pulled chicken within 2 hours and store it in its juices so it reheats without drying. Reheat to 165°F.","s":4}],"rl":"PXMWAXXXZZ"},"3619":{"lv":"ERRRREERRERERERO","w":{"1":"Browns the beef and sautes the vegetables; any neutral oil works.","2":"Sweet base that builds body.","3":"Adds sweetness and vegetal flavor; skipping thins the depth.","4":"Savory depth; skip and the chili is less rounded.","7":"Smoky depth; skipping makes it less complex.","8":"Earthy herbal note; skip with small loss.","10":"Concentrated tomato for depth; browning it first is what gives the chili richness.","12":"A second bean for texture; kidney beans alone are fine.","13":"Liquid; broth gives savoriness, water makes a plainer chili.","14":"Seasons the large pot; chili needs it.","15":"Slight heat; optional."},"sub":{"1":{"x":"2 tbsp vegetable oil","d":"Same function; the ground beef adds its own fat.","k":["vegetable oil"]},"13":{"x":"2 cups chicken broth or 2 cups water + 1 tsp soy sauce","d":"Chicken broth is milder; the soy adds umami if using water.","k":["chicken broth","water"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Cook the chili powder, cumin and tomato paste in the pot with the vegetables for the full 2 minutes until the paste darkens to brick red. This blooms the spices.","s":2},{"t":"Brown the beef in batches if the pot is crowded, and cook it to 160°F. Drain most but not all of the fat for flavor.","s":0},{"t":"For a batch this size, cool it in shallow containers so it drops below 40°F within a few hours. Add a splash of vinegar when reheating to wake up the flavors.","s":5}],"rl":"PFVVXXXXXAABBMZZ"},"3620":{"lv":"EERROROO","w":{"2":"Sweetens; honey works as well.","3":"Rounds out the flavor; skipping makes it plainer.","4":"Brings out sweetness; skip with small loss.","5":"Fruit and tartness on top; skip or use any berry.","6":"Fresh sweet topping; any fruit replaces it.","7":"Crunch contrast; adding at serving keeps it crisp."},"sub":{"2":{"x":"2 tbsp honey","d":"Slightly more floral and stickier; whisk it in well so it dissolves.","k":["honey"],"wn":"Do not give honey to children under 1 year old"}},"tips":[{"t":"Whisk twice, once right away and again after 5 minutes. This stops the seeds from clumping into a gel block at the bottom.","s":1},{"t":"The ratio is about 1 part chia to 4 parts milk for a soft pudding. If it is too thick, whisk in a few tablespoons of milk; if it is runny, add a tablespoon of chia and wait 30 minutes.","s":2},{"t":"Keep the berries and granola separate until serving. Wet granola goes soggy and the berries weep.","s":4}],"rl":"TMWXZGGG"},"3623":{"lv":"ERORROEEERR","w":{"1":"Roasting fat for chicken and broccoli; any neutral oil works.","2":"Savory background in the rub; the paprika and soy carry most of it.","3":"Smoky color and flavor on the chicken; skipping makes it plainer.","4":"Chicken breast and rice need seasoning; split between rub and broccoli.","5":"Slight bite; optional.","9":"Balances the soy; maple or brown sugar replaces it.","10":"Acid to balance the soy and honey; cider vinegar is close."},"sub":{"1":{"x":"2 tbsp vegetable oil or avocado oil","d":"Neutral; olive oil gives a slightly fruitier roast.","k":["vegetable oil"]},"9":{"x":"1 tbsp maple syrup","d":"Slightly woodsy; sauce is a bit thinner.","k":["maple syrup"]},"10":{"x":"1 tbsp apple cider vinegar + 1/4 tsp sugar","d":"A touch fruitier and sharper than rice vinegar.","k":["apple cider vinegar","sugar"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Large breasts cook unevenly. Butterfly or pound the thick end to an even thickness so every piece is done at 165°F at the same time.","s":0},{"t":"Roast the broccoli on its own pan with space between florets so it browns and crisps instead of steaming.","s":2},{"t":"For meal prep, spread the rice on a tray to cool within an hour before packing. Store the sauce separately and reheat everything until steaming hot.","s":5}],"rl":"PFXXZZBVXWA"},"17":{"lv":"EROOEEERO","w":{"1":"Gives the meat a savory crust; fresh garlic later adds the louder note.","2":"Adds a mild sweet-savory background; skip with small loss.","3":"Color and mild sweetness; barely noticeable.","7":"Chicken and the butter sauce need seasoning; the parmesan adds salt, so go light.","8":"Slight bite; optional."},"tips":[{"t":"Cut cutlets or cubes to an even size so they cook to 165°F together. Cook in two batches to avoid steaming.","s":1},{"t":"Drop the heat before adding the remaining butter and garlic. Garlic burns in about 30 seconds and turns bitter.","s":2},{"t":"Take the pan off the heat before adding the parmesan and use finely grated cheese. Over high heat it clumps into a rubbery mass instead of a sauce.","s":3}],"rl":"PXXXFXXZZ"},"35":{"lv":"EREOEE","w":{"1":"Cool tang that tames the heat; sour cream or ranch can replace it.","3":"Faint savory note; skip it with no real loss.","4":"Melts into a glue that holds the filling in the tortilla; skip and it falls apart."},"sub":{"1":{"x":"1/3 cup ranch dressing or sour cream","d":"Ranch is more herby; sour cream is a bit richer and thicker.","k":["ranch dressing","sour cream"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Shred the rotisserie chicken finely and remove the skin. Large pieces tear the tortilla and make the filling uneven.","s":0},{"t":"Do not overfill; fold like an envelope and cook seam side down first so it seals, on medium heat so the cheese melts before the tortilla scorches.","s":2},{"t":"Press lightly with a spatula while it toasts for an even, crisp surface. Rest 1 minute before cutting so the filling sets.","s":2}],"rl":"PFXXTB"},"38":{"lv":"ERERRROEEEO","w":{"1":"Browns the sausage and sautes the vegetables; any neutral oil works.","3":"Sweetness and base for the rice; skipping leaves it less savory.","4":"Savory depth; skipping is a small loss.","5":"Smoky echo of the sausage; plain paprika also works.","6":"A subtle warm note; barely noticed.","10":"Fresh finish; purely garnish."},"sub":{"1":{"x":"1 tbsp vegetable oil or butter","d":"Butter gives a richer, slightly browned base.","k":["vegetable oil","butter"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Toast the rice in the sausage fat for a minute before adding liquid. It keeps the grains separate and adds a nutty edge.","s":1},{"t":"A can of tomatoes plus 2 cups of broth is a lot of liquid for 1 cup of rice. If it is soupy after 20 minutes, uncover and cook another 3 to 5 minutes; if it is dry and hard, add a splash of broth and cover.","s":2},{"t":"Do not lift the lid while it simmers, and let it stand 5 minutes off the heat before stirring so the rice finishes steaming.","s":2}],"rl":"PFVVXXXBAMG"},"94":{"lv":"EEERRROO","w":{"3":"Roasted garlic note for the chicken and potatoes; skipping makes it plainer.","4":"Carries the seasoning and browns the potatoes; butter or any oil works.","5":"Herbal backbone; use oregano and thyme if you do not have it.","6":"Color and mild flavor; skip with small loss.","7":"Fresh finish; garnish."},"sub":{"4":{"x":"3 tbsp melted butter or vegetable oil","d":"Butter browns a bit faster and tastes richer.","k":["butter","vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Cut the potatoes small, about 3/4 inch. Bigger pieces will still be hard when the chicken is done.","s":0},{"t":"Use two pans if needed. A crowded pan steams instead of roasting, leaving pale skin and soft potatoes.","s":2},{"t":"Chicken is safe at 165°F but thighs are best at 175 to 185°F. Squeeze fresh lemon over the pan at the end because roasted lemon turns bitter.","s":2}],"rl":"PBAXFXXG"},"96":{"lv":"EREERRRR","w":{"1":"Cooking fat for the chops; any neutral oil works.","2":"Sticky sweet glaze; maple syrup or brown sugar works.","4":"Rounds out the glaze; skipping leaves it one-note.","5":"Warm spice that complements the soy; skip and it is simpler.","6":"Butter coats the green beans; oil works.","7":"Green vegetable side; omit if you serve something else."},"sub":{"1":{"x":"1 tbsp vegetable oil","d":"Same function; olive oil is fine at this heat.","k":["vegetable oil"]},"2":{"x":"3 tbsp maple syrup","d":"Less floral; works in the glaze, but reduces slightly slower.","k":["maple syrup"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Pat the chops dry and pull them at 140°F. They rise to 145°F while resting 3 minutes, and thin chops overcook fast.","s":0},{"t":"Lower the heat before adding the honey and soy. Honey scorches quickly, so cook the glaze just 1 to 2 minutes and keep spooning it over.","s":1},{"t":"Cover the green beans with a splash of water for 3 minutes to steam, then uncover to let the water cook off and finish in the butter.","s":2}],"rl":"PFWXXXFV"},"104":{"lv":"EERRRRRO","w":{"2":"Color and sweetness; swap in any vegetable.","3":"Soft, mild vegetable; skip or swap with more broccoli.","4":"Crisp-edged florets that brown nicely; fine to replace.","5":"Carries the seasoning and browns the vegetables.","6":"Herbal flavor; any dried herb mix replaces it.","7":"Color and mild smoke; skip with small loss."},"sub":{"5":{"x":"3 tbsp vegetable oil or melted butter","d":"Same roasting function; butter browns faster.","k":["vegetable oil","butter"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Cut dense vegetables smaller than soft ones. Halve the baby potatoes and cut zucchini and broccoli into larger pieces so they all finish together.","s":1},{"t":"Preheat the sheet pan so the potatoes sizzle on contact for browned edges. Spread everything in one layer, using two pans if necessary.","s":0},{"t":"Slice the sausage on a bias for more surface to brown, and flip the vegetables once midway through the 20 minutes.","s":2}],"rl":"PBVVVFXX"},"105":{"lv":"EEERRRR","w":{"3":"Salt and umami that balance the maple; skip and add a pinch of salt.","4":"Savory edge for the glaze; skipping makes it sweeter.","5":"Green vegetable side on the same pan; fine to swap or skip.","6":"Coats the asparagus so it roasts instead of drying; butter works.","1":"The maple syrup is the base of the glaze; without it the salmon is not maple dijon"},"sub":{"1":{"x":"3 tbsp honey","d":"Slightly more floral and a little thicker; glaze still caramelizes at 400°F.","k":["honey"],"wn":"Do not serve honey to children under 1 year"},"6":{"x":"1 tbsp melted butter","d":"Richer; same coating effect.","k":["butter"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Pat the salmon dry so the glaze clings. Reserve a spoonful of clean glaze for finishing, and do not reuse the brush on the raw fish.","s":1},{"t":"Salmon is fully cooked at 145°F (USDA); pull it at the first sign of white albumin seeping out, which means it is drying.","s":2},{"t":"Thin asparagus cooks in about 8 minutes, so add it partway through if your spears are slim. Line the pan with parchment since the maple burns on bare metal.","s":1}],"rl":"PWXXXVF"},"110":{"lv":"EEEEOORRR","w":{"4":"Color on the crust; skip and the tenders are paler.","5":"Mild savory note in the flour; panko and the sauce carry it.","6":"Sweet part of the dipping sauce; skip and it is a tangy mustard-mayo.","7":"Sharp tang in the dipping sauce; skipping makes it very sweet.","8":"Creamy base for the dip; skip and the honey-mustard is thinner."},"tips":[{"t":"Use one dry hand and one wet hand for dredging so the coating does not clump onto your fingers. Press the panko firmly onto each strip.","s":1},{"t":"Coat the panko generously with spray. The air fryer has no oil bath, and any dry panko stays pale.","s":2},{"t":"Cook in a single layer and flip halfway. The chicken is done at 165°F; salt the flour well since the breading has no other seasoning.","s":2}],"rl":"PTTTXXWXF"},"113":{"lv":"EEERERRO","w":{"3":"Savory garlic sauce note; skipping loses the dish's butter-garlic character.","5":"Silky sauce; half-and-half works but is thinner.","6":"Earthy herb to flavor the mushrooms; skip with small loss.","7":"Fresh finish; garnish."},"sub":{"5":{"x":"1/2 cup half-and-half","d":"Thinner and lighter; simmer gently, do not boil hard.","k":["half and half"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Pound the chicken to an even thickness so it reaches 165°F evenly without drying out at the thin end.","s":0},{"t":"Give the mushrooms space and do not salt them until they are browned. Salt draws water and makes them steam instead.","s":1},{"t":"Return the chicken only long enough to warm it through. Boiling the cream hard can break the sauce and toughen the meat.","s":3}],"rl":"PFVXMFXG"},"116":{"lv":"EEERRERROO","w":{"3":"Savory depth in the lean patty; skipping makes it plainer.","4":"Soaks juices so the lean turkey stays moist; rolled oats work.","6":"Melty, mild cheese; skip or swap with any cheese.","7":"Creamy lemony spread; skipping makes the burger drier.","8":"Crisp freshness; garnish-like.","9":"Fresh slice for juiciness; garnish-like."},"sub":{"4":{"x":"1/4 cup quick oats","d":"Slightly chewier; absorbs moisture the same way.","k":["oats"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Ground turkey is lean and bland. Add about 3/4 tsp salt to the mix (none is listed) and handle it gently to keep the patties tender.","s":0},{"t":"Press a shallow dimple in the center of each patty so it does not dome. Cook until a thermometer reads 165°F because color alone is unreliable.","s":1},{"t":"Zest the lemon before juicing it. The zest carries the lemon-pepper aroma that juice alone cannot.","s":0}],"rl":"PAZXTBGFGG"},"117":{"lv":"EEEEEEO","w":{"6":"Cool, tangy topping; skip it with no real change."},"tips":[{"t":"Rub the potatoes with oil and salt and bake them directly on the rack, not in foil, for crisp skin. Prick them first so they do not burst.","s":0},{"t":"Cook the roux for a full minute, add the milk gradually, and take the pan off the heat before the cheddar. Shred it from a block for a smooth sauce.","s":2},{"t":"If you microwave the potatoes, finish them 10 minutes in a hot oven to crisp the skin.","s":0}],"rl":"BVFTMFG"},"3137":{"lv":"EROOREEOR","w":{"1":"Seasons the beef so the butter sauce does not taste flat.","2":"Slight bite; optional.","3":"Seasons the crust; fresh garlic carries the dish.","4":"High-heat sear fat; canola or avocado oil works.","7":"Fresh finish; garnish.","8":"Savory depth for the pan sauce; skip and it is a little flatter."},"sub":{"4":{"x":"1 tbsp canola oil or avocado oil","d":"Higher smoke point; olive oil may burn at this heat.","k":["vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Sear in two batches in a very hot pan so the cubes brown rather than steam. Dry the steak well first.","s":2},{"t":"Lower the heat before adding the butter and garlic and cook garlic only 30 seconds. Burnt garlic ruins the sauce.","s":3},{"t":"Under-sear slightly in the first step, since the steak finishes in the butter for another minute and will keep cooking from residual heat.","s":4}],"rl":"PZZXFFXGX"},"3143":{"lv":"ERORERRRRO","w":{"1":"Season right after smashing; beef needs it.","2":"Slight bite; optional.","5":"Sharp, sweet bite on top; skip and it is milder.","6":"Acid and crunch that cut the fat; skip and the burger feels heavy.","7":"Creamy base of the sauce.","8":"Sweet and tangy part of the sauce.","9":"Small tang in the sauce; skip with little change.","3":"Melted cheese on the burgers; burgers still work but are less classic"},"sub":{"3":{"x":"8 slices mild cheddar or Monterey Jack","d":"Less creamy melt and sharper; still melts well on a hot thin patty.","k":["cheddar","monterey jack"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Keep the beef balls cold until they hit the pan and smash them in the first 10 seconds with parchment between spatula and meat so they do not stick.","s":2},{"t":"Use a very hot cast iron pan. The crisp, lacy edge comes from the Maillard crust forming in the first 2 minutes.","s":1},{"t":"Scrape under the patty with a stiff spatula to keep the crust, then cover with a bowl for 20 seconds so the cheese melts. Thin patties reach 160°F within the cook time.","s":3}],"rl":"PZZFBGGXXX"},"3151":{"lv":"EERERRO","w":{"2":"Creamy moisture for the toast; skipping makes the sandwich dry.","4":"Cool, crisp layer; skip and it is meatier and less fresh.","5":"Seasons the tomato, which is mostly water; skip and it tastes flat.","6":"Slight bite; optional."},"tips":[{"t":"Start the bacon in a cold pan on medium-low so it renders and crisps evenly without burning. Let it drain on paper towels.","s":0},{"t":"Salt the tomato slices for a couple of minutes and blot them on a towel. The sandwich gets all the flavor without extra juice soaking the bread.","s":2},{"t":"Put the lettuce against the bread, tomato in the middle. This keeps the bacon crisp and the bread from getting wet.","s":3}],"rl":"PBFVVZZ"},"3153":{"lv":"EERE","w":{"2":"Adds sharper flavor and a stretchy pull; skip and use only American for a milder sandwich.","3":"Butter browns the bread and adds richness; mayo gives a crisper crust."},"sub":{"3":{"x":"2 tbsp mayonnaise","d":"Spread thin on the outside; gives an extra-crisp, even golden crust with a faint tang.","k":["mayonnaise"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Cook on medium-low heat. High heat burns the bread before the cheese melts.","s":3},{"t":"Butter to the very edge of the bread so it browns evenly all over. Press down lightly with a spatula.","s":0},{"t":"Cover the pan for the last minute of cooking so the steam melts the cheese through. American melts creamily and the shredded cheddar melts faster than slices.","s":3}],"rl":"BFFF"},"3241":{"lv":"EROEERRROO","w":{"1":"Seasons the skin and meat; sauce does not carry all of it.","2":"Slight bite; optional.","3":"Sweet base of the glaze; maple works.","5":"Texture and pop of mustard; extra Dijon works.","6":"Helps the sauce coat and crisps the skin; butter works.","7":"Sharp background aroma in the glaze; skipping is a noticeable loss.","8":"Color and mild sweetness; skip with small loss.","9":"Fresh finish; garnish."},"sub":{"3":{"x":"1/4 cup maple syrup","d":"Less floral and slightly thinner; caramelizes similarly.","k":["maple syrup"]},"5":{"x":"2 tbsp more Dijon mustard","d":"Smoother, no seed texture.","k":["dijon mustard"]},"6":{"x":"2 tbsp melted butter","d":"Richer, browns a bit faster.","k":["butter"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Pat the skin very dry before seasoning, and bake skin side up so it renders and crisps instead of sitting in the sauce.","s":0},{"t":"Set aside the half of the sauce you will brush on later before tossing raw chicken in the rest. Do not reuse sauce that touched raw chicken.","s":2},{"t":"Thighs are safe at 165°F but taste best at 175°F or higher. Brush the glaze only at the end because honey scorches.","s":3}],"rl":"PZZWXXFXCG"},"3243":{"lv":"EEOOROEEERROR","w":{"2":"Background savory note in the coating; skipping is barely noticeable next to the sauce.","3":"Light color on the crust; skip with no real loss.","4":"Seasons the chicken itself, since the sauce does not.","5":"Slight bite; optional.","6":"Shallow-frying fat; canola or another neutral oil works.","8":"Sweet-tangy base of the sauce; honey, vinegar and extra sriracha stand in.","9":"The heat in the sauce; skip for mild or use chili flakes.","10":"Rounds out the sauce sweetness; the sweet chili sauce also sweetens.","11":"Fresh onion crunch to cut the richness; garnish.","12":"Mild base that soaks up the sauce; skip for lettuce cups."},"sub":{"6":{"x":"1/3 cup canola oil or peanut oil","d":"Neutral, high-smoke-point; olive oil may burn.","k":["vegetable oil","canola oil"],"wn":"Peanut oil: avoid for anyone with a peanut allergy; use canola."},"8":{"x":"1/4 cup honey + 1 tbsp rice vinegar + 1 tbsp water + 1 tsp extra sriracha","d":"Sweet, hot and tangy but without garlic notes; add a pinch of garlic powder.","k":["honey","rice vinegar","sriracha"]}},"tips":[{"t":"The cornstarch needs a slightly damp surface. Pat the chicken dry but not bone dry, then press the coating on so it forms a thin crust.","s":0},{"t":"Fry in batches so the oil stays around 350°F; crowding drops the temperature and gives greasy pieces. Check that 1-inch pieces reach 165°F.","s":1},{"t":"Toss the sauce with the hot chicken in a large bowl right before serving. The crust stays crisp for only a few minutes once coated.","s":3}],"rl":"PTXXZZFFWXWGB"},"3571":{"lv":"ERRREEROROR","w":{"1":"Browning fat for the chicken; butter or any oil works.","2":"Sweet base for the rice.","3":"Savory depth; skipping is a small loss.","6":"Color and mild flavor on the chicken.","7":"Subtle herb; skip with no real loss.","8":"Seasons the rice, which absorbs the broth; skipping makes it bland.","9":"Slight bite; optional.","10":"Sweetness and color; skip or swap with other frozen vegetables."},"sub":{"1":{"x":"1 tbsp butter or vegetable oil","d":"Butter browns faster; watch the heat.","k":["butter","vegetable oil"],"wn":"Butter contains dairy"}},"tips":[{"t":"Brown the chicken only on the outside; it finishes cooking in the rice. Check that it has reached 165°F before the 5-minute stand.","s":1},{"t":"Do not lift the lid while the rice cooks. Steam is what cooks it, and lifting adds uneven results.","s":4},{"t":"If the rice is still crunchy when the liquid is gone, add 1/4 cup hot water, cover, and cook another 5 minutes on low.","s":5}],"rl":"PFXXBMXXZZV"},"3573":{"lv":"EREEERRRO","w":{"1":"Sears the fish; butter alone burns, so oil helps.","2":"Sticky sweet glaze; maple syrup works.","5":"Brightens the sweet glaze; lime or vinegar work.","6":"Adds richness and gloss to the glaze.","7":"Seasons the fish itself.","8":"Slight bite; optional."},"sub":{"1":{"x":"1 tbsp vegetable oil","d":"Neutral; same sear.","k":["vegetable oil"]},"2":{"x":"3 tbsp maple syrup","d":"A little woodsy and thinner; reduces about the same.","k":["maple syrup"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Pat the fillets dry and sear in a hot pan without moving them. A dry surface and an undisturbed fish give a golden crust that does not stick.","s":2},{"t":"Add the honey mixture only in the last 2 minutes over medium heat. Honey and garlic scorch quickly, and a splash of water loosens the glaze if it thickens too fast.","s":4},{"t":"Salmon is fully cooked at 145°F; a thin layer of white albumin on the surface means it is starting to dry, so pull it promptly.","s":4}],"rl":"PFWXXAFZZ"},"3574":{"lv":"ERREEEERROERO","w":{"1":"Sweet savory base; skip and the sauce is a bit flat.","2":"Savory depth; skipping is a small loss.","3":"Cooks the pasta and flavors it; chicken broth or water plus bouillon works.","7":"Tang that makes it taste like a cheeseburger.","8":"Sweet and tangy; skip and add a pinch of sugar and vinegar.","9":"Color and mild flavor; skip with small loss.","11":"Seasons the sauce and pasta; needed.","12":"Slight bite; optional."},"sub":{"3":{"x":"2 cups chicken broth","d":"Milder, less beefy; add a splash of Worcestershire if available.","k":["chicken broth"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Cook the beef to 160°F and drain all but a spoonful of fat. Too much fat keeps the sauce from thickening.","s":0},{"t":"One-pot pasta needs frequent stirring from the bottom, since the starch thickens the sauce and can scorch. If it gets too thick, add a splash of milk or broth.","s":3},{"t":"Shred the cheddar from a block and stir it in off the heat. Pre-shredded cheese is coated with anti-caking starch and can turn grainy.","s":4}],"rl":"PXXMMMBXXXFZZ"},"3575":{"lv":"EERRRROROO","w":{"2":"Sweet color and flavor, added late so it stays slightly crisp.","3":"Savory sweetness; skipping is a small loss.","4":"Browning fat; butter or any oil works.","5":"Color and mild flavor for the potatoes.","6":"Faint savory note; skip.","7":"Seasons the potatoes, which are bland.","8":"Slight bite; optional.","9":"Fresh finish; garnish."},"sub":{"4":{"x":"2 tbsp vegetable oil or butter","d":"Butter browns faster; use half butter, half oil to prevent burning.","k":["vegetable oil","butter"],"wn":"Butter contains dairy"}},"tips":[{"t":"Dry the cut potatoes before they go in the pan. Surface water prevents browning.","s":1},{"t":"Do not stir constantly. Letting the potatoes sit between stirs builds the golden crust, and uncovering after the steam finishes evaporates the moisture.","s":3},{"t":"Add the pepper and onion late so they stay slightly firm. Browning the sausage first leaves fond that flavors the potatoes.","s":3}],"rl":"PBVVFXXZZG"},"3576":{"lv":"ERRORRREEEO","w":{"1":"A light coating that helps the pork brown and thickens the sauce slightly.","2":"Seasons the pork; tenderloin is mild.","3":"Slight bite; optional.","4":"Searing fat; any neutral oil works.","5":"Adds richness and nutty flavor in the pan sauce.","6":"Delicate onion flavor for the sauce; yellow onion works.","10":"Subtle herb; skip with small loss."},"sub":{"4":{"x":"1 tbsp vegetable oil","d":"Same sear.","k":["vegetable oil"]},"6":{"x":"2 tbsp finely minced yellow onion","d":"Sharper and less sweet; cook an extra minute.","k":["onion"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Cut off the silvery skin on the tenderloin first and press the slices to an even 1/2 inch. This prevents curling and toughness.","s":0},{"t":"Pork is done at 145°F with a short rest; the slices are thin, so check early so they do not dry out.","s":1},{"t":"Whisk the mustard in after the cream and simmer gently. Boiling hard can split the sauce and make it bitter.","s":4}],"rl":"PTZZFFXMFXX"},"3577":{"lv":"EERRRRROEERROR","w":{"2":"Savory depth in the blackening rub.","3":"Savory, sweet-onion note in the rub.","4":"Herbal note typical of blackening seasoning.","5":"The heat in the crust; reduce for a milder fish.","6":"Seasons the fish itself.","7":"Slight bite; optional.","8":"Searing fat; any neutral oil works.","10":"Sweet, juicy contrast in the salsa.","11":"Sharp bite in the salsa; skip for a milder salsa.","12":"Fresh herb finish; garnish.","13":"Acid for the salsa and fish; lemon juice works."},"sub":{"8":{"x":"2 tbsp vegetable oil","d":"Neutral and higher smoke point.","k":["vegetable oil"]},"13":{"x":"1/4 cup lemon juice","d":"Slightly less floral; works in salsa and over the fish.","k":["lemon"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Pat the fillets dry and press the spice rub on. Blackened means a dark spice crust, not burned fish, so use medium-high heat and do not move them.","s":2},{"t":"Open a window or run the vent. Blackening spices smoke in a hot pan.","s":3},{"t":"Mix the salsa right before serving. Lime softens the avocado and turns the salsa watery if it sits.","s":1}],"rl":"PXXXXXZZFVVXGA"},"3578":{"lv":"ERRREERRRRROE","w":{"1":"Sweet savory base for the sauce.","2":"Sweet, vegetal flavor; skipping thins the depth.","3":"Savory depth; skipping is a small loss.","6":"Balances the tang of the ketchup and mustard.","7":"Savory, tangy depth; skip and it is plainer.","8":"Sharp tang that cuts through the sweet sauce.","9":"Light warmth; skip with small loss.","10":"Seasons the sauce; needed.","11":"Slight bite; optional."},"tips":[{"t":"Drain the fat well after browning. Extra fat makes the sauce greasy and keeps it from clinging.","s":0},{"t":"Simmer until the sauce mounds on a spoon; if it is thin, cook uncovered a few more minutes. A thick filling soaks into the bun less.","s":3},{"t":"Toast the buns with a smear of butter. The toasted surface keeps them from getting soggy under the sloppy filling.","s":4}],"rl":"PXVXXMWXXXZZB"},"52813":{"lv":"EEEERERROROOROOOO","w":{"4":"Browns the crust and adds a hint of sweetness; skipping gives a paler coating.","6":"Color and mild sweetness in the coating.","7":"Part of the savory spice blend; salt-based, so skip and adjust the salt.","8":"Gentle heat and color in the blend; skip with small loss.","9":"Peppery bite is a signature of this style of coating; skipping softens it.","10":"A faint savory-celery note; one of many background spices.","11":"Background herb; barely noticeable alone.","12":"Savory note; garlic adds depth in the coating.","13":"A tiny warm note; background only.","14":"Background herb in the blend.","15":"Background herb in the blend.","16":"Background herb in the blend.","1":"The frying oil for the chicken; without it it cannot fry"},"sub":{"1":{"x":"2 quarts canola oil or peanut oil","d":"Both have high smoke points; peanut oil is closest to classic fried chicken.","k":["canola oil","peanut oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Cut the whole chicken into 8 or more pieces and halve the breasts so they cook evenly with the legs. A whole chicken will not fit the 30-minute time otherwise.","s":4},{"t":"Let the coated pieces rest 10 to 15 minutes so the flour hydrates and sticks. Press the flour in firmly, since loose coating falls off in the oil.","s":3},{"t":"Keep the oil at 325 to 350°F with a thermometer and fry in small batches. The oil temperature drops when pieces go in. Check for 165°F in the breast and 175°F in the thighs.","s":4}],"rl":"PFTTWZCXXZXXXXXXX"},"52818":{"lv":"EERERERRRREO","w":{"2":"Silky richness; half-and-half is lighter.","4":"Seasoning packets vary in salt; taste before adding.","6":"Browning fat for the vegetables and chicken.","7":"Sweet base for the sauce.","8":"Classic fajita flavor and color; skip and swap another vegetable.","9":"Savory depth; skipping is a small loss.","11":"Fresh finish; garnish."},"sub":{"2":{"x":"1/2 cup half-and-half","d":"Lighter and less rich; do not boil hard.","k":["half and half"]},"6":{"x":"2 tbsp vegetable oil","d":"Same function.","k":["vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Seasoning packets are salty. Hold back the added salt until you taste at the end, and make sure the diced chicken reaches 165°F.","s":0},{"t":"500 g of macaroni needs more liquid than 2 cups broth and 1/2 cup cream. Add hot water or extra stock a splash at a time while it simmers until the pasta is tender.","s":2},{"t":"Take the pan off the heat before adding the cheddar. Shred it from a block and stir until just melted so the sauce stays smooth.","s":2}],"rl":"BMFXZPFVVXFG"},"53013":{"lv":"ERERRRRERORRRO","w":{"1":"Cooking fat for the patties; any neutral oil works.","3":"Classic sweet-sharp crunch; skip and it is milder.","4":"Cool crunch that is part of the burger; skip and it is heavier.","5":"Melty layer; skip for a hamburger.","6":"Briny crunch that cuts the sauce; skip and add a splash of pickle brine to the sauce.","7":"Creamy base of the special sauce.","9":"Light bite; the sauce is already seasoned.","10":"Tang in the sauce.","11":"Savory note in the sauce.","12":"Savory note in the sauce.","13":"Color for the sauce; optional.","8":"Acid in the special sauce; the pickles still add tang"},"sub":{"1":{"x":"2 tbsp vegetable oil","d":"Same function.","k":["vegetable oil"]},"8":{"x":"2 tsp apple cider vinegar","d":"Slightly fruitier; lemon juice also works.","k":["apple cider vinegar"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Make the sauce first and chill it at least an hour. The onion salt and garlic powder need time to hydrate and blend.","s":0},{"t":"Press the patties thin and cook them hot and fast, to 160°F. Season only the outside, right as the patties go in.","s":1},{"t":"A real Big Mac has a middle bun slice. Toast all three pieces, since the extra layer keeps the sauce off the bread and adds crunch.","s":2}],"rl":"PFBGGFGFAZXXXC"},"3141":{"lv":"EERREEERRRO","w":{"2":"Sweet, mild onion note in the sauce; onion works.","3":"Savory depth; skipping is a small loss.","6":"Silky richness; half-and-half is lighter.","7":"Salty, nutty depth in the sauce.","8":"A classic pairing with cream and spinach; skip and it tastes plainer.","9":"Seasons the sauce; add at the end because parmesan is salty.","10":"Slight bite; optional."},"sub":{"6":{"x":"1/2 cup half-and-half","d":"Lighter and a touch thinner; simmer a minute longer.","k":["half and half"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Wring the thawed spinach hard in a towel until no more liquid comes out. Leftover water turns the sauce watery.","s":4},{"t":"Add the milk and cream slowly to the flour and butter while whisking. A smooth roux-based sauce does not lump.","s":2},{"t":"Grate the nutmeg fresh if you can, and wait to add salt until the end because the parmesan is salty.","s":3}],"rl":"VFXXTMFXXZZ"},"3142":{"lv":"ERERRERORO","w":{"1":"Rubs the skin for a crisp shell; any oil works.","3":"Tangy creaminess in the filling; Greek yogurt works.","4":"Loosens the filling to a fluffy mash.","6":"Smoky salty crunch.","7":"Fresh color and bite on top.","8":"Seasons the potato, which is bland.","9":"Slight bite; optional."},"sub":{"1":{"x":"1 tbsp vegetable oil or butter","d":"Same skin crisping.","k":["vegetable oil","butter"]},"3":{"x":"1/2 cup plain Greek yogurt","d":"Tangier and less rich; use full fat.","k":["greek yogurt"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Prick the potatoes, rub with oil and salt, and bake directly on the rack for crisp skin that holds up to a second bake.","s":0},{"t":"Scoop out the flesh while warm, leaving about 1/4 inch so the shells keep their shape. Mash with a fork or masher, not a mixer, which makes it gluey.","s":2},{"t":"The second bake should be hot enough that the tops brown. Freeze filled, unbaked halves for up to a month.","s":4}],"rl":"BFFFMFXGZZ"},"3149":{"lv":"EERRRROO","w":{"2":"Balances the vinegar tang; skip and it is sharper.","3":"Tang that cuts the mayo; white vinegar or lemon works.","4":"Sharp background tang.","5":"Seasons the cabbage; needed.","6":"Slight bite; optional.","7":"Classic deli flavor; skipping barely changes it."},"sub":{"3":{"x":"2 tbsp white vinegar or lemon juice","d":"White vinegar is sharper; lemon is fresher.","k":["white vinegar","lemon juice"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Whisk the dressing for a full minute so the sugar dissolves before adding the cabbage. Gritty sugar tastes harsh.","s":0},{"t":"Chill 30 minutes to soften, but dress no more than a couple of hours ahead. Cabbage releases water over time and thins the dressing.","s":2},{"t":"If it turns watery, drain the excess and stir in a spoon of mayo. Taste again after chilling since cold dulls seasoning.","s":3}],"rl":"VFWAXZZX"},"3343":{"lv":"EEREOO","w":{"2":"Silky richness; whole milk is lighter.","4":"Slight bite; optional.","5":"Mild onion garnish."},"sub":{"2":{"x":"3/4 cup whole milk, warmed","d":"Lighter and less rich; potatoes will be slightly less creamy.","k":["whole milk"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Start the potatoes in cold, salted water so they cook evenly from the outside in. Dropping them in boiling water leaves hard centers.","s":0},{"t":"Return the drained potatoes to the hot pot for a minute to steam off water. Wet potatoes make watery mash.","s":2},{"t":"Use a ricer or masher, never a blender or food processor, which turns the starch gluey. Fold in the butter before the cream so it coats the starch.","s":3}],"rl":"BFFZZG"},"3344":{"lv":"EERRRRRRORO","w":{"3":"Tang that brightens the dressing.","4":"Balances the vinegar.","5":"Crunch and freshness.","6":"Sweet crunch and color.","7":"Sharp bite against the creamy dressing.","8":"Color and a mild finish; skip with little change.","9":"Seasons the pasta and dressing; needed.","10":"Slight bite; optional.","2":"Tang for the dressing; the mayo and mustard still season it"},"sub":{"2":{"x":"2 tbsp white vinegar","d":"Sharper and cleaner; cider is fruitier.","k":["white vinegar"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Salt the pasta water well. Overcooking by one minute keeps the macaroni tender after it chills and soaks up dressing.","s":0},{"t":"The pasta absorbs dressing as it chills. Hold back a few tablespoons of mayo or add a splash of milk before serving to bring back creaminess.","s":4},{"t":"Rinse with cold water so the pasta stops cooking and will not clump. Drain it thoroughly so the dressing does not get watery.","s":1}],"rl":"BFAXWVVXGZZ"},"3345":{"lv":"EEEEERRO","w":{"5":"Boosts the corn's natural sweetness; skip for a more savory dish.","6":"Seasons the sauce; needed.","7":"Slight bite; optional.","2":"The cream is the sauce of the creamed corn; without it the corn is just boiled corn"},"sub":{"2":{"x":"1/2 cup half-and-half","d":"Lighter and slightly thinner; simmer a minute longer.","k":["half and half"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Thaw the corn and pat it dry first. Excess water thins the sauce and delays thickening.","s":1},{"t":"Cook the flour for the full minute before adding liquid so it does not taste raw, and add the cream and milk gradually while stirring.","s":2},{"t":"Blending a cup of corn with some sauce builds a natural creamy body without extra cream. If it tightens on standing, loosen it with a splash of milk.","s":4}],"rl":"VFFMTWZZ"},"3346":{"lv":"EEEREEERERO","w":{"3":"Savory depth in the mushroom sauce; skipping is a small loss.","6":"Silky richness; half-and-half works but is thinner.","7":"Adds umami to the mushroom sauce.","9":"Seasons the sauce; needed.","10":"Slight bite; optional."},"sub":{"6":{"x":"3/4 cup half-and-half","d":"Lighter, slightly thinner; simmer a minute longer.","k":["half and half"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Cook the mushrooms in a single layer without salt or stirring too much until they are browned and the liquid has cooked off.","s":1},{"t":"Make the sauce quite thick before adding the beans. The green beans release water in the oven and will thin it.","s":3},{"t":"Save half the fried onions for the last 5 minutes so they stay crisp rather than sinking into the sauce. Blanch and shock the beans so they stay green.","s":5}],"rl":"VFVXTMFXGZZ"},"3347":{"lv":"EEEOOREEEE","w":{"3":"Color and a mild background in the batter.","4":"Faint savory note; skip.","5":"Seasons the batter; onions are mild.","9":"The deep-frying oil for the onion rings; without it they cannot be fried"},"sub":{"9":{"x":"3 cups canola oil or peanut oil","d":"High smoke point and neutral; olive oil is not suitable for deep frying.","k":["canola oil","peanut oil"],"wn":"Peanut oil is a peanut allergen; use canola if anyone is allergic"}},"tips":[{"t":"Hold the oil at 375°F with a thermometer. Too cool and the rings are greasy; too hot and the panko burns before the onion softens.","s":3},{"t":"Shake off excess batter and press the panko firmly on each ring. Fry in small batches so the oil temperature stays up.","s":4},{"t":"Drain on a wire rack, not paper towels, and salt right away. Keep finished rings in a 200°F oven on a rack.","s":6}],"rl":"VSLXXZMTGF"},"3348":{"lv":"ERERRRRRORO","w":{"1":"Adds richness and the classic deli look; skip for a lighter salad.","3":"Tang that brightens the creamy dressing.","5":"Crunch and freshness.","6":"Sharp bite that cuts the mayo.","7":"Sweet pickle tang in the dressing.","8":"Color; skip with no real loss.","9":"Potatoes are bland and need salt; add in stages.","10":"Slight bite; optional.","4":"Tang for the dressing; mustard and mayo still season the salad"},"sub":{"4":{"x":"2 tbsp white vinegar","d":"Sharper and cleaner; cider is fruitier.","k":["white vinegar"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Start the potatoes in cold, salted water so they cook evenly. Stop when a knife meets slight resistance because they keep cooking as they cool.","s":0},{"t":"Toss the warm potatoes with vinegar and salt. Warm starch absorbs the seasoning inside rather than just coating the outside.","s":2},{"t":"Cool the potatoes completely before adding the mayo dressing, and keep the salad below 40°F. Do not leave it out more than 2 hours.","s":4}],"rl":"BPFXAVXXGZZ"},"3349":{"lv":"EERORO","w":{"2":"Seasons the sprouts; needed to bring out sweetness.","3":"Slight bite; optional.","4":"Sweet-tart finish that balances the bitterness; skip and add a squeeze of lemon.","5":"Salty nutty finish; skip with little loss.","1":"The roasting fat for the sprouts; without it they dry out and do not caramelize"},"sub":{"1":{"x":"3 tbsp vegetable oil","d":"Neutral and high heat friendly.","k":["vegetable oil"]},"4":{"x":"1 tbsp balsamic vinegar + 1/2 tsp honey","d":"Slightly thinner; warm it for a minute to thicken.","k":["balsamic vinegar","honey"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Dry the sprouts thoroughly after washing. Water steams them instead of caramelizing the cut sides.","s":1},{"t":"Lay them cut side down on the preheated sheet and leave them alone. The browning only happens against the hot pan.","s":2},{"t":"Add the glaze and cheese after roasting. The sugars in balsamic burn at 425°F.","s":4}],"rl":"VFZZAG"},"3350":{"lv":"EEEEROR","w":{"4":"Seasons the carrots and brings out sweetness.","5":"Fresh finish; garnish.","6":"Brightens the sweet glaze; skip and it tastes sweet.","2":"The honey makes the glaze; without it the carrots are not glazed"},"sub":{"2":{"x":"2 tbsp maple syrup","d":"A little woodsy; reduces the same way.","k":["maple syrup"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Cut the carrots on the bias to a consistent size so they finish together, and use a wide pan so the liquid can reduce quickly.","s":0},{"t":"Cook covered first to steam them nearly tender, then uncover. Once the water is gone the butter and honey become a glaze.","s":1},{"t":"Stir constantly at the end and take it off the heat as soon as it looks glossy. Overcooked sugar will turn sticky and dark.","s":2}],"rl":"VFWMZGA"},"3351":{"lv":"EERRRRRRO","w":{"2":"Sharp, sweet bite; skip with little change.","3":"Earthy flavor and chew.","4":"Rich, filling, and classic; skip for a lighter salad.","5":"Tangy base of the dressing; red wine vinegar works.","6":"Balances the vinegar and bacon.","7":"Emulsifies and adds tang.","8":"Bacon is salty, so only pepper is needed."},"sub":{"5":{"x":"2 tbsp red wine vinegar","d":"A little sharper and less fruity.","k":["red wine vinegar"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Dry the spinach well. Water thins the hot dressing, and a dry leaf wilts evenly.","s":0},{"t":"Pour the dressing over the spinach while it is hot but not boiling so the leaves wilt just slightly.","s":4},{"t":"Skip added salt; the bacon provides plenty. Serve immediately because the hot dressing keeps wilting the leaves.","s":5}],"rl":"VPXVPAWXZ"},"3352":{"lv":"EERRRRREEER","w":{"3":"Sweetens the mash; skip and it is more savory.","4":"Rounds out the flavor.","5":"Warm spice that suits sweet potato.","6":"Brings out sweetness; needed.","10":"Crunchy, nutty contrast; skip for an allergy or swap with oats.","2":"Loosens the mash; butter and other ingredients still bind it"},"sub":{"2":{"x":"1/2 cup half-and-half","d":"Richer and a bit thicker.","k":["half and half"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Drain the sweet potatoes very well and let them steam dry for a minute. Excess water makes a loose mash.","s":1},{"t":"Rub the cold butter into the flour and sugar to coarse crumbs and keep it cold. It bakes into crisp clusters instead of a greasy crust.","s":4},{"t":"Toast the pecans for 5 minutes in the oven first for a deeper flavor, and use a masher rather than a food processor.","s":4}],"rl":"VFMWXXZBWFG"},"3417":{"lv":"ERROERRO","w":{"1":"Coats the potatoes for browning.","2":"Seasons the potatoes.","3":"Slight bite; optional.","5":"Smoky, salty crunch.","6":"Cool, tangy contrast.","7":"Fresh color and bite."},"sub":{"1":{"x":"2 tbsp vegetable oil","d":"Same browning.","k":["vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Place the potatoes cut side down on parchment so the cut face browns against the pan. Preheat the sheet for a stronger crust.","s":1},{"t":"Choose potatoes of similar size so they finish together.","s":2},{"t":"Add the cheddar only for the last few minutes so it melts without separating, and keep the sour cream, bacon and onions cold for the contrast.","s":4}],"rl":"BFZZGGGG"},"3418":{"lv":"EERRRRRO","w":{"2":"Loosens the filling; half-and-half is richer.","3":"Sweetens the mash.","4":"Warm spice.","5":"Seasons the filling; sweet potatoes need salt.","6":"Crunchy topping; skip and the dish is softer.","7":"Glaze that makes the topping sticky; skip with little loss."},"tips":[{"t":"Bake until the potatoes slump and some syrup oozes. Under-baked potatoes mash lumpy.","s":0},{"t":"Scoop out the flesh while warm but leave a sturdy shell so the filling does not collapse.","s":1},{"t":"Toast the pecans first and spoon the topping on right before the second bake so the pecans stay crisp.","s":4}],"rl":"VFMWXZGW"},"3419":{"lv":"EEERRRREERO","w":{"3":"Classic stuffing aromatic that adds crunch and sweetness.","4":"Savory depth.","5":"The signature stuffing flavor; dried rubbed sage works.","6":"Earthy herb.","9":"Seasons the stuffing; needed.","10":"Slight bite; optional."},"sub":{"5":{"x":"1 tsp dried rubbed sage","d":"Slightly more concentrated and less fresh.","k":["dried sage"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Dry the cornbread in the oven first. Stale bread soaks up broth without turning to mush.","s":0},{"t":"Add the broth and egg mixture gradually. The cubes should be moist but not pooled, and sweet cornbread absorbs less than plain.","s":4},{"t":"Bake covered first so it steams through, then uncovered to crisp the top. Because it contains egg, the center should reach 165°F.","s":5}],"rl":"BFXXXXXMTZZ"},"3420":{"lv":"EERREEROEO","w":{"2":"Sweet base for the sauce.","3":"Savory depth.","6":"Silky richness; milk works if you want it lighter.","7":"Subtle herb.","9":"Slight bite; optional."},"sub":{"6":{"x":"1/2 cup additional milk","d":"Lighter, less rich; the flour roux still thickens it.","k":["milk"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Slice the potatoes evenly, about 1/8 inch, ideally with a mandoline. Do not rinse them, since the surface starch thickens the sauce.","s":4},{"t":"Make the sauce slightly thinner than you want. The potatoes absorb liquid as they bake.","s":3},{"t":"Cover with foil for the first 45 minutes so they steam tender, then uncover to brown. Rest 10 minutes so the sauce sets.","s":5}],"rl":"BFXXTMFXZZ"},"3421":{"lv":"EERRRERORO","w":{"2":"Loosens the cream; more cream replaces it.","3":"Savory depth.","4":"Greases the dish and browns the top.","5":"Nutty, melty cheese; Swiss is the closest swap.","6":"Salty depth on top.","7":"Classic cream-and-cheese pairing; skip with small loss.","8":"Potatoes and cream need seasoning.","9":"Slight bite; optional."},"sub":{"2":{"x":"1/2 cup more heavy cream","d":"Richer.","k":["heavy cream"]},"5":{"x":"1 1/2 cups shredded Swiss cheese","d":"Milder and less nutty.","k":["swiss cheese"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Do not rinse the potatoes. The surface starch thickens the cream without any flour.","s":2},{"t":"Warm the cream gently and do not let it boil. Too much heat can break it, and warm cream cuts the baking time.","s":1},{"t":"Rest the gratin 10 minutes before cutting so the cream sets and you get clean slices. It is done when a knife slides through the potatoes with no resistance.","s":6}],"rl":"BMMXFFGXZZ"},"3422":{"lv":"EERROROR","w":{"2":"Added late for sweet roasted garlic flavor.","3":"Seasons the cauliflower.","4":"Slight bite; optional.","5":"Salty, nutty crust.","6":"Fresh finish.","7":"Brightens the rich roasted flavor.","1":"The roasting fat for cauliflower; without it it dries and does not brown"},"sub":{"1":{"x":"3 tbsp vegetable oil","d":"Neutral; olive oil gives a fruitier taste.","k":["vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Dry the florets thoroughly and cut them to similar sizes. Wet or mismatched pieces steam and cook unevenly.","s":1},{"t":"Roast on a preheated sheet and give the florets space. Only contact with the hot metal browns the edges.","s":2},{"t":"Add the garlic and parmesan in the last 5 minutes, since garlic burns and turns bitter in a longer roast.","s":3}],"rl":"VFXZZGGA"},"3423":{"lv":"ERRRORERRRO","w":{"1":"Smoky, salty crunch.","2":"Sharp bite that cuts the creamy dressing.","3":"Sweet chew.","4":"Nutty crunch; skip with little change.","5":"Sharp, savory richness.","7":"Balances the vinegar.","9":"Seasons the dressing and broccoli.","10":"Slight bite; optional.","8":"Tang for the dressing; mayo and sugar still dress the salad"},"sub":{"8":{"x":"2 tbsp white vinegar","d":"Sharper and cleaner than cider.","k":["white vinegar"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Cut the florets small. Chilling softens them slightly, so smaller pieces absorb dressing and are easier to eat.","s":2},{"t":"Add the bacon and sunflower seeds just before serving so they stay crunchy.","s":4},{"t":"Chill at least an hour for the dressing to soak in, and keep the salad below 40°F. Do not leave it out more than 2 hours.","s":3}],"rl":"VGXVGGFWAZZ"},"3424":{"lv":"EEERORRRRORO","w":{"3":"Thickens and enriches the ranch so it clings to the pasta; skip it and the dressing is lighter and runs thinner.","4":"Only thins the dressing; skip it and loosen with a splash later if the salad tightens up in the fridge.","5":"Juicy sweet-acid bursts against the rich dressing; skip for a plainer, heavier salad.","6":"Cool crunch that balances the bacon and ranch; skip and the salad loses its fresh contrast.","7":"Sharp, savory chunks that make it a meal-style salad; skip for a lighter result.","8":"Mild onion bite cuts the richness; chives or a little red onion work, or skip.","9":"Ranch is already seasoned with garlic; this barely registers if skipped.","10":"Bacon, ranch and cheddar are all salty; add a little after tasting the chilled salad.","11":"Ranch already has pepper; this is just a bit of background heat."},"tips":[{"t":"Rinse the pasta cold but drain it very well; clinging water dilutes the ranch and leaves the salad soupy by day two.","s":0},{"t":"Fold in most of the bacon only just before the chill and save the rest for the top; bacon sitting in dressing for hours goes limp.","s":4},{"t":"Rotini keeps absorbing dressing as it sits. Hold back a few tablespoons of dressing or milk and stir it in right before serving to bring back the creaminess.","s":5}],"rl":"BPFFMVVGXXZZ"},"3425":{"lv":"EEERERRRO","w":{"3":"Adds a sharp tang that keeps the cream cheese filling from tasting flat; skip and use a little extra salt.","5":"Smoky crunch and salt that make this a popper-style egg; skip and it reads as a cheesy jalapeno egg.","6":"Sharp cheddar melts into the filling for the popper flavor; skip and the filling is milder and a bit softer.","7":"Seasons the yolk filling, which is mild and rich; use a pinch less if your bacon is very salty.","8":"Color and a faint sweet-smoky note on top; skip with no real change in taste."},"tips":[{"t":"Start the eggs in cold water, bring to a boil, then cover off the heat for 12 minutes. This sets the yolk without the green-gray ring, and eggs a week or so old peel much more cleanly than farm-fresh ones.","s":0},{"t":"Mash the yolks completely smooth with the cream cheese before adding anything chunky; lumps cannot be fixed later. Pressing the yolks through a sieve gives a silky filling.","s":2},{"t":"Pat the egg whites dry, then pipe the filling with a zip bag and snipped corner so it stays put. Leave a few jalapeno seeds in only if you want real heat.","s":4}],"rl":"PFFXXGFZG"},"3426":{"lv":"EEEROOR","w":{"3":"Salty, nutty crust on top that browns well; skip for plainer garlic bread (add a pinch more salt).","4":"Fresh color and a green note; purely a finish and easy to skip.","5":"Fresh garlic does the real work; the powder just rounds out the flavor.","6":"Seasons the butter; butter is usually unsalted here, so keep a pinch if yours is."},"sub":{"3":{"x":"1/4 cup grated pecorino romano","d":"Sharper and saltier than parmesan; cut the added salt to a pinch.","k":["pecorino"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Use butter soft enough to spread but not melted; melted butter soaks into the bread and makes it greasy instead of crisp-topped.","s":1},{"t":"Spread the butter all the way to the crust edges. Exposed edges dry out and scorch while the middle stays soft.","s":2},{"t":"Watch the broil closely: garlic and parmesan go from golden to bitter in under 30 seconds.","s":4}],"rl":"BFXGGXZ"},"3427":{"lv":"EEERRRORO","w":{"4":"Gives a sharp, stretchy cheese flavor; skip and the mash is milder and less rich.","5":"Adds salty, nutty depth; skip and add a pinch more salt.","6":"Fresh oniony finish and color; purely a garnish.","7":"Cauliflower is bland and needs seasoning; add gradually and taste at the end.","8":"Background heat only; skip it freely.","3":"Adds tang to the mash; other ingredients still season it"},"sub":{"3":{"x":"1/4 cup plain Greek yogurt","d":"Tangier and a bit less rich; add it off the heat so it does not curdle.","k":["greek yogurt"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Steam the cauliflower with the garlic rather than boiling; boiling waterlogs the florets, while steaming mellows the garlic into a sweet paste.","s":0},{"t":"Moisture is the enemy: let the steam escape, then blot. If the puree is still loose, stir it 2 to 3 minutes in a hot dry skillet to cook off the water.","s":1},{"t":"Shred the cheddar from a block; pre-shredded cheese has anti-caking starch that makes the mash grainy rather than silky.","s":3}],"rl":"VFXFFFGZZ"},"3428":{"lv":"ERRERRRRERO","w":{"1":"Sweet, savory base for the beef; skip and the filling tastes flatter and meatier.","2":"Adds savory depth to the filling; skip and the sauce still works.","4":"Loosens the condensed soup into a sauce; skip and the filling will be thick and pasty.","5":"Adds umami that makes the beef taste richer; skip and add a pinch more salt.","6":"Classic vegetable and bulk in the casserole; skip for a pure beef and cheese filling.","7":"Melts into the sauce for creaminess; skip and the casserole is less rich.","9":"Seasons the beef; condensed soup is salty so go easy.","10":"Background pepper; skip freely.","3":"The sauce binding the casserole; without it the tater tots and beef are dry"},"sub":{"3":{"x":"3 tbsp butter + 3 tbsp flour + 1 cup milk, cooked to a thick white sauce (skip the 1/2 cup milk in the recipe)","d":"Fresher and less salty; add 1/4 tsp salt and some sauteed mushrooms or garlic powder.","k":["butter","flour","milk"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Let the beef sit undisturbed for the first couple of minutes so it sears instead of steams, and cook it to 160F inside. Drain the fat so the sauce isn't greasy.","s":1},{"t":"Bake the tots straight from frozen in tight rows; thawed tots turn mushy. If they brown before the filling bubbles, tent loosely with foil.","s":4},{"t":"Rest the casserole 10 minutes out of the oven so the sauce sets and the portions cut cleanly.","s":5}],"rl":"PXXTMXVFBZZ"},"3429":{"lv":"EREERORO","w":{"4":"Fresh grassy note in the butter; skip or swap any soft herb.","5":"Mild onion flavor in the butter; skip with little change.","6":"Corn needs salt to taste sweet; half goes on the corn and half in the butter.","7":"Just a touch of heat in the butter; skip freely.","1":"Keeps the corn from sticking; the garlic butter also helps"},"sub":{"1":{"x":"1 tbsp vegetable oil or melted butter","d":"Same function: oil keeps the corn from sticking and helps char; butter browns faster.","k":["vegetable oil","butter"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Pat the corn dry and oil it lightly so the char is even; corn sugars scorch fast on a 450F grill, so turn every few minutes and pull the ears when the kernels are bright and plump with a few dark spots.","s":3},{"t":"Brush the garlic butter on after grilling, not before: raw garlic and herbs burn on the grates and turn bitter.","s":4},{"t":"Mash the butter with the salt first so it dissolves; raw garlic mellows when the hot corn melts the butter over it.","s":1}],"rl":"VFFXGGZZ"},"3430":{"lv":"EEEEEREOROER","w":{"5":"Salty, nutty depth in the cheese sauce and the crust; skip and the bites taste milder.","7":"Cheese and butter already carry flavor; this is minor background.","8":"Seasons the pasta and sauce; reduce if your cheeses are very salty.","9":"Faint background heat only.","10":"The crumb coating of the mac and cheese bites; without it they cannot be breaded and fried or baked","11":"Browns the crumbs; the bites still crisp a little"},"sub":{"10":{"x":"3/4 cup fine dry breadcrumbs","d":"Denser and less airy than panko; crushed buttery crackers also work and are richer.","k":["breadcrumbs"]},"11":{"x":"1 tbsp melted butter","d":"Browns the crumbs a bit faster and tastes richer.","k":["butter"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Cook the macaroni a minute under the package time; it keeps cooking in the sauce and in the oven, and fully cooked pasta turns mushy in a mini muffin cup.","s":1},{"t":"Let the macaroni and sauce cool 5 minutes before the egg goes in, otherwise the egg scrambles. The egg is the binder that lets the bites hold their shape.","s":4},{"t":"Grease every cup generously, press the mixture in firmly, and rest the baked bites 5 minutes so the cheese sets before you run a knife around each.","s":6}],"rl":"BFTMFXTXZZGF"},"3431":{"lv":"EEEROO","w":{"3":"Orange zest gives perfume the juice alone lacks; skip and the sauce tastes simply sweet-tart.","4":"Faint warm spice; skip it with almost no loss.","5":"Sharpens sweetness and tartness; skipping it makes little difference.","2":"The liquid in which the cranberries cook; without it the sauce scorches"},"sub":{"2":{"x":"3/4 cup apple juice (or 3/4 cup water + 1 tbsp lemon juice)","d":"Less orange flavor and a touch less acid; fine since the berries are tart.","k":["apple juice","lemon juice"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Cook only until most berries have popped, about 10 minutes. Longer cooking breaks down the pectin and turns the sauce bitter and gluey.","s":2},{"t":"The sauce looks too loose when hot but sets as it cools, so judge thickness only after chilling. If it is too thick, stir in a spoonful of juice or water.","s":4},{"t":"Frozen berries can go straight into the pot without thawing; just add a couple of minutes to the simmer.","s":0}],"rl":"VWMXXZ"},"3432":{"lv":"EERRRRRRROR","w":{"2":"Sweet crunch and color; skip or use any crisp vegetable like celery.","3":"Sharp bite that livens the beans; soak briefly in cold water if it is very strong.","4":"Creamy contrast that rounds out the acid; skip and the salad is leaner but still good.","5":"Fresh herbal lift that goes with lime and cumin; skip for a plainer salad.","8":"Warm earthy note; skip and the salad tastes brighter but less Tex-Mex.","9":"Mild heat and color; negligible if omitted.","10":"Beans and corn need salt to taste of anything; add to taste.","6":"Acid for the salad; other seasoning still flavors it","7":"Base of the dressing; the lime still dresses the salad"},"sub":{"6":{"x":"3 tbsp lemon juice","d":"Slightly sweeter, less floral than lime but still bright.","k":["lemon juice"]},"7":{"x":"3 tbsp vegetable oil","d":"Neutral instead of fruity; the dressing tastes cleaner.","k":["vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"For smoky depth, char the thawed and well-dried corn in a hot dry skillet until blistered before adding it.","s":1},{"t":"Rinse the canned beans until the water runs clear so the dressing stays bright instead of turning murky and starchy.","s":1},{"t":"Add the avocado last and fold gently; lime slows browning but stirring mashes it, so for make-ahead keep it separate.","s":3}],"rl":"PVVXVXAFXXZ"},"3433":{"lv":"ERERREEROOOR","w":{"1":"Seasons the beans, which are otherwise bland; skip and add cumin, chili powder, and salt instead.","3":"Helps keep the avocado from browning and adds brightness; skip if you serve right away.","4":"Seasons the avocado layer; guacamole needs it to taste like anything.","7":"Melty, salty topping that ties the layers together; skip for a lighter dip.","8":"Fresh color and a little juice; skip with no real loss.","9":"Salty accent; entirely optional.","10":"Oniony garnish for color; skip with no real loss.","11":"The dipper; any sturdy chip or cut vegetable works, but it is how the dip gets eaten.","5":"A main layer of the dip; without it the dip loses a creamy layer"},"sub":{"5":{"x":"1 cup plain Greek yogurt","d":"Tangier and a bit firmer; it is a cold layer so there is no curdling risk.","k":["greek yogurt"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Warm the refried beans 30 seconds in the microwave first; they spread smoothly without tearing, and a firm bean layer supports everything on top.","s":0},{"t":"Drain the salsa in a sieve and blot it; free liquid soaks down through the layers and makes the dip soggy.","s":3},{"t":"If not serving right away, press plastic wrap directly onto the guacamole layer before adding the rest; air contact browns avocado fastest.","s":1}],"rl":"BXVAZFXGGGGG"},"3434":{"lv":"EREERRORO","w":{"1":"Sharp, crunchy bite against the creamy dressing; soak in cold water if you want it milder.","4":"Adds body and richness to the dressing; skip and use more sour cream (it will be tangier).","6":"Rounds the vinegar's sharpness; leave it out with only a slightly sharper dressing.","7":"Dill is the signature flavor of this salad; skipping it makes a plain creamy cucumber salad.","8":"Background heat only; skip freely.","5":"Tang for the dressing; the sour cream and dill still season it"},"sub":{"5":{"x":"2 tbsp apple cider vinegar or 2 tbsp lemon juice","d":"Fruitier or brighter than white vinegar; both give the needed tang.","k":["apple cider vinegar","lemon juice"]},"7":{"x":"2 tsp dried dill","d":"Milder and less fresh; add it to the dressing early so it softens.","k":["dried dill"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Salting and draining pulls out cucumber water. Skip it and the dressing thins to soup within minutes, so pat or gently squeeze the slices dry in a towel.","s":0},{"t":"Slice the cucumbers about 1/8 inch thick (a mandoline helps) and scoop out big watery seeds from thick-skinned ones.","s":0},{"t":"Dress and chill no more than a couple of hours ahead; cucumbers keep releasing water, so stir before serving and loosen with a spoonful of sour cream if it tightens.","s":4}],"rl":"VXZFFAWXZ"},"3435":{"lv":"EREEROR","w":{"4":"Roasting dulls flavor, so salt is needed; the cheese adds some, so go light and taste.","5":"Background heat only; skip freely.","6":"Brightens the roasted beans and cuts the cheese; vinegar or zest work, skip and the dish is richer and flatter.","1":"Roasting fat; the beans still roast with the parmesan and garlic"},"sub":{"1":{"x":"2 tbsp vegetable oil or melted butter","d":"Same roasting function; butter browns faster so watch the last few minutes.","k":["vegetable oil","butter"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Preheating the sheet sears the undersides of the beans on contact, so they blister instead of steaming in their own moisture. Dry the beans well first.","s":0},{"t":"Add the garlic only for the last 4 to 5 minutes; minced garlic at 425F for the full roast burns and turns bitter.","s":3},{"t":"Do not crowd the pan; beans piled on top of each other steam and go olive-drab. Use two sheets if needed.","s":2}],"rl":"VFXGZZA"},"52779":{"lv":"EEEREEERRROOE","w":{"3":"Seasons the dough; a pinch is enough and it keeps the crust from tasting flat.","7":"Adds salty, nutty depth to the custard; skip and increase the salt in the filling slightly.","8":"The tart topping gives sweet-acid freshness against the rich custard; skip for a plain savory tart.","9":"Makes the tomato topping tangy; skip and the tomatoes taste raw and watery.","10":"Balances the vinegar in the tomato marinade; a pinch of sugar works instead.","11":"Fresh finish; decorative and easy to skip."},"sub":{"7":{"x":"100 g finely grated pecorino or aged cheddar","d":"Pecorino is saltier and sharper; cheddar is milder and melts softer.","k":["pecorino","cheddar"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Chill the pressed crust well and prick it with a fork; a cold crust holds its shape and keeps the bottom from puffing up in the oven.","s":1},{"t":"The custard is done when the edges are set and the center barely wobbles; foil halfway through stops the top from browning before the eggs set.","s":2}],"rl":"SFSZBMSXVAWGF"},"2":{"lv":"ERRREEEEEEROR","w":{"1":"Sweet savory base that makes the soup taste cooked-down; skip and use onion powder in a pinch.","2":"Sweetness and color, plus body; skip for a more broccoli-forward soup.","3":"Adds savory depth beneath the cheese; skip with a slightly flatter flavor.","10":"Salty, nutty edge that rounds out the cheddar; skip and salt a bit more.","11":"Color and mild warmth; skip with little change.","12":"Cheese and broth are salty; season at the end after the cheese melts.","6":"The cream is the base that makes the soup creamy; without it the cheese soup is thin"},"sub":{"6":{"x":"2 cups half-and-half","d":"Slightly lighter but still creamy; do not boil after adding cheese.","k":["half-and-half"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Whisk the broth in gradually, a splash at a time, before the pot returns to a simmer. This keeps the roux smooth and cooks out the raw flour taste.","s":1},{"t":"Keep the broccoli florets small and pull the pot as soon as they are tender; overcooked broccoli turns olive-colored and sulfurous. Add half later for a greener bowl.","s":2},{"t":"Take the pot off the boil before adding cheese in handfuls, and shred your own cheddar. Boiling or starch-coated pre-shredded cheese turns the soup grainy; an immersion blender rescues a broken soup. Make sure the rotisserie chicken heats through to 165F.","s":3}],"rl":"FXVXTMFVPFXXZ"},"3":{"lv":"EERREEEREOREOR","w":{"2":"Sweet base for the broth; skip and the soup is blander and more potato-forward.","3":"Savory depth that supports the bacon; skip with a modest loss.","9":"Smoky note that echoes the bacon; skip and the soup is milder.","10":"Tang and creaminess like the topping on a loaded potato; skip and add a little extra cream.","12":"Fresh oniony topping; skip and the bowl lacks a bright finish.","13":"Bacon and cheddar bring salt; season at the end after tasting.","7":"Enriches the soup; the milk, potato and cheese still make it creamy"},"sub":{"7":{"x":"1 cup half-and-half","d":"Slightly lighter but still creamy; keep the soup below a boil once added.","k":["half-and-half"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Render the bacon over medium-low heat so the fat clears and the pieces crisp evenly; the tablespoon of fat you keep seasons the whole soup.","s":0},{"t":"Yellow potatoes keep their shape and give a creamy texture; russets break down and thicken more. Mash only half so the soup stays chunky.","s":3},{"t":"Stir the sour cream in off the heat so it doesn't curdle, and add salt last because the bacon and cheddar both carry plenty. If the soup tightens in the fridge, thin it with milk.","s":3}],"rl":"XFXXTMMFBXFFGZ"},"5":{"lv":"ERRRRRRERREOEER","w":{"1":"Sweetness and body; skip and the soup is a bit more acidic.","2":"Adds sweetness once roasted; skip with a sharper, more tomato-forward soup.","3":"Roasted garlic is sweet and mellow and gives depth; skip and add 2 raw cloves to the roast pan.","5":"Adds body and a guaranteed rich tomato flavor, especially if fresh tomatoes are pale; skip if tomatoes are very ripe.","6":"Concentrates tomato flavor and depth; skip and use an extra spoonful of crushed tomatoes.","8":"Rounds out the acid and smooths the texture; use milk or skip for a lighter soup.","11":"Faint herbal background; skip and the fresh basil covers it.","4":"Helps roast the tomatoes; they still roast with less","9":"Adds creamy herb flavor; other cream still makes it rich","14":"Toasts the grilled cheese; the soup is the main dish"},"sub":{"4":{"x":"3 tbsp vegetable oil","d":"Fine for roasting; you lose some fruity flavor.","k":["vegetable oil"]},"9":{"x":"5 oz cream cheese + 1 small minced garlic clove + 1/2 tsp dried parsley or thyme","d":"Same creamy tang, plainer herbs; blend until smooth.","k":["cream cheese","garlic","dried herbs"]},"14":{"x":"3 tbsp mayonnaise, spread on the outside of the bread","d":"Browns evenly and tastes slightly tangy; many cooks prefer it.","k":["mayonnaise"],"wn":"Contains egg"}},"tips":[{"t":"Roast the tomatoes cut side up until the edges blacken in spots; that char gives the sweet-smoky depth canned tomatoes alone cannot.","s":0},{"t":"Blend hot soup in batches, filling the blender no more than half and leaving the lid vented under a towel; trapped steam can blow the lid off.","s":2},{"t":"Cook grilled cheese over medium-low heat with the pan covered; the trapped heat melts the cheese by the time the bread is golden instead of burning.","s":3}],"rl":"VVXXFVXMFFXXBFF"},"25":{"lv":"EREROROEEEERREO","w":{"1":"Sweet savory base for the broth; skip and use a little onion powder.","3":"Adds savory depth to the beef and tomato; skip with a slightly flatter soup.","4":"Garlic is already in fresh form; the powder only reinforces it.","5":"Italian-style herbal note for the tomato base; skip and use a little extra basil.","6":"Second herb that echoes the oregano; skip with almost no change.","11":"Adds umami that deepens the beef; skip and add a pinch more salt.","12":"Balances the acid in the canned tomatoes; skip and the soup tastes sharper.","14":"Fresh green finish only.","8":"The tomato base of the soup; without it the soup is a plain broth","10":"The liquid of the soup; without it it is a thick stew"},"sub":{"8":{"x":"1 can plain diced tomatoes + 1/4 tsp red pepper flakes","d":"Less heat and chile flavor; the flakes put some back.","k":["diced tomatoes","red pepper flakes"]},"10":{"x":"2 cups more beef broth (5 cups beef broth total)","d":"Beefier and slightly darker; use low-sodium broth if it is salty.","k":["beef broth"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Brown the beef until it is fully cooked to 160F and drain the fat, then give the peppers a few minutes to soften in the pot; peppers added late stay grassy and firm.","s":0},{"t":"Taste after the tomatoes go in: brown sugar and Worcestershire balance the canned-tomato acidity, and a pinch more sugar fixes a sharp pot.","s":1},{"t":"Rice keeps absorbing liquid as the soup sits, so leftovers turn stew-like; thin with a splash of broth when reheating.","s":2}],"rl":"PXVXXXXAAMMXWBG"},"26":{"lv":"EOERRRROREER","w":{"3":"Sweetness and body for the broth; skip and the soup is sharper and earthier.","4":"Gives a savory vegetable base note; skip with a minor loss.","5":"Garlic supports the sausage and tomato; skip and the soup is less rounded.","6":"Deepens the tomato and adds umami; skip and the soup is thinner in flavor.","7":"Sausage already has herbs; this adds only a little extra.","8":"Adds body and acidity that balance the lentils; skip and the broth is sweeter and plainer.","11":"Gives color and a pleasant bitterness against the sausage; spinach or chard work.","1":"Only starts the browning; the sausage releases most fat"},"sub":{"1":{"x":"1 tbsp vegetable oil or butter","d":"Only there to start the browning; sausage releases most of the fat itself.","k":["vegetable oil","butter"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Brown the sausage until it leaves a dark crust on the pot bottom, cooking it to 160F, then use a splash of broth to loosen those bits before the vegetables go in.","s":0},{"t":"Use brown or green lentils; they hold their shape through the 30-minute simmer, while red lentils dissolve. Hold off on salt until they are tender because the sausage and broth already carry plenty.","s":2},{"t":"Strip the kale from its tough ribs and add it at the end, then finish with a splash of red wine vinegar to wake up the rich, earthy broth.","s":3}],"rl":"PFXVVXXXABMV"},"46":{"lv":"ERERRRERERO","w":{"3":"Sweetness and color; skip for a plainer broth.","4":"Gives the classic chicken-soup aroma; skip and add a little extra thyme.","5":"Adds savory depth; skip with a modest change.","7":"Earthy herbal note typical of chicken soup; skip and the broth is blander.","9":"Brightens the whole pot; skip and the broth tastes heavier.","10":"Fresh finish; purely a garnish.","1":"Sweats the vegetables; the soup is mostly broth and still works"},"sub":{"1":{"x":"2 tbsp olive oil","d":"Slightly less rich; the soup is mostly broth so little difference.","k":["olive oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Hold the broth at a bare simmer when cooking the chicken; hard boiling toughens breast meat. It is done at 165F, so shred it once it reaches that.","s":1},{"t":"Noodles swell and drink broth as the soup sits. For leftovers, cook noodles separately and add them to each bowl.","s":2},{"t":"Add the lemon off the heat; cooked lemon juice turns bitter, but at the end it lifts the broth.","s":3}],"rl":"PFXVVXMXBAG"},"49":{"lv":"EERRRREEERR","w":{"2":"Sweet savory base for the creamy broth; skip and the soup tastes flatter.","3":"Sweetness and color; skip with a minor change.","4":"Gives a vegetable backbone; skip and use extra onion.","5":"Earthy, meaty flavor that pairs with wild rice; skip for a plainer soup.","10":"Herbal note that goes with chicken and mushrooms; skip and the soup tastes plainer.","9":"Enriches the soup; roux or milk still thicken it"},"sub":{"9":{"x":"1 cup half-and-half","d":"Less rich but still creamy; stir in at the end and do not boil.","k":["half-and-half"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Cook the mushrooms until their liquid evaporates and they brown before adding flour; wet mushrooms water down the soup and miss out on flavor.","s":0},{"t":"Rinse the wild rice first. It needs about 40 minutes and is done when the grains split open; the chicken must reach 165F before shredding.","s":1},{"t":"Stir in the cream at the very end and just warm it; the rice keeps absorbing liquid, so thin leftovers with broth.","s":2}],"rl":"PFXVVVTMBFX"},"50":{"lv":"ERRRREROR","w":{"1":"Roasts sweet and gives the soup a savory base; skip and it is a flatter, sweeter squash puree.","2":"Adds sweetness, color and body; skip with a modest loss.","4":"Roasted garlic turns sweet and mellow; skip for a simpler squash flavor.","6":"Silky richness; skip and the soup is lighter and more squash-forward, so thin with a little broth if it is too thick.","7":"Faint warm spice that lifts the squash; use a light hand so it does not taste like pie.","8":"Roasted squash needs salt to taste sweet rather than flat; season after blending.","3":"Helps roast the squash; the soup is blended anyway"},"sub":{"3":{"x":"3 tbsp melted butter or vegetable oil","d":"Butter browns the edges a bit more and adds richness.","k":["butter","vegetable oil"]},"6":{"x":"1/2 cup coconut milk","d":"Slightly sweet and tropical; still silky, and you can skip the dairy.","k":["coconut milk"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Spread the squash in a single layer with space between pieces; a crowded pan steams and gives a pale, watery soup instead of caramelized sweetness.","s":0},{"t":"Tuck the garlic cloves under the squash or leave them in their skins; at 425F exposed garlic can scorch and turn bitter.","s":0},{"t":"Add the cinnamon a pinch at a time. Butternut is already sweet, so finish with a squeeze of lemon if the soup tastes flat rather than adding more spice.","s":2}],"rl":"VXVFXMFXZ"},"51":{"lv":"ERERRRREERR","w":{"3":"Sweetness and body in the broth; skip and the soup is meatier and thinner.","4":"Savory vegetable backbone; skip or add an extra carrot.","5":"Adds depth to the beef broth; skip with a modest loss.","6":"Adds umami and a slight sweetness to the broth; skip and it tastes thinner.","9":"Earthy herb that suits beef; skip and use black pepper.","10":"Season at the end; barley and broth taste dull without it.","1":"Browns the beef; the beef renders fat"},"sub":{"1":{"x":"1 tbsp vegetable oil","d":"Same function for browning; you lose a little fruitiness.","k":["vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Pat the beef dry and brown it in two batches. Crowding steams the meat gray, and the brown crust left on the pot is what gives the broth its flavor.","s":0},{"t":"Stir the tomato paste into the vegetables for a minute before adding broth so it loses its raw edge and turns sweet.","s":1},{"t":"There is no salt listed, so season at the end. Barley keeps swelling and absorbs broth, so leftovers turn stew-like; add broth when reheating.","s":2}],"rl":"PFXVVXXMBXZ"},"52":{"lv":"RERRERERRO","w":{"2":"Sweetness and color; skip for a more savory bean soup.","3":"Gives a savory backbone; skip and the soup tastes flatter.","5":"Adds acidity and sweetness that brighten the broth; skip with a plainer soup.","7":"Adds color, bitterness and nutrients; spinach added at the end works.","8":"Herbal background; skip and use a pinch of dried oregano or thyme.","9":"Salty umami topping; skip with a milder soup.","0":"Softens the vegetables; they still cook in the broth"},"sub":{"0":{"x":"2 tbsp vegetable oil","d":"Fine for softening vegetables; finishing olive oil would add flavor, though.","k":["vegetable oil"]},"7":{"x":"4 cups fresh spinach, stirred in the last 2 minutes","d":"Softer and milder than kale and wilts almost instantly.","k":["spinach"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Mash about a cup of the beans against the pot to thicken the broth; the starch gives creamy body without any dairy.","s":2},{"t":"If you have a parmesan rind, simmer it with the broth and fish it out before serving; it adds savory depth the grated cheese on top cannot.","s":1},{"t":"Strip the kale from its tough center rib and add it at the end so it stays green, and finish with a drizzle of olive oil and a squeeze of lemon.","s":2}],"rl":"FXVXPAMVXG"},"56":{"lv":"EEREEEERO","w":{"2":"Aromatic note and a bit of crunch before it softens; skip with a minor loss.","8":"Fresh oniony garnish; purely a finish.","7":"Enriches the chowder; the milk and potato starch still thicken it"},"sub":{"7":{"x":"1 cup half-and-half","d":"Lighter but still creamy; stir in off the boil so it doesn't separate.","k":["half-and-half"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Soften the onion and celery in a spoonful of the bacon fat, not all of it. Too much leaves an oily film on the chowder.","s":1},{"t":"Blend only a third of the soup with an immersion blender in a few short pulses; the starch thickens it while the rest stays chunky. Blend it all and it turns baby-food smooth.","s":3},{"t":"For sweeter, smokier corn, char the frozen corn in a dry hot skillet before it goes in. Add the cream after blending and keep the soup below a boil.","s":2}],"rl":"XXXTMBVFG"},"3246":{"lv":"ERREEEERRRERO","w":{"1":"Sweet base that softens the beefy, tomato-heavy broth; skip and the soup is a bit flatter.","2":"Adds depth; skip with a modest loss.","7":"Umami that deepens the beef; skip and add a little extra salt.","8":"Gives a mild sweet color and warmth; skip with a small difference.","9":"Balances the acid from the canned tomatoes; skip and taste, adding a pinch if it is sharp.","11":"Season at the end; canned tomatoes and broth vary a lot in salt.","12":"Background heat only; skip freely."},"tips":[{"t":"Cook the beef to 160F and drain the fat before the cabbage goes in. Leftover fat floats and makes the tomato broth taste greasy.","s":0},{"t":"Cabbage needs the full 30 minutes to turn silky; if it is still crunchy the soup tastes raw and vegetal. A teaspoon of vinegar at the end brightens it.","s":3},{"t":"Stir the cooked rice in only for serving. If it sits in the pot overnight it swells and turns the soup to porridge, so keep rice and soup separate for leftovers.","s":4}],"rl":"PXXVVMMXXWBZZ"},"3247":{"lv":"ERRREERRERRERRROO","w":{"1":"Sweet base that rounds out the spice; skip and use 1 tsp onion powder.","2":"Sweet, vegetal note; skip with only a modest change.","3":"Backbone aroma; skip and use 1 tsp garlic powder.","6":"Color and mild sweetness; skip with a small loss.","7":"Earthy note that matches the cumin; skip with little change.","9":"Gives a smooth tomato body; skip and the chili is chunkier and less saucy.","10":"Concentrated tomato depth; skip and simmer longer.","12":"Second bean for texture; skip and use more kidney beans.","14":"Beans and tomatoes soak up salt; add gradually at the end.","15":"Cool, tangy topping; purely a serving extra.","16":"Melty topping; purely a serving extra.","13":"Extra liquid in the chili; tomatoes and other liquid still carry it"},"sub":{"13":{"x":"1 cup chicken broth or 1 cup water + 1/2 tsp extra salt","d":"Lighter and slightly less meaty; water needs the extra salt to compensate.","k":["chicken broth","water"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Stir the chili powder, cumin, paprika and oregano into the hot fat for a full minute before adding any liquid. This releases their fat-soluble flavors, but keep it moving so they don't scorch and turn bitter.","s":1},{"t":"Simmer partly covered so the chili reduces to a thick, spoon-coating texture, and salt only at the end because it concentrates as it cooks. Beef should be cooked to 160F.","s":3},{"t":"If the chili is too thin, stir in a tablespoon of masa harina or mash a few beans against the pot; finish with a splash of cider vinegar to wake up the flavors.","s":4}],"rl":"PXVXXXXXAMXBBMZGG"},"3250":{"lv":"EERRRERERRRORO","w":{"2":"Sweetness and color; skip with a more beef-and-potato soup.","3":"Savory vegetable backbone; skip or add extra carrot.","4":"Adds depth; skip with a modest loss.","6":"Acid and sweetness that make the broth taste fuller; skip and use extra tomato paste.","8":"Adds umami to the broth; skip and use a little more Worcestershire.","9":"Adds savory complexity that makes the beef taste richer; skip with a small loss.","10":"Herbal background; skip and use a pinch of oregano.","11":"Adds sweetness and color at the end; skip with little loss.","12":"Season at the end because broth and Worcestershire carry salt.","13":"Background heat only; skip freely."},"tips":[{"t":"Brown the beef to 160F and drain the fat before adding the vegetables; a greasy hamburger soup is the usual failure.","s":0},{"t":"Dice the potatoes to a uniform half inch so they finish in the 20-minute simmer. Larger chunks stay hard in the middle while the beef broth is ready.","s":2},{"t":"Add the frozen peas at the very end so they stay bright green and sweet; simmered longer they turn gray and mushy.","s":4}],"rl":"PXVVXBAMXXXVZZ"},"3251":{"lv":"ERRRREERRERRROR","w":{"1":"Sweet savory base for the creamy broth; skip and the soup tastes flatter.","2":"Sweetness and color; skip for a plainer soup.","3":"Classic pot pie aromatic; skip with a small loss.","4":"Adds depth; skip with a modest loss.","8":"Adds body and heartiness, the filling of a pot pie; skip for a lighter soup.","10":"Sweet pops and color; skip with a small change.","11":"Herbal note that makes it taste like pot pie; skip and the soup is plainer.","12":"Season at the end; broth and chicken vary in saltiness.","13":"Background heat only; skip freely.","14":"Gives the pie-crust element; skip and the soup is just a chicken and vegetable chowder.","7":"Makes the soup creamy; the broth and roux still make a soup"},"sub":{"7":{"x":"1 cup half-and-half","d":"Richer and silkier; keep the soup below a hard boil once it goes in.","k":["half-and-half"]},"14":{"x":"1 cup crushed buttery crackers or croutons","d":"Crisper and saltier than a biscuit; add at the table so they stay crunchy.","k":["crackers","croutons"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Cook the flour with the vegetables a full minute before adding liquid; skipping this leaves a raw, pasty taste.","s":1},{"t":"Pour the broth in first, then the milk, whisking constantly; this gives a smooth base. Do not boil hard after the milk goes in.","s":2},{"t":"Bake and crumble the biscuits separately and add them at the table; left in the pot they dissolve into dumplings. Reheat rotisserie chicken until it reaches 165F.","s":4}],"rl":"FXVVXTMMBPVXZZG"},"3252":{"lv":"ERREEEEEERORROO","w":{"1":"Sweet base that makes it taste like a burger; skip and add a pinch of onion powder.","2":"Adds depth; skip with a modest loss.","9":"Umami that makes the beef taste richer; skip and add a bit more salt.","11":"Gives the tang of a burger condiment; skip and the soup is milder.","12":"Season at the end because cheese, broth and pickles add salt.","10":"Sweet tang of burger sauce; skip with almost no loss.","13":"Background heat only; skip freely.","14":"Burger-style sour crunch on top; skip and add a little vinegar if the soup is heavy."},"tips":[{"t":"Once the milk and cheese are in, heat the soup gently and never boil it; boiling curdles the milk and turns the cheese grainy.","s":3},{"t":"Shred the cheddar yourself from a block, and add it a handful at a time so each addition melts before the next.","s":4},{"t":"Cook the beef to 160F and drain the fat before making the roux. Any leftover fat in the butter-flour paste makes the soup greasy.","s":0}],"rl":"PXXFTMBMFXXXZZG"},"3590":{"lv":"ERRRERORRO","w":{"2":"Gives savory base for the sweet pumpkin; skip and the soup tastes flatter.","3":"Adds depth; skip with a modest loss.","5":"Classic pumpkin spice; add sparingly because it is strong.","6":"Warm spice note; skip for a more savory soup.","7":"Silky richness; skip and thin with broth for a lighter soup.","8":"Season after blending; pumpkin needs salt to taste of anything.","9":"Background heat only; skip freely.","1":"Sweats the onion; the soup is blended and still works"},"sub":{"1":{"x":"2 tbsp olive oil","d":"Less rich but fine for sweating onion; the soup is blended anyway.","k":["olive oil"]},"7":{"x":"1/2 cup whole milk or coconut milk","d":"Thinner but still smooth; warm gently so it doesn't curdle.","k":["whole milk","coconut milk"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Use a sugar or pie pumpkin, kabocha or butternut, not a carving pumpkin, which is watery and stringy. In a pinch, 2 cans (15 oz) of plain pumpkin puree with 3 cups broth works; skip the long simmer.","s":2},{"t":"Simmer until the pumpkin falls apart under a spoon; undercooked pieces leave a fibrous, grainy texture even after blending.","s":3},{"t":"Add the cream off the heat and warm gently; a hard boil can make it separate. Taste before adding more nutmeg and cinnamon.","s":5}],"rl":"VFXXMXXFZZ"},"3591":{"lv":"RERRREEROEORRO","w":{"2":"Sweetness and color; skip for a plainer broth.","3":"Gives the classic chicken soup aroma; skip with a small loss.","4":"Adds depth; skip with a modest loss.","7":"Gives the broth an herbal, savory background; use 1 tsp dried thyme if you lack fresh.","8":"Subtle background note; skip with almost no difference.","10":"Fresh green finish; skip with a small loss.","11":"Brightens the soup; skip and it tastes heavier.","12":"Season at the end; the broth may already be salty.","13":"Background heat only; skip freely.","0":"Softens the vegetables; they still cook in the broth"},"sub":{"0":{"x":"2 tbsp butter","d":"Slightly richer; use medium heat so it doesn't brown.","k":["butter"],"wn":"Butter contains dairy"},"7":{"x":"1 tsp dried thyme","d":"Slightly less fresh; add with the broth so it has time to soften.","k":["dried thyme"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Pull the chicken as soon as it reaches 165F; overcooked breast meat turns dry and stringy in soup.","s":2},{"t":"Rice keeps swelling as the soup sits. For leftovers, store rice separately or add extra broth when reheating.","s":4},{"t":"Stir in the lemon juice and parsley at the very end; heat dulls both. Remove the bay leaves and thyme stems before serving.","s":5}],"rl":"FXVVXMPXXBGAZZ"},"3592":{"lv":"EEORRRREOROR","w":{"3":"Sweet savory base; skip and the soup is more beany and smoky.","4":"Sweetness and body; skip with a small loss.","5":"Gives a background aromatic; skip with minor difference.","6":"Adds depth to the ham and beans; skip with a modest loss.","8":"Subtle herbal background; skip with almost no change.","9":"Earthy note that suits ham and beans; skip with a small loss.","10":"Adds a bit of heat; ham is already smoky.","11":"Ham hock and broth are salty; only add at the end if needed.","2":"Only sweats the vegetables; the ham gives flavor anyway"},"sub":{"2":{"x":"1 tbsp vegetable oil or butter","d":"Just for sweating the vegetables; no meaningful flavor change.","k":["vegetable oil","butter"],"wn":"Butter contains dairy"}},"tips":[{"t":"Keep the simmer low and gentle; a hard boil splits the bean skins and turns them to mush before the centers are tender.","s":3},{"t":"Do not add salt or acid until the beans are fully tender; both toughen the skins. The ham hock and broth already carry plenty of salt, so taste at the very end.","s":5},{"t":"Fish out the ham hock, shred the meat, and mash a ladle of beans against the pot; the starch thickens the soup naturally.","s":4}],"rl":"BPFXVVXMXXZZ"},"3594":{"lv":"ERERRRRERERRRORO","w":{"3":"Sweetness and color; skip with a more beefy soup.","4":"Savory background; skip or add extra carrot.","5":"Adds depth; skip with a modest loss.","6":"Adds umami and sweetness to the broth; skip and it tastes thinner.","8":"Gives acidity and body to the broth; skip and use extra tomato paste.","10":"Fresh green vegetable; skip and double another vegetable.","11":"Sweetness added at the end; skip with little loss.","12":"Earthy herb that suits beef; skip with a small loss.","13":"Subtle background note; skip with almost no change.","14":"Season at the end; beef and broth vary in saltiness.","15":"Background heat only; skip freely.","1":"Browns the beef; the beef renders fat"},"sub":{"1":{"x":"2 tbsp butter or vegetable oil","d":"Just for browning the beef; butter browns faster, so watch the heat.","k":["butter","vegetable oil"],"wn":"Butter contains dairy"}},"tips":[{"t":"Brown the beef in two batches in a hot pot; crowding steams it gray, and the dark crust left on the bottom of the pot is the soup's flavor base.","s":0},{"t":"Chuck needs a long, gentle simmer to melt its collagen; if a cube still feels tight when you press it, give it another 15 minutes before adding the potatoes.","s":3},{"t":"Add the potatoes and green beans later so they hold their shape, and the corn last so it stays sweet and bright.","s":4}],"rl":"PFXVVXXMABVVXXZZ"},"3596":{"lv":"ERRREEERRRRRO","w":{"1":"Sweet savory base for the tomatoes; skip and the soup tastes sharper.","2":"Adds sweetness that balances the acid; skip and add a pinch more sugar.","3":"Adds depth; skip with a modest loss.","7":"Concentrates the tomato flavor and adds depth; skip with a slightly thinner taste.","8":"Balances the tomato acid; skip and taste, adding a pinch if sharp.","10":"Fresh herbal note that goes with tomato; dried basil is weaker, so skip or use less.","11":"Season at the end; canned tomatoes and broth vary in salt.","12":"Background heat only; skip freely.","9":"Silky finish for the bisque; the soup is fine less rich"},"sub":{"9":{"x":"1/2 cup half-and-half","d":"Lighter and slightly less silky; add it off the boil and warm gently.","k":["half-and-half"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Cook the tomato paste and flour with the garlic for a full 2 minutes; the paste caramelizes and the flour loses its raw taste.","s":1},{"t":"Cream curdles in hot acidic tomato soup. Take the pot off the boil, warm the cream with a ladle of soup first, then stir it in and keep it below a simmer.","s":4},{"t":"If the bisque tastes too sharp, a tiny pinch of baking soda (about 1/8 teaspoon) tames the acid, but it foams, so add it slowly.","s":2}],"rl":"FXVXTVMXWFXZZ"},"3599":{"lv":"RREREERRRO","w":{"1":"Sweet base that supports the carrots; skip and the soup tastes sweeter and plainer.","3":"Adds depth; skip with a modest loss.","6":"Silky richness and a faint sweetness; skip and the soup is lighter, thin with broth.","7":"Brightens the sweet carrots and the ginger; skip and the soup tastes heavy and flat.","8":"Season after blending; carrots need salt to taste sweet rather than flat.","9":"Background heat only; skip freely.","0":"Softens the onion; the soup is blended anyway","5":"The liquid base of the soup; without it there is only a thick puree"},"sub":{"0":{"x":"2 tbsp butter or vegetable oil","d":"Butter is richer; both just soften the onion.","k":["butter","vegetable oil"],"wn":"Butter contains dairy"},"5":{"x":"4 cups chicken broth","d":"Meatier; not vegetarian but works the same.","k":["chicken broth"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Grate the ginger finely and cook it only a minute with the garlic; burnt ginger turns bitter, and big pieces leave fibers after blending.","s":1},{"t":"Slice the carrots thin and uniform so they go soft in 20 to 25 minutes, and blend longer than you think; under-blended carrot soup is gritty.","s":3},{"t":"Shake the coconut milk can before opening, and add the lime off the heat so its brightness does not cook away.","s":4}],"rl":"FXXXVMFAZZ"},"52840":{"lv":"EEREROEEREO","w":{"2":"Smoky salty fat that is a classic in chowder; skip and the soup is cleaner but less deep.","4":"Earthy herbal note; skip with a small loss.","5":"Subtle background note; skip with almost no change.","10":"Fresh green finish; purely a garnish.","8":"Enriches the chowder; the milk and potato starch still make it creamy"},"sub":{"8":{"x":"2/3 cup half-and-half","d":"Lighter and slightly thinner; do not boil after it goes in.","k":["half-and-half"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Discard any clams that are cracked or stay open when tapped, and any that do not open after steaming. They are not safe to eat.","s":0},{"t":"Pour the clam cooking liquid through a fine sieve or coffee filter and leave the last gritty spoonfuls behind; it is the flavor base of the chowder.","s":0},{"t":"Crush a few potato pieces against the pot so their starch thickens the chowder, and add the clams back at the very end; boiling toughens them.","s":2}],"rl":"PFFVXXTMFBG"},"130":{"lv":"EEEROR","w":{"3":"Adds floral depth that makes the plain milk and rice taste like dessert; skip and it tastes flat.","4":"Warm spice garnish; skip freely.","5":"A pinch sharpens the sweetness; skipping it makes the pudding taste flat."},"tips":[{"t":"Use a heavy-bottomed pot over low heat and scrape the bottom as you stir; milk scorches easily and a burnt layer spoils the whole batch.","s":0},{"t":"Short-grain rice such as Arborio or pudding rice gives the creamiest result; long-grain stays separate and less creamy.","s":0},{"t":"The pudding thickens a lot as it cools, so stop cooking while it still looks slightly loose and thin it with a splash of cold milk if it sets too stiff.","s":2}],"rl":"BMWXGZ"},"131":{"lv":"ERRREEEEO","w":{"1":"Sweetens the tart apples; skip and the filling is sharper.","2":"Classic apple crisp spice; skip and the dessert tastes plain.","3":"Brightens the apples and keeps them from browning; skip with a flatter filling.","8":"Cold creamy contrast for the warm crisp; purely a serving extra."},"tips":[{"t":"Use firm, tart apples such as Granny Smith, or a mix with Honeycrisp; soft varieties collapse into applesauce under the topping.","s":0},{"t":"Rub the cold butter in until you have pea-to-marble-sized clumps and scatter them without pressing. Packing the topping makes a dense crust instead of a crisp.","s":1},{"t":"Let the crisp rest 15 minutes after baking so the juices thicken; served right away it will be soupy.","s":2}],"rl":"VWXABSWFG"},"132":{"lv":"EEEEREERE","w":{"4":"Rounds out the toffee flavor of the browned butter; skip and the cookies taste less rounded.","7":"Sharpens the sweetness and nuttiness; skip and the cookies taste flat."},"tips":[{"t":"Browning butter cooks off about a fifth of its weight in water, so scrape in every toasted brown bit. If the dough looks crumbly, add a tablespoon of milk.","s":0},{"t":"Chilling the dough lets the flour hydrate and deepens the toffee flavor; it also prevents the browned-butter dough from spreading too thin.","s":2},{"t":"Pull the cookies when the edges are set and the centers still look underdone; they finish on the hot tray, and a firm tap of the pan flattens them.","s":3}],"rl":"FWWSXSLZG"},"133":{"lv":"EEEEEORRR","w":{"5":"Faint background aroma; skip with almost no difference since the brown sugar and cinnamon dominate.","6":"Gives the cinnamon-roll swirl its caramel sweetness; skip and it tastes like plain vanilla cake.","7":"The signature cinnamon-roll flavor; skip and it is just a plain mug cake.","8":"Makes the glaze that gives the cinnamon-roll topping; skip for a plainer cake.","4":"The only fat in the mug cake; without it the cake is dry and rubbery"},"sub":{"4":{"x":"1 tbsp melted butter","d":"Richer, slightly firmer crumb; cool slightly before mixing.","k":["butter"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Use a large mug of 12 ounces or more; the batter rises well above the rim of a small cup and overflows in the microwave.","s":0},{"t":"Spoon the brown sugar and cinnamon on top and swirl with a knife; stirring it in makes it sink as syrup at the bottom.","s":1},{"t":"Microwaves differ, so start at 60 seconds. If the top is still wet, add 10 seconds at a time; overcooking makes the cake rubbery.","s":2}],"rl":"SWLMFXWXG"},"134":{"lv":"EEEEREEOR","w":{"4":"Rounds out the banana flavor; skip and the loaf is slightly less aromatic.","7":"Faint warm spice; skip freely.","8":"Sharpens the sweetness; skip and the loaf tastes a bit flat."},"tips":[{"t":"Use bananas that are heavily speckled or even black. Their sugar and moisture are what make the loaf sweet and tender; thawed frozen bananas work well, juices included.","s":0},{"t":"Stir only until the flour disappears. Overmixing builds gluten and gives a tough, tunneled loaf.","s":1},{"t":"Check at 55 minutes; a toothpick should show moist crumbs, not wet batter. If the top darkens before the center sets, tent with foil.","s":2}],"rl":"VFWSXLSXZ"},"135":{"lv":"ERRRO","w":{"2":"Melts into the butter to make a caramel-like syrup; skip and the apples taste plain and the sauce is thin.","3":"The signature flavor of the dish; use less only if you want a milder apple taste.","4":"Cool, creamy contrast to the warm apples; purely a serving extra.","1":"Makes a richer syrup for the apples; the apples still bake with sugar and cinnamon"},"sub":{"1":{"x":"2 tbsp coconut oil or neutral oil","d":"Loses the dairy richness and browned-butter flavor; the syrup is less silky.","k":["coconut oil","vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Slice the apples a uniform quarter inch thick and use a firm variety such as Honeycrisp or Granny Smith; thin, soft apples turn to mush before the edges caramelize.","s":0},{"t":"Cover the dish with foil for the first 15 minutes so the apples steam tender, then uncover to let the syrup reduce.","s":1},{"t":"For the skillet method, let the butter and sugar bubble to a caramel first, then add the apples and keep turning them gently so they stay in slices.","s":1}],"rl":"VFWXG"},"136":{"lv":"EEERO","w":{"3":"Gives bursts of chocolate and sweetness; skip or swap raisins or chopped nuts.","4":"Faint background aroma; skip with almost no difference.","2":"The sticky binder of the energy bites; without it the oats and peanut butter crumble"},"sub":{"2":{"x":"1/3 cup maple syrup","d":"Thinner and less sticky; add a couple of tablespoons more oats if the mix is loose.","k":["maple syrup"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Use old-fashioned rolled oats; quick oats go pasty and instant oats turn gummy. If the mix is crumbly, add a spoonful of honey; if it sticks to your hands, add a spoonful of oats.","s":0},{"t":"Chilling is not optional: it lets the oats absorb the honey so the bites hold their shape. Wet your hands slightly to stop the mixture from sticking while rolling.","s":1},{"t":"If you use natural peanut butter, stir the oil back in before measuring or the mix will be greasy and fall apart.","s":0}],"rl":"BTWGX"},"137":{"lv":"EREREEEEO","w":{"1":"Sweetens the tart berries; skip and the filling is sharp.","3":"Zest and juice brighten the berries; skip and the flavor tastes flat.","8":"Cold, creamy contrast to the warm fruit; purely a serving extra."},"tips":[{"t":"Do not thaw the berries. Frozen ones release their juice gradually as the crumble bakes, and the cornstarch thickens it as it bubbles; thawed berries flood the dish.","s":0},{"t":"Scatter the topping loosely with some gaps for steam to escape, and set a baking sheet under the dish to catch any bubbling juice.","s":1},{"t":"Bake until the juices bubble thickly around the edges and not just at the center, then rest 10 minutes so the filling sets.","s":2}],"rl":"VWTASBWFG"},"138":{"lv":"EEEEEEERR","w":{"7":"Melts into a warm cheese dip; skip and serve with mustard instead.","8":"Loosens the cheddar into a smooth dip; skip if you use mustard instead."},"tips":[{"t":"Keep the water at 105 to 110F; hot water kills the yeast. The mixture should foam in 5 to 10 minutes. The method also calls for salt in the dough though only coarse salt is listed, so add about 1 1/2 teaspoons fine salt.","s":0},{"t":"The baking soda bath sets the crust and gives pretzels their mahogany color and chew. Use a wide pot (it foams up), drain each batch well on a rack, and use a non-aluminum pot.","s":2},{"t":"Cut the ropes into even pieces so they bake at the same rate, and brush with butter while still hot so it soaks in.","s":1}],"rl":"MLWSFXZGM"},"140":{"lv":"ERREERRRR","w":{"2":"Adds tang and smoothness to the cream cheese base; skip and use extra sour cream.","5":"Savory backbone for the creamy dip; skip and use 1/2 tsp garlic powder.","6":"Stretchy melt that makes the top bubbly; skip and add extra parmesan.","7":"Salty, nutty depth that keeps the dip from tasting bland; skip with a milder result.","8":"The dipper; toasted bread or vegetables work equally.","1":"Adds tang to the dip; cream cheese and other ingredients still make it creamy"},"sub":{"1":{"x":"1/2 cup plain full-fat Greek yogurt","d":"Tangier and a little firmer; use full-fat so it doesn't weep when baked.","k":["greek yogurt"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Squeeze the thawed spinach in a towel until it is dry; leftover water is what turns this dip soupy. Drain and pat the artichokes dry too.","s":1},{"t":"Use truly soft cream cheese and beat it with the sour cream and mayo first; cold cream cheese leaves white lumps.","s":0},{"t":"Finish under the broiler for 2 minutes for a browned top, and keep the leftovers warm in a small slow cooker so the cheese stays dippable.","s":2}],"rl":"FFFVVXFXG"},"3155":{"lv":"EEEREERER","w":{"3":"Rounds out the chocolate flavor; skip and brownies taste slightly flat.","6":"Heightens the chocolate and balances the sweetness; skip and they taste flat.","8":"Pockets of melted chocolate; skip and the brownies are still fudgy."},"tips":[{"t":"Whisk the eggs and sugar into the warm butter for a full minute; this dissolves the sugar and makes the shiny, crackly top.","s":1},{"t":"Pull the pan when a toothpick shows a few moist crumbs, not wet batter. Overbaking turns fudgy brownies cakey and dry.","s":3},{"t":"Cool completely, then chill 30 minutes before cutting, and wipe the knife between cuts with a hot damp towel for clean edges.","s":4}],"rl":"FWSXXSZLG"},"3554":{"lv":"EEEREEEEO","w":{"3":"Balances the sweet and tart in the shortbread crust; skip and the crust is bland.","8":"Finishing dusting; skip if serving right away, since it melts into the filling over time."},"tips":[{"t":"Rub the zest into the sugar with your fingertips before adding the eggs; this releases the oils and gives far more lemon flavor than juice alone.","s":3},{"t":"Pour the filling over the hot crust so the bottom sets evenly, and pull the pan when the center barely jiggles; it finishes setting as it cools.","s":4},{"t":"Chill thoroughly, cut with a long sharp knife wiped between cuts, and dust with powdered sugar just before serving because it dissolves on the filling.","s":5}],"rl":"FWSZSWTAG"},"3555":{"lv":"EEEEREERRER","w":{"4":"Rounds out the brown sugar and butter flavor; skip with slightly less aroma.","7":"Warm spice that suits oatmeal and raisins; skip and the cookies taste plainer.","8":"Sharpens the sweetness; skip and the cookies taste flat.","10":"The signature fruity chew; skip or swap chopped dried cranberries or chocolate chips."},"sub":{"10":{"x":"1 cup chopped dried cranberries or chocolate chips","d":"Tarter with cranberries, richer with chocolate; no longer a classic raisin cookie.","k":["dried cranberries","chocolate chips"],"wn":"Chocolate chips usually contain milk and soy"}},"tips":[{"t":"Cream the butter and sugars for 2 to 3 minutes. The trapped air gives the cookies lift and the chewy-soft texture.","s":1},{"t":"Use rolled oats, not quick or instant oats; quick oats absorb too much moisture and make the cookies dry. Soak the raisins in hot water for 10 minutes and drain so they stay plump instead of stealing moisture.","s":3},{"t":"Pull them when the centers still look slightly soft; they finish on the hot sheet, and overbaked oatmeal cookies turn hard.","s":5}],"rl":"FWWSXSLXZBV"},"3556":{"lv":"EEEREEERRE","w":{"3":"Rounds out the buttery flavor; skip with slightly less aroma.","7":"Balances the sweetness; skip and the cookies taste flat.","8":"Forms the sugary crackle on the surface; skip and the cookies are paler with no crust."},"tips":[{"t":"Cream of tartar and baking soda together give snickerdoodles their tangy flavor and chewy texture; do not swap in baking powder alone or you lose both.","s":2},{"t":"Roll even balls and coat them generously in the cinnamon sugar; the coating is what cracks into the signature crinkled crust.","s":4},{"t":"Pull them while the centers still look puffy; they fall and crinkle as they cool, and overbaking dries them out.","s":5}],"rl":"FWSXSLLZGG"},"3558":{"lv":"EEEEEERRRR","w":{"6":"Rounds out the chocolate flavor; skip with little loss.","7":"Sharpens the chocolate flavor; skip with almost no difference.","8":"Prevents the flour film that coating with flour leaves on the cake; also helps it release cleanly.","9":"Keeps the cake from sticking when you invert it; skip and the cake tears.","2":"The sweetener for the lava cake batter; without it the cake is not sweet or structured"},"sub":{"2":{"x":"1/2 cup + 1 tbsp granulated sugar","d":"Slightly less smooth and the cake tastes a touch less delicate; whisk until fully dissolved.","k":["sugar"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Timing is everything: bake until the edges are firm and the center still jiggles. Test one ramekin first, since oven hot spots can overcook the molten center.","s":4},{"t":"Rest only 1 minute before inverting so the sides hold but the center stays molten; wait longer and it sets solid.","s":5},{"t":"The center is barely cooked, so use pasteurized eggs if serving anyone pregnant, young, elderly or immunocompromised, or bake 2 minutes longer.","s":4}],"rl":"FFWSSSXZGG"},"3559":{"lv":"EEOOE","w":{"2":"Rounds out the marshmallow flavor; skip with little change.","3":"Balances the sweetness; skip with almost no difference."},"tips":[{"t":"Keep the heat low and stir constantly; marshmallows scorch quickly and turn bitter and tough once browned. Take the pot off the heat as soon as they are smooth.","s":2},{"t":"Fold the cereal in quickly and press lightly with a greased spatula; packing hard makes dense, tough bars.","s":5},{"t":"For extra gooey bars, stir in 1 cup of mini marshmallows after removing the pot from the heat so they stay partly intact.","s":4}],"rl":"FTXZB"},"3561":{"lv":"EEEERR","w":{"4":"Defines the flavor; skip and it is just sweet cream, not vanilla ice cream.","5":"Sharpens the sweetness; skip and the ice cream tastes flat."},"tips":[{"t":"Cook the custard to 175F, no hotter; it should coat the back of a spoon. This gently cooks the yolks, and past about 180F they scramble. If it curdles, blend it smooth.","s":2},{"t":"Chill the base until completely cold, ideally below 40F, before churning. A warm base makes icy ice cream and a soupy churn.","s":3},{"t":"Press plastic wrap directly onto the surface of the finished ice cream before freezing to prevent ice crystals.","s":5}],"rl":"FMWSXZ"},"3562":{"lv":"EEEEER","w":{"5":"Rounds out the peanut butter flavor; skip with little loss.","4":"Sets and smooths the fudge; without it the fudge will not firm up"},"sub":{"4":{"x":"7 oz (about 4 cups) mini marshmallows, stirred in over very low heat until melted","d":"Slightly less smooth and a bit softer; needs a minute of gentle heat to melt completely.","k":["marshmallows"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Time the full rolling boil at exactly 5 minutes. Under-boiled fudge never sets and over-boiled fudge turns grainy; a heavy pan and a timer matter.","s":2},{"t":"Do not scrape the sugar crystals from the sides of the pan into the fudge; they can seed graininess.","s":2},{"t":"Stir in the peanut butter and marshmallow creme immediately once off the heat; the fudge starts to set fast, so work quickly and use a warm knife to cut small squares.","s":3}],"rl":"WFMFTX"},"3563":{"lv":"EEEREREEEEO","w":{"3":"Balances the sweet and tart in the filling; skip and it tastes flatter.","5":"Makes the filling silky and rich; skip and the filling is firmer and tarter.","10":"Faint background aroma in the meringue; skip with almost no difference.","8":"Stabilizes the whipped egg whites for the meringue; without it the meringue weeps and collapses"},"sub":{"8":{"x":"1/2 tsp lemon juice or white vinegar","d":"Gives the same stability to the whites; add with the first sugar.","k":["lemon juice","white vinegar"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Whisk some hot filling into the yolks, then cook the filling for the full 2 minutes at a bubble. Cooking it fully deactivates yolk enzymes that would otherwise thin the filling after slicing.","s":2},{"t":"Spread the meringue over the hot filling and seal it right to the crust edges; the heat cooks the underside and sealing prevents the meringue from shrinking and weeping.","s":5},{"t":"Cool the pie fully before chilling, then slice with a knife dipped in hot water and wiped between cuts. Eat within 2 days, as meringue weeps.","s":6}],"rl":"BWTZTFASSWX"},"3564":{"lv":"EEEREERE","w":{"3":"Gives classic pound cake aroma; skip with a plainer buttery flavor.","6":"Balances the sweetness and butter; skip and the cake tastes flat."},"tips":[{"t":"Beat the butter and sugar for the full 4 to 5 minutes with room-temperature (65 to 68F) butter and eggs. With so little leavening, the air whipped in here is most of the rise.","s":1},{"t":"Add the flour in three parts alternating with the milk, ending with flour, and stop mixing as soon as it is combined; overbeating makes the cake tough.","s":3},{"t":"A crack along the top is normal. If the crust browns too fast before the toothpick comes out clean, tent with foil for the last 15 minutes.","s":5}],"rl":"FWSXSLZM"},"3565":{"lv":"EEEREERRRO","w":{"3":"Gives classic sugar cookie flavor; skip and they taste plainer.","6":"Balances the sweetness; skip and the cookies taste flat.","7":"Makes the icing; skip for plain undecorated cookies.","8":"Thins the icing to spreading consistency; water works too.","9":"Faint background aroma in the icing; skip with almost no difference."},"sub":{"8":{"x":"2 tbsp water","d":"Slightly less creamy and a bit thinner; icing sets harder.","k":["water"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Chill the discs the full hour and roll between sheets of parchment with minimal flour; extra flour dries the dough and makes tough cookies.","s":2},{"t":"Cut the shapes, then slide the tray into the fridge 10 minutes before baking. Cold dough keeps sharp edges instead of spreading.","s":3},{"t":"Icing: keep it thick enough to hold a ribbon for 10 seconds for flooding, and adjust with a few drops of milk at a time.","s":6}],"rl":"FWSXSLZGMX"},"3566":{"lv":"EREREEEERERR","w":{"1":"Adds a touch of sweetness and helps the biscuits brown; skip and they are plainer and paler.","3":"Seasons the biscuits; skip and they taste flat against the sweet berries.","8":"Draws out the strawberry juices that soak into the biscuits; skip and the berries are tart and dry.","10":"Sweetens and stabilizes the whipped cream; skip and use granulated sugar.","11":"Adds aroma to the cream; skip with little loss."},"tips":[{"t":"Cut the cold butter into pea-size pieces and keep it cold; the butter melts in the oven and steams, leaving the flaky layers.","s":1},{"t":"Stir the milk and egg in only until the dough is shaggy; overworking makes tough, dense biscuits.","s":2},{"t":"Whip the cream in a cold bowl just to soft peaks, and split the biscuits while warm so the berry juices soak into the crumb.","s":5}],"rl":"SWLZFMSVWGWX"},"3567":{"lv":"EREEEERERRERO","w":{"1":"Sweetens the crust and helps it hold together; skip and the crust is more crumbly.","6":"Balances the sweetness; skip and the custard tastes flat.","8":"Makes the custard silky and rich; skip and it is leaner and firmer.","9":"Defines the custard's flavor; skip and it tastes only of milk and sugar.","11":"The classic topping; skip for a plainer pie, but it balances the sweetness.","12":"Lightly sweetens the whipped cream; skip and the cream is plain."},"tips":[{"t":"Boil the custard for a full minute, whisking, after it thickens. Cornstarch needs that heat to gelatinize fully, and the yolk enzyme that thins custard is deactivated.","s":2},{"t":"Press plastic wrap directly onto the custard surface while it cools so it doesn't form a skin.","s":3},{"t":"Slice the bananas just before assembling and seal them in custard on all sides; exposed banana browns quickly.","s":4}],"rl":"BWFMWTZTFXVGW"},"3568":{"lv":"EEEREEEEREEERO","w":{"3":"Rounds out the chocolate flavor; skip with a small loss.","8":"Balances the sweetness; skip and the cake and filling taste flat.","12":"Gives the filling its fluffy lightness; skip and the filling is denser and more like buttercream.","13":"Faint background aroma in the filling; skip with almost no difference.","9":"The acid and liquid for the baking soda in the cake; without it the whoopie pies do not rise"},"sub":{"9":{"x":"1 cup whole milk + 1 tbsp lemon juice or white vinegar, rested 5 minutes","d":"Slightly thinner and less tangy; the acid still activates the baking soda.","k":["whole milk","lemon juice","white vinegar"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Baking soda starts reacting with the buttermilk's acid as soon as they meet, so scoop and bake promptly after mixing to keep the cakes tall.","s":2},{"t":"Use a cookie scoop for even rounds so the pairs match; uneven cakes make wobbly sandwiches.","s":3},{"t":"Cool the cakes completely before filling; warm cakes melt the butter filling. Chill the finished pies 20 minutes so the filling sets.","s":6}],"rl":"FWSXSXLLZMFWTX"},"3569":{"lv":"EEEERRRR","w":{"4":"Heightens the chocolate and balances the sweetness; skip and the pudding tastes flat.","5":"Makes the pudding richer, glossier and fudgier; skip and it is lighter and more cocoa-like.","6":"Gives a silky mouthfeel and shine; skip and the pudding is leaner and less glossy.","7":"Rounds out the chocolate flavor; skip with a slight loss in aroma."},"tips":[{"t":"Whisk the dry ingredients with a little cold milk into a smooth paste before adding the rest. Cocoa and cornstarch dumped into hot liquid form lumps that never fully dissolve.","s":0},{"t":"Boil for a full minute while whisking. Cornstarch only reaches full thickening at a boil, and under-cooked pudding stays thin and tastes starchy once chilled.","s":2},{"t":"Strain the pudding through a sieve and press plastic wrap directly onto the surface so it chills smooth with no skin.","s":4}],"rl":"MWXTZFFX"},"52786":{"lv":"EEEOR","w":{"3":"Rounds out the chocolate flavor; skip with almost no difference.","4":"Adds crunch and a nutty counterpoint to the sweet fudge; skip and it is smoother but one-note."},"sub":{"4":{"x":"1 1/4 cups chopped almonds or walnuts","d":"Almonds give a similar crunch; walnuts are slightly bitter and earthier.","k":["almonds","walnuts"],"wn":"Contains tree nuts"}},"tips":[{"t":"Microwave the chips and peanut butter in 20-second bursts, stirring each time, and stop while a few chips are still unmelted; residual heat finishes them. Overheated chocolate turns grainy and seizes.","s":2},{"t":"Let the chocolate cool a few minutes before folding in the marshmallows; mixed in hot, they melt into streaks instead of staying as distinct pieces.","s":5},{"t":"Line the pan with foil or wax paper with an overhang so you can lift the set fudge out, and cut with a warm knife wiped between cuts.","s":8}],"rl":"BSFXG"},"52787":{"lv":"EREOER","w":{"1":"Keeps the dark chocolate layer soft and glossy; skip and use milk for a slightly firmer fudge.","3":"Rounds out the chocolate flavor; skip with almost no difference.","5":"Gives the hot-cocoa look and a chewy topping; skip and the fudge is plainer."},"sub":{"1":{"x":"2 tbsp whole milk","d":"Slightly less rich; condensed milk already supplies the fat.","k":["whole milk"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Use sweetened condensed milk, not evaporated milk; evaporated milk has no sugar and the fudge will not set. Microwave in 20-second bursts, stirring until just smooth.","s":1},{"t":"White chocolate scorches faster than dark, so microwave it in 15-second bursts, and let the dark layer firm slightly before spreading the white on top to keep the layers distinct.","s":4},{"t":"Press the marshmallows in lightly while the top layer is still tacky; chill the full 4 hours before cutting with a warm knife.","s":6}],"rl":"SFTXSG"},"52833":{"lv":"EREEREEREOOO","w":{"1":"Salty crunch that echoes the salted caramel; skip and use more biscuits.","4":"Rounds out the cream cheese flavor; skip with a slight loss.","7":"Gives the salted caramel contrast; skip and the cheesecake tastes only sweet.","9":"Finishing drizzle; purely decorative.","10":"Crunchy, sweet topping; purely decorative.","11":"Salty garnish and crunch; purely decorative.","0":"The biscuit crust base of the cheesecake; without it there is no base"},"sub":{"0":{"x":"250 g graham cracker crumbs (about 2 cups)","d":"Sweeter and slightly honeyed; still gives a buttery crust.","k":["graham crackers"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Press the crumbs firmly with the back of a glass, then chill the crust 20 minutes while you make the filling; a cold crust won't crumble when the cheesecake is sliced.","s":0},{"t":"The cream cheese must be at room temperature and beaten completely smooth with the sugar before the caramel and cream go in; lumps never disappear afterward.","s":1},{"t":"Whip the cream into the mix only until it is thick and mousse-like; overwhipping turns grainy. There is no gelatin, so chill overnight, then loosen with a warm knife around the springform before unmolding.","s":2}],"rl":"BBTSXWWZFGGG"},"52854":{"lv":"EEEROOO","w":{"4":"Sweet topping at serving; add to taste.","5":"Fresh, tart fruit topping; purely a serving extra.","6":"Fresh, sweet fruit topping; purely a serving extra.","3":"Fat in the batter; pancakes still work but are less tender"},"sub":{"3":{"x":"1 tbsp melted butter","d":"Richer and browns faster; cool slightly before whisking in.","k":["butter"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Rest the batter 30 minutes if you can; the flour hydrates and the gluten relaxes, giving tender, lacy pancakes. The batter should pour like single cream, so thin it with milk if it's thick.","s":0},{"t":"The first pancake is a test. The pan should sizzle on contact but not smoke; tilt it quickly to spread the batter thin, and flip when the edges lift and the underside is golden.","s":1},{"t":"To freeze, layer cooled pancakes between parchment so they don't stick, and reheat from frozen in a dry pan over medium heat.","s":2}],"rl":"SSMFWGG"},"52855":{"lv":"EEEOOOR","w":{"3":"A few drops round out the banana flavor; the pancakes still taste fine without it.","4":"Only greases the pan so the eggy batter does not stick; any fat works.","5":"Crunchy topping only; skipping loses texture contrast but not structure.","6":"Tart fruit cuts the sweetness of the banana; any berry or sliced fruit can stand in."},"sub":{"4":{"x":"1 tsp melted butter or a light spray of cooking oil","d":"Butter browns the pancakes a little faster and adds flavor; watch the heat.","k":["butter"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Use a very ripe banana with brown freckles; it supplies the sweetness and the body, and an underripe one gives a starchy, bland batter.","s":0},{"t":"Make them small (about 3 inches) and flip only when bubbles pop and stay open on top. This flour-free batter is delicate and a big pancake will tear when flipped.","s":1},{"t":"Keep the heat at medium-low; the banana sugar scorches quickly, so a dark crust with a raw middle means the pan is too hot.","s":1}],"rl":"VSLXFGG"},"52856":{"lv":"EEEREEEEERER","w":{"3":"A tablespoon in the pastry adds a little sweetness and tenderness; the crust works without it.","5":"Gives the filling its sticky, caramel body; omitting it makes a thinner, less glossy set.","9":"Rounds out the sugary filling; the pie is still good without it.","11":"Adds molten chocolate pockets; the pie is a classic pecan pie without them."},"sub":{"5":{"x":"200 ml (3/4 cup + 1 tbsp) honey","d":"Floral and a bit runnier; it browns faster, so cover the crust edge with foil early.","k":["honey"]}},"tips":[{"t":"The cream cheese pastry is soft and sticky, so keep it cold and roll it on a well-floured surface; if it warms and tears, patch it with scraps rather than re-rolling.","s":1},{"t":"Cool the melted butter-syrup-sugar mixture a few minutes before adding to the eggs, otherwise the eggs scramble into strings.","s":3},{"t":"Pull the pie when the edges are set but the middle still wobbles like jelly; it finishes setting as it cools. Let it cool fully before slicing or the filling will run.","s":3}],"rl":"SFFWFWWWSXBG"},"52857":{"lv":"EEOERRREREO","w":{"2":"Only for rolling out the pastry; use a little extra if the dough sticks.","4":"Sharpens the sweetness and spice; the custard tastes flat without it.","5":"Warm spice that defines pumpkin pie; omit and it tastes plain.","6":"Main pie spice; pie is dull without it, though the amount can be adjusted.","8":"Adds richness and a smoother custard; the pie still sets without it.","10":"Dusting for presentation only.","9":"The liquid of the custard; without it the pumpkin pie will not set right"},"sub":{"9":{"x":"175 ml (3/4 cup) half-and-half or light cream","d":"Richer, silkier custard; evaporated milk also works. Bake until just set so it doesn't crack.","k":["half and half"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Boiled pumpkin holds a lot of water. After sieving, let the puree drain in a fine strainer or blot it, or the custard will be loose and the base soggy.","s":2},{"t":"Blind-bake the pastry with baking beans first, then bake the filled pie hot to set the bottom before dropping the heat. Skipping the blind bake gives a pale, damp crust.","s":1},{"t":"Take it out when the edge is set and the center still jiggles slightly; overbaked custard cracks and turns grainy because the eggs tighten.","s":2}],"rl":"VSGWZXXSFMG"},"52858":{"lv":"EEREEEREERRR","w":{"2":"A spoonful of sugar sweetens the crust or butter layer; the cheesecake still works without it.","6":"A touch of acid brightens the dense cream cheese filling; skipping it tastes heavier.","9":"Part of the soured cream topping that gives the tangy white cap.","10":"Sweetens the topping so it is not sharp; minor.","11":"Adds tang to the topping; without it the topping is blander."},"tips":[{"t":"Start with cream cheese at room temperature and beat it with the sugar and flour before any eggs go in. Cold cheese leaves lumps that you cannot beat out later.","s":1},{"t":"Whisk the eggs in only until combined and never beat on high; air bubbles puff up in the oven and then collapse into cracks.","s":2},{"t":"Loosen the edges with a knife as soon as it comes out, then cool slowly. A cheesecake clinging to the tin tears itself as it shrinks and cracks across the top.","s":5}],"rl":"TFWSWTASFGWA"},"52859":{"lv":"EEEEEROO","w":{"0":"The biscuit crust needs a sweet, crumbly cookie; graham crackers are the standard American version.","5":"Only used for topping; the pie is complete without it, but the cream balances the tart lime.","6":"A spoonful to sweeten the whipped cream.","7":"Garnish only."},"sub":{"0":{"x":"300 g graham crackers (about 10 full sheets), crushed","d":"Slightly sweeter and honeyed, the classic Key lime base; the same amount of butter works.","k":["graham crackers"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Whisk the yolks for a full minute, then the condensed milk for 3 minutes. The mixture thickens as the lime acid reacts with the milk proteins, giving a smooth set rather than a runny filling.","s":1},{"t":"Bake only until the center has a slight wobble, about 15 minutes; it firms up as it chills. Chill at least 3 hours before cutting.","s":1},{"t":"Use real limes; bottled juice tastes flat and metallic. Pack the crust firmly with the back of a glass so it does not crumble when sliced.","s":0}],"rl":"BTSSAGWG"},"52860":{"lv":"EREEEEER","w":{"1":"Adds sweetness and a softer chocolate flavor; replacing with dark chocolate gives a slightly more bitter brownie.","7":"Tart fruit cuts through the richness; plain brownies are perfectly fine without them."},"sub":{"1":{"x":"100 g more dark chocolate","d":"Slightly darker and less sweet; texture is almost identical.","k":["dark chocolate"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Let the melted chocolate mixture cool for 5 minutes before beating in the eggs; if it is too hot the eggs scramble and the brownies turn grainy.","s":2},{"t":"Stop stirring as soon as the flour disappears. Overmixing develops gluten and gives a cakey brownie instead of a fudgy one.","s":2},{"t":"Pull the tray when the middle is slightly soft; it sets as it cools. Chill before slicing for clean edges, dipping the knife in hot water.","s":4}],"rl":"XXFWSSXV"},"52861":{"lv":"EEEEEEEEOO","w":{"1":"Peanut-flavored crumb base; any plain cookie plus 1 tbsp extra peanut butter works.","2":"Leaf gelatine sets the filling; powdered gelatine works if bloomed properly.","5":"Sweetens the filling and keeps it smooth; honey or corn syrup behaves similarly.","8":"Sweetens the whipped cream topping slightly.","9":"Crunchy garnish; the cheesecake is complete without it."},"sub":{"1":{"x":"175 g digestive biscuits or graham crackers + 1 tbsp extra melted butter","d":"Less peanut flavor but the same crunch; the base still holds with butter.","k":["graham crackers","butter"]},"2":{"x":"10 g (about 3 1/2 tsp) powdered gelatine, soaked in 3 tbsp cold water","d":"Same set, but bloom it in cold water first and melt gently; do not boil.","k":["gelatin"]},"5":{"x":"175 g honey or light corn syrup","d":"Honey is more floral; corn syrup is neutral. Texture stays the same.","k":["honey"],"wn":"Honey: not for children under 1 year old"}},"tips":[{"t":"Squeeze the soaked gelatine dry and dissolve it in warm milk below a simmer; boiled gelatine loses setting power.","s":2},{"t":"Beat the ricotta until it is smooth, or sieve it first. Cold gelatine liquid hitting cold ricotta will clump, so add the warm gelatine mixture while it is only just cooled.","s":1},{"t":"Line the tin with smooth cling film so the base releases cleanly, then chill at least 4 hours before unmolding.","s":0}],"rl":"TBSBFWMGWG"},"52862":{"lv":"RRRREEEEERE","w":{"0":"Thickens the fruit juices into a light syrup; without it the fruit is runny.","1":"Provides the liquid and acidity for the fruit; any juice with a splash of lemon works.","2":"Orange aroma that lifts the peaches; fine without it.","3":"Sweetens the tart fruit; adjust to ripeness.","9":"Warm spice in the sugar topping; skipping makes it plainer."},"sub":{"1":{"x":"150 ml apple juice + 1 tbsp lemon juice","d":"Sweeter and less citrusy; the lemon restores brightness.","k":["apple juice","lemon juice"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Dissolve the cornflour in the cold orange juice before heating, otherwise it clumps into lumps in the hot pan.","s":0},{"t":"Put the topping onto hot fruit so the base of the dough steams and cooks through instead of staying doughy.","s":2},{"t":"Handle the dough as little as possible; kneading briefly keeps the topping tender and scone-like instead of tough.","s":2}],"rl":"TMXWVVSFWXM"},"52958":{"lv":"EEE","tips":[{"t":"Use regular, stabilised peanut butter. Natural oil-separated butter makes crumbly, greasy cookies; if yours is unsalted, add a pinch of salt.","s":1},{"t":"These have no flour so they are fragile when hot. Leave them on the tray for 5 minutes to firm up before moving them.","s":5},{"t":"Press the fork in sugar or flour between presses so it does not stick, and flatten to about 1 cm so the center bakes through.","s":3}],"rl":"BWS"},"53015":{"lv":"EEEEEEEEEEERER","w":{"6":"Shortening gives a very tender crumb; butter works, but the donuts are a little less light.","8":"Frying oil; any neutral oil works.","11":"The listed quantity looks like an error; use about 1 teaspoon vanilla extract in the glaze.","13":"Smooths the glaze and gives a softer set; without it the glaze is sharper and drier."},"sub":{"6":{"x":"1/3 cup (75 g) butter","d":"More flavor but a slightly less light crumb.","k":["butter"]},"8":{"x":"enough vegetable oil or sunflower oil for deep frying","d":"Neutral flavor; do not use olive oil because it smokes at frying temperature.","k":["vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Hold the oil at 350F (175C) with a thermometer. Too cool and the doughnuts absorb oil; too hot and they brown before the middle cooks.","s":1},{"t":"Let the cut doughnuts rise until puffy and about doubled, then handle them gently. Over-proofed dough deflates and fries flat.","s":0},{"t":"Dip them in the glaze while still warm so it melts into a thin, translucent shell; cold donuts leave the glaze thick and opaque.","s":3}],"rl":"LMMWZSFSFMWXMF"},"3279":{"lv":"ERRERRR","w":{"1":"Cream cheese is the glue that holds the roll together; hummus or mayo are workable alternatives.","2":"Adds sharp tang that cuts through the creamy spread.","4":"Mild nutty cheese; fine to skip but you lose some richness.","5":"Fresh crunch; skipping makes the roll softer.","6":"Juicy freshness; skip if tortillas must hold longer."},"sub":{"1":{"x":"1/2 cup hummus","d":"Savory and garlicky rather than tangy; works as glue just as well.","k":["hummus"],"wn":"Contains sesame (tahini)"}},"tips":[{"t":"Pat the lettuce and tomato dry; wet fillings seep into the tortilla and make the roll soggy.","s":2},{"t":"Wrap the rolls tightly in plastic wrap and chill 15 minutes; the cream cheese firms up and the pinwheels slice cleanly.","s":4},{"t":"Use a serrated knife and trim the end pieces for neat spirals.","s":4}],"rl":"BTXPFVV"},"3280":{"lv":"EEERORR","w":{"3":"Melted richness; skipping makes the wrap leaner.","4":"Crunch only; skip without much loss.","5":"Sharp bite against the sweet sauce; mild if soaked in cold water.","6":"Cool creamy drizzle balancing the sweet and smoky sauce."},"tips":[{"t":"Cook the chicken until the thickest part reads 165F (74C); resting a few minutes before shredding keeps it juicy.","s":0},{"t":"Shred the chicken while it is still warm and toss with the sauce plus a spoonful of the poaching liquid so the meat absorbs flavor instead of sitting in a thick coat.","s":1},{"t":"Warm tortillas in a dry skillet or covered in the microwave so they fold without cracking.","s":2}],"rl":"PXBFVXF"},"3281":{"lv":"EERRRRROE","w":{"2":"Adds sharpness so the egg salad does not taste flat.","3":"Adds crunch against the soft eggs.","4":"Mild onion note; scallion is a fine swap.","5":"A little brightness to cut richness.","6":"Eggs need seasoning, but adjust.","7":"Minor warm spice.","1":"The binder of the egg salad; without it the eggs are a dry crumble"},"sub":{"1":{"x":"3 tbsp plain Greek yogurt + 1/2 tsp extra mustard","d":"Tangier and lighter; a bit less creamy.","k":["greek yogurt","mustard"],"wn":"Contains dairy"}},"tips":[{"t":"Steam-off method: remove from heat at the boil and cover for 11 minutes, then ice bath immediately; this prevents the gray-green ring around the yolk.","s":0},{"t":"Chop by hand to keep some texture. Salt the mix just before serving, as salt draws water and makes the salad weepy.","s":2},{"t":"Dry the lettuce leaves well and double them up as cups so the filling does not tear through.","s":3}],"rl":"PFXVXAZZG"},"3282":{"lv":"EERRRRER","w":{"2":"Sharpens the salad.","3":"Crunch against soft tuna.","4":"Raw sharpness; skipping gives a milder salad.","5":"Brightness that lifts the tuna.","7":"Fresh leafy layer that also shields the tortilla from the wet filling.","1":"The binder of the tuna salad; without it the tuna is dry"},"sub":{"1":{"x":"1/4 cup plain Greek yogurt","d":"Tangier and lighter; add a little salt.","k":["greek yogurt"],"wn":"Contains dairy"}},"tips":[{"t":"Press the drained tuna against the can lid or in a sieve to remove all liquid; extra water makes the wrap soggy.","s":0},{"t":"Rinse the minced red onion in cold water for 30 seconds to take off the harsh edge.","s":1},{"t":"Put the spinach down first as a moisture barrier, then the tuna; roll tight and wrap in parchment to hold shape.","s":2}],"rl":"PFXVXABV"},"3284":{"lv":"EOEEEROR","w":{"5":"Melting richness; skipping tastes lighter.","6":"Crunch only.","7":"Fresh juiciness that balances salty bacon.","1":"Only cooks the chicken; the bacon fat covers it"},"sub":{"1":{"x":"1 tbsp vegetable oil or bacon drippings","d":"Only the cooking fat; bacon fat adds smoky flavor.","k":["vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Cook the chicken in a single layer without crowding, and leave it undisturbed for 2 minutes to brown. Slice thin so it hits 165F (74C) quickly.","s":1},{"t":"Bake the bacon on a rack at 400F for 15-18 minutes so it comes out flat and evenly crisp.","s":0},{"t":"Spread the ranch to the edges so it seals the tortilla against moisture from the tomato.","s":2}],"rl":"PFPBFFVV"},"3285":{"lv":"EEREERR","w":{"2":"Moist spread that holds the layers together; use less if you want it lean.","5":"Fresh acidity cutting the bacon.","6":"Crunchy contrast."},"tips":[{"t":"Slice the avocado at the last moment and sprinkle with a little salt; it browns fast.","s":2},{"t":"Dry the tomato slices on a paper towel with a pinch of salt first so they do not wet the tortilla.","s":2},{"t":"Tuck the sides in tightly and roll away from you with a firm squeeze; slice on the diagonal with a sharp knife.","s":3}],"rl":"PBFPVVV"},"3287":{"lv":"ERREEERO","w":{"1":"Makes the eggs softer and more tender; water works too.","6":"Feta is salty, so go lightly.","7":"Minor warm note.","2":"Cooks the eggs; a nonstick pan can manage"},"sub":{"2":{"x":"1 tbsp olive oil","d":"Slightly fruitier; just the cooking fat.","k":["olive oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Squeeze the wilted spinach and drain off the liquid before adding the eggs; extra water makes the eggs watery and gray.","s":1},{"t":"Stir the eggs gently over medium heat and take the pan off while still glossy; they keep cooking in the tortilla. Cook to set, not runny.","s":2},{"t":"Fold in the feta off the heat so it softens but does not melt into the eggs.","s":3}],"rl":"PMFVXBZZ"},"53415":{"lv":"RREERREEEO","w":{"0":"Orange zest perfumes the milk soak; skipping gives a plainer, milkier torrija.","1":"A small amount of lemon zest brightens the sweet soak.","4":"Gentle warm spice; the soak is still good without it.","5":"Sherry adds nutty depth; substitute and add a little acid.","7":"Fries the bread; olive oil is traditional.","9":"Optional sweet finish; the sugar in the soak already sweetens."},"sub":{"5":{"x":"1/3 cup (80 ml) apple juice + 1 tsp lemon juice","d":"Fruity and non-alcoholic; add after the milk cools a minute so the acid does not curdle it.","k":["apple juice","lemon juice"]},"7":{"x":"1 cup vegetable or sunflower oil","d":"Neutral; olive oil gives a fruitier crust.","k":["vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Use day-old baguette cut thick on the bias. Fresh bread dissolves in the milk; stale bread soaks it up and holds its shape.","s":2},{"t":"Flip the slices once while soaking and lift them with a wide spatula; the center should be soaked but not mushy.","s":2},{"t":"Fry at medium-high until deep golden on both sides, then rest on a rack so the oil drains and the crust stays crisp.","s":4}],"rl":"XXMWXXBFTW"},"53416":{"lv":"EEERORO","w":{"3":"Gives the mash its savory punch; use less if you prefer a mild flavor.","4":"Garnish for color and freshness.","5":"Seasons the potato and cabbage; add more at the end if the mash tastes flat.","6":"Minor heat."},"tips":[{"t":"Drain the vegetables very well and let them steam dry in the hot pot. Trinxat made from wet mash steams in the pan instead of forming a crust.","s":3},{"t":"Fry the hash in the reserved bacon fat on medium heat without moving it; the crust forms in 6-8 minutes and the cake releases when ready.","s":8},{"t":"Flip using a plate over the pan and slide the hash back, adding more fat if it sticks.","s":10}],"rl":"VBPXGZZ"},"53417":{"lv":"EREER","w":{"1":"Rubbed on the toast for a mild garlic perfume; skip and the bread still tastes of tomato.","4":"Brings out the tomato; add a pinch."},"tips":[{"t":"Use very ripe, juicy tomatoes and rub the cut face hard until only the skin is left; the toasted bread acts like a grater.","s":2},{"t":"Rub garlic on lightly while the toast is still hot; the rough surface releases the oils, and too much will overpower.","s":1},{"t":"Choose a rustic bread with open holes that soaks up the tomato and oil; sandwich loaves turn soggy.","s":0}],"rl":"BXVFZ"},"53428":{"lv":"EERERER","w":{"2":"Fresh onion flavor in the sauce; scallion is mild.","4":"Seasons the sauce; adjust to taste.","5":"Vinegar is the sauce's acid; lemon juice makes a milder version.","6":"Loosens the paste so it brushes on easily."},"sub":{"5":{"x":"4 tbsp lemon juice","d":"Softer and fruitier; still gives the tangy kick the shrimp needs.","k":["lemon juice"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Set aside a portion of the sauce for serving before you brush the raw shrimp; do not reuse sauce that touched raw shellfish unless you boil it first.","s":1},{"t":"Soak wooden skewers for 30 minutes and thread the shrimp in a C shape so they cook evenly.","s":1},{"t":"Shrimp need just 2-3 minutes a side; pull them as soon as they turn opaque and curl loosely, since tight curls mean overcooked.","s":2}],"rl":"PXXXZAM"},"53429":{"lv":"EEER","w":{"3":"A small amount seasons the sugar coating; without it the peanuts taste only sweet."},"tips":[{"t":"When the water boils off, stir constantly; the syrup crystallizes into a sandy coating on the nuts, which is what you want.","s":0},{"t":"Break up clumps while they are warm and bake in a single layer to dry the coating; they crisp up as they cool.","s":2},{"t":"Cool completely before storing in an airtight tin or the coating draws moisture and turns sticky.","s":2}],"rl":"PWMZ"},"53430":{"lv":"EEERRRRO","w":{"1":"Callaloo is a leafy green; spinach is the normal stand-in.","3":"Aromatic base; skipping flattens the dish.","4":"Sweetness and body under the veg; skip if you like.","5":"Fat for sauteing; oil works too.","6":"Seasons the vegetables; taste at the end.","7":"Minor heat."},"sub":{"1":{"x":"8 oz fresh spinach","d":"Milder and a bit less earthy; add it at the end so it keeps its green.","k":["spinach"]},"5":{"x":"3 tbsp vegetable oil","d":"Less rich but works; coconut oil is a good local alternative.","k":["vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Drain the cooked vegetables very thoroughly, pressing on them in the colander; this dish is meant to be a thick mash, not soup.","s":5},{"t":"Mash roughly and leave some texture; a potato masher beats a blender here.","s":6},{"t":"Salt only after draining, as seasoning in the water is lost.","s":7}],"rl":"VVVXXFZZ"},"53432":{"lv":"ERRERRREEREEEEO","w":{"1":"Fresh herb note in the mojo; fine to reduce.","2":"Green freshness.","4":"Cool note against allspice and chili.","5":"Heat and color; use fewer for a milder pork.","6":"Fresh heat; skip it to keep it milder.","9":"Extra citrus and mild bitterness; more orange works.","14":"Minor heat."},"sub":{"9":{"x":"3/4 cup orange juice + 1 tbsp lemon juice","d":"Sweeter and less bitter than grapefruit; the lemon restores sourness.","k":["orange juice","lemon juice"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Marinate at least 12 hours in the refrigerator; the citrus and salt penetrate the fat and meat. Discard the leftover marinade or boil it for 5 minutes before serving.","s":0},{"t":"Pork is safe at 145F (63C), but belly is only tender at about 195F (90C); cook until a knife slides in with no resistance.","s":1},{"t":"For crisp skin, pat completely dry and score before the final blast; moist skin steams instead of puffing.","s":2}],"rl":"FXXXXXXAAAXXXZZ"},"53433":{"lv":"ERRRERO","w":{"1":"Mild onion crunch.","2":"The heat in the chow; wear gloves and use less for a milder dish. Habanero or jalapeno work.","3":"Fresh herb lift.","5":"Salt pulls juice from the mango and makes the chow.","6":"Barely noticeable."},"sub":{"2":{"x":"1 jalapeno (seeded) + a pinch of cayenne","d":"Less fruity and milder than scotch bonnet; heat is adjustable.","k":["jalapeno","cayenne"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Use a firm, barely ripe mango so it keeps a crisp bite; fully ripe fruit turns to mush.","s":0},{"t":"Toss with salt and lime and leave 10-15 minutes; the juices release and form a sharp, spicy dressing.","s":0},{"t":"Seed the scotch bonnet and wear gloves; the seeds and white pith carry the most heat.","s":0}],"rl":"VXXXAZZ"},"53431":{"lv":"EEEERRORRRRO","w":{"4":"Fresh heat; use less for mild.","5":"Seasons the corn; sweet corn needs a pinch.","6":"Minor.","7":"Browns the coconut and adds richness.","8":"Toasted crunch topping; skip and you lose texture.","9":"Mild sweet onion flavor in the coconut mixture.","10":"Fresh herb finish.","11":"Second herb; one is enough.","0":"The liquid of the soup; without it there is no soup"},"sub":{"0":{"x":"1 liter water + 1 tsp salt","d":"Less savory; the charring and coconut sauce carry most of the flavor.","k":["salt"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Turn off the heat after 5 minutes and let the corn sit in the stock; it stays plump and juicy instead of turning starchy.","s":0},{"t":"Char the corn in a dry, very hot pan and leave it undisturbed for a couple of minutes per side to blister.","s":1},{"t":"Toast the coconut over medium heat, keep it moving, and take it off the moment it turns golden; it burns in seconds.","s":2}],"rl":"MVMAXZZFGXGG"},"53133":{"lv":"EERE","w":{"2":"Morcilla (blood sausage) is traditional; skip if you cannot find it."},"tips":[{"t":"Build a bed of coals on one side so you have a hot zone and a cool zone; cook the thick cuts slowly on the cool side and sear at the end.","s":2},{"t":"Salt with coarse salt just before grilling; fresh pork chorizo must reach 160F (71C), while morcilla is already cooked and only needs warming through.","s":1},{"t":"Do not pierce the sausages and do not press the meat; juices stay in. Rest the beef about 10 minutes before slicing.","s":4}],"rl":"PPPZ"},"53134":{"lv":"EEEEEERRERRO","w":{"6":"Chopped hard-boiled egg is traditional filling; omit if you want a simpler filling.","7":"Briny bite typical of Argentine empanadas.","9":"Adds mild sweetness and color.","10":"Seasons the filling.","11":"Minor."},"tips":[{"t":"Cool the filling completely before filling the dough; a hot filling melts the butter and the empanadas leak and turn greasy.","s":1},{"t":"Cook the beef to 160F (71C), and keep the filling moist but not wet; liquid will burst the seams.","s":1},{"t":"Seal firmly, press with a fork or fold into a rope edge, and bake on a hot tray so the bottoms crisp.","s":2}],"rl":"SFMZPVPXXXZZ"},"53140":{"lv":"EEERRRO","w":{"3":"Gives the pizza-like aroma.","4":"Briny bite typical of pizza toppings.","5":"Seasons the steak.","6":"Minor."},"tips":[{"t":"Use a thick, gently cooked tomato sauce so it does not slide off; spread it thin.","s":1},{"t":"Close the grill lid or cover with foil so the cheese melts while the steak finishes without burning on the other side.","s":2},{"t":"Rest, then slice across the grain; flank is lean and chewy if cut with the grain.","s":3}],"rl":"PXGXXZZ"},"53141":{"lv":"EEREEEERO","w":{"2":"Adds sweetness and body; replace with extra pumpkin.","7":"Seasons the stew.","8":"Minor."},"tips":[{"t":"Dry the beef and brown in batches; crowding steams the meat and you lose the fond that flavors the broth.","s":0},{"t":"Keep the stew at a gentle simmer; boiling toughens the beef.","s":2},{"t":"Press a few pieces of pumpkin and potato against the pot to thicken the broth, and serve with the sweet apricots as a counterpoint to the beef.","s":2}],"rl":"PVVBVWMZZ"},"53146":{"lv":"EEEREERRRRO","w":{"3":"Adds body and starch; the stew is still hearty without it.","6":"Spicy fat that flavors the pot.","7":"Aromatic base.","8":"Warm color and flavor.","9":"Seasons a large pot; add gradually.","10":"Minor."},"tips":[{"t":"Soak the corn and beans overnight, then simmer gently; hard corn needs the full 2-3 hours and will not soften if rushed.","s":0},{"t":"Stir the bottom often because the starches settle and scorch; mash a few pumpkin pieces into the pot to thicken it.","s":2},{"t":"Finish with a quick sauce of scallions fried in oil with paprika, as Argentines do, spooned over each bowl.","s":3}],"rl":"BPVBPPPVXZZ"},"53135":{"lv":"EEERRRO","w":{"3":"Fresh note in the crust.","4":"Savory crust flavor.","5":"Seasons the cutlet.","6":"Minor."},"tips":[{"t":"Pound the cutlets to an even thickness of about 6 mm so they cook through before the crust darkens.","s":0},{"t":"Pat the beef dry before the egg and press the crumbs on firmly; rest the breaded cutlets 10 minutes so the coating sticks.","s":1},{"t":"Fry in shallow oil at about 175C and drain on a rack, not paper towels, to keep the crust crisp.","s":2}],"rl":"PTBXXZZ"},"53136":{"lv":"EEE","w":{"1":"Crusty roll holds the juicy sausage and sauce; baguette works fine."},"sub":{"1":{"x":"1 baguette cut into four 6-inch lengths","d":"Crustier and chewier; split and toast just the same.","k":["baguette"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Cook fresh pork chorizo slowly over medium heat so the casing does not burst, until it reaches 160F (71C).","s":0},{"t":"Butterfly the sausage after cooking and toast the rolls on the grill so they soak up the drippings.","s":1},{"t":"Make the chimichurri ahead; an hour of resting lets the garlic, vinegar and herbs meld.","s":2}],"rl":"PBX"},"53139":{"lv":"EEERO","w":{"3":"Seasons the plain chickpea batter; it tastes flat without enough salt.","4":"Traditional dusting on top; fine to skip."},"tips":[{"t":"Rest the batter at least 2 hours, ideally longer; the chickpea flour hydrates, loses its raw taste and bakes up creamy rather than grainy. Skim off any foam before baking.","s":0},{"t":"Heat the oil in the pan in the oven until shimmering before pouring in the batter; it should sizzle on contact so the bottom and edges turn crisp.","s":1},{"t":"Aim for a layer about 1 cm thick; a thicker pour stays custardy. Finish under the broiler for a deeper golden top.","s":1}],"rl":"SMFZZ"},"53137":{"lv":"EEER","w":{"3":"Rounds out the caramel; the dulce de leche still works without it.","2":"The method calls for baking soda, not baking powder; use 1/4 tsp baking soda to neutralize the milk's acidity so it does not curdle and to brown the caramel."},"tips":[{"t":"The ingredient list says baking powder but the method uses baking soda; use 1/4 tsp baking soda. It neutralizes the milk acidity so the milk does not curdle and helps the caramel color.","s":0},{"t":"Use a wide, heavy pot at least three times the volume of the milk, since it foams up at first. Keep the heat low and stir the bottom, not just the surface.","s":1},{"t":"It is done when you drag a spoon across the pot and the bottom stays visible for a second or two; it thickens a lot as it cools. If it is grainy, blend it while warm.","s":1}],"rl":"MWXX"},"53138":{"lv":"EEEEEREO","w":{"5":"Brightens the buttery dough; skip and cookies are just plainer.","7":"Traditional coating on the filled edge; not needed."},"tips":[{"t":"Do not overwork the dough once the flours go in; mix just until it comes together, or the cookies are tough rather than sandy and melting.","s":0},{"t":"Roll about 5 mm thick and bake until barely colored. Browned alfajores lose the pale, tender look and turn dry.","s":1},{"t":"Use a thick dulce de leche, called repostero, so it does not squeeze out, and let filled cookies rest a day to soften.","s":2}],"rl":"SSFWSXWG"},"53437":{"lv":"EEERRRRERR","w":{"3":"Fresh herbs perfume the marinade; skipping is fine, but use a bit more mint.","4":"Mild sweetness and moisture for the marinade.","5":"Gives gentle heat; adjust to taste.","6":"The acid and sweetness that tenderizes and flavors the lamb.","8":"Second herb note with a cooling flavor.","9":"Seasons deep into the meat over two days."},"sub":{"6":{"x":"1 cup red wine vinegar + 2 tbsp brown sugar","d":"Sharper and less syrupy; the sugar approximates the balsamic sweetness.","k":["red wine vinegar","brown sugar"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Keep the lamb refrigerated in the marinade and turn it twice a day; raw lamb should not sit at room temperature. Bring it to room temperature for an hour before roasting.","s":0},{"t":"Use a thermometer: 145F (63C) is the safe minimum for lamb with a 3-minute rest; the recipe's not red when sliced is about 160F (71C), which is well done.","s":1},{"t":"Rest the roast 15 minutes and carve against the grain. Discard the leftover marinade, or boil it 5 minutes before using as a sauce.","s":1}],"rl":"PXXXXZAFXZ"},"53434":{"lv":"EEEEEEEER","w":{"8":"A glaze of beaten egg with black seeds for shine and color; skip for a matte surface."},"tips":[{"t":"Handle the dough as little as possible; knead just a few times so the baking powder crumb stays light and the koulenje are not tough.","s":2},{"t":"Mahleb has a cherry-almond fragrance that fades fast, so use freshly ground; stale mahleb tastes dusty and flat.","s":1},{"t":"Brush the egg glaze carefully, avoiding the cut edges so the pieces rise evenly, and bake on parchment at high heat until just golden.","s":3}],"rl":"SSLFZMWXG"},"53435":{"lv":"EEEERRERRRRRO","w":{"1":"Adds savory depth; water plus extra salt works.","4":"Warm, sweet background spice typical of Armenian rice.","5":"Warm spice; use only a little.","7":"Bursts of sweetness against the savory rice.","8":"Fresh herb aroma.","9":"Rich crunch; swap almonds.","10":"Acid to brighten the rice.","11":"Seasons the rice; adjust to taste.","12":"Minor."},"sub":{"1":{"x":"2 cups vegetable broth or water + 1/2 tsp salt","d":"Lighter flavor; broth keeps more savory depth.","k":["vegetable broth"]},"9":{"x":"1/2 cup slivered almonds, toasted","d":"Slightly sweeter and less resinous.","k":["almonds"],"wn":"Contains tree nuts (almonds)."}},"tips":[{"t":"Rinse the rice until the water is clear; excess starch makes the pilaf gummy.","s":0},{"t":"Do not stir once it simmers, and rest it covered for 10 minutes off the heat so the grains finish steaming and separate.","s":4},{"t":"If stuffing grape leaves with it, cook the rice only 10 minutes first; it finishes cooking inside the rolls.","s":5}],"rl":"BMMFXXXXXGAZZ"},"53436":{"lv":"EERRRE","w":{"2":"Pungent garlic; crush with the salt for a smooth paste.","3":"Seasons the yogurt; taste after chilling.","4":"Cooling, slightly sweet herb note typical of Armenian yogurt soup."},"tips":[{"t":"Use full-fat plain yogurt, such as matsun, for a creamy body; low-fat versions taste thin.","s":0},{"t":"Dice the cucumber small and stir it in by hand; blending it releases watery juice that dilutes the yogurt.","s":1},{"t":"Chill for a few hours so the dried mint blooms, and add ice cubes only at serving.","s":2}],"rl":"MMXZXV"},"53438":{"lv":"EEEE","w":{"2":"Binds the cheese so the balls hold their shape in hot oil."},"sub":{},"tips":[{"t":"The mixture must be stiff enough to hold a ball; if it is wet, add cornstarch a tablespoon at a time. Chill or freeze the balls 30 minutes before frying so they do not burst.","s":6},{"t":"Fry at about 350F (175C) and in small batches; cold oil makes the cheese melt out.","s":6},{"t":"Make them small, no bigger than a walnut, so the cheese melts through before the crust over-browns.","s":6}],"rl":"PPTT"},"53443":{"lv":"REEREORRERR","w":{"0":"Aromatic base for the marinade.","3":"Balances the lemon and salty soy and helps caramelize.","5":"Soy is already salty; add only if needed.","6":"Warm citrusy spice; ground ginger for pork.","9":"Seasoned oil for rolling raw skewers; it touches raw meat, so discard it afterwards and never serve it as a dip unless boiled.","10":"Seasons the basting oil.","7":"Base of the marinade; the meat still absorbs the spices without it"},"sub":{"7":{"x":"1 cup canola oil","d":"Neutral and works in the marinade.","k":["canola oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Marinate only for a few hours in the refrigerator; the soy and lemon turn the surface of the meat salty and mushy if left overnight.","s":8},{"t":"Pat the meat dry and keep the cubes uniform so they brown rather than steam; cook beef to 145F (63C) or pork to 145F with a rest.","s":9},{"t":"Do not reuse oil that touched raw meat unless boiled; keep a separate batch for dipping.","s":14}],"rl":"XXAWXZXFPFX"},"53440":{"lv":"EERRRRROOOE","w":{"2":"Fresh sauce body for the fish balls.","3":"Sweet vegetable note.","4":"Aromatic base.","5":"Garlic accent.","6":"Gives heat; use more or less.","7":"A dash adds warmth.","8":"Salt cod is already salty; taste first.","9":"Minor."},"tips":[{"t":"Soak the salt cod for 24 hours and change the water several times; taste a flake before cooking, and it should be pleasantly salty, not harsh.","s":2},{"t":"Simmer the cod gently so it stays moist, then flake and mash until it holds together.","s":3},{"t":"Chill the mixture 30 minutes before shaping; if it is soft, add mashed potato or breadcrumbs.","s":12}],"rl":"PBVVXXXXZZT"},"53439":{"lv":"REE","w":{"0":"Seasons the dough; plain arepas taste flat.","1":"This must be precooked cornmeal (masarepa such as Harina PAN); true cornstarch will not make an arepa dough."},"tips":[{"t":"Use precooked cornmeal (masarepa, such as Harina PAN) for arepas; regular cornmeal or true cornstarch will not make a dough.","s":0},{"t":"Add the water slowly and knead; the dough should feel smooth like soft clay with no cracking at the edges, and wetting your hands fixes cracks.","s":0},{"t":"Cook on a griddle until a crust forms, then finish in the oven; they are done when they sound hollow when tapped.","s":2}],"rl":"ZBM"},"53442":{"lv":"EEREEORRE","w":{"2":"A little butter makes the dough more tender.","5":"Only a small amount for browning.","6":"Seasons the dough.","7":"Softens and flavors the dough.","8":"The oil for frying the cheese sticks; without it they cannot be fried"},"sub":{"8":{"x":"canola or sunflower oil for deep frying","d":"Neutral, high smoke point.","k":["canola oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Use low-moisture mozzarella sticks, not fresh mozzarella, and freeze the strips for 30 minutes so they hold their shape inside the dough.","s":0},{"t":"Pinch the seams completely closed; any gap lets the melted cheese leak out in the fryer.","s":2},{"t":"Fry at 350F (175C) in small batches; if the oil is too cool the dough soaks up oil and leaks cheese.","s":3}],"rl":"SMFTPWZFF"},"53441":{"lv":"EREEE","w":{"1":"Spices the syrup; plainer without it.","2":"Three tablespoons is a lot of flavor; start with one."},"tips":[{"t":"Choose firm, just-ripe bananas; overripe ones fall apart in the syrup.","s":0},{"t":"Simmer gently, not hard, and then reduce the syrup into a glaze before baking.","s":0},{"t":"Baking at 275F (135C) dries the glaze onto the bananas; serve them warm so the ice cream melts into the syrup.","s":1}],"rl":"VXXWM"},"53105":{"lv":"REEOREROROO","w":{"0":"Sharp garlic base for the paste.","3":"Adds only a hint of heat.","4":"Carries the spices; any oil.","6":"Burns on the grill and smokes the chicken.","7":"Second bunch adds more smoke; one is enough.","8":"Herb smoke and fragrance.","9":"Second bunch; unnecessary.","10":"Adds a faint background note."},"sub":{"4":{"x":"3 tbsp vegetable oil","d":"Neutral; olive oil gives a fruity flavor.","k":["vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Put the herb bunches on the grill grate and lay the chicken on top, away from direct flame; the smoldering herbs perfume the meat.","s":3},{"t":"Dark meat is safe at 165F (74C) but tastes best at 175-185F (80-85C), when the collagen has softened.","s":4},{"t":"Keep the marinade away from cooked chicken; do not use leftover marinade as a sauce unless boiled.","s":2}],"rl":"XAXXFPXXXXX"},"53110":{"lv":"EEEROR","w":{"3":"Helps the glaze brown and keeps the sugar from sticking.","4":"A small amount adds savory depth.","5":"Adds tang to balance the honey."},"tips":[{"t":"Line the tray with foil or parchment; the honey and soy caramelize and stick hard to bare metal.","s":2},{"t":"Slash the drumsticks deeply so the marinade gets in and the meat cooks evenly; they are done at 175F (80C) near the bone.","s":0},{"t":"Turn and baste every 10 minutes, and for extra stickiness spoon the pan juices over at the end.","s":2}],"rl":"PXWFXX"},"53102":{"lv":"EERRREEEE","w":{"2":"Fresh herb note.","3":"Gives a gentle heat.","4":"Raw garlic is sharp; mince fine."},"tips":[{"t":"Squid is either cooked 30-60 seconds in a very hot pan or simmered for 30 minutes; anything in between is rubbery. Pat it dry and do not crowd the pan.","s":2},{"t":"Steam the charred peppers covered in a bowl so the skins slide off, and keep the juices for the dressing.","s":0},{"t":"Use dry-cured Spanish chorizo, not fresh; dress while warm so the chickpeas absorb the lemon and oil.","s":4}],"rl":"VBXXXFPPA"},"53103":{"lv":"EEROOREEE","w":{"2":"Earthy warmth.","3":"Just a pinch of color.","4":"Slight heat.","5":"Garlic adds sharpness to the dressing."},"tips":[{"t":"Reserve half the dressing for serving in a separate bowl; do not spoon back what touched raw fish.","s":0},{"t":"The fish is done when the flesh at the thickest part is opaque and flakes at the bone and reaches 145F (63C).","s":1},{"t":"Slash the skin deeply; the cuts let the dressing in and help the thick fish cook evenly.","s":0}],"rl":"PXXXXXAFX"},"53107":{"lv":"ERERERRRR","w":{"1":"Yogurt makes the dip lighter and creamy; sour cream is the closest swap.","3":"Extra acid to keep the avocado bright; omit if limes are juicy.","5":"Roasting fat; any neutral oil works.","6":"Heat on the potatoes; reduce or skip for a mild dish.","7":"Warm earthy spice on the potatoes.","8":"For crispy tortilla dippers; skip if you just want potatoes."},"sub":{"1":{"x":"3/4 cup (200 g) sour cream","d":"Richer and a little tangier; thin with a spoon of water if too thick.","k":["sour cream"]},"5":{"x":"2 tbsp vegetable oil","d":"Neutral flavor; olive oil adds a fruity note.","k":["vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"The lime juice and zest in the dip stop the avocado browning; press cling film directly onto the surface if you make it ahead.","s":0},{"t":"After boiling, shake the drained potatoes in the pan to roughen their surface; rough edges roast up extra crisp.","s":1},{"t":"Roast the potatoes on a hot tray in a single layer and do not crowd, so they brown instead of steaming.","s":2}],"rl":"VFAABFXXG"},"53108":{"lv":"EEEOOR","w":{"1":"Used to brush and sear; any neutral oil.","3":"A dash adds nutty aroma only.","4":"Fresh garnish only.","5":"Sweet dipping sauce to complement the salty squid."},"sub":{"1":{"x":"3 tbsp sunflower or canola oil","d":"Neutral and heats the same.","k":["canola oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Dry the squid completely and score it in a criss-cross so it curls and cooks in under 2 minutes.","s":0},{"t":"Salt just before it hits the pan; salting early draws out water and steams the squid.","s":2},{"t":"Get the pan or grill very hot and cook for 1-2 minutes in total; longer turns squid rubbery.","s":1}],"rl":"PFXXGG"},"53099":{"lv":"ERERR","w":{"1":"An Australian burger staple that adds sweet earthiness and moisture.","3":"Peppery freshness; any salad leaf works.","4":"Cooling creamy topping.","2":"The bread of the burger; without it there is no burger"},"sub":{"2":{"x":"4 hamburger buns, toasted","d":"Softer and less chewy; naan is the Australian twist.","k":["hamburger buns"],"wn":"Some buns contain sesame; check the label"}},"tips":[{"t":"Mix the beef with wet hands and as little as possible; overworked beef gets dense. Press a dimple in each patty so it stays flat.","s":0},{"t":"Cook the burgers to an internal temperature of 160F (71C) because they are ground beef.","s":2},{"t":"Toast the naan on the griddle after the burgers have started, so both finish together and the bread soaks up some juices.","s":2}],"rl":"PVBVG"},"53106":{"lv":"ERRREREERR","w":{"1":"Vinegar is the tang in the dressing; lemon juice works.","2":"Sweet juicy accent.","3":"Roasting fat; any oil works.","5":"Rounds out the mustard and vinegar.","8":"Binds and sharpens the dressing.","9":"Peppery leaf that wilts slightly under the warm vegetables."},"sub":{"1":{"x":"2 tbsp lemon juice or white wine vinegar","d":"Brighter and fruitier.","k":["lemon juice"]},"3":{"x":"1 tbsp vegetable oil","d":"Fine for roasting.","k":["vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Roast the asparagus in a single layer on a hot tray; thin spears take 8-10 minutes and thick ones about 15.","s":0},{"t":"Dress the potatoes while they are warm so they absorb the vinaigrette.","s":2},{"t":"Toss the rocket with the dressing at the last moment so it stays crisp.","s":1}],"rl":"VAVFPWBFTV"},"53109":{"lv":"ERREEEEEORERO","w":{"1":"Cooking fat for the onion; any oil.","2":"Sweetness and body in the chilli.","8":"A pinch adds a warm, rich note.","9":"Adds body and absorbs the sauce. Use canned or fully cooked beans only; raw or undercooked dried kidney beans are toxic.","11":"Makes the mash rich.","12":"Garnish for mild onion."},"sub":{"1":{"x":"1 tbsp olive oil","d":"Slightly fruity.","k":["olive oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Reduce the chilli until it is thick; a wet filling soaks the pastry from the inside and bursts the pies.","s":0},{"t":"Cook the beef to 160F (71C), and cool the chilli before filling so it does not melt the pastry.","s":0},{"t":"Prick the pastry bases, and cut circles about 2 cm wider than the muffin cups; press them in without stretching.","s":1}],"rl":"BFXXXPXMXVBFG"},"53100":{"lv":"EEEEERR","w":{"5":"Lemon zest lifts the buttery almond cake.","6":"Fruit pops and adds juiciness; the friands are still good plain."},"tips":[{"t":"Whisk the egg whites only to a loose, floppy foam. Stiff peaks make the cakes dry and dense.","s":2},{"t":"Use ground almonds as fresh as possible and mix gently by hand so the batter stays moist.","s":1},{"t":"Fill the tins three-quarters full and bake until they spring back; cool 5 minutes before unmolding, otherwise they stick.","s":3}],"rl":"FWSSLXV"},"53101":{"lv":"EEEEEEREREE","w":{"3":"Self-rising flour has leavener built in.","6":"Loosens the batter for a moist crumb.","8":"Makes the icing glossy.","10":"Coconut coating gives the squares their name."},"sub":{"3":{"x":"140 g (1 cup + 1 tbsp) all-purpose flour + 2 tsp baking powder + 1/4 tsp salt","d":"Same rise as long as the baking powder is fresh.","k":["all-purpose flour","baking powder","salt"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Fold the flour in gently with a metal spoon; overmixing knocks out air and makes the sponge tough.","s":1},{"t":"Chill or freeze the squares for 20 minutes before dipping; a firm cake does not crumble in the icing.","s":3},{"t":"If the icing is too thick to dip, warm it gently; if too thin, beat in more icing sugar.","s":2}],"rl":"FWSSLXMXFWG"},"53104":{"lv":"EEEEERROREEEEE","w":{"5":"Seasons the sponge so it is not flat.","6":"Optional cream filling for a richer cake.","7":"Sweetens the cream.","8":"Sweet-tart filling between the layers.","3":"The flour and leavening of the sponge; without it the cake has no structure"},"sub":{"3":{"x":"250 g (2 cups) all-purpose flour + 1 tbsp baking powder","d":"Same rise; sift together well.","k":["all-purpose flour","baking powder"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Make the sponge a day ahead, or freeze it; day-old cake is firmer and does not crumble when dipped.","s":2},{"t":"Dip with two forks in thin icing and keep the bowl over warm water so the icing stays fluid.","s":5},{"t":"Roll in coconut right away while the icing is wet; use one bowl of coconut per few cakes to avoid clumps.","s":4}],"rl":"FWSSMZFWWGFMXW"},"53111":{"lv":"EEEEEEE","w":{"5":"Golden syrup gives the chew and caramel flavor; honey is the closest swap."},"sub":{"5":{"x":"1 tbsp honey","d":"Slightly floral; the cookies spread a little more.","k":["honey"],"wn":"Honey is not safe for infants under 12 months"}},"tips":[{"t":"Add the baking soda to the warm butter-syrup mixture; the foam is the reaction that gives the crisp, chewy texture.","s":0},{"t":"Space the biscuits well because they spread; bake 8-10 minutes for chewy, longer for crisp.","s":2},{"t":"Cool on the tray for a few minutes before moving them; they are soft when hot.","s":2}],"rl":"BBSWFWL"},"53444":{"lv":"EEEERRREROORO","w":{"4":"Sweet mild onion flavor.","5":"Sauteing fat for the shallots and ham.","6":"Fresh herb for the mixture.","8":"Seasons the dough.","9":"Minor.","10":"A pinch brings a classic Austrian aroma.","11":"Brown butter is the traditional finish.","12":"Garnish."},"tips":[{"t":"Use stale, dry bread cubes, and let the mixture rest 15 minutes so the bread absorbs the milk.","s":1},{"t":"Cook one test dumpling first; if it falls apart, add a little flour or breadcrumbs.","s":2},{"t":"Simmer gently, since a hard boil breaks the dumplings, and drain them well.","s":3}],"rl":"BMPTXFXTZZXGG"},"53447":{"lv":"ERRRORERRE","w":{"1":"Sweet root vegetables that flavor the broth.","2":"Earthy celery flavor typical of Tafelspitz broth.","3":"Adds sweetness.","4":"Garnish.","5":"Mild onion note.","7":"Subtle herb.","8":"Light spice."},"tips":[{"t":"Char the onion halves dry in a pan; the browned surface gives the broth its golden color.","s":0},{"t":"Keep the water at a bare simmer, never a boil, and skim the foam for a clear broth. The beef is tender when a skewer slides in without resistance.","s":1},{"t":"Salt only after two hours, and slice the meat against the grain, serving it in a little broth to keep it moist.","s":2}],"rl":"PVVVGXXXXZ"},"53445":{"lv":"EEEROREERE","w":{"3":"Seasons the bread mixture.","4":"Garnish.","5":"Moisture and binder.","8":"Golden fried onion for sweetness.","9":"Frying fat; clarified butter or oil works."},"sub":{"9":{"x":"2 tbsp clarified butter or vegetable oil","d":"Less buttery flavor but crisp.","k":["vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Use a strong, flavorful mountain cheese, such as Bergkaese or sour cheese; mild cheese gives bland dumplings.","s":1},{"t":"If the dough is too wet to hold, add a little flour or breadcrumbs, and chill 15 minutes.","s":3},{"t":"Fry in butter on medium heat so they brown without burning, about 3-4 minutes a side.","s":3}],"rl":"BXTZGBTMXF"},"53446":{"lv":"EEERROEO","w":{"3":"Seasons the dumplings.","4":"A pinch gives the classic aroma.","5":"Oils the board so shaped dumplings do not stick.","7":"Garnish."},"tips":[{"t":"Rest the paste 15-20 minutes so the semolina swells; skipping this gives dumplings that fall apart.","s":1},{"t":"Cook one test dumpling first and adjust the paste; simmer gently with the lid on.","s":3},{"t":"Cook the dumplings in salted water, not in the broth, which keeps the soup clear; add them to the hot soup just before serving.","s":4}],"rl":"BFTZXFMG"},"53449":{"lv":"EEERORORRERRRR","w":{"3":"Seasons the filling.","4":"Minor.","5":"Gives gold color and earthy flavor.","6":"Faint sweet warmth.","7":"Fresh herb note.","8":"Dill is the typical Azerbaijani herb.","10":"Adds substance to the filling.","11":"Adds richness and juiciness; a little butter works.","12":"Gives the tomato sauce its color.","13":"Thins the tomato sauce."},"sub":{"11":{"x":"3 tbsp melted butter","d":"Less gamey but still juicy.","k":["butter"],"wn":"Contains dairy"}},"tips":[{"t":"Core the cabbage and blanch it in boiling water, peeling leaves as they soften; shave down the thick spine so the leaves roll tight.","s":10},{"t":"Line the pot with spare cabbage leaves so the dolma do not scorch, and weigh them down with a plate to keep them from unrolling.","s":22},{"t":"Lamb filling must reach 160F (71C); simmer gently for the full 25 minutes.","s":24}],"rl":"PVXZZXXXXBVFAM"},"53448":{"lv":"EEEERO","w":{"4":"Gives shine and color.","5":"Traditional topping for the look; skip freely."},"tips":[{"t":"Use water at about 105-110F (40-43C), since hotter water kills the yeast.","s":5},{"t":"Preheat the baking sheet or a stone so the bottom of the bread gets a blast of heat and puffs up.","s":12},{"t":"Cut the crosshatches deep, so the bread does not balloon, and brush with yolk before the seeds so they stick.","s":10}],"rl":"LMZSCG"},"53450":{"lv":"ERE","w":{"1":"Grits are bland until salted in the cooking water; 1 tsp is generous for 1/4 cup, so start with 1/2 tsp if you plan to add butter or cheese."},"tips":[{"t":"Pour the grits into the boiling water in a thin stream while whisking, then drop to low. Dumping them in at once makes lumps that never fully hydrate.","s":0},{"t":"Grits keep thickening as they cool. If they set up too firm, whisk in a splash of hot water or milk (not cold) to loosen them, and finish with a pat of butter for gloss.","s":1}],"rl":"BZM"},"53453":{"lv":"EEEEREREREEO","w":{"4":"Adds fiery heat and red color; skip or halve for a mild curry, the other spices carry the flavor.","6":"Warm, aromatic finish; without it the curry tastes flatter but the curry powder still gives a spiced base.","8":"Earthy base note that overlaps with the curry powder; skipping is barely noticeable.","11":"Fresh herb finish added at the end; it brightens the curry but is not essential.","0":"The only fat for cooking the masala; without it the spices scorch and do not bloom"},"sub":{"0":{"x":"2 tbsp vegetable oil or ghee","d":"Ghee gives a nuttier, more traditional flavor; neutral oil is plainer but works equally well for cooking the masala.","k":["vegetable oil","ghee"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Keep stirring the spices for the full 5 minutes until the oil begins to separate at the edges of the masala. Spices that are only warmed through taste raw and dusty.","s":2},{"t":"Chicken breast dries out easily in a long simmer. Cut it into even chunks, add it with the potatoes, and pull the pot as soon as the chicken reaches 165F (74C) and the potatoes are tender.","s":2},{"t":"If the masala sticks to the pan during the spice step, add a splash of water instead of turning up the heat, so the spices do not scorch and turn bitter.","s":2}],"rl":"FXXAXXXXXPBG"},"53451":{"lv":"EEEEEEERRROR","w":{"7":"Earthy, citrusy backbone that rounds out the turmeric; skipping leaves a flatter, more one-note gravy.","8":"Gives the gravy its warmth and red tint; reduce or skip for a mild family version, the green chilies still add heat.","9":"Fresh heat and a green note added at the end; omit if the chili powder is enough for you.","10":"Fresh finishing herb; adds brightness, but the curry is complete without it.","11":"A squeeze at the table lifts the rich oil and cuts any fishy note; lemon works equally well.","2":"The only fat for cooking the curry base; without it the spices scorch"},"sub":{"2":{"x":"4 tbsp mustard oil or any neutral oil such as canola","d":"Mustard oil is the authentic Bengali choice and adds a pungent bite; neutral oil is milder.","k":["mustard oil","vegetable oil"],"wn":"Mustard oil must be heated until it smokes to mellow it; in some regions it is labeled for external use only"}},"tips":[{"t":"Grind the onion to a smooth paste and cook it until the raw smell is gone and it clings to the pan before adding water. This is what gives the gravy its body without any tomato.","s":3},{"t":"Coat the fish in the spiced base and then only shake the pan, never stir. White fish steaks break up fast once the paste has set; thin fillets should go in for just the last 8 minutes.","s":6},{"t":"Cut the daikon into even 2 cm pieces so they turn tender together; they should be soft but still hold shape. The fish is done when it flakes and reaches 145F (63C).","s":1}],"rl":"PVFXXZXXXXGA"},"53452":{"lv":"EEEEOR","w":{"4":"Faint warm sweetness; the recipe lists spices as optional and the molasses already carries most of the flavor.","5":"Gives the cakes a floral, aromatic note typical of Bengali sweets; skip and they taste plainer but still good."},"tips":[{"t":"Rest the batter the full 2 hours and whisk it again before frying; the rice flour sinks to the bottom, and an unwhisked batter fries up gritty and heavy.","s":2},{"t":"Keep the oil at low-medium after it comes up to heat. If it is too hot the outside colors before the center sets, and the frilled lacy edge will not form.","s":3},{"t":"If the batter splits or disperses in the oil, whisk in a couple of tablespoons of flour to stabilise it, and pour from a spouted jug in one steady stream into the center of the pan.","s":5}],"rl":"WMSSXX"},"53455":{"lv":"EERRRROOR","w":{"2":"A little sweetness once cooked; it also tames the metallic taste of liver.","3":"Softens into the base and balances the liver's bitterness; skipping makes it blunter.","4":"Gives gentle heat that sharpens the rich liver and bacon; skip and it tastes flatter.","5":"Liver and bacon need real seasoning and the pate tastes flat without it; adjust after blending, since cold food tastes less salty.","6":"Mild background heat; the mustard already supplies bite.","7":"Milk mellows the liver's bitterness while it braises.","8":"Adds a nutty, sweet-boozy finish that lifts the pate."},"sub":{"7":{"x":"Equal amount of half-and-half or light cream","d":"Richer and slightly smoother; plain water leaves a harsher, more bitter liver flavor.","k":["cream"]},"8":{"x":"1 tbsp brandy, or 1 tbsp chicken broth plus 1/2 tsp red wine vinegar","d":"Brandy is stronger and less sweet; the broth version is alcohol-free but milder.","k":["brandy"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Trim every sinew and any green-tinged patches where the gall touched the liver. One bitter spot flavors the whole batch.","s":0},{"t":"Blend while everything is still hot, and push the puree through a sieve for a silky texture. A cooled puree turns grainy and will not smooth out.","s":4},{"t":"Seal the top with a 3 mm layer of melted (clarified) butter and chill. It keeps air off the surface so the pate does not gray; eat within about 5 days.","s":7}],"rl":"PFXXXZZMX"},"53457":{"lv":"EEORRRROORORE","w":{"2":"Used only to wash the steak and then rinsed off, so it has little effect on the finished dish.","3":"Aromatic backbone of the stew; skipping makes the broth taste more plainly of meat.","4":"Gives the dish its fruity heat; scotch bonnets are the signature, so a milder swap changes the character.","5":"Sweet depth that softens the meats' richness; skip and the broth is meatier but flatter.","6":"Warm spice undertone typical of Bajan pepperpot; skip and it loses some aroma.","7":"Two cloves add a faint warmth; leaving them out is barely noticeable.","8":"A pinch rounds out the heat and salt; negligible if skipped.","9":"Seasons a big pot of meat; a pinch will not be enough for 5 lb of meat, so adjust to taste at the end.","10":"A minor extra herb; the thyme does most of the herbal work.","11":"The main herb in the pot; skipping flattens the aroma but not the basic dish."},"sub":{"4":{"x":"2 habanero peppers, left whole or pierced","d":"Slightly less fruity, similar heat; keep them whole for softer heat, burst them for fire.","k":["habanero"]}},"tips":[{"t":"The 70 minutes listed is too short for trotters and oxtail; plan on 2.5 to 3 hours at a bare simmer, until the meat is yielding and the trotter collagen has turned the broth silky.","s":10},{"t":"Boil the trotters first and discard that water, as the recipe does. It removes scum and strong flavor so the final broth is clean.","s":7},{"t":"Pepperpot is traditionally kept going for days: bring it to a full boil each day and reheat only what you serve. Do not leave it sitting warm on the counter overnight.","s":10}],"rl":"PPAXXXXXWZXXP"},"53459":{"lv":"EEREEORRRRORRRE","w":{"2":"Seasons the roti skins; without it they taste flat next to the spiced filling.","5":"Minor fresh note; thyme and onion carry the herb flavor.","6":"Part of the traditional Bajan seasoning; skipping makes the sauce less herbal.","7":"Adds warm spice and heat; skip and the stew loses much of its character.","8":"The chicken stew needs seasoning, so adjust to taste rather than skipping it.","9":"Caramelized first to give the stew its dark color and mild bitterness (Caribbean browning); skipping gives a paler sauce.","10":"Tiny seasoning; the scotch bonnet and jerk already provide heat.","11":"Melts into the sauce for sweetness and body; skipping makes it thinner and less sweet.","12":"Garlic gives a savory base; skipping dulls the stew.","13":"Added whole for fruity heat without burning spice; skipping makes a mild stew.","1":"The fat that makes the roti dough flaky; without it the dough is tough"},"sub":{"1":{"x":"3 tbsp cold butter or lard","d":"Butter makes a slightly richer, less flaky skin; lard is closest to the traditional dough.","k":["butter","lard"],"wn":"Butter contains dairy"}},"tips":[{"t":"Roll the dough wafer thin and cook each skin dry on a very hot surface for about a minute per side, just colored but not crisp. Stack under a towel so the roti stay soft and bendable for wrapping.","s":13},{"t":"Keep the scotch bonnet whole and lift it out carefully before serving. If it bursts the filling becomes painfully hot; taste the stew first and discard the pepper once it reaches the heat you want.","s":14},{"t":"Chicken breast turns stringy over a long simmer. Cut it into even chunks, keep the heat low, and make sure the pieces reach 165F (74C) without going far beyond it.","s":1}],"rl":"BFZMPXXXZWZXXXB"},"53461":{"lv":"EREEERRORRROR","w":{"1":"Coats the hot macaroni so it does not clump and adds richness before the cheese goes in.","5":"Gives the savory background typical of Bajan macaroni pie; skip and it tastes of cheese alone.","6":"Sharpens the cheese and gives the traditional tang; skipping softens the flavor.","7":"Mild background heat; the cayenne and mustard already add bite.","8":"Cheese-heavy baked dishes need salt, but cheddar is already salty, so adjust to taste.","9":"Stands in for Bajan pepper sauce, adding gentle heat; skip for a mild pie.","10":"Makes a crisp golden crust on top; skipping gives a softer cheesy surface.","11":"Helps the topping brown and adds richness; skip if the cheese on top is generous.","12":"Melts into a browned, bubbly top; skip and the topping relies on the crumbs."},"tips":[{"t":"Stop boiling the macaroni at a slightly firm bite; it will absorb the custard and keep cooking in the oven. Fully cooked pasta turns mushy and the pie will not slice.","s":0},{"t":"Grate the cheese yourself. Pre-shredded cheese has anti-caking starch that makes the custard grainy, and a sharp cheddar gives the real Bajan flavor.","s":3},{"t":"Rest the baked pie for 10 minutes before cutting so the egg custard finishes setting into clean squares. Overbaking curdles the egg and makes the cheese oily.","s":5}],"rl":"BFFSMXXZZXGFG"},"53456":{"lv":"EREREE","w":{"1":"Feeds the yeast and encourages browning; with only 2 tsp, skipping it works but the rolls brown less and rise slightly slower.","3":"Softens the crumb and keeps the crust tender; without it the rolls are drier and chewier."},"tips":[{"t":"Knead a full 10 minutes until the dough passes the windowpane test, stretching thin without tearing. Salt bread has little fat to tenderize it, so gluten development decides the texture.","s":1},{"t":"Place a tray of hot water on the oven's bottom rack while the rolls bake. The steam gives the thin, crackly crust typical of Bajan salt bread.","s":4},{"t":"The recipe's 'tubs' of yeast is unclear; use roughly 4 1/2 tsp (two 1/4 oz packets) of active dry or instant yeast for 6 cups of flour.","s":0}],"rl":"LWZFSM"},"53460":{"lv":"EEEREREREER","w":{"3":"Greases the tin and adds a little tenderness; butter or oil can do the job.","5":"Brightens the sweetness and lifts flavor; skipping makes the bread taste flatter.","7":"Raisins give sweet bursts and the classic fruit-studded look; skip for plain coconut bread.","9":"Gives the dough richness and a lightly caramelized milk flavor.","10":"A fragrant note that pairs with coconut; skip and it tastes plainer but still good."},"sub":{"9":{"x":"1 1/4 cups whole milk","d":"Less rich and less caramelized; if the dough feels loose, add 1 to 2 tbsp flour.","k":["whole milk"]}},"tips":[{"t":"This is a stiff, dense dough, not a batter. Press it firmly into the tins, because it will not level itself and loose packing leaves air pockets.","s":5},{"t":"Brush the loaves with the sugar syrup as soon as they come out of the oven so it soaks into the crust and gives the traditional shiny, sweet top.","s":7},{"t":"If the top browns too quickly during the full hour, tent with foil. Let the loaves cool in the tins before slicing, since the crumb is still setting.","s":8}],"rl":"BFSFLZWVTMX"},"53454":{"lv":"EOOEERERRROROO","w":{"1":"Flavors only the boiling water, which is drained away, so the effect is faint.","2":"A little oil keeps the pot from foaming and adds a hint of richness; skip freely.","5":"Adds savory depth to the butter sauce; skipping makes it plainer.","7":"Fresh herb notes inside the sauce, separate from the garnish; skipping dulls the sauce slightly.","8":"Acid that cuts through the butter; skipping makes the sauce heavy.","9":"Breadfruit is bland and needs seasoning, both in the boiling water and in the sauce.","10":"Minor seasoning; adjust to taste.","11":"Loosens the sauce so it coats the breadfruit; use less for a thicker, richer sauce.","12":"Optional heat; skip or pass hot sauce at the table.","13":"Only a finishing sprinkle; the sauce already contains parsley."},"tips":[{"t":"Cut and peel breadfruit with oiled hands and knife, because the sap is very sticky. Drop the pieces in salted water right away so the flesh does not brown.","s":18},{"t":"Boil it only until a knife slides in; overcooked breadfruit turns mushy and falls apart under the sauce. Drain well so the butter sauce does not get watery.","s":18},{"t":"Brown the onion rings in the butter until golden, then lower the heat before adding tomatoes and garlic so they do not scorch. Spoon the sauce over the slices while hot so it soaks in.","s":19}],"rl":"BXFFVXAXAZZMXG"},"53463":{"lv":"EERREERORORERORREEOO","w":{"2":"Keeps the meatballs moist and adds sweetness; skipping makes them denser and meatier.","3":"Adds savory depth to the meat mix; skip and they taste plainer.","6":"A little mustard in the mix seasons the meat; the sauce supplies plenty more.","7":"Minor freshness inside the meatballs; skip freely.","8":"Meatballs need seasoning in the mix itself, since the sauce cannot reach the center.","9":"Minor extra seasoning; mustard and Worcestershire-like depth from the sauce cover it.","15":"Adds the tang that balances the sweet syrup; skipping makes the sauce cloying.","18":"Subtle background herb; skipping is barely noticeable.","19":"A traditional Belgian touch that adds malty depth; the broth and syrup already provide plenty.","10":"Browns the meatballs; the meat renders fat","12":"Cooks the onions; oil or meat fat covers it","13":"Tiny sweetener; the liege syrup supplies sweetness","14":"Acid to balance the sweet syrup; the sauce is cloying without it","16":"The liege syrup defines the sauce; without it the dish is just meatballs in gravy","17":"The liquid of the sauce; without it the sauce is a paste"},"sub":{"10":{"x":"1 tbsp butter or extra 1 tbsp vegetable oil","d":"Butter browns faster and tastes richer; watch the heat so it does not burn.","k":["butter","vegetable oil"]},"12":{"x":"1 tbsp vegetable oil","d":"Slightly less rich; the onions caramelize the same way.","k":["vegetable oil"]},"13":{"x":"1 tbsp white sugar","d":"Slightly less molasses depth; the syrup supplies the dark flavor anyway.","k":["sugar"]},"14":{"x":"2 tbsp cider vinegar or red wine vinegar","d":"Cider vinegar adds a fruitier edge that suits the syrup; red wine vinegar is a bit sharper.","k":["cider vinegar","red wine vinegar"]},"16":{"x":"3 tbsp maple syrup plus 1 tbsp unsweetened applesauce","d":"Liege syrup is cooked apple and pear; this approximates the fruit and sweetness but is less dark and tangy.","k":["maple syrup","applesauce"]},"17":{"x":"1 cup chicken broth","d":"Lighter and less roasted in flavor; the syrup and onions keep the sauce rich.","k":["chicken broth"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Mix the meat mixture just until combined; overworking it makes dense, rubbery balls. Chill the shaped meatballs for 20 minutes so they hold their shape while browning.","s":2},{"t":"Brown the meatballs without crowding the pan so they sear instead of steam, and keep the browned bits: they are the base of the sauce. Make sure the meat reaches 160F (71C).","s":6},{"t":"Taste the sauce before serving: Belgian sweet-sour sauces need balance. Add syrup if too sharp, vinegar if cloying, and let it reduce until it coats a spoon.","s":21}],"rl":"PPXXTTXXZZFVFWAXWMXM"},"53464":{"lv":"EEREERRRRERRRO","w":{"2":"Gives a slightly sweet, earthy note traditional in hutsepot; extra carrots can replace it.","5":"Sweet aromatic base for the stew; skipping makes it plainer, though the leek helps.","6":"Savory depth in the broth; skip and the stew is milder.","7":"Background herbal note typical of Flemish stews; skipping is subtle.","8":"Adds warm earthy herb flavor; skipping makes the stew taste blander.","10":"Adds tang that cuts the richness of sausage and mash; skipping loses the Flemish flavor.","11":"Used to sweat the onion and add richness; oil works but gives less flavor.","12":"Needs seasoning to bring out the vegetables; adjust after the potatoes break down.","13":"Light background spice; the mustard already adds a bite."},"tips":[{"t":"Use floury potatoes that break down as they cook; they thicken the stew into the characteristic coarse, mashed-style hutsepot. Waxy potatoes stay in neat cubes and leave it watery.","s":4},{"t":"Rinse the leek well between the layers, where sand hides, before adding it with the carrots and turnip. Stir regularly so nothing catches on the bottom as the potatoes release starch.","s":2},{"t":"Brown the sausages separately so they get a crust, and cook them to 160F (71C), since the recipe does not say how. Serve them on top rather than burying them in the stew.","s":5}],"rl":"BVVVPXXXXMXFZZ"},"53468":{"lv":"REERRRERROOR","w":{"0":"Stoemp is a mash of potato and vegetables; the sausages are the traditional topping, but you can serve it without them for a vegetarian dish.","3":"Boiled with the potatoes so it mellows into a sweet garlic undertone; skip for plainer mash.","4":"Adds a fresh herb note to the mash; skipping loses some brightness.","5":"Makes the beer-onion sauce that goes over the sausages; skipping loses that sauce.","9":"A pinch of nutmeg is traditional in Belgian mash; skipping is barely noticeable.","10":"Mild background heat; the beer sauce supplies depth.","11":"Seasons the water and the mash; potatoes and cabbage are bland without it.","7":"Gives malty depth to the sauce; broth still makes it","8":"Loosens the mash; butter and other liquid still do it"},"sub":{"7":{"x":"1 cup chicken or beef broth plus 1 tsp cider vinegar","d":"Lacks the malty bitterness of beer, but the vinegar keeps the sauce tangy.","k":["chicken broth","cider vinegar"]},"8":{"x":"60 ml (1/4 cup) cream, or 1/4 cup of the potato cooking water plus 1 extra tbsp butter","d":"Cream gives a richer mash; the water version is lighter but still smooth.","k":["cream","butter"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Drain the potatoes well and let them steam dry for a minute before mashing. Wet potatoes make a loose mash that cannot carry the cabbage.","s":1},{"t":"Cook the Savoy cabbage down for the full 15 to 20 minutes until it is sweet and soft. Undercooked cabbage tastes sulfurous and stays crunchy in the mash.","s":3},{"t":"Mash coarsely with a fork or masher; a food processor makes gluey potato. Brown the sausages to 160F (71C), and reduce the beer in the pan with the onions until it is syrupy.","s":5}],"rl":"PBVXXXFMMXZZ"},"53467":{"lv":"EEREREERROR","w":{"2":"Traditional garnish that adds richness and protein; the salad works without it.","4":"Gives a sharp raw-onion bite against the bacon and potato; skipping makes it milder.","7":"Emulsifies the dressing and adds tang; without it the vinaigrette separates and is less sharp.","8":"Fresh herb finish that lifts the heavy potatoes and bacon; skipping is subtle.","9":"Light seasoning; adjust to taste.","10":"Potatoes and green beans are bland without salt, so season the water and the finished salad."},"tips":[{"t":"Dress the potatoes while still warm so they absorb the vinaigrette; once cold they only pick up flavor on the surface and the salad tastes flat.","s":3},{"t":"Cook the green beans in the potato water until just tender, then shock them in ice water to lock in color and keep a snap.","s":1},{"t":"Use the rendered bacon fat as part of the dressing: it is the soul of this salad. Cook the bacon until crisp so it stays crunchy and does not turn soggy in the vinaigrette.","s":2}],"rl":"BVGPXFATGZZ"},"53465":{"lv":"EOREERRO","w":{"1":"Optional soak that mellows any muddy flavor in frog legs and keeps them tender; skip if the legs are fresh and mild.","5":"Fresh herb finish that gives the butter a green, clean flavor; skip for pure garlic butter.","6":"Seasons the delicate meat; add at the end so the butter does not over-salt.","7":"A pinch adds mild heat; garlic butter already carries the flavor.","2":"Raises the smoke point for the butter; the frog legs still cook"},"sub":{"2":{"x":"1 tbsp vegetable oil or canola oil","d":"Oil only raises the smoke point so the butter does not burn; neutral oil works the same.","k":["vegetable oil","canola oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Pat the legs very dry after rinsing; any moisture steams the meat instead of crisping it. Cook until the meat is opaque and firm and reaches 165F (74C).","s":1},{"t":"Keep the pan hot but not smoking and add the butter so it foams without browning. Pan frying them for 10 minutes can overcook them, so stop at golden with moist meat at the bone.","s":3},{"t":"Add the second round of garlic butter and parsley only at the very end so the garlic stays sweet; garlic added early burns and turns bitter.","s":4}],"rl":"PMFXFGZZ"},"53466":{"lv":"EEERERRROERRROR","w":{"3":"Adds sweetness and color to the broth; skipping is subtle since the leek and celery carry the vegetable flavor.","5":"Gives classic savory depth in waterzooi; skipping makes the broth slightly flatter.","6":"Thickens the stew slightly and adds body; not essential, since many waterzooi omit it.","7":"Mild savory depth in the poaching liquid; skipping barely changes it.","8":"Adds a piney aroma; skip and the stew is milder and more classically Belgian.","10":"Background herbal note in the broth; skipping is subtle.","11":"Used to soften the vegetables and add silkiness; skip and the stew is slightly less rich.","12":"The traditional liaison with cream gives waterzooi its velvety body; skip and it is thinner, but still good.","13":"Light spice; adjust to taste at the end.","14":"Needed to bring out flavors, but add it gradually and taste at the end."},"tips":[{"t":"Poach the chicken gently, never at a rolling boil, then strain the stock: the clear broth is the base of the dish. The chicken should reach 165F (74C), and legs are best at 175F (80C) for tenderness.","s":0},{"t":"Temper the egg yolk by whisking in a couple of spoonfuls of warm cream before returning it to the pot, then keep the heat very low. If the stew boils after the yolk goes in it will curdle.","s":6},{"t":"Cut the leek, celery and carrot into fine julienne so they soften within minutes and stay silky rather than stringy; remove the herbs before serving so the rosemary does not take over.","s":1}],"rl":"PPMVVVBXXFXFTZZ"},"53462":{"lv":"EEEERER","w":{"4":"A pinch balances the sweetness and brings out the butter flavor; skipping is subtle but the cookies taste flatter.","6":"Mixes with the cinnamon to make the swirl; brown sugar gives moisture and caramel notes, white sugar also works."},"tips":[{"t":"Chill the dough for the full 15 minutes before rolling and again for 30 minutes once it is rolled up. Cold butter keeps the spiral sharp; a warm log smears when sliced.","s":5},{"t":"Roll the log tightly and turn it a quarter turn after each slice so it stays round instead of flattening on one side. Use a sharp thin knife for even rounds.","s":6},{"t":"Pull them when the edges are just golden, around 14 minutes; the cinnamon sugar swirl scorches easily and turns bitter if baked too long.","s":7}],"rl":"SFWSZXW"},"53470":{"lv":"ERREOE","w":{"1":"Sweetens and deepens the cooking liquid, though most of its flavor ends up in the shredded meat; skip and the meat is plainer.","2":"Gives a subtle background herbal note; skipping is barely noticeable.","4":"Light spice; seswaa is traditionally seasoned with little more than salt."},"tips":[{"t":"Choose a cut with fat and connective tissue, such as chuck or brisket on the bone. Lean cuts turn dry and stringy when pounded.","s":0},{"t":"Cook off the remaining liquid until almost nothing is left before pounding, so the meat fries slightly in its own fat. This gives seswaa its rich, concentrated flavor.","s":2},{"t":"Pound with a wooden spoon or potato masher rather than shredding with forks. The traditional texture is coarse and bruised, not fine threads; taste and salt at the end.","s":2}],"rl":"PXXZZM"},"53472":{"lv":"EREEE","w":{"1":"Feeds the yeast and helps it activate in the water; skip and the dough rises a bit slower."},"tips":[{"t":"Dumplings steam above the stew, not in it. Make sure the stew is simmering gently, not boiling hard, and keep the lid tight; peeking releases steam and gives dense dumplings.","s":11},{"t":"Use water at 105 to 110F (40 to 43C) to wake the yeast. Hotter water kills it, and cold water leaves the dumplings heavy.","s":2},{"t":"Give the dough its full hour to double. Test a dumpling by cutting one open: the center should be dry and fluffy, not gummy.","s":6}],"rl":"MWLSZ"},"53471":{"lv":"EEERE","w":{"3":"A touch of sweetness helps browning and softens the salt; skip and the flatbreads brown more slowly.","4":"The liquid of the flatbread dough; without it the dough will not form"},"sub":{"4":{"x":"1 cup water plus 1 tbsp vegetable oil","d":"Slightly less tender and less rich; milk gives a softer crumb and better browning.","k":["water","vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Let the dough rest 15 to 30 minutes; the baking powder and the rest relax the gluten, and the flatbreads roll out soft without springing back.","s":3},{"t":"Cook over medium heat with just a film of oil. If the skillet is too hot, the outside burns before the center cooks through and the bread turns doughy inside.","s":5},{"t":"Stack the cooked flatbreads under a clean towel so the steam keeps them soft and pliable instead of dry.","s":6}],"rl":"SLZWM"},"53473":{"lv":"ERREERR","w":{"1":"Adds acidity and body that tame the curry powder and spinach bitterness; skipping makes the dish drier and sharper.","2":"Sweet savory base for the sauce; skip and the dish tastes thinner.","5":"Spinach and curry need salt to taste lively; season at the end because spinach releases liquid.","6":"Helps the tomatoes break down into a sauce before the spinach goes in; spinach also releases a lot, so use less if you like it dry.","3":"The fat that blooms the curry powder and cooks the spinach; without it the spices scorch"},"sub":{"3":{"x":"2 tbsp butter or ghee","d":"Richer and slightly nuttier; keep heat moderate so the curry powder does not scorch.","k":["butter","ghee"],"wn":"Butter or ghee contains dairy"}},"tips":[{"t":"Toast the curry powder in the oil for only a few seconds. It burns quickly and turns bitter, so add the tomatoes straight after.","s":1},{"t":"Cook the tomatoes down until they collapse into a thick sauce before adding the spinach, otherwise the dish ends up watery.","s":2},{"t":"Wash spinach thoroughly to remove grit, then add it in batches. If excess liquid remains at the end, turn up the heat and cook it off.","s":3}],"rl":"VVXFXZM"},"53469":{"lv":"ERRRREERROERR","w":{"1":"Coats the beef for a better crust and thickens the stew; without it the stew is thinner and browns less.","3":"Adds sweetness and savory body to the base; skipping makes the stew plainer.","4":"Gives a rounded background flavor; skip and the stew tastes less savory.","7":"A umami and tang boost that deepens the beef; skipping makes it milder.","8":"A full tablespoon is a lot with regular broth; use low-sodium broth, or cut to 1 tsp and salt at the end.","9":"Light spice; the stew already has depth from beef and thyme.","11":"Adds sweetness and color; skipping leaves the pumpkin and potatoes as the vegetables.","12":"Adds an earthy, herbal note that suits beef; skipping makes it taste flatter.","2":"Browns the beef; the beef renders fat"},"sub":{"2":{"x":"3 tbsp vegetable oil","d":"Lose some richness and browning flavor from the butter; works fine for browning the beef.","k":["vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Use plain canned pumpkin puree, not pumpkin pie filling, which is sweetened and spiced. Fresh roasted pumpkin works too, but drain it so the stew is not thin.","s":2},{"t":"Brown the beef in small batches so it sears instead of steams, and keep the browned bits in the pot: they give the stew its savory backbone.","s":1},{"t":"Cook the beef for at least 2 hours at a low simmer before adding the potatoes and carrots, which would otherwise turn to mush. Chuck is tender when a fork slides in with no resistance.","s":3}],"rl":"PTFXXMVXZZBVX"},"53474":{"lv":"EERERROE","w":{"2":"Feeds the yeast and gives magwinya a light sweetness; skip and they are plainer and brown less.","4":"Seasons the dough; a small amount is enough, but skipping makes them taste flat.","6":"Dusting for sweetness at the end; skip or use cinnamon sugar, jam or honey instead.","5":"A little fat in the dough; the doughnuts still work","7":"The frying oil for the doughnut bites; without it they cannot be fried"},"sub":{"5":{"x":"1.5 tsp melted butter or any other neutral oil","d":"Butter adds a little flavor; oil keeps the dough neutral. Either softens the crumb.","k":["butter","vegetable oil"]},"7":{"x":"2 cups canola or sunflower oil","d":"Any neutral frying oil with a high smoke point works the same.","k":["canola oil","sunflower oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"The dough should be soft and slightly sticky; too much extra flour makes dense, dry doughnuts. Let it double fully before frying so the inside is light and airy.","s":5},{"t":"Hold the oil at 350F (180C). If they brown in a minute but are raw in the center the oil is too hot; if they are pale and greasy it is too cool. Fry about 2 to 3 minutes total and turn once.","s":7},{"t":"Fry 4 or 5 at a time so the temperature does not drop. Break one open to check there is no gummy center before you fry the whole batch.","s":9}],"rl":"SMWLZFGF"},"53478":{"lv":"EREROR","w":{"1":"Makes the mayonnaise taste of garlic and gives the corn bite; skipping leaves a plain creamy coat.","3":"Salty, nutty topping that sticks to the mayo; skip for a lighter version, or use another hard cheese.","4":"Fresh herb scatter; adds color and aroma but is not essential.","5":"Acid brightens the rich mayo and cheese; skipping makes it heavier."},"tips":[{"t":"Pat the boiled cobs dry before brushing on the mayonnaise; a wet cob makes the coating slide off and steam instead of char.","s":1},{"t":"Grill over high heat and turn often; the mayo scorches quickly, so you want browned marks, not black. Add the cheese while the corn is still hot so it clings.","s":2},{"t":"Boil the corn only 5 minutes and leave it in the water as the recipe says; it finishes cooking on the grill, so longer boiling makes it mushy.","s":0}],"rl":"VXFGGA"},"53480":{"lv":"EERRRROE","w":{"2":"Gives a milder, sweeter onion flavor and body; skipping makes it less rounded.","3":"Adds pungent savory flavor; skipping leaves it grassy and mild.","4":"Sharpens and brightens the sauce; skipping makes it heavier.","5":"Citrus acid and aroma that balance the mayo; skipping makes it flat, so add extra vinegar.","6":"A pinch of earthy warmth; skipping is subtle."},"tips":[{"t":"Seed the jalapenos for a mild green sauce, or leave the seeds and ribs in for heat. Blend them with the cilantro stems for the most flavor.","s":0},{"t":"Blend the herbs, chilies, garlic, acid and cumin first, then pulse in the mayonnaise at the end. Over-blending mayo thins it out.","s":0},{"t":"Rest it in the fridge for 20 to 30 minutes; the garlic mellows, and the sauce firms up. It keeps for 4 to 5 days.","s":0}],"rl":"XXXXAAXF"},"53483":{"lv":"ERRRREOEERRRRERROR","w":{"1":"Adds savory depth to the pea batter; skipping makes the fritters plainer.","2":"Gives a green heat inside the fritter; skip for a milder batter.","3":"Adds sweetness and texture to the batter; skipping makes them more uniform.","4":"A little flour helps bind the pea paste; skip and the batter may fall apart in the oil, so add one tablespoon if it feels loose.","6":"A touch of heat and color; skip for milder fritters.","9":"Sweet base for the shrimp filling; skipping makes the filling less rounded.","10":"Warm spice that pairs with shrimp; skipping makes it taste flat.","11":"Savory depth in the filling; skip for a milder paste.","12":"Adds heat to the filling; skip for milder shrimp.","15":"Adds juice and acidity to the filling; skipping makes it drier.","16":"Fresh herb finish; skip freely.","17":"Brightens the rich fried fritter and shrimp; skip and add vinegar or hot sauce instead.","8":"The oil for frying the fritters; without it they cannot be fried","14":"Cooks the shrimp filling; other fat or the filling juices cover it"},"sub":{"8":{"x":"Enough vegetable or canola oil to fill 8 cm of the pan","d":"Traditional dende oil adds a deep orange color and flavor; neutral oil is milder.","k":["vegetable oil","canola oil"]},"14":{"x":"1 tbsp olive oil or butter","d":"Slightly different flavor; both cook the shrimp filling the same way.","k":["olive oil","butter"],"wn":"Butter contains dairy"}},"tips":[{"t":"Drain and dry the peas thoroughly, then beat the batter by hand for a few minutes; aerating it gives light fritters. The batter should hold shape on a spoon, so add flour a tablespoon at a time if it is slack.","s":1},{"t":"Fry at 350F (180C) in 8 cm of oil, 4 or 5 at a time; if a drop of batter sinks and does not rise, the oil is too cool and the fritters will absorb grease.","s":2},{"t":"Cook the shrimp only until pink and opaque in the filling, about 2 to 3 minutes. They keep cooking on contact with the hot fritter, and overcooked shrimp turn rubbery.","s":0}],"rl":"BXXXTZXLFXXXXPFVGA"},"3079":{"lv":"RRRREROR","w":{"0":"Smoky, salty fat that flavors the whole farofa; skipping gives a plainer, vegetarian version, so use extra butter.","1":"Adds richness and helps toast the flour; skip if the bacon fat is enough.","2":"Sweet savory base; skipping makes the farofa taste of toasted flour alone.","3":"Aromatic punch that makes it taste like a classic Brazilian side; skipping is noticeable.","5":"Scrambled in to add richness and soft bits, as in farofa de ovo; skip for a drier, plain farofa.","6":"Fresh herb finish; adds color and brightness but is not essential.","7":"Bacon is salty, so season at the end; farinha is bland without salt."},"tips":[{"t":"Use farinha de mandioca (coarse toasted manioc flour) rather than cassava flour sold for baking or tapioca starch; those turn gummy and clump instead of staying sandy.","s":3},{"t":"Add the flour a handful at a time and keep stirring so each addition fries in the fat; it is done when it smells nutty and turns golden, not just warm.","s":3},{"t":"Cook the eggs fully before the flour goes in, until set with no runny parts, since the farofa will not get hot enough to finish them.","s":2}],"rl":"FFXXBTGZ"},"53476":{"lv":"OEEEEEE","w":{"0":"Only greases the baking sheet; butter, parchment or a nonstick sheet works just as well.","4":"Must be tapioca starch or flour (polvilho); tapioca pearls will not work. Starch gelatinizes in the hot milk for the chewy texture.","2":"The fat of the cheese bread dough; without it the dough is dry and does not puff"},"sub":{"2":{"x":"1/2 cup (120 ml) neutral vegetable oil","d":"Oil is common in Brazilian versions and gives a crisper shell and chewier center; butter is richer.","k":["vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Use tapioca starch (polvilho or tapioca flour), not tapioca pearls. The starch gelatinizes when you pour the hot milk over it, which is what gives pao de queijo its stretchy, chewy interior.","s":1},{"t":"Cool the dough until just warm before beating in the eggs one at a time. Hot dough scrambles the eggs; the finished dough should be sticky and stretchy, like soft mashed potato.","s":2},{"t":"Wet or oil your hands for shaping. They puff up and then settle slightly when cooled, so serve them warm, and freeze unbaked balls to bake straight from the freezer.","s":3}],"rl":"FMFZBSX"},"53479":{"lv":"RREEEREO","w":{"0":"Sharp, crisp bite that balances the smoky pepper; skipping makes the salsa sweeter and less punchy.","1":"Fresh herb brightness that makes this salsa lively; skipping makes it heavier and dull.","7":"A pinch balances the acidity of tomato and vinegar; skip if your tomatoes are sweet.","5":"Acid for the salsa; lime or tomato still brighten it"},"sub":{"5":{"x":"2 tbsp lime juice or white wine vinegar","d":"Lime is fresher and brighter; white wine vinegar is closer in sharpness.","k":["lime juice","white wine vinegar"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Use smoked paprika (pimenton), not sweet paprika; it provides the smoky flavor that names this salsa. For more smoke, char the bell pepper under a broiler first.","s":0},{"t":"Pulse just a few times; the salsa should stay chunky. Over-processing makes a watery puree and releases the bitter flavor of the cilantro stems.","s":0},{"t":"Let it sit 15 minutes before tasting; the vinegar and salt need time to soften the onion and bring out the tomato juices, and then adjust the seasoning.","s":1}],"rl":"XXVVFAXW"},"53484":{"lv":"EERRER","w":{"2":"Adds a mild, fresh onion bite; skipping makes the salad plainer but still balanced.","5":"Fresh herb finish that adds color and brightness; skipping is subtle.","3":"Acid for the dressing; lemon or other acid still seasons it"},"sub":{"3":{"x":"1 tbsp red wine vinegar or lemon juice","d":"Red wine vinegar is a bit sharper; lemon is brighter and fruitier.","k":["red wine vinegar","lemon juice"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Rinse the canned palm hearts and pat them dry to remove the briny packing liquid, which would otherwise dilute the dressing and add a tinny taste.","s":0},{"t":"Slice the palm hearts on the diagonal into thick coins; they are delicate and mush if cut too thin.","s":0},{"t":"Salt the tomatoes lightly and toss with the dressing just before serving. Dressing too early draws out water and the salad turns soggy.","s":2}],"rl":"VVXAFG"},"53481":{"lv":"ERRRRRROREERO","w":{"1":"Acid brightens the sauce and flavors the marinade; skipping makes it flat, so use lemon juice or vinegar.","2":"Garlic is part of the marinade and the base of the stew; skipping dulls the flavor.","4":"Fresh onion flavor and color; skipping is subtle since the onion is also there.","5":"Sweet base for the coconut sauce; skipping makes the moqueca less rounded.","6":"Adds sweetness and color, a classic part of the sofrito; skipping makes the sauce plainer.","7":"Adds gentle heat; skip for a mild stew.","8":"Gives color and a mild smoky sweetness; skipping makes the stew paler.","11":"Fresh herb finish that lifts the coconut and shrimp; skipping is subtle.","12":"Plain rice is served alongside to soak up the sauce; skip if you serve it with bread.","3":"Fat for cooking the prawns and base; coconut milk also supplies fat"},"sub":{"3":{"x":"3 tbsp vegetable oil","d":"Coconut oil gives a faint coconut note and is closer to the traditional dende; neutral oil is plainer.","k":["vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Do not marinate the shrimp longer than 1 hour. The lime juice starts to cure them and they turn mealy and tough; keep them refrigerated while they marinate.","s":0},{"t":"Add the shrimp last and cook gently for only 3 to 4 minutes, until they are pink and curled, then stop. Overcooked shrimp turn rubbery and the stew will not rescue them.","s":2},{"t":"Reduce the tomatoes and coconut milk for the full 10 minutes before the shrimp go in, so the sauce is thick and flavorful rather than thin and watery.","s":2}],"rl":"PAXFXXVXXVMGB"},"53482":{"lv":"ERERERROOR","w":{"1":"Smoky, salty fat that flavors the pot; skipping makes the stew less smoky.","3":"Smoked, spiced sausage that flavors the beans; skipping makes it a lighter dish.","5":"Sweet aromatic base that balances the salty meats; skipping makes it harsher.","6":"Savory depth in the broth; skipping dulls it.","7":"Adds a mild heat in the background; skip freely.","9":"A subtle herbal background for a long simmer; skipping is barely noticeable.","8":"A drizzle for cooking; the meats render fat"},"sub":{"8":{"x":"2 tbsp olive oil or lard","d":"Lard is more traditional and richer; olive oil is fruitier.","k":["olive oil","lard"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Soak the beans overnight and drain the soaking water; this shortens the cook time and helps with digestion. Simmer gently, never boil hard, so they stay whole and creamy.","s":2},{"t":"Do not add salt until the end. The bacon and sausage are salty already, and salt early can toughen the beans' skins.","s":2},{"t":"For a thick, silky broth, mash a ladleful of the beans against the pot wall and stir them back in. Serve with orange slices and greens to cut the richness.","s":3}],"rl":"BPPPPXXXFX"},"3078":{"lv":"EEEERRRROEEER","w":{"4":"Sweet savory base for the filling; skipping makes it blander.","5":"Adds depth to the chicken filling; skip and the filling tastes more plainly of chicken.","7":"Makes the filling creamy and moist so it binds; skipping gives a drier, crumbly filling.","8":"Fresh herb note in the filling; skipping is subtle.","12":"The dough and filling need seasoning; taste the filling before sealing.","6":"Sweats the onion for the filling; the chicken still cooks","11":"The frying oil for the coxinha; without it they cannot be fried"},"sub":{"6":{"x":"1 tbsp olive oil or butter","d":"Both sweat the onion the same way; butter adds a little more flavor.","k":["olive oil","butter"]},"11":{"x":"2 cups canola or peanut oil","d":"Any neutral oil with a high smoke point works the same way.","k":["canola oil","peanut oil"],"wn":"Peanut oil is a peanut allergen"}},"tips":[{"t":"Chill the filling until firm so it is easy to wrap; a warm filling softens the dough and leaks during frying.","s":0},{"t":"Add all the flour at once to the boiling broth and beat vigorously until a smooth dough pulls from the pan. Knead it while still warm so it becomes pliable and shaped easily.","s":1},{"t":"Fry in oil held at 350F (175C), a few at a time. Too cool and they absorb oil; too hot and the crust burns before the center warms. Seal the seams well so they do not burst.","s":5}],"rl":"MFSPXXFFGTGFZ"},"53475":{"lv":"EEEOOOR","w":{"3":"Rounds out the sweetness slightly; classic brigadeiro is made without it.","4":"Crunchy coating option; use any one of the toppings, or skip for sprinkles.","5":"Another coating option; skip freely.","6":"Classic rolling coating that keeps them from sticking; use sprinkles or cocoa if you skip it."},"tips":[{"t":"Stir constantly over medium heat until you can drag the spoon across the pot and see the bottom for 2 to 3 seconds before the mixture closes back over. That is the right set for rolling.","s":0},{"t":"Butter your hands lightly before rolling; the mixture is very sticky and will not release otherwise. If it is too soft to roll, chill it longer or cook a little further next time.","s":2},{"t":"Roll in the topping right after shaping so it adheres, then place in paper cups; the brigadeiro stays soft and fudgy at room temperature for a day.","s":2}],"rl":"FWXXGGG"},"53477":{"lv":"REEEEO","w":{"0":"Greases the ramekins and helps the sugar coating release; without it the quindim may stick.","5":"A small aromatic note; skipping is barely noticeable since the coconut dominates."},"tips":[{"t":"Press the egg yolks through a sieve before mixing; this removes the chalazae, giving a glossy, smooth custard without stringy bits.","s":1},{"t":"Bake in a water bath with hot water up to three quarters of the ramekins and do not let the oven go above 180C. The custard should just jiggle at the center when done; overbaking makes it rubbery and weeping.","s":2},{"t":"Chill the full 4 hours, then run a knife around the edge before inverting. The coconut layer separates to the top and becomes the base, giving the classic two-layer look.","s":3}],"rl":"FSWMBX"},"53485":{"lv":"EEEEEERRR","w":{"6":"Used for the chocolate drizzle that gets poured over the cake; the cake itself is moist without it.","7":"Gives the drizzle its chocolate flavor; skipping leaves a milk-sugar glaze.","8":"Sweetens the drizzle so it sets shiny; skipping makes it thin and bitter.","0":"The fat and moisture of the carrot cake; without it the cake is dry"},"sub":{"0":{"x":"250 ml (1 cup) canola or sunflower oil","d":"Any neutral oil gives the same moist, tender cake; olive oil would add flavor, melted butter makes it denser.","k":["canola oil","sunflower oil","vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Blend the carrots, eggs and oil in a blender until completely smooth before adding to the dry mix. This is the Brazilian method and is why the cake has no visible carrot pieces and an ultra-moist crumb.","s":0},{"t":"Do not open the oven in the first 30 minutes; the cake needs the steady heat to rise. Bake until a skewer comes out clean in the center.","s":1},{"t":"Level the cake and flip it so the flat bottom is on top, then pierce it all over before pouring the hot drizzle so the chocolate soaks in instead of running off.","s":3}],"rl":"FSWLVSMXW"},"3259":{"lv":"EEREERO","w":{"2":"Feeds the yeast and helps the muffins brown on the griddle; with only 1 tbsp, skipping works but browning is paler.","5":"Softens the crumb and adds richness; skipping gives a chewier, drier muffin.","6":"Gives the classic gritty crust and keeps them from sticking; use flour or semolina if you have no cornmeal."},"tips":[{"t":"Cook on a griddle at medium-low heat, about 6 to 7 minutes per side; any hotter and the outside browns while the center stays raw, which is why the finishing step in the oven exists.","s":5},{"t":"Rest the cut rounds on the cornmeal for the full 30 minutes; they should puff slightly, and handle them gently, as pressing them out deflates the nooks.","s":4},{"t":"Split with a fork, not a knife, so you get rough craggy surfaces that crisp up in the toaster and catch melting butter. The interior should reach about 200F (93C) when done.","s":7}],"rl":"MLWSZFG"},"3271":{"lv":"EREREEEOO","w":{"1":"Adds a little sweetness and helps browning; scones are good with less if serving with jam.","3":"Seasons the dough and balances the sweetness; skipping makes them taste flat.","7":"Light aroma that rounds out the butter and cream; skip for a plainer scone.","8":"Only a glaze for a shiny, golden top; skip and use a little milk or cream instead.","5":"The liquid that binds the scone dough; without it the dough will not come together"},"sub":{"5":{"x":"2/3 cup (160 ml) whole milk plus 2 tbsp extra cold butter rubbed into the flour","d":"Slightly less rich and tender, but still flaky; do not melt the butter or the dough gets greasy.","k":["whole milk","butter"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Keep the butter cold and stop rubbing in while some pea-size pieces remain. Those pieces melt in the oven and create the flaky layers.","s":1},{"t":"Press the cutter straight down without twisting. Twisting seals the edges and stops the scones rising tall; re-roll the scraps only once.","s":3},{"t":"Bake from cold: chill the cut rounds 15 minutes if the kitchen is warm, and use the egg only on the tops, not down the sides, which would glue the layers together.","s":4}],"rl":"SWLZFFSXC"},"3274":{"lv":"EEERER","w":{"3":"Softens the tang of the buttermilk and helps browning; classic soda bread can be made without it.","5":"Brushed on for a softer crust and richer flavor; skipping gives a harder, plainer crust.","4":"The acid and liquid for the baking soda; without it the bread will not rise"},"sub":{"4":{"x":"1 3/4 cups whole milk mixed with 1 3/4 tbsp lemon juice or white vinegar, rested 10 minutes","d":"Soured milk is a bit thinner and milder than true buttermilk, but supplies the acid the baking soda needs.","k":["milk","lemon juice","white vinegar"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Work fast and handle the dough as little as possible: the baking soda starts reacting with the buttermilk immediately, so get the loaf in the oven within a few minutes. Overkneading makes it tough.","s":3},{"t":"Cut the cross deep, nearly through the loaf, so heat reaches the center and the loaf can expand evenly. A shallow cut leaves a gummy middle.","s":3},{"t":"Wrap the warm loaf in a clean kitchen towel as it cools for a softer crust. The loaf is done when it sounds hollow when tapped on the bottom.","s":5}],"rl":"SLZWMF"},"3507":{"lv":"EREORERER","w":{"1":"Gives them their mild sweetness; they are meant to be only lightly sweet and get dusted with sugar.","3":"A faint warm spice note; skip for a plainer Welsh cake.","4":"Rounds out the flavor of the butter and flour; skipping makes them taste flat.","6":"Dried fruit sweetness and chew that define Welsh cakes; raisins work too, or skip for a plain version.","8":"Adjusts the dough consistency; add just enough to make a soft, workable dough."},"tips":[{"t":"Cook them on a barely greased griddle at medium-low heat, about 3 minutes per side. Too hot and the outsides scorch before the dense dough cooks through the middle.","s":4},{"t":"Roll to 1/4 inch and handle gently; the dough is short and crumbly, and rerolling too much makes tough cakes. Add milk by the spoonful until it just holds together.","s":3},{"t":"Dust with sugar while they are still hot so it sticks and melts slightly into the crust.","s":5}],"rl":"SWLXZFVSM"},"3624":{"lv":"EERROEEEO","w":{"2":"Seasons the scones; cheese is salty but the dough still needs its own salt.","3":"Sharpens the cheddar flavor in the classic British way; skipping makes the cheese taste milder.","4":"A tiny hit of heat that wakes up the cheese; skip freely.","8":"Only for a shiny, golden surface; milk can replace it."},"tips":[{"t":"Use a genuinely sharp, mature cheddar. Mild cheese vanishes into the dough, and extra-sharp cheese delivers flavor even in a small amount.","s":3},{"t":"Press the cutter straight down without twisting so the layers rise tall. Mix the milk in just until a soft dough forms; overmixing makes tough scones.","s":4},{"t":"Put the remaining cheese on top only after brushing with egg so it sticks, and bake at 425F until the tops are browned and lacy.","s":5}],"rl":"SLZXXFXMG"},"52895":{"lv":"EERRRER","w":{"2":"Traditional earthy side; skipping is common for a lighter plate.","3":"Sweet, juicy contrast to the rich meats; skipping is common but loses freshness.","4":"An authentic English breakfast element; skip if you cannot find it.","6":"Fried bread soaks up the juices and fat; skip for toast."},"tips":[{"t":"Cook the sausages slowly over low heat for 15 to 20 minutes, turning often, until browned and cooked through at 160F (71C). High heat splits the casings and leaves the center raw.","s":1},{"t":"Snip the fatty edge of the bacon so it does not curl and cooks evenly. Cook the mushrooms and tomatoes cut side down in the pan so they brown instead of steaming.","s":2},{"t":"Cook the eggs last and baste them with a little hot butter so the whites set without frying the yolk hard. Warm the plates so everything stays hot while you finish.","s":7}],"rl":"PPVVPPB"},"52896":{"lv":"EERRRERR","w":{"2":"Traditional earthy side; skipping is common for a lighter plate.","3":"Sweet, juicy contrast to the rich meats; skipping loses freshness.","4":"An authentic element of a full English; skip if you cannot find it.","6":"Fried bread soaks up the fat and juices; use toast if you prefer.","7":"A classic side; warm them in a separate pot so they do not turn the plate soggy."},"tips":[{"t":"Cook in order of time: sausages first, then bacon, then mushrooms and tomatoes, black pudding, and fry the bread and eggs last. Hold the finished items on a tray in a 200F (95C) oven.","s":0},{"t":"Use day-old bread for fried bread, fried in the bacon fat or butter, so it crisps without absorbing too much oil. Fresh bread soaks up grease and turns soggy.","s":6},{"t":"Heat the beans in a separate pan, and put them on the plate away from the toast and egg so their sauce does not make the rest soggy.","s":8}],"rl":"PPVVPPBB"},"3500":{"lv":"EEEEREEORO","w":{"4":"Seasons the batter; the chips are salted after frying, so use less for the batter if desired.","5":"Provides the cold carbonation that makes the batter crisp and light; sparkling water works but has less flavor.","6":"Fry oil must be neutral and high smoke point; any similar oil works.","7":"Squeezed over for freshness; skip if you use vinegar.","8":"Classic seasoning for chips; apple cider vinegar can replace it.","9":"A creamy, tangy dip; skip or use mayonnaise with lemon."},"sub":{"5":{"x":"3/4 cup ice-cold sparkling water","d":"Gives similar lightness through carbonation, though the batter tastes less malty than with beer.","k":["sparkling water"]},"6":{"x":"6 cups peanut oil or canola oil","d":"Any neutral oil with a high smoke point will fry the same.","k":["peanut oil","canola oil"],"wn":"Peanut oil is a peanut allergen"}},"tips":[{"t":"Fry the chips twice: first at 300F (150C) until soft, then at 375F (190C) until crisp. The first fry cooks the interior and the second crisps the outside.","s":1},{"t":"Keep the beer batter ice cold and barely mixed; lumps are fine. Warm or overmixed batter develops gluten and turns bready instead of crisp.","s":2},{"t":"Drain the fish on a rack, not paper towels, which steam the crust soft. The cod is done when it flakes and reaches 145F (63C).","s":4}],"rl":"BPSLZMFGAG"},"3501":{"lv":"EEEREREERRRORO","w":{"8":"Adds the deep savory tang that makes onion gravy taste of a British pub; skipping makes the gravy flatter.","9":"Gives a little tang and depth to the gravy; skip and add an extra splash of Worcestershire.","10":"Helps the onions caramelize and balances the sharper gravy; skip and cook the onions a little longer.","11":"A classic side that adds color and sweetness; swap any green vegetable.","12":"Needed for the mash and gravy; potatoes and gravy are bland without it.","13":"Light spice; adjust to taste at the end.","3":"Loosens the mash; butter and other liquid still do it","5":"Browns the sausages; they render fat","7":"The liquid of the gravy; without it there is no gravy"},"sub":{"3":{"x":"1/2 cup (120 ml) half-and-half or cream","d":"Richer, silkier mash; milk is lighter.","k":["half and half","cream"]},"5":{"x":"1 tbsp butter or olive oil","d":"Butter browns faster and adds flavor; watch the heat.","k":["butter","olive oil"]},"7":{"x":"2 cups chicken broth","d":"Lighter and less roasted, but the onions and Worcestershire keep the gravy savory.","k":["chicken broth"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Cook the onions slowly for the full 12 minutes until deep golden, and stir the flour in for a full minute so the gravy does not taste of raw flour.","s":2},{"t":"Do not prick the sausages; brown them gently in the pan to 160F (71C), and use the browned bits to deglaze when you add the broth.","s":1},{"t":"Let the drained potatoes steam dry for a minute and mash them while hot with warm milk. Cold milk or wet potatoes make a gluey, loose mash.","s":4}],"rl":"PBFMVFTMXXWGZZ"},"3502":{"lv":"EERERRRRRERRREORO","w":{"4":"Adds sweetness and body to the gravy; skip and increase the onion.","5":"Earthy, savory depth in the filling; skipping makes the pie more purely beefy.","6":"Savory backbone; skipping makes the gravy blander.","7":"Concentrates the beefy, umami flavor of the gravy; skip and add a little extra Worcestershire.","10":"Adds a deep tangy umami; skipping makes the gravy milder.","11":"Classic British herb for beef; skipping is noticeable but not essential.","12":"Subtle background herbal note; skip freely.","14":"Only a glaze for a shiny, golden crust; milk can replace it.","15":"Season the filling in stages; the beef and flour need it.","16":"Light spice; adjust to taste.","2":"Browns the beef; the beef renders fat","8":"Gives malty depth to the pie gravy; broth still makes it"},"sub":{"2":{"x":"2 tbsp olive oil or butter","d":"Both brown the beef well; butter browns faster, so keep the heat moderate.","k":["olive oil","butter"],"wn":"Butter contains milk."},"8":{"x":"1 1/2 cups additional beef broth plus 1 tsp malt or balsamic vinegar","d":"No malty bitterness, but the vinegar keeps the gravy lively.","k":["beef broth","malt vinegar","balsamic vinegar"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Cool the filling for at least 20 minutes, ideally longer, before topping with the pastry. A hot filling melts the butter in the puff pastry and makes the bottom of the crust soggy.","s":3},{"t":"Pat the beef dry and brown in batches; crowding steams it and you lose the browned bits that flavor the gravy. The beef is ready when a fork twists easily.","s":0},{"t":"Place a pie funnel or inverted egg cup in the center so the pastry stays raised. Cut a steam vent and bake on a hot tray so the pastry puffs and the base browns.","s":5}],"rl":"PTFXVVXXMMXXXSGZZ"},"3503":{"lv":"EREEEERRRORR","w":{"1":"Seasons the pastry; without it the crust tastes flat against the savory filling.","7":"Adds sweetness and moisture to the filling; skipping makes it drier and less rounded.","8":"Butter dotted on top melts into the filling as it bakes; skip and the filling is a little drier.","9":"Only for a glossy golden crust; milk works too.","10":"The raw filling needs real seasoning, since it is baked inside pastry and cannot be adjusted.","11":"A Cornish pasty has plenty of black pepper; skip and it will taste bland.","6":"A traditional sweet vegetable; potato and beef still fill the pasty"},"sub":{"6":{"x":"6 oz (170 g) turnip or an extra potato","d":"Rutabaga gives the traditional sweet, earthy note; turnip is similar and extra potato is plainer.","k":["turnip","potato"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Dice the beef and vegetables small, no larger than 1/2 inch. The filling goes in raw and steams inside the pastry, so large pieces cook unevenly.","s":1},{"t":"Crimp the edge firmly into a thick rope so the juices stay in; a poorly sealed seam leaks and leaves the filling dry.","s":3},{"t":"Start hot (400F) to set the crust, then lower to 350F to cook the filling through. Rest 10 minutes so the juices thicken and the pasty does not burn your mouth.","s":5}],"rl":"SZFMPBVXFGZZ"},"52803":{"lv":"EROEREOR","w":{"1":"Brushed on the seared beef for tang and flavor; skip for a milder Wellington.","4":"Adds salty flavor and acts as a moisture barrier between the beef and pastry; without it the pastry can go soggy.","6":"Only for dusting the work surface to keep the pastry from sticking.","7":"Gives the glaze its gloss and color; whole egg or milk works too.","2":"A dash to sear the beef; the beef can be seared with little fat"},"sub":{"2":{"x":"1 tbsp vegetable oil","d":"Any high-heat oil sears the beef equally well.","k":["vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Cook the mushroom paste until it is completely dry and holds its shape on the spoon. Any moisture left makes the pastry soggy and the beef steams.","s":0},{"t":"Chill the wrapped beef for at least 30 minutes before putting it in the pastry. Cold meat and cold pastry make a clean seam and a crisp crust.","s":3},{"t":"Use a probe thermometer: pull at 120F (49C) for rare-medium-rare, 125F (52C) for medium-rare, and rest 10 minutes before slicing. The beef will finish cooking while it rests.","s":5}],"rl":"VXFPXSGG"},"52822":{"lv":"EEEEOEO","w":{"4":"Gives a peppery bite that cuts through the sausage; skipping is common and the pudding is still good.","6":"Adds a little sweetness and color; skip or use sliced onion."},"tips":[{"t":"The fat in the tin must be shimmering hot before the batter goes in. The sizzle as it hits is what makes the batter rise high and crisp at the edges.","s":1},{"t":"Rest the batter at least 30 minutes if you have time, and use eggs at room temperature; this relaxes the gluten and gives a taller rise.","s":2},{"t":"Do not open the oven door during the first 25 minutes or the batter will collapse. The sausages should be browned first and reach 160F (71C) by the time the pudding is set.","s":3}],"rl":"PFSSXMV"},"52824":{"lv":"EREREEEE","w":{"1":"A green side that adds color and freshness; swap any green vegetable.","3":"A sweet side that balances the savory beef; swap any other vegetable.","7":"The hot fat in the Yorkshire pudding tin; without it the puddings stick and do not rise"},"sub":{"7":{"x":"Vegetable oil or beef dripping, about 1 tsp per Yorkshire pudding hole","d":"Beef dripping gives the best flavor and rise; neutral oil works but tastes plainer.","k":["vegetable oil","beef dripping"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Rest the roast beef, loosely covered, for 20 to 30 minutes while the Yorkshire puddings bake. The oven has to be at 230C for the batter, which would overcook the meat if it stayed in.","s":1},{"t":"Parboil the potatoes and shake them in the pot to roughen the edges before roasting; the starch paste on the surface becomes the crisp crust.","s":1},{"t":"Use equal volumes of flour, egg and milk for a reliable Yorkshire batter, and keep the oil smoking hot so the batter puffs on contact.","s":4}],"rl":"PVBVSSMF"},"52845":{"lv":"RRRRREEERRO","w":{"1":"Adds moisture and sweetness to lean turkey; skipping makes the meatloaf drier.","2":"Adds savory depth; skipping makes the meatloaf less flavorful.","3":"Adds umami and tang, making up for turkey's mild taste; skipping makes it blander.","4":"Adds concentrated sweet-savory depth to the mixture; skipping is subtle.","8":"Gives the tangy glaze and flavors the beans; skip and the dish is plainer.","9":"Cooked in the sauce alongside; they add body and make it a one-pan dinner, so skip if you serve another side.","10":"Fresh herb finish; skip freely.","0":"Softens the onion; the meatloaf still works"},"sub":{"0":{"x":"1 tbsp vegetable oil or butter","d":"Both soften the onion just as well; butter adds a little more flavor.","k":["vegetable oil","butter"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Ground turkey is lean and dries out quickly: the sauteed onion and breadcrumbs add moisture. Cool the onion mix before folding in so the egg does not cook, and do not overmix.","s":1},{"t":"Bake until the center reaches 165F (74C); poultry must hit this even though the loaf looks done earlier. Rest 10 minutes before slicing so it holds together.","s":3},{"t":"Add the beans for the last 15 minutes, as written, so they take on the glaze and do not turn to mush.","s":3}],"rl":"FXXXXPTTGBG"},"52846":{"lv":"ERREEOOEREO","w":{"1":"Sweet savory base for the sauce; skipping makes it plainer.","2":"Earthy flavor that makes the sauce taste like a proper hotpot; skip for a simpler filling.","5":"A pinch is classic in white sauces; skipping is barely noticeable.","6":"Adds a mild background sharpness to the creamy sauce; skip freely.","8":"Adds sweetness and color; skip or swap peas.","10":"Brushed on the potato top to help it brown and crisp; skip and brush with oil."},"tips":[{"t":"Cook the flour in the butter for a minute or two before adding any liquid, and add the stock a little at a time off the heat, whisking, so the sauce is smooth with no lumps.","s":1},{"t":"Make sure the chicken is cooked through to 165F (74C) in the sauce before the potatoes go on, since the oven time is short.","s":2},{"t":"Slice the potatoes thinly and evenly, about 3 mm, and overlap them like roof tiles. Brush with butter and cover with foil for the first 20 minutes, then uncover to brown.","s":3}],"rl":"FXVTMXXPVBF"},"52848":{"lv":"EEERR","w":{"4":"Sharpens the sweet tomato sauce; skipping makes it plainer and sweeter.","3":"Adds a deep sweetness to the sauce; the hotpot still works without it"},"sub":{"3":{"x":"1 tbsp dark brown sugar or molasses","d":"Gives similar sweetness; treacle is more bitter and deeper, molasses is the closest swap.","k":["brown sugar","molasses"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Brown the sausages well and leave the fond in the pan; the browned bits dissolve into the tomato sauce and give the dish its savory base.","s":0},{"t":"Drain and rinse the canned beans first so the starchy liquid does not make the sauce gluey; mash a handful against the pot to thicken it.","s":1},{"t":"Simmer with the lid slightly ajar for the last 10 minutes to reduce the sauce. The sausages must be cooked through to 160F (71C).","s":2}],"rl":"PMBWX"},"52874":{"lv":"EERRERRRERERORO","w":{"5":"Sweet aromatic base for the braise; skipping makes the gravy thinner.","6":"Adds sweetness and body to the gravy; skipping is subtle.","7":"A herbal note that suits beef; skipping is barely noticeable.","9":"Used to glue the pastry to the dish rim and glaze the top; skip and use milk.","11":"A side that adds color and freshness; skip or serve other vegetables.","13":"The beef and gravy need seasoning; season at the end.","14":"Light spice for the beans; adjust to taste.","12":"Tossed with the green beans for a glossy side; skip or use oil, or serve the pie without the vegetable side.","2":"Browns the beef; the beef renders fat","3":"Adds depth to the gravy; broth still makes it"},"sub":{"2":{"x":"2 tbsp olive oil or butter","d":"Both brown beef well; butter browns faster and needs a lower heat.","k":["olive oil","butter"]},"3":{"x":"200 ml additional beef broth plus 1 tbsp red wine vinegar","d":"Lacks the depth of wine but keeps the gravy lively; do not skip the acid.","k":["beef broth","red wine vinegar"]}},"tips":[{"t":"The beef is ready when a fork slides in with no resistance.","s":5},{"t":"Cool the filling completely before you add the pastry. Hot filling melts the butter in puff pastry and makes the bottom soggy.","s":6},{"t":"If the gravy is thin after braising, simmer it on the stove until it coats a spoon; mustard adds a tang that cuts the richness of the beef.","s":6}],"rl":"PTFMMXVXXGBVFZZ"},"52875":{"lv":"EEEEREERRRROEEREO","w":{"4":"Adds savory depth to the sauce; skipping makes the filling milder.","8":"Gives richness and a silky finish; skip and use extra milk, but the sauce will be thinner.","9":"Salty, savory contrast to the chicken; skipping makes it simpler but still good.","10":"Season the sauce well, since pastry is bland; taste the filling before adding the chicken.","11":"Light spice; adjust to taste.","14":"An egg in the pastry helps bind the dough, so it holds together when rolled; use a little extra water if skipping.","16":"Only for a golden glaze; milk works too.","7":"Adds acid and depth to the sauce; broth still makes it"},"sub":{"7":{"x":"3 tbsp additional chicken broth plus 1/2 tsp lemon juice","d":"Alcohol-free and slightly less complex; the lemon supplies the acid.","k":["chicken broth","lemon juice"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Poach the chicken gently so it stays tender, to 165F (74C), and keep the poaching stock: it is the base of the sauce.","s":0},{"t":"Cool the sauce completely before filling the pie, and press cling film on the surface to prevent a skin. A warm filling melts the pastry and makes the base soggy.","s":3},{"t":"Bake on a preheated tray so the base crisps, and cut a steam vent in the lid. Leeks hold water, so wash them thoroughly and cook them until they have lost their moisture.","s":9}],"rl":"MPFVXTMMFPZZSFTMG"},"52876":{"lv":"RERREREOEO","w":{"2":"Sweet, savory base for the filling; skipping makes it blander.","3":"Adds concentrated umami and deep color to the gravy; skipping makes it paler and flatter.","5":"Earthy depth that stretches the beef; skip for a pure beef filling.","7":"A dash adds tang and savory depth; skipping is subtle.","9":"Used to stick the decorations on and glaze the crust; milk or whole egg works.","0":"Browns the beef; the beef renders fat"},"sub":{"0":{"x":"2 tbsp olive oil or butter","d":"Both brown the beef; butter browns faster and tastes richer.","k":["olive oil","butter"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Brown the mince in a hot pan without crowding and let it sit undisturbed for a minute at a time. Stirring constantly leaves it gray and boiled instead of browned. It must reach 160F (71C).","s":1},{"t":"Cook the flour with the beef for a full minute before adding the stock, then simmer until the gravy coats the back of a spoon. A thin filling leaks out and makes the pastry soggy.","s":2},{"t":"Let the filling cool before topping and do not stretch the shortcrust when you drape it over the dish; stretched pastry shrinks in the oven and pulls away from the rim.","s":3}],"rl":"FPXXTVMXBG"},"52877":{"lv":"ERRRREEEO","w":{"1":"Lightly coats the lamb to brown it and thicken the gravy; skipping makes the gravy thinner.","3":"Sweet savory base for the gravy; skipping makes the filling plainer.","4":"Adds sweetness and body; skipping is subtle.","8":"Only a glaze for a golden crust; milk works too.","2":"Browns the lamb and onions; the lamb renders fat","5":"The liquid of the pie filling; without it the filling is dry"},"sub":{"2":{"x":"2 tbsp olive oil or butter","d":"Either browns the lamb and onions well; butter adds richness.","k":["olive oil","butter"]},"5":{"x":"350 ml chicken broth","d":"Richer and meatier than vegetable broth; use low-sodium and season at the end.","k":["chicken broth"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Simmer the lamb for at least an hour until it is fork-tender, and skim off excess fat from the top. Shoulder is fatty, and the filling should be rich, not greasy.","s":3},{"t":"Parboil the potatoes briefly and add them cool; they finish cooking in the oven under the pastry, so fully cooked potatoes turn to mush.","s":5},{"t":"Let the filling cool and thicken before covering, and cut slits in the lid for steam. Season the gravy well, as pastry is bland.","s":6}],"rl":"PTFXVMBBG"},"52878":{"lv":"ERRRRRREEREEREO","w":{"2":"Sweet, mild onion flavor in the gravy; skip and use a small onion.","3":"Savory depth in the gravy; skipping makes it milder.","4":"Smoky, salty richness for the stew; skipping makes the gravy leaner.","5":"Herbal note that suits beef and stout; skipping is subtle.","6":"Background herbal flavor over a long braise; skip freely.","12":"Pastry needs a little salt; skipping makes it bland.","14":"Only for a glossy golden glaze; milk works too.","1":"Browns the beef; the beef renders fat","7":"The main braising liquid of the pie filling; without it the filling is dry","9":"Thickens the gravy; the pie is still fine with a looser filling"},"sub":{"1":{"x":"3 tbsp vegetable oil","d":"Any neutral oil sears beef well; olive oil adds slight fruitiness.","k":["vegetable oil"]},"7":{"x":"330 ml additional beef broth plus 1 tsp malt or balsamic vinegar","d":"No stout bitterness or malty depth, but the vinegar keeps the gravy lively.","k":["beef broth","balsamic vinegar"]},"9":{"x":"1/4 cup (4 tbsp) all-purpose flour, whisked into cold water, simmered 5 minutes","d":"Thickens as well but must be cooked to remove the raw flour taste; the gravy is slightly less glossy.","k":["flour"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Add the shucked oysters at the end and tuck them into the filling just before the pastry goes on; they cook in the oven and turn rubbery if simmered in the gravy.","s":6},{"t":"Grate the frozen butter into the flour and keep everything cold for a flaky, sturdy crust. Rest the dough in the fridge before rolling.","s":5},{"t":"Cool the gravy completely before assembling and use a thick sauce so it does not flood the oysters or soften the base.","s":4}],"rl":"PFXXXXXMMTPSZFG"},"52880":{"lv":"RRRRRRRRERREREEO","w":{"0":"Toasted cumin gives the lamb filling its warm Indian-spiced edge; skipping makes it plain.","2":"Sweet aromatic base for the filling; skipping makes it blander.","3":"Gives pungent savory depth; skipping flattens the spiced lamb.","4":"Gives a fresh heat; skip for a mild pie.","5":"Adds sweetness and moisture to the filling; skipping makes it denser.","6":"A classic spice in Scotch pie (traditionally mace); skipping makes the filling less authentic.","7":"Pairs with cumin for warm, earthy spice; skipping makes it less aromatic.","9":"Traditionally white pepper adds gentle heat; skip and use less.","10":"Fresh herb brightness that cuts through the lamb; skipping is subtle.","12":"Seasons the pastry; skipping makes it taste flat.","15":"Only for a glossy golden glaze; milk or whole egg works too.","1":"Sweats the vegetables; they still cook","13":"The liquid of the hot-water crust; without it the pastry will not form","14":"The fat of the hot-water crust; without it the pastry is not formed"},"sub":{"1":{"x":"1 tbsp olive oil or vegetable oil","d":"Any cooking oil sweats the vegetables the same way.","k":["olive oil","vegetable oil"]},"13":{"x":"90 ml additional water","d":"Slightly paler and less rich crust, but still holds together.","k":["water"]},"14":{"x":"150 g butter or vegetable shortening","d":"Butter gives more flavor but a slightly less crisp crust; shortening is closest to lard.","k":["butter","shortening"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Use the hot-water pastry while it is still warm; it stiffens quickly as it cools and cracks when rolled. Keep the unused portion covered under a warm bowl.","s":6},{"t":"Pack the raw lamb filling tightly, working it in layers so there are no air gaps that make the pie collapse. Ground lamb must reach 160F (71C) at the center, so check with a thermometer; a pie this size needs about an hour.","s":7},{"t":"Cut a steam hole in the lid and bake on a tray to catch fat drips. Let the pie rest 10 to 15 minutes so the juices set before unmolding.","s":8}],"rl":"XFXXXVXXPZXSZMFG"},"52881":{"lv":"EOOREEREEROR","w":{"1":"Used as a glaze with the yolk; skipping is fine, use whole egg or milk.","2":"Gives a golden shine on the pastry; milk can replace it.","6":"Sweet savory depth in the gravy; skipping makes the filling plainer.","9":"Needed to season the filling; add at the end and taste.","10":"Light spice; adjust to taste.","11":"A dash adds tang and umami; skipping is subtle.","3":"Browns the meat; the meat renders fat"},"sub":{"3":{"x":"2 tbsp olive oil or butter","d":"Both brown the meat; butter browns faster and needs a lower heat.","k":["olive oil","butter"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Soak the kidneys in cold salted water for 30 minutes and cut out the white core and fat before dicing. This removes the strong ammonia-like flavor.","s":1},{"t":"The beef line reads only 70 ml, which looks like a data error; use about 500 g (1 lb) of stewing steak cut into 2 cm cubes for four servings.","s":1},{"t":"Simmer uncovered for the full 1 1/2 hours, topping up with stock if it dries out, then cool the filling completely so the puff pastry stays crisp.","s":4}],"rl":"BGGFPPXTMZZX"},"53096":{"lv":"EREERRO","w":{"1":"Sweet savory base for the hash; skipping makes it plainer but still good.","4":"Corned beef is salty, so add salt only at the end after tasting.","5":"The recipe calls for plenty at the end; it balances the saltiness of the corned beef.","6":"Fresh herb finish that adds color; skip freely.","0":"The frying fat for the hash; without it the hash sticks and does not crisp"},"sub":{"0":{"x":"3 tbsp vegetable oil or bacon fat","d":"Butter browns well; oil gives a cleaner crisp crust, and bacon fat adds a smoky taste.","k":["vegetable oil","bacon fat"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Dice the potatoes small and parboil them first (or use leftover cooked potatoes) so they are tender by the time the crust forms. Raw potatoes brown long before they cook through.","s":3},{"t":"Press the hash flat and leave it alone for several minutes at a time. Constant stirring prevents the crisp brown crust that defines a good hash.","s":5},{"t":"Taste before salting; canned or deli corned beef is very salty already. Top with a fried egg for a classic finish.","s":9}],"rl":"FXPBZZG"},"53097":{"lv":"EEEE","sub":{"3":{"x":"Beef dripping or lard, about 1 tsp per hole (or canola oil)","d":"Dripping gives the classic savory flavor; oil with a high smoke point works but tastes plainer.","k":["beef dripping","lard","canola oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Heat the oil in the tin until it just starts to smoke before pouring in the batter; it should sizzle loudly. Cold fat makes flat, greasy puddings.","s":1},{"t":"Rest the batter for 30 minutes and fill each hole only halfway. Do not open the oven for the first 20 minutes or they will collapse.","s":5},{"t":"To refresh frozen puddings, reheat from frozen at 400F (200C) for 5 to 8 minutes so they crisp again.","s":6}],"rl":"SSMF","w":{"3":"The hot fat in the tin; without it the puddings stick and do not rise"}},"53098":{"lv":"RRRORRRERREEERO","w":{"0":"Adds sweetness and body to the gravy; skipping makes it simpler.","1":"Sweet savory base for the braise; skipping makes the gravy thinner.","2":"Adds sweetness and color; skipping is subtle.","3":"Subtle background herbal note for a long braise; skip freely.","4":"Earthy herb that suits beef; skipping is subtle.","6":"Enriches the vegetable base; oil alone would work.","8":"Adds umami and color; skipping makes the gravy paler and flatter.","9":"Adds a deep tangy umami; skipping makes the gravy milder.","13":"Gives a sharp, savory crust on the potatoes; skip and add extra seasoning.","14":"A salty, nutty extra on the crust; skipping is subtle.","5":"Softens the vegetables; the mince still cooks","10":"The stock cubes make the savory liquid of the filling; without them the pie is bland and dry"},"sub":{"5":{"x":"2 tbsp olive oil","d":"Either softens the vegetables; butter adds a bit more richness.","k":["olive oil","butter"]},"10":{"x":"2 1/2 cups (600 ml) beef broth instead of 2 stock cubes plus water","d":"Richer and less salty; reduce further if too thin.","k":["beef broth"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Feather blade (chuck) needs the full time: the collagen only breaks down after about 3 hours at a low heat. When the meat shreds with a spoon and the sauce is thick, it is done.","s":1},{"t":"Parboil the potatoes to about three quarters done so they do not break up; slice them evenly and toss with oil and thyme so the edges crisp.","s":2},{"t":"Layer the potato slices in overlapping rows over the beef and sprinkle the cheeses on top. If the beef filling is cold when it goes in, give it extra bake time.","s":3}],"rl":"VXVXXFFTXXMPBGG"},"52802":{"lv":"EEEEEORRRRRORRR","w":{"5":"A grating of nutmeg is classic in white sauce; skipping is barely noticeable.","6":"Adds richness and a silky finish to the sauce; skip for a lighter pie.","7":"Sweet, nutty flavor and body that thickens the filling as it bakes; skip or use extra leek.","8":"Sweet oniony flavor that softens in the sauce; skipping makes the filling plainer.","9":"Adds sweetness and a seafood contrast; skip for a simpler fish pie, but use more fish.","10":"Fresh herbs are part of the flavor of fish pie; skipping makes it dull.","11":"A classic herb with fish; skipping is subtle if you have parsley.","12":"Adds fresh citrus aroma; skip and use more juice.","13":"Salty, nutty browned top; skip or use cheddar.","14":"Brightens the sauce and balances the cream; skipping makes it heavy.","1":"The fat for the roux of the fish pie sauce; without it the sauce does not thicken"},"sub":{"1":{"x":"2 tbsp butter or vegetable oil","d":"Butter gives a richer sauce; oil is fine for the roux.","k":["butter","vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Poach the fish only 3 minutes off the heat; it finishes cooking in the oven. Overpoached fish turns dry and falls apart in the sauce.","s":1},{"t":"Steam the drained potatoes dry before mashing, and keep the mash thick, so the topping stays on top instead of sinking. Rough up the surface with a fork for crisp, browned peaks.","s":4},{"t":"Make the sauce thick like pouring custard: the fish, shrimp and artichokes release liquid in the oven. Raw shrimp must turn pink and the fish reach 145F (63C) inside before serving.","s":3}],"rl":"BFMPTXFVVPXXXGA"},"52863":{"lv":"RERRRRERRREEROE","w":{"0":"Only the cooking fat for sweating the onion; any neutral oil or olive oil works.","2":"Sweet, savory depth in the tomato base; skipping leaves the stew flatter but still fine.","3":"Adds mild smokiness and red color to the base; a small seasoning, easy to skip.","4":"Earthy warmth that rounds out the paprika; skip with little loss.","5":"The main herb note of the stew; without it the casserole tastes only of tomato and lentil.","7":"Savory background sweetness; skip and add a little extra onion.","8":"Sweetness, color and bulk; skipping makes the stew leaner and less rounded.","9":"Second pepper, ideally a different color; skip if you have only one.","11":"Seasons the cooking liquid with salt and umami; plain water would make the stew taste flat.","12":"Soft, mild bulk that soaks up the tomato sauce; skip or use any summer squash.","13":"Fresh sprigs repeating the dried thyme; skip if you used the full amount of dried.","14":"Lentils are stirred in at the end, so use cooked or canned lentils; raw dry lentils need about 25 to 30 minutes of simmering with extra liquid."},"sub":{"0":{"x":"1 tbsp olive oil","d":"Slightly fruitier; no other change.","k":["olive oil"]},"11":{"x":"250 ml water + 1/2 tsp salt + 1 tsp soy sauce","d":"Soy sauce supplies the umami a stock cube would; less herbal.","k":["water","salt","soy sauce"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Sweat the onion slowly until truly soft (5-10 min) before adding anything else. There is no browned base in this stew, so undercooked onion tastes raw and cannot be fixed later.","s":0},{"t":"The recipe stirs the lentils in at the end, so use cooked or canned lentils (about 2 cans, drained). If you only have dry green or brown lentils, add them with the tomatoes and simmer 25-30 min, topping up water; red lentils would dissolve into the sauce.","s":3},{"t":"Cut carrots and peppers to the same size, and keep the zucchini in thick slices so it holds its shape through the long simmer.","s":2}],"rl":"FXXXXXVVVVVMVXP"},"52864":{"lv":"RERRRERRERRO","w":{"0":"Dried mushrooms give the soaking liquid that forms the savory backbone of the sauce; skip and use stock, with less depth.","2":"Gentle sweet onion base for the filling; skip and use a small onion.","3":"Garlic rounds out the mushroom filling; skipping makes it blander.","4":"Resinous herb that pairs with chestnut and mushroom; skip and use thyme.","6":"Adds umami and color to the mushrooms; replace with a pinch more salt and a splash of balsamic.","9":"Crisp topping that contrasts with the soft pasta; skipping loses the only crunch.","10":"Crisps in the breadcrumbs for an earthy, savory aroma; skip or use thyme.","11":"Pure aroma finish; skip with no change to the cooking.","7":"Adds acid and depth to the sauce; stock still makes it"},"sub":{"7":{"x":"125 ml vegetable stock + 1 tsp lemon juice","d":"Less fruity acidity; the lemon restores brightness.","k":["vegetable stock","lemon juice"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Strain the soaked dried-mushroom liquid through a coffee filter or fine sieve, leaving the last spoonful behind; the grit sinks to the bottom and ruins a smooth chestnut sauce.","s":0},{"t":"Cook the wild mushrooms in batches in a wide pan until their water has evaporated and they brown; crowding steams them and the filling turns watery.","s":1},{"t":"Blanch the lasagne sheets only until pliable, shock in ice water and dry them, so they roll without tearing and then finish cooking in the oven.","s":3}],"rl":"XBXXXVXMBGGG"},"52882":{"lv":"ERRREREREREEER","w":{"1":"Butter makes the mash rich and helps it brown; skip and use a little more milk or oil.","2":"Loosens the mash so it spreads; use cream or a little of the sauce if needed.","3":"Nutty topping that browns well; any strong cheddar can replace it.","5":"Soft, sweet onion flavor in the white sauce; skip and use a small onion.","9":"Fresh green note that cuts the richness; skip with some loss of freshness.","13":"Hard-boiled eggs add richness and texture; skip to save time with no structural effect.","7":"Adds acid and depth to the sauce; broth still makes it"},"sub":{"7":{"x":"150 ml chicken or vegetable broth + 1 tsp lemon juice","d":"Same acidity but without the wine's fruit; keep the sauce thick.","k":["chicken broth","lemon juice"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Steam-dry the drained potatoes in the colander for a few minutes before mashing. Stiff, dry mash holds a fork pattern and browns instead of sinking into the sauce.","s":1},{"t":"Cook the fish in the sauce for only about 2 minutes; it finishes in the oven (fish is done at 145F / 63C). Overcooked chunks turn dry and break up in the bake.","s":3},{"t":"Smoked haddock is salty, so taste the sauce before adding any salt, and let the filling cool slightly before topping so the mash does not sink.","s":3}],"rl":"BFMGFVTMMXPPPP"},"52887":{"lv":"ERRERORRREE","w":{"1":"Gentle aromatic for the poaching milk; skip with minimal change.","2":"Poaching milk softens the smokiness; plain water works but tastes sharper.","4":"Fresh green stirred through at the end; skip and use extra coriander.","5":"Second herb; skip if you have parsley.","6":"Cooking fat for the onion and curry powder; butter is traditional.","7":"Sweet base for the rice; skipping leaves the rice flatter.","8":"Rounds out the curry powder; skip with a slightly less complex flavor."},"sub":{"2":{"x":"300 ml water","d":"Less creamy poaching liquid; the fish tastes a bit more smoky.","k":["water"]},"6":{"x":"2 tbsp butter","d":"Richer and more traditional.","k":["butter"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Toast the curry powder with the onion for a minute before adding the rice so the spices bloom in oil; raw curry powder tastes dusty.","s":0},{"t":"Poach the haddock below a simmer, then check carefully for pin bones when flaking. Fold the fish and chopped egg into the rice gently with a fork so the grains stay separate.","s":2},{"t":"Boil the eggs 8-9 minutes (just set, with a slightly creamy yolk) and add them last so they keep their shape.","s":2}],"rl":"PXMPGGFXXXB"},"52959":{"lv":"ERRRREO","w":{"1":"Fresh herb finish with the fennel fronds; skip and use extra fronds.","2":"Brightens the oily salmon and sweet fennel; skipping makes the dish heavier.","3":"Sweet juicy pan sauce for the fish; skip with less moisture.","6":"Salty accent only; skip with no cooking effect.","4":"Coats the salmon; it still bakes with the tomatoes"},"sub":{"4":{"x":"1 tbsp melted butter or vegetable oil","d":"Butter is richer; fine either way.","k":["butter","vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Cut the fennel wedges through the core so each piece holds together, and boil until a knife just slides in; the oven only finishes the vegetables for 10 min.","s":1},{"t":"Zest the lemon before juicing: the recipe mixes zest with the fennel fronds and parsley, but only the juice is listed.","s":2},{"t":"Salmon is done at 145F (63C), when it just flakes but is still moist in the center; nestle it into the vegetables rather than burying it.","s":3}],"rl":"VGAVFPG"},"52960":{"lv":"EERERRRR","w":{"2":"Cool, crunchy contrast to the rich salmon and avocado; skipping loses crunch.","4":"Mint in the dressing provides most of the freshness; skip and use parsley.","5":"Acid carries the dressing; lemon works well.","6":"Balances the lime tartness; skip with a sharper dressing.","7":"Rubs the salmon and forms the dressing base; any neutral oil works."},"sub":{"5":{"x":"juice and zest of 1 lemon (3 tbsp juice)","d":"Slightly sweeter and less aromatic than lime.","k":["lemon"]},"6":{"x":"2 tsp maple syrup or 1 1/2 tsp sugar","d":"Same balance; maple adds mild caramel flavor.","k":["maple syrup","sugar"]},"7":{"x":"3 tbsp neutral vegetable oil","d":"Cleaner flavor; less fruity dressing.","k":["vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Pat the salmon very dry and do not move it for the first 3 minutes in a hot non-stick pan; that is how you get the crisp skin or crust while the center stays moist (aim for 145F / 63C).","s":3},{"t":"Slice the avocado at the last moment or toss it in a spoonful of the lime dressing; cut avocado browns quickly.","s":1},{"t":"Use baby spinach and dress it lightly with only half the dressing; 400 g of leaves wilts fast and warm salmon on top will soften it further.","s":2}],"rl":"PVVVXAWF"},"52884":{"lv":"RERERERERE","w":{"0":"Browning fat and the fat brushed on the potatoes; lard or oil also works, though butter gives better color.","2":"Traditional, tender richness; skip and add a handful more lamb.","4":"Sweetness and body in the gravy; skipping makes it plainer.","6":"Salty, tangy depth to the gravy; skip and use a splash of soy sauce.","7":"Liquid for the gravy; lamb or beef stock also works.","8":"Subtle aromatic; skip with minimal effect."},"sub":{"0":{"x":"6 tbsp vegetable oil, or 100 g lard or beef dripping","d":"Lard/dripping is the most authentic; oil gives a paler potato crust.","k":["vegetable oil","lard"]},"7":{"x":"500 ml beef broth","d":"A touch deeper and meatier; good with lamb.","k":["beef broth"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Brown the lamb in small batches without crowding the pan; crowded meat steams and loses the browned flavor that makes the gravy.","s":0},{"t":"Overlap the potato slices like roof tiles, keeping them thin and even, so they steam on top of the stew and then brown uniformly once the lid comes off.","s":1},{"t":"Brush the potato layer with fat before the final browning; finish under the grill if the oven top is not crisp enough.","s":2}],"rl":"FPPXVTXMXB"},"3337":{"lv":"EERROR","w":{"1":"Hot fat creates the crust; duck fat, lard or any high-heat oil also works.","2":"Seasons the potato inside and helps the surface break down for crunch; skip and they taste bland.","3":"Perfumes the oil as they roast; skip for plain potatoes.","4":"Small herb accent; skip with minor loss.","5":"Finishing crunch of salt on a hot crust; skip and season more before serving."},"sub":{"1":{"x":"1/3 cup melted duck fat or lard (or sunflower/canola oil)","d":"Duck fat or lard gives superior crunch and flavor; olive oil is too low in smoke point.","k":["duck fat","lard","canola oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Parboil until the edges are soft but the center is still firm, then shake the colander hard. The scuffed starchy surface is what fries into the crunchy crust.","s":1},{"t":"Let the oil get shimmering hot before adding the potatoes and do not crowd the pan; they should sizzle on contact or they absorb oil.","s":3},{"t":"Add the garlic and thyme only for the second half, as they burn and turn bitter over 50 minutes of high heat.","s":4}],"rl":"BFZXXZ"},"3338":{"lv":"ERRORRO","w":{"1":"Rounds out and gives gloss to the mash; olive oil works but is less creamy.","2":"Fresh mint is the classic partner for peas; skipping makes them taste plain.","3":"A pinch lifts the natural sweetness of the peas; skip with minimal change.","4":"Brightens the flavor so the peas do not taste flat or heavy; skip and add a splash of vinegar.","5":"Frozen peas need seasoning to taste like anything; adjust at the end.","6":"Mild background heat; skip with little change."},"sub":{"1":{"x":"3 tbsp olive oil","d":"Less creamy and more fruity; still good.","k":["olive oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Mash with a potato masher, not a blender; you want a coarse, part-whole texture. A little of the starchy cooking water loosens it without making it soupy.","s":3},{"t":"Add the mint and lemon at the very end; heat and acid dull the bright green color of peas and mint if they sit together.","s":4},{"t":"Do not boil the peas much longer than 5 minutes; overcooked peas turn gray-olive and taste of boiled vegetables.","s":0}],"rl":"VFXWAZZ"},"3339":{"lv":"EERERRO","w":{"2":"Sweet, savory background; skipping makes the cakes taste plainer.","3":"Browning fat with flavor; oil or bacon fat also works.","4":"Raises the frying temperature so the crust does not burn; use any neutral oil.","5":"Leftover mash and cabbage taste bland without plenty of salt.","6":"Mild heat; skip with minimal change."},"sub":{"3":{"x":"2 tbsp bacon fat or vegetable oil","d":"Bacon fat is the traditional flavor; oil is plainer.","k":["bacon fat","vegetable oil"]},"4":{"x":"1 tbsp more butter or bacon fat","d":"Butter browns faster; watch the heat.","k":["butter","bacon fat"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Press the mixture down firmly and leave it alone for the full 8 minutes; moving it breaks the crust that is the whole point of the dish.","s":4},{"t":"Use cold, firm, leftover mash; warm or freshly made mash is too wet and the cake falls apart.","s":1},{"t":"If you cannot flip the cake in one piece, cut it in quarters and flip each, or slide it onto a plate and invert it back into the pan with a little more fat.","s":4}],"rl":"BVXFFZZ"},"3340":{"lv":"EEROEEEEO","w":{"2":"Gives the sausage meat herbal depth; skip if the sausage is already seasoned.","3":"Mild heat; skip, most breakfast sausage is already peppered.","8":"Sharp, tangy foil to the fried sausage; serve with any mustard.","6":"The crumb coating of the Scotch eggs; without it they cannot be breaded","7":"The oil for frying the Scotch eggs; without it they cannot be fried"},"sub":{"6":{"x":"1 1/2 cups plain dry breadcrumbs","d":"Finer, less crunchy crust than panko.","k":["breadcrumbs"]},"7":{"x":"3 cups canola, sunflower or peanut oil","d":"Any neutral high-smoke oil works for a clean fry.","k":["canola oil","sunflower oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Chill the wrapped eggs for 20-30 minutes before crumbing and keep the oil near 340F (170C); too cool and the coating soaks up grease, too hot and the crust burns before the sausage cooks through to 160F (71C).","s":4},{"t":"Dry the peeled eggs and dust them well with flour before wrapping so the sausage grips and does not slip off in the pan.","s":2},{"t":"The 6-minute boil gives a jammy yolk that firms up a little in the hot oil. Drop them straight into ice water so the whites peel cleanly.","s":0}],"rl":"PPXZTTGFG"},"3341":{"lv":"ERRRRROERO","w":{"1":"Adds moisture and mild sweetness; skipping gives a drier, denser filling.","2":"Absorbs fat and keeps the filling tender; skip and the meat is firmer.","3":"Classic herb for pork; skip for a plainer filling.","4":"Sage is the signature flavor with pork; skip and use extra thyme.","5":"Seasons the pork; without salt the filling tastes flat.","6":"Mild heat; skip with little loss.","8":"Glues the pastry seam and gives a shiny brown finish; skip and use milk.","9":"Crunch and toasty garnish only; skip with no cooking effect."},"tips":[{"t":"Keep the pastry cold: if it softens while you roll the logs, chill the filled strips for 15 minutes before cutting. Warm puff pastry will not rise into flaky layers.","s":3},{"t":"Cut each roll with a sharp knife using a downward press, not a sawing motion, so the layers are not squashed. Place them seam-side down so they do not unroll.","s":4},{"t":"Pork must reach 160F (71C); check the center of the thickest roll. Bake on a preheated tray for a crisp base.","s":5}],"rl":"PXTXXZZSCG"},"3342":{"lv":"EEEEERORR","w":{"5":"Adds sharpness that keeps the cheese sauce from tasting heavy; skip and use a pinch of dry mustard or hot sauce.","6":"A hint of color and warmth; skip with minimal effect.","7":"Crisp topping that contrasts with the soft sauce; skip and add a bit more cheese.","8":"Cheese is salty, but the sauce still needs some seasoning; taste before baking."},"tips":[{"t":"Drain the cauliflower very well and let it steam off, or the florets weep and thin the sauce into a watery puddle in the dish.","s":0},{"t":"Add the cheese off the heat. Boiling cheddar goes grainy and splits; the sauce should be thick enough to coat the back of a spoon because it thins as it bakes.","s":3},{"t":"Do not overboil the florets: 5-6 minutes leaves them tender with a little bite, since they cook further in the oven.","s":0}],"rl":"VFTMFXCGZ"},"3446":{"lv":"EEEORO","w":{"3":"Light herbal accent while roasting; skip with minimal change.","4":"Brings out the sweetness of the parsnips; skipping makes them bland.","5":"Mild heat; skip with little effect.","1":"The roasting fat for the parsnips; without it they dry out and do not caramelize","2":"The honey is the glaze of the parsnips; without it they are not honey roasted"},"sub":{"1":{"x":"3 tbsp melted duck fat, lard or vegetable oil","d":"Duck fat crisps best; neutral oil also works.","k":["duck fat","vegetable oil"]},"2":{"x":"2 tbsp maple syrup","d":"Slightly less floral, equally sticky glaze.","k":["maple syrup"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Add the honey only for the last 8-10 minutes. Sugar scorches long before the parsnips are done, turning the glaze bitter.","s":4},{"t":"Cut out the woody core of any thick parsnip tops before cutting into batons, and keep them even in size, so they cook through at the same time.","s":0},{"t":"Roughen the parboiled parsnips by shaking the colander; the scuffed edges catch the oil and caramelize.","s":1}],"rl":"VFWXZZ"},"3447":{"lv":"EERRERRROOR","w":{"2":"Melts into the braise for sweetness and body; skip and add a bit more apple.","7":"Warm spice that suits the sweet-sour cabbage; skip with a plainer flavor.","8":"Strong and a little goes a long way; skip with minor loss.","9":"Subtle background aroma; skip with little change.","10":"Seasoning for the long braise; taste at the end and adjust.","3":"Softens the cabbage; oil or other fat covers it","4":"The acid that sets the red cabbage color and balances the braise; without it the cabbage turns blue and flat","5":"Balances the vinegar; the apple also sweetens","6":"Braising liquid and sweetness; vinegar and water cover it"},"sub":{"3":{"x":"3 tbsp vegetable oil or bacon fat","d":"Bacon fat adds a smoky note; oil is neutral.","k":["vegetable oil","bacon fat"]},"4":{"x":"1/4 cup apple cider vinegar","d":"Slightly fruitier; acidity is the same and still keeps the color.","k":["apple cider vinegar"]},"5":{"x":"1/4 cup white sugar + 1 tsp molasses","d":"Almost identical; molasses restores the brown-sugar depth.","k":["sugar","molasses"]},"6":{"x":"1/2 cup water + 1 tbsp sugar","d":"Less fruity; add a little more vinegar at the end.","k":["water","sugar"]}},"tips":[{"t":"The vinegar is not just for flavor: acid keeps red cabbage a vibrant purple-red. Without it, the pigments turn dull blue-gray as it cooks.","s":2},{"t":"Stir halfway and keep the pot tightly covered so the cabbage braises in its own juices; add a splash of water if it looks dry.","s":3},{"t":"Finish uncovered on the stove if it is wet, then balance with more vinegar or sugar. It tastes better reheated the next day.","s":4}],"rl":"VVXFAWMXXXZ"},"3448":{"lv":"ERRREOR","w":{"1":"Infuses the cooking water so the potatoes taste of mint inside; skip and use more chopped mint at the end.","2":"Seasons the potatoes from inside while they cook; add it to cold water.","5":"Mild heat; skip with little change.","6":"Final seasoning after butter is added; taste first.","3":"Glosses the potatoes; the mint and salt still season them"},"sub":{"3":{"x":"3 tbsp olive oil","d":"Less rich but fruity; still glossy.","k":["olive oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Start the potatoes in cold, well-salted water so they cook evenly from the outside to the center, and test for doneness with a knife rather than by time.","s":1},{"t":"Steam-dry the drained potatoes in the hot pot for a minute so the butter coats them instead of sliding off.","s":2},{"t":"Chop the mint right before tossing it in; cut mint blackens and loses its aroma if it sits.","s":3}],"rl":"BXZFXZZ"},"3449":{"lv":"EERRO","w":{"2":"Glazes the bacon and adds sweet-salty caramelization; skip with plainer flavor.","3":"Tang that cuts the richness; skip and use a pinch more honey.","4":"Small herb note; skip with little change."},"tips":[{"t":"Stretch each bacon slice with the flat of a knife until it is long and thin, then wrap it tightly with slight overlap so it crisps instead of staying fatty.","s":1},{"t":"Roast on a wire rack set over the tray so the bacon renders and crisps on all sides; the sausages need 160F (71C) inside.","s":4},{"t":"If the glaze darkens too fast, brush it on only for the last 10 minutes; honey burns easily.","s":3}],"rl":"PFWXX"},"3450":{"lv":"REERREROE","w":{"0":"Fat for the roux that carries the beer and cheese; oil works if you prefer.","2":"Adds bitter malt depth that defines rarebit; milk gives a milder, creamier result.","3":"Salty, tangy depth; skip and add a dash of soy sauce.","4":"Sharp heat that keeps the cheese from tasting heavy; Dijon also works.","6":"Enriches the topping and helps it puff and brown under the broiler; the yolk is only lightly cooked, so use a pasteurized egg for vulnerable guests.","7":"Mild warmth; skip with little change."},"sub":{"0":{"x":"1 tbsp vegetable oil","d":"Roux works the same; slightly less rich.","k":["vegetable oil"]},"2":{"x":"1/2 cup whole milk + 1/2 tsp extra mustard","d":"Loses the bitter, malty edge; mustard adds back some bite.","k":["milk","mustard"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Stir the cheddar in off the heat in handfuls, only until melted; overheated cheese turns stringy and oily, and the egg yolk can curdle if the sauce is too hot.","s":3},{"t":"Spread the mixture right to the crust edges, otherwise the bare toast burns under the broiler before the topping browns.","s":4},{"t":"The sauce keeps for days in the fridge; cold, it spreads even more thickly and browns better.","s":-1}],"rl":"FTMXXFTXB"},"3451":{"lv":"EREERRRRROO","w":{"1":"Sharp, creamy blue contrast to the cheddar; skip and add more cheddar.","4":"For spreading on the bread, as the traditional ploughman's pairs; skip with a drier plate.","5":"Sweet-tangy relish that balances the salty cheese and ham; the defining accompaniment.","6":"Sharp, crunchy acid that cleans the palate; skip if you have the pickles.","7":"Adds protein and a creamy bite; skip for a lighter board.","8":"Fresh sweet-tart crunch against the cheese; skip with little loss.","9":"Adds crunch only; skip.","10":"Briny crunch; skip if you have pickled onions."},"tips":[{"t":"Take the cheeses out of the fridge 30-45 minutes before serving; cold cheese tastes muted and waxy.","s":1},{"t":"Cut cheese in chunky wedges rather than thin slices, which dry out and sweat on the board. Pair a sweet chutney with a sharp pickle so the plate has both.","s":1},{"t":"Plunge the boiled eggs in cold water right after 10 minutes to stop the cooking and avoid a gray ring around the yolk. Do not leave ham and eggs out for more than 2 hours.","s":0}],"rl":"PPPBFXGPVVG"},"3452":{"lv":"EOEEREROROOR","w":{"1":"Used only to bloom the curry powder; butter works.","4":"Lightens and tangs up the mayonnaise; swap for sour cream or more mayo.","5":"Sweet fruit and acid that make the dressing taste like coronation chicken; jam plus lemon approximates it.","6":"Cuts the richness of the mayo; skip and add a bit more chutney.","7":"Adds color and rounded sweetness; skip with little change.","8":"Sweet chewy bursts that echo the chutney; skipping makes it less sweet.","9":"Crunch on top; skip or use another toasted nut.","10":"Fresh oniony garnish; skip.","11":"Mayonnaise and chutney need seasoning; taste after chilling."},"sub":{"1":{"x":"1 tsp butter","d":"Butter blooms the spices just as well.","k":["butter"]},"4":{"x":"1/4 cup sour cream","d":"Tangier and slightly richer; keeps the dressing thick.","k":["sour cream"]},"5":{"x":"2 tbsp apricot jam + 1/2 tsp lemon juice","d":"Sweeter and less spicy; the lemon restores acidity.","k":["apricot jam","lemon juice"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Cook the curry powder in oil for a minute, then cool it completely before whisking it into the mayo; raw curry powder tastes harsh and hot spices will split a warm dressing.","s":0},{"t":"Pull the rotisserie chicken into bite-size chunks rather than shredding fine, so the salad has texture. Keep it chilled below 40F (4C); do not leave it out for more than 2 hours.","s":3},{"t":"Chill for 30 minutes before serving so the flavors meld, then add the almonds at the last second to keep them crunchy.","s":4}],"rl":"PFXFFWACVGGZ"},"3453":{"lv":"EREREROO","w":{"1":"Draws water out of the cucumber so the sandwiches do not turn soggy.","3":"Butter seals the bread against moisture; skip and the bread goes damp.","5":"Fresh anise note that goes well with cucumber; skip or use chives.","6":"A little acid to wake up the cream cheese; skip with minor change.","7":"Mild background heat; skip.","4":"The spread that holds the cucumber on the bread; without it the sandwiches lose their base"},"sub":{"4":{"x":"1/2 cup mascarpone or well-drained ricotta","d":"Milder and creamier; add a pinch of salt.","k":["mascarpone","ricotta"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Slice the cucumber paper-thin with a mandoline, salt it and press it between paper towels; any leftover moisture will make the bread soggy within minutes.","s":0},{"t":"Spread the butter all the way to the edges of each slice; it is a waterproof barrier, and edge gaps are where moisture seeps in.","s":2},{"t":"Cut with a serrated knife using a gentle sawing motion; cover with a damp cloth and chill so the bread stays soft rather than curling.","s":4}],"rl":"VZBFFXAZ"},"3456":{"lv":"EERRERRORRO","w":{"2":"Softens the leeks; olive oil works.","3":"Mellow savory note; skip and add a bit more leek.","5":"Lightens the cream so the gratin is not heavy; more cream also works.","6":"Herbal note that pairs with cream and leeks; skip with minor loss.","7":"A pinch rounds off the cream; skip.","8":"Gives a golden, savory crust and sharpness; skip and use less salt.","9":"Thick layers of potato need salt; undersalted gratin tastes bland.","10":"Mild heat; skip."},"sub":{"2":{"x":"2 tbsp olive oil","d":"Less rich; still softens the leeks.","k":["olive oil"]},"5":{"x":"1/2 cup half-and-half or 1/2 cup more heavy cream","d":"More cream is richer; half-and-half stays closer to the lighter original.","k":["half and half","heavy cream"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Slice the potatoes evenly at 1/8 inch (3 mm), ideally with a mandoline, and do not rinse them; the surface starch helps thicken the cream.","s":3},{"t":"Pour the cream mixture over warm, not cold; warm cream gets the bake going and reduces the chance of curdling.","s":4},{"t":"Keep the foil on for the first 40 minutes so the potatoes steam tender, then uncover to brown; a knife should slide through with no resistance.","s":5}],"rl":"BVFXFMXXGZZ"},"3457":{"lv":"EERRRERORO","w":{"1":"Fat for sweating the leeks; olive oil works.","2":"Mellow savory lift; skip and add a little more leek.","3":"Lightly thickens the cream sauce; skip and simmer a bit longer.","4":"Liquid for the sauce; water plus salt works.","6":"Tang that cuts through the cream; skip with a heavier sauce.","7":"Garnish with a mild onion flavor; skip.","8":"Seasoning for the sauce; taste after thickening.","9":"Mild heat; skip."},"sub":{"1":{"x":"3 tbsp olive oil","d":"Less rich but still silky.","k":["olive oil"]},"4":{"x":"1/2 cup water + 1/4 tsp salt","d":"Less savory; add a bit more mustard if it tastes flat.","k":["water","salt"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Slice the leeks first, then swish them in a basin of cold water so the sand sinks; lifting them out leaves the grit behind.","s":1},{"t":"Cook the leeks covered over medium-low heat so they steam in their own moisture and turn silky without browning.","s":1},{"t":"Simmer the cream uncovered until it coats the leeks; it will thicken more as it stands, so stop a little before it looks done.","s":3}],"rl":"VFXTMFXGZZ"},"3458":{"lv":"EEEERERRO","w":{"0":"Fat that carries the sage and onion flavors; olive oil works.","4":"Earthy backup to the sage; skip and use extra sage.","5":"Moistens the bread; vegetable broth works well.","6":"Binds the stuffing so it slices; skipping makes it crumblier.","7":"Bread and onion need salt; taste the mixture before baking.","8":"Mild heat; skip."},"sub":{"0":{"x":"4 tbsp olive oil","d":"Less rich and no dairy; fine.","k":["olive oil"]},"5":{"x":"3/4 cup vegetable broth","d":"Good vegetarian option; use unsalted if you can.","k":["vegetable broth"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Use truly stale or oven-dried bread cubes; fresh bread turns gummy once the broth and egg go in. The stuffing should be moist but not soggy.","s":3},{"t":"Cook the onions slowly until translucent, 10-12 minutes; browned onion changes the flavor from sweet and mild to savory.","s":1},{"t":"If you cook the stuffing inside a bird instead of a dish, it must reach 165F (74C) in the center because of the egg and raw juices.","s":5}],"rl":"FXBXXMTZZ"},"3459":{"lv":"EEREERRORO","w":{"2":"Subtle aromatic for the milk; skip with minor loss.","5":"Adds gloss and richness; skip with a less rounded sauce.","6":"Silky finishing richness; milk can replace it.","7":"A pinch brings out the dairy flavor; skip.","8":"Needed to bring out the milk and onion flavors; taste first.","9":"Mild heat; skip."},"tips":[{"t":"Stud the onion halves with the cloves so they can be fished out whole; loose cloves are a hazard and bitter if left in.","s":0},{"t":"Infuse the milk off the heat for the full 20 minutes; that is where the flavor comes from, and heat would scorch the milk.","s":1},{"t":"Bread sauce thickens a lot as it sits, so loosen it with a splash of hot milk right before serving.","s":5}],"rl":"XXXMTFFXZZ"},"3504":{"lv":"ERRREERRROO","w":{"1":"Fat for sweating the vegetables; olive oil works.","2":"Sweet soft base for the soup; skipping leaves it thinner in flavor.","3":"Gentle onion-like sweetness; skip and add more onion.","6":"Makes the soup more savory; water works because the haddock is already salty.","7":"Gentle aroma for the poaching milk; skip.","8":"Finishing richness; skip for a lighter chowder.","9":"Fresh finish; skip.","10":"Mild heat; skip."},"sub":{"1":{"x":"2 tbsp olive oil","d":"Fine for sweating; slightly less rich.","k":["olive oil"]},"6":{"x":"2 cups water (no added salt)","d":"A less savory soup; the smoked haddock is salty, so extra salt is rarely needed.","k":["water"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Taste before salting: smoked haddock and chicken broth are salty, and the poaching milk carries that salt into the soup.","s":3},{"t":"Mash about a third of the potatoes against the pot side to thicken the soup naturally, with no flour; leave the rest in chunks.","s":4},{"t":"Add the flaked fish and cream at the end and keep below a simmer; boiling toughens the fish and can split the cream.","s":5}],"rl":"PFXVBMMXFGZ"},"3506":{"lv":"EERERRERRORO","w":{"2":"Breaks down and thickens the broth; skip and use a little more barley.","4":"Sweet, mildly peppery root; swede or parsnip can replace it.","5":"Soft, sweet onion flavor; skip and use more onion.","7":"Savory background note; skip with minor loss.","8":"Added last for fresh green sweetness and texture; skip with a more brothy soup.","9":"Fresh finishing herb; skip.","10":"Soup made with plain water needs seasoning; add gradually and taste.","11":"Mild heat; skip."},"tips":[{"t":"Start the lamb in cold water and bring it up slowly, skimming the foam; this gives a clear broth rather than a cloudy one.","s":0},{"t":"Barley and split peas keep absorbing liquid, so add hot water to thin the broth before serving leftovers.","s":5},{"t":"Skim the fat from the top after pulling the meat off the bones. Shoulder is fatty, and the broth is better for it.","s":4}],"rl":"PBBVVVXVVGZZ"},"52784":{"lv":"RERRERROROOEEREREOO","w":{"0":"Only the cooking fat for the base; any oil works.","2":"Gentle sweet onion note; skip and use more onion.","3":"Savory backbone of the base; skipping makes the chili flatter.","5":"Citrusy earthy depth beside the cumin; skip with a slightly narrower spice profile.","6":"Adds smoke and color; skip and add a bit of chili powder.","7":"Subtle warm background; skip with almost no change.","8":"Gives heat and the chili character; skip for a mild, cumin-forward stew.","9":"Deepens color and bitterness without tasting chocolatey; skip with little loss.","10":"Minor herbal note; skip.","13":"Sweetness and bulk to balance the spices; skip with a leaner pot.","15":"Seasoning for the lentils and sauce; taste at the end.","17":"Only for the cashew sour cream topping; skip and use yogurt or sour cream.","18":"Adds brightness to the cashew cream; lemon juice works."},"sub":{"0":{"x":"1 tbsp vegetable oil","d":"Neutral; no other change.","k":["vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Cook the lentils separately in plain water and drain, as the recipe does. Tomatoes and acid keep lentils firm, so adding them straight to the tomato base can leave them hard.","s":1},{"t":"Toast the ground spices in the oil with the onions for about a minute before adding tomato, to bloom the cumin and paprika; this is where the smoky flavor comes from.","s":2},{"t":"Roast the squash until the edges caramelize; steamed squash goes mushy when folded in, but roasted wedges keep their shape.","s":0}],"rl":"FXXXXXXXXXXVMVPZVGA"},"52841":{"lv":"RERRRRRRREERE","w":{"0":"Fat for sweating the vegetables; butter works.","2":"Savory sweetness behind the tomato; skip with little loss.","3":"Sweetness and body that tame the acidity; skip for a sharper soup.","4":"Starch thickens and makes the soup creamy; skip for a thinner soup.","5":"Subtle aroma while simmering; remove before blending.","6":"Concentrates tomato flavor and color; skip with a lighter taste.","7":"Offsets acidity of the tomatoes; skip and the soup tastes sharp.","8":"Gives acid balance and brightness; apple cider vinegar works.","10":"Smooth tomato base; crushed tomatoes also work.","11":"Provides salt and savory depth; real broth works.","12":"Softens acidity and gives creaminess; do not boil after adding."},"sub":{"0":{"x":"3 tbsp butter","d":"Richer and sweeter flavor.","k":["butter"]},"8":{"x":"2 tbsp apple cider vinegar","d":"Slightly fruitier acid; works the same.","k":["apple cider vinegar"]},"10":{"x":"500 g crushed tomatoes or extra diced tomatoes","d":"Slightly chunkier before blending; blend until smooth.","k":["crushed tomatoes"]},"11":{"x":"1 litre vegetable or chicken broth in place of water and cubes","d":"Fewer salt spikes and a cleaner flavor; season to taste.","k":["vegetable broth","chicken broth"]},"12":{"x":"200 ml heavy cream + 200 ml water","d":"Richer and silkier; stir in off the heat.","k":["heavy cream","water"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Sweat the onion, celery, carrot and potato slowly for the full 10-15 minutes before adding tomato; this base is what makes the soup sweet rather than just acidic.","s":0},{"t":"Remove the bay leaves before blending, and blend hot soup in batches with the lid vented; steam builds up fast.","s":1},{"t":"Add the milk only when reheating, and keep it below a boil so the acidic tomato does not make it curdle.","s":2}],"rl":"FXXVTXXWAVVZM"},"52842":{"lv":"RERRRREEE","w":{"0":"Fat for sweating the onion; olive oil works.","2":"Savory background note; skip and use more leek.","3":"Mild sweet onion flavor; skip and add more onion.","4":"Natural thickener for the soup; skip and it will be thinner.","5":"Adds richness and silky body; skip with a slightly lighter soup.","6":"Liquid for the soup; water plus bouillon works."},"sub":{"0":{"x":"2 tbsp olive oil","d":"Slightly fruitier; fine.","k":["olive oil"]},"6":{"x":"1 liter hot water + 1 vegetable bouillon cube","d":"Slightly less rounded; Stilton adds salt so go light on seasoning.","k":["water","bouillon cube"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Add the chunky broccoli stalks early and the florets only for the last 5 minutes; this keeps the soup bright green instead of dull olive.","s":2},{"t":"Stir in the Stilton off the heat and leave a few lumps; boiling cheese turns oily, and the lumps give pockets of sharp flavor.","s":3},{"t":"Taste before salting: Stilton and stock are both salty, so season at the end.","s":3}],"rl":"FXXXTFMVX"},"52867":{"lv":"EEER","w":{"3":"Serves as the base for the chilli; serve with rice or tortillas if you do not have it."},"tips":[{"t":"The recipe has no spices, so add them yourself: 1 tsp each of cumin and smoked paprika stirred into the vegetables as they roast gives the beans real chilli flavor.","s":0},{"t":"Drain and rinse the kidney beans well; the canning liquid is starchy and slightly metallic, and makes the chilli dull.","s":1},{"t":"Roast the vegetables until the edges char; browned edges bring a flavor that a 10-minute simmer cannot.","s":0}],"rl":"VPVB"},"52873":{"lv":"RREERERRRRRERROEEEE","w":{"0":"Browning fat; any oil works.","1":"Adds richness and browning; use oil if not available.","4":"Savory depth for the gravy; skip with a plainer stew.","6":"Savory background that sweetens as it cooks; skip with a simpler stew.","7":"Sweetness and body in the gravy; skip with a leaner stew.","8":"Mild onion flavor; skip and use more onion.","9":"Sweet, slightly peppery root; carrot or turnip works.","10":"Deglazes the pan and adds acidity; broth plus vinegar works.","12":"Subtle aroma while it cooks; skip.","13":"3 tbsp of dried thyme is very strong; 1 tbsp is plenty, or use fresh sprigs.","14":"Fresh finish; skip.","17":"Gives light, tender dumplings; very cold grated butter works."},"sub":{"0":{"x":"2 tbsp vegetable oil","d":"Neutral; no change.","k":["vegetable oil"]},"1":{"x":"2 tbsp more oil","d":"Less richness in the gravy.","k":["vegetable oil"]},"10":{"x":"150 ml beef broth + 1 tbsp red wine vinegar","d":"Less fruity depth; the vinegar replaces the wine's acid.","k":["beef broth","red wine vinegar"]},"17":{"x":"60 g very cold grated butter or vegetable shortening","d":"Dumplings are a bit denser and richer than with suet; keep it very cold.","k":["butter","shortening"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Brown the beef in small batches until dark on all sides; the crust is what flavors the gravy, and crowded meat just steams.","s":1},{"t":"Do not overwork the dumpling dough; mix just until it comes together, so the dumplings stay light rather than tough.","s":7},{"t":"Place the dumplings on top of the simmering stew, not submerged, and keep the lid on for the first part of the cooking so they steam; do not lift the lid early.","s":9}],"rl":"FFPTXXVVVVMMXXGSLFM"},"3508":{"lv":"EERRE","w":{"2":"Sweetness and Scottish flavor; maple syrup also works.","3":"Smoky warmth that makes it a cranachan; skip and add vanilla."},"sub":{"2":{"x":"3 tbsp maple syrup","d":"Less floral than heather honey; still sweet.","k":["maple syrup"]},"3":{"x":"1 tsp vanilla extract + 1 tbsp orange juice","d":"Alcohol-free; no smoky edge.","k":["vanilla extract","orange juice"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Toast the oats in a dry pan, stirring constantly, until nutty; they go from golden to burnt in seconds. Cool them fully or they will melt the cream.","s":0},{"t":"Whip the cream only to soft peaks; folding in the honey and whisky thickens it a bit more, so stiff cream turns grainy.","s":2},{"t":"Assemble just before serving; the oats lose their crunch quickly in the cream.","s":5}],"rl":"BFWXV"},"52767":{"lv":"EEEEEEEERR","w":{"8":"Boosts the almond flavor of the filling; skip and the tart tastes buttery, not distinctly Bakewell.","9":"Toasty crunch and the classic look; skip with a smooth top."},"tips":[{"t":"Bake the pastry blind until it is dry and set, then spread only a thin layer of jam; too much jam bubbles up through the frangipane and makes the base soggy.","s":3},{"t":"Stir the sugar into the melted butter off the heat and let it cool slightly before adding the egg, or the egg will start to scramble.","s":5},{"t":"Cover the tart loosely with foil once the flaked almonds are golden; the filling needs the full bake time to set in the center. Cool it in the tin before slicing.","s":6}],"rl":"SFMWFWSTXG"},"52768":{"lv":"EEEEEEERR","w":{"0":"Makes the crumb base; graham crackers work the same way.","2":"Tart cooking apples that hold a sharp edge against the sweet almond filling; Granny Smith keeps its shape better.","7":"Boosts the almond flavor; skip with a milder, buttery filling.","8":"Toasty crunch on top; skip."},"sub":{"0":{"x":"175 g graham crackers","d":"Slightly sweeter and more honeyed crust.","k":["graham cracker"]},"2":{"x":"200 g Granny Smith apples","d":"Holds its shape rather than collapsing like Bramley; tart and firm.","k":["apple"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Press the buttery crumbs firmly into the tin with the bottom of a glass so the base slices cleanly instead of crumbling.","s":1},{"t":"Slice the apples at the last minute and overlap them tightly; they shrink and brown quickly, and gaps show as bare patches.","s":3},{"t":"Cool the tart 15 minutes before removing the sides; stand the tin on a can and let the ring drop.","s":5}],"rl":"BFVFWTSXG"},"52788":{"lv":"EEEREE","w":{"2":"Gives the flapjacks their chew; corn syrup or honey works.","3":"Orange perfumes the rich dark sugar and fruit; skip with a slightly flatter flavor.","5":"Brings the Christmas spice and fruit; chopped dried fruit and spice replaces it."},"sub":{"2":{"x":"150 g light corn syrup or honey","d":"A little less caramel; honey makes a slightly softer, sweeter flapjack.","k":["corn syrup","honey"],"wn":"Honey is not safe for infants under 1 year"},"5":{"x":"250 g chopped dried fruit (raisins, currants) + 1 tsp cinnamon or mixed spice","d":"Same fruity chew without the booze and nuts; fine.","k":["raisins","cinnamon"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Melt the butter, sugar and syrup gently and stop as soon as the sugar dissolves; boiling it hardens the flapjacks into brittle toffee.","s":0},{"t":"Press the mixture firmly into the tin and take it out of the oven while the center still looks slightly soft; it firms up as it cools.","s":1},{"t":"Cool completely in the tin before cutting; warm flapjacks fall apart.","s":1}],"rl":"FWTXBV"},"52791":{"lv":"EEEOO","w":{"3":"Warm spiced note that lifts the berries; skip with a plainer cream.","4":"Garnish only; skip with no change."},"tips":[{"t":"Fold in the crushed meringue just before serving; its sugar dissolves into the cream within the hour, leaving a soggy, sweet pudding instead of crisp shards.","s":1},{"t":"Whip the cream to stiff peaks before adding the purée, since the juice loosens it; fold gently to keep a marbled, not uniformly pink, look.","s":1},{"t":"Macerate a few of the chopped strawberries in a pinch of sugar for 10 minutes if they are tart or underripe.","s":0}],"rl":"VFBXG"},"52792":{"lv":"RERREREEO","w":{"0":"Butter on the bread gives a crisp, golden top; margarine also works.","2":"Sweet fruit bursts through the custard; skip or use raisins.","3":"Warms the layers; skip with a plainer flavor.","5":"Makes the custard silkier; skip and use more milk.","8":"Classic custard spice; skip."},"sub":{"0":{"x":"2 tbsp margarine (25 g)","d":"Similar crisp top; less buttery flavor.","k":["margarine"]},"5":{"x":"50 ml more whole milk (400 ml total)","d":"A slightly less rich custard.","k":["milk"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Let the assembled pudding stand 30 minutes before baking so the bread soaks up the custard; bake it in a hot-water bath if you want the most silky texture.","s":6},{"t":"Warm the milk only to scalding; boiling it risks scrambling the eggs when combined. The custard is done when it is just set with a slight wobble and about 160F (71C).","s":3},{"t":"Strain the custard to remove any bits of egg white so the surface is smooth.","s":5}],"rl":"FBVXMFSWX"},"52793":{"lv":"EEREEEEEREOEEER","w":{"2":"Rounds off the dates; skip with a slightly less aromatic sponge.","3":"Leavening plus flour; substitute plain flour and baking powder.","7":"Gives a caramel note and moisture; light or dark brown sugar works.","8":"Adds deep bitter-sweet molasses flavor; molasses works the same.","10":"Serving extra; skip.","14":"Gives the sauce a darker, more complex flavor; skip with a sweeter, simpler sauce."},"sub":{"3":{"x":"175 g all-purpose flour + 2 1/2 tsp baking powder + pinch salt","d":"Works like self-rising flour; sift the baking powder in well.","k":["flour","baking powder"]},"7":{"x":"140 g light or dark brown sugar","d":"Slightly less crunchy-caramel; same moisture.","k":["brown sugar"]},"8":{"x":"2 tbsp dark molasses","d":"Milder and less bitter than black treacle.","k":["molasses"]},"14":{"x":"1 tbsp dark molasses","d":"Same dark, bitter-sweet depth.","k":["molasses"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Soak the dates in boiling water and stir the baking soda in only at the end. The soda reacts with the acid in the dates and treacle, so bake right after mixing or the sponge loses lift.","s":1},{"t":"Stir the sauce over medium heat until the sugar has fully dissolved before it boils; undissolved sugar makes the sauce grainy.","s":2},{"t":"Loosen the puddings from the tins before turning them out, and warm them under the sauce so they soak it up.","s":3}],"rl":"VMXSLSFWWMGWFFW"},"52853":{"lv":"EEERRRRO","w":{"4":"Cuts the richness and keeps the flavor bright; skip and the mousse tastes heavy.","5":"Rounds out the chocolate flavor; skip with a little less depth.","6":"Thins the blend to a smooth mousse texture; a splash of milk also works.","7":"Sharpens the chocolate flavor; skip.","3":"Sweetens the mousse; the chocolate still sweetens it"},"sub":{"3":{"x":"2 tbsp maple syrup","d":"Less floral; the mousse is just as sweet.","k":["maple syrup"],"wn":"Honey is not safe for infants under 1 year; maple syrup is a safe alternative"}},"tips":[{"t":"Use a very ripe, soft avocado with no green or stringy flesh, and blend for a full 1-2 minutes, scraping the sides, until it is completely silky.","s":0},{"t":"A frozen banana gives a thick, cold mousse; a regular banana works if you chill the mousse for an hour afterwards.","s":3},{"t":"Taste after blending and adjust: cacao is bitter, and a bit more honey or lemon changes the balance quickly.","s":1}],"rl":"VXFWAXMZ"},"52886":{"lv":"EREEERREO","w":{"0":"Provides rise and structure; plain flour plus baking powder works.","1":"Seasons the dough; skipping makes the pudding taste flat.","2":"Gives the light, soft crumb of a steamed pudding; very cold grated butter works.","5":"Zest brightens the rich dough and currants; skip with a plainer flavor.","6":"Pairs with the lemon; skip and use more lemon.","8":"Serving only; skip with a drier pudding."},"sub":{"0":{"x":"250 g all-purpose flour + 3 1/2 tsp baking powder","d":"Works like self-rising flour; sift well so the baking powder is evenly distributed.","k":["flour","baking powder"]},"2":{"x":"125 g very cold grated butter or vegetable shortening","d":"Denser and a bit richer than suet; work quickly to keep it cold.","k":["butter","shortening"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Wrap the roll loosely in a pleated parchment and foil sheet; the pudding expands as it steams, and a tight wrap makes it dense.","s":2},{"t":"Keep the steamer water boiling and top it up with boiling water; if the pan boils dry the pudding scorches and the wrap burns.","s":3},{"t":"Mix the dough to a firm but moist consistency, adding the milk gradually; too wet makes it heavy and sticky.","s":1}],"rl":"SZFVWXXMG"},"52888":{"lv":"EEREERERORRRO","w":{"2":"A little acid relaxes the pastry and brightens the dough; skip with minor change.","5":"Traditional chewy citrus note in the filling; skip and add more currants.","7":"Warm spice that makes the filling Christmassy; skip and use more allspice.","8":"Small warm accent; skip.","9":"Gives the traditional mixed-spice flavor; skip with a plainer filling.","10":"Brightens the fruit filling; skip.","11":"Glaze for a shiny, golden top; milk can replace it.","12":"Crunchy sparkle on top; skip."},"tips":[{"t":"Freeze the diced butter solid and keep the pastry cold throughout; the folds and the cold fat create the flaky layers, and warm butter smears into the flour.","s":0},{"t":"Roll the pastry out to just thicker than a coin, and cool the filling before spooning it on so it does not melt the layers.","s":3},{"t":"Pinch the seams tightly, flatten each cake gently with the seam side down, and slash the top so steam can escape; otherwise the filling bursts out and burns.","s":3}],"rl":"FSAFVVWXXXXGG"},"52889":{"lv":"EEREEE","w":{"2":"Adds tartness; extra raspberries or blackberries work."},"sub":{"2":{"x":"100 g more raspberries or blackberries","d":"Slightly sweeter and less tart.","k":["raspberries","blackberries"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Use sliced white sandwich bread that is a day old and has no crusts; it soaks up juice evenly without turning to paste.","s":1},{"t":"Dip every piece of bread fully in the hot fruit juice; any dry white patch will show on the turned-out pudding.","s":2},{"t":"Weigh the pudding down with a plate and tins overnight, and save the leftover juice to pour over any pale spots when it is turned out.","s":3}],"rl":"VVVVWS"},"52890":{"lv":"REREEEO","w":{"0":"Rubbed into the flour for a tender dough; shortening or lard also works.","1":"Provides rise; plain flour plus baking powder works.","2":"A little vanilla flavor; skip or use extract.","3":"Gives the traditional light texture; cold grated butter is a substitute.","6":"Served on the side; skip."},"sub":{"0":{"x":"50 g vegetable shortening or lard","d":"Slightly less buttery; same tenderness.","k":["shortening","lard"]},"1":{"x":"250 g all-purpose flour + 3 tsp baking powder + 1/2 tsp salt","d":"Equivalent to self-rising flour.","k":["flour","baking powder","salt"]},"3":{"x":"50 g very cold grated butter","d":"Denser and richer than suet; work quickly.","k":["butter"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Leave a border of about 2 cm clear of jam at one long edge so the roll seals; jam at the seam will bubble out and burn.","s":2},{"t":"Wrap the roll loosely in parchment and foil with a pleat on top so it has room to expand, as it swells while it cooks.","s":2},{"t":"Make the dough quickly and handle it as little as possible; overworking makes the pudding heavy.","s":1}],"rl":"FSXFMWG"},"52891":{"lv":"RRRRRREERRERO","w":{"0":"Only for the hazelnut biscuits served with the fool; skip with plain shop biscuits.","1":"Biscuit ingredient; make the biscuits or serve with shortbread.","2":"Biscuit ingredient; part of the biscuit dough only.","3":"Zest for the biscuit dough; skip.","4":"Biscuit ingredient; part of the biscuit dough only.","5":"Leavens the biscuits; needed only if you bake them.","8":"Extra sugar to adjust the sweetness of the purée; add to taste.","9":"Brightens the blackberries; skip and the fool tastes flat.","11":"Lightens the cream and adds tang; use more cream instead.","12":"Garnish; skip."},"sub":{"11":{"x":"100 ml more heavy cream, whipped (400 ml total)","d":"Richer and less tangy.","k":["heavy cream"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Press the cooked blackberries through a sieve to remove the seeds, and chill the purée completely before folding it in; warm purée deflates the whipped cream.","s":7},{"t":"Whip the cream and yogurt only to soft peaks; the acid in the fruit thickens the cream further when you fold them together.","s":8},{"t":"Fold just once or twice so the fool stays marbled; over-stirring turns it a flat purple.","s":9}],"rl":"GFWXSLVWWAFFG"},"52892":{"lv":"EEEERR","w":{"4":"Zest cuts the syrup sweetness; skip and add a squeeze of lemon juice.","5":"Egg wash for the lattice; milk also works."},"tips":[{"t":"Heat the syrup just enough to loosen it, then stir in the fresh breadcrumbs; the filling should be a thick paste, so add more crumbs if it runs.","s":7},{"t":"Bake the tart on a preheated heavy tray so the base crisps; a cold tin gives a soggy, pale bottom.","s":12},{"t":"Leave the tart to firm up in the tin; the filling sets as it cools, and cutting it hot makes it run.","s":14}],"rl":"SFWTXG"},"52893":{"lv":"EEEERRROO","w":{"4":"Caramelizes with the sugar to coat the apples; skip and use oil.","5":"Gives caramel flavor to the compote; light brown sugar works.","6":"Tart color and juice against the apples; skip with a plainer filling.","7":"Warm background spice; skip.","8":"Serving extra; skip."},"sub":{"5":{"x":"30 g light brown sugar","d":"Slightly less crunchy-caramel; fine.","k":["brown sugar"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Rub the cold butter into the flour only until it looks like coarse breadcrumbs with some pea-size lumps; those lumps give a crunchy, uneven topping.","s":0},{"t":"Cook the apples only until they are just tender, since they finish in the oven; mushy apples fall to sauce.","s":1},{"t":"Scatter the crumble on top without pressing it down so the topping stays crisp.","s":2}],"rl":"SWFVFWVXG"},"52894":{"lv":"EEEEEERREEEEEERORREO","w":{"6":"Gives the sponge its characteristic taste; skip with a plainer cake.","7":"Almond flavor in the plain sponge; skip and increase vanilla.","14":"Flavor for the pink sponge; skip.","15":"The recipe says to omit the extract in the pink sponge; leave it out.","16":"Gives the pink checkerboard; skip for a plain two-tone cake.","19":"Dusting for the work surface; skip.","2":"The flour and leavening of the sponge; without it there is no cake","10":"The flour and leavening of the second sponge; without it there is no cake","17":"Glues the marzipan to the cake; the cake still holds without it"},"sub":{"2":{"x":"140 g all-purpose flour + 1 tsp baking powder (on top of the 1/2 tsp listed)","d":"Equivalent to self-rising flour.","k":["flour","baking powder"]},"10":{"x":"140 g all-purpose flour + 1 tsp baking powder (on top of the 1/2 tsp listed)","d":"Equivalent to self-rising flour.","k":["flour","baking powder"]},"17":{"x":"200 g raspberry or strawberry jam, warmed and sieved","d":"Slightly tarter and pinker; still glues the marzipan.","k":["raspberry jam","strawberry jam"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Weigh the two batters so both sponges are the same height, and line each tin with a pleated foil or parchment divider so the cakes are even slabs.","s":1},{"t":"Trim the sponges into identical strips with a ruler before assembling; even strips give a crisp checkerboard.","s":2},{"t":"Brush the jam on thinly to act as glue, and roll the marzipan to an even thickness on a sugar-dusted surface.","s":3}],"rl":"FWSSLSXXFWSSLSXXCTGG"},"52897":{"lv":"EEEEERRERRRR","w":{"0":"Keeps the cake very moist for days; any neutral oil works.","5":"Sharpens the flavor of the sweet cake; skip.","6":"Classic spice for carrot cake; skip with a plainer flavor.","8":"Crunch and nutty richness; skip or use pecans.","9":"Makes the icing; skip with a plain cake.","10":"Sweetens the icing.","11":"Softens and enriches the icing."},"sub":{"0":{"x":"450 ml canola or sunflower oil","d":"Any neutral oil is equivalent.","k":["canola oil","sunflower oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Grate the carrots on the fine side and do not squeeze them; their moisture is part of the recipe and keeps the oil-based cake tender.","s":1},{"t":"Cool the cake completely before icing, or the cream cheese frosting melts and slides off; chill the finished cake because the icing is perishable.","s":3},{"t":"Bake until a skewer comes out clean, covering with foil if the top darkens before the center sets.","s":2}],"rl":"FSLWSZXVBFWF"},"52898":{"lv":"EEEEEEORERERR","w":{"3":"Enriches the dough; water works.","6":"Greases the bowl while the dough rises; any oil works.","7":"Melted butter brushed over the dough carries the sugar and spice; skip with a drier filling.","9":"Warm spice that defines the filling; skip with a plainer bun.","11":"Part of the sticky glaze; skip.","12":"Sugar for the glaze; skip."},"sub":{"3":{"x":"300 ml lukewarm water + 1 tbsp more butter","d":"Slightly less soft and rich crumb.","k":["water","butter"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Warm the milk only to lukewarm; if it is hot to the touch it kills the yeast and the dough will not rise.","s":1},{"t":"Knead until the dough is smooth and passes the windowpane test, then let it double; underproofed dough gives dense buns.","s":3},{"t":"Roll the filling up tightly, cut with a serrated knife or thread, and place the buns close together so they bake into soft sides. Glaze them while hot.","s":7}],"rl":"SZLMFSFFWXVMW"},"52899":{"lv":"REERREEEEREROO","w":{"0":"Blanched almonds arranged on top give the cake its signature look and a crunch; skip for a plain top.","3":"Orange zest brightens the heavy fruit; skip with a duller flavor.","4":"Adds sweetness and moisture to the batter; skip and add a spoonful of sugar.","9":"Loosens the batter to dropping consistency; water works.","11":"Sweet color and chew; skip or replace with extra dried fruit.","12":"Part of the shiny glaze; skip for a matte top.","13":"Part of the shiny glaze; skip."},"tips":[{"t":"Add the eggs a little at a time with a spoonful of flour each; the batter is heavy with fruit and almonds and curdles easily if the eggs go in all at once.","s":3},{"t":"Arrange the whole almonds in tight concentric circles before baking; they spread slightly and a neat pattern is the traditional signature of a Dundee cake.","s":5},{"t":"Test with a skewer after 50 minutes at the lower temperature and brush the glaze on while the cake is hot so it sinks in and sets as a shine.","s":7}],"rl":"GFWXWSLSBMVVGG"},"52900":{"lv":"EEEERRO","w":{"3":"Provides flour and leavening; plain flour plus baking powder works.","4":"Loosens the batter to a dropping consistency; add a spoonful at a time.","5":"The signature lemon fragrance of a Madeira; skip with a plainer cake.","6":"Traditional decoration only; skip."},"sub":{"3":{"x":"250 g all-purpose flour + 3 tsp baking powder + pinch salt","d":"Equivalent to self-rising flour; sift well.","k":["flour","baking powder"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Cream the butter and sugar for a full 3-4 minutes until pale; this builds the fine, even crumb a Madeira is known for.","s":1},{"t":"Add the eggs one at a time, with a spoonful of the flour if the mixture starts to look curdled.","s":1},{"t":"Traditionally the candied peel goes on the cake partway through baking so it sits on top instead of sinking; add it after about 20 minutes if you want it to stay visible.","s":5}],"rl":"FWSSMXG"},"52901":{"lv":"EEEEEERR","w":{"0":"Provides flour and leavening; plain flour plus extra baking powder works.","6":"Binds the dough without making it runny; add only a teaspoon more if it is dry.","7":"Rounds out the sweet fruit flavor; skip."},"sub":{"0":{"x":"225 g all-purpose flour + 1 1/2 tsp more baking powder (2 1/2 tsp total)","d":"Same rise; sift the baking powder with the flour.","k":["flour","baking powder"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Keep the dough stiff and lumpy; rock cakes need very little liquid, and a wetter dough bakes into flat cookies without the craggy surface.","s":3},{"t":"Rub the cold butter into the flour until fine crumbs before adding the egg, so the cakes are tender and short.","s":1},{"t":"Leave plenty of space between spoonfuls; they spread to double their size as they bake.","s":4}],"rl":"SWLFVSMX"},"52902":{"lv":"EEREEEEEE","w":{"2":"Loosens the batter slightly; skip and add water if it is too stiff.","3":"Provides sticky sweetness and moisture; light corn syrup or honey works.","4":"Gives the dark bitter-sweet flavor and color of parkin; molasses works.","6":"Gives the dense, crumbly texture; rolled oats pulsed briefly work.","7":"The flour and leavening of the cake; without it there is no cake"},"sub":{"3":{"x":"200 g light corn syrup or honey","d":"Less caramelly; honey is sweeter and less sticky.","k":["corn syrup","honey"],"wn":"Honey: do not serve to infants under 1 year."},"4":{"x":"85 g dark molasses","d":"Slightly milder than black treacle.","k":["molasses"]},"6":{"x":"100 g rolled oats, pulsed in a blender a few times","d":"Slightly coarser texture; same flavor.","k":["rolled oats"]},"7":{"x":"250 g all-purpose flour + 3 tsp baking powder + pinch salt","d":"Equivalent to self-rising flour.","k":["flour","baking powder"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Melt the syrup, treacle, sugar and butter gently and take the pan off the heat as soon as the sugar dissolves; boiling it makes the cake hard.","s":1},{"t":"Wrap the cooled parkin and keep it for 3-5 days before cutting; it goes sticky and moist as the syrup soaks in, and tastes better than when fresh.","s":2},{"t":"The batter is very thin, so line the tin with parchment with no gaps to prevent leaks.","s":0}],"rl":"FSMWWWBSX"},"52966":{"lv":"EEER","w":{"3":"Forms the chocolate top layer; skip with a stickier, softer bar."},"sub":{"3":{"x":"150 g semisweet or dark chocolate chips","d":"Less sweet and slightly firmer; balances the sweet caramel.","k":["chocolate chips"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Melt the Mars bars and butter over barely simmering water, with the bowl not touching the water, whisking until completely smooth; high heat splits the butter and makes the caramel grainy.","s":1},{"t":"Press the mixture in firmly with a spoon back or a spatula so it holds together when cut.","s":2},{"t":"Set the bars in a cool place rather than the fridge if you can; cold chocolate cracks when cut. Use a hot, dry knife for clean squares.","s":4}],"rl":"TFBG"},"52988":{"lv":"RERREEEEERE","w":{"0":"Adds crunch and nutty flavor; skip with a softer pudding.","2":"Chewy citrus accent; skip and add more raisins.","3":"The traditional spice; freshly grated nutmeg is far more fragrant than ground.","9":"Boozy flavor for the pudding; the dried fruit still carries it"},"sub":{"9":{"x":"2 tbsp dark rum or orange juice","d":"Rum is closer to brandy; orange juice is alcohol-free but milder.","k":["rum","orange juice"],"wn":"Orange juice makes it alcohol-free, but it will not keep for weeks; eat within 3 to 4 days of steaming, or freeze."}},"tips":[{"t":"Grate the cold butter into the mix a quarter at a time; this distributes it evenly through the fruit without melting.","s":2},{"t":"Steam the pudding for the full 8 hours and never let the pan boil dry; top it up with boiling water as it evaporates.","s":5},{"t":"Make it weeks ahead and store it in a cool dark place, as the flavor matures; reheat for an hour on the day and flame with warmed brandy ladled over the top.","s":7}],"rl":"XVVXVSTWSKF"},"52989":{"lv":"EROEEEEOO","w":{"1":"Caramelizes slightly with the orange juice into a syrup; skip with a less syrupy layer.","2":"Gives the orange layer a boozy lift; orange juice works.","5":"Makes the custard layer richer and thicker; skip with a thinner layer.","7":"Crunch on top; skip.","8":"Topping; skip."},"sub":{"2":{"x":"2 tbsp orange juice (or brandy)","d":"Alcohol-free and a little less intense.","k":["orange juice","brandy"]},"5":{"x":"250 g softened cream cheese beaten with 2 tbsp heavy cream","d":"Tangier and denser than mascarpone.","k":["cream cheese","heavy cream"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Cut every bit of white pith off the oranges before slicing; leftover pith makes the layer bitter.","s":0},{"t":"Beat the mascarpone until smooth before adding the custard, or the layer will be lumpy.","s":2},{"t":"Assemble up to a day ahead and chill, but add the almonds and chocolate at the last moment so they stay crisp.","s":3}],"rl":"VWXBBFFGG"},"52990":{"lv":"EEEERRRREERRROOE","w":{"4":"Adds moisture and a tender crumb; if skipped, replace with 50 g flour.","6":"Chewy citrus sweetness; skip and add more fruit.","7":"Sweet color and bite; skip and add more fruit.","10":"Buttery crunch; skip or use walnuts.","11":"Brightens the heavy fruit; skip.","12":"Gives the Christmas spice flavor; skip with a plainer fruitcake.","13":"1.5 tbsp is a strong, perfumed flavor; skip or reduce to 1 tsp.","14":"Rounds out the flavor; skip.","5":"Soaks the fruit and keeps the cake; the cake bakes without it but is less moist"},"sub":{"5":{"x":"100 ml orange juice or brandy","d":"Orange juice is alcohol-free but the cake keeps less well; brandy is stronger.","k":["orange juice","brandy"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Line the tin with a double layer of parchment and tie a band of brown paper around it; the long, slow bake would otherwise scorch the edges before the middle sets.","s":0},{"t":"The batter looks curdled after the sherry goes in; that is normal and the flour brings it back together.","s":1},{"t":"Make a slight dip in the middle before baking so the cake rises flat, not domed, and feed it a spoon of sherry weekly if you keep it.","s":2}],"rl":"FWSSSMVVVVVXXXXL"},"52991":{"lv":"EEEERO","w":{"4":"Glaze for a shiny, golden top; milk can replace it.","5":"Decorative dusting; skip."},"tips":[{"t":"Do not add any liquid to the pastry dough; it should be firm like shortbread. Press it into the tins by hand without rolling.","s":1},{"t":"Do not overfill with mincemeat, or the sugary filling bubbles out and sticks to the tin; about a spoonful per pie is enough.","s":3},{"t":"Leave the pies in the tin for 5 minutes to firm up before removing them, and grease the tin lightly if the mincemeat is very sticky.","s":6}],"rl":"FSWVGG"},"53005":{"lv":"ERREEEEEEOROOO","w":{"1":"Seasons the crust; skip with a flat taste.","2":"Helps the crust brown and tenderizes it; skip with a paler crust.","9":"A small warm note; skip.","10":"Brightens the fruit and balances the sugar; skip.","11":"Dotted over the filling for richness; skip.","12":"Brushed on the lattice for browning; skip.","13":"Crunchy sparkle on top; skip.","7":"Thickens the pie filling; without it the filling is runny"},"sub":{"7":{"x":"6 tbsp (45 g) all-purpose flour","d":"Thickens less cleanly, giving a slightly cloudy filling.","k":["flour"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Keep the dough cold and add the water just until it holds together when pinched; warm or wet dough makes a tough crust.","s":1},{"t":"Bake the pie on a preheated tray so the bottom crust browns, and cover the edges with a foil ring if they darken early.","s":8},{"t":"Wait until the juices bubble thickly through the lattice, then cool the pie for several hours; the cornstarch only fully thickens as it cools, and cutting it warm leaves a runny filling.","s":9}],"rl":"SZWFMVVTWXAFGG"},"53488":{"lv":"RERRRRRROROEOEO","w":{"0":"Cooking fat for browning the beef; any oil works.","2":"Sweet savory base; skip and add a bit more garlic.","3":"Sweetness and color that balance the cabbage; skip with a plainer stew.","4":"Adds sweetness and a fresh note; skip.","5":"Concentrates the tomato flavor; skip with a less rich sauce.","6":"Savory depth to the beef; skip.","7":"Cabbage and beef need salt; taste at the end.","8":"Mild heat; skip.","9":"Gives color and warmth; use smoked paprika for the best flavor.","10":"A little earthiness; skip.","12":"Brightens the cabbage; skip or use lemon juice.","14":"Garnish; skip."},"sub":{"0":{"x":"2 tbsp vegetable oil","d":"Neutral; no change.","k":["vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Brown the beef until it has caramelized bits in the pan, breaking it up with a masher; those bits dissolve into the sauce and carry most of the flavor. Cook it to 160F (71C).","s":3},{"t":"Cook the tomato paste with the vegetables for a minute or two until it darkens; raw paste tastes sharp and tinny.","s":5},{"t":"If the cabbage releases too much water, uncover and cook on higher heat for a few minutes to evaporate it, which turns a soupy pot into a thick stew.","s":11}],"rl":"FPXVVXXZZXXVAMG"},"53489":{"lv":"RERRRRROREEOEOEERERO","w":{"0":"Browns the beef and greases the pan; any oil works.","2":"Sweet savory background for the meat; skip and use extra garlic.","3":"Savory depth in the meat sauce; skip with a milder filling.","4":"Moist tomato base for the meat; use extra paste and a splash of water if skipped.","5":"Concentrates the tomato flavor of the meat sauce; skip with a lighter taste.","6":"Seasons the meat and potatoes; taste before baking.","7":"Mild heat; skip.","8":"Bulgarian signature herb (chubritsa); thyme with a pinch of oregano is a close substitute.","11":"Optional sweetness, as noted in the recipe; skip.","13":"Subtle fresh note; skip.","16":"Seasons the bechamel; skip and use a little more cheese.","18":"Salty tang and a golden crust; skip with a milder topping.","19":"A pinch lifts the bechamel; skip."},"sub":{"0":{"x":"1 tbsp olive oil","d":"Slightly fruitier; fine.","k":["olive oil"]},"8":{"x":"1/2 tsp dried thyme + pinch of dried oregano","d":"Close to savory's peppery-thyme flavor.","k":["dried thyme","dried oregano"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Cool the bechamel for 5 minutes before beating in the yolks; hot sauce scrambles the eggs, and cooled sauce gives a smooth, set topping.","s":9},{"t":"Bake the potatoes with the meat in water until it is absorbed and they are fully tender before the topping goes on; undercooked potatoes will not cook under a bechamel crust. Beef must reach 160F (71C).","s":5},{"t":"Cut the potatoes into even pieces of about 1/2 inch (1 cm) so the whole dish cooks at the same rate.","s":3}],"rl":"FPXXMXZZXMBVMXFTZTGX"},"53490":{"lv":"EEEOROR","w":{"3":"Mild onion note and green color; skip as the recipe says it is optional.","4":"Fresh grassy freshness for the filling; skip and use dill.","5":"Feta is already salty; taste before adding any.","6":"Brightens the creamy filling; skip with a heavier flavor."},"tips":[{"t":"Drain the cottage cheese in a sieve for a few minutes first; the whey otherwise leaks out and makes the filling watery inside the peppers.","s":1},{"t":"Choose thick-walled sweet peppers that hold their boat shape, and halve them through the stem so the stem stays as a handle.","s":0},{"t":"Mash the feta into the cottage cheese by hand for a creamier, even filling, and chill the stuffed peppers for 15 minutes so the filling firms up.","s":2}],"rl":"VXPGGZA"},"53491":{"lv":"EERRRREO","w":{"2":"Sharp bite that cuts through the sweet tomato; skip and add more cucumber.","3":"Crunchy, grassy sweetness; skip with a plainer salad.","4":"Draws the tomato and cucumber juices that become the dressing; skip and the salad tastes flat.","5":"Sunflower oil is traditional; olive oil works.","7":"Fresh garnish; skip."},"sub":{"5":{"x":"1/4 cup sunflower oil","d":"The traditional Bulgarian choice; milder than olive oil.","k":["sunflower oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Salt the vegetables and let them stand 5 minutes as the recipe says; the juices that drain out mix with the oil and become the dressing, so do not pour them off.","s":0},{"t":"Shopska is traditionally topped with a thick snowy layer of grated sirene (feta), so grate it rather than crumbling for the authentic look.","s":0},{"t":"Use ripe summer tomatoes; unripe ones give a watery, sour salad no dressing can fix.","s":0}],"rl":"VVXVZFGG"},"53492":{"lv":"ERERRRROO","w":{"1":"Peppery crunch and color; skip and use extra cucumber.","3":"Mild onion bite; skip.","4":"Brings out the vegetables' flavor; taste after tossing.","7":"Traditional garnish herb; skip.","8":"Garnish; skip.","5":"Acid for the dressing; lemon or other acid still seasons it","6":"Base of the dressing; the vinegar still dresses the salad"},"sub":{"5":{"x":"2 tbsp lemon juice","d":"Brighter and fruitier than cider vinegar.","k":["lemon juice"]},"6":{"x":"2 tbsp sunflower oil","d":"Milder than olive oil; traditional in Bulgaria.","k":["sunflower oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Dry the lettuce completely after washing; any water left on the leaves dilutes the dressing and wilts the salad.","s":0},{"t":"Toss with the vinegar, oil and salt only at the last minute, since salt and vinegar make the leaves limp within minutes.","s":3},{"t":"Soak the sliced radishes in ice water for 10 minutes to make them extra crisp, and add the hard-boiled eggs and olives mentioned in the final step for a heartier salad.","s":4}],"rl":"VVVXZAFGG"},"53494":{"lv":"EEEREEO","w":{"3":"Adds tang and a smoother, creamier crumb. Skipping it gives a slightly drier, eggier bake.","6":"Toasty crunch and a little nuttiness on top only. Skip it with no effect on structure."},"sub":{"3":{"x":"2 tbsp full-fat sour cream or plain Greek yogurt","d":"Slightly looser and tangier; the bake is a touch less rich but still sets fine.","k":["sour cream"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Whisk the baking powder in last and get the pan into the oven right away. It starts working on contact with the wet batter, so a delay costs you lift.","s":3},{"t":"Feta and mozzarella are both salty, so add no salt. Crumble the feta small so it melts into little pockets instead of leaving dense salty chunks.","s":3},{"t":"Cool the full 5-10 minutes in the pan. This cheese-heavy custard is fragile when hot and firms as it cools, so you get clean squares instead of a crumbly mess.","s":6}],"rl":"SFBFLXG"},"53486":{"lv":"EERRERERORRRO","w":{"2":"Softens the vegetables and carries flavor; olive oil or any neutral oil works.","3":"Roasts-in-the-pot sweet pepper that is peeled and chopped back in. Skipping loses sweetness and body.","5":"Adds sweetness and color that balances the tomato and beans. Skipping makes a plainer, sharper soup.","7":"Dried mint is typical of Bulgarian bean soup and gives it its fresh, cooling note. Skipping makes it flatter.","8":"A background herb. Skipping is barely noticeable next to the savory and mint.","9":"Savory (chubritsa) is the classic Bulgarian bean herb, peppery and thyme-like. Skipping loses some authenticity.","10":"Gives gentle warmth to a mild soup. Use less if you prefer it mild.","11":"Beans need seasoning to taste like anything; add it in stages and finish at the end.","12":"Fresh color and a green note on top only."},"sub":{"2":{"x":"2 tbsp olive oil","d":"Slightly fruitier; fine for a soup that simmers for hours.","k":["olive oil"]},"9":{"x":"1 tsp dried marjoram or 1/2 tsp extra dried thyme plus 1/2 tsp dried oregano","d":"Close in character to savory but a little sweeter and less peppery.","k":["dried marjoram"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Drain and discard the soaking water, then keep the soup at a gentle simmer. A hard boil splits the skins before the centers are tender and leaves a grainy broth.","s":2},{"t":"Pull the whole onion and peppers out, mash the onion and chop the peeled peppers, then stir them back in. The mashed onion thickens the broth without any flour.","s":3},{"t":"If the beans are still firm after the time given, they were old or the water was hard. Keep simmering with a splash of hot water; the tomato paste's acid can slow softening, so do not rush by adding more.","s":2}],"rl":"PXFVXVMXXXZZG"},"53487":{"lv":"EEREEEEEO","w":{"2":"Seasons the mushrooms and the thin white sauce. Add gradually, since the vinegar mixture adds punch at the table.","8":"Optional heat at the table only.","7":"The sour element of the soup; without it the soup is flat and not the dish"},"sub":{"7":{"x":"1/3 cup (80 ml) white wine vinegar or apple cider vinegar","d":"Slightly milder and less fruity than red wine vinegar; the garlic still dominates.","k":["white wine vinegar"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Do not crowd the pot. Oyster mushrooms hold a lot of water; cook until the liquid they release has mostly cooked off and they smell nutty, or the soup tastes of boiled mushrooms.","s":2},{"t":"Whisk the flour into cold water first, then pour it in while stirring. Hot liquid hitting dry flour makes lumps that will not whisk out.","s":3},{"t":"Mix the garlic and vinegar 10 minutes before serving so the raw garlic mellows. Spoon it in at the table rather than boiling it, which would kill the sharp, bright contrast against the creamy soup.","s":5}],"rl":"VFZMTMXAG"},"53493":{"lv":"EEEREERRERO","w":{"3":"Rounds out the honey and cinnamon. Skipping slightly flattens the flavor.","6":"Brings out sweetness and balances the honey. Skipping makes the cookies taste flat.","7":"Warm spice that pairs with honey. Skipping gives a plainer honey cookie.","9":"The chocolate coating is a big part of the finished cookie; skip and they are plain honey cookies.","10":"Only thins the melted chocolate so it coats smoothly. Skip with no effect on the cookie.","4":"Moistens and tenderizes the dough; 3 cups looks too high for 4 cups flour, so start with about 3/4 cup and add a spoonful at a time until the dough is soft and holds a ball."},"sub":{"9":{"x":"2 cups chopped dark or milk chocolate from bars","d":"Chopped bars melt a bit smoother than chips, and set slightly softer.","k":["chocolate"]}},"tips":[{"t":"The 3 cups of oil looks like a data error for 4 cups flour. Start with about 3/4 cup and add a spoonful at a time until the dough is soft, holds a ball and does not crack when pressed.","s":2},{"t":"Beat the eggs and powdered sugar the full 3 minutes until pale and thick. That aeration builds the cookie's structure since there is little other leavening.","s":2},{"t":"Honey browns fast, so pull the cookies when the edges are just golden. Cool them completely before dipping, or the chocolate streaks and will not set. Melt chocolate in 20-second bursts; a drop of water makes it seize.","s":5}],"rl":"SWWXFSZXLGF"},"3156":{"lv":"EEEEEREEERERRO","w":{"5":"Garlic adds depth after the vegetables soften. Skipping makes a flatter base.","9":"Gives a subtle herbal background. Skipping is mild.","11":"Classic Cajun herb; skipping is mild next to the seasoning blend.","12":"Gumbo is served over rice to soak up the roux gravy; skip if serving with bread.","13":"Fresh onion bite on top only."},"tips":[{"t":"Stir the roux constantly, scraping the corners, and use a flat wooden spoon. Any black specks mean it scorched and you must start over; scorched roux makes the whole pot bitter. It also splatters, so wear sleeves.","s":0},{"t":"Add the onion, pepper and celery to the roux the moment it hits dark chocolate. The cold vegetables drop the temperature and stop the browning.","s":1},{"t":"Add the broth warm and whisk in a ladle at a time. Cold broth on a hot roux seizes into lumps. Skim the orange sausage fat at the end, and chicken thighs need the full simmer to reach tenderness and 165F (74C).","s":2}],"rl":"FTXVVXMPPXXXBG"},"3157":{"lv":"OEEEEEREEEERRO","w":{"6":"Garlic deepens the base. Skipping makes the pot less savory.","11":"Herbal note; skipping is subtle next to the Cajun seasoning.","12":"Adds a sweet seafood layer on top. Skip for a sausage-and-chicken version, but do not skip cooking time otherwise.","13":"Fresh onion on top only.","0":"Only starts the browning; the sausage releases fat"},"sub":{"0":{"x":"1 tbsp butter or olive oil","d":"Only starts the browning; the sausage fat does most of the work.","k":["butter"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Do not wash the pot between browning steps. The browned bits on the bottom are the flavor of this dish; scrape them up with the broth.","s":3},{"t":"After the lid goes on, do not lift it or stir for the 20 minutes. Stirring releases starch and gives a gummy, mushy rice; the steam must stay in.","s":4},{"t":"Lay the shrimp on top for the last 5 minutes only. They are done when opaque and curled loosely in a C; a tight O means overcooked. The chicken must be cooked to 165F (74C).","s":5}],"rl":"FPPXVVXVBMXXPG"},"3159":{"lv":"EOEEEERERERRRO","w":{"6":"Garlic adds depth. Skipping gives a plainer pot.","8":"Subtle herbal background. Skipping is barely noticeable.","10":"Mild herb note, minor next to the seasoning blend.","11":"Red beans are served over rice; skip if you serve with cornbread instead.","12":"Needs salt at the end; amounts depend on your sausage and seasoning blend.","13":"Fresh onion on top only.","1":"Only starts the sear; the sausage fat covers it"},"sub":{"1":{"x":"1 tbsp butter or bacon fat","d":"Sausage fat renders anyway; this just starts the sear.","k":["butter"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Bring the beans to a hard boil for at least 10 minutes before dropping to a simmer. Raw kidney beans contain a toxin (phytohaemagglutinin) that only a full boil destroys; never cook them only at a low simmer or in a slow cooker from raw.","s":2},{"t":"Salt late. Cajun seasoning and andouille are already salty, and salting early can leave bean skins tough.","s":4},{"t":"Mash about a quarter of the beans against the pot wall and stir, so the beans make their own creamy gravy. Thin with a splash of hot water if it tightens as it sits.","s":4}],"rl":"PFPXVVXMXXXBZG"},"3160":{"lv":"EEEEEREREOEROR","w":{"5":"Garlic adds depth at the end of the vegetable cook. Skipping gives a flatter base.","7":"Gives a Creole tomato sweetness and body. Skipping makes it more of a Cajun brown etouffee.","9":"Mostly color; the Cajun seasoning already carries paprika.","11":"Fresh onion brightness at the end.","12":"Garnish only.","13":"Etouffee is served over rice to soak the sauce; skip if serving with bread."},"tips":[{"t":"Cook the butter roux only to peanut-butter color, which takes 8-10 minutes of constant whisking. A darker roux belongs in gumbo; etouffee should stay lighter and buttery.","s":0},{"t":"Simmer the sauce until it coats the back of a spoon before adding seafood. Shrimp release water, so a sauce that looks right before they go in will thin out.","s":2},{"t":"Add the shrimp last and take it off the heat when they just turn pink and curl into a loose C. If you use cooked crawfish tails, only warm them for 1-2 minutes or they turn rubbery.","s":3}],"rl":"FTXVVXMVXXPGGB"},"3164":{"lv":"EEERRREEERRORRR","w":{"3":"Gives the crust the Cajun kick; skipping makes a bland breading unless you add more to the remoulade.","4":"Seasons the crust. Cajun seasoning is salty, so you can reduce it.","9":"Gives tang and body to the remoulade. Skipping makes a milder sauce.","10":"Brightens the sauce and cuts the richness of the fry.","11":"Small kick in the remoulade only.","12":"Cool crunch against the hot shrimp.","13":"Juicy freshness. Skipping is fine if the sauce and pickle are strong.","14":"Sharp bite that cuts the fried richness.","5":"Wets the shrimp for the coating; egg still helps it stick"},"sub":{"5":{"x":"1 cup buttermilk","d":"Tangier and adheres even better; thicker coating.","k":["buttermilk"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Dredge the shrimp just before they hit the oil. Breading that sits turns gummy and slides off.","s":1},{"t":"Keep the oil at 350F (175C) and fry in small batches. Cold oil gives greasy shrimp and a crowded pan drops the temperature fast.","s":2},{"t":"Drain the fried shrimp on a wire rack instead of paper towels so the cornmeal crust stays crisp on the bottom. Toast the rolls so the juice from the tomato does not make them soggy.","s":3}],"rl":"PBBXZMFBFXAXVVG"},"100":{"lv":"EEERRRERO","w":{"4":"Adds savory depth; skipping makes a flatter cream sauce.","5":"Adds sweetness and color. Skipping gives a plainer sauce.","7":"Thickens and adds salty depth to the cream. Skipping gives a looser, less rich sauce.","8":"Garnish only.","3":"Cooks the shrimp and aromatics; the cream still makes the sauce"},"sub":{"3":{"x":"2 tbsp olive oil","d":"Slightly less rich; the cream makes up for it.","k":["olive oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Save a cup of pasta cooking water before draining. The starch loosens the sauce without thinning it so it clings to the penne.","s":0},{"t":"Sear the shrimp quickly, 1-2 minutes a side, then pull them out. They finish cooking in the sauce, so leaving them in the pan too long makes them tough.","s":0},{"t":"Remove the pan from the heat before stirring in the parmesan. A rolling boil makes the cheese clump and the cream split and turn greasy.","s":2}],"rl":"BPXFXVFTG"},"24":{"lv":"REEEREOOERREER","w":{"4":"Adds savory depth; skipping makes the broth less aromatic.","6":"Mostly color; the Cajun blend already has paprika.","7":"Minor herbal note.","9":"Makes the soup hearty and gives some body. Skipping lightens it.","10":"Subtle background aroma.","0":"Softens the vegetables; the cream still enriches","13":"Makes the soup creamy; the broth is still a soup"},"sub":{"0":{"x":"2 tbsp olive oil","d":"Slightly less rich; the cream adds richness later.","k":["olive oil"]},"13":{"x":"1 cup half-and-half","d":"Lighter and a little thinner. Do not boil it after adding or it may split.","k":["half-and-half"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Bloom the Cajun seasoning and paprika in the butter for 30 seconds with the garlic, not longer. It deepens the flavor, but paprika burns bitter.","s":0},{"t":"Noodles keep absorbing broth and turn mushy as the soup sits. If you expect leftovers, cook them separately and add them to each bowl.","s":2},{"t":"Rotisserie chicken is already cooked, so stir it in at the end only to warm through. Add the cream off a hard boil and keep it at a gentle simmer so it stays smooth.","s":3}],"rl":"FXVVXXXXMBXBPF"},"53496":{"lv":"EEERREREERRO","w":{"3":"Adds salty umami depth. Skipping makes the marinade a bit thinner in flavor; add a splash of soy.","4":"Helps the pork caramelize and char. Brown sugar works equally.","6":"Mild warmth against the sweet marinade. Skipping is subtle.","9":"Prevents sticking on the grill. Use a high-heat oil.","10":"Cool crunch against the rich pork; skipping loses the freshness.","11":"Garnish only."},"sub":{"4":{"x":"2 tbsp brown sugar or white sugar","d":"Same caramelization; slightly less floral. Dissolve it in the marinade.","k":["brown sugar"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Freeze the pork 20 minutes so you can slice it about 1/4 inch thick against the grain. Thin slices cook in 3-4 minutes and stay tender.","s":1},{"t":"The sweet marinade chars quickly. Keep the heat medium-high, shake off excess marinade and flip once; a little char is the point but black means too hot.","s":5},{"t":"Leftover marinade has touched raw pork, so only serve it after boiling it hard for the full 5 minutes. Pork is safe at 145F (63C) plus rest; thin slices will be well past that.","s":8}],"rl":"PFXXWXZBMFVG"},"53498":{"lv":"RERRREERRRRROO","w":{"2":"Adds sweetness and body to the base. Skipping makes a thinner sauce.","3":"Adds savory depth; skipping makes the curry flatter.","4":"Adds fragrant heat that balances the coconut.","7":"Adds savory depth. Water works with a bit more fish sauce.","8":"The salty umami backbone of the curry. Soy sauce is a milder stand-in.","9":"Rounds out the heat and salt. Skipping makes it sharper.","10":"Adds texture and a mild tang; skip or use extra pepper.","11":"Adds sweetness and color. Skipping is fine.","12":"Added at the end for aroma. Skipping is barely noticeable.","13":"Brightens the rich curry at the table.","0":"Cooks the curry paste; the coconut milk also carries fat"},"sub":{"0":{"x":"1 tbsp olive oil or any neutral oil","d":"Same browning job; olive oil is slightly fruitier.","k":["olive oil"]},"8":{"x":"1 tbsp soy sauce plus a pinch of salt","d":"Less funky and a little darker in color; a good swap if you have no fish sauce.","k":["soy sauce"],"wn":"Contains soy and wheat (gluten); unsuitable for soy or gluten allergies."}},"tips":[{"t":"Fry the curry paste in the oil for the full minute before adding liquid. It blooms the spices and mellows the raw chili taste; skipping this makes a harsher curry.","s":5},{"t":"Once the coconut milk goes in, keep it at a gentle simmer. A hard boil breaks the coconut and leaves the sauce greasy. Chicken thighs forgive a long simmer, but must reach 165F (74C).","s":8},{"t":"Stir in the basil off the heat so it wilts without turning black, and add the lime at the table so its acidity stays fresh.","s":10}],"rl":"FPXXXXMMZWVVGA"},"53499":{"lv":"EEERERRRRRO","w":{"5":"Adds sweet, mild onion base. Skipping makes the dip a little flatter.","6":"Gives heat and color; adjust to taste, or leave out for a mild dip.","7":"Balances the salty prahok. Skipping makes it harsher.","8":"Adds salt and umami; prahok is already salty, so taste before adding all.","9":"Adds the sour lift that makes the dip bright against the salty fish paste.","10":"Garnish only.","3":"Cooks the pork; the pork renders fat"},"sub":{"3":{"x":"2 tbsp peanut or canola oil","d":"Any neutral oil works.","k":["canola oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Fry the prahok in the oil with the pork for a full minute and open a window or run the hood. It is pungent raw but cooks into a deep savory flavor, and mashing it into the pork spreads it evenly.","s":4},{"t":"Keep the coconut milk at a steady gentle bubble. A hard boil makes it split and turn oily. Ground pork must reach 160F (71C), which this cook time covers.","s":5},{"t":"Serve it warm with raw cucumber, long beans, eggplant and steamed rice. Add the lime off the heat and taste last, since the salty-sweet-sour balance shifts as it cools.","s":8}],"rl":"PMXFXXXWZAG"},"53501":{"lv":"EEERRERERRRRR","w":{"8":"Balances the vinegar in the quick pickle. Skipping makes the pickle sharp.","9":"Gives the creamy base of the spicy sauce. Skipping makes the sandwich drier.","10":"Adds the heat and tang to the mayo; skip for a plain sauce.","11":"Fresh herbal note on the sandwich. Skipping loses brightness.","12":"Cool crunch and freshness.","3":"Sweetens the glaze; the other sauces still glaze the chicken","4":"Cooks the chicken; the pan can manage with less","6":"Pickle for the sandwich; the carrot still fills it","7":"The acid for the pickled vegetables; without it they are not pickled"},"sub":{"3":{"x":"2 tbsp sugar or brown sugar dissolved in 1 tbsp warm water","d":"Same sweet-salty glaze; less floral than honey.","k":["sugar"],"wn":"Do not give honey to infants under 1 year; sugar substitute is fine."},"4":{"x":"2 tbsp olive oil or any neutral oil","d":"Only used for cooking the chicken.","k":["olive oil"]},"6":{"x":"1/2 cup extra shredded carrot or thinly sliced radish","d":"Slightly sweeter and less peppery than daikon.","k":["carrot"]},"7":{"x":"1/4 cup apple cider vinegar plus 1/2 tsp sugar","d":"A bit fruitier and sharper than rice vinegar.","k":["apple cider vinegar"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Salt the julienned carrot and daikon for 5 minutes, squeeze, then pickle for at least 20 minutes. They stay crunchy instead of turning limp in the brine.","s":2},{"t":"Cook the chicken in a single layer in a hot pan. The soy-honey marinade burns quickly if the heat is too high, and crowding steams it instead of browning. Check for 165F (74C).","s":5},{"t":"Toast the baguette cut side up until the inside is crisp so the sandwich survives the sauce and pickles. Drain the pickles well so they do not soak the bread.","s":6}],"rl":"BPXWFVVAWFXGV"},"53502":{"lv":"EEEEREERER","w":{"4":"Gives the golden color and a mild earthy flavor. Skipping makes a pale pancake.","7":"Adds sweetness and softness to the filling. Skip if you prefer just pork and sprouts.","9":"Used for dipping; the pancake is fairly mild without it."},"tips":[{"t":"Stir the batter again right before each pour. The rice flour and cornstarch settle, and a settled batter makes thick, gummy pancakes.","s":4},{"t":"Swirl the batter very thin and let the edges turn lacy and crisp before folding. A pan that is not hot enough steams instead of crisps; the pancake should sizzle on contact.","s":6},{"t":"Serve it wrapped in lettuce and herbs with a sweet-sour fish sauce dip, the way it is traditionally eaten. Fold at the end, not before, or it softens.","s":7}],"rl":"STMMCPVXFZ"},"53419":{"lv":"EEREEERRR","w":{"6":"Balances the salty fermented soybeans. Skipping gives a saltier sauce, so rinse beans well.","7":"Loosens the sauce slightly so the beans do not stick or burn.","8":"Fresh onion note stirred in at the end.","2":"Dusts the fish for crispness; the fish still fries","3":"The frying oil for the fish; without it it cannot be fried"},"sub":{"2":{"x":"2 tbsp cornstarch or all-purpose flour","d":"Slightly less airy than tempura flour; still crisp if dusted thinly.","k":["cornstarch"]},"3":{"x":"2 cups canola or any neutral frying oil","d":"Any neutral high-heat oil. Avoid olive oil.","k":["vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Start the ginger in oil that is not too hot and take it out when golden. It keeps cooking and turns bitter if it goes dark.","s":0},{"t":"Dust the fish in only a thin layer of tempura flour and shake off the excess. A thick coating turns heavy and soggy once it meets the sauce.","s":1},{"t":"Add the fried fish at the last second before serving. The sauce softens the crust within a couple of minutes. If your soybeans are brined, rinse them first or the dish will be far too salty.","s":8}],"rl":"XPTFXXWMG"},"53423":{"lv":"EEERRRROREREER","w":{"3":"Bitter orange zest gives the characteristic floral bitterness. Skipping loses the citrus perfume; use lime zest.","4":"Fresh heat that wakes up the salad. Skipping makes it milder.","5":"Crunch and nutty richness. Skipping loses texture but not the flavor balance.","6":"Adds sharp, sweet crunch. Skipping lessens the pickled tang.","7":"A little extra sour-salty tang. Skip and add a bit more lime juice.","10":"Adds fruity sweetness and rounds out the lime. Skipping makes the dressing sharper.","8":"Aromatic for the salad; ginger and lime still season it","13":"Sweetens the dressing; the other seasonings still carry it"},"sub":{"8":{"x":"25 g fresh ginger, finely chopped, plus 1/2 tsp extra lime zest","d":"Less piney and floral than galangal, spicier. Still gives the aromatic heat the salad needs.","k":["ginger","lime"]},"13":{"x":"50 g (about 1/4 cup packed) brown sugar","d":"Slightly less caramel-like but dissolves into the dressing just as well.","k":["brown sugar"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Toast the rice in a dry pan, stirring, until it is deep gold and smells nutty, then grind it to a coarse powder. Under-toasted rice tastes raw and gives the salad a pasty texture.","s":0},{"t":"Toss the dressing with the shrimp, coconut and aromatics and add the rice powder and peanuts at the very end. They soak up liquid fast and turn gummy if they sit.","s":3},{"t":"Balance the dressing before it goes on: it should taste sharply sour, then sweet, then salty. Serve it as a wrap with lettuce or other leaves so the crunch and fresh herbs offset the intense filling.","s":4}],"rl":"PBVXXGVAXXAAZW"},"53495":{"lv":"EEERRRERRROO","w":{"4":"Rounds out the salty-sour balance in the sauce. Skipping makes the curry sharper.","5":"Loosens the sauce and adds a light savory background. Water works with a pinch more salt.","8":"Blooms the curry paste in oil before liquid. Skipping a bit of oil is mild.","10":"Garnish only.","11":"A squeeze at the table brightens the rich coconut.","3":"Salty umami seasoning; salt covers it","7":"Floral aroma of the curry; lime zest covers it","9":"Wraps the curry for steaming; parchment covers it and only the aroma is lost"},"sub":{"3":{"x":"1 tbsp soy sauce plus a pinch of salt","d":"Less funky and darker; fine in a fish curry.","k":["soy sauce"],"wn":"Contains soy and wheat (gluten); unsuitable for soy or gluten allergies."},"7":{"x":"2 strips lime zest (no white pith)","d":"Gives citrusy perfume but lacks the floral note of kaffir leaves.","k":["lime"]},"9":{"x":"4 small squares of parchment paper or 4 small heatproof bowls lined with parchment","d":"Holds the curry and steams like banana leaf; no leafy aroma.","k":["parchment paper"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Temper the beaten egg: whisk a ladle of the warm sauce into it first, then stir that back in. Dropping it straight into hot sauce makes scrambled bits instead of a silky custard.","s":5},{"t":"Steam gently and take it off when the curry just wobbles in the center like a soft custard. Aggressive steaming or overcooking makes the fish rubbery and the sauce grainy.","s":8},{"t":"Pass the banana leaf briefly over a flame until it turns glossy and flexible. A cold leaf cracks when folded and leaks the sauce.","s":6}],"rl":"PMXZWMTXFGGG"},"53418":{"lv":"EEEEERRO","w":{"5":"Adds salt and savory depth. The fermented soybeans are already salty, so taste first.","6":"A pinch balances the saltiness of the soybeans. Skipping makes it a little harsher.","7":"Heat sprinkled on top; skipping makes a milder dish.","4":"The only fat for stir-frying the greens and pork; without it they stick and burn"},"sub":{"4":{"x":"1 tbsp peanut, canola or any neutral oil","d":"Used just to fry; any neutral oil works.","k":["canola oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Dry the greens well after washing and keep the wok very hot. Wet greens steam and turn gray; they should hiss and stay bright green and crisp.","s":3},{"t":"Cut the thicker stems separately and drop them in about 30 seconds before the leaves. They need slightly longer so the whole dish is evenly crisp-tender.","s":3},{"t":"Fermented soybeans are already very salty, so taste before adding fish sauce. Ground pork must be cooked through to 160F (71C), so brown it fully before the greens go in.","s":2}],"rl":"VPXXFXWX"},"53421":{"lv":"EEERRRRERRRRRREE","w":{"4":"Seasons the beef marinade. The soy and oyster sauce are also salty, so do not add more.","6":"Adds savory depth and gloss to the marinade. Skipping makes it less rich.","8":"Gives the beef a nutty aroma. Skipping is minor.","9":"A cool bed that balances the rich beef.","10":"Juicy acidity against the beef. Skipping loses freshness.","11":"Raw sharpness that cuts the richness.","12":"Fried egg is the traditional topper; the runny yolk acts as a sauce.","13":"Used for the pepper-lime dip. Skipping makes the dip bland.","2":"The high-heat sear fat for the beef; without it the beef sticks and does not brown","3":"Sweetens the marinade; other seasonings still flavor the beef","5":"Adds flavor to the marinade; the soy still carries it","14":"The pepper is the heart of the lok lak dipping sauce; without it the sauce loses its character"},"sub":{"2":{"x":"2 tbsp canola or any neutral high-heat oil","d":"Needs high heat for searing; avoid olive oil.","k":["vegetable oil"]},"3":{"x":"1 1/2 tbsp brown sugar","d":"Slightly more molasses flavor than palm sugar; browns the beef nicely.","k":["brown sugar"]},"5":{"x":"2 tbsp dry sherry (or 2 tbsp chicken broth plus 1/2 tsp white vinegar)","d":"Adds a slightly sweeter wine flavor; the broth version is milder.","k":["dry sherry"]},"14":{"x":"1 tbsp freshly cracked black peppercorns, dry-toasted","d":"Kampot pepper is more floral and fruity; regular black pepper is plainer but works.","k":["black pepper"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Get the pan very hot and sear the beef in batches without crowding or stirring for the first minute. Crowded cubes steam and turn gray instead of getting a dark crust.","s":2},{"t":"Dry-toast the peppercorns, grind them, and mix with salt and lime juice right before serving. This dipping sauce is the dish's signature; it should taste sharp and peppery.","s":3},{"t":"Keep the egg yolks runny. Cook the beef to your preference, but remember tenderloin cut into cubes has more surface to sear, so it is at its best medium-rare to medium.","s":4}],"rl":"PXFWZXXXXVVVGZXA"},"53422":{"lv":"ERRRROOEERRE","w":{"1":"Gives the green color and a floral vanilla-like aroma to the dough and syrup. Skipping loses that fragrance.","2":"Colors one batch of dough pink. Skip for a plain white dough.","3":"Colors and flavors one batch of dough orange. Skip for fewer colors.","4":"Adds a mild nutty sweetness to one batch of dough.","5":"A sweet fruity topping. Skipping is fine.","6":"A light texture topping. Skipping is fine.","9":"Sharpens the sweetness of the coconut cream.","10":"Adds gentle warmth to the syrup. Skipping makes it sweeter and plainer."},"tips":[{"t":"The ingredient list has no sugar, but the syrup needs it. Add about 1 cup palm sugar or brown sugar to the coconut cream in step 0, to taste, or the dessert will be bland.","s":0},{"t":"Add each coloring liquid gradually. Dough that is smooth and slightly sticky to the touch is right; too wet means it will fall apart in boiling water, so add more flour.","s":5},{"t":"Drop the balls into boiling water and wait for them to float, then boil one more minute. Floating alone means just done on the outside; the center must be chewy, not pasty.","s":3}],"rl":"SXCCCGGMMZXF"},"53500":{"lv":"EEEREE","w":{"3":"Balances the sweetness of the sugar and rounds out the coconut flavor.","4":"The leaves wrap and steam the rice cakes; without them the cakes cannot be formed"},"sub":{"4":{"x":"8 squares of parchment paper (about 10 inches), wrapped in foil","d":"Holds the shape and steams the same, but loses the leafy aroma.","k":["parchment paper","aluminum foil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Use glutinous (sweet) rice only and drain it very well after soaking. Ordinary long-grain rice will not turn sticky and chewy.","s":0},{"t":"Pass the banana leaf briefly over a flame until glossy to make it flexible. A cold leaf cracks when folded.","s":4},{"t":"Check the steamer water halfway and top it up with boiling water, never cold. After steaming, cool 20 minutes before unwrapping; the sticky rice firms up as it cools, so it slices cleanly with an oiled knife.","s":10}],"rl":"BMWZGB"},"53497":{"lv":"EERRERRERRRO","w":{"2":"Gives the broth a savory aromatic base. Skipping makes it blander.","5":"Seasons the broth. Adjust with fish sauce rather than adding more.","6":"Balances the fish sauce. Skipping makes the broth sharper.","8":"Crisp fresh topping that contrasts with the hot broth.","9":"Gives a cooling herbal note. Skipping loses freshness.","10":"Acid at the table brightens the broth.","11":"Optional heat at the table.","3":"Toasts the garlic; the broth still cooks"},"sub":{"3":{"x":"2 tbsp olive oil or any neutral oil","d":"Only used to toast the garlic and start the broth.","k":["olive oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Skim any gray foam that rises as the water comes to a boil. A clear broth looks and tastes cleaner. Chicken thighs must reach 165F (74C), which this simmer covers.","s":3},{"t":"Cook the noodles separately and rinse under cold water. Cooking them in the broth clouds it with starch and they turn mushy.","s":5},{"t":"Season the broth in stages: fish sauce for salt, sugar to round it out, then adjust at the end, because simmering concentrates the salt. Shred the chicken before ladling if you want easier eating.","s":4}],"rl":"MPXFZZWBGGAG"},"53503":{"lv":"EREEERRRRRRO","w":{"5":"Softens the tamarind's sharpness. Skipping makes the broth harsher.","6":"Sweet pineapple is part of the sweet-sour balance of this stew.","7":"Bursts and adds sweetness and acidity. Skipping loses part of the sour.","8":"Adds crunch and freshness. Skipping makes the stew heavier.","11":"Adds heat at the end. Skipping makes it mild.","1":"Browns the chicken; the chicken renders fat","9":"Aromatic for the sour stew; lime leaves and tamarind still flavor it","10":"Aromatic for the stew; lemongrass still flavors it"},"sub":{"1":{"x":"1 tbsp olive oil or any neutral oil","d":"Used only to brown the chicken.","k":["olive oil"]},"9":{"x":"2 strips lemon zest (no white pith)","d":"Gives citrusy perfume but less grassy than lemongrass.","k":["lemon"]},"10":{"x":"2 strips lime zest (no white pith)","d":"Gives a citrus note, but lacks the floral perfume of kaffir lime leaves.","k":["lime"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Bruise the lemongrass with the back of a knife before it goes in, so it releases oils into the broth. Whole and unbruised stalks give little flavor.","s":4},{"t":"Add the green beans last. Long simmering in an acidic broth turns them olive-drab and soft, while the tamarind sours the stew.","s":6},{"t":"Add tamarind and fish sauce a little at a time and taste. The stew should land on sour first, then sweet, then salty. Chicken thighs must reach 165F (74C).","s":10}],"rl":"PFMAZWVVVXXX"},"53420":{"lv":"EEEEER","w":{"5":"Seasons the batter and balances the sweetness of the palm sugar.","3":"The sweetener of the waffle batter; without it the waffles are bland and do not brown"},"sub":{"3":{"x":"3/4 cup packed brown sugar","d":"Slightly less caramel-like but dissolves just as well. The sweet batter browns quickly either way.","k":["brown sugar"]}},"tips":[{"t":"There is no leavening here, so expect a thin, crisp, slightly chewy waffle, not a fluffy one. Rice flour is what gives the crisp edges.","s":0},{"t":"The high sugar content burns fast. Use medium heat or a medium iron setting and brush the iron with oil before every waffle, or the batter will stick and scorch.","s":1},{"t":"Let the batter sit 10 minutes before cooking so the rice flour hydrates. Cooked right away it can taste gritty.","s":0}],"rl":"SSMWSZ"},"52965":{"lv":"EERRROROO","w":{"2":"Adds smoky salt and rendered fat that crisps the potatoes. Skipping makes a plainer, vegetarian-style dish.","3":"Adds savory aroma at the end. Skipping is mild.","5":"Garnish only.","6":"Seasons the potatoes. Bacon is already salty, so go lightly.","7":"Mild heat in the background.","8":"A tiny background warmth. Skip with no loss.","1":"The cooking fat for the potatoes; without it they stick and do not crisp","4":"Glazes the potatoes; the potatoes still brown without it"},"sub":{"1":{"x":"1 tbsp vegetable oil, butter or bacon fat","d":"Any fat works for browning; butter browns faster.","k":["vegetable oil"],"wn":"Butter contains dairy."},"4":{"x":"1 tbsp brown sugar or honey","d":"Gives the same caramel glaze; maple adds a distinct flavor.","k":["honey"],"wn":"Do not give honey to infants under 1 year old."}},"tips":[{"t":"Dry the soaked potatoes thoroughly in a towel before they hit the pan. Wet cubes steam instead of browning and stick.","s":3},{"t":"Do not stir for the first few minutes. Let the potatoes sit so a crust forms and they release naturally from the pan.","s":4},{"t":"Add the maple syrup only in the last minute. The sugar burns quickly in a hot skillet, so toss it just until it glazes.","s":8}],"rl":"BFFXWGZZX"},"52926":{"lv":"ROEERRERREO","w":{"0":"Absorbs fat and juices and keeps the filling moist and cohesive. Skipping gives a looser, drier filling.","4":"Adds savory depth. Skipping makes the filling a bit flatter.","5":"Part of the warm spice blend traditional in tourtiere. Skipping lessens the holiday aroma.","7":"Adds a warm nutmeg note. Use a small pinch if you skip, as it adds depth.","10":"Gives a golden, shiny crust. Skipping gives a paler pastry.","1":"Only starts the browning; the pork renders fat","8":"Moistens the filling; other liquid covers it"},"sub":{"1":{"x":"1 tsp olive oil or butter","d":"Only used to start the pork browning.","k":["olive oil"]},"8":{"x":"100 ml chicken broth or water","d":"Chicken broth is closer in flavor; water makes the filling milder.","k":["chicken broth"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Let the filling cool fully before putting it into the pastry. A hot filling melts the fat in the dough and gives a soggy bottom crust.","s":1},{"t":"Cut a few steam vents in the top crust and seal the edges well. Without vents, steam builds and can force juices out and make the crust soggy.","s":1},{"t":"Cook the ground pork until no pink remains and it reaches 160F (71C). The mashed potato should be well mixed in so the filling is cohesive and sliceable.","s":0}],"rl":"TFPXXXXXMSG"},"52927":{"lv":"EEEEROORRRRROROOE","w":{"4":"Balances the salt in the cure and helps the bark form. Skipping gives a sharper cure.","5":"Adds a subtle herbal note to the cure. Minor.","6":"Very small clove warmth. Skip with little effect.","7":"Builds the peppery bark, a signature of Montreal smoked meat.","8":"Builds the citrusy bark; reinforces the cure.","9":"Adds color and mild sweetness to the bark.","10":"Adds savory depth to the crust.","11":"Adds savory depth to the crust.","12":"A tiny herbal accent. Skip with no loss.","13":"Adds a tangy bite to the rub.","14":"Savory vegetal note. Minor.","15":"A hint of heat. Skip for a milder brisket."},"tips":[{"t":"The list omits the pink curing salt (curing salt #1, sodium nitrite) that step 0 requires. It is essential for a safe multi-day cure; use the amount on the package, usually about 1 level tsp per 5 lb (2.3 kg) of meat, and never skip it.","s":0},{"t":"Do not skip the soak. Rinse and soak the cured brisket, changing the water, to draw out excess salt; fry a small piece to test. Brisket that is too salty can be fixed by soaking longer.","s":1},{"t":"Steaming after the smoke is what makes real Montreal smoked meat so tender and juicy. Steam until a probe slides in with no resistance, about 195-205F (90-96C), then slice thick against the grain by hand.","s":4}],"rl":"PKXXWXXXXCXXXXZXK"},"52930":{"lv":"ERREEEOORO","w":{"8":"Seasons the beef layer. Canned creamed corn is already slightly salty.","6":"Adds color on top only.","7":"Garnish only.","9":"Mild heat; skip with little effect.","1":"Enriches the mash; milk still moistens it","2":"Loosens the mash; butter still helps"},"sub":{"1":{"x":"60 ml (4 tbsp) margarine or olive oil","d":"Less rich in mashed potato; olive oil gives a fruitier note.","k":["margarine"]},"2":{"x":"1/2 cup half-and-half or cream","d":"Richer and creamier than milk.","k":["half-and-half"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Drain the boiled potatoes and let them steam dry a minute before mashing. Wet potatoes make a loose, watery topping that sinks into the corn layer.","s":1},{"t":"Brown the beef until it is golden and any liquid has cooked off. It must reach 160F (71C). Wet beef makes the pie soupy when sliced.","s":3},{"t":"Rest the pie 10 minutes after the broiler so the layers set and you can cut clean squares.","s":5}],"rl":"BFMPXVGGZZ"},"52933":{"lv":"RREEEEO","w":{"1":"Slowly caramelized onion adds sweetness and savory depth between the layers. Skipping makes the pie plainer.","6":"Mild heat; the dish is traditionally seasoned simply.","0":"Cooks the onions; other fat covers it"},"sub":{"0":{"x":"2 tbsp margarine, vegetable oil or butter","d":"Used only to cook the onions; any fat works.","k":["butter"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Squeeze the grated potato pulp until it is as dry as possible and discard the starchy liquid. The pulp must then be rehydrated with hot broth, and that ratio is what gives rappie pie its signature texture.","s":3},{"t":"Work fast. Grated potato turns gray quickly when exposed to air, so get it into the broth and the pan quickly.","s":3},{"t":"Bake until the top is deeply browned and crusty, about an hour. It will look gray and wet underneath at first, but it sets. Chicken must reach 165F (74C).","s":5}],"rl":"FXMPBZZ"},"53335":{"lv":"EEEEEERO","w":{"6":"Enriches the pease pudding; skipping makes it plain and dry.","7":"Seasons the pease pudding. The salt beef is already salty, so add pepper only."},"tips":[{"t":"Soak the salt beef at least 8 hours and change the water once. Taste a piece of the cooked meat; if it is still too salty, simmer a few minutes in fresh water.","s":0},{"t":"Tie the pease pudding bag loosely. The split peas swell a lot as they cook, and a tight bag can burst or give a hard, dense center.","s":2},{"t":"Add the vegetables in order of cooking time. Cabbage goes in first as called for, and the roots follow. Do not add salt; the beef's brine seasons everything.","s":4}],"rl":"PBVVVBFZ"},"53336":{"lv":"EERERRRRO","w":{"2":"Adds sweetness and savory depth as it melts into the sauce. Skipping makes the beans plainer.","5":"Cuts the sweetness of the molasses and sugar. Skipping makes the beans cloying.","6":"Gives sharp, earthy depth that balances the sweetness.","7":"Seasons the beans, which are bland. Bacon is salty, so add near the end.","8":"Light background heat.","4":"Adds sweetness; molasses still sweetens the beans"},"sub":{"4":{"x":"1/4 cup extra molasses or 1/4 cup maple syrup","d":"Slightly darker and bittersweet with extra molasses, or milder with maple.","k":["molasses"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Parboil the beans only until the skins crack when you blow on one. They finish cooking in the oven, and overcooked beans turn to mush.","s":1},{"t":"Molasses and vinegar are acidic and slow bean softening, so do not rush. If the beans are not tender after 3 hours, keep baking; add more reserved water, not new water, so the flavor stays.","s":4},{"t":"Check the liquid each hour. The sauce should be thick and syrupy at the end, and if the edges start to scorch, stir them back into the pot.","s":5}],"rl":"BPXWWAXZZ"},"53340":{"lv":"EEERRRERERO","w":{"3":"Adds sweetness and color. Skipping is fine.","4":"Fresh herbal note that defines the creamy broth; skipping makes it plain.","5":"Mild onion flavor and color.","9":"Seasons the creamy broth; add gradually.","10":"Light peppery warmth.","7":"Enriches the sauce; the milk and butter still carry it"},"sub":{"7":{"x":"1/2 cup whole milk plus 1 tbsp butter","d":"Slightly less rich and thinner. Do not boil hard or it may curdle.","k":["whole milk","butter"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Cut the potatoes and carrots to the same size. They cook at the same rate and the dish looks uniform. Cook them only until just fork tender, because they keep cooking in the milk.","s":0},{"t":"Bring the milk and cream to a gentle simmer, never a hard boil. High heat scorches the bottom and can curdle the milk.","s":2},{"t":"Let the hodge podge sit 10 minutes before serving. The vegetables absorb butter and cream and the broth thickens slightly.","s":3}],"rl":"BVVVXXMFFZZ"},"52804":{"lv":"EEEE","sub":{"0":{"x":"2 cups canola or peanut oil for deep frying","d":"Any neutral high-heat oil; avoid olive oil, which smokes at fry temperature.","k":["vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Soak the cut potatoes in cold water for 30 minutes and dry them completely. Removing surface starch and moisture is what makes fries crisp and keeps the oil from spattering.","s":2},{"t":"For fries that stay crisp under gravy, blanch them at 325F (160C) for 3-4 minutes, rest, then fry again at 375F (190C) until deep golden. A single fry gives limp fries that turn soggy under the gravy.","s":2},{"t":"Use room-temperature squeaky cheese curds and piping-hot gravy. The hot gravy softens the curds without fully melting them; cold curds stay rubbery. Assemble at the last second.","s":5}],"rl":"FMBG","w":{"0":"The frying oil for the fries; without it the fries cannot be fried"}},"52925":{"lv":"EEERRRRO","w":{"3":"Adds sweetness and body to the broth. Skipping makes a flatter soup.","4":"Gives a subtle herbal background to the ham stock.","5":"Adds aromatic depth along with onion and carrot.","6":"Stirred in at the end for bright green color and sweetness. Skipping gives a duller, earthier soup.","7":"Served with the soup for dipping. Skipping is fine."},"tips":[{"t":"Boil the gammon briefly and discard that first water. This removes much of the salt and impurities, so the finished soup is not overly salty. Do not add extra salt until the end.","s":0},{"t":"Shred the ham while it is hot; it comes apart far more easily than when cold. Keep some for topping and stir the rest back in for flavor.","s":1},{"t":"Stir in the frozen peas only at the end and cook just until warmed. Long cooking turns them gray-green. Blend part of the soup for a creamy body while leaving some texture.","s":1}],"rl":"PBXVXVVG"},"52923":{"lv":"EEERRERO","w":{"3":"Traditional in butter tarts, adding chewy sweetness. Skipping makes a smoother, plainer tart.","4":"Rounds out the butter and brown sugar flavor.","6":"Loosens the filling slightly. Milk works instead.","7":"Adds crunch and bitterness against the sweet filling. Skip for a smooth tart."},"sub":{"6":{"x":"4 tsp whole milk or heavy cream","d":"Slightly less rich; barely noticeable in the set filling.","k":["milk"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Cook the filling on the stove for the 3-4 minutes as directed, stirring constantly. It makes the sugar dissolve and prevents a gritty, runny tart.","s":1},{"t":"Fill the shells only about two-thirds full. Butter tart filling bubbles up and spills over, and caramelized sugar glues the tarts to the tin.","s":2},{"t":"Pull them when the edges are set and the center still jiggles slightly. Leave in the tin a few minutes to firm up before lifting out, so the filling does not run.","s":2}],"rl":"SSWVXFFG"},"52924":{"lv":"EEEEERREEEEEE","w":{"5":"Adds chewy texture and a toasty coconut flavor to the base. Skipping makes a denser base.","6":"Adds crunch and nuttiness to the base. Skip with minor change.","0":"Likely the 125 g base butter that melts with the sugar and cocoa in step 0, not custard; the custard powder is separate. Use butter.","9":"The custard powder gives the middle layer its flavor and color; without it it is not nanaimo bar icing"},"sub":{"9":{"x":"3 tbsp cornstarch plus 1 tsp vanilla extract and a drop of yellow food coloring (optional)","d":"No custard flavor, and paler. Still a sweet, creamy vanilla icing.","k":["cornstarch","vanilla"]}},"tips":[{"t":"The \"125 g custard\" in the list is most likely the base butter (step 0 melts butter with the sugar and cocoa). Use 125 g butter for the base.","s":0},{"t":"Take the melted butter-cocoa mixture off the heat for a minute before whisking in the egg. Hot liquid scrambles it; you want a thick, glossy custard that binds the crumb base.","s":0},{"t":"Chill the base until firm before the custard layer, and chill the icing before the chocolate. Let the melted chocolate cool slightly so it does not melt the layer below. Cut with a hot knife.","s":2}],"rl":"FWXSBBBFFTWGF"},"52928":{"lv":"EEEEEEEEEORR","w":{"9":"A squeeze of lemon on white sugar is one classic topping. Skip for the cinnamon sugar.","10":"Used to coat the hot pastry for sweetness. Skipping leaves the plain fried dough.","11":"Mixed with sugar for the classic cinnamon-sugar coating.","8":"The frying oil for the dough; without it it cannot be fried"},"sub":{"8":{"x":"3 cups canola or sunflower oil for frying","d":"Any neutral high-heat oil works.","k":["vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Test the water: it should feel like a warm bath, about 105-110F (40-43C). Hot water kills yeast, and the 2 yeast portions are roughly one standard packet of about 2 1/4 tsp.","s":0},{"t":"The dough is sticky. Use oiled or lightly floured hands and a floured board, and stretch the dough rather than rolling; it holds a rustic oval shape.","s":4},{"t":"Keep the oil at 350F (175C). Too cool gives greasy, heavy dough and too hot burns the outside before the inside cooks. Toss in the cinnamon sugar while hot so it sticks.","s":5}],"rl":"MLWMFSZSFGGG"},"52929":{"lv":"EEEREEREO","w":{"3":"Seasons and balances the sweetness.","8":"A dusting is optional; the step uses cinnamon sugar instead.","6":"A little fat in the dough; the Timbits still work","7":"The frying oil for the Timbits; without it they cannot be fried"},"sub":{"6":{"x":"3 tbsp melted butter","d":"Slightly richer and more flavorful; use as is.","k":["butter"]},"7":{"x":"2 cups canola or peanut oil for frying","d":"Any neutral oil for frying.","k":["vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Mix the batter just until smooth. Overmixing develops gluten and makes tough, bready doughnut holes.","s":1},{"t":"Keep the oil near 365F (185C) and fry only a few at a time. If the oil is too cool they soak up grease, and if too hot they brown outside while the center stays raw.","s":2},{"t":"Drop the batter in with two small spoons, about a teaspoon each. Larger balls will not cook through before browning. Roll in sugar while still warm so it sticks.","s":2}],"rl":"SWLZSMFFG"},"52931":{"lv":"EEERREE","w":{"3":"Rounds out the brown sugar flavor. Skipping makes it plainer.","4":"Sharpens the sweetness; the pie is very sugary without it."},"tips":[{"t":"As written, this is crustless in a greased pie dish. For a traditional pie, use an unbaked 9-inch shell and bake as directed.","s":0},{"t":"The batter may look curdled when the milk goes in. This is normal for a sugar-butter mix and it smooths in the oven.","s":1},{"t":"Pull it when the center is just set with a slight wobble; it keeps firming as it cools. Cool completely, at least an hour, so slices hold.","s":2}],"rl":"WFSXZTM"},"52932":{"lv":"EEEREEEEREE","w":{"3":"Rounds out the cake. Skipping is mild next to the maple sauce.","8":"Adds caramel depth to the sauce. White sugar plus a spoon of molasses is nearly the same.","9":"The cream is the sauce that bakes with the cakes; without it there is no pouding chomeur sauce"},"sub":{"9":{"x":"1 1/2 cups half-and-half or heavy cream","d":"Heavy cream is richer and thicker; half-and-half is closest.","k":["half-and-half"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Pour the hot sauce over the batter gently, preferably over the back of a spoon, and do not stir. The cake rises up through the sauce as it bakes, which is the whole idea.","s":8},{"t":"Set the pan on a baking sheet. The sauce bubbles up around the sides and can drip and smoke in the oven.","s":9},{"t":"Rest 10-15 minutes after baking. The sauce thickens as it cools and soaks the bottom of the cake; serve warm in a bowl.","s":9}],"rl":"FWSXSLMWWFF"},"53337":{"lv":"EREERREEEOEROO","w":{"1":"Seasons the pastry. Skipping makes a bland crust.","4":"Adds richness and binding to the dough. Skipping gives a slightly crumblier pastry.","5":"Acid slows gluten formation, keeping the pastry tender. Skipping makes it slightly tougher.","9":"Sprinkled on top for a crunchy sugar crust. Skip with no effect on structure.","11":"Dotted on the filling for richness. Skipping makes the filling a bit less glossy.","12":"Egg wash gives a glossy, golden crust. Skip with a pale crust.","13":"Thins the egg wash so it brushes on evenly.","3":"The fat of the crust; without it the pastry is tough"},"sub":{"3":{"x":"1 cup additional cold butter (2 cups butter total)","d":"More flavor but less flaky and tender, and the dough is softer. Keep it very cold.","k":["butter"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Keep the butter and shortening cold and leave some pea-sized pieces. Those melt in the oven and create flaky layers. If the dough gets warm and greasy, chill it before rolling.","s":0},{"t":"Toss the berries with the sugar and cornstarch just before filling. The pie is done when the filling bubbles through the vents, which means the cornstarch has activated and the juices will thicken.","s":4},{"t":"Bake on the foil-lined sheet in the lower third and let the pie cool 2-3 hours before slicing. A hot berry pie will run when cut.","s":5}],"rl":"SZFFSAMVWGTFGM"},"53338":{"lv":"EEREEEERE","w":{"2":"The 1.5 cup looks like a data error; it is likely 1.5 tsp vanilla. It rounds out the date filling.","7":"Balances the sweetness of the dates and brown sugar. Skipping makes the crumble flat."},"tips":[{"t":"The list's 1.5 cup of vanilla is surely 1.5 tsp. Using a cup would ruin the filling.","s":1},{"t":"Simmer the dates until they collapse into a thick, jammy paste that holds a spoon trail. A loose filling makes the bars slide apart when cut.","s":1},{"t":"Do not overprocess the crumble; it should stay clumpy and slightly coarse. Cool completely in the pan, then chill before cutting for clean squares.","s":4}],"rl":"VMXBSWLZF"},"53339":{"lv":"EEEEREEERREE","w":{"4":"Adds depth to the molasses cookie. Skipping is mild.","8":"Warm spice that pairs with molasses. Skipping makes a plainer cookie.","9":"Balances the sweetness and molasses.","5":"This 2 tsp is dissolved in the water in step 2 and acts as baking soda, which reacts with the acidic molasses to leaven the cookies.","7":"Looks like a typo for 2 tsp; 2 tbsp (6 tsp) of leavener tastes metallic and makes the cookies puff then collapse.","11":"The jam is the filling of the cookies; without it they are plain cookies"},"sub":{"11":{"x":"2 cups raspberry, strawberry or apricot jam","d":"Any thick jam works; a tart one balances the sweet cookie best.","k":["jam"]}},"tips":[{"t":"The list has two leaveners. The 2 tsp dissolved in water is really baking soda (step 2), which reacts with the acidic molasses. The 2 tbsp baking powder looks like a typo for 2 tsp; use 2 tsp so the cookies do not taste metallic or collapse.","s":2},{"t":"Press the dough balls flat with a floured glass or moistened palm. They do not spread much, so uniform thin discs bake evenly.","s":4},{"t":"Cool the cookies fully before sandwiching them with jam, and fill just before serving. The jam softens the cookie into a cake-like texture over a day.","s":6}],"rl":"SFWWXLMLXZSG"},"53341":{"lv":"EEROEEEERREER","w":{"2":"Helps bind the crumbs and sweetens the crust.","3":"Light warmth in the crust. Skip with little effect.","8":"Rounds out the custard flavor.","9":"Sharpens the custard sweetness.","12":"Stabilizes the egg whites so the meringue stays tall and does not weep."},"sub":{"12":{"x":"1/2 tsp lemon juice or white vinegar","d":"Same stabilizing acid; no flavor impact in this amount.","k":["lemon juice"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Bring the custard to a full bubbling boil while stirring and cook a minute. Cornstarch needs the heat to thicken fully, and undercooked custard tastes raw and runs.","s":2},{"t":"Make sure no yolk gets into the whites and the bowl is grease-free. Add the sugar gradually until the meringue is stiff and glossy.","s":3},{"t":"Spread the meringue over the warm filling and seal it to the crust edges so it does not shrink or weep. Eat within hours; the meringue breaks down overnight.","s":4}],"rl":"BFWXMWTSXZSWA"},"53342":{"lv":"EEERRREEEE","w":{"3":"Warm spice that brightens the molasses. Skipping makes a plainer pudding.","4":"A defining note in traditional duff. Skipping makes it less spiced.","5":"Warm background spice."},"tips":[{"t":"Dip the pudding cloth in boiling water and flour it before filling. The flour forms a skin that keeps water out of the pudding.","s":2},{"t":"Tie the bag leaving room for the pudding to expand, and put a small plate under it so it does not scorch on the bottom of the pot.","s":2},{"t":"Keep the water at a gentle boil and top it up with boiling water as needed. The duff is done when it is firm to the touch; serve warm with rum butter or molasses sauce.","s":3}],"rl":"SWLXXXVFWM"},"53504":{"lv":"EEEERERRRR","w":{"4":"Seasons the pasta water and balances the sweetness of the pudding.","6":"Adds richness. Skipping makes a leaner custard.","7":"Adds chewy sweetness. Skipping makes a plainer pudding.","8":"The signature warm spice of the pudding.","9":"Rounds out the custard flavor."},"tips":[{"t":"Do not overcook the macaroni past tender. It keeps absorbing the custard as it bakes and can turn mushy.","s":0},{"t":"Add the hot milk to the egg-sugar mixture slowly while whisking. This tempers the eggs so they do not scramble. 3 cups of sugar is very sweet; for a milder pudding start with 1 1/2 cups.","s":2},{"t":"Bake until the center is set and golden, and rest 15 minutes before cutting. The extra liquid sets into a custard as it cools.","s":4}],"rl":"BMMWZSFVXX"},"53511":{"lv":"EERRRREERRR","w":{"2":"Left whole for fruity heat without burning spice. Skipping makes a milder, less aromatic dish.","3":"Adds mild onion flavor.","4":"Adds an herbal note.","5":"Adds savory aroma.","8":"Adds subtle aroma to the coconut broth.","9":"Adds warm, peppery Caribbean spice. Skipping loses a classic flavor.","10":"Adds aroma to the beans."},"tips":[{"t":"Boil the salt beef in fresh water repeatedly, as step 0 says, until a taste of the broth is only mildly salty. Skipping this makes the dish inedibly salty.","s":0},{"t":"If the beans are dried, soak overnight, then boil hard for at least 10 minutes. Raw kidney beans contain a toxin destroyed only by a full boil.","s":1},{"t":"Keep the scotch bonnet whole and remove it carefully. A burst pepper turns the whole pot fiery. Keep the coconut milk at a gentle simmer so it does not split.","s":3}],"rl":"PBXXXXMMXXX"},"53505":{"lv":"ERRERRRRROOEEE","w":{"1":"Adds sweetness and color to the fritter mix.","2":"Adds sweetness and color to the fritter mix.","4":"Gives heat and bite. Skip for a milder fritter.","5":"Tenderizes the conch and adds acidity.","6":"Adds savory depth.","7":"Adds peppery warmth.","8":"Seasons the batter; conch is bland.","9":"A small herbal note.","10":"Adds small extra heat.","13":"The frying oil for the fritters; without it they cannot be fried"},"sub":{"13":{"x":"2 cups canola or peanut oil for frying","d":"Any neutral high-heat oil.","k":["vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Grind or chop the conch very finely. It is tough, and large pieces make rubbery fritters. Make the mix the day before so it tenderizes in the lime juice.","s":1},{"t":"As written there is no leavener, so the fritters are dense. Add 1 tsp baking powder with the flour for lighter ones.","s":1},{"t":"Fry at about 350F (175C) rather than low heat, which makes greasy fritters. Test one first: the inside should be cooked through and the outside golden before you fry the rest.","s":2}],"rl":"PVVVXAXZZXXTSF"},"53506":{"lv":"EERRRRE","w":{"2":"Adds sweetness and savory body to the rice.","3":"Left whole for fruity aroma and gentle heat. Skip for a plain rice.","4":"Warm pepper flavor in the rice.","5":"Seasons the rice and beans; rice and beans taste flat without it."},"tips":[{"t":"Use about 2 cups long-grain rice, rinsed, and the total liquid should be roughly 1 3/4 cups per cup of rice from bean liquid, coconut milk and water.","s":0},{"t":"Dried kidney beans must be boiled hard for at least 10 minutes before simmering, because raw beans are toxic at low heat. Cook them until tender before adding the rice.","s":0},{"t":"Lay the scotch bonnet on top whole and do not stir after the rice goes in. Stirring makes the rice gummy and bursts the pepper.","s":1}],"rl":"BFXXZZB"},"53509":{"lv":"EERORE","w":{"2":"Gives fruity heat and aroma. Seed it for less fire; skipping makes a milder, sweeter crab.","3":"Light background pepper; crab and scotch bonnet already carry the flavor.","4":"Seasons the crab, which is naturally sweet and mild.","5":"The frying fat for the crab; without it the crab sticks and does not fry"},"sub":{"5":{"x":"1/4 cup butter or olive oil","d":"Butter is richer and browns the onion faster; watch the heat.","k":["butter"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Cook live crabs by dropping them into a large pot of boiling water for the full 15 minutes, until the shells turn bright red. Discard any crab that was already dead before cooking.","s":0},{"t":"Pick through the crab meat for shell fragments before sauteing. Cooked crab is already done, so fold it into the onions only to warm through and season, or it turns dry and stringy.","s":1},{"t":"Wear gloves when seeding and dicing the scotch bonnet and keep the seeds out unless you want serious heat. Serve with starchy sides like plantain or breadfruit to absorb the heat.","s":1}],"rl":"PVXZZF"},"53510":{"lv":"EEEEERRRORRR","w":{"5":"Adds savory aroma to the sauce. Skipping makes it flatter.","6":"Gives fruity heat. Remove seeds for less fire; skipping makes a mild, sweet sauce.","7":"Seasons the sauce; the broth is already salty, so add gradually.","8":"Light background heat.","9":"A classic Caribbean herb that rounds out the peppers.","10":"Lightly thickens the sauce so it clings to the lobster.","11":"Fat for cooking the lobster; butter also covers it"},"sub":{"11":{"x":"2 tbsp butter","d":"Richer and a little sweeter than olive oil.","k":["butter"],"wn":"Butter contains dairy."}},"tips":[{"t":"Boil the lobster just until the shells are bright red and the meat is firm. The meat will be cooked again in the sauce, so do not let it go rubbery in the pot.","s":0},{"t":"Warm the chopped lobster in the sauce only briefly. Long simmering after the lobster goes in makes it tough and chewy.","s":2},{"t":"Mix the cornstarch with a little cold water first and stir it in while the sauce simmers. Adding it dry makes lumps, and the sauce should only lightly coat the lobster.","s":2}],"rl":"PMVVVXXZZXTF"},"53507":{"lv":"ERRRERROERR","w":{"1":"Adds sweet crunch and color. Skipping is fine.","2":"Adds sweet crunch and color. Skipping is fine.","3":"Adds raw onion bite. Skipping makes it milder.","5":"Adds tang to the dressing.","6":"Balances the mustard and beets.","7":"Light background heat.","9":"Adds sweetness and color. Skipping is fine.","10":"Adds richness and texture. Skip for a vegan salad."},"tips":[{"t":"Cut the potatoes in even cubes and drain them very well. Wet potatoes make a watery, bland salad.","s":0},{"t":"Fold in the beets gently at the end. They bleed and turn the whole salad pink, which is traditional, but stirring hard also breaks the potatoes into mush.","s":2},{"t":"Because it contains mayonnaise and eggs, chill within two hours and keep it below 40F (4C). The 2-3 hours in the fridge also lets the flavors blend.","s":2}],"rl":"BVVVFXWZVVF"},"53512":{"lv":"EERRRROOERRERRE","w":{"2":"Adds sweetness and color to the stew base.","3":"Adds sweetness and color to the stew base.","4":"Fresh onion flavor at the end.","5":"Seasons the stew; conch is bland.","6":"Light peppery warmth.","7":"A small spice boost; skip with little change.","9":"Thickens the broth slightly.","10":"Adds heat; leave out for a mild stew.","12":"Makes the sea pie dough slightly pliable.","13":"Seasons the sea pie dough."},"tips":[{"t":"Pound the conch thoroughly before cooking. It is very tough, and a mallet breaks down the fibers; a pressure cooker cuts the 2-3 hour boil to under an hour.","s":1},{"t":"Roll the sea pie dough thin and let it rest 30 minutes before stretching; thick dumplings stay doughy in the stew.","s":3},{"t":"Add the sea pie squares in the last 20-30 minutes and keep the stew at a gentle simmer. Thin the flour slurry before stirring it in so the broth does not lump.","s":3}],"rl":"PXVVXZZXMTXSFZM"},"53508":{"lv":"EEERRR","w":{"3":"Warm spice that defines the cake's aroma. Skipping makes a plainer cake.","4":"Warm spice that adds depth.","5":"Warm spice that adds depth."},"tips":[{"t":"Cassava must be fully peeled and cooked all the way through. Raw cassava contains cyanide-producing compounds that cooking removes, so never undercook the cake.","s":3},{"t":"Simmer the coconut-sugar mixture until it turns thick and caramel-colored. That reduction gives the cake its rich flavor and is the base of its chewy texture.","s":0},{"t":"Let the cake cool completely before cutting. It sets as it cools and is far too soft when warm.","s":4}],"rl":"BWMXXX"},"53514":{"lv":"RERREROERRRREEEO","w":{"2":"Adds savory aroma to the pino filling.","3":"Traditional herb in the filling. Skipping makes it less aromatic.","5":"Seasons the filling.","6":"A light peppery warmth.","8":"Sweet bursts against the salty meat, a classic empanada de pino touch.","9":"Salty, briny contrast to the sweet raisins.","10":"Hard-boiled egg slices are part of the traditional filling; skipping is fine but plainer.","11":"Goes with cornstarch, which is missing from the list, to thicken the filling.","15":"Brushed on top for a glossy golden crust. Skip for a paler finish.","0":"Cooks the onions; other fat covers it","12":"The fat of the empanada dough; without it the dough is dry and not flaky"},"sub":{"0":{"x":"1 tbsp olive oil or vegetable oil","d":"Only used to cook the onions.","k":["olive oil"]},"12":{"x":"1 cup melted lard or butter","d":"Butter gives a richer, flakier crust but browns faster; lard is traditional.","k":["butter"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Add about 1 tbsp cornstarch to the filling and about 1/2 cup warm milk to the dough.","s":1},{"t":"Cook the pino until it is thick and not wet, then cool it before filling. A hot or wet filling softens the dough and makes the empanadas leak. The pork must reach 160F (71C).","s":1},{"t":"Press the edges with a fork and brush with egg for a crisp, glossy crust. Place a slice of hard-boiled egg and an olive on top of the filling before sealing.","s":4}],"rl":"FXXXXZZPVGGMFSZG"},"53517":{"lv":"ERRRRERRREROORO","w":{"2":"Seasons the mash.","3":"Enriches the mashed potato and helps it brown on top.","6":"Adds savory aroma to the beef.","7":"Adds moisture and a fresh tomato note to the filling.","8":"Deepens the tomato flavor of the filling.","11":"Garnish only.","13":"Seasons the beef filling.","12":"Small heat accent.","14":"Light peppery warmth.","1":"Enriches the mash; milk still moistens it","4":"Cooks the filling; the meat renders fat","10":"A cheese for flavor; the pie works without it"},"sub":{"1":{"x":"2 tbsp margarine or olive oil","d":"Slightly less rich in the mash.","k":["margarine"]},"4":{"x":"1/2 cup vegetable oil, olive oil or melted butter","d":"Any cooking fat works; butter adds richness.","k":["olive oil"]},"10":{"x":"2 tbsp grated mozzarella, Gouda or mild cheddar","d":"Panquehue is a soft Chilean cheese; any mild melting cheese works.","k":["mozzarella"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Let the potatoes steam dry after draining, mash while hot and let them cool until just warm before beating in the eggs. Hot mash scrambles the eggs.","s":4},{"t":"Cook the beef until browned and all the liquid has evaporated, to 160F (71C). Wet filling makes the top potato layer sink and the pie watery.","s":2},{"t":"Smooth the top potato layer and rake it with a fork so more of it browns. Finish under the broiler for a golden crust.","s":6}],"rl":"BFZTFXXVXPFGXZZ"},"53520":{"lv":"RERROOERRREERRRR","w":{"0":"Hard-boiled egg in the pino is traditional, adding richness. Skipping is fine.","2":"Adds savory aroma to the beef.","3":"Seasons the filling.","4":"Light color and mild flavor.","5":"Light peppery warmth.","7":"Traditional second meat in the pino; skipping is fine but plainer.","8":"Sweet contrast to the salty meat and olives.","9":"Briny contrast to the sweet raisins.","12":"Adds richness to the corn paste.","14":"Caramelizes into a crackly sugar crust on top.","15":"Seasons the corn topping.","13":"Loosens the corn topping; butter and other liquid cover it"},"sub":{"13":{"x":"1 cup half-and-half or water plus a knob of butter","d":"Half-and-half is richer; water is lighter.","k":["half-and-half"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Blend the corn until it is smooth and cook it, stirring constantly, until it is as thick as mashed potatoes. It scorches easily, and a loose paste makes the top sink into the pino.","s":3},{"t":"Basil is the signature herb in the corn paste. Blend it in with the corn, as in step 3.","s":3},{"t":"Sprinkle sugar on top before baking; it caramelizes into a thin crackly crust. The pino should be moist, not soupy, and the beef and any chicken must be fully cooked to 160F/165F (71C/74C).","s":5}],"rl":"GXXZXZPPVGBXFMWZ"},"53518":{"lv":"EEREERE","w":{"2":"Seasons the dough.","5":"Seals the edges and gives a golden crust when baked.","3":"The fat of the empanada dough; without it the dough is dry and not flaky"},"sub":{"3":{"x":"2 tbsp butter or vegetable shortening","d":"Butter is richer and browns faster; shortening is closer to lard.","k":["butter"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Pour the hot milk-lard mixture into the flour right away and mix. The hot liquid makes the dough tender and easy to roll.","s":0},{"t":"Use a small mound of cheese and seal the edges tightly. Cheese leaks out and burns if you overfill or leave a gap.","s":2},{"t":"If you fry them, keep the oil at 375F (190C) and skip the egg wash on the outside; it burns. Drain on paper and serve right away while the cheese is melted.","s":4}],"rl":"SLZFMGP"},"53513":{"lv":"EEEREE","w":{"3":"Seasons the dough.","5":"The frying oil for the sopaipillas; without it they cannot be fried"},"sub":{"5":{"x":"2 cups sunflower or vegetable oil","d":"Any neutral oil works.","k":["vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Drain the cooked squash and mash it until smooth. Excess water makes the dough sticky and gives heavy, greasy sopaipillas.","s":0},{"t":"Roll the dough thin and prick with a fork, as directed, so the circles puff only slightly and do not balloon. Fry at 385F (195C).","s":2},{"t":"For authentic sopaipillas pasadas, serve them soaked in warm chancaca (raw cane sugar) syrup, or with pebre on the side.","s":3}],"rl":"BSLZFF"},"53516":{"lv":"EEEERE","w":{"4":"Seasons the dough."},"tips":[{"t":"Brush each circle generously with melted butter before folding. Those butter layers are what make the dobladitas flaky.","s":2},{"t":"Knead only until the dough comes together. Overworking makes it tough and keeps it from layering.","s":1},{"t":"Bake at the full 450F (230C). The high heat sets the layers quickly; serve while warm.","s":3}],"rl":"SFMLZF"},"53521":{"lv":"REEEEEEEO","w":{"8":"Sprinkled on top for mild heat and crunch only.","0":"Softens the onion; it still cooks in the broth","5":"The liquid of the stew; without it the beans scorch"},"sub":{"0":{"x":"1 tbsp vegetable oil or butter","d":"Only used to soften the onion.","k":["vegetable oil"]},"5":{"x":"3 cups vegetable broth, or 3 cups water plus 3/4 tsp salt","d":"Lighter, vegetarian-friendly; add salt carefully.","k":["vegetable broth"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Cook the squash until it breaks down on its own. The soft squash thickens the stew, so mash a little against the pot if needed.","s":1},{"t":"Add the basil near the end. It is the signature flavor of porotos granados, and long cooking turns it dull.","s":2},{"t":"Stop cooking when the stew is as thick as pancake batter. Canned beans are already soft, so add them late or they split apart.","s":2}],"rl":"FXVBBMVXG"},"53515":{"lv":"EERE","w":{"2":"Rounds out the caramel flavor. Skipping is mild.","3":"The step uses baking soda, not baking powder; use 1/4 tsp baking soda, which neutralizes acidity so the milk does not curdle and helps it brown."},"tips":[{"t":"The step uses baking soda, not baking powder; use 1/4 tsp baking soda. It lowers the milk's acidity so it does not curdle and speeds browning. Stir it in off the heat, since it foams up.","s":1},{"t":"Use a heavy-bottomed pot and keep stirring, scraping the bottom. Milk sugar scorches easily and a burnt layer ruins the whole batch.","s":0},{"t":"Test by dropping a little on a cold plate; it should hold a thick trail. It thickens a lot as it chills, so stop slightly short of the final texture.","s":1}],"rl":"MWXC"},"53519":{"lv":"ERREE","w":{"1":"Perfumes the custard. Skipping makes it plainer.","2":"Gives the warm spice that flavors the milk. Skipping loses the aroma."},"tips":[{"t":"Add the hot milk to the eggs a little at a time, whisking. This tempers the eggs so they do not scramble.","s":2},{"t":"Strain through a fine sieve and bake in a water bath if you can. The gentle heat keeps the custard silky; the top browns while the inside stays creamy.","s":3},{"t":"It is done when the edges are set and the center still wobbles slightly. Chill fully before serving; the custard firms as it cools.","s":3}],"rl":"MXXSW"},"3085":{"lv":"EEERREEERRRRRRERO","w":{"3":"Adds a little body to the crust; cornstarch alone also works but fries slightly lighter.","4":"Seasons the egg-coated chicken from the inside; skip it and lean on a bit more soy in the sauce.","8":"Sweetness and a molasses note that makes the glaze sticky and dark.","9":"Brightens the sweet, salty glaze; without it the sauce tastes cloying.","10":"Gives the nutty sesame aroma the dish is named for; skipping it leaves the sauce flatter.","11":"Stretches the sauce so it coats instead of sticking in clumps; the soy and honey carry the flavor.","12":"Savory backbone in the sauce; skip and it tastes sweeter and plainer.","13":"Warm bite that balances the honey; the sauce still works without it.","15":"Toasted seeds give crunch and the sesame identity on top; skipping loses texture, not the sauce.","16":"Raw fresh bite and color on top; purely a finishing garnish."},"sub":{"3":{"x":"1/4 cup extra cornstarch (3/4 cup total)","d":"Slightly lighter, glassier crust with less body.","k":["cornstarch"]},"8":{"x":"3 tbsp white sugar + 1 tsp honey or molasses","d":"Less caramel depth but the same sweetness.","k":["sugar","honey"]},"9":{"x":"2 tbsp apple cider vinegar or white vinegar","d":"A little sharper and fruitier; start with 1 1/2 tbsp and taste.","k":["apple cider vinegar"]},"11":{"x":"1/2 cup water + 1 tsp soy sauce","d":"Thinner savory depth; the sauce will taste a bit less rounded.","k":["water","soy sauce"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Fry in 2 or 3 batches and let the oil climb back to 350F between them; crowding drops the temperature and gives pale, greasy chicken. Cut into a thick piece to check it hits 165F.","s":2},{"t":"Pull the sauce when it coats the back of a spoon; it keeps thickening as it cools. If it turns gluey, loosen with a splash of broth.","s":4},{"t":"Toss the chicken in the sauce right before serving; the cornstarch crust turns soft within a few minutes of sitting in glaze.","s":5}],"rl":"PTTTZFXWWAXMXXTGG"},"3086":{"lv":"EEEEEEROEERRO","w":{"6":"Acid that cuts the richness of the mayonnaise sauce.","7":"Adds a milky sweetness and gloss that is classic in the restaurant version; the sauce is fine without it.","10":"Boiling the walnuts removes tannic bitterness so the candy coating tastes clean; skipping leaves a slightly harsh bite.","11":"Light seasoning for the shrimp coating; the sauce adds more flavor than the shrimp need.","12":"Serving side only; adds color and freshness but is not part of the dish itself."},"sub":{"6":{"x":"1 tbsp lime juice","d":"Slightly more floral; works identically to cut the mayo.","k":["lime juice"]}},"tips":[{"t":"After boiling, drain the walnuts well and shake off water before sugaring; wet walnuts make the sugar clump and the oil splatter.","s":0},{"t":"Pull the walnuts at deep golden; the sugar keeps darkening off the heat and burnt sugar is bitter. Spread them in a single layer on parchment so they do not weld together.","s":1},{"t":"Toss the shrimp in the mayo sauce in a bowl, not in a hot pan; heated mayonnaise breaks and turns oily. Add the walnuts last so they stay crunchy.","s":5}],"rl":"PTTFFWAWGWMZG"},"3093":{"lv":"EEEEEROREOR","w":{"4":"Sugar caramelizes into the dark, sticky lacquer; sweetness is part of char siu.","5":"Adds savory depth and gloss; soy plus a little extra hoisin covers it.","6":"Balances sweetness; a splash of any mild vinegar covers it.","7":"Aromatic backbone for the marinade; skipping makes it a bit one-dimensional.","9":"Only gives the red restaurant color; no flavor effect.","10":"Final honey glaze gives the shine and a last layer of sweetness; skip and the surface looks matte."},"sub":{"4":{"x":"3 tbsp white sugar + 1 tsp honey","d":"Slightly lighter caramel flavor; same sticky glaze.","k":["sugar","honey"]},"6":{"x":"1 tbsp apple cider vinegar or white vinegar","d":"A touch sharper; fine in this strongly flavored marinade.","k":["apple cider vinegar"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Reserve the 1/4 cup of marinade before the raw pork touches it, as the recipe says; never brush with marinade that held raw meat unless you boil it first.","s":1},{"t":"The water in the foil-lined pan stops the dripping sugar from scorching and smoking. Top it up if it evaporates.","s":2},{"t":"For restaurant-style charred edges, finish 2-3 minutes under the broiler, watching constantly since honey burns fast. Slice across the grain after a 10-minute rest.","s":5}],"rl":"PXWXWXAXXCW"},"3095":{"lv":"EEREEREREERERO","w":{"2":"Lightens and crisps the batter alongside cornstarch.","5":"Seasons the batter; a small amount, the sauce carries most of the flavor.","7":"Thins and flavors the sauce so it is not just sweet and sour; water plus soy covers it.","10":"Savory depth that keeps the sauce from tasting like plain lemonade.","12":"Fresh lemon slices on top add aroma and a look; the sauce does the flavoring.","13":"Color and mild bite as a garnish only."},"sub":{"2":{"x":"1/4 cup extra cornstarch","d":"A slightly lighter, crisper but less sturdy coating.","k":["cornstarch"]},"7":{"x":"3/4 cup water + 1/2 tsp chicken bouillon","d":"A little less savory depth; bouillon replaces the broth flavor.","k":["water","chicken bouillon"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Keep the batter cold and mix just until smooth; the baking powder puffs it in the hot oil. Coat and fry immediately, because standing batter loses its lift.","s":0},{"t":"Finish the sauce with a little fresh lemon juice or zest after it thickens; lemon loses its brightness when boiled for long.","s":4},{"t":"Use raw lemon slices only as a garnish; if they simmer in the sauce the pith turns bitter.","s":5}],"rl":"PTTTLZFMWAXTGG"},"3099":{"lv":"ERREEERRRRREERE","w":{"1":"Seasons and tenderizes the pork in the quick marinade.","2":"Velvets the pork so it stays tender in a hot wok; skip and it cooks slightly firmer.","6":"Adds sweetness and color; cabbage and mushrooms carry the filling if skipped.","7":"Earthy chew; shiitake or wood ear is classic, but it can be dropped.","8":"Fresh onion bite that rounds the filling.","9":"Aromatic base; skip and the filling is milder.","10":"Warm aromatic note typical of moo shu.","13":"Toasty aroma added at the end; skipping makes it less fragrant."},"tips":[{"t":"Scramble the eggs just to soft curds and pull them; they finish cooking when tossed back in, and overcooked eggs turn rubbery in the filling.","s":1},{"t":"Shred the cabbage thin and keep the wok very hot; steam from wet vegetables makes the filling soggy and the tortillas tear.","s":3},{"t":"Warm the tortillas wrapped in a damp towel for 20 seconds so they fold without cracking, and spread the hoisin edge to edge.","s":5}],"rl":"PXTFPVVVVXXXXXB"},"3102":{"lv":"ERREEOERRRRR","w":{"1":"Flavors the simmering water so the pork does not taste of plain boiled belly.","2":"Aromatic simmer note; skip and use just ginger.","5":"Fermented funk that deepens the sauce; doubanjiang alone still works.","7":"Sweet crunch and color, a secondary vegetable.","8":"Savory aroma in the stir-fry; milder without it.","9":"Adds salt and depth on top of the very salty bean paste.","10":"Rounds the spicy, salty bean paste.","11":"Brightens the fatty pork at the end; skip and it tastes heavier."},"tips":[{"t":"Chill the simmered belly for 20 to 30 minutes if you can; cold, firm pork slices thin and evenly, and thin slices curl and crisp properly.","s":1},{"t":"Fry the slices until they curl into a cup shape and the fat renders; this crisp texture is what twice-cooked pork is about.","s":2},{"t":"Fry the bean paste on medium heat in the rendered fat until the oil turns red; if it scorches it turns bitter. It is salty, so go easy on the extra soy.","s":3}],"rl":"PXXFXXVVXXWA"},"52945":{"lv":"OEREEEERRRERE","w":{"0":"Rice wine or sake adds depth to the marinade and sauce; broth plus a touch of acid replaces it.","2":"Nutty aroma in the marinade; the sauce is fine without it.","7":"Small acid note in the sauce; skip and it tastes slightly flatter.","8":"Balances the chili heat.","9":"Fresh onion note in the sauce.","11":"Crisp sweet bite that contrasts with the soft chicken."},"sub":{"0":{"x":"2 tbsp dry white wine","d":"Slightly sharper and less sweet than sake; works in the marinade and sauce.","k":["white wine"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Cut the chicken into even pieces so they all cook through; juices running clear and an internal 165F means done.","s":3},{"t":"Chili powder scorches quickly; stir it into the sauce liquid as written instead of dropping it into dry hot oil.","s":2},{"t":"Add the peanuts toward the end so they stay crunchy instead of going soft in the sauce.","s":2}],"rl":"XXXTMPXAWXXVG"},"52948":{"lv":"ERRERRRREER","w":{"1":"Adds savory aroma to the pork filling.","2":"Warm, fresh note that balances the pork.","4":"Nutty aroma in the filling; wontons still taste fine without it.","5":"Sweetness and moisture in the filling.","6":"Juicy crunch that lightens the dense pork.","7":"Fresh onion flavor in the filling.","10":"Used to seal the wrappers; without sealing they open in the oil."},"tips":[{"t":"Chop the carrots and celery very fine and pat them dry; wet filling makes the wrappers soggy and prone to bursting.","s":0},{"t":"Brush water on only two edges and press out the air before sealing; trapped air makes wontons pop in the oil.","s":3},{"t":"450F is too hot for this filling; the skin would burn long before the pork cooks. Fry at 350 to 375F until golden and the pork reaches 160F.","s":6}],"rl":"PXXXXVVXSFM"},"52950":{"lv":"ERROREERRRORRROERERE","w":{"1":"Seasons the beef in the marinade.","2":"Nutty finishing aroma in the marinade.","3":"Small background heat.","4":"Velvets the beef for a tender, silky bite; without it the beef is chewier.","7":"Aromatic base, adds fresh warmth.","8":"Savory base note.","9":"Sweet, soft body to the vegetable mix.","10":"Adds sweetness and color.","11":"Sweet crunch and color.","12":"Adds crunch and moisture.","13":"Earthy, meaty bulk.","14":"Aromatic depth; broth plus a little vinegar replaces it.","16":"Savory sweetness; extra soy covers it.","17":"Provides the heat, which defines Szechuan style.","18":"Rounds the sauce."},"sub":{"14":{"x":"1 tbsp chicken broth + 1/2 tsp rice vinegar","d":"Less aromatic depth, but the sauce stays balanced.","k":["chicken broth","rice vinegar"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Mix the egg white, cornstarch and a spoon of oil into the beef for 20 to 30 minutes; this velveting coat keeps it silky in the hot wok.","s":1},{"t":"Sear the beef in a single layer in a very hot wok and pull it while still a little under; it finishes in the sauce.","s":3},{"t":"Add the slurry in small splashes while stirring; the sauce fully thickens only after it returns to a boil, so stop when it coats the spoon.","s":9}],"rl":"PZXZTTFXXVVVVVXMXXWX"},"52952":{"lv":"ERORRREERRRRRREOE","w":{"1":"Light seasoning in the beef marinade.","2":"Tiny background warmth; skip freely.","3":"Nutty aroma in the marinade; the dish still tastes right without it.","4":"Half an egg velvets the beef; skip and the beef is slightly tougher.","5":"Coats the beef and thickens the sauce a little.","8":"Sweet savory base for the vegetables.","9":"Aromatic base.","10":"Aromatic warmth against the beef.","11":"Crunch and freshness; lo mein works without it.","12":"Earthy bulk and umami.","13":"Makes the pan sauce that coats the noodles; too little and the noodles dry out.","15":"A pinch of sweetness to round the oyster sauce."},"tips":[{"t":"Boil the noodles only briefly, rinse in cold water and toss with a teaspoon of oil; they finish cooking in the wok, and soft pre-cooked noodles turn to mush.","s":3},{"t":"Reduce the pan liquid for a minute or two before adding the noodles so the lo mein is glossy, not soupy.","s":7},{"t":"Slice the beef against the grain, thin, and let the marinade sit at least 15 minutes; the egg and cornstarch protect it in the hot wok.","s":1}],"rl":"PZZXTTFBVXXVVMXWX"},"52956":{"lv":"ERORRREEO","w":{"1":"Seasons the chicken directly; adjust the final salt at the end.","2":"Gentle background heat; optional.","3":"Ginger juice neutralizes any poultry smell in the marinade.","4":"Warm, clean aromatic; congee tastes flat without it.","5":"Fresh onion finish that lifts the plain porridge.","8":"Fresh garnish only; the congee is complete without it."},"tips":[{"t":"Keep the heat low and stir every few minutes; the starch settles and scorches on the pot bottom. The rice should burst into a creamy porridge after about an hour.","s":6},{"t":"Slice the chicken thin so it cooks in the last 10 minutes; make sure no pink remains and it reaches 165F before serving.","s":8},{"t":"Freezing the rinsed rice for an hour or stirring a spoon of oil into it before adding water helps the grains break down faster into a silky porridge.","s":4}],"rl":"PZZXXGBMG"},"53367":{"lv":"EREERRRRREROER","w":{"1":"Seasons the chicken and helps it hold moisture during the quick sear.","4":"Sweet softness in the stir-fry base.","5":"Savory aroma; milder without it.","6":"Warm note; use a smaller amount if dried, as here.","7":"Sweetness and color.","8":"Pops of sweetness and color.","10":"Fresh onion flavor and color at the end.","11":"A pinch of five spice gives warmth; skipping is barely noticeable.","13":"Toasty aroma stirred in at the end."},"tips":[{"t":"If cooking rice fresh, spread it out to cool and refrigerate within an hour, and use it within a day; rice left warm at room temperature can grow Bacillus cereus, which reheating does not kill.","s":0},{"t":"Press the rice flat against the hot wok and leave it 30 seconds before stirring so some grains toast; cold, dry grains separate instead of clumping.","s":13},{"t":"Cut the chicken small so it cooks through quickly, and make sure the pieces reach 165F before removing them from the pan.","s":8}],"rl":"PZFPVXXVVBGXXX"},"80":{"lv":"EEEERRERO","w":{"4":"Provides sweetness and mild heat in the glaze; honey plus chili flakes gives a similar effect.","5":"Fresh warmth in the sauce; skip and the glaze is sweeter and flatter.","7":"Crunch and sweetness inside the cup.","8":"Fresh onion bite on top."},"sub":{"4":{"x":"1 1/2 tbsp honey + 1/2 tsp red pepper flakes + 1 tsp rice vinegar","d":"Less garlicky-sweet than bottled sweet chili; heat is easy to adjust.","k":["honey","red pepper flakes","rice vinegar"],"wn":"Do not give honey to infants under 1 year old."}},"tips":[{"t":"Press the tofu for 20 minutes and pat it dry before the cornstarch; leave the cubes alone until the bottoms release from the pan, then turn.","s":0},{"t":"Toss the tofu with the sauce off the heat at the very end and serve right away; the sauce softens the crust within minutes.","s":1},{"t":"Pick cup-shaped butter lettuce leaves by cutting the core and separating them whole; chill them so the warm filling meets a cold crisp leaf.","s":2}],"rl":"PTFXXXGVG"},"3101":{"lv":"EEEEERERR","w":{"5":"A pinch of warm spice that gives the classic restaurant aroma.","7":"Fresh heat; skip it and it is garlicky but mild.","8":"Sharp onion flavor and color at the finish."},"tips":[{"t":"Dry the shrimp very well before the cornstarch and cut a shallow slit down the back to devein; a shell-on shrimp fried this way is sweeter and the shell eats crisp.","s":0},{"t":"Fry only 1 to 2 minutes at 375F; the shrimp should be pink and just opaque (145F). Overcooked ones curl into a tight rubbery O.","s":1},{"t":"Toast the salt, pepper and five spice in a dry pan for a minute before sprinkling for a deeper, roasted flavor.","s":4}],"rl":"PTFZZXXXG"},"3608":{"lv":"EERRRRROEEERRR","w":{"2":"Color and a sweet crunch.","3":"Sweet crunch and color.","4":"Fresh onion bite in the salad.","5":"Herbal brightness typical of this salad.","6":"Crunchy topping; skip and the salad is softer.","7":"Nutty garnish on top.","11":"Balances the vinegar and soy; skip and the dressing is sharper.","12":"Fresh spice in the dressing.","13":"Mellows the sesame oil and vinegar so the dressing is not harsh."},"tips":[{"t":"Dress the salad only right before serving; napa weeps water in minutes and goes limp.","s":3},{"t":"Whisk or shake the dressing in a jar and taste it on a cabbage leaf, not off the spoon; cabbage mutes flavor, so it should taste slightly too strong.","s":0},{"t":"Poach the chicken until it reaches 165F, cool it, then shred it by hand along the grain for the best texture.","s":1}],"rl":"PVVVXXGGAXFWXF"},"52946":{"lv":"EERRREEERERR","w":{"2":"Sweet, tangy color in the sauce.","3":"Coats the prawns and lightly thickens the sauce.","4":"Balances the chili and soy.","8":"Deeper sweetness that rounds the heat.","10":"Crisp-sweet crunch.","11":"Warm aromatic note."},"tips":[{"t":"Get the pan very hot and fry the prawns in a single layer, about 1 minute per side; they should be opaque and a little golden (145F).","s":1},{"t":"Stir-fry the peanuts with the chilies just until the peanuts color; they darken fast, and the chilies should only turn a shade deeper, not black.","s":2},{"t":"Add the garlic only after the chilies and peanuts have colored; it burns in under 30 seconds and turns bitter.","s":2}],"rl":"PXATWFGXWXVX"},"52953":{"lv":"EERORRERRRRROEOOE","w":{"2":"Half an egg velvets the shrimp; skip and they are a bit less silky.","3":"Tiny pinch of heat in the marinade.","4":"Nutty aroma in the marinade.","5":"Coats the shrimp; also used to thicken the sauce at the end.","7":"Savory aroma in the stir-fry base.","8":"Warm freshness against the shrimp.","9":"Sweet savory base.","10":"Crisp bite and freshness.","11":"Mild onion flavor and color.","12":"Aromatic depth; a splash of chicken broth plus a little vinegar replaces it.","14":"A pinch of sweetness to balance the oyster sauce.","15":"Small acid note that gets lost in the sauce."},"sub":{"12":{"x":"1 tbsp dry white wine","d":"Slightly sharper than cooking wine; the sauce is otherwise unchanged.","k":["white wine"]}},"tips":[{"t":"An overnight soak followed by 10 to 15 minutes of boiling will turn rice noodles to mush. Soak until pliable, then blanch just 30 to 60 seconds and rinse cold.","s":3},{"t":"Sear the shrimp in a single layer for under a minute per side, then remove; they should be pink and opaque (145F) and finish in the sauce.","s":8},{"t":"Handle the wide noodles gently, folding rather than stirring, so they do not tear; add the thickener slowly until the sauce just coats them.","s":11}],"rl":"BPTZXTFXXVVVXXWAX"},"53368":{"lv":"RERERRRRORRRRRREEO","w":{"0":"Nutty aroma in the sauce; soy and vinegar still season the noodles without it.","2":"Seasoned rice vinegar is sweet and tart; plain vinegar plus a pinch of sugar replaces it.","4":"Ribbons of egg add richness and body.","5":"Warm bite; 2 tbsp reads as fresh grated, not dried.","6":"Aromatic base.","7":"Sweet crunch and color.","8":"Fresh heat; the curry powder already adds warmth.","9":"Sweet softness in the vegetable mix.","10":"Seasons the vegetables as they cook.","11":"Smoky salty bits; classic but not essential.","12":"Soft sweet crunch that bulks the noodles.","13":"Fresh onion note.","14":"Sweet color and crunch.","17":"Fresh garnish at the end."},"sub":{"2":{"x":"2 tbsp white vinegar + 1/2 tsp sugar","d":"Sharper; the sugar mimics the seasoned vinegar.","k":["white vinegar","sugar"]}},"tips":[{"t":"If the 2 tbsp of ginger means dried ground, cut it to 2 tsp; it is meant as freshly grated ginger and the dried powder would taste harsh.","s":9},{"t":"Bloom the curry powder in the hot oil with the vegetables for 15 to 20 seconds; raw curry powder tastes dusty, and Singapore noodles are defined by that curry flavor.","s":11},{"t":"Snip the cooled noodles with scissors, and toss them in two batches so the sauce spreads without the pan cooling; they should stay separate, not clump.","s":14}],"rl":"XXABPXXVXVZPVVVXPG"},"53369":{"lv":"EEEOO","w":{"2":"Chinese sesame paste is toasted; tahini plus a little sesame oil comes close.","3":"Fresh onion bite on top.","4":"Nutty crunch garnish."},"sub":{"2":{"x":"2 tbsp tahini + 1/2 tsp sesame oil + 2 tsp warm water","d":"Milder and less toasty than Chinese sesame paste; the oil restores some roast.","k":["tahini","sesame oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Silken tofu is fragile; blot it dry gently after sliding it onto the plate and drain the water that pools, or it dilutes the sauce.","s":1},{"t":"Thin the sesame sauce with a teaspoon of warm water so it drizzles instead of landing in a clump, and pour the soy first so the paste does not slide off.","s":3}],"rl":"PXXGG"},"53370":{"lv":"EERROEEEEERORREEO","w":{"0":"A savory, slightly sweet gravy base; unsalted chicken broth works the same.","2":"Aromatic depth in the gravy; dry sherry replaces it.","3":"Savory body and gloss in the gravy.","4":"Gentle background heat.","10":"Seasons the egg mixture; eggs taste flat without it.","11":"A tiny pinch for roundness.","12":"Fresh onion flavor in the omelet.","13":"Crisp, fresh bulk inside the omelet.","16":"Serving base only; the omelets and gravy stand alone."},"sub":{"0":{"x":"1 cup unsalted chicken broth","d":"Slightly lighter flavor; the gravy is otherwise identical.","k":["chicken broth"]},"2":{"x":"1 tbsp dry sherry","d":"Slightly sweeter and less complex than Shaoxing.","k":["dry sherry"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Dissolve the cornstarch in the water before whisking it into the eggs; this makes the omelet puffy and tender, and whisking until no clear egg white strands remain gives an even color.","s":4},{"t":"Hold the oil at 350F and lower the batter in from just above the surface with a ladle; if the oil is too cool the omelets soak up grease instead of puffing.","s":7},{"t":"Bring the gravy to a simmer before streaming in the starch slurry, or it will not thicken; it should coat the back of a spoon.","s":2}],"rl":"MXXXZTMTMPZWXVPFB"},"53371":{"lv":"EERERORR","w":{"1":"Any neutral oil fries the beans just as well.","2":"Smoky heat; the numbing pepper still carries the flavor if you skip it.","4":"Seasons the beans as they blister.","5":"A touch of sugar to round the chilies and salt.","6":"Nutty aroma added at the end.","7":"A splash of savory salt."},"sub":{"1":{"x":"1 tbsp vegetable oil","d":"Loses a faint peanut flavor; behaves the same.","k":["vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Pat the beans completely dry and cook them until the skins wrinkle and blister; they should be tender with browned spots, not squeaky and green.","s":2},{"t":"Toast the dried chilies and Sichuan peppercorns in the oil on medium-low for 20 to 30 seconds; scorched chilies turn bitter.","s":2},{"t":"Cut the beans into 3-inch lengths and cook in a single layer, in two batches if needed, so they fry instead of steam.","s":2}],"rl":"VFXXZWXX"},"53372":{"lv":"EEERRROROO","w":{"3":"Nutty aroma added to the tomatoes; skip and add a pinch more salt.","4":"Savory depth; soy sauce and a little extra salt replaces it.","5":"Balances the tomato acidity; essential to the home-style taste but small.","6":"Faint background warmth.","7":"Seasoning; adjust with taste.","8":"Fresh onion garnish.","9":"Serving base only."},"sub":{"4":{"x":"1 1/2 tsp soy sauce + 1/4 tsp salt","d":"Darker and saltier; no chicken flavor.","k":["soy sauce","salt"],"wn":"Contains soy and wheat"}},"tips":[{"t":"Start the eggs in a cold pan and pull them while still shiny and a little wet; they finish cooking when tossed with the hot tomatoes.","s":3},{"t":"Cook the tomatoes until the juices thicken into a sauce; underripe tomatoes need extra sugar, and overcooking them turns the sauce to mush.","s":6},{"t":"Serve immediately over rice; the eggs weep and the sauce thins if it sits.","s":8}],"rl":"VFPXXWZZGB"},"53374":{"lv":"EERRREERERRRO","w":{"1":"Any neutral oil handles the high heat.","2":"Adds savory depth; water plus a pinch of salt works.","3":"Balances the chili paste.","4":"Light seasoning in the sauce.","7":"Warm aromatic in the paste mixture.","9":"Gives the sauce a glossy body.","10":"Sharpness that lifts the rich eggplant; rice vinegar works.","11":"Fresh onion bite at the end.","12":"Garnish only."},"sub":{"1":{"x":"2 tbsp vegetable oil","d":"Loses a faint peanut flavor; same heat tolerance.","k":["vegetable oil"]},"2":{"x":"1/4 cup water + 1/4 tsp soy sauce","d":"Less savory depth.","k":["water","soy sauce"]},"10":{"x":"2 tsp rice vinegar","d":"Milder and a little sweeter than cider vinegar.","k":["rice vinegar"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Microwave the cut eggplant for 2 to 3 minutes first to collapse the sponge-like flesh; it then absorbs far less oil in the wok.","s":4},{"t":"Add the vinegar and scallions only for the last 15 seconds so the scallion bite mellows while the vinegar keeps its brightness.","s":17},{"t":"Keep the chili bean paste and aromatics in the oil only about 30 seconds; the paste scorches and turns bitter fast.","s":11}],"rl":"VFMWXXXXXTAXG"},"53375":{"lv":"ROREERRERRRR","w":{"0":"Marinade starch that protects the shrimp from the hot wok.","1":"Aromatic wine for the marinade; dry white wine replaces it.","2":"Seasons the shrimp.","4":"Neutral oil also fine; peanut adds a slight nutty note.","5":"Warm freshness against the shrimp.","6":"Savory aroma.","8":"Seasoning; the broth also adds salt.","9":"Light sauce base; water with a pinch of salt works.","10":"Fresh onion note.","11":"Added off the heat for aroma."},"sub":{"1":{"x":"1 tbsp dry white wine","d":"Slightly less nutty than sherry.","k":["white wine"]},"4":{"x":"2 tbsp vegetable oil","d":"Loses a faint peanut flavor.","k":["vegetable oil"]},"9":{"x":"1/4 cup water + 1/4 tsp soy sauce","d":"Less savory depth.","k":["water","soy sauce"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Heat the wok for a full minute before adding the oil; a cold wok steams the shrimp rather than searing it.","s":4},{"t":"Pull the shrimp when they are just pink and opaque, about 145F; they keep cooking in the residual sauce, and overcooked shrimp turn rubbery.","s":6},{"t":"Strip the strings from the snow peas first; they stay crisp-tender with just 2 minutes in the hot wok.","s":0}],"rl":"TXZPFXXVXMXX"},"53377":{"lv":"EREERRRRRRO","w":{"1":"Soaking liquid softens the shrimp; keep and strain it for extra umami in the slurry.","4":"Sweet fresh onion flavor.","5":"Warm aromatic.","6":"Light savory seasoning.","7":"Dilutes the slurry; use the strained shrimp liquid for more flavor.","8":"Thickens the sauce so it clings.","9":"Seasoning; adjust at the end.","10":"Faint background spice.","3":"The stir-frying fat for the cabbage; without it it sticks and steams"},"sub":{"3":{"x":"2 tbsp vegetable oil","d":"Loses a faint peanut note.","k":["vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Cut the thick white stems and green leaves separately; the stems need 3 to 4 minutes while the leaves wilt in 2, so adding them together overcooks the leaves.","s":5},{"t":"Pat the rehydrated shrimp dry before it hits the oil; wet shrimp splatters violently. Strain and keep the soaking liquid to use in the slurry.","s":10},{"t":"Slice the shrimp small after rehydrating so every bite has a little of the salty umami.","s":7}],"rl":"XMVFXXXMTZZ"},"43":{"lv":"EEREERERROO","w":{"2":"Any neutral oil browns the pork.","5":"Sweet, thick soy; soy sauce plus brown sugar replaces it.","7":"Savory depth.","8":"A little extra sweetness.","9":"Gentle heat; skip if you do not want any.","10":"Fresh finish at the end."},"sub":{"2":{"x":"1 tbsp olive oil","d":"Mild fruity note; fine for browning.","k":["olive oil"]},"5":{"x":"1 1/2 tbsp soy sauce + 2 tsp brown sugar","d":"Slightly less syrupy, similar sweetness.","k":["soy sauce","brown sugar"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Let the pork brown undisturbed for 2 minutes before breaking it up; that browning is where the savory flavor comes from, and cook it to 160F.","s":1},{"t":"Reserve a splash of the noodle water to loosen the sauce so it coats instead of clumping.","s":3}],"rl":"PBFXXXXXWXG"},"44":{"lv":"ERRREROOE","w":{"1":"Sweet, sharp bite in the filling.","2":"Bulk and a little crunch.","3":"Sweetness and color.","5":"Sweetens and glazes; brown sugar replaces it.","6":"Background savory; can skip.","7":"Background savory; can skip."},"sub":{"5":{"x":"1 tbsp brown sugar + 1 tsp water","d":"Less floral, a little more molasses flavor.","k":["brown sugar"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Ground chicken is very lean; add a teaspoon of oil to the pan and cook it to 165F, breaking it into small pieces.","s":0},{"t":"Keep the cabbage slightly crunchy by stopping at 4 minutes; the filling should be hot but still crisp to contrast with the cool lettuce.","s":1}],"rl":"PXVVXWXXG"},"21":{"lv":"ERRRREERRRROE","w":{"1":"Sweet savory base.","2":"Aromatic base.","3":"Warm freshness.","4":"Crunch that echoes the egg roll filling.","7":"Sweet, dark savory note.","8":"Savory umami; extra soy replaces it.","9":"Brightness against the rich chicken.","10":"Nutty aroma stirred in at the end.","11":"Fresh onion garnish."},"tips":[{"t":"Brown the chicken in batches on high heat; piling 2 pounds into the pan steams it and leaves it grey. Cook it to 165F.","s":1},{"t":"Cook the coleslaw mix only 3 to 4 minutes; it should be just wilted with some bite, or the bowl turns soggy.","s":2},{"t":"Add the sesame oil off the heat; its flavor burns away when it gets too hot.","s":3}],"rl":"PXXXVVXXXAXGB"},"16":{"lv":"EEERRRO","w":{"3":"Gives the bowl its egg roll aroma.","4":"Savory warmth; adds a lot with the soy.","5":"Fresh warmth.","6":"Serving base."},"tips":[{"t":"Brown the beef undisturbed, then drain most of the fat before adding the cabbage so the bowl is not greasy; cook it to 160F.","s":0},{"t":"Add the soy sauce a little at a time and taste; coleslaw mix releases water that dilutes it, and the final dish settles after a minute.","s":1}],"rl":"PVXXXXB"},"34":{"lv":"EOEEEEEROER","w":{"1":"Color and mild warmth.","7":"Deepens the sticky glaze; white sugar plus honey replaces it.","8":"Gentle heat that cuts the sweetness.","10":"A convenient side for the bowl."},"sub":{"7":{"x":"1 tbsp white sugar + 1 tsp honey","d":"Slightly lighter caramel flavor.","k":["sugar","honey"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Pat the chicken dry before the cornstarch and cook it undisturbed in the first minute so it crisps; the cornstarch coating also helps the glaze stick.","s":1},{"t":"Lower the heat before adding the garlic and honey; the honey and sugar scorch fast and garlic goes bitter. Simmer 2 to 3 minutes until syrupy.","s":2}],"rl":"PXTFXWXWXBV"},"37":{"lv":"EEEERRROEEO","w":{"4":"Sweetens and darkens the sauce; white sugar plus honey replaces it.","5":"Savory aromatic.","6":"Fresh warmth.","7":"Tiny background warmth.","10":"Serving base."},"sub":{"4":{"x":"3 tbsp white sugar + 1 tsp molasses or honey","d":"Slightly less caramel depth.","k":["sugar","honey"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Do not exceed 1/4 teaspoon baking soda for a pound of beef or 15 to 20 minutes; more makes it soft and soapy-tasting.","s":0},{"t":"Freeze the steak for 20 minutes before slicing it thin across the grain so the slices are even.","s":0},{"t":"Steam the broccoli for exactly 3 minutes and shock it in cold water if it will wait; it keeps its color and snap.","s":2}],"rl":"PXTXWXXZVFB"},"111":{"lv":"EEEEREEEROO","w":{"4":"Sweet crunch and color.","8":"Nutty aroma at the end.","9":"Serving base.","10":"Garnish."},"tips":[{"t":"Press the tofu 20 minutes, cube it, and coat it in cornstarch; leave it alone until the bottoms release.","s":0},{"t":"Add a splash of water and cover for 30 seconds to steam the broccoli; it finishes tender-crisp in the same pan.","s":1}],"rl":"PTFVVXWXXBG"},"3087":{"lv":"EEEEREEERR","w":{"4":"Fresh warmth in the sauce.","8":"Savory sweetness; extra soy covers it.","9":"Fresh onion bite at the end."},"sub":{"7":{"x":"3/4 cup white sugar + 1 tbsp molasses or honey","d":"Slightly lighter flavor.","k":["sugar","honey"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Freeze the flank steak for 20 minutes, then slice 1/4 inch thick against the grain; this keeps the pieces tender.","s":0},{"t":"Sear the beef in batches so it browns; the cornstarch residue left in the pan thickens the sauce.","s":1},{"t":"Simmer until the sauce is glossy but pourable; it thickens on cooling, and the 3/4 cup sugar turns syrupy if overreduced.","s":3}],"rl":"PTFXXXMWXV"},"3088":{"lv":"EEREEERRRRRER","w":{"2":"Velvets the beef.","6":"Savory aromatic.","7":"Fresh warmth.","8":"Savory broth; chicken broth replaces it.","9":"Adds umami.","10":"Rounds the savory flavors.","12":"Extra pepper heat."},"sub":{"8":{"x":"1/2 cup chicken broth","d":"Slightly lighter flavor.","k":["chicken broth"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Cut the peppers into wide strips and stir-fry just 3 minutes so they stay crisp-tender.","s":2},{"t":"Sear the steak for 2 minutes only; it finishes in the sauce, and overcooking makes flank chewy.","s":1},{"t":"Stir in the slurry once the sauce simmers and cook for a minute; stir the cornstarch right before pouring because it settles.","s":5}],"rl":"PXTFVVXXMXWTZ"},"3090":{"lv":"EEEEERRRRRRRO","w":{"5":"Pops of sweetness and color.","6":"Sweet crunch and color.","7":"Sweet savory base.","8":"Aromatic base.","9":"Savory umami; extra soy replaces it.","10":"Nutty aroma at the end.","11":"Fresh onion finish.","12":"Mild heat."},"tips":[{"t":"Dice the pork small, about 1/4 inch, so it is fully cooked and browned in 3 to 4 minutes; it should reach 145F or more.","s":1},{"t":"Add the peas straight from frozen late in the cooking; frozen vegetables cool the wok, so keep the heat high.","s":3},{"t":"Drizzle the soy sauce around the edge of the wok so it sizzles and reduces instantly; poured over the rice it makes the grains soggy.","s":5}],"rl":"BPXFPVVVXXXGZ"},"3091":{"lv":"EERERRRRRERORRO","w":{"2":"Light coating that keeps the chicken tender in the wok.","4":"Sweet crunch and bulk.","5":"Sweet color.","6":"Crisp, fresh bulk.","7":"Sweetness that wilts into the noodles.","8":"Savory aromatic.","10":"Adds umami and gloss.","11":"Tiny sweetness for balance.","12":"Nutty aroma at the end.","13":"Crisp, fresh bulk added at the end.","14":"Mild heat."},"tips":[{"t":"Cook the noodles to al dente, rinse, and toss with a little oil; they finish in the wok, and soft noodles turn to paste.","s":0},{"t":"For crisp edges, press the noodles flat in the oiled wok and leave them undisturbed for 2 minutes before tossing.","s":4},{"t":"Add the bean sprouts only in the last minute so they stay crisp.","s":5}],"rl":"BPTFVVVVXXXWXVZ"},"3092":{"lv":"EEEEEEEEERRRRR","w":{"2":"Any neutral oil browns the pork.","9":"Balances the vinegar, soy, and chili.","10":"Loosens the sesame paste into a sauce; plain noodle water works.","11":"Fresh green bulk.","12":"Fresh onion garnish.","13":"Crunchy topping that adds texture."},"sub":{"2":{"x":"1 tbsp olive oil","d":"Mild fruity note; fine for browning.","k":["olive oil"]},"10":{"x":"1/2 cup noodle cooking water","d":"Starchy water helps emulsify the paste; slightly less savory.","k":["water"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Cook the pork until it is browned and a bit crisp (160F); this crunchy minced pork is a signature of dan dan noodles.","s":0},{"t":"Toast the Sichuan peppercorns in a dry pan and grind them fresh; stale ones lose their numbing quality.","s":1},{"t":"Thin the sesame paste into the broth gradually so it emulsifies into a smooth sauce, and let the noodles sit in it before tossing.","s":3}],"rl":"BPFXAFFXXWMVGG"},"3094":{"lv":"ERERRRRRRERROEEO","w":{"1":"Light coating that keeps the chicken tender.","3":"Sweet crunch and color.","4":"Crisp bulk.","5":"Earthy meaty bulk.","6":"Savory aroma.","7":"Warm freshness.","8":"Light sauce base; water with a splash of soy replaces it.","10":"Savory umami.","11":"Sweet depth.","12":"Small brightness; skip freely.","15":"Fresh garnish."},"sub":{"8":{"x":"1/2 cup water + 1/2 tsp soy sauce","d":"Less savory depth.","k":["water","soy sauce"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Cook the chicken in a single layer until browned and no pink remains; pieces should reach 165F. Crowding steams it.","s":1},{"t":"Stir in the slurry once the sauce simmers; it thickens in seconds, so stop when it coats the spoon.","s":4},{"t":"Add the cashews in the last minute so they stay crunchy; if raw, toast them in a dry pan first.","s":5}],"rl":"PTFVVVXXMXXXATGG"},"3098":{"lv":"EREERERRERRRRE","w":{"1":"Light coating that keeps the beef tender.","4":"Sweet crunch and color.","6":"Savory aroma.","7":"Warm freshness.","9":"Adds savory-sweet depth.","10":"Sharp tang; cider vinegar replaces it.","11":"Rounds the heat.","12":"Thins and flavors the sauce; chicken broth replaces it."},"sub":{"10":{"x":"1 tbsp apple cider vinegar","d":"Slightly fruitier.","k":["apple cider vinegar"]},"12":{"x":"1/2 cup chicken broth","d":"Lighter flavor.","k":["chicken broth"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Fry the whole dried chilies in the oil for 20 to 30 seconds until they darken; snip them open for more heat or remove the seeds for less.","s":3},{"t":"A splash of water and a lid steams the broccoli in the pan so it stays bright green.","s":2},{"t":"Slice the flank against the grain, sear it in a single layer for 2 minutes, and add it back at the end so it stays tender.","s":1}],"rl":"PTFVVXXXXXAWMT"},"3100":{"lv":"EEREERRRRRRRO","w":{"2":"Light coating that keeps the beef tender.","5":"Dark soy gives the chow fun its color and slight molasses sweetness.","6":"Adds umami.","7":"Balances the soy.","8":"Savory aroma.","9":"Crisp bulk.","10":"Fresh onion bite.","11":"Sweet softness.","12":"Mild heat."},"sub":{"5":{"x":"1 tbsp soy sauce + 1/2 tsp brown sugar","d":"Lighter color.","k":["soy sauce","brown sugar"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Separate fresh wide noodles gently (microwave 30 seconds to loosen them) and fold instead of stir so they do not tear.","s":3},{"t":"Get the wok smoking hot and sear the beef in a single layer for the charred, wok-hei flavor; add the sauce around the edge of the wok.","s":2},{"t":"Add the sprouts at the very end; they should stay crisp.","s":5}],"rl":"BPTFXXXWXVVVZ"},"3572":{"lv":"EERRERERRREROO","w":{"2":"Sweet crunch and color.","3":"Crisp, sweet bite.","5":"Sweetness and gloss; brown sugar replaces it.","7":"Light sauce base; water works.","8":"Warm freshness.","9":"Savory aroma.","11":"Nutty aroma in the sauce.","12":"Fresh onion garnish.","13":"Serving base."},"sub":{"5":{"x":"1 tbsp brown sugar","d":"Less floral; slightly darker glaze.","k":["brown sugar"]},"7":{"x":"1/4 cup water","d":"Lighter flavor; the sauce stays glossy.","k":["water"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Sear the shrimp 1 to 2 minutes per side just until pink and curled, about 145F, then remove; they finish in the sauce, and overcooked shrimp turn rubbery.","s":1},{"t":"Stir the sauce again right before pouring it in; the cornstarch settles to the bottom and the sauce thickens unevenly otherwise.","s":4},{"t":"Add the snap peas only for the last minute so they stay snappy.","s":2}],"rl":"PVVVXWTMXXFXGB"},"52947":{"lv":"EEOERRREEEEORRRRE","w":{"2":"Mixed into the beef; a tiny nutty note.","4":"Fermented depth; doubanjiang carries most of the savory.","5":"Reads as chili flakes in the method; adds the visible heat.","6":"Seasons the dish; the soy also adds salt.","10":"Any neutral oil fries the beef and paste.","11":"Garnish of chopped green onion.","12":"The white parts flavor the oil.","13":"Savory aroma.","14":"Warm aromatic.","15":"Dissolves the starch; needed for the slurry."},"sub":{"10":{"x":"2 tbsp vegetable oil","d":"Cleaner flavor than olive oil.","k":["vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Blanch the tofu cubes in salted water for 1 minute; this seasons and firms them so they do not break in the sauce.","s":2},{"t":"Fry the doubanjiang on low heat until the oil turns red; the 1 tbsp of black pepper in the list reads as chili flakes in the method, so use chili flakes (or 1 tsp black pepper) for authentic heat.","s":4},{"t":"Add the starch in two additions and push the tofu gently with the back of the spoon rather than stirring, which breaks it.","s":6}],"rl":"PPXXXXZXXMFGXXXMT"},"52949":{"lv":"EERREREEEO","w":{"2":"Loosens the marinade and the starchy sauce.","3":"Seasons the pork.","5":"Adds savory depth to the marinade.","9":"Garnish only."},"tips":[{"t":"Fry until golden and the pork reaches 145F or more.","s":9},{"t":"Cook the tomato paste in the oil for a minute until it darkens and sweetens before adding sugar; the sauce is mild, so taste and add more sugar or vinegar to balance.","s":10},{"t":"Add the starchy mix slowly while stirring so it thickens evenly; stop as soon as the sauce coats the pork.","s":12}],"rl":"PTMZWXTAAG"},"52951":{"lv":"EEEEEROOERRERERREROR","w":{"5":"Seasons the batter and the chicken.","6":"Background savory; skip it.","7":"Background savory; skip it.","9":"Light sauce base; water plus bouillon replaces it.","10":"Sweet fruity body; apricot jam replaces it.","12":"Adds sweetness and gloss.","14":"Nutty aroma in the sauce.","15":"Fermented heat and sweetness.","17":"Savory aroma.","18":"Fresh garnish.","19":"Warm freshness."},"sub":{"9":{"x":"1/2 cup water + 1/2 tsp chicken bouillon","d":"Lighter flavor.","k":["water","chicken bouillon"]},"10":{"x":"1/4 cup apricot jam + 1 tsp water","d":"Slightly sweeter and less tangy than duck sauce.","k":["apricot jam"]}},"tips":[{"t":"The baking powder in the batter puffs it into a light crisp crust; fry at 350F in batches and cook the chicken to 165F.","s":6},{"t":"Taste the sauce for sweet, tangy, and hot balance before thickening; it should be assertive, since the chicken dilutes it.","s":2},{"t":"Stir the slurry right before adding it; add slowly until the sauce coats the chicken.","s":10}],"rl":"PSTTLZXXMMWXWAXXTXGX"},"53365":{"lv":"REEREEREEEROEEERROOO","w":{"0":"Gives the orange flavor its fragrance; the juice alone makes a duller sauce.","3":"Thins the sauce.","4":"Sharp tang that balances the sugar; cider vinegar replaces it.","6":"Nutty aroma in the sauce.","10":"Seasons the chicken.","11":"Mild heat in the egg dip.","13":"Gives the crust some body; extra cornstarch replaces it.","15":"Savory aroma.","16":"Mild sweet depth.","17":"Garnish.","18":"Garnish.","19":"Serving base."},"sub":{"4":{"x":"1/3 cup apple cider vinegar","d":"Fruitier and slightly milder.","k":["apple cider vinegar"]},"13":{"x":"1/2 cup extra cornstarch (1 cup total)","d":"Crisper, thinner crust.","k":["cornstarch"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Stir some of the orange zest in at the very end; the aromatic oils fade during a long simmer.","s":11},{"t":"Fry the thighs in two batches at 350F; if the crust browns before the inside cooks, lower the heat. The chicken must reach 165F.","s":7},{"t":"Toss the chicken in the sauce and serve at once; the sauce keeps thickening and the crust softens.","s":11}],"rl":"XAXMATXWPTZZTTFXXGGB"},"53366":{"lv":"ROROEEOEREEREE","w":{"0":"Seasons the beef marinade.","2":"Velvets the beef.","3":"Tiny pepper note.","11":"Savory aroma.","1":"Small marinade splash; skipping barely changes the dish.","6":"Small splash in the sauce; skipping barely changes the dish.","8":"Adds savory depth to the sauce; water or other liquid still gives a working dish."},"sub":{"1":{"x":"1 tsp dry white wine","d":"Slightly less nutty than sherry.","k":["white wine"]},"6":{"x":"1 tsp dry white wine","d":"Slightly less nutty than sherry.","k":["white wine"]},"8":{"x":"1/4 cup water + 1/4 tsp soy sauce","d":"Less savory depth.","k":["water","soy sauce"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Heat the pan until a water drop skitters and evaporates, add the beef in a single layer, and leave it for a full minute; this develops the browned crust.","s":8},{"t":"Blanch the broccoli for 2 minutes and rinse it in cold water to keep its bright green color.","s":6},{"t":"Stir the slurry right before pouring it in; it settles quickly.","s":12}],"rl":"XXTZPXXXMVFXTM"},"53376":{"lv":"ERRREREEEEERR","w":{"1":"Velvets the chicken.","2":"Seasons the chicken and the sauce.","3":"Light coating on the chicken.","5":"Sweet fruity body; orange juice replaces it.","8":"Sweetens the sauce.","11":"Extra color and crunch.","12":"Warm freshness."},"sub":{"5":{"x":"1/4 cup orange juice","d":"Less tropical.","k":["orange juice"]},"8":{"x":"3 tbsp white sugar + 1 tsp honey","d":"Lighter caramel flavor.","k":["sugar","honey"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Coat the chicken with the egg white and cornstarch for 15 minutes; this velvet coat keeps it juicy.","s":1},{"t":"The chicken should still be pinkish after the first sear; it finishes in the sauce, so check the thickest piece reaches 165F.","s":7},{"t":"Taste the sauce before serving and adjust with brown sugar or vinegar.","s":14}],"rl":"PTZTVMAAWFVVX"},"3089":{"lv":"EERRROEEO","w":{"2":"Fresh onion bite that cuts the richness.","3":"Savory aroma.","4":"Adds umami and depth.","5":"A salty note that is already in the Worcestershire.","8":"Dipping sauce; the rangoon are good without it."},"tips":[{"t":"Drain and squeeze the crab well; wet filling bursts the wrappers.","s":0},{"t":"Use only about 2 teaspoons of filling per wrapper and seal firmly with water; overfilled rangoon leak in the oil.","s":2},{"t":"Hold the oil at 350F; if it is too cool the wrappers soak it up and turn greasy.","s":3}],"rl":"FPXXXXBFG"},"3096":{"lv":"EERRREROREEEOO","w":{"2":"Fresh onion note in the filling.","3":"Warm freshness.","4":"Savory aroma.","6":"Nutty aroma.","7":"Rounds the savory filling.","8":"Binds the filling juices.","12":"Part of the dipping sauce.","13":"Part of the dipping sauce."},"tips":[{"t":"Salt the cabbage and squeeze out the liquid; otherwise the filling turns watery and the wrappers tear.","s":0},{"t":"Stir the filling in one direction until it turns sticky; this makes it hold together and juicy.","s":1},{"t":"Add the water and cover right away, standing back from the splatter; steam until the pork reaches 160F, then uncover to crisp the bottoms.","s":4}],"rl":"PVXXXXXWTBFMGG"},"3097":{"lv":"EEEEEEOO","w":{"6":"Dipping sauce.","7":"Dipping sauce."},"tips":[{"t":"Hot water gelatinizes some of the flour, making the dough tender; rest it 30 minutes so it rolls out thin without springing back.","s":0},{"t":"Brush the oil on each layer and roll tightly; the oil separates the layers so the pancake flakes.","s":2},{"t":"Cook on medium heat so the layers cook through before the outside burns, and press the pancake with a spatula as it fries.","s":4}],"rl":"SMZFXFGG"},"3361":{"lv":"EEERERRR","w":{"3":"Warm freshness.","5":"Savory sweetness; extra soy replaces it.","6":"Nutty aroma.","7":"Makes steam to cook the stems."},"tips":[{"t":"Rinse the bok choy thoroughly since grit hides at the base; dry the halves so the oil does not splatter.","s":0},{"t":"Sear the cut side down without moving it for 2 minutes to get a golden crust before adding the sauce.","s":3},{"t":"Cook just until the stems are tender-crisp; overcooked bok choy turns slimy.","s":4}],"rl":"VFXXXXXM"},"3364":{"lv":"EEEERRRO","w":{"4":"Sharp note that balances the soy and garlic.","5":"Nutty aroma added off the heat.","6":"Crunch and toasty flavor on top.","7":"Small kick; the dish stays savory without it."},"tips":[{"t":"Resist stirring for the first minutes so the beans blister in spots; constant stirring steams them pale.","s":1},{"t":"Add the garlic only for the last 30 seconds so it stays sweet and does not burn.","s":2},{"t":"Cover for the final 2 minutes of steaming so the beans are tender but keep a snap, and add the sesame oil off the heat.","s":3}],"rl":"VFXXAXGX"},"3471":{"lv":"EEEEREERR","w":{"4":"Savory aroma.","5":"Chinkiang vinegar is malty; rice vinegar plus a little balsamic comes close.","7":"Balances the sharp vinegar.","8":"Seasons the cabbage; the soy adds some salt too."},"sub":{"5":{"x":"2 tbsp rice vinegar + 1 tbsp balsamic vinegar","d":"Less smoky and malty; still hot and sour.","k":["rice vinegar","balsamic vinegar"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Keep the heat at medium while the Sichuan peppercorns and chilies infuse the oil; if they scorch they turn bitter.","s":1},{"t":"Turn the heat to high only for the cabbage and stir-fry 2 to 3 minutes; it should wilt but stay crisp at the edges.","s":3},{"t":"Pour the sauce in last and serve at once; the cabbage keeps releasing water.","s":4}],"rl":"VFXXXAXWZ"},"53373":{"lv":"RERRRREEREEOOO","w":{"0":"Any oil sautes the filling.","2":"Savory aroma.","3":"Warm freshness.","4":"Sweetness.","5":"Fresh onion note.","8":"Brightness in the filling.","11":"Dipping sauce.","12":"Dipping sauce.","13":"Dipping sauce."},"sub":{"0":{"x":"1 tbsp vegetable oil","d":"Cleaner flavor.","k":["vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Cook the pork until no pink remains (160F), then cool the filling before rolling; hot, wet filling steams the wrappers and tears them.","s":2},{"t":"Keep unused wrappers covered with a damp towel, roll tightly, and seal the seam with water so the rolls do not open in the air fryer.","s":6},{"t":"Brush or spray all sides with oil, do not stack, and flip halfway so they brown evenly.","s":9}],"rl":"FPXXVXVXASFGGG"},"53378":{"lv":"EEEEORO","w":{"4":"Basil is unusual; adds a fresh herbal note, skip if you do not have it.","5":"Gentle heat.","6":"Nutty garnish.","3":"Dressing acid; skipped, the cucumber salad is flat and not pickled-bright."},"sub":{"3":{"x":"2 tbsp white vinegar + 1/2 tsp sugar","d":"Sharper; the sugar mimics seasoned rice vinegar.","k":["white vinegar","sugar"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Salt the cucumbers for 15 to 20 minutes and drain the liquid; this keeps the salad crisp instead of watery.","s":1},{"t":"Toss with the dressing just before serving.","s":3}],"rl":"VZXAXXG"},"52954":{"lv":"RRERERORERERERO","w":{"0":"Earthy chew.","1":"Crunchy, springy texture; classic to hot and sour soup.","3":"Adds meaty richness.","5":"Seasons the soup.","6":"Rounds the sour.","7":"Nutty aroma.","9":"Extra heat.","11":"Savory depth.","13":"Makes the starch slurry.","14":"Garnish.","10":"The amount is likely 1 1/2 tablespoons, not cups; add off the heat and adjust to taste."},"tips":[{"t":"The list gives the vinegar as 1 1/2 cups, which would overwhelm 2 cups of broth; treat it as 1 1/2 tablespoons, add it off the heat, and adjust to taste.","s":2},{"t":"Rehydrate dried wood ear in warm water for 20 to 30 minutes and trim any hard knobs.","s":3},{"t":"Stir the slurry in slowly until the soup lightly coats the spoon, then stream in the beaten egg while stirring gently for ribbons.","s":4}],"rl":"VVPPMZWXXXAXTMG"},"52955":{"lv":"ERORRORRROE","w":{"1":"Seasons the broth.","2":"Tiny sweetness.","3":"Warm heat.","4":"Nutty aroma.","5":"Bits of sweetness.","6":"Earthy bulk.","7":"Makes the soup lightly silky so the egg stays suspended.","8":"Dissolves the starch.","9":"Garnish."},"tips":[{"t":"The ingredient list omits the egg; use 2 eggs for 3 cups of broth.","s":4},{"t":"Hold the broth at a gentle simmer, not a rolling boil, and stream the beaten egg in from a height while stirring slowly in one direction for silky ribbons.","s":4},{"t":"Keep the slurry thin; a thick soup makes the egg gluey.","s":3}],"rl":"MZWZXVVTMGP"},"53383":{"lv":"EEEEOORO","w":{"4":"Heat.","5":"Garnish.","6":"Seasons the broth.","7":"Heat."},"tips":[{"t":"Lower the eggs into boiling water and time 6 1/2 minutes for a jammy yolk, or 7 for firmer; use pasteurized eggs if serving anyone vulnerable.","s":0},{"t":"The recipe has no broth; simmer the noodles in the water with the soy sauce and a spoonful of the egg marinade for flavor.","s":2},{"t":"Do not marinate the eggs longer than overnight; they turn salty and rubbery.","s":2}],"rl":"BMPXXGZX"},"53525":{"lv":"EERERRROE","w":{"1":"Provides the heat; jalapenos give a milder version.","2":"Adds creaminess, but it is raw egg; skip it or use a hard-boiled egg for safety.","4":"Seasons the sauce.","5":"Sharp tang.","6":"Raw onion bite.","7":"Mild onion note."},"sub":{"1":{"x":"2 jalapenos","d":"Milder, greener heat; add cayenne for more.","k":["jalapeno"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Wear gloves handling a habanero and remove the seeds and white ribs for less heat.","s":0},{"t":"Raw egg in a sauce that is not cooked carries a salmonella risk; use a hard-boiled egg or omit it, especially for children or older guests.","s":0},{"t":"Press plastic wrap on the surface; the lime juice slows browning, but the sauce is best the same day.","s":2}],"rl":"VXTAZAXXX"},"53528":{"lv":"EERREEERRO","w":{"2":"Savory aroma in the marinade.","3":"Tangy marinade that tenderizes slightly.","7":"Fresh herb note.","8":"Seasons the marinade.","9":"Mild heat.","1":"Main or only cooking fat; skipped, the food sticks, browns poorly and the dish loses richness."},"sub":{"1":{"x":"2 tbsp olive oil","d":"Fine for medium-heat browning.","k":["olive oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Do not marinate longer than overnight; the salt and mustard start to cure the meat.","s":1},{"t":"Cook the chops to 145F with a 3-minute rest; thin chops overcook quickly, so finish them in the sauce.","s":2},{"t":"Cook the tomatoes and onions on high heat until jammy, scraping up the browned bits from the pan.","s":3}],"rl":"PFXXXVVXZZ"},"53530":{"lv":"ERROERREEERO","w":{"1":"Color and mild flavor.","2":"Seasons the chicken.","3":"Mild heat.","5":"Savory aroma.","6":"Sweet base for the sauce.","10":"Rounds the mustard tang.","11":"Garnish.","4":"Main or only cooking fat; skipped, the food sticks, browns poorly and the dish loses richness."},"sub":{"4":{"x":"4 tbsp vegetable oil","d":"Cleaner flavor.","k":["vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Sear the chicken until deeply browned and then cook it to 165F; the legs take about 45 minutes.","s":3},{"t":"Do not boil the cream hard or it can break; finish the sauce gently.","s":3},{"t":"If the sauce is thin, remove the chicken and reduce it uncovered.","s":4}],"rl":"PXZZFXXMXFWG"},"53531":{"lv":"ERRRRRROEERO","w":{"3":"Sweet base.","4":"Sweet color.","5":"Savory aroma.","6":"Seasons the chicken.","7":"Mild heat.","11":"Garnish.","1":"One of several fats or only sweats aromatics; skipping loses some flavor but dish still cooks.","2":"One of several fats or only sweats aromatics; skipping loses some flavor but dish still cooks.","8":"Sauce liquid for the chicken; skipped, the sauce lacks volume and cooking liquid.","10":"Sweetens and caramelizes the orange sauce; skipped, it is sharper."},"sub":{"1":{"x":"2 tbsp vegetable oil","d":"Cleaner flavor.","k":["vegetable oil"]},"2":{"x":"2 tbsp olive oil","d":"Less rich.","k":["olive oil"]},"8":{"x":"1 cup water + 1 tsp chicken bouillon","d":"Lighter flavor.","k":["water","chicken bouillon"]},"10":{"x":"3 tbsp white sugar","d":"Less molasses note.","k":["sugar"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Uncover the pot for the last 10 minutes to reduce the orange juice and sugar into a glaze; the chicken legs must reach 165F.","s":2},{"t":"Pat the chicken dry and brown it well; the browned bits flavor the sauce.","s":1}],"rl":"PFFXXXZZMAWG"},"53533":{"lv":"EEEEREROOE","w":{"1":"Colombian seasoning marinade; a homemade mix replaces it.","4":"Warm spice in the coating.","6":"Seasons the steak.","7":"Mild heat.","8":"Garnish."},"sub":{"1":{"x":"3 minced garlic cloves + 1 tsp cumin + 2 tbsp lime juice + 1 tbsp mustard + 1/2 cup water + 1 tsp salt","d":"A simpler homemade aliños-style marinade.","k":["garlic","cumin","lime juice","mustard","salt"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Pound the steaks to an even 1/4 to 1/2 inch so they cook in minutes and tenderize.","s":0},{"t":"Let the breaded steaks rest 10 minutes in the fridge so the coating sticks.","s":3},{"t":"Fry at about 350F, flip only once, and drain on a rack so the crust stays crisp.","s":4}],"rl":"PXTTXTZZGF"},"53534":{"lv":"ERRRREEORROEOE","w":{"1":"Seasons the potatoes as they cook.","2":"Water to cook the potatoes.","3":"Richness in the sauce base.","4":"Fat for sauteing.","7":"Background savory.","8":"Warm spice.","9":"Seasons the sauce.","10":"Mild heat.","12":"Garnish.","13":"Core of the cheese sauce; skipped, the potatoes lose their cheesy sauce."},"sub":{"13":{"x":"1 cup shredded mild cheddar","d":"Sharper and less stretchy.","k":["cheddar"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Start the potatoes in cold salted water and simmer gently until fork-tender; a hard boil splits the skins.","s":0},{"t":"Cook the tomatoes and green onions down until jammy before adding the cream.","s":1},{"t":"Melt the cheese over low heat; boiling the cream with cheese can make it grainy.","s":2}],"rl":"BMMFFVXXXZZFGF"},"53527":{"lv":"EE","tips":[{"t":"Heat gently; the seeds should sizzle faintly. Remove from the heat as soon as the oil turns orange-red, since scorched seeds make the oil bitter.","s":0},{"t":"Strain through a fine sieve and refrigerate the oil for longer storage; discard it if it turns cloudy or smells off.","s":2}],"rl":"FC"},"53523":{"lv":"EEEEEEEROE","w":{"0":"Frying medium; any neutral oil works, but you need enough for the balls to float freely.","7":"Adds light sweetness and a bit of browning against the salty cheeses; skipping gives a plainer, paler bunuelo.","8":"Feta and farmer cheese already carry plenty of salt, so a pinch barely registers.","9":"Loosens the dough to a soft, smooth consistency; water works if milk is not on hand."},"sub":{"0":{"x":"Equal amount of canola or sunflower oil","d":"Any neutral high-heat oil behaves the same at 300-320F.","k":["canola oil","sunflower oil"]},"9":{"x":"5 tbsp water","d":"Slightly less rich and tender; dough may need a teaspoon more water.","k":["water"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Keep the oil at 300-320F (150-160C), cooler than most frying. The dough has to puff and cook through before the outside colors; if they brown in under 2 minutes or crack open, the oil is too hot.","s":2},{"t":"The dough should feel like soft play-dough. If it cracks when you roll it, knead in a teaspoon of milk; if it sticks to your palms, work in a spoonful of cornstarch.","s":0}],"rl":"FSSBBTLWZM"},"53526":{"lv":"EERRRRRRRRROEEEOOE","w":{"2":"Seasons the potatoes from the inside while they boil; unsalted mash tastes flat even with a savory filling.","4":"Gives the hogao-style filling juiciness and acidity; without it the beef filling is drier and plainer.","5":"Builds the sweet aromatic base of the filling; skipping leaves it flatter.","6":"Adds a fresh, mild onion note to the filling; extra yellow onion can replace it.","7":"Classic savory backbone of the filling; omit and the beef tastes bland.","8":"Earthy warmth that makes the filling taste Latin rather than plain ground beef.","9":"Gives the filling its golden-orange color and savory seasoning; mild flavor if skipped.","10":"Seasons the beef filling; the potato and batter are bland without enough salt in the filling.","11":"Light heat in the filling; minimal impact.","15":"Tiny salt boost for the batter; the sazon already seasons it.","16":"Colors and seasons the batter golden; skipping gives a paler crust.","0":"Deep frying medium; needs enough depth to submerge the potato patties.","3":"Just sautes the filling; any neutral oil or butter works.","17":"Thins the coating batter to a pourable cling."},"sub":{"0":{"x":"Equal amount of canola or sunflower oil","d":"Same results at 360F.","k":["canola oil","sunflower oil"]},"3":{"x":"1 tbsp vegetable oil","d":"Slightly less fruity; fine for sauteing the filling.","k":["vegetable oil"]},"17":{"x":"1/4 cup water","d":"Batter is a little thinner and crisper, slightly less browned.","k":["water"]}},"tips":[{"t":"After draining, return the potatoes to the hot dry pot for a minute to steam off moisture before mashing. Wet mash makes patties that split or burst in the fryer.","s":2},{"t":"Cook the beef filling until almost dry and let it cool completely before stuffing; a juicy or warm filling weeps through the seam. Cook the beef to 160F (71C).","s":4},{"t":"Pinch the potato seam fully closed, chill the stuffed balls 15 minutes, and fry only 2-3 at a time so the oil stays near 360F and the coating crisps rather than soaks up oil.","s":10}],"rl":"FBZFVXXXXXZZPTTZXM"},"53529":{"lv":"EEEREOEE","w":{"3":"Adds richness and tenderness to the dough; without it the almojabanas are a bit drier and less flavorful.","5":"The two cheeses are already salty, so this small amount adds little.","7":"Hydrates the dough to a soft ball; water works in a pinch.","0":"Use precooked white corn flour (masarepa); ordinary gritty cornmeal does not hydrate and makes sandy rolls."},"sub":{"7":{"x":"4 tbsp water","d":"Slightly less rich; use only enough to keep the dough from cracking.","k":["water"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Use precooked white corn flour (masarepa, such as Harina PAN), the ingredient the steps call for. Ordinary gritty cornmeal does not hydrate and yields sandy, crumbly rolls.","s":1},{"t":"Pulse the cheeses fine with the corn flour before adding the eggs; big cheese bits melt out as holes and leaks on the tray. Space the balls apart because they spread and puff.","s":2},{"t":"Eat them warm: the cheese center is soft and stretchy at that point, then firms up as it cools. Reheat in a 350F oven for a few minutes, not the microwave.","s":4}],"rl":"BBBFLZSM"},"53532":{"lv":"EEERRORERR","w":{"2":"Seasoned stock base for the soup; sweet corn alone tastes thin without it.","3":"Mellow savory depth under the sweet corn; skip and the soup is flatter.","4":"Brings out the corn sweetness; the soup tastes dull without proper salt.","5":"Only a light background heat.","6":"Fresh herbal lift that is part of the classic corn soup flavor; skipping makes it plainer.","8":"Mild oniony sweetness that rounds the base; regular onion can replace it.","9":"Gives a silky, rich finish."},"sub":{"2":{"x":"2 vegetable bouillon cubes or 2 cups vegetable broth in place of 2 cups water","d":"Vegetable base is a bit sweeter and less meaty; soup stays vegetarian.","k":["vegetable bouillon","vegetable broth"]},"9":{"x":"1 cup half-and-half","d":"Lighter and less rich; heat gently and do not boil hard after adding.","k":["half and half"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Strip the kernels and simmer the bare cobs in the pot while you cook; they give up a lot of sweet corn flavor. Remove the cobs before blending.","s":0},{"t":"Blend in batches with the lid vented to avoid hot splashes, then strain through a sieve for a velvety soup, since corn skins stay gritty even after blending.","s":1},{"t":"Add the cream after blending and keep the soup at a bare simmer; hard boiling can make the potato-thickened soup scorch on the bottom.","s":1}],"rl":"VMMXZZXBXF"},"53522":{"lv":"EREERRREE","w":{"1":"Defines the cinnamon flavor; ground cinnamon works but makes the pudding speckled.","4":"Balances the sweetness of the sugar and condensed milk.","5":"Gives the pudding a silkier mouthfeel and rounded richness; skipping makes it less creamy.","6":"Warm aromatic note that rounds out the cinnamon; plain pudding tastes flatter without it."},"sub":{"1":{"x":"2 tsp ground cinnamon, stirred into the water","d":"Stronger and cloudier; pudding will have brown flecks instead of clear infusion.","k":["cinnamon"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Infuse the cinnamon in the water first and cook the rice in that liquid before adding any milk; rice swells in water while milk and sugar can scorch and keep rice firm.","s":1},{"t":"Stir often and keep the heat medium-low once the condensed milk goes in; it catches on the bottom of the pot quickly during the long cook.","s":3},{"t":"The pudding thickens a lot as it chills. If it turns stiff, loosen with a splash of warm milk before serving.","s":4}],"rl":"BXMMZFXWW"},"53524":{"lv":"EEEERRERRORO","w":{"4":"Adds coconut texture and sweetness; skipping gives a smoother but plainer natilla.","5":"Infuses warm cinnamon flavor into the milk; ground cinnamon can stand in.","7":"Rounds out sweetness and aroma; the dessert is fine without it.","8":"Extra sweetness beyond the condensed milk; reduce to taste.","9":"A pinch sharpens the sweetness; barely noticeable.","10":"Smooths the finish and gives gloss and richness.","11":"Dusted on top as a garnish only."},"tips":[{"t":"Dissolve the cornstarch in cold milk before it meets heat; hot milk makes it clump instantly. If lumps still form, pass the natilla through a sieve or blend it quickly.","s":0},{"t":"Stir constantly and scrape the corners of the pot; cornstarch needs to come fully to a bubble to reach its full thickness and lose its raw taste. It is ready when a spoon drawn across the bottom leaves a trail that closes slowly.","s":4},{"t":"Press plastic wrap directly onto the surface or pour into a mold while hot to prevent a skin; it sets firm after about 2 hours in the fridge.","s":4}],"rl":"MTTMXXWXWZFG"},"53540":{"lv":"ERRRORREERE","w":{"1":"Herbal, earthy note typical of Latin chicken stews; skipping makes the salsa plainer.","2":"Warm earthy spice that deepens the sauce.","3":"Seasons the chicken and sauce; add more to taste after cooking since pressure cooking mutes salt.","4":"Slight heat only.","5":"Gives the dish its orange color and mild earthy flavor; skipping loses color.","6":"Core savory aromatic of the sauce; skipping makes it flat.","9":"Adds sweetness and a green note to the salsa.","10":"Liquid needed to build pressure and cook the chicken; skipped, the cooker will not seal."},"sub":{"10":{"x":"2 cups water + 1 tsp bouillon (or 1/2 tsp extra salt)","d":"Thinner flavor; the pressure cooker needs at least this much liquid to seal.","k":["water","bouillon"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Sear the thighs in the hot pot before the pressure cooking and dry them first; the browned bits dissolve into the sauce and give flavor a pressure cooker cannot build alone.","s":1},{"t":"Dissolve the achiote paste in a ladle of hot broth before adding; it spreads evenly and avoids orange lumps.","s":2},{"t":"Chicken must reach 165F (74C); thighs are best at 175F+ for tenderness. Pressure cooking does not reduce sauce, so simmer on sauté for 5 minutes to thicken before serving over rice.","s":2}],"rl":"PXXZZXXXVVM"},"53536":{"lv":"EERRRRRRROE","w":{"2":"Adds sweet vegetable depth to the broth.","3":"Second pepper adds more sweetness and body; either alone works.","4":"Mild savory background that rounds out the pot liquor.","5":"Classic aromatic depth in the beans.","6":"Earthy warmth typical of Latin black beans.","7":"Herbal note that completes the seasoning.","8":"Seasons the beans; salting before pressure cooking does not toughen black beans.","9":"Light heat only; the beans carry the dish."},"tips":[{"t":"Dry beans cook unsoaked at 35 minutes on high; the natural release matters because a quick release can split skins and make the beans blow out and foam.","s":1},{"t":"Do not fill the cooker past half with beans and water since beans foam; a spoonful of oil helps tame it.","s":0},{"t":"For a creamier pot, mash a ladleful of beans against the pot wall and stir it back in so the liquid thickens without cream.","s":1}],"rl":"PXVVXXXXZZM"},"53541":{"lv":"EERRRRRRO","w":{"2":"Sweetens and flavors the base of the rice and bean mix.","3":"Steps say peppers; this is most likely diced sweet pepper. If it is real black pepper, use only 1/4 tsp.","4":"Fresh herbal lift that is part of the classic gallo pinto flavor.","5":"Signature Costa Rican tangy-sweet sauce; subs below give a close match.","6":"Seasons the rice and beans.","7":"Earthy depth.","8":"Light background heat only."},"sub":{"5":{"x":"1/2 tsp Worcestershire sauce + a tiny pinch of sugar","d":"Slightly sharper and saltier than Lizano; still gives the right tang.","k":["worcestershire sauce","sugar"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Use day-old cold rice; freshly cooked rice steams and turns mushy when fried with beans. If you must use fresh rice, spread it on a tray to dry for 20 minutes first.","s":2},{"t":"Add a splash of the bean cooking liquid to color the rice and make it moist. Stir gently, so the grains do not break.","s":1},{"t":"Cool leftovers within 1 hour and refrigerate; reheat thoroughly to 165F since cooked rice can grow bacteria.","s":3}],"rl":"PBXVXXZXZ"},"53539":{"lv":"EERRRRROE","w":{"2":"Sweet aromatic base for the picadillo.","3":"The listed amount points to diced sweet pepper per the steps; it adds sweetness and body. Real black pepper should be only 1/2 tsp.","4":"Savory depth that carries through the beans and beef.","5":"Herbal note that is the main seasoning besides salt.","6":"Seasons the beans and beef; needs it since there is little other flavor.","7":"Only a light background kick."},"tips":[{"t":"This assumes cooked, shredded flank steak. If yours is raw, simmer it first in salted water for about 1.5 hours until it shreds.","s":2},{"t":"Chop the green beans finely; small pieces cook in the 15 minutes and mix evenly into the shredded meat.","s":1},{"t":"Cover to steam the beans, then uncover for the final 5 minutes so the liquid evaporates and the picadillo is moist but not soupy.","s":2}],"rl":"VPXVXXZZM"},"53538":{"lv":"RREERRERO","w":{"0":"Sharp bite that cuts through the sweet mango; a milder green onion can replace it.","1":"Crunch and fresh sweetness; skipping lowers texture contrast.","4":"Adds creamy richness that balances the acid; salsa works without it, with a sharper taste.","5":"Fresh herbal lift; the salsa tastes sweeter and flatter without it.","7":"Seasons the salsa; salt also draws juice from the fruit, so add it just before serving.","8":"Slight background heat only."},"tips":[{"t":"Salt pulls water from the tomatoes and mango, which is why the salsa gets soupy. Seed the tomatoes and add the salt, and the avocado, just before serving.","s":0},{"t":"Use firm-ripe mango, not soft; it holds its shape in chunks. The lime juice also keeps the avocado from browning.","s":0},{"t":"Drain excess liquid with a slotted spoon before putting the salsa on chips, so they stay crisp.","s":2}],"rl":"XVVVVXAZZ"},"53535":{"lv":"EREREE","w":{"1":"Adds richness and sheen to the custard; omit and the tamal is a little less silky.","3":"Aroma and warmth; plain milk custard tastes flatter without it."},"tips":[{"t":"Whisk the eggs and cornstarch smooth first, then add the hot milk a ladle at a time; this tempers the eggs so they do not scramble.","s":2},{"t":"Cook the thickened mixture until a spoon drawn across the bottom leaves a clear trail; cornstarch needs to boil to reach full thickness and lose raw flavor.","s":4},{"t":"Let it cool completely, ideally chilled, before cutting; the slices hold clean edges only after the starch sets.","s":6}],"rl":"MFWXSS"},"53537":{"lv":"EEEEREEER","w":{"4":"Aroma; the bread is still sweet and tender without it.","8":"Sharpens the sweetness; barely noticeable in this batter."},"tips":[{"t":"Shred the yuca finely and discard any woody core; coarse strands stay chewy after baking. If using frozen, thaw and squeeze out the water so the batter is not wet.","s":2},{"t":"Fold the yuca in with a spoon rather than mixing hard, and check doneness with a toothpick in the center; a damp middle means more time.","s":5},{"t":"Cool at least 30 minutes before cutting; the starch from the yuca sets as it cools and gives the dense, chewy slice.","s":6}],"rl":"BFWSXFSLZ"},"53542":{"lv":"EEREERROO","w":{"2":"Sweetens the pancake and helps it brown; less sugar makes them taste more of corn.","3":"Loosens the batter; water works but gives a plainer flavor.","5":"Adds tenderness and flavor; the pancakes are a bit drier without it.","6":"Warm aroma; mostly background.","7":"Light spice note; skipping barely changes it.","8":"Pinch for balance only."},"sub":{"3":{"x":"1/3 cup water","d":"Slightly less rich and tender.","k":["water"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Pulse only until the mixture looks coarse and crumbly; over-blending turns it into a smooth batter and you lose the rustic corn texture.","s":0},{"t":"Use a non-stick pan at medium heat and wait for bubbles and a dry edge before flipping; corn batter browns fast and the center needs the time to set.","s":1},{"t":"Serve with sour cream or fresh white cheese; the cool tang balances the sweetness of the corn.","s":2}],"rl":"VSWMTFXXZ"},"53055":{"lv":"EERRORERRRER","w":{"2":"Adds moisture and sweet savory flavor; skip it and the cevapi are dry and flat.","3":"Classic savory note; less rounded flavor without it.","4":"Mild fresh note; minor impact on the final taste.","5":"Gives color and mild sweetness; skip and they taste plainer.","7":"Absorbs juices and softens the texture; skipping makes them denser.","8":"Needed to loosen and mix the meat to a sticky paste.","9":"Adds savory depth; use extra salt if skipped.","11":"Mild heat, supportive."},"tips":[{"t":"Mix the meat until it turns sticky and starts to pull from the bowl; this bind is what holds the rolls together on the grill. Let the mix rest 1-2 hours so the salt and baking powder work.","s":1},{"t":"Wet your hands to shape the rolls and keep them thumb-thick; thin rolls dry out before the center is done.","s":2},{"t":"Cook to an internal 160F (71C). Brown on a hot, oiled grill pan and turn often, which gives an even crust without drying them.","s":3}],"rl":"PPXXGXLTMXZZ"},"53056":{"lv":"ERRRREERRERRO","w":{"1":"Soaks up the lamb juices; skip and the vegetables are less varied.","2":"Adds sweetness to the juices.","3":"Sweet vegetable note and color.","4":"Soaks up the sauce and adds creamy texture.","7":"Garlic flavor in the marinade; see substitute.","8":"Adds sweetness, color, and umami to the sauce.","10":"Herbal note; skip and use extra rosemary or sage.","12":"Light seasoning; salt is still needed.","11":"Adds depth and acidity; skipped, the sauce is flatter but the dish still works."},"sub":{"7":{"x":"3 cloves garlic, finely minced (about 1 tbsp)","d":"Sharper, fresher garlic bite than the mellow puree.","k":["garlic"]},"11":{"x":"1 cup chicken or vegetable broth + 1 tbsp white wine vinegar or lemon juice","d":"Less depth and acidity; vinegar keeps the sauce balanced.","k":["chicken broth","vinegar"]}},"tips":[{"t":"The ingredient list has no salt but the marinade needs it; add about 1 1/2 tsp for this much meat and vegetables.","s":4},{"t":"Cut the potatoes into large chunks; small ones turn to mush over the 2-hour cook. Keep the herbs on top so they perfume the dish and are easy to remove.","s":6},{"t":"The final uncovered 20-30 minutes browns the top and reduces the juices; skim excess fat if the shoulder is fatty.","s":17}],"rl":"BVVVVVPXXFXMZ"},"53057":{"lv":"EERRRRERRORE","w":{"2":"Grated carrot melts into the sauce for body and sweetness.","3":"Savory depth; mellows in the long simmer.","4":"Herbal background; minimal if skipped.","7":"Marinade for tenderness and tang.","8":"Seasons the meat and sauce; needs to be salted properly.","10":"Mild sweetness and color; more could be added for a deeper red.","9":"Slight bite only.","5":"Adds depth and acidity; skipped, the sauce is flatter but the dish still works.","11":"Main or only cooking fat; skipped, the food sticks, browns poorly and the dish loses richness."},"sub":{"5":{"x":"3/4 cup beef broth + 1 tbsp red wine vinegar","d":"Less depth; vinegar mimics the acidity, so stir it in at the end.","k":["beef broth","vinegar"]},"11":{"x":"2 tbsp lard or any neutral oil","d":"Lard gives a more traditional flavor; other oil is neutral.","k":["lard","canola oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Brown the beef in batches in a hot pot; crowding steams the meat and you lose the browned base that gives the stew its flavor.","s":1},{"t":"Cook the finely cut onions and grated carrots low for the full 15 minutes until soft and golden; this builds body and the thick sauce without flour.","s":2},{"t":"Use a cut like chuck or shin, which turns tender in a long simmer; lean cuts stay tough. Add the water in stages as it reduces.","s":4}],"rl":"PXVXXMMXZZXF"},"53060":{"lv":"EEEERO","w":{"3":"Used to cook the filling and brush the pastry; see substitute.","4":"Seasons the filling; filo itself has none.","5":"Slight bite only."},"sub":{"3":{"x":"3 tbsp melted butter","d":"Richer and browns more; brush filo lightly.","k":["butter"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Cook the beef and onion until the pan is dry and cool the filling fully; wet or warm filling turns the filo gummy and tears it.","s":0},{"t":"Keep unused filo under a damp towel and brush every layer lightly with oil; dry sheets crack and the layers do not separate into crisp flakes.","s":1},{"t":"Score the top into portions before baking, since the baked pastry shatters if you cut it later. Ground beef must be cooked to 160F (71C).","s":2}],"rl":"SPVFZZ"},"53061":{"lv":"ERER","w":{"1":"Frying medium; use enough to submerge the fish.","3":"Seasoning for the crust; add right after frying."},"sub":{"1":{"x":"Equal amount of sunflower or canola oil","d":"Any neutral frying oil works the same at 350F.","k":["sunflower oil","canola oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Dry the sardines thoroughly and flour them just before frying; wet fish makes the coating gummy and splatters in the oil.","s":0},{"t":"Fry at about 350F (180C) in small batches. Season with salt as they come out of the oil, since salting earlier draws out moisture.","s":0},{"t":"Squeeze lemon over them hot; the acid cuts the oily fish and brightens the flavor.","s":1}],"rl":"PFTZ"},"53058":{"lv":"EEERROE","w":{"3":"Sweet, mild onion base; use a regular small onion if you cannot find them.","4":"Savory aroma in the sauce.","5":"Fresh color and flavor, tiny amount.","1":"Main or only cooking fat; skipped, the food sticks, browns poorly and the dish loses richness."},"sub":{"1":{"x":"3 tbsp olive oil","d":"Fruitier flavor; fine in a stew.","k":["olive oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Render the chorizo first and cook the shallots in its paprika-red fat; this seasons the whole stew and saves oil.","s":0},{"t":"Rinse the canned beans and mash a few against the pot to thicken the stew. Taste before salting, since chorizo is already salty.","s":1},{"t":"The stew tastes better the next day as the beans soak up the sauce; reheat gently with a splash of water.","s":1}],"rl":"PFVXXGP"},"53059":{"lv":"EEERRRRRRO","w":{"2":"Used to saute the aromatics; any neutral oil works.","3":"Sweet base of the soup; skip and it tastes flatter.","4":"Savory depth.","5":"Quiet herbal background.","6":"Stock flavor for the broth; see substitute.","7":"Adds creamy tang and body; omit for a clearer broth.","8":"Brightens the earthy mushroom flavor, add a few drops at a time.","9":"Garnish only."},"sub":{"2":{"x":"4 tbsp sunflower or olive oil","d":"Same function.","k":["sunflower oil","olive oil"]},"6":{"x":"1 tsp salt + 1 tsp soy sauce, in the water","d":"Less rounded than stock but adds salt and umami.","k":["salt","soy sauce"],"wn":"Contains soy (and usually wheat)"}},"tips":[{"t":"Let the mushrooms brown properly in the oil before adding water; browned mushrooms taste meaty, and steamed ones taste watery.","s":1},{"t":"Mix the sour cream with a ladle of hot broth before stirring it in and keep the soup below a boil, so the cream does not curdle.","s":2},{"t":"Wash buckwheat briefly and do not overcook; it turns mushy past the point of just tender.","s":0}],"rl":"VBFXXXXFAG"},"53062":{"lv":"EEEEEEEEERROO","w":{"9":"Sweetens the filling and adds molasses depth; white sugar plus honey also works.","10":"Warm spice that complements the walnuts.","11":"Brushed on the hot loaf for shine.","12":"Finishing dusting; looks and tastes sweeter."},"sub":{"9":{"x":"85 g white sugar (about 6 tbsp) + 1 tbsp honey or molasses","d":"Less molasses flavor; similar sweetness.","k":["sugar","honey"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Knead until the dough is smooth and passes the windowpane test before the first rise; this enriched dough needs gluten to hold the thick filling.","s":0},{"t":"Roll the dough thin and tight, seal the seam well, and bake seam-side down so the filling stays inside.","s":1},{"t":"Warm the milk only to lukewarm; hot milk kills the yeast. Brush with milk and dust sugar while the bread is still hot so they stick.","s":2}],"rl":"SWLZMSFBFWXGG"},"3072":{"lv":"EEEEEEREER","w":{"6":"Light background heat; the cumin, garlic and citrus carry the mojo.","8":"Carries the garlic and spice flavor and keeps the meat moist; any neutral oil works.","9":"Makes a bed that flavors the pan juices and keeps the pork off the pan; skipping loses some sweetness in the juices."},"sub":{"8":{"x":"1/4 cup vegetable or canola oil","d":"Less fruity; mojo still works.","k":["vegetable oil","canola oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Poke the shoulder deeply in many places and marinate 12-24 hours; more than about 24 hours in this acidic juice turns the surface mushy.","s":2},{"t":"Pull the pork only at 195F (90C) in the thickest part; pork shoulder needs far more than the 145F food-safe minimum to melt its collagen so it shreds.","s":4},{"t":"Rest it 20 minutes and shred it into the pan juices; for crisp edges, finish the shredded meat for a few minutes under the broiler.","s":5}],"rl":"PXAAXXZZFX"},"3076":{"lv":"ERERRRRRREEEOR","w":{"1":"Seasons the chicken and rice; the dish is bland without enough salt.","2":"Browns the chicken and starts the sofrito; see substitute.","3":"Sweet base of the sofrito.","4":"Adds sweetness and a green note.","5":"Savory depth in the rice.","6":"Warm earthy flavor typical of Cuban rice dishes.","7":"Color and mild sweetness.","8":"Gives the rice its golden color; tiny flavor impact.","11":"Cooks the rice; see substitute for liquid.","12":"Adds color and sweetness; the dish works without them.","13":"Briny, salty accent; skipping makes it plainer."},"sub":{"2":{"x":"2 tbsp vegetable oil","d":"Less fruity but works for browning.","k":["vegetable oil"]},"11":{"x":"3 cups water + 1 chicken bouillon cube","d":"Less rich; add salt after tasting.","k":["water","bouillon"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Brown the chicken in the Dutch oven first and leave the drippings behind to cook the vegetables; the fond seasons the rice.","s":0},{"t":"Toast the rice in the sofrito for 2 minutes so each grain is coated; this helps keep it separate rather than sticky.","s":2},{"t":"Do not lift the lid during the 25 minutes. Chicken must reach 165F (74C) at the bone; thighs taste best near 175F. Let the rice rest 5 minutes after the peas.","s":4}],"rl":"PZFXXXXXCABMVX"},"3077":{"lv":"EEEREEEERRO","w":{"3":"Subtle herbal note in the poaching liquid; a minor effect.","8":"Earthy warmth in the marinade.","9":"Herbal note; works with the lime and garlic.","10":"Light pepper only.","6":"Main or only cooking fat; skipped, the food sticks, browns poorly and the dish loses richness."},"sub":{"6":{"x":"2 tbsp vegetable oil","d":"Neutral but fine for crisping the beef.","k":["vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Simmer the steak gently, not at a hard boil, until it shreds easily; then reserve the broth for rice or soup.","s":0},{"t":"Pat the shredded beef dry after marinating; it fries rather than steams if it is not wet.","s":3},{"t":"Sear in batches in a hot pan and leave it untouched for 4-5 minutes to build crisp edges; stirring early gives you soft, not crisp, beef.","s":3}],"rl":"PXXXMAFZXXZ"},"53545":{"lv":"ERERREERRRRRERROOO","w":{"1":"Seasons the beef and sauce in layers; add the rest to taste.","2":"Browns the steak and softens the vegetables; see substitute.","3":"Sweet base of the sauce.","4":"Second pepper adds more color and sweetness; one pepper works too.","7":"Adds concentrated tomato richness and color.","8":"Smoky sweetness and color.","9":"Earthy warmth typical of the dish.","10":"Herbal depth.","11":"Adds acidity and body; see substitute.","13":"Quiet herbal background.","14":"Salty, briny accent that is traditional; skipping makes it plainer.","15":"Serving side only.","16":"Serving side only.","17":"Fresh garnish."},"sub":{"2":{"x":"4 tbsp olive oil","d":"Fruitier flavor; works for searing at medium heat.","k":["olive oil"]},"11":{"x":"1/2 cup chicken broth + 1 tbsp white wine vinegar","d":"Less depth; vinegar supplies the acidity.","k":["chicken broth","vinegar"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Pat the steak very dry and sear it in batches over medium-high heat; a deep brown crust gives the stew its flavor.","s":1},{"t":"Braise the steak gently until it shreds easily with a fork, often about 2.5-3 hours for flank; high heat makes the lean fibers tough.","s":3},{"t":"Shred along the grain with two forks, then cook the shredded beef in the sauce until it coats the meat; it should be saucy but not soupy.","s":4}],"rl":"PZFVVVXXXXXAAXXGGG"},"53547":{"lv":"ERRRRRORRREREEE","w":{"1":"Seasons the pork; the roast is bland without it.","2":"Coats the pork so the marinade clings; see substitute.","3":"Citrus juice and zest for the mojo, tenderizing and flavoring the pork.","4":"Adds the sharp acid that defines the marinade.","5":"Herbal note in the marinade.","6":"Light spice only.","7":"Savory depth in the marinade.","8":"Tangy spread that cuts through the rich pork and cheese.","9":"Makes the mustard spread creamier and milder.","11":"Butters the outside for browning in the press; see substitute."},"sub":{"2":{"x":"2 tbsp olive oil","d":"Same function; slightly fruitier.","k":["olive oil"]},"11":{"x":"1 tbsp mayonnaise spread thinly on the outside","d":"Browns well; slightly tangy and not as buttery.","k":["mayonnaise"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Roast the pork to 145F (63C) in the thickest part, then rest 3 minutes; slice it thin across the grain so each bite is tender.","s":3},{"t":"Layer cheese on both sides of the ham and pork so it glues the sandwich as it melts; put the pickles under the cheese so they stay put.","s":4},{"t":"Press the sandwich in a cast-iron pan with a heavy pan on top at medium heat; high heat toasts the bread before the cheese melts.","s":5}],"rl":"PZFAAXZXXFBFFPA"},"3071":{"lv":"EERREERRRRRORO","w":{"2":"Sweet vegetable background.","3":"Savory aroma that cooks into the sauce.","6":"Earthy warmth typical of picadillo.","7":"Herbal depth.","8":"Briny, salty bite that is part of the classic flavor.","9":"Sweet counterpoint to the olives; skipping makes it more savory.","11":"Quiet background aroma.","12":"Season at the end; olives and tomato sauce are salty.","13":"Serving side only.","0":"For sauteing the vegetables; any neutral oil or olive oil works.","10":"Adds acidity; see substitute."},"sub":{"0":{"x":"2 tbsp vegetable oil","d":"Neutral flavor; fine.","k":["vegetable oil"]},"10":{"x":"2 tbsp chicken broth + 1/2 tsp white vinegar","d":"Less depth; adds a little acid.","k":["chicken broth","vinegar"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Break the beef finely and brown it undisturbed first; cook to 160F (71C), then drain excess fat before adding the tomato sauce.","s":1},{"t":"Add olives and raisins near the end so they stay plump and the olives keep their bite.","s":4},{"t":"Simmer until thick, not soupy; the picadillo should be moist enough to spoon over rice without pooling.","s":3}],"rl":"FXVXPAXXXWAXZB"},"3073":{"lv":"ERRRRREEEORE","w":{"0":"For sauteing; any neutral oil works.","1":"Sweet base of the sofrito.","2":"Adds green sweetness.","3":"Savory aroma.","4":"Earthy warmth.","5":"Herbal depth.","8":"Cooks the rice; see substitute.","9":"Quiet background aroma.","10":"Brightens the beans and balances richness."},"sub":{"0":{"x":"2 tbsp vegetable oil","d":"Neutral; fine.","k":["vegetable oil"]},"8":{"x":"1 cup water + 1/2 tsp extra salt + a pinch of bouillon","d":"Less rich; taste the salt before serving.","k":["water","bouillon"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Use the bean liquid as the cooking liquid; it dyes the rice dark and gives the dish its flavor.","s":1},{"t":"Hold the beans until the rice is mostly cooked so they stay intact instead of breaking down into mush.","s":4},{"t":"Do not lift the lid during cooking, and let it rest for the 5 minutes at the end so steam finishes the top grains before you fluff.","s":5}],"rl":"FXVXXXBPMXAZ"},"3074":{"lv":"EERRRR","w":{"1":"Frying medium; use enough to cover the plantain pieces.","2":"Seasons the hot fried slices.","3":"Main flavor in the mojo dip.","4":"Carries the garlic in the dip; see substitute.","5":"Adds the sharp acid that balances the dip."},"sub":{"1":{"x":"1 cup canola oil","d":"Same results.","k":["canola oil"]},"4":{"x":"1/4 cup vegetable oil","d":"Less fruity, still works for the dip.","k":["vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Use fully green, firm plantains; the first fry at 325F cooks them through, the second at 375F crisps them.","s":1},{"t":"Smash while they are still hot; cooled plantain cracks and crumbles instead of flattening.","s":2},{"t":"Salt immediately after the second fry so it sticks; warm the garlic in the olive oil for the mojo for a milder flavor.","s":4}],"rl":"BFZXFA"},"3075":{"lv":"EER","w":{"1":"Frying medium; use enough to cover the pan bottom.","2":"Sharpens the sweetness of the caramelized plantain."},"sub":{"1":{"x":"1/2 cup canola oil or 3 tbsp butter","d":"Butter browns faster; keep heat lower.","k":["canola oil","butter"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Choose plantains with mostly black skins; they are full of sugar and caramelize, while yellow ones turn starchy.","s":0},{"t":"Fry at medium heat; the high sugar content burns quickly, so lower the heat if edges darken before the center softens.","s":2},{"t":"Do not move the slices while the first side browns; they caramelize only on contact with the pan.","s":2}],"rl":"BFZ"},"3497":{"lv":"EEERERRE","w":{"3":"Gives sweetness to balance the lime; see substitute.","5":"Herbal note in the mojo.","6":"Earthy warmth."},"sub":{"3":{"x":"1/4 cup water + 1 tbsp extra lime juice + 1 tsp sugar","d":"Less fruity; the sugar balances the acid.","k":["water","lime juice","sugar"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Remove the woody center from each yuca chunk before or after boiling; it is fibrous and inedible.","s":1},{"t":"Cook the garlic gently without browning; burnt garlic makes the whole mojo bitter. Add the juices off the heat since the pan sputters.","s":2},{"t":"Pour the mojo over the hot yuca so it absorbs the sauce; cool yuca stays pale and bland.","s":4}],"rl":"BXFAAXXZ"},"53548":{"lv":"EERROERRR","w":{"1":"Coats the fries so they crisp; see substitute.","2":"Warmth and color on the fries.","3":"Seasons the fries.","4":"Light spice only.","6":"Adds tang and creaminess to the dip; see substitute.","7":"Adds acid and prevents the avocado from browning.","8":"Fresh herb note in the dip."},"sub":{"1":{"x":"2 tbsp vegetable oil","d":"Neutral; fine for baking.","k":["vegetable oil"]},"6":{"x":"1/4 cup plain Greek yogurt","d":"Tangier and lighter; works since the dip is not heated.","k":["greek yogurt","plain yogurt"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Dry the boiled yuca thoroughly on towels before oiling; surface moisture steams on the pan instead of crisping.","s":1},{"t":"Heat the baking sheet in the 450F oven first and spread the fries in one layer; crowding gives soft fries.","s":2},{"t":"Remove the woody center of each yuca piece while cutting; it stays tough after baking.","s":0}],"rl":"BFXZZGGAG"},"53546":{"lv":"EERRRRORREEOOOOR","w":{"0":"For sauteing; any neutral oil or olive oil works.","2":"Adds mild heat and a green note.","3":"Savory depth.","4":"Adds concentrated tomato richness and color.","5":"Season at the end since broth and beans already carry salt.","6":"Light seasoning.","7":"Earthy warmth.","8":"Smoky warmth and mild heat.","10":"Liquid base of the soup; see substitute.","11":"Quiet herbal background.","12":"Garnish only.","13":"Garnish only.","14":"Garnish only.","15":"A squeeze at the end brightens the soup."},"sub":{"0":{"x":"2 tbsp vegetable oil","d":"Neutral; fine.","k":["vegetable oil"]},"10":{"x":"3 cups vegetable broth or 3 cups water + 1 tsp salt","d":"Lighter flavor; water version needs seasoning.","k":["vegetable broth","water"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Cook the tomato paste and spices in the oil for a minute before adding the beans; it deepens the flavor.","s":0},{"t":"Blend only part of the soup, or mash with a potato masher, so it is thick but still has whole beans.","s":2},{"t":"Finish with a squeeze of lime and taste for salt at the end, since canned beans vary in saltiness.","s":3}],"rl":"FXXXXZZXXPMXGGGA"},"53543":{"lv":"EEEEEERR","w":{"0":"Crust base; graham crackers work.","6":"Warm aroma; the filling is still rich without it.","7":"Marbles into the filling and finishes the top; skipping loses the caramel note."},"sub":{"0":{"x":"7 oz graham crackers (about 9 full sheets)","d":"Slightly more honey-wheat flavor and less buttery.","k":["graham cracker"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Beat the cream cheese smooth first and add the eggs on low; extra air makes the cheesecake puff and crack as it cools.","s":5},{"t":"Pull it when the edge is set and the center still jiggles, then let it cool in the oven with the door ajar, which prevents cracks.","s":8},{"t":"Chill at least 4 hours before releasing the ring; use a hot, dry knife for clean slices.","s":11}],"rl":"BFBBWSXG"},"53544":{"lv":"EEREERE","w":{"2":"Sharpens the sweetness and coffee flavor.","5":"Rounds out the coffee flavor."},"tips":[{"t":"Cook the custard to 170-175F (77-80C), stirring until it coats a spoon; do not let it boil or the yolks scramble.","s":4},{"t":"Strain the custard and chill it completely, ideally overnight, before churning; a cold base freezes into smoother ice cream.","s":6},{"t":"Use strong espresso or dissolved Cafe La Llave; weak coffee gets lost in the cream and goes icy.","s":5}],"rl":"FMZWTXX"},"53553":{"lv":"EOEEREEERE","w":{"1":"Small amount; adds mild savory flavor.","4":"Binder and tenderizer; the meatballs stay together even without it.","8":"Light seasoning.","9":"Frying fat that browns the crust; see substitute."},"sub":{"9":{"x":"2 tbsp neutral oil","d":"Less nutty; butter browns more.","k":["vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Mix the salt into the pork first, then the other ingredients; salt makes the meat sticky so the frikadeller are tender and bound.","s":1},{"t":"Fry a small test patty first to check seasoning before shaping the rest.","s":2},{"t":"Brown over medium heat, then lower the heat so the centers reach 160F (71C) without burning the outside. A bit of oil with the butter prevents it from burning.","s":3}],"rl":"XXPTTMTZZF"},"53549":{"lv":"ERERRRRRORO","w":{"1":"Mild, sweet onion bite in the dressing.","3":"Adds cream and tang; see substitute.","4":"Sharp flavor that lifts the creamy dressing.","5":"Brightens the dressing.","6":"Smoky, salty topping.","7":"Earthy contrast to the creamy salad.","8":"Garnish only.","9":"Season at the end.","10":"Light seasoning."},"sub":{"3":{"x":"4 tbsp sour cream","d":"Slightly tangier and less rich.","k":["sour cream"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Simmer the chicken gently and cook it to 165F (74C) throughout; let it cool in the cooking water so it stays moist.","s":0},{"t":"Fry the mushrooms in the bacon fat without crowding and salt them at the end, so they brown instead of releasing water.","s":4},{"t":"Dress the chicken only when it is cool; warm chicken turns the mayonnaise oily.","s":2}],"rl":"PXFFXAGVGZZ"},"53554":{"lv":"EEEOERRER","w":{"3":"Garnish only.","5":"Crispy onion topping that adds crunch.","6":"Coats the onions so they fry crisp.","7":"For frying the onions; see substitute.","8":"Seasons the crispy onions."},"sub":{"7":{"x":"1 1/4 cups sunflower or canola oil","d":"Same function at medium heat.","k":["sunflower oil","canola oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Cool the potatoes before slicing so they hold their shape.","s":0},{"t":"Shake excess flour from the onions and fry in a single layer; crowding drops the oil temperature and makes them greasy.","s":5},{"t":"Salt the onions right out of the oil so the salt sticks.","s":6}],"rl":"BPFGBVTFZ"},"53552":{"lv":"EEEEEEEER","w":{"8":"Brushed on the buns for shine and a golden crust."},"tips":[{"t":"Keep the milk lukewarm, no more than about 40C; hot milk kills the yeast.","s":0},{"t":"Add the butter after the dough has started to develop and knead until it passes the windowpane test; fat added too early slows gluten.","s":2},{"t":"Grind the cardamom fresh if possible; pre-ground loses its fragrance and the buns taste flat. Shape each bun tight so it rises up.","s":4}],"rl":"MLSWZXSFG"},"53556":{"lv":"EEEEEEREE","w":{"2":"Beer adds malty depth and some acid for the rye; see substitute.","3":"Acid tang and softness that rye needs; see substitute.","6":"Adds crunch, nutty flavor, and moisture; the loaf is plainer and denser without them."},"sub":{"2":{"x":"3/4 cup water + 1 tbsp molasses","d":"Less bitter malt complexity; the molasses gives similar dark color and sweetness.","k":["water","molasses"]},"3":{"x":"3/4 cup milk + 2 tsp lemon juice or white vinegar, rested 5 minutes","d":"Close to buttermilk; slightly less tang.","k":["milk","lemon juice","vinegar"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Rye has little gluten, so the dough is a sticky paste rather than a springy dough; use a wet spatula to smooth it and do not add extra flour.","s":3},{"t":"Bake until the center registers about 205F (96-98C); rye bakes long, and a pale or damp crumb means it is underdone.","s":6},{"t":"Wait until the loaf is completely cool, and ideally rest it a day, before slicing; rye starch sets slowly and a warm loaf is gummy.","s":7}],"rl":"BMMMLZBSS"},"53559":{"lv":"EERROREREEOO","w":{"2":"Warm spice in the jam.","3":"The listed 1/4 cup looks like a typo; use about 1 stick or 1/4 tsp ground. Warms the jam.","4":"Fragrant, but the jam still works with a splash of vanilla extract or none.","5":"Brightens the jam and cuts the sugar.","7":"Egg wash for shine and sealing the pastry.","10":"Duplicate of the jam made above; extra for serving only.","11":"Mixed with the egg for a thinner wash."},"tips":[{"t":"Cook the jam until thick and let it cool fully; hot or thin jam leaks out of the pastry and makes it soggy.","s":3},{"t":"After wrapping, chill the brie in the freezer for 20-30 minutes before baking; the pastry puffs and sets before the cheese turns runny.","s":11},{"t":"Place seam-side down and cut a small steam vent; rest 10 minutes after baking so the cheese thickens a bit before cutting.","s":15}],"rl":"VWXXXXMGSPGM"},"53550":{"lv":"EEEEERREEEEREO","w":{"5":"Seals the crust against the custard so it stays crisp; skipping risks a soggy shell.","6":"Vanilla flavor in the custard; vanilla extract works.","11":"Whipped in for a lighter, richer custard.","13":"Garnish only."},"tips":[{"t":"Scatter the chocolate over the warm shell right out of the oven and spread it as it melts; this makes a barrier against the custard.","s":4},{"t":"Whisk the custard constantly and let it bubble gently for a minute so the cornstarch cooks out; cool it with plastic wrap pressed onto the surface to avoid a skin.","s":6},{"t":"Fold the whipped cream into cool custard in two additions and assemble just before serving, since the berries bleed into the custard.","s":7}],"rl":"SWFSSFXTWTMFVG"},"53551":{"lv":"ERRREEEER","w":{"1":"Starts the apples steaming so they do not scorch.","2":"Warm flavor in the apples; plain apple tastes flatter.","3":"Keeps the apples bright and adds tartness.","8":"Lightens the dessert and adds richness between the layers."},"tips":[{"t":"Use a tart cooking apple like Bramley; it breaks down into a coarse puree and balances the sweet crumbs. Add sugar after the apples release their juices.","s":1},{"t":"Toast the buttered crumbs slowly and pull them while pale gold; they continue to color off the heat and turn bitter if dark.","s":6},{"t":"Cool the crumbs completely and assemble right before serving so they stay crisp.","s":9}],"rl":"VMXAWFBWF"},"53557":{"lv":"EEOROEE","w":{"2":"Light heat only.","3":"Savory depth in the marinade.","4":"Mostly color; barely tastes.","5":"Frying medium; see substitute.","6":"Use commercially processed cassava flour or tapioca starch only. This is what makes the crust crisp; never use raw grated cassava."},"sub":{"5":{"x":"Equal amount of canola or sunflower oil","d":"Same results at 325-375F.","k":["canola oil","sunflower oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Pat the wings completely dry before seasoning and coating; surface moisture keeps the crust from crisping.","s":0},{"t":"Let the coated wings rest on a rack for 30 minutes so the flour hydrates and clings during frying.","s":5},{"t":"Fry in two stages: first until light brown and cooked through, rest 10 minutes, then fry hot until golden for a crisp crust. Wings need 165F (74C).","s":8}],"rl":"PZZXCFT"},"53565":{"lv":"ERRREE","w":{"1":"Seasons the coating; moderate heat.","2":"Color and mild sweetness.","3":"Savory depth; garlic powder works best in a dry coating."},"tips":[{"t":"Skip washing the chicken with lemon; rinsing raw poultry spreads bacteria around the sink. Pat it dry instead.","s":0},{"t":"To form the lollipop, cut around the bone at the thin end and push the meat down to the thick end; the neat shape cooks evenly.","s":2},{"t":"Fry at about 350F (175C) in batches until cooked to 165F (74C); brush on the sauce and finish in a hot oven so it sets sticky.","s":7}],"rl":"PZXXZT"},"53561":{"lv":"EEROROOOR","w":{"2":"Tangy bite that cuts the rich yolk filling.","3":"Light seasoning.","4":"Season the filling.","5":"Dusting for color.","6":"Garnish only.","7":"Garnish only.","8":"Salt cod must be soaked in several changes of water and cooked (boiled or sauteed) until flaky before it tops the eggs; it is not ready to eat as sold."},"tips":[{"t":"Start the eggs in cold water and hold at a gentle boil about 12 minutes before an ice bath; 15 plus 5 minutes gives a green ring on the yolk.","s":0},{"t":"Mash the yolks with a fork until fine before adding the mayonnaise; a smooth filling pipes cleanly.","s":5},{"t":"Desalt the codfish by soaking it first; keep the eggs refrigerated and serve within 2 hours at room temperature.","s":9}],"rl":"PFXZZGGGG"},"53573":{"lv":"EERRRROR","w":{"2":"Seasons the salad.","3":"Color and sweetness.","4":"Juicy sweetness.","5":"Mild onion bite.","6":"Garnish for crunch only.","7":"Raw garlic bite; use less if you want it mild."},"tips":[{"t":"Use unripe green papaya, which is crisp and mild; ripe papaya turns to mush when shredded.","s":0},{"t":"Soak the shredded papaya in ice water for 10 minutes and drain well; it crisps up and the dressing does not water down.","s":0},{"t":"Dress the salad right before serving, since the salt and lemon pull out water and soften the shreds.","s":4}],"rl":"VAZVVXGX"},"53558":{"lv":"EE","w":{"1":"Frying medium; enough to submerge the pieces."},"sub":{"1":{"x":"3 cups canola or sunflower oil","d":"Same results.","k":["canola oil","sunflower oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Use firm green plantains; ripe ones are too soft to press into cups.","s":0},{"t":"Press the cups while the slices are still hot from the first fry; they crack when cool.","s":7},{"t":"Dry the pressed cups and fry again at about 375F until golden, then salt right away and fill just before serving so they stay crisp.","s":9}],"rl":"VF"},"53560":{"lv":"EEEEEER","w":{"6":"Used to brush the buns for shine; the buns bake fine without it."},"tips":[{"t":"Warm the milk only to about 110F (43C); hotter kills the yeast.","s":1},{"t":"Add the butter once the dough is smooth and knead until it passes the windowpane test; this makes the buns soft and rich.","s":2},{"t":"Four pounds of flour is a bakery batch; halve it for a home mixer. Proof until the buns are puffy and spring back slowly when poked.","s":7}],"rl":"SMWLZFG"},"53562":{"lv":"EEROE","w":{"2":"Salt cod must be soaked overnight in several water changes and cooked until flaky before it fills the shells; it is cured and not ready to eat as sold.","3":"Garnish only.","4":"Frying medium; see substitute."},"sub":{"4":{"x":"Equal amount of canola or sunflower oil","d":"Same results for deep frying.","k":["canola oil","sunflower oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Choose a mature but firm breadfruit; a soft ripe one falls apart when sliced and folded.","s":0},{"t":"Fold the slices while they are still pliable and secure with toothpicks, then fry them submerged until golden.","s":5},{"t":"Soak the salt codfish overnight with a few water changes before using it as filling.","s":13}],"rl":"BMPGF"},"53563":{"lv":"EEEEEEO","w":{"4":"Makes the crumb tender and soft; see substitute.","6":"Dusting for the board only."},"sub":{"4":{"x":"1/2 cup (113 g) butter or lard","d":"Butter gives more flavor but a slightly less tender crust; lard is closest to traditional.","k":["butter","lard"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Knead the full 8-10 minutes until the dough is smooth and elastic; it is the key step for a tight, soft crumb.","s":7},{"t":"Add water gradually and judge by feel, since flour absorbs differently; the dough should be firm but not sticky.","s":4},{"t":"Put a pan of hot water on the oven floor for steam; it gives a thin, shiny crust. Bake until the loaves sound hollow when tapped.","s":13}],"rl":"SWZLFMG"},"53564":{"lv":"EEEO","w":{"3":"Pinch of salt; barely noticeable."},"tips":[{"t":"Let the mixed dough sit about 10 minutes so the farine and cassava flour absorb the water; it will be much easier to shape.","s":1},{"t":"Press the rounds to about 1/4 inch and roast on a dry, heavy griddle at medium heat; a hot, oil-free pan gives a dry, crisp surface.","s":5},{"t":"Coating the discs in extra farine gives the coarse crust that makes the bread traditional.","s":4}],"rl":"BMBZ"},"53566":{"lv":"EEEO","w":{"1":"Frying medium; see substitute.","3":"Garnish only.","2":"Salt cod must be soaked in several changes of water and cooked until flaky before filling; it is cured and not ready to eat as sold."},"sub":{"1":{"x":"1 cup canola oil","d":"Same results.","k":["canola oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Use ripe yellow-black plantains and slice them lengthwise thin; they stay pliable when rolled warm, and green ones crack.","s":1},{"t":"Roll them while still warm and secure with a toothpick; once cool they stiffen and split.","s":6},{"t":"Soak the salt codfish before filling to avoid an over-salty roll.","s":6}],"rl":"VFPG"},"53567":{"lv":"EEERERE","w":{"3":"Slight sweetness and browning; omit for a more savory bake.","5":"Adds tenderness to the dough.","6":"Frying medium; see substitute."},"sub":{"6":{"x":"2 cups canola or sunflower oil","d":"Same results at medium heat.","k":["canola oil","sunflower oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Keep the dough soft and slightly sticky; a stiff dough fries up dense instead of puffy.","s":4},{"t":"Rest the shaped balls 15-20 minutes so the baking powder works and they puff in the oil.","s":6},{"t":"Fry at medium heat, around 350F; too hot browns the outside before the center cooks. Press a dimple in the center so it cooks evenly.","s":8}],"rl":"SLZWMFF"},"53568":{"lv":"REREREERRROORORO","w":{"0":"Slight sweetness and browning in the crust; skipping gives a more savory pastry.","2":"Seasons the crust; without it the dough tastes flat.","4":"Makes the crust tender and flaky; all-butter works, see substitute.","7":"Sweetens the filling and gives caramel notes.","8":"Warm spice that makes it taste like apple pie.","9":"Browned butter adds nutty richness to the filling.","10":"Light warm spice only.","11":"Balances the sweet apples.","12":"Keeps the apples bright and balances the sugar.","13":"Sprinkled on top for crunch and shine.","14":"Egg wash for the golden color and for sealing the edges.","15":"Thins the egg wash."},"sub":{"4":{"x":"1 tbsp cold butter (5 oz total butter)","d":"Slightly less tender but richer flavor.","k":["butter"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Keep the butter cold and in pea-size chunks; those pockets melt in the oven and create flaky layers. Chill the dough for at least an hour.","s":3},{"t":"Cool the apple filling completely before filling; warm filling melts the dough and steam bursts the seams.","s":11},{"t":"Seal the edges with a fork and a dab of egg wash, then cut vents in the top rounds; small pops burst otherwise. Use a firm apple like Granny Smith, diced small.","s":18}],"rl":"WSZFFMVWXFXZAGGM"},"53569":{"lv":"EORR","w":{"1":"Rounds out the mango flavor; barely noticeable.","2":"Sweetens and makes it creamy; see substitute.","3":"Chills and thickens the smoothie; skip and it is thinner and warmer."},"sub":{"2":{"x":"1/2 cup whole milk + 3 tbsp sugar","d":"Less thick and creamy; blend until the sugar dissolves.","k":["milk","sugar"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Use fiberless, very ripe mangoes such as honey/Ataulfo types; stringy mango leaves fibers in the glass.","s":0},{"t":"Frozen mango chunks thicken the smoothie without watering it down; add ice last so it stays creamy.","s":2},{"t":"Slice the mango rose thin and keep it chilled until serving; it dries out and darkens as it sits.","s":8}],"rl":"VXWM"},"53570":{"lv":"EEEEEROOEER","w":{"5":"Aroma and flavor of the custard; plain cream tastes flat without it.","6":"Garnish only.","7":"Garnish only.","10":"Adjusts the sweetness of the mango layer; add depending on the fruit.","0":"This line is unflavored powdered gelatin, not custard powder. It is bloomed in cold water and sets both the cream and the mango layers."},"tips":[{"t":"The 'custard' line is actually gelatin, as the steps show. Sprinkle it over cold water to bloom for 7-10 minutes before dissolving in warm milk.","s":1},{"t":"Heat the cream and milk only until the sugar dissolves and do not boil; boiling can weaken the gelatin and curdle the cream. Pour in at an angle for the layered look.","s":2},{"t":"Let the custard set fully and cool the mango mixture before pouring on top; a warm topping melts into the custard.","s":15}],"rl":"SFMWMXGGVMW"},"53571":{"lv":"EREEREEERROO","w":{"1":"Adds chew and texture to the dense cake.","4":"Rounds out the sweet spices.","8":"Warm spice that defines the flavor.","9":"Aromatic spice, secondary to the cinnamon.","10":"A little citrus aroma; minor impact.","11":"Chewy sweet bursts; the cake is fine without."},"tips":[{"t":"The baking soda needs the acid in brown sugar to react; do not swap in white sugar without adding a spoon of vinegar or lemon juice.","s":0},{"t":"Mix the stiff batter just until combined; overmixing makes the rough cake tough rather than crumbly.","s":1},{"t":"Bake until deeply brown and firm; the center should be dry, since a pale cake stays gummy.","s":5}],"rl":"SBWBXMLLXXXV"},"53572":{"lv":"REEEE","w":{"0":"Warm spice in the egg coating; the fried banana is plainer without it.","4":"Deep frying medium; see substitute."},"sub":{"4":{"x":"5 cups canola or sunflower oil","d":"Same results at 350F.","k":["canola oil","sunflower oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Use firm, barely ripe bananas; very soft ones turn to mush in the hot oil.","s":0},{"t":"Coat just before frying and press the crumbs on; a double coat of egg and crumbs makes a thicker crisp shell.","s":2},{"t":"Fry at about 350F (175C) and do not crowd; crumbs burn before the inside is hot if the oil is too hot.","s":4}],"rl":"XVTTF"},"53379":{"lv":"EEEREERRR","w":{"3":"Greases the pan; see substitute.","6":"Gentle vanilla flavor.","7":"Traditional topping; melts into the hot poffertjes.","8":"Traditional finish for sweetness and looks."},"sub":{"3":{"x":"1 tbsp neutral oil brushed on the pan","d":"Less flavor; butter browns more.","k":["vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Rest the batter 45 minutes; it thickens slightly and the yeast gives the airy texture. It should be a thick pourable batter.","s":2},{"t":"Heat the pan well and butter each hole; fill halfway, flip with a fork when the top is dry, and they puff into balls.","s":3},{"t":"Dissolve the yeast in lukewarm milk, no more than about 40C, so it does not die; buckwheat gives the nutty flavor.","s":0}],"rl":"SSSFMLXGG"},"52979":{"lv":"EEEROEROREEE","w":{"3":"Sweet savory base in the ragout.","4":"Garnish touch only.","6":"Season the ragout; coating adds nothing.","7":"Light seasoning.","8":"Traditional warm note in the ragout."},"tips":[{"t":"Use cooked, finely shredded beef, such as chuck simmered until tender, and chill the ragout overnight so it is stiff enough to roll.","s":0},{"t":"Double coat: flour, egg, crumbs, egg, crumbs; a thin shell bursts and leaks hot ragout in the oil.","s":2},{"t":"Fry at about 375F (190C) for 2-3 minutes in small batches; they split if the ragout is warm or the oil is cooler.","s":3}],"rl":"FTMXXPZZXTTT"},"52980":{"lv":"EORREEEORO","w":{"1":"Quiet background aroma while boiling.","2":"Sweet onion note in the kale.","3":"Adds richness to the mash and sauteed kale.","7":"Traditional spice touch.","8":"Season at the end; salt the water as well.","9":"Light seasoning."},"tips":[{"t":"Steam the potatoes dry in the pot after draining and use a masher or ricer; a blender makes them gluey.","s":6},{"t":"Warm the milk before adding and work quickly so the mash stays fluffy.","s":6},{"t":"Heat the sausage by laying it on top of the potatoes for the last 10 minutes of boiling or in a covered pan with a little water; serve with a well of gravy.","s":8}],"rl":"BXXFVPMXZZ"},"52769":{"lv":"EERORRRE","w":{"2":"Cool, tangy sauce to balance the meat and fries.","3":"Adds heat; use as much or little as you like.","4":"Cool fresh crunch on top.","5":"Juicy freshness.","6":"Cool crunch."},"tips":[{"t":"Sear the doner strips in a hot pan until the edges crisp, and heat the meat through to 165F (74C); soft, pale meat is the main failure.","s":0},{"t":"Bake or fry the fries until very crisp first, since the meat, cheese and sauce soften them.","s":1},{"t":"Melt the cheese in the oven or under the broiler, then add the cold salad and sauces last so the lettuce stays crisp.","s":5}],"rl":"BPXXVVVF"},"53388":{"lv":"ERREERORRR","w":{"1":"Savory aroma in the mash.","2":"Sweet savory base in the bean mix.","5":"Tangy kick that cuts the rich mash.","6":"Loosens the mash; see substitute.","7":"Adds richness and saltiness to the mash.","8":"Salty, smoky topping.","9":"Season to taste; the bacon and cheese are salty."},"sub":{"6":{"x":"1 to 2 tbsp cream or the bean cooking water","d":"Cream is richer; cooking water is lighter.","k":["cream"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Remove the strings from the runner beans and slice them on the diagonal; cook just until tender so they keep color and bite.","s":0},{"t":"Drain the potatoes well and mash before adding the butter, then fold in the beans so the mash does not get gluey.","s":4},{"t":"Add the mustard at the end so it stays sharp; the cheese melts into the hot mash for a creamy texture.","s":4}],"rl":"VXXBFXMFGZ"},"53389":{"lv":"EEROORRRRO","w":{"2":"Scents the mash gently; it must be fished out before mashing.","3":"Only glazes the pan-fried apple slices; a pinch of sugar does the same job.","4":"Final adjustment on the mash; skip it if the stick already perfumed the pot.","5":"Salty, crisp topping that balances the sweet apple; the mash stands alone without it.","6":"The breaded fried cheese is the signature topping; skipping it loses the crunchy, molten contrast.","7":"Apple and potato need salt to taste balanced rather than sweet; add before serving.","8":"Glue for the sesame crust on the cheese; only needed if you fry the cheese.","9":"Gives the fried cheese its crisp toasted crust; breadcrumbs work if you have no sesame."},"sub":{"2":{"x":"1/2 tsp ground cinnamon","d":"Cannot be removed, so the mash tastes a little stronger and looks speckled. Add it after draining.","k":["ground cinnamon"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Give the potatoes a 10 minute head start and drain both very well; then steam them dry in the hot empty pot for a minute. Apples release a lot of water and a wet mash turns into soup.","s":0},{"t":"Freeze the cheese sticks for 15 minutes before coating and double-dip them (egg, sesame, egg, sesame). The crust sets in the hot pan before the cheese melts out and leaks.","s":4},{"t":"Use a firm apple that cooks down cleanly (Elstar, Braeburn, Goudreinet). Very mealy apples go grainy once mashed.","s":-1}],"rl":"VBXWXGGZTG"},"52981":{"lv":"EEERRRRRRRE","w":{"3":"Seasons 2 L of water and adds savory depth that the pork alone does not provide.","4":"Celery is part of the classic snert flavor base; skipping it makes the soup flatter.","5":"Adds sweetness and body; the soup works without it but tastes less rounded.","6":"Dissolves slightly and thickens the soup; extra carrot or celeriac can replace it.","7":"Supporting aromatic that melts into the broth.","8":"Gives the classic mild oniony sweetness; use a second onion if you have no leek.","9":"Earthy celery-root flavor typical of snert; extra potato and celery approximate it."},"sub":{"3":{"x":"2 cups chicken or vegetable broth in place of 2 cups of the water","d":"Slightly less concentrated; taste before adding any salt, since the pork and sausage are already salty.","k":["broth"]},"9":{"x":"1 cup diced potato plus 1 extra celery stalk","d":"Loses the sweet nutty celeriac note but still thickens and tastes of the same family.","k":["potato","celery"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Stir from the bottom every 10 minutes: split peas settle and scorch easily, and a burnt layer ruins the whole pot.","s":1},{"t":"Authentic snert is thick enough for a spoon to stand upright, and it thickens further overnight. Thin with water when reheating, and it tastes better on day two.","s":5},{"t":"Smoked rookworst is already cooked, so it only needs warming through. Add it late and keep the soup at a gentle simmer so the casing does not split.","s":4}],"rl":"MBPXXVTXXVP"},"53380":{"lv":"EEEERREOO","w":{"4":"Rounds out the sweetness; the cake is still fine without it.","5":"Cinnamon is the classic partner of apple and gives the cake its warm character.","7":"A pinch sharpens the sweetness; skipping it is barely noticeable.","8":"Finishing dusting only; skip without any effect on texture.","2":"Flour and leavening for the cake; skipped, the cake has no structure or rise."},"sub":{"2":{"x":"200 g all-purpose flour + 2 1/2 tsp baking powder + 1/4 tsp salt (sift together)","d":"Same rise; sift well so the baking powder is evenly distributed.","k":["all-purpose flour","baking powder"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Whisk the eggs and sugar until a ribbon of batter holds its shape for a few seconds. This trapped air is the cake's only mechanical lift besides the self-rising flour.","s":1},{"t":"Toss the diced apple in a spoonful of the flour (or the cinnamon) before folding it in so the pieces do not sink to the bottom of the cake.","s":4},{"t":"Probe the skewer test in a spot with no apple chunk; hitting wet fruit gives a false negative. If the top darkens before 45 minutes, tent with foil.","s":7}],"rl":"LWSFXXVZG"},"53381":{"lv":"EEEREEEEOO","w":{"0":"Provides structure and a little lift for the dough; plain flour plus baking powder acts the same.","1":"Brown sugar gives caramel notes typical of speculaas.","2":"The signature flavor of speculaas; a homemade blend works just as well.","3":"Seasons the dough and sharpens the spices.","5":"Binds the dough; water works too, but milk gives a slightly softer bite.","8":"Egg wash is already used; leftover milk is only a second option for brushing.","9":"Decorative almonds; they look good but do not change the filling."},"sub":{"0":{"x":"300 g all-purpose flour + 3 tsp baking powder + 1/2 tsp salt","d":"Same rise and crumb; mix the baking powder in well.","k":["all-purpose flour","baking powder"]},"1":{"x":"150 g white sugar + 1 tbsp molasses or dark honey","d":"Gives the same dark caramel flavor; mix well so no molasses streaks remain.","k":["sugar","molasses"]},"2":{"x":"2 tsp cinnamon + 1/2 tsp ground ginger + 1/4 tsp ground cloves + 1/4 tsp nutmeg + a pinch of white pepper","d":"Close to a store-bought speculaas blend; add the pepper sparingly.","k":["cinnamon","ginger","cloves","nutmeg"]},"5":{"x":"10 tbsp water (start with 6 and add as needed)","d":"Slightly less rich and pale; the dough is still easy to handle.","k":["water"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Rest the dough overnight so the flour fully hydrates and the spices bloom; the dough is also much easier to roll once the butter is firm.","s":1},{"t":"Knead the almond paste with the egg until completely smooth and spreadable, then press it right to the corners. A gap at the edge makes the cut squares collapse.","s":2},{"t":"Use real almond paste (at least 50 percent almonds). Cheap marzipan is too sweet and stiff and turns hard after baking.","s":-1}],"rl":"SWXZFMBTGG"},"53382":{"lv":"OEEEEEREEER","w":{"0":"Only dissolves the yeast; 15 ml of warm water does the same.","6":"A pinch for the dough; it balances the sweetness.","7":"Dutch stroop gives the typical dark, slightly bitter caramel taste; golden syrup is the easiest swap.","10":"Warm spice in the filling; the waffle still tastes like caramel without it."},"sub":{"0":{"x":"15 ml (1 tbsp) lukewarm water","d":"No noticeable difference; milk only adds trace richness.","k":["water"]},"7":{"x":"200 g golden syrup, or 150 g light corn syrup + 50 g molasses","d":"Golden syrup is milder; the molasses restores some of the dark stroop flavor.","k":["golden syrup","corn syrup","molasses"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Cut the circle and split the waffle immediately while it is still hot and pliable. Once it cools it turns brittle and cracks along the cut.","s":5},{"t":"Keep the filling warm over a pot of hot water while you work. If it only just comes to a gentle simmer it stays spreadable, whereas boiling it hard makes it set into toffee on cooling.","s":2},{"t":"Rewarm a finished stroopwafel on top of a hot mug of coffee for a minute to soften the filling again.","s":-1}],"rl":"MLSFWSZWWFX"},"53385":{"lv":"EEERROO","w":{"3":"Boterkoek is very buttery; salt keeps it from tasting flat or greasy.","4":"A caramel filling that is not part of the classic plain boterkoek; skipping gives the traditional version.","5":"Chopped chocolate adds contrast to the caramel; skip it freely.","6":"Only brushed on top for color and shine; milk or nothing also works."},"sub":{"4":{"x":"200 g almond paste or thick jam, in small dollops","d":"Almond paste gives the classic Dutch gevulde boterkoek taste; jam is tangier and can bubble over.","k":["almond paste","jam"],"wn":"Almond paste contains tree nuts (almonds); not in the original recipe."}},"tips":[{"t":"Cream the butter and sugar only until combined and keep the dough cool. Warm, greasy dough bakes up oily and spreads. Chill it 20 minutes if it feels soft.","s":1},{"t":"Leave a 1 cm border without filling and press the top layer to the edge so the dulce de leche does not leak and burn on the tin.","s":4},{"t":"Run a fork across the egg-washed top in a crosshatch pattern for the classic look. Let the cake cool completely: the center stays wobbly until the butter resets.","s":6}],"rl":"FWSZWGG"},"53386":{"lv":"EEEERROER","w":{"4":"Brings out the flavor of the pastry; skip only if your butter is salted.","5":"The classic apple pie spice; without it the filling is plain.","6":"Adds chewy sweetness but is not essential.","8":"Adds a little moisture to the dough or loosens the egg wash; add by the teaspoon only if the dough is crumbly."},"tips":[{"t":"Do not skip the crushed rusks (or breadcrumbs) on the base: they soak up the apple juices so the bottom crust stays crisp and not soggy.","s":7},{"t":"Cut the apples in small even cubes of about 1 cm. Big chunks leave air gaps and the lattice sinks as they cook down. Use a tart, firm variety such as Granny Smith.","s":4},{"t":"Keep the dough cold for the lattice strips: warm dough stretches and tears. Let the pie cool in the tin so the filling sets and slices cleanly.","s":8}],"rl":"FSSWZXVVM"},"53387":{"lv":"EEEOEREO","w":{"0":"Gives structure and rise; plain flour needs extra baking powder to match.","1":"Sweet and moist crumb with a molasses note.","2":"Hydrates the flour; unsweetened plant milk behaves the same.","3":"Pearl sugar on top gives a crunchy sweet crust; skip it or use coarse sugar.","5":"Ginger-spiced cakes taste better with a little cardamom, but it is secondary.","7":"A pinch only; not noticeable."},"sub":{"0":{"x":"250 g all-purpose flour + 2 tsp baking powder + 1/2 tsp salt (in addition to the 1 tsp baking powder listed)","d":"Same rise as self-rising flour; sift well.","k":["all-purpose flour","baking powder"]},"1":{"x":"250 g white sugar + 2 tbsp molasses or dark honey","d":"Recreates the dark taste of brown sugar and keeps the cake moist.","k":["sugar","molasses"]},"2":{"x":"250 ml (1 cup) soy or oat milk, heated until steaming","d":"Very similar; the flavor is a little plainer.","k":["oat milk"],"wn":"Soy milk contains soy; oat milk may contain gluten."}},"tips":[{"t":"Stir the batter only until smooth; over-whisking makes the crumb tough and tunneled. Pour it in right away because the baking powder starts acting as soon as the hot milk hits it.","s":2},{"t":"Ontbijtkoek is meant to be sticky and dense. Wrap it for 1 to 2 days after baking: the crumb softens and the spices mellow, and it slices much more cleanly.","s":-1},{"t":"Tent with foil if the top darkens before the 50 minute check, and retest every 10 minutes since a long low bake is typical of this cake.","s":3}],"rl":"SWMGXXLZ"},"53391":{"lv":"EEEEEEEERROREO","w":{"1":"Makes the dough tender; butter works equally well.","2":"Hydrates the yeast; regular milk is fine.","7":"Cooks the rice into a creamy pudding; whole milk gives the best richness.","8":"Rounds the sweet filling.","9":"Perfumes the filling in the classic Limburg way.","10":"A subtle aniseed note; the filling is still good without it.","11":"Warm spice that infuses the milk; remove before filling.","13":"A tiny amount to balance sweetness and brighten the filling."},"sub":{"1":{"x":"75 g butter, softened","d":"Richer, slightly less crisp crust; the dough bakes up golden.","k":["butter"],"wn":"Contains dairy (butter)."},"2":{"x":"100 ml lukewarm whole milk","d":"Same function; the dough is a little richer.","k":["whole milk"],"wn":"Contains dairy (milk)."},"7":{"x":"700 ml whole milk","d":"Creamier and richer; stir often as dairy milk scorches faster.","k":["whole milk"],"wn":"Contains dairy (milk)."}},"tips":[{"t":"Stir the rice pudding constantly along the bottom of the pan once it simmers; milk scorches quickly. The pudding should be thick enough to hold a spoon trail before it goes on the dough.","s":1},{"t":"Fish out the cinnamon, star anise and cardamom and let the pudding cool before spreading it. Hot filling melts the dough and makes the base soggy.","s":2},{"t":"The dough is sticky: flour your hands and the counter rather than kneading in extra flour, which makes the crust dry. Bake until the edges are golden and the filling is just set with a slight wobble.","s":2}],"rl":"SFMLWBWMXXXXLA"},"53392":{"lv":"EERRO","w":{"2":"Gives the sugar crust warmth; the cookies are still good with plain vanilla sugar.","3":"Gives the Almere twist; skip it for the plain version.","4":"A fresh citrus note that cuts the sweetness; lemon works the same.","1":"Sugar crust and sweetness; skipped, the pastry is not sweet or caramelized."},"sub":{"1":{"x":"100 g granulated sugar + 1/2 tsp vanilla extract (mix well)","d":"Same caramelizing sugar crust; the vanilla flavor is slightly stronger.","k":["sugar","vanilla extract"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Roll the stacked sheets thin (about 3 mm) and let the cut shapes rest on the tray before baking, as the recipe says: the pastry shrinks back in the pan instead of in the oven, giving flat, crisp cookies.","s":6},{"t":"Line the tray with baking paper, never bare metal: the sugar caramelizes and welds to the pan. Slide the cookies off the paper while still warm.","s":8},{"t":"Watch the last 5 minutes: the sugar goes from deep amber to burnt very quickly. Pull them when the coating is a deep caramel color, not dark brown.","s":8}],"rl":"SWXXX"},"53393":{"lv":"EEEER","w":{"4":"Gives a touch of browning and helps the yeast; the batter still rises without it.","1":"Main liquid of the dough or batter; skipped, the mix is too dry to come together."},"sub":{"1":{"x":"500 ml lukewarm water","d":"Less rich and slightly paler; the doughnuts are a little chewier.","k":["water"]}},"tips":[{"t":"The batter must be very slack and sticky, closer to thick pancake batter than bread dough: stir, do not knead. A stiff batter fries up dense.","s":0},{"t":"Dip the spoons in the hot oil before scooping so the batter slides off. Test one doughnut first and cut it open: if the center is raw the oil is too hot, so lower the heat.","s":3},{"t":"Hold the oil at 180 C and fry only a few at a time; a crowded pot drops the temperature and the doughnuts soak up grease. Roll in sugar while still warm.","s":2}],"rl":"SMLZW"},"53394":{"lv":"EEEEEEEEOEE","w":{"1":"Makes the dough soft and rich; plant milk works too.","8":"Not part of the classic bolus; a little adds a floral note, but skipping it keeps it traditional.","9":"Brown sugar in the coating melts into a sticky caramel.","10":"The thick sugar-cinnamon coating is the defining part of a bolus."},"sub":{"1":{"x":"200 ml lukewarm oat or soy milk","d":"Slightly less rich; the bolus stays soft.","k":["oat milk"],"wn":"Soy milk contains soy; oat milk may contain gluten."},"9":{"x":"75 g white sugar + 2 tsp molasses or dark honey","d":"Recreates the brown-sugar caramel note.","k":["sugar","molasses"]},"10":{"x":"250 g white sugar + 2 tbsp molasses or dark honey","d":"Recreates the brown sugar; mix with the cinnamon thoroughly.","k":["sugar","molasses"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Warm the milk only to lukewarm (below 40 C) before adding the yeast; hot milk kills it and the rolls will not rise.","s":0},{"t":"Bake very hot and very briefly (about 8 minutes) so the inside stays soft and the sugar becomes sticky caramel. Longer baking dries them out.","s":9},{"t":"After baking, flip the rolls over onto the rack so the syrupy sugar that pooled underneath ends up on top as a glossy sticky glaze.","s":10}],"rl":"LMWSSFZXXWW"},"53395":{"lv":"EEREEROERR","w":{"2":"Vanilla note in the sponge; sugar plus a drop of vanilla extract matches it.","4":"Cornstarch lowers the gluten and makes the sponge extra tender; extra flour gives a slightly denser cake.","5":"Thin jam layers add moisture and a fruity tang between the cream; skip for a plainer cake.","6":"Crunchy decoration pressed around the sides; purely for looks and texture.","8":"Sweetens the whipped cream; it stabilizes it slightly.","9":"Topping fruit adds freshness against the rich cream."},"sub":{"2":{"x":"10 g granulated sugar + 1/4 tsp vanilla extract","d":"Same sweetness; slightly more vanilla flavor.","k":["sugar","vanilla extract"]},"4":{"x":"25 g extra all-purpose flour (100 g total)","d":"Sponge is a touch denser and less tender.","k":["all-purpose flour"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Beat the eggs and sugar until a ribbon trails over the surface and holds for a few seconds. There is no chemical leavener, so this foam is the whole lift of the sponge.","s":0},{"t":"Do not open the oven in the first 20 minutes and do not overmix when folding in the flour, or the sponge deflates and turns rubbery.","s":1},{"t":"Whip the cream with a chilled bowl and beaters, and stop at stiff peaks: over-whipped cream turns grainy and butter-like. Cut the cooled sponge with a serrated knife into even layers.","s":4}],"rl":"LWWSSWGFWG"},"3318":{"lv":"EEEREROE","w":{"1":"Bread flour adds gluten for a chewy pocket; all-purpose works fine here.","3":"Feeds the yeast at the start; the dough still rises without it, just a little slower.","5":"Softens the dough slightly; vegetable oil does the same.","6":"Dusting that stops sticking and gives the bread its rustic baladi look."},"sub":{"1":{"x":"1 cup all-purpose flour","d":"Slightly softer chew; the bread still puffs into a pocket.","k":["all-purpose flour"]},"5":{"x":"2 tbsp vegetable or melted butter","d":"Similar softness; olive oil adds only a faint flavor.","k":["vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Preheat the stone or inverted sheet for at least 30 minutes. A blast of heat on the underside turns the moisture in the dough to steam, which is what inflates the loaf into its characteristic pocket.","s":3},{"t":"Roll evenly to a uniform thickness with no thin spots and do not tear the surface, since a hole lets the steam escape and the round will not puff. Handle gently when moving to the stone.","s":4},{"t":"Whole wheat flour absorbs water slowly: let the dough rest after kneading before judging the hydration, and keep it slightly tacky; a stiff dough bakes into a cracker.","s":1}],"rl":"SSLWZFGM"},"3319":{"lv":"EEEEEROOE","w":{"4":"Gives a softer crumb and richer flavor; water works if you have no milk.","5":"Makes the interior soft; butter or olive oil both work.","6":"Only for the glossy golden top; milk gives a duller shine.","7":"Decorative and gives a slight nutty crunch; skip it."},"sub":{"4":{"x":"1/2 cup warm water + 1 tsp butter","d":"Slightly less soft and pale; texture is still good.","k":["water","butter"]},"5":{"x":"3 tbsp melted butter or olive oil","d":"Butter adds flavor; olive oil adds a faint fruity note.","k":["butter","olive oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Cut a deep lengthwise slash (about 1 cm) just before baking; the rolls spring open along it and the shape stays traditional.","s":4},{"t":"Put a pan of boiling water on the lowest rack for the first 10 minutes. The steam keeps the crust thin and crisp, which is the hallmark of fino.","s":5},{"t":"The recipe gives no water amount, so add warm water a tablespoon at a time until the dough is soft but not sticky; a rich dough with a stiff texture bakes dense. Warm the liquid to no more than about 45 C for the yeast.","s":1}],"rl":"SLWZMFGGM"},"3320":{"lv":"EEREEREEROE","w":{"2":"Gives the yeast a quick start; a very small amount.","4":"Tenderizes the dough; water also works.","5":"Soft, flavorful crumb; vegetable oil is a fine replacement.","7":"Mozzarella gives the stretchy melt that balances the salty feta; other mild cheeses do the same.","8":"Fresh green flavor in the filling.","9":"Only for the glossy golden top."},"sub":{"4":{"x":"1/4 cup warm water","d":"Slightly less soft; negligible difference.","k":["water"]},"5":{"x":"1/4 cup vegetable oil or melted butter","d":"Butter adds richness; oil is neutral.","k":["vegetable oil","butter"]},"7":{"x":"1 cup shredded mild cheddar or Monterey Jack","d":"Less stretch but equally melty; a little sharper.","k":["cheddar"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Taste your feta: if it is very salty, rinse or soak it in cold water for 10 minutes first. The cheese concentrates in the bake and can taste harsher than expected.","s":3},{"t":"Do not overfill and pinch the ends tightly into a boat shape; filling that leaks out scorches on the pan. Brush egg wash only on the dough, not on the cheese.","s":5},{"t":"Roll the ovals thin (about 3 mm) so the dough bakes through while the cheese melts. If it springs back, rest it for 5 minutes and roll again.","s":4}],"rl":"SLWZMFPPXGM"},"3021":{"lv":"EERRREROERO","w":{"2":"Dill adds the typical fresh note; use extra parsley if you have none.","3":"Sweet savory base; cooking it first prevents raw onion juices from watering the eggs.","4":"Gives body to the eggah so it holds a firm slice; without it it is more like an omelet.","6":"Warm earthy spice typical of Egyptian eggah.","7":"A tiny secondary spice.","9":"Needed to season six eggs; add a pinch more if the herbs are very green.","10":"Mild pepper heat; optional.","8":"Main or only cooking fat; skipped, the food sticks, browns poorly and the dish loses richness."},"sub":{"8":{"x":"3 tbsp ghee or olive oil","d":"Ghee tastes richer; olive oil gives a lighter, slightly fruity eggah.","k":["ghee","olive oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Dry the washed herbs thoroughly and chop them finely. Wet herbs release water into the egg and leave puddles around the eggah.","s":3},{"t":"Sift the baking powder with the flour before whisking so you do not get bitter lumps. The mixture puffs in the oven and falls a little on cooling, which is normal.","s":2},{"t":"Cool the sauteed onion before stirring it in, and let the finished eggah rest 5 minutes: the eggs should be fully set (no liquid at the center) and it cuts into clean squares.","s":5}],"rl":"PVXXTLXXFZZ"},"3293":{"lv":"EEREOR","w":{"0":"Fat for frying; basterma renders its own, so ghee or oil works equally well.","2":"Adds juiciness and acidity against the fatty cured beef; skip it for a purer basterma flavor.","4":"Basterma is already peppery and salty; pepper adds only a little extra.","5":"Scoops up the runny yolk and salty meat; can be replaced by toast or pita."},"sub":{"0":{"x":"2 tbsp ghee or olive oil","d":"Ghee tastes closest to the traditional samna; olive oil is lighter.","k":["ghee","olive oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Do not add salt: pastrami and basterma are heavily cured and salt concentrates as the fat renders. Taste before seasoning.","s":5},{"t":"Fry the pastrami until the fat has rendered and the edges are crisp before adding eggs, since the rendered fat is what flavors the whites.","s":1},{"t":"Cover the pan to set the whites with steam without flipping. For pregnant or immunocompromised guests, cook until the yolks are firm.","s":4}],"rl":"FPVPZB"},"3294":{"lv":"RERRREOR","w":{"0":"Just enough fat to start the sausage; the sausage fat does the rest, so any oil works.","2":"Sweet base that picks up the rendered sausage fat.","3":"Adds juice and mild acidity that balances the spicy sausage.","4":"Cumin is a typical Egyptian pairing with eggs and sausage.","6":"Sausage is already salty; add only at the end.","7":"For scooping the yolk and sauce; toast or pita is fine."},"sub":{"0":{"x":"1 tbsp butter or vegetable oil","d":"Butter adds a little richness; sausage fat dominates either way.","k":["butter","vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Use a garlicky, spicy, fully cooked beef sausage (sogo is similar to sujuk). If you use a raw sausage, cook it to 160 F (71 C) before adding the onion and eggs.","s":0},{"t":"Reduce the tomato until the pan is almost jammy before making the wells. Watery sauce makes the eggs spread and the whites cook unevenly.","s":2},{"t":"Cover and cook on low heat so steam sets the whites while the yolks stay soft; stop as soon as the whites turn opaque.","s":4}],"rl":"FPXVXPZB"},"3295":{"lv":"EEOR","w":{"1":"Blackstrap molasses is the classic sweetener here; date syrup or dark honey are milder swaps.","2":"A faint spice; the dish is traditionally just tahini and molasses.","3":"The scoop for the dip; any warm flatbread works."},"sub":{"1":{"x":"1/4 cup date syrup or dark honey","d":"Sweeter and less bitter-mineral than blackstrap; swirl just as lightly.","k":["date syrup","honey"],"wn":"Honey must never be given to infants under 12 months."}},"tips":[{"t":"Stir the jar of tahini very well first; the oil separates and the paste at the bottom can be stiff. Warm the jar in hot water or add 1 tsp neutral oil to loosen it, but never water, which makes tahini seize.","s":0},{"t":"Swirl the two only a couple of times so you get streaks of bitter-sweet molasses against nutty tahini; stirring fully gives a muddy, overly sweet paste.","s":2},{"t":"Blackstrap is intensely bitter and mineral. Start with less and add more at the table if you like it stronger.","s":1}],"rl":"FWXB"},"3296":{"lv":"EEERRRO","w":{"3":"A traditional shortcut thickener that gives the set cream a softer texture; skip it and add 1 tsp extra cornstarch.","4":"Classic sweet topping; sugar syrup or maple syrup work.","5":"Warm bread to scoop up the cream; any soft flatbread works.","6":"Adds crunch and color; skip without losing the dish."},"sub":{"4":{"x":"1/2 cup maple syrup, date syrup or simple syrup (equal parts sugar and water boiled)","d":"Less floral than honey, but equally sweet and pourable.","k":["maple syrup","sugar"]}},"tips":[{"t":"This is the cornstarch shortcut. Authentic eishta is the clotted layer skimmed from slowly simmered buffalo milk; for a more authentic result cook the milk gently longer before thickening.","s":2},{"t":"Whisk constantly and bring the mixture to a gentle bubble for a full minute so the cornstarch fully thickens, otherwise it will stay thin and taste starchy.","s":2},{"t":"Chill uncovered only briefly, then press plastic wrap onto the surface once it is cool to prevent a skin from forming on the cream.","s":3}],"rl":"MFTTWBG"},"3001":{"lv":"EERREREEEEORRO","w":{"2":"Gives sweetness and body to the meat sauce.","3":"Garlic rounds out the sauce.","5":"Egyptian macarona bechamel uses a touch of cinnamon in the meat; without it the sauce is plainer.","10":"A pinch in the bechamel; skipping it is barely noticeable.","11":"Provides a salty, nutty crust on top; roumi cheese or pecorino are authentic, but it can be left off.","12":"Needed in the pasta water, meat and bechamel layers.","13":"A mild accent in the meat.","4":"Sauce base for the macarona; skipped, there is no tomato layer."},"sub":{"4":{"x":"1 cup crushed tomatoes, or 3 tbsp tomato paste + 3/4 cup water","d":"Crushed tomatoes are chunkier; simmer a little longer until thick.","k":["crushed tomatoes","tomato paste"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Undercook the penne by 2 minutes as written: it keeps absorbing sauce in the oven and will turn mushy otherwise. Toss with butter so the pieces do not stick together.","s":0},{"t":"Simmer the meat sauce until almost dry. A watery filling seeps into the pasta and the layers will slide apart when cut.","s":2},{"t":"Cook the bechamel until it thickly coats a spoon, and whisk a ladle of it into the beaten eggs before returning them to the pan to prevent scrambling. The eggs let it set into a custardy top.","s":3}],"rl":"BPXXMXFTMTXGZZ"},"3003":{"lv":"EERERRRRRRRREERO","w":{"2":"Peppers are the second classic mahshi vegetable; skip them and fill extra zucchini.","4":"Dill is part of the typical mahshi herb trio and gives it its fresh, slightly sweet scent.","5":"Cilantro adds brightness; extra parsley can replace it.","6":"Adds moisture and sweetness to the rice filling.","7":"Colors and flavors the filling and sauce.","8":"Enriches the rice filling so it does not taste dry.","9":"Earthy warm spice; Egyptian mahshi usually has cumin.","10":"A gentle citrusy spice.","11":"Flavors the cooking liquid.","14":"Needed for both filling and sauce, since rice absorbs a lot.","15":"Optional accent.","13":"Main cooking liquid; skipped, the dish or grain is dry and underdone."},"sub":{"13":{"x":"2 cups water + 1/2 tsp salt","d":"Lighter sauce; the tomato sauce still carries most of the flavor.","k":["water","salt"]}},"tips":[{"t":"The filling should be almost as green as it is white: herbs should be nearly equal to the rice by volume. Use raw, rinsed rice, never cooked; it finishes cooking inside the vegetable.","s":0},{"t":"Only fill about three-quarters full and do not pack it, because the rice swells. Overstuffed vegetables burst while cooking.","s":1},{"t":"Simmer gently, never boil. Rapid bubbling splits the vegetables and spills the filling. A plate on top keeps them submerged; line the bottom of the pot with leftover cores or sliced onion so nothing scorches.","s":4}],"rl":"BVVXXXXXFXXXMMZZ"},"3007":{"lv":"ERRRRROOEOOR","w":{"1":"Squeezing it dry prevents a loose mixture that falls off the skewers while adding sweetness.","2":"Fresh herbal flavor typical of Egyptian kofta.","3":"Garlic gives the mix its savory backbone.","4":"Cumin is the characteristic spice of Egyptian kofta.","5":"Citrusy warmth that rounds out the cumin.","6":"Part of the baharat-style blend; very minor.","7":"A secondary note; skip it.","9":"Mild heat; optional.","10":"Only for brushing the grill; a hot, well-cleaned grate also works.","11":"Serving bread; the kofta is also fine over rice."},"tips":[{"t":"Use 80/20 beef. Lean ground beef gives dry, crumbly kofta. Knead until the mixture turns tacky and pulls away from the bowl; this is what makes it cling to the skewer.","s":0},{"t":"Squeeze the grated onion in a towel until bone dry and chill the mix before shaping. Wet hands make it easier to mold around flat skewers.","s":2},{"t":"Cook to 160 F (71 C) inside and turn only when the kofta releases from the grate; flipping early tears it. Brush the grate, not the meat, to prevent sticking.","s":4}],"rl":"PXXXXXXXZZFB"},"3009":{"lv":"EREEEREEREROR","w":{"1":"Sweet base for the stew; skip it and the sauce is a little sharper.","5":"Adds depth and body to the stew.","8":"Cumin deepens the stew base.","10":"Necessary to season the stew and rice.","11":"Optional accent.","12":"Standard accompaniment; bread is also fine.","6":"Main or only cooking fat; skipped, the food sticks, browns poorly and the dish loses richness.","9":"Main cooking liquid; skipped, the dish or grain is dry and underdone."},"sub":{"6":{"x":"2 tbsp olive oil, ghee or butter","d":"Ghee tastes closest to traditional samna; olive oil is lighter.","k":["olive oil","ghee","butter"]},"9":{"x":"2 cups water + 1 beef bouillon cube","d":"Slightly lighter body; add salt only after tasting.","k":["water","bouillon cube"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Garlic and coriander for the ta'liya must sizzle in hot oil only until golden and fragrant. Browned garlic turns bitter and ruins the okra. Pour it straight into the pot and cover for a minute.","s":5},{"t":"Do not cut or stir okra roughly; keep it whole and trim only the caps. Sauteing first sets the surface so it stays intact and less slimy in the stew.","s":3},{"t":"Finish the beef until nearly tender before adding the okra; okra only needs about 20 minutes and overcooks into mush if added earlier.","s":4}],"rl":"PXVXMXFXXMZZB"},"3014":{"lv":"ERREERROEERO","w":{"1":"Sweet peppers are traditional in Egyptian mesaka'a and add body to the sauce.","2":"Sweet aromatic base.","5":"Concentrates the tomato flavor and thickens the sauce.","6":"Warm spice that anchors the sauce.","7":"Mild color and sweetness; skip it.","10":"Seasons the sauce and also draws moisture from the eggplant.","11":"Optional accent.","8":"Fat for cooking and roasting the eggplant; skipped, it turns dry and tough."},"sub":{"8":{"x":"1/2 cup olive oil or any neutral oil","d":"Olive oil adds a slightly fruity note; you can use less since the eggplant is roasted.","k":["olive oil","vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Salt and rinse or pat the eggplant dry, then roast it on hot trays; roasting instead of frying cuts oil but still needs a generous brush so it browns, not dries out.","s":1},{"t":"Cook garlic only a minute with the spices; it burns quickly and makes the sauce bitter. Egyptian mesaka'a leans on garlic and a touch of vinegar for its tang.","s":2},{"t":"Simmer gently without stirring so the eggplant slices stay whole. Mesaka'a tastes better after resting; serve warm or at room temperature.","s":5}],"rl":"VVXXMXXXFAZZ"},"3015":{"lv":"EERREROEERRRO","w":{"2":"Sweet base for the meat filling.","3":"Garlic rounds out the meat.","5":"Warm spice in the filling.","6":"A small secondary note.","7":"Butter in the phyllo gives richness and crispness; ghee or oil also crisp it.","9":"Enriches the milk soak; use extra milk if you have no cream.","10":"Melts into the filling for a gooey center.","11":"Needed in the filling and milk.","12":"Optional accent.","4":"Sauce base of the filling; skipped, the filling is dry."},"sub":{"4":{"x":"1 cup crushed tomatoes or 3 tbsp tomato paste + 3/4 cup water","d":"Crushed tomatoes are chunkier; simmer a little longer so the filling is not watery.","k":["crushed tomatoes","tomato paste"]},"7":{"x":"1/2 cup melted ghee, or 1/2 cup melted butter mixed with 2 tbsp neutral oil","d":"Ghee crisps best; oil alone lacks flavor.","k":["ghee","butter","vegetable oil"]},"9":{"x":"1/2 cup extra whole milk + 1 tbsp melted butter","d":"Slightly less rich; the phyllo still absorbs it.","k":["whole milk","butter"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Keep the unused phyllo under a damp towel while you assemble; exposed sheets dry out and shatter. Brush butter between every one or two sheets for flaky layers.","s":2},{"t":"Cut the pastry into squares before baking: the milk soaks down through the cuts, and cutting after baking would crush the crisp top.","s":4},{"t":"Cook the meat filling until thick and not wet, and cool it slightly first, so the phyllo does not turn gummy. Pour the milk evenly and bake until absorbed.","s":5}],"rl":"SPXXMXXFMFFZZ"},"3016":{"lv":"ERRREROEERO","w":{"1":"Sweet base that melts into the sauce.","2":"Adds savory depth.","3":"Concentrates tomato flavor and color in the stew.","5":"Earthy Egyptian spice that supports the beef.","6":"A small warming note.","9":"Seasons the stew and potatoes.","10":"Optional accent.","4":"Main or only cooking fat; skipped, the food sticks, browns poorly and the dish loses richness.","7":"Main cooking liquid; skipped, the dish or grain is dry and underdone."},"sub":{"4":{"x":"2 tbsp olive oil, ghee or butter","d":"Ghee tastes richest; olive oil is lighter.","k":["olive oil","ghee","butter"]},"7":{"x":"3 cups water + 1 beef bouillon cube","d":"Slightly thinner flavor; the browning of the beef helps compensate.","k":["water","bouillon cube"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Brown the beef in batches so it sears instead of steaming. The brown crust is the base of the stew's flavor.","s":0},{"t":"Fry the potato chunks first as the recipe suggests: the golden crust helps them keep their shape in the stew instead of dissolving.","s":3},{"t":"Add the potatoes only when the beef is almost tender, and stir as little as possible; waxy potatoes like Yukon Gold hold shape and their starch thickens the sauce.","s":4}],"rl":"PXXXFXXMBZZ"},"3017":{"lv":"EERREERREROR","w":{"2":"Sweet base that melts into the stew.","3":"Adds savory depth.","6":"Coriander is a classic Egyptian pairing with cumin and tomato.","7":"Earthy warmth.","9":"Seasons the stew.","10":"Optional accent.","11":"Standard accompaniment; bread is also fine.","5":"Main or only cooking fat; skipped, the food sticks, browns poorly and the dish loses richness."},"sub":{"5":{"x":"2 tbsp olive oil, ghee or butter","d":"Ghee tastes closest to the traditional samna.","k":["olive oil","ghee","butter"],"wn":"Ghee and butter contain milk."}},"tips":[{"t":"Cook the beans until completely soft, not crisp-tender; Egyptian fasolia is meant to be silky and melting, which is why they stew for the final 20 minutes.","s":3},{"t":"Cut the meat in small cubes and simmer it until tender before adding the beans so everything finishes at the same time.","s":2},{"t":"If the sauce is thin at the end, reduce it uncovered rather than adding starch; the tomato should cling to the beans.","s":4}],"rl":"VPXXMFXXMZZB"},"3018":{"lv":"EEERRRRERERRERO","w":{"3":"Sweet peppers add flavor and color to the medley.","4":"Adds sweetness and a different texture.","5":"Sweet base that melts into the sauce.","6":"Adds savory depth.","8":"Optional; with beef it is heartier, without it the dish is vegetarian and still works.","10":"Warm earthy spice.","11":"Complements the cumin.","13":"Seasons the vegetables.","14":"Optional accent.","9":"Main or only cooking fat; skipped, the food sticks, browns poorly and the dish loses richness."},"sub":{"9":{"x":"3 tbsp olive oil or melted butter","d":"Olive oil adds fruitiness; butter adds richness.","k":["olive oil","butter"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Slice the potatoes and carrots thinner than the zucchini and eggplant. They are the slowest to soften, and thick slices stay hard while the softer vegetables turn to mush.","s":3},{"t":"Seal the dish tightly with foil for the first stage: the trapped steam braises the vegetables in the tomato sauce, and uncovering at the end thickens and browns it.","s":4},{"t":"If you use beef, cut it small and brown it first; raw cubes will not become tender in the short bake.","s":1}],"rl":"BVVVVXXMPFXXMZZ"},"3026":{"lv":"EEERERO","w":{"2":"Gives savory depth; water plus a pinch more salt is acceptable.","3":"Enriches the rice; whole milk plus butter is a good swap.","5":"Seasons the rice; add slightly more if you swap broth for water.","6":"Optional accent."},"sub":{"2":{"x":"1 cup water + 1/4 tsp extra salt","d":"Less savory; the dairy still makes it rich.","k":["water","salt"]},"3":{"x":"1/2 cup whole milk + 1 tbsp extra butter","d":"Slightly lighter than cream but still very creamy.","k":["whole milk","butter"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Rinse the rice but do not soak it; heated liquid poured over it ensures it starts cooking immediately and absorbs the dairy evenly.","s":2},{"t":"Cover tightly with foil or a lid and do not open it while baking: roz mu'ammar steams in its own creamy liquid. Traditionally it is baked in a clay dish with samna for extra richness.","s":4},{"t":"Broil for a few minutes to get the golden skin on top, but watch it, because the milk solids brown quickly.","s":5}],"rl":"BMMFFZZ"},"3305":{"lv":"EEREERROREO","w":{"1":"Tenderizes the chicken and gives a creamy, tangy coating; plain yogurt is just as good.","2":"Tangy marinade acid; do not marinate more than 24 hours, or the surface turns mushy.","5":"Earthy sweetness that complements the cumin.","6":"Color and mild flavor.","7":"Mostly color; skipping it barely changes the flavor.","8":"Helps carry the spices and keeps the meat juicy; any oil works.","10":"Optional accent."},"sub":{"1":{"x":"1 cup plain whole-milk yogurt","d":"A little thinner; strain through a cloth for 20 minutes if very runny.","k":["plain yogurt"]},"2":{"x":"3 tbsp white wine vinegar + 1 tbsp water","d":"Sharper and less fruity than lemon; reduces the marinating time slightly.","k":["white vinegar"]},"8":{"x":"3 tbsp vegetable oil or melted butter","d":"Butter adds richness; oil is neutral.","k":["vegetable oil","butter"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Wipe off most of the marinade before cooking so the yogurt browns and chars instead of burning and sliding off. The chicken must reach at least 165 F (74 C); thighs are best and juicier at 175 F (80 C).","s":3},{"t":"Marinate at least 2 hours but not more than about 24; acid and yogurt break down the surface and give a mushy texture if left too long.","s":1},{"t":"Roast on a rack set over a sheet pan so the hot air crisps the skin on all sides, then broil briefly at the end for the char.","s":4}],"rl":"PMAXXXXCFZZ"},"3308":{"lv":"EEERRRROROO","w":{"4":"Sweet base in the stuffing.","5":"Adds crunch and richness to the freekeh stuffing.","6":"Warm spice that makes the stuffing taste Egyptian.","7":"A small secondary note.","8":"Seasons the hens and stuffing; hens need a good amount of salt inside and out.","9":"Optional accent.","10":"Butter already browns the skin, so this is only a bit of extra lubrication.","2":"Main cooking liquid; skipped, the dish or grain is dry and underdone.","3":"Adds richness and browning; skipped, the hens are slightly less rich."},"sub":{"2":{"x":"2 1/2 cups water + 1 chicken bouillon cube","d":"Slightly less savory stuffing.","k":["water","bouillon cube"]},"3":{"x":"3 tbsp ghee or olive oil","d":"Ghee is closest to samna; olive oil is lighter and the skin browns a bit less.","k":["ghee","olive oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Stuff the hens only right before roasting and fill them loosely, never ahead of time, since a hot or pre-stuffed cavity is a food safety risk. The stuffing must reach 165 F (74 C) as well as the thigh.","s":3},{"t":"Cook the freekeh only until almost tender: it keeps absorbing juices and cooking inside the bird. Fully cooked freekeh would turn mushy.","s":0},{"t":"Pat the skin completely dry and truss the legs for even browning; rest 10 minutes before cutting so the juices redistribute.","s":4}],"rl":"PBMFXGXXZZF"},"3309":{"lv":"EROROEORRR","w":{"1":"Sweetens and flavors the broth.","2":"Subtle herbal background; skip it.","3":"Gives the lamb the characteristic Egyptian floral perfume.","4":"A faint warmth; secondary.","6":"Mild background heat.","7":"Gives richness and browning during the roast.","8":"Roasted garlic cloves become sweet and soft and flavor the pan juices.","9":"Rice for serving; cook it in the strained broth for the best flavor."},"tips":[{"t":"Skim the foam in the first 10 minutes, then keep the broth at a gentle simmer; a hard boil toughens the meat and clouds the broth.","s":1},{"t":"Stop simmering when the meat is tender but still holds together. It finishes in the oven and will fall apart if cooked fully beforehand.","s":1},{"t":"Strain the leftover broth and use it to cook the rice; it is full of lamb flavor. Pat the shanks dry before roasting so they brown instead of steam.","s":4}],"rl":"PXXXXZZFXB"},"3310":{"lv":"EERRRERROROE","w":{"2":"Sweet base for the tomato sauce.","3":"Garlic gives the sauce its savory backbone.","4":"Adds sweetness and a vegetal note; skip it for a plainer tagine.","6":"Concentrates the sauce and deepens its color.","7":"Earthy cumin is the typical Egyptian partner for tomato and potato.","8":"Mostly color and mild sweetness.","9":"Potatoes soak up salt, so season the sauce a bit generously.","10":"Optional accent.","1":"Main or only cooking fat; skipped, the food sticks, browns poorly and the dish loses richness.","11":"Main cooking liquid; skipped, the dish or grain is dry and underdone."},"sub":{"1":{"x":"2 tbsp vegetable oil, ghee or butter","d":"Ghee adds a nutty richness; neutral oil keeps it light.","k":["vegetable oil","ghee","butter"],"wn":"Contains dairy (ghee or butter)"},"11":{"x":"1 cup water + 1/2 tsp extra salt","d":"Slightly less savory; the tomato still carries the flavor.","k":["water","salt"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Slice the potatoes a consistent 1/4 inch thick (a mandoline helps) so they all cook at the same pace; thick slices stay hard while thin ones dissolve.","s":3},{"t":"Submerge the slices under the sauce and cover tightly for the first stage: potatoes exposed above the liquid dry out and stay chalky.","s":4},{"t":"Make the sauce a little overseasoned, because the potatoes absorb a lot of salt. Rest the tagine 10 minutes so the sauce thickens on the starch.","s":5}],"rl":"BFXXVMXXXZZM"},"3312":{"lv":"EERREERERRO","w":{"2":"Sweet base for the stew.","3":"Garlic adds savory depth.","6":"Concentrates the tomato flavor and deepens color.","8":"Allspice is the characteristic flavor of Egyptian peas stew; use a pinch of cinnamon if missing.","9":"Seasons the stew.","10":"Optional accent.","1":"Main or only cooking fat; skipped, the food sticks, browns poorly and the dish loses richness."},"sub":{"1":{"x":"2 tbsp olive oil, ghee or butter","d":"Ghee gives the best flavor; olive oil is lighter.","k":["olive oil","ghee","butter"],"wn":"Contains dairy (ghee or butter)"}},"tips":[{"t":"Simmer the beef until fully tender before adding vegetables; peas and carrots cook quickly and cannot wait for tough meat.","s":1},{"t":"Add the frozen peas only for the last 10 to 12 minutes so they stay green and sweet; long simmering turns them gray and mushy.","s":3},{"t":"Allspice is the signature note of this stew: add it gently since it can overpower the tomato. The sauce should be thick, not soupy; reduce it uncovered if needed.","s":2}],"rl":"PFXXVVXMXZZ"},"3313":{"lv":"EEERRRROROE","w":{"3":"Garlic adds savory depth to the onion sauce.","4":"Grated tomatoes add sweetness and acidity to the onion gravy.","5":"Concentrates tomato flavor and color.","6":"Gives a floral Egyptian note typical of lamb stews.","7":"A faint warmth; secondary.","8":"Seasons the stew and lamb.","9":"Optional accent.","1":"Main or only cooking fat; skipped, the food sticks, browns poorly and the dish loses richness.","10":"Main cooking liquid; skipped, the dish or grain is dry and underdone."},"sub":{"1":{"x":"2 tbsp ghee or vegetable oil","d":"Ghee tastes closest to samna; oil is neutral.","k":["ghee","vegetable oil"]},"10":{"x":"2 cups water + 1 beef bouillon cube","d":"Slightly lighter; reduce the sauce a little longer.","k":["water","bouillon cube"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Brown the lamb in batches in a very hot pot so it sears rather than steams; the fond left behind seasons the onion gravy.","s":0},{"t":"Cook the onions slowly until deep golden (the full 10 minutes) because they become the sauce. Pale onions leave a thin, sharp-tasting stew.","s":1},{"t":"Grate the tomatoes and discard the skins, which would otherwise curl up in the sauce. Skim the fat at the end and rest the stew: lamb shoulder tastes even better the next day.","s":2}],"rl":"PFVXAXXXZZM"},"3314":{"lv":"ERRRROEEE","w":{"1":"Perfumes the poaching water and chicken.","2":"Earthy flavor in both the poaching liquid and the coating.","3":"Gives color and mild sweetness to the crust.","4":"A subtle citrus note in the coating.","5":"A small acid addition to the poaching water; skip it.","8":"Frying medium; without it nothing can be deep or shallow fried and crisped."},"sub":{"8":{"x":"2 cups canola, sunflower or peanut oil","d":"Any neutral oil with a high smoke point works the same.","k":["vegetable oil","canola oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Poach first, then fry: simmering cooks the thighs through and seasons them, so the fry only needs to crisp the crust before the coating burns. Reserve the poaching broth for rice.","s":0},{"t":"Dry the poached chicken thoroughly with towels. Any moisture turns the flour into paste and makes the oil spit.","s":0},{"t":"Keep the oil at about 350 F (175 C) and fry in small batches. Thighs should reach 175 F (80 C) for tender texture; drain on a rack so the underside stays crisp.","s":4}],"rl":"PXXXXAZTF"},"3315":{"lv":"ERREERRROR","w":{"1":"Sweet base for the stew.","2":"Garlic adds savory depth.","5":"Earthy spice that pairs with spinach and tomato.","6":"Citrusy undertone.","7":"Spinach and sauce need salt.","8":"Optional accent.","9":"Brightens the spinach and cuts the richness.","4":"Sauce base of the stew; skipped, the stew lacks sauce."},"sub":{"4":{"x":"3 tbsp tomato paste + 3/4 cup water","d":"A little more concentrated; stir well to dissolve the paste.","k":["tomato paste"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Squeeze the thawed spinach as dry as you can, in handfuls or a towel. Wet spinach waters down the stew and dilutes the tomato and spices.","s":2},{"t":"Break the ground beef into small crumbs while browning and cook until no pink remains (160 F / 71 C) before adding the onion.","s":0},{"t":"Finish with the lemon juice uncovered for the last 5 minutes so the sauce reduces and the bright flavor does not boil off.","s":4}],"rl":"PXXVMXXZZA"},"53028":{"lv":"ERERORRRRROOOORRR","w":{"1":"Citrusy backbone of the shawarma spice blend.","3":"Gives the blend its warm, sweet-perfumed shawarma note; 1 tbsp is generous, so use less if you dislike it.","4":"Adds heat; skip it for a milder version.","5":"Color and sweetness.","6":"Acid that tenderizes and brightens the marinade.","8":"Base of the yoghurt sauce that cools the spiced chicken.","9":"Raw garlic gives the yoghurt sauce its punch; use a clove or two.","10":"Seasons the yoghurt sauce.","11":"A splash that lifts the yoghurt sauce.","12":"Fresh crunch inside the wrap.","13":"Fresh juicy contrast inside the wrap.","14":"Serving bread for wraps; the chicken is also good over rice.","15":"Seasons the marinade; add a pinch to the yogurt sauce too.","16":"A savory backbone for the marinade; skipping it makes the chicken plainer.","7":"Adds richness to the marinade; skipped, the meat is a bit drier."},"sub":{"7":{"x":"3 tbsp vegetable oil or melted butter","d":"Same function; olive oil only adds a little flavor.","k":["vegetable oil","butter"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Use thighs, not breast: the fat and collagen survive high heat and stay juicy. Marinate overnight, then shake off excess marinade so the surface chars rather than burns.","s":2},{"t":"Cook to at least 165 F (74 C) in the thickest part; thighs taste best at around 175 F (80 C). Rest the chicken for 5 minutes under foil.","s":6},{"t":"For restaurant-style edges, slice the rested chicken thin and re-sear it in a very hot pan for 1 minute. Make the yoghurt sauce ahead so the raw garlic mellows.","s":7}],"rl":"PXXXXXAFGXXAGGBZX"},"53031":{"lv":"EREEERERRRERRRO","w":{"1":"Aromatic base for the meat broth.","5":"Deepens the red sauce; a spoonful is enough.","7":"Toasted vermicelli is classic in Egyptian rice, but plain rice works.","9":"Fries the bread and the red sauce; only a few tablespoons are needed, not cups.","11":"Adds earthy warmth to the garlic sauce, bread and meat.","13":"Needed in the broth, rice and sauce.","14":"Optional accent.","2":"Liquid for soaking and moistening the bread and rice; skipped, the dish is dry.","8":"Adds richness; skipped, the dish is slightly less rich.","12":"Garlic vinegar sauce acid; skipped, the sauce is less tangy."},"sub":{"2":{"x":"1 beef or chicken bouillon cube dissolved in the cooking water","d":"Same function; use unsalted water for the rest.","k":["bouillon cube"]},"8":{"x":"1/4 cup ghee or olive oil","d":"Ghee is closest to samna; olive oil is lighter.","k":["ghee","olive oil"]},"12":{"x":"3 tbsp white vinegar","d":"Slightly sharper than wine vinegar; add a bit of water if too harsh.","k":["white vinegar","apple cider vinegar"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Fry the torn pita until crisp and golden before assembling, then moisten it only with the hot meat broth right before serving. Pre-soaked bread turns to paste.","s":0},{"t":"The red sauce is classic daqqa: fry the crushed garlic, then add the tomato and vinegar and let it sizzle. Garlic must not brown, or the sauce tastes bitter.","s":5},{"t":"Simmer the beef until fork-tender (an hour or more) with the onion, skimming foam. Cook the rice in some of the meat broth, with golden vermicelli, so every layer tastes of beef.","s":2}],"rl":"PXMAXXBBFFBXAZZ"},"3012":{"lv":"EEEEEERRRRROE","w":{"6":"Supports the cumin in the onion-fish spice mix.","7":"Adds depth to the rice.","8":"Dusting helps the fish brown and keep its shape in the pan.","9":"A little acid for the marinade; keeps fish bright.","10":"Fish, onions and rice all need salt.","11":"Optional accent.","4":"Fat for frying the onions that color the rice; skipped, the rice is pale and less flavorful."},"sub":{"4":{"x":"1/3 cup olive oil or any neutral oil","d":"Olive oil adds a fruity note; the onion oil is what colors the rice.","k":["olive oil","vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Cook the onions slowly in the oil (20 to 25 minutes) until deep brown but not burnt. Their color tints the rice and their sweetness flavors it, which is what makes it sayadeya.","s":1},{"t":"Use the onion-flavored oil to toast the rice and spices; do not drain it off. Pat the fish dry before dusting with flour so it fries crisp.","s":2},{"t":"Choose a firm white fish and cook it only until it flakes (145 F / 63 C); turn it once, gently, since cod fillets break easily. Serve the fish on top of the rice with the reserved onions.","s":4}],"rl":"PXBMFXXXTAZZF"},"3306":{"lv":"EEERRRRRO","w":{"3":"Seasons the paste and the flesh; whole fish need salt in the cavity.","4":"Acid brightens the fish; do not marinate for long, as the flesh turns firm and pale.","6":"Steams inside the cavity and keeps the fish moist.","7":"Fresh herbal note in the cavity.","8":"A little heat in the marinade; optional.","5":"Binds the paste and keeps the skin from sticking; skipped, fish may stick."},"sub":{"5":{"x":"3 tbsp vegetable oil","d":"Same role: bonds the paste and keeps the skin from sticking.","k":["vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Dry the skin well, then oil the grate or broiler pan generously. Fish skin sticks badly, so do not try to flip until it releases easily.","s":3},{"t":"The diagonal scores let heat reach the thick part and help the paste penetrate; cut through the skin to the bone without going deeper.","s":0},{"t":"Cook until the flesh is opaque and flakes at the bone (145 F / 63 C); a one pound fish needs about 8 to 10 minutes per side under high heat. Marinate only 15 minutes, as the lemon starts to firm the flesh.","s":4}],"rl":"PXXZAFVXX"},"3307":{"lv":"ERERREROE","w":{"1":"Perfumes the fish and the marinade.","3":"Acid that firms and seasons the fish; keep the marinating time to 15 minutes.","4":"Seasons the fish; fry-coated fish needs it in the marinade.","6":"Pairs with the cumin in the crust.","7":"Color and slight sweetness in the crust.","8":"Frying medium; without it nothing can be deep or shallow fried and crisped."},"sub":{"8":{"x":"2 cups canola, sunflower or peanut oil","d":"Any neutral high-smoke-point oil works the same.","k":["vegetable oil","canola oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Pat the fish dry after marinating; wet fish makes the flour clump and the coating slide off in the oil.","s":3},{"t":"Keep the oil at 350 F (175 C) and fry in small batches. If it drops, the fish soaks up oil and turns greasy.","s":2},{"t":"Cod is delicate: turn once with a thin spatula and cook only until it flakes (145 F / 63 C). Serve immediately, since the crust softens within minutes.","s":3}],"rl":"PXXAZTXXF"},"3311":{"lv":"EERRRERRROOR","w":{"2":"Sweet base for the sauce.","3":"Garlic adds savory depth.","4":"Adds sweetness and a vegetal note.","6":"Deepens the tomato flavor and color.","7":"Earthy Egyptian spice that goes well with shrimp.","8":"Color and sweetness.","9":"A kick of heat; optional.","10":"Fresh finish; skip it.","11":"Needed since the sauce is tomato-based.","1":"Main or only cooking fat; skipped, the food sticks, browns poorly and the dish loses richness."},"sub":{"1":{"x":"2 tbsp olive oil or ghee","d":"Olive oil is lighter; ghee is richer.","k":["olive oil","ghee"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Reduce the sauce until jammy before adding the shrimp; a thin sauce waters out in the oven and leaves bland, rubbery shrimp.","s":2},{"t":"Pat the shrimp dry and add them only for the oven stage: 10 to 12 minutes is enough for them to turn pink and opaque (about 145 F / 63 C). Longer makes them tough.","s":4},{"t":"If using frozen shrimp, thaw them completely in the fridge first, since leftover ice waters down the sauce.","s":3}],"rl":"PFXXVVXXXXGZ"},"52963":{"lv":"ERRROEREORO","w":{"1":"Sweet, mellow base for the sauce.","2":"Adds gentle heat; leave it out for a mild shakshuka.","3":"Adds savory depth; skipping it leaves the sauce plainer.","4":"Garnish; the stalks perfume the base and the leaves finish the dish.","6":"Balances the acidity of the tomatoes.","8":"Salty tangy finish; omit for a dairy-free version.","9":"Eggs and sauce both need seasoning; add at the end.","10":"Minor seasoning.","0":"Main or only cooking fat; skipped, the food sticks, browns poorly and the dish loses richness."},"sub":{"0":{"x":"1 tbsp vegetable oil or butter","d":"Butter gives a richer, less fruity base.","k":["vegetable oil","butter"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Cook the tomatoes down until jammy: a watery sauce makes the eggs spread out and cook unevenly. Salt the sauce before adding the eggs, since the recipe lists none, and salt sprinkled on whites after cooking is unpleasant.","s":0},{"t":"Press shallow wells with the back of a spoon, then lower the heat and cover. The steam sets the whites while keeping the yolks runny; start checking at 6 minutes.","s":1},{"t":"Cherry tomatoes burst slowly: press them down with the back of a spoon after a few minutes to release their juices and speed up the sauce.","s":0}],"rl":"FXXXGVWPGZZ"},"53025":{"lv":"EREEEERO","w":{"1":"Fresh herb finish over the beans; skip it if you have none.","6":"Beans need salt at the end; add it to your taste.","7":"A little heat on the side; skip it for a milder bowl."},"tips":[{"t":"Dried fava beans need a long, gentle simmer (often 2 hours or more, longer if the beans are old). Never salt the water early and cook until completely soft: undercooked fava beans are tough and indigestible.","s":-1},{"t":"Stir in the oil, lemon, crushed garlic and cumin at the table so everyone adjusts their own bowl. Crush the beans slightly with a fork so they absorb the dressing.","s":-1},{"t":"If the broth is thin, mash a ladle of the beans against the side of the pot, or add a handful of red lentils at the start as the recipe suggests, for a thicker sauce.","s":-1}],"rl":"PGFAXXZX"},"53026":{"lv":"ERRRREOOERRRRO","w":{"1":"Gives the tamiya its green color and fresh bite.","2":"Garlic is a main seasoning in the mix.","3":"Fresh herbs give it the vivid green interior; more parsley or cilantro can fill in.","4":"Earthy spice characteristic of tamiya.","6":"Adds a touch of heat; skip it for a mild version.","7":"Only dusts the balls to help the crust and prevent sticking.","9":"The fritter mix tastes flat without it; add a little more if your beans are unsalted.","11":"Gives the batter its green, fresh flavor; the fritters are still good without it.","12":"Classic warm note in ta'ameya; fine to skip.","13":"Minor seasoning.","8":"Frying medium; without it nothing can be deep or shallow fried and crisped."},"sub":{"8":{"x":"vegetable or sunflower oil for frying, at least 1 1/2 inches deep","d":"Any neutral oil works.","k":["vegetable oil","sunflower oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Use dried, peeled fava beans soaked overnight, never canned or cooked. They must stay raw so the patties are fluffy and green, not pasty.","s":0},{"t":"No salt is listed, so add about 1 tsp to the mixture. Grind only until the texture resembles coarse couscous; a smooth paste fries up dense.","s":1},{"t":"Fry a test patty first: if it falls apart, add 1 to 2 tbsp flour; if it is too dense, add a splash of water. Keep the oil at 365 F (185 C) and add the baking powder just before frying for a lighter interior.","s":3}],"rl":"PXXXXLXGFZXXXZ"},"53027":{"lv":"EEREEEREEEERROO","w":{"2":"A warm spice for the rice and lentils; add cumin if you have it.","6":"Seasons the rice and onions; the listed amount is just for the onions, so season the lentils and rice too.","7":"Fries the onions and moistens the rice; reuse the onion oil for extra flavor.","11":"Gives the tomato sauce and daqqa their classic koshari flavor; the dish is still good without it.","13":"Minor seasoning.","14":"Traditional in Egyptian koshari rice; browned in oil for a nutty flavor. The rice is fine without it."},"sub":{"7":{"x":"1/2 cup canola or sunflower oil","d":"Any neutral oil works; you can use some of the onion-frying oil for the rice.","k":["vegetable oil","canola oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"This ingredient list omits the tomato sauce and garlic-vinegar daqqa that complete authentic koshari. Make them (see the Dakka recipe) and layer rice-lentils, macaroni, chickpeas, sauce and onions.","s":6},{"t":"They keep darkening after you remove them, so pull them at deep golden.","s":5},{"t":"Par-cook the lentils only until barely tender, since they finish with the rice. Use some of the onion-frying oil to cook the rice for a richer flavor.","s":1}],"rl":"PBXBPGZFMXAXTZB"},"3002":{"lv":"ERRRRROORRE","w":{"1":"Adds moisture and sweetness to the meat mixture.","2":"Fresh vegetal sweetness.","3":"Fresh herbal note typical of hawawshi.","4":"Adds juiciness; deseed it so the bread does not get soggy.","5":"Earthy cumin is the typical hawawshi spice.","6":"Mostly color.","7":"Mild heat.","8":"Seasons the meat.","9":"Brushed on bread for browning; skipped, the bread is paler and drier."},"sub":{"9":{"x":"2 tbsp melted butter or ghee","d":"Butter makes the bread browner and richer.","k":["butter","ghee"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Use 80/20 beef: the fat renders into the bread and makes it crisp and juicy. Lean beef gives dry hawawshi.","s":1},{"t":"Press the meat thin and all the way to the edges of the pita; thick pockets in the center stay raw while the bread burns. Bake until the beef reaches 160 F (71 C).","s":2},{"t":"Deseed and drain the tomato before mixing in; extra liquid makes the bread soggy inside. Rest a few minutes before cutting so the juices settle.","s":4}],"rl":"PXVXVXXZZFB"},"3011":{"lv":"EERREERROROR","w":{"2":"Sweet crunch that balances the rich liver.","3":"Alexandria style is often hot; skip for a mild version.","6":"Coriander pairs with cumin and garlic in the classic spice mix.","7":"Acid cuts the richness of the liver and brightens the pan juices.","8":"Mild color and sweetness; barely noticeable.","9":"Season at the end; salt early can toughen the liver.","10":"Optional accent.","11":"Traditional sandwich bread; also good with rice.","4":"Main or only cooking fat; skipped, the food sticks, browns poorly and the dish loses richness."},"sub":{"4":{"x":"2 tbsp ghee or butter","d":"Gives a richer flavor but burns at very high heat, so use ghee if possible.","k":["ghee","butter","vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Cut the liver into thin even strips and remove all membranes, which are tough. Pat it completely dry so it sears instead of boiling.","s":0},{"t":"Cook over very high heat in a single layer, in two batches if needed; a crowded pan steams the liver and makes it rubbery. Stop as soon as the strips lose their pink, since liver turns tough and grainy when overcooked.","s":2},{"t":"Add the garlic after searing and finish with lemon juice off the main heat. Burnt garlic is bitter and the lemon should sizzle, not boil away.","s":3}],"rl":"PXVXFXXAXZZB"},"53029":{"lv":"EREREEEER","w":{"1":"Sweet aromatic base for the broth.","3":"Seasons the meat and stew; add at the end.","5":"Garlic is the ta'liya that perfumes the mulukhiyah; use the whole listed amount.","8":"The classic sharp finish; vinegar works in a pinch.","6":"Fat for the garlic and onion taqliya; skipped, the soup loses its defining flavor."},"sub":{"6":{"x":"1/2 cup ghee or vegetable oil (use 3 to 4 tbsp for the onion and the rest for the garlic)","d":"Ghee is closest to the traditional samna; olive oil is fruitier.","k":["ghee","vegetable oil","olive oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Never boil mulukhiyah hard or cover it once the leaves are in: it turns dark, stringy and slimy. Heat gently until it just comes to a simmer.","s":4},{"t":"Fry the garlic (and ground coriander if you have it) in oil until it is just golden and fragrant, then pour it sizzling into the pot. Brown garlic is bitter.","s":5},{"t":"Use a rich broth, since the leaves alone are mild. Cook the beef until tender in the pressure cooker or long simmer, and serve with lemon or vinegar on the side.","s":3}],"rl":"VXPZMXFXA"},"3004":{"lv":"EERRERRERREEO","w":{"2":"Sweet aromatic base for the filling.","3":"Adds moisture and acidity so the rice stays tender.","5":"Fresh dill gives the classic Egyptian warak enab fragrance; use extra parsley if you have none.","6":"Adds tomato flavor and color.","8":"Gives the tang that balances the rich oil.","9":"Warm spice that anchors the filling.","12":"Optional accent.","7":"Fat for cooking the stuffed leaves; skipped, the filling is dry."},"sub":{"7":{"x":"1/3 cup vegetable oil","d":"Olive oil gives a fruity, richer taste; neutral oil is plainer.","k":["vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Rinse jarred leaves in hot water to remove the brine, since the filling should be seasoned carefully. Lay leaves shiny side down and roll snugly but not rock-hard; the rice needs room to swell.","s":1},{"t":"Line the pot with torn or broken leaves and set a plate on top of the rolls so they cannot unroll while cooking; cook at a bare simmer, never boil.","s":3},{"t":"Let the rolls cool in the pot so they absorb the last of the liquid. They taste even better the next day at room temperature with lemon wedges.","s":5}],"rl":"BBXVXXXFAXZMZ"},"3013":{"lv":"EEEER","w":{"4":"Seasons the rice; broth already contains salt, so adjust to taste.","2":"Main or only cooking fat; skipped, the food sticks, browns poorly and the dish loses richness.","3":"Main cooking liquid; skipped, the dish or grain is dry and underdone."},"sub":{"2":{"x":"3 tbsp ghee or vegetable oil","d":"Ghee is closest to samna; oil gives a lighter flavor.","k":["ghee","vegetable oil"]},"3":{"x":"3 cups water + 1 chicken bouillon cube","d":"Slightly less rich; check salt before adding more.","k":["water","bouillon cube"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Toast the vermicelli to a deep golden brown, stirring constantly. Pale noodles taste raw, but dark ones turn bitter, and they go from golden to burnt in seconds.","s":1},{"t":"Rinse the rice until the water runs clear and drain well; excess starch makes it sticky. Roz bel shareya should have separate grains.","s":0},{"t":"Do not lift the lid once it is on and rest it for the full 10 minutes off the heat so the steam finishes cooking and evens out the moisture.","s":5}],"rl":"BBFMZ"},"3024":{"lv":"EEREERROREO","w":{"2":"Seasons the dough.","5":"Sweet aromatic in the filling.","6":"Earthy spice typical of Egyptian meat pastries.","7":"A faint warm note in the filling; skip it.","8":"Adds crunch and richness to the beef filling; almonds or walnuts also work.","10":"Optional accent.","1":"Fat for the dough; skipped, the dough is dry and will not be tender.","9":"Frying medium; without it nothing can be deep or shallow fried and crisped."},"sub":{"1":{"x":"1/4 cup melted butter or ghee","d":"Richer and more crumbly dough.","k":["butter","ghee"]},"9":{"x":"2 cups sunflower, canola or vegetable oil","d":"Any neutral frying oil works the same.","k":["vegetable oil","canola oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Cook the beef filling until completely dry, drain off excess fat and cool it before filling; wet or warm filling softens the dough and makes the pastries burst.","s":1},{"t":"Roll the dough thin (about 2 mm) and seal the edges firmly, brushing them lightly with water first. Press out air so they do not puff open in the oil.","s":3},{"t":"Fry at 350 F (175 C) in small batches. Oil that is too cool makes greasy pastries and too hot browns the outside before the dough cooks.","s":4}],"rl":"SFZMPXXXXFZ"},"3025":{"lv":"EEEROERO","w":{"3":"Earthy spice in the garlicky vinegar dressing.","4":"Fresh green garnish.","6":"Seasons the dressing and the eggplant.","7":"Optional accent.","2":"Defining garlic vinegar of the dish; skipped, the eggplant has no tang.","5":"Frying medium; without it nothing can be deep or shallow fried and crisped."},"sub":{"2":{"x":"1/4 cup apple cider vinegar or 1/4 cup lemon juice","d":"Cider vinegar is slightly fruitier; lemon is milder and fresher.","k":["apple cider vinegar","lemon juice"]},"5":{"x":"1 cup vegetable oil for shallow frying","d":"Any neutral oil works; olive oil is also used.","k":["vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Salt the slices and pat them completely dry: this draws out moisture and bitterness and means the eggplant absorbs less oil.","s":0},{"t":"Fry in hot oil and do not crowd the pan; eggplant soaks up oil like a sponge if the oil is not hot enough, so add slices only when it is shimmering.","s":2},{"t":"Pour the garlic-vinegar dressing over the eggplant while it is still warm so it soaks in, and mash the garlic with the salt first for a smoother dressing.","s":4}],"rl":"VXAXGFZZ"},"3297":{"lv":"EEEERR","w":{"4":"Earthy note that rounds out the chilies.","5":"Frying cooks off the raw edge of the paste; skip it for a fresh, sharper sauce that keeps less long.","2":"Acid that preserves and defines the sauce; skipped, flavor and keeping quality suffer."},"sub":{"2":{"x":"1/4 cup apple cider vinegar or lemon juice","d":"Cider vinegar preserves nearly as well; lemon makes it taste fresher but it keeps a shorter time.","k":["apple cider vinegar","lemon juice"]},"5":{"x":"2 tbsp olive oil or any neutral oil","d":"Olive oil adds a bit of fruitiness.","k":["olive oil","vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Wear gloves and ventilate the kitchen: frying chili paste releases irritating fumes. Cover the pan partially to limit splatter.","s":0},{"t":"Fry until the paste darkens and the oil separates around the edges; if it still smells raw and sharp, it needs a few more minutes.","s":2},{"t":"Keep it in a clean, dry jar with a thin film of oil on top, refrigerated and used with a clean spoon, so it keeps for the stated 3 weeks.","s":4}],"rl":"VXKZXF"},"3298":{"lv":"EERRROE","w":{"2":"Rounds out the vinegar's sharpness.","3":"Earthy spice typical in daqqa for koshari.","4":"Draws out the garlic and seasons the sauce.","5":"Optional accent.","1":"Base of the garlic vinegar sauce; skipped, there is no sauce."},"sub":{"1":{"x":"1/2 cup apple cider vinegar","d":"Fruitier and milder than white vinegar.","k":["apple cider vinegar"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Mash the garlic with the salt into a paste first; the salt acts as an abrasive and releases more flavor than chopping does.","s":0},{"t":"Let it stand at least 30 minutes so raw garlic mellows. Do not worry if the garlic turns bluish-green over time, which is a harmless reaction with the acid.","s":3}],"rl":"XAAXZXM"},"3299":{"lv":"EEEEOR","w":{"4":"A little pepper heat; optional.","5":"Seasons the blend; coarse salt keeps it crunchy and dry.","0":"Main component of the duqqa; skipped, it is not duqqa."},"sub":{"0":{"x":"1/2 cup roasted unsalted almonds or peanuts","d":"Slightly less sweet and fragrant than hazelnuts; still excellent.","k":["almonds","peanuts"],"wn":"Peanuts are a major allergen not in the original recipe."}},"tips":[{"t":"Toast each ingredient separately, as they cook at different speeds: the sesame is done in 2 minutes while the nuts need 5. Cool everything completely before grinding or the mixture clumps.","s":2},{"t":"Pulse, do not run the processor continuously, or the nuts release oil and turn into a paste. The finished duqqa should be dry, sandy and coarse.","s":3},{"t":"Use whole seeds and toast them in the pan: pre-ground coriander and cumin lack the aroma. Serve by dipping bread in olive oil, then in duqqa.","s":4}],"rl":"BXXXZZ"},"3300":{"lv":"EEERRRRR","w":{"4":"Adds nutty crunch; almonds are one of the classic nuts in roz bel mekassarat.","5":"Sweet chewy contrast to the salty rice.","6":"A hint of warm spice.","7":"Seasons the rice; adjust to your broth.","1":"Main or only cooking fat; skipped, the food sticks, browns poorly and the dish loses richness.","2":"Main cooking liquid; skipped, the dish or grain is dry and underdone.","3":"Adds richness and crunch; skipped, the rice is plainer."},"sub":{"1":{"x":"3 tbsp ghee or vegetable oil","d":"Ghee is closest to samna; oil is lighter.","k":["ghee","vegetable oil"]},"2":{"x":"3 1/2 cups water + 1 chicken bouillon cube","d":"Slightly less savory.","k":["water","bouillon cube"]},"3":{"x":"1/4 cup extra slivered almonds or chopped cashews","d":"Pine nuts are more buttery; almonds are crunchier.","k":["almonds","cashews"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Toast nuts over medium-low heat and pull them off at golden; pine nuts burn within seconds, and they keep darkening in the hot pan.","s":0},{"t":"Rinse the rice and stir it in butter for a couple of minutes so each grain is coated; this keeps the grains separate and fluffy.","s":1},{"t":"Plump the raisins by soaking them in hot water for 5 minutes before scattering over the rice, and keep the lid on for the 10 minute rest with a towel under it to catch condensation.","s":4}],"rl":"BFMGGVXZ"},"3301":{"lv":"EERRRERRR","w":{"2":"Sharp bite that sweetens as it sits in the juices.","3":"Green pepper adds crunch and its typical Egyptian flavor.","4":"Fresh herb note.","7":"Earthy cumin is typical of Egyptian salata baladi.","8":"Draws out the juices that make the dressing.","6":"Dressing richness; skipped, the salad is drier but still works."},"sub":{"6":{"x":"2 tbsp vegetable oil","d":"Olive oil adds fruitiness; neutral oil keeps it plain.","k":["vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Dice everything to a similar size, around 1/2 inch, and scoop out the watery cucumber seeds so the salad does not become soup.","s":0},{"t":"Dress the salad only 10 minutes before serving. Salt draws out liquid quickly, and the juices are meant to be mopped up with bread.","s":3},{"t":"Use ripe, flavorful tomatoes; a pale winter tomato cannot be rescued by dressing. Soak the onion in cold water for 5 minutes first if it is sharp.","s":0}],"rl":"VVXVXAFXZ"},"3302":{"lv":"EERROR","w":{"2":"Raw garlic gives the sharp bite of the salad; start with one clove if yours is strong.","3":"Dried mint gives the traditional flavor; crush it between your fingers first.","4":"Just a finishing drizzle.","5":"Needed to season the yogurt and draw out cucumber."},"tips":[{"t":"Salt the diced cucumber and drain it for 10 minutes before folding in; otherwise it releases water and thins the yogurt.","s":1},{"t":"Crush the dried mint between your palms to wake up its oils and mash the garlic with the salt so it spreads evenly.","s":0},{"t":"Chill for at least 20 minutes: raw garlic mellows and the flavors blend. Use thick Greek-style yogurt.","s":2}],"rl":"BVXXFZ"},"3303":{"lv":"EOOREE","w":{"1":"Adds color and sweetness; the pickle works fine without it.","3":"Mellows into a sweet, savory note in the brine.","5":"Acid that keeps the brine safely sour while it ferments; do not reduce it. Any swap must be at least 5% acidity vinegar."},"sub":{"5":{"x":"1/2 cup apple cider vinegar","d":"Slightly fruitier and less sharp; the color of the pickle may be a little duller.","k":["apple cider vinegar"]}},"tips":[{"t":"The beet colors the whole jar a bright pink within a day or two; use only a few slices, as too much beet makes the turnips sweet and mushy.","s":0},{"t":"Keep everything fully submerged (weigh it down with a small plate or bag) so nothing grows mold. If you see mold or smell something off instead of tangy, discard the batch.","s":2},{"t":"Use coarse non-iodized salt and a clean jar. Start tasting at day 3 and refrigerate once the turnips are tangy and pink; they are crunchy and keep for about a month.","s":3}],"rl":"VVCXKK"},"3304":{"lv":"ERRREEERE","w":{"1":"Light coating that keeps the florets crisp.","2":"Warm earthy spice in the coating.","3":"Seasons the coating; the sauce also needs a pinch.","7":"Raw garlic gives the tahini sauce its bite; use less if it is strong.","4":"Frying medium; without it nothing can be deep or shallow fried and crisped."},"sub":{"4":{"x":"2 cups canola, sunflower or vegetable oil for frying","d":"Any neutral oil works the same.","k":["vegetable oil","canola oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Blanch only 3 minutes and dry the florets very well. Wet cauliflower makes the oil spit and the coating slide off.","s":0},{"t":"Whisk the tahini with lemon first (it seizes into a thick paste), then add water a tablespoon at a time until smooth and creamy. Stopping early gives a gritty sauce.","s":4},{"t":"Fry at 350 F (175 C) in batches and serve right away, as the coating softens quickly once the cauliflower sits.","s":3}],"rl":"VTXZFFAXM"},"53030":{"lv":"EEEEE","w":{"3":"Provides the flaky layers; ghee is traditional and handles high heat better.","4":"Helps the dough stretch thin and prevents sticking."},"sub":{"3":{"x":"1 cup ghee or clarified butter","d":"More authentic (samna) and less likely to burn; the dough is a little more flaky.","k":["ghee","clarified butter"]},"4":{"x":"1/4 cup melted ghee or vegetable oil","d":"Same function: coats the dough balls and lets them stretch.","k":["vegetable oil","ghee"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Keep the fat warm, not hot, in the soaking bowl. Hot fat starts to cook the dough, which then tears instead of stretching.","s":3},{"t":"Stretch each ball on an oiled counter until you can see through it (a windowpane); thin dough is what creates the many flaky layers, and tears can be patched by folding.","s":6},{"t":"Bake at the highest heat, then turn on the broiler only when the feteer puffs, and watch closely; brushing with butter at the end keeps it soft.","s":13}],"rl":"SMZFF"},"3010":{"lv":"ERRREERERRO","w":{"1":"Sweet savory base; without it the soup tastes thinner and less rounded.","2":"Adds sweetness and a golden tint; skip and add a little more onion.","3":"Savory depth under the cumin; skipping leaves a flatter, earthier soup.","4":"Carries the cumin and turmeric; any neutral oil or butter does the same job.","6":"Earthy warmth and golden color; skipping changes color more than taste.","7":"Adds savory body; water works if you season well and lean on cumin.","8":"Acid at the table cuts the lentils' heaviness; the soup tastes dull without it.","9":"Lentils and mild broth need salt to taste like anything; adjust after blending.","10":"Barely noticeable next to the cumin; skip freely."},"sub":{"4":{"x":"2 tbsp vegetable oil or 2 tbsp butter","d":"Neutral oil is lighter; butter adds a richer, rounder flavor.","k":["vegetable oil","butter"],"wn":"Butter contains milk."},"7":{"x":"6 cups water plus 1 bouillon cube (or 6 cups vegetable broth)","d":"Lighter and less savory; add an extra pinch of cumin and salt.","k":["vegetable broth","water","bouillon cube"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Cook the cumin and turmeric in the oil for the full minute before adding liquid; blooming in fat is what gives Egyptian lentil soup its toasty cumin backbone instead of a raw, dusty one.","s":1},{"t":"Red lentils throw foam as they come to a boil; skim it in the first few minutes for a cleaner taste, and keep the lid ajar so the pot does not boil over.","s":2},{"t":"The soup keeps thickening as it sits, so thin it with hot water, not cold, and re-season. Finish each bowl with lemon at the table; the acid is what makes the lentils taste bright.","s":4}],"rl":"BXVXFXXMAZZ"},"3023":{"lv":"EREERREERERR","w":{"1":"Half sweetens the soup, half becomes the fried topping; skipping loses the sweet-savory contrast.","4":"Peppery green note; skip and use extra parsley, the soup will be slightly milder.","5":"Adds body and a gentle green flavor; without it the soup is a little thinner and paler.","8":"Used for the fried garlic topping and drizzling; vegetable oil or ghee work equally well.","10":"Fava is bland and needs salt; add it after blending so you can judge.","11":"Acid lifts the earthy fava; served at the table so each person can adjust."},"sub":{"8":{"x":"3 tbsp vegetable oil or 3 tbsp ghee","d":"Ghee is traditional in Egyptian homes and richer; plain oil is cleaner.","k":["vegetable oil","ghee"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Use peeled split fava and skim the foam once the pot boils; the beans should be falling apart soft before the greens go in, since the herbs and lettuce only need 10 minutes to stay vivid green.","s":1},{"t":"Blend thoroughly until silky; bessara thickens a lot as it cools and turns pasty, so keep hot water on hand and thin to a thick-cream consistency just before serving.","s":3},{"t":"Fry the onion and garlic until golden but not dark; burnt garlic turns bitter and will overwhelm the mild fava. Pour the cumin-garlic oil over each bowl, the way Egyptian homes serve it with warm baladi bread.","s":4}],"rl":"BXXXXVXXFMZA"},"3289":{"lv":"ERRROEREROR","w":{"1":"Sweetens the broth; skipping leaves it plainer but still chicken-forward.","2":"Adds sweetness; skip and use a few extra slices at the end for the bowl.","3":"Light herbal note typical of simple broths; negligible if omitted.","4":"Faint background note; skip with little loss.","6":"Toasts the vermicelli; ghee or a neutral oil also works.","8":"Salt makes the broth taste like chicken; add gradually after the vermicelli.","9":"Barely noticeable beside the cardamom; skip freely.","10":"Brightens the rich broth and balances the cardamom; skip and it tastes heavier."},"sub":{"6":{"x":"1 tbsp ghee or 1 tbsp vegetable oil","d":"Ghee gives the nuttiest result; oil is neutral. Either browns the noodles well.","k":["ghee","vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Start the chicken in cold water and skim the foam as it first boils; this keeps the Egyptian-style broth clear and clean. Bone-in thighs give far more flavor than breast, and cook to at least 165F (74C), though they are best tender at around 40 minutes.","s":1},{"t":"Toast the vermicelli in butter until deep golden, stirring constantly; it burns in seconds past that point. The nutty toasted noodle is the signature of Egyptian shorbet firakh.","s":3},{"t":"Vermicelli keeps drinking broth as it sits. If making ahead, keep the noodles separate from the soup and add them to the simmering broth just before serving.","s":4}],"rl":"PXVXXXFBZZA"},"3290":{"lv":"EROERRERROO","w":{"1":"Sweetens the broth; skipping leaves a beefier but flatter flavor.","2":"Background aroma only; skip with little loss.","4":"Subtle warmth that pairs with beef and cardamom; skipping slightly flattens the aroma.","5":"Toasts the orzo; ghee or neutral oil is a fine swap.","7":"Gives a rosy color and savory depth; skip and the soup is paler.","8":"Beef broth needs salt to taste full; add after the beef returns so you can judge.","9":"Minimal impact in a cardamom-scented soup; skip.","10":"Fresh color on top only."},"sub":{"5":{"x":"1 tbsp ghee or 1 tbsp vegetable oil","d":"Ghee is nuttier; oil is neutral. Orzo toasts evenly either way.","k":["ghee","vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Use bone-in shank or chuck; the bones and collagen give the broth body and a silky mouthfeel. Keep the pot at a bare simmer, as a hard boil clouds the broth and toughens the meat.","s":1},{"t":"Strain the broth through a fine sieve so you get a clear, amber soup. Fry the tomato paste for 30 seconds with the orzo to cook off its raw edge; this is what gives lisan asfour its pink-gold color.","s":3},{"t":"Pull the orzo while it still has a slight bite; it keeps swelling in the hot broth and turns mushy on the second serving. Thin leftovers with hot water or extra broth.","s":4}],"rl":"PXXXXFBXZZG"},"3291":{"lv":"ERREERRERERROO","w":{"0":"Carries the onion and garlic; any neutral oil or butter works.","1":"Sweet savory base for the broth; skip and the soup tastes thinner.","2":"Adds savory depth; skipping leaves a milder soup.","5":"Adds sweetness and tender chunks; swap with extra potato or carrot if missing.","6":"Adds sweetness and color at the end; skip with little change.","8":"Deepens tomato flavor and color; grated tomatoes alone are enough.","10":"Gives the soup an Egyptian earthy warmth; skip and it tastes more generic.","11":"Vegetable broth is mild, so salt is needed; add gradually.","12":"Minor in this soup; skip freely.","13":"Fresh finish only; the soup works without it.","9":"Main cooking liquid; skipped, the dish or grain is dry and underdone."},"sub":{"0":{"x":"2 tbsp vegetable oil or 2 tbsp butter","d":"Slightly less fruity than olive oil; butter adds richness.","k":["vegetable oil","butter"]},"9":{"x":"6 cups water plus 1 bouillon cube (or chicken broth)","d":"Water makes it lighter; chicken broth is richer but not vegetarian.","k":["water","bouillon cube","chicken broth"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Grate the tomatoes on a box grater and discard the skin; they dissolve into the soup and give a sweet, even tomato base without chunks of peel.","s":2},{"t":"Cut the potatoes and carrots to the same size so they finish together, and hold the zucchini and peas until the last 8 minutes; added early, the zucchini goes to mush and the peas turn drab olive.","s":4},{"t":"Mash a few potato pieces against the pot wall before serving to thicken the broth naturally, and add the parsley off the heat so it stays bright.","s":5}],"rl":"FXXVBVVXXMXZZG"},"3292":{"lv":"ERRERRORRROR","w":{"1":"Sweet base that melts into the broth; skip and use a little more butter.","2":"Browns the chicken and onion; ghee or oil also works.","4":"Gentle sweet warmth; skip and the soup is still fine but less aromatic.","5":"Floral Egyptian aroma; skipping leaves the broth a bit plain.","6":"Background note only; skip with little loss.","7":"Gives color and savory depth to the broth; skip and it will be paler.","8":"Warm baking-spice note that rounds the flavor; skip with mild loss.","9":"Freekeh and chicken need salt to taste complete; add at the end.","10":"Minor against the allspice and cinnamon; skip.","11":"Brightens the rich, smoky grain; skipping makes it taste heavier."},"sub":{"2":{"x":"2 tbsp ghee or 2 tbsp vegetable oil","d":"Ghee is traditional and nutty; oil is neutral.","k":["ghee","vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Rinse the freekeh in a sieve and pick through it for stones or chaff. Cracked freekeh cooks in about 25 minutes; whole-grain freekeh needs much longer, so check your package.","s":4},{"t":"Cook the chicken to at least 165F (74C) in the broth; thighs stay juicy past that and shred easily. Brown lightly in step 1 only for flavor, since the soup is meant to stay pale and aromatic.","s":2},{"t":"Freekeh releases starch and keeps thickening, so thin with hot water. Add the lemon at the very end; a long boil dulls it and the smoky grain needs that brightness.","s":5}],"rl":"PXFBXXXXXZZA"},"3005":{"lv":"EEEERRRRRO","w":{"4":"Rounds out the milk with warm perfume; skip with only a mild loss.","5":"Classic toasted crunch and richness; swap with walnuts or more pistachios.","6":"Traditional green topping, adds crunch and color; skip with little structural change.","7":"Plump bursts of sweetness between the layers; skip or swap dried currants.","8":"Chewy sweetness typical of Om Ali; skip if disliked.","9":"Dusting only, adds warm aroma on the finished top."},"sub":{"5":{"x":"1/2 cup chopped walnuts or 1/2 cup extra pistachios","d":"Walnuts are slightly more bitter; both toast nicely under the cream.","k":["walnut","pistachio"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Bake the puff pastry until deeply golden and dry before breaking it up; underbaked pastry collapses into a gluey paste when the hot milk hits.","s":0},{"t":"Pour the hot milk slowly and evenly; the pieces should be moist and just floating in a little pooled milk, not drowning. The pastry keeps absorbing in the oven, so stop pouring when it looks barely covered.","s":3},{"t":"For the signature blistered top, finish under the broiler for 1 to 2 minutes watching constantly; the sugars in the cream brown fast. Serve right away so the layers stay creamy and crisp on top.","s":5}],"rl":"BMFWXGGVGG"},"3006":{"lv":"EEREEEROEEE","w":{"2":"Adds chew and a sweet toasted flavor; skip and the cake is a bit plainer.","3":"Moistens and gives acid so the baking powder lifts fully; sour cream is the best swap.","4":"Ghee (samna) is the traditional fat for Egyptian basbousa; it gives richer flavor.","6":"Rounds out the buttery flavor; skip with little effect.","7":"Decoration on each diamond, no effect on the batter; skip.","10":"Prevents the syrup from crystallizing and cuts sweetness; do not skip without a swap."},"sub":{"3":{"x":"1 cup sour cream or 1 cup plain regular yogurt","d":"Sour cream is richer and slightly denser; thin regular yogurt works 1:1.","k":["sour cream","yogurt"]},"4":{"x":"1/2 cup melted ghee, or 1/2 cup neutral oil","d":"Ghee is more authentic and nutty; oil gives a moister, less rich crumb.","k":["ghee","vegetable oil"]},"10":{"x":"1 tbsp lime juice or 1/4 tsp cream of tartar","d":"Same anti-crystallizing effect; lime adds a slightly different tang.","k":["lime juice","cream of tartar"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Rest the batter the full 10 minutes (or up to 30); fine semolina needs time to absorb the yogurt and butter, or the cake bakes up gritty instead of tender.","s":1},{"t":"Cut the diamonds before baking and re-score after; syrup will only reach the middle of the cake through those lines. Pour cool syrup over hot cake (or hot over cold) so it absorbs rather than puddling.","s":2},{"t":"Do not stir the syrup once it boils; stirring triggers crystals. If the top is pale at the end, run the pan under the broiler 1 minute for color before syruping.","s":4}],"rl":"SWVMFLXGWMA"},"3008":{"lv":"EREEEROROO","w":{"1":"Softens the rice before the milk goes in, since milk slows starch softening; swap with extra milk.","5":"Rounds the milk with warm sweetness; skip with only a mild loss.","6":"Traditional floral perfume but optional in many homes; skip if you dislike it.","7":"Adds richness and a silkier finish; skipping gives a lighter, more milky pudding.","8":"Garnish for color and crunch; skip freely.","9":"Dusting for aroma only; skip."},"tips":[{"t":"Pre-cook the short-grain rice in water first; milk proteins and sugar slow starch softening, so skipping this gives hard cores in the finished pudding.","s":0},{"t":"Stir often with the heat low and scrape the pot corners; milk scorches fast and a burnt layer taints the whole batch. Add the cornstarch slurry only once the rice is fully soft.","s":1},{"t":"The pudding thickens a lot as it chills. Pull it off the heat while still loose like thin custard, press plastic wrap onto the surface to prevent a skin, and loosen leftovers with a splash of cold milk.","s":4}],"rl":"BMMWTXXFGG"},"3019":{"lv":"EEEEREERREEEE","w":{"1":"Gives the pancakes their slight grainy chew; swap with extra all-purpose flour.","4":"Feeds the yeast and aids browning; only 1 tbsp so skipping is minor.","7":"Plump sweetness against the walnuts; skip or swap with dates or currants.","8":"Warm spice that makes the filling taste classic; skip with a mild loss.","11":"Prevents the syrup from crystallizing; lime juice does the same.","12":"Needs a neutral oil with a high smoke point; any vegetable oil works."},"sub":{"1":{"x":"1/2 cup extra all-purpose flour","d":"Pancakes are a bit softer and less textured; batter may need a splash more water.","k":["flour"]},"11":{"x":"1 tbsp lime juice or 1/4 tsp cream of tartar","d":"Same anti-crystallizing job in the syrup; lime adds a different tang.","k":["lime juice","cream of tartar"]},"12":{"x":"2 cups canola oil, sunflower oil, or other neutral oil","d":"Any neutral frying oil works; avoid olive oil which smokes and tastes strong.","k":["canola oil","vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"The batter should be thin and pourable like crepe batter; if it is thick the pancakes will be heavy and will not form the characteristic bubbled top. Thin with a spoonful of water if needed.","s":0},{"t":"Cook the pancake on one side only and keep finished ones under a damp towel; they dry out and crack when folded if left uncovered.","s":3},{"t":"Pinch the edges completely shut or the filling leaks into the oil, and fry at 350F (175C). Dip into cool syrup right after frying so the hot crisp shell soaks it in without going soggy.","s":5}],"rl":"SSLLWMXXXWMAF"},"3020":{"lv":"EEEEEERRRRR","w":{"1":"Samna (ghee) is traditional and gives kahk its signature sandy tenderness; butter is a good swap.","2":"Toasted sesame oil flavor is typical of kahk; any neutral oil works but loses that character.","3":"Hydrates the yeast; warm water works since fat already provides richness.","6":"Seasons the dough and balances the sweetness; do not skip in a large batch.","7":"Food for the yeast; skip only if using enough milk sugars.","8":"Classic filling; swap with pecans, almonds, or chopped dates.","9":"Warm spice in the filling; skip with minimal loss.","10":"Heavy dusting is the signature look and sweetness of kahk; skip for a less sweet cookie."},"sub":{"1":{"x":"1 cup melted ghee","d":"More traditional and crumbly-tender; butter has water that makes cookies slightly less short.","k":["ghee"]},"2":{"x":"1/4 cup vegetable oil or 1/4 cup extra melted butter","d":"Loses the nutty sesame note; dark Asian sesame oil is stronger, use half.","k":["vegetable oil","butter"]},"3":{"x":"1/2 cup warm water","d":"Slightly less rich and tender; the high fat content makes up for it.","k":["water"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Rub the fat into the flour until it clumps when squeezed in your fist, like wet sand; this coats the flour and is what makes kahk crumbly rather than bready. Rest it 30 minutes so the flour absorbs the fat.","s":0},{"t":"Do not add extra flour if the dough feels soft; it firms up as it rests. The high fat dough is fragile, so handle gently and keep the balls walnut-sized so they bake through.","s":3},{"t":"Bake only until the bottoms just turn golden; kahk should stay pale on top. Cool them fully before dusting or the sugar will melt into a sticky film.","s":4}],"rl":"SFFMLLZWXXG"},"3022":{"lv":"EEERROOO","w":{"3":"Rounds the milk with warm sweetness; skip with only a mild loss.","4":"Gives the drink its floral perfume typical of Egyptian sahlab; stir in off the heat so it does not fade.","5":"Crunchy topping and color; skip freely.","6":"Chewy, sweet topping typical of sahlab cups; skip if not on hand.","7":"Aroma and finish only; skip."},"tips":[{"t":"Dissolve the cornstarch in cold milk completely before it touches heat, and whisk it into the hot milk in a thin stream; adding it to hot milk directly makes lumps that never go away.","s":2},{"t":"The mixture must come to a gentle bubble and cook for a few minutes to reach full thickness and lose the starchy taste; pull it when it coats a spoon, as it thickens more as it cools.","s":3},{"t":"Stir in the rose water off the heat so its aroma is not cooked off. Real sahlab uses orchid root flour; cornstarch is the common stand-in and gives the same pudding-drink texture.","s":4}],"rl":"MTWXXGGG"},"3321":{"lv":"EEERRO","w":{"3":"The floral note that makes this taste like Egyptian mahalabiya; skipping gives a plain milk pudding.","4":"Warms the milk flavor; skip and use more rose water if you prefer.","5":"Topping for crunch and color; skip freely."},"tips":[{"t":"Use a heavy-bottomed pan and keep stirring in a figure eight so the bottom never catches; a scorched layer cannot be hidden in a white pudding.","s":1},{"t":"Cook until it just coats the back of a spoon and the cornstarch has bubbled gently once or twice; undercooked pudding stays loose when chilled, overcooked turns rubbery.","s":2},{"t":"Press plastic wrap right against the surface after pouring to stop a skin forming, and chill the full 3 hours. For a smoother result, pour the cooked pudding through a fine sieve first.","s":4}],"rl":"MWTXXG"},"3322":{"lv":"EEEEEEROERREEE","w":{"3":"Provides moisture and acid that activates the baking powder; sour cream is the best swap.","4":"Ghee (samna) is traditional; it gives a nuttier, richer crumb.","6":"Rounds the buttery flavor; skip with little effect.","7":"Decoration only; skip.","9":"Gives the cream body so it holds when cut; without it the filling is softer.","10":"Sweetens and stabilizes the filling slightly; skip and the cream is less sweet.","12":"Stops the syrup from crystallizing; lime juice or a pinch of cream of tartar works."},"sub":{"3":{"x":"1 cup sour cream or 1 cup plain regular yogurt","d":"Sour cream gives a denser, richer cake; thin regular yogurt works 1:1.","k":["sour cream","yogurt"]},"4":{"x":"1/2 cup melted ghee or 1/2 cup neutral oil","d":"Ghee is more authentic and nutty; oil gives a moister, less rich crumb.","k":["ghee","vegetable oil"]},"12":{"x":"1 tbsp lime juice or 1/4 tsp cream of tartar","d":"Same anti-crystallizing effect; lime adds a slightly different tang.","k":["lime juice","cream of tartar"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Let the batter stand the full 10 minutes so the fine semolina hydrates; this prevents a gritty crumb and helps the cake rise evenly.","s":1},{"t":"Let the cake cool completely before splitting; a warm crumb tears. Chill it 20 minutes, then split with a long serrated knife using a gentle sawing motion.","s":6},{"t":"Whip the cream and cream cheese to stiff peaks and chill the filling; fill just before serving. Once filled, the cake must stay refrigerated since it contains dairy.","s":6}],"rl":"SSWMFLXGFTWWAM"},"3323":{"lv":"EEERO","w":{"3":"Small background perfume in an otherwise buttery cookie; skip with little change.","4":"Decoration on top; skip or use an almond."},"tips":[{"t":"Make sure the melted butter is cooled; warm butter makes the dough greasy and the cookies spread. Chill the dough the full 15 minutes if it feels soft so the balls hold their shape.","s":2},{"t":"Ghorayeba should stay pale; bake at the low 300F (150C) until just set and the bottoms barely colored. Browning makes them hard instead of melt-in-your-mouth.","s":4},{"t":"Leave them on the hot pan to cool; they are too delicate to move warm. Use powdered sugar, not granulated, which makes them sandy rather than smooth.","s":5}],"rl":"FWSXG"},"3324":{"lv":"EEERERERRO","w":{"3":"Lightly sweetens the dough; the date filling carries most of the sweetness.","4":"Binds the dough; a few tablespoons of water also work.","5":"Floral note that tastes like traditional maamoul; skip or use orange blossom water.","7":"Softens the date paste and makes it easier to roll; skip with a stiffer filling.","8":"Warm spice in the filling; skip with minimal loss.","9":"Dusting only; skip freely."},"sub":{"4":{"x":"1/3 cup water or 1/3 cup warm water plus a pinch of sugar","d":"Slightly less rich in flavor but the dough still holds together.","k":["water"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Rest the semolina dough with the butter for the full 30 minutes; semolina must absorb the fat or the cookies turn sandy and fall apart when filled.","s":0},{"t":"Date paste is sticky: oil your hands or work on lightly oiled parchment. If the dates are dry and stiff, a spoonful of warm water blends them smooth.","s":2},{"t":"Keep the shell thin and the cookies pale; bake until just golden on the bottom only. Cool completely before dusting or the sugar dissolves into the warm surface.","s":5}],"rl":"SSFWMXVFXG"},"3325":{"lv":"EEEREREEEREE","w":{"1":"Makes the fried dough lighter and crisper; replace with the same amount of flour for a slightly chewier result.","3":"Feeds the yeast; only 1 tbsp so skipping slows the rise a little.","4":"Adds tang and tenderness to the batter; sour cream or thinned yogurt behave the same way.","5":"Seasons the dough and balances the syrup; a small amount but do not omit entirely.","6":"Needs a neutral frying oil; any vegetable oil works, avoid olive oil.","8":"Stops the syrup from crystallizing; lime juice or a pinch of cream of tartar works.","9":"Perfumes the syrup in the traditional way; skip for a plain sweet syrup."},"sub":{"1":{"x":"1/4 cup extra all-purpose flour","d":"Zalabia are a little less crisp and slightly chewier.","k":["flour"]},"4":{"x":"1/2 cup sour cream or 1/2 cup plain regular yogurt","d":"Same tang; thin regular yogurt with a spoonful of water if the batter is too stiff.","k":["sour cream","yogurt"]},"6":{"x":"3 cups canola, sunflower, or other neutral oil","d":"Any neutral high-smoke-point oil works; olive oil smokes and tastes strong.","k":["canola oil","vegetable oil"]},"8":{"x":"1 tbsp lime juice or 1/4 tsp cream of tartar","d":"Same anti-crystallizing effect in the syrup; lime adds a different tang.","k":["lime juice","cream of tartar"]}},"tips":[{"t":"The batter is meant to be wet and sticky, so water goes in until it falls from a spoon in thick ribbons. Rest it the full 45 minutes until bubbly; underproofed batter fries up dense and greasy.","s":1},{"t":"Keep the oil at 350F (175C) and fry in small batches; too hot and the outside burns before the inside cooks, too cool and the dough soaks up oil. Use two oiled spoons or a wet hand to drop rounds in.","s":4},{"t":"Dip the zalabia in cool syrup the moment they leave the oil, but only for a few seconds; leaving them in turns the crisp shell soggy. Serve within the hour, as they soften as they sit.","s":5}],"rl":"STLWMZFWAXMM"},"3326":{"lv":"EEEEEEROE","w":{"2":"Stops the syrup from crystallizing; lime juice or a pinch of cream of tartar does the same.","6":"Sweetens the cream layer; reduce it if your syrup is generous.","7":"Traditional topping for crunch and color; skip freely."},"sub":{"2":{"x":"1 tbsp lime juice or 1/4 tsp cream of tartar","d":"Same anti-crystallizing effect; lime gives a slightly different tang.","k":["lime juice","cream of tartar"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Toast the bread until completely dry and crisp, not just golden; it has to soak up syrup without dissolving into paste.","s":3},{"t":"Cool the cooked cream fully before layering. Hot cream melts the syrup into the bread and the layers lose their definition. Stir it a few times as it cools or press plastic wrap on the surface to avoid a skin.","s":2},{"t":"Drizzle the syrup rather than pouring; the bread should be damp but still hold its shape. Chill at least 3 hours so the cream sets enough to cut into clean squares.","s":5}],"rl":"BWAMFTWGM"},"3327":{"lv":"EEEEO","w":{"2":"Keeps the caramel from crystallizing and adds chew; corn syrup or maple syrup works similarly.","3":"Acid helps prevent crystallization; lime juice or a splash of vinegar does the same.","4":"Used only to grease the pan and rolling pin; any neutral oil works."},"sub":{"2":{"x":"1/4 cup light corn syrup or 1/4 cup golden syrup or maple syrup","d":"Corn syrup is the most neutral; maple adds its own flavor and darkens the bars.","k":["corn syrup","maple syrup"]},"3":{"x":"1 tbsp lime juice or 1 tsp white vinegar","d":"Same anti-crystallizing effect with a slightly different tang.","k":["lime juice","white vinegar"]},"4":{"x":"1 tbsp vegetable oil","d":"Greases the pan and rolling pin just as well; no flavor impact.","k":["vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Cook the sugar and honey to a deep amber, not dark brown; at about 300F (150C) the mixture sets hard and snappy, but a few degrees past that it turns bitter.","s":2},{"t":"Have the greased sheet and rolling pin ready before you start; once the sesame goes in, the caramel sets in under a minute. Roll between a greased pin and a sheet of parchment and score quickly.","s":4},{"t":"If the mixture hardens before you finish rolling, return the sheet to a 250F (120C) oven for 2 minutes to soften it. Cool completely before storing, as warm bars turn sticky.","s":4}],"rl":"BWWAF"},"3328":{"lv":"EEERRROO","w":{"3":"Plump sweetness against the chewy wheat; skip or swap with currants or chopped dates.","4":"Gives richness and chew typical of Egyptian belila; skip with a plainer result.","5":"Crunch and toasted flavor on top; swap with walnuts or skip.","6":"Traditional color and crunch on top; skip freely.","7":"Finishing aroma only; skip."},"tips":[{"t":"Soak the wheat berries overnight to cut the simmer time substantially, or use a pressure cooker for about 35 to 40 minutes. They should stay slightly chewy, never mushy.","s":0},{"t":"Cook the berries in plain water first and only add the milk and sugar afterward; sugar and dairy slow the softening of the grain and can leave hard centers.","s":1},{"t":"The pudding thickens as the starch releases, so stop when it is a creamy porridge; it sets further when chilled, and a splash of milk loosens leftovers.","s":2}],"rl":"BMWVFGGX"},"3316":{"lv":"ERRRRREOO","w":{"1":"Browns the sausage and cooks the onions; if the sausage is very fatty you can use less.","2":"Sweet softness that balances the spicy sausage; skip and the filling is meatier and sharper.","3":"Adds sweetness and crunch to the filling; skip or swap with green pepper.","4":"Provides the juicy, slightly tangy sauce that binds the filling; skip and the sandwich is dry.","5":"Earthy spice that is a staple in Egyptian street fillings; skip and flavor leans only on the sausage.","7":"Cooling creamy drizzle; optional and the sandwich works without it.","8":"Sausage is already salty, so add only a pinch at the end."},"sub":{"1":{"x":"1 tbsp vegetable oil","d":"Neutral and works the same; use less if the sausage releases lots of fat.","k":["vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Sogo (Egyptian beef sujuk) is cured and fatty, so brown it first in a hot pan and cook the onion and peppers in the rendered fat. If you use raw rather than precooked sausage, cook it to 160F (71C).","s":0},{"t":"Cook the tomatoes down until the juices are jammy; a watery filling makes the pita soggy and leaks out the bottom.","s":2},{"t":"Warm the pita in a dry pan so it is pliable but not crisp, and loosen the tahini with a little lemon juice and water so it drizzles instead of clumping.","s":4}],"rl":"PFVVVXBGZ"},"3317":{"lv":"EERREO","w":{"1":"Provides the melty layer that defines this sandwich; rumi is sharper, provolone is a fine swap.","2":"Fresh acidity cuts the salty meat and rich cheese; skip and the sandwich is heavier.","3":"Crisps the pastrami; the meat already carries fat, so oil works equally well.","5":"Pastrami is already peppery; skip freely."},"sub":{"1":{"x":"4 slices provolone, Swiss or mild cheddar","d":"Melts well; rumi is sharper and saltier, provolone is the closest.","k":["provolone","swiss cheese","cheddar"]},"3":{"x":"1 tbsp vegetable oil or olive oil","d":"Crisps the edges just as well with less dairy flavor.","k":["vegetable oil","olive oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Pastrami is already cured and salty, so add no salt and only fry it briefly until the edges crisp; longer and it turns tough and leathery.","s":0},{"t":"Cover the pan with a lid after laying on the cheese so the trapped steam melts it in a minute without drying the meat. Traditional Egyptian basterma is a spiced air-dried beef cured with a fenugreek paste, so pastrami is a stand-in here.","s":1},{"t":"Add the sliced tomato only when assembling, never in the pan; the heat makes it release juices that soak the bread.","s":3}],"rl":"PFVFBZ"},"53576":{"lv":"EERREOOROOE","w":{"2":"Savory punch that keeps the fritters from tasting like sweet carrot cakes; skip and they are milder.","3":"Light binder; add an extra spoon of breadcrumbs in its place.","5":"Pinch of herbal flavor; the second thyme line is a duplicate so one is plenty.","6":"Garnish-level pinch; skip.","7":"Beans and carrot are bland; a pinch lifts both.","8":"Minor; skip if desired.","9":"Duplicate thyme line; use only once.","10":"Needed to fry; any neutral oil works."},"sub":{"10":{"x":"3 tbsp canola, sunflower, or other neutral oil","d":"Any neutral oil works; olive oil adds a heavier flavor.","k":["vegetable oil","canola oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Drain the canned beans well and pat them dry, and squeeze handfuls of grated carrot in a towel; excess water makes the mixture loose and the fritters fall apart in the pan.","s":1},{"t":"Mash the beans only partly so a few whole pieces remain for texture, and chill the formed fritters 15 minutes so they hold their shape.","s":2},{"t":"Fry just long enough to brown each side over medium heat, then finish in the 200C (400F) oven so the carrot cooks through without burning the crumb. Serve with a sour cream or yogurt dip.","s":3}],"rl":"VPXTTXGZZXF"},"53577":{"lv":"EERRERO","w":{"2":"Adds richness and a mild balance to the sharp sauerkraut and smoked fish.","3":"Gives hearty crunch when toasted and a nutty rye flavor; skip and the salad is lighter.","4":"Base of the dressing; 200 g looks like an error for 4 servings, start with 3 to 4 tbsp.","5":"Balances the sour kraut and smoky fish; skip and the dressing is sharper.","6":"Sauerkraut and smoked fish are already salty; a pinch at most."},"sub":{"4":{"x":"3 to 4 tbsp sunflower, canola, or olive oil","d":"Any mild oil works for the dressing; use far less than 200 g.","k":["vegetable oil","olive oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Check the fish label: ready-to-eat hot-smoked haddock can go straight into the salad, but undyed cold-smoked haddock is often sold raw and needs poaching for 5 to 8 minutes first.","s":2},{"t":"Squeeze the sauerkraut dry before mixing, or the salad turns soggy and sour; a quick rinse tames it if it is very sharp.","s":2},{"t":"Toss the rye toast in at the last moment so it stays crunchy, and boil the eggs 9 to 10 minutes then cool in cold water so the yolks stay golden.","s":0}],"rl":"VPPGFWZ"},"53574":{"lv":"EEREEEREEERO","w":{"2":"Feeds the yeast; skipping slows the rise a little.","6":"The signature Estonian spice in sweet yeast dough; skip and the dough is plainer.","8":"Sweet fresh cheese filling; well-drained ricotta or farmer cheese is the closest swap.","10":"The rhubarb filling needs more sugar than a pinch; add 2 to 3 tbsp to balance the tartness.","11":"Pinch of warmth; skip freely."},"sub":{"8":{"x":"200 g well-drained full-fat ricotta plus 1 tbsp sugar, or farmer cheese","d":"Slightly milder and wetter than quark; drain it in a sieve first so the rolls do not leak.","k":["ricotta","farmer cheese","cottage cheese"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Keep the milk at about 37C (98F); hotter kills the yeast, and cold milk makes a sluggish rise. The 300 ml of flour is a guide, so add it gradually until the dough is soft and stops sticking to the bowl.","s":1},{"t":"Toss the rhubarb with the sugar and let it drain in a sieve for 10 minutes before spooning it on; wet rhubarb floods the dough and makes the rolls soggy.","s":9},{"t":"Cut the roll into slices with unflavored dental floss or a sharp knife to keep them round, tuck the empty edge underneath, and leave space in the pan as they double. Brush with the reserved egg for a deep gloss.","s":10}],"rl":"MLWSFZXSPVWX"},"53575":{"lv":"EEEEREE","w":{"4":"Balances the sweetness and brings out the apple; a pinch is enough.","6":"Provides the acid that activates the baking soda and keeps the crumb tender; use milk plus acid if missing."},"sub":{"6":{"x":"1 cup milk plus 1 tbsp lemon juice or white vinegar, rested 5 minutes (or 1 cup buttermilk)","d":"Gives the acid baking soda needs; the cake is equally tender with slight tang.","k":["milk","lemon juice","buttermilk"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Whip the eggs and sugar to a pale foam for several minutes; this is the main source of lift and gives the cake a light crumb.","s":1},{"t":"Baking soda needs acid, which comes from the sour milk, so do not swap in plain milk without adding lemon or vinegar. If the baked cake tastes soapy, cut the soda to 1/2 tsp next time.","s":3},{"t":"Toss the apple slices in a spoonful of the flour before folding them in, and mix gently just until combined; overmixing makes the cake tough and sinks the fruit.","s":3}],"rl":"VSWSZLM"},"53578":{"lv":"EEREROER","w":{"1":"Brings malty, slightly bitter depth to the gingerbread flavor; milk plus molasses mimics it without alcohol.","2":"Thins the batter slightly; water works.","4":"Sweetens and helps the waffles brown; skip for a lightly sweet waffle.","5":"Pinch; skip.","7":"Makes waffles tender and helps them release; oil works equally well."},"sub":{"1":{"x":"3/4 cup plus 1 tbsp milk plus 1 tbsp molasses or dark honey","d":"No malt bitterness or fizz, so the waffles will be slightly denser and sweeter.","k":["milk","molasses"]},"2":{"x":"50 ml water or 50 ml extra beer","d":"Barely changes the result.","k":["water","milk"]},"7":{"x":"1 tbsp melted vegetable oil","d":"Same tenderness and release; less buttery flavor.","k":["vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"As written the batter is very thin, since 100 ml of flour to 250 ml liquid is closer to crepe batter. Add flour by the tablespoon until it falls from a spoon in a ribbon.","s":2},{"t":"There is no baking powder in this recipe, so lift comes from the whipped egg and beer fizz. Whip the egg and sugar thoroughly and cook the batter soon after mixing, or add 1/2 tsp baking powder for fluffier waffles.","s":0},{"t":"Preheat the waffle iron fully and brush it with butter; the sugar and beer make a sticky batter that can stick. Dark beer brings a roasted note, so choose one that is not bitter.","s":5}],"rl":"SMMSWZXF"},"53579":{"lv":"EEE","w":{"0":"Only sweetener and binder; syrup or molasses replace it with a slightly different flavor."},"sub":{"0":{"x":"50 g (about 2 1/2 tbsp) maple syrup or dark corn syrup","d":"Similar sweetness and binding; honey's floral flavor will be missing.","k":["maple syrup","corn syrup"]}},"tips":[{"t":"These are lean rustic cookies, with no fat, egg or leavener, so expect a dense, crunchy bite rather than a soft chew. They are lightly sweet and good with butter or tea.","s":2},{"t":"Rye flour has little gluten and soaks up water slowly, so let the dough sit the few minutes as written, then add a tablespoon of water if it feels crumbly.","s":1},{"t":"Wet the teaspoon and your fingers to scoop the sticky dough, and press the tops flat; bake until the bottoms are browned, since the cookies crisp as they cool.","s":1}],"rl":"WSM"},"3207":{"lv":"EEEERRRRO","w":{"2":"The sour backbone of adobo; white or rice vinegar work, but avoid balsamic.","4":"Background aroma that adds depth to the braise; skip with little loss.","5":"Whole or cracked pepper is traditional and gives a gentle heat; skip and it is noticeably blander.","6":"Rounds out the vinegar's sharpness and helps the glaze; skip for a drier, sharper sauce.","7":"Browns the chicken; any neutral oil works.","8":"For serving only; the sauce is meant to be spooned over steamed rice."},"sub":{"2":{"x":"1/2 cup white vinegar or 1/2 cup rice vinegar","d":"Cane vinegar is traditional; distilled is sharper, rice vinegar milder.","k":["white vinegar","rice vinegar"]},"7":{"x":"1 tbsp canola or sunflower oil","d":"Neutral oil browns the chicken the same.","k":["vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Pat the marinated chicken completely dry before searing; wet skin steams instead of browning, and the browned fond is half the flavor of the sauce.","s":1},{"t":"After adding the marinade, let it come to a boil uncovered for a couple of minutes before you cover the pot; this softens the raw vinegar edge.","s":3},{"t":"Thighs are done at 165F (74C) but get more tender cooking longer. Reduce the sauce uncovered until it clings to the chicken, and note adobo tastes better the next day.","s":4}],"rl":"PXAXXZWFB"},"53068":{"lv":"RREEEERREORR","w":{"0":"Savory aromatic in the tomato-soy braise; skip and it tastes flatter.","1":"Sweetens the sauce as it cooks down; skip with some loss of body.","5":"For browning the beef; any neutral oil works.","6":"A slice adds the acidity that calamansi usually provides; skip and add a splash of vinegar.","7":"Absorbs the sauce and thickens it slightly; swap with carrots.","9":"Minor note; skip.","10":"Background aroma typical of Filipino braises; skip with little loss.","11":"Soy sauce already seasons the sauce, so add only after tasting."},"sub":{"5":{"x":"3 tbsp vegetable oil","d":"Neutral oil browns the beef the same.","k":["vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"The method text with this recipe describes a grilled beef kebab, not mechado; mechado is a braise, so brown the beef cubes, add onion, garlic, tomato paste, soy, water, lemon and bay, and simmer covered for about 1.5 to 2 hours.","s":-1},{"t":"Brown the beef in batches, not all at once; a crowded pot steams the meat and loses the browned base that deepens the tomato sauce.","s":-1},{"t":"Add the potatoes in the last 25 to 30 minutes so they hold their shape, and cook the tomato paste in the oil for a minute first to remove its raw edge.","s":-1}],"rl":"XXPAMFABXZXZ"},"53069":{"lv":"EEEREERO","w":{"2":"The acid that tenderizes and gives bistek its tang; calamansi is traditional, lime is the closest.","3":"Savory depth in the sauce; skip and flavor is less rounded.","5":"For frying onions and searing the beef; any neutral oil works.","6":"Loosens the sauce for spooning over rice; skip and reduce less.","7":"Soy is salty, so only add a pinch after tasting."},"sub":{"2":{"x":"3 tbsp lime juice or 2 tbsp lemon juice plus 1 tbsp orange juice","d":"Lime is closest to calamansi; orange softens the sharpness.","k":["lime","lemon","orange juice"]},"5":{"x":"4 tbsp vegetable oil","d":"Neutral oil works the same.","k":["vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Slice the beef thin against the grain; thick or with-the-grain slices turn chewy in the quick simmer. Marinate 1 to 4 hours, since overnight in soy and lemon turns the surface mushy.","s":0},{"t":"Sear the beef for only about a minute per side in a very hot pan, then take it out; it finishes in the sauce, and overcooking makes thin steak tough.","s":2},{"t":"Fry half the onion rings separately and keep them crisp for the top, adding raw onion to the sauce for sweetness; they are the signature finish of bistek.","s":6}],"rl":"PXAXVFMZ"},"53070":{"lv":"EERERRRRERRRREO","w":{"1":"Adds beefy depth to the sauce; a bouillon cube or beef stock in place of water both work.","2":"Adds salt and savory depth; skip and add salt to taste.","4":"Adds sweetness and color; the recipe lists two lines, so use one red and one green.","5":"Duplicate line of bell pepper; use one red and one green for two total.","6":"Soaks up the sauce and thickens it; swap with more carrots.","7":"Sweetness that balances the chili and tomato; skip with some loss.","9":"Thickens and enriches the sauce in place of liver spread; skip for a thinner sauce.","10":"The quantity is unclear; start with 1 to 2 tsp chili powder or 2 fresh chilies and add heat to taste.","11":"Sweet aromatic base; skip and the sauce is flatter.","12":"Savory base typical of Filipino stews; skip with less depth.","13":"For sauteing; any neutral oil works.","14":"Traditional richness; skip it if you cannot find it."},"sub":{"1":{"x":"1 beef bouillon cube or 1 cup beef stock replacing 1 cup water","d":"Same savory depth; stock gives a rounder flavor.","k":["beef bouillon","beef broth"]},"13":{"x":"3 tbsp vegetable oil","d":"Neutral oil works the same.","k":["vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Cut the beef in 1.5 inch cubes and brown it well before adding liquid; the browned fond gives caldereta its deep sauce. Pressure cook 30 minutes, or simmer covered for 1.5 to 2 hours if you lack a pressure cooker.","s":3},{"t":"Brown the potato and carrot separately in oil, as the method says; they hold their shape and add a caramelized sweetness against the chili and tomato.","s":4},{"t":"Add the bell peppers at the very end so they stay crisp and colorful.","s":6}],"rl":"PMXMVVBVATXXXFT"},"53071":{"lv":"EEEEERRRRRRERRRR","w":{"1":"Adds beefy depth to the braising liquid; a bouillon cube or stock works.","5":"Provides tang in the marinade and sauce; cider or plain white vinegar works.","6":"Crushed peppercorn is a signature heat in asado; 2 tbsp is bold, reduce to 1 tbsp if you like.","7":"Background herb aroma typical of Filipino braises; skip with little loss.","8":"Citrus acid brightens the marinade; a squeeze of lime works the same.","9":"Adds color and tang to the marinade; skip with little change.","10":"Swirled in at the end for a glossy, rich sauce; skip and the sauce is less silky.","11":"Mostly for frying the potatoes and carrots; any neutral oil works, use less if you prefer.","12":"Sweet aromatic base for the sauce; skip with some loss of depth.","13":"Savory backbone of the sauce; skipping leaves it flatter.","14":"Fried and added at the end for body; skip for a pure beef version.","15":"Fried and added at the end for sweetness and color; fine to skip."},"sub":{"1":{"x":"1 beef bouillon cube or 1 cup beef stock replacing 1 cup water","d":"Same savory depth; stock is rounder.","k":["beef bouillon","beef broth"]},"5":{"x":"1 tbsp apple cider vinegar or 1 tbsp white vinegar","d":"Slightly fruitier or sharper but works the same.","k":["apple cider vinegar","white vinegar"]},"11":{"x":"1/2 cup vegetable oil (or 1/4 cup, shallow-frying in batches)","d":"Neutral oil fries the potatoes the same.","k":["vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Simmer the beef on low until a fork slides in easily, about 1.5 hours for stew cuts; if you boil it hard the meat toughens before the collagen breaks down.","s":2},{"t":"Whisk the butter into the reduced sauce off a hard boil so it emulsifies into a glossy finish rather than separating, and reduce until the sauce coats the back of a spoon.","s":7}],"rl":"PMAMXAZXAMFFXXBV"},"53072":{"lv":"EEREROE","w":{"2":"Toasty crunch in the coating and nutty flavor; skip and use all breadcrumbs.","4":"Used to draw out moisture from the eggplant and season the egg; do not skip the salting.","5":"Minor; skip freely.","6":"Needed for frying; any neutral oil works."},"sub":{"6":{"x":"about 1 cup canola, sunflower, or other neutral oil","d":"Any neutral oil for shallow frying; olive oil smokes and tastes strong.","k":["vegetable oil","canola oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Salt the slices for the full 30 minutes, rinse, then squeeze and pat them very dry; any water left on the surface makes the coating slide off and the slices greasy.","s":1},{"t":"Use one hand for the egg and the other for the crumbs to avoid clumpy fingers, and press the crumbs on firmly so they stay put in the hot oil.","s":4},{"t":"Fry in batches in oil at about 350F (175C) and drain on a wire rack instead of paper towels; steam trapped under paper softens the crust. Serve right away.","s":4}],"rl":"VBXTZZF"},"53073":{"lv":"ERRREEREER","w":{"1":"Balances the vinegar and helps draw out moisture from the eggplant; skip for a sharper sauce.","2":"Draws water out of the eggplant so it browns instead of steaming; do not skip the salting step.","3":"Traditional adobo heat; skip with a milder dish.","5":"For frying garlic and eggplant; any neutral oil works.","6":"Adds savory richness; skip for a vegetarian dish with a bit less depth.","7":"The tang of adobo; white or cane vinegar also work.","9":"Background aroma typical of adobo; skip with little loss."},"sub":{"5":{"x":"3 tbsp vegetable oil","d":"Neutral oil works the same.","k":["vegetable oil"]},"7":{"x":"3 tbsp white vinegar or 3 tbsp apple cider vinegar","d":"Slightly sharper than rice vinegar; reduce by half a teaspoon if too tangy.","k":["white vinegar","apple cider vinegar"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Salt and sugar the eggplant and blot it dry before cooking; it collapses less and absorbs far less oil, so it browns instead of soaking up grease.","s":3},{"t":"Fry the sliced garlic until just pale gold and pull it out immediately; it keeps cooking in the hot oil and turns bitter if it goes brown.","s":1},{"t":"Brown the eggplant in batches so the pot is not crowded, and simmer the vinegar uncovered for a couple of minutes to cook off its harsh edge before adding the eggplant back.","s":4}],"rl":"VWZZXFPAXX"},"53074":{"lv":"EERROR","w":{"2":"Acid balances the rich coconut; lime juice is a straight swap.","3":"Eggplant and coconut both taste flat without salt.","4":"Gentle heat; skip for a mild dish.","5":"Fresh sharp crunch on top, adds contrast to the soft eggplant; skip freely."},"sub":{"2":{"x":"1 tbsp lime juice or 1 tbsp white vinegar","d":"Lime is brighter; vinegar is sharper, use a little less.","k":["lime juice","white vinegar"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Char the skin until it is black and the flesh is collapsed; underdone eggplant stays bitter and spongy. A gas flame or hot grill both work.","s":1},{"t":"Instead of soaking the charred eggplant in water, which dilutes the smoky flavor, steam them in a covered bowl for 10 minutes so the skin peels away cleanly, then blot dry.","s":2},{"t":"Stir the coconut milk well before opening the can so the cream and water recombine, and spoon the cold dressing over the warm eggplant just before serving.","s":3}],"rl":"VMAZXG"},"53075":{"lv":"EERE","w":{"2":"Seasons the egg coating so the bland eggplant tastes of something; skip and salt at the table.","3":"Main or only cooking fat; skipped, the food sticks, browns poorly and the dish loses richness."},"sub":{"3":{"x":"1 to 2 tbsp vegetable oil","d":"Any neutral oil works. 4 tsp is tight for four omelets, so use a bit more to keep them from sticking.","k":["vegetable oil","olive oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Leave the stem on while charring; it works as a handle for peeling and for laying the eggplant flat. Peel the skin off dry rather than rinsing, so the smoky flesh does not get waterlogged.","s":1},{"t":"Press the flesh flat with a fork, fanning it out from the stem like a paddle, and let it soak for a few seconds in the egg so it picks up a good coating before it hits the pan.","s":4},{"t":"Fry on medium heat so the egg sets before the eggplant scorches, and flip with a wide spatula in one smooth move. Serve with rice and banana ketchup or tomato-onion relish.","s":7}],"rl":"VPZF"},"3208":{"lv":"ERRRREROREEO","w":{"1":"Adds sweetness and moisture to the filling; skipping makes it denser and meatier.","2":"Sweet aromatic note; skip with some loss of flavor depth.","3":"Savory backbone of the filling; skipping leaves it flatter.","4":"Fresh onion bite and color; skip and add a bit more onion.","6":"Salty umami boost; skip and increase the salt slightly.","7":"Minor warmth; skip freely.","8":"Seasons the pork inside the wrapper; do not skip as it cannot be adjusted after frying.","10":"Needs a neutral oil with a high smoke point for frying; any vegetable oil works.","11":"Dipping extra; ketchup, banana ketchup or any sweet-sour sauce is fine."},"sub":{"10":{"x":"2 cups canola, sunflower, or other neutral oil","d":"Any neutral frying oil works; avoid olive oil.","k":["vegetable oil","canola oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Fry a spoonful of the filling in a small pan and taste it before rolling; once sealed in a wrapper you cannot fix the seasoning. Ground pork must reach 160F (71C), so cook the first lumpia a little longer than you think.","s":0},{"t":"Roll thin and tight, about as thick as a finger; fat rolls stay raw in the middle by the time the wrapper is brown. Keep unused wrappers under a damp towel so they do not dry and crack.","s":1},{"t":"Fry at 350F (175C) in small batches; cold oil makes greasy lumpia, and a crowded pan drops the temperature. Uncooked rolls freeze well and can be fried straight from the freezer for an extra minute.","s":4}],"rl":"PVXXXTXZZSFG"},"3267":{"lv":"EEEEEEER","w":{"0":"Only dissolves the yeast; warm water works, but milk gives a slightly softer crumb.","7":"Gives the loaf its deep golden gloss; skip and it bakes up paler and matte."},"sub":{"0":{"x":"1/4 cup warm water (about 105F / 40C)","d":"Slightly less tender crumb and paler crust, but the rich dough tolerates it.","k":["water"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Develop the gluten fully before the butter goes in; add it a few cubes at a time and expect the dough to look broken and greasy before it comes back together smooth and glossy.","s":2},{"t":"The overnight chill is not optional; the cold dough is stiff enough to shape, and the slow rise adds buttery flavor. Shape straight from the fridge.","s":3},{"t":"Brioche browns fast because of the eggs and sugar. If the top is dark at 20 minutes, tent with foil; it is done when the center reads about 190F (88C).","s":6}],"rl":"MLWSZSFG"},"3272":{"lv":"EEEEE","w":{"0":"Bread flour gives chew and a high rise; all-purpose works with a slightly softer crumb."},"sub":{"0":{"x":"2 cups all-purpose flour","d":"Slightly lower protein so the loaf is a bit softer and flatter; add a spoon less water.","k":["flour"]}},"tips":[{"t":"The dough should be wet and shaggy, as the long rest does the gluten work that kneading would.","s":0},{"t":"Judge the first rise by the dough, not the clock: the surface should be bubbly and the dough more than doubled and jiggly. Use a floured surface and fold gently so you keep those bubbles.","s":1},{"t":"Bake covered for the first 30 minutes; the trapped steam keeps the crust soft so the loaf can expand, then uncover to brown. Cool a full hour, since the crumb is still setting inside.","s":5}],"rl":"SSZLM"},"3628":{"lv":"EEEEEEER","w":{"7":"Gives the croissants their lacquered golden shine; skip and they bake up pale and dull."},"tips":[{"t":"The butter block and the dough must have the same firmness: cold but bendy. If the butter is harder than the dough it shatters into chunks, and if softer it leaks out and the layers merge.","s":2},{"t":"Chill between every fold; if the butter starts to soften or shine, stop and refrigerate 20 minutes. Keep edges square and brush off excess flour so the layers stay distinct.","s":3},{"t":"Proof in a cool spot around 75F (24C), not a warm oven; hot proofing melts the butter out and leaves greasy, flat croissants. They are ready when they jiggle like gelatin and the layers show.","s":5}],"rl":"SWZLMFSG"},"3629":{"lv":"EEEE","w":{"0":"Bread flour gives the chew and open crumb; all-purpose works with a slightly softer loaf."},"sub":{"0":{"x":"4 cups all-purpose flour","d":"Less chewy and the crumb is a bit tighter; reduce the water by a spoonful.","k":["flour"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Steam is what gives baguettes their thin, crackly crust. Have a hot pan on the bottom rack and pour in the water just as you close the door, then stay clear of the steam.","s":5},{"t":"Slash with a sharp blade at a shallow angle, overlapping each cut by a third; deep or straight-down cuts tear the dough. A proofed loaf springs back slowly when poked.","s":4},{"t":"Bake until the crust is a deep golden, not pale tan, and listen for a faint crackle as it cools; thin baguettes go stale in hours, so eat them the same day.","s":5}],"rl":"SZLM"},"3511":{"lv":"EEEERROOO","w":{"3":"Makes crepes tender and helps them release; neutral oil works, but butter gives better flavor.","4":"Light sweetness and browning; skip for savory crepes.","5":"Brings out the sweet and buttery flavors; do not skip.","6":"Soft background aroma; skip with little loss.","7":"Classic acidic finish with sugar; skip if filling otherwise.","8":"Sweet finishing sprinkle with lemon; skip for savory fillings."},"sub":{"3":{"x":"2 tbsp melted vegetable oil, and brush the pan with butter or oil","d":"Slightly less buttery taste but just as tender.","k":["vegetable oil","butter"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Rest the batter the full 30 minutes; it lets the flour absorb the milk and the air bubbles settle, which makes crepes tender and keeps them from tearing.","s":1},{"t":"Treat the first crepe as a test: if it tears, the batter is too thick (thin with a splash of milk); if it is pale and rubbery, the pan is too cool. It should be as thin as heavy cream.","s":3},{"t":"Swirl immediately as the batter hits the pan, flip when the edges lift and the underside is golden, and stack with the pretty side down. Crepes freeze well with parchment between them.","s":4}],"rl":"SSMFWZXGG"},"3130":{"lv":"EERERRRRER","w":{"2":"Seasons the steak so it browns well; salt first, then press on the pepper.","3":"For searing the steak; any neutral oil with a high smoke point works.","4":"Rounds out the sauce and adds flavor; use all or a little less.","5":"Sweet aromatic base for the sauce; skip and use a little finely chopped onion.","6":"Provides depth and flame; use extra broth and a teaspoon of vinegar if you do not want alcohol.","7":"Base of the pan sauce; chicken stock works too.","9":"Adds tang that cuts the cream; skip with a milder sauce."},"sub":{"3":{"x":"1 tbsp canola or sunflower oil","d":"Neutral oil with a high smoke point; olive oil can burn at this heat.","k":["vegetable oil","canola oil"]},"6":{"x":"1/4 cup extra beef broth plus 1 tsp red wine vinegar","d":"No alcohol or flame; sauce is less complex but still savory and tangy.","k":["beef broth","red wine vinegar"]},"7":{"x":"1/2 cup chicken broth","d":"Slightly lighter flavor; beef broth is deeper.","k":["chicken broth","beef broth"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Crush the peppercorns coarsely with the bottom of a heavy pan rather than grinding; fine pepper burns in the hot pan and tastes bitter, while cracked pieces toast into a fragrant crust.","s":0},{"t":"Take the pan off the heat before adding the cognac and keep the lid and a hood fan in mind; the alcohol can flare. Return to heat and cook until the alcohol smell is gone and the liquid reduces by half.","s":3},{"t":"Tent the steaks and add any resting juices to the sauce at the end. Simmer the cream only until it coats a spoon; a hard boil can break it.","s":4}],"rl":"PXZFFXXMFX"},"3132":{"lv":"EEERORRRO","w":{"3":"Seasons the steak and the fries; use the coarse salt right after frying so it sticks.","4":"Minor seasoning for the steak crust; skip freely.","5":"The base of the herb butter that melts over the steak; skipping loses the signature finish.","6":"Fresh flavor in the herb butter; skip and it is plain garlic butter.","7":"Savory sharpness in the herb butter; skip with mild loss.","8":"A bright spark that cuts the richness of the butter; skip freely.","1":"Frying medium; without it nothing can be deep or shallow fried and crisped."},"sub":{"1":{"x":"4 cups canola, sunflower, or peanut oil","d":"Any neutral high-smoke-point oil; olive oil is too strong and smokes.","k":["vegetable oil","canola oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Double-fry the potatoes: blanch at 325F (160C) until tender but pale, then fry again at 375F (190C) for crispness. The first fry cooks the inside and the second dehydrates the surface.","s":3},{"t":"Dry the soaked potatoes thoroughly; water makes the oil spit and keeps them from crisping. Never fill the pot more than halfway with oil.","s":1},{"t":"Rest the steak 5 minutes, slice against the grain, and put the herb butter on while hot so it melts into a sauce. Salt the fries the second they leave the oil.","s":4}],"rl":"BFPZZFXXA"},"3239":{"lv":"EEEREERORORR","w":{"3":"First layer of the breading that helps the egg cling; skipping lets the crumbs slide off.","6":"Browns the crumb and adds savory flavor; skip or use more panko.","7":"Light savory note in the crust; skip freely.","8":"Seasons the chicken; do not skip since the stuffing is mild.","9":"Minor; skip.","10":"Browns and flavors the crumb in the oven; without it the panko stays pale.","11":"Tangy glue that also seasons the inside; skip with mildly less flavor."},"tips":[{"t":"Pound the breasts to an even 1/4 inch and roll tightly; uneven thickness means the thin end is dry by the time the thick end reaches 165F (74C).","s":0},{"t":"Chill the rolled and secured chicken 20 to 30 minutes before breading so the roll holds and the cheese does not leak out in the oven.","s":1},{"t":"For a better crust, toss the panko in 1 tbsp of the melted butter and toast it in a dry pan until golden first, then bake the chicken on a rack so the bottom crisps too. Always check 165F (74C) in the center.","s":3}],"rl":"PPFTTTXXZZFX"},"3514":{"lv":"ERRRRRERREEEERRORO","w":{"1":"Sweet clove-studded aromatic that flavors the broth; skip and the broth tastes plainer.","2":"Adds sweetness to the broth; discarded later.","3":"Mild onion-sweet note; skip with little loss.","4":"Classic background aromatics for the poaching liquid; skip with little loss.","5":"Classic background aromatics for the poaching liquid; skip with little loss.","6":"Poaching liquid and sauce base; water with a bouillon cube is acceptable, veal stock is best.","7":"Earthy garnish cooked separately to keep the sauce white; skip with a plainer stew.","8":"Traditional sweet garnish; skip or use a few small shallots.","13":"Brightens the rich cream and yolk sauce; skipping makes it taste heavy.","14":"A pinch that is the classic finish for blanquette; skip with a small loss.","15":"Serving starch; any rice, noodles or potatoes work.","16":"Veal and sauce need salt; add in the end after tasting.","17":"White pepper is traditional; a pinch is enough."},"sub":{"6":{"x":"4 cups water plus 1 chicken bouillon cube","d":"Lighter and less rich; add an extra teaspoon of butter to the sauce.","k":["water","bouillon cube","chicken broth"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Skim the foam diligently during the first 10 minutes and keep the poach at a bare simmer; boiling clouds the broth and the sauce loses the pale ivory color that blanquette is known for.","s":0},{"t":"Cook the mushrooms and onions separately, as in the method, so they do not dull or stain the sauce. Keep the roux blond; do not let it color.","s":4},{"t":"Finish with the yolk and cream liaison off the heat and then warm gently without boiling; a boil scrambles the yolks and breaks the sauce. Heat until it reaches about 160F (71C) to cook the yolks safely but stay below a simmer.","s":6}],"rl":"PXVVXXMVVFTTFAXBZZ"},"52815":{"lv":"ERRRERORR","w":{"1":"Sweet savory base for the lentils; skip and they taste thinner.","2":"Savory depth; skipping leaves a flatter flavor.","3":"Adds sweetness and color; skip with minor loss.","5":"Earthy herb that suits lentils; skip and add more garlic.","6":"Faint background aroma; skip.","7":"Salt flavors the lentils as they swell; undersalted lentils taste flat.","8":"Aromatic base of the soffritto; skip with mild loss.","0":"Main or only cooking fat; skipped, the food sticks, browns poorly and the dish loses richness."},"sub":{"0":{"x":"3 tbsp vegetable oil or 3 tbsp butter","d":"Slightly less fruity; butter gives a richer finish.","k":["vegetable oil","butter"],"wn":"Contains dairy"}},"tips":[{"t":"French (Puy-style) lentils hold their shape and need no soaking, so keep the simmer gentle; a hard boil splits the skins and turns them to mush.","s":1},{"t":"Add the garlic for the last minute of sauteing the vegetables, not at the start, to avoid scorching it.","s":0},{"t":"Finish with a splash of red wine vinegar and a drizzle of olive oil once cooked; the acid lifts the earthy lentils. Use stock for part of the water if you want a deeper dish.","s":2}],"rl":"FXXVPXXZV"},"52832":{"lv":"ERREEERRERRRORORRR","w":{"0":"For browning the chicken; any neutral oil works.","1":"Adds smoky salty depth to the sauce; skipping makes the dish less rounded.","2":"Sweet braised garnish traditional in coq au vin; swap with pearl onions or small onions.","5":"Breast dries out in a long braise; extra thighs are more forgiving.","6":"Aromatic depth under the wine; skip with some loss.","7":"Deglazes the pan with a little extra flavor; extra broth plus vinegar is a fair swap.","9":"Adds body to the braising liquid; water also works.","10":"Adds color and savory depth to the sauce; skip with little loss.","11":"Classic herb partner of red wine; skip and add more bay.","12":"Faint pine note; skip freely.","13":"Traditional bouquet garni element; skip with little loss.","14":"Fresh finish only; skip.","15":"Earthy classic garnish, cooked separately; skip and the dish is less authentic.","16":"Used with butter to thicken the sauce at the end; skip and reduce the sauce further instead.","17":"Part of the flour-butter thickener; skip and reduce the sauce further instead."},"sub":{"0":{"x":"1 1/2 tbsp vegetable oil or butter","d":"Neutral oil browns similarly; butter adds richness but burns faster.","k":["vegetable oil","butter"]},"5":{"x":"3 more chicken thighs (about 450 g)","d":"More forgiving and stays juicy in a long braise.","k":["chicken thighs"]},"7":{"x":"3 tbsp extra chicken broth plus 1 tsp red wine vinegar","d":"No alcohol; slightly less depth but the sauce is still savory.","k":["chicken broth","red wine vinegar"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Brown the chicken in batches without crowding and get the skin deep golden; the browned fond left in the pan is the base of the sauce, so scrape it up when you deglaze.","s":1},{"t":"Cook the breast pieces for only the last 20 to 25 minutes of braising so they stay moist; legs and thighs can go the full time. All chicken must reach 165F (74C).","s":3},{"t":"Thicken with the flour and butter paste (beurre manie), whisked in a little at a time at a gentle simmer, and strain or reduce further if the sauce is thin. The dish improves if made a day ahead.","s":5}],"rl":"FXVPPPXXMMXXXXGVTT"},"52847":{"lv":"EERRRRRERROEROOO","w":{"0":"Goose fat gives a deep savory flavor; olive oil or lard work, butter will brown faster.","2":"Sweet base for the stew; skip with a flatter flavor.","3":"Ten cloves melt into sweetness in the long braise; fewer makes it less aromatic.","4":"Sweetness and color; skip with minor loss.","5":"Gives the classic Mediterranean aniseed hint; skip with a plainer flavor.","6":"Cuts the richness and deglazes the pan; white wine vinegar or lemon juice also work.","7":"Braising liquid; chicken broth or water plus a bouillon cube works.","8":"Adds color and savory depth; skip with little loss.","9":"Piney note that suits pork and beans; skip and use more parsley.","10":"Fresh finish only; skip.","12":"Forms the crisp golden crust on top; skip and the surface stays soft.","13":"Helps the crumb brown; skip.","14":"Serving extra; skip.","15":"Serving extra; skip."},"sub":{"0":{"x":"4 tbsp olive oil or lard","d":"Less rich than goose fat; lard is closest, olive oil is lighter.","k":["olive oil","lard"]},"6":{"x":"2 tbsp white wine vinegar or 1 tbsp lemon juice","d":"Similar acid to cut the fat; lemon is brighter.","k":["white wine vinegar","lemon juice"]},"7":{"x":"600 ml chicken broth or water plus 1 bouillon cube","d":"Meatier flavor; not vegetarian.","k":["chicken broth","water","bouillon cube"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Brown the pork in batches to get a good crust before adding liquid; a crowded pan steams the meat and the stew lacks depth. Toast the fennel seeds briefly first to wake up their aroma.","s":0},{"t":"Keep the oven low (140C/275F) and the pan tightly covered; the beans should be creamy, not split, and the pork fork-tender after the long, gentle cook.","s":1},{"t":"For the crust, break the top gently and press the crumbs in, then crisp under the grill; traditional cassoulet is crusted several times. Drizzle with oil to help it brown.","s":2}],"rl":"FPXXVXAMXXGBGFGG"},"52879":{"lv":"ERRRRERRRRREEEROR","w":{"1":"Makes the mash rich and silky; skipping makes it dry and heavy.","2":"Loosens and enriches the mash; milk works.","3":"Gives the golden color and richness of the topping; skip with a paler, plainer mash.","4":"Softens the vegetables and adds flavor; use oil if needed.","6":"Sweetness in the filling; skip with minor loss.","7":"Savory aromatic depth; skip with a milder filling.","8":"Savory kick; skip with a flatter flavor.","9":"Deglazes the pan and adds acid; extra broth plus a squeeze of lemon replaces it.","10":"Adds color and depth to the sauce; skip with little loss.","12":"Moistens the filling; chicken stock cube in water works.","14":"Briny pop that brings the classic French touch; skip freely.","15":"Fresh finish; skip.","16":"Browns on top and adds nutty savoriness; skip and the top is plainer."},"sub":{"9":{"x":"4 tbsp extra chicken broth plus 1 tsp lemon juice","d":"No alcohol; a little less depth but still savory.","k":["chicken broth","lemon juice"]},"12":{"x":"350 ml water plus 1 chicken bouillon cube","d":"Slightly less rich but works fine in a baked filling.","k":["water","bouillon cube"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Steam-dry the drained potatoes in the hot pot for a minute before mashing, and use a ricer or masher rather than a blender; wet or overworked potatoes turn gluey.","s":0},{"t":"Make sure the chicken is fully cooked to 165F (74C) and let the filling cool a few minutes before topping it; hot filling melts the mash into a layer rather than a crust.","s":1},{"t":"Rake the mash with a fork and grate the Gruyere over it; the ridges catch the heat and brown into crisp peaks. Let it rest 5 minutes so the filling firms.","s":3}],"rl":"BFFTFXVVXAXVMPXGG"},"52904":{"lv":"RERRRRRRERROORO","w":{"0":"For browning the beef; olive oil or butter plus oil work.","2":"Smoky salty depth in the sauce; skip with a lighter flavor.","3":"Sweet braised garnish; swap with pearl onions or small onions.","4":"Earthy classic bourguignon garnish; skip with a plainer stew.","5":"Savory aromatic depth; skip with mild loss.","6":"Provides the classic thyme, parsley and bay combination; skip and use the herbs listed.","7":"Adds color and body to the sauce; skip with little loss.","9":"Serves as the side mash; potatoes are the usual swap.","10":"Used to brown the celeriac; any oil works.","11":"Reinforces the herb flavor; skip as the bouquet garni covers it.","12":"Skip; the bouquet garni covers the herbs.","13":"Classic bay aroma in a braise; skip with little loss.","14":"Odd addition; a quiet floral note that you can skip."},"sub":{"0":{"x":"3 tsp duck fat, lard, or vegetable oil","d":"Goose fat is richer; oil works for browning.","k":["vegetable oil","lard"]},"9":{"x":"600 g potatoes, boiled and mashed with butter","d":"Milder and starchier than celeriac; use the same mash method.","k":["potato"]},"10":{"x":"2 tbsp vegetable oil","d":"Same browning with a neutral flavor.","k":["vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Pat the beef dry and brown it in batches over high heat; the dark crust and the browned bits stuck to the pan are what give bourguignon its deep, savory sauce.","s":0},{"t":"Keep the oven at 150C (300F) and the stew at a bare simmer; shin needs 3 to 3 1/2 hours so the collagen melts, and a hard boil toughens it. A foil cartouche pressed on the surface limits evaporation.","s":3},{"t":"Skim fat from the surface at the end and reduce the sauce uncovered until it coats a spoon; the stew tastes better made a day ahead and gently reheated. Season with salt only at the end.","s":5}],"rl":"FPXVVXXXMVFXXXX"},"52907":{"lv":"ERRRER","w":{"1":"Aromatic cure for the duck; skip and the flavor is plainer.","2":"Perfumes the cure and the meat; skip with a milder flavor.","3":"Classic herbal note for duck; skip and add more bay.","5":"Provides a little steaming liquid; broth plus a splash of vinegar replaces it."},"sub":{"5":{"x":"100 ml chicken broth plus 1 tsp white wine vinegar","d":"No alcohol; slightly less acidity.","k":["chicken broth","white wine vinegar"]}},"tips":[{"t":"The salt cure is the whole point: cure for the full 24 hours but no longer, then brush off all the salt before cooking or the duck is far too salty.","s":0},{"t":"Cook the legs very gently, skin-side down in the wine, at a bare simmer until the meat pulls from the bone; fierce heat toughens the meat. Confit is cooked far beyond safe temperatures, so doneness is about tenderness.","s":1},{"t":"To crisp, start skin-side down in a hot ovenproof pan so the skin renders and crackles. Stored fully covered in its own fat in the fridge, confit keeps for weeks and gets better.","s":3}],"rl":"KXXXPM"},"52912":{"lv":"EREOEROEERRO","w":{"1":"Coats the ramekins so the soufflé climbs the walls and gives a savory crust; skip and use fine breadcrumbs.","3":"Faint aromatic infusion for the milk; skip.","5":"Sharpens the cheese flavor; skip with a milder soufflé.","6":"Small heat that lifts the cheese; skip.","9":"Melts on top and provides the third cheese; skip for a simpler soufflé.","10":"Poured over for the second bake to make it rich and bubbly; milk is not a good swap.","11":"Serving extra; skip."},"tips":[{"t":"Whisk the whites in a spotlessly clean bowl; even a trace of yolk or grease prevents them from reaching stiff peaks. Beat until peaks just begin to form, not dry and grainy.","s":3},{"t":"Fold the whites in three stages, with a spatula cutting down and sweeping up, so you keep the air; stirring deflates them and the soufflés stay flat.","s":4},{"t":"These are twice-baked, so the collapse after the first bake is expected and desirable; they puff again on the second bake and hold well. Make sure they are cooked through and hot before serving.","s":6}],"rl":"FXMXTXXXLGFG"},"52915":{"lv":"EEORORR","w":{"2":"A pinch of savory salt; skip freely.","3":"A classic fines herbes herb with anise notes; skip and use more parsley and chives.","4":"Second herb of the fines herbes mix; skip.","5":"Mild onion note and color; skip with a plainer omelette.","6":"Gives melty savory richness inside; skip for a pure classic omelette."},"tips":[{"t":"Three eggs make a single omelette in a 20 cm pan, so for four servings you need four separate omelettes and 12 eggs. Make them one at a time for the right texture.","s":0},{"t":"Cook over medium heat once the butter stops foaming, and move the eggs constantly with a fork; the goal is small soft curds and a pale, unbrowned surface, not a crisp edge.","s":1},{"t":"Roll the omelette out of the pan onto the plate seam-down while the center is still creamy (baveuse); it finishes cooking from residual heat. Cook it through if serving anyone vulnerable to egg illness.","s":2}],"rl":"PFXXXXX"},"52920":{"lv":"EREERRO","w":{"0":"For browning the mushrooms and chicken; any oil works.","1":"Earthy and savory in the sauce; skip with a thinner dish.","4":"Adds salt and savory depth to the passata; the dish needs no extra salt.","5":"Briny classic accent in Marengo; skip with a plainer sauce.","6":"Fresh finish only; skip."},"sub":{"0":{"x":"1 tbsp vegetable oil","d":"Browns the mushrooms and chicken the same.","k":["vegetable oil","olive oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Brown the chicken legs well before simmering; the passata is thin, so the flavor comes from the fond and the crisped skin. The olives and stock cube are salty, so do not add salt until the end.","s":0},{"t":"Simmer gently and keep the chicken partly submerged; legs are cooked at 165F (74C) but are most tender after 40 minutes, when the meat pulls from the bone.","s":2},{"t":"If the sauce is too thin at the end, lift the lid and simmer 10 minutes to reduce it. Spoon off any fat that floats to the top before serving.","s":2}],"rl":"FVPMXXG"},"52934":{"lv":"ERERERRRERRORERRORO","w":{"1":"Browns the chicken with butter flavor; all olive oil also works.","2":"Browns the chicken and sweats the vegetables; any neutral oil works.","3":"Sweet base that melts into the rice; skip with less depth.","5":"Gives smoky paprika-rich fat that colors the rice; skip and add more paprika.","6":"Concentrated sweet-tangy tomato note; skip with a less Mediterranean flavor.","7":"Savory aromatic depth; skip with a milder dish.","9":"Adds color and depth to the rice; skip with a paler dish.","10":"Warm color and the Basque pepper note; piment d'Espelette is the authentic choice.","11":"Faint background aroma; skip.","12":"Herbal note in the braise; skip with little loss.","13":"Cooks the rice and moistens the chicken; water plus a bouillon cube works.","14":"Adds acidity and depth to the pan juices; broth plus lemon replaces it.","15":"Slices on top add brightness; skip with a slightly richer finish.","16":"Briny garnish; skip freely.","17":"Chorizo and olives are salty, so taste before adding.","18":"Minor; skip."},"sub":{"1":{"x":"2 tbsp extra olive oil","d":"Slightly less rich browning flavor but works the same.","k":["olive oil"]},"2":{"x":"6 tbsp vegetable oil","d":"Less fruity than olive oil but neutral and heat stable.","k":["vegetable oil"]},"13":{"x":"350 ml water plus 1 chicken bouillon cube","d":"Slightly less rich but fine since the chorizo flavors the pot.","k":["water","bouillon cube"]},"14":{"x":"180 ml extra chicken broth plus 1 tbsp lemon juice","d":"No alcohol; a bit less depth but the acid keeps it bright.","k":["chicken broth","lemon juice"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Brown the chicken skin-side down first and do not move it until it releases easily; a deep golden skin gives the sauce flavor and the pan juices their color.","s":0},{"t":"Sweat the peppers and onions slowly until very soft; this sweet, jammy base is what makes a Basquaise, so do not rush it.","s":1},{"t":"Once the pot goes into the oven, do not stir or lift the lid; the rice cooks in the steam and stirring makes it gluey. The chicken must reach 165F (74C) and the rice should be tender with a little bite.","s":3}],"rl":"PFFVVXXXBXXXXMMAGZZ"},"52935":{"lv":"EEERRRRRERROOORR","w":{"0":"For searing; any neutral oil.","2":"Reduced into the sauce base; chicken broth is a fair swap.","3":"Rounds the sauce and cooks the aromatics; skip with a leaner sauce.","4":"Savory depth in the pan sauce; skip with mild loss.","5":"Sweet mild onion note that defines pan sauces; skip with a sharper sauce.","6":"Earthy classic Diane garnish; skip with a smoother sauce.","9":"Adds tang that cuts the cream; skip with a milder sauce.","10":"A savory, tangy punch that is the signature of Steak Diane; skip with a flatter sauce.","11":"A dash for gentle heat; skip.","12":"Fresh finish; skip.","13":"Fresh finish; skip.","14":"Seasons the steak and sauce; add at the end after tasting.","15":"Seasons the steak; use freshly ground.","7":"Adds depth and acidity; skipped, the sauce is flatter but the dish still works."},"sub":{"0":{"x":"2 tbsp vegetable oil","d":"Same high-heat searing performance.","k":["vegetable oil","canola oil"]},"2":{"x":"1 1/2 cups chicken broth","d":"Lighter, less beefy sauce.","k":["chicken broth","beef broth"]},"7":{"x":"1/4 cup extra broth plus 1 tsp lemon juice or red wine vinegar","d":"No alcohol or flame; less depth but tangy and savory.","k":["chicken broth","lemon juice"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Reduce the broth separately to a concentrate, as the method does; it gives the sauce a deep beefy backbone without having to boil the cream, which can break.","s":1},{"t":"Take the pan off the heat before adding the brandy, then ignite it only with the pan away from the hood and anything flammable; let the flames die on their own. A tight lid snuffs it if it gets too tall.","s":3},{"t":"Return the steaks to the sauce only to warm them; they keep cooking in the hot sauce, so pull them a few degrees under your target. Fillet is lean, so rest it 5 minutes before saucing.","s":4}],"rl":"FPMFXXVXFXXXGGZZ"},"82":{"lv":"EERRRRRERR","w":{"2":"Classic crisp-tender Niçoise element; skip and add more potato or lettuce.","3":"Adds creamy richness and protein; skip with a lighter, less traditional plate.","4":"A soft bed that carries the dressing; any salad greens work.","5":"The salty Provencal accent that defines a Niçoise; skip and it tastes plainer.","6":"Juicy sweetness and acid on the plate; skip with a drier salad.","8":"Emulsifies the dressing so it clings; skip and the dressing separates.","9":"Acid for the dressing; white wine vinegar also works."},"sub":{"9":{"x":"2 tbsp lemon juice or 2 tbsp white wine vinegar","d":"Same acidity; the lemon adds a fruitier note.","k":["lemon juice","white wine vinegar"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Dress the warm potatoes straight after draining; they absorb the vinaigrette as they cool and taste seasoned all the way through.","s":2},{"t":"Shock the green beans in ice water right after boiling to keep them bright green and snappy. Boil the eggs 7 minutes for a jammy yolk, or 10 minutes for fully set yolks.","s":0},{"t":"Roast the salmon at 400F until just opaque and flaking; the USDA safe temperature is 145F (63C), but many cooks pull it earlier for a moister center. Arrange the components separately on the lettuce rather than tossing.","s":1}],"rl":"PBVPVXVFTA"},"3513":{"lv":"EREREORRRO","w":{"1":"Sweats the shallots and rounds out the broth; skip with a slightly thinner broth.","3":"Savory depth that blooms in the wine; skip with a milder broth.","5":"Faint background aroma in the broth; skip.","6":"Makes the broth richer, closer to moules a la creme; classic marinieres leave it out.","7":"Fresh color and flavor in the broth; skip with a plainer finish.","8":"For dipping up the broth; any crusty bread does the same.","9":"Minor; mussels are already salty so use only a pinch.","4":"Steaming liquid that cooks the mussels; skipped, they dry out."},"sub":{"4":{"x":"1 cup chicken broth plus 1 tbsp white wine vinegar or lemon juice","d":"No alcohol; less depth but still bright and savory.","k":["chicken broth","white wine vinegar","lemon juice"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Tap open mussels; any that do not close are dead and must be thrown away, and any that stay shut after steaming must also be discarded. Debeard just before cooking.","s":0},{"t":"Steam over high heat with the lid on and shake the pot; pull it off as soon as most shells open since mussels turn rubbery quickly. The mussel liquor is salty, so do not add salt.","s":3},{"t":"Stir the cream and parsley into the broth only after removing the mussels, and ladle the broth carefully; the last bit may be sandy, so leave it behind in the pot.","s":4}],"rl":"PFXXMXFGBZ"},"52852":{"lv":"EERRRERRER","w":{"1":"Roasts the potatoes and is part of the dressing; any oil works.","2":"Adds protein and a creamy yolk; skip with a lighter salad.","3":"The acid of the dressing; white wine vinegar or lemon juice work.","4":"Briny pops that define a Nicoise; skip with a plainer salad.","5":"Part of the dressing; olive oil is the better choice.","6":"Sharp raw bite against the tuna; skip or use a little scallion.","7":"Fresh greens base; any salad greens work.","8":"Main protein; drain tinned tuna well.","9":"Classic in a Nicoise; skip them if good tomatoes are out of season."},"sub":{"1":{"x":"2 tbsp vegetable oil","d":"Roasts the potatoes just as well; less fruity.","k":["vegetable oil","olive oil"]},"3":{"x":"1 tbsp white wine vinegar or 1 tbsp lemon juice","d":"Same acidity; lemon is a little brighter.","k":["white wine vinegar","lemon juice"]},"5":{"x":"50 g (about 3 tbsp) olive oil","d":"Better flavor in a dressing than plain vegetable oil.","k":["olive oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Roast the potatoes until crisp at the edges; they stay firm in the salad when dressed.","s":0},{"t":"Simmer eggs 8 minutes for slightly soft yolks, 10 for set, then plunge into cold water to stop cooking and prevent the gray-green ring around the yolk.","s":1},{"t":"Whisk the dressing in the bowl first and toss the spinach and onion in it, then add the tuna and potatoes gently so the flakes stay in chunks; drain tinned tuna well first.","s":2}],"rl":"BFPAXFXVPV"},"52906":{"lv":"EEREEEREREERE","w":{"2":"Seasons the leeks as they sweat; skip and the filling tastes flat.","6":"Classic warm spice that complements leeks and cream; skip with a plainer custard.","8":"Seasons the pastry; skip and it tastes bland.","10":"Lard makes the crust short and tender; use more butter if you do not have it.","11":"Savory topping that browns; skip for a plainer finish."},"sub":{"10":{"x":"60 g extra butter (120 g total)","d":"Crisper, more buttery and slightly less tender than lard pastry.","k":["butter","shortening"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Wash the sliced leeks very well in a bowl of water; grit hides between the layers. Sweat them covered then uncovered until completely soft and dry, since extra water makes the custard weep.","s":1},{"t":"Chill the pastry before rolling, rest it, and blind bake until the edges are biscuit-colored so the base stays crisp under the wet filling.","s":2},{"t":"Bake until the custard is just set with a slight wobble in the middle; it continues to firm up as it cools. Serve warm, not hot, so it slices cleanly.","s":3}],"rl":"FVZFSSXSZFFGM"},"52908":{"lv":"EEEEERRROO","w":{"5":"Fresh anise-sweet note that finishes the dish; skip with less Provencal character.","6":"Sweet base that melts into the sauce; skip with a flatter flavor.","7":"Savory depth in the vegetable stew; skip with a milder dish.","8":"A tiny lift of acidity; skip.","9":"Rounds the tomato acidity slightly; skip."},"tips":[{"t":"Brown each vegetable separately in batches so they keep their shape and develop flavor; piling them together steams them into mush and loses the identity of each piece.","s":2},{"t":"Score and blanch the tomatoes so the skins slip off; tough skins curl into the stew. Fold the basil in at the end, off the heat, so it stays fragrant.","s":3},{"t":"The stew tastes better rested; serve warm or at room temperature, and extend the simmer to 20 to 30 minutes if you want the flavors to marry more fully.","s":3}],"rl":"VVVVFXXXAW"},"52911":{"lv":"ERREERREER","w":{"1":"Sweet mild onion flavor that softens the base; skip with a more vegetal soup.","2":"Adds tender vegetable body; skip or use more green beans.","5":"Fresh crunch and green flavor; skip with a softer soup.","6":"Adds sweetness and tang to the blended pistou; skip with a less rounded paste.","9":"Adds savory saltiness to the pistou; skip and add salt.","0":"Main or only cooking fat; skipped, the food sticks, browns poorly and the dish loses richness.","3":"Main cooking liquid; skipped, the dish or grain is dry and underdone."},"sub":{"0":{"x":"1 tbsp olive oil or 1 tbsp butter","d":"Same softening job; olive oil is more Provencal.","k":["olive oil","butter"]},"3":{"x":"1 liter water plus 1 vegetable bouillon cube","d":"Lighter; add extra salt and a squeeze of lemon.","k":["water","vegetable broth","bouillon cube"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Stir the pistou in at the end and do not boil it afterwards; heat dulls the basil's color and aroma, and the fresh, raw punch is what makes the soup.","s":1},{"t":"Keep the green beans and zucchini just tender; this is a summer soup that should still have texture, so do not overcook the vegetables while the beans warm through.","s":0},{"t":"Rinse the canned beans and use some for the blended pistou, which thickens it and gives it body without any cream or oil.","s":1}],"rl":"FVVMBVVXXX"},"52921":{"lv":"EERRRROERRROO","w":{"2":"Sweet, tender filling vegetable; swap with more pepper or spinach.","3":"Mild onion flavor in the egg layers; skip and add chives.","4":"Adds sweetness and color in the filling; skip with a plainer cake.","5":"Savory aromatic depth; skip with a milder filling.","6":"A hint of heat; skip freely.","8":"Loosens the cream cheese to a spreadable consistency; use cream if you have it.","9":"Fresh onion flavor in the cream cheese filling; skip and use more basil.","10":"Fresh herb note for the filling; skip.","11":"Peppery topping for serving; skip.","12":"Finishing shavings; skip.","1":"Main or only cooking fat; skipped, the food sticks, browns poorly and the dish loses richness."},"sub":{"1":{"x":"1 tbsp vegetable oil or 1 tbsp butter","d":"Same softening job in the pan.","k":["vegetable oil","butter"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Cook each omelette thin and fully set but not browned, one third of the mixture at a time; thin layers stack neatly and slice clean, while thick ones are rubbery.","s":1},{"t":"Line the tin with cling film, cool the omelettes completely before layering, and chill overnight; the cream cheese firms and the cake slices in clean wedges.","s":2},{"t":"The eggs must be fully cooked since this is served cold and holds for hours; keep it in the fridge until serving and do not leave it out for more than an hour or two.","s":3}],"rl":"PFVXVXXFMXXGG"},"3509":{"lv":"EREEEEEEROO","w":{"1":"Seasons the pastry; an unsalted butter crust tastes flat next to the rich custard.","7":"Thins the custard to a delicate, sliceable set; half-and-half works if you want it richer.","8":"Adds nutty, savory depth. Classic Lorraine is just bacon and custard, so skipping gives a plainer but authentic quiche.","9":"Rounds out the custard; barely noticeable if skipped because bacon and cheese carry the flavor.","10":"Minor heat; the bacon and cheese carry the seasoning."},"sub":{"7":{"x":"1 cup half-and-half","d":"Slightly richer and silkier; sets the same. Do not use all cream or the custard turns heavy.","k":["half-and-half"]},"8":{"x":"1 cup shredded Swiss or Emmental","d":"Milder and less nutty but melts the same way.","k":["swiss cheese"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Brush the hot blind-baked shell with a little beaten egg and return it to the oven for 1 minute. The thin seal stops the custard soaking into the pastry and leaves a crisp base.","s":2},{"t":"Pull the quiche when the center 2 inches still wobbles like set gelatin. Carryover heat finishes it during the rest, while overbaking makes the custard grainy and weepy.","s":5}],"rl":"SZFMPSFMFXZ"},"3510":{"lv":"EEEEEOERRRO","w":{"5":"Classic bechamel seasoning; the sauce is still good without it, just plainer.","7":"Sharp tang cuts the rich cheese and bechamel; without it the sandwich tastes heavier and flatter.","8":"Browns and crisps the bread outside; spread it to the edges for even color.","9":"Ham and Gruyere are salty, so go light, but the bechamel needs some seasoning.","10":"Minor; a little in the sauce is enough."},"sub":{"8":{"x":"2 tbsp mayonnaise","d":"Browns evenly and crisps well; slight tang, no buttery flavor.","k":["mayonnaise"],"wn":"Contains egg"}},"tips":[{"t":"Whisk the warm milk into the roux a splash at a time at first, working it to a thick smooth paste before thinning. This is what prevents lumps.","s":0},{"t":"Broil with the rack in the middle, not right under the element. The bechamel should blister and brown in 2 minutes without scorching the bread edges.","s":5}],"rl":"BPFTMXFXFZZ"},"3512":{"lv":"EEERRRREERORO","w":{"3":"Lets the butter brown without burning; swapping in more butter works if you watch the heat.","4":"Speeds browning and balances the onions; without it they still caramelize, just slower.","5":"Earthy herbal note that keeps sweet onions from tasting flat; 1 tsp dried works too.","6":"Deglazes the pan and adds acid to balance the sweetness.","9":"Nutty melted topping; without it the tart is softer and sweeter but still good.","10":"Tiny background note; skip freely.","11":"Draws moisture out of the onions and seasons the custard; skipping makes the tart taste flat.","12":"Minor."},"sub":{"3":{"x":"1 tbsp more butter","d":"Butter alone browns faster, so keep the heat at medium-low and watch for scorching.","k":["butter"]},"6":{"x":"1/4 cup chicken broth + 1/2 tsp white wine vinegar","d":"Less fruity but gives the same deglazing and acid lift.","k":["chicken broth","white wine vinegar"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Do not rush the onions with high heat. When the pan dries and sticks, add a tablespoon of water and scrape up the browned film; that is what gives the deep, jammy color.","s":1},{"t":"Weight the pastry with parchment and dried beans or a second pan while it pre-bakes so puff pastry stays flat and the custard cannot pool under raised bubbles.","s":3}],"rl":"SVFFWXASFGXZZ"},"3369":{"lv":"EEERRRRO","w":{"3":"Emulsifies the dressing and adds a gentle bite; without it the dressing separates faster.","4":"Softens the lemon and brings out the carrots' sweetness; a pinch of sugar does the same.","5":"Fresh herbal color and lift; the salad works without it but tastes flatter.","6":"Pulls moisture and flavor into the carrots; the dressing needs it.","7":"Minor.","2":"Dressing acid; skipped, the carrot salad is dull."},"sub":{"2":{"x":"1.5 tbsp white wine vinegar + 1/2 tbsp water","d":"Sharper and less fruity than lemon.","k":["white wine vinegar"]},"4":{"x":"3/4 tsp sugar","d":"Same sweetness balance; whisk until dissolved so it does not stay gritty.","k":["sugar"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Grate on the fine side of a box grater or a food processor fine disc. Thick shreds stay crunchy and never absorb the dressing the way fine ones do.","s":0},{"t":"Taste after chilling, not before: cold mutes salt and acid, so you will usually need a little more lemon or salt. It keeps 2 days.","s":3}],"rl":"VFATWXZZ"},"3370":{"lv":"EEERRRO","w":{"3":"Quick savory note; skipped, the dish is still good but less rounded.","4":"Lifts the nutty browned butter; a splash of vinegar works.","5":"Salting the boiling water seasons the beans from inside; also season at the end.","6":"Minor."},"sub":{"4":{"x":"2 tsp white wine vinegar","d":"Sharper, less fruity; add off the heat.","k":["white wine vinegar"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Boil in a large pot of well-salted water (about 1 tbsp per quart) and shock in cold water. That fast cook-and-chill sets the bright green and keeps them crisp-tender.","s":0},{"t":"Almonds keep browning off the heat. Pull the pan the moment the butter smells like hazelnuts and add the garlic and beans right away to stop it.","s":2}],"rl":"VFGXAZZ"},"3371":{"lv":"EEEREEEOO","w":{"1":"Makes the dough tender and golden; all water works but the crust is crisper and paler.","3":"Seasons the dough; unsalted choux tastes flat though it puffs the same.","7":"Minor spicy note.","8":"A pinch of classic cheese-puff seasoning; skip if you must."},"sub":{"1":{"x":"1/2 cup water","d":"Puffs just as well; slightly crisper, paler shell with a less tender crumb.","k":["water"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Cook the paste until a film forms on the pan bottom. That drives off moisture so the dough can absorb all 4 eggs and puff high.","s":2},{"t":"Add the last egg gradually. The dough should be glossy and fall from the spoon in a thick V shape; too wet and the gougeres bake flat.","s":3},{"t":"Keep the oven closed for the first 15 minutes; steam does the puffing. Prick the base of each with a skewer when done to let steam out so they stay crisp.","s":6}],"rl":"MMFZSSFZX"},"52913":{"lv":"EEEEEREER","w":{"5":"Egg wash gives the glossy deep brown brioche crust; skipping leaves it pale and matte.","8":"Salty savory wrap that balances the sweet brioche; skip and it is a plain Brie-in-bread parcel.","3":"Main liquid of the dough or batter; skipped, the mix is too dry to come together."},"sub":{"3":{"x":"75 ml warm water","d":"Slightly less tender crumb and paler crust, but the dough rises fine.","k":["water"]},"5":{"x":"2 tbsp milk or cream brushed on","d":"Browns less deeply and with less shine than egg.","k":["milk"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Add the butter a little at a time once the dough is smooth and elastic. It looks greasy and broken first, but keep mixing until it is silky again.","s":0},{"t":"The long cold rest firms this very rich dough so it can be rolled. Work fast; it turns sticky if it warms above room temperature.","s":1},{"t":"Chill the wrapped parcel before baking and pinch the seams tightly. Warm dough or thin seams let the Brie leak out as it melts.","s":4}],"rl":"SWLMSCFPX"},"52914":{"lv":"ERREE","w":{"1":"Earthy herb note; without it the potatoes taste plainer. Use 1 tsp dried or 3 to 4 fresh sprigs.","2":"Softens the onions and oils the dish; butter gives more flavor.","4":"Main cooking liquid; skipped, the dish or grain is dry and underdone."},"sub":{"2":{"x":"2 tbsp butter","d":"Richer and browns more; also butter the dish.","k":["butter"]},"4":{"x":"425 ml chicken broth","d":"Deeper, meatier flavor than vegetable broth; use low-sodium and taste.","k":["chicken broth"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Slice the potatoes evenly at about 2 to 3 mm, ideally on a mandoline, and do not rinse them. The surface starch thickens the stock and holds the layers together.","s":1},{"t":"Pour the stock until it just reaches the underside of the top layer, not over it. The exposed layer crisps while the rest cooks in the stock.","s":2}],"rl":"XXFBM"},"52919":{"lv":"EERRERO","w":{"2":"Sweet, mellow savoriness infused into the cream; skipped, the gratin is plainer.","5":"Stops the gratin sticking and gives a buttered edge.","6":"Finishing sprinkle only; skip if cheese is not at hand.","3":"Moistens the gratin; skipped, the sauce is thicker."},"sub":{"3":{"x":"75 ml half-and-half","d":"Richer, slightly thicker sauce; keep the simmer gentle.","k":["half-and-half"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Simmering the potatoes and fennel in the milk and cream first releases starch that thickens the sauce and prevents curdling in the oven. Keep it gentle; no hard boil.","s":1},{"t":"Cut the fennel the same thickness as the potatoes so both finish together, and keep the fronds for snipping at the end.","s":0}],"rl":"BVXMFFG"},"23":{"lv":"EERRRRRERRREE","w":{"2":"Lets the butter brown without burning; more butter works too.","3":"Speeds caramelization and adds sweetness; onions still brown without it, just slower.","4":"Background savory note; the soup is classic without it.","5":"Gives light body; skipping makes the soup thinner but still good.","6":"Deglazes and adds acid to cut the sweetness.","8":"Adds umami and depth the broth may lack; 1 tbsp soy sauce is close.","9":"Herbal note that balances the sweetness.","10":"Subtle background; skip freely."},"sub":{"2":{"x":"1 tbsp more butter","d":"Butter browns faster, so watch for scorching.","k":["butter"]},"6":{"x":"1/2 cup beef broth + 1 tsp white wine vinegar","d":"Less fruity but gives the same deglaze and acidity.","k":["beef broth","white wine vinegar"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Use a wide heavy pot and scrape the fond as it forms. When the bottom sticks, add a splash of water and keep scraping; that browned film is where the color and flavor come from.","s":0},{"t":"Toast the baguette slices dry first so they do not dissolve, then lay the cheese so it overlaps the bowl rim. The crusty burnt cheese edge is the best part.","s":3}],"rl":"VFFWXTAMXXXBG"},"3589":{"lv":"EERRREERORRRO","w":{"2":"Sweetness and body in the base; skipping leaves the soup less rounded.","3":"Aromatic backbone alongside onion and carrot.","4":"Savory depth; skip and it is milder.","7":"Adds acidity, body and color; the soup is earthier and plainer without it.","8":"Subtle background note; thyme already carries the herbal flavor.","9":"Classic herb for lentils.","10":"Brightens an otherwise earthy pot; lemon juice also works.","11":"Lentils and broth need salt to taste like anything; season at the end.","12":"Minor.","0":"Main or only cooking fat; skipped, the food sticks, browns poorly and the dish loses richness."},"sub":{"0":{"x":"2 tbsp butter","d":"Slightly richer and sweeter; sweat the vegetables gently so it does not brown.","k":["butter"],"wn":"Contains dairy"},"10":{"x":"1 tbsp lemon juice","d":"Fruitier, brighter acid; add off the heat.","k":["lemon juice"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Pick through and rinse the lentils. French green lentils hold their shape, but the tomatoes slow softening, so expect the full 35 minutes before tasting for doneness.","s":3},{"t":"Stir in the vinegar at the very end, a teaspoon at a time. It wakes up the earthy lentils; added early it cooks off and does nothing.","s":4},{"t":"Blend only a third for body and leave the rest whole, so the soup is thick but still has bite.","s":5}],"rl":"FXVVXPMAXXAZZ"},"3593":{"lv":"EERREERRRRO","w":{"2":"Sweet aromatic backbone; skipped, the soup tastes more one-note.","3":"Savory depth that works with the mushrooms.","6":"Earthy herb that pairs naturally with mushrooms.","7":"Silky richness; without it the blended mushrooms and flour still make it creamy but lighter.","9":"Mushrooms and broth need seasoning; add at the end.","10":"Minor.","8":"Adds brightness; skipped, the dish still works but tastes flatter."},"sub":{"7":{"x":"1/2 cup half-and-half","d":"Lighter and a little less rich; warm gently and do not boil.","k":["half-and-half"]},"8":{"x":"1 tbsp lemon juice","d":"Brighter, fruitier acid; add off the heat.","k":["lemon juice"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Do not crowd the pot or salt the mushrooms early. They need to brown, not steam: wait for their liquid to evaporate, which takes most of the 8 minutes.","s":0},{"t":"Blend only about three quarters if you want a little texture. The reserved seared mushrooms on top then add contrast.","s":4},{"t":"Add the cream off the heat and just warm through. Boiling dairy with the acid can make the soup look grainy.","s":5}],"rl":"VFXXTMXFAZZ"},"3597":{"lv":"EEREERRORO","w":{"2":"Subtle savory note; the leeks carry most of the flavor.","5":"Woodsy herb note; skipped, the soup is milder.","6":"Silky finish; the potato already makes it creamy so it can be skipped or swapped for milk.","7":"Fresh onion note and color; skip freely.","8":"Potatoes absorb a lot of salt; the soup tastes flat without it.","9":"Minor."},"sub":{"6":{"x":"1/2 cup whole milk","d":"Lighter, less rich; add at the end and do not boil.","k":["whole milk"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Slice the leeks lengthwise and rinse them fanned under running water; grit hides between the layers. Sweat gently without color for a mellow flavor.","s":0},{"t":"Blend in batches with the blender lid vented, since hot soup expands, and stop as soon as smooth. Over-blending potatoes releases starch and turns the soup gluey.","s":3}],"rl":"FVXBMXFGZZ"},"52918":{"lv":"EERERERRROORREREOO","w":{"4":"Sweet aniseed note typical of Provencal fish stews; skipped, the base is less aromatic.","6":"Savory base; skipping flattens the sauce.","7":"Thickens the stew slightly and makes it a meal; skip for a lighter broth.","8":"A strip of zest gives the bright Provencal note; skipped, the sauce is less fragrant.","9":"One pod adds a faint aniseed warmth; skip freely.","10":"Subtle background herb.","11":"Adds heat and color to the sauce and the rouille; without it the rouille is just plain mayo.","12":"Concentrated tomato depth and color.","14":"Briny shellfish flavor; skip and the stew relies on fish and prawns.","16":"Minor herbal note.","17":"For mopping up and dipping; the stew eats fine without it.","2":"Adds depth and acidity; skipped, the sauce is flatter but the dish still works.","3":"Main cooking liquid; skipped, the dish or grain is dry and underdone."},"sub":{"2":{"x":"150 ml extra fish or chicken stock + 1 tsp white wine vinegar","d":"Less fruity but gives the same acid lift.","k":["chicken broth","white wine vinegar"]},"3":{"x":"200 ml chicken or vegetable broth","d":"Less marine depth; frying the prawn shells makes up for much of it.","k":["chicken broth"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Fry the prawn shells until they turn deep pink and smell toasty, then press out their flavor. This is the quickest way to give a quick stew real shellfish depth.","s":0},{"t":"Add the fish and prawns at the very end and keep the simmer gentle. Cook the fish until opaque, and discard any mussels that stay shut after cooking.","s":4},{"t":"Spoon the rouille onto toast or stir it into the broth at the table; it melts and thickens the soup, so do not mix it in the pot beforehand.","s":5}],"rl":"PFMMXXXBXXXXXVPPXG"},"52922":{"lv":"EEEERRRREORR","w":{"4":"Sweetness and body in the base; skipping gives a leaner, more savory bisque.","6":"Classic depth; 1 tbsp dry sherry is the usual swap, or skip.","7":"Adds color, sweetness and a touch of acidity.","9":"Smoky warmth and color; skip freely.","10":"Silky finish; without it the bisque is rustic and thinner.","11":"Whole prawns for garnish; the soup tastes the same without them.","1":"Main or only cooking fat; skipped, the food sticks, browns poorly and the dish loses richness.","5":"Adds depth and acidity; skipped, the sauce is flatter but the dish still works.","8":"Main cooking liquid; skipped, the dish or grain is dry and underdone."},"sub":{"1":{"x":"4 tbsp butter","d":"Richer and nuttier; do not let it brown past the shells' color.","k":["butter"]},"5":{"x":"150 ml extra fish stock + 1 tsp white wine vinegar","d":"Less fruity but keeps the deglaze and acidity.","k":["chicken broth","white wine vinegar"]},"8":{"x":"1 l chicken broth","d":"Less marine depth; the toasted shells carry most of the flavor.","k":["chicken broth"]}},"tips":[{"t":"The shells are the soup. Fry them hard until bright red and toasty before adding the vegetables, since this is where the color and flavor come from.","s":0},{"t":"Press the blitzed mixture through a fine sieve with the back of a ladle. Skip it and shell fragments make the bisque gritty.","s":1},{"t":"Reheat gently and cook the garnish prawns only 2 to 3 minutes until pink and firm; longer makes them rubbery.","s":2}],"rl":"PFXVVMXVMXFG"},"3515":{"lv":"EREEERO","w":{"1":"Makes the mousse glossier and slightly softer; skipping gives a firmer, less silky set.","5":"Rounds out the chocolate flavor; skipped, it tastes a bit one-dimensional.","6":"A pinch helps the whites whip and sharpens the chocolate; barely noticeable if skipped."},"tips":[{"t":"Let the melted chocolate cool to just warm, about 100F, before adding the yolks. Too hot scrambles them; too cool and it seizes, giving a streaky mousse.","s":1},{"t":"This mousse contains raw egg whites and yolks. Use pasteurized eggs for children, pregnant guests or anyone vulnerable, and keep it refrigerated.","s":3},{"t":"Fold the whites in thirds, cutting down the middle and sweeping up the side of the bowl. Stirring deflates the foam and leaves a dense pudding.","s":4}],"rl":"SFLWFXZ"},"3516":{"lv":"EEEEERORO","w":{"5":"Rounds out the custard; a drop of almond extract gives a more traditional cherry-pit note.","6":"Sharpens the sweetness; barely noticeable if skipped.","7":"Prevents sticking and browns the edge; a thin coat of oil works too.","8":"Finishing dusting only."},"sub":{"7":{"x":"1 tbsp neutral oil, brushed on","d":"No buttery edge, but the clafoutis releases just as well.","k":["vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Rest the batter 20 to 30 minutes if you have time. The flour relaxes and the clafoutis bakes up tender and custardy instead of rubbery.","s":3},{"t":"Warm the buttered dish in the oven for 5 minutes before pouring. The batter sets at the edges on contact and gets a golden crust.","s":4}],"rl":"VSWSMXZFG"},"52776":{"lv":"EEREEE","w":{"2":"Loosens the dense batter slightly; a spoonful of water does the same."},"sub":{"2":{"x":"2 tbsp water","d":"Same loosening effect with slightly less richness.","k":["water"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Use room-temperature eggs and add them one at a time. Cold eggs split the creamed butter; if it looks curdled, beat in a spoonful of the flour to bring it back.","s":2},{"t":"Cool the chocolate to lukewarm before combining. Hot chocolate melts the creamed butter and loses the air you beat in.","s":2},{"t":"Bake until a skewer comes out clean but the center is just firm. This dense cake keeps setting as it cools, so overbaking only dries it out.","s":3}],"rl":"FFMSWS"},"52905":{"lv":"RRRREEEREERO","w":{"0":"Makes the hot chocolate sauce poured into the souffle at the table; skip the sauce and the souffles are still good.","1":"Sweetens the sauce.","2":"The chocolate for the sauce; without it there is no sauce.","3":"Gives the sauce gloss and a silky finish.","7":"Loosens the chocolate base so the whites fold in easily.","10":"Loosens the chocolate base so the whites fold in easily.","11":"Finishing dusting only."},"sub":{"0":{"x":"100 ml heavy cream + 40 ml milk","d":"Same sauce, a touch richer.","k":["heavy cream","milk"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Preheat a baking tray on the top shelf and stand the ramekins on it. The blast of bottom heat gives the souffles a fast, even rise.","s":0},{"t":"Brush the ramekins with melted butter in upward strokes from base to rim, coat with sugar, then run your thumb around the inner rim after filling. The clean groove helps the souffle climb straight.","s":1},{"t":"Beat the whites only to stiff, glossy peaks. Grainy over-beaten whites will not fold in evenly and the souffle will collapse the moment it leaves the oven, so serve immediately.","s":2}],"rl":"FWXFFWXFSLFG"},"52909":{"lv":"EOEEEO","w":{"1":"Only for rolling the pastry without sticking; any dry flour works.","5":"Cool tang cuts the sweet caramel; vanilla ice cream or whipped cream works too."},"tips":[{"t":"Take the caramel to a deep amber, a shade past where you feel nervous. Pale caramel makes the tart cloyingly sweet instead of rich and slightly bitter.","s":1},{"t":"Pack the apples in very tightly; they shrink a lot as they cook, and any gaps become holes in the caramel layer.","s":2},{"t":"Invert while the caramel is still loose, after about an hour of cooling. If a few apples stick, set the dish over low heat for 30 seconds and try again.","s":4}],"rl":"SSVWFG"},"52910":{"lv":"ERERROO","w":{"1":"Caramelizes on the apples and tart edges; white sugar works too.","4":"Cool tangy cream balances the sweet warm tarts; whipped cream or yogurt also works.","5":"Sweetens the cream and adds a sheen; skip freely.","6":"Perfumes the creme fraiche; a pinch of ground cinnamon would do.","3":"Glaze for the tarts; skipped, they are less shiny."},"sub":{"3":{"x":"4 tbsp apricot jam, warmed and sieved","d":"Fruitier and less spiced with no wine depth, but gives the same glaze.","k":["apricot jam"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Prick the pastry only in the middle and leave a clear rim; the border puffs into a natural frame that holds the apples in.","s":1},{"t":"Slice the apples 2 mm thin and overlap them tightly like roof tiles. Thick slices stay raw while the pastry browns.","s":2},{"t":"Crush the cardamom pods and use only the seeds, ground fine; whole husks are unpleasant to chew in the cream.","s":3}],"rl":"SWVWGWX"},"52916":{"lv":"EEERRRRE","w":{"3":"Warm aniseed note that makes the caramel taste of more than sugar; skip and it is a simple pear tatin.","4":"Floral citrusy warmth that suits pears.","5":"Warm spice running through the caramel.","6":"Deglazes and adds a boozy depth; a splash of apple juice does the job."},"sub":{"6":{"x":"2 tbsp apple juice + 1/2 tsp lemon juice","d":"Same deglazing; fruitier with less depth than brandy.","k":["apple juice","lemon juice"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Dry the peeled pears uncovered in the fridge if you can. Surface moisture dilutes the caramel and makes it runny.","s":0},{"t":"Keep cooking the pears in the sauce until deeply caramelized. Taste the sauce; if it turns bitter rather than toasty, it has gone too far.","s":2},{"t":"Rest the baked tart 5 minutes before inverting so the caramel thickens just enough to cling. Use a plate with a rim to catch it.","s":5}],"rl":"VWFXXXXS"},"52917":{"lv":"EEREEE","w":{"2":"Flavors the cream with a deep, floral vanilla; extract is a close swap."},"sub":{"2":{"x":"1 tsp vanilla extract","d":"Stir it in after heating; a bit less complex than a real pod but fine.","k":["vanilla extract"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Warm the cream and white chocolate only until the chocolate melts. White chocolate splits easily and becomes greasy if boiled.","s":0},{"t":"Strain the custard and skim any bubbles. Bake in the water bath until the edges are set but the center still wobbles like jelly; it firms up while chilling.","s":1},{"t":"Blot the chilled custard dry and use a thin, even layer of fine sugar for the top. Torch it constantly moving, and serve within 30 minutes before the caramel softens.","s":2}],"rl":"FXXSWG"},"3531":{"lv":"ERRRRREEEO","w":{"1":"Sweetens tart apples; use a little less with sweet apple varieties.","2":"Chewy sweet pockets; dried cranberries or chopped dried apricots work.","3":"Crunch and nutty richness; pecans work, or skip.","4":"The warm spice that makes strudel taste like strudel.","5":"Brightens the apples and keeps them from browning; skip if the apples are very tart.","9":"Finishing dusting only."},"sub":{"2":{"x":"1/2 cup dried cranberries","d":"Tarter and chewier; same texture.","k":["dried cranberries"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Toast the crumbs to golden. They soak up the apple juices as the strudel bakes, which keeps the bottom layers of phyllo crisp rather than soggy.","s":0},{"t":"Keep unused phyllo under a damp towel. It dries and cracks in minutes, and a thin film of butter between sheets is enough; too much makes it greasy.","s":2},{"t":"Cut with a serrated knife using a sawing motion after the 15-minute cool. Pressing straight down crushes the flaky layers.","s":5}],"rl":"VWVXXATFSG"},"3524":{"lv":"EEEEERRORO","w":{"5":"Browns the crumbs and adds buttery flavor; all oil works but is less rich.","6":"The acid cuts through the fried breading; it is traditional and worth having.","7":"Garnish only.","8":"Seasons the cutlet itself; the breading alone is bland.","9":"Minor."},"tips":[{"t":"Pound from the center outward between plastic wrap to an even 1/4 inch. Even thickness cooks in 2 to 3 minutes; if using pork, cook to 145F with a rest.","s":0},{"t":"Do not press the crumbs on. A loose coating puffs and billows away from the meat during frying, which is the signature of a real Wiener Schnitzel.","s":2},{"t":"Fry in about 1/2 inch of fat at 350F and keep swirling or spooning fat over the top. Too cool and the crust turns greasy and flat.","s":4}],"rl":"PTTTFFAGZZ"},"3525":{"lv":"EERRERRORO","w":{"2":"Sweet savory base that cooks into the kraut.","3":"Sweet-tart foil to the sour kraut; skip and it is harsher.","6":"Classic with sauerkraut; skip for a milder dish.","7":"Softens the sour; the apple and beer already add sweetness.","8":"A hearty side; skip and serve with bread instead.","9":"Serving extra.","1":"Main or only cooking fat; skipped, the food sticks, browns poorly and the dish loses richness.","5":"Adds depth and acidity; skipped, the sauce is flatter but the dish still works."},"sub":{"1":{"x":"1 tbsp butter","d":"Richer and browns faster; keep the heat medium.","k":["butter"]},"5":{"x":"1/2 cup chicken broth","d":"Less malty; the sausages still add plenty of depth.","k":["chicken broth"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Brown the bratwurst over medium heat and do not prick them. Browning in intact skins keeps the juices inside.","s":0},{"t":"Fresh pork sausage must reach 160F; check with a thermometer rather than guessing by color.","s":2},{"t":"Rinse the sauerkraut in cold water for a mild flavor; leave it unrinsed if you want the authentic sharp tang.","s":1}],"rl":"PFXVVMXWBG"},"3528":{"lv":"EEEERERRRRERRO","w":{"4":"Classic tart crunch that cuts the richness; skip and the filling is heavier.","6":"Sweet base for the gravy.","7":"Aromatic base for the gravy.","8":"Depth and color in the gravy; skip and it is paler and less savory.","12":"Seasons the beef directly; unsalted meat is flat.","13":"Minor.","5":"Main or only cooking fat; skipped, the food sticks, browns poorly and the dish loses richness.","9":"Adds depth and acidity; skipped, the sauce is flatter but the dish still works.","11":"Thickens the gravy; skipped, the sauce is thinner."},"sub":{"5":{"x":"2 tbsp olive oil","d":"Fine for browning at this heat; a little fruitier.","k":["olive oil"]},"9":{"x":"1/2 cup beef broth + 1 tsp red wine vinegar","d":"Less depth but gives the acid needed to cut the richness.","k":["beef broth","red wine vinegar"]},"11":{"x":"2 tbsp all-purpose flour mixed with 3 tbsp cold water","d":"Makes a slightly more opaque gravy; simmer 5 minutes so it does not taste raw.","k":["flour"],"wn":"Contains wheat/gluten"}},"tips":[{"t":"Pound the slices thin and even and roll tightly from the short end; loose rolls leak filling during the long braise.","s":1},{"t":"Brown the rolls deeply on all sides without crowding. That color is the base of the gravy, so it is worth the 8 minutes.","s":2},{"t":"Simmer, never boil; boiling tightens the lean round and makes it tough. Finish the strained gravy with a splash of pickle brine for brightness.","s":4}],"rl":"PXXXAFVVXMMTZZ"},"3529":{"lv":"EEEROEEEOO","w":{"2":"Makes the batter tender and rich; water works but gives chewier noodles.","3":"Seasons the dough; also salt the pot water generously.","4":"A pinch of classic seasoning.","8":"Garnish only.","9":"Minor."},"sub":{"2":{"x":"1/2 cup water","d":"Slightly chewier and less rich, but still tender if rested.","k":["water"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Beat the batter until it is thick and stretchy and bubbles form. It should fall from a spoon in slow ribbons; too thin gives mushy blobs, too thick will not pass through the holes.","s":0},{"t":"Press in small batches so the water stays at a gentle boil and the noodles do not clump together.","s":2},{"t":"Layer the hot spaetzle and cheese in alternating layers so it melts between them, and bake only until bubbling. The golden onions on top are what make this dish.","s":5}],"rl":"BTMZXFFXGZ"},"3407":{"lv":"EERREREORR","w":{"2":"Sweet savory base for the braise.","3":"Sweet-tart; it breaks down into the sauce.","5":"Balances the vinegar; white sugar works too.","7":"Subtle background note.","8":"Classic warm spice; without it this tastes like generic braised cabbage.","9":"Cabbage needs salt to taste of anything.","1":"Main or only cooking fat; skipped, the food sticks, browns poorly and the dish loses richness.","4":"Acid that sets the red color and tang; skipped, cabbage goes blue and flat."},"sub":{"1":{"x":"2 tbsp vegetable oil","d":"Less rich; goose fat or bacon fat would be more traditional.","k":["vegetable oil"]},"4":{"x":"1/3 cup red wine vinegar","d":"Slightly fruitier; still keeps the cabbage red.","k":["red wine vinegar"]},"5":{"x":"1/4 cup white sugar","d":"Slightly less caramel depth.","k":["sugar"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Add the vinegar early. Acid keeps red cabbage bright magenta, while without it the cabbage turns blue-purple.","s":2},{"t":"Keep it at a low, covered simmer and stir occasionally. If it dries out, add a splash of water rather than turning up the heat.","s":3},{"t":"Rotkohl tastes better the next day. Reheat gently and balance with a little more sugar or vinegar.","s":5}],"rl":"VFXVAWMXXZ"},"3408":{"lv":"EEEERORO","w":{"4":"Seasons the dough; also salt the pot water generously.","5":"A pinch of classic seasoning.","6":"Frying the noodles in butter adds nutty flavor and golden color; oil works but is less flavorful.","7":"Garnish only.","2":"Main liquid of the dough or batter; skipped, the mix is too dry to come together."},"sub":{"2":{"x":"1/2 cup more water (1 cup total)","d":"Slightly chewier and less rich, but still tender if the batter rests.","k":["water"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Rest the batter 10 minutes so the gluten relaxes; add flour by the tablespoon if it runs off the spoon, since it should fall slowly.","s":0},{"t":"If scraping from a board, dip the knife in the boiling water between strokes so the batter does not stick to it.","s":2},{"t":"Shake the noodles dry before they hit the skillet. Wet spaetzle steam instead of browning; toss in hot butter until patchy golden.","s":4}],"rl":"BTMMZXFG"},"3409":{"lv":"ERRRROOO","w":{"1":"Rendered fat flavors and crisps the potatoes; skipping loses the smoky flavor.","3":"Sweet savory note; goes in late so it does not burn.","4":"Seasons the potatoes; add after crisping.","5":"Minor.","6":"Color and a mild sweet note.","7":"Garnish only.","2":"One of several fats or only sweats aromatics; skipping loses some flavor but dish still cooks."},"sub":{"2":{"x":"2 tbsp vegetable oil","d":"Less rich but fine since the bacon fat is already in the pan.","k":["vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Boil the potatoes in their skins a day ahead and chill them. Cold, firm waxy potatoes slice cleanly and fry crisp; warm ones crumble.","s":0},{"t":"Leave the potatoes untouched for the full 8 minutes. Stirring early breaks the crust before it forms.","s":2},{"t":"Salt at the end. Early salt draws moisture out and steams the slices instead of crisping them.","s":4}],"rl":"BXFXZZXG"},"3410":{"lv":"EERREREEO","w":{"3":"Gentle onion flavor throughout.","5":"Fresh herb flavor and color.","8":"A pinch of classic seasoning.","2":"One of several fats or only sweats aromatics; skipping loses some flavor but dish still cooks."},"sub":{"2":{"x":"2 tbsp vegetable oil","d":"Less rich; fine for softening the onion.","k":["vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Use truly stale bread. Fresh bread turns gluey; if yours is fresh, dry the cubes in a 250F oven for 15 minutes first.","s":0},{"t":"Test-cook one dumpling first. If it falls apart, add 1 to 2 tbsp flour or breadcrumbs to the mix.","s":3},{"t":"Keep the water barely simmering. Boiling bursts the dumplings; they are done when they float and feel springy.","s":4}],"rl":"BMFXTGTZX"},"3411":{"lv":"ERROEEEOR","w":{"1":"Counters the slight bitterness of white asparagus.","2":"Seasons the asparagus through the cooking water.","3":"Adds gloss and sweetness to the cooking water; skip freely.","7":"A pinch of heat; skip freely.","8":"Hollandaise tastes flat without it.","5":"Acid of the hollandaise; skipped, the sauce is flat."},"sub":{"5":{"x":"2 tsp white wine vinegar","d":"Sharper and less fruity than lemon.","k":["white wine vinegar"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Peel white asparagus from just below the tip all the way down and cut the woody ends. Unpeeled stalks stay stringy and bitter.","s":0},{"t":"Whisk the yolks over barely simmering water, with the bowl not touching it, and heat them to about 160F. They thicken and become safe to eat; any hotter and they scramble.","s":2},{"t":"If the sauce splits, whisk in a teaspoon of hot water or a fresh yolk to bring it back. Keep it warm, never hot.","s":3}],"rl":"VWZFTAFXZ"},"3412":{"lv":"EERERRROR","w":{"2":"Smooths the spread and keeps it creamy; skip for a firmer, sharper result.","5":"Sharp fresh bite that is traditional in Obatzda.","7":"Garnish only.","8":"Brie varies in saltiness, so adjust to taste.","4":"Seasoning of the spread; skipped, it is milder.","6":"Loosens and flavors the spread; skipped, it is thicker."},"sub":{"4":{"x":"1/2 tsp ground caraway","d":"More authentic Bavarian flavor; slightly sweeter and more anise-like.","k":["caraway seed"]},"6":{"x":"2 tbsp milk","d":"No malty note; slightly milder.","k":["milk"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Use properly ripe cheese. Underripe Brie is firm and chalky and will not mash smooth; leave it at room temperature for an hour.","s":0},{"t":"Mix by hand with a fork, not in a processor. It should stay slightly chunky, and resting an hour lets the flavors meld.","s":2},{"t":"Serve at room temperature; fridge-cold it is stiff and the flavor is muted.","s":4}],"rl":"PFFXXXMGZ"},"3530":{"lv":"EEREERRORO","w":{"2":"Sweet savory base for the dressing.","5":"Adds tang and body; skip and use a little more vinegar.","6":"Balances the sharp vinegar.","7":"Garnish only.","8":"Potatoes soak up salt; needed at the end.","9":"Minor.","3":"Hot dressing liquid; skipped, the potatoes are dry."},"sub":{"3":{"x":"1/2 cup chicken broth, hot","d":"Lighter, less beefy; fine in the dressing.","k":["chicken broth"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Peel and slice while still warm; cold potatoes break apart, and warm ones absorb the dressing instead of just being coated by it.","s":0},{"t":"Make the dressing noticeably sharp. The potatoes soak up acid as they sit, so it mellows once combined.","s":3},{"t":"Fold gently and let it sit 15 minutes. Keep a splash of broth back to loosen the salad before serving.","s":4}],"rl":"BXXMAXWGZZ"},"119":{"lv":"EREERR","w":{"1":"Keeps the muffins tender and slightly soft; water works.","4":"Sweet juicy pockets; skip and the muffins are plainer and a bit drier.","5":"Eggs need salt; feta is salty, so go lightly."},"sub":{"1":{"x":"1/4 cup water","d":"Slightly firmer, less tender.","k":["water"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Squeeze or pat the spinach and tomatoes dry before filling. Wet vegetables release water and leave the muffins spongy and weepy.","s":2},{"t":"Bake until the eggs reach 160F and the center springs back. Rest 5 minutes and run a knife around the edges before lifting out.","s":3}],"rl":"PMVXVZ"},"3230":{"lv":"ERRERRERRRROEEEOER","w":{"2":"Sweet base for the meat sauce.","4":"Savory depth in the sauce.","5":"Concentrates the tomato flavor and color of the sauce.","8":"The signature warm Greek note; without it the dish tastes like a plain baked pasta.","9":"Classic herb for the meat sauce.","10":"Needed in both the meat sauce and bechamel.","11":"Minor.","15":"A pinch of classic bechamel seasoning.","17":"Salty nutty flavor for the pasta and top crust; pecorino works.","1":"One of several fats or only sweats aromatics; skipping loses some flavor but dish still cooks.","7":"Adds depth and acidity; skipped, the sauce is flatter but the dish still works."},"sub":{"1":{"x":"1 tbsp butter","d":"Slightly richer; fine for browning the onion and beef.","k":["butter"]},"7":{"x":"1/2 cup beef broth + 1 tsp red wine vinegar","d":"Less depth but still deglazes and adds acid.","k":["beef broth","red wine vinegar"]},"17":{"x":"1 cup grated pecorino romano","d":"Sharper and saltier; reduce added salt slightly.","k":["pecorino"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Stop the pasta 2 minutes short of al dente and toss with cheese while hot. It keeps cooking in the oven and will turn mushy if you boil it fully.","s":0},{"t":"Let the bechamel cool 5 minutes before whisking in the eggs, or they scramble. The eggs are what set the topping into a sliceable custard layer.","s":3},{"t":"Rest the pastitsio a full 15 minutes before cutting. Skipping the rest makes the layers slide apart rather than slice cleanly.","s":5}],"rl":"BFXPXXVMXXZZFTMXSG"},"3236":{"lv":"ERREEROREOOEOR","w":{"1":"Grated onion keeps the meatballs moist; skipping makes them drier.","2":"Savory sharpness.","5":"Fresh mint is the Greek signature; without it they taste like plain meatballs.","6":"Fresh lift alongside the mint.","7":"Classic dried herb.","9":"Minor.","10":"Gives a light crust and helps browning; skip and they are fine.","12":"For serving.","13":"Cool tangy sauce for the warm spiced meat; skip and the dish is drier.","11":"Frying medium; without it nothing can be deep or shallow fried and crisped."},"sub":{"11":{"x":"1/3 cup vegetable oil","d":"Less fruity but fries cleanly at a higher heat.","k":["vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Mix just until combined. Overworking packs the proteins and gives dense, bouncy meatballs instead of tender ones.","s":0},{"t":"Chill the mixture so the fat firms and the balls hold their shape, and dust with flour for a thin browned crust.","s":1},{"t":"Keep the heat at medium. If the outside darkens before the center reaches 160F, finish them in a 350F oven.","s":3}],"rl":"PXXTTXXXZZTFBX"},"53008":{"lv":"EOERRERRERRRO","w":{"1":"A pinch offsets the tomato acidity.","3":"Sweet base for the filling.","4":"Savory depth.","6":"Warm spice that pairs with lamb; skipped, the filling is less distinctive.","7":"Depth and color for the filling.","10":"Fresh dill is the classic Greek herb in stuffed vegetables.","11":"Fresh herb lift.","12":"Minor addition.","9":"Adds savory depth to the sauce; water or other liquid still gives a working dish."},"sub":{"9":{"x":"100 ml water + a pinch of salt","d":"Less savory; the lamb and tomato paste carry the flavor.","k":["water"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Use firm, slightly underripe tomatoes and scoop gently so the skin stays intact; reserve the chopped pulp for the filling.","s":0},{"t":"Do not pack the filling hard; the rice still swells as it finishes in the tomato juices and can split the skin.","s":2},{"t":"Cook the lamb until no pink remains, to 160F, before it goes in the tomatoes.","s":1}],"rl":"VWFXXPXXBMXXX"},"53009":{"lv":"REEEREO","w":{"0":"Pounded with the salt it becomes a paste that flavors the lamb evenly; skip and the marinade is blander.","4":"Fresh herbal note; skip for a pure lemon-garlic marinade.","6":"Serving extra."},"tips":[{"t":"Pound the garlic with coarse salt until it is a paste. It spreads through the marinade evenly instead of leaving harsh raw chunks.","s":0},{"t":"Marinate no longer than overnight. The lemon acid turns lamb mealy and tough if left much longer.","s":1},{"t":"Cook hot and fast, 2 to 3 minutes a side, to about 145F, then rest 5 minutes. Cubes cut evenly cook evenly.","s":4}],"rl":"XZFAXPB"},"12":{"lv":"EERRRRRRREREOR","w":{"2":"Sweet jammy onions in the sauce.","3":"Mashed into the sauce with the feta; skip and it is blander.","4":"Green vegetable for freshness; swap any seasonal veg.","5":"Sweetness and bulk.","6":"Mild bulk; swap any vegetable.","7":"Briny pops that season the pasta.","8":"Protein and heft that make it a meal.","10":"Herb blend; dried oregano works.","12":"Finishing cheese; the feta is already salty so it can go.","13":"Brightens the rich, creamy sauce."},"sub":{"10":{"x":"1 tsp dried oregano","d":"Same herbal flavor with less variety.","k":["oregano"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Use a big pan so the vegetables lie in a single layer. A crowded dish steams the vegetables rather than roasting them, and leave the feta whole to brown and soften.","s":1},{"t":"Add the pasta water a splash at a time while mashing the feta and garlic into the vegetables. The starchy water emulsifies the oil and feta into a creamy sauce.","s":3},{"t":"Squeeze the lemon in last, off the heat, so the sauce stays bright.","s":3}],"rl":"PVVXVVVXPFXBGA"},"83":{"lv":"ERRRREERR","w":{"1":"Cool crunch.","2":"Sweet, juicy pops.","3":"Sharp bite; soak slices in cold water for 5 minutes to mellow.","4":"Briny pops that season the salad.","8":"Classic Greek herb that flavors the dressing.","7":"Adds brightness; skipped, the dish still works but tastes flatter."},"sub":{"7":{"x":"2 tbsp red wine vinegar + 1 tbsp water","d":"Sharper and less fruity than lemon with no zest.","k":["red wine vinegar"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Cook the orzo to al dente in salted water and toss it with a little oil after rinsing. It stops it clumping as it cools.","s":0},{"t":"Orzo soaks up dressing as it sits, so hold a little back and re-dress before serving. Fold in the feta last so it stays in chunks.","s":2}],"rl":"BVVXXPFAX"},"87":{"lv":"EREERRRE","w":{"1":"Slightly softer, more tender set; water works.","4":"Sweet savory base.","5":"Color and a mild mineral note.","6":"Sweet juicy pockets; skip and it is plainer.","2":"Main or only cooking fat; skipped, the food sticks, browns poorly and the dish loses richness."},"sub":{"2":{"x":"1 tbsp butter","d":"Richer; sautes the vegetables gently.","k":["butter"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Cook off the moisture from the spinach and tomatoes before adding the eggs. Wet vegetables make a watery frittata.","s":1},{"t":"Keep the heat medium-low once the eggs go in; high heat makes it rubbery. Finish in the oven until the center reaches 160F, puffed and set.","s":3}],"rl":"PMFVXVVX"},"92":{"lv":"EEERORRRO","w":{"3":"Earthy spice for the chickpeas.","4":"Color and mild smoke.","5":"Cool tangy yogurt balances the sweet potato; skipped, it is drier.","6":"Brightens the yogurt and cuts the sweetness.","7":"Salty tang.","8":"Garnish only.","2":"Main or only cooking fat; skipped, the food sticks, browns poorly and the dish loses richness."},"sub":{"2":{"x":"1 tbsp vegetable oil","d":"Less fruity but roasts the chickpeas the same.","k":["vegetable oil"]},"5":{"x":"1/2 cup sour cream","d":"Richer and less tangy; thin it with a little lemon juice.","k":["sour cream"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Bake the sweet potatoes on a foil-lined tray so leaking sugars do not burn on the pan. They are done when a knife meets no resistance.","s":0},{"t":"Pat the chickpeas thoroughly dry before oiling. Moisture is why they never get crisp.","s":1},{"t":"Salt the yogurt and add lemon zest as well as juice for a brighter sauce.","s":2}],"rl":"BPFXXXAGG"},"3227":{"lv":"EEEREEOORROO","w":{"3":"Savory punch in the marinade.","6":"Minor.","7":"Color and a mild warmth.","8":"Wraps the chicken with yogurt and veg.","9":"Cool tangy sauce for the grilled chicken.","10":"Crunch and freshness.","11":"Freshness."},"tips":[{"t":"Marinate no longer than 4 hours. The lemon juice turns chicken mushy beyond that.","s":0},{"t":"Leave a small gap between the chicken cubes on each skewer so the heat reaches all sides and they char instead of steam.","s":1},{"t":"Thighs forgive overcooking, but pull them at 165F and rest 5 minutes so they stay juicy.","s":2}],"rl":"PFAXXZZXBXVV"},"3228":{"lv":"EERRRRORERRRREO","w":{"2":"Brightens the marinade; red wine vinegar works.","3":"Savory punch.","4":"Greek herbal flavor.","5":"Gyro-style warmth.","6":"Color and mild warmth.","7":"Chicken needs salt; add as you marinate.","9":"Cool crunch.","10":"Juicy sweetness.","11":"Sharp bite.","12":"Salty creamy tang.","14":"Briny extra."},"sub":{"2":{"x":"2 tbsp red wine vinegar","d":"Sharper and less fruity.","k":["red wine vinegar"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Slice the thighs thin against the grain before marinating so the edges char like gyro meat.","s":0},{"t":"Cook in a single layer and in two batches if needed. A crowded pan steams the chicken; cook to 165F.","s":1},{"t":"Season the yogurt sauce boldly with grated garlic and salt, since the rice and chicken are mild.","s":2}],"rl":"PFAXXXXZBVVXGXG"},"3604":{"lv":"EERRRRRREERRO","w":{"2":"Cool crunch.","3":"Juicy sweetness.","4":"Briny pops.","5":"Sharp bite.","6":"Salty creamy tang.","7":"Fresh herbal color.","10":"Classic Greek flavor for the dressing.","11":"Quinoa and chickpeas are bland without it.","12":"Minor.","9":"Dressing acid; skipped, the salad is flat."},"sub":{"9":{"x":"2 tbsp red wine vinegar + 1 tbsp water","d":"Sharper and less fruity.","k":["red wine vinegar"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Rinse quinoa well to remove its bitter saponin coating before cooking, and let it steam covered off the heat for 5 minutes.","s":0},{"t":"Spread the quinoa out to cool so it does not clump, then dress it while barely warm so it absorbs the dressing.","s":1},{"t":"Add the feta and parsley just before serving so they do not turn watery or dark.","s":4}],"rl":"BPVVXXGGFAXZZ"},"52764":{"lv":"EERORERE","w":{"2":"Sweet base for the tomato sauce.","3":"Minor fresh note.","6":"Savory depth; a small clove is plenty.","4":"Adds depth and acidity; skipped, the sauce is flatter but the dish still works."},"sub":{"4":{"x":"230 ml chicken broth + 1 tbsp white wine vinegar","d":"Less fruity but gives the same acidity.","k":["chicken broth","white wine vinegar"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Boil the shrimp only until pink, then pull them. They finish in the sauce, so overcooking at this step makes them rubbery.","s":0},{"t":"Reduce the sauce until it coats a spoon, and use the reserved shrimp liquid sparingly; it adds sea flavor but is salty.","s":1},{"t":"Add the feta off the heat and stir only briefly. It should soften into creamy chunks rather than melting into the sauce.","s":3}],"rl":"PFXGMVXX"},"53012":{"lv":"EERRREOROO","w":{"2":"Sweet savory base.","3":"Savory depth.","4":"Concentrates the tomato sauce.","6":"A pinch to balance the tomato acidity.","7":"Classic dried herb.","8":"A pinch of warmth; skip freely.","9":"Garnish only."},"tips":[{"t":"Cook the beans until just tender before they go into the tomato. Acid in the sauce stops beans from softening further.","s":1},{"t":"Bake uncovered and do not stir. The top forms a caramelized skin while the beans absorb the sauce; stirring makes them mushy.","s":4},{"t":"Let it cool to room temperature before serving; the flavor improves, and a drizzle of raw olive oil on top finishes it.","s":5}],"rl":"PFXXXVWXXG"},"70":{"lv":"ERRRRRERRO","w":{"1":"Cool crunch.","2":"Juicy sweetness.","3":"Sharp bite; soak in cold water to mellow.","4":"Briny pops.","5":"Salty creamy tang.","8":"Classic Greek flavor for the dressing.","9":"Garnish only.","7":"Adds brightness; skipped, the dish still works but tastes flatter."},"sub":{"7":{"x":"2 tbsp red wine vinegar + 1 tbsp water","d":"Sharper and less fruity.","k":["red wine vinegar"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Rinse and drain the chickpeas, then pat them dry so they soak up the dressing. Lightly mash a few to thicken it.","s":0},{"t":"Soak the sliced onion in cold water for 5 minutes. This salad has no cooking to soften the raw bite.","s":0},{"t":"Dress just before serving; the cucumber and tomato release water and dilute the dressing.","s":2}],"rl":"PVVXXGFAXG"},"71":{"lv":"ERRRREERRO","w":{"3":"Greek herbal flavor for the marinade.","4":"Savory punch.","7":"Crunch in the pita and the tzatziki.","8":"Freshness.","9":"Sharp bite.","1":"One of several fats or only sweats aromatics; skipping loses some flavor but dish still cooks.","2":"Adds brightness; skipped, the dish still works but tastes flatter."},"sub":{"1":{"x":"2 tbsp vegetable oil","d":"Less fruity but works the same for marinating and cooking.","k":["vegetable oil"]},"2":{"x":"1.5 tbsp white wine vinegar + 1 tbsp water","d":"Sharper and less fruity.","k":["white wine vinegar"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Pound the chicken to an even thickness so it cooks through to 165F in 5 to 6 minutes a side without drying; rest 5 minutes before slicing.","s":0},{"t":"Squeeze the grated cucumber dry and salt it, otherwise the tzatziki turns watery.","s":1},{"t":"Warm the pitas briefly in a dry pan so they bend without cracking.","s":2}],"rl":"PFAXXBXVVX"},"18":{"lv":"EEEEERORO","w":{"5":"Wilts into the sauce for color and freshness.","6":"Heat; skip freely.","7":"Feta is salty, so go light.","8":"Minor."},"tips":[{"t":"Use a dish that holds the tomatoes in a snug layer under the oil so they blister and burst rather than dry out.","s":0},{"t":"Add pasta water a splash at a time while mashing. It emulsifies the oil, feta and tomato into a creamy sauce.","s":3}],"rl":"VPXFBVXZZ"},"115":{"lv":"EERRRRER","w":{"2":"Cool crunch against the hot salty halloumi.","3":"Juicy sweetness that cuts the salty cheese.","4":"Protein and heft; skip and the bowl is lighter.","5":"Creamy sauce that holds the bowl together; skip and it is drier, so add extra oil and lemon.","7":"Cuts the salty halloumi; the bowl is heavy without it.","6":"Main or only cooking fat; skipped, the food sticks, browns poorly and the dish loses richness."},"sub":{"6":{"x":"2 tbsp vegetable oil","d":"Less fruity; still fine for frying and drizzling.","k":["vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Toast the dry quinoa in the pot for a minute before adding water for a nuttier flavor.","s":0},{"t":"Pat the halloumi dry and fry in a hot nonstick pan, 2 minutes a side, until deep golden. It softens but does not melt.","s":1},{"t":"Top the bowls with the halloumi just before serving. It turns rubbery as it cools.","s":3}],"rl":"PBVVPXFA"},"53006":{"lv":"EEEERERE","w":{"4":"Salty nutty crust in the topping; grated cheddar works.","6":"Deepens the tomato flavor; skipped, the base is thinner and less rich."},"sub":{"4":{"x":"3 tbsp grated cheddar","d":"Milder, melts and browns a little more.","k":["cheddar"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Brown the beef without stirring for the first minutes so it sears; it must reach 160F before the sauce goes in.","s":0},{"t":"Microwave the pricked eggplant until completely collapsed and soft. It should yield with no resistance, since it will not cook further under the grill.","s":1},{"t":"Spread the yogurt topping to the edges and grill until golden, 3 to 4 minutes. The egg sets it into a bechamel-like crust, so keep watch.","s":3}],"rl":"PVTTGVXB"},"53010":{"lv":"RERRORERRER","w":{"0":"Absorbs the juices and keeps the burgers tender; skip and they are denser.","2":"Warm earthy spice that gives the burgers their flavor.","3":"Citrusy warmth that complements the cumin.","4":"Color and mild warmth.","5":"Savory note in the lamb.","7":"A bun is a serving choice; use pita or lettuce wraps instead.","8":"Provides the crunch and body of the tzatziki.","10":"Fresh note in the tzatziki.","6":"Frying medium; without it nothing can be deep or shallow fried and crisped."},"sub":{"0":{"x":"1/4 cup fine breadcrumbs","d":"Binds and lightens similarly, without the nutty chew.","k":["breadcrumbs"]},"6":{"x":"1 tbsp vegetable oil","d":"Less fruity but fries the same.","k":["vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Squeeze the cooked bulgur dry in a towel. Wet bulgur makes mushy burgers that fall apart on the grill.","s":0},{"t":"Twist the grated cucumber in a towel to wring it out or the tzatziki turns watery.","s":1},{"t":"Press a thumb dimple into each patty so it does not dome, and cook to 160F; lamb burgers are done at 160F with no pink.","s":2}],"rl":"TPXXXXFBVXX"},"53011":{"lv":"EROREERRRRR","w":{"2":"Adds heat to the chili-garlic butter; skip if you prefer mild.","3":"Savory note in the paste.","6":"Briny pops that season the salad.","7":"Sharp bite and crunch.","8":"Salty creamy tang.","9":"Fresh herbal lift.","1":"One of several fats or only sweats aromatics; skipping loses some flavor but dish still cooks.","10":"Adds brightness; skipped, the dish still works but tastes flatter."},"sub":{"1":{"x":"2 tbsp olive oil","d":"Less rich; mix with the chili and garlic as a paste.","k":["olive oil"]},"10":{"x":"1 tbsp red wine vinegar","d":"Sharper and less fruity.","k":["red wine vinegar"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Drain the rinsed quinoa very well; leftover water dilutes the dressing and makes the salad soggy.","s":0},{"t":"Cook the chicken to 165F on a hot griddle and rest it a few minutes before slicing so it stays juicy.","s":1},{"t":"Quinoa is bland, so season the salad boldly with salt and lemon zest as well as juice.","s":2}],"rl":"BFXXPFXXGXA"},"3229":{"lv":"ERRRRERERROOEE","w":{"2":"Sweet base for the filling.","3":"Fresh sharp onion note.","4":"Savory depth.","6":"Creamy richness that balances the salty feta; skipped, the filling is saltier and drier.","8":"The signature herb of spanakopita; skipped, it tastes plain.","9":"Fresh herb lift.","10":"A pinch of classic seasoning.","11":"Minor.","1":"One of several fats or only sweats aromatics; skipping loses some flavor but dish still cooks."},"sub":{"1":{"x":"2 tbsp butter","d":"Richer; sweat the onions gently.","k":["butter"]},"6":{"x":"1 cup cottage cheese, drained well","d":"Lumpier and tangier; drain it so the filling is not wet.","k":["cottage cheese"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Wring the thawed spinach in a towel until it is bone dry. Wet spinach makes the filling soak into the phyllo and turns it soggy.","s":1},{"t":"Brush each sheet lightly from edge to edge, since dry corners shatter. Butter between layers is what gives the shatteringly crisp finish.","s":2},{"t":"Score the squares before baking, cutting only through the top layers. It cuts cleanly after the rest rather than crushing.","s":4}],"rl":"VFXXXPFTXXXZSF"},"3232":{"lv":"EERRREERRR","w":{"2":"Sharp bite.","3":"Crunch and mild bitterness.","4":"Briny pops that season the salad.","8":"The classic Greek herb; skipped, it tastes like a generic salad.","9":"Feta and olives are salty, so go light.","7":"Adds brightness; skipped, the dish still works but tastes flatter."},"sub":{"7":{"x":"1 tbsp lemon juice","d":"Fruitier and brighter than red wine vinegar.","k":["lemon juice"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Use ripe in-season tomatoes cut in thick wedges. Pale off-season ones taste watery; a pinch of sugar helps.","s":0},{"t":"Dress right before serving. The tomato juice mixing with the oil is the best part of the bowl, so serve with bread to mop it up.","s":1},{"t":"Put the feta on top as a slab with the oregano and a drizzle of oil, and let people break it up.","s":2}],"rl":"VVXVXPFAXZ"},"3233":{"lv":"EEERRRR","w":{"3":"Gives silkiness and body.","4":"Brightens the yogurt; a splash of vinegar works.","5":"Fresh herbal note; mint works too.","6":"Needed to bring out the yogurt and cucumber."},"sub":{"5":{"x":"1 tbsp chopped fresh mint","d":"Cooler and sweeter than dill; both are used in Greece.","k":["mint"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Salt the cucumber and wring it out in a towel until no more liquid drips. Otherwise the tzatziki weeps and turns thin.","s":0},{"t":"Use full-fat Greek yogurt. If it is loose, strain it in a cloth for an hour first.","s":1},{"t":"Raw garlic sharpens overnight. If you are making it a day ahead, use less garlic and taste before serving.","s":2}],"rl":"VBXFAXZ"},"3235":{"lv":"EEEREEROO","w":{"3":"Savory depth.","6":"Potatoes need salt to taste like anything.","7":"Minor.","8":"Garnish only.","5":"Main cooking liquid; skipped, the dish or grain is dry and underdone."},"sub":{"5":{"x":"1 cup water + 1/4 tsp salt + 1 tbsp more olive oil","d":"Less savory but still tastes bright and lemony.","k":["water","olive oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Pour the liquid around the potatoes, not over them. They roast in it at the bottom and crisp on top.","s":2},{"t":"Turn the potatoes only once or twice. Leave them alone so a golden crust forms; the starch and lemon make a thick sauce.","s":3},{"t":"Squeeze fresh lemon on at the end, since roasting mellows the lemon flavor.","s":4}],"rl":"BFAXXMZZG"},"3353":{"lv":"EREROO","w":{"1":"Makes a light crust that fries golden; without it the cheese sticks and browns unevenly.","3":"The squeeze of acid cuts the salty fried cheese.","4":"A pinch of herbal flavor.","5":"Sweet contrast to the salty cheese.","2":"Frying medium; without it nothing can be deep or shallow fried and crisped."},"sub":{"2":{"x":"2 tbsp vegetable oil","d":"Less fruity but fries the same.","k":["vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Pat the cheese dry and dredge right before frying. Wet flour turns to paste and burns before the cheese colors.","s":0},{"t":"Fry in shimmering oil and leave the slabs alone for the first minute so the crust forms. Halloumi softens but does not melt.","s":2},{"t":"Serve immediately. The cheese turns rubbery as it cools.","s":4}],"rl":"PTFAXW"},"3354":{"lv":"EREERRRR","w":{"1":"Simmered with the peas for sweet flavor and discarded after.","4":"Brightens the earthy puree.","5":"Briny pops; the classic Santorini topping.","6":"Raw sharp crunch against the smooth puree.","7":"Without it the puree is bland."},"tips":[{"t":"Skim the foam and stir often; split peas sink and stick to the bottom as they cook. Add water if it dries out before they are soft.","s":1},{"t":"Blend while the peas are still hot. They puree smoother and absorb the oil better.","s":2},{"t":"It sets as it cools to a hummus-like thickness. If it is too stiff, loosen with a little warm water.","s":4}],"rl":"PXMFAXGZ"},"3355":{"lv":"EEERREERORO","w":{"3":"Sweet base.","4":"Savory depth.","7":"Classic herb.","8":"Garnish, but adds fresh color at the end.","9":"A lot of vegetables need 1 1/2 teaspoons to taste seasoned.","10":"Minor."},"tips":[{"t":"Cut the vegetables to similar sizes, with the potatoes thinnest, so they all finish together.","s":1},{"t":"Stir halfway so the edges brown, but only once or twice; more makes it mush.","s":3},{"t":"Let briam rest 10 minutes. It tastes best warm or at room temperature with a drizzle of raw olive oil.","s":5}],"rl":"BVVXXVFXGZZ"},"3482":{"lv":"ERERORRO","w":{"1":"Raw sharpness that makes the dip pop.","4":"Fresh herbal note.","5":"Extra brightness alongside the vinegar.","6":"Needed to bring out the smoky eggplant.","7":"Minor.","3":"Adds brightness; skipped, the dish still works but tastes flatter."},"sub":{"3":{"x":"2 tbsp white wine vinegar","d":"Slightly sharper and less fruity.","k":["white wine vinegar"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Char the eggplant directly over a flame until the skin blisters black and the flesh collapses. The oven alone gives much less smoky flavor.","s":1},{"t":"Drain the flesh in a colander and press gently; the bitter liquid makes the dip watery.","s":2},{"t":"Beat the oil in slowly so it emulsifies into the pulp, and keep the flesh chopped rather than pureed for texture.","s":4}],"rl":"VXFAGAZZ"},"3483":{"lv":"EEEERE","w":{"4":"Extra brightness alongside the vinegar.","3":"Defining sharp note of skordalia; skipped, the dip is flat."},"sub":{"3":{"x":"3 tbsp white wine vinegar","d":"Slightly sharper and less fruity; use 2 tbsp if tasting too sharp.","k":["white wine vinegar"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Rice or mash the potatoes while hot. Cold potatoes or a food processor turn them gluey.","s":1},{"t":"Pound the garlic with the salt until a fine paste; raw garlic is fierce, and it sharpens as it sits, so go lighter if making ahead.","s":2},{"t":"Beat in the oil in a slow stream like mayonnaise, loosening with the reserved water. Too much oil at once leaves it greasy.","s":3}],"rl":"BXFAAZ"},"3488":{"lv":"EERRERRRRO","w":{"2":"Sweet savory base that melts into the rice; skipping gives a lighter, greener-tasting pilaf.","3":"Adds a fresh, mild oniony note; leave out and use a little more yellow onion.","5":"Dill is the signature herb of spanakorizo; skipping loses the classic Greek flavor and leaves the rice tasting plain.","6":"Gives gentle sweetness, color and umami to the rice; without it the pilaf is paler and more lemony.","7":"Brightens the spinach and cuts the olive oil richness; skipping makes the rice taste flat.","8":"Seasons the rice and spinach as the liquid is absorbed; it can't be fixed evenly later, so add it with the water.","9":"Background warmth only; skip without much loss."},"tips":[{"t":"Cook the spinach until it has released and mostly lost its water before the rice goes in. Spinach adds moisture, so if the rice is still firm at the end, add a splash of hot water and cover for a few minutes.","s":1},{"t":"Stir the dill and lemon in only at the end, off the heat. Heat dulls both, and the 5-minute rest lets the rice finish steaming without turning gluey.","s":5},{"t":"Finish with a thread of raw olive oil and crumbled feta at the table, which is how many Greek tavernas serve it.","s":-1}],"rl":"VBXXFXXAZZ"},"3490":{"lv":"ERROERERRO","w":{"1":"Simmers into the puree for sweetness; skip and add an extra garlic clove, but the flavor will be flatter.","2":"Mild savory background after the long simmer; skipping leaves a blander puree.","3":"Subtle herbal depth during the long simmer; skipping is barely noticeable.","5":"Cuts the earthy richness of the peas and lifts the olive oil; skipping makes the puree heavy.","7":"Salty briny punch that is traditional on Santorini fava; skip and add more salt and lemon at the table.","8":"Sharp crunchy contrast to the silky puree; skip or use extra capers instead.","9":"Garnish for color and freshness; no effect on the puree."},"sub":{"8":{"x":"1/4 cup finely chopped yellow onion, rinsed in cold water","d":"Harsher and less sweet than red onion; the rinse tames the bite.","k":["onion"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Stir often during the last 15 minutes. Split peas are starchy and scorch on the bottom of the pot, which gives the puree a bitter taste you cannot blend away.","s":1},{"t":"Blend while the peas are still hot and stream in the oil. The emulsion turns glossy and silky, and if it is too thick, loosen it with a few spoonfuls of the hot cooking water.","s":3},{"t":"The puree stiffens as it cools. Spread it while still warm so it sets into a smooth surface that holds the toppings, and serve with lemon wedges.","s":4}],"rl":"PXXXFAZXGG"},"3231":{"lv":"EEEEEROO","w":{"5":"Broth is often already salted, so season at the end after the lemon goes in, since lemon changes how salty it tastes.","6":"Light warmth only; white pepper is traditional, and skipping is fine.","7":"Fresh herbal lift on top; skip and the soup is still classic, just plainer."},"tips":[{"t":"Ladle hot broth into the eggs a few spoonfuls at a time while whisking nonstop. If the eggs warm too fast they scramble, so it is better to go slower than feels necessary.","s":3},{"t":"Once the egg mixture is back in the pot, hold it just under a simmer. Boiling curdles it, but if you see flecks, blend briefly with an immersion blender to bring it back to a smooth soup.","s":4},{"t":"The rice keeps absorbing broth as the soup sits. Thin it with hot broth when reheating, and reheat gently, never at a boil.","s":-1}],"rl":"MBPTAZZG"},"3234":{"lv":"ERROEEEERRO","w":{"1":"Sweetens and slightly moistens the nuts; without it the filling tastes bland against the syrup.","2":"Warm spice that is classic in Greek baklava; skip for a plainer nut flavor.","3":"Tiny background perfume; omit with no real loss.","8":"Adds floral flavor and helps keep the syrup from crystallizing; sugar alone works.","9":"Cuts the sweetness and helps prevent a grainy, cloying syrup; skipping makes it taste one-note.","10":"Rounds the syrup; barely noticeable beside honey and cinnamon."},"sub":{"8":{"x":"1/2 cup sugar + 2 tbsp water, added to the syrup","d":"Less floral and slightly more prone to crystallizing, so keep the lemon juice.","k":["sugar"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Keep the unused phyllo under a slightly damp towel and brush butter thinly from edge to edge. Torn sheets are fine in the middle of the stack, and clarified butter bakes up crisper.","s":1},{"t":"Cut all the way through to the pan bottom before baking. Cutting after baking shatters the brittle top layers.","s":3},{"t":"Pour cooled syrup over the hot pastry. The temperature contrast lets the layers soak up syrup while staying crisp, whereas hot syrup on hot baklava goes soggy.","s":6}],"rl":"BWXXSFMWWAX"},"53007":{"lv":"EREEEERREO","w":{"0":"Digestive biscuits give the crust a toasty, slightly salty crumb; any plain sweet biscuit works.","1":"Adds crunch and nuttiness to the crust and topping; skip and use extra biscuit crumbs.","6":"Brightens the rich mascarpone; skip and use more orange zest.","7":"Aromatic citrus note that pairs with the honey; lemon alone works.","9":"Fresh acidity to cut the richness; a serving extra only."},"sub":{"0":{"x":"100 g graham crackers or shortbread biscuits","d":"Graham crackers are slightly sweeter and less salty; shortbread makes a richer, buttery crust.","k":["graham cracker","shortbread"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Press the crumb mix firmly with the base of a glass, and up the sides if you like. A tightly packed crust holds together when sliced instead of crumbling.","s":0},{"t":"Stir the mascarpone and yogurt gently and stop once smooth, because overworked mascarpone turns grainy. Add the eggs one at a time and mix only until combined so the batter does not puff and crack.","s":1},{"t":"Bake until the edges are set but the center still wobbles slightly, then cool slowly in the switched-off oven with the door ajar. Fast temperature changes cause cracks.","s":-1}],"rl":"SGFSFSXXWG"},"3286":{"lv":"ERRREERERR","w":{"1":"Carries the garlic and oregano into the chicken and helps it brown; any neutral oil works.","2":"Acid brightens and lightly tenderizes; skipping makes the chicken blander.","3":"Flavors both the marinade and the tzatziki; skip and the sauce tastes flat.","6":"Gives tzatziki its cool, fresh body; skip and it is just garlicky yogurt.","7":"Soft pita is the traditional wrap; flatbread works but changes the texture.","8":"Juicy freshness and acidity; skip and add extra onion or shredded lettuce.","9":"Sharp crunch against the rich chicken; skip or quick-pickle it."},"sub":{"7":{"x":"4 large flour tortillas or naan, warmed","d":"Thinner and less pillowy than pita; warm well so they fold without cracking.","k":["flour tortilla","naan"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Marinate only the 15 minutes called for, or a few hours at most. Lemon juice makes chicken mealy if it sits too long.","s":0},{"t":"Squeeze the grated cucumber hard in a clean towel until dry. Leftover water thins the tzatziki and makes the wraps soggy within minutes.","s":1},{"t":"Cook the chicken in a very hot skillet in a single layer, in two batches if needed, so it browns instead of steaming. Thighs are safe at 165F and stay juicy up to 175F.","s":2}],"rl":"PFAXXXVBVX"},"3209":{"lv":"EEEERRRRO","w":{"4":"Adds sweetness, body and gloss to the glaze; without it the sauce is thinner and less tangy.","5":"Warm, zingy note that defines the marinade; skip and the chicken tastes sweeter and flatter.","6":"Savory depth in both marinade and glaze; skip with a milder result.","7":"Acid to balance the sugar; any mild vinegar or lemon works.","8":"Background warmth only; skip freely."},"sub":{"4":{"x":"2 tbsp tomato paste + 2 tbsp water + 1 tbsp brown sugar","d":"Less tangy than ketchup; add a splash of vinegar if the glaze tastes too sweet.","k":["tomato paste","brown sugar"]},"7":{"x":"1 tbsp apple cider vinegar or 2 tsp lemon juice","d":"Slightly fruitier and sharper than rice vinegar; the difference is small in the glaze.","k":["apple cider vinegar","lemon juice"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Marinate bone-in thighs for 4 to 12 hours, but not much longer. Pineapple juice contains bromelain, an enzyme that turns the meat mushy over a long soak.","s":1},{"t":"Reduce the reserved sauce until it coats the back of a spoon. It thickens more as it cools, and using sauce that never touched raw chicken keeps the glaze safe.","s":2},{"t":"Keep the heat at medium and turn the chicken often (that is what huli huli means). Brown sugar scorches fast, so brush the glaze on only in the last 10 minutes, and pull thighs at 175F for tender meat.","s":4}],"rl":"PMXWXXXAZ"},"3210":{"lv":"ERRROEREEREE","w":{"1":"Absorbs juices so the patties stay tender; skip and they are a bit denser.","2":"Binds the patty; skip and use an extra 2 tbsp breadcrumbs soaked in milk.","3":"Seasons the beef all the way through; unsalted patties taste flat even under gravy.","4":"Minor background heat; skip freely.","5":"Fat for the roux; butter adds flavor, but oil or beef fat from the pan works.","6":"Gives the gravy sweetness and body; skip and the gravy is plainer.","9":"Adds salt, color and umami to the gravy; skip and reduce the broth a little longer, then salt to taste."},"sub":{"5":{"x":"2 tbsp vegetable oil or rendered beef fat from the pan","d":"Gravy is slightly less rich and rounded; cook the flour a full minute either way.","k":["vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Mix the beef gently and only until combined; overworked patties turn tough. Press a shallow dimple in the center so they stay flat as they cook, and cook to 160F inside with no pink.","s":0},{"t":"Leave the browned bits in the pan to build the gravy. Cook the flour a full minute in the fat to lose the raw taste, then whisk in the broth gradually to avoid lumps.","s":2},{"t":"Fry the eggs sunny side up over medium-low heat so the whites set while the yolk stays runny; the yolk melts into the gravy as a second sauce.","s":4}],"rl":"PTTZZFXTMXPB"},"3600":{"lv":"EEERORERRROO","w":{"3":"Gives the dressing bright acidity; skip and use a bit of extra soy, though it will taste saltier.","4":"Rounds the salty soy; omit or use a pinch of sugar.","5":"Fresh bite and color in the dressing and on top; skip for a milder bowl.","7":"Creamy contrast to the lean tuna; skip for a firmer, leaner bowl.","8":"Cool crunch; skip or use radish instead.","9":"Adds protein and texture; skip and use extra cucumber.","10":"Nutty crunch garnish; skip with little loss.","11":"Mild heat only; skip or use red pepper flakes."},"tips":[{"t":"Cut the tuna with a very sharp knife in long drawing strokes so the cubes stay clean instead of shredded. Keep it on ice or in the fridge until the moment you dress it.","s":1},{"t":"Dress the fish only 10 to 15 minutes ahead. Soy salt and vinegar acid begin to firm the surface, and longer turns the texture dense and cooked-looking.","s":1},{"t":"Assemble and serve right away, adding avocado last so it does not brown or wet the rice. Use only fish sold as sushi-grade and eat it the same day.","s":4}],"rl":"PXFAWXBFVPGX"},"52964":{"lv":"RRRROOEEEEOR","w":{"0":"Fat that carries the spices and coats the rice; ghee or oil also works.","1":"Sweet aromatic base for the rice; skipping gives a thinner flavor.","2":"Floral perfume that is part of kedgeree's pilaf-style rice; skip and the rice is less aromatic.","3":"Gives the rice its golden color and earthy note; skip for paler rice.","4":"Faint warm sweetness; omit with little change.","5":"Subtle background; omit.","10":"Garnish for freshness and color.","11":"Squeezed over at the table to cut the richness of the smoked fish and butter; skipping makes it feel heavy."},"sub":{"0":{"x":"3 tbsp ghee or 3 tbsp vegetable oil","d":"Ghee is nearly identical; oil loses the buttery richness.","k":["ghee","vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Stir once when the stock boils to free rice from the bottom, then keep the lid on. Lifting it or stirring releases starch and makes the rice gummy.","s":3},{"t":"Poach the haddock only until it just flakes, about 4 minutes, then lift it out. The residual steam in the rice finishes it, and overcooked smoked haddock turns dry and stringy.","s":4},{"t":"Fold the fish and eggs in with a fork rather than a spoon so the grains stay separate and the fish keeps its flakes. Serve immediately and refrigerate leftovers within an hour or two.","s":7}],"rl":"FXXXXXBMPPGA"},"53076":{"lv":"EER","w":{"2":"Seasons the egg coating; bread is bland, so skipping leaves the omelette flat."},"tips":[{"t":"Use slightly stale or lightly toasted bread. Fresh, soft slices soak up too much egg and turn soggy in the middle.","s":2},{"t":"Cook until no wet egg is visible on either side so it is fully set, 2 to 3 minutes per side over medium heat; higher heat browns the outside before the egg cooks.","s":3},{"t":"For the street-style version, beat finely chopped onion, green chili and cilantro into the egg before dipping.","s":0}],"rl":"BPZ"},"7":{"lv":"EERRREREROEEREO","w":{"2":"Sweet base for the sauce; skip and the sauce tastes thinner.","3":"Savory depth in the sauce; skipping makes it noticeably flatter.","4":"Fresh warm heat that is part of butter chicken's flavor; ground ginger is a fair stand-in.","6":"Earthy backbone to the spice blend; skip and rely on the garam masala.","8":"Adds earthy color to the sauce; skip for a paler sauce.","9":"Faint warm-sweet note; omit with little loss.","12":"Brightens the rich cream and butter; skip and the sauce feels heavy.","14":"Garnish for freshness only."},"sub":{"4":{"x":"1/2 tsp ground ginger","d":"Warmer and less zingy than fresh ginger; bloom it with the other spices.","k":["ground ginger"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Brown the chicken only until golden outside; it finishes in the sauce, which keeps lean breast from drying out. It is done at 165F inside.","s":0},{"t":"Cook the tomato paste and spices in the butter for a full minute until the paste darkens. This removes the raw tomato taste and blooms the spices.","s":1},{"t":"Lower the heat before stirring in the cream, and add the lemon last. A hard boil with acid can split the sauce.","s":2}],"rl":"PFXXXXXXXXMFABG"},"67":{"lv":"EEERREREEEEROO","w":{"3":"Earthy backbone to the spice blend; skip and lean on extra garam masala.","4":"Mild sweet pepper that gives the red color; skipping makes the sauce paler.","6":"Fresh warmth in the masala; skip and the sauce is a bit duller.","7":"Fat for the masala base; ghee or oil also works.","11":"Serving base for the sauce; skip if you are serving with naan only.","12":"Serving extra; use rice alone if you don't have it.","13":"Garnish only."},"sub":{"7":{"x":"3 tbsp ghee or 3 tbsp vegetable oil","d":"Ghee is nearly the same; oil is less rich, so finish with the cream.","k":["ghee","vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Marinate at least 4 hours, ideally overnight. The lactic acid in yogurt tenderizes the thighs gently, and the coating chars into the smoky flavor of a tandoor.","s":0},{"t":"Broil on a rack close to the element until the edges blacken in spots. Those charred bits dissolve into the sauce and give tikka masala its signature flavor. Chicken must reach 165F, though thighs stay juicy beyond that.","s":1},{"t":"Simmer the tomatoes until the oil separates so the sauce tastes rich rather than raw, and stir in the cream off a hard boil so it stays smooth.","s":2}],"rl":"PXXXXXXFXVFBBG"},"3171":{"lv":"EEEERRRERRRORR","w":{"4":"Savory depth in the sauce; skip and the sauce is flatter.","5":"Fresh warmth that lifts the spinach; skip and add a pinch of ground ginger.","6":"Adds acidity and body to the sauce; skip and the gravy is flatter.","8":"Earthy, citrusy backbone of the spice mix; skip and add a bit more cumin.","9":"Color and mild earthiness; skipping makes the sauce duller.","10":"Aromatic finish added at the end; skipping loses the fragrant top note.","11":"Heat only; skip for a mild palak paneer.","12":"Makes the sauce silky and softens the spinach edge; skip for a lighter, more vegetal dish.","13":"Seasons the sauce; add gradually since paneer and spinach are bland.","2":"Main or only cooking fat; skipped, the food sticks, browns poorly and the dish loses richness."},"sub":{"2":{"x":"2 tbsp ghee or vegetable oil","d":"Ghee tastes nearly the same; oil is less rich, so keep the cream.","k":["ghee","vegetable oil"]},"12":{"x":"1/4 cup whole milk + 1 tbsp butter, stirred in off the heat","d":"Less rich; add at the end off the heat since milk can split in an acidic sauce.","k":["whole milk","butter"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Shock the blanched spinach in ice water to lock in the green, and blend it smoothly. If using frozen, thaw it and squeeze out all the water first or the sauce turns thin and watery.","s":0},{"t":"After frying, rest the paneer cubes in warm salted water for about 10 minutes. They stay soft and spongy in the sauce instead of rubbery.","s":1},{"t":"Stir in the cream and garam masala at the end and keep the sauce at a gentle simmer. Long cooking dulls the spinach to olive-drab and can split the cream.","s":5}],"rl":"VPFXXXVXXXXXFZ"},"3172":{"lv":"EERREERROEERRRO","w":{"0":"Cooking fat for the base; ghee or olive oil also works.","2":"Savory depth to the masala; skipping makes it flatter.","3":"Fresh warmth that rounds the tomatoes; skip and add a pinch of ground ginger.","6":"Earthy color and mild bitterness; skipping leaves it duller.","7":"Mild sweet pepper for color; skip with little loss.","8":"Heat only; skip for a mild chana masala.","11":"Aromatic finish added at the end; skipping loses top-note fragrance.","12":"Brightens the tomato and spices at the finish; skip and the dish is flatter.","13":"Seasons the sauce and the chickpeas; add gradually.","14":"Garnish for freshness."},"sub":{"0":{"x":"2 tbsp ghee or olive oil","d":"Ghee adds nutty richness; olive oil is a bit fruitier but fine here.","k":["ghee","olive oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Take the onions to deep golden, about 8 minutes. That caramelized base gives the sweetness and color; pale onions leave the masala sharp and thin.","s":0},{"t":"Cook the tomatoes until the oil visibly separates at the edges before adding the chickpeas. Skipping this step leaves the sauce acidic and raw tasting.","s":3},{"t":"Mash a few chickpeas against the side of the pot to thicken the sauce without flour. If it tightens up, loosen with a splash of water.","s":4}],"rl":"FXXXXXXXXAPXAZG"},"3173":{"lv":"EERRRRREEERRRRROE","w":{"2":"Warm, fresh note in the marinade; skip and the chicken is milder.","3":"Savory depth in the marinade; skipping makes it flatter.","4":"Fragrant spice blend that defines the masala; skipping loses aroma, though cumin and cayenne still carry flavor.","5":"Gives golden color and earthy depth; skip with a paler dish.","6":"Heat; reduce or skip for a milder biryani.","9":"Fat for frying the onions; any neutral oil works, ghee adds richness.","10":"Perfumes the rice as it cooks; skip and the rice is less aromatic.","11":"Warm, sweet perfume in the rice; omit with small loss.","12":"Fresh green layer that brightens the rich rice; skipping makes it heavier.","13":"Cool fragrant note typical of biryani; skip and use extra cilantro.","14":"Saffron milk gives color and floral aroma; skip and the biryani is still good but plainer.","15":"Adds richness to the top layer as it steams; skip with little change."},"sub":{"9":{"x":"1/4 cup ghee or sunflower oil","d":"Ghee gives a richer, nuttier flavor; sunflower oil is neutral and also browns onions well.","k":["ghee","sunflower oil"]},"14":{"x":"1/4 cup warm milk + 1/4 tsp turmeric","d":"Gives yellow color but not saffron's floral aroma.","k":["milk","turmeric"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Fry the onions slowly and stir often until deep brown, then spread them on paper to crisp. Adding them at the right brownness balances sweetness without bitterness.","s":1},{"t":"Parboil the rice to about 70 percent: a grain should break when pressed but still have a white core. It finishes cooking in steam, so overcooked rice here turns mushy.","s":3},{"t":"Seal the pot with foil under a tight lid and keep the heat very low, using a diffuser or tawa if you have one. The steam cooks the rice and chicken together; bone-in thighs should reach at least 165F inside.","s":5}],"rl":"PMXXXXXBXFXXGGXFZ"},"3177":{"lv":"EEEERRRRRRERER","w":{"4":"Savory base for the sauce; skipping flattens the korma.","5":"Fresh warmth that supports the nuts and cream; skip and use a pinch of ground ginger.","6":"Mild citrus warmth typical of korma; skipping makes it less aromatic.","7":"Earthy spice; skip and add a bit more coriander.","8":"Gentle color; skipping makes the sauce paler.","9":"Aromatic finish; skipping loses fragrance but the sauce is still good.","11":"Adds richness and mellows the spices; skip for a lighter korma.","13":"Seasons the sauce; the marinade salt is separate, so taste at the end.","2":"Main or only cooking fat; skipped, the food sticks, browns poorly and the dish loses richness.","10":"Thickens and flavors the korma; skipped, the sauce is thin."},"sub":{"2":{"x":"2 tbsp ghee or vegetable oil","d":"Ghee is nearly identical; oil is less rich.","k":["ghee","vegetable oil"]},"10":{"x":"1/3 cup ground cashews or 3 tbsp smooth cashew or almond butter","d":"Cashews are classic and sweeter; nut butter blends in more easily.","k":["cashews","almond butter"],"wn":"Contains tree nuts (cashew); not safe for nut allergies"}},"tips":[{"t":"Cook the onions only to pale gold, never brown. Korma is a pale, delicate sauce, and dark onions turn it into something closer to a regular curry.","s":1},{"t":"Take the marinated chicken out of the fridge 20 minutes ahead and keep the heat at a simmer. Cold yogurt added to a boiling pot is what makes korma split.","s":3},{"t":"Stir the ground almonds in well and scrape the bottom as the sauce simmers because they thicken and can stick. Add the cream and garam masala only at the end, and cook chicken to 165F.","s":4}],"rl":"PMFXXXXXXXTFMZ"},"3178":{"lv":"EERRRRERREEROR","w":{"2":"Fresh warmth that supports the vinegar paste; skip and use a pinch of ground ginger.","3":"Savory depth; skipping leaves the sauce sharper and flatter.","4":"Earthy backbone of the spice paste; skip and use more coriander or cayenne.","5":"Deep red color with mild heat; skip and the curry looks and tastes less rounded.","7":"Earthy color; skipping leaves the sauce duller.","8":"Adds pungency typical of vindaloo; skip and add a pinch more cayenne.","9":"Fat for browning the onions; ghee or another neutral oil works.","11":"Adds body and umami to the sauce; skipping makes it a little thinner.","12":"Stretches the dish and absorbs the sauce; skipping leaves a thicker, meat-only curry.","13":"Seasons the sauce; taste at the end because the vinegar masks salt."},"sub":{"9":{"x":"3 tbsp ghee or any neutral oil","d":"Ghee adds richness; oil is cleaner and lets the vinegar and spices stand out.","k":["ghee","vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Marinate at least an hour but under 24. The vinegar tenderizes at first, but too long gives the chicken a mushy, chalky texture.","s":0},{"t":"Brown the onions well, about 10 minutes. Their sweetness is what balances the tart vinegar and the heat.","s":1},{"t":"Vindaloo should be thick and cling to the meat. Uncover and reduce if it is thin, and expect it to taste even better the next day as the vinegar mellows.","s":4}],"rl":"PAXXXXXXXFXXBZ"},"3185":{"lv":"ERERRERROOERRO","w":{"0":"Cooking fat for the cumin and onion; ghee or any neutral oil works.","1":"Tempered in oil for a toasty base; skipping loses an earthy note.","3":"Fresh warmth for the beans; skip and use a pinch of ground ginger.","4":"Savory depth in the gravy; skipping makes it flat.","6":"Mild citrusy backbone of the spices; skip and add more cumin.","7":"Color and earthiness; skipping makes the gravy duller.","8":"Adds color and mild heat; skip with little loss.","9":"Heat only; skip for a mild rajma.","11":"Aromatic finish; skipping loses top-note fragrance.","12":"Seasons the gravy and the beans; add gradually since canned beans may already be salted.","13":"Garnish for freshness."},"sub":{"0":{"x":"2 tbsp ghee or any neutral oil","d":"Ghee adds nutty richness; neutral oil keeps it lighter.","k":["ghee","vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Toast the cumin in hot oil until it sizzles and darkens, about 30 seconds. That tempering flavors the whole base, but it burns quickly, so have the onion ready.","s":0},{"t":"Mash a handful of beans against the side of the pot to make the gravy creamy. Rajma should be thick and gravy-like, not brothy.","s":3},{"t":"If you use dried kidney beans instead of canned, boil them hard for at least 10 minutes after soaking. Raw kidney beans contain a toxin (lectin) that gentle simmering or a slow cooker will not destroy.","s":-1}],"rl":"FXXXXAXXXXPXZG"},"3186":{"lv":"EERRERRRROERRO","w":{"0":"Cooking fat for the onion; ghee or any neutral oil works.","2":"Fresh warmth that lifts the beef; skip and use a pinch of ground ginger.","3":"Savory depth that balances the rich beef; skipping makes it flat.","5":"Adds moisture and acidity to the keema; skip and add a spoonful of tomato paste.","6":"Earthy backbone of the spice mix; skip and add more coriander.","7":"Mild citrusy spice; skipping makes the keema less rounded.","8":"Color and earthiness; skipping makes it paler.","9":"Heat only; skip for a mild keema.","11":"Aromatic finish; skipping loses fragrance.","12":"Seasons the beef and peas; add gradually.","13":"Garnish for freshness."},"sub":{"0":{"x":"2 tbsp ghee or any neutral oil","d":"Ghee adds richness; neutral oil keeps it lighter.","k":["ghee","vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Let the beef sit undisturbed for a minute between stirs and break it into small crumbles so it browns instead of steaming. Cook it to at least 160F.","s":1},{"t":"Stir the dry spices into the browned beef and fat for a minute before adding any liquid. Blooming them in fat deepens their flavor and removes any raw taste.","s":2},{"t":"Add the frozen peas straight from the freezer near the end. Five minutes is enough, and any longer turns them olive-drab and mushy.","s":4}],"rl":"FXXXPAXXXXVXZG"},"52805":{"lv":"ROREERRRRREEOORRRERE","w":{"0":"Ground into the paste, it thickens and enriches the gravy; skip and the sauce is thinner and less creamy.","1":"Poppy seed adds subtle body to the paste; skipping makes little difference.","2":"Toasty background spice in the paste; skip with a slightly flatter masala.","5":"Extra garlic punch in the base; skipping leaves the paste enough garlic anyway.","6":"Fresh, cooling note typical of biryani; skipping makes it heavier.","7":"Fresh green note in the masala and layers; skipping makes it flatter.","8":"Gives floral aroma and golden color; skipping still gives a good biryani, just plainer.","9":"Fat for the masala and the top layer; butter or oil works.","12":"Flavors the rice cooking water; skip with minimal loss.","13":"Minor background aroma; skip freely.","14":"Warm sweet perfume in the masala; skip with small loss.","15":"Warm clove aroma; skipping is fine, but it adds the classic biryani smell.","16":"Floral perfume of the whole spices; skipping reduces aroma.","18":"Heat and color; reduce for a milder biryani."},"sub":{"0":{"x":"1/4 cup ground almonds","d":"Almonds are less sweet than cashews, but thicken the gravy the same way.","k":["almonds"],"wn":"Contains almonds (tree nut); cashews are also a tree nut"},"1":{"x":"1/2 tbsp sesame seeds or extra ground cashews","d":"Sesame gives a slightly stronger nutty flavor but the same body.","k":["sesame seed","cashews"],"wn":"Sesame seeds are a common allergen"},"8":{"x":"1/2 tsp turmeric stirred into the 1/2 cup warm milk","d":"Gives yellow color but not saffron's floral aroma.","k":["turmeric","milk"]},"9":{"x":"2 tbsp butter or vegetable oil","d":"Butter is close to ghee but less nutty; oil is neutral.","k":["butter","vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Take the pan off the heat when stirring in the yogurt and mix quickly, then return to the heat. Cold yogurt hitting a hot pan is the classic way to curdle a biryani gravy.","s":6},{"t":"Boil the rice only about 70 percent in well-salted water, until it breaks when pressed but has a white core. It finishes cooking in the layered steam, so overcooked rice here turns mushy.","s":8},{"t":"Seal the pot tightly for the final step, using foil under the lid, so no steam escapes. Check that the lamb itself is tender before layering since it cooks about an hour.","s":10}],"rl":"TTXXXXXXXFBMXXXXXPXX"},"52806":{"lv":"ERRERERRERRO","w":{"1":"Gives the red tandoori color with mild heat; skip and the chicken looks paler.","2":"Adds sweetness and juices to the first marinade; skipping makes little difference.","4":"Brushed on the chicken to promote char and prevent drying; skipping risks stuck, drier chicken.","6":"Fresh warmth in the marinade; skip and use a pinch of ground ginger.","7":"Savory punch in the marinade; skipping leaves it flatter.","9":"Earthy background spice; skip and use a little extra garam masala.","10":"Heat; skip for a milder tandoori.","11":"Faint color and earthiness; skip with little loss."},"tips":[{"t":"Slash the thighs deep in three places so the marinade penetrates and heat reaches the center evenly. Keep the lemon juice stage short, because acid makes the surface chalky.","s":0},{"t":"Use thick, strained yogurt. Thin yogurt runs off and burns in the drips instead of charring on the meat, and an overnight marinade tenderizes and flavors the most.","s":1},{"t":"Grill on a rack so heat reaches all sides and turn once. Thighs are safe at 165F but juiciest at 175F; if the char darkens before the center is done, move to a lower position.","s":2}],"rl":"ACXPFMXXXXXX"},"52808":{"lv":"EERRERRRROREEO","w":{"1":"Fat for the onion base; ghee or any neutral oil works.","2":"Savory depth in the sauce; skipping makes it flatter.","3":"Fresh warmth that is classic in rogan josh; skip and use a pinch of ground ginger.","4":"Supplies the chili and spice backbone; a homemade spice mix works but needs salt and tasting.","5":"Deep red color with mild heat, as with Kashmiri chili; skipping leaves a paler sauce.","6":"Sweet woody warmth of the whole spices; skipping thins the aroma.","7":"Floral perfume of the whole spices; skipping reduces the aroma.","8":"Warm clove perfume; skip and the sauce is a little less aromatic.","9":"Subtle background; omit.","10":"Adds body and gentle acidity; skip and add a little more paprika.","13":"Garnish for freshness."},"sub":{"1":{"x":"4 tbsp ghee or any neutral oil","d":"Ghee is richer and more traditional; neutral oil is lighter.","k":["ghee","vegetable oil"]},"4":{"x":"1 tbsp garam masala + 1 tsp ground cumin + 1 tsp ground coriander + 1/2 tsp cayenne + 1/2 tsp salt","d":"Less complex than curry paste; the paste also carries salt, so taste before adding more.","k":["garam masala","cumin","coriander","cayenne"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Process the onions very fine and fry them with the lid on, stirring now and then. The steam softens them first, then they brown into a jammy base that thickens the sauce without any cream.","s":0},{"t":"Brown the lamb on all sides before adding the water, and stir the yogurt in a spoonful at a time off a hard boil. Cold yogurt added all at once can curdle.","s":1},{"t":"Use lamb shoulder or leg; lean cuts dry out. Simmer gently and skim off excess oil that rises, and if the sauce is thin, uncover for the last 10 minutes to reduce it.","s":2}],"rl":"XFXXXXXXXXAPMG"},"53358":{"lv":"EEERRREERRRRRRO","w":{"3":"Sweet base for the chicken and rice; skipping leaves a less rounded flavor.","4":"Savory depth in the rice; skipping makes it flatter.","5":"Mild heat and fresh green flavor; skip for a gentler mandi.","7":"Frying fat for the onion; ghee gives a more authentic taste, but any neutral oil works.","8":"Gives the rice its golden color and earthy flavor; skipping leaves it paler.","9":"Warm citrusy spice typical of mandi; skipping loses depth.","10":"Floral perfume of the spice mix; skipping thins the aroma.","11":"Warm clove aroma; use sparingly and skip if you dislike it.","12":"Sweet-woody background; omit with small loss.","13":"Peppery warmth that rounds the spice mix; skipping makes it milder.","14":"Subtle background; omit."},"sub":{"7":{"x":"3 tbsp ghee or 3 tbsp butter","d":"Ghee or butter gives a richer, more traditional flavor than plain oil.","k":["ghee","butter"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Measure the liquid after removing the chicken and match it to the rice: roughly 1.5 cups of liquid per cup of soaked basmati. Too much makes it soft and clumpy, too little leaves the grains hard.","s":5},{"t":"Make sure the chicken reaches 165F inside after the final steam. Laying a clean towel under the lid absorbs condensation and keeps the rice fluffy.","s":6},{"t":"For the smoky touch, use natural charcoal heated until glowing, set in a foil cup, add a little ghee and cover at once for a few minutes. Work in a ventilated kitchen and remove the coal before serving.","s":7}],"rl":"PBMXXXZFXXXXXXX"},"53359":{"lv":"EERRRREERRROE","w":{"2":"Sweet base for the rice and beef; skipping leaves a less rounded flavor.","3":"Savory depth in the rice; skipping makes it flatter.","4":"Mild heat and fresh green flavor; skip for a gentler mandi.","5":"Adds sweetness and a little acidity to the base; skip with small loss.","7":"Frying fat for the onions; ghee gives a more authentic taste, but any neutral oil works.","8":"Gives the rice its golden color and earthy flavor; skipping leaves it paler.","9":"Floral perfume of the spice mix; skipping thins the aroma.","10":"Warm clove aroma; use sparingly and skip if you dislike it.","11":"Subtle background; omit.","1":"Cooks the rice; 5 cups only cooks about 3 cups of rice, so top up with water to 1.5 to 2 cups of liquid per cup of rice."},"sub":{"7":{"x":"3 tbsp ghee or 3 tbsp butter","d":"Ghee or butter gives a richer, more traditional flavor than plain oil.","k":["ghee","butter"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Use about 2 to 3 lb of bone-in beef such as shank or chuck, and season it with the salt and turmeric before it goes in the pot.","s":0},{"t":"Simmer the beef low and slow until a knife slips in with no resistance. A hard boil toughens the meat and clouds the broth you are going to cook the rice in.","s":5},{"t":"Five cups of broth is only enough for about 3 cups of rice at 1.5 to 2 cups liquid per cup, so top up with water or more beef broth to match the amount of rice you cook. If you use smoke, do it in a ventilated kitchen.","s":7}],"rl":"BMXXXAZFXXXXP"},"52785":{"lv":"EERRERRRORROOROO","w":{"2":"Seasons the dal as it cooks; lentils need salt early to taste seasoned through.","3":"Gives color and earthy depth; skipping leaves the dal paler.","4":"Fat for the tadka that carries the spice flavors; ghee is traditional, but oil or butter works.","5":"Adds acidity and body to the tadka; skip and add a squeeze of lemon at the end.","6":"Toasty backbone of the tempering; skipping loses the signature dal fry aroma.","7":"Pops in the hot ghee for a nutty pungency; skip and use more cumin.","8":"Subtle background; omit.","9":"Fresh heat in the tadka; skip for a mild dal.","10":"Fresh warmth that perfumes the tadka; skip and add a pinch of ground ginger.","11":"Garnish for freshness.","12":"Listed as bell pepper but the step uses chili powder; heat only, skip for a mild dal.","13":"Seasons the tomato tadka; taste at the end.","14":"Balances the tomato acidity; omit with minimal change.","15":"Aromatic finish; skip freely."},"sub":{"4":{"x":"3 tbsp butter or vegetable oil","d":"Butter is closest to ghee but can brown faster; oil is neutral and lighter.","k":["butter","vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Cook the dal until it breaks down into a thick soup and mash with a whisk if some lentils hold their shape. Skim off any foam that rises as it boils.","s":1},{"t":"Heat the ghee until shimmering before adding the seeds, and wait for the mustard seeds to crackle. If they smell burnt or bitter, discard the ghee and start the tadka again.","s":2},{"t":"Pour the sizzling tadka over the dal and cover the pot for a minute so the aroma is absorbed. Thin with hot water if the dal has thickened while waiting.","s":5}],"rl":"PMZXFAXXXXXGXZWX"},"52807":{"lv":"EEERROEOR","w":{"3":"Garlic can be tucked into the eggplant before roasting; skipping makes the bharta less savory.","4":"Fresh heat that complements the smoky eggplant; skip for a mild bharta.","5":"Heat and color; skip with little change.","6":"Cooking fat for the masala; ghee or any neutral oil works.","7":"Garnish for freshness.","8":"Seasons the mashed eggplant; add gradually and taste."},"sub":{"6":{"x":"1 1/2 tbsp ghee or olive oil","d":"Ghee is richer and more traditional; olive oil is fruitier but works.","k":["ghee","olive oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Char the eggplant until the skin is blistered black and the flesh collapses. Undercooked eggplant is spongy and bitter. Over a flame turn it every few minutes with tongs; under a broiler it takes 30 to 40 minutes.","s":1},{"t":"Cooling the roasted eggplant in water makes the skin slip off easily, but drain the flesh well and pat it dry so the bharta does not turn watery.","s":5},{"t":"Cook the tomatoes until the oil separates from the masala before adding the eggplant. That reduction (bhuno) is what gives the deep, concentrated flavor.","s":21}],"rl":"VXVXXXFGZ"},"52809":{"lv":"EERRRRRORORREE","w":{"2":"Fresh warmth in the paste; skipping makes it less rounded.","3":"Sharp savory punch that is typical of Goan recheado; skip and the paste is flatter.","4":"Peppery heat and bite; skipping reduces the spice complexity.","5":"Earthy backbone of the spice paste; skipping makes it milder.","6":"Gives color and earthiness; skipping makes the paste paler.","7":"Strong spice the recipe itself says can be avoided; omit if you dislike it.","8":"Warm clove aroma; skipping thins the aroma.","9":"Minor floral note; omit.","10":"Balances the tamarind and vinegar; skipping makes the paste sharper.","11":"Sour fruity note that balances the vinegar; skip and use lime juice.","13":"Frying fat; any neutral oil works."},"sub":{"11":{"x":"2 tbsp lime juice","d":"Brighter and less fruity-sour than tamarind; reduce the vinegar slightly.","k":["lime juice"]},"13":{"x":"coconut oil or any neutral oil, enough for shallow frying","d":"Coconut oil adds a Goan flavor; any neutral oil works.","k":["coconut oil","vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Grind the masala thick, using only as much vinegar as it needs. A watery paste slides off the fish and fries to a runny, bitter mess instead of a crust.","s":4},{"t":"Slit the belly and score the sides so the paste reaches the flesh. Mackerel is oily and strongly flavored, so it carries this fiery masala well, but it must be very fresh.","s":5},{"t":"Shallow-fry in medium-hot oil and flip once. The chili paste scorches quickly, so lower the heat if it darkens before the fish reaches 145F inside.","s":7}],"rl":"PXXXXXXXXXWAAF"},"52865":{"lv":"EERRRRREEROO","w":{"0":"Frying fat for the paneer and masala; ghee or any neutral oil works.","2":"Fresh warmth that gives the sauce brightness; skip and use a pinch of ground ginger.","3":"Earthy backbone of the sauce; skipping makes it less rounded.","4":"Gives color and earthiness; skipping leaves the sauce paler.","5":"Mild citrusy spice; skipping loses depth.","6":"Fresh heat; skip for a mild matar paneer.","9":"Aromatic finish; skipping loses the fragrant top note.","10":"Garnish for freshness.","11":"A serving extra; use rice instead or skip."},"sub":{"0":{"x":"1 tbsp ghee or butter","d":"Ghee or butter adds richness; butter may brown faster, so keep the heat moderate.","k":["ghee","butter"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Pat the paneer dry and get the oil shimmering before it goes in. A hot pan browns the edges in about a minute per side, and wet paneer splatters and steams instead of browning.","s":0},{"t":"Mash the tomatoes against the pan as they soften, and let them cook down to a jammy paste before adding any water. That is what gives the sauce body in just 20 minutes.","s":2},{"t":"Add the peas and paneer only for the last couple of minutes. Overcooked paneer turns rubbery and peas lose their sweetness and color.","s":4}],"rl":"FPXXXXXVVXGG"},"52868":{"lv":"EERRORRREER","w":{"0":"Cooking fat for the onion; ghee or any neutral oil works.","2":"Savory depth in the sauce; skipping makes it flatter.","3":"Fresh warmth for the beans; skip and use a pinch of ground ginger.","4":"Fresh garnish at the end; skip with little effect.","5":"Earthy backbone of the spice mix; skipping loses depth.","6":"Color and mild sweetness; skip with little loss.","7":"Aromatic finish; skipping makes the sauce plainer.","10":"A serving extra; use naan or bread instead."},"sub":{"0":{"x":"1 tbsp ghee, butter or olive oil","d":"Ghee or butter gives a richer, slightly nuttier base.","k":["ghee","butter","olive oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Cook the onion slowly with a pinch of salt. The salt draws out moisture so it softens and sweetens without browning too fast, which builds a rounder base than the quick cook.","s":0},{"t":"Keep the starchy liquid from the beans rather than draining it. It thickens the curry naturally as it simmers.","s":1},{"t":"Press a few beans against the pan with the back of a spoon in the last 5 minutes to thicken the sauce and give it a creamier body.","s":2}],"rl":"FXXXGXXXAPG"},"101":{"lv":"EERREEERRRR","w":{"0":"Cooking fat for the base; any neutral oil or ghee works.","2":"Savory depth for the sauce; skipping makes it flatter.","3":"Fresh warmth that lifts the coconut; skip and use a pinch of ground ginger.","7":"Adds acidity and body to the sauce; skip and add more coconut milk or water.","8":"Adds greens and color at the end; skip or use kale.","9":"Brightens the rich coconut milk; skipping makes it taste heavy.","10":"A serving base; use naan or bread instead."},"sub":{"0":{"x":"1 tbsp ghee, butter or coconut oil","d":"Coconut oil adds a hint of coconut; ghee or butter adds richness.","k":["coconut oil","ghee","butter"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Stir the curry powder into the hot oil for a full minute until fragrant. Raw curry powder tastes dusty and gritty, but blooming it in fat rounds it out.","s":1},{"t":"Use full-fat coconut milk and keep the simmer gentle. Light coconut milk makes a thin sauce, and a rolling boil can make it separate.","s":2},{"t":"Add the spinach at the very end so it wilts in the residual heat, and squeeze the lime in off the heat to keep it fresh and bright.","s":3}],"rl":"FXXXXPMVVAB"},"3182":{"lv":"EEEERRRRRRRERRO","w":{"1":"Searing and stir-fry fat; any neutral oil works.","4":"Adds color and sweetness; skip and use more bell pepper.","5":"Savory depth in the sauce; skipping makes it flat.","6":"Fresh warmth for the masala; skip and use a pinch of ground ginger.","7":"Earthy backbone of the spices; skipping loses depth.","8":"Mild citrusy spice; skipping makes the sauce less rounded.","9":"Color and earthiness; skipping leaves it paler.","10":"Heat that defines jalfrezi's kick; reduce for a milder dish.","12":"Aromatic finish; skipping loses the fragrant top note.","13":"Seasons the sauce; taste at the end.","14":"Garnish for freshness."},"sub":{"1":{"x":"2 tbsp ghee or any neutral oil","d":"Ghee is richer; use a neutral oil if you want a lighter sauce.","k":["ghee","vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Sear the chicken over high heat in a single layer, without moving it, so it browns instead of steaming. It finishes cooking in the sauce, so pull it when it is golden outside.","s":0},{"t":"Jalfrezi means stir-fry, so the peppers should still have bite. Set half aside after 4 minutes, so some stay crisp while the rest soften into the sauce.","s":1},{"t":"Finish the chicken in the sauce until it reaches 165F inside. Add the garam masala and cilantro at the very end for the best aroma.","s":4}],"rl":"PFVVVXXXXXXAXZG"},"52795":{"lv":"EERRRERRRRRERRRR","w":{"2":"Adds acidity and body to the gravy; skip with a thinner, less tangy sauce.","3":"Savory depth; skipping makes the sauce flatter.","4":"Fresh warmth for the masala; skip and use a pinch of ground ginger.","5":"Frying fat for the onions; ghee is traditional but oil works.","6":"Toasty backbone of the spices; skipping loses depth.","7":"Citrusy, floral spice; skipping makes the handi less rounded.","8":"Gives color and earthiness; skip and the gravy is paler.","9":"Heat and color; reduce for a milder dish.","10":"Fresh heat; skip for a gentler dish.","12":"Adds richness and smooths the sauce; skip for a lighter handi.","13":"Kasuri methi gives the dish its savory aroma; skipping loses a signature handi note.","14":"Aromatic finish; skipping loses the fragrant top note.","15":"Seasons the chicken and sauce; taste at the end."},"sub":{"5":{"x":"1/4 cup ghee or any neutral oil","d":"Ghee is richer and traditional; neutral oil is lighter.","k":["ghee","vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Fry the onions until deep golden before the garlic goes in. They provide sweetness and body for the whole gravy, so stay at the pot and watch for scorching.","s":0},{"t":"Add the yogurt only when the oil separates from the spices, with the heat on the lowest setting. Stir it in steadily so it does not split; whisking the yogurt smooth first helps.","s":6},{"t":"Crush the kasuri methi between your palms as you sprinkle it on to release its aroma, and make sure the chicken has reached 165F before serving.","s":8}],"rl":"PXAXXFXXXXXMFXXZ"},"52851":{"lv":"RRRREEEEE","w":{"0":"Heat and fresh pungency in the paste; skip for a milder curry.","1":"Fresh warmth that gives the paste its brightness; skip and use a pinch of ground ginger.","2":"Savory depth in the sauce; skipping makes it flatter.","3":"Stalks and leaves flavor the paste and garnish; skip and use parsley for the garnish.","4":"Browning fat; any neutral oil works."},"sub":{"4":{"x":"1 tbsp butter or any neutral oil","d":"Butter adds richness but can brown faster; neutral oil is fine.","k":["butter","vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Use the coriander stalks in the paste. They carry more flavor than the leaves and give the sauce its green, herby backbone.","s":0},{"t":"Use plain, unsweetened peanut butter; sweetened brands make the sauce taste like dessert. Stir it in over moderate heat so it melts smoothly rather than clumping.","s":1},{"t":"Keep the sauce at a gentle bubble, since hard boiling splits the yogurt. Chicken breast is done at 165F, so do not simmer much beyond 10 minutes or it dries out.","s":2}],"rl":"XXXXFPTMM"},"3174":{"lv":"ERRRREERRORRRO","w":{"0":"Cooking fat for the cumin and onion; ghee or any neutral oil works.","1":"Toasted in oil for a nutty base; skipping loses an earthy note.","2":"Sweet base for the masala; skipping makes the dish less rounded.","3":"Fresh warmth for the vegetables; skip and use a pinch of ground ginger.","4":"Savory depth in the masala; skipping makes it flatter.","7":"Color and earthiness; skipping leaves it paler.","8":"Mild citrusy spice; skipping loses depth.","9":"Heat only; skip for a mild aloo gobi.","10":"Adds a little moisture and acidity to the masala; skipping is fine.","11":"Aromatic finish; skipping loses the top note.","12":"Seasons the vegetables; taste at the end.","13":"Garnish for freshness."},"sub":{"0":{"x":"3 tbsp ghee or any neutral oil","d":"Ghee adds richness; neutral oil is lighter.","k":["ghee","vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Cut the potatoes slightly smaller than the cauliflower florets so both finish together. Cook covered with only a quarter cup of water so they steam in their own moisture and do not go mushy.","s":3},{"t":"Uncover for the last stretch and stir only now and then. Resist stirring too much, since leaving the vegetables in contact with the pan lets them develop browned edges.","s":4}],"rl":"FXXXXBVXXXAXZG"},"3176":{"lv":"EEEEERERRO","w":{"4":"Adds tang and tenderness to the dough; milk with lemon works in a pinch.","5":"Makes the naan soft and pliable; skip with a slightly drier crumb.","7":"Brushed on for flavor and gloss; skipping makes the naan plainer.","8":"Garlic is the point of garlic naan; skipping makes it a plain buttered naan.","9":"Garnish for freshness."},"sub":{"4":{"x":"1/2 cup buttermilk, or 1/2 cup whole milk + 1 tsp lemon juice","d":"Slightly less tangy and softer crumb; add 1 to 2 tbsp flour if the dough is too sticky.","k":["buttermilk","whole milk","lemon juice"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Use warm water around 105F to 110F. Water that is too hot kills the yeast, and a foamy surface after 10 minutes tells you it is alive.","s":0},{"t":"Cook on a very hot dry cast-iron skillet and cover for the first 30 seconds. The trapped steam puffs the dough into big bubbles; flip when the underside is spotted brown.","s":4},{"t":"Brush the garlic butter on straight from the pan. The heat softens the raw garlic's bite without burning it, and brush plenty on both sides.","s":5}],"rl":"MWLSMFZFXG"},"3179":{"lv":"EERREROORRRRR","w":{"2":"Fresh warmth in the marinade; skip and use a pinch of ground ginger.","3":"Savory punch in the marinade; skipping makes the paneer flatter.","5":"Gives the tikka its red color and mild sweetness; skipping leaves it paler.","6":"Faint earthiness and color; skip with little change.","7":"Heat only; skip for a mild tikka.","8":"Brightens the marinade and slightly tenderizes; skip and finish with lemon wedges.","9":"Helps the spices cling and the edges char; skipping risks dry spots.","10":"Sweet, juicy vegetable that alternates with the paneer; skip for paneer-only skewers.","11":"Sweet, charred layers that balance the rich marinade; skip with little loss.","12":"Seasons the marinade; paneer is bland, so taste the marinade first."},"tips":[{"t":"Use thick, strained yogurt for the marinade. Thin yogurt slides off the skewers and steams the paneer instead of charring into a crust.","s":0},{"t":"Marinate at least 30 minutes, but not more than a few hours with the lemon juice in it. The acid makes the paneer's surface chalky.","s":1},{"t":"Cook under a very hot broiler or on a grill, turning once. Paneer does not melt, but it turns rubbery if it cooks too long, so stop as soon as the edges are charred.","s":4}],"rl":"PMXXXXXXAFVVZ"},"3180":{"lv":"EEROROR","w":{"2":"Toasted cumin is the classic raita seasoning; skipping makes it plain.","3":"Pungent kick; skip for a gentler raita.","4":"Cooling herbal note; skip and use cilantro.","5":"A dusting for color and a little heat; skip freely.","6":"Brings out the yogurt's flavor; add gradually and taste."},"tips":[{"t":"Toast the cumin seeds until they smell nutty and darken a shade, then grind. Pre-ground cumin never gives raita the same smoky depth.","s":0},{"t":"Whisk the yogurt with the water until perfectly smooth before folding in the vegetables, so the raita is creamy and pourable rather than lumpy.","s":1},{"t":"Salt just before chilling and serve within a few hours. Salt pulls water from the cucumber and slowly thins the raita, so stir before serving if it has separated.","s":3}],"rl":"BVXXXGZ"},"3184":{"lv":"ERRRRREEERE","w":{"1":"Lightens the batter slightly for a crisper, airier crust; skip and the pakoras are denser.","2":"Earthy backbone of the spice mix; skipping makes the batter plain.","3":"Mild citrusy spice; skipping loses depth.","4":"Gives color and earthiness; skipping leaves it paler.","5":"Heat only; skip for mild pakoras.","9":"Adds greens and flavor; skip and use more onion.","10":"Frying medium; any neutral high-heat oil works."},"sub":{"10":{"x":"2 cups canola or sunflower oil","d":"Any neutral oil with a high smoke point works; avoid olive oil, which smokes and tastes too strong.","k":["canola oil","sunflower oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Add the water a little at a time. The batter should be thick enough to cling to the vegetables rather than drip off, because a thin batter fries into a greasy, soggy coating.","s":0},{"t":"Use a thermometer and keep the oil near 350F. Too cool and the pakoras soak up oil, too hot and they brown outside while the potato inside stays raw.","s":2},{"t":"Fry in small batches so the oil temperature does not drop. Slice the potato thin and for extra crunch, fry the pakoras a second time for a minute before serving.","s":3}],"rl":"SLXXXXZVVVF"},"3474":{"lv":"EEEOOEE","w":{"1":"Ghee gives jeera rice its nutty flavor; butter or oil also works.","3":"Subtle background aroma; omit with little loss.","4":"Faint sweet warmth; omit with little loss."},"sub":{"1":{"x":"2 tbsp butter or vegetable oil","d":"Butter is close to ghee but less nutty; oil is neutral and lighter.","k":["butter","vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Rinse the basmati until the water runs mostly clear, then soak. Removing surface starch gives separate, fluffy grains rather than sticky ones.","s":0},{"t":"Let the cumin seeds sizzle and darken for 30 seconds but not burn. Burnt cumin turns bitter and will flavor the whole pot.","s":1},{"t":"Do not lift the lid during the cook or the rest. The trapped steam finishes the rice, and the rest lets the grains firm up so they do not break when fluffed.","s":4}],"rl":"BFXXXZM"},"3475":{"lv":"EERERRRRROORR","w":{"1":"Fat for browning the okra and the masala; ghee or any neutral oil works.","2":"Tempered in the hot oil for a toasty aroma; skipping loses an earthy note.","4":"Savory depth in the masala; skipping makes it flatter.","5":"Fresh warmth that supports the tomatoes; skip and use a pinch of ground ginger.","6":"Forms the thick masala that coats the okra; skipping leaves it drier.","7":"Gives color and earthiness; skipping makes it paler.","8":"Mild citrusy spice; skipping loses depth.","9":"Heat only; skip for a mild dish.","10":"Aromatic finish; skip with little loss.","11":"Brightens the masala and cuts any remaining sliminess; skipping makes it taste flatter.","12":"Seasons the okra and masala; taste at the end."},"sub":{"1":{"x":"2 tbsp ghee or any neutral oil","d":"Ghee adds richness; neutral oil keeps it lighter.","k":["ghee","vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Dry the okra completely before cutting. Cut surfaces on wet okra release sticky mucilage, and never cover the pan while the okra cooks.","s":0},{"t":"Cook the okra in a single layer in batches if needed, with minimal stirring, so it sears instead of steaming. Browning it in a separate step is what keeps it from going slimy.","s":1},{"t":"Add the lemon juice off the heat at the end. Acid cooked with okra for a long time dulls its color and toughens it.","s":6}],"rl":"VFXXXXAXXXXAZ"},"3476":{"lv":"EEERROREOR","w":{"1":"Frying fat; ghee or any neutral oil works.","3":"Gives color and earthy depth; skipping leaves the potatoes plainer.","4":"Mild citrusy spice; skipping loses depth.","5":"Heat only; skip for a mild aloo jeera.","6":"Fresh heat; skip for a gentler dish.","8":"Garnish for freshness.","9":"Brightens the finished potatoes; skipping makes them taste flat."},"sub":{"1":{"x":"3 tbsp ghee or any neutral oil","d":"Ghee adds richness; neutral oil gives a cleaner crisp.","k":["ghee","vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Parboil the cubes only until barely tender, then let them steam dry. Dry, slightly roughed surfaces crisp up in the pan; wet potatoes steam instead.","s":0},{"t":"Stir the ground spices for just 10 seconds before adding the potatoes, since they burn quickly and turn bitter in hot oil.","s":2},{"t":"Spread the potatoes in a single layer and turn only occasionally. Leaving them in contact with the pan builds a golden crust, and crowding makes them steam.","s":4}],"rl":"BFXXXXXZGA"},"3477":{"lv":"EEERRERRR","w":{"3":"Fresh heat; skip for a mild salad.","4":"Fresh herbal note that is part of kachumber's flavor; skipping makes it plainer.","6":"Earthy warmth; skipping loses depth.","7":"Tangy, funky spice mix that gives street-food flavor; skip and add a bit more cumin and lemon.","8":"Draws out juices and seasons the vegetables; add gradually."},"tips":[{"t":"Seed the tomatoes and scoop out the cucumber seeds so they do not leak water into the bowl and dilute the dressing.","s":0},{"t":"Dice everything to the same size, about 1/4 inch, so every bite has a balanced mix. A quick 30-second rinse of the diced onion in cold water removes sharpness.","s":0},{"t":"Dress at the last minute. Salt and lemon pull water out of the vegetables within about 10 minutes, turning a crisp salad soggy.","s":3}],"rl":"VVVXXAXXZ"},"3478":{"lv":"EERREROR","w":{"2":"Gives the chutney its fresh heat; reduce for a milder chutney.","3":"Warm, zingy note that rounds the herbs; skip and use a pinch of ground ginger.","5":"Earthy warmth that rounds the herbs; skipping makes it greener and sharper.","6":"Softens the lemon and chili sharpness; omit with little change.","7":"Seasons and keeps the chutney tasting vibrant; add gradually and taste."},"tips":[{"t":"Blend with cold water or a couple of ice cubes. Friction heat from the blender can darken the herbs and turn them bitter.","s":1},{"t":"Add the extra water a tablespoon at a time and scrape down the sides. The chutney should be thick enough to cling to a spoon rather than run off.","s":2},{"t":"Make it right before serving for the brightest green, or keep it covered with a thin film of lemon juice to slow browning.","s":3}],"rl":"VVXXAXWZ"},"3479":{"lv":"EREERRRROOE","w":{"1":"Sweet savory backbone that cooks down into the sauce; skipping makes the chutney fruitier and sweeter.","2":"Sweetens and balances the vinegar, and helps set the chutney; brown sugar adds a molasses note.","3":"The acid that balances the sugar and preserves the chutney; keep it at 5 percent acidity.","4":"Adds chew and concentrated sweetness; skip and add an extra tablespoon of sugar.","5":"Warm zing that is classic in mango chutney; skip and use 1/2 tsp ground ginger.","6":"Mild savory depth; skipping is barely noticed.","7":"Adds subtle pungency and texture; skipping is fine.","8":"Gentle heat only; skip freely.","9":"Faint warmth; omit."},"sub":{"2":{"x":"1/2 cup white sugar + 1 tbsp honey","d":"Slightly less deep than brown sugar; the honey supplies a hint of caramel flavor.","k":["sugar","honey"],"wn":"Contains honey; not for infants under 12 months"},"3":{"x":"1 cup white wine vinegar or distilled white vinegar","d":"Sharper and less fruity than cider vinegar; same acidity, so it keeps just as well.","k":["white wine vinegar","white vinegar"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Use firm-ripe mangoes, and add them after the onion has softened. Very ripe ones dissolve into a puree, while firm ones hold shape and give the chutney its chunky texture.","s":2},{"t":"Cook until a spoon dragged across the bottom leaves a trail that holds for a second. The chutney thickens a lot as it cools, so stop while it still looks slightly loose.","s":3},{"t":"Spoon into a clean jar and refrigerate. This is not canned, so it should not be stored at room temperature; the flavor mellows and improves after a day.","s":4}],"rl":"VXWKVXXXXXZ"},"3480":{"lv":"ERRRRRRROO","w":{"1":"Thickens the chutney and adds nutty body; skip and use a few cashews, or it will be looser and more coconut-forward.","2":"Gives heat and fresh bite; skipping leaves the chutney bland.","3":"Warm zing that supports the coconut; skip and use a pinch of ground ginger.","4":"Seasons the coconut, which is bland by itself; under-salting makes it taste flat.","5":"Fat for the tempering; coconut oil adds the traditional South Indian aroma, but any neutral oil works.","6":"Pops in the hot oil for the crackle and nutty bite of the tadka; skipping loses the classic finish.","7":"Gives the tempering its characteristic nutty, citrusy aroma; no real substitute, so skip if unavailable.","8":"Adds smoky heat to the tadka; skip freely.","9":"Tiny savory-onion note in the tempering; skip freely."},"sub":{"5":{"x":"1 tbsp vegetable oil","d":"Loses the coconut aroma; otherwise the tempering works the same.","k":["vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Use cold water and add it gradually. Roasted chana dal gives the chutney body and a nutty flavor, and extra water quickly turns it soupy instead of spoonable.","s":0},{"t":"Make the tadka last and pour it sizzling straight onto the chutney so it blooms in the oil. Heat the oil until shimmering, cover the pan when the mustard seeds pop, and keep the curry leaves and chilies only 10 seconds.","s":5},{"t":"Serve the same day or refrigerate for a day or two. Fresh coconut turns sour quickly at room temperature.","s":-1}],"rl":"BTXXZFXXXX"},"3481":{"lv":"EERRORRERR","w":{"1":"Tempering fat; coconut oil adds the traditional aroma, but any neutral oil works.","2":"Pops in the oil for nutty bite; skipping loses the classic poriyal flavor.","3":"Fried golden for a nutty crunch; skipping loses texture, but the dish still works.","4":"Smoky heat; skip freely.","5":"Aromatic, slightly citrusy note typical of South Indian cooking; skip if you can't find it.","6":"Gives color and earthy depth; skipping makes it paler.","8":"Adds sweetness and richness at the finish; skip for a lighter dish.","9":"Fresh heat; skip for a mild poriyal."},"sub":{"1":{"x":"2 tbsp vegetable oil","d":"Loses the coconut aroma; the dish is otherwise the same.","k":["vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Fry the chana dal in the hot oil until golden and no further. It gives the poriyal its crunchy bits, and a burnt lentil tastes bitter.","s":1},{"t":"Do not add water. Salt draws moisture from the cabbage, so it steams in its own liquid, and covering only for the first 5 minutes keeps it from turning soggy.","s":3},{"t":"Stir in the coconut at the end off the heat and serve right away; the cabbage should stay tender with a little crunch.","s":5}],"rl":"VFXXXXXZGX"},"3175":{"lv":"EEEERERRRRRORE","w":{"4":"Sweet pop of color and texture in the filling; skip and use more potato.","5":"Fat for toasting the spices; ghee or butter also works.","6":"Toasty base spice in the filling; skipping makes it plainer.","7":"Fresh warmth that lifts the potato; skip and use a pinch of ground ginger.","8":"Citrusy spice that is part of the filling's flavor; skipping loses depth.","9":"Aromatic finish for the filling; skipping loses top-note fragrance.","10":"Gives color and earthiness; skipping makes the filling paler.","11":"Heat only; skip for mild samosas.","12":"Brightens the filling and balances the starch; skipping makes it taste heavy.","13":"Frying medium; any neutral high-heat oil works."},"sub":{"5":{"x":"1 tbsp ghee or butter","d":"Richer flavor in the filling; keep the heat moderate so butter doesn't brown.","k":["ghee","butter"]},"13":{"x":"2 cups canola or sunflower oil","d":"Any neutral oil with a high smoke point works; avoid olive oil, which smokes and tastes too strong.","k":["canola oil","sunflower oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Make a stiff dough with cold water and let it rest the full 30 minutes. A stiff dough with oil worked in fries into a blistered, flaky shell, while a soft dough turns bready and soaks up oil.","s":0},{"t":"Cool the filling completely before shaping. Warm filling steams the dough from inside and tears it, and seal the wet edge well so no oil gets in.","s":2},{"t":"Fry at a low 325F so the thick dough cooks through before it browns. Hot oil browns the outside quickly and leaves raw dough inside, and crowded batches drop the oil temperature.","s":5}],"rl":"SFZBVFXXXXXXAF"},"3181":{"lv":"EEEEEEEERR","w":{"5":"Frying medium; ghee is traditional, but any neutral oil works.","8":"Floral perfume of the syrup; skipping makes the jamun taste only sweet.","9":"Prevents the syrup from crystallizing and cuts the sweetness; skipping risks a grainy syrup."},"sub":{"5":{"x":"2 cups ghee or any neutral oil","d":"Ghee gives the traditional flavor and richer color; neutral oil is less aromatic.","k":["ghee","vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Knead only until the dough is just smooth and soft. Overkneading develops gluten and gives dense, hard jamun.","s":0},{"t":"Roll each ball until perfectly smooth, with a little ghee on your palms. Cracks widen in the oil and the balls can break apart as they fry.","s":1},{"t":"Keep the oil near 275F and fry slowly, turning gently. They need time to cook through and swell, and a test ball should rise slowly rather than shoot to the top. Drop the hot jamun into warm, not boiling, syrup so they soak it up evenly.","s":3}],"rl":"SSLFMFWMXA"},"3183":{"lv":"EEERROO","w":{"3":"Signature perfume of kheer; skipping makes it a plain rice pudding.","4":"Adds crunch and nuttiness; skip or use cashews.","5":"Adds chewy sweetness; omit with little change.","6":"Gives a subtle floral aroma and golden color; skip and kheer is still lovely."},"tips":[{"t":"Use a heavy pot and rinse it with water before adding the milk, which helps stop a film sticking to the bottom. Stir often, scraping the base, while the rice softens.","s":1},{"t":"Add the sugar only after the rice is fully soft. Sugar added earlier firms up the rice grains and keeps them from cooking tender.","s":2},{"t":"Kheer thickens a lot as it cools, so stop while it still looks slightly loose and thin it with warm milk if chilling.","s":3}],"rl":"BMWXGGC"},"53580":{"lv":"EEO","w":{"2":"Optional filling or garnish for texture and sweetness; skip freely."},"tips":[{"t":"If you are using dry coconut flakes, they will not bind on their own. Moisten with 1 to 2 tbsp warm milk or ghee, or warm the mixture in a pan for 3 to 4 minutes until the jaggery melts and holds together.","s":3},{"t":"Press firmly into a greased modak mould or your palm, then chill 15 minutes to set so they hold their shape. A pinch of cardamom is traditional.","s":4}],"rl":"BWG"},"3505":{"lv":"EEEEREROOO","w":{"4":"Sweetness and color in the broth; skip and add more onion.","6":"Earthy herb that complements the pork; skip and use sage or parsley.","7":"Subtle background; omit.","8":"Garnish for freshness.","9":"Bacon and sausage are already seasoned; pepper is only a light finish."},"tips":[{"t":"Brown the sausages only lightly. Coddle is a pale, gentle dish and it is not meant to look like a roast, so skip hard searing.","s":0},{"t":"Keep the liquid at a bare simmer, barely trembling, since that is the point of a coddle. A hard boil breaks the potatoes apart and toughens the sausages.","s":4},{"t":"Cut one sausage open to check that it is no longer pink and has reached 160F. Serve in wide bowls with soda bread to soak up the thick broth.","s":5}],"rl":"PPBVVMXXGZ"},"52998":{"lv":"EERE","w":{"2":"Sweet, earthy side vegetable; skip and use extra potatoes."},"tips":[{"t":"Simmer gently rather than boil, and plan on roughly 50 minutes per pound, so a 3 lb brisket needs about 2.5 to 3 hours. The listed time is short, and the meat is ready only when a fork slides in with no resistance.","s":0},{"t":"Stage the vegetables: potatoes and carrots first, cabbage wedges only for the last 15 minutes. That way each one cooks through without turning to mush.","s":1},{"t":"Rest the meat for 15 minutes, then slice thinly across the grain, which runs in long fibers. Slicing with the grain leaves the slices stringy and tough.","s":2}],"rl":"PBVV"},"52999":{"lv":"EEERRRERO","w":{"3":"Mellow savory notes through the baked greens; skipping makes the greens plainer.","4":"Sweetens the greens as it bakes; skip with a slightly more bitter result.","5":"Adds meaty umami that rounds the greens; the recipe calls it optional but recommended.","7":"Seasons the greens, which are bland as they wilt; add after covering time.","8":"Light finish; skip freely."},"tips":[{"t":"Strip the tough ribs from the kale before tearing the leaves. The stems stay chewy after baking, though the leaves turn silky.","s":0},{"t":"Bake covered first so the greens steam and wilt, then uncover so the cabbage edges caramelize. Salt after the foil comes off, since salting early pulls out water and makes the greens soggy.","s":3},{"t":"Prick the sausages and brown them slowly over medium-high heat, rolling them, until they reach 160F inside. Slice after a short rest and fold into the greens so they stay crisp at the edges.","s":5}],"rl":"VPVXXVMZZ"},"53000":{"lv":"EEERERREROEEERRRORO","w":{"2":"Enriches the potato topping; olive oil with milk makes a lighter dairy-free-ish mash.","3":"Dried porcini add meaty umami to the lentil filling; skip and use extra fresh mushrooms.","5":"Savory depth in the filling and the roasted vegetables; skipping makes it flatter.","6":"Seasons the lentils as they cook; lentils need salt for flavor.","8":"Adds color and umami to the gravy; skip and use a splash more soy sauce.","9":"Subtle background in the gravy; omit.","10":"Cooking fat for the onions and vegetables; any neutral oil or butter works.","11":"Deglazes the pot and adds acidity to the gravy; broth with vinegar works.","13":"Thickens the gravy; flour slurry also works.","14":"Salty, savory depth in the vegetarian gravy; skipping makes it less meaty.","15":"Piney herb that perfumes the roasted vegetables; skip and use thyme.","16":"Garnish for freshness.","17":"Earthy herb that suits the mushrooms; skip and use extra parsley.","18":"Mild onion garnish; skip."},"sub":{"2":{"x":"1/3 cup olive oil + 1/4 cup warm milk","d":"Less buttery; the mash is lighter but still smooth.","k":["olive oil","milk"]},"10":{"x":"5 tbsp vegetable oil or butter","d":"Butter adds richness; neutral oil keeps the dish vegan.","k":["vegetable oil","butter"]},"11":{"x":"2 cups vegetable broth + 2 tbsp white wine vinegar or lemon juice","d":"Less depth than wine; the acid keeps the gravy from tasting flat.","k":["vegetable broth","white wine vinegar","lemon juice"]},"13":{"x":"1/4 cup flour whisked into 1/4 cup cold water, simmered 5 minutes","d":"Less glossy than cornstarch; simmer long enough to lose the raw flour taste.","k":["flour"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Bake the potatoes whole instead of boiling them, then press them through a ricer or food mill while hot. They stay dry and fluffy, so the topping holds peaks that brown nicely.","s":5},{"t":"Deglaze with the wine and scrape up the browned bits. Pour in the porcini liquid slowly and stop before the gritty sediment at the bottom.","s":9},{"t":"Let the pie stand 15 minutes after baking so the filling sets. Cutting it straight from the oven makes it slump.","s":13}],"rl":"BBFXPXZXXXFMMTXXGXG"},"53036":{"lv":"EREEEERRORR","w":{"1":"Fresh onion sweetness in the batter; skip and use chives.","4":"The baking soda needs an acid to rise, and the method calls for buttermilk; plain milk gives flat boxty.","6":"Frying fat and flavor; oil alone works.","7":"Raises the smoke point of the butter; skip and use more butter.","8":"Side garnish for the plate; skip freely.","9":"Salty crisp side that completes the breakfast; skip for a vegetarian plate.","10":"Fried eggs served on the side; skip if you want just the boxty."},"sub":{"4":{"x":"150 ml buttermilk, or 150 ml milk + 2 tsp lemon juice stirred and rested 5 minutes","d":"The baking soda needs acid to rise; plain milk gives flatter, denser boxty.","k":["buttermilk","milk","lemon juice"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Squeeze the grated raw potato dry in a towel, then pour off the liquid and scrape the starch settled at the bottom back into the mix. That starch is the glue that holds the boxty together.","s":0},{"t":"Fold the soft-peak egg white and the buttermilk in gently and cook right away. The baking soda starts reacting with the acid at once, so waiting costs you lift.","s":1},{"t":"Fry over medium heat, 3 to 4 minutes per side, without pressing down. Keep finished boxty warm in a low oven while you cook the rest.","s":2}],"rl":"BXSLMLFFGPP"},"53037":{"lv":"RERERRRORE","w":{"0":"Fat for searing the pork; oil works, but butter adds richness.","2":"Salty, smoky flavor that seasons the whole stew; skip and add a pinch of salt.","4":"Sweetness and color; skip and add more potato.","5":"Sweet earthy root, similar to turnip; skip and use extra carrot or parsnip.","6":"Soft sweet greens that cook down into the broth; skip for a heartier potato-only coddle.","7":"Subtle background; omit.","8":"Adds fruity acidity that balances the pork; apple juice with a little vinegar works."},"sub":{"0":{"x":"1 tbsp olive oil or vegetable oil","d":"Loses the buttery flavor but sears just as well.","k":["olive oil","vegetable oil"]},"8":{"x":"100 ml unsweetened apple juice + 1 tsp cider vinegar","d":"Sweeter and less tangy than dry cider; the vinegar keeps it balanced.","k":["apple juice","cider vinegar"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Brown the chops hard in the hot butter for 2 to 3 minutes per side. The brown fond left in the casserole is what flavors the cider broth later.","s":0},{"t":"Cut the swede and potatoes small, about 1 cm, so the stew is ready in 35 minutes. Set the chops on top of the vegetables so they steam gently instead of boiling.","s":1},{"t":"Pork chops are done at 145F with a 3-minute rest, so check them before the vegetables overcook.","s":-1}],"rl":"FPPBVVVXMM"},"53035":{"lv":"EERERRRER","w":{"1":"Fat for wilting the cabbage; butter adds flavor but oil also works.","2":"Savory punch in the cabbage; skipping makes the colcannon milder.","4":"Mild sweet onion flavor that is classic in colcannon; skip and add chives.","5":"Smooths and enriches the mash; milk with butter also works.","6":"Tangy bite that cuts the richness of ham and cream; skip with a heavier result.","8":"Fried eggs on top make it a complete meal; skip for a side dish."},"sub":{"1":{"x":"3 tbsp olive oil or vegetable oil","d":"Loses the buttery flavor; cabbage still wilts nicely.","k":["olive oil","vegetable oil"]},"5":{"x":"100 ml whole milk + 1 tbsp butter","d":"Slightly less rich; warm the milk before mashing.","k":["whole milk","butter"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Start the potatoes in cold salted water so they cook evenly, then drain and let them steam-dry for a couple of minutes. Wet potatoes turn gluey and loose when mashed.","s":0},{"t":"Wilt the cabbage in the butter without letting it brown. It should stay bright green and keep a little bite so it still has texture in the mash.","s":1},{"t":"Mash the potatoes off the heat and fold in the cabbage and ham gently. Overworking releases starch and makes it gummy, and the runny egg yolk on top acts as a sauce.","s":2}],"rl":"BFXVXFXPP"},"3454":{"lv":"EEEE","w":{"3":"The acid in buttermilk activates the baking soda and tenderizes the crumb; milk with lemon juice works."},"sub":{"3":{"x":"1 cup whole milk + 1 tbsp lemon juice or white vinegar, rested 5 minutes","d":"Slightly thinner and less tangy; add an extra tablespoon of flour if the dough is too wet.","k":["whole milk","lemon juice","white vinegar"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Mix quickly and gently with your hand. The baking soda starts fizzing as soon as it meets the buttermilk, so overmixing knocks out the gas and makes dense farls.","s":1},{"t":"Cook on a dry skillet over medium heat. Too hot and the outside browns before the center cooks, and a hollow sound when tapped means the farls are done.","s":4},{"t":"Cut a shallow cross in each round before cooking so the center cooks through, and eat soon, since soda bread stales quickly.","s":3}],"rl":"SLZM"},"3455":{"lv":"EREER","w":{"1":"Adds richness and keeps the farls tender; skipping makes them plainer and slightly drier.","4":"Pan fat for browning; butter or oil works."},"sub":{"4":{"x":"1 tbsp butter","d":"Browns better and tastes richer but can scorch, so keep the heat at medium.","k":["butter"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Use floury potatoes such as russets and let them steam dry. Wet potatoes need more flour, and that makes the farls heavy and doughy.","s":0},{"t":"Work in the flour while the mash is still warm and stop once the dough just comes together. Overworking turns it tough.","s":2},{"t":"Cook until deep golden, about 3 to 4 minutes per side, and flip only once. They are fragile when hot, so use a wide spatula.","s":4}],"rl":"BFZSF"},"53038":{"lv":"EEEEER","w":{"5":"Appears to duplicate the green onion line; one bunch is cooked in butter, and extra may be used as a raw garnish."},"tips":[{"t":"Drain the potatoes and let them steam-dry in the hot pot for a minute before mashing. Wet potatoes make a loose, gluey champ.","s":0},{"t":"Warm the milk and half the butter before beating them in. Cold liquid cools the mash and turns it sticky.","s":1},{"t":"Fry the spring onions for only 2 minutes until just soft and bright green, then fold them in at the end so they keep color and bite.","s":2}],"rl":"BMFXXG"},"52781":{"lv":"EEERERREROROE","w":{"0":"Adds chewy grain that thickens the stew; barley or farro cooks the same way.","2":"Used for searing the lamb and vegetables in batches; any neutral oil works.","3":"Sweet whole alliums that melt into the stew; skip and use 2 sliced onions.","5":"Peppery root vegetable that sweetens as it cooks; skip and use extra carrot.","6":"Earthy, celery-like body in the stew; skip and use potato or parsnip.","8":"Deglazes the pan and adds acidity; broth with vinegar works.","9":"Offsets the wine's acidity; omit with little change.","10":"Earthy herb that suits lamb; skip and use dried thyme.","11":"Second herb for background; skip freely."},"sub":{"0":{"x":"300 g pearl barley or farro, cooked until tender","d":"Barley is softer and makes the stew thicker; farro keeps more chew.","k":["pearl barley","farro"]},"2":{"x":"120 ml vegetable oil, used in batches for searing","d":"Neutral oil lets the lamb and herbs lead; olive oil adds fruitiness.","k":["vegetable oil"]},"8":{"x":"150 ml chicken broth + 1 tbsp white wine vinegar or lemon juice","d":"Less depth than wine; the acid keeps the stew bright.","k":["chicken broth","white wine vinegar","lemon juice"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Brown the lamb in batches in a hot pan without crowding it. Crowding steams the meat, and the browned bits left behind are the base of the stew's flavor.","s":1},{"t":"Boil the wine hard to deglaze the pan and cook off its raw alcohol before adding the stock. Scrape up everything stuck on the bottom.","s":3},{"t":"Braise it in the oven at 350F rather than on the stove. The even heat keeps it at a gentle simmer, and if the liquid is thin at the end, boil it a few minutes uncovered.","s":4}],"rl":"BPFVVVVBMWXXM"},"3268":{"lv":"EEEEERRRE","w":{"5":"Flaky finishing salt gives the classic crunchy, salty top; skip and the focaccia is blander.","6":"Piney herb that perfumes the crust; skip for plain focaccia.","7":"Final drizzle for gloss and flavor; skipping leaves a drier top."},"tips":[{"t":"Use about 1 3/4 cups warm water, at 105F to 110F, which is why the dough is wet and sticky.","s":0},{"t":"Keep the dough wet and sticky and handle it with oiled hands rather than adding flour. High hydration is what gives focaccia its open, airy crumb.","s":1},{"t":"Dimple all the way down to the pan with oiled fingers so the oil pools in the holes and the dough does not puff into a pillow. The oil in the pan also fries the base to a crisp golden crust.","s":5}],"rl":"LWSZFZXFM"},"3269":{"lv":"EEEERE","w":{"4":"Tenderizes the dough and adds flavor; skip and the crust is chewier and plainer."},"tips":[{"t":"Use about 3/4 to 1 cup warm water, at 105F to 110F, so the dough is smooth and slightly tacky.","s":0},{"t":"Knead until it passes the windowpane test: stretch a small piece thin enough to see light through it without tearing. That gives a chewy, elastic crust.","s":1},{"t":"Preheat the stone or sheet pan for at least 30 minutes. The very hot surface gives a crisp base, and if the dough springs back when stretching, rest it 10 minutes.","s":3}],"rl":"LWSZFM"},"3630":{"lv":"EEERE","w":{"3":"Adds flavor and slightly softens the crumb; skip and the crust is crisper but plainer."},"tips":[{"t":"Do the folds with wet hands, lifting an edge and folding it over itself. This builds gluten in a very wet dough without kneading, which is what gives ciabatta its large holes.","s":1},{"t":"Flour the surface generously and handle the dough gently when stretching. Do not knead or press out the bubbles, since the air pockets are the prize.","s":3},{"t":"Bake until the loaf sounds hollow and the inside reaches about 205F. A small pan of boiling water on a lower rack adds steam for a crackly crust, and cooling for 30 minutes finishes the crumb.","s":5}],"rl":"SZLFM"},"8":{"lv":"EERERRERE","w":{"2":"Seasons the chicken evenly; skip it and use a little extra pesto or a minced garlic clove instead.","4":"Blistered tomatoes add sweetness and acidity that cut the rich cream cheese; without them the sauce is heavier.","5":"Wilts into the sauce for color and a mild green note; skipping loses freshness but not structure.","7":"Adds salty, nutty depth and tightens the sauce; skip it and add a pinch more salt.","8":"Only the cooking fat for the chicken; a neutral oil or butter does the same job."},"sub":{"8":{"x":"1 tbsp butter or vegetable oil","d":"Butter adds a little richness; neutral oil browns the chicken just as well.","k":["butter","vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Pat the thighs dry and leave them untouched for 3 to 4 minutes so they brown instead of steaming. Thighs are safe at 165F (74C) but are most tender cooked to about 175F.","s":1},{"t":"Add the pesto at the end over moderate heat; boiling dulls its basil color and turns the raw garlic harsh. A splash of starchy pasta water smooths the cream cheese into a glossy sauce.","s":2}],"rl":"BPXFVVXXF"},"9":{"lv":"ERREERRRREEERO","w":{"1":"Gives warm color and mild sweetness to the browned chicken; skipping changes little.","2":"Herbal backbone for chicken and sauce; skip or use a pinch of dried thyme or oregano.","3":"Browns the chicken and enriches the broth; olive oil can stand in.","5":"Adds sweetness and body to the soup-like base; skipping makes it less rounded.","6":"Adds savory depth to the base; skip and use a bit more onion.","7":"Rounds out the base; without it the sauce tastes flatter.","8":"Gives savory, slightly sweet tomato depth and a blush of color; skip for a plainer white sauce.","12":"Salty finish that slightly thickens the sauce; skip and season with a pinch more salt.","13":"Fresh garnish only."},"sub":{"3":{"x":"3 tbsp olive oil","d":"Still browns the chicken well; you lose some dairy richness in the broth.","k":["olive oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Brown the chicken in batches in a single layer; crowded pieces steam and turn pale. They only need color now since they finish cooking in the sauce (165F/74C).","s":0},{"t":"Make sure the broth is at a gentle simmer before adding the gnocchi, and stir the first minute so they do not stick. They are done when they float and plump; any longer and they turn mushy.","s":2}],"rl":"PXXFXVVXXMFBXG"},"31":{"lv":"EERRRREREERO","w":{"2":"Sweetens and builds the meat sauce; skipping makes it blunt.","3":"Deepens the sauce; skip and add a pinch of garlic powder.","4":"Only the fat for browning; the beef renders its own, so a little butter or any oil works.","5":"Adds herb flavor; jarred marinara already carries some, so skipping is mild.","7":"Adds chunkiness and fresh acidity to jarred sauce; without it the sauce is smoother and sweeter.","10":"Salty browned crust on top; skip and add extra mozzarella.","11":"Garnish only."},"sub":{"4":{"x":"1 tbsp butter or vegetable oil","d":"Either works for browning onion and beef.","k":["butter","vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Pull the rigatoni 2 minutes before al dente; it keeps absorbing sauce in the oven, and fully cooked pasta bakes into mush.","s":0},{"t":"Dollop the ricotta in spoonfuls instead of stirring it in; pockets of cool creamy cheese contrast with the saucy pasta and stay moist.","s":2},{"t":"Rest the dish 10 minutes out of the oven so the cheese sets and portions hold their shape.","s":3}],"rl":"BPXXFXAVFGGG"},"47":{"lv":"EEERRERO","w":{"3":"Garlic echoes the earthiness of the mushrooms; without it the sauce is flatter.","4":"Thyme underlines the mushroom flavor; skip or use a pinch of Italian seasoning.","6":"Adds salty nuttiness and thickens the cream; skip and add salt.","7":"Fresh color garnish."},"tips":[{"t":"Use a wide hot pan and brown the mushrooms in batches without crowding, and hold the salt until they are browned; salt early draws out water and they steam instead of caramelize.","s":1},{"t":"Grate the parmesan fine and add it to the cream at a gentle simmer, not a boil; boiling cheese turns grainy.","s":2},{"t":"Save a mug of pasta water before draining; its starch loosens the sauce and helps it cling to the fettuccine.","s":0}],"rl":"BVFXXFTG"},"60":{"lv":"EEEREEEO","w":{"3":"Garlic adds savory depth; skipping gives a milder risotto.","7":"Fresh garnish only."},"tips":[{"t":"Keep the broth at a bare simmer in a second pot; cold broth stalls the rice and makes it gluey. Add the next ladle when the pan looks nearly dry.","s":2},{"t":"Brown the mushrooms in a single layer in the hot butter and set them aside; adding them raw to the rice releases water that dilutes the risotto.","s":0},{"t":"Finish off the heat with the butter and parmesan and beat hard (mantecatura). The risotto should flow in a slow wave when you tilt the pan; loosen with hot broth if it stiffens.","s":3}],"rl":"VFXXBMTG"},"61":{"lv":"EEEEREEEO","w":{"4":"Seasons the crumb crust and adds salty nuttiness; skip and add salt to the crumbs.","7":"Only greases the pans and crisps the crust; any neutral oil works.","8":"Garnish only."},"sub":{"7":{"x":"3 tbsp vegetable oil","d":"Neutral flavor; olive oil adds a fruitier note but either crisps the crust.","k":["vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Salting draws out water and bitterness; pat the slices very dry afterward or the breading slides off and the baked slices turn soggy.","s":0},{"t":"Bake the breaded slices on a preheated, oiled pan so the underside sizzles and crisps, flipping once.","s":1},{"t":"Use a thin layer of marinara between layers; too much wet sauce softens the crust you just worked for. Rest 10 minutes before serving so it slices neatly.","s":2}],"rl":"VTTTXAGFG"},"62":{"lv":"EEEREERE","w":{"3":"Adds salty depth to the bland ricotta filling; skip and add a pinch of salt.","5":"Adds greens and color to the cheese filling; skipping gives a plain cheese filling.","6":"Gives savory lift to the mild ricotta; skip and use a pinch of garlic powder."},"tips":[{"t":"Boil a few extra shells and drain them 1 to 2 minutes early; they tear easily and finish cooking in the oven. Spread on an oiled sheet so they do not stick together.","s":0},{"t":"Squeeze the thawed spinach in a towel until no more water comes out; leftover water makes the filling weep and thins the sauce.","s":1},{"t":"Spoon the filling in with a zip-top bag with the corner snipped; it is faster and neater than a spoon. Keep the dish covered for the first bake so the pasta steams tender.","s":2}],"rl":"BPFXTVXA"},"66":{"lv":"EERERERR","w":{"1":"Broth gives the polenta savory depth; water works but needs more salt.","2":"Softens the polenta and adds creaminess; extra broth or half-and-half can replace it.","4":"Salty creamy finish; skip and add salt.","6":"Garlic enhances the mushrooms; skipping makes them flatter.","7":"Thyme pairs naturally with mushrooms; skip with little loss."},"sub":{"1":{"x":"4 cups water + 1 tsp salt + 1 extra tbsp butter","d":"Less savory; taste the finished polenta and season more.","k":["water","salt","butter"]},"2":{"x":"1 cup half-and-half, or 1 more cup of broth","d":"Half-and-half is richer; extra broth is lighter but still works.","k":["half-and-half","chicken broth"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Whisk the polenta in a thin stream into the simmering liquid to avoid lumps. If it tightens as it cooks, loosen with a splash of hot water since polenta keeps thickening as it sits.","s":0},{"t":"Add the butter and parmesan off the heat and taste before salting; the cheese and broth already carry salt.","s":1},{"t":"Let the mushrooms sit untouched in the hot butter for 3 minutes before stirring so they brown rather than steam.","s":2}],"rl":"BMMFXVXX"},"69":{"lv":"EREERERR","w":{"1":"Garlic scents the butter; skipping makes a milder pasta.","4":"Makes the orzo creamy; milk with butter gives a lighter version.","6":"Adds color and a light fresh note; skipping leaves a plainer parmesan orzo.","7":"Brightens the rich cream and cheese; skip and add a splash of vinegar or a pinch of salt."},"sub":{"4":{"x":"1/2 cup whole milk + 1 tbsp butter","d":"Thinner and less rich; stir it in off the heat to avoid curdling.","k":["whole milk","butter"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Toast the dry orzo in the butter until it smells nutty and turns pale gold; it adds flavor and helps the grains stay separate.","s":0},{"t":"Stir often; orzo releases starch that makes a risotto-like sauce but sticks to the bottom. Add a splash of hot broth if it tightens before the pasta is tender.","s":1},{"t":"Add the lemon last and off the heat; acid in hot dairy can curdle.","s":2}],"rl":"FXBMFTVA"},"3211":{"lv":"EEEERRROEEERO","w":{"4":"Adds salty nuttiness to the crust; skip for a milder crust.","5":"Seasons the crumb coating; skip and use garlic powder or oregano.","6":"Seasons the cutlets; unsalted breaded chicken tastes bland, so use about 1/2 tsp.","7":"A mild bite; skip.","8":"Only the frying fat; any neutral oil works.","11":"Traditional bed for the cutlets; any pasta or a salad works instead.","12":"Garnish only."},"sub":{"8":{"x":"1/2 cup vegetable oil or canola oil","d":"Neutral flavor and higher smoke point; olive oil adds a fruitier note.","k":["vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Pound from the center outward to an even 1/2 inch. Uneven cutlets dry out at the thin end while the thick end is still raw.","s":0},{"t":"Press the panko firmly into each cutlet and let them rest 5 minutes so the coating sets and does not slide off in the pan.","s":2},{"t":"Spoon sauce and cheese only on the center, leaving the edges bare so the crust stays crisp. Check that the thickest part reaches 165F (74C).","s":4}],"rl":"PTTTXXZZFAFBG"},"3213":{"lv":"EEROEEERERRO","w":{"2":"Seasons the chicken and flour; unsalted cutlets taste bland, so season them.","3":"Light bite; skip.","4":"Browning fat for the chicken; any neutral oil works.","7":"Garlic adds savory depth to the mushroom sauce; skipping makes it milder.","9":"Builds the sauce body; water with a bouillon cube works in a pinch.","10":"Mellows the wine and adds silkiness; skip for a sharper, thinner sauce.","11":"Garnish only."},"sub":{"4":{"x":"2 tbsp vegetable oil","d":"Neutral oil browns the cutlets as well; olive adds a faint fruity note.","k":["vegetable oil"]},"9":{"x":"3/4 cup water + 1/2 chicken bouillon cube","d":"Slightly less rich; taste for salt before seasoning.","k":["water","bouillon cube"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Shake off excess flour after dredging. A thin coat browns and lightly thickens the sauce, while a heavy coat turns pasty.","s":0},{"t":"Use dry Marsala for a savory sauce, and simmer it until it smells caramelized and loses its raw alcohol edge before adding broth. Sweet Marsala makes the dish taste like dessert.","s":3},{"t":"Let the mushrooms brown without stirring for the first 3 minutes; the fond they build is the flavor of the sauce. Finish the chicken in the sauce to 165F (74C).","s":2}],"rl":"PTZZFFVXMMFG"},"3215":{"lv":"ERERERROEORREOE","w":{"1":"Pork adds fat and tenderness; all beef works but is a little drier.","3":"Milk with the crumbs forms a panade that keeps the meatballs moist; water works too.","5":"Adds salty depth in the meatballs; skipping makes them less savory.","6":"Seasons the meat; skip and add a pinch of garlic powder.","7":"Fresh herb note in the meat; skipping barely changes the dish.","9":"A light bite; skip.","10":"Only the fat for cooking the onion; any neutral oil or butter works.","11":"Sweetens and builds the sauce base; skipping makes it plainer.","13":"Mild herb note; skip or use Italian seasoning."},"sub":{"1":{"x":"1/2 lb more ground beef (1 1/2 lb total)","d":"Slightly leaner and less tender; do not overmix.","k":["ground beef"]},"3":{"x":"1/4 cup water","d":"Hydrates the crumbs equally; milk adds a little richness.","k":["water"]},"10":{"x":"2 tbsp vegetable oil","d":"Neutral; olive oil gives a slightly fruitier base.","k":["vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Mix the meat gently, only until combined. Overworked meat turns dense and rubbery, and the milk-soaked crumbs keep the meatballs tender.","s":1},{"t":"Check one meatball with a thermometer; ground beef and pork should reach 160F (71C) before going into the sauce.","s":2},{"t":"Simmer the sauce gently, not at a boil; a hard boil toughens the meatballs. 20 minutes lets them take on tomato flavor and the sauce take on their savoriness.","s":4}],"rl":"PPTMTXXGZZFXVXB"},"3217":{"lv":"ERRRERREEROR","w":{"1":"Only the cooking fat; butter or another oil works.","2":"Sweet base for the sauce; skipping makes it thinner in flavor.","3":"Adds savory depth; skip and add a pinch of garlic powder.","5":"Gives the sauce its gentle heat; skip for a mild version.","6":"Vodka releases tomato flavors water cannot, but a splash of broth with lemon is close.","9":"Salty finish that thickens the sauce; skip and add a pinch of salt.","10":"Fresh garnish.","11":"Seasons the sauce; taste and add as needed."},"sub":{"1":{"x":"2 tbsp butter","d":"Adds a little richness; do not let it brown.","k":["butter"]},"6":{"x":"1/3 cup water or chicken broth + 1 tsp lemon juice","d":"Misses the vodka's sharp edge; the sauce is slightly flatter but still creamy.","k":["water","chicken broth","lemon juice"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Cook the tomato paste until it turns brick-red and starts to stick; this caramelizes its sugars and removes the tinny raw taste.","s":2},{"t":"Let the vodka simmer until the sharp smell of alcohol is gone before adding the tomatoes; otherwise the sauce tastes harsh.","s":3},{"t":"Loosen the sauce with pasta water a splash at a time; the starch keeps the cream emulsified and the sauce clinging to the penne.","s":5}],"rl":"BFXXAXMVFXGZ"},"3218":{"lv":"RERRERRERREEEOERR","w":{"0":"Only the fat for the soffritto; any neutral oil or butter works.","2":"Adds sweetness to the ragu base; skipping makes it sharper.","3":"Adds savory depth to the base; skip and use a little more onion.","5":"Pork adds fat and tenderness; all beef works but is a little leaner.","6":"Wine adds acidity and depth; broth with a splash of vinegar is close.","8":"Concentrates tomato flavor and deepens color; skipping gives a lighter ragu.","9":"Seasons the ragu through the long simmer; taste at the end.","13":"A pinch of nutmeg is the classic bechamel note; skipping barely changes it.","15":"Salty nutty layers between sauce and noodles; skip and add salt.","16":"Gives the melty top; skip and use more parmesan."},"sub":{"0":{"x":"2 tbsp butter or vegetable oil","d":"Either works for softening vegetables.","k":["butter","vegetable oil"]},"5":{"x":"1/2 lb more ground beef (1 1/2 lb total)","d":"Slightly leaner; add a splash of milk during simmering for tenderness.","k":["ground beef"]},"6":{"x":"1/2 cup chicken broth + 1 tsp white vinegar","d":"Less depth but similar acidity; reduce fully before adding tomatoes.","k":["chicken broth","white vinegar"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Simmer the ragu low with the lid ajar for the full hour; slow reduction concentrates flavor. Add a splash of water if it sticks, and it should be thick, not soupy, or the lasagna will weep.","s":1},{"t":"Whisk the milk into the roux gradually. The bechamel should coat a spoon but still pour; if lumpy, whisk hard or use an immersion blender.","s":2},{"t":"Rest the lasagna 15 minutes after baking so the layers set and slices keep their shape.","s":5}],"rl":"FXVVPPMVXZFTMXBXG"},"3238":{"lv":"EROERERERRR","w":{"1":"Seasons the chicken; skip only if seasoning later.","2":"Light bite; skip.","3":"Only the cooking fat; butter or another oil works.","4":"Gives the cream sauce savory depth; skipping makes it milder.","6":"Loosens the sauce and lifts browned bits; water with a pinch of salt works.","8":"Salty and thickens the sauce; skip and add salt.","9":"Herb note in the sauce; skip or add dried oregano.","10":"Adds color and mild greens; skipping simplifies the sauce."},"sub":{"3":{"x":"2 tbsp butter or vegetable oil","d":"Butter adds richness; brown at a slightly lower heat.","k":["butter","vegetable oil"]},"6":{"x":"1/2 cup water + a pinch of salt","d":"Less savory; taste and season the sauce.","k":["water","salt"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Leave the chicken untouched until it releases easily from the pan; the browned bits it leaves behind give the cream sauce its flavor. Cook to 165F (74C).","s":1},{"t":"If your sun-dried tomatoes are oil-packed, drain and blot them; dry-packed ones should be soaked in hot water for 10 minutes first.","s":2},{"t":"Simmer the cream until it coats a spoon before adding the spinach; the spinach releases water that will thin the sauce.","s":3}],"rl":"PZZFXXMFXXV"},"3242":{"lv":"EEREEEEEROORO","w":{"2":"Garlic adds savory depth; skipping makes a milder sauce.","5":"Loosens the cream sauce; half-and-half or cream thinned with water works.","8":"Seasons the sauce; taste and add as needed.","9":"A light bite; skip.","10":"Classic cream-sauce spice; a pinch is subtle and skippable.","11":"Gives the melty top; skip and use more parmesan.","12":"Garnish."},"sub":{"5":{"x":"3/4 cup heavy cream + 1/4 cup water, or 1 cup half-and-half","d":"Richer and slightly thicker; fine for the baked sauce.","k":["heavy cream","water","half-and-half"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Cook the flour in the butter for a full minute, then add the dairy slowly while whisking. The roux keeps the sauce from breaking in the oven.","s":1},{"t":"Grate your own parmesan and add it off the heat; pre-shredded cheese has anti-caking starch that can make the sauce grainy.","s":3},{"t":"Undercook the penne by 2 minutes; it keeps absorbing the sauce during baking.","s":0}],"rl":"BFXTFMXPZZXGG"},"3244":{"lv":"EEERRRRERR","w":{"3":"Softens the tomato acidity and gives a pink sauce; skip for a plainer, tangier sauce.","4":"Adds color and mild greens; skipping simplifies the dish.","5":"Adds herb flavor; jarred marinara already has some.","6":"Adds savory depth; skipping makes a milder sauce.","8":"Salty sharp finish on top; skip and add extra mozzarella.","9":"Seasons the sauce; jarred marinara is already salted, so use sparingly."},"tips":[{"t":"Refrigerated tortellini finishes cooking in the oven, so pull it 2 minutes early; fully cooked tortellini split and turn mushy.","s":0},{"t":"Make sure every piece is coated in sauce; exposed tortellini dry out. If the mix looks dry, add a splash of water or 1/2 cup extra marinara.","s":2},{"t":"Keep the foil on for the first bake so steam finishes the filling, then uncover to brown the cheese.","s":4}],"rl":"BPMFVXXGGZ"},"3587":{"lv":"EERRERROERERORO","w":{"2":"Sweetens and builds the meat sauce; skipping makes it blunt.","3":"Deepens the sauce; skip and add a pinch of garlic powder.","5":"Smooths and thickens the sauce; skip and use a little more crushed tomato.","6":"Adds herb flavor; skip or use oregano.","7":"Tames the tomato acidity; skip if your tomatoes taste sweet.","9":"Binds the ricotta so it sets into sliceable layers; skipping gives a looser, creamier layer.","11":"Salty nutty flavor in the ricotta and top; skip and add extra mozzarella.","12":"Garnish only.","13":"Seasons the sauce and ricotta; taste as you go, since ricotta is bland.","14":"A light bite; skip."},"tips":[{"t":"Pull the ziti 3 minutes early; it continues cooking in the oven and fully cooked pasta turns soft and mushy.","s":0},{"t":"Drain the fat from the browned beef before adding tomatoes; otherwise the sauce is greasy and the baked top looks oily.","s":1},{"t":"Season the ricotta mixture with salt, because ricotta is bland. The egg sets it into creamy layers that slice cleanly after a 10 minute rest.","s":3}],"rl":"BPXXVMXWFTGXGZZ"},"52770":{"lv":"ERRERREERREO","w":{"1":"Only the fat for softening the onion; butter or another oil works.","2":"Adds savory depth; skip and add a pinch of garlic powder.","4":"Adds earthy depth and bulk to the meat sauce; skipping makes it plainer.","5":"Herbal note that makes the sauce taste Italian; skip with milder flavor.","7":"Provides the liquid and savory depth for the sauce; water with a bouillon cube works.","8":"Concentrates tomato flavor and sweetness; skipping gives a thinner tomato taste.","9":"Adds umami and tang; soy sauce is close.","11":"Topping only."},"sub":{"1":{"x":"1 tbsp butter or vegetable oil","d":"Either softens the onion fine.","k":["butter","vegetable oil"]},"7":{"x":"300 ml hot water + 1 beef bouillon cube","d":"Similar savoriness; taste for salt before adding more.","k":["water","bouillon cube"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Brown the mince undisturbed in a hot pan, breaking it up only after it forms a crust. Crowded or constantly stirred meat turns grey and bland.","s":0},{"t":"Simmer partly covered, then uncover for the last 10 minutes if it looks loose; the sauce should coat a spoon without running off.","s":1},{"t":"Finish the spaghetti in the sauce with a splash of pasta water so the sauce clings to the strands instead of pooling.","s":2}],"rl":"XFXPVXVMXXBG"},"52780":{"lv":"EERREREROO","w":{"2":"Softens the onions; butter works too.","3":"Adds flavor to the potatoes as they cook; water with a pinch of salt works.","5":"Gives the gratin its golden crust; skip for a paler top.","7":"Smoky salty crispness served beside the gratin; skipping makes it milder.","8":"Side greens; skip.","9":"Side greens; skip."},"sub":{"2":{"x":"2 tbsp butter","d":"Adds dairy richness; do not let it brown.","k":["butter"]},"3":{"x":"100 ml water + a pinch of salt","d":"Less savory, but the cream carries most of the flavor.","k":["water","salt"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Slice the potatoes evenly and dry them after draining, otherwise extra water dilutes the cream and the gratin turns soupy.","s":1},{"t":"Add cream only to cover the potatoes, not drown them. Too much liquid prevents the top from browning.","s":1},{"t":"Season the chicken breasts and cook to 165F (74C), then rest 5 minutes before slicing so they stay juicy; crisp the bacon separately.","s":2}],"rl":"BVFMFGPXVV"},"52783":{"lv":"EEERORRREORERRREE","w":{"0":"Only the fat for browning; any neutral oil works.","3":"Sweet aniseed softness that echoes the sausage; skipping makes it less layered.","4":"Adds smoky notes; skip with little loss.","5":"Adds savory depth; skip and use a pinch of garlic powder.","6":"Boosts the fennel flavor of the sausage; skip if your sausages are already strongly fennel.","7":"Wine adds acidity and depth; broth with vinegar is close.","9":"Balances the acid in tinned tomatoes; skip if tomatoes are sweet.","10":"Briny, salty note that cuts the rich sausage; skipping makes it plainer.","12":"Adds salty sharpness to the pesto; skip and add salt and a little parmesan.","13":"Gives hidden umami to the pesto; skip and add a pinch of salt.","14":"Garlic for the pesto; skip and the pesto is milder."},"sub":{"0":{"x":"2 1/2 tbsp vegetable oil","d":"Neutral oil; olive oil gives a fruitier base.","k":["vegetable oil"]},"7":{"x":"100 ml chicken broth + 1 tsp red wine vinegar","d":"Less depth, similar acidity; reduce it fully before adding tomatoes.","k":["chicken broth","red wine vinegar"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Let the sausage pieces sit untouched until deeply browned; the fond left in the pan seasons the whole ragu.","s":0},{"t":"Pulse the pesto to a rough paste, not a puree. Over-blitzing heats and bruises the basil so it turns dark and bitter.","s":2},{"t":"Spoon the pesto over the ragu at serving time; its fresh basil and anchovy lift the rich sauce, and heating it dulls the color.","s":3}],"rl":"FPXVXXXMVWXBXXXFX"},"52796":{"lv":"REERRRRRRROREEEEROO","w":{"0":"Part of the cooking fat; olive oil works.","1":"Part of the cooking fat; butter works.","3":"Seasons the chicken; unsalted chicken breast tastes bland.","4":"Adds sweetness and color to the veggie mix; skipping makes it less primavera.","5":"Adds texture and color; skipping reduces vegetable variety.","6":"Adds earthy bulk; skipping changes little.","7":"Sweet pepper (likely a red bell pepper); skip for fewer veggies.","8":"Sweet base for the sauce; skipping makes it less rounded.","9":"Adds savory depth to the cream sauce.","10":"Gentle heat; skip for a mild sauce.","11":"Adds acidity to cut the cream; broth and lemon is close.","12":"Loosens the sauce; half cream with water works.","16":"Seasons the sauce; taste at the end.","17":"A light bite; skip.","18":"Garnish only."},"sub":{"0":{"x":"1 tbsp more olive oil","d":"Loses the dairy flavor but cooks the chicken the same.","k":["olive oil"]},"1":{"x":"3 tbsp butter","d":"Browns faster; watch the heat.","k":["butter"]},"11":{"x":"1/2 cup chicken broth + 1 tsp lemon juice","d":"Less depth; still brightens the cream.","k":["chicken broth","lemon juice"]},"12":{"x":"1/4 cup heavy cream + 1/4 cup water","d":"Slightly richer; fine in this sauce.","k":["heavy cream","water"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Cook the chicken to 165F (74C) and rest it before slicing so the juices stay in; slice it across the grain.","s":4},{"t":"Add the vegetables in order of firmness, broccoli and squash first, and hold the salt until they are tender and browned so they do not steam.","s":5},{"t":"Add the parmesan off the heat and use pasta water to loosen the sauce; the sauce thickens as it cools.","s":8}],"rl":"FFPZVVVVXXXMMFXBZZG"},"52810":{"lv":"ERRREERRRRRRRRRRRR","w":{"1":"Browns for a flavorful crust and lightly thickens the sauce; skip for a thinner braise.","2":"Browning fat; any neutral oil works.","3":"Adds richness to browning and finishing; olive oil works.","6":"Adds savory depth to the soffritto; skip and use more onion.","7":"Adds aniseed sweetness; skip with a plainer sauce.","8":"Adds savory depth; skip and the sauce is milder.","9":"Gives a bright citrus note; skip and add extra lemon zest in the gremolata.","10":"Herbal note; skip or use thyme.","11":"Quiet background aroma; skip.","12":"Wine adds acidity to cut the veal richness; broth with lemon is close.","13":"Adds body to the braise; water with a bouillon cube works.","14":"Adds sweetness and acidity; skipping makes the sauce less rounded.","15":"Gremolata base; skip and the finish loses freshness.","16":"Gremolata punch; skip with less bite.","17":"Gremolata brightness against the rich veal; skip and add orange zest."},"sub":{"2":{"x":"2 tbsp vegetable oil","d":"Neutral; olive adds a fruitier note.","k":["vegetable oil"]},"3":{"x":"3 tbsp more olive oil","d":"Loses the dairy flavor in the sauce.","k":["olive oil"]},"12":{"x":"1 cup chicken broth + 1 tbsp lemon juice","d":"Less depth; simmer to reduce by half as in the recipe.","k":["chicken broth","lemon juice"]},"13":{"x":"1/2 cup water + 1/2 chicken bouillon cube","d":"Slightly less rich; taste for salt.","k":["water","bouillon cube"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Brown the shanks deeply on every side in a hot pot without crowding; the caramelized fond is the base of the sauce. Tie them with kitchen twine to hold their shape.","s":2},{"t":"Braise with the bone standing upright so the marrow stays in place. Keep the oven at 300F and cook until fork-tender, which takes about 2 hours.","s":5},{"t":"Add the gremolata at the very end, because heat flattens its raw citrus and garlic aroma; sprinkle it just before serving.","s":11}],"rl":"PTFFXVVVXXXXMMVGGG"},"52838":{"lv":"EEERRRRERRROOER","w":{"0":"Only browning fat; the duck renders plenty of its own.","3":"Adds savory depth; skipping makes a milder sauce.","4":"The warm Venetian spice that sets this ragu apart; skipping makes it a plain tomato ragu.","5":"Lightly thickens the sauce; skip with a thinner sauce.","6":"Wine adds acidity and depth; broth with vinegar is close.","8":"Adds savory depth and liquid to the braise; water with a bouillon cube works.","9":"Gives the sauce a piney aroma; skip or use thyme.","10":"Quiet background aroma; skip.","11":"Balances tomato acidity; skip if the tomatoes are sweet.","12":"Rounds off acidity and gives silkiness; skip.","14":"Salty nutty finish; skip and add salt."},"sub":{"0":{"x":"1 tbsp vegetable oil","d":"Neutral oil.","k":["vegetable oil"]},"6":{"x":"250 ml chicken broth + 1 tbsp red wine vinegar","d":"Less depth and tannin; reduce a little longer.","k":["chicken broth","red wine vinegar"]},"8":{"x":"1 cup (250 ml) hot water with 1 chicken bouillon cube","d":"Similar savoriness; taste before adding salt.","k":["water","bouillon cube"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Brown the duck legs skin-side down until the fat renders and the skin is deep golden, and pour off most of the fat so the ragu is not greasy.","s":0},{"t":"Simmer low until the meat pulls from the bone; duck legs are done well past safe temperature, when they are tender enough to shred. Skim any fat from the surface.","s":1},{"t":"Finish the paccheri in the ragu with a splash of pasta water; the wide tubes trap shreds of duck and sauce.","s":2}],"rl":"FPXXXTMVMXXWMBG"},"52844":{"lv":"RRERRREREOEERRO","w":{"0":"Only the fat for softening the bacon and vegetables; any oil works.","1":"Adds smoky saltiness; skipping makes the ragu milder.","3":"Adds savory depth to the base; skip and use a little more onion.","4":"Adds sweetness; skipping makes the sauce sharper.","5":"Adds savory depth; skipping makes it milder.","7":"Concentrates tomato flavor; skipping gives a lighter ragu.","9":"Balances the acid in canned tomatoes; sugar does the same.","12":"Gives a melty topping; skip and use more parmesan.","13":"Salty nutty finish; skip and add salt.","14":"Garnish only."},"sub":{"0":{"x":"1 tbsp butter or vegetable oil","d":"Either works.","k":["butter","vegetable oil"]},"9":{"x":"1 tbsp sugar","d":"Same balancing job without honey notes.","k":["sugar"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Cook the bacon until the fat renders before adding the vegetables so they soften in the flavorful fat.","s":0},{"t":"Loosen the creme fraiche with the water so it pours as a sauce; stiff creme fraiche bakes thick and greasy and leaves dry noodle corners.","s":4},{"t":"Cover every noodle with sauce, since exposed edges stay hard, and rest the lasagne 10 minutes before cutting.","s":3}],"rl":"FXXVVXPXVWBFGGG"},"53014":{"lv":"EREEEREEORO","w":{"1":"Feeds the yeast and helps the crust brown; skipping gives a slightly slower rise.","5":"Enriches the dough and adds fruity aroma; skip for a leaner crust.","8":"Classic pizza herb; skip with a plainer sauce.","9":"Fresh peppery finish that defines Margherita; skip and it tastes plainer.","10":"A light bite; skip."},"tips":[{"t":"Use lukewarm water around 100 to 110F (38 to 43C); hot water kills the yeast, and if no foam forms in 10 minutes, start again.","s":1},{"t":"Knead until the dough is smooth and springs back; it is ready when you can stretch a small piece thin enough to see light through it. Add flour sparingly, since too much makes a dry crust.","s":6},{"t":"Preheat the oven fully and put the pizza on a hot tray for a crisp base. Add the basil after baking, since heat blackens it.","s":10}],"rl":"MWLSZFXFXGZ"},"53064":{"lv":"EEEEOO","w":{"4":"Garnish only.","5":"A light bite; skip."},"tips":[{"t":"Reserve pasta water before draining; its starch emulsifies the butter and cream into a sauce that clings to the fettuccine.","s":0},{"t":"Add the parmesan off the heat in handfuls and grate it fresh; boiling cheese clumps and turns oily.","s":1}],"rl":"BFFXGZ"},"88":{"lv":"EEEROO","w":{"3":"Adds sweetness and color; skip for a simpler pesto flatbread.","4":"Fresh finish; skip.","5":"Sweet tangy finishing note; skip."},"tips":[{"t":"Bake the naan plain for 2 minutes first so the base crisps before the toppings weigh it down.","s":2},{"t":"Add basil and balsamic after baking; the oven blackens basil and burns the balsamic sugar.","s":2}],"rl":"BXFVGG"},"3602":{"lv":"EREREEERREROR","w":{"1":"Crunchy croutons add texture; skipping makes it less of a Caesar.","2":"Only the oil for the croutons; any oil or melted butter works.","3":"Adds sharp savory depth to the dressing; skip with a milder dressing.","7":"Helps emulsify the dressing and adds tang; skip.","8":"Adds extra umami; skip if you use anchovies.","10":"Salty nutty flavor; skipping makes it less savory.","11":"A light bite; skip.","12":"Seasons the croutons; the anchovies and cheese are already salty, so taste first."},"sub":{"2":{"x":"2 tbsp melted butter","d":"Richer, browns slightly faster; watch the oven.","k":["butter"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Mash the anchovies and garlic to a paste with a fork before whisking in the yolk; chunks leave sharp pockets.","s":1},{"t":"Add the oil drop by drop at first, then in a thin stream. If the dressing breaks, whisk a teaspoon of water into a fresh yolk and drizzle the broken dressing into it. Use a pasteurized yolk for vulnerable guests.","s":2},{"t":"Dress the romaine just before serving with dry, cold leaves so they stay crisp.","s":3}],"rl":"VGFXXTATXFXZZ"},"52771":{"lv":"EEEEERRO","w":{"5":"Adds a mild herb note; skip with a plainer sauce.","6":"Fresh herb finish that lifts the sauce; skip.","7":"Salty finish; skip, since arrabbiata is traditionally cheese-free."},"tips":[{"t":"Cook the garlic gently just until fragrant; browned garlic turns bitter.","s":1},{"t":"Add the pepper flakes to the oil early so the heat blooms and spreads through the sauce; use more for the traditional fiery version.","s":1},{"t":"Finish the penne in the sauce with a splash of pasta water so it clings to the ridges.","s":2}],"rl":"BFXVXXGG"},"52775":{"lv":"EREROREEEERO","w":{"1":"Adds sweetness to the lentil filling; skipping makes it less rounded.","3":"Adds moisture and bulk; skipping makes it plainer.","4":"A small herb sprinkle; skip.","5":"Adds color and greens; skipping simplifies the filling.","7":"Fat for the white sauce; olive oil works.","9":"Base for the white sauce; any plant milk works.","10":"Adds tang to the white sauce; skip with a blander sauce.","11":"Acid that thickens and brightens the plant milk sauce; lemon juice works."},"sub":{"7":{"x":"2 1/2 tbsp olive oil","d":"Gives a slightly fruitier white sauce.","k":["olive oil"]},"9":{"x":"300 ml (1 1/4 cups) unsweetened oat or almond milk, or whole milk if not keeping it vegan","d":"Oat milk is creamiest; unsweetened only.","k":["oat milk","almond milk","whole milk"],"wn":"Almond milk contains tree nuts; oat milk may contain gluten; whole milk is dairy and not vegan"},"11":{"x":"1 tsp lemon juice","d":"Brighter flavor; same effect.","k":["lemon juice"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Use green or brown lentils for filling that holds its shape; red lentils turn to mush. Add water as needed, stirring so it does not stick.","s":1},{"t":"Whisk the plant milk into the roux gradually to avoid lumps; it thickens further as it bakes.","s":4},{"t":"Rest the lasagna 10 minutes after baking so the layers set and slice cleanly.","s":5}],"rl":"PVXVGVBFTMXA"},"52777":{"lv":"REREERERO","w":{"0":"Adds creamy bites; skipping makes a drier salad.","2":"Fresh herb note that defines the salad; skip and it tastes plainer.","5":"Adds briny bite; skipping makes it blander.","7":"Seasons the salad; taste first, since olives and tuna are salty.","8":"A light bite; skip."},"tips":[{"t":"Rinse the pasta in cold water until cool before tossing in the mozzarella, or the cheese will melt and smear.","s":4},{"t":"Resting lets the pasta absorb the dressing; refresh with a drizzle of oil and a squeeze of lemon before serving, since cold pasta soaks it up.","s":6},{"t":"Drain the tuna well and add it in chunks last so it does not break up into paste.","s":6}],"rl":"FVXBFXPZZ"},"52811":{"lv":"EERRREREROEERR","w":{"2":"Adds sweetness to the soup base; skip with less depth.","3":"Adds savory depth; skip and use a bit more onion.","4":"Adds savory depth; skipping makes the soup flatter.","6":"Adds acidity and a hint of sweetness; skipping gives a milder soup.","8":"Gives a piney aroma; skip or use thyme.","9":"Quiet background herb; skip.","12":"Browns on top for sweet crispness; skip.","13":"Salty nutty finish; skip and add salt."},"tips":[{"t":"Cook the vegetables slowly for 10 to 15 minutes until soft and sweet; this soffritto is the flavor base for the whole soup.","s":0},{"t":"Mash some of the beans against the pot to thicken the soup, since the bread soaks up the liquid and it should end up thick.","s":1},{"t":"Ribollita means reboiled, and it is best made a day ahead so the bread melts into the soup. Reheat it before the final bake.","s":3}],"rl":"FXVVXPVMXXVBGG"},"52823":{"lv":"EEEREREEROO","w":{"3":"Adds acidity to the risotto; broth with lemon is close.","5":"Brightens the rich seafood and rice; skipping makes it heavier.","8":"Adds green freshness and bite; skip with a simpler dish.","9":"A light bite; skip.","10":"Seafood risotto is often made without cheese; skip or use sparingly."},"sub":{"3":{"x":"125 ml vegetable broth + 1 tsp lemon juice","d":"Less depth; reduce it slowly as in the recipe.","k":["vegetable broth","lemon juice"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Use arborio or carnaroli rice and keep the stock hot; add each ladle when the last is absorbed, stirring to release the starch.","s":3},{"t":"Cook the shrimp until pink and opaque and the salmon to 145F (63C) so they are safe but still tender; add them at the end, since they overcook quickly.","s":6},{"t":"Add the lemon off the heat; heat and acid together dull its aroma.","s":4}],"rl":"FXBMMAPPVZG"},"52849":{"lv":"RRRRERERRREREORE","w":{"0":"Only the fat for the garlic; any oil works.","1":"Adds savory depth to the tomato sauce; skipping makes it plain.","2":"Balances the acid in canned tomatoes; skip if the tomatoes are sweet.","3":"Adds tang; skipping makes the sauce flatter.","5":"Adds a fresh herb note; skip.","7":"Loosens the mascarpone sauce; water or half-and-half works.","8":"Salty nutty flavor in the white sauce; skip and add salt.","9":"Gives a melty top; skip and use more parmesan.","11":"Salty nutty flavor in the filling; skip and add salt.","13":"Classic filling spice; skip.","14":"Adds bulk and protein to the filling; skipping makes it plainer."},"sub":{"0":{"x":"3 tbsp vegetable oil","d":"Neutral oil.","k":["vegetable oil"]},"7":{"x":"3 tbsp water or half-and-half","d":"Water loosens just as well; half-and-half is richer.","k":["water","half-and-half"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Squeeze the wilted spinach in a towel until bone dry; leftover water makes the ricotta filling runny and the sauce watery.","s":1},{"t":"Fill them with a piping bag or a zip bag with a corner cut off, since a spoon tears them.","s":2},{"t":"Simmer the tomato sauce until thickened, with the sugar and vinegar balancing the tomato acidity.","s":0}],"rl":"FXWAVXFMXGVXBXPB"},"52866":{"lv":"EREER","w":{"1":"Roasted garlic turns sweet and blends into the sauce; skipping makes it plainer.","4":"Fried crisp sage is the aromatic finish; skip and it loses its main herbal note."},"tips":[{"t":"Roast the squash until the edges caramelize; browned edges give a sweeter, deeper sauce.","s":1},{"t":"Blend with the starchy pasta water a little at a time; it makes the sauce silky and helps it cling to the linguine.","s":3},{"t":"Fry the sage 30 seconds until it stops sizzling and turns crisp; it burns quickly, so drain it at once.","s":3}],"rl":"VXFBX"},"15":{"lv":"EEREERROO","w":{"2":"Sweet, tangy chew that cuts the creamy sauce; skipping makes it milder.","4":"Gives body and protein to the blended sauce; ricotta works instead.","5":"Loosens the sauce; water or extra pasta water works.","6":"Salty nutty depth; skip and add a pinch of salt.","7":"Fresh garnish.","8":"A bit of heat on top; skip."},"sub":{"4":{"x":"1/2 cup ricotta","d":"Milder and slightly creamier; blend the same way.","k":["ricotta"]},"5":{"x":"1/4 cup water","d":"Thinner but still smooth; add pasta water for body.","k":["water"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Blend the cottage cheese sauce until completely smooth; any curds read as grainy. Warm it only by tossing with the hot pasta, since heating it on the stove can make it separate.","s":1},{"t":"Loosen the sauce with a splash of pasta water if it clings too tightly after tossing; it thickens as it cools.","s":2}],"rl":"BPVXTMXGG"},"73":{"lv":"EEERRRO","w":{"3":"Fresh herb note that defines a caprese; skipping makes it plainer.","4":"Adds a salty herbal layer; skipping makes it milder.","5":"Brushed on the outside for browning and crunch; softened butter works.","6":"Sweet tangy drizzle; skip."},"sub":{"5":{"x":"1 tbsp softened butter, spread on the outside","d":"Browns faster and tastes richer; watch the heat.","k":["butter"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Blot the tomato slices on a towel and season them lightly; wet tomato soaks the bread and makes the panini soggy.","s":1},{"t":"Press with a heavy skillet on medium-low heat so the cheese melts before the bread over-browns; high heat burns the crust and leaves the mozzarella cold.","s":2}],"rl":"BPVXXFG"},"74":{"lv":"EEEEERRR","w":{"2":"Gives the dressing its creaminess; Greek yogurt with mustard works.","5":"Adds crunch; skipping makes it softer.","6":"Adds sharp crunch and color; skip for milder flavor.","7":"Adds sweetness and color; skip with little loss."},"sub":{"2":{"x":"1/2 cup plain Greek yogurt + 1 tsp dijon mustard","d":"Tangier and lighter; stir in a little olive oil if it seems thin.","k":["greek yogurt","dijon mustard"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Rinse the pasta under cold water and drain well; warm or wet pasta thins the dressing and makes the salad watery.","s":0},{"t":"Zest the lemon before juicing it, because the zest carries most of the aroma. Chill the salad for 30 minutes before serving; the pasta drinks up the dressing, so loosen it with a splash of lemon juice if needed.","s":1}],"rl":"BPFAXVVV"},"3609":{"lv":"EERRERRRREEROO","w":{"2":"Adds sweet smoky flavor; skipping makes it saltier and spicier.","3":"Adds peppery bite to the meat layer; skip for a milder sub.","5":"Adds cool crunch; skipping makes it less fresh.","6":"Adds juicy sweetness; skip and add more pepperoncini.","7":"Adds sharp bite; skip and it is milder.","8":"Adds tangy heat; skip or use sliced banana peppers.","11":"Classic Italian deli herb; skip with less aroma.","12":"Seasons the dressing; taste, since the meats are salty.","13":"A light bite; skip."},"tips":[{"t":"Brush the dressing onto the cut rolls, not just over the filling; it seasons the bread and creates a barrier so the tomato juice does not make it soggy.","s":1},{"t":"Put the lettuce on last, over the dressed tomato and onion, so it stays crisp.","s":3},{"t":"Wrap and rest the sub for 10 minutes; the bread absorbs the vinegar dressing and the flavors meld into one.","s":4}],"rl":"BPPPFVVXAFAXZZ"},"3615":{"lv":"EEREROEEEER","w":{"2":"Salty nutty depth in the meatballs; skip and add extra salt.","4":"Seasons the meat; skip and use a pinch of garlic powder.","5":"Fresh herb note; skip.","10":"Brushed on the rolls for toasting; skipping gives a drier bread."},"tips":[{"t":"Mix the meat just until combined; overmixed meatballs turn dense and tough for a sandwich.","s":0},{"t":"Bake to 160F (71C) in the center, then simmer in sauce so they finish absorbing flavor and stay moist.","s":1},{"t":"Toast the rolls before filling so they hold up to the sauce, and put the cheese on top of the meatballs so it melts into the sauce.","s":3}],"rl":"PTXTXGZMBFF"},"3618":{"lv":"EEERERROOEO","w":{"1":"Pork adds fat and tenderness; all beef works but is leaner.","3":"Milk with the crumbs keeps the meatballs moist; water works.","5":"Salty nutty depth; skipping makes them less savory.","6":"Seasons the meat; skip and use garlic powder.","7":"Fresh herb note; skip.","8":"Mild herb note; skip.","10":"A light bite; skip."},"sub":{"1":{"x":"1 lb more ground beef (2 lb total)","d":"Leaner and less tender; do not overmix.","k":["ground beef"]},"3":{"x":"1/2 cup water","d":"Hydrates the crumbs just as well; milk adds a little richness.","k":["water"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Use a cookie scoop to portion the meatballs so they are the same size and bake evenly; overmixing makes them dense.","s":3},{"t":"Check that the meatballs reach 160F (71C) before cooling.","s":4},{"t":"Cool completely before bagging; freeze on the sheet first so they do not clump. Reheat in sauce from frozen for 15 to 20 minutes until hot through.","s":5}],"rl":"PPTMTXXGXZZ"},"97":{"lv":"EEEERRERO","w":{"4":"Adds gentle heat; skip for a mild sauce.","5":"Loosens the sauce and adds savory depth; starchy pasta water with a pinch of salt works.","7":"Salty nutty finish; skip and add salt.","8":"Fresh garnish."},"sub":{"5":{"x":"1/2 cup starchy pasta cooking water + a pinch of salt","d":"Less savory but helps the sauce cling.","k":["water","salt"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Pat the shrimp dry and sear them in a hot pan for about a minute per side only; they finish in the sauce, and overcooked shrimp turn rubbery.","s":1},{"t":"Cook the garlic in the butter for 30 to 60 seconds, just until fragrant, before adding the broth; browned garlic is bitter.","s":2},{"t":"Add the lemon just before tossing so the sauce stays bright.","s":3}],"rl":"BPFXXMAXG"},"99":{"lv":"ERERRRERREO","w":{"1":"Only the fat for browning; any neutral oil or butter works.","3":"Grated carrot melts in, adds sweetness and keeps the lean turkey moist.","4":"Adds savory depth; skip and add garlic powder.","5":"Concentrates tomato flavor quickly; skipping gives a lighter sauce.","7":"Adds herb flavor; skip or use oregano.","8":"Softens acidity and adds richness; skip for a sharper sauce.","10":"Topping only."},"sub":{"1":{"x":"1 tbsp butter or vegetable oil","d":"Either works for browning lean turkey.","k":["butter","vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Lean ground turkey dries out easily, so keep the oil and the grated carrot, and cook it to 165F (74C) as ground poultry.","s":0},{"t":"Cook the tomato paste for a full minute until it darkens; this speeds up flavor that usually takes a long simmer.","s":1},{"t":"Add the cream off the heat; it softens the acidity of the tomatoes.","s":2}],"rl":"PFXVXXAXFBG"},"106":{"lv":"EEEEREER","w":{"1":"Cooks the pasta and adds savory depth; water with salt and extra butter works.","2":"Makes the sauce creamy; water with extra butter is thinner.","4":"Adds savory depth; skip and add garlic powder.","7":"Adds bite; skip."},"sub":{"1":{"x":"3 cups water + 1 tsp salt + 1 extra tbsp butter","d":"Less savory; season the sauce at the end.","k":["water","salt","butter"]},"2":{"x":"1 cup water + 1 extra tbsp butter","d":"Thinner and less creamy.","k":["water","butter"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Use a wide pot so the spaghetti is mostly submerged, and stir often; the starch released thickens the liquid into the sauce.","s":0},{"t":"Add a splash of hot water if the liquid disappears before the pasta is tender.","s":1},{"t":"Add the lemon juice and parmesan off the heat; hot acid curdles milk, and zest it first for the strongest aroma.","s":2}],"rl":"BMMFXAFZ"},"108":{"lv":"EEEERR","w":{"4":"Adds sweetness and juicy contrast; skipping makes it plainer.","5":"Adds color and fresh greens; skip."},"tips":[{"t":"Spread the pesto thinly since it is oily; too much makes the flatbread greasy. Slice the cooked chicken thin so it heats through in the short bake.","s":0},{"t":"Bake on a hot sheet so the naan base crisps, and add the spinach after baking so it wilts from the heat but stays green.","s":2}],"rl":"BXPFVV"},"114":{"lv":"ERRERRRR","w":{"1":"Only the fat for the garlic; butter or any oil works.","2":"Adds savory depth to the tomato sauce; skipping makes it plainer.","4":"Softens the acidity and gives a creamy sauce; skip for a sharper tomato sauce.","5":"Salty nutty finish; skip and add salt.","6":"Gives the melty top; skip and use more parmesan.","7":"Fresh herb note named in the dish; skipping loses its freshness."},"sub":{"1":{"x":"1 tbsp butter or vegetable oil","d":"Either works.","k":["butter","vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Cook the garlic only 30 seconds before adding the tomatoes; burned garlic makes the sauce bitter.","s":0},{"t":"Gnocchi are done when they float and plump, in about 4 minutes; longer and they fall apart in the sauce.","s":1},{"t":"Add the cream and cheese on low heat and cover to melt the mozzarella without breaking the sauce.","s":2}],"rl":"BFXVFXFG"},"3212":{"lv":"EEROEERRREEO","w":{"2":"Seasons the chicken and flour; unsalted cutlets taste bland.","3":"A light bite; skip.","4":"Browning fat; any neutral oil works.","6":"Adds savory depth to the pan sauce; skipping makes it milder.","7":"Wine adds acidity; chicken broth with lemon is close.","8":"Builds sauce volume and body; water with a pinch of salt works.","11":"Garnish only."},"sub":{"4":{"x":"2 tbsp vegetable oil","d":"Neutral oil.","k":["vegetable oil"]},"7":{"x":"1/2 cup chicken broth + 1 tsp lemon juice","d":"Less depth; still brightens the sauce.","k":["chicken broth","lemon juice"]},"8":{"x":"1/2 cup water + a pinch of salt","d":"Less savory; taste the sauce before adding more salt.","k":["water","salt"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Dredge the cutlets right before frying and shake off the excess; flour that sits turns gummy.","s":0},{"t":"Whisk the cold butter into the sauce off the heat a piece at a time for a glossy emulsion. If it turns oily, whisk in a splash of broth.","s":4},{"t":"Add the capers at the end so they stay briny and firm.","s":4}],"rl":"PTZZFFXMMAXG"},"3214":{"lv":"EEROEEERREO","w":{"2":"Seasons the shrimp; salt matters here.","3":"A light bite; skip.","4":"Browning fat for the shrimp; any neutral oil works.","7":"Adds gentle heat; skip for a mild sauce.","8":"Adds acidity; chicken broth with extra lemon is close.","10":"Garnish only."},"sub":{"4":{"x":"2 tbsp vegetable oil","d":"Neutral oil.","k":["vegetable oil"]},"8":{"x":"1/2 cup chicken broth + 1 tsp white vinegar","d":"Less depth, similar acidity.","k":["chicken broth","white vinegar"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Shrimp are done when they turn opaque pink and curl into a loose C; a tight O means overcooked.","s":2},{"t":"Add the garlic after lowering the heat and cook only 30 seconds; minced garlic browns quickly and turns bitter.","s":3},{"t":"Return the shrimp to the sauce only for the final toss so they stay tender; the pasta water makes it glossy.","s":5}],"rl":"BPZZFFXXAAG"},"3216":{"lv":"EEERR","w":{"3":"Helps the sauce emulsify and stay smooth; skip for the traditional version.","4":"Season the pasta water sparingly; the pecorino is salty."},"tips":[{"t":"Use less water than usual so the pasta water is cloudy and starchy; that starch is what binds the cheese into a sauce.","s":0},{"t":"Grate the pecorino very fine and take the pan off the heat before adding it; too much heat makes it clump into rubbery lumps. If it clumps, whisk in warm pasta water a splash at a time.","s":4},{"t":"Toast the pepper until fragrant; it blooms the oils and gives much more flavor than raw.","s":1}],"rl":"BXTFZ"},"3225":{"lv":"EEERROR","w":{"3":"Adds gentle heat; skip for a mild dish.","4":"Adds freshness; skip.","5":"Not traditional; skip for a purer garlic and oil flavor.","6":"Season the pasta water well; it is the pasta's main seasoning."},"tips":[{"t":"Start the garlic in cold oil on low heat so it infuses gently; pale gold is the goal, since browned garlic turns bitter.","s":1},{"t":"Toss the pasta with the pasta water vigorously over heat; the starch emulsifies the oil into a sauce that clings.","s":3}],"rl":"BFXXGGZ"},"3226":{"lv":"EEEROEERRR","w":{"3":"A light dusting helps the chicken brown and slightly thickens the pan sauce.","4":"Prosciutto is salty, so only pepper is needed.","5":"Browning fat; any neutral oil works.","7":"Wine adds acidity; broth with lemon is close.","8":"Builds sauce volume; water works.","9":"Brightens the rich butter sauce; skip with a milder sauce."},"sub":{"5":{"x":"2 tbsp vegetable oil","d":"Neutral oil.","k":["vegetable oil"]},"7":{"x":"1/2 cup chicken broth + 1 tsp lemon juice","d":"Less depth, similar acidity.","k":["chicken broth","lemon juice"]},"8":{"x":"1/2 cup water","d":"Less savory; taste before adding salt.","k":["water"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Press the sage and prosciutto firmly onto the chicken and start prosciutto-side down so it seals and crisps before you flip.","s":0},{"t":"Cook to 165F (74C) in the thickest part.","s":2},{"t":"Do not add salt; the prosciutto is salty and the sauce reduces further, so taste it at the end.","s":3}],"rl":"PXXTZFFMMA"},"52835":{"lv":"EEREOEO","w":{"0":"Gives the sauce its richness; heavy cream thickens the same way.","2":"Stabilizes the sauce so it does not split; skipping risks a thinner sauce.","4":"Classic spice; skip.","6":"Garnish only."},"sub":{"0":{"x":"1 cup (240 ml) heavy cream","d":"Lighter than clotted cream; simmer 3 to 4 minutes longer to thicken, keep the cornstarch.","k":["heavy cream"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Keep the heat low with the cornflour; a gentle simmer keeps the cream from splitting.","s":0},{"t":"Do not add salt to the cheese until you have tasted; the parmesan and salted pasta water usually provide enough.","s":1},{"t":"Fold in a little pasta water to loosen; the sauce thickens as it sits.","s":3}],"rl":"FFTXXBG"},"52837":{"lv":"EERRRREEO","w":{"1":"Only the fat for softening the onion; any oil works.","2":"Sweetens the sauce base; skipping makes it sharper.","3":"Adds savory depth; skip and add garlic powder.","4":"Gives the puttanesca heat; skip for a mild sauce.","5":"Deepens the tomato flavor; skipping gives a thinner sauce.","8":"Not traditional with fish; skip."},"sub":{"1":{"x":"1 tbsp vegetable oil","d":"Neutral oil.","k":["vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Cook the tomato paste for a full minute until it darkens before adding the pilchards, to remove its raw tinny taste.","s":1},{"t":"Break up the pilchards gently and use all of their tomato sauce; it is the base of the sauce. Add the olives at the end so they stay briny.","s":1},{"t":"Add the 2 to 3 tablespoons of pasta water and toss over heat so the sauce coats the spaghetti.","s":2}],"rl":"BFXXXXPXG"},"52982":{"lv":"EEREEE","w":{"2":"Both the bacon and pecorino are salty, so salt only the pasta water and taste before adding more."},"tips":[{"t":"Take the pan off the heat before adding the egg and cheese mixture; the pasta's residual heat thickens the sauce, and direct heat scrambles the eggs. Use pasteurized eggs for anyone vulnerable.","s":9},{"t":"Render the bacon slowly until crisp so the fat becomes the base of the sauce.","s":6},{"t":"Loosen with pasta water a spoonful at a time; the sauce should be creamy and moist, not wet. If it clumps, toss with hot pasta water.","s":10}],"rl":"BTZPXZ"},"3221":{"lv":"EERREORO","w":{"2":"Fresh herb note that defines the topping; skipping makes it plainer.","3":"Garlic in the topping and rubbed on the toast; skipping makes it milder.","5":"A sweet tangy drizzle; skip.","6":"Brings out the tomato juices; taste before serving.","7":"A light bite; skip."},"tips":[{"t":"Spoon the salted tomato mixture onto the toast with a slotted spoon so excess juice stays in the bowl and the bread stays crisp.","s":1},{"t":"Rub the garlic on while the toasts are still hot; the rough crust grates the clove into the bread.","s":2},{"t":"Assemble just before serving; the bread softens within minutes.","s":3}],"rl":"BVXXFAZZ"},"3223":{"lv":"EEEEORO","w":{"4":"A sweet tangy drizzle; skip.","5":"Draws out tomato sweetness; without it the tomatoes taste flat.","6":"A light bite; skip."},"tips":[{"t":"Use tomatoes that have never been refrigerated; cold dulls their flavor. Salt them first so juices pool and mingle with the oil as the dressing.","s":0},{"t":"Pat the mozzarella dry before slicing so it does not water down the platter.","s":0},{"t":"Add the basil whole just before serving; cutting or cooking blackens it.","s":1}],"rl":"VPGFAZZ"},"3329":{"lv":"EEEORR","w":{"3":"Garnish only.","4":"Adds salty nutty flavor and a golden top; skipping gives a plain garlic butter.","5":"Seasons the butter; use a bit more if your butter is unsalted."},"tips":[{"t":"Soften the butter at room temperature rather than melting it, and mash the garlic with the salt into a paste so it spreads evenly.","s":1},{"t":"Spread the butter edge to edge; bare crust edges burn first under the broiler.","s":2},{"t":"Stay at the oven for the broil; garlic butter goes from golden to burnt in seconds.","s":4}],"rl":"BFXGXZ"},"3330":{"lv":"EREEEEERR","w":{"1":"Adds salty depth to the rice; skip and add salt.","6":"For frying; any neutral high-heat oil works.","7":"A dipping sauce; skipping leaves the arancini plainer.","8":"Seasons the rice; use a pinch."},"sub":{"6":{"x":"2 cups canola or sunflower oil","d":"Neutral and high smoke point; avoid olive oil for frying.","k":["vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Use cold, day-old risotto rice; fresh warm rice is too loose to shape and falls apart in the oil.","s":0},{"t":"Chill the coated balls for 15 minutes so the breading sets and stays on during frying.","s":3},{"t":"Hold the oil at 350F (175C) and fry only a few at a time. Cooler oil makes them greasy, hotter oil browns the crust before the mozzarella melts.","s":5}],"rl":"BXTFTTFGZ"},"3331":{"lv":"EEERRRREEOR","w":{"3":"Adds crunch and savory depth; skipping makes it softer.","4":"Adds sweet acidity; skipping makes the caponata drier.","5":"Adds briny bite; skipping makes it less Sicilian.","6":"Adds sharp saltiness; skip and add more olives.","9":"Fresh finish; skip.","10":"Seasons the dish; adjust at the end since capers and olives are salty."},"tips":[{"t":"Salting the eggplant and patting it dry draws out moisture and bitterness so it browns instead of steaming and absorbs less oil.","s":0},{"t":"Brown the eggplant in batches so the pan stays hot; crowding steams it into mush.","s":1},{"t":"Caponata tastes best after a few hours or the next day, once the sweet and sour flavors settle. Adjust the vinegar at the end.","s":5}],"rl":"VFXVVXXAWGZ"},"3332":{"lv":"EERORRR","w":{"2":"Slices crisp and sweeten at the edges; they may brown, so tuck them under the stalks.","3":"Adds gentle heat; skip.","4":"Brightens the roasted char; skipping makes it heavier.","5":"Salty nutty finish; skip and add salt.","6":"Seasons the broccolini; use as needed."},"tips":[{"t":"Dry the broccolini thoroughly and put it on a preheated pan so it chars instead of steaming. Split thick stalks lengthwise so they cook as fast as the tips.","s":2},{"t":"Tuck the garlic slices under the stalks so they soften rather than burn.","s":3},{"t":"Add lemon after roasting, since heat dulls its brightness.","s":4}],"rl":"VFXXAGZ"},"3333":{"lv":"ERRREERORO","w":{"1":"Adds sharp crunch; skipping makes the salad softer.","2":"Adds sweetness and juiciness; skip with a plainer salad.","3":"Adds fresh herbaceous brightness; skip with a heavier flavor.","6":"Adds savory bite; skip and use a pinch of garlic powder.","7":"Adds a mild herb note; skip.","8":"Seasons the beans; taste at the end.","9":"A light bite; skip."},"tips":[{"t":"Soaking the sliced onion in cold water mellows its sharpness, so it does not overpower the beans.","s":0},{"t":"Mash a spoonful of beans into the dressing to make it creamy and help it cling.","s":1},{"t":"Let the salad stand for the full 10 minutes; the warm absorbent beans take up the lemon and oil, so retaste and add salt and lemon before serving.","s":3}],"rl":"PXVXFAXXZZ"},"3334":{"lv":"EERRREOO","w":{"2":"Adds savory depth to the filling; skipping makes it milder.","3":"Adds texture and absorbs the juices; skipping makes the filling looser.","4":"Salty nutty top and filling; skip and add extra breadcrumbs.","6":"Garnish only.","7":"Only oils the baking sheet; skip with a nonstick pan."},"tips":[{"t":"Wipe the mushrooms clean instead of rinsing them; wet caps release water and turn the filling soggy.","s":0},{"t":"Cook the sausage and stems until their liquid evaporates; the filling should be dry, since the mushrooms add more moisture as they bake. Make sure the sausage is cooked through.","s":1},{"t":"Bake until juices pool in the caps and the tops are browned.","s":5}],"rl":"VPXTXTGF"},"3335":{"lv":"EEERREOR","w":{"3":"Adds richness to the polenta; skipping makes it less tender.","4":"Adds salty nutty flavor; skip and add salt.","5":"Coats the fries for crisping; melted butter or other oil works.","6":"Light seasoning; skip.","7":"A dipping sauce; skipping leaves the fries plainer."},"sub":{"5":{"x":"2 tbsp melted butter or vegetable oil","d":"Butter browns faster; either crisps the fries.","k":["butter","vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Stir the polenta often and keep the heat low; it spits as it thickens. It is ready when a spoon stands up in it.","s":1},{"t":"Chill the polenta fully, at least 1 hour, or the sticks will break when cut and soften in the oven.","s":3},{"t":"Bake on a hot lined sheet with space between the sticks and flip once; crowded sticks steam and stay soft.","s":5}],"rl":"BMZFXFXG"},"3336":{"lv":"EEERRRERRO","w":{"3":"Adds cool crunch; skipping makes it softer.","4":"Adds sharp bite; skip for a milder salad.","5":"Fresh herb note that completes the Tuscan flavor; skip with less aroma.","7":"Adds savory depth to the dressing; skip with a milder dressing.","8":"Draws out tomato juices that become the dressing; skipping makes it blander.","9":"A light bite; skip."},"tips":[{"t":"Use day-old bread and toast the cubes so they absorb the juices without dissolving into mush.","s":0},{"t":"Salt the tomatoes so the juices pool; they mix with the oil and vinegar to make the dressing.","s":2},{"t":"Let it stand 15 minutes so the bread soaks up the juices, and eat it within an hour before it turns soggy.","s":5}],"rl":"BFVVXXAXZZ"},"3372":{"lv":"EEROERO","w":{"2":"Seasons the fennel and draws out moisture so the edges caramelize; skipped, the fennel tastes flat.","3":"A light background spice; the dish works without it.","5":"Bright acid cuts the fennel sweetness and the rich cheese; skipped, the dish reads heavier and flatter.","6":"Fresh green color and a mild herbal note only; safe to leave out.","1":"Roasting fat; skipped, the fennel dries out."},"sub":{"1":{"x":"3 tbsp melted butter or vegetable oil","d":"Butter is richer and browns faster, so watch the edges in the last 10 minutes.","k":["butter","vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Keep a bit of the core attached to every wedge so the layers hold together, and lay each wedge on a cut face. The flat contact with the hot pan is what gives the deep brown, sweet crust.","s":1},{"t":"Add the parmesan only for the last 5 minutes. Earlier and it scorches and turns bitter before the fennel is done.","s":3}],"rl":"VFZZXAG"},"3373":{"lv":"EREERRR","w":{"1":"Salted blanching water seasons the rabe from inside and tames bitterness; with unsalted water it stays harsher and blander.","4":"Gentle heat to balance the bitter greens; reduce it for a milder dish or skip it.","5":"Acid brightens and rounds off the bitterness; skipped, the greens taste heavier.","6":"Final seasoning after tossing in the oil; taste first, the blanching water already seasons the greens."},"tips":[{"t":"The 2 minute blanch is what mellows the bitterness of the rabe. Skip it and the greens come out aggressively bitter and tough in the stems.","s":1},{"t":"Let the blanched greens steam dry, or squeeze gently, before they hit the oil. Wet rabe spatters, dilutes the garlic oil, and turns watery.","s":1},{"t":"Pull the garlic when it is just pale gold. Browned garlic goes acrid and fights the bitter greens.","s":2}],"rl":"VZFXXAZ"},"3374":{"lv":"EEERERRER","w":{"3":"Adds salty, nutty depth to the ricotta filling and the crust; skipped, the filling tastes mild and sweet.","5":"Fresh herbal lift in the creamy filling; skipped, the filling is blander but still works.","6":"Seasons the filling; ricotta is bland without it. The sauce and cheeses add some salt, so keep it modest.","8":"Gives the gooey, golden top; skipped, the rolls are still good but less melty.","1":"Main or only cooking fat; skipped, the food sticks, browns poorly and the dish loses richness."},"sub":{"1":{"x":"1/4 cup vegetable or canola oil","d":"Less fruity than olive oil, but the eggplant browns just as well.","k":["vegetable oil","canola oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Cut the slices an even 1/4 inch and cook them until fully limp. Under-cooked slices crack when rolled and the rolls spring open in the oven.","s":1},{"t":"Drain the ricotta in a sieve for 15 minutes first. Wet ricotta weeps into the baking dish and turns the sauce watery.","s":2},{"t":"Place a spoonful of filling on the wide end and roll toward the narrow end. Set the rolls seam side down on the sauce so the weight holds them shut.","s":4}],"rl":"VFBXTXZMG"},"3375":{"lv":"EREERERO","w":{"1":"Melts into a soft, salty center; the blossoms are delicious plain, just more delicate and less rich.","4":"Seasons the batter lightly; the coarse salt at the end does most of the work.","6":"Salt on the hot fried blossoms is what makes them taste seasoned; do not skip it.","7":"A squeeze of acid cuts the fried richness; serving extra only.","5":"Frying medium; without it nothing can be deep or shallow fried and crisped."},"sub":{"5":{"x":"2 cups canola or sunflower oil","d":"Any neutral high-heat oil works; avoid olive oil, which smokes at frying temperature.","k":["canola oil","sunflower oil","vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Use ice-cold water and stop whisking while the batter is still lumpy. Overmixing builds gluten and makes the coating tough instead of lacy and crisp.","s":1},{"t":"Pat the blossoms dry, drain the mozzarella well, and twist the petals shut firmly. Wet cheese and loose petals make the blossoms burst and spatter in the oil.","s":0},{"t":"Fry only a few at a time and let the oil come back to 350F between batches. Crowding drops the temperature and gives greasy, soggy blossoms.","s":3}],"rl":"VFTMZFZA"},"3376":{"lv":"EERRREERERRO","w":{"2":"Adds crunch and a savory note to the mix; skip it and replace with more carrot.","3":"Adds color and mild sweetness; any other firm vegetable can fill in.","4":"Gives a sharp bite and a pink color to the jar; skipped, the mix is milder.","7":"Softens the vinegar edge and rounds the brine; skipped, the pickles are noticeably sharper.","9":"Gives a gentle garlic flavor to the brine; skipped, the pickles taste plainer.","10":"Gives the classic heat of giardiniera; skip it for a mild version.","11":"A minor herbal note on top of the garlic and chile; the pickles are fine without it."},"tips":[{"t":"Cut every vegetable to a similar thin size. Pouring the hot brine over raw vegetables gives crisp-tender pickles; boiling them would make them mushy.","s":2},{"t":"This is a refrigerator pickle, not a shelf-stable canned one. Keep the jar cold, keep the vegetables under the brine, and use within 3 weeks.","s":4},{"t":"Use coarse non-iodized salt. Iodized salt can cloud the brine and darken the vegetables.","s":1}],"rl":"VVVVVKMWKXXX"},"3377":{"lv":"EERREE","w":{"2":"Seasons the potatoes through the roast; they taste flat without it, though the cheese adds some salt.","3":"Carries the pepper flavor and helps the cheese cling; you can replace it with olive oil.","1":"Roasting fat; skipped, the potatoes dry out."},"sub":{"1":{"x":"3 tbsp melted butter or vegetable oil","d":"Butter browns the edges more and tastes richer; it burns sooner, so check the potatoes at 25 minutes.","k":["butter","vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Preheat the baking sheet and put the potatoes on it cut side down. The hot metal sears the cut faces and makes a crisp golden crust.","s":1},{"t":"Toast the cracked pepper in the butter for a minute until it smells fragrant. This blooms the oils in the pepper and is the heart of the cacio e pepe flavor.","s":3},{"t":"Use pecorino romano for the authentic sharp flavor, grated very finely so it melts. Toss it with the potatoes while they are still very hot so it clings rather than clumping.","s":4}],"rl":"BFZFXX"},"3378":{"lv":"EEERRRO","w":{"3":"Moistens and ties the flavors together; skipped, the skewers taste drier.","4":"Sweet-tart contrast to the fresh cheese and tomato; balsamic vinegar reduced slightly can replace it.","5":"Seasoning the tomatoes and cheese; it matters, since fresh mozzarella is mild.","6":"A small background spice; leave it out if you like."},"tips":[{"t":"Pat the mozzarella pearls dry before skewering. Surface moisture makes the skewers weep and dilutes the glaze.","s":0},{"t":"Salt only just before serving. Salt pulls water out of the tomatoes and leaves a puddle on the platter.","s":3},{"t":"Use room-temperature tomatoes and cheese. Fridge-cold produces muted flavors, since the aroma of ripe tomato and basil is released only as they warm.","s":-1}],"rl":"VPXFAZZ"},"3379":{"lv":"EEEEOERRO","w":{"4":"A pinch of heat in the oil; the beans are fine without it.","6":"Loosens the tomato into a light sauce; broth would add more flavor.","7":"Seasoning matters with canned beans, which are bland; taste at the end.","8":"Optional background spice."},"sub":{"6":{"x":"1/2 cup vegetable or chicken broth","d":"Adds more savory depth than plain water; reduce added salt a little.","k":["vegetable broth","chicken broth"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Fry the sage and lightly crushed garlic gently in the oil until the oil smells of sage. That scented oil is the whole point of the dish, as the name says the beans taste like a game bird.","s":1},{"t":"Press a few beans against the pan with the back of a spoon. Their starch thickens the sauce so it clings instead of pooling.","s":3},{"t":"Rinse the canned beans well to remove the starchy canning liquid, which clouds the sauce. A final drizzle of raw olive oil on serving brightens everything.","s":4}],"rl":"PFXXXVMZZ"},"3380":{"lv":"ERREEEERO","w":{"1":"Acid stops the cut artichokes from browning; skipped, they discolor but taste the same.","2":"Adds a savory note to the stuffing; skipped, the herb mix is milder.","7":"Seasons the stuffing and braising liquid; taste the juices at the end.","8":"A background spice; the herbs and lemon carry the flavor."},"tips":[{"t":"Cut the artichokes down to the tender heart and inner leaves and trim the stems as well, peeling the stem. Remaining tough outer leaves stay fibrous even after 45 minutes.","s":0},{"t":"Stand them upside down, stems up, in a snug pot and lay a round of parchment on top before the lid. This keeps them submerged in the oil and water and braising evenly.","s":2},{"t":"Test the base, not the leaves, with a knife. It should slide in with almost no resistance. Let them cool in the juices, since they taste best warm or room temperature.","s":3}],"rl":"VAXXXFMZZ"},"3381":{"lv":"EEERROO","w":{"3":"Loosens the pesto and helps it coat the vegetables; the reserved cooking water can do the same job.","4":"Seasons the potatoes inside; unsalted water leaves them bland even with pesto.","5":"Pesto already contains cheese; a small extra sprinkle adds salt and looks nice.","6":"A light spice finish; the pesto brings plenty of flavor."},"tips":[{"t":"Start the potatoes in cold, well-salted water so they cook evenly from the outside in. Starting in boiling water gives mushy outsides and raw centers.","s":0},{"t":"Loosen the pesto with starchy cooking water before adding it. The starch turns it into a creamy sauce that coats the vegetables instead of clumping.","s":3},{"t":"Fold the pesto in while the vegetables are warm but not steaming hot. Too much heat dulls the basil to a muddy brown and cooks off the fresh flavor.","s":3}],"rl":"BVXFZGZ"},"3382":{"lv":"EERROERRR","w":{"2":"Roasts sweet and soft in the oil and gives an aroma; skipped, the tomatoes are still good.","3":"Seasons the tomatoes and brings out their sweetness; needed with mild burrata.","4":"A small herbal note in the oil; skip it and use more basil.","6":"Fresh herbal aroma on top; skipped, the dish is richer and flatter.","7":"Sweet and tangy contrast to the creamy cheese; a squeeze of lemon or a dash of vinegar can replace it.","8":"For scooping up the tomato juices and cheese; serve with a spoon if you leave it out."},"tips":[{"t":"Roast until the tomatoes blister and burst and their juices turn syrupy. These juices spooned over the cheese are the sauce.","s":2},{"t":"Take the burrata out of the fridge 20 minutes ahead and tear it open at the table. Cold burrata is rubbery and its cream center will not flow.","s":3},{"t":"Burrata has a cream-filled center; plain fresh mozzarella works but will not be creamy, so add a spoonful of cream or olive oil.","s":3}],"rl":"VFXZXPGAG"},"3383":{"lv":"EERRROR","w":{"2":"Mellow, sweet garlic flavor in the marinade; skipped, the olives taste less rounded.","3":"Fragrant citrus oils make the olives taste bright; skipped, they taste flatter.","4":"Resinous aroma that complements the olives; thyme or oregano can stand in.","5":"Gentle heat; skip for a mild version.","6":"Adds a sweet anise note that complements olives; skip it if you do not have it."},"tips":[{"t":"Keep the oil warm and barely sizzling, never frying. Browned garlic turns bitter, and hot oil can dull the lemon zest and rosemary.","s":0},{"t":"Store the olives covered in the refrigerator. Garlic submerged in oil at room temperature can grow botulism, so do not leave the jar on the counter.","s":3},{"t":"The oil solidifies in the cold, so let the jar sit at room temperature for 30 minutes before serving. Olives with pits keep their texture better than pitted ones.","s":3}],"rl":"VFXXXXX"},"3384":{"lv":"EEERREEEER","w":{"3":"Gives the rice richness and helps it bind; it is fine with a splash more broth.","4":"Adds savory depth and helps the rice stick together; skipped, the rice is blander and looser.","9":"Seasoning for the rice; the broth, sauce and cheese are already salty, so go lightly.","8":"Frying medium; without it nothing can be deep or shallow fried and crisped."},"sub":{"8":{"x":"2 cups canola or sunflower oil","d":"Any neutral frying oil works; avoid olive oil because of its low smoke point.","k":["canola oil","sunflower oil","vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Cool the rice completely, spread out on a tray, before shaping. Warm rice is sticky and will not hold, and the cold starch is what sets the croquettes.","s":1},{"t":"Rice should be cooled quickly and refrigerated within 1 to 2 hours, not left on the counter all day, because cooked rice can grow bacteria that survive frying.","s":1},{"t":"Chill the breaded supplì for 15 minutes before frying and keep the oil at 350F. Seams burst when the rice is warm or the oil is too cool.","s":4}],"rl":"BMMFXTFTFZ"},"3385":{"lv":"EERERRRO","w":{"2":"Sweet softened onion base; skipped, the frittata is plainer.","4":"Salty, nutty flavor that seasons the eggs; skipped, increase the salt slightly.","5":"Fresh herbal note that complements the zucchini; skipped, it tastes plainer.","6":"Seasons the eggs; eggs and zucchini are bland without it.","7":"Optional background spice.","1":"Main or only cooking fat; skipped, the food sticks, browns poorly and the dish loses richness."},"sub":{"1":{"x":"2 tbsp butter","d":"A little richer and nuttier; watch the heat so it does not brown too quickly.","k":["butter"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Cook the zucchini until its liquid has evaporated before pouring in the eggs. Wet zucchini releases water in the oven and leaves a weepy, spongy frittata.","s":1},{"t":"Stir once, set the edges on the stove, then finish in the oven. Pulling it when the center still has a slight jiggle works since it keeps cooking as it rests.","s":4},{"t":"The skillet handle will be very hot out of the oven; cover it with a towel or oven mitt as a reminder.","s":4}],"rl":"VFXPXXZZ"},"3386":{"lv":"EEEORR","w":{"3":"A trace of heat; skip it for a mild side.","4":"Seasons the spinach, which is bland; skip it and the dish tastes flat.","5":"A squeeze of acid wakes up the greens; skipped, the spinach is heavier and a little dull."},"tips":[{"t":"Use a very large pan and add the spinach in batches. Crowding steams the leaves and makes them soggy instead of letting the water evaporate.","s":2},{"t":"Keep the garlic pale gold. Browned garlic turns bitter and takes over the delicate spinach.","s":1},{"t":"Tilt the pan and press the spinach to the side to drain off the liquid before serving. Excess water dilutes the garlic oil and makes the plate swim.","s":3}],"rl":"VFXXZA"},"3387":{"lv":"ERREEORR","w":{"1":"Sweet juicy contrast to the bitter radicchio; skipped, the salad is sharper and drier.","2":"Adds crunch and a toasty bitterness that goes with the gorgonzola; pecans are a good swap.","6":"Balances the bitter radicchio and the sharp cheese; skipped, the salad is harsh.","7":"Seasons the dressing; gorgonzola is salty, so go light.","5":"A drizzle; skipped, barely noticeable."},"sub":{"5":{"x":"1 tbsp balsamic vinegar plus an extra 1/2 tsp honey","d":"Thinner and sharper than glaze; the extra honey replaces the syrupy sweetness.","k":["balsamic vinegar","honey"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Soak the torn radicchio in cold water for 10 minutes, then dry it very well. This mellows the bitterness and crisps the leaves, but wet leaves will dilute the dressing.","s":1},{"t":"Let the radicchio sit in the dressing for 5 minutes. The acid softens the tough leaves and makes them taste less harsh.","s":1},{"t":"Toast the walnuts in a dry pan or 350F oven for 5 minutes until fragrant. Raw walnuts taste tannic and bitter next to radicchio.","s":2}],"rl":"VVGXFAWZ"},"3388":{"lv":"ERRRRRRERRRO","w":{"1":"Sweet burst and acidity; skipped, the salad loses freshness.","2":"Crisp, cool crunch that offsets the rich cheese and salami; any crisp vegetable can stand in.","3":"Briny, salty pops of flavor; skip them if you do not like olives.","4":"Adds salty, spicy meat flavor; leave it out for a vegetarian version and add a little more salt and seasoning.","5":"Creamy bites that balance the sharp dressing; skipped, it is a lighter, tangier salad.","6":"Sharp bite; rinse it in cold water to mellow it, or leave it out.","9":"Gives the classic herby flavor; use dried oregano and basil if you have no blend.","10":"Seasons the dressing; pepperoni and olives are salty, so it is a modest amount.","11":"Small salty finish; the mozzarella and pepperoni already season it.","8":"Adds brightness; skipped, the dish still works but tastes flatter."},"sub":{"8":{"x":"3 tbsp white wine vinegar, or 2 tbsp lemon juice plus 1 tbsp water","d":"White wine vinegar is milder; lemon juice gives a fresher, brighter taste.","k":["white wine vinegar","lemon juice"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Salt the pasta water generously and cook the pasta 1 minute under the package time. The pasta keeps absorbing dressing as it sits and will turn soft otherwise.","s":0},{"t":"Rinse the pasta in cold water so it stops cooking and does not clump, then dress it while still slightly damp so the oil coats it evenly.","s":0},{"t":"Pasta soaks up dressing as it chills. Toss in a splash more oil and vinegar just before serving to restore the glossy coating.","s":4}],"rl":"BVVXPFXFAXZG"},"3389":{"lv":"EEERRROR","w":{"3":"Briny, salty pops that season the bitter greens; skipped, add a little more salt.","4":"Salty, tangy bite; skip them and add extra olives.","5":"Sweet counterpoint to the bitter escarole and salty capers; skipped, the dish tastes sharper.","6":"A pinch of heat; the dish works without it.","7":"Add last; the olives and capers are already salty."},"tips":[{"t":"Do not shake the washed escarole dry. The water clinging to the leaves steams them in the covered pot, so no extra liquid is needed.","s":1},{"t":"Wash the leaves well, since escarole hearts hide grit.","s":0},{"t":"Cook uncovered until the liquid has evaporated. If the greens are left wet and watery, they dilute the capers and olives and taste bland.","s":3}],"rl":"VFXXXWXZ"},"3390":{"lv":"EEEREORRR","w":{"3":"Sweet roasted garlic note; skipped, the stew is a little flatter.","5":"Tangy, salty accent; the dish works without it.","6":"Sweet-sour finish that lifts the stew; red wine vinegar with a pinch of sugar can replace it.","7":"Seasons the peppers as they stew; taste at the end, since capers add salt.","8":"Fresh fragrance at the end; skipped, the dish is a bit duller."},"tips":[{"t":"Cook the onion slowly until sweet first, then give the peppers 10 minutes before the tomatoes go in. Acidic tomatoes toughen the pepper skins if added too early.","s":1},{"t":"Use red and yellow peppers rather than green. Green peppers turn bitter and sharp over a long cook.","s":1},{"t":"Peperonata tastes better after a few hours or the next day. Serve it at room temperature with a drizzle of olive oil.","s":4}],"rl":"VVFXVXAZG"},"3391":{"lv":"EERRERRO","w":{"2":"Sharp, crunchy contrast to the sweet oranges; soak it first to mellow the bite, or skip it.","3":"Salty, briny pops against the sweet fruit; skipped, the salad is milder.","5":"Extra acid; the oranges are already tart, so it only sharpens the dressing.","6":"Brings out the sweetness of the oranges; the olives are also salty.","7":"A pinch of heat that contrasts with the sweetness; optional."},"tips":[{"t":"Cut the peel and white pith away and slice the oranges into rounds so the dressing can cling. Pith is bitter and spongy.","s":1},{"t":"Shave the fennel paper thin with a mandoline and keep it in ice water if you are not serving right away. It stays crisp and curls attractively.","s":1},{"t":"Dress just before serving. Salt draws juice from the oranges and the salad turns watery within minutes.","s":3}],"rl":"VVXXFAZX"},"3392":{"lv":"EEREERO","w":{"2":"Sweet garlic flavor that infuses the marinade; skipped, the zucchini tastes plainer.","5":"Seasons each layer as it marinates; the zucchini is bland without it.","6":"Light background spice; the mint and vinegar are the star.","1":"Frying medium; without it nothing can be deep or shallow fried and crisped."},"sub":{"1":{"x":"1/2 cup vegetable or canola oil","d":"Less fruity than olive oil, but the zucchini fries just as well; do not use extra virgin at this heat.","k":["vegetable oil","canola oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Pat the slices dry and fry in batches without crowding. Wet or crowded zucchini steams and turns limp instead of golden.","s":1},{"t":"Layer the zucchini while it is still hot. The heat helps it absorb the vinegar, garlic and mint so the dish tastes marinated rather than just dressed.","s":2},{"t":"Make it a few hours ahead or the day before. The flavor deepens as the vinegar softens the garlic and mint.","s":4}],"rl":"VFXAXZZ"},"3393":{"lv":"EEROO","w":{"2":"A light drizzle adds a fruity sheen and ties the sweet and salty flavors together; skipped, it is fine.","3":"Adds a spicy bite to the sweet melon and salty ham; optional.","4":"A fresh herbal garnish; not traditional, so leave it out if needed."},"tips":[{"t":"Choose a melon with a sweet fragrance at the stem end and a slight give. A hard, unripe melon tastes bland and gives no contrast to the salty prosciutto.","s":0},{"t":"Wrap the prosciutto only just before serving and let the ham come to room temperature. Melon juice makes the slices wet and sticky within minutes, and cold ham tastes muted.","s":1},{"t":"Look for prosciutto sliced paper thin and keep the slices separated with parchment. Thick slices are chewy and overly salty.","s":1}],"rl":"VPFZG"},"3394":{"lv":"EEEREEERR","w":{"3":"Thins the egg dip so it coats evenly; water works just as well.","7":"Seasons the egg coating; the cheese is mild, so a small amount helps.","8":"Tart tomato dip that cuts through the fried cheese; skipped, the sandwiches are richer and heavier.","6":"Frying medium; without it nothing can be deep or shallow fried and crisped."},"sub":{"6":{"x":"1/2 cup canola or vegetable oil","d":"Any neutral frying oil works well; avoid olive oil, which smokes and tastes strong.","k":["canola oil","vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Pat the mozzarella very dry and press the bread edges together firmly. Moisture and loose seams make the cheese leak and burn in the oil.","s":0},{"t":"Chill the breaded sandwiches for 15 minutes before frying. The coating sticks better and the cheese stays inside.","s":2},{"t":"Keep the oil near 350F and fry until deep golden. Too cool and the sandwich soaks up oil; too hot and the crust burns before the cheese melts.","s":3}],"rl":"BFTMTTFZG"},"3395":{"lv":"EEERERRREEER","w":{"3":"Adds salty, nutty savoriness to the filling; skipped, the stuffing is blander.","5":"A classic warm spice in Marche stuffing; a small amount, but it defines the flavor.","7":"Adds depth and a little sweetness to the meat; skipped, the filling is plainer.","11":"Seasons the meat; the olives and cheese are salty, so go light.","6":"One of several fats or only sweats aromatics; skipping loses some flavor but dish still cooks.","10":"Frying medium; without it nothing can be deep or shallow fried and crisped."},"sub":{"6":{"x":"1 tbsp vegetable oil or butter","d":"Only used to brown the meat, so any fat works.","k":["vegetable oil","butter"]},"10":{"x":"2 cups canola or sunflower oil","d":"Any neutral frying oil works; avoid extra virgin olive oil.","k":["canola oil","sunflower oil","vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Cook the meat until no pink remains (160F / 71C) and cool it before grinding. The fry only reheats the filling and browns the crust; it does not cook raw meat.","s":0},{"t":"Grind the meat in the processor to a smooth paste. It packs tightly into the olive and stays put when frying.","s":1},{"t":"Chill the breaded olives for 20 minutes before frying and keep the oil at 350F. Cold coating sticks better and the olives will not burst.","s":4}],"rl":"VPPXTXFXTTFZ"},"3396":{"lv":"EERROER","w":{"2":"Seasons the chickpea batter, which is bland; do not skip it.","3":"Fresh herb flavor and green flecks; skipped, the panelle tastes plain.","4":"A background spice; optional.","6":"Tart squeeze that cuts the fried richness; serving extra only.","5":"Frying medium; without it nothing can be deep or shallow fried and crisped."},"sub":{"5":{"x":"2 cups canola or sunflower oil","d":"Any neutral frying oil works well; panelle are traditionally fried in sunflower oil.","k":["canola oil","sunflower oil","vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Pour the chickpea flour into cold water gradually while whisking. Hot water or dumping it in fast makes lumps that never smooth out.","s":0},{"t":"Cook until the paste is so thick that it pulls away from the pan. A looser paste sets soft and falls apart in the oil.","s":1},{"t":"Let the paste cool fully on an oiled tray before cutting. Warm paste is sticky and breaks, and the cold set gives the crisp outside and creamy center.","s":2}],"rl":"BMZXZFA"},"1":{"lv":"ERRREEERRRRO","w":{"1":"Sweet savory base; skipped, the soup tastes thinner.","2":"Adds depth to the beef and sauce; skipped, the soup is flatter.","3":"Gives the classic Italian-American flavor; basil and oregano can replace it.","7":"The creamy cheesy topping is the lasagna signature; skipped, it tastes like pasta soup.","8":"Melts into stringy cheese that makes it taste like lasagna; skipped, it is less rich.","9":"Salty and nutty; it also seasons the ricotta topping.","10":"Marinara and broth already carry salt; taste first.","11":"Optional."},"tips":[{"t":"Brown the beef well and drain the extra fat before adding the sauce. Greasy beef makes the soup oily on top.","s":0},{"t":"Break the lasagna noodles into rough bite-size pieces and stir often. They stick to the bottom of the pot and clump if left alone.","s":2},{"t":"Noodles keep absorbing broth, so leftovers turn thick. Add a splash of broth when reheating and cook the beef to 160F (71C).","s":2}],"rl":"PXXXAMBGGGZZ"},"4":{"lv":"ERRRRRREERRR","w":{"1":"Sweet savory base; skipped, the soup is thinner in flavor.","2":"Adds sweetness and body; skipped, the soup is slightly harsher.","3":"Aromatic backbone; skipped, the broth tastes thinner.","4":"Gives savory depth; skipped, the soup tastes flatter.","5":"Adds richness and color; skipped, the broth is paler and less deep.","6":"Gives the classic herby note; sausage already carries fennel and herbs.","10":"Adds color and a little bitterness; spinach is a good swap.","11":"Salty, nutty finish; skipped, the soup is less rich.","9":"Adds richness; skipped, the soup is lighter."},"sub":{"9":{"x":"1 cup half-and-half","d":"Lighter and less rich. Keep the soup below a simmer after adding to avoid curdling.","k":["half-and-half"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Add the tortellini only for the last 5 to 7 minutes. They continue to swell in the hot broth and turn mushy, so leftovers need extra broth.","s":3},{"t":"Cook the sausage until no pink remains (160F / 71C) and leave a couple of tablespoons of fat to sweat the vegetables in. Skipping the browned fat loses flavor.","s":0},{"t":"Stir in the cream after turning the heat down. Boiling cream can curdle and thin out the soup.","s":3}],"rl":"PXVVXXXMBFVG"},"22":{"lv":"RRRRRREREEEROO","w":{"1":"Adds a rich, buttery flavor to the vegetables; skipped, use all oil.","2":"Sweet savory base; skipped, the soup is thinner.","3":"Aromatic sweetness; skipped, the broth is flatter.","4":"Savory backbone; skipped, the broth is less rounded.","5":"Adds depth; skipped, the soup is milder.","7":"Subtle herbal note in the broth; skipped, the soup is slightly flatter.","11":"Adds salty, nutty depth; skipped, the soup is less rich.","12":"A fresh herb finish; parsley can replace it.","13":"Fresh color and flavor at the end; skip if you use dill.","0":"One of several fats or only sweats aromatics; skipping loses some flavor but dish still cooks."},"sub":{"0":{"x":"1 tbsp additional butter (2 tbsp butter total)","d":"Richer and slightly sweeter; it browns faster than oil.","k":["butter"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Stir the lemon juice in at the end, off the heat. Cooking dulls the fresh lemon flavor; zest also brightens the broth.","s":3},{"t":"Orzo keeps absorbing broth and turns thick and mushy in leftovers. Keep leftover soup with extra broth, or cook the orzo separately.","s":2},{"t":"Add the cooked rotisserie chicken only at the end to warm it through. Longer cooking makes it stringy and dry.","s":3}],"rl":"FFXVVXMXBPAXGG"},"48":{"lv":"EEERRRRREEE","w":{"3":"Adds salty, nutty depth to the meatballs; skipped, they taste blander.","4":"Seasons the meatballs; skipped, they are flatter.","6":"Sweet savory base for the broth; skipped, the soup is thinner.","7":"Adds sweetness and color; skipped, the broth is less rounded.","5":"One of several fats or only sweats aromatics; skipping loses some flavor but dish still cooks."},"sub":{"5":{"x":"1 tbsp butter","d":"Slightly richer and sweeter; used only to cook the vegetables.","k":["butter"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Roll the meatballs tiny, about a teaspoon each, with damp hands. They cook through in the simmering broth and stay tender.","s":0},{"t":"Keep the broth at a gentle simmer and do not stir hard for the first few minutes. A rolling boil breaks the meatballs apart.","s":2},{"t":"Cook the meatballs until they reach 160F (71C). Orzo swells in leftovers, so cook it separately if you plan to store the soup.","s":2}],"rl":"PTTXXFXVMBV"},"57":{"lv":"RRREERERRR","w":{"1":"Sweet savory base; skipped, the soup is thinner.","2":"Adds depth; skipped, the tomato broth is flatter.","5":"Gives the classic herby flavor; basil and oregano can replace it.","8":"Adds color and a little earthiness; skipped, the soup is a bit plainer.","9":"Salty, nutty finish; skipped, the soup is less rich.","0":"One of several fats or only sweats aromatics; skipping loses some flavor but dish still cooks.","7":"Adds richness; skipped, the soup is lighter."},"sub":{"0":{"x":"1 tbsp butter","d":"Slightly richer and sweeter; used only to cook the aromatics.","k":["butter"]},"7":{"x":"1/2 cup half-and-half","d":"Lighter. Tomato acid can curdle dairy, so lower the heat and stir it in slowly; do not boil.","k":["half-and-half"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Take the soup off the boil and stir the cream in slowly. Tomato acid and high heat can curdle it into flecks.","s":2},{"t":"If the canned tomatoes taste sharp, add a pinch of sugar. It rounds the acid and makes the soup taste cooked-down.","s":1},{"t":"Add the tortellini last and serve right away. They keep absorbing broth, so the soup thickens and the pasta gets soft.","s":2}],"rl":"FXXAMXBFVG"},"3222":{"lv":"REEEREEERRERRROR","w":{"4":"Adds savory depth; skipped, the broth is flatter.","8":"Adds sweetness and bulk; any other soft vegetable works.","9":"Adds a fresh, green crunch; skipped, the soup is a little less varied.","11":"Gives the classic herby flavor; basil and oregano can replace it.","12":"Wilts in at the end for color and freshness; kale or chard works.","13":"Taste before adding; canned tomatoes and broth carry salt.","14":"Optional.","15":"Salty, nutty finish; skipped, the soup is less rich.","0":"One of several fats or only sweats aromatics; skipping loses some flavor but dish still cooks."},"sub":{"0":{"x":"2 tbsp vegetable oil or butter","d":"Used to sweat the vegetables; olive oil adds a fruitier taste, but it is not essential.","k":["vegetable oil","butter"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Sweat the onion, carrot and celery slowly for the full 7 minutes without browning. This soffritto is the flavor base of the soup.","s":0},{"t":"Drop a parmesan rind into the pot while it simmers and fish it out at the end. It adds a savory depth no extra salt can.","s":2},{"t":"Pasta keeps swelling in the broth. For leftovers, cook the pasta separately or add more broth when reheating.","s":3}],"rl":"FXVVXVMPVVBXVZZG"},"3245":{"lv":"ERRROEEERRO","w":{"1":"Smoky, salty crunch on top and flavor in the base; skipped, use more sausage.","2":"Sweet savory base for the broth; skipped, the soup is thinner.","3":"Adds depth; skipped, the soup is flatter.","4":"Adds heat; sausage may already be spicy, so it is optional.","9":"Taste first; sausage, bacon and broth all carry salt.","10":"Optional.","8":"Adds richness; skipped, the soup is lighter."},"sub":{"8":{"x":"1 cup half-and-half","d":"Lighter and less rich. Add it off the boil to keep it from curdling.","k":["half-and-half"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Slice the potatoes a consistent 1/4 inch thick and leave them in slices. Russets release starch that thickens the broth without any flour.","s":3},{"t":"Cook the sausage until no pink remains (160F / 71C) and leave about 2 tablespoons of the rendered fat in the pot for the onion. That fat carries the flavor.","s":0},{"t":"Add the cream and kale at the end and only simmer gently. Boiling the cream can curdle it and overcooks the kale to a dull color.","s":4}],"rl":"PXXXXMBVFZZ"},"3248":{"lv":"RERRREEEREROR","w":{"2":"Sweet base for the broth; skipped, the soup is a little plainer.","3":"Aromatic savory note; skipped, the soup is thinner.","4":"Adds depth; skipped, the soup is flatter.","8":"Gives the classic herby flavor; basil and oregano can replace it.","10":"Taste first; canned beans and broth carry salt.","11":"Optional.","12":"Salty, nutty finish; skipped, the soup is less rich.","0":"One of several fats or only sweats aromatics; skipping loses some flavor but dish still cooks."},"sub":{"0":{"x":"2 tbsp vegetable oil or butter","d":"Used to sweat the vegetables; olive oil has a fruitier flavor but is not essential.","k":["vegetable oil","butter"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Mash the second can of beans before adding them. The mashed beans thicken the soup into a creamy base that clings to the pasta, with no flour or cream needed.","s":2},{"t":"Pasta keeps absorbing broth after cooking, so the soup turns into a stew overnight. Add a splash of water or broth when reheating.","s":3},{"t":"Toss in a parmesan rind with the broth and a drizzle of olive oil on each bowl at the end for authentic depth.","s":4}],"rl":"FXVVXVMPXBZZG"},"3249":{"lv":"EEEOORRO","w":{"3":"Fresh color and a mild herbal note; skipped, the soup is a bit plainer.","4":"A tiny pinch is traditional; skip it if you do not have it.","5":"Adds color and a green flavor; skipped, the soup is lighter.","6":"Taste at the end; the parmesan and broth carry salt.","7":"A background spice; optional."},"tips":[{"t":"Keep the broth at a gentle simmer, not a rolling boil, and pour the egg in a thin stream while stirring in one direction with a fork. This makes thin ribbons rather than scrambled clumps.","s":3},{"t":"Taste and salt only after the egg and parmesan go in. Both add salt and a stock-based soup is easy to oversalt.","s":4},{"t":"A good, well-seasoned broth is the whole soup. Bring a store-bought one to a simmer with a parmesan rind or garlic clove if it tastes thin.","s":0}],"rl":"MPXGXVZZ"},"52839":{"lv":"ERERRERROORRO","w":{"1":"Crisp, sweet crunch; skipped, the dish has fewer fresh textures.","3":"Aromatic base for the chili oil; skipped, the dish is less savory.","4":"Provides the heat the dish is named for; deseed for a milder dish or skip it.","6":"Sweet burst of juice that sauces the pasta; skipped, the dish is drier.","7":"Fresh herbal lift; skipped, the pasta is plainer.","8":"Only a side salad for serving; not part of the pasta.","9":"Serving extra to mop up the sauce.","10":"Creamy tang in the lime dressing; plain yogurt is a good swap.","11":"Zest gives the dressing and salad its citrus fragrance; skipped, the dressing is flat.","12":"A pinch of sweetness to balance the lime; optional.","2":"Main or only cooking fat; skipped, the food sticks, browns poorly and the dish loses richness."},"sub":{"2":{"x":"2 tbsp vegetable oil or butter","d":"Used to cook the garlic, chili and shrimp; olive oil has more flavor but is not essential.","k":["vegetable oil","butter"]},"10":{"x":"2 tbsp plain Greek yogurt or sour cream","d":"Slightly tangier and a bit thicker; thin with a splash of water if needed.","k":["greek yogurt","sour cream"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Cook the shrimp for only 2 to 3 minutes, until pink and opaque with a loose C shape. A tight O shape means they are overcooked and rubbery.","s":3},{"t":"Add the sugar snaps to the pasta pot for the last minute so they stay bright and crisp, then drain together to save a pan.","s":1},{"t":"Keep the garlic and chili over gentle heat for 30 seconds, no browning. Burnt garlic turns bitter and spoils the fresh dish.","s":2}],"rl":"BVFXXPVXGGFAW"},"139":{"lv":"EERRROR","w":{"2":"Fresh garlic gives the pungent bread flavor; garlic powder can replace it at 1/2 tsp.","3":"Salty, nutty crust; skipped, the bread is milder.","4":"Pockets of stretchy cheese; skipped, the bread is drier and less rich.","5":"Herby background flavor; skipped, the bread is slightly plainer.","6":"For dipping; the bread works without it."},"tips":[{"t":"Toss the biscuit pieces so every surface is coated with the butter and cheese. Dry spots bake into dense, pale bread.","s":1},{"t":"Bake until the center pieces are deep golden and no longer doughy. Pull one from the middle to check; if the top browns too fast, tent it with foil.","s":2},{"t":"Let the bread cool for 5 minutes in the pan. The melted cheese holds the pieces together as it cools and it will pull apart cleanly.","s":3}],"rl":"BFXXFXG"},"141":{"lv":"EEREERO","w":{"2":"Gives the garlic bread flavor in the butter; skipped, the bread is plainer.","5":"Salty, spicy toppings; vegetarian version works without them.","6":"Light herbal note; the sauce already has seasoning.","1":"Garlic butter that defines the bread; skipped, it is dry bread."},"sub":{"1":{"x":"4 tbsp olive oil plus a pinch of salt","d":"Crisps just as well; less creamy flavor than butter.","k":["olive oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Toast the buttered bread for 3 minutes before adding the sauce and cheese. This seals the crumb and keeps the bread crisp instead of soggy under the toppings.","s":0},{"t":"Use a thin layer of marinara and spread the cheese to the edges. Too much sauce makes the bread soggy; bare edges burn.","s":1},{"t":"Finish under the broiler for 1 minute for blistered cheese, and watch it closely because it will burn quickly.","s":2}],"rl":"BFXXGGX"},"3219":{"lv":"EEEEEOER","w":{"5":"Adds a deeper, roasted note; skipped, add a bit more espresso.","7":"Bitter contrast to the sweet cream; skipped, the top is plain."},"tips":[{"t":"Whisk the yolks and sugar over simmering water to 160F (71C). That cooks the yolks, making them safe, and the custard thick and stable.","s":0},{"t":"Cool the yolk mixture before beating in the mascarpone. Warm custard breaks mascarpone into a grainy, oily mess; if it does split, whisk in a spoonful of cold cream.","s":1},{"t":"Dip the ladyfingers for only 1 to 2 seconds per side. They keep soaking while it chills, and over-soaked fingers collapse into mush.","s":3}],"rl":"SWFFMXSG"},"3220":{"lv":"EEERORO","w":{"3":"Rounds out the sweetness and gives the cannoli-shop aroma; skipped, it is flatter.","4":"A pinch of warmth; skip it if you do not have it.","5":"Adds the classic chocolate flecks; skipped, the dip is plain but good.","6":"Serving extras for dipping; use any crisp cookie or cracker."},"tips":[{"t":"Drain the ricotta in a fine strainer and press it. Wet ricotta makes the dip loose and grainy.","s":0},{"t":"Use powdered sugar, not granulated. Granulated sugar stays gritty in the cold dip, while powdered sugar dissolves into a smooth cream.","s":1},{"t":"Fold the chips in at the end so they stay crisp. Chill 30 minutes for the flavors to meld and the dip to thicken.","s":2}],"rl":"BFWXXGG"},"3224":{"lv":"EEEERRO","w":{"4":"Rounds out the cream with warm aroma; skipped, the panna cotta tastes plain.","5":"Fresh, tart berries cut the richness; any other fruit or caramel can replace them.","6":"Draws juices out of the berries to make a quick sauce; skipped, the berries are drier.","1":"Used to bloom gelatin; skipped, the panna cotta will not set properly."},"sub":{"1":{"x":"3 tbsp cold water","d":"Only used to bloom the gelatin; milk adds a tiny bit of richness.","k":["water"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Sprinkle the gelatin over the cold liquid and let it sit undisturbed for 5 minutes until it looks like applesauce. Stirring early leaves clumps that never dissolve.","s":0},{"t":"Heat the cream only until steaming and the sugar has dissolved. Whisk the gelatin in off the heat and check the spoon for undissolved granules; strain it if there are any.","s":2},{"t":"For unmolding, dip the ramekins in hot water for 5 seconds and run a knife around the edge. A properly set panna cotta has a gentle wobble.","s":5}],"rl":"SMFWXGW"},"52961":{"lv":"EEEERRRO","w":{"4":"Warm spice that goes with the ricotta and lemon; skipped, the cake is milder.","5":"Fresh citrus aroma that cuts the richness; skipped, the cake is heavy and flat.","7":"Dusting for looks and a touch of sweetness; skip it.","6":"Adds flavor; skipped, the budino is less aromatic."},"sub":{"6":{"x":"5 tbsp orange juice or milk plus 1 tsp vanilla extract","d":"No alcohol warmth; orange juice adds a pleasant citrus note.","k":["orange juice","milk","vanilla"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Drain the ricotta in a strainer for 30 minutes before mashing. Excess whey makes the batter loose and the center stays wet.","s":0},{"t":"Whip the whites to stiff peaks in a clean bowl with no trace of yolk, then fold gently in two additions. Stirring hard knocks out the air that lifts the cake.","s":1},{"t":"Expect it to puff and then sink slightly as it cools; that is normal for a ricotta cake. Wait until it is firm at the center, or the middle stays raw.","s":1}],"rl":"BSSWXXXG"},"3288":{"lv":"EEERRR","w":{"3":"Melted, creamy bites that balance the pesto; skipped, the wrap is drier.","4":"Fresh juice and acidity; skipped, it is a bit heavier.","5":"Peppery crunch that cuts the richness; any salad greens work."},"tips":[{"t":"Cook the chicken until the thickest part reads 165F (74C), then rest it for 5 minutes before slicing so the juices stay in.","s":0},{"t":"Warm the tortillas for 10 seconds in a dry pan so they bend without cracking, and spread the pesto to the edges to seal in the filling.","s":1},{"t":"Blot the tomato slices on a paper towel and put the arugula against the tortilla. This keeps the wrap from getting soggy.","s":2}],"rl":"PXBFVV"},"53363":{"lv":"EEERRROR","w":{"3":"Rounds out the creamy flavor; skipped, the porridge tastes plainer.","4":"Warm spice that is part of the Jamaican flavor; skipped, it is milder.","5":"Warm, fragrant spice; skipped, it is less authentic.","6":"A tiny pinch; the other spices carry the flavor.","7":"Adds sweetness and richness; skipped, the porridge is plainer."},"sub":{"7":{"x":"1/3 cup sugar plus 1/4 cup whole milk","d":"Slightly less creamy and caramelized than condensed milk; add the sugar before serving.","k":["sugar","whole milk"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Whisk the cornmeal into cold water first to make a slurry, then pour it into the boiling coconut milk while whisking. Dry cornmeal in hot liquid clumps instantly.","s":1},{"t":"Cook over low heat with a tight lid and stir every few minutes. The porridge scorches on the bottom of the pot easily.","s":2},{"t":"It thickens a lot as it cools, so thin leftovers with a splash of milk when reheating.","s":4}],"rl":"MMBXXXXW"},"52937":{"lv":"ERRRRRERRRRREEERRRRE","w":{"1":"Roasted on the side and squeezed over the finished chicken for a smoky citrus finish.","2":"Sweet, fresh onion flavor in the marinade; skipped, the jerk is less rounded.","3":"Gives warm bite to the marinade; skipped, it is less fiery.","4":"Savory depth in the marinade; skipped, the marinade is flatter.","5":"Sweet base for the jerk paste; skipped, it is less rounded.","7":"Earthy herbal note typical of jerk; skipped, it is less authentic.","8":"Acid that brightens and helps tenderize; skipped, the marinade is heavier.","10":"Loosens the paste so it blends and coats the chicken; any oil works.","15":"Gives a mild onion flavor to the rice; skipped, the rice is plainer.","16":"Gives the rice an herbal note; skipped, it is less authentic.","17":"Savory depth in the rice; skipped, the rice is plainer.","18":"Warm spice in the rice; skipped, the rice is milder.","9":"Adds salty umami; skipped, the marinade is less savory.","11":"Helps the chicken char; skipped, less caramelized."},"sub":{"9":{"x":"2 tbsp Worcestershire sauce, or 3/4 tsp salt plus 1 tbsp water","d":"Salty and savory like soy; Worcestershire adds a slight tang.","k":["worcestershire sauce","salt"],"wn":"Worcestershire contains fish (anchovy)"},"11":{"x":"3 tbsp granulated sugar plus 1 tsp molasses or honey","d":"Slightly less caramelized depth; the sugar helps the chicken char.","k":["sugar","honey"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Marinate at least overnight. Jerk needs time for the salt and chili to get past the surface; slash the thighs so the paste reaches the bone.","s":2},{"t":"Cook the thighs until they reach at least 165F (74C), though dark meat is more tender at 175F. Grill indirectly over medium heat and finish over the coals to char without burning the sugar.","s":3},{"t":"Rinse the rice, keep the lid on and do not stir once it simmers. Peeking or stirring makes the rice gummy; the beans go in only after.","s":4}],"rl":"PGXXXXXXAXFWXBMXXXXB"},"52938":{"lv":"ERREEERORERRRRRRROOR","w":{"1":"Seasons the pastry; skipped, the crust tastes flat.","2":"Gives the pastry its yellow color and mild spice; skipped, the crust is pale and plain.","6":"Warm Jamaican spice for the beef; skipped, the filling is less authentic.","7":"A background spice; the other seasonings carry the flavor.","10":"Hot pepper for heat; use less for a milder filling.","11":"Savory depth in the filling; skipped, it is flatter.","12":"Herbal note typical of the filling; skipped, it is plainer.","13":"Seasons the filling; taste and adjust at the end.","14":"Adds color, sweetness and tang to the filling.","15":"Simmered away to leave a moist, cohesive filling; do not skip or the beef is dry.","16":"Fresh onion bite at the end; skipped, the filling is slightly flatter.","17":"Only for the golden shine on top; patties are fine without.","18":"Thins the egg wash; only needed if you use it.","19":"Extra ice water added a few tablespoons at a time until the dough comes together.","8":"One of several fats or only sweats aromatics; skipping loses some flavor but dish still cooks."},"sub":{"8":{"x":"2 tbsp canola oil or butter","d":"Only used to cook the onions; any fat works.","k":["canola oil","butter"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Keep the butter cold and rub it into the flour only until it looks like pea-size crumbs. Warm, smeared butter makes the pastry greasy rather than flaky.","s":2},{"t":"Cool the filling completely before filling the dough. Hot filling melts the butter in the pastry and makes the crust soggy and prone to leaking.","s":13},{"t":"Cook the beef until no pink remains (160F / 71C) and let the liquid reduce until the filling is moist but not wet. A runny filling breaks through the crust.","s":12}],"rl":"SZXFMPXZFXXXXZXMXGGM"},"52943":{"lv":"ERRRRRRREEERR","w":{"1":"Sweet savory base for the sauce; skipped, the stew is thinner.","2":"Fresh onion flavor; skipped, the dish is a bit flatter.","3":"Savory depth; skipped, the sauce is flatter.","4":"Warm bite typical of Jamaican stews; skipped, it is milder.","5":"Provides fruity heat; skip it for a mild dish.","7":"Earthy herbal note; skipped, the stew is less authentic.","12":"Mixed with the cornstarch to make the thickening slurry.","6":"Adds salty umami; skipped, less savory.","8":"Main or only cooking fat; skipped, the food sticks, browns poorly and the dish loses richness.","11":"Thickens the sauce; skipped, thinner."},"sub":{"6":{"x":"2 tbsp Worcestershire sauce, or 3/4 tsp salt plus 1 tbsp water","d":"Savory and salty like soy sauce; Worcestershire is slightly tangier.","k":["worcestershire sauce","salt"],"wn":"Worcestershire contains fish (anchovy)"},"8":{"x":"2 tbsp canola oil","d":"Only used to brown the meat; any neutral oil works.","k":["canola oil","vegetable oil"]},"11":{"x":"2 tbsp all-purpose flour mixed with the 2 tbsp water","d":"Slightly cloudier sauce; simmer an extra 2 minutes so it does not taste raw.","k":["flour"],"wn":"Contains wheat/gluten"}},"tips":[{"t":"Brown the oxtail well in batches without crowding. The fond left in the pan is the flavor of the whole sauce.","s":1},{"t":"Release the pressure according to your cooker. Oxtail is done when the meat nearly falls from the bone; if it is still tight, give it another 10 minutes under pressure.","s":2},{"t":"Skim the fat from the surface before thickening. Oxtail releases a lot of fat that makes the sauce greasy.","s":3}],"rl":"PXXXXXXXFMVTM"},"53345":{"lv":"EERERRRRRERRRO","w":{"2":"Savory Jamaican blend that rounds the curry; skipped, use extra salt and thyme.","4":"Mild onion flavor; skipped, the curry is a bit plainer.","5":"Adds sweetness and body; skipped, the curry is slightly flatter.","6":"Savory depth in the sauce; skipped, the curry is less rounded.","7":"Fruity heat typical of Jamaican curry; use half or skip for a mild dish.","8":"Earthy herbal note; skipped, the curry is less authentic.","10":"Just enough moisture to start; the chicken releases its own juices.","11":"Absorbs the gravy and thickens it; skipped, serve with rice instead.","12":"Taste at the end; the seasoning blend may already contain salt.","13":"Optional.","9":"Main or only cooking fat; skipped, the food sticks, browns poorly and the dish loses richness."},"sub":{"9":{"x":"3 tbsp vegetable oil or butter","d":"Only used to fry the curry powder and brown the chicken; any fat works.","k":["vegetable oil","butter"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Fry the remaining curry powder in the hot oil for 20 to 30 seconds before adding the onions. This blooms the spices and removes the raw, dusty taste.","s":4},{"t":"Cook the thighs until they reach at least 165F (74C), though 30 to 35 minutes covered gets them tender enough to pull from the bone. Keep the heat medium-low so the gravy does not scorch.","s":5},{"t":"Add the potatoes 15 minutes before the end. They absorb the gravy and the starch thickens it; adding them early makes them disintegrate.","s":6}],"rl":"PXXXXVXXXFMBZZ"},"53354":{"lv":"EERRRRRREERRRO","w":{"2":"Warm bite typical of Jamaican curry; skipped, it is milder.","3":"Savory Jamaican blend; skipped, add extra salt and thyme.","4":"Warm background spice; the curry powder carries most of the flavor.","5":"Sweet savory base; skipped, the curry is a bit plainer.","6":"Savory depth; skipped, the curry is flatter.","7":"Earthy herbal note; skipped, the curry is less authentic.","10":"Left whole it gives fruity aroma with little heat; skip for a mild dish.","11":"Soaks up and thickens the gravy; skipped, serve with rice.","12":"Taste at the end; the seasoning blend may add salt.","13":"Optional.","8":"Main or only cooking fat; skipped, the food sticks, browns poorly and the dish loses richness."},"sub":{"8":{"x":"2 tbsp canola oil or butter","d":"Only used to brown the meat and bloom the curry powder.","k":["canola oil","vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Do not skip the long marinade. Goat is lean and tough, and the salt and spices need hours to season it through.","s":3},{"t":"Brown the goat in batches and fry the remaining curry powder in the pot for 20 seconds. The browned bits and toasted spice build the gravy.","s":5},{"t":"Goat is done when the meat is tender and pulls from the bone, which takes longer than the safe temperature. Add the potatoes and whole scotch bonnet after releasing the pressure so they do not turn to mush.","s":7}],"rl":"PXXXXXXXFMXBZZ"},"52936":{"lv":"EEROROORRRROEERE","w":{"2":"Sweet savory base; skipped, the dish is a bit plainer.","3":"Mild smoky color; skipped, the dish loses a little color.","4":"Gives the dish its yellow color and Jamaican spice; skipped, it is milder.","5":"Looks like a mis-parsed line; not part of traditional saltfish and ackee, so skip it.","6":"Adds heat; skipped, the dish is mild.","7":"Sweet color and body; skipped, the dish is a bit drier.","8":"A second pepper for color and sweetness; skip or use extra of the first.","9":"Adds juiciness and acidity; skipped, the dish is richer and drier.","10":"Taste first; salt cod and spices already carry salt.","11":"Optional.","14":"Seasons the dumplings; skipped, they are bland.","13":"Fat for the dumplings; skipped, they are dry.","15":"Frying medium; without it nothing can be deep or shallow fried and crisped."},"sub":{"13":{"x":"2 tbsp cold butter or shortening, cut in","d":"Less crisp and slightly less rich than suet; still tender.","k":["butter","shortening"]},"15":{"x":"1/2 cup vegetable oil","d":"Only used for shallow-frying the dumplings; any neutral oil works.","k":["vegetable oil","canola oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Soak the salt cod overnight and change the water a couple of times, then boil it with a water change. Skipping it leaves the dish unbearably salty.","s":0},{"t":"Use canned ackee and fold it in last, very gently, off a rolling boil. Ackee turns to mush if stirred, and unripe fresh ackee is poisonous, so never use fresh unless you know it is fully ripe.","s":8},{"t":"Fry the dumplings at a steady medium heat until deep golden. Oil that is too cool leaves greasy dumplings; too hot leaves raw centers.","s":10}],"rl":"PVXXXVXVVVZZSFZF"},"52944":{"lv":"EERRRRRRERRRORREO","w":{"2":"Sweet garlic flavor in the pickle; skipped, the sauce is plainer.","3":"Warm bite in the pickle; skipped, it is milder.","4":"Earthy herbal note; skipped, the pickle is less authentic.","5":"Subtle background flavor; skipped, the pickle is slightly flatter.","6":"Crisp-tender, sweet color in the pickled vegetables; skip one for simplicity.","7":"Second pepper for color and sweetness; use only one if needed.","9":"Sweet crunch in the pickle; skipped, the topping is a bit less varied.","10":"Balances the sharp vinegar; skipped, the sauce is harsher.","11":"Warm Jamaican spice; skipped, it is less authentic.","12":"Adds umami; skipped, the sauce is slightly flatter.","13":"Fruity heat typical of escovitch; use less or skip for a mild dish.","14":"Rubbed on the fish for freshness; skipped, the fish is plainer.","16":"A pinch for seasoning; optional.","1":"Frying medium; without it nothing can be deep or shallow fried and crisped.","15":"Pickling acid of escovitch; skipped, there is no pickle."},"sub":{"1":{"x":"1/2 cup canola or vegetable oil","d":"Only used to fry the fish and the vegetables; any neutral oil works.","k":["canola oil","vegetable oil"]},"15":{"x":"3/4 cup white vinegar or apple cider vinegar","d":"Cleaner and sharper than malt vinegar; add a pinch more sugar if too sharp.","k":["white vinegar","apple cider vinegar"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Pat the fish very dry and fry until crisp and cooked through (145F / 63C). The crisp crust holds up against the hot vinegar pickle.","s":1},{"t":"Spoon the hot pickle over the fish. The fish soaks up the vinegar and the flavor improves over a few hours.","s":4},{"t":"Cook the garlic in the pickle for only a minute. Burnt garlic turns the whole sauce bitter, and the sauce can be made up to 2 days ahead.","s":2}],"rl":"PFXXXXVVVVWXXXAAZ"},"53349":{"lv":"EERREERRRRR","w":{"2":"Savory Jamaican seasoning blend; skipped, add extra salt, garlic and thyme.","3":"Gives the shrimp their orange-red color and a mild earthy note; paprika can stand in.","6":"Sweet savory base for the pan sauce; skipped, the sauce is thinner.","7":"Savory depth in the sauce; skipped, the dish is flatter.","8":"A splash of tang that cuts the heat and richness; skipped, the sauce tastes flatter.","9":"Earthy herbal note; skipped, the dish is less authentic.","10":"Taste first; the seasoning blend and stock may already carry salt.","5":"Liquid for the shrimp; skipped, the dish lacks sauce."},"sub":{"5":{"x":"3/4 cup chicken broth or water plus a pinch of salt","d":"Shrimp stock has a sweeter seafood flavor; broth tastes meatier.","k":["chicken broth","water"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Cook the shrimp only until they are pink and curled into a loose C. A tight O means rubbery. Cooking in the stock for 5 to 7 minutes covered is the upper limit.","s":2},{"t":"If you buy shell-on shrimp, simmer the shells in water for 15 minutes to make the stock. This gives the strongest seafood flavor.","s":1},{"t":"Wear gloves when chopping scotch bonnets and do not touch your eyes. For flavor without full heat, remove the seeds and white pith.","s":0}],"rl":"PXXCXMXXAXZ"},"53352":{"lv":"RRRREEERROER","w":{"1":"Sweet savory base for the rice; skipped, the rice is plainer.","2":"Savory depth; skipped, the rice is a bit flatter.","3":"Fresh onion flavor and color; skipped, the rice is milder.","7":"Seasons the rice and beans through the cook; coconut milk does not supply salt, so skipping makes it taste flat.","8":"Warm Jamaican spice; skipped, the dish is less authentic.","9":"Optional background spice.","11":"Earthy herbal note; skipped, the rice is less authentic.","0":"One of several fats or only sweats aromatics; skipping loses some flavor but dish still cooks."},"sub":{"0":{"x":"1 tbsp vegetable oil or butter","d":"Only used to cook the onions; any fat works.","k":["vegetable oil","butter"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Pour the undrained beans on top and do not stir. Stirring breaks the beans and releases starch that makes the rice gummy.","s":2},{"t":"Do not skip the natural release. A 10 minute rest lets the rice finish absorbing liquid; quick-releasing at once leaves it wet and uneven.","s":4},{"t":"On the stovetop, keep the heat at the lowest setting and do not lift the lid for 18 minutes. Lifting the lid lets steam escape.","s":9}],"rl":"FXXXBMMZXZPX"},"53357":{"lv":"EEEERRORRR","w":{"4":"Seasons the rice; coconut milk does not supply salt, and unsalted rice tastes flat.","5":"Warm Jamaican spice; skipped, the dish is less authentic.","6":"Optional background spice.","7":"Gives a fresh onion aroma to the rice; skipped, it is milder.","8":"Earthy herbal note; skipped, the rice is less authentic.","9":"Left whole, it gives fruity aroma with little heat; skipped, the rice is milder."},"tips":[{"t":"Float the scotch bonnet whole and do not pierce it. This gives the rice its fruity aroma without much heat; if it bursts the rice will be very hot.","s":1},{"t":"Rinse the rice in cold water until it runs mostly clear. Excess surface starch is what makes coconut rice gummy.","s":1},{"t":"Resist stirring or lifting the lid while it cooks and rests. The steam finishes the rice, and fluffing it gently with a fork at the end keeps the grains whole.","s":2}],"rl":"BMPMZXZXXX"},"53362":{"lv":"EERERRRRREORRO","w":{"2":"Savory Jamaican seasoning blend; skipped, add extra salt and thyme.","4":"Sweet savory base for the sauce; skipped, the curry is thinner.","5":"Sweetness and color; skipped, the sauce is flatter.","6":"Sweetness and color; use just one if you have only one.","7":"Fruity heat; use half or skip for a mild curry.","8":"Savory depth in the sauce; skipped, the curry is less rounded.","10":"A touch of sweetness and tang; the sauce is fine without.","11":"Earthy herbal note; skipped, the curry is less authentic.","12":"Taste first; the seasoning blend may carry salt.","13":"Optional.","3":"Main or only cooking fat; skipped, the food sticks, browns poorly and the dish loses richness."},"sub":{"3":{"x":"2 tbsp vegetable oil or butter","d":"Only used to cook the vegetables and bloom the curry powder.","k":["vegetable oil","butter"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Fry the curry powder for a minute in the oil before adding the coconut milk. Raw curry powder tastes dusty and bitter.","s":2},{"t":"Add the shrimp only when the sauce is already simmering and cook just 3 to 4 minutes, flipping once. Overcooked shrimp turn rubbery while the sauce keeps reducing.","s":3},{"t":"Keep the coconut milk at a gentle simmer. A hard boil makes it split and look oily.","s":4}],"rl":"PXXFXVVXXMXXZZ"},"52939":{"lv":"ERRRORRRRR","w":{"1":"Smoky, salty fat that flavors the greens; skipped, use more oil and paprika.","2":"Savory depth; skipped, the greens are flatter.","3":"Sweet savory base; skipped, the dish is plainer.","4":"A pinch of smoky color; the bacon already adds smokiness.","5":"Earthy herbal note; skipped, the dish is less authentic.","6":"Adds sweetness and juice to the greens; skipped, the dish is drier.","7":"Sweetness and color; the steps also call for a hot pepper, which gives heat.","8":"These are plantains, served alongside the greens; skip them for just the greens, but they are the sweet contrast in the dish.","9":"Fries the plantains; skipped, they are not fried."},"sub":{"9":{"x":"1 tbsp butter or any neutral oil","d":"Only used to fry the plantains.","k":["butter","vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Cook the bacon until crisp first and sauté the onion and garlic in the fat. That rendered fat flavors the whole pot of greens.","s":3},{"t":"Steam the greens covered with only a splash of water for 6 to 8 minutes. More water makes them soggy and dilutes the flavor.","s":5},{"t":"For the plantains choose yellow with black spots. They are sweeter and caramelize; green ones stay starchy and dry.","s":6}],"rl":"VFXXXXVVVF"},"53350":{"lv":"RERRRERRORO","w":{"2":"Sweet, mild crunch; skipped, the dish is a bit plainer.","3":"Sweet savory base; skipped, the dish is flatter.","4":"Savory depth; skipped, the dish is less rounded.","6":"Adds juiciness and mild acidity; skipped, the dish is drier.","7":"Earthy herbal note; skipped, the dish is less authentic.","8":"A touch of sweetness and tang; skipped, the dish is slightly less rounded.","9":"Corned beef is very salty; taste before adding any.","10":"Optional.","0":"One of several fats or only sweats aromatics; skipping loses some flavor but dish still cooks."},"sub":{"0":{"x":"1 tbsp vegetable oil or butter","d":"Only used to cook the vegetables; any fat works.","k":["vegetable oil","butter"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Break the canned corned beef up and let the fat render with the vegetables. The salty fat flavors the cabbage.","s":3},{"t":"Cook the cabbage only until it softens but still has a little bite, about 3 to 5 minutes. Overcooking makes it watery and flat.","s":2},{"t":"Taste before salting; corned beef is already well salted and extra salt makes it hard to eat.","s":3}],"rl":"FVVXXPVXXZZ"},"53353":{"lv":"ERERE","w":{"1":"Seasons the dumplings as they cook; skipped, they taste bland.","3":"Seasons the dough; skipped, the dumplings are bland, since they are served with salty stews."},"tips":[{"t":"Add the water gradually and knead until the dough is smooth and firm. Sticky dough makes gummy dumplings, and a dry one cracks.","s":1},{"t":"Flatten the dumplings slightly so the center cooks through. Thick balls stay raw and doughy inside.","s":2},{"t":"Boil gently and stir occasionally so they do not stick to the bottom. They float when nearly done; cut one in half to make sure the middle is cooked.","s":4}],"rl":"MZBZM"},"53355":{"lv":"EEEERREE","w":{"4":"Gives the festival its sweet aroma; skipped, they taste plainer.","5":"Balances the sugar and seasons the dough; skipped, they taste flat.","7":"Frying medium; without it nothing can be deep or shallow fried and crisped."},"sub":{"7":{"x":"3 cups canola or vegetable oil, enough for 3 inches","d":"Any neutral frying oil works; avoid olive oil.","k":["canola oil","vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Form the dough into long ovals rather than balls. The shape cooks evenly and gives the classic festival look.","s":3},{"t":"Keep the oil at 350F. The sugar in the dough browns quickly, so oil that is too hot will brown the outside before the center cooks.","s":4},{"t":"Do not overwork the dough once it comes together. Kneading too long makes the festival tough instead of crumbly and tender.","s":2}],"rl":"SBWLXZMF"},"53356":{"lv":"EEREE","w":{"2":"Seasons the dough; skipped, they taste flat.","3":"Main liquid of the dough or batter; skipped, the mix is too dry to come together.","4":"Frying medium; without it nothing can be deep or shallow fried and crisped."},"sub":{"3":{"x":"3/4 cup water","d":"Slightly less rich and tender, but the dumplings still puff up.","k":["water"]},"4":{"x":"1 1/2 cups canola or vegetable oil","d":"Any neutral frying oil works; avoid olive oil.","k":["canola oil","vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Flatten each ball a little before frying. Thick balls brown outside before the center cooks and stay doughy.","s":1},{"t":"Fry at about 350F and flip once. If the oil is too cool, they soak it up and turn greasy; too hot, they burn before cooking through.","s":2},{"t":"Drain them on a rack rather than flat on paper towels so the bottoms do not steam and soften.","s":4}],"rl":"SLZMF"},"53361":{"lv":"ERERRRRRRO","w":{"1":"Smoky, salty fat that flavors the dish; skipped, use more oil and expect a plainer taste.","3":"Sweet savory base; skipped, the dish is plainer.","4":"Fresh onion flavor; skipped, the dish is milder.","5":"Savory depth; skipped, the dish is flatter.","6":"Gives fruity heat; skip it for a mild dish.","7":"Adds juiciness and mild acidity; skipped, the dish is drier.","8":"Earthy herbal note; skipped, the dish is less authentic.","9":"Optional."},"tips":[{"t":"Soak the salt cod overnight, changing the water, then bring it to a boil and drain. Skipping this makes the dish far too salty.","s":0},{"t":"Drain canned callaloo well and heat it only briefly. It is already cooked, so more time makes it mushy and dull.","s":3},{"t":"Do not add any salt. The salt cod and bacon provide plenty.","s":3}],"rl":"PFVXXXXVXZ"},"53364":{"lv":"ERRERRRRRERRO","w":{"1":"Adds sweetness and color; skipped, the dish is plainer.","2":"Adds juiciness; skipped, the dish is drier.","4":"Savory depth; skipped, the dish is flatter.","5":"Fresh onion flavor; skipped, the dish is milder.","6":"Earthy herbal note; skipped, the dish is less authentic.","7":"Fruity heat; use half or skip for a mild dish.","8":"Small amount to start the steam; the cabbage releases its own juices.","10":"Rich flavor that coats the cabbage; use more oil if you skip it.","11":"Seasons the cabbage, which is bland and sweet; do not skip it.","12":"Optional.","9":"Main or only cooking fat; skipped, the food sticks, browns poorly and the dish loses richness."},"sub":{"9":{"x":"2 tbsp butter","d":"Richer and sweeter; any oil also works.","k":["butter","vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Shred the cabbage thin and keep the lid on. The cabbage steams in its own juices with little added water, so the flavor stays concentrated.","s":0},{"t":"Cook it just until tender but not collapsed, about 15 minutes total. Overcooked cabbage turns watery and sulfurous.","s":2},{"t":"Cook the scotch bonnet whole to get its fruity aroma with little heat, and remove it with the thyme stalks before serving.","s":2}],"rl":"VVVXXXXXMFFZZ"},"52940":{"lv":"ERERRRRRRRRER","w":{"1":"Sweet and juicy in the sauce; skipped, the stew is thinner.","3":"Savory depth; skipped, the sauce is flatter.","4":"Sweetness and body; skipped, the stew is plainer.","5":"Adds sweetness and color; skipped, the stew is plainer.","6":"Rubbed on the chicken to freshen it; skipped, the chicken tastes plainer.","7":"Earthy herbal note; skipped, the stew is less authentic.","8":"Warm Jamaican spice (pimento); skipped, the stew is less authentic.","9":"Adds salty umami; skipped, less savory.","10":"Thickens the stew; skipped, thinner.","12":"Browns the chicken; skipped, less browned."},"sub":{"9":{"x":"2 tbsp Worcestershire sauce, or 3/4 tsp salt plus 1 tbsp water","d":"Salty and savory like soy; Worcestershire is slightly tangier and lighter in color.","k":["worcestershire sauce","salt"],"wn":"Worcestershire contains fish (anchovy)"},"10":{"x":"1 tbsp all-purpose flour","d":"The steps call for flour anyway; cook it a couple of minutes so it does not taste raw.","k":["flour"],"wn":"Contains wheat/gluten"},"12":{"x":"1 tbsp canola oil","d":"Only used to brown the chicken; any neutral oil works.","k":["canola oil","vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Shake the marinade off and brown the chicken in very hot oil in batches. Wet chicken steams instead of browning, and the brown crust is the base of the sauce.","s":2},{"t":"The reserved marinade has touched raw chicken, so it must come to a full simmer before serving. Cook the chicken to at least 165F (74C).","s":4},{"t":"Stir the flour or cornstarch with the coconut milk until smooth before adding it, and keep the heat low. A hard boil makes the coconut milk split.","s":5}],"rl":"PVXXVVAXXXTMF"},"52941":{"lv":"ERRRRORREEERRRE","w":{"1":"Sweetness and body in the broth; skipped, the soup is thinner.","2":"Fresh onion flavor; skipped, the soup is milder.","3":"Earthy herbal note; skipped, the soup is less authentic.","4":"Sweet savory base; skipped, the soup is plainer.","5":"Optional; the salted meat already seasons the pot.","6":"Adds sweetness and body; skipped, the soup is plainer.","7":"Savory depth; skipped, the soup is flatter.","11":"Makes the soup hearty; skipped, it is thinner but still good.","12":"For the spinner dumplings; skipped, the soup is still complete.","13":"Makes the dough for the spinners."},"tips":[{"t":"Dry kidney beans must be boiled hard for at least 10 minutes, and cooked until fully tender. They contain a toxin that survives low heat; a slow cooker alone is not safe.","s":2},{"t":"Soak or par-boil the salted pigtail and beef to pull out the salt. Taste the soup before adding any salt, since the cured meat seasons it.","s":1},{"t":"Roll the spinner dumplings thin and add them in the last 15 minutes. They swell and thicken the soup as they cook.","s":4}],"rl":"BVXXXZVXXPMBBMF"},"53344":{"lv":"EEEERRRRORRROOO","w":{"4":"Gives rounded aroma to the pudding; skipped, it is less fragrant.","5":"Warm Jamaican spice; skipped, the pudding is less authentic.","6":"Warm spice; skipped, the pudding is milder.","7":"Warm spice; skipped, the pudding is milder.","8":"A small background spice; optional.","9":"Heightens the sweetness and spices; skipped, the pudding tastes flat.","10":"Chewy sweet pockets; skip them or use another dried fruit.","11":"Topping that gives a creamy finish; skipped, the pudding is drier.","12":"A little sweetness for the topping; optional.","13":"A small spice dusting; optional.","14":"A small spice dusting; optional."},"tips":[{"t":"Grate or process the sweet potatoes finely so the pudding sets smooth and dense. Chunks make it uneven and watery.","s":1},{"t":"Toss the raisins in a little of the flour before folding them in so they do not sink to the bottom.","s":1},{"t":"Pull it when it is mostly set with a slightly soft center. It firms as it cools; slice only after cooling completely or it will fall apart.","s":3}],"rl":"VMWSXXXXXZVGGGG"},"53346":{"lv":"EERE","w":{"2":"Rounds out the flavor of the cream; skipped, the ice cream tastes flatter.","1":"Sweetens and sets the base; 1 1/4 oz looks like a typo for a standard 14 oz can (about 1 1/4 cups), which 2 cups of cream needs."},"tips":[{"t":"Whip the cream cold in a chilled bowl to stiff peaks, then fold the condensed milk in gently. Overmixing knocks out the air that keeps it scoopable without an ice cream machine.","s":0},{"t":"The 1.25 oz condensed milk looks like an error for a standard can, about 1 1/4 cups. A few spoonfuls will not sweeten 2 cups of cream or keep it soft.","s":0},{"t":"Grape-Nuts soften as they sit in the ice cream. Stir some in just before freezing and sprinkle a few on top before serving for crunch.","s":1}],"rl":"LWXG"},"53347":{"lv":"EERERRRER","w":{"0":"Plumps the raisins and its alcohol keeps the frozen base scoopable; the flavor is the dish's signature.","2":"Rounds out the rum and brown sugar; skipping leaves the flavor a bit flat but fine.","4":"Adds a molasses note to the condensed milk; skipping makes it plainer and sweeter-tasting.","5":"Warm spice that pairs with rum; skip and the ice cream is still good, just less spiced.","6":"Gives the Jamaican allspice character; a tiny amount, skip if you do not have it.","8":"A pinch sharpens the sweetness; skipping makes it taste cloying."},"sub":{"0":{"x":"1/2 cup brandy or bourbon (use 1/4 cup in the soak, 1/4 cup in the base)","d":"Less molasses sweetness than dark rum but still gives the alcohol that keeps it soft. Alcohol-free versions freeze harder.","k":["brandy","bourbon"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Warm the rum only until steaming, never boiling; the heat opens the raisins so they plump quickly. The alcohol you keep in the base is also what stops this ice cream from freezing rock-hard.","s":0},{"t":"Chill the bowl and beaters and whip the cream only to stiff peaks; under-whipped cream gives a dense icy block, over-whipped turns grainy and buttery.","s":2},{"t":"Dip the scoop in hot water between scoops for clean rounds, and press plastic wrap onto the surface before freezing to prevent ice crystals.","s":5}],"rl":"XVXWWXXLZ"},"53348":{"lv":"EERREEEERERORRER","w":{"2":"Main warming spice of the bun; omit and it tastes closer to plain fruit loaf.","3":"Balances the sweetness and brings out the spices; the loaf tastes flat without it.","6":"Soaking liquid and batter liquid; malt gives the bun its dark, slightly bitter edge.","7":"Softens the crumb; plain water works with a bit more butter.","8":"Moisture and sweetness, keeps the loaf from drying; liquid sweetener of any kind works.","10":"Deepens the color and gives bittersweet flavor; skipping lightens the loaf.","11":"Adds color and a slight burnt-sugar note; extra molasses covers it, it can be skipped.","12":"Rounds out the spices; subtle if omitted.","13":"Tart contrast to the sweet bun; replace with extra raisins.","15":"Sweet-tart fruit; replace with more raisins or chopped dates."},"sub":{"6":{"x":"1 cup strong brewed black tea or apple juice","d":"Loses the malty bitterness; tea gives a closer tannic note, apple juice makes it sweeter.","k":["black tea","apple juice"]},"7":{"x":"1/3 cup oat or soy milk, or 1/3 cup water plus 1 tsp extra melted butter","d":"Slightly less rich crumb, otherwise no change.","k":["oat milk","soy milk","water"],"wn":"Soy milk contains soy; oat milk may contain gluten"},"8":{"x":"1/4 cup maple syrup or light corn syrup","d":"Maple is a little more caramel-like; the loaf stays moist.","k":["maple syrup","corn syrup"]},"13":{"x":"1/2 cup additional raisins","d":"Less tart, more uniformly sweet.","k":["raisins"]},"15":{"x":"1/2 cup additional raisins or chopped dates","d":"Less tart; dates make it stickier and sweeter.","k":["raisins","dates"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Use a dark stout or malt beer for the soak: its roasted bitterness is balanced by the honey and molasses, whereas a light lager gives a thin, flat flavor.","s":0},{"t":"Toss the drained fruit in the 2 tbsp of reserved flour and shake off the excess; the flour coat stops the fruit sinking to the bottom of the loaf.","s":4},{"t":"If the top browns before the center sets (around 45 minutes), tent with foil and keep baking until a skewer comes out clean. The bun is better the next day once the spices settle, with sharp cheddar as the classic pairing.","s":6}],"rl":"SLXZSWMMWFWCXVVV"},"53351":{"lv":"ERRORREE","w":{"1":"Adds warm aroma that lifts the banana; skipping is fine.","2":"Classic spice for banana fritters; leaving it out makes them taste plain.","3":"A tiny amount of warmth; skip if you lack it.","4":"Helps browning and adds caramel flavor; ripe bananas are sweet so less sugar is workable.","5":"Sharpens the sweetness; skipping makes them taste flat.","7":"Frying medium; without it nothing can be deep or shallow fried and crisped."},"sub":{"7":{"x":"Same amount of canola, sunflower or peanut oil, about 1 inch deep in the skillet","d":"No flavor difference; avoid olive oil which smokes and tastes strong.","k":["canola oil","sunflower oil","peanut oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Use very ripe, heavily freckled bananas; they are sweeter and mash to a smoother batter than yellow ones.","s":0},{"t":"Stir the batter only until the flour disappears; overmixing makes the fritters tough. This batter has no leavener, so whisk in 1/2 tsp baking powder with the flour if you want them puffier.","s":1},{"t":"Fry at medium heat, about 350F, and test with one spoonful first; oil that is too hot browns the banana sugars before the center cooks through.","s":2}],"rl":"VXXXWZSF"},"53360":{"lv":"EE","tips":[{"t":"Block tamarind pulp still holds seeds and woody strings, so pull them out by hand first; if the block is stiff, microwave it 20 to 30 seconds so it mashes smoothly with the sugar.","s":0},{"t":"Lightly oil or dampen your hands before rolling; the tamarind-sugar paste is very sticky. Keep the balls to about a teaspoon each, since they are intensely sweet-sour candies.","s":0},{"t":"Roll in the sugar right away so the coating sticks, then store in an airtight container with parchment between layers; humidity makes the sugar coat weep.","s":1}],"rl":"VW"},"3117":{"lv":"EEEEROEEERRRR","w":{"4":"Seasons the meat directly; without it the cutlet tastes bland even with the sauce.","5":"Mild background heat; safe to skip.","6":"Frying oil; any neutral oil works.","9":"Adds savory depth and salt to the sauce; skipping makes it more tomato-sweet.","10":"Balances the Worcestershire acidity; skip if your ketchup is sweet.","11":"Crisp, cooling contrast to the fried cutlet; the traditional side.","12":"Classic base for the sauce and cutlet; without it you have a snack, not a meal."},"sub":{"6":{"x":"1/2 cup canola, peanut or sunflower oil","d":"No flavor change; keep the depth at about 1/2 inch.","k":["canola oil","peanut oil","sunflower oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Pound the chicken to an even 1/2 inch so the thickest part reaches 165F (74C) before the panko burns.","s":0},{"t":"Use one hand for the dry steps (flour, panko) and the other for the egg, or your fingers grow a heavy clump of breading.","s":1},{"t":"Rest the fried cutlets on a wire rack, not paper towels; steam trapped underneath softens the bottom crust.","s":3}],"rl":"PTTTZZFXXXWVB"},"52772":{"lv":"EERRREERE","w":{"2":"Sweet side of the teriyaki; the sauce needs sugar in some form.","3":"Warm spice note for the glaze; skip and the sauce is still good.","4":"Background aroma; the sauce still works without it, though a bit flatter.","7":"Adds color, crunch and fiber to the casserole; any frozen or fresh vegetables work, or skip for a chicken-and-rice bake."},"sub":{"2":{"x":"1/4 cup granulated sugar plus 1 tsp molasses, or 3 tbsp honey","d":"Honey is a bit floral and thickens faster; the molasses keeps the deeper color.","k":["sugar","molasses","honey"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Dissolve the cornstarch in cold water first, then pour it into the boiling sauce while stirring; cornstarch dropped into hot liquid forms lumps. Boil about a minute so it loses the starchy taste and reaches full thickness.","s":2},{"t":"Chicken breast thicknesses vary, so check the thickest part for 165F (74C) with a thermometer rather than relying on 35 minutes.","s":3},{"t":"Use fully cooked brown rice; it will not soften much in the oven, and the dish thickens as it sits, so keep some sauce back to drizzle.","s":5}],"rl":"XMWXXTPVB"},"52820":{"lv":"EEEEERERREEERROR","w":{"4":"Frying oil; any neutral oil works.","5":"Fat for cooking the aromatics.","7":"Savory base for the curry sauce; adds depth. Skip and the sauce is plainer.","8":"Natural sweetness that gives the Japanese-style curry its mellow body.","12":"A touch of sweetness balances the curry spices.","13":"Adds salt and umami to the sauce; replace with a pinch of salt if missing.","14":"Mild herbal note, barely noticeable; skip it.","15":"Fragrant spice finish stirred in at the end; skipping loses some warmth."},"sub":{"4":{"x":"230 ml canola or sunflower oil","d":"Same result; use a neutral oil.","k":["canola oil","sunflower oil"]},"5":{"x":"2 tbsp butter","d":"Adds a bit of richness; fine for softening onions.","k":["butter"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Cook the flour and curry powder in the fat for a full minute, stirring, before adding stock; this cooks out the raw flour taste, blooms the spices and prevents lumps when you add the liquid gradually.","s":2},{"t":"Sieve the sauce for the smooth restaurant texture, pressing the onion and carrot through to extract their sweetness. Keep it slightly thinner than you want, since it thickens as it stands.","s":3},{"t":"At 1 cm thick the chicken needs only 3 to 4 minutes a side; make sure it reaches 165F (74C) inside, and fry in oil that is hot enough to avoid greasy crumbs.","s":5}],"rl":"PTTTFFXXVTXMWXXX"},"52831":{"lv":"EEREOREER","w":{"2":"Classic partner to ginger in the marinade; milder if skipped.","4":"Tenderizes and adds sweet depth; can be replaced.","5":"Rounds the salt of the soy and helps browning.","6":"Gives the crackly shatter; cornstarch is the common stand-in.","7":"Frying oil; keep about 1 inch depth in a heavy pot.","8":"A squeeze at the table cuts the fried richness; skipping is fine."},"sub":{"4":{"x":"1 tbsp dry sherry or dry white wine","d":"Close in acidity and depth; a bit fruitier than sake.","k":["dry sherry","white wine"]},"6":{"x":"1/3 cup cornstarch","d":"Slightly harder, less delicate crust than potato starch.","k":["cornstarch"]},"7":{"x":"Canola or sunflower oil, 1 inch deep in a heavy pot","d":"No flavor difference.","k":["canola oil","sunflower oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Do not marinate beyond 2 to 4 hours; the soy and salt start to cure the surface and the meat turns dry and salty. Shake off excess marinade before dredging.","s":0},{"t":"Dredge in potato starch one handful at a time, right before frying; starch that sits on wet chicken turns gluey and blows off in the oil.","s":2},{"t":"For extra-crisp skin, fry once, rest the pieces on a rack for a few minutes and fry again briefly at a higher temperature. Check pieces reach 165F (74C) inside.","s":3}],"rl":"PXXXXWTFG"},"89":{"lv":"EEEERRRRERO","w":{"2":"Needed to crisp the tofu; any neutral oil works.","4":"Sweet and sticky side of the teriyaki; any liquid sweetener works.","5":"Brightens the sauce; any mild acid works.","6":"Aromatic base for the sauce; skip and it is sweeter and plainer.","7":"Gives the teriyaki warmth; fine to skip.","9":"Green vegetable that makes it a balanced bowl; swap for any steamed veg.","10":"Garnish for crunch and look only."},"sub":{"2":{"x":"2 tbsp canola or sunflower oil","d":"No difference.","k":["canola oil","sunflower oil"]},"4":{"x":"3 tbsp maple syrup (or 2 tbsp brown sugar plus 1 tbsp water)","d":"Slightly smoky-caramel instead of floral.","k":["maple syrup","brown sugar"]},"5":{"x":"1 tbsp apple cider vinegar or lemon juice","d":"A little fruitier; same brightness.","k":["apple cider vinegar","lemon juice"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Press the tofu under a weight for 15 to 20 minutes and pat dry; surface moisture is the enemy of crispness. Let each side crust undisturbed for 3 to 4 minutes before turning.","s":1},{"t":"Toss the tofu in the sauce at the last second and serve; if it sits, the cornstarch crust goes soft. Simmer the sauce until it coats a spoon so it clings.","s":2}],"rl":"PTFXWAXXBVG"},"52773":{"lv":"ERERR","w":{"1":"Fat for pan-frying; any neutral oil or butter works.","3":"Sweet-savory depth for the glaze; replaceable.","4":"Mostly garnish and a little crunch; skip if you want.","2":"Salty base of the glaze; a honey teriyaki also needs about 1 to 2 tbsp honey (not listed) to balance it, or it is only salty."},"sub":{"1":{"x":"1 tbsp vegetable oil or butter","d":"Butter browns faster, so keep heat low.","k":["vegetable oil","butter"]},"3":{"x":"2 tbsp dry sherry or white wine plus 1/2 tsp sugar","d":"Close balance of acid and sweetness.","k":["dry sherry","white wine","sugar"]}},"tips":[{"t":"The ingredient list shows no sweetener for the glaze; a honey teriyaki needs about 1 to 2 tbsp honey (plus a splash of mirin or sugar) whisked in with the soy and sake, or it tastes only salty.","s":0},{"t":"Honey and soy burn quickly, so keep the heat medium-low and turn the salmon as soon as the glaze starts to darken. Cook until the thickest part reaches 145F (63C) and flakes easily.","s":1}],"rl":"PFXMG"},"52871":{"lv":"EERERRRERR","w":{"1":"Cooking fat and nutty flavor; plain oil loses the aroma.","2":"Sweetness and body for the sauce; skip and the noodles are more savory.","4":"Meaty depth; swap for other mushrooms.","5":"Fresh bite and color at the end.","6":"Gives the glossy sweet-salty sauce.","8":"Balances the soy and Worcestershire; skipping makes the sauce harsher.","9":"Tangy, fruity depth typical of yaki udon sauce; skip if you have extra soy."},"sub":{"1":{"x":"2 tbsp vegetable oil","d":"Loses the toasty sesame aroma; add a few drops of sesame at the end if possible.","k":["vegetable oil"]},"6":{"x":"3 tbsp dry white wine plus 2 tsp sugar","d":"Less glossy and complex but gives the same balance.","k":["white wine","sugar"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Fresh or frozen udon is the better choice: loosen the block under hot water, then drain very well, since wet noodles steam rather than stir-fry.","s":1},{"t":"Toss the noodles in a very hot pan and add the sauce in the last minute so it reduces to a glaze that coats instead of pooling; the sugar caramelizes slightly on contact.","s":3}],"rl":"BFVVVGWXWX"},"53065":{"lv":"EEERRRR","w":{"3":"Creamy spread that gives the roll richness; skip for a cleaner taste.","4":"Extra sushi seasoning to adjust the rice flavor; do not add sake or mirin here.","5":"For dipping; the rolls are fine without but taste flatter.","6":"Crisp, fresh filling for rolls; swap with any other vegetable filling.","1":"This is the sushi rice seasoning: use unseasoned rice vinegar warmed with the sugar, not sake or mirin."},"tips":[{"t":"In this recipe the 'rice wine' is the sushi seasoning: use unseasoned rice vinegar warmed with the sugar until dissolved, not sake or mirin. Cut it into hot rice with a slicing motion while fanning so the grains turn glossy.","s":0},{"t":"Never refrigerate seasoned sushi rice, as it hardens, and eat it the same day; keep rolls with raw or smoked fish cold. If using raw tuna, buy sashimi-grade fish that has been previously frozen.","s":2},{"t":"Cut rolls with a very sharp knife wetted between slices; dull or dry blades crush the rice.","s":5}],"rl":"BAWFAXV"},"75":{"lv":"EEERORERROO","w":{"5":"Gives the dressing zing; skip and it is sweeter and rounder.","7":"Sweet crunch and color.","8":"Adds protein and a fresh green bite; replace with peas or leave out.","9":"Mild onion garnish.","10":"Garnish for crunch and nutty aroma.","3":"Adds brightness; skipped, the dish still works but tastes flatter.","4":"A drizzle of sweetness; skipped, barely noticeable."},"sub":{"3":{"x":"1 tbsp lime or lemon juice","d":"Brighter and less mellow than rice vinegar.","k":["lime juice","lemon juice"]},"4":{"x":"1 tbsp maple syrup or 2 tsp sugar","d":"Slightly different sweetness; no other change.","k":["maple syrup","sugar"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Rinse soba under cold running water, rubbing gently to wash off surface starch; this keeps buckwheat noodles from clumping into a sticky mat. Stop cooking at 4 to 5 minutes, as soba turns mushy quickly.","s":0},{"t":"Dress the noodles just before serving; soba soaks up liquid and the vegetables release water over time. Keep a little dressing back to loosen leftovers.","s":2}],"rl":"BXFAWXVVVGG"},"3124":{"lv":"EEEEROREEERRRO","w":{"4":"Fresh onion bite that cuts through the sauce; skip for a plainer pancake.","5":"Adds crunch and savoriness; safe to skip.","6":"Gives a salty, smoky topping and fat; omit for vegetarian.","7":"Frying fat for each pancake.","10":"Adds sweetness and umami to the sauce; extra Worcestershire stands in.","11":"Balances the vinegary sauce; skip and it tastes sharper.","12":"Creamy, tangy finish that is part of the classic look and taste.","13":"Topping for aroma and umami; skip with little loss."},"sub":{"7":{"x":"2 tbsp canola or sunflower oil","d":"No difference.","k":["canola oil","sunflower oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Do not overmix the batter or press the cabbage; you want just enough batter to hold the cabbage so the pancake stays airy. Replacing water with dashi adds noticeable savory depth.","s":0},{"t":"Cook it thick over medium-low heat so the center sets before the outside darkens; resist pressing down with the spatula.","s":2},{"t":"Flip with a wide spatula in one quick motion, and cook until the center no longer looks wet; the egg and bacon must be fully cooked.","s":4}],"rl":"SMTVXGPFXXXWGG"},"98":{"lv":"EEERORREERR","w":{"5":"Savory aromatic that perfumes the sauce; skipping flattens the flavor.","6":"Warm note in the sauce; optional if you add more garlic.","9":"Sweet crunch and color; swap with any veg.","10":"Fresh green bite; interchangeable with other vegetables.","3":"Balances the sauce; skipped, it is less rounded and glossy but still works.","4":"A splash; skipped, barely noticeable.","8":"Main or only cooking fat; skipped, the food sticks, browns poorly and the dish loses richness."},"sub":{"3":{"x":"3 tbsp maple syrup or 2 tbsp brown sugar plus 1 tbsp water","d":"Slightly less floral.","k":["maple syrup","brown sugar"]},"4":{"x":"1 tbsp apple cider vinegar or lemon juice","d":"A bit fruitier.","k":["apple cider vinegar","lemon juice"]},"8":{"x":"2 tbsp canola or sunflower oil","d":"No difference.","k":["canola oil","sunflower oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Freeze the flank steak for 20 minutes, then slice thinly across the grain; thin slices stay tender because flank is lean and fibrous.","s":2},{"t":"Sear the beef in two batches over very high heat for 1 to 2 minutes; crowded meat steams and turns grey.","s":2},{"t":"Whisk the sauce again right before pouring, because cornstarch settles; unstirred it makes lumps and a thin sauce.","s":3}],"rl":"PBXWAXXTFVV"},"3118":{"lv":"EEEEORRRROO","w":{"2":"Sweet glossy depth to the glaze.","4":"Adds depth; water works instead.","5":"Fresh warmth for the sauce; skip and it tastes sweeter.","6":"Aromatic base; one clove makes a small but real difference.","7":"Fat for searing the chicken.","8":"Thickens to a glossy glaze; the sauce still reduces without it, just thinner.","9":"Garnish for crunch.","10":"Garnish for freshness."},"sub":{"2":{"x":"1/4 cup dry sherry or white wine plus 1 tbsp more sugar","d":"Less syrupy and complex than mirin.","k":["dry sherry","white wine","sugar"]},"4":{"x":"2 tbsp water, or dry sherry","d":"Slightly less depth; no real difference.","k":["water","dry sherry"]},"7":{"x":"1 tbsp canola or sunflower oil","d":"No difference.","k":["canola oil","sunflower oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Pat the skin dry and leave the thighs untouched until they release on their own; the skin must be deeply golden and rendered before the sauce goes in, or it turns rubbery.","s":1},{"t":"Pour off all but a spoonful of rendered fat; otherwise the glaze turns greasy and will not cling.","s":2},{"t":"Stir the slurry in a thin stream and look for a sauce that coats a spoon. Chicken is safe at 165F (74C), though thighs are more tender if taken to about 175F.","s":4}],"rl":"PXWWMXXFTGG"},"3120":{"lv":"EEEERRREOOO","w":{"5":"Rounds the sauce; mirin already adds some sweetness so use less if desired.","6":"Warm freshness to the broth; skip with minor loss.","8":"Optional soft yolk that enriches the bowl.","9":"Tangy accent that cuts the richness.","10":"Garnish for freshness.","4":"Balances the sauce; skipped, it is less rounded and glossy but still works."},"sub":{"4":{"x":"3 tbsp dry sherry or white wine plus 1 tsp sugar","d":"Less glossy and complex than mirin.","k":["dry sherry","white wine","sugar"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Freeze the ribeye for 30 minutes so you can slice it paper-thin, and separate the slices with chopsticks as they hit the broth so they do not clump.","s":2},{"t":"Keep the broth at a gentle simmer; thin beef toughens in a hard boil. Skim the foam for a cleaner sauce.","s":3},{"t":"A raw yolk is not safely cooked; use pasteurized eggs, or skip it for children, pregnant women or anyone vulnerable.","s":5}],"rl":"PVMXWWXBGGG"},"3121":{"lv":"EREERREEOO","w":{"1":"Sweetens the broth as it softens; skipping is noticeable.","5":"Rounds the sauce; mirin adds some sweetness too.","8":"Garnish for freshness.","9":"Garnish for a hint of sea flavor.","4":"Balances the sauce; skipped, it is less rounded and glossy but still works."},"sub":{"4":{"x":"2 tbsp dry sherry or white wine plus 1 tsp sugar","d":"Less glossy and complex than mirin.","k":["dry sherry","white wine","sugar"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Make sure the chicken reaches 165F (74C) before adding any egg; the egg is cooked by residual heat and will not safely finish raw chicken.","s":1},{"t":"The two-stage pour is the point: the first pour sets into custard, the second stays silky. Stir the beaten egg only a few times so some streaks of white remain.","s":2},{"t":"Take the pan off the heat while the egg still looks barely set; it keeps cooking on the hot rice. If anyone at the table is vulnerable, cook until fully set.","s":3}],"rl":"PVMXWWSBGG"},"3122":{"lv":"EEEERREERRROO","w":{"4":"Sweetness and color; replace with extra cabbage.","5":"Sweetens as it fries; skip and the dish is less mellow.","8":"Adds savory body to the sauce; extra Worcestershire stands in.","9":"Salt and savor; skip if your oyster sauce is salty.","10":"Rounds the sharp sauce.","11":"Fresh finish, garnish only.","12":"Tangy pickled ginger and bonito add aroma; skip with little loss.","2":"Main or only cooking fat; skipped, the food sticks, browns poorly and the dish loses richness."},"sub":{"2":{"x":"2 tbsp canola or sunflower oil","d":"No difference.","k":["canola oil","sunflower oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Slice the pork very thin (partly frozen helps) and cook it through, to at least 145F (63C) with no pink; high heat from a hot wok browns it fast.","s":1},{"t":"Loosen the noodles with water, not by pre-boiling: a splash in the hot pan steams the clumps apart without making them mushy.","s":3},{"t":"Add the sauce at the end over high heat; Worcestershire-based sauces are sugary and 2 to 3 minutes of tossing caramelizes them onto the noodles.","s":4}],"rl":"BPFVVVXXXXWGG"},"53032":{"lv":"EEEEEEERR","w":{"7":"Adds savory depth to the tonkatsu sauce; skip and add a little extra Worcestershire.","8":"Balances the sauce; reduce it if your ketchup is sweet.","4":"Frying medium; without it nothing can be deep or shallow fried and crisped."},"sub":{"4":{"x":"Canola or sunflower oil, 2 cm (about 3/4 inch) deep","d":"No difference.","k":["canola oil","sunflower oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Snip the connective tissue between the fat and the meat in a few places so the chops stay flat while frying, then pound to an even 1 cm so they cook evenly; pork needs 145F (63C).","s":0},{"t":"Fry only two cutlets at a time; crowding drops the oil temperature and gives greasy crumbs. Shake the pan gently for the first 30 seconds so the panko puffs.","s":2},{"t":"Rest the cutlets on a wire rack for 3 to 5 minutes before slicing; cutting while hot pushes out the juices and the steam softens the crust.","s":3}],"rl":"PTTTFXXXW"},"53034":{"lv":"REEEEOREEO","w":{"3":"Savory base for the sauce; replaceable.","6":"Rounds the sauce; mirin gives some sweetness too.","9":"Garnish for freshness.","0":"One of several fats or only sweats aromatics; skipping loses some flavor but dish still cooks.","5":"A teaspoon; skipped, barely noticeable."},"sub":{"0":{"x":"1 tbsp canola or sunflower oil","d":"No difference.","k":["canola oil","sunflower oil"]},"3":{"x":"150 ml chicken broth or water plus 1 tsp extra soy sauce","d":"Less seafood savor; the dish is still good.","k":["chicken broth","water","soy sauce"]},"5":{"x":"1 tsp dry sherry or white wine plus a pinch of sugar","d":"Slightly less glossy.","k":["dry sherry","white wine","sugar"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Set the cutlet in the middle of the pan so only its underside touches the broth; the top crust stays crisp against the soft egg.","s":0},{"t":"Pour the egg around the cutlet, not on top, and cook until set (2 to 3 minutes). Cover for even cooking; the egg should not stay liquid.","s":1}],"rl":"FVPMXWWSBG"},"3125":{"lv":"EREERERRO","w":{"1":"Sweet, charred pieces that alternate with the chicken; skip for plain skewers.","6":"Gives the tare background savoriness.","7":"Warm note for the tare.","8":"Garnish for crunch.","3":"Core of the tare; skipped, the glaze is flat.","4":"Adds depth; skipped, water works."},"sub":{"3":{"x":"1/2 cup dry white wine or sherry plus 2 tbsp more sugar","d":"Less syrupy than mirin; tare still thickens when reduced.","k":["white wine","dry sherry","sugar"]},"4":{"x":"1/4 cup dry sherry, white wine or water","d":"Water makes the tare lighter but still works.","k":["dry sherry","white wine","water"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Reduce the tare until it coats the back of a spoon, then set a portion aside that never touches the raw chicken or brush, to use at the table. A brush that touched raw chicken must be kept off cooked food.","s":1},{"t":"Cut the chicken into equal 1-inch pieces and leave a small gap between pieces on the skewer so the middles cook as fast as the edges.","s":2},{"t":"Brush the tare on only after the first sear, in 2 to 3 thin coats; sugar burns over direct heat. Cook until the chicken reaches 165F (74C).","s":4}],"rl":"PVXWMWXXG"},"3126":{"lv":"ERRRREO","w":{"1":"Gives the traditional sweet-leaning tamagoyaki; skip for a more savory roll.","3":"Seasons the egg and adds color; skipping makes it bland.","4":"Makes the custard tender and moist; without it the omelet is denser.","5":"Greases the pan between layers.","6":"Garnish; the grated daikon refreshes the rich egg.","2":"Balances the sauce; skipped, it is less rounded and glossy but still works."},"sub":{"2":{"x":"1 tbsp water plus 1/2 tsp extra sugar","d":"Less depth and gloss, but the same sweet-savory balance.","k":["water","sugar"]},"5":{"x":"1 tbsp canola or sunflower oil","d":"No difference.","k":["canola oil","sunflower oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Whisk by cutting through the whites with chopsticks, without lifting, and strain through a sieve; bubbles leave holes and a pitted surface on the roll.","s":0},{"t":"Wipe the pan with an oil-soaked paper towel before each layer instead of pouring oil in, and lift the cooked egg so the raw egg runs underneath; layers fuse when the top is still wet.","s":3},{"t":"Press the warm roll in a bamboo mat or paper towel for a minute or two to set a neat rectangle; a torn layer is hidden by the next layer.","s":5}],"rl":"PWWXMFG"},"3360":{"lv":"EREORR","w":{"1":"Nutty base for the garlic; plain oil works.","3":"A touch of heat; skip if you do not like spicy.","4":"Adds savor and color; skipping leaves it garlicky and salty only.","5":"Finishes the seasoning; skip and use soy sauce only if the edamame was boiled in salted water."},"sub":{"1":{"x":"1 tbsp olive or vegetable oil","d":"Loses the toasted sesame aroma.","k":["olive oil","vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Salt the boiling water generously (about 1 tablespoon per quart); edamame in the pod only pick up seasoning from the water and the pod surface.","s":0},{"t":"Garlic burns within seconds in sesame oil and turns bitter; take the pan off the heat the moment it smells fragrant and is pale gold.","s":1},{"t":"Drain and pat dry before tossing, or the oil will not cling to the pods.","s":2}],"rl":"VFXXXZ"},"3363":{"lv":"EEEEROO","w":{"1":"Helps the eggplant brown and soften.","4":"Brightens the sweet-salty miso glaze; replaceable.","5":"Garnish for crunch and nutty aroma.","6":"Garnish for freshness."},"sub":{"1":{"x":"2 tbsp olive or canola oil","d":"Olive adds a slightly fruity flavor.","k":["olive oil","canola oil"]},"4":{"x":"2 tbsp lemon juice or apple cider vinegar","d":"Brighter and sharper than rice vinegar.","k":["lemon juice","apple cider vinegar"]}},"tips":[{"t":"If you only have large globe eggplant, salt the cut sides for 20 minutes, pat dry and roast 10 to 15 minutes longer; Japanese eggplants have thinner skin and fewer seeds.","s":0},{"t":"Roast cut-side down so the flesh steams and collapses; scoring lets the glaze sink in.","s":1},{"t":"Miso and sugar go from caramelized to burnt in seconds under the broiler, so stay by the oven and pull as soon as it bubbles and browns in spots.","s":4}],"rl":"VFXWAGG"},"3460":{"lv":"EEERRRROO","w":{"3":"Gives the nutty restaurant flavor of seaweed salad.","4":"Balances the vinegar and soy; skip and it is sharper.","5":"Fresh zing; skip and it is mellower.","6":"Cool crunch that contrasts with the soft wakame.","7":"Garnish for crunch.","8":"Garnish for freshness.","1":"Dressing acid; skipped, flat."},"sub":{"1":{"x":"2 tbsp apple cider vinegar or lemon juice","d":"Sharper and fruitier than rice vinegar; add a pinch more sugar.","k":["apple cider vinegar","lemon juice"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Dried wakame swells to roughly 8 to 10 times its volume, so use a large bowl, and stop soaking once it is supple; over-soaking turns it slimy.","s":0},{"t":"Trim any thick central rib and squeeze the wakame hard; leftover water dilutes the dressing.","s":1},{"t":"Dress it 10 to 15 minutes before serving so it takes on flavor without the cucumber going limp.","s":3}],"rl":"VAXFWXVGG"},"3461":{"lv":"EEEEOO","w":{"4":"Adds a little fruity brightness; skip with little change.","5":"Mild spice accent in the jar; skip with little change.","1":"Pickling liquid; skipped, no pickle."},"sub":{"1":{"x":"1/2 cup apple cider vinegar or white vinegar","d":"Sharper and less mellow than rice vinegar; use 1 extra tsp sugar.","k":["apple cider vinegar","white vinegar"],"wn":"Use a vinegar of at least 5% acidity"}},"tips":[{"t":"Salting first pulls out water and the sulphurous smell, so the slices stay crunchy; squeeze them hard before packing.","s":0},{"t":"Pour the brine over lukewarm, not hot; hot brine partly cooks the daikon and makes it limp.","s":3},{"t":"Keep the slices fully submerged, pressing with a spoon, and expect the flavor to be best after about 24 hours.","s":4}],"rl":"VKWKAX"},"3464":{"lv":"ERERERRR","w":{"1":"Sweet color and crunch; skip and use more cabbage.","5":"Balances the vinegar; skipping makes it tart.","6":"Gives roasty depth to the dressing.","7":"Makes the dressing creamy and mild; skip for a thinner, sharper dressing.","3":"Adds brightness; skipped, the dish still works but tastes flatter."},"sub":{"3":{"x":"2 tbsp apple cider vinegar or lemon juice","d":"Sharper; add a pinch more sugar.","k":["apple cider vinegar","lemon juice"]}},"tips":[{"t":"After the ice bath, dry the cabbage thoroughly in a towel or salad spinner; leftover water dilutes the dressing and it slides off.","s":0},{"t":"Toast the sesame seeds until golden and grind them while warm; they release more oil and aroma. Leave some texture instead of making a paste.","s":1},{"t":"Dress just before serving; shredded cabbage starts weeping within about 10 minutes.","s":3}],"rl":"VVXAXWFF"},"3465":{"lv":"EEEEERROO","w":{"6":"Fresh, cooling topping that is classic with agedashi.","7":"Garnish for freshness.","8":"Optional topping for smoky umami.","1":"Coating for crispness; skipped, the tofu is soggy.","2":"Frying medium; without it nothing can be deep or shallow fried and crisped.","5":"Balances the sauce; skipped, it is less rounded and glossy but still works."},"sub":{"1":{"x":"1/3 cup cornstarch","d":"Slightly harder, less delicate crust.","k":["cornstarch"]},"2":{"x":"2 cups canola or sunflower oil","d":"No difference.","k":["canola oil","sunflower oil"]},"5":{"x":"3 tbsp dry sherry or white wine plus 1 tsp sugar","d":"Less gloss; broth still works.","k":["dry sherry","white wine","sugar"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Dust the tofu with potato starch only right before frying and shake off the excess; the thin coating becomes the silky shell that also lightly thickens the broth.","s":3},{"t":"Hold the oil near 350F; if too cool the tofu soaks up oil, and if too hot the coating darkens before it crisps.","s":2},{"t":"Ladle the broth around the tofu, not over it, and serve right away; the crisp shell softens within a couple of minutes by design.","s":4}],"rl":"PTFMXWGGG"},"3466":{"lv":"ERRRRERREO","w":{"1":"Sweetness and color; skipping is fine.","2":"Fresh crunch against the creamy potato; skip for a smoother salad.","3":"Adds richness and protein; skipping makes it plainer.","4":"Sharp bite that cuts the mayonnaise; skip for milder.","7":"Balances the vinegar.","9":"Mild heat.","6":"Adds brightness; skipped, the dish still works but tastes flatter."},"sub":{"6":{"x":"1 tbsp apple cider vinegar or lemon juice","d":"Sharper and less mellow.","k":["apple cider vinegar","lemon juice"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Season the potatoes with vinegar and sugar while still warm; hot potato absorbs flavor best. Mash only partway, leaving about a third in chunks for texture.","s":3},{"t":"Cool the potatoes completely before adding the mayonnaise; warm potatoes make it oily and thin.","s":4},{"t":"Because it contains mayonnaise and eggs, refrigerate it within 2 hours and do not leave it out at a picnic.","s":5}],"rl":"BVVPXFAWZZ"},"3468":{"lv":"EREERROO","w":{"1":"Sweet color and extra crunch.","5":"Balances the soy and mirin; skip for a more savory glaze.","6":"Garnish for crunch.","7":"Optional heat.","2":"Main or only cooking fat; skipped, the food sticks, browns poorly and the dish loses richness.","4":"Balances the sauce; skipped, it is less rounded and glossy but still works."},"sub":{"2":{"x":"1 tbsp vegetable oil plus a few drops of sesame oil if you have it","d":"Loses some toastiness.","k":["vegetable oil"]},"4":{"x":"2 tbsp dry sherry or white wine plus 1 tsp sugar","d":"Less gloss.","k":["dry sherry","white wine","sugar"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Cut the burdock into thin, even matchsticks; thick pieces stay woody. The cold soak removes tannin that browns the water and the cut surfaces.","s":0},{"t":"Cook uncovered over medium-high, stirring often, until the pan is almost dry and the vegetables look glossy; add a splash of water if they start to scorch before they soften.","s":3}],"rl":"VVFXWWGX"},"3470":{"lv":"EEERRR","w":{"4":"Rounds the soy and mirin; kabocha is already sweet so you can use less.","5":"Warm fresh edge in the broth; skip and it is sweeter.","3":"Balances the sauce; skipped, it is less rounded and glossy but still works."},"sub":{"3":{"x":"2 tbsp dry sherry or white wine plus 1 tsp sugar","d":"Less syrupy.","k":["dry sherry","white wine","sugar"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Kabocha skin is very hard: pierce it and microwave the whole squash for 2 to 3 minutes to soften it before cutting, and use a heavy knife on a stable board.","s":0},{"t":"Press a parchment or foil circle (or a drop lid) on the surface so the broth circulates and the squash cooks evenly without being stirred and broken.","s":3},{"t":"Let it rest off the heat for 10 minutes; the squash continues to absorb broth as it cools, and it is even better the next day.","s":4}],"rl":"VMXWWX"},"53033":{"lv":"EOOO","w":{"1":"A tiny sweet note and shine; plain rice is fine without it.","2":"Tangy garnish for some meals; skip freely.","3":"Garnish for color."},"tips":[{"t":"Rinse until the water runs nearly clear; excess surface starch makes the rice gluey, but it should still be a little sticky. Soaking for 20 to 30 minutes helps even cooking.","s":0},{"t":"Do not lift the lid during the simmer; steam is what cooks the rice, and an opening ruins the texture.","s":2},{"t":"Fluff with a wet paddle using cutting motions rather than stirring, which mashes the grains.","s":3}],"rl":"BWGG"},"54":{"lv":"RRRREERERRO","w":{"0":"Jammy egg is the signature topping; ramen is fine without it, just less rich.","2":"Savory aromatic base for the broth.","3":"Warm freshness in the broth; skip with some loss.","6":"Adds depth to the miso broth; extra miso stands in.","8":"Meaty bite and umami; replace with any vegetable.","9":"Green color and mild flavor; interchangeable.","10":"Garnish for freshness.","1":"One of several fats or only sweats aromatics; skipping loses some flavor but dish still cooks."},"sub":{"1":{"x":"1 tbsp vegetable oil","d":"Loses the toasty aroma.","k":["vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Lower the eggs straight from the fridge into boiling water for 6 minutes 30 seconds, then ice bath for 5 minutes; peel under running water for a clean white and a jammy yolk.","s":0},{"t":"Dissolve the miso in a ladle of hot broth, then stir it into the pot off the boil; boiling dulls its aroma and makes the soup grainy.","s":1},{"t":"For a clearer broth, cook the noodles in a separate pot of water; ramen starch clouds the soup.","s":2}],"rl":"PFXXXMXBVVG"},"3119":{"lv":"ERRRRRRRRREOEEO","w":{"1":"Meaty topping; the broth works without it.","2":"Sweet flavor in the broth.","3":"Adds savor to the broth.","4":"Clean, warm note that offsets pork richness.","5":"Adds aroma to the broth.","6":"Braises the pork belly and seasons the egg marinade.","7":"Sweet gloss for the belly and egg marinade.","8":"Balances the braise.","9":"Classic topping; skipping makes the bowl less rich.","11":"Aromatic finishing drizzle.","14":"Garnish for texture."},"sub":{"7":{"x":"2 tbsp dry sherry or white wine plus 1 tsp sugar","d":"Less gloss.","k":["dry sherry","white wine","sugar"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Blanch the bones and scrub off the dark scum and blood; otherwise the broth turns grey and tastes bitter.","s":0},{"t":"Tonkotsu is the exception to never boil stock: a hard rolling boil emulsifies collagen and fat into the milky broth. Top up with boiling water so the bones stay covered.","s":1},{"t":"Cool leftover broth quickly in shallow containers and refrigerate within 2 hours; it sets to jelly, which is a good sign.","s":4}],"rl":"PPXXXXXWWPBFZZG"},"3123":{"lv":"EEERRO","w":{"3":"Soft protein that gives body; the soup is fine as a plain broth.","4":"Mild sea flavor and texture; skip with little change.","5":"Garnish for freshness."},"tips":[{"t":"Dissolve the miso in the ladle against the sieve and take the pot off the boil first; boiling kills the aroma and makes it grainy.","s":4},{"t":"Stir before each serving since the miso settles, and add it last so it does not cook off.","s":4},{"t":"Handle silken tofu gently; slide cubes in from the side rather than stirring.","s":2}],"rl":"MXXPVG"},"52967":{"lv":"EEEEEO","w":{"0":"Self-rising flour gives the lift; with all-purpose you must add the leavener.","3":"Makes the dough tender and rich; water gives a drier mandazi.","5":"Traditional coastal scent; skip it for a plainer mandazi."},"sub":{"0":{"x":"750 g all-purpose flour plus 3 tbsp baking powder and 1 1/2 tsp salt","d":"Same result if whisked well; do not skip the baking powder.","k":["all-purpose flour","baking powder","salt"]},"3":{"x":"1 cup coconut milk, or 1 cup water plus 1 tbsp melted butter","d":"Coconut milk is the coastal-style version and tastes richer.","k":["coconut milk","butter"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Rest the dough 15 minutes under a damp cloth so the gluten relaxes; it rolls to an even 1 cm without springing back.","s":7},{"t":"Fry at about 350F; too hot and they brown while raw inside, too cool and they soak up oil. Turn once when puffed and light brown.","s":10}],"rl":"SWSMFX"},"53114":{"lv":"EE","tips":[{"t":"Use fine white maize meal (not coarse polenta or grits) for the right smooth texture; add it in a thin steady stream, whisking, then switch to a sturdy wooden spoon.","s":1},{"t":"Press the dough against the pot wall to crush lumps, and finish by covering on the lowest heat for a couple of minutes to steam away the raw cornmeal taste.","s":2},{"t":"Shape into a mound with a wet spoon; ugali is eaten by pinching off a piece, making an indent and scooping up stew or greens.","s":3}],"rl":"MB"},"52968":{"lv":"EEREROO","w":{"2":"The base of the kachumbari salad that brightens the rich meat.","4":"Sharp bite for the kachumbari; skip and it is milder.","5":"Heat; skip for a mild kachumbari.","6":"Fresh herb finish.","1":"This should be white maize meal (cornmeal) for the ugali; cornstarch makes a gluey paste, not ugali."},"tips":[{"t":"The ingredient list says 1 kg cornstarch, but the ugali needs white cornmeal (maize meal); cornstarch makes a gluey paste, not ugali.","s":-1},{"t":"Goat is lean and can be tough; cook it to 145F (63C) and rest 3 minutes, and cut leg or shoulder into small pieces so it tenderizes in the roasting time.","s":1},{"t":"Dress the kachumbari at the last minute with salt and a squeeze of lemon; tomatoes weep and turn watery if left sitting.","s":7}],"rl":"PBVZVXG"},"53112":{"lv":"EERREEEROERROO","w":{"2":"Aromatic base; the stew tastes flatter without it.","3":"Warm spice base for the pre-simmered beef.","7":"Color and mild sweet pepper flavor.","8":"Light heat; skip.","10":"Concentrated tomato depth; extra tomatoes can partly cover it.","11":"Seasoning; add to taste.","12":"Fresh heat for garnish.","13":"Garnish for freshness.","4":"Only cooking fat; skipped, food sticks and browns poorly, so use an oil or substitute."},"sub":{"4":{"x":"2 tbsp canola or sunflower oil","d":"No difference.","k":["canola oil","sunflower oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Simmering the beef first with garlic and ginger tenderizes it and yields a flavorful broth to add back later; keep it at a gentle simmer, not a boil.","s":0},{"t":"Cook the paprika, curry powder and tomato paste in the pot for a minute before adding water; they lose rawness and the paste caramelizes.","s":5},{"t":"Simmer uncovered until the sauce coats the meat and oil separates at the edges, a sign the tomatoes have fully cooked down.","s":6}],"rl":"MPXXFXVXZXXZGG"},"53113":{"lv":"EEERR","w":{"3":"Seasoning, add gradually; kale is bland without it.","4":"Non-traditional enrichment that thickens the greens; classic sukuma wiki skips it.","0":"Only cooking fat; skipped, food sticks and browns poorly, so use an oil or substitute."},"sub":{"0":{"x":"2 tbsp butter or olive oil","d":"Butter adds richness; olive oil adds fruitiness.","k":["butter","olive oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Strip the leaves from the tough ribs and slice them very finely; the ribs stay stringy. Cook only until tender and still green.","s":1},{"t":"Cook the onions until they are soft and lightly browned first; they are the sweetness that balances the bitter kale.","s":0},{"t":"Stir the cream in gently and keep the heat at a simmer; a hard boil can make it split. The classic version skips it and adds chopped tomato instead.","s":2}],"rl":"FXVZF"},"3104":{"lv":"EERRRRRRRRREEEROO","w":{"2":"Salty depth for the beef.","3":"Nutty flavor for the beef and vegetables.","4":"Caramelizes the beef and balances the soy.","5":"Savory base for the beef and spinach.","6":"Fresh green, lightly seasoned namul.","7":"Sweet color and crunch.","8":"Mild fresh vegetable and color.","9":"Crisp, nutty namul traditional in the bowl.","10":"Earthy umami vegetable.","14":"Lets the gochujang sauce spread and gives aroma.","15":"Brightens the sauce; skip with little change.","16":"Garnish for crunch.","12":"Only cooking fat; skipped, food sticks and browns poorly, so use an oil or substitute."},"sub":{"12":{"x":"2 tbsp canola or sunflower oil","d":"No difference.","k":["canola oil","sunflower oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Season each vegetable separately and squeeze the spinach and sprouts dry; leftover water dilutes everything and makes the bowl soggy.","s":1},{"t":"For a crisp bottom (nurungji), press the rice into a hot oiled skillet or stone bowl for 3 to 4 minutes until it crackles, then add the toppings.","s":5},{"t":"Cook the ground beef to at least 160F (71C). Mix everything thoroughly at the table so the gochujang coats every bite.","s":0}],"rl":"BPXFWXVVVVVPFXFAG"},"3106":{"lv":"EREEERRRRRREOO","w":{"1":"Adds meaty richness; the dish is still good vegetarian.","5":"Savory aroma in the sauce.","6":"Sweetness, color and crunch.","7":"Sweetens as it softens.","8":"Classic green; skipping loses color.","9":"Meaty bite.","10":"Color and crunch.","12":"Garnish for nutty crunch.","13":"Light heat.","11":"Only cooking fat; skipped, food sticks and browns poorly, so use an oil or substitute."},"sub":{"11":{"x":"2 tbsp canola or sunflower oil","d":"No difference.","k":["canola oil","sunflower oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Cook the dangmyeon until chewy (not soft), rinse in cold water, then toss with sesame oil immediately so the strands do not stick; cut with scissors for easy eating.","s":1},{"t":"Stir-fry the vegetables one at a time, firmest first, so each keeps its color and texture; combining them gives a muddy flavor.","s":3},{"t":"Toss the noodles with the sauce while still slightly warm so they absorb it, and add the soy gradually; the noodles soak up a lot.","s":5}],"rl":"BPXWFXVVVVVFGZ"},"3108":{"lv":"EEEEOREERRRRRRO","w":{"4":"Mild heat in the coating.","5":"Savory background in the crust.","6":"Frying oil.","8":"Sweet gloss for the sauce.","9":"Salty depth.","10":"Deepens the sweetness.","11":"Brightens the sticky sauce.","12":"Aromatic.","13":"Nutty finish.","14":"Garnish for crunch."},"sub":{"6":{"x":"3 cups canola or peanut oil","d":"No difference.","k":["canola oil","peanut oil"]},"8":{"x":"3 tbsp maple syrup or corn syrup","d":"Slightly different flavor; stays sticky.","k":["maple syrup","corn syrup"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Fry first at 325F for 8 to 10 minutes until the wings reach 165F (74C), then rest 10 minutes so moisture escapes the crust before the second fry at 375F crisps it.","s":1},{"t":"Pat the wings very dry before coating; the cornstarch and flour mixture gives a thin, glassy shell only if the skin is dry.","s":0},{"t":"Toss the wings in the hot, syrupy sauce at the last moment and serve; the crust softens within minutes.","s":4}],"rl":"PTTZZXFXWXWAXFG"},"3109":{"lv":"EEERRRERROOO","w":{"3":"Nutty depth in the marinade.","4":"Natural sweetener and meat tenderizer; replace with pear.","5":"Sweet, savory depth from grated onion.","7":"Warm edge that balances the sweet marinade.","8":"Adds gloss and depth; replaceable.","9":"Garnish for freshness.","10":"Garnish for crunch.","11":"Mild heat."},"sub":{"4":{"x":"1/2 ripe pear, grated (or 1/4 cup unsweetened apple juice)","d":"Pear tenderizes slightly better; apple juice only adds sweetness.","k":["pear","apple juice"]},"8":{"x":"2 tbsp dry white wine or sherry plus 1/2 tsp sugar","d":"Slightly less sweet than mirin.","k":["white wine","dry sherry","sugar"]}},"tips":[{"t":"The cold soak draws out surface blood so the marinade stays clear and the beef tastes cleaner; pat the ribs very dry before marinating.","s":0},{"t":"Do not marinate longer than about 24 hours: the enzymes in grated fruit tenderize the thin ribs, but past a day they turn the meat mushy.","s":2},{"t":"Grill over very high heat and watch for flare-ups; the sugar chars quickly. Snip the meat from the bone with scissors once rested.","s":4}],"rl":"PXWFWXXXXGGZ"},"3112":{"lv":"EEEERRRRRERRRROO","w":{"4":"Sweetness that balances the heat.","5":"Gloss and stickiness for the sauce.","6":"Savory aromatic base.","7":"Warm edge that cuts the sweetness.","8":"Nutty aroma in the marinade.","10":"Cabbage cooks down sweet and adds moisture to the sauce.","11":"Sweet, soft counterpoint to the spicy sauce.","12":"Sweetens as it softens.","13":"Chewy texture and the classic add-in; skip for a lighter dish.","14":"Fresh anise-like herb; garnish.","15":"Mild heat.","9":"Only cooking fat; skipped, food sticks and browns poorly, so use an oil or substitute."},"sub":{"9":{"x":"1 tbsp canola or sunflower oil","d":"No difference.","k":["canola oil","sunflower oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Start the sweet potato with a splash of water, covered, so it steams through before the chicken goes in; it takes longer than the thighs.","s":2},{"t":"Stir often: the sugar and honey scorch at the pan bottom. Add a splash of water if the sauce sticks, and cook the chicken to 165F (74C).","s":3},{"t":"Finish the Korean way: after eating, fry rice with the leftover sauce, a little sesame oil and seaweed in the pan (bokkeumbap).","s":5}],"rl":"PXXXWWXXFFVVVBGZ"},"3115":{"lv":"EREOEERRRRRR","w":{"1":"Seasons the rice with a nutty aroma.","3":"Garnish for crunch.","6":"Sweet color and crunch.","7":"Cool crunch; deseed so it does not weep.","8":"Classic tangy yellow pickle that cuts the richness.","9":"Green color and savor.","10":"Savory meaty filling; the roll is fine without it.","11":"Cooks the egg and vegetable fillings; skipped, a nonstick pan manages with little change."},"sub":{"11":{"x":"1 tbsp canola or sunflower oil","d":"No difference.","k":["canola oil","sunflower oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Let the seasoned rice cool to warm, not hot; hot rice wilts and tears the nori.","s":0},{"t":"Squeeze the spinach and blot every filling dry; moisture makes the roll soggy and burst. Cook the ground beef to 160F (71C).","s":4},{"t":"Roll tightly, wipe a sharp knife with a damp cloth between cuts and brush with sesame oil. Keep kimbap cool and eat within a day since it contains egg and cooked rice.","s":5}],"rl":"BFZGSPVVXVPF"},"102":{"lv":"ERREEROEROO","w":{"1":"Savory aromatic.","2":"Warm edge for the sauce.","5":"Nutty finish.","6":"Mild heat; skip if you want it mild.","8":"Cool crunch against the rich beef.","9":"Garnish for freshness.","10":"Garnish for crunch."},"tips":[{"t":"Brown the beef hard and break it into small crumbles, then drain excess fat before adding the sauce; add the garlic after the beef browns so it does not burn. Cook to 160F (71C).","s":1},{"t":"Simmer until the sauce reduces and clings to the meat rather than pooling.","s":2},{"t":"Salt the cucumber slices for 5 minutes or add a splash of vinegar for a quick pickle that cuts the sweetness.","s":3}],"rl":"PXXXWFXBVGG"},"3103":{"lv":"EEERERRROOEO","w":{"3":"Nutty aroma in the marinade.","5":"Warm edge.","6":"Tenderizing and sweetness from grated fruit; replace with pear.","7":"Sweetness and moisture as the onion caramelizes.","8":"Garnish for freshness.","9":"Garnish for crunch.","10":"Needed to sear; any neutral oil.","11":"Mild heat."},"sub":{"6":{"x":"1/2 ripe pear, grated (or 1 tbsp extra sugar plus 1 tbsp water)","d":"Pear is closest; sugar alone gives sweetness only, not tenderizing.","k":["pear","sugar"]},"10":{"x":"1 tbsp canola or sunflower oil","d":"No difference.","k":["canola oil","sunflower oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Freeze the sirloin for 30 to 45 minutes and slice about 1/8 inch thick against the grain; this is what makes bulgogi tender.","s":0},{"t":"Marinate 20 minutes to overnight, but not much longer than 24 hours; the grated fruit's enzymes turn thin slices mushy.","s":1},{"t":"Cook in two batches in a very hot pan; crowded meat steams and loses its caramelized edges.","s":3}],"rl":"PXWFXXWVGGFZ"},"3107":{"lv":"EEERRERRROO","w":{"3":"Deepens heat and color; skip and the sauce is milder.","4":"Salt and depth.","6":"Savory aromatic.","7":"Chewy, savory bite; replace with extra eggs or skip.","8":"Fresh onion bite.","9":"Hearty topping.","10":"Garnish for crunch."},"tips":[{"t":"Use anchovy-kelp stock instead of plain water for authentic depth; the sauce depends on it.","s":1},{"t":"Stir often: starch leaks from the rice cakes and scorches on the bottom. Add a splash of water if the sauce gets too thick.","s":2},{"t":"Serve right away; rice cakes harden as they cool and overcooking turns them mushy.","s":3}],"rl":"BMXXXWXPGPG"},"3111":{"lv":"EERRRORORROR","w":{"2":"Boosts the sour, savory flavor.","4":"Fat and salty crunch that fry the kimchi.","5":"Garnish for freshness.","6":"Sweet heat and body.","7":"Salty finishing seasoning; kimchi may already be salty.","8":"Nutty aroma finish.","9":"A runny yolk is the sauce for the rice.","10":"Garnish for crunch.","11":"Balances the sourness of the kimchi.","3":"Bacon fat also renders in the pan; skipped, the rice still fries."},"sub":{"3":{"x":"2 tbsp canola or sunflower oil, or use the bacon fat","d":"No difference.","k":["canola oil","sunflower oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Fry the kimchi in the bacon fat until the edges caramelize; this mellows its raw sourness and the sugar balances the acid.","s":1},{"t":"Use cold, day-old rice and press it flat against the pan for a minute at a time to get crisp bits; warm fresh rice turns mushy.","s":3},{"t":"Fry the eggs in a hot pan with plenty of oil for lacy crisp edges; use pasteurized eggs if you worry about runny yolks.","s":5}],"rl":"BVAFFGXXFPGW"},"3114":{"lv":"EERERRRERROOEO","w":{"2":"Adds heat and color; skip and the sauce is milder and less red.","4":"Balances the heat.","5":"Gloss and caramelization.","6":"Nutty aroma.","8":"Warm edge.","9":"Sweetens and softens in the sauce.","10":"Fresh heat.","11":"Garnish for freshness.","13":"Garnish for crunch.","12":"Only cooking fat; skipped, food sticks and browns poorly, so use an oil or substitute."},"sub":{"12":{"x":"1 tbsp canola or sunflower oil","d":"No difference.","k":["canola oil","sunflower oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Slice the pork shoulder about 1/8 inch thick (partly frozen helps) and keep some of the fat; it keeps thin slices juicy and carries the sauce.","s":0},{"t":"Let the pork sit undisturbed for a minute or two between stirs to char; the sugars caramelize. Cook until no pink remains and it reaches at least 145F (63C).","s":3},{"t":"Serve in lettuce leaves with a slice of raw garlic and rice; the cool leaf cuts the heat and richness.","s":5}],"rl":"PXXXWWFXXVXGFG"},"3113":{"lv":"EREREEOERROO","w":{"1":"Makes the edges crisp and lacy; skipping gives a softer pancake.","3":"Binds and enriches the batter.","6":"Seafood sweetness; skip for a vegetarian pancake.","7":"Fat for crisping each pancake.","8":"Salty dip.","9":"Tangy dip.","10":"Heat for the dip.","11":"Garnish for the dip."},"sub":{"7":{"x":"3 tbsp canola or sunflower oil","d":"No difference.","k":["canola oil","sunflower oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Use ice-cold water and stir only until combined, leaving a few lumps; cold batter fries crisper and overmixing makes it chewy.","s":0},{"t":"Be generous with oil and drizzle extra around the edges; the pancake needs to fry, not steam, to get a crisp lacy edge.","s":2},{"t":"Lay the green onions in the same direction so the pancake holds together when flipped. If using shrimp, cook until opaque and pink.","s":2}],"rl":"STMTZVPFXAXG"},"3116":{"lv":"EEEROOO","w":{"4":"Garnish for freshness.","5":"Nutty aroma finish.","6":"Garnish for crunch.","3":"Light umami seasoning; skipped, the steamed egg is milder but still works."},"sub":{"3":{"x":"1 tsp soy sauce","d":"Less funky; slightly darker color.","k":["soy sauce"],"wn":"Contains soy and wheat; removes the fish allergen"}},"tips":[{"t":"Strain the eggs for a smooth custard and keep the egg-to-liquid ratio near 1:1 by volume; less water makes a firmer set and more makes a wobbly, soupy one.","s":1},{"t":"Cook gently over the lowest heat in a heavy small pot so it does not scorch, with the lid slightly ajar for a flat top.","s":4},{"t":"Cook until the egg is fully set (it reaches 160F / 71C) and the center barely jiggles.","s":4}],"rl":"PMZZGFG"},"3362":{"lv":"EERRERERRO","w":{"2":"Loosens the seasoning paste so it coats evenly.","3":"Feeds fermentation and balances acidity.","7":"Warmth and aroma.","8":"Fresh note and mild crunch.","9":"Sweet color and crunch.","5":"Adds umami depth; skipped, the kimchi still ferments, since salt does the preserving."},"sub":{"5":{"x":"2 tbsp soy sauce","d":"Less funky and a bit sweeter; still ferments.","k":["soy sauce"],"wn":"Contains soy and wheat; removes the fish allergen"}},"tips":[{"t":"Use non-iodized coarse salt; taste a leaf after rinsing, it should be pleasantly salty like soup. The cabbage should bend without breaking.","s":0},{"t":"Pack firmly and press down so the brine rises above the cabbage; leave 1 inch of headspace because it bubbles. Burp the jar daily.","s":4},{"t":"Taste daily and refrigerate once pleasantly sour; it keeps improving in the fridge for weeks.","s":5}],"rl":"VKMWXZXXVV"},"3365":{"lv":"EEREROR","w":{"2":"Raw garlic bite that defines the namul; skip and it is plainer.","4":"Garnish for nutty crunch.","5":"Garnish for freshness.","6":"Seasoning; add gradually."},"tips":[{"t":"Blanch in a large pot of boiling water for 30 to 45 seconds; a crowded pot cools and the spinach turns drab.","s":0},{"t":"Squeeze the spinach in handfuls until almost dry; the drier it is, the more it holds the seasoning.","s":1},{"t":"Season with your fingertips, tossing gently so the leaves stay fluffy; cut after squeezing.","s":3}],"rl":"VFXZGXZ"},"3462":{"lv":"EROEEOR","w":{"1":"Savory bite; skip for a milder side.","2":"Garnish for freshness.","5":"Garnish for crunch.","6":"Seasoning; taste first as the soy is salty."},"tips":[{"t":"Mince the garlic very fine and use sparingly if you plan to keep leftovers; raw garlic turns sharper as it sits.","s":3},{"t":"Taste before adding any salt, since the soy sauce already seasons the spinach.","s":4},{"t":"Serve within a day; spinach leaches water as it sits and the dish gets soggy.","s":4}],"rl":"VXXZFGZ"},"3463":{"lv":"ERREEOO","w":{"1":"Fresh bite; skip with little change.","2":"Savory kick that defines the dish.","5":"Garnish for crunch.","6":"Optional heat."},"tips":[{"t":"Keep the lid on for the full cooking time; lifting it releases a raw, beany smell. Use only 1/2 cup water so the sprouts steam.","s":1},{"t":"Cool the sprouts quickly under cold running water so they stay crunchy.","s":2},{"t":"Dress while barely warm and drain well; wet sprouts dilute the seasoning. Save the cooking water for soup.","s":3}],"rl":"VXXFZGX"},"3467":{"lv":"EEEERRROO","w":{"5":"Savory aromatic.","6":"Nutty aroma finish.","7":"Garnish for crunch.","8":"Garnish for freshness.","1":"Only cooking fat; skipped, food sticks and browns poorly, so use an oil or substitute.","4":"Adds sweetness and gloss to the braise; skipped, the potatoes are less glossy."},"sub":{"1":{"x":"1 tbsp canola or sunflower oil","d":"No difference.","k":["canola oil","sunflower oil"]},"4":{"x":"1 tbsp maple syrup or corn syrup","d":"Slightly different flavor, same sticky gloss.","k":["maple syrup","corn syrup"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Use waxy potatoes such as Yukon Gold; russets break down and turn the sauce cloudy. Rinsing the starch off helps them hold their shape.","s":0},{"t":"Add the honey only for the last 5 minutes; stirring it in earlier risks burning, and it gives the lacquered finish.","s":3},{"t":"Stir often at the end as the sauce thickens, until each piece is glossy.","s":3}],"rl":"BFXWWXFGG"},"3469":{"lv":"EEROREO","w":{"2":"Savory aromatic, added late so it does not burn.","3":"Garnish for freshness.","4":"Nutty aroma finish.","6":"Garnish for crunch.","1":"Only cooking fat; skipped, food sticks and browns poorly, so use an oil or substitute."},"sub":{"1":{"x":"1 tbsp canola or sunflower oil","d":"No difference.","k":["canola oil","sunflower oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Salting the zucchini draws out water so it sautes instead of steaming; pat it dry thoroughly before it goes in the pan.","s":0},{"t":"Cook in a single layer in a hot pan without stirring for the first couple of minutes so it gets golden spots.","s":2},{"t":"Pull it while still slightly firm; residual heat keeps cooking it.","s":3}],"rl":"VFXGFZG"},"3105":{"lv":"EERRRRRERORR","w":{"3":"Sweetens the broth.","4":"Savory aromatic.","5":"Adds heat and color; kimchi already supplies some.","6":"Adds depth and body to the broth.","8":"Soft protein that absorbs the broth; skip for just stew.","9":"Garnish for freshness.","10":"Balances the kimchi sourness.","11":"Adds savor; the kimchi juice is salty too.","2":"Toasted aroma finish; skipped, the stew loses some fragrance but works."},"sub":{"2":{"x":"1 tbsp vegetable oil","d":"Loses the toasted aroma.","k":["vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Use properly aged, sour kimchi; fresh kimchi gives a flat stew. Fry it in the pork fat until it softens and smells sweet-sour before adding liquid.","s":1},{"t":"Cover and simmer the full 20 minutes; the kimchi's acid needs time to mellow into the broth. If still sharp, add a pinch more sugar, and if too salty add water.","s":4},{"t":"Add the tofu only at the end; it is delicate and absorbs the broth. Cook the pork through.","s":5}],"rl":"VPFXXXXMPGWX"},"3110":{"lv":"EREREERRERORR","w":{"1":"Adds richness and savor; skip for a lighter stew.","3":"Savory aromatic.","6":"Umami and salt that deepen the broth.","7":"Sweetens the broth.","9":"Briny sweetness; skip and the stew is milder.","10":"Garnish for freshness.","11":"Enriches the broth and adds protein.","12":"Nutty aroma finish.","0":"Only cooking fat; skipped, food sticks and browns poorly, so use an oil or substitute."},"sub":{"0":{"x":"1 tbsp canola or sunflower oil","d":"No difference.","k":["canola oil","sunflower oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Cook the gochugaru in the oil over medium-low heat for only about 30 seconds so the oil turns red; burnt flakes make the stew bitter.","s":1},{"t":"Spoon in the soft tofu in large chunks and do not stir; it is very delicate and breaks up in the bowl as you eat.","s":4},{"t":"Crack the egg into the bubbling stew and cook until the white sets (fully cooked is safest). Discard any clams that do not open.","s":5}],"rl":"FPXXMXXXPPGPX"},"53427":{"lv":"EEEERRERRRRRRRROO","w":{"4":"Tangy crunch typical of naem khao; skip with a milder sourness.","5":"Brightens the dish; the sausage is already sour.","7":"Balances the acidity.","8":"Sharp crunch; soak first to mellow it.","9":"Sweet aromatic crunch.","10":"Fresh onion bite.","11":"Fresh herb that is part of the flavor.","12":"Adds cooling, peppery lift.","13":"Adds cooling lift.","14":"Adds crunchy, nutty bite.","15":"Served alongside for freshness.","16":"Served alongside for wrapping.","2":"Frying medium for the rice balls; skipped, they cannot be fried crisp."},"sub":{"2":{"x":"Neutral oil, enough for deep frying (about 2 cups)","d":"No difference; use canola, sunflower or peanut oil.","k":["canola oil","sunflower oil","peanut oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Naem is fermented raw pork, which can carry parasites and bacteria; cook it to 160F (71C), or use a commercial, fully cured product, and avoid raw naem for pregnant women, children or anyone vulnerable.","s":9},{"t":"The crisp fried rice balls are the dish: fry until deep amber and crunchy, and toss them in right before serving or they soften within 15 to 20 minutes.","s":6},{"t":"Soak the sliced onion and shallot in ice water to remove sharpness and crisp them, then drain very well.","s":0}],"rl":"BFFPXAZWXXXXXXGGG"},"53426":{"lv":"EREE","w":{"1":"Adds flavor and color to the ferment; skip with little change."},"tips":[{"t":"Use non-iodized salt; iodine and anti-caking agents can cloud the brine and slow fermentation. Wash the cabbage well, as leftover grit spoils the brine.","s":2},{"t":"The steps stop short: keep the cabbage submerged under the brine with a weight, leave it covered at room temperature 1 to 2 days until pleasantly sour, then refrigerate.","s":5},{"t":"Taste after a day; if it smells off or grows fuzzy mold, discard it rather than skimming.","s":5}],"rl":"VVMK"},"53424":{"lv":"EER","w":{"2":"The ripe plantains are already sweet; sugar deepens and caramelizes the top."},"tips":[{"t":"Use very ripe plantains with mostly black skins; green or yellow ones are starchy and not sweet and bake up dry.","s":1},{"t":"Grease the muffin cups or use sturdy liners; without flour they are custardy and cling to paper.","s":3},{"t":"Bake until set and golden, then cool in the pan for 15 minutes; the muffins firm up as they cool.","s":5}],"rl":"FVW"},"53425":{"lv":"EEEERERR","w":{"6":"Duplicate of the 4 cups boiling water in line 1; the method uses 4 cups total, so don't add it twice.","7":"A pinch sharpens the coconut and banana sweetness; skipping leaves the pudding flatter but still fine.","4":"Sweetens the pudding; skipped, it is milder, though bananas add some sweetness."},"sub":{"4":{"x":"2 tbsp brown sugar or palm sugar","d":"Adds a faint caramel note and slightly darker color.","k":["brown sugar"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Use small-pearl tapioca for this timing; large pearls need an overnight soak or 10+ extra minutes. They are done when fully translucent with no white dot in the center.","s":0},{"t":"Rinse the soaked pearls in cold water until the slimy starch runs off, and stir right after adding them to the boiling pot so they do not weld into one clump.","s":3},{"t":"Add the coconut milk off a hard boil and only warm it gently; a rolling boil can split it and leave oily streaks. Choose firm, just-ripe bananas so the slices hold their shape.","s":5}],"rl":"MMBFWVMZ"},"3031":{"lv":"EEREEEEE","w":{"2":"Only 1 tsp: gives the yeast a quick meal and a bit of browning; you can skip it if you give the dough a slightly longer rise.","5":"Fat in the dough or batter; skipped, the result is dry, tough or less tender."},"sub":{"5":{"x":"2 tbsp vegetable oil","d":"Dough is just as soft but loses a little olive fruitiness.","k":["vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Keep the water around 105-110F (40-43C). Hot water kills the yeast, and if the mixture is not foamy after 5 minutes, start over rather than hoping for a rise.","s":0},{"t":"Bake on a sheet that has been preheating in the oven. The blast of bottom heat gives the base a blistered, chewy crust instead of a pale, doughy one.","s":3},{"t":"Make the za'atar-oil mix a loose paste, not a soup, and leave a half-inch rim. Too much oil runs off and fries the edges; if your za'atar blend is already salty, add no extra salt to the topping.","s":5}],"rl":"SLWZMFXF"},"3030":{"lv":"EEEEEREOR","w":{"5":"Seasons the rice and lentils through the cooking water; without it the dish tastes flat even with sweet onions.","7":"A small grind adds background heat; skippable since cumin and onions carry the dish.","8":"Cool, tangy contrast to the rich onions; the dish is fine without it, but it is the classic partner."},"tips":[{"t":"Slice the onions uniformly and fry them slowly, undisturbed for the first 10 minutes, then stir often. They should be deep brown and crisp at the edges, not black; scorched onions turn the whole pot bitter.","s":1},{"t":"Par-cook the lentils first as written so they finish at the same time as the rice. Measure the liquid carefully: too much turns it to porridge, and you can rescue a wet pot by cooking it uncovered for 3-4 minutes.","s":3},{"t":"Rest the covered pot with a clean towel under the lid; it absorbs steam so the grains stay separate, and you can fluff without breaking them.","s":5}],"rl":"PBVFXZMZG"},"3032":{"lv":"EEEERRRREO","w":{"4":"A small amount adds the sweet-warm note typical of Lebanese 7-spice; skipping makes the kibbeh taste more plainly of allspice.","5":"Gives the filling its classic crunch and richness; walnuts or slivered almonds work, or omit for a softer filling.","7":"Poured over the scored top it crisps the crust and keeps the kibbeh from drying out; olive oil can replace it.","9":"Minor background heat; allspice and cinnamon already carry the seasoning.","6":"Greases and enriches the bake; skipped, the lamb and spices still carry it."},"sub":{"5":{"x":"1/2 cup chopped toasted walnuts or slivered almonds","d":"Slightly less buttery than pine nuts but same crunch.","k":["walnut"],"wn":"Contains tree nuts (walnuts or almonds)"},"6":{"x":"3 tbsp vegetable oil","d":"Neutral flavor; the filling relies on the lamb and spices anyway.","k":["vegetable oil"]},"7":{"x":"1/4 cup ghee or 1/4 cup olive oil","d":"Ghee is the traditional samna and gives a nuttier crust; oil is less rich.","k":["ghee","olive oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Squeeze the soaked bulgur bone dry in a towel, and keep the lamb ice cold while processing, adding a few ice chips if the paste warms up. A wet or warm paste turns gummy and crumbles when sliced.","s":1},{"t":"Dip your hands in cold water to press the bottom layer in and smooth the top; a thin even shell bakes through and does not crack.","s":3},{"t":"Cut the diamonds all the way to the bottom before baking and pour the butter into the cuts so it seeps down. Ground lamb should reach 160F (71C) in the center before you pull it.","s":5}],"rl":"BPXXXGFFZZ"},"3028":{"lv":"ERERREEROO","w":{"1":"A small amount of mint gives the cool top note typical of the Lebanese version; without it the salad is greener and sharper.","3":"Adds mild sharpness; a little finely diced white or red onion can take its place.","4":"Traditional but minor in Lebanese tabbouleh, which is mostly parsley; skipping makes it lighter and gluten-free.","7":"Brings out the lemon and tomato sweetness; add it at the last minute so the tomatoes don't weep.","8":"Optional background heat; the lemon and olive oil already drive the flavor.","9":"Just a scoop-and-eat vessel; skip it or serve with a spoon."},"tips":[{"t":"Dry the washed parsley completely (salad spinner, then towels) and chop it with a very sharp knife. A dull knife or a food processor bruises the leaves into a wet green paste.","s":1},{"t":"Seed the tomatoes and dice them small, and dress the salad only right before serving. Salt pulls water from the tomatoes and parsley, and a pool at the bottom of the bowl means it sat too long.","s":3},{"t":"Soaking the bulgur in the lemon juice rather than water means it absorbs flavor; do not drain it away, since the leftover lemon is what dresses the salad.","s":0}],"rl":"VXVXBAFZZG"},"3029":{"lv":"EERERRRREEERRR","w":{"2":"Adds cool crunch and water; skip if you have none, but increase tomato or lettuce.","4":"Peppery crunch that cuts the dressing; omit and the salad is slightly sweeter and milder.","5":"Mild onion bite; replace with a bit of thinly sliced red onion.","6":"Fresh herbal backbone; skipping makes it a plain chopped salad, so use at least some herbs.","7":"Cool counterpoint to the sumac; if missing, add extra parsley.","11":"Adds fruity tang and a touch of sweetness to the dressing; the sumac and lemon still work without it.","12":"Gives the dressing its sharp bite; skipping gives a milder, cleaner dressing.","13":"Lifts the dressing; add to taste, but note that the pita chips are salted too."},"sub":{"11":{"x":"2 tsp lemon juice + 1 tsp honey","d":"Less fruity and deep than pomegranate molasses but gives the sweet-tart balance.","k":["lemon juice","honey"],"wn":"Contains honey; do not serve to infants under 12 months"}},"tips":[{"t":"Bake the pita until fully dried and snappy, then cool it completely before it touches any dressing. Pita that is still slightly soft turns to paste within a minute of tossing.","s":1},{"t":"Let the sumac sit in the lemon juice and oil for a few minutes so it hydrates and its tartness spreads through the dressing instead of sitting in gritty specks.","s":2},{"t":"Dry the romaine well and dress the salad only when it is going to the table; add the pita chips last. Some cooks hold back a handful of chips to crumble over the top.","s":5}],"rl":"BVVVVXXXFAAAXZ"},"3027":{"lv":"EERRREREOR","w":{"2":"Acid that brightens and helps tenderize; the yogurt is already acidic, so don't cut it below about 2 tbsp.","3":"Keeps lean breast meat from drying out and helps browning on the grill.","4":"Adds color, umami and a little sweetness; without it the chicken is paler and less savory.","6":"Gives the characteristic red-gold color and mild pepper flavor; skipping leaves it paler.","8":"Minor; garlic, paprika and cumin already season the marinade.","9":"Earthy warmth that rounds out the garlic and lemon; skipping leaves it brighter and simpler."},"sub":{"2":{"x":"3 tbsp lime juice","d":"Slightly more floral than lemon; nearly identical in effect.","k":["lime juice"]},"3":{"x":"3 tbsp vegetable oil","d":"Neutral; loses a little fruitiness but protects the meat the same way.","k":["vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Marinate between 2 hours and 24 hours. Past a day the yogurt and lemon acid make chicken breast mushy and chalky instead of tender.","s":1},{"t":"Wipe off the thickest clumps of marinade before grilling; the yogurt and tomato paste scorch quickly over high heat, so you want char, not black crust.","s":3},{"t":"Cut the cubes evenly and pull the skewers when the thickest cube reads 165F (74C). Pulling a few minutes earlier risks undercooked chicken, and a long grill after that dries it out.","s":4}],"rl":"PMAFXXXZZX"},"3040":{"lv":"EEEEE","tips":[{"t":"Cut each garlic clove in half and pull out any green germ in the center. The germ is what makes toum harsh and bitter, especially when the sauce is kept for days.","s":0},{"t":"Go very slowly at first: the first few tablespoons of oil should go in by the drop. If the sauce breaks and turns soupy, restart with one fresh garlic clove and a spoonful of the broken sauce, then drizzle the rest back in slowly.","s":1},{"t":"Use a neutral oil, not extra virgin; olive oil turns bitter when whipped at speed. Toum tastes harsh on day one and mellows after a day in the fridge.","s":5}],"rl":"XZFAM"},"3041":{"lv":"EEERERRR","w":{"1":"Used for frying the potatoes; vegetable oil works but olive oil adds a nicer flavor.","3":"Fresh herbal finish that lifts the fried potatoes; parsley is a fair stand-in.","5":"Warm citrusy background note that pairs with the cilantro; chili and garlic still carry the dish.","6":"Bright acid cuts through the oil; skip and the potatoes taste heavier.","7":"Season to taste at the end; the salted boil only seasons the potatoes lightly."},"sub":{"1":{"x":"1/4 cup vegetable oil","d":"Neutral; less fruity but crisps the potatoes the same.","k":["vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"After draining, let the boiled potatoes steam dry for a few minutes and shake the colander to scuff the edges. A dry, roughed-up surface fries into a much crunchier crust.","s":0},{"t":"Cook the potatoes in one layer and leave them alone for the first 5 minutes before turning. Crowding or stirring early steams them instead of crisping them.","s":2},{"t":"Garlic goes in only for the last minute; browned garlic turns bitter. If the pan is too hot, pull it off the burner as you add the garlic and chili.","s":3}],"rl":"BFXGXXAZ"},"3042":{"lv":"EEREEEERERRO","w":{"2":"Feeds the yeast and gives light browning; fine to skip, the dough just rises slightly slower.","7":"Adds sweetness and bite to the filling; skipping leaves it more plainly lemony.","9":"The tangy red note in the filling; add extra lemon juice or a pinch of zest if you don't have it.","11":"Optional; the lemon and sumac already provide sharpness.","5":"Fat in the dough or batter; skipped, the result is dry, tough or less tender.","10":"Binds and enriches the filling; skipped, it is drier but holds."},"sub":{"5":{"x":"1/4 cup vegetable oil","d":"Dough is just as soft but a bit less aromatic.","k":["vegetable oil"]},"10":{"x":"3 tbsp vegetable oil","d":"Binds the filling the same; loses a little olive flavor.","k":["vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Wring the salted spinach in a clean towel until almost no liquid comes out. Any remaining water makes the dough soggy from the inside and bursts the seams in the oven.","s":1},{"t":"Mix the lemon juice into the filling only just before shaping; acid left on spinach turns it dull olive and drab.","s":2},{"t":"Brush the edge of each round with water and pinch the three corners hard together, sealing the seams fully. A tiny gap lets the filling leak and the triangle opens up.","s":4}],"rl":"SLWZMFVXAAFZ"},"3484":{"lv":"EEERRE","w":{"3":"Garlic gives the salad its signature punch; skip for a milder cabbage slaw.","4":"Typical Lebanese flavor with the lemon; skip or use fresh mint for a more herbal result."},"sub":{"4":{"x":"3 tbsp finely chopped fresh mint","d":"Fresher and greener; use triple the amount since dried mint is more concentrated.","k":["mint"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Shred the cabbage as thin as you can, with a mandoline or a very sharp knife. Thin shreds soften in the salt and lemon; thick pieces stay tough and squeaky.","s":0},{"t":"The salt and massaging break down the cell walls so the cabbage turns silky but stays crunchy. If it turns watery during the rest, drain off the liquid before dressing.","s":1},{"t":"Dress and let it rest, but serve within about an hour; the lemon keeps working and the cabbage softens further. Taste again after resting, since the salt mellows.","s":4}],"rl":"VAFXXZ"},"3485":{"lv":"ERRROO","w":{"1":"Seasons the labneh and helps draw out whey; the labneh will taste flat without it.","2":"The richness on top; labneh is plain and tangy without it, so use a good oil.","3":"Herbal, nutty, sour topping that makes the dish; can be replaced with dried thyme and sesame.","4":"Salty garnish; skip if you don't like olives.","5":"A fresh garnish; skip with no loss."},"tips":[{"t":"Use full-fat plain yogurt without gelatin or starch thickeners; stabilized yogurts refuse to drain and stay loose.","s":0},{"t":"Strain 8 hours for a thick, spreadable labneh, or 24 hours for one you can roll into balls. Save the whey for bread dough or smoothies.","s":2},{"t":"Serve it at cool room temperature. Straight from the fridge it is dull and stiff; a little warmth lets the oil and za'atar bloom.","s":4}],"rl":"BZFGGG"},"3487":{"lv":"EEEEORR","w":{"4":"Minor heat; skip freely since za'atar and garlic do the seasoning.","5":"Added late so it stays pungent; skipping gives a plainer potato.","6":"Bright acid at the end wakes up the roasted flavor; skip and the potatoes taste a little heavy.","1":"Only cooking fat; skipped, food sticks and browns poorly, so use an oil or substitute."},"sub":{"1":{"x":"3 tbsp vegetable oil","d":"Crisps the same; loses some olive flavor against the za'atar.","k":["vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Put the empty baking sheet in the oven while it preheats and lay the potatoes cut side down on the hot metal. That gives you a golden, crunchy cut face instead of a pale one.","s":2},{"t":"Za'atar can scorch and turn bitter after 40 minutes. If yours is very fine, toss half on at the 20-minute flip instead of at the start.","s":1},{"t":"Garlic goes in only for the last 5 minutes, otherwise it burns; squeeze the lemon on while the potatoes are still very hot so it soaks in.","s":4}],"rl":"BFXZZXA"},"53050":{"lv":"EEEERERRRRERE","w":{"4":"Adds heat to the spice paste; scale it to taste and skip for a mild version.","6":"Earthy warmth in the paste; skipping makes the sauce less rounded.","7":"Citrusy base note; skipping is noticeable but not fatal.","8":"Sweet licorice note typical of percik; skip and the marinade is a little less aromatic.","11":"Balances the tamarind sour and the chili heat; a little less is fine.","9":"Gives sourness to the marinade; skipped, it is less tangy."},"sub":{"9":{"x":"2 tbsp lime juice + 1 tsp brown sugar","d":"Brighter and less fruity than tamarind; sweeten slightly to compensate.","k":["lime juice","brown sugar"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Fry the paste until the oil visibly separates and the raw edge of the shallots and chili is gone. Under-fried paste tastes sharp and raw in the finished sauce.","s":1},{"t":"Chill the sauce completely before pouring it over raw chicken; hot sauce partly cooks the surface and warms the meat into the bacterial danger zone. The overnight rest is what flavors the thighs through.","s":3},{"t":"Bake until the thickest thigh reaches at least 165F (74C); bone-in thighs are better around 175F (80C) for tender meat. Baste with the reserved sauce for the glossy finish.","s":5}],"rl":"PXXXXXXXXAFWM"},"53053":{"lv":"ERRRRREERRRE","w":{"2":"Sweet-warm background in the spice paste; skipping is subtle.","3":"Floral and pungent; a pinch of ground cloves can replace the whole ones.","4":"Aromatic note typical of rendang; skip if you do not have it.","5":"Adds citrusy-floral depth; skip with only a slight loss.","9":"Likely kaffir lime leaves in the source; adds citrus perfume. Skip, or use a strip of lime zest, but don't add 6 whole limes.","10":"Balances the tamarind and chili; a bit less is fine, taste at the end.","1":"Fries the paste, but coconut milk also renders fat; skipped, rendang still works.","8":"Adds sour fruity balance; skipped, the rendang is richer and less balanced."},"sub":{"1":{"x":"5 tbsp coconut oil or neutral cooking oil","d":"Coconut oil adds a little extra coconut flavor; neutral oil is just as good for frying.","k":["coconut oil"]},"8":{"x":"2 tbsp lime juice","d":"Sharper, less fruity-sweet than tamarind; add at the end so cooking doesn't dull it.","k":["lime juice"]}},"tips":[{"t":"This is a long reduction: keep going until the coconut sauce splits and the beef fries in its own oil, turning deep brown. Stir more often near the end so the thick paste doesn't scorch.","s":2},{"t":"Use chuck or shin with some connective tissue; lean cuts go dry and stringy over this long cook, while collagen melts into the gravy.","s":-1}],"rl":"PFXXXXFMAXWX"},"52821":{"lv":"EREREREREO","w":{"0":"Just a base for frying the chili and paste; any cooking oil works.","1":"Adds fresh heat on top of the curry paste; skip if the paste is hot enough for you.","3":"Provides the salted broth base; plain stock or broth works.","5":"Provides salty, savory depth; soy sauce gives a similar saltiness.","7":"Fresh acid at the end brightens the coconut broth; skipping leaves it heavy.","9":"Garnish only; adds freshness but nothing else."},"sub":{"0":{"x":"1 tbsp vegetable oil","d":"No noticeable difference.","k":["vegetable oil"]},"3":{"x":"700 ml chicken or vegetable broth (instead of cube + water)","d":"Often milder; add a pinch more salt or fish sauce.","k":["chicken broth"]},"5":{"x":"2 tsp soy sauce","d":"Less funky and a bit darker; no seafood note.","k":["soy sauce"],"wn":"Contains soy and wheat; removes the fish allergen"}},"tips":[{"t":"Fry the curry paste in the oil for the full minute until it smells toasted and looks slightly darker; raw paste tastes harsh and flat in the broth.","s":0},{"t":"Keep the broth at a gentle simmer once the coconut milk is in, since a hard boil can curdle it. Add the lime juice only at the end so it stays bright.","s":1},{"t":"Shrimp need only 2-3 minutes; pull them as soon as they turn pink and curl loosely, because tight C-shaped shrimp are overcooked and rubbery.","s":1}],"rl":"FXXMFZBAPG"},"53048":{"lv":"REREERREERRREER","w":{"0":"Adds nutty body and richness to the spice paste; skipping makes it thinner but still savory.","2":"Dried shrimp gives the paste umami depth; a splash of fish sauce can step in.","5":"Adds the sweet-sour note that balances the chili; skipping is noticeable but not fatal.","6":"Traditional mamak ingredient that adds heartiness; skip if you want a lighter dish.","9":"A secondary vegetable adding crunch; any similar greens can replace it.","10":"Leafy crunch; bok choy or spinach can replace it.","11":"Adds protein and chew; skip and the dish is vegetarian-light but still fine.","14":"Adds richness and a velvety coating when stirred in; can be skipped.","3":"Only cooking fat; skipped, food sticks and browns poorly, so use an oil or substitute."},"sub":{"3":{"x":"5 tbsp peanut oil or canola oil","d":"No flavor change; peanut oil gives a faintly nutty note.","k":["peanut oil"]},"5":{"x":"1/2 tbsp lime juice + 1/4 tsp brown sugar","d":"Sharper and lacking the fruity depth of tamarind.","k":["lime juice","brown sugar"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Keep sauteing the blended paste on low heat until the oil separates and the paste darkens. Pulling it early leaves a raw, sharp chili taste.","s":1},{"t":"Cook one portion at a time in a very hot wok; crowding the pan steams the noodles instead of giving them that smoky, slightly charred wok flavor.","s":4},{"t":"Boil the potatoes until just tender and let them steam-dry before stir-frying; wet or overcooked chunks fall apart and make the noodles gluey.","s":2}],"rl":"XXXFMABXXVVPZBP"},"53051":{"lv":"EEOROERRRREEREERRR","w":{"2":"A tiny extra pinch of ginger over the fresh root; skipping changes nothing noticeable.","3":"Fresh ginger perfumes the coconut rice; skipping leaves it creamier but plainer.","4":"A faint aroma standing in for the traditional pandan leaf; a knotted pandan leaf would be better, or leave it out.","6":"Traditional topping adding protein; its mildness balances the sambal.","7":"Cool, crisp contrast to the spicy sambal; skip if you have none.","8":"Fried peanuts add crunch and nutty richness; skipping makes the plate softer.","9":"Crispy fried anchovies add salty crunch as a topping; skipping loses some of the dish's saltiness.","12":"Builds the savory base of the sambal; the onion and shallots cover most of it.","15":"Second anchovy line: one portion is fried crisp for the topping, this one goes into the sambal for umami.","16":"Balances the tamarind sour and chili heat in the sambal; use a little less for a sharper sambal.","10":"Only cooking fat; skipped, food sticks and browns poorly, so use an oil or substitute.","17":"Gives sambal its sour note; skipped, the sambal is less balanced but still spicy."},"sub":{"10":{"x":"2 tbsp coconut oil or any neutral oil","d":"Coconut oil adds a faint coconut note; neutral oil is plain.","k":["coconut oil"]},"17":{"x":"3 tbsp lime juice + 1 tbsp extra brown sugar","d":"Brighter, less fruity sourness; the sambal loses a little depth.","k":["lime juice","brown sugar"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Cook the coconut rice covered over low heat and don't lift the lid. Coconut milk scorches on the bottom easily, so stay on the lowest flame and rest the rice off the heat for 10 minutes.","s":0},{"t":"Start the eggs in cold water, bring to a boil, then stand them covered off the heat as the recipe says; the gentle residual heat keeps the yolks creamy, not chalky green. Cool them in cold water so they peel cleanly.","s":1},{"t":"Cook the sambal down until the oil visibly separates and the paste darkens; a short cook leaves a raw chili taste. Fry the peanuts and anchovies at medium heat and pull them early, since they keep crisping as they cool.","s":3}],"rl":"FMXXXBPVGGFXXXXXWA"},"53052":{"lv":"EREREROO","w":{"1":"Adds moisture and sweetness to the egg and beef filling; skipping makes it a bit drier and blander.","3":"Gives the egg mix its heat and color; skip for a mild sandwich or use sambal on top.","5":"Seasons the egg and beef mix; a pinch is needed since beef and egg alone are bland.","6":"Minor; the chili and onion already season it.","7":"A finishing sauce for richness and tang; skip it or use sambal or ketchup instead."},"tips":[{"t":"Chop the onion and chili very fine and keep the beef crumbled small. Large pieces stop the egg from setting evenly and the omelet falls apart when you flip it.","s":0},{"t":"The beef is raw in this mixture, so spread it thin and cook until there is no pink and it reaches 160F (71C). Press the bread down firmly so the egg bonds to it.","s":3},{"t":"A day-old baguette split lengthwise soaks up the egg without turning soggy; fresh soft bread goes limp. Flip it only after the underside is deep golden.","s":2}],"rl":"PXTXBZZG"},"53049":{"lv":"EEEEERRER","w":{"5":"Seasons the batter and balances the sweet filling; skipping gives a flatter taste.","6":"Melts into the hot pancake and adds richness and flavor; skipping makes the filling drier.","8":"A stand-in for the crushed peanuts the method calls for; adds the nutty, savory contrast to the sugar.","3":"Listed as 1600 g, which is a typo; about 160 g (1 1/4 cups) gives a pourable batter with 200 ml milk and 2 eggs.","1":"Fat in the dough or batter; skipped, the result is dry, tough or less tender."},"sub":{"1":{"x":"1/4 cup melted butter (cooled)","d":"Richer, slightly more tender and golden; flavor is buttery.","k":["butter"]}},"tips":[{"t":"The flour amount is surely a typo: 1600 g would be a solid dough. With 200 ml milk and 2 eggs, use about 160 g (1 1/4 cups) of flour for a pourable pancake batter.","s":0},{"t":"Cook over medium-low heat with a lid on. The steam sets the top without flipping, and you are done when the surface is covered with small bubbles that pop and leave holes.","s":1},{"t":"Add the butter, sugar and peanuts while the pancake is hot so the butter melts into the filling. Fold when the edge is crisp and golden, and eat it warm; it goes chewy as it cools.","s":2}],"rl":"MFTSLZFWG"},"53054":{"lv":"EEEREEEEE","w":{"3":"Seasons the rice layer so the sweet custard has a savory counterpoint; skipping makes it taste flat.","0":"Must be glutinous (sticky) rice; ordinary rice will not hold together into a firm, chewy base."},"tips":[{"t":"The rice must be glutinous (sticky) rice; ordinary rice will not hold together into a firm, chewy base. The pandan in the method is also crucial: the knots in the rice and the juice in the custard give the dish its flavor and green color, so add them.","s":0},{"t":"Press the steamed rice down firmly with a greased spatula, leaving no gaps. Loose rice lets the custard seep through and the layers won't cut cleanly.","s":4},{"t":"Stir the custard constantly over simmering (not boiling) water until it thickens to a pudding, then strain it. Overheating scrambles the eggs; cool the finished cake fully, then cut with an oiled knife.","s":9}],"rl":"BFMZTSSFW"},"3626":{"lv":"EEEEE","sub":{"3":{"x":"1/3 cup vegetable shortening, or 1/4 cup vegetable oil","d":"Shortening is the closest; oil gives a slightly less tender, less flaky tortilla.","k":["shortening","vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"The ingredient list omits the water; use about 3/4 to 1 cup warm water, added gradually until the dough is soft and not sticky. Warm water makes the lard blend easier and the dough more tender.","s":2},{"t":"Do not skip the rest; it lets the gluten relax so the dough rolls thin without springing back. Roll from the center out, rotating a quarter turn, for a round tortilla.","s":3},{"t":"Cook on a hot, dry skillet only until puffed with light brown spots, then stack them wrapped in a towel. Overcooked tortillas dry out and turn crackery.","s":5}],"rl":"SLZFM","w":{"4":"Start with 3/4 cup warm water and add up to 1 cup, a little at a time, until the dough is soft but not sticky.","3":"Fat for tender, pliable tortilla dough; skipped, tortillas are dry and stiff."}},"127":{"lv":"EEEEO","w":{"4":"A dipping garnish; the quesadilla is fine without it.","3":"Only cooking fat; skipped, food sticks and browns poorly, so use an oil or substitute."},"sub":{"3":{"x":"1 tbsp vegetable oil","d":"Crisps just as well; less buttery flavor.","k":["vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Take the eggs off the heat while still soft and glossy; they finish cooking inside the hot tortilla. Fully set scrambled eggs turn dry and rubbery once pressed.","s":0},{"t":"Cook over medium heat and let the cheese melt before flipping. High heat burns the tortilla before the cheese melts and glues the folds.","s":2},{"t":"Scatter some cheese under and over the eggs so it acts as a seal between the egg and the tortilla; the filling stays inside when you cut.","s":1}],"rl":"PBFFG"},"3052":{"lv":"ERRERERRROO","w":{"1":"Softened onion adds sweetness to the sauce; skipping makes it taste more raw.","2":"Mellow garlic depth in the sauce; skipping is fine if your salsa is already garlicky.","4":"Thins the salsa into a sauce that coats the chips; water works in a pinch.","6":"A runny yolk enriches the sauce and makes it a meal; skip for a vegetarian side.","7":"Salty, creamy finish; feta or any crumbly cheese works.","8":"Cool tang against the red sauce; thinned yogurt or crema works.","9":"Garnish for freshness.","10":"Garnish for sharp crunch.","0":"Only cooking fat; skipped, food sticks and browns poorly, so use an oil or substitute."},"sub":{"0":{"x":"1 tbsp olive oil","d":"Slightly fruitier; fine here.","k":["olive oil"]},"4":{"x":"1/2 cup water + a pinch of salt","d":"Lighter flavor; add a bouillon cube if you have one.","k":["water"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Simmer the salsa until it clings to the back of a spoon before adding the chips; a thin sauce makes them mushy in seconds. Sturdy, thick-cut chips hold up best.","s":1},{"t":"Toss the chips for 1 to 2 minutes only and plate immediately. If you prefer the traditional soft style, simmer them in the sauce for 3 to 4 minutes.","s":2},{"t":"Fry the eggs sunny side up in butter or oil over medium-low heat so the whites set but the yolk stays runny; the yolk becomes a second sauce over the chips.","s":4}],"rl":"FXXMMBPGGGG"},"3066":{"lv":"ERRREREERROR","w":{"1":"Adds sweetness and body to the sauce; skipping makes it thinner and more acidic.","2":"Gives the sauce its fresh green heat; skip for a milder sauce.","3":"Mellow savoriness in the ranchero sauce; skipping is only slightly noticeable.","5":"A small earthy note; the fire-roasted tomatoes already carry flavor.","8":"The creamy, earthy base that anchors the eggs; skip if needed, or use black beans.","9":"Salty, crumbly finish; feta works.","10":"Garnish for freshness.","11":"Needed to bring out the tomatoes; taste after blending.","0":"Only cooking fat; skipped, food sticks and browns poorly, so use an oil or substitute."},"sub":{"0":{"x":"1 tbsp olive oil","d":"Slightly fruitier but fine here.","k":["olive oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Blend the sauce only partway so it stays a little rustic, and simmer until it thickens. A thin sauce pools on the plate and makes the tortillas soggy.","s":1},{"t":"Fry the tortillas until crisp at the edges but still flexible; this barrier keeps the beans and sauce from soaking in.","s":2},{"t":"Cook the eggs over low heat, covering the pan for the last minute so the whites set fully while the yolks stay soft. Anyone avoiding runny yolks should cook them through.","s":3}],"rl":"FXXXVXBPBGGZ"},"32":{"lv":"ERRREEEE","w":{"1":"Seasons the filling; fresh minced garlic can replace it.","2":"Adds mild sweetness to the filling; skipping is barely noticeable.","3":"Warm, earthy note that points the dish toward Tex-Mex; skipping leaves it creamier and plainer."},"tips":[{"t":"Stir the sour cream into the enchilada sauce cold and bake gently; boiling or overheating sour cream can make the sauce break into grainy streaks. Use full-fat sour cream for the best stability.","s":2},{"t":"Warm the tortillas for 15 to 20 seconds in the microwave before rolling so they bend without cracking, and pack them seam side down in the dish.","s":1},{"t":"The chicken is already cooked, so you are just heating to 165F (74C) and melting the cheese. Pull it once the sauce bubbles at the edges and the cheese is spotty brown, before it dries out.","s":3}],"rl":"PXXXFFBM"},"3043":{"lv":"EERRRERRRREEROOOR","w":{"2":"Adds sweetness and acidity that tame the chile bitterness; skipping makes the sauce darker and harsher.","3":"Sweet, savory depth in the sauce; skipping thins the flavor slightly.","4":"Mellow garlic backbone for the braise; skipping is noticeable but not fatal.","7":"Earthy note; the chiles carry most of the spice, so skipping is a small loss.","8":"Herbal note typical of birria; Mexican oregano is best.","9":"Minor aroma in the long braise.","12":"Melts into the crisped taco for the popular quesabirria style; skip for classic tacos.","13":"Raw crunch to cut through the rich meat.","14":"Fresh garnish; skipping is fine.","15":"Squeeze at the table for brightness.","16":"Season the beef generously; the braise needs salt to taste right.","6":"Adds brightness; skipped, the dish is flatter but still works.","10":"Liquid the dish simmers or cooks in; skipped, there is not enough liquid."},"sub":{"6":{"x":"2 tbsp white vinegar","d":"Slightly sharper and less fruity; same tenderizing tang.","k":["white vinegar"]},"10":{"x":"4 cups chicken broth","d":"Lighter flavor; the chuck builds the beefy richness. Taste for salt.","k":["chicken broth"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Toast the dried chiles only 30 seconds per side until fragrant and just pliable. Even slightly scorched chiles make the whole sauce bitter; if you smell acrid smoke, start over with fresh ones.","s":0},{"t":"Strain the blended sauce through a fine sieve, pressing on the solids. This gives the smooth, brick-red consomme that birria is known for.","s":1},{"t":"Do not discard the red fat you skim. Dipping each tortilla in that fat before frying is what gives birria tacos their color and crisp, flavorful shell.","s":5}],"rl":"XXVXXPAXXXMBFGGGZ"},"3044":{"lv":"EERRRRRRROEOR","w":{"2":"Sweetness and mild acidity balance the sharp lime; skipping makes the marinade tarter.","3":"Pungent backbone; skipping loses depth.","5":"Fresh herb note in the marinade; skipping is a small loss.","6":"Gentle heat and green flavor; skip for milder steak.","7":"Earthy depth that marries with the chili powder; skipping is subtle.","8":"Adds color, mild heat and smokiness; skipping makes it milder.","9":"Minor herbal note.","11":"Minor; the salt, cumin and chili already season the meat.","12":"The traditional carrier; the steak also works over rice or in a salad.","4":"Carries flavor in the marinade or dressing; skipped, the dish is drier but intact."},"sub":{"4":{"x":"1/4 cup vegetable oil","d":"Neutral; loses a little fruitiness, same job.","k":["vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Do not marinate longer than 8 hours: the lime and orange acid begins to cook the surface of the flank steak and turns it mealy instead of tender.","s":1},{"t":"Pat off the excess marinade before it hits the grate. Wet meat steams and the sugars in the orange juice burn; a dry surface sears and chars properly. Pull at 130F for medium-rare, though USDA guidance is 145F (63C) with a 3-minute rest if you prefer to follow it.","s":3},{"t":"Find the long grain lines running along the flank steak and slice thinly across them at a slight angle. Cutting with the grain leaves the meat chewy even when cooked perfectly.","s":4}],"rl":"PAAXFXXXXXZZB"},"3045":{"lv":"ERRRRERREEEERRO","w":{"1":"Adds smoke and heat; skip for a milder pastor or use a pinch of smoked paprika.","4":"Pungent backbone in the marinade; skipping is noticeable.","6":"Earthy depth in the marinade; skip with minor loss.","7":"Herbal note; Mexican oregano is the usual choice.","12":"Raw sharpness cuts through the rich pork.","13":"Fresh finishing herb; skipping is a small loss.","14":"Acid squeeze at the table.","2":"Marinade juice with tenderizing enzyme; skipped, the pork is less tangy and tender.","3":"Adds brightness; skipped, the dish is flatter but still works."},"sub":{"2":{"x":"1/2 cup orange juice + 1 tbsp lime juice","d":"Lacks pineapple's tenderizing enzyme and tropical flavor; the seared pineapple helps compensate.","k":["orange juice","lime juice"]},"3":{"x":"1/4 cup apple cider vinegar","d":"A bit fruitier; fine in the marinade.","k":["apple cider vinegar"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Partly freeze the pork shoulder for 20 minutes so you can slice it thin (about 1/4 inch). Thin slices pick up marinade fast and char at the edges the way spit-roasted pastor does.","s":2},{"t":"Do not marinate longer than overnight: the pineapple juice enzyme (bromelain) breaks proteins down and makes the pork mushy. Cook the slices to at least 145F (63C), though they will usually be well beyond that after charring.","s":2},{"t":"Get the cast iron very hot and sear in batches; crowding steams the meat. Cook the pineapple in the same pan afterwards so it picks up the pork drippings.","s":3}],"rl":"XXAAXXXXZPVBGGG"},"3047":{"lv":"EERREEROEREO","w":{"2":"Half poaches with the chicken for flavor and half is sauteed for sweetness in the sauce; skipping thins the flavor.","3":"Savory backbone in the sauce and poaching broth.","6":"Herbal note that cuts the smoke; Mexican oregano is best.","7":"Small earthy note; chipotle and tomato cover most of it.","9":"Season at the end, after the sauce reduces; the chicken and sauce need it.","11":"Cool tangy topping; skip with only a small loss.","1":"Liquid the dish simmers or cooks in; skipped, there is not enough liquid.","8":"Only cooking fat; skipped, food sticks and browns poorly, so use an oil or substitute."},"sub":{"1":{"x":"4 cups water + 1 tsp salt","d":"Lighter flavor; add a bouillon cube if you have one.","k":["water"]},"8":{"x":"1 tbsp olive oil","d":"Slightly fruitier; works fine.","k":["olive oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Poach the chicken at a bare simmer, not a boil, to 165F (74C). Boiling hard toughens the breast, and the poaching liquid is reused for the sauce.","s":0},{"t":"Start with 1 tablespoon of chipotle and adjust: the adobo sauce packs more heat than you expect. Blend the sauce completely smooth, since a chunky tinga sauce tastes raw.","s":2},{"t":"Simmer the sauce until it clings to a spoon before adding the chicken; a watery tinga soaks the tortillas. For crisp tostadas, shred the chicken coarsely.","s":3}],"rl":"PMXXVXXXFZBG"},"3050":{"lv":"EEEEEERROR","w":{"6":"Sweet savory base for the tomato sauce; skipping makes it thinner tasting.","7":"Savory depth in the sauce; skip with a small loss.","8":"Small earthy note; skipping is hardly noticeable.","9":"Needed in both the sauce and the batter to bring out the cheese and pepper flavor.","4":"Frying medium; skipped, the food cannot be fried."},"sub":{"4":{"x":"1 cup canola or sunflower oil","d":"Neutral and high smoke point, same frying result.","k":["canola oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Steam the blistered poblanos in a covered bowl so the skins slip off; keep the stem on and cut a short slit so they hold the cheese. Pat the peppers completely dry, then dust them in flour or the batter will slide off.","s":0},{"t":"Whip the whites to stiff peaks just before frying and fold the yolks in gently; a deflated batter fries up flat and greasy. Work quickly, since the foam breaks down as it stands.","s":3},{"t":"Hold the oil at 350F (175C). Cooler oil makes the batter soak up grease; hotter oil browns the coating before the cheese melts.","s":4}],"rl":"VFLSFMXXXZ"},"3060":{"lv":"ERRRREREEEER","w":{"1":"Adds smoky, mild pepper flavor to the green sauce; skip and add a jalapeno for heat.","2":"Sweetness and body in the sauce.","3":"Savory backbone of the salsa verde.","4":"Fresh herbal lift that balances the cream; skipping makes it a bit dull.","11":"Needed to balance tomatillo tartness and the cream.","5":"Cream forms the suiza sauce; skipped, the dish loses its defining creamy sauce.","6":"Adds savory depth to a small amount of liquid; skipped, flavor is just muted.","9":"Softens tortillas so they roll; skipped, they crack."},"sub":{"5":{"x":"1 cup sour cream thinned with 2 tbsp milk","d":"Tangier; blend it in and bake, never boil, or it can curdle.","k":["sour cream","milk"]},"6":{"x":"1/2 cup water + 1/4 tsp salt","d":"Slightly lighter flavor; add a bouillon cube if you have one.","k":["water"]},"9":{"x":"2 tbsp canola oil","d":"Same job of making the tortillas pliable.","k":["canola oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Simmer the tomatillos just until they turn olive green and soft, then drain well. Overcooking turns them mushy and the sauce watery. Rinse the sticky film off the husked fruit first, or the sauce tastes bitter.","s":0},{"t":"Taste the sauce before baking; if it is too tart, a pinch of sugar rounds it, and if the cream seems thin, simmer the blended sauce briefly first.","s":1},{"t":"Dip each tortilla in the hot oil for only a few seconds per side. This waterproofs them so they don't dissolve in the sauce, but frying them longer makes them brittle and they crack when you roll them.","s":2}],"rl":"AVXXXFMPBFFZ"},"3068":{"lv":"EEERRRRRRRROREEER","w":{"3":"Adds dark, raisiny depth; use an extra ancho in its place.","4":"Nutty body that thickens the sauce; skipping loses some silkiness.","5":"Nutty richness and thickening; peanuts are a classic stand-in.","6":"Sweetness that balances the chile bitterness; skip and add an extra pinch of sugar.","7":"A little sweetness and acidity to round out the sauce.","8":"Savory base for the sauce.","9":"Savory backbone of the sauce.","10":"The warm spice that shows up in mole poblano; skipping makes it less complex.","11":"Minor background heat; the chiles already provide it.","12":"Thickens and mellows the sauce; a toasted tortilla or crackers can do the same.","16":"Season at the end; mole needs a good pinch to balance the sweetness.","14":"Liquid the dish simmers or cooks in; skipped, there is not enough liquid.","15":"Only cooking fat; skipped, food sticks and browns poorly, so use an oil or substitute."},"sub":{"5":{"x":"1/4 cup peanuts","d":"A bit earthier and less delicate; works well and is traditional in many versions.","k":["peanut"],"wn":"Contains peanuts"},"14":{"x":"4 cups water + 1 chicken bouillon cube","d":"A little less savory and rich; use the broth from simmering the chicken to recover flavor.","k":["water","chicken bouillon"]},"15":{"x":"3 tbsp lard or olive oil","d":"Lard is more traditional; olive oil is fine.","k":["lard","olive oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Toast the chiles just 30 seconds: a burnt chile makes the entire mole bitter and no amount of chocolate will fix it. Wear gloves for seeding if your skin is sensitive.","s":1},{"t":"Fry the blended sauce in the pot, stirring and partially covering it, because it spatters. Wait until it darkens and thickens before adding broth; this step deepens the flavor.","s":4},{"t":"Mole tastes better after a night in the fridge; if too thick, thin with chicken broth. Thighs are best cooked to about 175F (80C) for tenderness, and a minimum of 165F (74C) for safety.","s":6}],"rl":"PXXXTTWAXXXZTXMFZ"},"52765":{"lv":"EEEE","tips":[{"t":"Poach the chicken pieces in the sauce on low heat, covered, until no pink remains and they reach 165F (74C); cutting the breast into thirds speeds this up and lets the meat soak up sauce.","s":0},{"t":"Tear the tortillas to fit and overlap them in the layers. Spread sauce under each layer so the corn tortillas soften and don't turn gummy and dry on top.","s":3},{"t":"Let the casserole stand 10 minutes after baking so the layers set and slice cleanly instead of sliding apart.","s":5}],"rl":"MTBP"},"52830":{"lv":"EERRRREROORRRO","w":{"2":"Earthy spice for the chicken; skipping makes it blander but the dressing still flavors it.","3":"Color and mild pepper flavor; skip with small loss.","4":"Adds savory garlic flavor to the slow-cooked chicken.","5":"Creamy layer that anchors the taco filling and keeps shells from getting soggy.","7":"Melts to glue the filling in the shells; skipping makes a drier taco.","8":"Fresh topping; skipping is fine.","9":"Heat and freshness on top; skip for a milder taco.","10":"Used to make the green guacamole sauce.","11":"Gives the guacamole sauce tang and a little heat.","12":"Cool, creamy finish that balances the spiced chicken.","13":"Thins the sour cream for drizzling; water works.","1":"Cooking liquid and seasoning for the chicken; skipped, it cooks dry and bland."},"sub":{"1":{"x":"1 1/2 cups total: 1 cup chicken broth + 1/4 cup olive oil + 1/4 cup vinegar + pinch of salt","d":"Lacks the dressing's seasonings; the spices carry the flavor. Slightly less tangy.","k":["chicken broth","olive oil","vinegar"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Check the chicken for doneness at 3 hours; breasts are done at 165F (74C) and turn stringy and dry if the slow cooker keeps going. Shred while warm for the best texture.","s":1},{"t":"Moisten the shredded chicken with the cooking liquid a little at a time. Too much liquid soaks the shells; just enough keeps the meat juicy.","s":4},{"t":"Stand the hard shells tightly upright in the baking dish so they don't tip, and watch them at 450F; the edges brown fast and can burn in a few extra minutes.","s":8}],"rl":"PMXXXBBTGGGXGM"},"11":{"lv":"EERROOERERRORRRO","w":{"2":"Earthy depth in the spice rub; skipping makes it less complex.","3":"Savory background to the spice rub.","4":"Minor herbal note in the rub.","5":"Minor savory note in the rub; garlic powder and paprika carry most of it.","7":"Adds richness and browning; all oil works fine.","9":"Crunch and cool contrast; shredded lettuce can replace it.","10":"Acid for the slaw and sauce; skipping makes everything flat.","11":"Fresh garnish; skip with little loss.","12":"Lightens the sauce and adds tang; use all sour cream if you lack it.","13":"Creaminess for the sauce; extra yogurt works.","14":"Smoky heat that gives the sauce character; skip for a mild creamy sauce.","15":"Rounds the chipotle heat; skip with little effect.","6":"Only cooking fat; skipped, food sticks and browns poorly, so use an oil or substitute."},"sub":{"6":{"x":"1 tbsp olive oil","d":"Slightly fruitier; fine for searing at medium-high.","k":["olive oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Pat the shrimp very dry and sear in a very hot pan with the pan not overcrowded. A dry surface lets the spice rub form a dark crust instead of steaming and turning gray.","s":2},{"t":"Shrimp take only 1 to 2 minutes per side; pull them as soon as they turn opaque and curl into a loose C. A tight O shape means overcooked and rubbery.","s":2},{"t":"Dress the cabbage with lime and salt only just before serving, or leave it 10 minutes to soften slightly if you prefer a more tender slaw. Blackened means deeply spiced and well browned, not burnt; keep the exhaust fan on.","s":1}],"rl":"PXXXXXFFBVAGFFXW"},"52819":{"lv":"ERERERRORRRR","w":{"1":"Adds heat to the coating; skip if your cajun blend is already hot.","5":"Cool, creamy contrast to the spiced fish.","6":"Crunch and freshness; any crisp lettuce or cabbage works.","7":"Mild onion crunch; skip.","8":"Adds juicy tang as a topping.","9":"Base of the zesty dressing; skipping loses the cooling element.","10":"Acid for the dressing that brightens the fish.","11":"Savory kick in the dressing; skip with small loss.","3":"Small amount to coat the pan; skipped, a nonstick pan works."},"sub":{"3":{"x":"1 tsp olive oil","d":"Same function; slightly more flavor.","k":["olive oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Pat the fillets dry, then press the spice mix in on all sides. Cajun blends already contain salt, so don't add extra seasoning to the fish.","s":1},{"t":"Lay the fish in the pan and leave it alone until a crust forms and it releases easily, then flip once. Fish is done when it turns opaque and flakes at 145F (63C).","s":2},{"t":"Flake the fish into large pieces over the tortilla instead of cutting it so the juicy chunks stay moist and you get the crust in every bite.","s":4}],"rl":"XXPFBGVGGFAX"},"52870":{"lv":"EERRRERRORREO","w":{"2":"A pinch adds smokiness to the roasted chickpeas; skip with small loss.","3":"Fresh juicy topping in the pickled salsa.","4":"Pickles quickly in the vinegar for a bright crunchy topping.","6":"Mashed into a quick guacamole for creaminess; skipping makes the fajitas drier.","7":"Brightens the guacamole and cream.","8":"Wedges for squeezing at the table; skip.","9":"The base of the harissa cream; skipping loses a cooling element.","10":"Adds the smoky heat in the cream and on the chickpeas; build your own blend from the sub if you lack it.","12":"Garnish; skip.","1":"Only cooking fat; skipped, food sticks and browns poorly, so use an oil or substitute.","5":"Acid for the quick pickle; skipped, it is no longer a pickle."},"sub":{"1":{"x":"1 tbsp vegetable oil","d":"Roasts the chickpeas just as well; less fruity.","k":["vegetable oil"]},"5":{"x":"2 tsp white vinegar or lime juice","d":"A little sharper; the quick pickle still works.","k":["white vinegar"]},"10":{"x":"1 tsp paprika + 1/2 tsp cumin + 1/4 tsp cayenne","d":"Close to harissa spice but less complex.","k":["paprika","cumin","cayenne"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Dry the drained chickpeas thoroughly in a towel before roasting, and give them space on the tray. Moisture is what keeps them soft; dry ones turn crisp and nutty at the edges.","s":0},{"t":"Salt the pickled tomato and onion mixture lightly and leave it for at least 10 minutes; the vinegar softens the sharpness of the raw onion and turns it pink.","s":1},{"t":"Char the tortillas on a smoking-hot dry griddle for under a minute per side so they pick up grill marks but stay pliable. Overlong cooking makes them stiff.","s":2}],"rl":"PFXVVAVAGFXBG"},"53067":{"lv":"EERREERRRRORORO","w":{"2":"Sweet savory base in the filling.","3":"Savory backbone for the filling.","6":"Adds sweetness and texture to the filling.","7":"Adds moisture and acidity binding the quinoa; drain it so the filling isn't wet.","8":"Earthy flavor that makes this taste Mexican; skip with a noticeable loss.","9":"Warm heat and color.","10":"Smoky note; skipping is subtle.","11":"Needed to season the quinoa and beans.","12":"Minor extra seasoning.","13":"Melts over the top for the finish; skipping makes it lighter.","14":"Fresh garnish; skip.","1":"Only cooking fat; skipped, food sticks and browns poorly, so use an oil or substitute."},"sub":{"1":{"x":"1 tbsp vegetable oil","d":"Same function; less fruity.","k":["vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Rinse quinoa under cold water before cooking to wash off the bitter saponin coating, and cook it separately (about 2 parts water to 1 part quinoa, 15 minutes) before mixing into the filling.","s":3},{"t":"Drain the beans and the canned tomatoes well before stirring in. Excess liquid pools in the pepper cavity and makes the filling soggy.","s":3},{"t":"Slice a thin piece off the bottom of any wobbly pepper so it sits flat, and give the shells a head start in the oven so they soften before you add the filling.","s":1}],"rl":"VFXXBPVMXXXZZGG"},"3063":{"lv":"EERERRORRO","w":{"2":"Seasons the masa so the sope base isn't bland; skipping makes the base taste flat against the toppings.","4":"A creamy, earthy base layer that glues the toppings on; skipping makes a drier sope.","5":"The main topping protein; skip for a bean-only vegetarian sope.","6":"Cool, crisp contrast to the rich toppings; skip freely.","7":"Adds acidity and heat; skip if the beans and chicken are well seasoned.","8":"Salty, crumbly finish that seasons the toppings; feta works.","9":"Cool creamy drizzle; skip with minor loss.","3":"Frying medium; skipped, the food cannot be fried."},"sub":{"3":{"x":"1/4 cup lard or canola oil","d":"Lard is more traditional and richer; canola is cleaner tasting.","k":["lard","canola oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Use masa harina (nixtamalized corn flour), not cornmeal or regular flour. The dough should feel like soft Play-Doh; if the edges crack when you press a disc, add water a tablespoon at a time.","s":0},{"t":"Pinch the rim up while the discs are still hot from the dry skillet; once cool they crack. Make the rim about 1/2 inch high so it holds the toppings.","s":3},{"t":"Fry the sopes in shallow oil at medium heat so the outside crisps while the center stays tender. Drain on paper towels and top them just before serving so the beans don't soften the base.","s":4}],"rl":"BMZFGPGGGG"},"3064":{"lv":"ERREEOOO","w":{"1":"Moistens and seasons the chicken filling; skipping makes it dry.","2":"Binds the filling and adds richness; skip for a leaner flauta.","5":"A cool bed that contrasts with the hot crisp flautas.","6":"Cool tang; skip freely.","7":"A creamy topping; skip with minor loss.","4":"Frying medium; skipped, the food cannot be fried."},"sub":{"4":{"x":"1/2 cup canola oil","d":"Neutral, high smoke point; same crisping.","k":["canola oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Wrap the tortillas in a damp towel and warm them until they bend without cracking. If a tortilla still splits, dip it in the hot oil for 5 seconds before filling.","s":1},{"t":"Roll them tight and fry seam side down first so the seam seals itself in the oil. Keep the oil at 350F (175C); if it is too cool, the flautas absorb grease instead of turning crisp.","s":4},{"t":"Drain on a wire rack rather than a stack of paper towels so the bottoms stay crisp. Serve immediately, since steam softens them within minutes.","s":5}],"rl":"PXTBFGGG"},"3046":{"lv":"EEORRRRORREEO","w":{"2":"Minor; salt and cumin season the meat and the pan juices.","3":"Earthy depth in the rub; skipping is a small loss.","4":"Herbal note typical of carnitas; Mexican oregano works best.","5":"Sweetens the braising liquid; skipping makes it less rich.","6":"Mellow garlic in the pan juices; skipping loses savoriness.","7":"Subtle aroma in the long braise.","9":"Adds acid that balances the sweet orange and the fatty pork.","12":"Garnish; skip.","8":"Tenderizes and flavors the braise; skipped, the carnitas are less bright."},"sub":{"8":{"x":"1 cup orange juice (about 2 oranges' worth)","d":"Carton juice is sweeter and less tart; add an extra squeeze of lime.","k":["orange juice"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Leave the fat cap on the pork shoulder; it bastes the meat as it renders. The pork is safe at 145F (63C), but carnitas needs to reach about 195-205F (90-96C) for the collagen to melt into shreddable tenderness.","s":2},{"t":"Toss the shredded pork with the rendered fat and juices from the pot before broiling; this fat is what fries the edges into crisp, caramelized bits.","s":4},{"t":"Spread the pork in a thin layer on the sheet pan and broil in batches if needed. A piled-up pan steams the meat instead of crisping it; stir and rebroil for the crunchy bits.","s":4}],"rl":"PZZXXXXXMAMBG"},"3051":{"lv":"EEERREEEEE","w":{"3":"Savory depth in the chile filling.","4":"Earthy note in the filling; skipping is a small loss.","6":"Fat gives tamale masa its tenderness; skipped, the tamales are dry and dense.","8":"Hydrates the masa; skipped, the dough is too dry to spread."},"sub":{"6":{"x":"1 1/2 cups vegetable shortening","d":"Neutral; lard gives a more traditional pork flavor and tenderness.","k":["shortening"]},"8":{"x":"4 cups warm water + 2 tsp chicken bouillon (or use the saved pork broth)","d":"Pork broth is richest; bouillon water is lighter but works.","k":["water","chicken bouillon"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Whip the lard until very fluffy and test the masa by dropping a small spoonful into cold water: if it floats, it is aerated enough for light tamales. If it sinks, keep beating.","s":3},{"t":"Spread the masa thin (about 2 tbsp) and leave a border so it doesn't squeeze out; a thick layer makes dense, pasty tamales. Fold the empty end up and keep them standing open end up.","s":4},{"t":"Keep water in the bottom of the steamer, topping up with boiling water as needed, since a dry pot scorches. They are done when the masa pulls cleanly from the husk; they firm up as they rest.","s":5}],"rl":"GPXXXBFLMZ"},"3067":{"lv":"EERRRRRROEEE","w":{"3":"Acid for brightness and tenderizing; skipping makes the barbacoa heavy.","4":"Savory backbone in the sauce.","5":"Earthy depth; skipping is a small loss.","6":"Herbal note; Mexican oregano is best.","7":"Signature warm spice of barbacoa; skipping makes it noticeably less traditional, but use only the 1/2 tsp.","8":"Subtle aroma in the long braise.","2":"Adds brightness; skipped, the dish is flatter but still works.","9":"Liquid the dish simmers or cooks in; skipped, there is not enough liquid."},"sub":{"2":{"x":"1/4 cup white vinegar","d":"Sharper and less fruity.","k":["white vinegar"]},"9":{"x":"1 cup water + 1/2 tsp salt","d":"Less beefy depth; chuck still makes it rich.","k":["water"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Leave the chuck in large chunks rather than small cubes. Bigger pieces stay juicy over a long braise, and shred into long strands.","s":1},{"t":"It is done when the meat shreds with almost no pressure. If it resists, it simply needs more time; the collagen has not melted yet, and extra time will not dry it out when it is covered in liquid.","s":2},{"t":"Skim the fat from the cooking juices before moistening the meat, then crisp some of the shredded beef in a hot skillet for contrast in texture.","s":3}],"rl":"PXAAXXXXXMZB"},"3617":{"lv":"EEROREEROO","w":{"2":"Adds fiber, protein and heartiness to the filling; skipping makes the enchiladas more chicken-and-cheese.","3":"Mild onion freshness in the filling; skip freely.","4":"Earthy warmth that makes the filling taste Mexican; skipping is noticeable but not fatal.","7":"Adds sharpness and a golden top; extra jack works instead.","8":"Not used in the steps as written; a little can grease the pans, but you can skip it.","9":"Garnish for serving after baking; skip."},"tips":[{"t":"Fill each tortilla with about 1/3 cup and roll them snugly, packing them seam side down so they don't unroll in the freezer. Overstuffing makes them split.","s":2},{"t":"Wrap the pan tightly with foil, pressing it against the surface of the food, to prevent freezer burn. Label with the date and the baking instructions.","s":4},{"t":"Thaw overnight in the fridge, never on the counter. If baking from frozen, keep it covered for the full time so the center heats through; the dish is ready when the center reaches 165F (74C).","s":5}],"rl":"PFVXXBMFFG"},"3621":{"lv":"EEEERRE","w":{"4":"Adds moisture and flavor to the filling; skipping makes it drier.","5":"Melts in the middle and binds the filling; skipping makes the burritos less cohesive.","1":"Main seasoning of the filling; skipped, the dish is bland."},"sub":{"1":{"x":"1 tbsp chili powder + 1 tsp cumin + 1/2 tsp garlic powder + 1/2 tsp paprika + 1/2 tsp salt + 1/4 tsp oregano","d":"Close to the packet; a little less salty and no thickener.","k":["chili powder","cumin","garlic powder","paprika","salt"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Drain the fat after browning and let the filling cool completely before rolling. Hot filling steams and softens the tortillas and freezes into ice crystals.","s":1},{"t":"Warm each tortilla briefly so it folds without cracking. Fold the sides over the filling first, then roll tightly from the bottom so the burrito doesn't unravel.","s":3},{"t":"Wrap individually and freeze flat in a bag. Ground beef must be cooked to 160F (71C); when reheating from frozen, make sure the center is steaming hot (165F/74C) and flip halfway through.","s":5}],"rl":"PXBBMFB"},"36":{"lv":"EOORROEEEOO","w":{"1":"Minor savory note; the other spices cover it.","2":"Minor savory note; skip.","3":"Adds mild heat and color to the chicken.","4":"Earthy warmth that makes the chicken taste more Tex-Mex; skipping is a small loss.","5":"Pinch of color and sweetness; skip.","9":"A dipping accompaniment; skip.","10":"A cool dip; skip.","8":"Only cooking fat; skipped, food sticks and browns poorly, so use an oil or substitute."},"sub":{"8":{"x":"1 tbsp butter","d":"Browns faster and tastes richer; watch the heat.","k":["butter"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Shred the cheese yourself if you can; bagged cheese has anti-caking starch that melts less smoothly and doesn't glue the tortilla as well.","s":1},{"t":"Cook over medium heat, not high. The cheese needs time to melt before the tortilla blackens, and you want a gentle sizzle, not smoke.","s":2},{"t":"Rest the finished quesadilla for a minute before slicing so the cheese sets slightly; cut right away and the filling slides out.","s":3}],"rl":"PXXXXXBFFGG"},"103":{"lv":"EEEREERRRR","w":{"3":"Adds mild heat and color to the crust; skipping leaves it plain.","6":"Crunchy, cool topping; shredded cabbage or lettuce works.","7":"The base of the crema that cools the crispy fish.","8":"Provides acid for the crema and the table; skipping makes the crema flat.","9":"Creamy contrast to the crisp fish; skip with a modest loss.","4":"Frying medium; skipped, the food cannot be fried."},"sub":{"4":{"x":"2 tbsp canola oil","d":"Neutral and high-heat tolerant; same crisping.","k":["canola oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Pat the cod completely dry and salt the fish or the panko lightly; the recipe has no salt for the fish itself, and plain panko-coated cod tastes flat.","s":0},{"t":"Press the panko firmly on all sides so it adheres, and fry over medium-high heat in about 1/4 inch of oil, flipping once. Cod is done when it is opaque and flakes at 145F (63C).","s":0},{"t":"Assemble immediately; the crunch lasts only a few minutes once it meets the slaw and crema. Dry the slaw mix well if it is wet.","s":2}],"rl":"PTTXFBVFAV"},"3053":{"lv":"EERRRROR","w":{"2":"Adds tang and lightens the mayo; skipping makes it heavier and sweeter.","3":"Salty, crumbly coating that defines street corn; feta or parmesan are stand-ins.","4":"Adds smoky heat and color; skipping leaves it mild.","5":"A squeeze of acid wakes up the rich coating.","7":"Cotija is already salty; taste before adding.","6":"Fresh garnish; skipping loses a little color and freshness."},"sub":{"3":{"x":"1/2 cup crumbled feta or grated parmesan","d":"Feta is tangier and wetter; parmesan is nuttier and saltier. Both work.","k":["feta","parmesan"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Grill the corn over direct high heat, turning every 2 to 3 minutes, until charred in spots. The char adds the sweet, smoky flavor that boiled corn lacks.","s":1},{"t":"Brush the creamy mix on while the corn is still hot so it clings and the cheese sticks. If you have no grill, char the corn in a dry cast iron skillet or under the broiler.","s":2},{"t":"Roll the ear in cotija on a plate so every side is coated. Cotija is salty, so taste before adding any extra salt.","s":3}],"rl":"VFFGXAGZ"},"3054":{"lv":"EERREERRO","w":{"2":"Sweet savory base; skipping makes the rice plainer.","3":"Mellow savory note; skipping is a small loss.","6":"Earthy warmth that gives Mexican rice its characteristic taste.","7":"Seasons the rice; adjust if your broth is salted.","8":"Adds color and sweetness; skip.","0":"Only cooking fat; skipped, food sticks and browns poorly, so use an oil or substitute.","5":"Liquid the dish simmers or cooks in; skipped, there is not enough liquid."},"sub":{"0":{"x":"2 tbsp olive oil","d":"Slightly fruitier; toasts the rice the same.","k":["olive oil"]},"5":{"x":"2 1/2 cups water + 1 chicken bouillon cube","d":"A bit lighter in flavor; good if you dissolve the cube fully.","k":["water","chicken bouillon"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Rinse the rice, then drain very well before toasting; wet rice steams instead of frying. Toast, stirring, until the grains are opaque and golden and sound sandy; this keeps them separate.","s":0},{"t":"Don't lift the lid during the 18 minutes; steam is what cooks the rice. If the grains are still firm at the end, add a splash of hot water, cover, and steam 3 more minutes.","s":3},{"t":"Rest the pot off the heat for 5 minutes, then fluff from the bottom with a fork. This lets the moisture redistribute so the grains don't clump.","s":4}],"rl":"FBXXXMXZV"},"3055":{"lv":"ERRERRRO","w":{"1":"Sweet, savory base; skipping makes the beans plainer.","2":"Savory backbone; skip for a milder bean.","4":"Earthy warmth; skipping is a small loss.","6":"Taste after mashing; canned beans and broth are already salted.","7":"A salty, melty finish; skip for vegan or lighter beans.","0":"Only cooking fat; skipped, food sticks and browns poorly, so use an oil or substitute.","5":"Adds savory depth to a small amount of liquid; skipped, flavor is just muted."},"sub":{"0":{"x":"2 tbsp lard or olive oil","d":"Lard is the traditional flavor; olive oil is lighter.","k":["lard","olive oil"]},"5":{"x":"1/2 cup water","d":"Less savory depth; add a pinch more salt.","k":["water"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Keep the canning liquid: it is starchy and gives you the creamy texture. If the beans get too thick as they cool, stir in hot water a tablespoon at a time.","s":2},{"t":"Mash directly in the pan while the beans simmer so they absorb the broth; mashing off the heat leaves a grainy paste.","s":3},{"t":"Wait to salt until the end, because canned beans and the broth are already salted and the beans reduce and concentrate as they thicken.","s":4}],"rl":"FXXBXMZG"},"3056":{"lv":"ERRREEO","w":{"1":"Sharp crunch that contrasts the creamy avocado; skipping makes it milder and heavier.","2":"Fresh heat; skip for a mild guacamole.","3":"Herbal freshness; skipping is a small loss.","6":"Adds juiciness and color; many traditional versions omit it, and it can water down the guacamole."},"tips":[{"t":"Use avocados that give slightly when pressed. Underripe fruit is bland and gritty, and overripe has brown stringy spots; ripe ones mash creamy.","s":0},{"t":"Mash the onion, jalapeno, cilantro and salt into a paste first (a molcajete is ideal), then fold in the avocado. The salt breaks down the aromatics, so the flavor spreads evenly.","s":2},{"t":"To store, press plastic wrap directly against the surface so no air touches it. Lime juice slows browning, but air contact is what really causes it.","s":4}],"rl":"VXXXAZV"},"3057":{"lv":"EERREE","w":{"2":"Fresh heat; skip for a mild salsa.","3":"Herbal freshness characteristic of pico; skipping makes it plainer."},"tips":[{"t":"Drain the diced tomatoes in a sieve with a pinch of salt as the recipe says; otherwise the salsa floods the bowl. Use firm, ripe romas, which have less water and seeds.","s":0},{"t":"Rinse the diced onion under cold water and shake it dry to take off the harsh bite, so it adds crunch without overpowering.","s":1},{"t":"Let it stand 10 minutes so the salt and lime draw flavor together, and eat within a day; after that it turns watery and the cilantro fades.","s":3}],"rl":"VXXXAZ"},"3356":{"lv":"EEERREROOR","w":{"3":"Adds tang and lightens the mayo; skipping makes the dressing heavier and sweeter.","4":"Salty, crumbly finish; feta or cotija both work, and skipping loses the savory edge.","6":"Adds mild smoky heat and color; skipping leaves it milder.","7":"Fresh garnish; skip.","8":"Optional heat; skip for a mild cup.","9":"Cotija or feta is already salty, so add only as needed after tasting.","1":"Only cooking fat; skipped, food sticks and browns poorly, so use an oil or substitute."},"sub":{"1":{"x":"2 tbsp vegetable oil","d":"Chars the corn the same way; less buttery richness.","k":["vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Thaw the corn and pat it very dry before it hits the skillet. Wet corn steams, so you get soft kernels instead of the sweet charred spots esquites needs.","s":1},{"t":"Use a very hot pan and leave the corn undisturbed for a minute or two between stirs. The kernels pop and brown only when they stay in contact with the metal.","s":1},{"t":"Let the corn cool a few minutes before folding in the mayo and sour cream. Dressing scorching-hot corn makes the dairy break and turn oily.","s":2}],"rl":"VFFFGAXGXZ"},"3357":{"lv":"EERRRRROOER","w":{"2":"Sweet savory base cooked in the bacon fat.","3":"Savory backbone of the broth.","4":"Adds acidity and body to the broth; skipping gives a less saucy, plainer pot.","5":"Adds green heat and freshness; skip for a milder pot.","6":"Earthy flavor that rounds out the pot.","7":"Minor herbal note.","8":"Finishing herb; skip.","10":"Needed for flavor; add near the end as directed so the broth tastes balanced."},"tips":[{"t":"Render the bacon slowly over medium heat until crisp so the fat is released; this fat is the flavor base of the whole pot. If the beans are old (over a year), they may stay hard no matter how long you cook them.","s":0},{"t":"Hold the tomatoes and salt until the beans are nearly tender, as the recipe directs. Acidic tomatoes can keep bean skins from softening if added too early.","s":4},{"t":"Keep the pot at a gentle simmer and top up with hot water if the beans become uncovered; adding cold water stalls the cooking and toughens the beans.","s":3}],"rl":"BFXXVXXXGMZ"},"3358":{"lv":"EERRRRERRR","w":{"2":"Adds mild smoky heat and body; green bell pepper works as a stand-in.","3":"Sweet savory base.","4":"Savory depth in the sauce.","5":"Adds juicy acidity that makes a light sauce around the vegetables.","7":"Makes the dish creamy and mellows the vegetables; skip for a lighter, vegan side.","8":"Melts into the sour cream for a silky finish.","9":"Season at the end; the cheese is also salty.","6":"Only cooking fat; skipped, food sticks and browns poorly, so use an oil or substitute."},"sub":{"6":{"x":"1 tbsp vegetable oil","d":"Same sauteing; slightly less fruity.","k":["vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Cut the zucchini into even dice and use a wide pan over medium-high heat. A crowded or low-heat pan steams the squash into mush instead of leaving it tender-crisp.","s":2},{"t":"Season gradually, since zucchini releases water once salted. The recipe holds most of the salt until the tomatoes go in.","s":3},{"t":"Take the pan off direct heat or turn it very low before stirring in the sour cream; high heat can curdle it and turn the sauce grainy.","s":4}],"rl":"VVVXXVFFFZ"},"3359":{"lv":"EEEREOOR","w":{"3":"Softens the vinegar's sharpness and rounds the brine; skipping gives a sharper pickle.","5":"A subtle Mexican herbal note; skip with little effect.","6":"Mild background savoriness; skip.","7":"Adds citrus brightness on top of the vinegar; skipping makes the pickle tangier and flatter.","1":"Pickling acid; skipped, there is no pickle and no preservation."},"sub":{"1":{"x":"1 cup white vinegar","d":"Sharper and less fruity than cider vinegar; add an extra pinch of sugar.","k":["white vinegar"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Slice the onions evenly about 1/8 inch thick so they pickle at the same speed; a mandoline gives the best results.","s":0},{"t":"Pour the hot brine over the raw onions; do not boil them in it. The heat softens the bite fast but they keep a pleasant crunch, and they turn bright pink as the acid hits the color in the skins.","s":2},{"t":"Press the onions under the liquid so none are exposed to air. Use a clean jar and refrigerate; they taste best after a few hours and keep up to 2 weeks as the recipe says.","s":3}],"rl":"VKMWKXXA"},"3492":{"lv":"EERRRRRRROR","w":{"2":"Sweet savory base for the vegetables.","3":"Savory backbone; skip with a small loss.","4":"Adds sweetness and body; frozen corn works.","5":"Adds mild smoky green flavor; green bell pepper works.","6":"Adds juiciness and a light sauce around the squash.","7":"Earthy warmth; skipping is a small loss.","8":"Soft, salty topping that it warms through; feta works.","10":"Needed to bring out the zucchini and tomato flavor.","9":"Finishing herb that adds freshness; skip with a small loss.","1":"Only cooking fat; skipped, food sticks and browns poorly, so use an oil or substitute."},"sub":{"1":{"x":"2 tbsp vegetable oil","d":"Same function; slightly less fruity.","k":["vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Cut the zucchini into 1/2-inch pieces so they cook through in the 8-minute covered stage but still keep some bite. Larger chunks stay raw inside and thin ones turn to mush.","s":2},{"t":"Salt only after the zucchini has had its first sear; early salt draws out water and makes the vegetables stew instead of browning.","s":3},{"t":"Queso fresco softens but doesn't melt into a sauce, so just crumble it on top and cover for a minute. Stirring it in would make it vanish into the juices.","s":4}],"rl":"VFXXVVVXGGZ"},"3493":{"lv":"ERRRERRRE","w":{"1":"Adds cool, watery crunch; skipping makes the salad firmer and drier.","2":"Sharp bite and color; skip for a milder salad.","3":"Fresh herbal note; skipping is a small loss.","5":"Rounds the lime acid with a little sweetness; skipping makes it sharper.","7":"Adds heat and a touch of earthiness on top; skipping leaves it mild.","6":"Mild sweetener balancing lime; skipped, the salad is a bit tart."},"sub":{"6":{"x":"1 tbsp sugar dissolved in 1 tsp warm water","d":"Slightly less floral than honey but sweetens just as well.","k":["sugar"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Peel jicama thoroughly. Under the thin brown skin is a fibrous layer; if it stays on, the matchsticks turn out stringy and tough instead of crisp and juicy.","s":0},{"t":"Don't dress it much longer than the 10-minute chill. Jicama stays crunchy for hours and doesn't brown, but the cucumber waters out and dilutes the dressing.","s":4},{"t":"Whisk the honey into the lime juice until fully dissolved so you don't get pockets of sweetness; taste and add a little more salt or chili for balance.","s":1}],"rl":"VVXXAAWXZ"},"3494":{"lv":"ERRREEORREO","w":{"1":"Adds sweetness and crunch to the pickle mix and mellows the jalapeno heat; skipping gives a hotter pickle.","2":"Sweet savory component of the brine.","3":"Mellow savory flavor in the brine.","6":"Subtle aroma in the brine.","7":"Adds the herbal note typical of escabeche; skipping is a small loss.","8":"Softens the vinegar's sharpness; skipping makes a more sour brine.","10":"Minor warm spice; skip.","5":"Only cooking fat; skipped, food sticks and browns poorly, so use an oil or substitute."},"sub":{"5":{"x":"2 tbsp vegetable oil","d":"Cooks the onion the same way; less fruity.","k":["vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Add the jalapenos last and simmer only 4 to 5 minutes, until they turn from bright to olive green. Longer cooking turns them soft and gray; if you want crisper pickles, pour the hot brine over the raw chilies instead.","s":3},{"t":"Wear gloves when slicing the jalapenos, and let the jar rest in the refrigerator at least a day; the flavor rounds out as the brine penetrates the vegetables.","s":5}],"rl":"VVVXKFXXWKX"},"3495":{"lv":"ERRROEE","w":{"1":"Sweet, savory aroma in the bean broth; skipping makes the broth plainer.","2":"Savory depth in the pot liquor.","4":"Traditional herb with a distinct, medicinal-herbal flavor; skip if you can't find it, and don't substitute oregano.","3":"Adds richness; skipped, the beans still cook in water."},"sub":{"3":{"x":"2 tbsp lard or olive oil","d":"Lard gives the most traditional flavor; olive oil is lighter.","k":["lard","olive oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Keep the beans covered with water the whole time; top up with boiling water, never cold, since cold water shocks them and keeps the skins tough.","s":2},{"t":"Salt late as the recipe says, when the beans are almost tender, so the skins stay soft and the beans cook through evenly.","s":3},{"t":"To thicken the broth without cream, mash a ladleful of beans against the side of the pot and stir back in.","s":4}],"rl":"BXXFXZM"},"3498":{"lv":"EERERRERO","w":{"2":"Gives the green rice its mild peppery flavor; green bell pepper is a fair replacement.","4":"Mild sweet backbone of the purée.","5":"Savory depth in the purée.","7":"Seasons the rice; adjust if your broth is salted.","8":"Adds heat; skip for a mild rice.","1":"Only cooking fat; skipped, food sticks and browns poorly, so use an oil or substitute.","6":"Liquid the dish simmers or cooks in; skipped, there is not enough liquid."},"sub":{"1":{"x":"2 tbsp olive oil","d":"Same function; slightly fruitier.","k":["olive oil"]},"6":{"x":"2 cups water + 1 chicken bouillon cube","d":"Slightly lighter in flavor.","k":["water","chicken bouillon"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Measure the blended mixture plus the extra broth to a total of about 2 cups liquid. Too much purée makes the rice gummy; it should be pourable but not watery.","s":2},{"t":"Toast the rice in the oil until it is lightly golden and smells nutty before adding the green purée; this keeps the grains separate and fluffy.","s":1},{"t":"Use a full cup of packed cilantro and don't extend the cook time; the color fades from bright green to olive if the pot sits on the heat too long.","s":3}],"rl":"BFXXXXMZX"},"3499":{"lv":"EORRRRERROR","w":{"1":"A small piece added to the boiling water gives a savory note; skip with little loss.","2":"Adds juiciness and sweetness to balance the tangy nopales.","3":"Sharp crunch that contrasts the soft cactus.","4":"Fresh herbal flavor central to the salad; skipping makes it plainer.","5":"Adds clean heat; jalapeno works.","7":"Softens the lime and adds a smooth texture.","8":"Salty, soft topping; feta works.","9":"Subtle herbal note; skip.","10":"Needed to season the salad; taste at the end."},"tips":[{"t":"Rinse the boiled nopales under cold water until the slime is gone, then drain them in a colander and pat them dry. This is the key to a clean-tasting salad instead of a gooey one.","s":1},{"t":"Cook until tender but still olive green; overcooked nopales turn dull and mushy. Fresh pads need the spines and eyes removed first.","s":0},{"t":"Dress the salad just before serving. The acid and salt draw out any remaining liquid from the nopales and tomatoes, which thins the dressing.","s":4}],"rl":"VXVXXXAFGXZ"},"3048":{"lv":"EERROEREROEOOR","w":{"2":"Adds sweetness and savory depth to the broth and the chile sauce.","3":"Savory backbone in the broth and sauce.","4":"Subtle aroma in the pot; skip.","6":"Adds fruity, deep sweetness; extra guajillo makes a lighter, tangier sauce.","8":"Herbal note typical of pozole; Mexican oregano is best.","9":"Minor earthy note; skip.","11":"Crunch and freshness on top; skip, but it is part of the dish's character.","12":"Peppery crunch for contrast; skip.","13":"Squeeze at the table to brighten the rich broth."},"tips":[{"t":"Skim the foam as the pork first comes to a boil, then hold the pot at a bare simmer. Boiling hard clouds the broth and toughens the meat.","s":0},{"t":"Rinse the canned hominy under cold water first to remove the canning liquid. Simmering it in the broth for 30 minutes lets it absorb flavor and puff up.","s":3},{"t":"Strain the blended chile sauce through a fine sieve, pressing on the solids, so the broth is silky and not gritty with chile skins.","s":2}],"rl":"PMXXXXXBXXZGGG"},"3049":{"lv":"RRRRRRREERRERORRO","w":{"1":"Sweet savory base for the soup.","2":"Savory depth that rounds out the broth.","3":"Fresh green heat; skip for a milder soup.","4":"Adds body, acidity and smokiness to the broth; skipping thins the flavor.","5":"Gives color, mild heat and depth; the soup is plainer without it.","6":"Earthy warmth; skipping is a small loss.","9":"Adds protein and heartiness; skip for a lighter soup.","10":"Adds sweetness and texture; skip.","12":"Cool, creamy topping that balances the spicy broth.","13":"Finishing herb; skip.","14":"Acid at the end brightens the whole pot.","15":"Season at the end, adjusting for the salt in the broth.","16":"Melty garnish; skip.","0":"Sautes aromatics; skipped, the soup still works."},"sub":{"0":{"x":"1 tbsp olive oil","d":"Same sauteing; slightly fruitier.","k":["olive oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Cook the chili powder and cumin in the oil with the garlic for a full minute before adding liquid; this blooms the spices and removes any raw taste.","s":1},{"t":"Poach the chicken breast at a gentle simmer until it reaches 165F (74C), then shred it. A hard boil makes lean breast stringy.","s":2},{"t":"Keep the baked tortilla strips separate and add them at serving so they stay crisp. If they go in the pot too early they dissolve into mush.","s":5}],"rl":"FXXXVXXMPVVGGGAZG"},"3065":{"lv":"ERRREERERROR","w":{"1":"Adds beefy depth and meatier bites; tripe alone is milder and more gelatinous. Skipping gives a lighter but still authentic broth.","2":"The halved onion sweetens the broth over the long simmer; skipping leaves it a little flat.","3":"A whole halved garlic head perfumes the broth and tames the tripe smell; skipping dulls the aroma.","6":"Ancho adds fruity sweetness and body to the red chile sauce; extra guajillo makes it brighter and sharper.","8":"Mexican oregano gives the classic herbal lift at the table; without it the soup loses a traditional note.","9":"Seasons the broth through the long simmer; the soup tastes flat without it, so adjust at the end.","10":"Raw onion adds crunch and bite against the rich, fatty broth; skip for a softer bowl.","11":"Lime juice cuts the richness of the broth; without it the soup tastes heavier."},"sub":{"6":{"x":"2 more dried guajillo chiles (8 total)","d":"Brighter, tangier and less fruity-sweet than ancho.","k":["guajillo chile"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Blanch the tripe first: cover with cold water, boil 10 minutes, drain and rinse before the long simmer. This removes the barnyard smell that otherwise lingers in the broth.","s":0},{"t":"Toast the chiles in a dry skillet for 20 to 30 seconds per side, just until fragrant, before soaking. Scorched chiles make the sauce bitter.","s":1},{"t":"Strain the chile puree through a fine sieve and skim the orange chile fat before serving; menudo tastes even better the next day once the collagen and chile settle.","s":3}],"rl":"PPXXMXXBXZGG"},"3595":{"lv":"ERRRERRRRERRO","w":{"2":"Browned onion adds sweetness and body to the sauce; skipping leaves the stew sharper and greener.","3":"Raw garlic blended into the sauce gives it depth; without it the tomatillo flavor is one-note.","5":"Poblanos add mild, smoky body; extra jalapeno or green bell pepper gives a thinner version of that flavor.","6":"Jalapenos supply the heat; leave them out for a mild stew or keep some seeds for more fire.","7":"Cumin adds earthy warmth; skipping makes the stew brighter and more tomatillo-forward.","8":"Oregano adds a mild herbal note that is easily masked by the cilantro and cumin.","10":"Blended into the sauce, cilantro gives it a fresh, grassy lift; skipping makes it more acidic.","11":"Salt brings out the tomatillo and pork flavor; under-salted chile verde tastes sharp and flat.","12":"A background bite only; skipping hardly changes the stew.","1":"Pork browns in its own fat; skipped, the stew still works.","9":"Liquid the dish simmers or cooks in; skipped, there is not enough liquid."},"sub":{"1":{"x":"1 tbsp lard or canola oil","d":"Lard gives a richer pork flavor; canola is neutral.","k":["lard","canola oil"]},"9":{"x":"2 cups water + 1 chicken bouillon cube","d":"Slightly less body; the long-braised pork supplies most of the flavor.","k":["water","bouillon cube"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Char the tomatillos until blistered and collapsing and keep the juices pooled on the pan; they go into the blender and carry the tart flavor.","s":0},{"t":"Brown the pork in batches without crowding. Crowded cubes steam and turn gray, and the fond left in the pot is what gives the sauce depth.","s":2},{"t":"If the sauce is thin at the end, simmer uncovered for 15 minutes. If it is too tart, a pinch of sugar rounds off the tomatillos without making it sweet.","s":5}],"rl":"PFXXVVXXXMXZZ"},"3598":{"lv":"EEERRRRRRREEOORO","w":{"3":"Mint is the traditional albondigas herb, cooling and fresh against the beef; skipping loses the signature note.","4":"Adds earthy depth to the meatballs; without it they taste plainer.","6":"Sweet aromatic base for the broth; skipping makes the soup taste thinner.","7":"Garlic deepens the broth; skipping leaves it mild.","8":"Carrots add sweetness and body to the broth; skip for a simpler soup.","9":"Tender vegetable that makes it a full meal; add late or it turns mushy.","12":"A small herbal note that tomato and cumin largely cover; skip freely.","13":"A fresh finish stirred in at the end; skipping loses little.","14":"Both the meatballs and broth need salt; unsalted meatballs taste dull.","15":"Background heat only; skipping barely changes it.","5":"Sautes aromatics; skipped, the soup still works."},"sub":{"5":{"x":"2 tbsp vegetable oil or butter","d":"Neutral oil or butter for sauteing; olive oil adds a fruity note.","k":["vegetable oil","butter"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Roll the meatballs loosely and lightly. The raw rice swells as it cooks, and tightly packed meatballs turn dense.","s":0},{"t":"Drop meatballs into a gentle simmer, not a boil, and do not stir for the first 5 minutes until they set, so they do not break apart.","s":3},{"t":"Cut a meatball open to check: it must reach 160F (71C) and the rice must be tender. Zucchini goes in late so it keeps its shape.","s":4}],"rl":"PBTXXFXXVVVMXGZZ"},"52826":{"lv":"EERORRRRRORERERRR","w":{"2":"Garlic adds sweetness and depth to the chilli base; skipping leaves it flatter.","4":"Chorizo adds smoky paprika fat and salt that build the sauce; if omitted, add 1 tsp smoked paprika.","5":"Cumin is the earthy backbone of chilli spicing; skipping makes it taste less like chilli.","6":"Allspice adds a warm, sweet-peppery depth typical of Mexican braises; skipping is a subtle loss.","7":"Very pungent; a little gives festive warmth, but leaving it out gives a cleaner chilli.","8":"Cinnamon adds background warmth and sweetness; skipping is subtle.","9":"A faint savory background note; skipping makes almost no difference.","10":"Gives a dry herbal note that holds up to a long braise; skipping is minor.","14":"Adds sweetness and tang that round off the sauce; a spoon of tomato paste plus a pinch of sugar replaces it.","3":"Any cooking fat works for searing; olive oil adds a fruity note.","12":"Adds sweet-sour depth; skipped, the chilli is less rounded.","15":"Adds sweetness and depth; skipped, the chilli is a bit sharper.","16":"Adds bulk and body; skipped, it is a beef chilli without beans."},"sub":{"3":{"x":"3 tbsp vegetable oil","d":"Neutral; olive oil gives a slightly fruitier base.","k":["vegetable oil"]},"12":{"x":"3 tbsp red wine vinegar + 1 tsp sugar","d":"Sharper and less fruity than balsamic; the sugar replaces its sweetness.","k":["red wine vinegar","sugar"]},"15":{"x":"2 tbsp white sugar or maple syrup","d":"Less caramel and molasses flavor but the same sweetness.","k":["sugar","maple syrup"]},"16":{"x":"2 x 400 g tins kidney beans or pinto beans","d":"Slightly firmer and less creamy than borlotti.","k":["kidney beans","pinto beans"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Sear the beef in two or three batches; crowding steams the meat and you lose the fond that flavors the braise.","s":1},{"t":"Keep the oven low and the pot covered; the beef is ready when a piece shreds with no resistance. If it is still firm at 3 hours, give it another 30 minutes, since collagen needs time, not heat.","s":3},{"t":"Fish out the cinnamon stick and bay leaves before shredding, then taste for acid. A splash of vinegar wakes up a long-braised sauce.","s":4}],"rl":"PXXFPXXXXXXXAVWWB"},"3059":{"lv":"EEEEEREEEERO","w":{"4":"Gives the yolk batter its loose, spongy texture; a plant milk or half-and-half also works.","5":"Rounds out the egg and dairy flavor; the sponge tastes plainer without it.","6":"Evaporated milk gives the soak its cooked-milk depth; half-and-half works with a little less caramel flavor.","8":"Adds fat and richness to the milk soak; whole milk is a lighter swap.","10":"Sweetens and slightly stabilizes the whipped cream; granulated sugar works but can leave grit.","11":"Dusting only; skipping loses a traditional touch of spice."},"sub":{"4":{"x":"1/3 cup water + 1 tbsp melted butter","d":"Slightly less rich crumb; the soak adds plenty of dairy.","k":["water","butter"]},"6":{"x":"1 1/2 cups half-and-half","d":"Less caramelized flavor and a bit thinner; add 1 tbsp sugar to the soak.","k":["half-and-half"]},"8":{"x":"1/2 cup whole milk","d":"Lighter soak; the cake still absorbs it fully.","k":["whole milk"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Fold the whites into the yolk batter in thirds, stopping when the streaks disappear. This foam is the main lift, and overmixing gives a dense, rubbery sponge.","s":2},{"t":"Cool the cake completely and pierce it deeply with a fork. A warm cake collapses under the milk, and shallow holes leave the center dry.","s":4},{"t":"Pour the milk in stages, letting each addition soak in, and chill overnight if you can. The milk spreads evenly and the cake does not pool at the bottom.","s":5}],"rl":"SLLWMXMMFGWG"},"3278":{"lv":"EERRRERO","w":{"2":"Cooked rice adds bulk and soaks up the juices; skipping gives a softer, beans-and-beef burrito.","3":"A creamy glue that holds the filling together; skip and add more rice or cheese.","4":"Melts to bind the fillings together; without it the burrito is drier.","6":"Adds moisture and acid; skip if you use sour cream.","7":"Cooling creaminess against the seasoned beef; skip or serve on the side.","1":"Main seasoning of the filling; skipped, the dish is bland."},"sub":{"1":{"x":"1 tbsp chili powder + 1 tsp ground cumin + 1/2 tsp garlic powder + 1/2 tsp paprika + 1/2 tsp salt + 1/4 tsp oregano","d":"Close to a packet; packets vary in salt, so taste and adjust.","k":["chili powder","cumin","garlic powder","paprika","salt","oregano"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Break the beef into small crumbles and let it sit undisturbed for a couple of minutes at a time so it browns. Ground beef must reach 160F (71C).","s":0},{"t":"Warm tortillas 15 seconds per side in a dry pan, or wrap them in a damp paper towel and microwave for 20 seconds. Cold tortillas crack when folded.","s":3},{"t":"Keep the filling in a strip down the center, about 1/2 cup per burrito. Fold the sides in first and roll tightly; overstuffed burritos split in the pan.","s":5}],"rl":"PXBBFBGG"},"3270":{"lv":"EREERE","w":{"1":"A little sugar gives the yeast a quick start; skipping slows the rise slightly but still works.","4":"Softens the crumb and keeps pitas pliable; without it they are drier and chewier."},"tips":[{"t":"Water should feel like a warm bath, about 105 to 110F. Hotter kills the yeast; if it does not foam in 5 minutes, start over.","s":0},{"t":"Roll the rounds to an even thickness, about 1/4 inch, with no thin spots or thick edges. Uneven dough will not puff into a pocket.","s":4},{"t":"Bake on a stone or sheet that has preheated for 30 minutes and keep the oven shut. The blast of bottom heat turns the moisture inside into steam, which inflates the pocket.","s":5}],"rl":"LWSZFM"},"78":{"lv":"ERREEOEERR","w":{"1":"Adds sweetness and color to the roasted mix; skip with little structural change.","2":"Roasts sweet and jammy and adds bite; skipping makes the filling a bit plainer.","5":"Adds mild smokiness and color to the roasted vegetables; skip with little loss.","8":"Salty, tangy bite against the mild hummus; skip and season the vegetables a bit more.","9":"Adds fresh green crunch to contrast the roasted vegetables; skip for a softer wrap.","4":"Only cooking fat; skipped, food sticks and browns poorly, so use an oil or substitute."},"sub":{"4":{"x":"3 tbsp canola or avocado oil","d":"Neutral; olive oil gives a slightly fruitier flavor.","k":["canola oil","vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Cut the eggplant and zucchini into 1-inch pieces and spread them in a single layer, using two sheets if needed. Crowded vegetables steam and turn mushy.","s":0},{"t":"Warm the tortillas 15 seconds before spreading so they do not crack when rolled.","s":1},{"t":"Lay the spinach against the hummus first so the roasted vegetables do not soak the tortilla. Roll tightly and cut on the diagonal.","s":2}],"rl":"VVVVFXBTGV"},"3039":{"lv":"EERERRRERR","w":{"2":"Thickens and gives body; skipping makes a looser, richer dip, so add a few more walnuts.","4":"A single clove gives a sharp background kick; skip for a sweeter dip.","5":"Earthy warmth that rounds out the sweet peppers; skipping is subtle.","6":"Gentle heat; Aleppo pepper is ideal. Skipping makes it sweeter and milder.","8":"Brightens the dip; pomegranate molasses carries most of the acidity.","9":"Brings out the pepper's sweetness; the dip tastes flat without it.","3":"Defining tart-sweet flavor of muhammara; skipped, the dip tastes flat."},"sub":{"3":{"x":"1 tbsp lemon juice + 1 tbsp honey","d":"Tart-sweet, but lacks the fruity depth of pomegranate molasses.","k":["lemon juice","honey"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Toast the walnuts until fragrant but not dark, then cool them fully. Warm nuts turn pasty and release oil in the processor.","s":0},{"t":"Pulse and do not puree. Muhammara should stay coarse and spoonable, unlike smooth hummus. Drain the peppers well first.","s":2},{"t":"Rest at least 30 minutes, ideally overnight, so the breadcrumbs hydrate and the flavors blend. Loosen with a teaspoon of water or oil if it firms up in the fridge.","s":4}],"rl":"VBTAXXXFAZ"},"3366":{"lv":"EEREOOO","w":{"2":"Pools on top and adds fruity richness; labneh is dry and a little bland without it.","4":"Adds fresh color and cool flavor; skip freely.","5":"Cool crunch for dipping; a serving extra only.","6":"For scooping; serve with any bread or crackers."},"tips":[{"t":"Use full-fat yogurt without gelatin or starch stabilizers; those will not release whey and leave the labneh gummy.","s":1},{"t":"Eight hours of draining gives a soft spread, 24 hours gives firm balls you can roll and keep in oil. Keep the whey for baking or smoothies.","s":2},{"t":"Swirl a well into the top with the back of a spoon so the olive oil pools and the za'atar sits in it, the way restaurants plate it.","s":3}],"rl":"BZFXGGG"},"3367":{"lv":"EEROEEREOR","w":{"2":"Earthy spice that browns onto the florets; without it the cauliflower leans on the tahini alone.","3":"Adds color and a little sweetness; skip with little loss.","6":"Raw garlic gives the sauce its bite; skipping makes a milder, sweeter sauce.","8":"A fresh, green finish; skip freely.","9":"Seasons both the cauliflower and the tahini sauce; unsalted tahini sauce tastes bitter.","1":"Only cooking fat; skipped, food sticks and browns poorly, so use an oil or substitute."},"sub":{"1":{"x":"3 tbsp canola or avocado oil","d":"Neutral and stable at 450F; olive oil adds a fruitier flavor.","k":["canola oil","vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Roast on a rimmed sheet and do not crowd. The florets need to touch the hot metal to caramelize, and steamed cauliflower turns gray.","s":2},{"t":"Tahini seizes into a thick paste when the water first hits it; keep whisking and add warm water a tablespoon at a time until it relaxes into a pourable cream.","s":3},{"t":"Taste the sauce and adjust. Some tahini brands are bitter, and a little extra lemon plus a pinch of salt fixes it.","s":4}],"rl":"VFXXFAXMGZ"},"3368":{"lv":"EERRRRRORO","w":{"2":"Sharp crunch; rinse in cold water to mellow it, or skip for a milder salad.","3":"Fresh mint gives Persian salads their cooling character; skipping makes it plainer.","4":"Adds green freshness; skipping loses a layer of herb flavor.","7":"A faint extra note that fresh mint largely covers; skip freely.","8":"Draws out juices and seasons the vegetables; the salad is bland without it.","9":"Optional background heat; skipping hardly changes it.","5":"Adds brightness; skipped, the dish is flatter but still works.","6":"Carries flavor in the marinade or dressing; skipped, the dish is drier but intact."},"sub":{"5":{"x":"3 tbsp lemon juice","d":"Slightly sweeter and less floral than lime.","k":["lemon juice"]},"6":{"x":"2 tbsp vegetable oil","d":"Milder; olive oil adds a fruity note.","k":["vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Seed the tomatoes and use small, firm cucumbers. Watery cores turn the bowl to soup within minutes.","s":0},{"t":"Soak the diced onion in cold water for 5 minutes and drain; it takes the raw edge off so it does not overpower the herbs.","s":0},{"t":"Ten minutes is the resting limit. After an hour the salt pulls out too much liquid, so serve it quickly or drain any pool before serving.","s":3}],"rl":"VVXXXAFXZZ"},"3540":{"lv":"EREREEEEO","w":{"1":"Gives msemen its slight grit and chew; replacing it with flour makes a softer, plainer bread.","3":"Feeds the yeast and helps browning; skipping makes a very small difference.","8":"A serving extra for dipping; skip for a more savory msemen.","7":"Butter brushed between layers creates flaky layers; skipped, the msemen is gummy."},"sub":{"1":{"x":"1/2 cup additional all-purpose flour","d":"Softer and less crisp-chewy, without the semolina grit.","k":["flour"]},"7":{"x":"4 tbsp ghee or 4 tbsp vegetable oil","d":"Ghee is closest to traditional smen; oil gives crisp layers with less flavor.","k":["ghee","vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Rest the balls for at least 20 minutes. If the dough springs back while stretching, rest it 5 more minutes so the gluten relaxes and it can be stretched paper-thin.","s":1},{"t":"Brush fat between every fold. The trapped fat steams the layers apart as it cooks and creates the flaky texture.","s":3},{"t":"Cook on medium heat; hot pans brown the outside before the inner layers cook. While still hot, clap each msemen between your palms to fluff the layers apart.","s":5}],"rl":"SSZWLMFFG"},"3532":{"lv":"ERERRRRRRREEERRRO","w":{"3":"Garlic adds depth to the onion base; skipping makes the sauce less savory.","4":"Ginger is part of the classic Moroccan spice trio; without it the sauce loses warmth.","5":"Gives earthy flavor and golden color; skipping lightens the sauce.","6":"Earthy warmth that rounds out the spice mix; skip with a subtle loss.","7":"Adds sweet color and mild flavor; skipping is subtle.","8":"A small amount adds sweet warmth; skipping is barely noticeable.","9":"Floral aroma and golden color; turmeric already tints the sauce, so skipping is a small loss.","13":"Fresh herbal lift in the sauce; skipping makes it heavier.","14":"Adds green freshness; skipping loses a layer of herb flavor.","15":"Needed to season the chicken and sauce; undersalted tagine tastes flat.","16":"A background bite only; skipping is barely noticeable.","1":"Skin-on thighs also supply fat; skipped, the tagine still works.","10":"Liquid the dish simmers or cooks in; skipped, there is not enough liquid.","11":"Defining fermented lemon flavor; skipped, the dish stops being this tagine."},"sub":{"1":{"x":"2 tbsp vegetable oil or butter","d":"Neutral; olive oil adds a fruity note.","k":["vegetable oil","butter"]},"10":{"x":"1 1/2 cups water + 1/4 tsp extra salt","d":"Lighter body, but skin-on thighs and onions supply most of the flavor.","k":["water"]},"11":{"x":"zest of 2 lemons + 1/2 tsp extra salt","d":"Brighter and less funky; lacks the fermented depth of preserved lemon.","k":["lemon","salt"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Brown the thighs skin-side down and leave them alone until they release. The rendered fat becomes the base for the onions.","s":1},{"t":"Add the olives and preserved lemon late. Long cooking turns the rind bitter and the olives mushy. Rinse the lemon and use only the rind.","s":4},{"t":"Thighs are safe at 165F (74C), but braised thighs are best around 175 to 185F, when the connective tissue has melted. If the sauce is thin, reduce it uncovered.","s":5}],"rl":"PFVXXXXXXXMAXGGZZ"},"3533":{"lv":"RERRRRRROEERRRRREOEROR","w":{"2":"Garlic adds sweetness to the aromatic base; skipping leaves it a bit flat.","3":"Adds umami and sweet depth; fresh tomatoes alone give a thinner broth.","4":"Gives earthy flavor and golden color to the broth; skipping lightens both.","5":"Part of the classic Moroccan warmth; skipping is subtle.","6":"Earthy note that rounds out the spice mix; skip with little loss.","7":"Gives gentle sweetness and warmth; skipping leaves the broth more savory.","8":"A background bite only; skip freely.","11":"Peppery, earthy root; carrots or extra squash work in its place.","12":"Sweet and soft; skip or swap for more squash.","13":"Adds sweetness and color; skipping makes the stew less sweet.","14":"Tender vegetable that makes the dish more colorful; add late so it holds its shape.","15":"Adds acidity and body to the broth; skipping makes it less balanced.","17":"Fresh finish stirred in at the end; skip with little loss.","20":"Served on the side for heat; skip for a mild dish.","21":"Brings out the sweetness of the vegetables; undersalted broth tastes dull.","0":"Sautes aromatics; skipped, the couscous stew still works.","19":"Finishing richness; skipped, the couscous is a bit plainer."},"sub":{"0":{"x":"2 tbsp vegetable oil","d":"Neutral; olive oil adds a fruity note.","k":["vegetable oil"]},"19":{"x":"2 tbsp olive oil","d":"Dairy-free and fruitier; butter gives a slightly richer couscous.","k":["olive oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Add vegetables in order of cooking time, with roots first and zucchini last, so nothing collapses before the stew is done.","s":1},{"t":"Fluff the couscous with a fork and rub the grains between your fingertips to separate them. The butter melted in prevents clumping.","s":4},{"t":"Serve the broth separately so the couscous stays fluffy. Stirring harissa into a ladle of hot broth makes the traditional thin hot sauce.","s":5}],"rl":"FVXXXXXXZMVVVVVVPGBFGZ"},"3535":{"lv":"EERRRRERREREREERO","w":{"2":"Part of the Moroccan spice base; skipping dulls the chicken flavor.","3":"Gives earthy color and flavor to the sauce; skip for a paler filling.","4":"The sweet-savory signature of b'stilla; without it the pie tastes like a plain chicken pie.","5":"Floral aroma and color; turmeric already tints the filling, so skipping is a small loss.","7":"Fresh herbal lift in the filling; skipping makes it heavier.","8":"Adds green freshness; skipping loses a layer of herb flavor.","10":"Brightens the rich egg and chicken filling; skipping leaves it heavier.","12":"Gives the sweet note that balances the savory filling and crust; without it the pie is purely savory.","15":"Seasons the chicken and the sauce; the filling tastes flat without it.","16":"A background bite only; skip freely.","6":"Poaching liquid and sauce base; water gives a lighter sauce."},"sub":{"6":{"x":"2 cups water + 1/2 tsp extra salt","d":"Lighter sauce; the chicken and bones give most of the flavor.","k":["water"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Reduce the sauce well before adding eggs. A wet filling makes the phyllo soggy and the bottom tears.","s":1},{"t":"Stir the eggs over low heat until they form soft curds that are fully set, not runny, since they only bake for 30 minutes. Cool the filling completely before assembly or it will melt the butter and tear the phyllo.","s":2},{"t":"Keep unused phyllo under a damp towel and brush each sheet lightly with butter, all the way to the edges, so the layers crisp and flake.","s":4}],"rl":"PVXXXXMGGTABWSFZZ"},"3537":{"lv":"ERRRRROOREREORERO","w":{"1":"Adds moisture and binds the meatballs; skipping makes them denser.","2":"Fresh green flavor in the kefta; skipping makes the meatballs plainer.","3":"Adds fresh herbal lift; skipping makes the meatballs a bit heavier.","4":"Warm earthy flavor typical of kefta; skipping makes them taste plainer.","5":"Adds sweet color and mild flavor; skipping is subtle.","6":"Faint sweetness; skipping is barely noticeable.","7":"A touch of heat; skip for a mild dish.","10":"Garlic deepens the sauce; skipping makes it blander.","12":"A small amount only adds color; skipping is barely noticeable.","15":"Seasons the meatballs and sauce; the dish tastes flat without it.","16":"A background bite only; skip freely.","8":"Softens aromatics; skipped, the kefta fat still carries it.","13":"Adds savory depth to a small amount of liquid; skipped, flavor is just muted."},"sub":{"8":{"x":"2 tbsp vegetable oil","d":"Neutral; olive oil adds a fruity note.","k":["vegetable oil"]},"13":{"x":"1/2 cup water + pinch of salt","d":"Lighter sauce; tomatoes carry most of the flavor.","k":["water"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Grate the onion and squeeze out the liquid, then mix just until combined. Overworked ground beef turns tight and rubbery.","s":0},{"t":"Meatballs simmer in the sauce rather than browning, so cut one open to check that it has reached 160F (71C).","s":3},{"t":"Make the wells with the back of a spoon and spoon a little sauce over the whites so they set while the yolks stay soft. Cook until the whites are fully opaque.","s":4}],"rl":"PXXXXXXXFVXMXMPZZ"},"3538":{"lv":"EERRRROREEREEREOORO","w":{"2":"Garlic adds depth to the onion base; skipping makes the tagine milder.","3":"Earthy backbone of Moroccan spicing; skipping makes it taste flatter.","4":"Warm sweetness that pairs with the apricots and sweet potato; skipping is subtle.","5":"Adds color and mild sweetness; skipping is a small loss.","6":"A small amount mostly adds color; skipping is barely noticeable.","7":"Adds gentle heat and warmth to the spice mix; skip with little loss.","10":"A tender vegetable added late for color and bulk; skip or use more carrot.","13":"Sweet-tart chew that contrasts the savory spices; skipping makes the tagine more savory. Raisins work.","15":"Fresh green finish; skip with little loss.","16":"Crunch against the soft vegetables; skipping loses texture but not flavor.","17":"Brings out the sweet potato and apricot flavors; undersalted tagine tastes flat.","18":"A background bite only; skip freely.","0":"Only cooking fat; skipped, food sticks and browns poorly, so use an oil or substitute.","14":"Liquid the dish simmers or cooks in; skipped, there is not enough liquid."},"sub":{"0":{"x":"2 tbsp vegetable oil","d":"Neutral; olive oil adds a fruity note.","k":["vegetable oil"]},"14":{"x":"1 1/2 cups water + 1/2 tsp extra salt","d":"Slightly lighter, but the vegetables and spices carry the flavor.","k":["water"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Cook the spices in the oil for a minute until fragrant, then add the liquid. Blooming them in fat releases flavor that water alone will not, but stop before they darken and turn bitter.","s":0},{"t":"Cut the sweet potato and carrots to the same 3/4-inch size so they finish together. The zucchini goes in late so it keeps its shape.","s":1},{"t":"Toast the almonds in a dry pan and scatter them at the table; added early they go soft.","s":5}],"rl":"FVXXXXXXVVVPMWMGGZZ"},"52782":{"lv":"RRREROERERRERRORRRR","w":{"1":"Adds a warm, bright bite to the tomato sauce; skipping makes it milder.","2":"Gives the sauce a savory backbone; skipping is a modest loss.","4":"Acid that brightens the tomato sauce; skipping makes it sweeter and heavier.","5":"A small amount balances the acidity of the tomatoes; skip if they are ripe and sweet.","7":"Anise-scented bed that protects the rolls and perfumes the sauce; skip and line with extra vine leaves.","9":"Adds moisture and sweetness to the filling; skipping makes it denser.","10":"Grated potato binds the filling and keeps the lamb moist; skipping makes it crumbly.","12":"Fresh herbs give the filling its green flavor; skipping makes it plainer.","13":"Fresh herbs give the filling its green flavor; skipping makes it plainer.","14":"A little acid in the filling; skipping is barely noticeable.","15":"Adds depth to the filling; skipping makes it milder.","16":"Very potent; a small amount gives the lamb its warm, sweet spice.","17":"Warm spice that suits lamb and tomato; skipping is subtle.","18":"Grated into the filling for moisture; skipping makes the stuffing slightly drier.","0":"Lamb supplies fat too; skipped, the dish still works."},"sub":{"0":{"x":"2 tbsp vegetable oil","d":"Neutral; olive oil adds a fruity note.","k":["vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Rinse brined vine leaves and blanch them for a minute or two to remove the salt, then snip off the thick stems. Skipping this makes the dish salty and the leaves tough.","s":4},{"t":"Roll the leaves snug but not tight, about 2 teaspoons of filling per leaf. The rice swells as it cooks, and over-tight rolls split.","s":5},{"t":"Weigh the rolls down with an upturned plate so they do not float and unravel, and simmer gently. The lamb must reach 160F (71C) and the rice should be tender.","s":6}],"rl":"FXXMAWVVPXTBXXAXXXM"},"52843":{"lv":"RERERRRRRRRRERO","w":{"2":"Adds sweetness and body to the sauce; skipping makes it thinner.","4":"Garlic adds savory depth to the sauce; skipping makes it milder.","5":"Earthy warmth typical of tagines; skipping is subtle.","6":"Gives a warm, peppery note; skipping is a small loss.","7":"Floral aroma and golden color; 1/2 tsp turmeric replaces the color if skipped.","8":"A touch of sweet warmth that pairs with apricots; skipping is subtle.","10":"Sweet-tart fruit balances the spices and lamb; skipping makes the stew more savory.","13":"For serving; rice or bread works too.","14":"A garnish only; skipping hardly changes it.","0":"Lamb supplies fat too; skipped, the tagine still works.","9":"Balances sweetness; skipped, the dish is a bit sharper but still fine.","11":"Adds savory depth to a small amount of liquid; skipped, flavor is just muted."},"sub":{"0":{"x":"2 tbsp vegetable oil","d":"Neutral; olive oil adds a fruity note.","k":["vegetable oil"]},"9":{"x":"1 tbsp brown sugar","d":"Less floral than honey but gives the same sweetness.","k":["brown sugar"]},"11":{"x":"500 ml chicken or beef broth instead of water + cube","d":"Richer, less salty; taste for salt at the end.","k":["chicken broth","beef broth"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Brown the lamb in batches in a hot pan, then add the spices to the pan for a minute. Spices added to a crowded, wet pan taste raw.","s":1},{"t":"Keep the tagine at the barest simmer. A rolling boil tightens the lamb leg and keeps it chewy; it is done when a piece shreds easily with a fork.","s":2},{"t":"Add the butternut squash only in the last 20 to 30 minutes so it keeps its shape rather than dissolving into the sauce.","s":3}],"rl":"FVVPXXXXXWWMVBG"},"3539":{"lv":"EERERROEEERRR","w":{"2":"Softens the cilantro's edge and adds a fresh green layer; skipping makes the chermoula one-note.","4":"Earthy warmth that defines chermoula; skipping makes it taste plainer.","5":"Adds color and sweet pepper flavor; skipping is a small loss.","6":"A touch of heat; skip for a mild dish.","10":"Adds juicy acidity as the fish bakes; skipping makes the bake drier.","11":"Sweetness and color; skipping is a modest loss.","12":"Seasons the potatoes and fish; the bake tastes flat without it."},"tips":[{"t":"Slice the potatoes thin, about 1/8 inch, and give them the head start in the oven. The fish needs only 12 to 15 minutes, which is not enough to cook thick potatoes.","s":1},{"t":"Salt the fish just before baking and pat it dry. A long salted or lemon marinade turns cod mushy.","s":3},{"t":"Cod is done when it flakes and turns opaque, about 145F (63C). Pull it promptly, as carryover heat dries it out.","s":4}],"rl":"PXXXXXXFABVVZ"},"52797":{"lv":"EERRRRRRORO","w":{"4":"Raw green onion adds a mild bite; skipping is a small loss.","5":"Peppery base that contrasts the starchy potato; baby spinach or lettuce also works.","6":"Salty, creamy tang that balances the spicy potatoes; skipping makes it plainer.","7":"Fresh cooling herb against the harissa; skipping is a subtle loss.","8":"Crunch and richness; skip freely.","9":"Seasons the potatoes; they will taste flat without it.","10":"A pinch only; skip freely.","1":"Defining spicy flavor of the salad; skipped, it loses its heat.","2":"Dresses the potatoes; skipped, the salad is drier.","3":"Adds brightness; skipped, the dish is flatter but still works."},"sub":{"1":{"x":"1/2 tsp paprika + 1/4 tsp ground cumin + 1/4 tsp cayenne","d":"Similar warmth; real harissa blends have extra coriander and caraway.","k":["paprika","cumin","cayenne"]},"2":{"x":"2 tsp vegetable oil","d":"Neutral; olive oil adds a fruitier note.","k":["vegetable oil"]},"3":{"x":"1 tbsp white wine vinegar","d":"Sharper and less fruity; start with 2 tsp and taste.","k":["white wine vinegar"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Start the potatoes in cold, salted water so they cook evenly from the outside in, and drain them as soon as a knife slides in with no resistance.","s":0},{"t":"Dress the potatoes while they are still warm. They absorb the harissa and lemon far better than cold ones.","s":3},{"t":"Add the rocket just before serving, since the warm potatoes wilt it quickly.","s":5}],"rl":"BXFAXVGXGZZ"},"52869":{"lv":"EERRRRRRRE","w":{"3":"Adds sweet bite and color; skipping makes the stir-fry plainer.","4":"Garlic adds savory depth to the quick stir-fry; skip for a milder dish.","5":"Adds color and sweetness; swap for any quick-cooking vegetable.","6":"Gives crunch and freshness; swap for other green vegetables.","7":"A tender vegetable for bulk; swap for any other quick-cooking one.","8":"A leafy green that wilts into the sauce; spinach also works.","2":"Sautes aromatics; skipped, the lentils still work."},"sub":{"2":{"x":"2 tbsp vegetable oil","d":"Neutral; olive oil adds a fruity note.","k":["vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Tahini thickens as soon as water hits it; keep whisking with cold water and it relaxes into a creamy dressing. Taste it, since tahini needs more salt than you expect.","s":0},{"t":"Cook the vegetables in order of toughness over high heat, with beans and pepper first, zucchini next and kale last, so everything stays crisp-tender.","s":1},{"t":"Keep the pan on the heat only a couple of minutes once the tahini goes in; cooked too long it tightens, so loosen it with a splash of water.","s":2}],"rl":"FAFVXVVVVP"},"52850":{"lv":"RREOERREEO","w":{"1":"Sweet base that mellows the harissa; skipping makes it harsher.","3":"A pinch only; skip freely.","5":"Sweet fruit balances the harissa's heat; skipping makes the dish spicier and more savory.","6":"Adds protein and a nutty bite; swap for extra chicken.","9":"A fresh finish; skip with little loss.","0":"Softens aromatics; skipped, the dish still works.","4":"Defining spice of the dish; skipped, the chicken couscous is bland.","8":"Liquid the dish simmers or cooks in; skipped, there is not enough liquid."},"sub":{"0":{"x":"1 tbsp vegetable oil","d":"Neutral; olive oil adds a fruity note.","k":["vegetable oil"]},"4":{"x":"1 tbsp paprika + 1 tsp ground cumin + 1/2 tsp cayenne","d":"Milder than harissa spice, which is smokier; add more cayenne for heat.","k":["paprika","cumin","cayenne"]},"8":{"x":"200 ml hot water + 1/2 chicken bouillon cube","d":"Slightly lighter, but the chicken and harissa carry most of the flavor.","k":["water","bouillon cube"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Cut the chicken into small 1/2-inch pieces so it cooks through to 165F (74C) with no pink.","s":0},{"t":"Stir the harissa into the hot pan with the chicken and onion for a full minute so its flavors bloom in the oil.","s":1},{"t":"Cover tightly and do not stir during the rest. Steam cooks the couscous; stirring makes it gummy. Fluff with a fork at the end.","s":2}],"rl":"FXPXXWPBMG"},"3536":{"lv":"EEEEERORRRR","w":{"5":"Adds sweet color and mild flavor; skipping is a small loss.","6":"A touch of heat; skip for a milder zaalouk.","7":"Fresh herbal lift stirred in at the end; skipping makes it heavier.","8":"Adds green freshness; skipping loses a layer of herb flavor.","9":"Brightens the rich eggplant; skipping leaves it heavier and flatter.","10":"Eggplant needs plenty of salt; the dish tastes flat without it.","3":"Zaalouk is cooked in oil; skipped, the dish is dry and flat."},"sub":{"3":{"x":"3 tbsp vegetable oil","d":"Neutral; olive oil gives a fruitier flavor.","k":["vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Roast the eggplant until collapsed and golden. Salting it draws out moisture, which helps it brown instead of steam.","s":0},{"t":"Cook the tomatoes until jammy before adding the eggplant. If they are too wet, zaalouk turns watery instead of spreadable.","s":2},{"t":"Keep mashing and stirring until oil separates at the edges. That is the sign it is done; the flavor deepens if left to rest.","s":4}],"rl":"VVXFXXXGGAZ"},"3534":{"lv":"RERRRRROOREEEERRRRRR","w":{"2":"Adds sweetness and aroma to the base; skipping makes the soup flatter.","3":"Gives meaty depth; harira is often made without meat, in which case the soup is lighter.","4":"Gives earthy flavor and golden color; skipping lightens both.","5":"Part of the classic Moroccan spice trio; skipping loses warmth.","6":"Gentle sweet warmth; skipping is subtle.","7":"A background bite only; skip freely.","8":"A pinch only; turmeric already colors the soup.","9":"Adds umami and sweet depth; skipping makes the broth thinner.","14":"Fine noodles add body and a gentle starch; skip and the soup is still good.","15":"The flour slurry gives harira its velvety body; skipping makes it thinner.","16":"Fresh herbal lift stirred in at the end; skipping makes it heavier.","17":"Adds green freshness; skipping loses a layer of herb flavor.","18":"Acid that brightens the rich soup; skipping makes it heavier.","19":"Brings out the flavor of the lentils and tomatoes; the soup tastes flat without it.","0":"Sautes aromatics; skipped, the soup still works."},"sub":{"0":{"x":"2 tbsp vegetable oil","d":"Neutral; olive oil adds a fruity note.","k":["vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Whisk the flour with cold water until completely smooth and pour it in a thin stream while stirring. Hot water makes lumps. Simmer the full 8 minutes so the raw flour taste cooks out.","s":4},{"t":"Brown or green lentils hold their shape; red lentils would dissolve and thicken the soup. Taste a lentil for tenderness before adding the slurry.","s":3},{"t":"Add the lemon juice at the very end. Acid added earlier keeps lentils and chickpeas firm and dulls the fresh herbs.","s":5}],"rl":"FVVPXXXZXXMMPPBTGGAZ"},"53047":{"lv":"ERRRRROR","w":{"1":"Roasts sweet and adds savory depth; skipping makes the soup taste of carrot only.","2":"Roasts to a mellow sweetness; skipping makes it plainer.","3":"Earthy warmth; skipping makes the soup sweeter.","4":"Adds a citrusy, floral note; skipping is subtle.","6":"A small pinch of warm spice blend; skip freely.","7":"Lifts the carrot's sweetness; skipping makes the soup taste flat and sweet.","5":"Sautes aromatics; skipped, the soup still works."},"sub":{"5":{"x":"1 tbsp vegetable oil","d":"Neutral; olive oil adds a fruity note.","k":["vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Roast until the edges caramelize slightly, not just until soft. The color is where the sweetness and flavor come from.","s":2},{"t":"Blend with just enough water to move the blades, then strain for a silky soup, adding the rest of the water in the pan.","s":3},{"t":"Add the lemon juice off the heat and taste. A little acid keeps carrot soup from tasting merely sweet, but too much curdles the flavor.","s":5}],"rl":"VXXXXFXA"},"53118":{"lv":"EEERRRO","w":{"3":"Balances the tang of the sour cream; the porridge tastes flat without it.","4":"Traditional topping for a sweet-tart contrast; skipping is for a more savory bowl.","5":"Traditional dusting that adds warmth; skip for a plainer bowl.","6":"Only needed with low-fat sour cream, which releases little butterfat; skip otherwise."},"tips":[{"t":"Use full-fat (about 35 percent) sour cream. Low-fat splits and will not release the golden butterfat that is the traditional topping.","s":0},{"t":"Cook until the butterfat separates and rises, then spoon it off to serve on top. Add the flour gradually, whisking, to avoid lumps.","s":1},{"t":"Keep the heat low and scrape the bottom of the pot. Milk and flour scorch easily, and a burnt layer taints the whole porridge.","s":2}],"rl":"FTMZWXF"},"53123":{"lv":"EEEEER","w":{"5":"Optional thickener sprinkled between layers; skipping gives a thinner, clearer broth."},"tips":[{"t":"Layer the lamb fatty-side down with bone-in pieces. The bones and fat provide the flavor, and the whole peppercorns float in the broth, so warn guests.","s":2},{"t":"Cook low and never at a boil. It is ready when the lamb falls away from the bone and no pink remains.","s":4},{"t":"Fårikål is traditionally even better the second day. Chill it and skim the fat before gently reheating.","s":5}],"rl":"PVXZMT"},"53124":{"lv":"ROEEEREEORRRRO","w":{"0":"Optional salted meat that also gives a savory broth for cooking the dumplings; skip and use beef or vegetable broth.","1":"Optional serving meat; skip for a vegetarian version.","5":"Used for dusting and adjusting the dough; skipping is a small loss.","8":"Garnish only; skip freely.","9":"Sweet, earthy mash served alongside; skip for a simpler plate.","10":"Adds sweetness and color to the side mash; skip with little loss.","11":"Makes the mash creamy; milk works.","12":"Adds richness to the mash; skip with little loss.","13":"A pinch of warm spice for the mash; skip freely.","7":"Makes the broth the dumplings cook in; skipped, they cook in plain water."},"sub":{"7":{"x":"2 quarts store-bought beef or vegetable broth","d":"Same use, slightly less salty; taste before adding the dumplings.","k":["beef broth","vegetable broth"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Squeeze the grated raw potato dry in a clean towel. Excess water makes the dumplings fall apart or turn gluey.","s":2},{"t":"Cook the dumplings at barely a simmer; a rolling boil breaks them up. Test-cook one first and add a spoonful of flour if it falls apart.","s":4},{"t":"Dip the spoon in cold water between dumplings so the dough does not stick, and shape them smooth to prevent cracks.","s":5}],"rl":"PPBBTTZMGVVFFX"},"53115":{"lv":"EEEROO","w":{"3":"Softens the sharpness of the vinegar; skipping gives a harsher pickle.","4":"A little spice in the pickling jar; skip freely.","5":"Adds a subtle herbal note; skip freely.","2":"Pickling acid; skipped, there is no pickle."},"sub":{"2":{"x":"200 ml white wine vinegar or rice vinegar","d":"Milder and slightly cleaner than cider vinegar; make sure it is 5 percent acidity.","k":["white wine vinegar","rice vinegar"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Salting the onions draws out water and harshness so the pickle stays crisp. Rinse and drain them well so the pickle is not oversalted.","s":0},{"t":"Use warm rather than boiling brine; hot liquid cooks the onions soft and dulls their color.","s":1},{"t":"Pack the onions tightly and keep the jar in the fridge. This is not a tested canning recipe, so do not store it at room temperature.","s":2}],"rl":"VKKWXX"},"53117":{"lv":"EEROOOROEEERROOO","w":{"2":"Adds brightness to the horseradish cream; skip and use extra horseradish.","3":"A pinch only; skip freely.","4":"A pinch only; skip freely.","6":"Crispy shallots add crunch and savory sweetness on top; skipping makes it plainer.","7":"A pinch to season the shallots; skip freely.","11":"Gives the rye toast a crisp, rich finish; skip and toast the bread dry.","13":"A final garnish; skip freely.","14":"A pinch only; skip freely.","15":"A pinch only; skip freely.","0":"Base of the horseradish cream; skipped, the topping has no cream.","5":"Tiny amount; skipped, there is little noticeable effect.","8":"The star vegetable of the dish; skipped, the smorrebrod loses its main topping.","12":"Nutty salty cheese topping; skipped, the dish is less rich."},"sub":{"0":{"x":"150 ml sour cream or thick Greek yogurt","d":"A bit tangier and thinner; yogurt gives a lighter cream.","k":["sour cream","greek yogurt"]},"5":{"x":"1/4 cup canola or vegetable oil for shallow frying","d":"Neutral; any frying oil works.","k":["vegetable oil","canola oil"]},"8":{"x":"8 more green asparagus spears","d":"Greener and less delicate; no peeling needed.","k":["asparagus"]},"12":{"x":"100 g aged cheddar or parmesan, finely grated","d":"Västerbotten is nutty and salty; parmesan is the closest.","k":["parmesan","aged cheddar"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Use fresh, finely grated horseradish or well-drained jarred horseradish, not the creamed type. Add it gradually and chill 30 minutes, since its heat blooms as it sits.","s":0},{"t":"Peel white asparagus from just below the tip. The skin is bitter and woody, and unpeeled spears stay stringy.","s":3},{"t":"Plunge the blanched asparagus into ice water right away to keep the green color and crisp texture, and shave the raw spears paper-thin for contrast.","s":4}],"rl":"FXAZZFGZVVBFGGZZ"},"53125":{"lv":"EREEORRRE","w":{"4":"Background heat only; skip freely.","5":"Classic Scandinavian warmth; skipping makes the patties plainer.","7":"Water and starch keep lean beef patties juicy; skipping makes them drier.","1":"Cooks the onions; skipped, oil can do it.","6":"Thickens or binds; skipped, it is a bit looser.","8":"Cooking fat for browning; skipped, it sticks and browns poorly."},"sub":{"1":{"x":"1 tbsp vegetable oil","d":"Less rich; butter gives better caramelization.","k":["vegetable oil"]},"6":{"x":"1 tbsp all-purpose flour","d":"Binds similarly; slightly less clean texture.","k":["flour"]},"8":{"x":"2 tbsp vegetable oil","d":"Less browned-butter flavor; use oil and butter together to avoid scorching.","k":["vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Caramelized onions need time, closer to 20 minutes on low heat than the stated cook time. Add a splash of water if they stick.","s":0},{"t":"Mix gently and add the water gradually. The starch and water make lean beef patties juicy, but overmixing makes them rubbery.","s":1},{"t":"Score the grid so the patties cook evenly and hold the sauce. Ground beef patties must reach 160F (71C).","s":2}],"rl":"VFPZZXTMF"},"53121":{"lv":"EEREEERRRO","w":{"2":"Adds a touch of sweetness and helps browning; skipping is a small loss.","6":"Used for rolling; use as little as possible, since too much makes tough lefse.","7":"For spreading on the finished lefse; skip for plain lefse.","8":"For the butter-sugar spread; skip for a savory lefse.","9":"Loosens the spread; skip freely.","4":"Fat and moisture in the lefse dough; skipped, it is drier and less tender."},"sub":{"4":{"x":"1/4 cup half-and-half","d":"Slightly less rich; whole milk also works.","k":["half-and-half","whole milk"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Use starchy russets and rice them, never mash or process; any lumps tear the dough and overworked potatoes turn gluey.","s":0},{"t":"Chill the dough thoroughly. Warm dough sticks and absorbs too much flour, while cold dough rolls out thin.","s":1},{"t":"Roll thin enough to see through and cook on a hot, dry griddle until freckled. Stack under a damp cloth so the lefse stays soft and pliable.","s":6}],"rl":"BZWFFTTFWF"},"53122":{"lv":"ERRRREEEEERO","w":{"1":"Garlic browned in butter flavors the roux; skipping makes the soup milder.","2":"Adds body and starchy sweetness; skip for a thinner, more fish-forward soup.","3":"Adds sweetness and color; skipping is a modest loss.","4":"Gives a mild, sweet onion flavor; skip with little loss.","10":"Sweet shellfish that makes the soup richer; skip and use more fish.","11":"A fresh garnish; skip freely.","5":"Fat for the roux; skipped, the soup is not thickened.","7":"Liquid the dish simmers or cooks in; skipped, there is not enough liquid.","9":"Gives the soup its creaminess; skipped, it is no longer a creamy fish soup."},"sub":{"5":{"x":"3 tbsp olive oil","d":"Less rich than butter; the roux works the same way.","k":["olive oil"]},"7":{"x":"4 cups chicken broth + 1 tbsp lemon juice","d":"Less seafood flavor; clam juice mixed with water is closer.","k":["chicken broth","clam juice"]},"9":{"x":"1 1/4 cups sour cream","d":"Tangier and less stable; add it off the heat and never boil.","k":["sour cream"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Cook the butter-flour roux for a minute before adding the stock, then pour the stock in gradually while whisking. A roux that is not cooked tastes of raw flour, and adding stock too fast gives lumps.","s":1},{"t":"Cut the vegetables into thin strips so they cook in 10 minutes. The fish goes in only at the end and needs just a few minutes.","s":3},{"t":"Add the fish and shrimp off the heat or at the barest simmer; fish is done at 145F (63C) and shrimp when opaque. Boiling sour cream or crème fraîche will curdle it.","s":4}],"rl":"PXVVVFTMMFPG"},"53126":{"lv":"REEERRERERO","w":{"4":"Sweet, earthy root that is part of lapskaus's classic flavor; carrots or potatoes work in its place.","5":"Earthy sweetness; swap for more carrot or potato.","7":"Adds herbal sweetness; skip with little loss.","9":"Mild onion sweetness; skip or use more onion.","10":"A garnish only; skip freely.","0":"Browns the beef; skipped, the beef still browns lightly.","3":"Liquid the dish simmers or cooks in; skipped, there is not enough liquid."},"sub":{"0":{"x":"2 tbsp vegetable oil","d":"Neutral; olive oil adds a fruity note.","k":["vegetable oil"]},"3":{"x":"3 cups water + 1 beef bouillon cube","d":"Slightly lighter; the browned beef adds depth.","k":["water","bouillon cube"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Pat the beef dry and brown it in batches; the browned bits on the bottom of the pot are the flavor base of the stew.","s":0},{"t":"Chuck-type beef needs the long simmer. If it is still firm after the vegetables are done, simmer longer, and add the roots later so they do not dissolve.","s":1},{"t":"Press a few potato cubes against the side of the pot to thicken the broth to a stew-like consistency.","s":3}],"rl":"FPXMVVVVBVG"},"53116":{"lv":"EEEEEEREEOEEER","w":{"6":"Gives the buns their Scandinavian aroma; skipping makes them taste like plain cinnamon rolls.","9":"Only oils the counter for kneading; skip and use flour or a bit of butter.","13":"Gives gloss and holds the cinnamon sugar on top; skipping gives paler buns.","4":"Strong flour gives the buns their structure; skipped, there is no dough."},"sub":{"4":{"x":"250 g plain (all-purpose) flour","d":"Slightly softer and less chewy; use a little less milk if the dough is slack.","k":["all-purpose flour"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Cool the milk and butter to lukewarm, below about 110F. Hot liquid kills yeast and scrambles the eggs.","s":2},{"t":"Resist the urge to add flour to the sticky dough. Knead on an oiled surface until it is smooth and stretches thin without tearing.","s":3},{"t":"Cut the rolled log with unflavored dental floss for clean slices, and pack the buns close in the tin so they rise up rather than out.","s":8}],"rl":"FMZSSLXWSFFWXG"},"53119":{"lv":"EEERREEEEEERRRR","w":{"3":"Gives the dough its Scandinavian aroma; skipping makes the buns plainer.","4":"Adds sweet floral flavor to the dough; skipping is a small loss.","12":"Makes the white glaze that finishes the buns; skip for plain buns with custard.","13":"Thins the glaze to a spreadable consistency; add a little at a time.","14":"Traditional topping that sticks to the glaze; skip for a plainer finish.","11":"Vanilla flavor of the custard; skipped, the custard is plainer."},"sub":{"11":{"x":"1 tbsp vanilla extract","d":"Fewer visible specks; stir it in after the custard comes off the heat.","k":["vanilla extract"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Warm the milk to lukewarm, around 100 to 110F, before adding the yeast. Hot milk kills the yeast.","s":0},{"t":"Cook the custard until it thickens and bubbles once. The cornstarch must come to a boil to set, and the custard should be covered with plastic touching the surface so no skin forms.","s":-1},{"t":"Cool the buns completely before glazing and topping with coconut, or the glaze melts and runs.","s":-1}],"rl":"MLWXXSFMWTTXWMG"},"53127":{"lv":"EEERRR","w":{"3":"Used for the icing that decorates the rings; skip for plain rings.","4":"Binds the icing and helps it set; skip with the icing.","5":"Keeps the icing white and helps it set; skip with the icing."},"tips":[{"t":"Grind the almonds finely and knead with warm hands. The dough should be firm but not sticky; add the last egg white drop by drop so it does not go slack.","s":1},{"t":"Bake just until golden. The rings should stay soft and chewy inside, and overbaked ones turn hard. Cool completely in the forms before turning them out.","s":5},{"t":"This icing uses raw egg white, so use pasteurized egg whites or meringue powder for pregnant guests, small children, or anyone who should avoid raw egg.","s":-1}],"rl":"BTWWTA"},"53128":{"lv":"EEEEEREEEEEEEERE","w":{"14":"Toasts on the meringue for a nutty crunch; skipping loses crunch but not flavor.","5":"Vanilla flavor of the custard; skipped, the custard is plainer."},"sub":{"5":{"x":"2 tsp vanilla extract","d":"No visible vanilla specks; stir it in after the custard comes off the heat.","k":["vanilla extract"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Temper the yolks by pouring in about half the hot milk slowly while whisking, then return everything to the pan and cook until the custard bubbles. The cornstarch must boil to thicken, and boiled custard is safe for the eggs.","s":3},{"t":"Whip the whites with the sugar until stiff and glossy, and rub a little between your fingers to check that the sugar is dissolved. Grit means keep whipping.","s":9},{"t":"Fold the cold custard into the whipped cream gently so the cream stays airy. Assemble no more than a few hours before serving or the meringue will weep.","s":12}],"rl":"TTWFMXFWSSLMSWGF"},"53129":{"lv":"EEERORER","w":{"3":"Gives krumkake its distinct Scandinavian aroma; skipping makes them plainer.","4":"A pinch only; skip freely.","5":"Balances the sweetness and lifts the flavor; skip and they taste flat.","7":"Only to thin the batter if it is too thick; skip if the batter pours easily."},"tips":[{"t":"Whisk the eggs and sugar until pale and thick. The aerated base gives krumkake its lacy, crisp texture.","s":0},{"t":"Rest the batter at least 30 minutes so the flour hydrates and the batter bakes into thin, crisp cones.","s":3},{"t":"Roll each cookie around the cone within about 10 seconds of lifting it. Once they cool they harden and crack; keep the iron clean and lightly greased.","s":5}],"rl":"SWFXXZSM"},"53130":{"lv":"EEEEEEEERE","w":{"8":"Perfumes the egg cream; skipping makes it taste plainer.","6":"Makes the cake filling creamy; skipped, the filling is thin or too dry."},"sub":{"6":{"x":"1/2 cup half-and-half","d":"Slightly thinner and less rich; cook a few minutes longer.","k":["half-and-half"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Keep part of the almonds coarse for texture, and whisk the eggs and sugar only until combined. Too much air makes the base rise and then collapse.","s":3},{"t":"Stir the egg cream constantly over low-medium heat until it coats the back of a spoon and reaches at least 160F (71C). If it starts to scramble, pull it off and whisk hard.","s":6},{"t":"Cool the cake completely before frosting, or the cream melts into it.","s":9}],"rl":"SSWSLTFWXF"},"53131":{"lv":"EREEEEEEEEORR","w":{"1":"Rounds out the crust flavor; skipping makes it plainer.","10":"Optional aroma; skip and add an extra drop of almond extract.","11":"Boosts the almond flavor; skipping makes the filling milder.","12":"Gives the lattice a golden shine; skipping gives a paler top.","7":"Sweetener and moisture in the pastry; skipped, it is bland and dry."},"sub":{"7":{"x":"3/4 cup white sugar","d":"Less caramel and molasses flavor but still sweet.","k":["sugar"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Keep the dough cold. Warm butter makes the crust greasy and hard to roll, so chill it at least 30 minutes.","s":2},{"t":"Press the dough into the pan with your fingertips and patch any cracks. Perfection is not needed since the filling covers it.","s":9},{"t":"Roll the lattice strips between parchment sheets and chill them before cutting. Bake until the top is golden; the filling stays soft, so cool before slicing.","s":11}],"rl":"WXFSSLBWFTXXG"},"53132":{"lv":"EEEEEEERRERRRO","w":{"7":"Rounds out the almond flavor; skip with little loss.","8":"A small amount stabilizes the filling; skipping is a small loss.","10":"Boosts the almond flavor of the filling; skipping makes it milder.","11":"Makes the glaze; skip for a plain tartlet.","13":"A hint of almond in the glaze; skip freely.","12":"Thins and glosses the glaze; skipped, use milk or water."},"sub":{"12":{"x":"3 tbsp milk or water","d":"Slightly thinner, less glossy glaze; add a little at a time.","k":["whole milk","water"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Press the dough thinly into the muffin pan and chill it before filling. A cold shell shrinks less and bakes tender.","s":2},{"t":"Let the melted butter cool before stirring it into the egg mixture. Hot butter scrambles the eggs.","s":5},{"t":"Fill the shells only three-quarters full since the filling puffs, and cool completely on a rack before glazing.","s":7}],"rl":"SWFTTWFXTBXWMX"},"3081":{"lv":"EERRREROOER","w":{"2":"Adds savory umami and helps the skin brown; skipping makes the chicken less deep in flavor.","3":"Acid that tenderizes and brightens the marinade; lemon or extra vinegar works.","6":"Gives color and mild flavor; skipping makes the chicken paler.","7":"A faint herbal note; skip freely.","8":"Background heat; skip freely.","10":"Adds tang and helps the skin crisp; white vinegar or extra lime works.","4":"Carries flavor in the marinade or dressing; skipped, the dish is drier but intact."},"sub":{"3":{"x":"2 tbsp lemon juice","d":"Slightly sweeter and less floral than lime.","k":["lemon juice"]},"4":{"x":"2 tbsp canola or olive oil","d":"Neutral; helps crisp the skin.","k":["canola oil","olive oil"]},"10":{"x":"1 tbsp white vinegar","d":"Slightly sharper; use a bit less if you want it gentler.","k":["white vinegar"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Pat the chicken dry and rub the paste under the skin as well as on it. Leave it uncovered in the fridge for the last hour so the skin dries out and crisps.","s":1},{"t":"Start at 450F so the skin renders and browns, then drop to 375F so the meat cooks through without drying.","s":3},{"t":"Check the thickest part of the thigh, not touching bone; it must reach 165F (74C). Rest the bird 10 minutes so the juices stay in.","s":4}],"rl":"PXXAFXXXZZA"},"3083":{"lv":"EEEEERRERRRRROR","w":{"5":"Sweet base for the sauce; skipping makes it less rounded.","6":"Adds savory depth to the aji paste; skip with a small loss.","9":"Salty, nutty and helps thicken; skip and add a pinch more salt.","10":"Traditional base for the sauce; rice or bread can take their place.","11":"Soaks up the sauce; skip if you serve with potatoes.","12":"A traditional garnish adding richness; skip freely.","13":"Salty garnish; skip freely.","14":"Seasons the sauce; taste near the end.","3":"Liquid of the creamy sauce; skipped, the sauce is too thick.","4":"Only cooking fat; skipped, food sticks and browns poorly, so use an oil or substitute.","8":"Thickens and enriches the sauce; skipped, it is thinner."},"sub":{"3":{"x":"1 cup half-and-half","d":"Richer than milk; evaporated milk also works.","k":["half-and-half","evaporated milk"]},"4":{"x":"3 tbsp vegetable oil","d":"Neutral; olive oil adds a fruity note.","k":["vegetable oil"]},"8":{"x":"1/2 cup pecans or almonds","d":"Similar richness; almonds are less earthy and pecans are slightly sweeter.","k":["pecans","almonds"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Poach the chicken gently, never at a rolling boil, until it reaches 165F (74C), and keep the broth since it forms the sauce base.","s":0},{"t":"Cook the aji amarillo paste in oil for 3 minutes until it darkens and smells sweet. Raw aji tastes harsh.","s":2},{"t":"The sauce should coat a spoon like thick cream. The bread keeps thickening as it simmers, so thin with a little broth if it tightens.","s":4}],"rl":"PMTMFXXXTXBBGGZ"},"3082":{"lv":"EERRRRERO","w":{"2":"Adds sweet crunch and bite; rinse it so it does not overpower the fish.","3":"Adds fruity heat; skipping makes the ceviche milder.","4":"Fresh herbal lift; skipping is a small loss.","5":"Adds aroma; skipping is a small loss.","7":"Traditional sweet foil to the acid and heat; skipping makes the plate sharper.","8":"Adds starchy crunch on the side; skip freely."},"tips":[{"t":"Use the freshest fish you can find, kept very cold. Citrus does not make fish safe the way cooking does, so avoid serving it to pregnant or immunocompromised guests.","s":1},{"t":"Marinate only 10 to 15 minutes. Longer and the fish turns chalky and firm as the acid keeps cooking it.","s":3},{"t":"Fold in the onion and cilantro at the last minute and serve right away. The citrus marinade left in the bowl is the prized leche de tigre.","s":4}],"rl":"PAXXGXZBB"},"3080":{"lv":"EEEEERRRRRERR","w":{"5":"Garlic adds savory depth to the stir-fry; skipping is a small loss.","6":"A chifa touch that deepens the sauce; skip with a small loss.","8":"Adds heat and freshness; skipping makes it milder.","9":"Fresh herbal lift at the end; skipping is a small loss.","11":"Served underneath to soak up the sauce; skip for a lighter meal with only fries.","12":"Seasons the beef; the soy sauce adds salt too, so go lightly.","2":"Only cooking fat; skipped, food sticks and browns poorly, so use an oil or substitute.","7":"Adds brightness; skipped, the dish is flatter but still works."},"sub":{"2":{"x":"2 tbsp canola oil","d":"Neutral and high-heat tolerant; avoid olive oil here.","k":["canola oil"]},"7":{"x":"2 tbsp apple cider vinegar","d":"Slightly fruitier and sweeter; rice vinegar also works.","k":["apple cider vinegar","rice vinegar"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Get the pan very hot and sear the beef in small batches. Overcrowding steams it and you lose the charred, smoky flavor lomo saltado is known for.","s":1},{"t":"Cook the tomatoes and onions only until they are just tender. They should keep some crunch and not turn to sauce.","s":3},{"t":"Fold in the fries at the last second so they absorb some sauce but stay partly crisp.","s":4}],"rl":"PXFVVXXAXGBBZ"},"3084":{"lv":"EEEERREROO","w":{"4":"Thickens the sauce and adds body; skip and add a bit more cheese.","5":"Adds a subtle savory depth; skipping is a small loss.","7":"Traditional garnish that adds protein; skip for a lighter plate.","8":"A salty garnish; skip freely.","9":"Just for presentation; skip freely.","2":"Cheese is the base of the huancaina sauce; skipped, there is no sauce.","3":"Liquid body of the sauce; skipped, it is too thick.","6":"Emulsifies the sauce into a smooth body; skipped, it is thicker and less silky."},"sub":{"2":{"x":"8 oz queso fresco or drained cottage cheese plus 1/2 tsp salt","d":"Milder and less salty; add a pinch of salt.","k":["queso fresco","cottage cheese"]},"3":{"x":"1/2 cup half-and-half","d":"Slightly thinner and less caramel flavor.","k":["half-and-half"]},"6":{"x":"2 tbsp canola oil","d":"Neutral; olive oil adds a fruity note.","k":["canola oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Add the oil gradually while blending so the sauce emulsifies and thickens. Taste the aji for heat and adjust with milk.","s":0},{"t":"The sauce should coat the back of a spoon. It thickens as it chills, so loosen it with a splash of milk before serving.","s":1},{"t":"Serve the potatoes warm or at room temperature. Cold from the fridge, they taste starchy and the sauce does not cling.","s":3}],"rl":"BXFMTXFGGG"},"3496":{"lv":"EERRRRO","w":{"2":"Stands in for aji limo with fruity heat; skipping makes it milder.","3":"Fresh herbal lift; skipping is a small loss.","4":"Softens the sharp acid; skipping makes the salad harsher.","5":"Seasons the onions; the salad tastes flat without it.","6":"Adds earthy depth; skip freely."},"tips":[{"t":"Soaking the sliced onions in cold salted water removes the sulfurous bite and keeps them crisp. Drain and pat them dry, or the dressing dilutes.","s":0},{"t":"Slice the onions paper-thin and across the grain, since thick slices stay sharp and chewy.","s":0},{"t":"Serve within 30 minutes. As the acid works on the onions they soften, turn bright pink, and lose their crunch.","s":4}],"rl":"VAXGFZX"},"53017":{"lv":"EREEEERREERO","w":{"1":"A small amount seasons the dough; skipping makes the pastry bland.","6":"Adds sweetness and moisture to the filling; skipping makes it plainer and denser.","7":"Sweet, earthy root that balances the beef; carrot works in its place.","10":"Seasons the filling; it tastes flat without it.","11":"A background bite only; skip freely."},"tips":[{"t":"Keep the butter cold and knead only until the dough is smooth, then chill it so it rolls thin without shrinking back.","s":4},{"t":"Cook the filling until nearly dry and the meat is no longer pink, ideally 160F (71C). A wet filling soaks the dough and bursts the seams.","s":8},{"t":"Cool the filling before you stir in the egg and before you enclose it. Hot filling scrambles the egg and melts the butter in the dough.","s":9}],"rl":"SZSSFFXVPTZZ"},"53021":{"lv":"ERRERERROE","w":{"2":"Cooked until soft and mixed in; adds sweetness and moisture to the filling.","6":"Adds savory depth to the filling; skipping is a small loss.","7":"Seasons the filling; it tastes bland without it.","8":"A background bite only; skip freely.","1":"Cooks the onion; skipped, the filling is slightly less rich.","4":"Part of the meat filling; skipped, use all beef."},"sub":{"1":{"x":"2 tbsp vegetable oil","d":"Less rich; cooks the onion the same way.","k":["vegetable oil"]},"4":{"x":"1/2 lb more ground beef","d":"Slightly leaner and drier; add a spoonful of grated onion for moisture.","k":["ground beef"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Freeze the whole cabbage overnight and thaw it, and the leaves peel off whole and pliable without boiling. Otherwise core it and simmer until they loosen.","s":0},{"t":"Use about 1/3 cup filling per leaf and leave room, since the raw rice swells as it cooks. Pack the rolls seam-side down in the dish so they do not unravel.","s":5},{"t":"Cover the dish tightly with foil and bake until the cabbage is tender, the rice is soft, and the meat reaches 160F (71C). A layer of chopped cabbage on the bottom prevents scorching.","s":7}],"rl":"VFXPPBXZZM"},"53294":{"lv":"ERRRERRERRROOR","w":{"3":"Sweet, softened onions form part of the topping; skipping makes it plainer.","5":"Adds savory depth to the mushrooms; skipping is a small loss.","6":"Keeps the topping creamy and adds richness; skip and use a little more butter.","8":"Salty, savory meat in the topping; skip for a vegetarian version.","9":"Smoky Polish sausage that gives the topping its street-food flavor; skip or use another smoked sausage.","10":"Gives the stretchy melt; cheddar alone browns but does not stretch.","11":"A garnish only; skip freely.","12":"A fresh garnish; skip freely.","13":"The traditional street-food finish; skip if you prefer.","1":"Toasts or sautes; skipped, oil can do it.","2":"Sautes the filling; skipped, it is slightly drier."},"sub":{"1":{"x":"1 tbsp vegetable oil","d":"Less rich than butter; browns the same.","k":["vegetable oil"]},"2":{"x":"2 tsp butter","d":"Richer; keep the heat low so the butter does not burn.","k":["butter"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Pre-bake and cool the baguette so the crumb stays crisp under the mushroom topping.","s":0},{"t":"Cook the mushrooms until their liquid has evaporated and they brown. A wet topping soaks into the bread and makes it soggy.","s":1},{"t":"Bake or broil until the cheese is bubbling and the edges are browned, and drizzle the ketchup on after baking so it does not scorch.","s":2}],"rl":"BFFVVXFGPPGGGG"},"53301":{"lv":"EERERREERERR","w":{"2":"Gives an earthy, caraway-like flavor that suits sauerkraut; skipping makes the goulash plainer.","4":"Adds savory depth; skipping makes it milder.","5":"Thickens the sauce slightly; skipping gives a thinner, more brothy goulash.","8":"Adds a subtle herbal note in the long simmer; skip with little loss.","10":"Softens the sourness of the sauerkraut; skipping makes it tangier and thinner.","11":"Adds a final tang and creamy swirl; skipping is a small loss.","0":"Only cooking fat; skipped, food sticks and browns poorly, so use an oil or substitute.","7":"Liquid the dish simmers or cooks in; skipped, there is not enough liquid."},"sub":{"0":{"x":"3 tbsp vegetable oil or butter","d":"Less porky flavor but browns the onions the same.","k":["vegetable oil","butter"]},"7":{"x":"1.5 l water + 2 beef bouillon cubes","d":"Slightly thinner flavor; the pork and sauerkraut supply depth.","k":["water","bouillon cube"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Paprika burns quickly and turns bitter, so stir it in with the liquid or off the heat, never into dry hot fat.","s":0},{"t":"Keep the simmer low and covered until the pork shoulder is fork-tender. If it is still firm, it just needs more time.","s":1},{"t":"Do not boil after adding the creams, which curdles them. Stir a ladle of hot liquid into the sour cream first to temper it.","s":2}],"rl":"FVXPXTXMXAFF"},"53302":{"lv":"ERERREE","w":{"1":"Brightens the rich pork fat; skipping makes the flavor heavier.","3":"Gives a peppery bite to the crust; ten grams is a lot, so reduce it for milder pork.","4":"Carries flavor in the marinade or dressing; skipped, the dish is drier but intact.","6":"Main braising and roasting liquid; skipped, the ham dries out."},"sub":{"4":{"x":"50 ml olive oil","d":"Fruitier flavor; both work to bind the paste.","k":["olive oil"]},"6":{"x":"500 ml chicken broth + 2 tbsp white wine vinegar","d":"Less aroma but similar acidity and moisture.","k":["chicken broth","white wine vinegar"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Add a small handful of leaves to the paste.","s":0},{"t":"Pork shoulder is a collagen-rich cut, so for sliceable, tender meat roast to about 195F (90C), far above the 145F (63C) safe minimum, until a fork twists in easily.","s":1},{"t":"Pat the skin dry and give it the final blast at 240C for puffy crackling. Watch it closely as it can scorch in minutes.","s":3}],"rl":"XXZZFPM"},"53306":{"lv":"ERRRRROOERRROOR","w":{"1":"A subtle herbal background in the long simmer; skip with little loss.","2":"Dried beans must be soaked overnight and boiled hard for at least 10 minutes, then simmered until completely tender; undercooked beans can cause illness. Use canned beans added late.","3":"Sweetness and body to the broth; skipping is a modest loss.","4":"Sweet aromatic base; skipping makes it plainer.","5":"Adds savory depth; skipping is a small loss.","6":"Sweetness and color; skip freely.","7":"Optional heat; skip for a mild borscht.","9":"Adds body and heartiness; skipping makes a lighter soup.","10":"Adds umami and sweet-tart depth to the broth; skipping makes it thinner.","11":"Sweet crunch that makes the borscht heartier; skip for a lighter soup.","12":"Fresh garnish; skip freely.","13":"Traditional finishing herb; skip freely.","14":"A cooling, creamy swirl that balances the sweet and sour broth; skip and it is more brothy."},"tips":[{"t":"Skim the foam during the first 10 to 15 minutes for a clearer broth. Ribs are safe at 145F (63C) but need to reach about 190F for tender meat.","s":0},{"t":"Add the beets after the other vegetables so they do not stain everything, and finish with a splash of vinegar or lemon to keep the color ruby red.","s":2},{"t":"Stir some hot broth into the sour cream before adding it, or swirl it into individual bowls. Boiling sour cream curdles it.","s":2}],"rl":"PXBVXXVXVBAVGGF"},"53023":{"lv":"ERERRO","w":{"1":"Mild, sweet onion that pairs with the salty herring; skipping is a small loss.","3":"A warm spice traditional with Polish pickled herring; skipping is a small loss.","4":"Adds a mild bite; skipping is subtle.","5":"A subtle herbal note; skip freely.","2":"The oil covers the fish; skipped, it is not the same dish."},"sub":{"2":{"x":"1/2 cup vegetable oil","d":"Neutral; olive oil adds more flavor, and any oil keeps the fish covered.","k":["vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Taste a piece after soaking. If it is still very salty, soak it again in fresh cold water or milk until pleasantly salty.","s":0},{"t":"Keep the fish completely under the oil in a clean jar. Exposed pieces spoil, and top up with oil if needed.","s":2},{"t":"Keep it in the refrigerator and use within two weeks, and do not leave it at room temperature.","s":3}],"rl":"PGFXXX"},"53296":{"lv":"RRERERERORRR","w":{"1":"Gently cooked shallots add sweetness to the filling; skipping makes it tangier.","3":"Sweet cabbage balances the sourness of the sauerkraut; skip for a tangier filling.","5":"Makes the dough softer and easier to roll; skip with little loss.","8":"Crispy shallots add crunch and savory sweetness on top; skip for a simpler finish.","9":"For dusting the shallots and the work surface; use as needed.","10":"A knob of butter gives the pierogi a rich finish; skipping makes them drier.","11":"A tangy, cooling finish; skip if you prefer.","0":"Sautes onion; skipped, the filling still works.","7":"Pan-fries the pierogi for crispness; skipped, they can be boiled only."},"sub":{"0":{"x":"1 tbsp vegetable oil","d":"Neutral; butter also works.","k":["vegetable oil","butter"]},"7":{"x":"1/2 cup vegetable oil for frying","d":"Any neutral frying oil works.","k":["vegetable oil","canola oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Squeeze the sauerkraut dry and let the filling cool completely. Wet or warm filling leaks and bursts the seams.","s":2},{"t":"Keep the seam free of flour and dampen the edges with water, then press firmly to seal. Re-roll the offcuts once for extra pierogi.","s":6},{"t":"Boil gently in batches, and once they float give them a minute more. A quick sear in butter afterward gives crisp edges.","s":7}],"rl":"FXVVSFSFGGFG"},"53297":{"lv":"RRROERROER","w":{"2":"Balances the lemon and softens the salad's acidity; skip if the pomegranate is sweet.","3":"A subtle herbal note; skip freely.","5":"Adds sweetness and juice; skipping is a modest loss.","6":"A mild onion bite; skip with little loss.","7":"Heat and color; skip for a milder salad.","9":"Salty, creamy bite against the sweet pomegranate; skipping is a modest loss.","0":"Carries flavor in the marinade or dressing; skipped, the dish is drier but intact.","1":"Adds brightness; skipped, the dish is flatter but still works."},"sub":{"0":{"x":"1/4 cup vegetable oil","d":"Milder; olive oil adds a fruity note.","k":["vegetable oil"]},"1":{"x":"1 1/2 tbsp white wine vinegar","d":"Sharper and less fruity; taste and adjust.","k":["white wine vinegar"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Break the pomegranate apart under a bowl of water so the seeds sink and the white pith floats off.","s":2},{"t":"Add the lettuce last and toss just before serving. Dressed leaves wilt in minutes.","s":1},{"t":"Scatter the pomegranate seeds and feta on top at the end so they stay bright and do not sink.","s":3}],"rl":"FAWXVVXXGG"},"53305":{"lv":"ERRRRERRERO","w":{"2":"Browned onion adds sweetness to the filling; skipping makes it plainer.","3":"Adds a piney aroma that suits the chestnuts; skipping is a small loss.","4":"Adds sweetness and freshness to the filling; skip with little loss.","6":"Nutty, sweet and starchy filling; chopped pecans or walnuts work.","7":"Tart contrast to the chestnuts; raisins can replace them.","10":"A touch of sweetness; skip freely.","1":"Softens aromatics; skipped, the braise still works.","8":"Liquid the dish simmers or cooks in; skipped, there is not enough liquid.","9":"Adds brightness; skipped, the dish is flatter but still works."},"sub":{"1":{"x":"2 tbsp vegetable oil","d":"Neutral; olive oil adds a fruity note.","k":["vegetable oil"]},"6":{"x":"140 g chopped walnuts or pecans","d":"Not as sweet or starchy as chestnuts but similar richness.","k":["walnuts","pecans"]},"8":{"x":"300 ml water + 1/2 vegetable bouillon cube","d":"Slightly lighter; add a pinch more salt.","k":["water","bouillon cube"]},"9":{"x":"1 tbsp red wine vinegar + 1/2 tsp sugar","d":"Sharper and less sweet than balsamic.","k":["red wine vinegar","sugar"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Blanch the leaves only 1 to 2 minutes until pliable, then shave the thick rib flat so the leaf rolls neatly without cracking.","s":0},{"t":"Roll the cabbage seam-side down and pack the rolls snugly in a single layer so they do not unravel as they cook.","s":2},{"t":"Cover with foil while it bakes so the rolls steam in the broth, then uncover to finish if the top needs color.","s":2}],"rl":"VFXXXBBVMAW"},"53307":{"lv":"EERRE","w":{"2":"Adds sweetness and helps the fermentation along; skipping makes the kraut plainer.","3":"The classic kraut spice; skipping loses its distinctive flavor.","4":"Salt is the preservative that makes this ferment safe; 1 tsp is far below the usual 2% by weight, so weigh the vegetables and use about 2 to 2.5% (about 15 to 20 g for this amount)."},"tips":[{"t":"Massage the vegetables firmly with gloves until they release enough liquid to cover themselves. Beets stain badly.","s":0},{"t":"Keep everything submerged under the brine by weighting it down. Any vegetable exposed to air can grow mold.","s":1},{"t":"Discard the batch if you see fuzzy mold in any color. This is a lightly salted ferment, so refrigerate it once it tastes pleasantly sour.","s":3}],"rl":"VVXXK"},"53309":{"lv":"EOEERRR","w":{"1":"Helps draw water from the cucumber and balances the salt; skip freely.","4":"Brightens the sour cream dressing; skipping makes the salad heavier.","5":"Adds tang; lemon alone is fine.","6":"Its anise note echoes the fennel; skipping makes the salad plainer."},"tips":[{"t":"Salt the cucumber for 10 minutes and pat it dry. That removes the water that would otherwise dilute the dressing.","s":0},{"t":"Slice the fennel paper-thin, on a mandoline if possible, since thick slices are tough and stringy.","s":0},{"t":"Dress just before serving. Even with salted cucumber, the sour cream loosens as the vegetables release water.","s":1}],"rl":"VWVFAAX"},"3413":{"lv":"EEERROO","w":{"3":"Brightens the creamy dressing; skipping makes it flat.","4":"Fresh herbal flavor central to Polish mizeria; skipping makes it plainer.","5":"Rounds out the tang; skip freely.","6":"A background bite only; skip freely.","2":"Creamy base of the salad; skipped, it is just dry cucumber."},"sub":{"2":{"x":"3/4 cup plain Greek yogurt","d":"Tangier and thicker; thin with 1 tbsp milk if needed.","k":["greek yogurt"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Slice the cucumbers paper-thin and salt them for 10 minutes. The salt pulls out water so the dressing stays creamy.","s":0},{"t":"Squeeze gently. Wringing too hard bruises the slices and makes them limp.","s":1},{"t":"Chill briefly and serve the same day, since the salt keeps drawing water out of the cucumbers and thins the cream.","s":4}],"rl":"VZFAXWZ"},"3414":{"lv":"EEEERRO","w":{"4":"Browned until nutty and tossed with the dumplings; skipping makes them plain.","5":"Gives crunch and a toasty flavor; skipping makes the dumplings softer and plainer.","6":"A fresh garnish; skip freely."},"sub":{"4":{"x":"3 tbsp olive oil","d":"Less nutty than butter, but still browns the crumbs.","k":["olive oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Let the drained potatoes steam dry for a few minutes. Wet potatoes need more flour, and the extra flour makes the kopytka heavy.","s":0},{"t":"Use only as much flour as needed for a soft, barely cohesive dough, and boil one test dumpling first to check it holds together.","s":2},{"t":"Cook in batches and lift them out as soon as they float, about 3 minutes. Overcooked kopytka turn gummy.","s":4}],"rl":"BSTZFGG"},"3415":{"lv":"EERRRRRO","w":{"2":"Thickens the sauce into a light roux; skipping makes it thinner.","3":"Makes the sauce that coats the beets; skipping makes them dry.","5":"Balances the lemon's tartness; adjust to taste.","6":"Seasons the beets; they taste flat without it.","7":"A fresh finish; skip freely.","1":"Fat for the roux; skipped, the beets are not bound.","4":"Adds brightness; skipped, the dish is flatter but still works."},"sub":{"1":{"x":"2 tbsp vegetable oil","d":"Less rich than butter; the roux works the same way.","k":["vegetable oil"]},"4":{"x":"2 tsp white or cider vinegar","d":"Sharper and less fruity; add a little at a time.","k":["white vinegar","apple cider vinegar"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Cook the roux for a full minute until it smells nutty and stops tasting of raw flour before adding the water.","s":0},{"t":"Acid keeps beets bright ruby red; without it they turn dull and brownish.","s":2},{"t":"Taste at the end and balance the sweet and sour. Polish buraczki should be pleasantly tart, not sugary.","s":4}],"rl":"VFTMAWZG"},"3416":{"lv":"EEEREROO","w":{"1":"Cooking liquid that seasons the kasha; water plus a little extra salt works.","2":"Fat for the onions and mushrooms; butter gives the classic rich, nutty flavor.","3":"Sweet, golden onion rounds out the earthy mushrooms; skipping leaves it flatter.","5":"Seasons the groats and mushrooms; without it the kasha tastes flat and bitter.","6":"A small peppery lift against the earthy kasha; easy to omit.","7":"Fresh green garnish; skipping barely changes the taste."},"sub":{"1":{"x":"2 cups water + 1/2 tsp extra salt","d":"Less savory depth; the kasha tastes more purely of toasted buckwheat.","k":["water","salt"]},"2":{"x":"3 tbsp olive oil or vegetable oil","d":"Less rich and no dairy sweetness, but the mushrooms still brown well.","k":["olive oil","vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Toss the dry groats with a beaten egg and toast them in a dry pan until the grains separate and smell nutty before adding hot liquid. This coats each grain so the kasha stays fluffy instead of gluey.","s":0},{"t":"Use a wide skillet and leave the mushrooms alone for 3 to 4 minutes before stirring; crowding or constant stirring makes them steam and turn grey instead of browning.","s":3},{"t":"Do not lift the lid or stir while the groats cook or rest; fluff with a fork only at the end so the grains stay whole.","s":1}],"rl":"BMFXVZZG"},"53019":{"lv":"RRERREEEERE","w":{"0":"Fat for frying the sauerkraut filling's onion; oil works too but butter tastes better.","1":"Sweetens and softens the tangy kraut filling; skipping makes the filling sharper.","3":"Fat for the potato-filling onion; any neutral oil will do.","4":"Adds sweetness and savoriness to the potato filling; without it the filling is bland.","9":"Seasons the dough; the potato filling and boiling water add some salt, but dough tastes flat without it."},"sub":{"0":{"x":"2 tbsp vegetable oil","d":"Less buttery flavor in the sauerkraut filling.","k":["vegetable oil"]},"3":{"x":"3 tbsp vegetable oil","d":"Less buttery flavor in the potato filling.","k":["vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Squeeze the sauerkraut hard in a towel before frying; wet kraut makes a soggy filling that pushes seams open while boiling.","s":0},{"t":"This sour-cream dough is soft and sticky. Rest it 15 to 20 minutes covered, and dust the board rather than kneading in extra flour, which makes tough, dry pierogi.","s":2},{"t":"Cool both fillings fully before filling, keep flour off the dough edge, and press out air as you seal. Simmer gently rather than at a hard boil, then pan-fry the boiled pierogi in butter for color.","s":3}],"rl":"FXVFXBSFSZL"},"53299":{"lv":"RRREEERRE","w":{"0":"Soaked roll acts as a panada that keeps the patties moist and tender; crushed crackers or bread slices work equally.","1":"Softens the roll; water works if you have no milk.","2":"Sweet fried onion keeps the meat juicy; skipping makes the patties plainer and drier.","3":"Fries the onion and patties; any neutral oil is fine.","6":"Gives savory depth; omit if you must, the marjoram still carries the flavor.","7":"Marjoram is the classic Polish seasoning for these patties; without it they taste like generic meatballs."},"sub":{"0":{"x":"2 slices white bread, crusts removed","d":"Slightly softer texture; same moisture-holding role as the roll.","k":["bread"]},"1":{"x":"120 ml water","d":"Slightly less rich; the soaked roll still softens fully.","k":["water"]},"3":{"x":"6 tbsp sunflower or canola oil","d":"Nearly identical; lard gives a more traditional taste.","k":["sunflower oil","canola oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Cool the fried onion before mixing it in; hot onion starts cooking the egg and fat in the beef and loosens the mixture.","s":0},{"t":"Mix just until combined and shape the patties with wet hands. Overworked ground beef turns rubbery.","s":2},{"t":"Press the crumbs on firmly and let the coated patties sit for 5 minutes so the coating sets. Fry over medium heat, not high, so the crust browns just as the beef reaches 160F (71C).","s":3}],"rl":"TMXFPTXXG"},"53308":{"lv":"EEEERR","w":{"4":"Feeds the yeast and gives a hint of sweetness that balances rye's tartness; use sugar or molasses, or skip with a slightly paler crust.","5":"Caraway is the traditional rye flavor; without it the loaf tastes plainer but still good."},"sub":{"4":{"x":"1 tbsp sugar or molasses","d":"Molasses gives a darker, deeper crumb; sugar is neutral.","k":["sugar","molasses"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Rye dough is sticky because it has little gluten. Wet your hands instead of adding flour, and don't expect the smooth stretch of wheat dough.","s":0},{"t":"Stop the rise when the dough is just about doubled; rye flour has weak structure and collapses if it overproofs.","s":1},{"t":"Cool the loaf completely, ideally 2 hours, before slicing. Rye crumb sets as it cools and slices gummy if cut hot.","s":3}],"rl":"SSLZWX"},"53310":{"lv":"EEEEEOE","w":{"4":"Makes the crumb soft and keeps it fresh; butter works but makes the loaf dairy rather than pareve.","5":"Garnish that adds crunch and a nutty note; skipping changes nothing structural."},"sub":{"4":{"x":"5 tbsp melted butter (about 70 g)","d":"Richer flavor, slightly less moist; no longer dairy-free.","k":["butter"]}},"tips":[{"t":"The ingredient list omits the 2 tsp fine salt mentioned in step 1; add it, because bread without salt tastes flat and ferments too fast.","s":1},{"t":"Weigh the three strands, roll them tapered at the ends, and braid loosely. The dough expands a lot, and a tight braid cracks open in the oven.","s":3},{"t":"Egg-wash twice, once before the final rise and once just before baking. The finished loaf should read about 190F (88C) inside and sound hollow when tapped underneath.","s":4}],"rl":"SWLSFGZ"},"53018":{"lv":"REERRRREERRORROOERRO","w":{"0":"Renders smoky fat for browning the meat; skipping loses some smokiness, though kielbasa covers most of it.","3":"Coats the pork so it browns and lightly thickens the stew.","4":"Background savoriness; the onion and sausage hold the stew up without it.","5":"Sweetens and rounds out the sour kraut; skipping makes it harsher.","6":"Earthy depth, traditionally dried forest mushrooms; skipping gives a plainer stew.","9":"Deglazes and adds acidity; swap broth plus a splash of vinegar.","10":"Subtle herbal background note over the long braise.","11":"A minor herb since marjoram and bay already cover it; skip freely.","12":"Classic Polish herb that perfumes the stew; noticeably flatter without it.","13":"Adds color and mild sweetness; the stew will be paler without it.","14":"Tiny amount; sauerkraut and kielbasa already supply plenty of flavor.","15":"A dash of heat; skip if you don't have it.","17":"Adds umami and rich color to the braising liquid.","18":"Adds mild sweetness and acidity; fine to omit if you have the paste.","19":"A dash of umami; the beef broth and sausage already provide it."},"sub":{"9":{"x":"1/4 cup beef broth + 1 tsp red wine vinegar","d":"Less fruity depth but still brightens and deglazes the pan.","k":["beef broth","vinegar"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Dry the pork cubes, flour them lightly, and brown in batches; crowding steams the meat. The browned bits on the bottom flavor the whole stew when you deglaze.","s":2},{"t":"Taste the sauerkraut first. If it is very sharp, rinse it before adding; if it is mild, use its brine for extra tang.","s":4},{"t":"Keep the pot tightly covered during the long oven braise. Bigos tastes even better after cooling and reheating the next day, so make it ahead.","s":5}],"rl":"PPPTXXVVAAXXXXXXMXVX"},"53020":{"lv":"EEERROORRROOR","w":{"3":"Leek adds sweet, mild onion flavor to the broth; skip and add a bit more onion.","4":"Celery supplies the aromatic backbone; the broth is thinner without it.","5":"A small piece of cabbage adds sweetness; skip freely.","6":"A single clove is a background spice; skip.","7":"Warm, peppery note typical of Polish broth; skipping gives a milder soup.","8":"Classic aromatic that deepens the broth.","9":"Parsley adds fresh, green flavor to the broth.","10":"Dill is a fresh finishing herb; add it if available, not worth a trip.","11":"Adds mild bite to the broth; ground pepper at the table works instead.","12":"Seasons the stock; a clear, flavorful rosol needs salt, but adjust at the end."},"tips":[{"t":"Start the chicken in cold water and skim the grey foam in the first 20 to 30 minutes. Keep the pot at a bare simmer; a hard boil emulsifies the fat and makes the broth cloudy.","s":2},{"t":"For the traditional golden color and light smoky flavor, char the halved onions in a dry pan or over a gas flame before adding them.","s":3},{"t":"Cook the noodles separately and add them to each bowl rather than the pot; starch from the noodles clouds the clear broth.","s":9}],"rl":"PXVXXVXXXXGZZ"},"53022":{"lv":"EEEEROE","w":{"4":"Seasons the batter; sweet or savory crepes taste flat without it.","5":"A tiny amount for browning and balance; skipping makes no real difference for savory fillings.","6":"Fat in the batter and pan; skipped, the crepes stick and are less tender."},"sub":{"6":{"x":"3 tbsp vegetable oil","d":"Same non-stick effect with a neutral taste; less buttery.","k":["vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Rest the batter 20 to 30 minutes so the flour hydrates and the air bubbles settle; the crepes then come out thin and tender instead of rubbery or torn.","s":0},{"t":"Treat the first crepe as a test. If it tears, whisk in 2 tbsp water; if it is lacy and full of holes, add a spoon of flour.","s":2},{"t":"Lift the edge to check; flip when the surface is matte and the underside is lightly golden. Stack the cooked crepes under a cloth so they stay supple for rolling.","s":3}],"rl":"SSMMZWF"},"53024":{"lv":"EEEEEE","w":{"5":"The filling defines rogaliki; any thick preserve works, but a runny jam leaks and burns."},"sub":{"5":{"x":"1 cup thick fruit preserves or marmalade","d":"Marmalade is tarter; use any thick preserve that won't run in the oven.","k":["marmalade","fruit preserves"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Use block cream cheese at room temperature, not whipped, and chill the dough until firm. Roll only half at a time while the rest stays cold; the dough turns greasy and tears quickly as it warms.","s":1},{"t":"Put only about 1/2 tsp of thick jam near the wide end of each triangle and roll toward the point with the tip tucked underneath. Too much jam leaks out and scorches on the sheet.","s":3},{"t":"Pull the rogaliki when they are just golden on the bottom; they firm up as they cool. Dust with powdered sugar only once cool or it melts into the surface.","s":6}],"rl":"FSFLSW"},"53292":{"lv":"EEEEERROOERRRO","w":{"5":"Rounds out the warmth of the ginger; piernik is still gingerbready without it.","6":"Gives the classic gingerbread depth; a bit sharp, so slightly flatter if skipped.","7":"A background spice; ginger and cinnamon carry the flavor.","8":"A tiny peppery kick typical of gingerbread; easy to skip.","10":"Plum jam is the traditional filling; skipping loses the tart contrast to the honey.","11":"Makes the chocolate glaze glossy and silky.","12":"The chocolate glaze coats the cake; skipping leaves it plain but fine.","13":"A little honey in the glaze for gloss and sweetness; sugar works.","3":"Flour and leavening for the cake; skipped, it does not rise."},"sub":{"3":{"x":"375 g plain flour + 4 1/2 tsp baking powder + 3/4 tsp salt","d":"Same rise as self-rising flour; mix the leavener in well.","k":["flour","baking powder","salt"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Let the melted honey, sugar, and butter cool until just warm before whisking in the eggs, or they scramble. Don't overmix the batter; it makes the crumb tough.","s":1},{"t":"Honey and sugar brown fast, so tent the cake with foil if the top darkens before a skewer comes out clean.","s":0},{"t":"Piernik improves with age; wrap the cooled layers and rest a day or two before filling and glazing, so the spices mellow and the crumb turns moist.","s":2}],"rl":"FWWSXXXXXSWFGW"},"53293":{"lv":"EEEEEERRERRRR","w":{"6":"Rounds out the flavor of the cake; skipping is barely noticeable next to the pistachio.","7":"Sweetens the mascarpone cream; unsweetened cream tastes sharp against the cake.","9":"Loosens the mascarpone so it spreads smoothly; skip for a firmer, richer filling.","10":"Boosts the pistachio flavor in the cream; without it the cream is plain.","11":"Tartness cuts the richness of the cream and nuts; skip for a plainer cake.","12":"Lime zest brightens the sweet cake; skip with a small flavor loss.","8":"Main filling or frosting base; skipped, the cake is unfinished."},"sub":{"8":{"x":"250 g full-fat cream cheese (softened) + 2 tbsp extra cream","d":"Tangier and less sweet; beat only briefly so it stays smooth.","k":["cream cheese","heavy cream"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Pulse the pistachios in short bursts; grinding too long releases oil and turns them to paste, which makes the cake heavy.","s":0},{"t":"Whisk the mascarpone cream only until it holds soft peaks. Overbeaten mascarpone turns grainy and curdled; if it does, stir in a spoonful of cold cream to rescue it.","s":1},{"t":"Cool the sponge completely before cutting and filling, and chill the assembled cake for an hour so the cream firms for clean slices.","s":2}],"rl":"FBSLWSXWFFXGX"},"53295":{"lv":"EEEEEEERRRR","w":{"7":"Helps stabilize the nut meringue so it doesn't weep; skip and use a bit more ground walnut.","8":"Stabilizes the meringue and keeps it from going sticky; cornstarch works the same.","9":"Makes the chocolate glaze silky; no good way to skip it.","10":"Melts into the glaze; chopped dark chocolate bar works better than chips.","1":"The method sifts this with the flour as the cocoa for the chocolate sponge, so use unsweetened cocoa powder here; without it the sponge is plain."},"sub":{"8":{"x":"1 tbsp cornstarch","d":"Same stabilizing effect in the meringue.","k":["cornstarch"]},"10":{"x":"100 g chopped dark chocolate bar","d":"Melts smoother and shinier than chips, which contain stabilizers.","k":["dark chocolate"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Make sure the bowl and beaters are completely free of yolk or grease before whipping the whites; a single fleck of yolk stops them from reaching stiff peaks.","s":1},{"t":"Add the sugar to the meringue one spoon at a time and keep whisking until it is stiff and glossy and no longer gritty when rubbed between fingers. Fold in the ground walnuts gently to keep the volume.","s":1},{"t":"Let the ganache cool a few minutes after melting; if it's too hot it runs off the cake instead of setting glossy. Cut the cooled layers with a serrated knife, sawing gently.","s":3}],"rl":"SXSWSWBTTFF"},"53298":{"lv":"EREEEERREEEERERROO","w":{"1":"Feeds the yeast in the starter; the leaven still works slowly without it.","6":"Vanilla sugar adds fragrance; skipping barely changes the dough.","7":"Perfumes the dough; omit with a slightly plainer taste.","12":"Thickens the jam so it doesn't run out of the doughnut during frying.","14":"Used for the glaze or dusting; paczki are good plain but less sweet and pretty.","15":"Lemon juice brightens the glaze; skip with a slightly flatter flavor.","16":"A dash that adds almond aroma; skip freely.","17":"Garnish for the glaze; purely decorative.","9":"Liquid and richness of the dough; skipped, the dough is dry.","11":"Defining filling; skipped, the doughnuts are plain.","13":"Frying medium; skipped, the food cannot be fried."},"sub":{"9":{"x":"200 ml whole milk + 2 tbsp melted butter","d":"Slightly less rich and tender than cream.","k":["whole milk","butter"]},"11":{"x":"125 ml thick fruit preserves or marmalade","d":"Rose or plum jam is traditional; any thick preserve works if it doesn't run.","k":["fruit preserves","marmalade"]},"13":{"x":"equal amount canola or sunflower oil (lard also traditional)","d":"Any neutral high-heat fat works.","k":["canola oil","sunflower oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Whisk the yolks over simmering water only until thick and about 160F (71C); stop before they scramble, and cool before adding to the flour so they don't hurt the yeast.","s":1},{"t":"Add a spoonful of vodka or spirit to the dough; it evaporates fast and traditionally keeps the doughnuts from soaking up too much oil.","s":3},{"t":"Fry at 175-180C (350F), covered for the first minute on the first side, then flip and fry uncovered. The pale band around the middle shows the paczki proofed well.","s":5}],"rl":"LWSMSWWXSFFWTFWAXG"},"53303":{"lv":"EERRE","w":{"0":"Sets the mousse; powdered gelatin works, but check it is bloomed first.","2":"Sweetens the tart berries; skip for a sharper mousse.","3":"Brightens the raspberry flavor and balances the cream."},"sub":{"0":{"x":"1 1/4 tsp powdered gelatin (about half a 7 g packet), bloomed in 2 tbsp cold water","d":"Sets the same; dissolve it fully in the warm purée.","k":["gelatin powder"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Sieve the cooked raspberries well to remove all seeds. Stir the squeezed gelatine into the warm (not boiling) purée until fully dissolved, then cool the purée completely before folding in the cream.","s":1},{"t":"Whip the cream only to soft peaks. Stiffer cream goes grainy when folded in and gives a heavy, dense mousse.","s":2},{"t":"Chill the mousse overnight; it needs the full time to set cleanly if you plan to unmould it.","s":2}],"rl":"SVWAF"},"53304":{"lv":"EEEEERRO","w":{"5":"Colors half the batter for the marbled look; skip for plain vanilla cakes.","6":"Loosens the batter a bit; add a splash more if it's stiff.","7":"Final dusting for looks; skip."},"tips":[{"t":"Brush melted butter into every ridge of the molds, then flour and tap out the excess. This is what releases the cakes cleanly.","s":0},{"t":"Alternate dollops of plain and cocoa batter and drag a skewer through once for a marbled effect; more stirring muddies the swirl.","s":1},{"t":"Mini bundts overbake in minutes, so check at 15 minutes with a skewer, and unmould after about 5 minutes in the tin; wait longer and the sugar sets and they stick.","s":3}],"rl":"FSWSLCMG"},"3519":{"lv":"EEEERREROO","w":{"4":"Sweet onion forms the base that the eggs and cod are cooked into.","5":"Garlic adds aroma; skipping leaves the dish milder.","7":"Fresh color and flavor against the rich eggs.","8":"Traditional garnish, salty and briny; skip freely.","9":"A small seasoning; the cod is already salty.","2":"Frying medium; skipped, the food cannot be fried.","3":"Only cooking fat; skipped, food sticks and browns poorly, so use an oil or substitute."},"sub":{"2":{"x":"2 cups sunflower or canola oil","d":"Any neutral oil fries the potato sticks just the same.","k":["sunflower oil","canola oil"]},"3":{"x":"1/4 cup vegetable oil or 4 tbsp butter","d":"Less fruity; butter adds richness.","k":["vegetable oil","butter"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Taste a flake of cod after soaking; if still very salty, soak longer. Cooking does not draw salt out, and the dish can't be fixed later.","s":0},{"t":"Dry the matchsticks thoroughly and fry them in small batches so they crisp rather than steam; they should stay crunchy under the eggs.","s":1},{"t":"Take the pan off the heat while the eggs are still glossy and creamy; residual heat finishes them. Serve to guests who are pregnant or elderly only when fully set.","s":4}],"rl":"PBFFXXTGGZ"},"3523":{"lv":"EERRREEEEOROO","w":{"2":"Sweet, golden onions between the layers balance the salty cod.","3":"Adds aroma to the onions; milder dish without it.","9":"Classic bechamel seasoning; tiny amount, optional.","10":"Browns on top and adds savory salt; skip for a milder, less crusty top.","11":"Fresh garnish; skip freely.","12":"Cod and parmesan are salty already; pepper is just a light accent.","4":"Softens the onions; skipped, the bake is a bit less rich.","5":"Fat for the roux; skipped, the sauce does not thicken.","8":"The cream sauce defines the dish; skipped, it is dry."},"sub":{"4":{"x":"3 tbsp vegetable oil or butter","d":"Slightly less fruity flavor; both soften the onions fine.","k":["vegetable oil","butter"]},"5":{"x":"3 tbsp olive oil","d":"Makes a looser, less rich roux; cook the flour for a full minute.","k":["olive oil"]},"8":{"x":"1 cup half-and-half","d":"Less rich and slightly thinner; simmer a minute longer to thicken.","k":["half and half"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Boil the potatoes in unsalted water and slightly underdo them; the cod and parmesan are salty, and the potatoes finish cooking in the oven.","s":1},{"t":"Cook the bechamel until it thickly coats a spoon. The cod and potatoes release liquid in the oven, and a thin sauce will turn watery.","s":3},{"t":"If the top is pale after baking, put the dish under the broiler for 2 minutes for a deeper, bubbling golden crust.","s":5}],"rl":"PBXXFFTMFXGGZ"},"53039":{"lv":"EEEORRRRRRRRERRRO","w":{"3":"Minor background note next to the chili and garlic; skip.","4":"Gives herbal depth to the marinade; mild change if skipped.","5":"Warm, citrusy note typical of piri-piri; skip with slightly less depth.","6":"Adds color and sweet, smoky notes to the marinade.","9":"Sliced raw for crunch and bite in the slaw.","10":"Adds sweetness and color to the slaw.","11":"Gives earthy sweetness and color; skip with a plainer slaw.","13":"Gives creaminess to the dressing; yogurt can cover it.","14":"Adds tang and lightens the mayo.","16":"A pinch of warm spice in the slaw; skip freely.","7":"Adds brightness; skipped, the dish is flatter but still works.","8":"Carries flavor in the marinade or dressing; skipped, the dish is drier but intact.","15":"Adds brightness; skipped, the dish is flatter but still works."},"sub":{"7":{"x":"2 tbsp lemon juice","d":"Brighter, citrusy flavor instead of sharp tang.","k":["lemon juice"]},"8":{"x":"2 tbsp olive oil","d":"More fruity flavor; works fine in the marinade.","k":["olive oil"]},"15":{"x":"2 tbsp lemon juice","d":"Brighter and fresher; lighter tang in the slaw dressing.","k":["lemon juice"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Wear gloves when chopping the chilies, and blitz the marinade until smooth. A smooth marinade clings to the skin and doesn't scorch like chunks.","s":0},{"t":"Spatchcock the bird (remove the backbone and flatten) for even, faster roasting. Cook until a thermometer in the thickest part of the thigh reads at least 165F (74C), then rest it.","s":1},{"t":"Dress the slaw only when serving. Salted cabbage and beetroot weep liquid and stain everything pink if dressed early.","s":2}],"rl":"PXXXXXXAFVVVVFFAX"},"53042":{"lv":"RERROEREEERRRO","w":{"0":"Rubbed over the steaks for aroma; skip and the sauce still has garlic.","4":"Parsley stalks perfume the marinade; skip them.","6":"Peppery rocket adds freshness in the roll.","11":"Mild onion bite in the green sauce.","12":"Adds punch to the sauce; skipping leaves it milder.","13":"A pinch to balance the vinegar; easy to omit.","2":"Carries flavor in the marinade or dressing; skipped, the dish is drier but intact.","3":"Adds sharpness; skipped, the dish is a bit flatter.","10":"Adds tang to the sauce; skipped, it is less lively."},"sub":{"2":{"x":"2 tbsp vegetable oil","d":"Less fruity, but fine as a marinade carrier.","k":["vegetable oil"]},"3":{"x":"1 tbsp red wine vinegar or lemon juice","d":"Slightly sharper than sherry vinegar.","k":["red wine vinegar","lemon juice"]},"10":{"x":"1 tbsp white wine vinegar or lime juice","d":"Keeps the sauce tangy; lime is brighter.","k":["white wine vinegar","lime juice"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Pound the fillets to an even thickness of about 1 cm. They then cook in about 2 minutes per side and stay tender.","s":0},{"t":"Sear in a very hot pan without crowding. Rest the steaks 3 to 5 minutes before slicing so the juices don't soak the bread.","s":2},{"t":"Blend the green sauce just before serving; the herbs lose their bright color if it sits. The recipe makes extra that keeps a few days in the fridge.","s":1}],"rl":"XPFAXBVXXXAXXW"},"53044":{"lv":"ERRORROER","w":{"1":"Marinade liquid that flavors the pork; chicken broth plus lemon works.","2":"Adds color and mild sweetness to the marinade.","3":"Served as wedges; skip.","4":"Brightens the marinade; the lemon wedge is not a replacement.","5":"Rubbed on the pork to keep it from sticking; any oil works.","6":"Traditional dollop on top; skip if you dislike mayo.","8":"Frying oil; any neutral oil is fine."},"sub":{"1":{"x":"200 ml chicken broth + 1 tbsp lemon juice","d":"Less tangy, more savory, no wine flavor.","k":["chicken broth","lemon juice"]},"8":{"x":"canola or sunflower oil for frying","d":"Any neutral oil; same result.","k":["canola oil","sunflower oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Bash the pork into thin escalopes of even thickness so it cooks in about 2 to 3 minutes per side. Pork is done at 145F (63C) followed by a 3-minute rest.","s":0},{"t":"Double-fry the chips: blanch at 130C until soft, drain and cool, then fry at around 180C until golden. This gives a fluffy middle and crisp shell.","s":3},{"t":"Don't marinate beyond 30 minutes in wine and lemon; the acid makes the surface mealy. Pour the plate juices over the chips so they soak up the flavor.","s":5}],"rl":"PMXGAFGBF"},"53040":{"lv":"EORREREEER","w":{"2":"Adds gentle heat to the shrimp and spring onion filling; skip for a milder filling.","3":"Garlic adds aroma to the filling; the onion and shrimp carry most of the flavor.","5":"Salty, tangy bite that seasons the filling; skipping leaves it blander.","9":"A small amount of acid relaxes the dough; skip with a slightly tougher, less tender crust.","1":"Tiny amount; skipped, there is little noticeable effect."},"sub":{"1":{"x":"1 tbsp vegetable oil or butter","d":"Butter adds richness, neutral oil is plainer; both soften the onions.","k":["vegetable oil","butter"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Keep the dough cold and handle it as little as possible, adding water a spoonful at a time. Rest it 20 minutes so it rolls thin without shrinking back.","s":0},{"t":"Cool the filling completely before folding, and keep it away from the dough edges. Warm, wet filling steams the dough and breaks the seal.","s":2},{"t":"Pat the shrimp dry and add them only briefly in the pan; they finish cooking inside the empanada, so cook them just until pink and they won't turn rubbery. Brush with egg yolk for a deep golden finish.","s":1}],"rl":"XFXXPXFSSA"},"53041":{"lv":"ERRERRO","w":{"2":"Garlic perfumes the marinade; skipping leaves the sardines milder.","4":"Brightens the oily fish; squeeze lemon on at the table if you skip it.","5":"Resinous aroma that suits oily fish; skip with a milder marinade.","6":"A little heat; skip if you don't want spice.","1":"Carries flavor in the marinade or dressing; skipped, the dish is drier but intact."},"sub":{"1":{"x":"2 tbsp vegetable oil","d":"Less fruity, but still carries the marinade and prevents sticking.","k":["vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Marinate only for a few hours at most; the lemon starts to cure the delicate flesh and makes it mushy if left overnight.","s":1},{"t":"Get the grill or pan very hot and oil the grate. Sardines stick until they've formed a crust, so don't try to move them until they release on their own.","s":2},{"t":"Turn once only. The skin should be charred and the flesh opaque at the bone; serve at once with lemon, since cold grilled sardines taste strongly fishy.","s":2}],"rl":"PFXXAXX"},"53043":{"lv":"EERRRORREEEE","w":{"2":"Gives gentle heat to the fish cakes; skip for a milder cake.","3":"Fresh, citrusy flavor typical of fofos; skipping makes them plainer.","4":"Warm earthiness that pairs with the haddock; noticeably flatter without it.","5":"Light peppery note; the chili and cumin already add spice.","6":"Adds aroma and savoriness.","7":"Adds gentle warmth; skip if you don't have fresh ginger.","8":"Binds the fish cakes; skipped, they fall apart.","11":"Frying medium; skipped, the food cannot be fried."},"sub":{"8":{"x":"2 tbsp cornstarch or fine breadcrumbs","d":"Binds the same way as flour.","k":["cornstarch","breadcrumbs"]},"11":{"x":"sunflower or canola oil, 1 cm deep","d":"Any neutral oil fries them the same.","k":["sunflower oil","canola oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Mash the potatoes dry and let them cool before mixing. Wet or warm potato makes the mixture sticky and the cakes fall apart in the pan.","s":1},{"t":"Check the poached fish for bones while flaking, and don't break it too fine; some texture keeps the fofos from turning pasty.","s":0},{"t":"Chill the shaped fofos for 20 minutes, then coat them in egg and crumbs. Fry at medium heat so the crust browns while the middle heats through.","s":2}],"rl":"PBXXXZXXTTTF"},"3403":{"lv":"ERERROO","w":{"1":"Seasons the crust; the potatoes are boiled in salted water too, but the coarse salt gives crunch and flavor.","3":"Crushed garlic perfumes the oil; skipping leaves the potatoes plainer.","4":"Piney aroma that complements the garlic; skip with a small loss.","5":"A light accent; the garlic and salt carry the flavor.","6":"Fresh green garnish added at the end; skip."},"tips":[{"t":"Punch the potatoes while they're still hot and dry from boiling, so the skins split and the flesh crumbles into craggy surfaces that crisp up.","s":2},{"t":"Roast on a hot tray and don't crowd them. Add the garlic and rosemary partway or keep the garlic in its skin, since loose garlic burns and turns bitter.","s":4},{"t":"Drain the potatoes well and let them steam dry for a minute before the oven; moisture is the main enemy of a crisp edge.","s":1}],"rl":"BZFXXZG"},"3404":{"lv":"EEEREER","w":{"3":"Seasons the batter; without it the coating is bland.","6":"Squeeze over the hot fritters to cut the fat; skip if you prefer.","5":"Frying medium; skipped, the food cannot be fried."},"sub":{"5":{"x":"2 cups sunflower or canola oil","d":"Any neutral oil at 350F fries them the same.","k":["sunflower oil","canola oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Use ice-cold sparkling water and stop mixing while the batter is still lumpy. Overmixing builds gluten and makes the coating heavy.","s":1},{"t":"Dry the blanched beans completely before dipping; any moisture makes the batter slide off in the fryer.","s":0},{"t":"Hold the oil at 350F (175C) and fry in small batches. If the oil cools, the fritters soak it up and turn greasy; salt them immediately after draining.","s":3}],"rl":"VSLZMFA"},"3405":{"lv":"EEEEERO","w":{"5":"Fresh lemon at the end lifts the whole dish; skipping leaves the broth flat.","6":"A small accent; clams and garlic dominate.","3":"Cooking liquid for steaming clams; skipped, they have no broth."},"sub":{"3":{"x":"1/2 cup chicken broth + 1 tbsp extra lemon juice","d":"Less fruity and acidic than wine, but still steams the clams.","k":["chicken broth","lemon"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Soak the clams in salted cold water so they spit out sand, and lift them out of the water rather than pouring through a strainer, which pours the grit back over them.","s":0},{"t":"Steam only until the shells open, then take the pot off the heat. Overcooked clams are tough and rubbery. Throw away any that stay closed afterward.","s":2},{"t":"Stir in the cilantro off the heat so it stays bright and green; serve with plenty of bread to soak the broth.","s":3}],"rl":"PFXMXAZ"},"3406":{"lv":"EERRREEORO","w":{"2":"Sweet onion is the base of the tomato flavor; skipping leaves the rice thinner tasting.","3":"Adds aroma; the tomatoes and onion carry the dish.","4":"Deepens and concentrates the tomato flavor and color.","7":"Subtle herbal background; skip.","8":"Seasons the rice; the broth adds salt too, so taste before adding all of it.","9":"Fresh garnish; skip.","1":"Only cooking fat; skipped, food sticks and browns poorly, so use an oil or substitute.","6":"Liquid the dish simmers or cooks in; skipped, there is not enough liquid."},"sub":{"1":{"x":"2 tbsp vegetable oil or butter","d":"Slightly less fruity; fine for sauteing.","k":["vegetable oil","butter"]},"6":{"x":"2 cups water + 1/2 tsp extra salt","d":"Less savory; rice tastes more purely of tomato.","k":["water","salt"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Cook the tomato paste in the oil for a minute until it darkens to brick red; this removes the raw, tinny flavor.","s":1},{"t":"Make sure the liquid is at a boil and well seasoned before the rice goes in, then cover and don't stir. Stirring releases starch and makes the rice gummy.","s":3},{"t":"Rest the covered pot for 5 minutes off the heat so the steam evens out, then fluff gently with a fork.","s":4}],"rl":"BFXXXVMXZG"},"3520":{"lv":"RRREEEERO","w":{"1":"Soup base that adds sweetness; potatoes alone make a plainer soup.","2":"Adds aroma; mild loss if skipped.","7":"Adjust at the end after tasting, since chorizo and broth are salty.","8":"Light accent against the paprika-spiced chorizo.","0":"Sautes aromatics; skipped, the olive oil drizzle adds flavor.","4":"Liquid the dish simmers or cooks in; skipped, there is not enough liquid."},"sub":{"0":{"x":"2 tbsp vegetable oil or butter","d":"Slightly less fruity; the final olive oil drizzle adds it back.","k":["vegetable oil","butter"]},"4":{"x":"6 cups water + 1 tsp salt + 2 tbsp extra olive oil","d":"Less savory body; the chorizo adds most of the flavor.","k":["water","salt","olive oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Stack the kale leaves, roll them tightly, and slice them into the thinnest ribbons you can manage; this is the classic caldo verde cut and it keeps the kale tender in a short cook.","s":4},{"t":"Cook the kale only 5 to 8 minutes, until it is tender but still bright green; longer turns it grey and bitter.","s":4},{"t":"Don't discard the chorizo fat from the skillet; drizzle a little over each bowl for extra smoky flavor.","s":5}],"rl":"FXXBMPVZZ"},"53045":{"lv":"ERRRREOREEERRRRO","w":{"1":"Sweet pepper adds body and sweetness to the stew base.","2":"Coriander stalks cook in the base and the leaves finish the dish; skip for less fresh flavor.","3":"Gentle heat; skip for a milder stew.","4":"Garlic adds aroma; the onion and tomato still carry flavor.","6":"Gives color and floral aroma; skip without much loss.","7":"Background herb.","11":"Adds chewy texture and sweetness; swap more fish if skipped.","12":"Adds sweetness and richness; skip with a plainer stew.","13":"Clams add briny flavor; skipping reduces the seafood richness.","14":"Mussels add briny flavor; skipping reduces the seafood richness.","15":"Served alongside to soak up broth; any bread works.","5":"Main liquid of the stew; skipped, there is too little broth."},"sub":{"5":{"x":"350 ml chicken broth + 2 tbsp white wine vinegar","d":"Less aromatic than wine but keeps the acidity.","k":["chicken broth","white wine vinegar"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Cook the potatoes until almost tender before adding any seafood. They take 20 minutes and the seafood only 6 to 8, so adding them together overcooks the fish.","s":1},{"t":"Lay the fish in gently and don't stir. Cod breaks apart easily, so let the lid and steam do the work.","s":2},{"t":"Discard any clams or mussels that stay closed after cooking, and any that were open and didn't close when tapped before cooking.","s":2}],"rl":"XVXXXMCXBVPPPPPG"},"53046":{"lv":"OOEERREEEERO","w":{"0":"Only dusts the work surface to stop sticking; use any flour.","1":"Only for dusting the work surface; skip.","4":"Lemon zest perfumes the syrup; skip with a flatter flavor.","5":"Cinnamon infuses the syrup, the signature note; noticeably flatter without it.","11":"Dusted on at the end; skip.","10":"Vanilla flavor of the custard; skipped, the custard is plainer."},"sub":{"10":{"x":"2 tsp vanilla extract, stirred in after the custard has thickened","d":"A little less aromatic than fresh pod seeds.","k":["vanilla extract"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Roll the dough into a log with the spiral showing, then cut and press each slice flat; this makes the layered flaky shell with thin sides and a thicker base.","s":1},{"t":"Cook the custard until it just coats the spoon, then sieve it. Thin custard bakes out watery and thick lumpy custard won't pour smoothly.","s":11},{"t":"Bake at the highest heat the oven allows. The dark blistered spots on top are the signature, and a hot oven cooks the pastry through before the custard curdles.","s":15}],"rl":"GGSWXXSSTMXG"},"53083":{"lv":"EROREEREERO","w":{"1":"Sweet-tart prunes balance the rich lamb and rice; skipping makes it plain and less festive.","2":"Only used in the soaking water to brighten the fruit; skip.","6":"Garlic adds savory depth to the lamb; the dish is milder without it.","9":"Gives the rice its golden color and floral aroma; skipping makes a plainer pilaf.","10":"Fresh garnish at the end; skip.","3":"Lamb supplies fat; skipped, the pilaf still works.","7":"Liquid the dish simmers or cooks in; skipped, there is not enough liquid."},"sub":{"3":{"x":"2 tbsp vegetable oil","d":"Less rich; browning works the same.","k":["vegetable oil"]},"7":{"x":"600 ml chicken broth","d":"A touch more savory; slightly meatier pilaf.","k":["chicken broth"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Rinse the rice until the water runs clear so the grains stay separate instead of clumping into a gummy mass.","s":3},{"t":"Brown the lamb in batches and don't skip it; the caramelized crust is what gives the broth and rice their savory flavor. Make sure the ground lamb cooks through to 160F (71C).","s":1},{"t":"Steep the saffron in a few tablespoons of warm broth for 10 minutes first, then stir it in so its color spreads evenly through the rice.","s":3}],"rl":"PVAFXPXMBCG"},"53081":{"lv":"EEROERRRRE","w":{"2":"Seasons the cooking water so the potatoes and carrots taste seasoned.","3":"Helps the potatoes and carrots hold their shape while boiling; skip with a softer set.","5":"Classic salty meatiness; ham or bologna works, vegetarians can skip.","7":"Adds sweetness, color and pop against the creamy mayo; skip for a plainer salad.","8":"Fresh onion bite that cuts the mayonnaise richness."},"tips":[{"t":"Cut the potatoes and carrots into small, equal dice so they finish together, and start them in cold, salted water with a splash of vinegar so they hold their shape.","s":2},{"t":"Drain and cool the vegetables completely before adding mayo. Warm potatoes make the dressing oily and the salad soggy.","s":7},{"t":"Chill for at least an hour; the flavors blend and the salad firms up. Keep it refrigerated, since a mayo-and-egg salad shouldn't sit out longer than two hours.","s":8}],"rl":"BVZAPPXVXT"},"53080":{"lv":"EEEEERR","w":{"6":"The yolk enriches the batter and the whipped white lightens it; skip for denser, plainer blini.","5":"Enriches the blini; skipped, they are plainer."},"sub":{"5":{"x":"2 tbsp melted butter or vegetable oil","d":"Oil is less rich and loses the buttery taste.","k":["butter","vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Warm the milk only to lukewarm, around 100F (38C). Hot milk kills the yeast, and cold milk delays the rise.","s":1},{"t":"Whip the white to stiff but not dry peaks and fold it in gently; deflating it makes dense blini instead of airy ones.","s":6},{"t":"The first blin is a test. Adjust the heat and add a splash of milk if the batter is too thick; the small ones should bubble on top and flip after about a minute.","s":10}],"rl":"SSZLMFL"},"53077":{"lv":"RRERRREERRO","w":{"1":"Onion forms the sweet base of the soup; skipping makes it flatter.","3":"Adds sweetness and color.","4":"Adds a subtle aromatic depth.","5":"Gentle background herb.","8":"Add brightness and gentle sweetness at the end; skip with a less rounded soup.","9":"Classic tangy topping; skip for a cleaner soup.","10":"Fresh finishing herb; skip.","0":"Sautes the vegetables; skipped, the soup still works.","6":"Liquid the dish simmers or cooks in; skipped, there is not enough liquid."},"sub":{"0":{"x":"3 tbsp vegetable oil","d":"Less rich and lacks buttery flavor.","k":["vegetable oil"]},"6":{"x":"8 cups chicken or beef broth","d":"Meatier and more savory than vegetable broth.","k":["chicken broth","beef broth"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Sweat the cabbage with the onion and carrot first so it softens and loses its raw sulfurous smell before the liquid goes in.","s":1},{"t":"Add the potatoes after the cabbage has had its head start, and the tomatoes last, since the acid slows the cooking of the vegetables.","s":4},{"t":"Shchi tastes better after a rest; let it sit off the heat 15 minutes or make it a day ahead, and finish with a spoonful of sour cream and fresh dill.","s":6}],"rl":"FXVVXXMBAGG"},"53079":{"lv":"RRREEEREE","w":{"1":"Adds sweetness and depth to the broth; skip with a plainer soup.","2":"Adds sweetness and color.","6":"Gives gentle herbal background to the broth.","0":"Sautes aromatics; skipped, the soup still works.","3":"Liquid the dish simmers or cooks in; skipped, there is not enough liquid."},"sub":{"0":{"x":"2 tbsp vegetable oil or butter","d":"Neutral oil is fine; butter is richer.","k":["vegetable oil","butter"]},"3":{"x":"3 cups extra water + 1/2 tsp salt + 1 extra bay leaf","d":"Lighter fish flavor; the cod and salmon still season the broth.","k":["water","salt"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Keep the broth at a gentle simmer; a hard boil turns it cloudy, and clear broth is the hallmark of ukha.","s":1},{"t":"Add the fish only at the end and take the pot off the heat as soon as the flesh turns opaque. Fish reaches 145F (63C) quickly, and overcooking makes it dry.","s":2},{"t":"Finish with a squeeze of lemon and a handful of dill or parsley; the brightness lifts the rich broth.","s":3}],"rl":"FXVMMBXPP"},"53082":{"lv":"ERRERR","w":{"1":"Draws out the juices and sweetens the berries; skip for sharper, drier berries.","4":"Adds a gentle tang that cuts the sweetness of the cream.","5":"Sweetens the whipped cream; granulated sugar works if you whip it a little longer.","2":"Liqueur flavor for the berries; skipped, the dessert is plainer."},"sub":{"2":{"x":"4 tbsp orange juice + 1/2 tsp orange zest","d":"Fresh and fruity but without the liqueur's depth.","k":["orange juice","orange"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Macerate the berries 1 to 2 hours, no longer; after that they turn soft and mushy. The ingredient list omits the 1/4 cup powdered sugar used with the whipped cream in step 2.","s":0},{"t":"Chill the bowl and beaters, then whip the cream with the sugar to stiff peaks before folding in the sour cream; this keeps it from collapsing.","s":2},{"t":"Fold the cream through the berries only at the last minute and use the syrup to moisten, since the juice thins the cream if it sits.","s":3}],"rl":"VWXFFW"},"53225":{"lv":"EREEROR","w":{"1":"Sweet base that softens the tomato; the dish is thinner without it.","4":"Warm spice typical of Yemeni dishes; skipping gives a plainer scramble.","5":"Light peppery note.","6":"Melted cheese spread stirred over the eggs for creaminess; cream cheese or mozzarella works.","3":"Only cooking fat; skipped, food sticks and browns poorly, so use an oil or substitute."},"sub":{"3":{"x":"2 tbsp butter","d":"Richer and slightly less fruity.","k":["butter"]},"6":{"x":"3 tbsp cream cheese or shredded mozzarella","d":"A little thicker than cheese spread; melts smoothly.","k":["cream cheese","mozzarella"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Cook the onion until soft and translucent and the tomato until it breaks down and loses its liquid; wet tomato makes the eggs watery.","s":0},{"t":"Stir the eggs briefly into the vegetables, then cover on low heat so they set gently; high heat makes them rubbery.","s":1},{"t":"Cook the eggs until fully set with no runny egg; the cheese goes on for the last minute under the lid to melt.","s":1}],"rl":"PXVFXZF"},"53217":{"lv":"EERERRRRORRO","w":{"2":"Acid brightens the rich, slow-cooked beef; skip and it tastes heavier.","4":"Gives color and mild sweetness.","5":"Seasons the meat; add more at the end if needed, since the broth is also salty.","6":"Warm note typical of shawarma blends; a minor loss if skipped.","7":"Adds earthy flavor and golden color.","8":"A bit of heat; skip for a milder shawarma.","9":"Gives warm sweetness that rounds out the blend.","10":"Citrusy note that complements cumin; mild loss if skipped.","11":"Very strong in small amounts; skip freely.","1":"Liquid the dish simmers or cooks in; skipped, there is not enough liquid."},"sub":{"1":{"x":"1 cup water + 1/2 tsp extra salt","d":"Less meaty depth; the beef still makes its own juices.","k":["water","salt"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Sear the chuck roast before it goes into the slow cooker if you have a few minutes; the browned crust adds much deeper flavor.","s":1},{"t":"After shredding, skim the fat and reduce the liquid in a pot to a thicker sauce before tossing it with the beef; watery sauce soaks the pita.","s":4},{"t":"For crisp shawarma-style edges, spread the shredded beef on a sheet and broil it for a few minutes before building the wraps.","s":5}],"rl":"PMAXXZXXXXXX"},"53218":{"lv":"ERRERRRRRREROO","w":{"1":"Garlic is the savory backbone of the marinade; skipping makes the chicken blander.","2":"Citrusy warmth that rounds the cumin; a minor loss if skipped.","4":"Adds a sweet, floral note typical of shawarma; use sparingly.","5":"Gives gentle heat; skip for a milder chicken.","6":"Gives color and sweetness.","7":"Seasons the chicken throughout; the meat tastes flat without it.","8":"Acid helps flavor and tenderize; skipping makes a duller marinade.","11":"Gives the sauce its pungency; skipping makes it milder.","12":"A little extra warmth in the sauce.","13":"A squeeze of brightness at the end; skip.","9":"Carries flavor in the marinade or dressing; skipped, the dish is drier but intact."},"sub":{"9":{"x":"3 tbsp vegetable oil","d":"Less fruity but carries the spices the same.","k":["vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Slice the chicken thin and evenly so it cooks through quickly in the hot pan. Chicken breast is done at 165F (74C); don't push it much beyond or it dries out.","s":0},{"t":"Cook in two batches so the pan stays hot; crowded chicken steams and turns pale and watery instead of browned.","s":3},{"t":"Don't marinate beyond about 8 hours; the lemon acid toughens the surface of the chicken. Stir fresh mint and parsley into the yogurt sauce just before serving.","s":1}],"rl":"PXXXXXXZAFFXXA"},"53220":{"lv":"EERROORORERO","w":{"2":"Sweet pepper adds flavor and color to the rice.","3":"Second pepper adds color; one is fine.","4":"Adds earthiness; skip.","5":"Adds sweetness and color; skip.","6":"Adds sweetness and color.","7":"Traditional sweet bursts; skip for a more savory rice.","8":"Gives golden color and earthiness.","10":"A small seasoning; the stock cube and kabse spice already salt the dish.","11":"Light accent."},"tips":[{"t":"Brown the chicken properly before boiling; the caramelized crust gives the broth, and so the rice, its color and flavor. Chicken must reach 165F (74C).","s":0},{"t":"Cook the rice in the chicken broth rather than plain water for much deeper flavor, and rinse the rice first so the grains stay separate.","s":2},{"t":"After the rice has absorbed the liquid, rest it covered for 10 minutes off the heat before going into the oven so it fluffs and doesn't dry out.","s":3}],"rl":"PBVVVVVWCXZZ"},"53222":{"lv":"ERERROREOOO","w":{"1":"Adds sweetness and body to the sauce; skip for a simpler tomato base.","3":"Warm, earthy note typical of shakshuka; skipping gives a plainer sauce.","4":"Gives savory depth; 1 minced fresh garlic clove works.","5":"Gentle heat; skip for a milder sauce.","6":"Adds color and mild sweetness.","8":"Fresh garnish; skip.","9":"Heat garnish; skip.","10":"For serving and dipping; any bread works."},"sub":{"4":{"x":"1 small garlic clove, minced","d":"Fresher and sharper than granules; cook it 30 seconds.","k":["garlic"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Simmer the sauce until it is thick enough that a spoon drawn across the pan leaves a trail; a thin sauce makes the eggs sink and spread into soup.","s":3},{"t":"Make wells with the back of a spoon, cover the pan, and cook on low heat until the whites are set and the yolks still jiggle; for guests who prefer, cook the yolks through.","s":4},{"t":"Crack each egg into a small cup first so you can slide it in without breaking the yolk or dropping shell into the sauce.","s":4}],"rl":"XVVXXXXPGGG"},"53215":{"lv":"EEEERR","w":{"4":"Toasted with the garlic at the start for aroma; skip with a milder sauce.","5":"Seasons the sauce; taste and adjust."},"tips":[{"t":"Toast the pepper and garlic in the dry pan until fragrant before adding the oil; the heat blooms their flavor, but pull the garlic at once if it goes past pale gold, as it turns bitter.","s":0},{"t":"Mash the tomatoes and reduce the sauce until it's thick; the eggs need a firm base or they sink into liquid.","s":3},{"t":"Crack the eggs into the craters so they touch the hot pan and set from below; keep the lid on and don't lift it, then finish off the heat for 1 to 2 minutes until the whites are fully set.","s":4}],"rl":"VFPXZZ"},"53219":{"lv":"EEERORRRRRRO","w":{"3":"Sweet, mild onion base; swap a small onion if needed.","4":"Adds crunch and savory depth; skip.","5":"Adds aroma.","6":"Adds color and iron-rich flavor; the dish is still fine without it.","7":"Salty, tangy finish that seasons the eggs; skip and add more salt.","8":"Adds sweetness and body to the sauce.","9":"Warm, earthy note that defines shakshuka; noticeably flatter without it.","10":"Seasons the sauce; feta adds salt too, so go easy.","11":"Light accent.","2":"Only cooking fat; skipped, food sticks and browns poorly, so use an oil or substitute."},"sub":{"2":{"x":"1 tbsp butter","d":"Richer; softens the vegetables just as well.","k":["butter"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Cook the tomato sauce down until thick before the eggs go in; eggs poached in thin sauce spread and overcook.","s":2},{"t":"Add the spinach and feta only at the end; they wilt and soften from the residual heat, and the spinach stays bright green instead of olive.","s":3},{"t":"Crack each egg into a small cup first, nestle it into a well, and keep the lid on until the whites are fully set.","s":3}],"rl":"PVFXXXVGVXZZ"},"53223":{"lv":"ERRRRREERRRROOO","w":{"1":"Garlic is a core flavor in the filling; skipping makes the meat blander.","2":"Ginger adds warmth; skip with a flatter filling.","3":"Binds the filling and helps it hold together in the pastry.","4":"Adds juiciness and fresh acidity to the meat.","5":"Fresh onion bite that lifts the rich filling.","8":"Seasons the filling; it tastes flat without it.","9":"Gives color and mild sweetness.","10":"Warm, earthy spice typical of mutabbaq; skipping makes it plainer.","11":"Adds warm, sweet spice depth.","12":"A small color and earthy accent.","13":"Light heat.","14":"Rounds out the savory spices; skip.","7":"Butter between the layers makes the pastry crisp; skipped, it is dry and doughy."},"sub":{"7":{"x":"1 cup vegetable oil, brushed thinly between the layers","d":"Crisp but less rich; butter browns better and tastes better.","k":["vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Cook the minced meat until it is no longer pink (160F / 71C) and let it cool before mixing in the egg, tomato, and onion. Hot filling steams the filo and tears it.","s":0},{"t":"Keep the unused filo sheets under a barely damp towel while you work; exposed filo dries out and cracks in minutes.","s":2},{"t":"Brush between each layer and fold into a tight envelope; fry on medium heat so the pastry crisps before the outside burns, and drain well.","s":3}],"rl":"PXXTVXSFZXXXCZX"},"53226":{"lv":"EEOERE","w":{"2":"Barely noticeable; skip.","4":"Softens the dough and keeps the bread pliable; skip for a drier, tougher bread."},"sub":{"4":{"x":"1 tbsp olive oil or melted butter","d":"Slightly more flavor; works the same in the dough.","k":["olive oil","butter"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Add the water a spoonful at a time; the dough should be soft but not sticky. About 1/3 cup is needed for this much flour, but humidity changes it.","s":1},{"t":"Roll the rounds thin, about 2 to 3 mm, and cook them on a very hot dry pan. The steam inside is what puffs the pocket, and thick dough won't puff.","s":3},{"t":"Stack the cooked breads under a cloth as they come off the pan; the trapped steam keeps them soft and pliable for wrapping.","s":6}],"rl":"SZWLFM"},"53216":{"lv":"EEOEREREEERR","w":{"2":"Only gives the traditional orange color; skip with no change in taste.","4":"Makes the cheese filling creamy and milder; skip and use more mozzarella.","6":"Sweetens the cheese filling lightly; skip with a more savory filling.","10":"The classic perfume of the syrup; a lighter taste without it.","11":"Keeps the syrup from crystallizing; a few drops of any citrus works."},"tips":[{"t":"Cook the cheese custard until it is very thick, since it firms as it cools. Thin custard leaks out and makes the bottom layer soggy.","s":2},{"t":"Press the bottom half of the knafeh dough firmly into the dish so it holds together when inverted and sliced.","s":4},{"t":"Pour cool syrup over the hot knafeh the moment it leaves the oven; the temperature contrast keeps it crisp. Don't stir the syrup after it boils.","s":10}],"rl":"BFCPFMWTWMXA"},"53221":{"lv":"EEEERE","w":{"4":"Brings the dough together; use water if you have no milk, then add slowly.","2":"Fat in the dough or batter; skipped, the result is dry, tough or less tender."},"sub":{"2":{"x":"25 g (2 tbsp) melted butter","d":"Richer flavor; slightly less tender than oil.","k":["butter"]},"4":{"x":"50 ml warm water","d":"Slightly less rich and tender.","k":["water"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Mix the dough only until it comes together; kneading develops gluten and makes tough, bready mamoul rather than a crumbly, short biscuit.","s":2},{"t":"Use about 2 parts dough to 1 part filling by weight and press the filling ball in snugly. Dust the mould lightly with flour and tap it out firmly on the counter.","s":8},{"t":"Bake just until the bottoms are golden and the tops are pale. They will be soft when hot and firm as they cool, so let them set before moving them.","s":10}],"rl":"SSFFMW"},"53224":{"lv":"RERREEEEOEEEE","w":{"0":"Makes the pistachio paste creamy and sweet; skip with a grainier paste.","2":"Sweetens the pistachio paste; sugar works.","3":"Loosens the pistachio paste; skip by adding a little more.","8":"A small pinch in the cake; barely noticeable.","10":"Fat for toasting the vermicelli; skipped, it burns or stays raw.","12":"Cream base of the ganache; skipped, there is no ganache."},"sub":{"10":{"x":"1 tbsp butter","d":"Less nutty; roast the vermicelli slowly and stir constantly so it doesn't burn.","k":["butter"]},"12":{"x":"4 tbsp heavy cream","d":"Works the same in the ganache; slightly less thick.","k":["heavy cream"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Roast the sevaiiya slowly on low heat, stirring constantly until it is golden and nutty smelling. Pale vermicelli tastes raw and burnt pieces turn bitter.","s":5},{"t":"Cool the roasted vermicelli completely before mixing in the pistachio paste; warm vermicelli makes the paste oily and soggy.","s":5},{"t":"Let the ganache cool until it is thick but still pourable before spreading; hot ganache melts the kunafa layer and runs off.","s":7}],"rl":"FXWMSMLAZBFFF"},"3526":{"lv":"EEREREERREEEEEOORO","w":{"4":"Grated onion keeps the meatballs moist and adds sweetness; skip with drier meatballs.","7":"Gives the characteristic warm Scandinavian spice note; skipping is subtle.","8":"Seasons the meatballs; without it they taste bland.","14":"Adds a little umami depth to the gravy; skip.","15":"A bit of tang that balances the cream; skip.","16":"The traditional sweet-tart counterpoint; skip for a plainer plate.","17":"Light accent.","2":"Binds the meatballs; skipped, they are denser.","9":"Only cooking fat; skipped, food sticks and browns poorly, so use an oil or substitute.","10":"Fat for the gravy roux; skipped, the gravy does not thicken."},"sub":{"2":{"x":"12 crushed saltine crackers (about 1/2 cup)","d":"Same binding role; slightly saltier, so go easy on added salt.","k":["crackers"]},"9":{"x":"2 tbsp vegetable oil","d":"Browns the meatballs the same; less buttery.","k":["vegetable oil"]},"10":{"x":"3 tbsp vegetable oil","d":"Makes a roux that works the same; less buttery.","k":["vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Grate the onion and let the breadcrumb and milk mixture soak fully; this panade keeps the meatballs tender. Mix lightly, since overworking makes them rubbery.","s":0},{"t":"Chill the mix and roll with damp hands; shake the pan as they brown so they stay round. Meatballs are done at 160F (71C).","s":2},{"t":"Stir the cream in at the end and keep the gravy below a boil so it stays smooth; the browned bits in the pan make the gravy.","s":4}],"rl":"PPTMXTXXZFFTMFXXGZ"},"3527":{"lv":"EEEREORRRRR","w":{"3":"Adds a gentle bite to the cure; skipping barely changes it.","5":"Gives a subtle aromatic lift; skip or use vodka.","6":"Base of the hovmastarsas sauce; the sauce is lost without it.","7":"Balances the mustard in the sauce.","8":"Adds tang to the sauce.","9":"Emulsifies into the sauce to make it creamy.","10":"Fresh dill flavor in the sauce; skip."},"tips":[{"t":"Use very fresh salmon from a trusted source. For raw-cured fish, freeze it first (-4F / -20C for 7 days) unless it was already commercially frozen; pregnant people and the immunocompromised should avoid it.","s":0},{"t":"Weigh the fish down lightly and flip it every 12 hours, spooning off the liquid it releases; the cure turns the flesh firm, silky, and deeply seasoned.","s":3},{"t":"Slice against the grain with a long, thin knife at a shallow angle, and keep the slices very thin; the thinner they are, the more delicate the texture.","s":6}],"rl":"PKKXXXTWAFX"},"53188":{"lv":"ERRRREROROOREEE","w":{"1":"Adds sweetness and moisture to the meat.","2":"Adds aroma.","4":"Softens the bread; water works.","6":"Seasons the meat; add it or the patties are bland.","7":"Light accent.","8":"Classic Central European herb; skipping makes them plainer.","9":"A hint of tang; skip.","10":"Fresh garnish; skip.","11":"Helps the egg and crumbs stick to the patties; skip with a patchy coating.","3":"Binds and softens the patties; skipped, they are denser.","14":"Frying medium; skipped, the food cannot be fried."},"sub":{"3":{"x":"1/2 cup breadcrumbs soaked in 1/2 cup milk","d":"The same binder with a slightly finer texture.","k":["breadcrumbs","milk"]},"14":{"x":"sunflower or canola oil, 1/2 cm deep","d":"Any neutral oil works; lard is traditional.","k":["sunflower oil","canola oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Squeeze the soaked bread well before mashing it into the pork; too much liquid makes the patties fall apart in the pan.","s":2},{"t":"Shape the patties with wet hands and keep them thin, about 1.5 cm. Thick patties brown before the center is done.","s":8},{"t":"Fry on medium heat and check the center with a thermometer; ground pork is done at 160F (71C). Note the 15-minute total time is not realistic, so allow 30.","s":14}],"rl":"PXXTMTZZXXGTTBF"},"53190":{"lv":"EERREEO","w":{"2":"Binds the potato dough; some cooks skip it, but dumplings come out softer.","3":"Seasons the dumplings; they taste flat without it.","6":"Fresh chives add a mild oniony finish; skip.","4":"Defining cheese of the halusky; skipped, the dish is not itself."},"sub":{"4":{"x":"250 g feta + 3 tbsp sour cream, mashed together","d":"Saltier and tangier; the closest common replacement for bryndza.","k":["feta","sour cream"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Grate the potatoes finely and mix the dough right away; grated potato oxidizes and turns grey, and the dough should be soft and sticky, like a thick batter.","s":1},{"t":"Dip the board or the press in hot water between batches so the dough doesn't stick. Cook in batches so the water stays at a boil.","s":3},{"t":"Toss the hot halusky with the bryndza and a ladle of cooking water; the water loosens the cheese into a creamy coating. Top with the bacon and its rendered fat.","s":8}],"rl":"BBTZFPG"},"53189":{"lv":"ERREERROE","w":{"1":"Adds sweetness and moisture; skip with a flatter pancake.","2":"Adds aroma.","5":"Classic Central European herb that defines the taste; skip with a plainer flavor.","6":"Seasons the batter; taste.","7":"Light accent.","8":"Frying medium; skipped, the food cannot be fried."},"sub":{"8":{"x":"sunflower or canola oil (lard also traditional)","d":"Any neutral oil works; lard is the traditional choice.","k":["sunflower oil","lard"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Squeeze the grated potatoes dry in a towel and mix the batter right away; excess liquid makes soft placky, and grated potato turns grey if left sitting.","s":1},{"t":"Fry in about 5 mm of oil at medium heat and keep them thin; thick placky stay raw in the middle.","s":5},{"t":"Drain on a wire rack instead of paper towels so the bottoms stay crisp, and serve immediately.","s":9}],"rl":"BXXTTXZZF"},"53187":{"lv":"EERRERR","w":{"2":"Gives a firmer, slightly toothsome bite; use more flour instead if you lack it.","3":"Seasons the dough; the dumplings taste flat without it.","5":"Sweetens the poppy seeds and coats the dumplings.","6":"Prevents sticking and adds richness; use oil if you're out."},"sub":{"2":{"x":"100 g additional flour","d":"Slightly softer dumplings.","k":["flour"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Cool the boiled potatoes completely, even overnight, and mash them smooth; warm or wet potatoes need more flour and give tough dumplings.","s":2},{"t":"Add only as much flour as needed for a soft, non-sticky dough, and handle it quickly. Overworked dough turns heavy.","s":5},{"t":"Grind the poppy seeds fresh just before using them in a spice grinder; pre-ground seeds go bitter and taste stale.","s":14}],"rl":"BSSZGWF"},"3273":{"lv":"EEOREER","w":{"2":"A tiny amount for browning and balance; skipping barely changes the taste.","3":"Seasons the biscuits; flat without it.","6":"Brushed on top for browning and shine; skip with a paler, drier top.","5":"Acidic liquid for the biscuit dough; skipped, they do not rise or hydrate."},"sub":{"5":{"x":"1 cup cold milk + 1 tbsp lemon juice, rested 5 minutes","d":"Slightly less tangy and tender than real buttermilk.","k":["milk","lemon juice"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Keep the butter and buttermilk cold, and leave pea-sized pieces of butter in the flour. They melt in the hot oven and create steam, which makes flaky layers.","s":1},{"t":"Cut the biscuits straight down without twisting the cutter; twisting seals the edges and stops them from rising tall.","s":3},{"t":"Bake in a very hot oven with the biscuits touching so they support each other and rise upward instead of spreading.","s":4}],"rl":"SLWZFMF"},"3147":{"lv":"ERERRROEEREREEER","w":{"3":"Adds color and mild flavor to the crust.","4":"Savory background to the crust.","5":"Seasons the crust; the fried chicken tastes flat without enough salt.","6":"A light accent in the crust.","9":"Lightly sweetens the waffles and aids browning; skip with paler waffles.","11":"Seasons the waffle batter; sweet waffles without salt taste flat.","15":"Traditional finish; skip it and the plate is much less rounded.","1":"Tenderizing marinade; skipped, the chicken is less tender.","7":"Frying medium; skipped, the food cannot be fried.","14":"Fat in the dough or batter; skipped, the result is dry, tough or less tender."},"sub":{"1":{"x":"1 cup buttermilk","d":"Slightly thicker and tangier marinade; works the same.","k":["buttermilk"]},"7":{"x":"3 cups canola, sunflower or peanut oil","d":"Any high-heat neutral oil works.","k":["canola oil","sunflower oil","peanut oil"]},"14":{"x":"1/3 cup vegetable oil","d":"Less buttery flavor; the waffle iron still crisps it.","k":["vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Press the flour firmly onto the chicken and let it sit 10 minutes before frying so the coating hydrates and sticks.","s":1},{"t":"Hold the oil at 350F (175C), fry in batches, and cook the chicken until it reaches 165F (74C) at the thickest part; thighs taste best closer to 175F (79C). Keep the finished pieces on a rack in a warm oven.","s":2},{"t":"Don't overmix the waffle batter; a few lumps are fine. Serve the waffles immediately, since they steam and soften within minutes.","s":3}],"rl":"PATXXZZFSWLZSMFG"},"3154":{"lv":"EEREEEEERR","w":{"2":"Seasons the biscuits; they taste flat without it.","8":"Plenty of black pepper is the signature of sausage gravy; skipping makes it bland.","9":"Season after tasting, since sausage is salty."},"tips":[{"t":"Keep the butter cold and don't overmix the dough; a shaggy dough makes flaky biscuits and a smooth one makes tough ones.","s":1},{"t":"Leave the sausage fat in the pan; if the sausage is lean, add a spoonful of butter so there are enough drippings to cook the flour into a roux.","s":4},{"t":"Add the milk gradually while whisking so no lumps form. The gravy thickens as it stands, so thin it with a splash of milk if it gets too thick.","s":5}],"rl":"SLZFMPTMZZ"},"59":{"lv":"EERRREEREEEOR","w":{"2":"Sweet aromatic base for the broth; skipping makes a plainer broth.","3":"Adds sweetness and color.","4":"Gives gentle aromatic depth.","7":"Makes the broth richer and silkier; skip for a thinner, lighter stew.","11":"Fresh garnish; skip.","12":"Without it the dumplings taste flat.","1":"Fat for the roux; skipped, the dumpling gravy is thin.","6":"Liquid the dish simmers or cooks in; skipped, there is not enough liquid."},"sub":{"1":{"x":"4 tbsp vegetable oil","d":"Less rich; the flour roux still works.","k":["vegetable oil"]},"6":{"x":"6 cups water + 2 chicken bouillon cubes","d":"Thinner flavor than stock; adjust the salt after the bouillon dissolves.","k":["water","bouillon cube"]}},"tips":[{"t":"The ingredient list omits the salt used in the dumpling dough in step 3; add about 1 tsp, or the dumplings taste flat.","s":2},{"t":"Drop the dumplings onto a gently simmering stew, not a rolling boil, cover tightly, and don't lift the lid; steam is what cooks them through and makes them fluffy.","s":3},{"t":"Cook the chicken until it reaches 165F (74C), then shred it and return it to the pot. Cutting one dumpling open should show no raw dough in the center.","s":0}],"rl":"PFXVXTMFSLMGZ"},"63":{"lv":"ERRROERERRR","w":{"1":"Adds sweetness and helps the rub caramelize into a crust.","2":"Gives color and mild flavor.","3":"Savory background to the rub.","4":"A small savory accent to the rub.","8":"Slaw mix adds crunch and freshness to the rich pork.","9":"Makes the slaw creamy.","10":"Adds tang to the slaw.","6":"Adds savory depth to a small amount of liquid; skipped, flavor is just muted."},"sub":{"6":{"x":"1/2 cup water","d":"Slightly less savory; the pork's juices fill in.","k":["water"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Pork shoulder is ready when it reaches about 195 to 205F (90 to 96C) and a fork twists in with no resistance; that is when the collagen has melted.","s":1},{"t":"After shredding, toss the meat with some of the cooking juices as well as the sauce; the juices keep it moist, and drained, dry meat tastes flat.","s":2},{"t":"Dress the slaw just before serving, and toast the buns, so neither goes soggy under the juicy pork.","s":3}],"rl":"PWXXXXMBVFA"},"3136":{"lv":"EERROOEREE","w":{"2":"Seasons the crust; the steak tastes bland if you skip it.","3":"Seasons the crust and the cream gravy, where pepper is traditional.","4":"Adds color and mild flavor to the crust.","5":"A savory accent to the flour; skip.","7":"Part of the egg wash that helps the flour stick.","8":"Frying medium; skipped, the food cannot be fried."},"sub":{"8":{"x":"1 cup canola, peanut or sunflower oil","d":"Any high-heat neutral oil gives the same crust.","k":["canola oil","peanut oil","sunflower oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Dredge flour, then egg, then flour again, pressing firmly, and let the coated steaks sit 10 minutes before frying so the crust adheres and doesn't slide off.","s":1},{"t":"Keep the oil at 350F (175C); if it's too cool the crust gets greasy, too hot and it scorches before the steak is cooked. Cube steak is mechanically tenderized, so cook it to at least 145F (63C).","s":2},{"t":"Pour off the oil but keep the browned bits, then cook the flour in the fat for a full minute before adding milk; this removes the raw flour taste and gives a smooth gravy.","s":4}],"rl":"PTZZXXTMFM"},"3158":{"lv":"EERRERRORORO","w":{"2":"Melts into the grits for creamy, tangy richness; skip for plainer grits.","3":"Adds richness to the grits; they're drier without it.","5":"Smoky, salty crunch; the bacon fat also cooks the shrimp.","6":"Garlic is a central flavor in the shrimp pan sauce; skipping makes it plainer.","7":"Light color and mild warmth.","8":"Brightens the dish; skip with a heavier plate.","9":"Fresh garnish.","10":"Seasons the grits, which taste flat without enough salt.","11":"A light accent.","1":"Liquid the dish simmers or cooks in; skipped, there is not enough liquid."},"sub":{"1":{"x":"2 cups water + 2 cups whole milk + 1/2 tsp salt","d":"Creamier but less savory; stir often so the milk doesn't scorch.","k":["water","whole milk","salt"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Whisk the grits into boiling liquid in a thin stream and stir often; stone-ground grits need the full 25 to 30 minutes. If they thicken too much, loosen them with a splash of hot broth.","s":0},{"t":"Shred the cheddar yourself and stir it in off the heat; pre-shredded cheese has coatings that make the grits grainy.","s":1},{"t":"Cook the shrimp just until pink and opaque, about 2 minutes per side. They become tough and rubbery quickly, so pull them as soon as they curl into a loose C.","s":3}],"rl":"BMFFPFXXAGZZ"},"3163":{"lv":"ERERRROOER","w":{"3":"Helps the crust stick and brown; skip with a grainier, more fragile crust.","4":"Gives the classic Southern spice kick; skipping makes the fish milder.","5":"Seasons the crust; the fish tastes flat without enough salt.","6":"A small accent; cajun seasoning already contains pepper.","7":"Adds color; skip.","9":"Squeezed on after frying to brighten the rich fish.","1":"Tenderizing soak; skipped, the coating sticks less.","8":"Frying medium; skipped, the food cannot be fried."},"sub":{"1":{"x":"1 cup milk + 1 tbsp lemon juice, rested 5 minutes","d":"Slightly less tangy and thinner; works the same as a soak.","k":["milk","lemon juice"]},"8":{"x":"3 cups canola, peanut or sunflower oil","d":"Any high-heat neutral oil works.","k":["canola oil","peanut oil","sunflower oil"],"wn":"Peanut oil: avoid for anyone with a peanut allergy; use canola or sunflower."}},"tips":[{"t":"Let the excess buttermilk drip off before dredging; too much makes the cornmeal clump and fall off in patches.","s":3},{"t":"Hold the oil at 350F (175C) and fry only a couple of fillets at a time; crowding drops the temperature and the cornmeal crust absorbs oil instead of crisping.","s":2},{"t":"Drain on a wire rack, not paper towels, so steam doesn't soften the crust. The fish is done when it flakes and reads 145F (63C).","s":5}],"rl":"PABTXZZCFG"},"3167":{"lv":"ERREERROEEEROORR","w":{"2":"Adds tang and heat to the marinade; skip with a slightly milder chicken.","5":"Gives color and mild flavor to the crust.","6":"Savory background in the crust.","7":"A light accent in the dredge.","11":"Balances the cayenne heat with a hint of sweetness; skip for a hotter, sharper sauce.","12":"Adds color and sweetness to the paste.","13":"Savory background in the paste.","14":"Traditionally the base for serving, which soaks up the spicy oil.","15":"Traditional cooling, tangy contrast to the heat.","1":"Tenderizing marinade; skipped, the chicken is less tender.","8":"Frying medium; skipped, the food cannot be fried.","9":"Spiced oil is the Nashville hot sauce; skipped, the chicken is not hot chicken."},"sub":{"1":{"x":"1 1/2 cups milk + 1 1/2 tbsp lemon juice, rested 5 minutes","d":"Thinner and less tangy; still tenderizes and helps the crust stick.","k":["milk","lemon juice"]},"8":{"x":"3 cups canola, peanut or sunflower oil","d":"Any high-heat neutral oil works.","k":["canola oil","peanut oil","sunflower oil"],"wn":"Peanut oil: avoid for anyone with a peanut allergy; use canola or sunflower."},"9":{"x":"1/2 cup of the hot frying oil","d":"Same flavor; ladle it from the fryer after cooking the chicken.","k":["vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Dredge twice (flour, buttermilk, flour) and let the coated chicken rest 10 to 15 minutes before frying so the craggy crust sets and doesn't slide off.","s":1},{"t":"Fry at 325F (165C), turning, until the thighs reach at least 175F (79C); bone-in thighs need that high a temperature to be tender, and the lower oil temperature keeps the crust from burning before the inside is cooked.","s":2},{"t":"Whisk the spices into hot oil carefully, since it can spit, and brush it on while the chicken is still hot. Wear gloves, and add the cayenne in stages if you want less heat.","s":3}],"rl":"PAXZBXXZFFXWXXGG"},"3588":{"lv":"EREOOROEREOR","w":{"1":"Browns into the crust and helps thicken the gravy as it simmers; skip with a paler, thinner result.","3":"Adds color and mild flavor to the crust.","4":"A savory accent to the flour dredge; skip.","6":"A light accent; the paprika and Worcestershire carry the flavor.","5":"Seasons the pork and the gravy; flat without enough salt.","8":"Cooks down into a sweet base for the gravy; skipping gives a plainer sauce.","10":"Subtle herbal note; skip.","11":"Adds tangy, savory depth; skip with a slightly flatter gravy.","7":"Only cooking fat; skipped, food sticks and browns poorly, so use an oil or substitute.","9":"Liquid the dish simmers or cooks in; skipped, there is not enough liquid."},"sub":{"7":{"x":"3 tbsp canola or olive oil","d":"Works the same for searing.","k":["canola oil","olive oil"]},"9":{"x":"2 cups beef broth","d":"Deeper color and richer flavor.","k":["beef broth"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Shake off the excess flour after dredging so the crust is thin, and sear the chops without moving them to build a deep golden crust; the browned bits become the gravy.","s":1},{"t":"Cook the flour with the onion for a full minute, then add the broth gradually while whisking so the gravy is smooth rather than lumpy.","s":3},{"t":"Cook the pork until it reaches 145F (63C), then rest it for 3 minutes; the long, gentle braise tenderizes the chops, but pull them before they dry out.","s":5}],"rl":"PTTXXZZFVMXX"},"3579":{"lv":"ERERRRROEO","w":{"3":"Keeps the patties moist and binds; skip with drier, crumblier cakes.","4":"Fresh onion bite that balances the rich salmon.","5":"Brightens the canned salmon's flavor.","6":"Adds the signature Old Bay-style spice; use paprika and celery salt instead.","7":"A light accent.","9":"Squeezed over at the table; skip.","1":"Binder or crumb; skipped, the patties are softer.","8":"Only cooking fat; skipped, food sticks and browns poorly, so use an oil or substitute."},"sub":{"1":{"x":"1/2 cup fine breadcrumbs or crushed saltine crackers","d":"Slightly denser and less crisp than panko.","k":["breadcrumbs","crackers"]},"8":{"x":"3 tbsp canola or sunflower oil","d":"Any neutral oil fries them equally.","k":["canola oil","sunflower oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Drain the canned salmon thoroughly and mix gently; leave small flakes so the patties have texture. The soft bones are edible and add calcium, but mash them in if you prefer.","s":0},{"t":"Rest the mix for 5 minutes, then chill the formed patties briefly; the panko absorbs moisture and the patties hold together in the pan.","s":2},{"t":"Fry over medium heat and flip only once the first side has a firm golden crust; they are fragile. Cook until hot all the way through, since the raw egg in the mix needs to set fully.","s":4}],"rl":"PTTFXAXZFG"},"3148":{"lv":"EEEERREEE","w":{"4":"Seasons the bread and sharpens the corn flavor; without it the cornbread tastes flat. Keep at least 1/2 tsp.","5":"Only a little; it rounds off the cornmeal and helps browning. Skip for a more savory, traditional Southern loaf.","6":"This is the acid that activates the baking soda for lift, so it must stay acidic. Plain milk alone will give a dense, dull loaf."},"sub":{"6":{"x":"1 1/4 cups buttermilk","d":"Slightly thicker and tangier than soured milk, with the same lift. Leave out the lemon juice.","k":["buttermilk"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Put the butter in the skillet only after it has preheated: the batter hitting sizzling fat is what fries the outside into a crisp, brown crust.","s":3},{"t":"Stir only until the streaks of flour disappear. Lumps are fine, and overmixing makes cornbread tough and tunnelled.","s":2},{"t":"Cool in the skillet for the full 5 minutes before cutting, because the crumb is still setting and a hot cut crumbles.","s":5}],"rl":"BSLLZWMSF"},"3161":{"lv":"ERRREROORO","w":{"1":"Supplies the smoky pork fat and savory depth of classic collards. Vegetarian version: use 3 tbsp oil plus 1/2 tsp smoked paprika.","2":"Adds sweetness and body to the pot liquor; without it the greens taste thinner and more bitter.","3":"Gives the savory backbone that offsets the greens' bitterness; the dish is flatter without it.","4":"Braising liquid and the base of the pot liquor. Water works only with extra seasoning.","5":"Cuts the bitterness and brightens the long-cooked greens; without it they taste heavy and dull.","6":"Just a teaspoon to soften bitterness. Skipping it is barely noticeable.","7":"Adds a little background heat; skip it for a milder pot.","8":"Needed to bring out flavor in a long braise, especially if the broth is low-sodium. Add at the end.","9":"A light seasoning; the red pepper already adds bite."},"sub":{"4":{"x":"4 cups water + 1 chicken bouillon cube (or 1 tsp salt)","d":"Thinner, less rich pot liquor; taste and add salt at the end. The bacon helps make up the savory depth.","k":["water","bouillon cube"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Stack the leaves, roll them like a cigar and slice thin ribbons, then rinse in a sink of water twice: collards hold sand in the crinkles.","s":0},{"t":"Keep the simmer lazy and the pot covered; boiling hard breaks the leaves into mush before the tough ribs soften. Taste at 60 minutes, because tenderness matters more than the clock.","s":4},{"t":"Don't discard the pot liquor: spoon it over the greens and sop it up with cornbread. A few drops of hot pepper vinegar at the table is the traditional finish.","s":5}],"rl":"VFXXMAWXZZ"},"3162":{"lv":"EEERROREEE","w":{"3":"Seasons the batter and the crust; hush puppies taste bland without it.","4":"A little sweetness that helps browning and balances the salt; skip for a more savory puppy.","5":"Slight background heat; the onion and cornmeal carry the flavor.","6":"Gives the sweet-savory flavor and moisture in the center; without it they're plain fried cornbread.","9":"Needs a neutral oil with a high smoke point; olive oil is too strong and smokes."},"sub":{"9":{"x":"3 cups peanut or canola oil","d":"Both fry cleanly at 350F with a neutral flavor. Peanut gives a slightly crisper crust.","k":["peanut oil","canola oil"],"wn":"Peanut oil: avoid for anyone with a peanut allergy; use canola instead."}},"tips":[{"t":"Rest the batter 5 minutes as written: the cornmeal absorbs milk and thickens so the puppies hold a rounded shape in the oil.","s":1},{"t":"Use a thermometer and keep the oil at 350F. If it is cooler they soak up grease; if hotter the outside burns before the middle cooks through.","s":2},{"t":"Fry only 5 or 6 at a time so the oil temperature does not crash, and cut one open to check that the center is cooked, not doughy.","s":3}],"rl":"BSLZWZXTMF"},"3168":{"lv":"EREERRO","w":{"1":"Makes it creamy and spreadable and tames the mayo's tang; without it the spread is looser and greasier.","4":"Adds savory background; skip it if you have fresh garlic to add in a pinch.","5":"Gives the signature gentle kick; skipping makes it milder but still correct.","6":"Barely noticeable next to the cayenne and cheddar."},"tips":[{"t":"Shred the cheddar yourself from a block. Pre-shredded cheese is coated with starch that makes the spread dry and gritty instead of creamy.","s":2},{"t":"Blot the drained pimentos on a paper towel so they don't thin out the spread. The extra liquid separates in the fridge.","s":1},{"t":"Chill the full hour; the cheddar and cayenne mellow into the base and the spread firms up so it holds on a cracker.","s":3}],"rl":"PTFVXXZ"},"3169":{"lv":"EERRROO","w":{"2":"Adds sharp tang that keeps the filling from tasting only of mayo.","3":"Brightens the rich yolk filling; a squeeze of lemon works if you are out.","4":"Yolks and mayo are bland on their own; a pinch of salt makes the filling taste of egg.","5":"Purely a color and visual garnish, though it adds a light peppery note.","6":"Fresh oniony crunch on top; skip if you don't have them."},"tips":[{"t":"Cover off the heat for 12 minutes and then ice-bath the eggs: this avoids the green sulfur ring around the yolk and makes the shells slip off.","s":0},{"t":"Press the cooked yolks through a fine sieve before mixing and the filling will be silky, with no lumps to pipe around.","s":2},{"t":"Keep the filled eggs cold until serving and don't leave them out more than two hours, since the mayo and egg filling is perishable.","s":4}],"rl":"PFXAZGG"},"3436":{"lv":"ERREEROROE","w":{"1":"Tang and acidity help the coating cling and slightly tenderize the okra.","2":"Binds the cornmeal coating to the okra; without it the crust shears off more easily.","5":"Seasons the coating; fried okra tastes flat without it.","6":"A light pepper note; not necessary.","7":"Adds savory warmth to the crust; skip if you have none.","8":"A small kick; skip for kids or a milder dish.","9":"Needs a neutral oil; olive oil would taste too strong."},"sub":{"1":{"x":"1/2 cup milk + 1 1/2 tsp lemon juice (rest 5 min)","d":"Close to buttermilk but a touch thinner; coating adheres nearly as well.","k":["milk","lemon juice"]},"9":{"x":"1 1/2 cups peanut or canola oil","d":"Same frying result with a clean, neutral flavor.","k":["peanut oil","canola oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Don't stir the okra in the buttermilk too long or the sap turns slimy. Toss it just before breading and dredge right away.","s":0},{"t":"Fry in small batches without crowding and keep the oil near 350F. Crowded okra steams, turns slimy and the cornmeal crust slips off.","s":4},{"t":"Salt the okra right out of the fryer while it is still hot and oily so the salt sticks.","s":5}],"rl":"VMTBTZZXXF"},"3437":{"lv":"EREEREROOE","w":{"1":"Draws water out of the tomatoes, so the coating stays crisp instead of sliding off watery slices.","6":"Adds a little warm color and sweet-smoky flavor to the crust.","7":"Only a background note; the cayenne does more.","8":"A pinch of heat that offsets the tart tomato; skip for a milder dish.","9":"Needs neutral oil with a high smoke point for shallow frying.","4":"Helps the coating stick; skipped, the egg wash binds."},"sub":{"4":{"x":"1/2 cup milk + 1 1/2 tsp lemon juice (rest 5 min)","d":"Slightly less tangy but binds the egg wash fine.","k":["milk","lemon juice"]},"9":{"x":"1 cup canola or peanut oil","d":"Same shallow-fry result; avoid olive oil since it burns at this temperature.","k":["canola oil","peanut oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Use truly firm, hard green tomatoes, not just underripe pink ones; they stay tart and hold their shape instead of turning to mush inside the crust.","s":0},{"t":"Press the cornmeal in firmly with your palm so it forms a shell, and let the breaded slices rest 5 minutes before frying so the coating sets.","s":2},{"t":"Drain on a wire rack rather than paper towels; the underside steams and softens on paper.","s":5}],"rl":"VZTTMBXZXF"},"3438":{"lv":"ERRRERRORO","w":{"1":"Smoky pork fat tames the peppery mustard bite; without it use 2 tbsp oil and a pinch of smoked paprika.","2":"Gives sweetness and body to the pot liquor that balances the sharp greens.","3":"Savory depth to counter bitterness; the pot tastes thinner without it.","4":"The simmering liquid and the pot liquor base; water works with extra salt.","5":"Added at the end it brightens the greens; skipping leaves them heavy and bitter.","6":"Helps round off the sharpness of mustard greens, which are bitterer than collards.","7":"Gentle heat; skip for a milder pot.","8":"Needed to season a big pile of greens.","9":"A light seasoning."},"sub":{"4":{"x":"3 cups water + 1 chicken bouillon cube","d":"Thinner pot liquor; the bacon gives the savory depth. Adjust salt at the end.","k":["water","bouillon cube"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Mustard greens are sandier than collards. Rinse in several changes of water until the water in the bottom of the sink is clear.","s":0},{"t":"They cook faster than collards: check for tenderness at 35 minutes. Overcooked mustard greens turn khaki and mushy, not silky.","s":4},{"t":"Stir in the vinegar right at the end; if added early its brightness cooks off and the greens taste sharper and bitterer.","s":5}],"rl":"VFXXMAWXZZ"},"3439":{"lv":"EERRROREROO","w":{"2":"Sweet base for the pot liquor; skipping gives a flatter, less rounded peas.","3":"Adds aromatic depth and a little sweetness; peas taste plainer without it.","4":"Rounds out the smoky ham flavor; skipping it makes the pot taste hollow.","5":"A mild herbal note in a long simmer; the thyme covers much of it.","6":"Gives herbal depth that goes with the smoky ham.","7":"Cooking liquid; adds body and flavor. Water works if the ham hock is salty and flavorful.","8":"Peas are bland and need salt; add after they soften so the hock's salt is accounted for.","9":"Light seasoning; the hot sauce brings heat.","10":"Serving extra for a vinegary kick at the table; not required."},"sub":{"7":{"x":"4 cups water + 1 chicken bouillon cube","d":"Less rounded but the ham hock does the heavy lifting; salt carefully at the end.","k":["water","bouillon cube"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Simmer gently with the lid cracked, never a hard boil: rapid boiling bursts the skins so you get mush with hard centers.","s":3},{"t":"Taste for salt only at the end. The smoked hock leaches a lot of salt into the pot, and it's easy to oversalt early.","s":5},{"t":"Skim any foam and excess fat after the hock comes out, then mash a spoonful of peas against the pot to thicken the liquor to a creamy gravy.","s":4}],"rl":"PPXXXXXMZZG"},"3440":{"lv":"ERRERERRRO","w":{"1":"Adds sweetness and savory background to the mild squash; without it the casserole tastes watery.","2":"Gives savory richness and a golden finish; skipping makes it blander.","4":"Gives tang and creaminess in the custard-like sauce; do not skip, but you can swap.","6":"Provides the buttery, crisp topping; without it the casserole is soft on top.","7":"Browns the crumbs and makes them crisp.","8":"Seasoning for the mild squash; it needs it.","9":"Light seasoning only."},"sub":{"4":{"x":"1/2 cup plain Greek yogurt","d":"Tangier and slightly less rich; bakes fine at 350F in a casserole without curdling.","k":["greek yogurt","plain yogurt"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Drain the cooked squash in a colander, then press it between clean towels. Squash is mostly water and skipping this makes a soupy casserole.","s":1},{"t":"Fold the squash in gently so the slices stay intact and don't turn to puree.","s":3},{"t":"Rest the casserole 10 minutes out of the oven so the egg-and-mayo custard sets and it scoops cleanly.","s":5}],"rl":"VXFTTTGFZZ"},"3441":{"lv":"EEEEEEOO","w":{"1":"Gives more savory depth than milk alone; water works for a simpler, milder pot.","6":"A background note; the cayenne and cheddar are the flavor accents.","7":"Subtle heat that wakes up the cheese; skip if serving kids."},"sub":{"1":{"x":"2 cups water + 1/4 tsp extra salt","d":"Milder, less savory grits; the cheddar and butter carry most of the flavor.","k":["water","salt"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Stream the grits in with one hand and whisk with the other. If you dump them in, you get lumps that won't smooth out.","s":1},{"t":"Stone-ground grits need the full 35 to 40 minutes at a bare simmer; if they're still gritty, add hot milk, not cold, and keep cooking.","s":2},{"t":"Take them off the heat before adding the cheddar, as a boiling pot makes the cheese go stringy or greasy. If they stiffen at the table, stir in hot milk or broth.","s":4}],"rl":"BMMFFZZX"},"3442":{"lv":"ERRRRROOR","w":{"1":"Provides rendered fat and smoky salt that define Southern fried cabbage. Skip and use extra butter plus a pinch of smoked paprika.","2":"Sweetens and builds the base flavor as it softens in the fat.","3":"Adds savory depth; skipping makes it taste plainer.","4":"Extra richness and helps browning; the bacon drippings already cover most of it.","5":"Seasoning for a big volume of cabbage.","6":"Light pepper bite; the bacon and pepper flakes already add spice.","7":"Small kick against the sweet cabbage; skip for a milder side.","8":"Cuts the richness at the end and wakes up the flavor; lemon juice works similarly."},"sub":{"4":{"x":"2 tbsp bacon drippings or olive oil","d":"Drippings add more smoke; olive oil is fruitier but works fine.","k":["bacon drippings","olive oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Cook the cabbage uncovered in a wide pan and in batches. Covering or crowding it makes it steam into soft, gray cabbage rather than browned and sweet.","s":4},{"t":"Leave the cabbage alone for a minute or two between stirs so the edges caramelize against the pan.","s":2},{"t":"Return the bacon at the very end so it stays crisp, and add the vinegar off the heat so its brightness isn't cooked away.","s":5}],"rl":"VFXXFZZXA"},"3443":{"lv":"EEERRRORR","w":{"3":"Adds sweetness and syrup body on top of the brown sugar; skipping makes a less sweet, slightly thinner glaze.","4":"Provides the liquid for the syrup and a citrus note that offsets the sweetness; water alone makes a flat glaze.","5":"Warm spice that defines the flavor of candied sweet potatoes.","6":"A faint warm note; cinnamon carries most of it.","7":"Rounds off the sweetness with a floral note; stirred in at the end so it doesn't cook off.","8":"A pinch sharpens sweetness so it isn't cloying."},"sub":{"4":{"x":"1/2 cup apple juice (or 1/2 cup water + 1 tsp lemon juice)","d":"Apple juice gives a mellow sweetness; water + lemon makes it less fruity.","k":["apple juice","water","lemon juice"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Cut the sweet potatoes into even 1-inch chunks and stir only gently; vigorous stirring breaks the soft edges into mash.","s":3},{"t":"Uncover for the last 8 to 10 minutes to reduce the syrup into a glaze. It looks thin when hot but sets as it cools slightly.","s":4},{"t":"Pull the pan when the largest chunk is just fork-tender; the potatoes keep softening in the hot syrup while resting.","s":5}],"rl":"VFWWMXXXZ"},"3444":{"lv":"ERRREORO","w":{"1":"Smoky, salty pork fat gives the beans their traditional flavor; use extra butter and smoked paprika for a vegetarian version.","2":"Adds sweetness and savory depth to the creamy liquor.","3":"Stirred in at the end for richness and body of the pot liquor.","4":"The cooking liquid that becomes the creamy sauce; water plus bouillon works.","5":"A minor herbal note.","6":"Seasons the beans, which are starchy and mild.","7":"A light pepper note."},"sub":{"4":{"x":"2 cups water + 1/2 chicken bouillon cube","d":"Less rich liquor, but the bacon and butter compensate; go easy on salt.","k":["water","bouillon cube"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Cook the frozen beans straight from the freezer without thawing; thawing makes the skins split and the beans fall apart.","s":2},{"t":"Keep a low simmer, not a boil. A gentle bubble keeps the beans whole and lets the starch slowly thicken the liquid into a creamy pot likker.","s":3},{"t":"Mashing a few beans against the pot at the end thickens the sauce without any flour or cornstarch.","s":4}],"rl":"BXXFMXZZ"},"3445":{"lv":"EEREREROOO","w":{"2":"Draws water from the tomatoes so the pie doesn't turn soggy; most of the salt is blotted away.","4":"Adds stretch and mild milky flavor to the topping; extra cheddar can replace it.","6":"Fresh herb note that pairs with ripe tomatoes; the pie is less fragrant without it.","7":"Mild oniony freshness between layers.","8":"A light pepper accent.","9":"A bit of savory background in the mayo-cheese topping."},"sub":{"4":{"x":"1/2 cup additional shredded cheddar","d":"Sharper and slightly greasier, with less stretch. Still browns well.","k":["cheddar"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Salt, drain and blot the tomatoes thoroughly: tomatoes release a lot of water, and skipping this makes a soggy bottom and loose filling.","s":1},{"t":"Blind bake the shell until it is lightly golden and dry. The filling is wet, and an underbaked crust will stay pale and gummy.","s":0},{"t":"Rest 15 minutes after baking so the filling sets enough to slice cleanly instead of sliding out of the crust.","s":5}],"rl":"BVZFFTXXZX"},"3165":{"lv":"EERRREEEREE","w":{"2":"Thickens the peach juices so the cobbler isn't soupy; flour works if you are out.","3":"Brightens the fruit and balances the sweetness.","4":"Warm spice that complements peaches; the cobbler is still fine without.","8":"Seasons the sweet batter so it doesn't taste flat."},"sub":{"2":{"x":"2 tbsp all-purpose flour","d":"Slightly cloudier, less glossy juices but the same thickening.","k":["flour"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Use frozen peaches straight from the freezer without thawing and add a few extra minutes of baking; thawed fruit floods the dish with water.","s":0},{"t":"Spoon the batter in dollops and leave gaps so steam can escape; the batter spreads and puffs into a tender golden crust as it bakes.","s":3},{"t":"Set the dish on a baking sheet to catch bubbling juices, and cool 15 minutes so the filling thickens enough to scoop.","s":5}],"rl":"VWTAXSWLZMF"},"3166":{"lv":"EEREERREER","w":{"2":"Sharpens the custard's sweetness; skipping makes it taste flat.","5":"Adds silkiness and a rounded flavor to the custard.","6":"Gives the classic fragrant custard flavor; the pudding is plainer without it.","9":"Light, cool topping that offsets the sweet custard; skip or use a dollop of store-bought whipped topping."},"tips":[{"t":"Whisk constantly and bring the custard to a gentle bubble for a full minute so the flour cooks out and the yolks reach a safe temperature. Pull it off when it coats a spoon thickly.","s":1},{"t":"If lumps form, pour the custard through a fine strainer before layering to rescue the texture.","s":2},{"t":"Chill at least 4 hours so the wafers soften into cake-like layers, and use ripe but not brown bananas so they don't turn dark and bitter.","s":4}],"rl":"WTZMSFXVBG"},"3170":{"lv":"EEEEEERRRR","w":{"5":"Loosens the filling and adds creaminess; richer than plain milk.","6":"Gives rounded sweetness that lifts the sweet potato; the pie is noticeably flatter without it.","7":"Defines the warm spiced flavor of the pie.","8":"Pairs with cinnamon; skipping it makes the spice less complex.","9":"Balances sweetness and sharpens the sweet potato flavor."},"sub":{"5":{"x":"1/2 cup half-and-half","d":"Slightly richer and silkier filling; evaporated milk also works in the same amount.","k":["half and half","evaporated milk"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Roast the potatoes instead of boiling them. Roasting concentrates their sugars and avoids a watery filling.","s":0},{"t":"Beat the mash well and pull out any stringy fibers (or press through a sieve) for a silky, smooth custard.","s":2},{"t":"Pull the pie when the center still jiggles slightly. It sets as it cools, and overbaking cracks the top.","s":4}],"rl":"VSFWSMXXXZ"},"3557":{"lv":"EEREREEERREEEER","w":{"2":"Gives the subtle chocolate note that makes red velvet more than a red vanilla cake.","4":"Seasons the cake; it tastes flat and overly sweet without it.","8":"Provides the signature color; the cake is still tender and tangy without it, but won't be red.","9":"Rounds the flavor with warm, fragrant sweetness.","14":"Rounds the cream cheese frosting; it tastes tangy and blunt without it.","6":"Acid that activates baking soda and moisture; skipped, the cake is dense and not red."},"sub":{"6":{"x":"1 cup whole milk + 1 tbsp lemon juice (rest 5 min)","d":"Slightly less thick and tangy; still gives the acid that activates the baking soda.","k":["whole milk","lemon juice"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Mix the wet and dry ingredients only until the batter is smooth. Overmixing develops gluten and makes the crumb tough instead of velvety.","s":2},{"t":"Use gel food coloring if you can, which gives a deeper red with less liquid and no bitter edge.","s":2},{"t":"Cool the layers completely before frosting; warm cake melts the cream cheese frosting. A thin crumb coat chilled for 15 minutes keeps red crumbs out of the white.","s":6}],"rl":"SWXLZFMSCXAFFWX"},"3560":{"lv":"EERREEEREEO","w":{"2":"Thickens the berry juices so the filling is jammy, not runny.","3":"Brightens and sharpens the berries' flavor.","7":"Seasons the biscuit topping so it isn't flat.","10":"Crunchy sugary top; purely a finishing touch."},"sub":{"2":{"x":"2 tbsp all-purpose flour","d":"Juices are slightly cloudier and less glossy but thicken fine.","k":["flour"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Cut the cold butter into pea-sized pieces and stop there; those pockets melt in the oven and make the biscuit topping flaky.","s":2},{"t":"Don't smooth the dough; a sticky, rough, dolloped topping bakes up crisp and craggy. Leave gaps so steam from the berries can escape.","s":4},{"t":"Bake until juices bubble through the center, not just the edges, as that is when the cornstarch has fully thickened. Frozen berries need no thawing.","s":5}],"rl":"VWTASWLZFMG"},"53165":{"lv":"ERERREEOO","w":{"1":"Adds richness to the soaking custard; extra milk works and the torrijas are just slightly less rich.","3":"Lightly sweetens the custard; sherry and the dusting sugar also sweeten, so you could cut it.","4":"Gives the nutty, raisiny aroma that makes these torrijas; without it they taste like plain French toast.","6":"Frying fat that crisps the edges; a neutral oil works with less fruity flavor.","7":"Finishing dusting only.","8":"A cool, tangy contrast to the sweet bread; plain yogurt is fine or skip."},"sub":{"1":{"x":"2 tbsp extra milk (4 tbsp milk total)","d":"Slightly lighter custard and less rich center, still soaks in fine.","k":["milk"]},"6":{"x":"1 1/2 tbsp sunflower or vegetable oil","d":"Cleaner, more neutral taste; olive oil gives a fruitier crust.","k":["vegetable oil","sunflower oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Use bread that is a day or two stale and thick-cut. Fresh sandwich bread collapses in the custard, while sturdy dry bread soaks through and stays custardy in the middle.","s":0},{"t":"Dip each piece for a few seconds per side and let it drain; soaking too long makes the slices fall apart in the pan.","s":0},{"t":"Fry over medium heat, not high: the eggy soak scorches fast before the center is warmed through. Give each side time to turn dark golden.","s":1}],"rl":"SFMWXBFGG"},"3517":{"lv":"EEERERRRRERRREEOORO","w":{"3":"Soaked with the breadcrumbs into a panade that keeps the meatballs tender; water works equally well.","5":"Gives the meatballs savory bite; the sauce garlic adds more.","6":"Fresh herb note inside the meatballs; skipping it makes them blander but fine.","7":"Adds warm Spanish color and flavor to the meatballs; the sauce is still good without it.","8":"Light dredge that helps browning and slightly thickens the sauce; skipping is fine but meatballs brown less evenly.","9":"Browning fat; any neutral oil works.","10":"Sweet base of the sauce; the sauce tastes thinner without it.","11":"Savory sauce depth that tomato alone doesn't give.","12":"Deglazes and adds acidity; broth plus a splash of vinegar substitutes well.","15":"Subtle background in a short simmer.","16":"Sweet pop and color; the dish is complete without them.","17":"Salt is mostly needed in the meatballs; adjust the sauce at the end.","18":"Light seasoning in the meat."},"sub":{"3":{"x":"1/4 cup water or chicken broth","d":"Same softening of the crumbs; slightly less richness but equally tender.","k":["water","chicken broth"]},"9":{"x":"3 tbsp vegetable oil","d":"Browns the same; loses the fruity olive taste.","k":["vegetable oil"]},"12":{"x":"1/2 cup chicken broth + 1 tsp white wine vinegar","d":"Less depth but similar acidity to lift the tomato sauce.","k":["chicken broth","white vinegar"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Mix the meat mixture only until combined; overworked ground meat turns dense and rubbery. Wet hands keep the meatballs from sticking as you roll.","s":0},{"t":"Brown the meatballs only to a crust; they finish in the sauce. The meatballs must reach 160F (71C) inside, so cut one open to check.","s":2},{"t":"Simmer gently with the lid on; a hard boil makes pork-and-beef meatballs tough and breaks them up, and sauce that is too loose can be simmered uncovered for the last few minutes.","s":4}],"rl":"PPTMTXXXTFXXAVMXVZZ"},"3518":{"lv":"EEERRRORRRRO","w":{"5":"Gives a sweet herbal layer against the garlic; the dish is fine without it.","6":"Subtle in a 25 minute simmer.","7":"Adds color and mild smoky flavor to the chicken skin and sauce.","8":"Fresh finish that cuts the richness; skipping loses brightness but not the dish.","9":"Brightens the heavy garlic and oil; a splash of vinegar is similar.","10":"Seasons the chicken skin and sauce; essential to taste, though the amount is flexible.","11":"A mild accent only.","3":"Deglazes the browned bits and adds acidity; broth plus vinegar works.","4":"Braising liquid; water plus a pinch of salt will do."},"sub":{"3":{"x":"1/2 cup chicken broth + 1 tbsp white wine vinegar","d":"Less aromatic than wine or sherry but gives similar acidity to deglaze.","k":["chicken broth","white vinegar"]},"4":{"x":"1/2 cup water + 1/4 chicken bouillon cube","d":"Slightly thinner sauce; the garlic and chicken fat carry the flavor.","k":["water","bouillon cube"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Start the chicken skin side down in the oil and don't move it for 6 to 8 minutes. It will release from the pan on its own once the skin is crisp and golden.","s":1},{"t":"Add the garlic at medium heat and watch it closely: golden is sweet and nutty, but a few seconds past that turns it bitter. If it darkens, discard it and start again with fresh cloves.","s":2},{"t":"Bone-in thighs are safe at 165F (74C) but taste best past 175F, when the connective tissue has softened, so use the full 25 minutes.","s":4}],"rl":"PXFAMXXXGAZZ"},"3522":{"lv":"EERERRRORRR","w":{"2":"Adds rich, iron-y depth and a creamy stew; skip it if you can't find it and add a little more chorizo.","4":"Sweet aromatic body for the broth; the stew tastes flatter without it.","5":"Mellow savory depth as the cloves soften into the broth.","6":"Smoky color and flavor that tie the stew to the chorizo.","7":"A very small pinch gives color and aroma but little taste.","8":"Gentle herbal background in a long simmer.","9":"Small amount of fat; the cured meats supply most of the fat.","10":"Add near the end because chorizo and bacon are already salty; the beans need some salt to taste right."},"tips":[{"t":"Do not boil hard: a bare simmer keeps the beans whole and creamy. The classic method is to add a splash of cold water to stop the boil (asustar) at least twice.","s":1},{"t":"Shake and swirl the pot rather than stirring with a spoon, which breaks the beans and clouds the broth.","s":3},{"t":"Salt only in the last 15 minutes since the cured pork keeps adding salt as it cooks, and rest the stew 10 minutes (or a day) because fabada is better after the broth thickens.","s":4}],"rl":"BPPPXXXCXFZ"},"53086":{"lv":"EEER","w":{"3":"Pork bits (chorizo, pancetta or bacon) add the salt and savory fat; without them the migas are plain garlic bread crumbs.","1":"Frying fat; the amount is a data error. Use only enough olive oil to generously coat the pan, a few tablespoons, adding more by the spoonful as the crumbs absorb it."},"tips":[{"t":"Use day-old, hard bread and sprinkle with lightly salted water, not a soaking. It should be damp but not mushy so the crumbs fry into separate, golden bits.","s":0},{"t":"Keep the heat moderate and stir constantly. The crumbs scorch quickly, and the long stirring is what dries them into crisp, fluffy crumbs.","s":2},{"t":"Use only enough olive oil to coat the pan generously, not a liter and a half. Migas absorb a lot but shouldn't be swimming; add more oil by the spoonful.","s":1}],"rl":"BFXX"},"53143":{"lv":"ERERRERERREO","w":{"0":"Just to start the onion and peppers; the chorizo releases fat as well, so any oil works.","1":"Sweet base for the sauce.","3":"Adds sweetness and color to the sauce.","4":"Second pepper adds volume and sweetness; one pepper is fine.","6":"Savory depth; sauce tastes flat without it.","8":"Briny, salty bite that cuts the rich chorizo sauce.","9":"Makes the dish a one-pot meal and thickens the sauce; skipping makes it thin.","11":"Fresh finish only."},"sub":{"0":{"x":"2 tbsp vegetable oil","d":"Same frying result; olive oil adds a slightly fruitier flavor.","k":["vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Brown the onion and chorizo over medium heat until the chorizo stains the oil red. That paprika-rich fat is the base flavor of the sauce.","s":0},{"t":"Nestle the chicken skin side up so the skin stays above the sauce and crisps in the oven; they are done at 165F (74C), though thighs are better closer to 175F.","s":1},{"t":"If the sauce looks thin at the end, put the pan back over medium heat for 5 minutes, since the beans thicken it as they break.","s":1}],"rl":"FXXVVXXVXBPG"},"53145":{"lv":"EEEEREEEE","w":{"0":"Gives the garlicky, green flavor of the name. Without wild garlic season is out; use spinach plus garlic.","2":"Fat for the roux; butter gives a richer, creamier flavor.","4":"Nutty, salty cheese; any hard cheese like cheddar or parmesan works.","8":"Deep-frying oil; any neutral oil works."},"sub":{"0":{"x":"150 g baby spinach + 2 finely chopped garlic cloves","d":"Milder, less pungent than wild garlic; blanch and squeeze dry the same way.","k":["spinach","garlic"]},"2":{"x":"45 g (3 tbsp) butter","d":"Slightly richer and sweeter; the roux works the same way.","k":["butter"]},"8":{"x":"1 liter sunflower or canola oil","d":"Neutral and clean for frying; olive oil would be too strong.","k":["sunflower oil","canola oil","vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Squeeze the blanched wild garlic bone dry before chopping. Extra water makes the béchamel loose, and the croquetas burst in the fryer.","s":0},{"t":"Chill the mixture at least overnight; it must be firm enough to shape. Then freeze the balls 30 minutes so the crumb coating sets before they hit the oil.","s":3},{"t":"Double-coat in egg and crumbs, sealing every gap, and fry at 180C. Too cool and they leak; too hot and the outside burns before the middle is hot.","s":4}],"rl":"XMFTXXTTF"},"53155":{"lv":"EERRREERO","w":{"2":"A small amount to soften the onions; any cooking oil works.","3":"Sweet, soft base for the sauce.","4":"Savory depth that boosts the tomato filling.","7":"Adds sweetness and a smoky note to the filling; skipping makes it plainer.","8":"Briny pop in the filling; skip if you don't like olives."},"sub":{"2":{"x":"2 tsp vegetable oil","d":"Same softening of the onions; slightly less fruity.","k":["vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Steam-dry the drained potatoes in the hot pan for a minute before mashing. Wet mash sinks into the sauce rather than sitting on top as a crust.","s":0},{"t":"Simmer the filling until it is thick and spoonable, not soupy; the mash needs a firm base to rest on.","s":1},{"t":"Rake the top of the mash with a fork before baking so the ridges brown and crisp.","s":2}],"rl":"BXFVXVPVG"},"53156":{"lv":"RERRRRRREOREOER","w":{"2":"Adds iron-rich, spiced depth and richness; the pork belly and bacon carry the dish without it.","3":"Smoky saltiness to the rice and broth; skipping means seasoning more with salt and paprika.","4":"Sweet base for the rice.","5":"Adds sweetness and color through the rice; skipping loses some freshness.","6":"Gives a little acidity and body to the sofrito.","7":"Savory depth that perfumes the rice.","9":"A little background heat.","10":"Hearty and starchy; dried beans won't soften in this time, so use canned or pre-cooked beans or skip.","12":"Minor herbal note.","14":"Brightens the rich pork and rice at the end.","0":"Meats supply fat; skipped, the rice still bakes."},"sub":{"0":{"x":"2 tbsp vegetable oil","d":"Same for browning the meats; olive oil is fruitier.","k":["vegetable oil"]},"10":{"x":"1 can (400 g) white beans, drained and rinsed","d":"Already cooked, so they heat through in the rice time; add with the rice.","k":[]}},"tips":[{"t":"Brown the pork belly hard over high heat to render its fat first; that fat then flavors the sofrito and the rice.","s":0},{"t":"Do not stir the rice after it goes in. Level it, and leave it uncovered so the top dries and the bottom forms a golden, crisp crust.","s":1},{"t":"Dried beans must be soaked and cooked before this recipe or they stay hard; use 1 can (about 400 g) of white beans, drained, and add them with the rice.","s":1}],"rl":"FPPPXVVXXXBMXBA"},"53157":{"lv":"EERERRO","w":{"2":"Adds crunch and freshness to the salad; potatoes and chorizo could carry it on their own.","4":"Mellows in the chorizo fat and gives the warm dressing its savory note.","5":"The acidity of the warm dressing; red wine vinegar is a close substitute.","6":"Fresh, green note at the end."},"sub":{"5":{"x":"2 tbsp red wine vinegar","d":"Slightly sharper, less nutty than sherry vinegar but just as bright.","k":["red wine vinegar"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Adding the eggs after 6 minutes in the pot gives 6-minute eggs with a jammy yolk; plunge them in cold water straight away so the yolks stop cooking.","s":0},{"t":"Make the dressing in the chorizo fat off the heat. Adding the vinegar to a ripping-hot pan flashes it off and loses its sharpness.","s":2},{"t":"Toss the potatoes while they are still warm so they soak up the paprika-red dressing, and add the eggs last so they don't break.","s":2}],"rl":"BPVPXAG"},"53161":{"lv":"RERRRERRREEO","w":{"0":"Only to brown the chicken; chorizo fat adds more.","2":"Sweet base for the rice.","3":"Adds sweetness and color; the rice pot is fine with half a pepper.","4":"Savory depth to the rice.","6":"Adds umami and color to the rice; skipping makes it paler.","7":"Earthy herb note that goes with chicken and chorizo.","8":"Deglazes the fond and adds acidity; use extra stock plus a splash of vinegar.","11":"Fresh finish only."},"sub":{"0":{"x":"1 tbsp olive oil","d":"Browns the chicken the same way, with a fruitier flavor.","k":["olive oil"]},"8":{"x":"150 ml chicken broth + 1 tsp white wine vinegar","d":"Less aromatic but gives similar acidity; use a splash less broth overall.","k":["chicken broth","white vinegar"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Brown the chicken in batches so the pot isn't crowded; the browned bits on the bottom flavor the whole pot of rice.","s":0},{"t":"Let the chicken simmer until it reaches 165F (74C) before adding the rice, and don't stir the rice after it goes in.","s":3},{"t":"Rice must not sit in a warm pot for long: refrigerate leftovers within 2 hours and reheat until steaming hot.","s":3}],"rl":"FPXVXXXXAMBG"},"53162":{"lv":"REEEERERRREEROREO","w":{"0":"Saffron gives the traditional golden color and floral aroma; turmeric adds color but not flavor.","1":"Used for the picada and browning; any cooking oil works.","5":"Fresh herb note to finish and in the sauce.","7":"Sweet base for the sauce.","8":"Sweet body for the sauce.","9":"Aromatic background for the sauce.","10":"Deglazes and adds the nutty depth. Broth plus a splash of vinegar will do.","12":"Gentle warm spice that is typical of pepitoria.","13":"A whisper of spice; skipping is fine.","14":"Subtle herbal background.","16":"Crunchy garnish only."},"sub":{"1":{"x":"4 tbsp vegetable oil","d":"Same frying result, less fruity.","k":["vegetable oil"]},"10":{"x":"250 ml chicken broth + 1 tbsp white wine vinegar","d":"Loses the nutty sherry flavor but adds similar acidity.","k":["chicken broth","white vinegar"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Fry the garlic, almonds and bread until golden, then grind them into a paste. This picada thickens the sauce, so don't skip grinding it fine.","s":0},{"t":"Brown the chicken skin side down first and don't crowd the pan; the fond is what the sherry lifts into the sauce.","s":1},{"t":"Chicken thighs are safe at 165F (74C) but better past 175F. Whisk the mashed yolks into the warm sauce off a hard boil so it stays smooth.","s":3}],"rl":"CFXTTGPXXXMMXXXTG"},"53175":{"lv":"ERRRREERRRRERO","w":{"1":"Just for browning the vegetables; any oil works.","2":"Sweet aromatic base for the braise.","3":"Sweetness and body in the cooking juices.","4":"Roasts soft and sweet with the lamb; the braise is milder without it.","5":"Braising liquid; water plus a bouillon cube works.","7":"Adds sweetness and smoke to the beans; skipping makes the dish less colorful.","8":"Briny bite that cuts through the rich lamb.","9":"Fresh finish for the rub and dish.","10":"Garlic in the rub that seasons the lamb; keep it if possible.","12":"Carries the spice rub into the meat; any oil works.","13":"A minor herb note beside the paprika."},"sub":{"1":{"x":"1 tbsp vegetable oil","d":"Same for browning vegetables; less fruity.","k":["vegetable oil"]},"5":{"x":"2 cups water + 1 chicken bouillon cube","d":"Thinner cooking liquid but the lamb adds its own richness.","k":["water","bouillon cube"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Rub the paste in and leave the lamb in the fridge for several hours or overnight if you can; salt and paprika season deep into a big shoulder.","s":0},{"t":"Cook it low and slow, covered with foil or a lid, until the meat is pull-apart tender (around 195 to 205F inside). This tough cut needs time to break down its connective tissue.","s":1},{"t":"Skim the fat before adding the beans, and rest the lamb for 20 minutes covered so the juices settle.","s":2}],"rl":"PFXVXMBVXGXXFX"},"53176":{"lv":"EREROER","w":{"1":"Adds a gentle garlic bite to the tomato topping; you can also rub the toasted bread with a cut clove.","3":"Brings out the tomato's sweetness; the topping is flat without salt.","4":"Only a pinch; the olive oil and ham are the accents.","6":"Salty, savory topping; without it you have classic pan con tomate."},"tips":[{"t":"Drain the chopped tomatoes in a sieve for 10 minutes before mixing so the topping doesn't soak the toast.","s":0},{"t":"Toast the baguette slices until crisp and golden, then rub with a cut garlic clove while they are warm; the toasted surface works like a grater.","s":1},{"t":"Top each slice just before serving. Tomato on toast goes soggy within minutes, and the ham stays silky if torn at the last moment.","s":2}],"rl":"VXFZZBG"},"53179":{"lv":"EEEEEREERER","w":{"3":"Deep-frying oil; any neutral oil.","4":"Fat for the roux; butter makes a richer filling.","5":"Sweet, mild aromatic that rounds the ham flavor.","8":"Thins the filling with a bit more flavor; extra milk works.","10":"Classic béchamel seasoning; the croquetas are still good without it."},"sub":{"3":{"x":"1 liter sunflower or canola oil","d":"Neutral and clean for frying.","k":["sunflower oil","canola oil","vegetable oil"]},"4":{"x":"4 tbsp butter","d":"Richer and a little sweeter; works the same in the roux.","k":["butter"]},"8":{"x":"75 ml extra milk","d":"Slightly creamier, less savory; keep the total liquid the same.","k":["milk"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Add the hot milk to the roux a few spoonfuls at a time, beating until smooth before adding more. Dumping it in all at once creates lumps.","s":1},{"t":"The filling should be thick enough to leave the sides of the pan and hold a spoon. If it is too soft, the croquetas will burst in the oil.","s":2},{"t":"Chill overnight, roll with oiled hands, and fry at 180C a few at a time; check the oil is hot with a breadcrumb that sizzles at once.","s":8}],"rl":"TTTFFXXTMMX"},"53181":{"lv":"ERROOOERER","w":{"1":"Sweet base for the beans.","2":"Gives the stew its smoky red color and flavor.","3":"Herb note that helps the beans; the chorizo is the main flavor.","4":"Mild background in a long simmer.","5":"Fresh finish only.","7":"Adds substance and soaks up the broth.","9":"Adds color and greens at the end; the stew is fine without it."},"tips":[{"t":"Cover the beans by several inches and start them at a boil for 10 minutes, then drop to a gentle simmer. Don't add salt or acid early, which toughens the skins.","s":0},{"t":"Cook the chicken until it reaches 165F (74C), then pull it off the bone. Thighs are best shredded into big chunks.","s":2},{"t":"Stir in the spinach at the very end so it wilts and stays green, instead of going gray in the stew.","s":2}],"rl":"BXXXXGPBXV"},"53185":{"lv":"EROREE","w":{"1":"Sharp, crunchy bite to cut the oil and chorizo; slice thin and soak in cold water if too strong.","2":"Faint herbal note; the vinegar and chorizo carry the salad.","3":"Adds brightness; skipped, the dish is flatter but still works."},"sub":{"3":{"x":"1 tbsp red wine vinegar","d":"A little sharper and less nutty than sherry vinegar.","k":["red wine vinegar"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Salt the tomatoes and let them sit 10 minutes before dressing. They release juice that mixes with the oil and vinegar into the dressing.","s":1},{"t":"Cook the chorizo in a dry pan so its own paprika-red fat renders; drizzle that oil over the tomatoes as the finishing touch.","s":4},{"t":"Use ripe tomatoes at room temperature; cold tomatoes taste muted.","s":0}],"rl":"VXXAFX"},"53186":{"lv":"EERRRERRRO","w":{"1":"Browns the chicken; any cooking oil works.","2":"Savory aroma that melts into the sherry sauce.","3":"Gives the sauce the golden, floral note of the dish; skipping makes it plainer.","6":"Herbal note that goes with chicken and saffron.","7":"Sweet, chewy contrast against savory chicken; the dish loses balance without them.","8":"Toasty crunch and richness on top; almonds are a close substitute.","9":"Fresh finish only.","4":"Provides acidity and nutty depth to the sauce."},"sub":{"1":{"x":"2 tbsp vegetable oil","d":"Same browning; less fruity flavor.","k":["vegetable oil"]},"4":{"x":"125 ml chicken broth + 1 tbsp white wine vinegar","d":"Loses the nutty sherry note but keeps the acidity.","k":["chicken broth","white vinegar"]},"8":{"x":"2 tbsp slivered almonds","d":"Slightly less buttery but toasts the same way.","k":["almonds"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Joint the chicken so the legs go in first and the breast later; the breast dries out if it cooks as long as the legs.","s":2},{"t":"Cook until legs reach at least 175F and the breast 165F (74C). Resting it in the sauce keeps the breast moist.","s":2},{"t":"Toast the pine nuts until golden and watch them closely; they go from toasted to burnt in under a minute.","s":3}],"rl":"PFXCAMXWGG"},"52836":{"lv":"REREERRRRRERRO","w":{"0":"Adds briny shellfish flavor and the look of the dish; the fideuà is still good with other seafood.","2":"Gives golden color and a floral aroma that defines paella-style noodle dishes; the dish is paler and plainer without it.","5":"Sweet base for the sofrito.","6":"Savory depth and aroma; skip only if you must.","7":"Warm color and mild smokiness in the broth.","8":"Firm, meaty fish that holds up in the pan; any firm white fish works.","9":"Adds tenderness and sweet flavor, but don't overcook; skip if not on hand.","11":"Grated into the sofrito for sweetness and body.","12":"Squeezed over at the end to brighten the rich seafood.","13":"Fresh garnish only."},"sub":{"8":{"x":"about 400 g firm white fish such as cod or halibut, in chunks","d":"Flakier and milder than monkfish; add it at the same point and stir gently.","k":["cod","halibut","white fish"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Toast the broken vermicelli in the oil until it turns deep golden before adding liquid. This gives the nutty flavor and helps it stay separate instead of gluey.","s":2},{"t":"Once the stock goes in, stop stirring so the pasta forms a crisp, golden crust on the bottom (socarrat), the prized part of a fideuà.","s":3},{"t":"Discard any mussels that stay closed after cooking and any that were open and wouldn't close before; shrimp should be pink and opaque. Serve with alioli.","s":4}],"rl":"PPXBFXXXPPMVAG"},"52872":{"lv":"REREREOOOO","w":{"0":"Slow-cooked until sweet and silky, it gives the tortilla its mellow flavor; it will be plainer without it.","2":"Adds a buttery note to the oil; olive oil alone works.","4":"Used to rub the toasted bread and add a little to the tortilla; skip if not serving the bread.","6":"Fresh garnish only.","7":"Part of the accompaniment, not the tortilla.","8":"Grated onto the toast for pan con tomate.","9":"Finishing drizzle for the bread."},"tips":[{"t":"Cook the onions and potatoes slowly in the oil, covered, without browning. They should poach until soft; browned potatoes make a firm, dry tortilla.","s":0},{"t":"Stir the hot potatoes into the beaten eggs and let them sit for 5 minutes before cooking. The eggs soak into the potatoes so the tortilla is juicy.","s":1},{"t":"Rub the toasted baguette with raw garlic and grate the tomato onto it (discard the skin) for a fresh, rustic pan con tomate.","s":3}],"rl":"XFFBXSGGGG"},"52942":{"lv":"EERRRERRREROORO","w":{"2":"Adds sweetness and color to the roasted vegetable mix.","3":"Soft roasted vegetable for body; the dish still works without it.","4":"The sofrito base that flavors the rice.","6":"Gives the rice its smoky color and flavor, which is needed since there's no meat.","7":"Golden color and floral note; the dish is paler without it.","8":"Deglazes and adds acidity; extra broth plus vinegar works.","10":"Sweet pops of color; swap in green beans or skip.","11":"Wedges for squeezing over at the table; adds brightness but isn't needed.","12":"Fresh garnish only.","13":"Seasoning for the roasting vegetables; use it generously.","14":"A light finishing accent."},"sub":{"8":{"x":"200 ml extra vegetable broth + 1 tbsp white wine vinegar","d":"Less depth than wine, but keeps the acidity needed to lift the dish.","k":["vegetable broth","white vinegar"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Roast the vegetables until the edges char. Browning builds the sweet, smoky flavor that replaces the meat in this paella.","s":0},{"t":"Add the stock and then leave the rice alone; stirring releases starch and makes it creamy like risotto instead of separate grains.","s":2},{"t":"Turn the heat up for the last minute until you hear a crackle to form the toasted socarrat, then rest it covered for 5 minutes.","s":3}],"rl":"VVVVXBXCAMVGGZZ"},"53142":{"lv":"ERRERERE","w":{"0":"Just to fry the onion; any cooking oil works.","1":"Sweet base that balances the curry spices.","2":"Adds fresh heat and a bright note; half goes in the pan and half on top.","4":"Burst into juicy, sweet pockets in the eggs; skipping makes the tortilla plainer.","6":"Stalks are fried in; leaves add a fresh finish."},"sub":{"0":{"x":"1 tbsp olive oil","d":"Fruitier but works the same for frying the onion.","k":["olive oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Slice the potatoes thin, or microwave or parboil them briefly first. Otherwise 500 g of raw potato will still be hard when the eggs are set.","s":1},{"t":"Fry the curry powder in the oil for a full minute before adding anything wet. Toasting removes the raw, dusty taste and blooms the spices.","s":1},{"t":"Cook the tortilla over low heat until the edges set, then finish under the grill so you don't have to flip it.","s":2}],"rl":"FXXXVBGS"},"53144":{"lv":"EEERO","w":{"3":"Dried or fresh chilli gives the characteristic mild heat; skip for plain garlic prawns.","4":"Fresh garnish only."},"tips":[{"t":"Start the garlic in the oil as it heats so it perfumes the oil, then add the prawns the moment it turns pale gold; garlic that turns brown is bitter.","s":2},{"t":"Pat the prawns completely dry and season just before cooking so they sizzle and don't steam; cook only 1 to 2 minutes a side until pink and opaque.","s":2},{"t":"Serve at once with crusty bread. The garlic and chilli-infused oil is half the dish.","s":3}],"rl":"PXFXG"},"53147":{"lv":"EERORERREE","w":{"1":"For frying the sofrito; any cooking oil works.","2":"Sweet base for the rice.","3":"Faint herbal note in the stock.","4":"Gives the golden color and floral aroma; paler and plainer without it.","6":"Adds depth and color to the rice; skip for a lighter, less tomato-ey rice.","7":"Deglazes and adds acidity; use extra stock and a splash of vinegar."},"sub":{"1":{"x":"2 tbsp vegetable oil","d":"Same sofrito base, less fruity.","k":["vegetable oil"]},"7":{"x":"200 ml extra seafood stock + 1 tbsp white wine vinegar","d":"Less aromatic, still gives the acidity that balances the rice.","k":["seafood stock","chicken broth","white vinegar"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Squid gets tough in between: add it with the stock so it either cooks quickly or simmers long enough to become tender, not halfway.","s":1},{"t":"Keep a few whole prawns on top and fold the peeled ones in at the end; the shells flavor the rice and prawns turn rubbery if cooked too long.","s":2},{"t":"Prawns must be pink and opaque before serving. Let the rice rest covered for 5 minutes so the grains finish absorbing the broth.","s":2}],"rl":"PFXXCBXAMP"},"53150":{"lv":"EE","tips":[{"t":"Dry the peppers well and use a very hot pan; wet peppers steam and turn olive-gray instead of blistering with dark, charred spots.","s":0},{"t":"Salt right after they come off the heat with flaky sea salt so it sticks to the hot oil. Eat them whole, holding the stem, as about one in ten can be hot.","s":1}],"rl":"FV"},"53151":{"lv":"EEORRRRRRRRERO","w":{"0":"To fry the base; any cooking oil works.","2":"Parsley stalks flavor the prawn stock and leaves are a fresh garnish.","3":"Deglazes the prawn heads and adds a nutty sweetness; use broth plus a splash of vinegar.","4":"Briny shellfish flavor and a nice presentation; swap with extra squid or prawns.","6":"Smoky, paprika-rich oil that colors and flavors the rice.","7":"Sweet base for the rice.","8":"Savory depth that rounds out the sofrito.","9":"Tender seafood note; overcooked it gets rubbery.","10":"Grated or chopped into the sofrito for sweetness and acid.","12":"Sweet green bursts; peas work as well.","13":"Bright finish only."},"sub":{"0":{"x":"3 tbsp vegetable oil","d":"Same frying result, with less fruitiness.","k":["vegetable oil"]},"3":{"x":"100 ml chicken broth + 1 tsp white wine vinegar","d":"Loses the nutty sherry note but keeps acidity.","k":["chicken broth","white vinegar"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Fry the prawn heads and mash them to release their fat; that orange oil is the foundation of the broth. Strain it before using.","s":0},{"t":"Don't stir the paella once the rice goes in. Let it cook undisturbed and listen for a crackle for the last minute to develop a thin, crisp socarrat.","s":3},{"t":"Add the mussels on top at the end, and discard any that stay closed after cooking. Rest it under a tea towel so the grains finish absorbing the stock.","s":4}],"rl":"FPGAPCXXXPVBVG"},"53158":{"lv":"ERRREREO","w":{"2":"Sweet base for the bravas sauce.","3":"Savory depth for the sauce.","5":"Adds concentrated tomato flavor and color to the sauce.","7":"Fresh garnish only.","1":"Air fryer needs little oil; skipped, the potatoes are paler and drier."},"sub":{"1":{"x":"3 tbsp vegetable oil","d":"Crisps the potatoes the same way; olive oil is fruitier.","k":["vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"The hot-water soak pulls out surface starch. Dry them well so the air fryer makes a crisp shell and a fluffy middle rather than steaming.","s":0},{"t":"Shake the basket halfway so the potatoes crisp evenly, and don't overload the basket, or the pieces stick together.","s":0},{"t":"Cook the sauce down until it clings to a spoon, then pour it over the potatoes only at serving so they stay crisp. Add chilli powder or hot paprika for authentic heat.","s":2}],"rl":"BFXXXXVG"},"53160":{"lv":"ERREOROEEEEO","w":{"1":"Slow-cooked until sweet, it forms the sweet backbone of the pisto.","2":"Savory aroma that rounds out the vegetable stew.","4":"A light herbal note.","5":"Fresh herb note that brightens the stew.","6":"Subtle background that fades in a long stew; skip if you don't have it.","11":"Fresh finish only."},"tips":[{"t":"Cook the onions covered with a little salt over low heat so they sweat until very soft and sweet before adding anything else.","s":0},{"t":"Cook the vegetables down until thick and jammy and most of the liquid has evaporated; a watery pisto makes the eggs slide around and cook unevenly.","s":2},{"t":"Make wells with a spoon so each egg sits in its own pocket, cover the pan, and cook until the whites are set and opaque. Cook the yolks firm for kids or anyone vulnerable.","s":3}],"rl":"FXXVXXXVVVPG"},"53167":{"lv":"RRRREERE","w":{"0":"Just to soften the leek; chorizo adds more fat. Any oil works.","1":"Sweet, mild onion flavor for the base of the rice.","2":"Smoky, paprika-red fat flavors the rice; the dish is bland without it.","3":"Gives golden color (a budget stand-in for saffron) but little flavor.","6":"Sweet green pops of color; skipping is fine."},"sub":{"0":{"x":"1 tbsp vegetable oil","d":"Same softening of the leek, with a more neutral flavor.","k":["vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Coat the rice in the chorizo oil before adding stock so every grain is lightly toasted and stays separate.","s":1},{"t":"Use hot stock and simmer gently with only occasional stirring, since too much stirring makes the rice creamy rather than fluffy.","s":1},{"t":"Add the frozen seafood right at the end; it only needs a minute or two to heat through and cook fully. Overcooked it turns rubbery.","s":2}],"rl":"FVXCBMVP"},"53171":{"lv":"EREREOE","w":{"0":"For frying the onion and cooking the tortilla; any oil works.","1":"Sweet base that balances the salty cod.","3":"Savory depth in the mix.","5":"Fresh garnish only."},"sub":{"0":{"x":"4 tbsp olive oil or vegetable oil","d":"Olive oil is traditional and fruity; vegetable oil is neutral.","k":["olive oil","vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Soak the salt cod in several changes of cold water for 24 to 48 hours before using. Skipping that makes the tortilla far too salty.","s":1},{"t":"Flake the cod and drain it very dry so it doesn't water down the eggs.","s":1},{"t":"Cook over medium-low heat and cover the pan; high heat browns the outside before the center sets.","s":2}],"rl":"FXBXSGP"},"53172":{"lv":"ERREREOOOEE","w":{"0":"To soften the onion; any oil works.","1":"Sweet base for the sauce.","2":"Savory depth in the sauce.","4":"Concentrates the tomato flavor and color.","6":"Provides the heat that makes these bravas.","7":"A pinch balances the tomato's acidity.","8":"Fresh garnish only."},"sub":{"0":{"x":"3 tbsp vegetable oil","d":"Same softening of the onion, less fruity.","k":["vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Pat the potatoes bone dry and preheat the roasting tin with the oil so they sizzle when added. This gives the crisp, golden crust.","s":2},{"t":"Simmer the sauce until pulpy and thick, since a thin sauce makes the potatoes soggy. It keeps chilled, so make it a day ahead.","s":1},{"t":"Spoon the sauce over just before serving, or serve it on the side if you need the potatoes to stay crisp.","s":3}],"rl":"FXXVXXXWGBF"},"53174":{"lv":"EERRREOEERE","w":{"2":"Adds gentle heat and a fruity note to the romesco; the sauce is milder without it.","3":"Adds sweetness and body to the sauce.","4":"Gives richness and a sweeter nutty flavor; extra almonds work as a swap.","6":"Adds a fresh herbal note to the sauce.","9":"Gives the sharp tang that balances the nuts and oil."},"sub":{"4":{"x":"10 extra almonds (toasted)","d":"Slightly less sweet than hazelnuts but thickens and flavors the sauce the same way.","k":["almonds"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Char the pepper until blackened, then steam it covered for a few minutes so the skin slips off. The smoky sweetness is the backbone of romesco.","s":1},{"t":"Toast the nuts until fragrant and golden, since raw nuts taste flat. Pulse in the processor to a coarse paste, not a smooth puree.","s":2},{"t":"Sear the prawns about 2 minutes a side until pink and opaque, and let the sauce rest for a few hours; it tastes better the next day.","s":4}],"rl":"VXXVTTGTFAP"},"53178":{"lv":"EERRREO","w":{"2":"Fry in the flour as crisp salty bursts; skip if you dislike briny bits.","3":"Goes into the mayo for a quick aioli dip; skip for plain mayo.","4":"Dipping sauce for the calamari; lemon alone works.","5":"Needs a neutral oil with a high smoke point.","6":"Squeezed over to cut the grease."},"sub":{"5":{"x":"vegetable or sunflower oil, enough for 7 cm depth","d":"Neutral, high smoke point and clean.","k":["vegetable oil","sunflower oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Pat the squid dry, then flour it only just before frying so the coating is dry and light. Wet squid makes a gummy crust.","s":0},{"t":"Fry in small batches at about 180C for about 3 minutes: squid is tender when cooked briefly and rubbery if left in longer.","s":2},{"t":"Stir the crushed garlic into the mayo and let it sit for a few minutes to make a quick aioli.","s":2}],"rl":"PTXXGFG"},"53180":{"lv":"EEERO","w":{"3":"Adds sweetness and acidity to the pan sauce; white wine or lemon works instead.","4":"Fresh garnish only."},"sub":{"3":{"x":"1 tbsp white wine or chicken broth + 1 tsp lemon juice","d":"Lacks the nutty sherry flavor but gives a similar acidity.","k":["white wine","chicken broth","lemon juice"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Pat the prawns dry and don't crowd the pan; they cook in minutes, so they only need to turn pink and opaque.","s":2},{"t":"Add the garlic just before the prawns and keep it moving; it burns in seconds and turns bitter.","s":1},{"t":"Serve with crusty bread to mop up the sherry and garlic oil.","s":4}],"rl":"FXPAG"},"53182":{"lv":"ERRRREERERO","w":{"0":"Just to soften the vegetables; any oil works.","1":"Sweet base for the rice.","2":"Adds sweetness and color.","3":"A second pepper adds more sweetness and color; one is enough.","4":"Savory aroma through the rice.","7":"Golden color and floral aroma; the dish is plainer without it.","9":"Brightens the rich seafood at the end.","10":"Fresh garnish only."},"sub":{"0":{"x":"1 tbsp vegetable oil","d":"Same for softening, less fruity.","k":["vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Cook the paella rice for a minute in the oil before the stock so it turns glossy and keeps its shape.","s":0},{"t":"Simmer gently without a lid and stir only occasionally. Constant stirring makes it gluey instead of loose and separate.","s":1},{"t":"Frozen seafood must be cooked through until piping hot; stir it in at the end for just a couple of minutes, since longer makes it rubbery.","s":2}],"rl":"FXVVXBMCPAG"},"53184":{"lv":"RRRERREEE","w":{"0":"Sweet base for the rice.","1":"Adds sweetness and color to the rice.","2":"A second pepper adds more sweetness and color; one is enough.","4":"Savory aroma through the rice.","5":"Chorizo adds fat; skipped, the rice still works."},"sub":{"5":{"x":"1 tbsp vegetable oil","d":"Same frying result; chorizo adds its own fat.","k":["vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Fry everything over high heat for the first 3 minutes so the chorizo releases its paprika oil, which tints and flavors the rice.","s":0},{"t":"Use boiling water and a tight lid for a steady simmer; 12 minutes is enough for almost-tender rice, and stirring only once avoids gumminess.","s":1},{"t":"Add the prawns last, only until pink and opaque, with a splash of water if the rice has dried out.","s":2}],"rl":"XVVXXFBVP"},"53159":{"lv":"RREEOR","w":{"0":"Adds a hearty, crisp-edged filling; without it it's a chorizo-and-cheese omelette.","1":"Just to crisp the chorizo and potato; chorizo adds its own fat.","4":"Fresh garnish only.","5":"Melts into the folded omelette for creaminess; skip if you prefer it lighter."},"sub":{"1":{"x":"1 tsp vegetable oil","d":"Same frying result; chorizo supplies most of the fat anyway.","k":["vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Steam-dry the boiled potato in the colander for a minute before frying; a dry surface crisps in the chorizo fat instead of turning mushy.","s":0},{"t":"Cook the omelette in a clean, medium-heat pan and pull it while the top is still slightly glossy. It sets from the residual heat when folded, so it stays tender, not rubbery.","s":2},{"t":"Add the filling and cheese only when the eggs are almost set, then fold at once so the cheese melts with the steam.","s":3}],"rl":"VFXPGF"},"53177":{"lv":"EERRRR","w":{"2":"Fresh green note that cuts the chorizo and gives the dish brightness.","3":"Sweet, juicy strips that balance the spicy chorizo; the dish is still savory without them.","4":"To start the peppers; chorizo adds its own fat, so any oil works.","5":"Salty, nutty finish that coats the pasta; any hard cheese like pecorino works."},"sub":{"4":{"x":"2 tbsp vegetable oil","d":"Same cooking result, with less fruitiness.","k":["vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Snip the chorizo straight into the cold pan and let it render slowly; that paprika oil is the sauce for the spaghetti.","s":2},{"t":"Finish cooking the pasta in the pan with the chorizo and splashes of the starchy water. The starch emulsifies the oil and cheese into a glossy coating instead of oily strands.","s":3},{"t":"Add the parmesan off the heat, tossing quickly, so it melts into the sauce rather than clumping.","s":3}],"rl":"XBGVFF"},"53183":{"lv":"RRRRRREEEEERREROO","w":{"0":"To soften the shallots; olive oil works the same.","1":"Sweet base for the meatball mix; use a small onion in their place.","2":"Smoky, sweet flavor and color in the meatballs.","3":"Seasons the meatballs; the sliced garlic is added later.","4":"Added to the chorizo and squid for an aromatic layer.","5":"Soaked into the breadcrumbs for flavor; broth plus a splash of vinegar works.","9":"Used to brown the meatballs; any neutral oil works.","11":"Adds tender seafood flavor; skip or swap with extra clams.","12":"Deglazes the pan and adds acidity; use broth plus a splash of vinegar.","14":"Briny shellfish juice flavors the sauce; add extra squid if you can't find clams.","15":"Fresh garnish only.","16":"Finishing drizzle only."},"sub":{"0":{"x":"2 tbsp olive oil","d":"Same softening result, with a fruitier flavor than butter.","k":["olive oil"]},"5":{"x":"2 tbsp chicken broth + 1/2 tsp white wine vinegar","d":"Less nutty than sherry but still moistens and brightens the breadcrumbs.","k":["chicken broth","white vinegar"]},"9":{"x":"50 ml vegetable oil","d":"Same browning, less fruity.","k":["vegetable oil"]},"12":{"x":"100 ml chicken broth + 1 tsp white wine vinegar","d":"Less aromatic but gives the acid needed to cut the richness.","k":["chicken broth","white vinegar"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Cool the breadcrumb mixture before adding the pork and yolk, and beat it well. This panade keeps the meatballs tender and holds them together.","s":2},{"t":"Only brown the meatballs, since they finish in the sauce. Pork must reach 160F (71C) inside, so give them the full 5 minutes in the sauce.","s":3},{"t":"Discard any clams that stay shut after cooking and any that were open and don't close when tapped; scrub the shells and rinse the clams in cold water first.","s":6}],"rl":"FXXXXMTPTFXPMVPGG"},"3397":{"lv":"EEEEEERRER","w":{"6":"Adds warm, smoky color; the cumin and garlic still make a good dish without it.","7":"Stirred into the fried-bread paste for the tangy kick that makes the dish; lemon juice works in a pinch.","9":"Seasons the spinach and chickpeas; the paste itself isn't salted.","2":"Only cooking fat; skipped, food sticks and browns poorly, so use an oil or substitute."},"sub":{"2":{"x":"3 tbsp vegetable oil","d":"Fries the bread and garlic fine, with less fruity flavor than olive oil.","k":["vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Fry the bread cubes and half the garlic until deep golden but not burnt. This picada thickens the stew and gives the Andalusian flavor, so grind it to a smooth paste.","s":0},{"t":"Bloom the cumin and paprika in the oil for only 30 seconds; paprika turns bitter if scorched.","s":1},{"t":"Add the paste only after the spinach and chickpeas have simmered, then cook until it clings. If it's too thick, loosen it with a splash of water.","s":3}],"rl":"PVFTXXXAMZ"},"3398":{"lv":"EREREEO","w":{"1":"Soaking draws out bitterness and makes the eggplant creamier inside; skipping it gives a slightly more bitter, denser piece.","3":"Seasons the eggplant and the flour coating; half goes in the soak.","4":"Needs a neutral frying oil; any works.","6":"Fresh herb note on top; skip if you have none."},"sub":{"4":{"x":"2 cups sunflower or vegetable oil","d":"Fries clean and crisp; olive oil is more traditional but pricier.","k":["sunflower oil","vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Cut the eggplant into long, thin sticks and pat dry. Wet slices dilute the flour and turn heavy and greasy.","s":0},{"t":"Dredge in flour right before frying and keep the oil at 360F so the eggplant crisps before it absorbs oil. Fry in small batches.","s":3},{"t":"Warm the honey so it drizzles, and pour it on at the table; it softens the crust within minutes. Dark cane honey is the classic choice.","s":4}],"rl":"VMTZFWG"},"3399":{"lv":"EEERERRRO","w":{"3":"Roasts sweet and juicy, and adds a tangy note; the dish is traditionally good without them.","5":"Fresh garlic adds sharp savory bite; skipping makes the dish milder.","6":"Gives the sharp, bright note that balances the sweet, smoky vegetables.","7":"Brings out the smoky sweetness; roasted vegetables taste flat without it.","8":"Fresh garnish only."},"tips":[{"t":"Roast them whole, with the skins on, until blackened and collapsed. Roast the onions and peppers until soft, since that char gives the dish its smoky flavor.","s":1},{"t":"Steam the roasted vegetables under foil so the skins loosen. Peel while still warm and drain the liquid from the eggplant, which would otherwise dilute the dressing.","s":2},{"t":"Dress them while still warm so they absorb the oil, garlic and vinegar. Serve at room temperature for the best flavor.","s":4}],"rl":"VVVVFXAZG"},"3400":{"lv":"ERERO","w":{"1":"Adds a crunchy, nutty contrast to the soft dates; plain dates and bacon still work.","3":"Gives a glossy, sticky glaze; the dates are already sweet, so it can be omitted.","4":"A light dusting for color and a faint smoky note."},"tips":[{"t":"Use thin-sliced bacon, not thick-cut; thick slices will not crisp in the short bake and leave a chewy, fatty wrap.","s":2},{"t":"Place seam side down on a rack set over the foil-lined sheet so the bacon fat drips away and the bottoms crisp.","s":3},{"t":"Bacon must be cooked through and crisp. Brush the honey on only near the end so it doesn't burn on the pan.","s":4}],"rl":"VVPWX"},"3401":{"lv":"EEEORRRR","w":{"3":"A touch of heat; skip for a mild dish.","5":"Seasons the mushrooms; add near the end so it doesn't draw out water before they brown.","6":"Fresh herb finish that lightens the garlic and oil.","7":"Brightens the rich garlic oil; a splash of vinegar works too.","4":"Deglazes and adds acidity; skipped, the mushrooms are plainer."},"sub":{"4":{"x":"1/4 cup chicken broth + 1 tsp white wine vinegar","d":"Less aromatic than sherry or wine but deglazes and adds acidity.","k":["chicken broth","white vinegar"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Wipe the mushrooms clean instead of rinsing them; washed mushrooms soak up water and steam instead of browning.","s":1},{"t":"Sear them in one layer without stirring for 4 minutes so they brown rather than steam. Salt at the end so the mushrooms don't weep early.","s":1},{"t":"Add the garlic only after the mushrooms are browned, and take it off the heat when it is just golden. Browned garlic turns bitter.","s":2}],"rl":"VFXXMZGA"},"3402":{"lv":"EERERROR","w":{"2":"Adds sweetness and a vegetal note; the dish is still potato-and-onion without it.","4":"Added late for a fresh garlic flavor.","5":"Seasons the potatoes as they cook in the oil; they taste flat without it.","6":"Fresh garnish only.","7":"A splash of tang that cuts the richness of the oil-poached potatoes."},"tips":[{"t":"Slice the potatoes evenly thin so they all cook at the same speed in the oil, and keep the heat medium-low so they poach in the oil rather than fry.","s":1},{"t":"Turn them gently and only now and then; too much stirring breaks the tender slices into mash.","s":2},{"t":"Drain well with a slotted spoon, and keep the leftover oil for another use, since it tastes of potato and garlic. Add the vinegar only when serving.","s":4}],"rl":"BVVFXZGA"},"53169":{"lv":"EEERR","w":{"3":"One raw clove gives the sharp bite that balances the sweet almonds; skip it and the soup tastes rounder but flatter.","4":"Acid cuts the richness of almonds and oil; without it the soup tastes heavy and dull."},"sub":{"4":{"x":"1 tbsp lemon juice","d":"Brighter and fruitier than red wine vinegar. Add gradually and taste.","k":["lemon juice"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Use blanched, skinless almonds and blend for a full 2 to 3 minutes, then push through a fine sieve if you want it silky. Leftover almond skin shows as speckles and a gritty mouthfeel.","s":1},{"t":"Chill the soup well and re-season at the end. Cold mutes salt and acid, so it will almost always need more vinegar and salt than tasted at room temperature.","s":2}],"rl":"TBFXA"},"53173":{"lv":"EERROR","w":{"2":"Adds a gentle heat; skip for a milder soup or use a pinch of cayenne.","3":"Raw garlic is the sharp backbone of gazpacho; skipping it leaves a plain tomato-pepper puree.","5":"Brightens the cooked-tasting passata; skip it and add a little extra vinegar instead.","4":"Tiny amount; skipped, there is little noticeable effect."},"sub":{"4":{"x":"1 tsp red wine vinegar","d":"Slightly sharper and less nutty than sherry vinegar.","k":["red wine vinegar"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Passata is cooked, so it lacks fresh tomato edge. Blend in a spoonful of olive oil and a tiny pinch of sugar and salt generously to bring it back to life.","s":0},{"t":"Blend a full minute so the pepper skin breaks down completely, then chill. Add the ice cubes to each bowl rather than the jug, or the soup turns watery.","s":1}],"rl":"VVXXAA"},"3521":{"lv":"EERERRRR","w":{"2":"Raw garlic gives a sharp edge; one small clove keeps it balanced, and skipping gives a sweeter, milder soup.","5":"Cold food mutes salt, so this is what makes the tomato taste sweet; start with 3/4 tsp and adjust.","6":"Traditional topping that adds richness and a creamy bite; skipping loses little flavor.","7":"Salty cured contrast to the sweet cold soup; omit for a vegetarian version and add flaky salt on top.","4":"Adds brightness; skipped, the dish is flatter but still works."},"sub":{"4":{"x":"2 tbsp red wine vinegar","d":"Slightly harsher than sherry vinegar; start with 1.5 tbsp and taste.","k":["red wine vinegar"]}},"tips":[{"t":"The texture should be thick enough to coat a spoon, much denser than gazpacho. If it is too thin, blend in more torn bread; if too thick, thin with a spoon of cold water.","s":1},{"t":"Drizzle the oil in slowly with the blender running so it emulsifies into a pale coral cream. Dumping it in fast leaves a greasy, separated soup.","s":3},{"t":"Chill the bowls and add the egg and ham only at serving so the ham stays dry and the egg stays bright.","s":5}],"rl":"VTXFAZGG"},"53152":{"lv":"ERROEREREEOE","w":{"1":"Whole cloves perfume the beans and one is fried for the croutons; skipping leaves the beans and broth flatter.","2":"Adds subtle herbal depth to the bean cooking water; small loss if skipped.","3":"A background herbal note; fine to leave out.","5":"Fried bread adds crunch and can be crushed to thicken the broth; skip for a plain broth.","7":"Sweet base that carries the paprika; skip and the broth tastes thinner and less rounded.","9":"Provides the liquid body of the broth; vegetable broth or water works because chorizo adds richness.","10":"Fresh finish that lifts the rich broth; fine to skip."},"sub":{"9":{"x":"1 l vegetable broth","d":"Lighter, less meaty but chorizo and paprika supply plenty of flavor.","k":["vegetable broth"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Dried beans need an overnight soak first, and the pot should barely tremble. A hard boil bursts the skins before the centers soften; save a ladle of the bean liquid for loosening the broth.","s":0},{"t":"Take the pan off the heat or turn it low before stirring in the paprika and add liquid within 20 seconds. Scorched paprika turns the whole broth bitter.","s":2},{"t":"Pat the hake very dry, lay it skin-side down and press gently with a spatula for 10 seconds so the skin does not curl. Do not move it until it releases, then flip once; cook until it flakes (145F/63C).","s":3}],"rl":"BXXXFGPXXMGP"},"53154":{"lv":"ERROREEREO","w":{"1":"Sweet base that cooks in the red chorizo oil; skip and the stew tastes thinner.","2":"Cooks in the chorizo fat and rounds out the tomato broth; modest loss if skipped.","3":"Fresh herb finish; leave out with little effect.","4":"Thins the tomatoes into a broth the clams steam in; water works but needs a pinch more salt.","7":"Sharpens the tomatoes and cuts the chorizo fat; a squeeze of lemon does the same job.","9":"Mops up the broth; nice but not required."},"sub":{"4":{"x":"200 ml chicken broth","d":"Slightly meatier; use low-sodium since the clams add salt.","k":["chicken broth"]},"7":{"x":"1 tsp lemon juice","d":"Brighter and less mellow than sherry vinegar.","k":["lemon juice"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Soak the clams in cold salted water for 20 minutes to purge sand, and tap any open ones; discard those that stay open. After steaming, discard any that did not open.","s":2},{"t":"Start the chorizo in a cold pan so it renders slowly, then cook the onion in that red oil. Taste before seasoning, because the clam liquor and chorizo are already salty.","s":0}],"rl":"XXXGMVBAPG"},"53166":{"lv":"ERRRORREORERE","w":{"1":"Sweet base for the stew; skip and the flavor is thinner.","2":"Adds sweetness and body to the vegetable base.","3":"Adds an earthy savory note; spinach and chorizo can carry the stew without it.","4":"A small herbal note; fine to omit.","5":"Adds a subtle savory depth while simmering; remove before serving.","6":"Mellows into the oil and rounds out the chorizo; skipping loses savoriness.","8":"A tiny pinch gives the warm Andalusian touch; skip and it is still a good stew.","9":"Adds smoky color and depth; the chorizo already supplies some, so skip if you lack it.","11":"Adds brightness; skipped, the dish is flatter but still works."},"sub":{"11":{"x":"2 tbsp red wine vinegar","d":"Slightly sharper; add 1.5 tbsp and taste at the end.","k":["red wine vinegar"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Cook the vegetables in the red oil released by the chorizo; that fat is the flavor base. Cook gently so the onion softens without browning.","s":2},{"t":"Press a ladle of the chickpeas against the side of the pan to thicken the broth without any flour or cream.","s":2},{"t":"Stir the spinach in off the heat so it wilts in the residual warmth and keeps its color; heat it any longer and it turns drab and watery.","s":3}],"rl":"FXVVXXXXXXBAV"},"53168":{"lv":"EEROERO","w":{"2":"Adds sweetness and bulk to a short soup; kale or spinach can replace it, and skipping makes it thinner.","3":"A small kick; skip for a milder soup.","6":"Nice for mopping up the broth, but the soup stands alone.","5":"Adds savory depth to a small amount of liquid; skipped, flavor is just muted."},"sub":{"5":{"x":"400 ml chicken broth in place of the can of water (omit the cube)","d":"Cleaner and less salty than a cube; add salt at the end.","k":["chicken broth"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Slice the cabbage into thin ribbons, not chunks, so it softens in the short 8 minute simmer. Chunky pieces stay raw in the middle.","s":1},{"t":"Crumble in only half the stock cube first and taste. Chorizo is already salty, and you can always add more.","s":1}],"rl":"VXVXBMG"},"53148":{"lv":"EERRREEEE","w":{"1":"Adds richness and silk to the custard; milk makes it lighter and a touch less rich.","2":"The traditional warm spice of the infusion; skip it and the custard is purely citrus.","3":"Orange zest gives the classic floral note; skip for a more lemon-forward custard.","4":"Lemon zest brightens the rich egg custard; skip with a slightly flatter flavor."},"sub":{"1":{"x":"150 ml whole milk (550 ml milk total)","d":"Lighter, slightly less rich custard that sets a little softer.","k":["whole milk"]},"2":{"x":"1/2 tsp ground cinnamon","d":"Clouds the custard with fine specks and is a bit harsher; strain well.","k":["ground cinnamon"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Pour the hot cream onto the yolks in a thin stream while whisking, then strain the custard through a sieve before filling the ramekins. It catches any scrambled bits and zest.","s":1},{"t":"The custard must bubble gently for a minute or two once thick so the cornstarch fully cooks; undercooked cornstarch gives a runny or chalky set. It should coat a spoon and hold a line drawn through it.","s":2},{"t":"Blot any condensation from the chilled custard surface, sprinkle a thin even layer of sugar, and torch until deep amber. Eat within minutes, while the crust is still crackly.","s":4}],"rl":"MFXXXSWWT"},"53149":{"lv":"EEEEOE","w":{"4":"Stops the very sticky dough from tearing while you stretch it; any neutral oil does the same job.","5":"Lard gives the tender, flaky, traditional texture; butter works but is less authentic."},"sub":{"4":{"x":"2 tbsp light olive oil","d":"Same job of keeping dough from sticking; a faint fruitiness is harmless.","k":["olive oil"]},"5":{"x":"100 g softened unsalted butter","d":"Butterier and slightly less flaky than lard; spread thinly since butter contains water.","k":["butter"]}},"tips":[{"t":"The dough is sticky by design, so oil the surface, your hands and the rolling pin rather than adding flour. Stretch it until you can nearly see the work surface through it; if it springs back, rest it covered for 10 minutes and try again.","s":1},{"t":"Leave a 1 cm gap between turns of the spiral and let it proof until doubled. If packed tight, it cannot expand and bakes into a dense coil.","s":3},{"t":"Dust with icing sugar only after fully cool; on warm pastry it melts into a sticky film.","s":4}],"rl":"LWSSFF"},"53153":{"lv":"ROEEEOERERORR","w":{"0":"Adds richness and tenderness to the dough; skip for plainer, slightly more brittle churros.","1":"A faint aroma in the dough; the cinnamon sugar masks it.","5":"Likely a stray data line with no role in the recipe; ignore it.","10":"Mild aroma in the chocolate sauce; chocolate and cinnamon carry it.","11":"Coats the hot churros so they are sweet and slightly crunchy; skip and dust with icing sugar.","12":"The classic coating spice; skip for plain sugar churros.","7":"Makes the dipping sauce silky and glossy.","8":"Thins and loosens the sauce.","9":"Adds shine and body to the sauce."},"sub":{"7":{"x":"100 ml extra whole milk plus 1 tbsp butter","d":"Thinner and less rich sauce; the butter helps restore gloss.","k":["whole milk","butter"]},"8":{"x":"100 ml extra heavy cream","d":"Richer, thicker sauce.","k":["heavy cream"]},"9":{"x":"3 tbsp honey","d":"More floral; gives similar gloss and sweetness.","k":["honey"],"wn":"Honey is not safe for children under 1 year"}},"tips":[{"t":"Boiling water gelatinizes the flour, which gives the crisp shell and soft middle. Rest the dough until thick enough to pipe in a firm ribbon.","s":0},{"t":"Use a star nozzle: the ridges expose more surface for crunch and let steam escape so the churros do not burst in the oil.","s":3},{"t":"Fry in small batches at about 180C/350F because each batch drops the oil temperature. Pale, greasy churros mean the oil was too cool; dark outside and raw inside means it was too hot.","s":2}],"rl":"FXSLFGGFMWXWX"},"53163":{"lv":"EERRRRRR","w":{"2":"Adds tartness and chew; skip and the balls are sweeter and softer.","3":"Adds tart contrast to the very sweet fig paste.","4":"Adds aroma and depth; it does not make the balls shelf-stable, so use dried figs and store in a cool dry place.","5":"Helps bind and sweeten, but the fig paste is already sticky; skipping makes them less sweet.","6":"Gives the traditional warm spice; 1 tsp is strong so measure level, and use ground cloves.","7":"Coats the balls so they do not stick and adds nutty crunch; ground almonds can replace it.","1":"Use dried figs, not fresh; fresh figs make a wet paste that cannot dry and will mold within days."},"sub":{"4":{"x":"1 tbsp orange juice","d":"Less aromatic and the balls keep for less time since the alcohol helps preserve.","k":["orange juice"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Use dried figs, not fresh. Fresh figs make a paste that is too wet to dry for a week and will mold well before the 2 month keeping time.","s":0},{"t":"Wet your hands lightly with water or oil when rolling, since the paste is very sticky.","s":1},{"t":"Store layered between sheets of baking parchment in an airtight tin so they do not weld together.","s":3}],"rl":"BVVVXWXG"},"53170":{"lv":"EOEEEERREEEOR","w":{"1":"Cold contrast for dipping; a serving extra.","6":"Coats the hot churros for sweetness and crunch; skip and dust with icing sugar.","7":"Classic warm coating spice; skip for plain sugar.","11":"Mild aroma in the cocoa dough; the chocolate dominates.","12":"Adds richness and tenderness to the dough; skip and use a splash more oil."},"tips":[{"t":"Add the cream to the melted sugar and butter off the heat in stages because it will spit hard. Stir the chocolate in off the heat so the sauce stays glossy and does not split; add flaky salt at the end for the salted caramel effect.","s":0},{"t":"Cocoa dough is stiffer than plain: rest it 5 minutes, then pipe. Cocoa hides browning, so judge doneness by time (about 3 to 4 minutes) and crisp feel, not color.","s":2},{"t":"Keep the oil at about 170C and no more than a third full. Test with a small piece of dough; it should sizzle gently, not violently, and rise within a few seconds.","s":2}],"rl":"FGWFFXWXSXLXF"},"53093":{"lv":"EEEEERO","w":{"4":"Carries the crunch of the baked top and keeps the strands from drying; olive oil works equally well.","5":"Pasta is boiled in lightly salted water, so the sauce needs its own seasoning.","6":"A dash adds a little bite; skip."},"sub":{"4":{"x":"1/4 cup olive oil","d":"Adds a slight fruitiness that suits the tomato and cinnamon.","k":["olive oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Cook the spaghetti 1 to 2 minutes under al dente because it bakes for an hour afterward. Loosen the thick paste with a splash of pasta water so it coats every strand instead of clumping.","s":1},{"t":"The crunchy top is the point: bake uncovered and do not stir. If the top browns too fast, check the oven temperature, and if it stays soft, add 10 minutes.","s":2},{"t":"Cinnamon reads as warm and savory against tomato, not sweet. Keep to 1 tsp and let the dish rest 10 minutes before cutting in squares so the base sets.","s":2}],"rl":"BMXXFZZ"},"53095":{"lv":"EEREROEERR","w":{"0":"Browns the beef and carries the spices; ghee or olive oil works the same.","2":"Seasons 2 lb of beef and a big pot of rice; skip it and the dish tastes flat, so taste the broth.","4":"Adds a warm sweet note that pairs with allspice in the Syrian style; skip for a plainer flavor.","5":"A small background bite; fine to skip.","6":"Gives the rice its savory depth; water plus a bouillon cube is a fair substitute.","8":"Toasts the pine nuts; olive oil works just as well.","9":"The classic crunchy, buttery finish; slivered almonds are a good swap."},"sub":{"0":{"x":"1/4 cup olive oil or ghee","d":"Ghee tastes closest to butter; olive oil is lighter and slightly fruity.","k":["olive oil","ghee"]},"6":{"x":"5 cups water plus 1 chicken bouillon cube","d":"Less rounded flavor; taste and adjust salt.","k":["chicken bouillon"]},"9":{"x":"1/2 cup slivered almonds","d":"Less buttery and a bit firmer; toast slowly as they burn quickly.","k":["almond"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Brown the beef until the moisture cooks off and it starts to sizzle and stick, breaking it into small crumbs. Ground beef must reach 160F/71C, and this browning step builds most of the flavor.","s":0},{"t":"Rinse the rice until the water runs clear and do not lift the lid during the 20 minutes. Let it rest 10 minutes off the heat, then fluff with a fork.","s":1},{"t":"Toast the pine nuts over medium heat and pull them when pale gold; they keep cooking in the hot butter and burn within seconds.","s":2}],"rl":"FPZXXZMBFG"},"53091":{"lv":"EEEEROERRRROO","w":{"4":"Raw garlic gives the tahini sauce its bite; skip and the sauce tastes flatter.","5":"Adds a little color and mild flavor; fine to skip.","7":"Fresh crunch against the hot falafel.","8":"Adds juicy freshness and sweetness.","9":"Cool crunch that balances the rich tahini.","10":"Adds acid and salt that cut the richness; skip with a slightly heavier sandwich.","11":"A sharp bite; skip if you dislike raw onion.","12":"Listed but not used in the steps; sprinkle on for heat if you like it."},"tips":[{"t":"Tahini seizes into a thick paste when you first add water, then loosens as you keep whisking. Add the water gradually and stop when the sauce is pourable and pale.","s":2},{"t":"Bake the falafel on a rack or lightly oiled sheet so the bottom re-crisps; microwaving makes them soggy.","s":1},{"t":"Warm the pitas in foil for 2 minutes before cutting the pocket so they do not crack when you stuff them.","s":3}],"rl":"PXMAXXBVVVGGX"},"53092":{"lv":"EERRR","w":{"2":"Salt draws moisture from the beans so they steam in their own juices; add a pinch more at the end if needed.","3":"Gives a sharp, fragrant finish when added at the end; skip and the dish tastes plainer.","4":"Traditional fresh herb finish; skip with little loss."},"tips":[{"t":"Do not add water. Keep the lid on and cook over medium heat so the beans steam in their own moisture; they should collapse to a soft olive-brown, not stay bright green.","s":1},{"t":"Crush the garlic with a little salt and stir it in with the cilantro for the last 2 minutes only. Cooked longer, the garlic turns bitter and the cilantro loses its fragrance.","s":2},{"t":"It tastes even better at room temperature or the next day, once the olive oil has absorbed the flavors.","s":3}],"rl":"VFZXG"},"53089":{"lv":"EEREEE","w":{"1":"Softens the crumb and keeps the bread pliable so it folds; olive oil works equally well.","2":"Gives the yeast a quick boost and a little browning; skip with a slightly slower rise."},"sub":{"1":{"x":"2 tbsp olive oil","d":"A subtle fruity flavor; the bread stays equally soft.","k":["olive oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"A very hot oven (475F) and a preheated baking sheet or stone are what make the bread puff into a pocket; the bottom heat flash-steams the dough.","s":1},{"t":"Roll the rounds to an even thickness. Thin spots tear and let steam escape so the bread will not puff.","s":3},{"t":"Cover the baked breads with a clean towel as they come out so they steam and stay soft.","s":3}],"rl":"MFWZSL"},"53094":{"lv":"ERREEROOOR","w":{"1":"Raw garlic gives the dip its bite; use less if you dislike it raw, but without it the flavor is flat.","2":"Seasons the eggplant and tightens the flavors; skipping leaves the dip bland, so taste and adjust.","5":"Adds richness and a smooth mouthfeel; a drizzle on top works if you cut it back.","6":"A non-traditional creamy note; skip for a more classic, tahini-driven dip.","7":"A pinch of heat; skip.","8":"A single leaf adds a cooling hint; skip.","9":"Adds fresh green flavor at the end; skip with little loss."},"tips":[{"t":"Char the skin completely black and let the flesh collapse; the smoke is the flavor of the dish. On a gas burner, under a broiler, or on a grill all work.","s":1},{"t":"Let the scraped flesh drain in a colander. Excess liquid dilutes the dip and carries bitterness.","s":2},{"t":"Mash by hand with a fork, not in a food processor, which turns the flesh gluey. Taste again after chilling since cold dulls salt and lemon.","s":3}],"rl":"VXZAFFFXGG"},"118":{"lv":"EERREEO","w":{"2":"Adds bulk and crisp texture to the filling; skip for a lighter burrito.","3":"Melted cheese binds the filling and keeps it creamy after reheating; skip for a drier burrito.","5":"Cooks the eggs gently; bacon fat or oil works as well.","6":"A serving extra; add after reheating, not before freezing."},"sub":{"5":{"x":"1 tbsp bacon fat or vegetable oil","d":"Bacon fat adds smoky flavor; either gives a slightly less buttery egg.","k":["bacon fat","vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Take the eggs off the heat while still slightly soft and glossy; they firm up further when rolled hot and again when reheated, and overcooked eggs turn rubbery and weep.","s":1},{"t":"Cool every filling component completely before rolling, and squeeze out excess air. Warm filling creates condensation that turns the tortilla soggy and forms ice crystals.","s":2},{"t":"Wrap each burrito individually and freeze within 2 hours. Reheat until steaming hot throughout (165F/74C), flipping halfway, and microwave from frozen may need 3 minutes.","s":3}],"rl":"PPBFBFG"},"3070":{"lv":"ERERRREEEERO","w":{"1":"Coats the beef for browning and thickens the gravy; cornstarch works but is added differently.","2":"The fat for browning the beef; lard or bacon drippings add richer flavor.","3":"Sweet base for the gravy; skip and the stew tastes thinner.","4":"Adds sweetness and a mild vegetal flavor; skip with little loss.","5":"Rounds out the gravy; skipping makes it less savory.","9":"The liquid for braising; water plus bouillon is a fair swap.","10":"Seasons the meat; the dish tastes flat without it, so taste at the end.","11":"For serving; the stew is also great over rice."},"sub":{"1":{"x":"1 tbsp cornstarch stirred into 1/4 cup cold broth, added after returning the beef","d":"Thickens the gravy similarly, but the beef does not get a floured crust.","k":["cornstarch"]},"2":{"x":"2 tbsp lard or bacon drippings","d":"Richer and more savory; olive oil also works.","k":["lard","bacon fat"]},"9":{"x":"2 cups water plus 1 beef bouillon cube","d":"Slightly less rounded; check salt before adding more.","k":["beef bouillon","water"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Pat the beef dry and brown in batches without crowding. The dark crust and browned bits on the pot are the flavor base of the gravy.","s":1},{"t":"When you return the beef, scrape up the browned bits as the broth goes in. Simmer very gently, barely a bubble, so the stew meat turns tender rather than stringy.","s":3},{"t":"If the gravy is thin at the end, uncover and simmer 15 minutes; if it thickens too much, loosen with a splash of broth.","s":4}],"rl":"PTFXVXVXXMZG"},"3606":{"lv":"ERERRRRRRRR","w":{"1":"The seasoning that flavors the beef; a homemade mix works.","3":"Adds protein and heartiness.","4":"Fresh sweetness and juicy color.","5":"Adds richness and a salty tang.","6":"Creamy, cooling contrast.","7":"Adds crunch; add at the last second.","8":"The base of the dressing; skip and the salad is drier.","9":"Creamy base for the dressing; Greek yogurt works.","10":"Brightens the dressing and the rich toppings."},"sub":{"1":{"x":"1 tbsp chili powder, 1 tsp cumin, 1/2 tsp garlic powder, 1/2 tsp paprika, 1/2 tsp oregano and 1/2 tsp salt","d":"Less salty and more customizable than the packet.","k":["chili powder","cumin","garlic powder","paprika","oregano"]},"9":{"x":"1/2 cup plain Greek yogurt","d":"Tangier and lighter; fine in a cold dressing.","k":["greek yogurt"]},"10":{"x":"1 tbsp lemon juice","d":"Slightly less floral than lime.","k":["lemon juice"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Crisp the lettuce in ice water and spin it dry. Wet leaves repel dressing and wilt quickly under the warm beef.","s":3},{"t":"Dress and add the crushed chips at the last second; chips go soggy within minutes in the salsa dressing.","s":4},{"t":"Drain the beef well and cook to 160F/71C; leftover fat makes the dressing greasy and the lettuce limp.","s":0}],"rl":"PXVVVFFGXFA"},"14":{"lv":"ERREERRRERORO","w":{"1":"Seasons the beef; skip and the patty is plain.","2":"Adds color and mild smoky flavor.","5":"Adds sweetness and crunch pressed into the beef; skip and add raw onion on top.","6":"Cool crunch against the rich beef and cheese.","7":"Fresh juicy sweetness.","9":"Sweetness for the burger sauce.","10":"Small tang; skip.","11":"Adds the burger-sauce tang.","12":"Adds savory depth; skip with little change.","4":"Melty cheese that defines the tacos; skipped, they lose their identity."},"sub":{"4":{"x":"1 cup shredded cheddar or 8 slices cheddar","d":"Melts less smoothly and is sharper than American cheese.","k":["cheddar"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Use cold ground beef and press it paper-thin onto the tortilla all the way to the edges with a wet hand or spatula. The thin layer sears into a crust in 2 to 3 minutes and cooks through to 160F/71C.","s":1},{"t":"Leave the beef-side down in the hot pan without moving it so a dark crust forms; flip only when it releases easily.","s":2},{"t":"Add the cheese right after flipping and cover the pan for 20 seconds for a quick steam-melt.","s":2}],"rl":"PXXBFXVVFXXAX"},"3622":{"lv":"ERREEERRER","w":{"1":"Sweet base for the soup; skip and it tastes thinner.","2":"Adds savory depth to the seasoning.","6":"Adds a second bean for heartiness; skip and use more of the other.","7":"Sweetness and texture; frozen corn holds up in the freezer.","9":"Mild smoky heat; skip with little change.","3":"Main seasoning of the filling; skipped, the dish is bland.","8":"Liquid the dish simmers or cooks in; skipped, there is not enough liquid."},"sub":{"3":{"x":"2 tbsp chili powder, 2 tsp cumin, 1 tsp paprika, 1 tsp garlic powder, 1 tsp oregano and 1 tsp salt","d":"Less salty than packets; adjust to taste.","k":["chili powder","cumin","paprika","garlic powder","oregano"]},"8":{"x":"4 cups water plus 2 beef bouillon cubes","d":"Slightly thinner flavor; taste and adjust salt.","k":["beef bouillon","water"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Cool the soup quickly in shallow containers and get it into the freezer within 2 hours. Leave an inch of headspace, since it expands as it freezes.","s":4},{"t":"Freeze flat in zip bags to stack and thaw quickly. Reheat until steaming (165F/74C) and add dairy and chips only when serving.","s":5},{"t":"Simmer no longer than 20 minutes; the beans and corn soften further when reheated, so they should still have texture going into the freezer.","s":3}],"rl":"PXXXMBBVMX"},"39":{"lv":"EERRREEERE","w":{"2":"Adds sweetness and the fajita identity; skip and it is closer to a plain chicken pasta.","3":"Sweet base that melts into the sauce.","4":"Adds savory depth to the creamy sauce.","5":"The fajita seasoning provides the flavor; homemade works.","8":"Melts on top for a cheesy crust; cheddar or Monterey Jack work."},"sub":{"5":{"x":"2 tsp chili powder, 1 tsp cumin, 1 tsp paprika, 1 tsp garlic powder and 1/2 tsp salt","d":"Less salt than a packet; adjust to taste.","k":["chili powder","cumin","paprika","garlic powder"]},"8":{"x":"1 cup shredded cheddar or Monterey Jack","d":"Sharper flavor; slightly oilier melt.","k":["cheddar","monterey jack"]}},"tips":[{"t":"The dry pasta must be mostly submerged and the dish tightly covered with foil, or the exposed pieces stay hard. If the pasta is still firm at the end, add 1/4 cup hot water, stir, and bake 5 more minutes.","s":1},{"t":"Cut the chicken into small, even pieces so it cooks through to 165F/74C by the time the pasta softens. Stir well to break up the cream cheese so the sauce is smooth.","s":2},{"t":"Let the dish stand 5 minutes after baking; the sauce tightens as the pasta absorbs it.","s":3}],"rl":"PBVVXXVTFM"},"95":{"lv":"EREEREEROO","w":{"1":"Sweet base for the beef.","4":"Adds protein and heartiness.","7":"Melts into a cheesy topping; skip and sprinkle after.","8":"Cooling tang for serving.","9":"Fresh garnish.","2":"Main seasoning of the filling; skipped, the dish is bland.","6":"Liquid the dish simmers or cooks in; skipped, there is not enough liquid."},"sub":{"2":{"x":"1 tbsp chili powder, 1 tsp cumin, 1/2 tsp paprika, 1/2 tsp garlic powder and 1/2 tsp salt","d":"Needs salt adjusted, but similar flavor.","k":["chili powder","cumin","paprika","garlic powder"]},"6":{"x":"2 cups water plus 1 chicken bouillon cube","d":"Slightly less rich; taste for salt.","k":["chicken bouillon","water"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Stir the rice into the hot beef fat for a minute before adding liquid. Toasting coats the grains so they stay separate.","s":1},{"t":"Tomatoes are acidic and slow rice softening, so keep the lid on and do not stir; if the rice is still crunchy after 20 minutes, add a splash of broth and cook 5 minutes more.","s":1},{"t":"Rest the pot off the heat, covered, for 5 minutes before adding cheese so the rice finishes steaming and the cheese melts evenly.","s":2}],"rl":"PXXBVVMFGG"},"109":{"lv":"ERRERRERO","w":{"1":"Adds sweetness and color.","2":"Sweet base that keeps the lean turkey moist.","4":"Adds protein and heartiness.","5":"Sweetness and texture.","7":"Melts into a topping; skip and sprinkle after.","8":"For scooping; serve extras.","3":"Main seasoning of the filling; skipped, the dish is bland."},"sub":{"3":{"x":"1 tbsp chili powder, 1 tsp cumin, 1/2 tsp paprika, 1/2 tsp garlic powder and 1/2 tsp salt","d":"Needs salt adjusted, similar flavor.","k":["chili powder","cumin","paprika","garlic powder"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Ground turkey is lean and dries out. Add a tablespoon of oil to the pan, and do not stir constantly; let it brown in sections. Cook to 165F/74C.","s":0},{"t":"The salsa adds moisture and acid; simmer only 5 minutes so the turkey stays juicy.","s":1},{"t":"Cover the skillet briefly after adding cheese so it melts through without drying the filling.","s":2}],"rl":"PVXXVVXFG"},"112":{"lv":"EEERRR","w":{"3":"Adds creamy richness and holds the filling together.","4":"Adds moisture and tang; skip and the burrito is drier.","5":"Gives the beans a smoky warmth."},"tips":[{"t":"Warm the refried beans with a splash of water so they spread easily and do not tear the tortilla.","s":0},{"t":"Warm the tortillas first so they stay pliable, and use about 1/2 cup filling per burrito; overfilled ones split.","s":1},{"t":"Toast seam-side down first; the heat seals the seam so it stays shut.","s":2}],"rl":"BBBFXX"},"3061":{"lv":"ERREERRREEEOR","w":{"5":"Adds color and mild smoky flavor.","6":"Adds savory depth to the marinade.","7":"Seasons the chicken; it tastes bland without it.","11":"Cooling tang for serving.","12":"Creamy, cooling contrast.","1":"Carries flavor in the marinade or dressing; skipped, the dish is drier but intact.","2":"Adds brightness; skipped, the dish is flatter but still works."},"sub":{"1":{"x":"3 tbsp olive oil","d":"Slightly fruitier; works for marinade and cooking.","k":["olive oil"]},"2":{"x":"3 tbsp lemon juice","d":"Less floral than lime but equally acidic.","k":["lemon juice"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Do not marinate longer than about 30 minutes; acid turns chicken breast chalky and mealy.","s":0},{"t":"Get the skillet very hot and cook the chicken in a single layer so it browns; crowded chicken steams and turns gray. It must reach 165F/74C.","s":2},{"t":"Do not stir the peppers constantly; let them sit 1 to 2 minutes at a time so the edges char.","s":3}],"rl":"PFAXXXXZVVBGG"},"3062":{"lv":"EERERREROOOR","w":{"2":"Loosens the seasoning into a sauce that coats the beef.","4":"Adds protein and heartiness.","5":"Sweetness and texture.","7":"Creamy, cooling contrast.","8":"Adds crunch and freshness.","9":"Adds a salty, melty finish.","10":"Cooling tang.","11":"Brightens the whole bowl.","1":"Main seasoning of the filling; skipped, the dish is bland."},"sub":{"1":{"x":"2 tbsp chili powder, 1 tsp cumin, 1/2 tsp paprika, 1/2 tsp garlic powder and 1/2 tsp salt","d":"Needs salt adjusted, similar flavor.","k":["chili powder","cumin","paprika","garlic powder"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Drain the fat after browning to 160F/71C, but leave a spoonful in the pan so the seasoning clings.","s":0},{"t":"Squeeze a lime wedge and a pinch of salt into the hot rice before building the bowls; it lifts everything.","s":3},{"t":"Warm the beans and corn separately and keep the cold toppings cold so each spoonful has contrast.","s":2}],"rl":"PXMBVVXGGGGA"},"3134":{"lv":"ERRREERROEEEOR","w":{"3":"Adds savory depth to the marinade.","6":"Adds color and mild smoky flavor.","7":"Seasons the meat.","8":"A small bite.","12":"Creamy, cooling contrast.","13":"Adds freshness and acidity.","1":"Carries flavor in the marinade or dressing; skipped, the dish is drier but intact.","2":"Adds brightness; skipped, the dish is flatter but still works."},"sub":{"1":{"x":"3 tbsp vegetable oil","d":"Neutral flavor; fine for marinade and searing.","k":["vegetable oil"]},"2":{"x":"3 tbsp lemon juice","d":"Less floral than lime.","k":["lemon juice"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Pat the marinade off the steak before searing so it sears instead of steaming; do not marinate in lime longer than 2 hours or skirt turns mushy.","s":1},{"t":"Cook skirt hot and fast; 130F is medium-rare and it rises about 5 degrees while resting. Let it rest 5 to 10 minutes.","s":1},{"t":"Slice thinly against the grain on a slight bias; skirt has very long fibers and is chewy if sliced with them.","s":3}],"rl":"PFAXXXXZZVVBGG"},"3570":{"lv":"EEREERRORREO","w":{"2":"Adds sweetness and color.","5":"Warm earthy note; skip and rely on chili powder.","6":"Adds color and mild smoky flavor.","7":"A background note; skip.","8":"Seasons everything.","9":"Brightens the shrimp and peppers.","11":"A fresh finish.","3":"Only cooking fat; skipped, food sticks and browns poorly, so use an oil or substitute."},"sub":{"3":{"x":"3 tbsp vegetable oil","d":"Neutral; works at roasting temperature.","k":["vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Start the peppers and onions first and add the shrimp later; shrimp cook in 6 to 8 minutes and overcook easily. They are done when opaque and curled into a C; a tight O means overcooked.","s":4},{"t":"Pat the shrimp dry and do not marinate them in lime more than 15 minutes; acid begins to cook and toughen them.","s":3},{"t":"Preheat the sheet pan in the oven so the vegetables sizzle and brown on contact.","s":0}],"rl":"PVVFXXXXZABG"},"3058":{"lv":"RRREEERROO","w":{"1":"Sweet base; skip and the queso is plainer.","2":"Adds savory depth.","6":"Adds body and a little tang.","7":"Warm earthy flavor.","8":"A fresh finish.","9":"For dipping.","0":"Softens onion; skipped, the queso still works.","4":"Thins the queso; skipped, it is too thick."},"sub":{"0":{"x":"2 tbsp vegetable oil","d":"Less rich but works for softening the onion.","k":["vegetable oil"]},"4":{"x":"1/2 cup evaporated milk","d":"Creamier and more stable against separation.","k":["evaporated milk"]}},"tips":[{"t":"American cheese contains emulsifying salts that keep the queso smooth. Do not swap in aged cheddar, which turns greasy and grainy.","s":3},{"t":"Add the cheese a handful at a time on low heat and stir until melted before adding more. Never let it boil.","s":3},{"t":"If it separates, whisk in a splash of warm milk and a pinch of cornstarch; if it gets too thick as it cools, loosen with a little milk.","s":3}],"rl":"FXXVMTTXGG"},"3069":{"lv":"ERERERRORO","w":{"3":"Adds protein and heartiness.","5":"Adds tang and heat; skip and add salsa.","6":"Fresh acidity that balances the rich cheese and beef.","7":"Cooling tang.","8":"Creamy, cooling contrast.","9":"A fresh finish.","1":"Seasons the beef; skipped, other toppings carry it."},"sub":{"1":{"x":"1 tbsp chili powder, 1 tsp cumin, 1/2 tsp paprika, 1/2 tsp garlic powder and 1/2 tsp salt","d":"Needs salt adjusted, similar flavor.","k":["chili powder","cumin","paprika","garlic powder"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Build two layers of chips and toppings so the bottom chips are not bare.","s":2},{"t":"Shred the cheese yourself; pre-shredded cheese is coated with starch and melts less smoothly.","s":2},{"t":"Add the cold toppings, the sour cream and avocado, after baking and serve immediately; the chips go soft within minutes. Cook the beef to 160F/71C.","s":4}],"rl":"PXBVFXGGGG"},"3283":{"lv":"EERRRRREORR","w":{"2":"Warm earthy flavor.","4":"Adds protein and heartiness.","5":"Sweetness and texture.","6":"Melts to bind the filling.","8":"Crunch and freshness.","9":"Cooling creaminess for the sauce.","10":"Smoky heat; skip and the wrap is mild.","3":"Cooks the filling; skipped, it still works."},"sub":{"3":{"x":"1 tbsp vegetable oil","d":"Neutral; works fine.","k":["vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Slice the chicken thin and cook it to 165F/74C; thin strips cook quickly and stay juicy.","s":1},{"t":"Use about 3/4 cup filling per wrap. Fold in the sides first, then roll tightly, and sear seam-side down in a dry skillet to seal.","s":4},{"t":"Freeze leftover chipotle in adobo in tablespoon portions so you do not waste the can.","s":2}],"rl":"PXXFVVFBGFX"},"3193":{"lv":"EEREERRERRO","w":{"2":"Adds chili heat and aromatics to the mild yellow curry powder; skip and the curry is gentler.","5":"Sweet, soft wedges that melt into the sauce; skip and the curry is less rounded.","6":"Thins the coconut milk so it does not get heavy; water works with a bit more salt.","7":"The salty umami backbone; soy sauce works in a pinch.","8":"Balances salt and heat; white sugar or honey work.","9":"Brightens and lifts the rich coconut finish.","10":"A serving extra."},"sub":{"6":{"x":"1 cup water plus 1/2 tsp salt","d":"Less savory depth; the fish sauce and chicken make up much of the difference.","k":["water","salt"]},"7":{"x":"2 tbsp soy sauce","d":"Less funky and a bit saltier; add lime at the end to keep it bright.","k":["soy sauce"],"wn":"Contains soy (and wheat unless tamari)"},"8":{"x":"1 tbsp white sugar or honey","d":"Slightly less caramel depth; sweetness is identical.","k":["sugar","honey"],"wn":"Do not give honey to children under 1 year"}},"tips":[{"t":"Fry the curry powder and paste in the thick coconut cream first until the oil separates and shimmers on top. Blooming the spices in fat cooks off their raw edge.","s":0},{"t":"Chicken thighs stay juicy through the 20 minute simmer; breast would dry out. Keep the pot at a gentle bubble, since a hard boil can make coconut milk look curdled.","s":3},{"t":"Add the lime juice off the heat; the acid sours the coconut and loses brightness if cooked too long.","s":4}],"rl":"MXXPBXMXWAB"},"3198":{"lv":"EERRRERRO","w":{"2":"Traditionally the pounded roots and stems give the marinade an earthy herb punch; skip and use more garlic.","3":"Adds a citrusy lift to the marinade; skip with a less authentic flavor.","4":"Gives salt and umami; soy sauce works.","6":"Sweetness helps the skin caramelize.","7":"The signature Thai heat in the marinade; reduce for a milder bird, but do not skip entirely.","8":"For dipping; serve extra."},"sub":{"4":{"x":"2 tbsp extra soy sauce (4 tbsp total)","d":"Less funky and a bit saltier.","k":["soy sauce"]},"6":{"x":"1 tbsp brown sugar","d":"Slightly deeper caramel flavor.","k":["brown sugar"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Marinate at least an hour and up to overnight. Scrape off the thickest bits of paste before roasting so the garlic does not burn.","s":1},{"t":"Roast on a rack so the fat drips away and the skin crisps all around. Bone-in thighs are best at 175F/79C so the meat near the bone is tender.","s":3},{"t":"For deeper char, finish under the broiler for the last 3 minutes, watching the honey in the marinade closely.","s":3}],"rl":"PXXXXXWXG"},"52774":{"lv":"EEERRRRERERE","w":{"3":"Dark soy gives the noodles their color and depth; skip and they look paler.","4":"A small tang that balances the sweet sauce.","5":"Balances the soy and oyster sauce and helps char the noodles.","6":"Loosens the sauce so it coats the noodles evenly.","8":"Fragrant base that flavors the oil; skip with a flatter flavor.","10":"Adds richness and smoky char; skip for a vegetarian-ish noodle.","7":"Only cooking fat; skipped, food sticks and browns poorly, so use an oil or substitute.","11":"The main green vegetable of the dish; skipped, it loses its character."},"sub":{"7":{"x":"2 tbsp vegetable oil","d":"No peanut flavor; fine at high heat.","k":["vegetable oil"]},"11":{"x":"4 cups broccolini or regular broccoli, sliced","d":"Less bitter and a bit less leafy; cut stems thin so they cook fast.","k":["broccoli"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Use fresh wide rice noodles at room temperature; if cold from the fridge they break. If dried, soak only until pliable, not soft, since they finish in the wok.","s":0},{"t":"Pour the sauce over the noodles and let them sit untouched for 30 seconds to char before tossing; the smoky wok flavor is the point of the dish.","s":3},{"t":"Cook chicken to 165F/74C before adding the noodles, and work in two batches if your wok is small; crowded noodles steam and tear.","s":1}],"rl":"BXXXAWMFXPTV"},"52827":{"lv":"REEEERRORRROO","w":{"0":"Adds crunch and nutty richness; skip for a smoother curry.","5":"Adds sweet softness to the sauce.","6":"Floral citrus note; skip and add a squeeze of lime at the end.","7":"A warm spice in the braise; the paste has most of the flavor already.","8":"Provides sour balance; lime juice works.","9":"Rounds out the heat and sour; white sugar works.","10":"Provides salt and umami; soy sauce works.","11":"A fresh, hot garnish.","12":"A serving extra."},"sub":{"8":{"x":"1 tbsp lime juice","d":"Brighter and less fruity-deep than tamarind.","k":["lime juice"]},"9":{"x":"1 tbsp light brown sugar or white sugar","d":"Slightly less caramel flavor.","k":["brown sugar","sugar"]},"10":{"x":"1 tbsp soy sauce","d":"Less funky; add lime at the end.","k":["soy sauce"],"wn":"Contains soy (and wheat unless tamari)"}},"tips":[{"t":"Fry the curry paste in the thick coconut cream until the oil separates and it smells toasted; rushing this leaves a raw, flat flavor.","s":1},{"t":"Massaman beef needs a long slow braise at a bare simmer. Cook until a piece falls apart when pressed with a spoon; stewing beef can need 2 hours.","s":1},{"t":"Taste at the end and balance: salt from fish sauce, sour from tamarind, sweet from sugar. It tastes better the next day.","s":2}],"rl":"GMXPBVXXAWXGB"},"53197":{"lv":"ERREEERRERRO","w":{"1":"Adds a mild onion bite and fresh color.","2":"The stalks bring aroma; skip and add cilantro leaves at the end.","6":"Adds sweetness that rounds the peanut and salt.","7":"Adds salt and umami.","9":"Adds sweetness and texture.","10":"Brightens the rich sauce.","11":"A serving extra.","0":"Only cooking fat; skipped, food sticks and browns poorly, so use an oil or substitute."},"sub":{"0":{"x":"1 tbsp any neutral oil","d":"No change in flavor.","k":["vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Freeze the tenderloin for 15 minutes before slicing so you can cut thin, even slices. Thin slices cook through quickly, to 145F/63C, and stay tender.","s":0},{"t":"Use smooth, unsweetened peanut butter and stir it into the paste; it thickens the sauce and emulsifies with the coconut milk.","s":1},{"t":"Bubble uncovered at the end to tighten the sauce, and add the lime off the heat.","s":2}],"rl":"FXXPXTWXMVAB"},"53207":{"lv":"EEEERERRREROO","w":{"0":"The broth base; water plus a bouillon cube works.","2":"Galangal gives the signature piney, citrusy warmth; ginger is the closest swap.","4":"Provide the floral citrus aroma; use zest of limes instead.","6":"Adds earthy, meaty texture.","7":"Adds fresh heat; use fewer for mild.","8":"Balances sour and spicy.","9":"The salty backbone; soy sauce works.","10":"The sour element; lemon juice works.","11":"A garnish.","12":"For serving."},"sub":{"0":{"x":"3 1/3 cups water plus 1 chicken bouillon cube","d":"Less savory depth; taste before adding fish sauce.","k":["water","chicken bouillon"]},"2":{"x":"2 tbsp grated fresh ginger","d":"Hotter and sharper, less piney; still reads as tom kha.","k":["ginger"]},"4":{"x":"zest of 2 limes, added at the end","d":"Loses the leafy fragrance, but gets the citrus.","k":["lime"]},"9":{"x":"5 tbsp soy sauce, added gradually to taste","d":"Less funk; start with 3 tbsp.","k":["soy sauce"],"wn":"Contains soy (and wheat unless tamari)"},"10":{"x":"juice of 3 lemons","d":"Less floral than lime.","k":["lemon"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Bruise the lemongrass with the back of a knife before adding it. Keep the soup at a gentle simmer; a hard boil can separate the coconut milk.","s":0},{"t":"Stir in the lime juice and fish sauce off the heat and taste: it should be hot, sour and salty in balance.","s":1},{"t":"Lemongrass, galangal and lime leaves are for flavor, not eating; remove them before serving.","s":2}],"rl":"MMXXXPVXWXAGB"},"53212":{"lv":"RRRREE","w":{"1":"Adds citrus aroma; skip and use more juice.","2":"Adds acidity and brightness to the glaze.","3":"Gives savory depth; skip and the glaze is sweeter.","0":"The sweet-hot glaze base; honey plus chili flakes works."},"sub":{"0":{"x":"2 tbsp honey plus a pinch of chili flakes","d":"Less tangy and garlicky than sweet chili sauce.","k":["honey","chili flakes"],"wn":"Do not give honey to children under 1 year"}},"tips":[{"t":"Drumsticks should reach 175F/79C near the bone so the meat is tender; 165F/74C is safe but tougher.","s":2},{"t":"Pat the drumsticks dry and space them apart on the foil so the skin crisps instead of steaming.","s":1},{"t":"The sugars in the sauce burn easily; if they darken too quickly, tent loosely with foil.","s":2}],"rl":"XXAXXP"},"3190":{"lv":"EERERRRRERR","w":{"4":"Balances the sour and salty seasoning.","5":"Adds heat; adjust to taste.","6":"Adds mild sweetness and crunch.","7":"A mild onion bite.","9":"Adds a second herb note.","10":"Cups for scooping.","2":"Adds brightness; skipped, the dish is flatter but still works.","3":"Main salt and umami seasoning; skipped, the dish tastes flat and underseasoned."},"sub":{"2":{"x":"3 tbsp lemon juice","d":"Slightly less floral than lime.","k":["lemon juice"]},"3":{"x":"2 tbsp soy sauce plus 1 tsp extra lime juice","d":"Less funky but saltier.","k":["soy sauce"],"wn":"Contains soy (and wheat unless tamari)"}},"tips":[{"t":"Toast the rice until deep golden before grinding; the nutty toasted rice powder is the signature texture of larb.","s":0},{"t":"Cook the chicken gently with a little water so it stays tender and loose. Cook to 165F/74C, and do not brown it.","s":1},{"t":"Season off the heat and fold in the herbs and rice powder last; serve right away, since the powder softens quickly.","s":3}],"rl":"PTAXWXXXXXG"},"3191":{"lv":"ERRRREERR","w":{"1":"Adds pungent depth when pounded.","2":"Adds heat; reduce for a milder salad.","3":"Adds crunch and a fresh green note.","4":"Adds sweetness and juice.","8":"Adds richness and crunch.","6":"Main salt and umami seasoning; skipped, the dish tastes flat and underseasoned.","7":"Balances sweetness; skipped, the dish is a bit sharper but still fine."},"sub":{"6":{"x":"2 tbsp soy sauce","d":"Less funky and saltier.","k":["soy sauce"],"wn":"Contains soy (and wheat unless tamari)"},"7":{"x":"1 tbsp white sugar","d":"Slightly less caramel flavor.","k":["sugar"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Soak the shredded papaya in ice water for 10 minutes before using; this makes it crisp and keeps it crunchy.","s":3},{"t":"Dissolve the sugar into the lime and fish sauce first, then taste; the dressing should be sour, salty, sweet and hot in balance.","s":2},{"t":"Pound gently to bruise, not mash, and serve immediately; the salad weeps its liquid after a few minutes.","s":3}],"rl":"VXXVVAXWG"},"53191":{"lv":"EERREEERRROOO","w":{"2":"A little heat; skip for a milder noodle.","7":"Adds mild onion flavor and color.","8":"Adds crunch and freshness.","9":"Adds crunch and richness.","10":"A fresh finish.","11":"Squeezed over the plate at the end.","12":"For serving.","3":"Balances sweetness; skipped, the dish is a bit sharper but still fine.","4":"Main salt and umami seasoning; skipped, the dish tastes flat and underseasoned.","5":"Only cooking fat; skipped, food sticks and browns poorly, so use an oil or substitute."},"sub":{"3":{"x":"2 tsp white sugar","d":"Slightly less deep sweetness.","k":["sugar"]},"4":{"x":"2 tbsp soy sauce","d":"Less funky and saltier; start with 1.5 tbsp.","k":["soy sauce"],"wn":"Contains soy (and wheat unless tamari)"},"5":{"x":"2 tbsp any neutral oil","d":"No change.","k":["vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Soak noodles only until flexible but still firm; they finish cooking in the wok. Rinsing in cold water stops the cooking and removes sticky starch.","s":0},{"t":"Cook raw prawns until pink and opaque, not just warmed, and the sauce should go in along the edge of the wok.","s":2},{"t":"Cook in a very hot wok, working fast; crowded noodles steam and clump.","s":2}],"rl":"BAXWXFPXVGGGG"},"53198":{"lv":"ERRREERERRRO","w":{"1":"Adds sweetness and color.","2":"Fragrant base for the wok.","3":"Adds heat; skip for a mild rice.","6":"Adds sweetness and color.","9":"Adds a fresh finish.","10":"A fried egg on top adds richness and a runny yolk sauce.","11":"For serving.","0":"Only cooking fat; skipped, food sticks and browns poorly, so use an oil or substitute.","8":"Supporting umami and salt; skipped, the dish still works but is less complex."},"sub":{"0":{"x":"2 tbsp any neutral oil","d":"No change.","k":["vegetable oil"]},"8":{"x":"1 tbsp extra soy sauce plus a squeeze of lime","d":"Less funk and saltier.","k":["soy sauce","lime"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Use cold, day-old rice that was cooled quickly and refrigerated within an hour; warm freshly cooked rice clumps, and rice left out can grow harmful bacteria.","s":1},{"t":"Brown rice stays firmer and separate, so toss it a bit longer until very hot, and have everything ready before you start.","s":1},{"t":"Fry the eggs in hot oil until the edges are crisp and lacy, and the yolk is runny; break it over the rice as a sauce.","s":2}],"rl":"FXXXPBVXXGGG"},"53200":{"lv":"ERERRREERREORROO","w":{"1":"Adds heat and freshness.","3":"The stalks bring a roasty herbal aroma to the marinade.","4":"Balances the lime and fish sauce.","5":"Adds sour brightness to the marinade.","8":"Adds warm spice.","9":"Adds mild onion sweetness.","11":"Adds crunch; skip.","12":"Adds freshness and crunch.","13":"Adds extra salt and depth.","14":"For serving.","15":"For serving.","7":"Only cooking fat; skipped, food sticks and browns poorly, so use an oil or substitute."},"sub":{"7":{"x":"2 tbsp any neutral oil","d":"No peanut flavor.","k":["vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Marinate the prawns in the herb paste for only 10 to 15 minutes; the lime acid starts to firm and toughen the flesh.","s":0},{"t":"Cook the prawns in a hot wok for 1 to 2 minutes until pink and curled into a C. Overcooked ones curl tight and become rubbery.","s":2},{"t":"Cook the vegetables first and keep them crisp; remove them before the prawns so nothing stews.","s":1}],"rl":"PXXXWAXFXXVVVXBG"},"53202":{"lv":"ERRRERR","w":{"1":"Adds warm freshness that suits trout.","2":"Adds savory depth.","3":"Adds heat; use less for mild.","5":"Cooks in the same steam and adds greens.","6":"Seasons the steaming sauce; skipped, the fish is milder."},"sub":{"6":{"x":"2 tbsp tamari or 1 tbsp fish sauce plus 1 tbsp water","d":"Fish sauce is funkier and saltier.","k":["tamari","fish sauce"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Leave an air gap in the foil so steam circulates. A tight seal with no space steams unevenly.","s":2},{"t":"Trout fillets are thin and may be cooked in 10 to 12 minutes; the fish is done at 145F/63C when it flakes easily and is opaque.","s":3},{"t":"Open the parcel carefully away from your face to avoid a steam burn, and pour the juices over rice.","s":3}],"rl":"PXXXAVX"},"53203":{"lv":"EREERRREROOROEO","w":{"1":"Adds crunch and freshness.","4":"Balances the lime and fish sauce.","5":"Adds a sharp bite.","6":"Adds crispness and cooling freshness.","8":"Seasons the pork version.","9":"A pinch of heat for the pork version; skip.","11":"Forms a crunchy crust on the steak version.","14":"Adds heat.","3":"Main salt and umami seasoning; skipped, the dish tastes flat and underseasoned.","10":"Tiny amount; skipped, there is little noticeable effect.","12":"Tiny amount; skipped, there is little noticeable effect."},"sub":{"3":{"x":"2 tbsp soy sauce","d":"Less funky and saltier.","k":["soy sauce"],"wn":"Contains soy (and wheat unless tamari)"},"10":{"x":"1 tsp any neutral oil","d":"No change.","k":["vegetable oil"]},"12":{"x":"1 tsp any neutral oil","d":"No change.","k":["vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"This recipe gives two toppings, pork mince or sirloin steak. Choose one and use only that amount; do not make both.","s":2},{"t":"Cook minced pork until no pink remains, at least 160F/71C. For steak, rub with oil, press into the sesame seeds, sear and rest before slicing thin across the grain; 130F is medium-rare.","s":3},{"t":"Soak vermicelli only until tender and rinse in cold water so it stays separate and does not turn gummy.","s":0}],"rl":"BVAXWVVPXXFGFPG"},"53205":{"lv":"RRREEEEO","w":{"1":"Sweet base for the curry.","2":"Adds warm spice and fresh aroma.","7":"A fresh garnish.","0":"Cooks the paste; skipped, the curry still works."},"sub":{"0":{"x":"1 tbsp any neutral oil","d":"No change.","k":["vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Thaw the frozen prawns and pat them dry before adding; otherwise they dilute the sauce.","s":2},{"t":"Cook the prawns only until pink and opaque, 5 to 10 minutes depending on size; overcooked prawns are rubbery.","s":2},{"t":"Fry the paste in the oil for a full minute before adding liquid so the spices bloom.","s":1}],"rl":"FXXXVFPG"},"53209":{"lv":"ERRRRERRORO","w":{"1":"Adds toasty aroma to the omelette and stir-fry.","2":"Adds protein and richness.","3":"Adds heat.","4":"Adds a warm bite.","7":"Adds a fresh finish.","8":"Adds a cool minty finish.","9":"Adds sharp brightness.","10":"Adds crunch; skip.","6":"Seasons the noodles; skipped, the dish is milder."},"sub":{"1":{"x":"1 tbsp vegetable oil","d":"Loses toasty flavor.","k":["vegetable oil"]},"6":{"x":"1 tbsp tamari or 1 tsp salt dissolved in 1 tbsp water","d":"Less depth.","k":["tamari"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Rinse the cooked noodles in cold water and toss with a little oil so they do not clump.","s":0},{"t":"Cook the shrimp for only about 1 to 2 minutes until pink and curled in a C; they continue cooking in the hot wok.","s":2},{"t":"Stir in the herbs and lime off the heat so they stay bright and fragrant.","s":3}],"rl":"BFTXXPXGGAG"},"42":{"lv":"RRRREEEOREOR","w":{"1":"Sweet base for the curry broth.","2":"Fragrant base that mellows in the soup.","3":"Adds a warm bite that complements the curry.","7":"Rounds the heat and salt; a small amount.","10":"A fresh garnish.","11":"Brightens the rich coconut broth.","6":"The broth base; water plus fish sauce works.","8":"Adds salt and umami; soy sauce works.","0":"For cooking the aromatics; any oil works."},"sub":{"0":{"x":"1 tbsp any neutral oil","d":"No change.","k":["vegetable oil"]},"6":{"x":"3 cups water plus 1 chicken bouillon cube","d":"Less savory; taste before adding salt.","k":["water","chicken bouillon"]},"8":{"x":"1 tbsp soy sauce","d":"Less funky; add lime at the end.","k":["soy sauce"],"wn":"Contains soy (and wheat unless tamari)"}},"tips":[{"t":"Cook the curry paste for a full minute until fragrant and the oil separates before adding the liquid; this removes the raw taste.","s":0},{"t":"Add the frozen dumplings straight from the freezer and keep the soup at a gentle simmer; a hard boil bursts the wrappers. Dumplings must be cooked hot through (165F/74C).","s":2},{"t":"Squeeze the lime in at the table so it stays fresh and the coconut does not turn sour from long cooking.","s":3}],"rl":"FXXXXMMWZPGA"},"107":{"lv":"EEERRRRROR","w":{"6":"Adds toasty aroma to the sauce.","7":"Adds a savory bite.","9":"Adds cool crunch against the rich sauce.","8":"A fresh garnish.","3":"Balances sweetness; skipped, the dish is a bit sharper but still fine.","4":"Adds brightness; skipped, the dish is flatter but still works.","5":"Adds heat; skipped, the noodles are milder."},"sub":{"3":{"x":"1 tbsp brown sugar or maple syrup","d":"Slightly less floral than honey.","k":["brown sugar","maple syrup"]},"4":{"x":"1 tbsp lime juice or apple cider vinegar","d":"A bit sharper; add gradually.","k":["lime juice","apple cider vinegar"]},"5":{"x":"1 tbsp any hot sauce or 1/2 tsp red pepper flakes plus 1 tsp water","d":"Less garlicky and sweet.","k":["hot sauce","red pepper flakes"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Save a cup of the starchy pasta water before draining; it is what thins the peanut butter into a smooth, glossy sauce.","s":0},{"t":"Whisk the sauce with hot water until smooth, and toss while the noodles are hot; the sauce thickens as it cools, so loosen with water if needed.","s":1},{"t":"Do not rinse the noodles; the surface starch helps the sauce cling.","s":2}],"rl":"BFZWAXXXGV"},"3187":{"lv":"EEERRRERRRO","w":{"3":"Thins the coconut milk so the sauce is not heavy; water works with a pinch of salt.","4":"Adds sweetness and crunch; skip and add more bamboo.","5":"Adds a mild crunchy texture; rinse canned shoots well.","6":"Salty umami backbone; soy sauce works.","7":"Balances salt and heat.","8":"Adds a sweet anise aroma; skip and the curry is less fragrant.","9":"Brightens the rich coconut sauce.","10":"A serving extra."},"sub":{"3":{"x":"1 cup water plus 1/2 tsp salt","d":"Less savory.","k":["water","salt"]},"6":{"x":"2 tbsp soy sauce","d":"Less funky; add lime to compensate.","k":["soy sauce"],"wn":"Contains soy (and wheat unless tamari)"},"7":{"x":"1 tbsp white sugar","d":"Less caramel depth.","k":["sugar"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Fry the paste in the thick coconut cream until it separates and smells toasted, about 2 minutes; this is where the curry gets its depth.","s":0},{"t":"Rinse the canned bamboo shoots well to remove the tinny taste, and add the pepper late so it stays slightly crisp.","s":3},{"t":"Add the basil and lime off the heat; cooked basil turns dull and loses its aroma.","s":4}],"rl":"MXPMVVZWXAB"},"3194":{"lv":"ERERRREERRROO","w":{"1":"Lightly coats the chicken so it browns and holds the sauce; flour works.","3":"Fragrant base; skip and use garlic powder.","4":"Adds sweetness and color.","5":"Sweet, soft wedges.","8":"Adds a salty note.","9":"Adds umami.","10":"Balances the salty sauces.","11":"A fresh garnish.","12":"For serving.","2":"Fat for the stir-fry; any neutral oil works."},"sub":{"2":{"x":"2 tbsp any neutral oil","d":"No change.","k":["vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"The cornstarch-tossed chicken forms a light crust that holds the sauce and stays tender. Cook it in a single layer and make sure it is 165F/74C inside.","s":1},{"t":"Mix the sauce first so the final tossing takes seconds; the cornstarch left in the pan thickens it to a glossy coating.","s":3},{"t":"Add the cashews at the end so they stay crunchy.","s":4}],"rl":"PTFXVVGXXXWGB"},"3195":{"lv":"EERRRERRERRO","w":{"2":"Fragrant base.","3":"Adds sweetness.","4":"Adds richness; skip for a lighter rice.","6":"Adds sweetness and color.","7":"The golden curry flavor; skip and it tastes like plain pineapple fried rice.","10":"Adds crunch and richness.","11":"A fresh garnish.","9":"Adds salt and umami; soy sauce works.","1":"Cooking fat."},"sub":{"1":{"x":"2 tbsp any neutral oil","d":"No change.","k":["vegetable oil"]},"9":{"x":"1 tbsp extra soy sauce","d":"Less funky.","k":["soy sauce"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Use cold, day-old rice and break up the clumps before it hits the pan; moist rice turns gummy instead of frying.","s":2},{"t":"Pat the pineapple dry and add it last; fresh pineapple weeps liquid and turns the rice mushy. Canned must be drained very well.","s":4},{"t":"Sprinkle the curry powder onto the hot rice rather than the oil, where it can scorch and turn bitter.","s":3}],"rl":"BFXXTVVXXXGG"},"3196":{"lv":"EEEERRROO","w":{"5":"Adds salt and depth.","6":"Balances the salty and spicy paste.","7":"Adds citrus aroma; optional.","8":"A serving extra.","1":"Cooking fat.","4":"Supporting umami and salt; skipped, the dish still works but is less complex."},"sub":{"1":{"x":"2 tbsp any neutral oil","d":"No change.","k":["vegetable oil"]},"4":{"x":"1 tbsp extra soy sauce","d":"Less funky.","k":["soy sauce"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Pad prik king is a dry stir-fry; the paste should cling to the beans instead of making a sauce, so use only the 2 tablespoons of water.","s":3},{"t":"Freeze the pork for 20 minutes before slicing very thin; it cooks in 3 minutes and stays tender.","s":1},{"t":"Fry the paste in the oil until fragrant and reddish before adding the pork so the spices bloom.","s":0}],"rl":"PFXVXXWXB"},"3197":{"lv":"EEERERREOO","w":{"6":"Balances the salty sauces.","8":"A serving extra.","9":"Cool crunch against the garlicky pork.","3":"Adds savory sweetness; soy plus sugar works.","5":"Adds salt and umami.","7":"Cooking fat."},"sub":{"3":{"x":"2 tbsp soy sauce plus 1 tsp sugar","d":"Less rich and more salty.","k":["soy sauce","sugar"]},"5":{"x":"1 tbsp extra soy sauce","d":"Less funky.","k":["soy sauce"]},"7":{"x":"2 tbsp any neutral oil","d":"No change.","k":["vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Crack the black pepper coarsely in a mortar and mince the garlic; the pepper and garlic should be strong, not fine.","s":0},{"t":"Cook the pork in a single layer in batches over high heat so it browns; crowded pork steams and turns gray. Cook to 145F/63C.","s":2},{"t":"Watch the garlic bits in the pan; they should turn golden, not black, or they will be bitter.","s":2}],"rl":"PXXXXXWFBG"},"52814":{"lv":"RREREEROERRO","w":{"0":"Adds heartiness to the curry.","1":"Adds crunch and color.","3":"Fragrant start; fried until golden.","9":"Adds citrus aroma; use zest if you lack leaves.","10":"Adds fresh anise aroma.","11":"A serving extra.","2":"Only cooking fat; skipped, food sticks and browns poorly, so use an oil or substitute.","6":"Supporting umami and salt; skipped, the dish still works but is less complex.","7":"Tiny amount; skipped, there is little noticeable effect."},"sub":{"2":{"x":"1 tbsp any neutral oil","d":"No change.","k":["vegetable oil"]},"6":{"x":"2 tsp soy sauce","d":"Less funky.","k":["soy sauce"],"wn":"Contains soy (and wheat unless tamari)"},"7":{"x":"1 tsp white sugar","d":"Less depth.","k":["sugar"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Store-bought green curry paste varies widely in heat and salt; start with 3 tsp and taste before adding more.","s":1},{"t":"Fry the garlic for only a few seconds until golden; if it gets dark, it turns bitter and the dish tastes burnt.","s":1},{"t":"Slice the lime leaves very thin and discard the tough central vein; add the basil off the heat.","s":3}],"rl":"BVFXXMXWPXXB"},"53192":{"lv":"EEERERRRORRRRRRRORRR","w":{"3":"Adds crunch and a fresh green note.","6":"Adds citrus aroma.","7":"Adds fresh anise aroma.","8":"A serving extra.","9":"Part of the homemade paste; skip if using bought paste (1 tbsp above).","10":"Part of the homemade paste; skip if using bought paste.","11":"Adds umami depth to the homemade paste; skip if using bought paste.","12":"Part of the homemade paste; skip if using bought paste.","13":"Part of the homemade paste; skip if using bought paste.","14":"Part of the homemade paste; skip if using bought paste.","15":"Part of the homemade paste; skip if using bought paste.","16":"A small warm note in the paste.","17":"Part of the homemade paste; skip if using bought paste.","18":"Part of the homemade paste; skip if using bought paste.","19":"Gives the panang its nutty richness; skip if using bought paste.","4":"Provides salt and umami; soy sauce works.","5":"Balances the salty and spicy; white sugar works."},"sub":{"4":{"x":"2 tbsp soy sauce","d":"Less funky.","k":["soy sauce"],"wn":"Contains soy (and wheat unless tamari)"},"5":{"x":"1 tbsp white sugar","d":"Less depth.","k":["sugar"]}},"tips":[{"t":"The panang paste and the chilies, spices and aromatics listed at the end are alternatives: use the store-bought paste, or pound the homemade one and use 1 to 2 tbsp of it.","s":0},{"t":"Panang is thicker and less soupy than other curries; fry the paste in the thick coconut milk until the oil separates, then simmer to reduce.","s":1},{"t":"Slice the chicken thin and cook until no longer pink (165F/74C); add half the lime leaves early and half at the end.","s":3}],"rl":"MXPVXWXXBXXXXXXXXXXX"},"53193":{"lv":"EERREREEEOR","w":{"2":"Fragrant base.","3":"Adds the signature heat.","5":"Adds crunch and a green vegetable.","9":"A small hit of umami.","10":"Balances the salty sauces.","1":"Cooking fat."},"sub":{"1":{"x":"2 tbsp any neutral oil","d":"No change.","k":["vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Separate fresh noodles by hand before adding them to the wok; cold clumps break and burn.","s":0},{"t":"Mix the sauce ahead and keep the wok smoking hot; the steak should sear quickly, not stew.","s":1},{"t":"Add the basil off the heat so it wilts without turning black.","s":2}],"rl":"BFXXPVXXXXW"},"53199":{"lv":"EERER","w":{"2":"Adds heat.","4":"Adds aroma at the end.","0":"Cooking fat."},"sub":{"0":{"x":"2 tbsp any neutral oil","d":"No change.","k":["vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Get the pan smoking hot and cook the beef in small batches; slice it thin against the grain so it stays tender in 3 minutes.","s":1},{"t":"Pour the oyster sauce in at the end; its sugar scorches quickly if added early.","s":2},{"t":"Add the basil off the heat so it wilts without turning black.","s":3}],"rl":"FPXXX"},"53204":{"lv":"EERRRRO","w":{"3":"Adds sweetness and color.","4":"Adds a mild juicy bite.","5":"Adds sweetness and char.","6":"Squeezed over at the end.","2":"Loosens the paste so it coats the chicken."},"sub":{"2":{"x":"2 tbsp plain yogurt","d":"Tangier; it tenderizes the chicken a little.","k":["plain yogurt"],"wn":"Contains dairy"}},"tips":[{"t":"Soak wooden skewers for 30 minutes and cut the chicken into even 1 inch pieces so it cooks through to 165F/74C.","s":1},{"t":"Do not pack the skewers tight; leave small gaps so heat reaches every side.","s":1},{"t":"The sugar in the curry paste scorches; cook over medium-high heat and turn often.","s":1}],"rl":"PXMVVVG"},"3189":{"lv":"ERERRREERRRRO","w":{"1":"Tenderizes and flavors the chicken.","3":"Adds salt and depth.","4":"Adds sweetness and helps char.","5":"Adds umami.","8":"Adds spice and depth to the sauce.","9":"Adds salt.","10":"Adds brightness.","11":"Balances the sauce.","12":"Adjust at the end.","7":"Thins the sauce."},"sub":{"7":{"x":"1/2 cup whole milk","d":"Thinner and less rich; add lime off the heat to avoid curdling.","k":["whole milk"],"wn":"Contains dairy; add lime off the heat and keep below a simmer"}},"tips":[{"t":"Soak the skewers and thread thin strips of chicken in an accordion pattern so they cook evenly and the ends do not burn.","s":1},{"t":"Grill hot until charred and cooked to 165F/74C.","s":3},{"t":"Use smooth unsweetened peanut butter; the sauce thickens as it cools, so thin with warm water.","s":2}],"rl":"PMXXWXTMXXAWZ"},"3472":{"lv":"EEERRROR","w":{"3":"Seasons the pickle and draws out water.","4":"Adds a mild sharp bite.","5":"Adds heat.","6":"A fresh garnish.","7":"Adds crunch and richness.","1":"Provides the sour tang; white vinegar with water works."},"sub":{"1":{"x":"1/3 cup white vinegar plus 2 tbsp water","d":"Sharper and less mellow than rice vinegar.","k":["white vinegar"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Cool the syrup completely before pouring it over; hot syrup cooks the cucumber and makes it limp.","s":1},{"t":"Slice the cucumber thin and uniform so it pickles quickly; a mandoline helps.","s":2},{"t":"Keep the peanuts and cilantro separate until just before serving so they stay crisp.","s":4}],"rl":"VKWZVXGG"},"55":{"lv":"ERRREEERRRO","w":{"2":"Adds warm aroma.","3":"Fragrant base.","7":"Adds earthy texture.","9":"Brightens the rich coconut broth.","10":"A fresh garnish.","1":"Cooking fat.","6":"The broth base.","8":"Adds salt and umami."},"sub":{"1":{"x":"1 tbsp any neutral oil","d":"No change.","k":["vegetable oil"]},"6":{"x":"3 cups water plus 1 chicken bouillon cube","d":"Less savory.","k":["water","chicken bouillon"]},"8":{"x":"1 tbsp soy sauce","d":"Less funky.","k":["soy sauce"],"wn":"Contains soy (and wheat unless tamari)"}},"tips":[{"t":"Slice the chicken thin and add it to a gentle simmer; a hard boil can split the coconut milk and toughen the meat. Cook to 165F/74C.","s":2},{"t":"Add the fish sauce and lime off the heat in small amounts and taste; the broth may already be salty.","s":3},{"t":"Slice the mushrooms thin so they cook in the short simmer.","s":2}],"rl":"PFXXXMMVZAG"},"3192":{"lv":"REEREEEERERROR","w":{"3":"Gives the broth its golden color and earthy note; skipping it leaves a paler but still good curry.","10":"Raw sharp crunch against the rich broth; skip it and the bowl is heavier.","11":"Acid to cut the coconut richness; skipping makes the bowl taste flat and heavy.","12":"Fresh herb garnish only.","13":"Only used for frying the crispy noodle topping; skip it and top with plain noodles, but you lose the signature crunch.","0":"Blooms the paste; skipped, the soup is less fragrant.","7":"Main salt and umami seasoning; skipped, the dish tastes flat and underseasoned.","8":"Balances sweetness; skipped, the dish is a bit sharper but still fine."},"sub":{"0":{"x":"2 tbsp canola oil","d":"Any neutral oil works for blooming the paste.","k":["canola oil"]},"7":{"x":"2 tbsp soy sauce plus a squeeze of lime","d":"Less funky and less fermented; add a little at a time.","k":["soy sauce","lime"],"wn":"Contains soy (and wheat unless tamari)"},"8":{"x":"1 tbsp white sugar or honey","d":"Slightly less molasses depth.","k":["sugar","honey"],"wn":"Do not give honey to children under 1 year"}},"tips":[{"t":"Fry the noodle nest in oil that sizzles around a test strand; the strands should puff in 2-3 seconds and turn golden in under 30. Oil that is too cool makes them greasy and chewy.","s":0},{"t":"Fry the curry paste and powder until the oil separates and turns red and the smell turns toasty. That bloom is where the khao soi flavor starts.","s":1},{"t":"Keep the simmer gentle once the coconut milk is in. A hard boil can split it, and chicken thighs stay tender when cooked low.","s":3}],"rl":"FXXXPMMXWBGAGF"},"53194":{"lv":"RRREEERREEERRO","w":{"0":"Sweetens and rounds the broth; skip it and the broth is thinner and sharper.","1":"Adds light acidity and body to the base; skip it and the soup is a little flatter.","2":"Supplies the heat; reduce or skip for a milder soup, but you lose the tom yum fire.","7":"Absorbs the broth and adds a meaty bite; mushrooms can be left out with little change in flavor.","11":"Softens the sourness and makes the broth creamy; skip it for a clear, sharper tom yum.","12":"Adds a sweet, roasted-chili depth; a pinch of sugar and chili flakes stands in.","13":"Fresh herb finish only.","6":"Adds savory depth to a small amount of liquid; skipped, flavor is just muted.","9":"Main salt and umami seasoning; skipped, the dish tastes flat and underseasoned."},"sub":{"6":{"x":"1 tsp chicken or vegetable bouillon powder","d":"Same seasoning job as the stock cube; adjust fish sauce for salt.","k":["bouillon"]},"9":{"x":"1 tbsp plus 2 tsp soy sauce with an extra squeeze of lime","d":"Less fishy and less complex; add gradually.","k":["soy sauce","lime"],"wn":"Contains soy (and wheat unless tamari)"}},"tips":[{"t":"Bruise the lemongrass with the back of a knife and tear the lime leaves before they go in. It releases their oils so the broth tastes of them.","s":0},{"t":"Add the lime juice and fish sauce only after the broth is off the boil or just at the end. Prolonged heat turns the lime bitter and dulls its brightness.","s":2},{"t":"Simmer the shrimp heads in the stock and strain them out. The orange fat they release gives tom yum its color and sweet shellfish flavor.","s":0}],"rl":"VAXXXPMVXXAMXG"},"53195":{"lv":"REREEEEOROO","w":{"0":"Only blooms the paste; skip it and fry the paste in a splash of the coconut milk.","2":"Gives crunch and color; any quick-cooking veg works, so use what you have.","7":"Fresh finish; skip with little change.","8":"Anise-like freshness lifts the curry; skip it and add extra coriander.","9":"Extra heat and color on top; curry paste already supplies spice.","10":"Mild onion note on top; skip."},"tips":[{"t":"Cook the green curry paste for the full minute until the smell turns toasty. It blooms in the oil and loses its raw taste.","s":0},{"t":"Udon is very thick; for this quick soup, use fresh or pre-cooked udon so it heats through in 5 minutes without overcooking the veg.","s":1},{"t":"Drop the herbs in only at serving. Basil turns black and bitter if it simmers in the broth.","s":3}],"rl":"FXVPMMBGGGG"},"53201":{"lv":"EEEREERRE","w":{"3":"Sweet, mild shallots balance the sharp chili and fish sauce; use a little onion if you have none.","6":"Adds saltiness and a dark savory color; skip it and use slightly more fish sauce.","7":"Balances the salty fish sauce and chili; a pinch is enough.","0":"Only cooking fat; skipped, food sticks and browns poorly, so use an oil or substitute.","5":"Main salt and umami seasoning; skipped, the dish tastes flat and underseasoned."},"sub":{"0":{"x":"2 tbsp canola oil","d":"Any high-heat neutral oil works.","k":["canola oil"]},"5":{"x":"1 tbsp plus 1 tsp soy sauce plus a squeeze of lime","d":"Less funky and less complex; balance with a pinch of sugar.","k":["soy sauce","lime"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Cut the chicken in thin small pieces or use ground chicken, and cook it until it hits 165F (74C) inside. Cook it in a very hot wok in batches; a crowded pan steams the meat instead of browning it.","s":0},{"t":"Fry the garlic and shallots until golden, not dark brown. Burnt garlic turns bitter and cannot be fixed.","s":1},{"t":"Add the basil off the heat. It wilts instantly and keeps its peppery aroma that way.","s":2}],"rl":"FPXXXXXWX"},"53206":{"lv":"ERRRRREROOO","w":{"1":"Seasons the cakes; skipping makes them bland.","2":"Warm spice that balances the coriander; skip for a plainer cake.","3":"Adds moisture and sweetness to the lean chicken breast; skip and the cakes may be drier.","4":"Fresh herb that is part of the cake flavor; skip and use more green onion.","5":"Heat inside the cake; skip for a mild version.","7":"Provides the sweet-spicy dip and moisture on the plate.","8":"Fresh acidity to serve; skip with little change.","9":"Fresh garnish.","10":"Garnish and heat.","6":"Frying medium; skipped, the food cannot be fried."},"sub":{"6":{"x":"2 tbsp canola oil","d":"Any neutral oil is fine for frying.","k":["canola oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Pulse the chicken in short bursts so it stays coarsely ground. A purée turns rubbery; the mixture should barely hold together.","s":0},{"t":"Wet your hands before shaping so the sticky mixture does not cling. Chill the formed cakes 15 minutes so they hold their shape in the pan.","s":1},{"t":"Cook the cakes until the center reads 165F (74C); breast meat dries out quickly, so do not go far past it.","s":1}],"rl":"PXXXXXFGGGG"},"53208":{"lv":"EREERERRROROO","w":{"4":"Balances the heat and salt and rounds the coconut; skip it and the broth is a bit sharper.","6":"Sweetness and color; any quick-cooking veg works.","7":"Adds soft crunch and mild sweetness; shredded cabbage works instead.","8":"Fresh crunch added at the end; skip.","9":"Adds a pop of acidity and color; skip.","10":"Brightens the rich coconut broth; add to taste.","11":"Mild raw onion on top.","12":"Fresh herb finish.","1":"Blooms the paste; skipped, the broth is slightly less fragrant."},"sub":{"1":{"x":"1 tsp canola oil","d":"Only helps bloom the paste.","k":["canola oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Fry the paste until it smells toasty and the oil turns red. A curry broth built from unfried paste tastes raw.","s":0},{"t":"Add the lime juice last and off the heat, a little at a time. It balances the coconut and sweetness and tastes bitter if boiled.","s":3},{"t":"Stir the bean sprouts in only at the end so they keep their crunch.","s":2}],"rl":"XFMMWBVVVVAGG"},"53210":{"lv":"ERRRREEERRO","w":{"2":"Sweet savory base for the soup; skip and it tastes thinner.","3":"Warm spicy note that supports the curry paste.","4":"Floral citrus aroma; skip it and add lime zest at the end.","8":"Cuts the sweetness of the pumpkin and coconut; add to taste.","9":"Balances the lime and chili; add a little.","10":"Garnish and heat.","1":"Cooks aromatics; skipped, the soup still works."},"sub":{"1":{"x":"4 tsp canola oil","d":"Any neutral oil works.","k":["canola oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Roast the pumpkin until the edges caramelize. Browned edges bring sweetness and depth that boiling cannot.","s":0},{"t":"Take out the lemongrass stalk before blending. It is fibrous and will not blend smooth.","s":2},{"t":"Hold back some coconut milk for the drizzle; a swirl on top gives the soup a restaurant look and fresh coconut taste.","s":4}],"rl":"VFXXXXMMAWG"},"53211":{"lv":"RRERRRERROEEO","w":{"0":"Adds warmth and sweetness to the purée; skip it and the sauce is less rounded.","1":"Savory backbone to the purée.","3":"Fresh herb flavor blended into the base.","4":"Heat; reduce or skip for a mild stew.","8":"Warm anise-cinnamon background; skip it and the stew is brighter but plainer.","9":"Balances the soy and chili; skip it with little change.","12":"Squeeze of acid at the table; skip it.","5":"Fries the puree; skipped, the stew is a bit less rich.","7":"Supporting umami and salt; skipped, the dish still works but is less complex."},"sub":{"5":{"x":"2 tbsp canola oil","d":"Any neutral oil works for frying the purée.","k":["canola oil"]},"7":{"x":"1 tbsp fish sauce plus 1 tbsp water","d":"More funky and salty; lime at the end balances it.","k":["fish sauce"],"wn":"Contains fish"}},"tips":[{"t":"Choose a tough braising cut like shin or chuck and cook it until it pulls apart. Lean steak would turn dry and stringy in this long braise.","s":2},{"t":"Cook the purée on gentle heat until the raw lemongrass smell turns sweet and the oil separates. Rushing leaves it grassy.","s":1},{"t":"Cook the noodles separately and rinse them. Left in the stew they swell and soak up the broth.","s":3}],"rl":"XXXXXFPXXWMBG"},"53213":{"lv":"EEEREERRO","w":{"3":"Aromatic citrus perfume that sets the broth apart; skip it and add a little lime zest.","6":"Adds sweetness and seafood depth; skip it and use extra fish.","7":"Green crunch; any leafy green works.","8":"Fresh herb garnish.","4":"Main salt and umami seasoning; skipped, the dish tastes flat and underseasoned."},"sub":{"4":{"x":"1 tbsp soy sauce plus a squeeze of lime","d":"Less fishy; lime keeps the brightness.","k":["soy sauce","lime"],"wn":"Contains soy (and wheat unless tamari)"}},"tips":[{"t":"Add the fish only after the broth has simmered. Fish cooks in 2 minutes and turns tough and flaky if it boils hard.","s":2},{"t":"Rinse the cooked noodles in cold water so they do not clump or keep cooking, and add them just before serving.","s":0},{"t":"Shrimp are done as soon as they curl and turn pink; remove from heat right after.","s":3}],"rl":"BMXXXPPVG"},"53214":{"lv":"RREREEERERRRRR","w":{"1":"Sweet base for the broth; skip it and the soup is a bit thinner.","3":"Adds savory depth and aroma to the chicken.","7":"Citrus perfume that is part of Thai green curry; skip it and add lime zest.","9":"Mild onion in the soup and sliced tops for finishing.","10":"Green crunch and sweetness; any firm green veg works.","11":"Crunchy texture and mild flavor; skip with little change.","12":"Acid and the green paste dressing for the soup; it brightens the rich coconut broth.","13":"Fresh aroma blitzed into the lime paste; skip and the soup tastes heavier.","0":"Cooks the paste; skipped, the soup still works.","8":"Main salt and umami seasoning; skipped, the dish tastes flat and underseasoned."},"sub":{"0":{"x":"2 tbsp canola oil","d":"Any neutral oil works.","k":["canola oil"]},"8":{"x":"2 tbsp soy sauce plus a squeeze of lime","d":"Less funky; add gradually and adjust the lime.","k":["soy sauce","lime"],"wn":"Contains soy (and wheat unless tamari)"}},"tips":[{"t":"280 g of paste is a whole large jar; taste after 12 minutes and keep adding only if the broth needs it, since brands vary hugely in heat and salt.","s":1},{"t":"Blitz the lime juice and basil into a green paste and add it at the end. Heating basil dulls its color and flavor.","s":2},{"t":"Simmer the chicken thighs until they reach 165F (74C) inside, which with thighs just makes them more tender.","s":1}],"rl":"FXPXXMMXXXVVAX"},"3188":{"lv":"EEERERO","w":{"3":"Sharpens the sweet coconut rice and sauce; skipping makes it cloying.","5":"Thickens the topping sauce so it clings; without it the sauce is thin.","6":"Nutty crunch on top; skip with little change."},"tips":[{"t":"Use real Thai glutinous (sweet) rice and steam it; sushi or regular rice will turn mushy and never sticky. Soaking for 4 hours lets the grains cook evenly.","s":0},{"t":"Fold the rice into the coconut milk while the rice is still hot. Hot grains drink the milk and become glossy instead of dry.","s":2},{"t":"Serve with the mango at room temperature; cold mango dulls the sweetness and cold rice hardens.","s":4}],"rl":"BFWZVTG"},"3277":{"lv":"EEERRRRREERO","w":{"6":"Adds a sharp, warm note to the sauce; use a pinch of powder if you have none.","7":"Brightens the rich peanut sauce.","10":"Sweetness and color to the crunch; skip and add more coleslaw.","11":"Fresh herb finish.","1":"Only cooking fat; skipped, food sticks and browns poorly, so use an oil or substitute.","3":"Seasons the sauce; skipped, it is bland.","4":"Adds brightness; skipped, the dish is flatter but still works.","5":"Balances sweetness; skipped, the dish is a bit sharper but still fine."},"sub":{"1":{"x":"1 tbsp canola oil","d":"Any neutral oil works.","k":["canola oil"]},"3":{"x":"2 tbsp tamari or 1 tbsp Worcestershire plus 1 tbsp water","d":"Tamari is nearly identical; Worcestershire is tangier.","k":["tamari","worcestershire sauce"],"wn":"Worcestershire contains fish (anchovies) and may contain wheat"},"4":{"x":"1 tbsp apple cider vinegar","d":"Slightly fruitier but just as sharp.","k":["apple cider vinegar"]},"5":{"x":"1 tbsp brown sugar or maple syrup","d":"Similar sweetness; sugar dissolves best if mixed with the warm water.","k":["brown sugar","maple syrup"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Slice the chicken thin and cook it to 165F (74C), no higher. Thin breast strips go from cooked to dry in about a minute.","s":0},{"t":"Whisk the sauce with warm water a tablespoon at a time. Peanut butter seizes up when cold, and it should be pourable but thick enough to cling.","s":1},{"t":"Warm the tortillas until pliable so they do not crack when rolled; fold the sides in before rolling for a tight wrap.","s":5}],"rl":"PFTXAWXABVVG"},"52969":{"lv":"EEREERRRRRE","w":{"2":"Sweet base for the tomato sauce; skip it and the sauce is sharper.","5":"Savory aroma in the sauce.","6":"North African warmth that distinguishes this from other shakshuka styles; skip and it tastes plainer.","7":"Adds color and mild sweetness.","8":"Brings out the vegetable flavor; add to taste.","9":"Heat; reduce or skip for a mild version, or add harissa.","1":"Only cooking fat; skipped, food sticks and browns poorly, so use an oil or substitute."},"sub":{"1":{"x":"2 tbsp canola oil","d":"Less fruity than olive oil.","k":["canola oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Reduce the sauce until a spoon drawn across the pan leaves a trail. A thin sauce makes the eggs slide around and cook unevenly.","s":2},{"t":"Make real wells with the back of a spoon, crack the eggs in, and cover. The steam sets the whites while the yolks stay runny.","s":3},{"t":"Pull the pan off the heat when the whites are just set; the yolks keep cooking from the hot pan.","s":3}],"rl":"VFXVVXXXZXP"},"52974":{"lv":"EEEERRRR","w":{"4":"Provides the braising liquid; the onions and lamb release more as they cook.","5":"Fresh herbal finish that cuts the richness.","6":"Gives the sauce a glossy, round finish.","7":"Fresh acid to cut the rich lamb at serving.","0":"Only cooking fat; skipped, food sticks and browns poorly, so use an oil or substitute."},"sub":{"0":{"x":"4 tbsp canola oil","d":"Less fruity than olive oil.","k":["canola oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Bloom the saffron in a spoonful of warm water for 10 minutes before adding it so its color and aroma spread through the sauce.","s":1},{"t":"Brown the lamb in batches; a crowded pan steams the meat and you lose the browned flavor that builds the sauce.","s":0},{"t":"Cook the lamb covered until tender before reducing, since tough cuts need time; the sauce should coat the back of a spoon.","s":2}],"rl":"FPXXMGFA"},"52971":{"lv":"EEEEREERO","w":{"4":"Spice blend that gives the dish its Tunisian flavor; skip it and use cumin, paprika and a pinch of cinnamon.","7":"Needed to season the potatoes and eggs.","8":"A pinch for background heat only.","6":"Binds the fried vegetables into a firm, omelet-like cake; 4 to 6 eggs is enough for 4 servings.","1":"Frying medium; skipped, the food cannot be fried."},"sub":{"1":{"x":"2 tbsp olive oil plus extra for frying (or canola oil)","d":"Canola is neutral and works for frying but is less fruity.","k":["canola oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Pat the potato cubes dry and keep the oil very hot. They crisp outside before the center turns mushy.","s":1},{"t":"Fry the onions slowly until deep gold; they turn sweet, while rushed onions stay sharp.","s":3},{"t":"Cook the eggs gently under a lid until set through, as this is a firm omelet-like dish.","s":5}],"rl":"BFVVXVSZZ"},"52975":{"lv":"EREEEEORRRO","w":{"1":"Mild sweetness with the spinach; skip it and the filling is plainer.","6":"Background heat only.","7":"Fresh salad side with a sharp bite.","8":"Cool crunch for the salad.","9":"Acid that dresses the tomato salad.","10":"Sweet-tart chutney served alongside; skip it.","0":"Only cooking fat; skipped, food sticks and browns poorly, so use an oil or substitute."},"sub":{"0":{"x":"2 tbsp canola oil","d":"Less fruity but works for sauteing and brushing the filo.","k":["canola oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Squeeze the cooked spinach very dry; excess water makes the filling wet and the filo go soggy.","s":2},{"t":"Keep unused filo covered with a damp towel. It dries and cracks within minutes in open air.","s":3},{"t":"Brush only a thin layer of oil between sheets so the briks bake crisp, not greasy; do not overfill or they burst.","s":4}],"rl":"FXVSPTXGGAG"},"52972":{"lv":"ERRREREEERERO","w":{"1":"Savory base for the lamb and tomato paste.","2":"Sweet base that melts into the broth.","3":"Adds color, iron-like depth and body; skip and add a handful of any greens.","5":"Earthy warmth typical of Tunisian soups.","9":"A squeeze of acid at the end lifts the rich lamb broth.","11":"Seasons the soup; add to taste since broth is salted.","12":"Light background heat."},"tips":[{"t":"Brown the ground lamb in clumps and leave it alone until a crust forms, then drain all but a spoonful of fat. Crust adds depth, and too much fat makes the soup greasy.","s":0},{"t":"Fry the tomato paste and harissa in the fat for a minute before adding the broth. The paste darkens and the harissa loses its raw edge.","s":2},{"t":"Add the macaroni late and serve soon; it keeps absorbing broth and turns soft. If it thickens too much, loosen with hot water.","s":3}],"rl":"PXXVXXMXBABZZ"},"52973":{"lv":"RREEEEREOR","w":{"1":"Sweet base for the chickpea broth.","6":"Seasons the soup; essential at this broth volume, so adjust at the end.","8":"A pinch for mild heat.","9":"Traditional squeeze of acid that lifts the garlicky broth.","0":"Sautes the onion; skipped, the soup still works."},"sub":{"0":{"x":"2 tbsp canola oil","d":"Less fruity, still carries the onion.","k":["canola oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Toast the whole cumin seeds in a dry pan until fragrant and then grind them fresh. The aroma is far stronger than pre-ground cumin.","s":2},{"t":"Pounding the garlic with salt turns it to a paste so it melts into the soup instead of leaving raw chunks.","s":2},{"t":"Soak the chickpeas overnight and keep the simmer gentle; hard boiling splits the skins and clouds the broth.","s":1}],"rl":"FXBMXXZXZA"},"52970":{"lv":"EEEEEER","w":{"6":"Rounds out the orange flavor; skipping it is barely noticeable.","2":"Fat in the dough or batter; skipped, the result is dry, tough or less tender."},"sub":{"2":{"x":"75 ml (5 tbsp) canola or vegetable oil","d":"Neutral oil keeps the cake moist; the olive flavor is lost.","k":["canola oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Use the whole orange, peel and all; pureeing it gives the cake its bitter-sweet orange flavor and moisture.","s":1},{"t":"Beat the eggs and sugar until they are pale and fall in a thick ribbon. That trapped air is the only lift besides the baking powder.","s":3},{"t":"Fold the flour in gently in two additions; stirring hard deflates the batter and gives a dense cake.","s":3}],"rl":"VWFSLSX"},"52976":{"lv":"EEERRO","w":{"3":"Gives the Tunisian floral perfume; skip it and the biscuits taste like plain nut sweets.","4":"Rolling coat that gives the signature cracked white look and stops sticking.","5":"A decorative topping only."},"sub":{"3":{"x":"1 tsp vanilla extract plus 1 tbsp water","d":"Vanilla instead of floral; the biscuits are still good.","k":["vanilla extract"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Grind the cashews fine but not so long that they turn oily. Over-processing makes a greasy paste that spreads.","s":1},{"t":"Chill the paste for 15 minutes if sticky; roll balls the same size so they bake evenly.","s":2},{"t":"Pull them while still pale. They firm as they cool, and overbaked ghoriba turn hard and dry.","s":3}],"rl":"SWTXGG"},"3034":{"lv":"EEERERROO","w":{"3":"Sweet base; the dish still works without it.","5":"Adds the smoky, mild heat typical of menemen.","6":"Needed to season the eggs and tomatoes.","7":"Light seasoning.","8":"Served on the side for scooping.","4":"Only cooking fat; skipped, food sticks and browns poorly, so use an oil or substitute."},"sub":{"4":{"x":"2 tbsp olive oil","d":"Fruitier and a little less rich than butter.","k":["olive oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Cook the tomatoes down until the liquid mostly evaporates before adding eggs. A watery base makes the eggs scrambled in tomato soup.","s":1},{"t":"Stir the eggs in gently and take the pan off the heat while they are still glossy; residual heat finishes them.","s":4},{"t":"Serve in the pan right away, because the eggs keep cooking and tighten as they sit.","s":5}],"rl":"PVVVFXZZG"},"3033":{"lv":"ERRRREEREERO","w":{"2":"Warm spice that flavors the meat; skip it and use more paprika.","3":"Color and mild sweetness on the meat.","4":"Seasons the meat, which is only salted by this step.","7":"Deepens the tomato sauce; skip it and simmer the sauce longer.","10":"Sharpens the yogurt; skip and the yogurt is milder.","11":"Classic grilled tomato side.","1":"Carries flavor in the marinade or dressing; skipped, the dish is drier but intact."},"sub":{"1":{"x":"2 tbsp canola oil","d":"Neutral oil is fine for tossing the steak.","k":["canola oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Slice the steak very thin across the grain, and sear in small batches. A crowded pan steams the beef and turns it gray and chewy.","s":3},{"t":"Pour the sizzling brown butter over the dish at the table. The nutty smell and the hot butter soaking into the pita are what make iskender iskender.","s":5},{"t":"Warm the pita squares but leave them a bit crisp underneath the sauce; they soak and soften quickly.","s":1}],"rl":"PFXXZBVXFGXG"},"3035":{"lv":"EERRRRREERO","w":{"2":"Sweet savory base for the filling.","3":"Aroma in the filling.","4":"Moist, fresh tomato flavor on and in the filling; skip and use canned tomatoes.","5":"Fresh herb note in the stuffing.","6":"Gives the baking sauce body and color; skip for a lighter sauce.","9":"Seasoning for the meat and sauce.","10":"Light background heat.","7":"Frying medium; skipped, the food cannot be fried."},"sub":{"7":{"x":"1/2 cup canola oil","d":"Any neutral oil is fine for frying the eggplant.","k":["canola oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Salt-soak the eggplants to draw out bitter juices and then pat dry. They also take up less oil when frying.","s":0},{"t":"Fry until golden and tender on the outside; they finish cooking in the oven, so do not fry until mushy.","s":1},{"t":"Make sure the beef is cooked through before stuffing, and bake until it is 160F (71C) in the center.","s":5}],"rl":"VPXXVXXFMZZ"},"3038":{"lv":"EEEEEROERERRE","w":{"5":"Moist, sweet note in the filling; skip and the filling is drier.","6":"Light seasoning for the filling.","8":"Gives the yogurt its garlic bite; skip for plain yogurt.","10":"Color and warm sweet flavor in the butter sauce.","11":"Heat in the butter; adjust to taste."},"tips":[{"t":"Roll the dough as thin as you can; thick dough turns doughy. Cut into small squares and keep the filling to a pea-size bit.","s":2},{"t":"Pinch the corners closed tightly so the filling stays inside and the manti hold together in the broth.","s":2},{"t":"Heat the paprika and pepper flakes gently in the butter and serve it still sizzling. Burnt paprika turns bitter, so remove from heat as soon as it bubbles.","s":5}],"rl":"SSMZPXZGXFXXM"},"53252":{"lv":"EEERE","sub":{"1":{"x":"3 tbsp olive oil","d":"Fruitier, still crisp the vermicelli.","k":["olive oil"]},"3":{"x":"2 tbsp olive oil","d":"Loses butter's richness and nutty smell.","k":["olive oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Rinse the rice until the water runs clear. Surface starch is what makes pilaf sticky and gummy.","s":0},{"t":"Brown the vermicelli to a rich golden brown but watch it; it darkens in seconds and bitter ones cannot be saved.","s":1},{"t":"Once the stock boils, cover tight, cook low, and let it rest 10 minutes off the heat before fluffing, so the grains steam fully and stay separate.","s":2}],"rl":"BFBFM","w":{"1":"Only cooking fat; skipped, food sticks and browns poorly, so use an oil or substitute.","3":"Adds flavor; skipped, oil is enough."}},"53253":{"lv":"EEEEREREORERR","w":{"4":"Warm background note in the stuffing; skip it with only a mild difference.","6":"Fresh herb in the stuffing.","8":"A dusting for color on the lamb.","9":"Squeeze of acid for the lamb and garnish.","11":"Cool crunch in the tzatziki; skip and use more yogurt and mint.","12":"Fresh flavor for the tzatziki; skip and use dill or parsley.","1":"Only cooking fat; skipped, food sticks and browns poorly, so use an oil or substitute."},"sub":{"1":{"x":"2 tbsp canola oil","d":"Less fruity; olive oil is the traditional flavor for imam bayildi.","k":["canola oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Score the eggplant flesh deeply and roast until very soft; the filling then sinks in and the pulp turns silky.","s":0},{"t":"Let the tomato filling cook down so it is jammy; a watery filling floods the baking dish.","s":1},{"t":"Season the lamb chops well and cook them medium (145F / 63C) then rest a few minutes. They are lean and dry out fast.","s":4}],"rl":"VFXXXVXPXAGGG"},"53260":{"lv":"EERREERR","w":{"3":"Adds sweetness and caramelization to the crust; skip and the lamb is more savory.","6":"Earthy warmth to go with the fennel; skip it and use more cinnamon.","7":"Savory aroma that works with the spices.","2":"Carries flavor in the marinade or dressing; skipped, the dish is drier but intact."},"sub":{"2":{"x":"4 tbsp canola oil","d":"Neutral, so you lose the fruity flavor, but it does the same job.","k":["canola oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Take the lamb out of the fridge an hour before roasting so it cooks evenly. Seal the wrap tightly so it braises in its own juices instead of drying out.","s":1},{"t":"Rest the lamb still wrapped for 30 minutes. The juices redistribute and the meat stays moist when carved.","s":3},{"t":"Toast the fennel seeds and crush them just before use; their aroma fades quickly once ground.","s":2}],"rl":"PAFWXXXX"},"53261":{"lv":"EREERR","w":{"1":"Savory aroma in the marinade; skipping makes the wings milder.","5":"Gives a sticky glaze and balances the lemon; skip for plain tangy wings.","4":"Carries flavor in the marinade or dressing; skipped, the dish is drier but intact."},"sub":{"4":{"x":"2 tbsp canola oil","d":"Neutral oil; less fruity.","k":["canola oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Dry the wings before marinating and bake on a rack so hot air crisps all sides.","s":2},{"t":"Add the honey only for the last 10 minutes. It burns if it sits in a 200C oven for the full cook.","s":2},{"t":"Cook wings to at least 165F (74C), though they are best well past that, until the skin is crisp and the meat pulls from the bone.","s":2}],"rl":"PXAXFW"},"53262":{"lv":"REEER","w":{"0":"Sweet moisture and mild fruit flavor; skip it and use more red pepper paste.","4":"Carries flavor in the marinade or dressing; skipped, the dish is drier but intact."},"sub":{"4":{"x":"3 tbsp canola oil","d":"Neutral oil for the mixture and brushing.","k":["canola oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Squeeze the chopped peppers dry; too much juice makes the mince wet and it falls off the skewer.","s":0},{"t":"Knead the lamb mixture a few minutes until sticky so it holds to the skewer, and chill it before shaping.","s":1},{"t":"Cook until the center reaches 160F (71C), turning often for color, and let the kofte rest a couple of minutes before serving.","s":2}],"rl":"VPXXF"},"53268":{"lv":"ERRRREERRRRO","w":{"1":"Warm earthy rub for the chicken.","2":"Color and mild sweetness on the skin.","4":"Thickens the sauce; only one is used in the sauce and the rest are for serving. Breadcrumbs also work.","7":"Savory base in the walnut sauce.","8":"Aroma in the sauce.","10":"Adds acid that balances the rich nuts.","11":"Fresh herb stirred into the sauce at the end.","3":"Roasts the chicken; skipped, the skin carries fat.","9":"Enriches the sauce; skipped, it is less creamy."},"sub":{"3":{"x":"2 tbsp canola oil","d":"Fine for roasting; less fruity.","k":["canola oil"]},"9":{"x":"3 tbsp whole milk or half-and-half","d":"Thinner and less rich; keep below a simmer.","k":["whole milk","half and half"]},"4":{"x":"1/2 cup fresh breadcrumbs","d":"Thickens the same way, slightly less nutty.","k":["breadcrumbs"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Toast the walnuts in a dry pan until fragrant and blend them to a coarse paste; they taste bitter if raw or burnt.","s":1},{"t":"Soak the pita in warm stock before blending. It thickens the sauce without any flour.","s":1},{"t":"Roast the thighs until the skin is crisp and they reach at least 165F (74C), though dark meat is best around 175F (79C).","s":0}],"rl":"PXXFTMFXXFAG"},"53254":{"lv":"EERRRRERRRROE","w":{"2":"Heat; skip it for a milder ezme.","3":"Raw bite and crunch; soak in cold water if too sharp.","4":"Pungent aroma; skip and use less.","5":"Fresh herb note.","7":"Adds depth and color to the base.","9":"Mild heat and fruity flavor; use chili flakes if you have none.","10":"Lemony tang; add extra lemon juice instead.","11":"Gives a dried herb aroma at the end.","8":"Adds sour-sweet depth; skipped, the ezme is brighter."},"sub":{"8":{"x":"2 tbsp lemon juice plus 1 tsp honey","d":"Gets sweet-sour balance but without the deep fruity flavor.","k":["lemon juice","honey"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Strain the chopped tomato and pepper well; excess liquid makes the ezme watery instead of a scoopable relish.","s":0},{"t":"Chop by hand or pulse briefly. A purée is soup, and ezme should keep distinct bits.","s":1},{"t":"Let it sit for 20 minutes so the flavors blend, and serve with bread for scooping.","s":2}],"rl":"VVXXXXXXAXAXF"},"53255":{"lv":"ERRRRERREERRO","w":{"1":"Sweet savory base for the chickpeas.","2":"Heat in the chickpeas.","3":"Warm fresh bite that matches the chili.","4":"Earthy note to the chickpeas.","6":"Makes the chickpeas saucy; canned tomatoes are fine.","7":"Brightens the chickpeas.","10":"Savory kick in the yogurt sauce.","11":"Crunch and nutty richness on top.","12":"Fresh herb finish.","0":"Only cooking fat; skipped, food sticks and browns poorly, so use an oil or substitute."},"sub":{"0":{"x":"4 tbsp canola oil","d":"Neutral oil for frying and brushing.","k":["canola oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Salt the eggplant slices and let them sit 10 minutes, then pat dry. They brown better and absorb less oil.","s":1},{"t":"Grill until the flesh is fully soft; undercooked eggplant is spongy and bitter.","s":1},{"t":"Keep the yogurt sauce cool and spoon it on at the last moment so it does not melt on the warm aubergine.","s":2}],"rl":"FXXXXBVAVGXGG"},"53266":{"lv":"ERREERRR","w":{"1":"Sweet savory base in the mix; skip and the patties are blander.","2":"Savory aroma in the mix.","5":"Citrusy, floral note alongside the cumin.","6":"Fresh herb freshness; use dried herbs if you have none.","0":"Frying medium; skipped, the food cannot be fried.","7":"Binds the patties; skipped, they are a bit looser."},"sub":{"0":{"x":"2 tbsp canola oil","d":"Fine for frying the onion and the patties.","k":["canola oil"]},"7":{"x":"2 tbsp flour plus 1 tbsp water","d":"Binds the patties but they are a bit denser.","k":["flour"],"wn":"Contains wheat/gluten"}},"tips":[{"t":"Pat the canned chickpeas dry and leave the mash a little chunky. A smooth paste turns pasty and the patties fall apart.","s":0},{"t":"Chill the shaped patties 15 minutes before frying so they hold together in the pan.","s":2},{"t":"Fry on medium heat; too hot browns the outside before the center warms through.","s":2}],"rl":"FXXBXXXT"},"53267":{"lv":"EREERRRR","w":{"4":"Sweet juicy bursts that go with the creamy cheese.","5":"Fresh cool herb that lifts the salad.","6":"Tangy and creamy; skip it for a vegan salad or use feta.","7":"Adds brightness; skip it and the salad tastes flat.","1":"Carries flavor in the marinade or dressing; skipped, the dish is drier but intact.","3":"Liquid that hydrates the couscous; skipped, it is raw."},"sub":{"1":{"x":"3 tbsp canola oil","d":"Neutral oil; less flavor.","k":["canola oil"]},"3":{"x":"225 ml hot water plus 1/2 tsp salt","d":"Plainer; the couscous is less savory but still works.","k":["water","salt"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Pour boiling stock over the couscous, cover tight and leave 5 minutes, then fork it. Stirring early makes it gummy.","s":1},{"t":"Brush the aubergine lightly with oil; it soaks up oil like a sponge and turns greasy.","s":0},{"t":"Dress the salad just before serving so the mint stays green and the couscous does not go soggy.","s":2}],"rl":"VFBMVXGA"},"53273":{"lv":"EERRRRROORO","w":{"2":"Juicy burst and sweetness to balance the vinegar.","3":"Crunchy base that soaks up the dressing.","5":"Fresh note in the dressing.","6":"Sharp-sweet bite in the dressing.","7":"A crisp-raw topping.","8":"Heat in the dressing; skip for a milder dish.","9":"Creamy and salty finish.","10":"Peppery garnish.","1":"Only cooking fat; skipped, food sticks and browns poorly, so use an oil or substitute.","4":"Adds brightness; skipped, the dish is flatter but still works."},"sub":{"1":{"x":"3 tbsp canola oil","d":"Neutral oil; works for roasting and dressing.","k":["canola oil"]},"4":{"x":"3 tbsp red wine vinegar plus 1 tsp honey","d":"Less syrupy and sweet, but still tangy.","k":["red wine vinegar","honey"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Roast the eggplant until fully browned and soft; pale slices are rubbery and taste bitter.","s":0},{"t":"Dress the salad right before serving; the flatbread goes soft once the vinegar hits it.","s":2},{"t":"Let the shallot sit in the vinegar a few minutes to take off its sharpness.","s":1}],"rl":"VFVBAXXGXGG"},"53274":{"lv":"EEEERRROO","w":{"4":"Savory bite in the dressing.","5":"Acid that balances the tahini; skip it and the dressing is heavy.","6":"Fresh herb in the dressing.","7":"Another herb; skip it.","8":"Another herb; skip it.","1":"Only cooking fat; skipped, food sticks and browns poorly, so use an oil or substitute."},"sub":{"1":{"x":"2 tbsp canola oil","d":"Neutral oil for brushing.","k":["canola oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Cut the aubergine into thick slices and salt them for 10 minutes; pat dry, as that removes bitterness and lets them brown rather than steam.","s":0},{"t":"Griddle on medium-high until grill marks form and the flesh is soft all through. Underdone eggplant is tough.","s":1},{"t":"Thin the tahini yogurt with a little cold water if it is stiff; it should be spoonable.","s":2}],"rl":"VFFFXAXGG"},"53275":{"lv":"EERRORRR","w":{"2":"Sweet raw bite in the dressing.","3":"Fresh mild onion crunch.","4":"Mild herb garnish.","1":"Only cooking fat; skipped, food sticks and browns poorly, so use an oil or substitute.","5":"Adds brightness; skipped, the dish is flatter but still works.","6":"Carries flavor in the marinade or dressing; skipped, the dish is drier but intact.","7":"Balances sweetness; skipped, the dish is a bit sharper but still fine."},"sub":{"1":{"x":"1 tbsp canola oil","d":"Fine for roasting.","k":["canola oil"]},"5":{"x":"5 tbsp apple cider vinegar","d":"Fruitier, less sweet than sherry vinegar.","k":["apple cider vinegar"]},"6":{"x":"2 tbsp canola oil","d":"Neutral; loses the fruitiness.","k":["canola oil"]},"7":{"x":"2 tbsp maple syrup","d":"Gives a slightly different sweetness but still balances the vinegar.","k":["maple syrup"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Cut the sweet potatoes into even chunks and spread them in one layer so they roast and brown rather than steam.","s":0},{"t":"Dress the potatoes while still just warm. They soak up the vinegar dressing better than cold ones.","s":2},{"t":"Toss gently with your hands so the soft potato chunks do not break up.","s":2}],"rl":"BFXXGAFW"},"53278":{"lv":"EERERRRRR","w":{"2":"Crumbed crust that crisps under the grill; use panko or crushed crackers.","4":"Crunch and richness in the salad.","5":"Fresh herb salad base.","6":"Sweet juicy bite.","7":"Brightens the salad.","8":"Light dressing for the salad.","1":"Only cooking fat; skipped, food sticks and browns poorly, so use an oil or substitute."},"sub":{"1":{"x":"2 tbsp canola oil","d":"Neutral oil for brushing the eggplant.","k":["canola oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Brush the eggplant sparingly with oil. It acts like a sponge and turns greasy if you add too much.","s":0},{"t":"Press the hummus side into the crumbs firmly so the crust sticks and crisps under the grill.","s":1},{"t":"Dress the walnut salad at the last minute so the parsley stays bright.","s":2}],"rl":"VFTPGVVAF"},"3036":{"lv":"EEEEERERRRRRO","w":{"7":"Sweet moist base for the filling.","8":"Fresh sweetness in the filling.","9":"Juice and tang in the filling.","10":"Rich color and deep tomato flavor.","11":"Warm, mild peppery color.","12":"Gives the crust a shine and color; skip with little change.","5":"Carries flavor in the marinade or dressing; skipped, the dish is drier but intact."},"sub":{"5":{"x":"1 tbsp canola oil","d":"Neutral oil; the dough is slightly less fragrant.","k":["canola oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Let the dough rise until doubled in a warm place; underproofed dough bakes dense and gummy.","s":0},{"t":"Preheat the baking sheet in the oven so the pide bottom crisps as soon as it lands on it.","s":2},{"t":"Cook the ground beef filling until it is 160F (71C) in the center; spread it thin so it cooks through in the short bake.","s":5}],"rl":"SLWZMFPVVVXXG"},"3037":{"lv":"EEEEEERRO","w":{"6":"Sweet mild base; skip and the filling is less rounded.","8":"Light seasoning; feta is salty already.","3":"Fat in the dough or batter; skipped, the result is dry, tough or less tender.","7":"Brushes the bread for richness; skipped, it is drier."},"sub":{"3":{"x":"2 tbsp canola oil","d":"Neutral oil for the dough.","k":["canola oil"]},"7":{"x":"3 tbsp olive oil","d":"Fruitier, less rich than butter.","k":["olive oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Squeeze the wilted spinach very dry; a wet filling makes the dough soggy and the gozleme tear.","s":1},{"t":"Roll the dough thin enough to see your hand through it, then seal the edges well so the filling stays in.","s":2},{"t":"Cook in a hot dry pan and press as it cooks; blisters and brown spots are the sign it is done.","s":4}],"rl":"SZMFVPXFZ"},"53257":{"lv":"ERREROERROR","w":{"1":"Moisture and savory flavor in the burger; skip and they are drier.","2":"Savory aroma; a head of garlic is a lot, so use a few cloves.","4":"Fresh herb freshness in the mix.","5":"Heat in the mix; skip for a milder burger.","7":"Fresh topping; skip it.","8":"Crunch and color for serving.","9":"Sharp bite for serving.","10":"Cooling sauce for serving."},"tips":[{"t":"Mix the lamb well with your hands; the mixture should turn sticky so it holds together under the grill.","s":0},{"t":"Make a small dent in the center of each patty so it does not puff up, and cook until it reaches 160F (71C).","s":2},{"t":"Freeze them uncooked on a tray, then bag them; they cook from frozen with a little more time.","s":1}],"rl":"PXXXXXBGGGG"},"53258":{"lv":"ERERRROERRE","w":{"3":"Balances the vinegar in the slaw.","5":"Sweet crunch in the slaw.","6":"Mild fresh onion; skip.","8":"Sweet heat and color; use hot sauce for a similar bite.","9":"Adds creaminess and heat in the sauce; skip and use yogurt.","1":"Carries flavor in the marinade or dressing; skipped, the dish is drier but intact.","4":"Adds brightness; skipped, the dish is flatter but still works."},"sub":{"1":{"x":"2 tsp canola oil","d":"Fine for rubbing the lamb.","k":["canola oil"]},"4":{"x":"3 tbsp apple cider vinegar","d":"Slightly fruitier; same tang.","k":["apple cider vinegar"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Get the pan very hot and cook the lamb steaks 3-4 minutes a side for medium (145F / 63C), then rest before slicing across the grain.","s":0},{"t":"Toss the slaw in the sweetened vinegar and let it sit while the lamb cooks; it softens the cabbage slightly.","s":1},{"t":"Warm the pitas briefly so they fold without cracking.","s":2}],"rl":"PFXWAVVVXFB"},"53263":{"lv":"RREREERRO","w":{"0":"Buttery crunch against the soft rice; use almonds or skip.","3":"Warm sweet spice note; skip it and the pilau is plainer.","7":"Sweet chewy bites that balance the lamb; skip and add a pinch of sugar.","8":"Fresh herb finish.","1":"Fries the onion; skipped, the lamb fat carries it.","6":"Adds savory depth to a small amount of liquid; skipped, flavor is just muted."},"sub":{"1":{"x":"1 tbsp canola oil","d":"Fine for frying the onion.","k":["canola oil"]},"6":{"x":"1 beef or chicken stock cube in 500 ml hot water","d":"Milder than lamb stock but still savory.","k":["stock cube"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Cut the lamb small and dry it before browning; large pieces cannot get tender in a 12-minute simmer.","s":1},{"t":"Rinse the rice first and then stir it in the fat for a minute so the grains stay separate.","s":1},{"t":"Keep the lid on and do not stir after adding the stock; rest 5 minutes off the heat so the rice steams through.","s":2}],"rl":"GFXXPBMVG"},"53264":{"lv":"ERRREROO","w":{"2":"Sweet anise notes; skip and add extra cumin.","3":"Warm earthy note.","5":"Savory aroma; a small amount is enough in a marinade.","7":"A dipping sauce for serving; skip it.","1":"Carries flavor in the marinade or dressing; skipped, the dish is drier but intact.","6":"Tiny amount; skipped, there is little noticeable effect."},"sub":{"1":{"x":"2 tbsp canola oil","d":"Neutral oil for marinade and frying.","k":["canola oil"]},"6":{"x":"1 tsp lemon juice","d":"A brighter acid but still works in the marinade.","k":["lemon juice"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Cut the thighs into even 3 cm pieces so they cook through at the same time and reach 165F (74C) without drying out.","s":0},{"t":"Do not crowd the pan; sear in batches so the chicken browns instead of steaming.","s":3},{"t":"Use smoked paprika, not sweet; it gives the barbecue flavor in a frying pan.","s":1}],"rl":"PFXXXXAG"},"53270":{"lv":"EERREERRO","w":{"2":"Herbal aroma in the yogurt coating.","3":"Fresh cool herb in the yogurt.","6":"Crisp, cool crunch in the wrap.","7":"Sharp raw bite in the wrap.","8":"Fresh acid to finish."},"tips":[{"t":"Do not overcook the lamb; slice thickly and let the yogurt coating grill until it forms a golden crust.","s":1},{"t":"Cook to 145F (63C) and rest a couple of minutes, since lamb leg is lean.","s":0},{"t":"Warm the pitas so they fold without splitting.","s":2}],"rl":"PXXXGBVVA"},"53277":{"lv":"ERRRREORERRO","w":{"1":"Sweet base shared by the meatballs and sauce.","2":"Savory aroma.","3":"Earthy spice for lamb.","4":"Citrusy spice note.","6":"A little sugar takes the edge off the tomatoes.","7":"Fresh flavor in the meatballs.","9":"Sweet chew that balances the lamb; skip it with little change.","11":"To serve; skip it.","0":"Frying medium; skipped, the food cannot be fried.","10":"Binds the meatballs; skipped, they are looser."},"sub":{"0":{"x":"2 tbsp canola oil","d":"Fine for frying.","k":["canola oil"]},"10":{"x":"50 g (3/4 cup) crushed crackers or rolled oats","d":"Binds just as well, a little less tender.","k":["crackers","rolled oats"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Cool the onion mixture before mixing it into the lamb; warm onions start to cook the meat and make the meatballs tough.","s":1},{"t":"Wet your hands to shape small meatballs and cook them to 160F (71C) in the center.","s":2},{"t":"Simmer the tomato sauce gently with the meatballs for a few minutes so they take up flavor.","s":2}],"rl":"FXXXXVWXPVTG"},"3486":{"lv":"EERRRRERO","w":{"2":"Richness and flavor; skip it and use more oil.","3":"Sweet base.","4":"Fresh mild flavor.","5":"Adds color and tomato depth when fried.","7":"Seasons the bulgur.","8":"Light seasoning.","1":"Only cooking fat; skipped, food sticks and browns poorly, so use an oil or substitute."},"sub":{"1":{"x":"2 tbsp canola oil","d":"Neutral oil; less fruity.","k":["canola oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Fry the tomato paste until it darkens. It loses its raw taste and gives the pilaf its red color.","s":2},{"t":"Stir the bulgur in the oil before adding liquid so each grain is coated and stays separate.","s":3},{"t":"Rest the pot covered off the heat for 10 minutes; the last steam finishes the grain, and fork it rather than stirring.","s":5}],"rl":"BFFXVXMZZ"},"3489":{"lv":"EERRRRRRR","w":{"2":"Juicy sweetness that balances the beans.","3":"Fresh herb freshness.","6":"Lemony tang and the traditional piyaz flavor; use a little lemon zest.","7":"Traditional protein topping; skip it.","8":"Seasons the dressing.","4":"Carries flavor in the marinade or dressing; skipped, the dish is drier but intact.","5":"Adds brightness; skipped, the dish is flatter but still works."},"sub":{"4":{"x":"3 tbsp canola oil","d":"Neutral, so the dressing is less fruity.","k":["canola oil"]},"5":{"x":"3 tbsp lemon juice","d":"Brighter and lighter; use a bit less if sharp.","k":["lemon juice"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Rub the sliced onion with sumac and salt to take away its harshness. It also turns the onion a pretty pink.","s":0},{"t":"Rinse the beans well and drain them. Canned bean liquid dilutes the dressing.","s":2},{"t":"Let the salad sit a few minutes before serving so the beans soak up the dressing.","s":3}],"rl":"BXVXFAXGZ"},"3491":{"lv":"EERRRRRR","w":{"2":"Sweetness and color; skip it with a milder flavor.","3":"Absorbs the braising liquid and gives a light body; skip and the dish is looser.","4":"Balances the lemon and brings out the leek sweetness.","5":"Sharp acid at the end brightens the oily leeks.","6":"Fresh herb finish traditional for zeytinyagli.","7":"Seasons the braise; add to taste at the end."},"tips":[{"t":"Zeytinyagli means olive-oil dishes, so use a generous amount of good olive oil. It carries the flavor and the leeks braise gently in it.","s":0},{"t":"Rinse the leeks well between the layers to remove grit, and keep the rounds upright so they hold together.","s":1},{"t":"Serve cool or at room temperature; chilled braised leeks taste sweeter and the lemon stays fresh.","s":5}],"rl":"VFVBWAGZ"},"52977":{"lv":"EERRERRROOEER","w":{"2":"Sweetness and color; soften it into the soup.","3":"Deepens the color and adds tang; skip it and the soup is paler.","5":"Warm color and mild heat.","6":"Traditional herb note; skip for a plainer soup.","7":"Earthy aroma that fits the cumin.","8":"A pinch of pepper heat only.","9":"A pinch of heat; add at the table if wanted.","12":"Seasons the lentils; add at the end to taste."},"tips":[{"t":"Briefly fry the tomato paste and the ground spices in the oil for about 10 seconds. It blooms their aroma and prevents a raw taste.","s":2},{"t":"Blend the soup until smooth and velvety, then thin with hot water if it gets too thick; lentil soups thicken as they sit.","s":5},{"t":"Finish each bowl with a squeeze of lemon and a drizzle of oil or paprika butter. Acid wakes up the lentils.","s":6}],"rl":"PXVXXXXXZXMMZ"},"52978":{"lv":"EEERRO","w":{"3":"Fresh crunch that cuts the buttery potato.","4":"Sweet color and crunch for topping.","5":"A pinch of heat."},"tips":[{"t":"Bake the potatoes until the skin is crisp and the inside very soft, then mash the flesh with butter and cheese while hot so the cheese melts.","s":0},{"t":"Use a waxy-fluffy potato like Russet; a firm one will not mash creamy.","s":0},{"t":"Mash the filling right inside the skin so the shell stays crisp; load toppings in rows on top.","s":3}],"rl":"BFFGGG"},"53256":{"lv":"ERRRER","w":{"1":"Adds a slight tang that lifts the yogurt.","2":"Silky richness to finish and soften the mint.","3":"Traditional cooling herb note.","5":"Sharp savory bite."},"tips":[{"t":"Strain the yogurt for several hours; thick yogurt keeps the cacik creamy instead of watery when the cucumber releases liquid.","s":0},{"t":"Salt and squeeze the grated cucumber dry, then stir it in at the end.","s":2},{"t":"Soak the dried mint in the lemon juice and oil first so it rehydrates and releases flavor.","s":1}],"rl":"BAFXVX"},"53259":{"lv":"ERRER","w":{"1":"Acid that brightens the smoky aubergine.","2":"Savory bite; skip for a milder purée.","4":"Fresh herb that adds a grassy note."},"tips":[{"t":"Char the aubergine until the skin blisters and collapses; the smoky flavor is the point of this dish.","s":0},{"t":"Let the cooked flesh drain in a sieve for a few minutes to remove bitter juices before mashing.","s":1},{"t":"Beat the lemon juice in before the yogurt so the aubergine does not discolor.","s":2}],"rl":"VAXFX"},"53269":{"lv":"EERRE","w":{"2":"Savory sharpness; use less if raw garlic is too strong.","3":"Makes it creamier and lighter; skip and add a bit more tahini and liquid."},"tips":[{"t":"Add the chickpea liquid a spoonful at a time; the hummus should be smooth and creamy but still hold a peak.","s":1},{"t":"Blend longer than you think, a full 3-4 minutes, for a silky texture.","s":0},{"t":"Taste and add lemon juice at the end; its acidity fades over time.","s":2}],"rl":"BFXFA"},"53272":{"lv":"EEEEE","sub":{"0":{"x":"250 g more strong white bread flour (500 g in total)","d":"Lighter, softer flatbread with less nutty flavor.","k":["bread flour"]},"4":{"x":"2 tbsp canola oil","d":"Neutral oil; works fine.","k":["canola oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Let the dough rise until doubled; it should feel soft and springy, not stiff.","s":0},{"t":"Roll the dough thin and cook on a very hot dry griddle until it puffs and blisters. A cool pan makes dry, leathery bread.","s":2},{"t":"Wrap the cooked flatbreads in a cloth so the steam keeps them soft.","s":2}],"rl":"BBLWF","w":{"0":"Flour is the dough structure; skipped, there is no flatbread.","4":"Fat in the dough or batter; skipped, the result is dry, tough or less tender."}},"53265":{"lv":"RRRRRRE","w":{"0":"Aromatic savory marinade note; skip and the lamb is milder.","1":"Fresh heat that pairs with the chili.","2":"Acid that tenderizes lightly and brightens the lamb.","4":"Heat; skip for a mild marinade.","5":"Earthy warmth in the marinade.","3":"Carries flavor in the marinade or dressing; skipped, the dish is drier but intact."},"sub":{"3":{"x":"1 tbsp canola oil","d":"Neutral oil; carries the spices.","k":["canola oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Blend the marinade smooth so it clings to the chops; chunks of garlic and ginger burn on the grill.","s":0},{"t":"Pat the chops lightly after marinating, then grill over hot coals for 3 minutes a side for medium rare. For safety, rest to at least 145F (63C).","s":2},{"t":"Rest the chops for 3-5 minutes so the juices stay in the meat.","s":2}],"rl":"XXAFXXP"},"53271":{"lv":"EREEREERR","w":{"1":"Warm spice; skip it with little difference.","4":"Sweetness and moisture; the rest is for drizzling on top.","7":"Sweet chewy pockets of fruit.","8":"Crunchy topping and nutty flavor.","0":"Flour and leavening for the cake; skipped, it does not rise."},"sub":{"0":{"x":"225 g plain flour plus 3 tsp baking powder and 1/4 tsp salt","d":"Same rise as self-raising flour; mix well.","k":["flour","baking powder"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Use very ripe, spotty bananas; they give the cake moisture and sweetness, and underripe ones taste starchy.","s":1},{"t":"Bake until a skewer comes out clean; tent with foil if the top browns too fast.","s":2},{"t":"Cool completely before drizzling the honey so it does not sink into the cake and make it sticky.","s":3}],"rl":"SXFWWSVVG"},"53276":{"lv":"ERRREEROO","w":{"1":"Lightens the mascarpone and adds tang.","2":"Sweetens the cream; skip it and add less.","3":"Floral perfume that is traditional with Turkish delight.","6":"Chewy sweet bites and floral flavor; skip it and use extra apricot.","7":"Crunch on top.","8":"Garnish."},"tips":[{"t":"Whisk the mascarpone only until it thickens; overwhisking turns it grainy and split.","s":0},{"t":"Fold in the meringue at the last minute so it stays crisp; prolonged contact makes it soggy.","s":1},{"t":"Use ripe apricots, because underripe fruit is sharp and will clash with the sweet cream.","s":1}],"rl":"FFWXBVGGG"},"53279":{"lv":"ERERRRRERRREEO","w":{"1":"Keeps the syrup from crystallizing and adds brightness.","3":"Richness and sweetness; walnuts alone also work.","4":"Crunch and a mild sweet flavor.","5":"Warm spice, a signature baklava flavor.","6":"Floral citrus-like aroma; skip and use a bit more cinnamon.","8":"Fresh citrus brightness in the ricotta.","9":"Fresh citrus brightness in the ricotta.","10":"Rounded sweet aroma in the filling.","13":"Green finishing touch on top."},"tips":[{"t":"Pour cool syrup over hot baklava, or hot over cool. The temperature contrast makes the pastry soak up syrup and stay crisp instead of sogginess.","s":3},{"t":"Keep the filo covered with a damp towel while you work and brush every layer with butter.","s":2},{"t":"Cut the baklava before baking so the layers do not shatter, and let it rest several hours so the syrup sinks in.","s":3}],"rl":"WABBBXXFXXXFSG"},"53311":{"lv":"ERRERERERRRERRRO","w":{"1":"Flavors the stock; discard it after.","2":"Light herbal note in the stock.","6":"Sweetness and body; skip it and the soup is more sour.","8":"Sweet note; skip it with little change.","9":"Gives color and acidity to the soup.","10":"Adds a sweet-sour depth, which many cooks use in borscht.","12":"Protein and body; skip it and the soup is lighter.","13":"Cooling tang finish for the earthy broth.","14":"Fresh herb finish, classic with beet soup.","15":"Served for dipping.","4":"Sautes vegetables; skipped, the soup still works."},"sub":{"4":{"x":"2 tbsp canola oil","d":"Neutral oil; it works the same for sauteing.","k":["canola oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Simmer the beef stock at a bare tremble for 90 minutes and skim off the foam. Boiling clouds the broth and makes the beef stringy.","s":0},{"t":"Add a splash of vinegar or lemon with the beetroot to keep its color bright red.","s":2},{"t":"Rest the soup several hours or overnight; borscht tastes better the next day.","s":3}],"rl":"PXXBFXVVVAWVBGGG"},"53315":{"lv":"ERRRERRRRE","w":{"2":"Richness at the end; skip with little change.","3":"Adds sweetness and softness to the braise.","6":"Gentle heat to cut the sweetness.","7":"Piney aroma that works with the vinegar.","8":"Adds tart fruit and helps soften the cabbage.","1":"Cooks the onion; skipped, the dish still works.","4":"Acid for the braised cabbage and its color; skipped, the cabbage tastes flat.","5":"Balances sweetness; skipped, the dish is a bit sharper but still fine."},"sub":{"1":{"x":"1 tbsp canola oil","d":"Neutral oil for cooking the onion.","k":["canola oil"]},"4":{"x":"125 ml apple cider vinegar","d":"Fruitier and a little gentler than red wine vinegar.","k":["apple cider vinegar"]},"5":{"x":"140 g (3/4 cup) granulated sugar","d":"Lacks the molasses note but sweetens the same.","k":["sugar"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Braise covered on low so the cabbage turns silky and does not scorch; add water a splash at a time if it sticks.","s":1},{"t":"Add the kabanos late; they are already cured and cooked, so they only need to warm and flavor the cabbage.","s":2},{"t":"Taste at the end for the sweet-sour balance and add a splash of vinegar if too sweet.","s":2}],"rl":"VFFXAWXXVP"},"53312":{"lv":"ERRRRERRRR","w":{"1":"Zest and juice give brightness; skip and the slaw is heavy.","3":"Tangy bite and texture in the dressing.","4":"Savory seasoning with a celery flavor; use a pinch of salt and some celery seed.","6":"Color and sweet crunch; use white cabbage if missing.","7":"Sweetness and crunch.","8":"Sharp bite; skip or soak in water.","9":"Fresh crunch and a savory note.","2":"Adds brightness; skipped, the dish is flatter but still works."},"sub":{"2":{"x":"2 tbsp white wine vinegar","d":"Slightly sharper; cuts the mayo similarly.","k":["white wine vinegar"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Shred the cabbage very fine and let the slaw rest in the fridge an hour; the salt softens it into the dressing.","s":1},{"t":"Season the dressing generously before adding the cabbage; the vegetables dilute it as they release water.","s":0},{"t":"If the slaw gets watery after a day, drain it and add a spoonful of fresh mayo.","s":1}],"rl":"FAAXZVVVXV"},"53313":{"lv":"ERRROEEERRRO","w":{"1":"Cool tang to go with the earthy beetroot.","2":"Fresh herb note in the yogurt.","3":"Peppery green side.","4":"Fresh salad bite.","8":"Savory note in the batter.","9":"Gives the traditional earthy seed flavor.","10":"Warm spice.","11":"Fresh citrus aroma.","0":"Frying medium; skipped, the food cannot be fried."},"sub":{"0":{"x":"1 tbsp canola oil","d":"Neutral oil for frying.","k":["canola oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Squeeze grated beetroot in a towel to remove its juice; otherwise the latkes steam and fall apart.","s":0},{"t":"Fry on moderate heat and flatten the mix; crisp edges form before the beet center cooks.","s":1},{"t":"Finish in the oven so the centers cook through without burning the outside.","s":1}],"rl":"FGGGGVTTXXXX"},"53314":{"lv":"RRERERERRRRR","w":{"1":"Sweet filling base.","3":"Softens in the filling for body.","7":"Used for deep frying the shallots.","8":"Crispy shallot topping for crunch.","9":"Used to roll the dough and prevent sticking.","10":"Rich finish.","11":"Cool tang; skip it.","0":"Cooks the filling; skipped, the dish still works.","5":"Adds tenderness; skipped, the dough is a bit firmer."},"sub":{"0":{"x":"1 tbsp canola oil","d":"Neutral oil for the filling.","k":["canola oil"]},"5":{"x":"1/2 tbsp melted butter","d":"Slightly richer dough.","k":["butter"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Rest the dough 30 minutes under a bowl so the gluten relaxes and it rolls thin without springing back.","s":3},{"t":"Cool the filling completely before filling the dough; warm filling softens the dough and makes sealing harder.","s":6},{"t":"Seal the edges firmly, leaving no air pockets, and boil gently until they float, then 1-2 minutes more.","s":7}],"rl":"FXVVSFSFGGFG"},"53078":{"lv":"ERREERO","w":{"5":"Adds protein and creamy body to the soup; skip it for a thinner beet and potato soup.","6":"Fresh herb garnish.","1":"Sautes vegetables; skipped, the soup still works.","2":"Adds savory depth to a small amount of liquid; skipped, flavor is just muted."},"sub":{"1":{"x":"4 tbsp canola oil","d":"Neutral oil; adds richness the same way.","k":["canola oil"]},"2":{"x":"1 tsp bouillon powder","d":"Same seasoning role as the stock cube.","k":["bouillon"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Cut beetroot small so it cooks in the short time; large pieces stay hard.","s":0},{"t":"Add the beans only at the end so they stay whole and do not turn mushy.","s":2},{"t":"A splash of vinegar or lemon at the end brightens the beet flavor and keeps the color vivid.","s":2}],"rl":"VFXMBBG"},"53316":{"lv":"EREEROERROO","w":{"4":"Sweetens the batter and softens the earthy beetroot; skip it and the pancakes taste more vegetal, so add a spoon of sugar.","5":"Rounds out sweetness; with beetroot and maple dominating, you will barely miss it.","8":"Cooked into the compote topping; skip and serve with yogurt and jam only.","9":"Sweetens and thickens the berry compote; replace with 1 tbsp sugar if you skip it.","10":"Cool tangy topping against the sweet, earthy pancakes; sour cream or any yogurt works.","1":"Thins the batter; skipped, water works.","7":"Cooks the pancakes; skipped, oil can do it."},"sub":{"1":{"x":"50 ml water","d":"Batter is a touch less rich; the beetroot supplies most of the flavor so the difference is small.","k":["water"]},"7":{"x":"1 tbsp neutral oil for the pan","d":"Loses buttery flavor and foamy browning but still cooks fine.","k":["vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Blend the beetroot with the milk until completely smooth, then rest the batter 5 minutes. Any fibrous bits stay raw and gritty in a thin pancake.","s":0},{"t":"Keep the heat at medium-low: beetroot and maple sugars scorch fast. Flip only when bubbles pop across the surface and the rim looks matte, and wipe out the pan if the butter turns brown.","s":1},{"t":"Simmer the berries until they burst and the juices coat a spoon, then mash a few against the pan to thicken the compote without cornstarch.","s":2}],"rl":"VMSLWXSFGGG"},"53063":{"lv":"EERRERRROO","w":{"2":"Cool, crisp contrast to the hot steak and egg; skip if you prefer a purely meaty sandwich.","3":"Adds juiciness and acidity against the rich ham, bacon and cheese; skip and add a pickle or mustard instead.","5":"Melts over the hot steak and holds the layers together; sliced provolone or mild cheddar also works.","6":"Smoky, salty crunch that is part of a classic chivito; skipping it makes the sandwich plainer.","7":"Fried egg is a classic chivito layer and adds richness; skipping makes it drier.","8":"Not used in the method; add a few fried or raw rings for sharpness, or leave it out.","9":"Seasoning only; salt the steak while it browns."},"sub":{"5":{"x":"100 g sliced provolone or mild cheddar","d":"Slightly sharper, still melts well over the hot steak.","k":["cheddar","provolone"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Use a tender cut (sirloin or tenderloin) pounded to about 5 mm instead of brisket, which needs hours of braising. Sear 1 to 2 minutes per side on a very hot griddle so it stays tender.","s":0},{"t":"Fry the egg last in the bacon fat with a lid on for a minute so the whites set while the yolk stays soft; a runny yolk sauces the sandwich.","s":1},{"t":"Put the cheese directly on the hot steak so it melts, and add the cold lettuce and tomato last so they stay crisp. Toast the cut sides of the bread so the juices do not soak through.","s":2}],"rl":"PBGGPFPPXZ"},"53317":{"lv":"EEEERRERERROOROROO","w":{"4":"Gives the filling moisture and mild acidity; skip and the filling is drier, so add a spoon of tomato paste.","5":"Savory backbone in the meat filling; skipping makes it plainer.","7":"Fresh onion sweetness in the filling; skip and it is a little flatter.","9":"Herbal note in the dough and filling; omit for a plainer flavor.","10":"Adds color and mild warmth to the filling; skip and it is milder.","11":"Pinch of heat only; skip for a family-friendly filling.","12":"Minor fresh note; the oregano and green onion already carry the herbs.","13":"Seasons the beef; unsalted filling tastes flat, so taste and adjust before filling.","14":"Minor background heat; skip it.","15":"Chopped hard-boiled eggs add richness and bind the filling, classic in empanadas; skip for a plainer filling.","16":"Only for gloss and golden color; beaten egg or milk works, or leave them pale.","17":"Garnish/dipping sauce; skip or use lime juice and salt.","0":"Fat in the dough or batter; skipped, the result is dry, tough or less tender."},"sub":{"0":{"x":"60 g cold butter","d":"Richer, buttery flavor and slightly less flaky than lard; shortening also works.","k":["butter"],"wn":"Contains dairy"}},"tips":[{"t":"Rest the dough covered for 30 minutes after kneading so the gluten relaxes and it rolls paper-thin without snapping back. Lard gives the short, tender bite.","s":0},{"t":"Cook the filling until nearly dry and cool it completely before filling. Warm or wet filling steams the dough from inside and bursts the seams.","s":1},{"t":"Do not overfill: 2 to 4 teaspoons is plenty. Press the edges flat, then fork or twist-crimp, and chill filled empanadas 10 minutes so they hold their shape in the oven.","s":2}],"rl":"FMZSVXXXPXXXGZZTGG"},"53318":{"lv":"EEEEEEERO","w":{"7":"Seasons the batter; without it the cake tastes flat even with salty ham and cheese.","8":"Light herbal accent on top; skip it.","3":"Fat in the dough or batter; skipped, the result is dry, tough or less tender."},"sub":{"3":{"x":"100 g (about 7 tbsp) melted butter, cooled","d":"Richer buttery flavor; the crumb is slightly firmer and it browns faster.","k":["butter"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Line the pan and set it on a baking sheet: the batter is very thin and leaks from springform seams. Grease the parchment so the cheesy edges release.","s":0},{"t":"Scatter the ham and cheese in an even layer between the two halves of batter. Piled in a clump they sink to the bottom and leave the top plain.","s":3},{"t":"Bake until a skewer in the center comes out clean, not just until the top is golden. Wait a few minutes before unmolding so the cheese sets and the slice holds together.","s":4}],"rl":"FPSFMSLZG"},"53319":{"lv":"EEREEEEEOOREORRRRROO","w":{"8":"Only for the glossy brown crust; brushing with milk works too.","9":"Decorative crunch on the buns; skip it.","10":"Slowly caramelized onions add sweetness and moisture to the sandwich; skip for a leaner chivito.","12":"Used for rubbing the steaks; a pinch of garlic powder or skipping is fine.","13":"Salty, smoky crunch that is part of a classic chivito.","14":"Adds salty savoriness and the traditional layering.","15":"Melts over the steak and ties the layers together; provolone also works.","16":"Fresh juicy acidity against the rich meat; skip and add pickles.","17":"Cool crunch to balance the hot meats.","18":"Tangy condiment; skip or use mayo.","19":"Herby garlic sauce for serving; skip and use mustard.","2":"Color and flavor of the bread; skipped, the dough is paler."},"sub":{"2":{"x":"1 tbsp honey (or brown sugar)","d":"Lighter flavor and color than molasses; the dough still browns and softens fine.","k":["honey","brown sugar"],"wn":"Honey: not for children under 1 year"}},"tips":[{"t":"This is a sticky, no-knead dough: rest it overnight in the fridge for flavor and flour your hands generously rather than working extra flour into the dough, which would make the buns dense.","s":1},{"t":"Brush the buns with egg wash twice, 5 minutes apart, for a deep glossy crust. They are done when golden and sound hollow when tapped on the bottom.","s":3},{"t":"Caramelize the onions low and slow with a pinch of salt, adding a splash of water if they catch. Sear the thin steaks 1 minute per side so they stay tender.","s":4}],"rl":"MFWMSSZLGGVPXPPFGGGG"},"53320":{"lv":"EEEEEEEEEERRO","w":{"3":"Adds moisture and soft chew to the dough; skipping makes the cookies drier and more crumbly.","10":"The classic chocolate shell; skip and dust with powdered sugar or eat plain.","11":"Makes the coating glossy and softer to bite; without it the chocolate sets hard and may crack.","12":"Orange perfumes the chocolate; skip for a plainer coating.","8":"Duplicate baking powder line; the method uses about 1/2 tsp baking soda here, so use soda plus 1 tsp baking powder."},"sub":{"3":{"x":"25 g (1 tbsp) maple syrup","d":"Slightly different flavor; keeps the moisture and light browning.","k":["maple syrup"]}},"tips":[{"t":"The list repeats baking powder, but the method calls for baking soda plus baking powder: use about 1/2 tsp baking soda with 1 tsp baking powder (natural cocoa and honey supply the acid).","s":0},{"t":"Roll the dough thin (2 mm) between parchment and keep the unused half chilled; this cornstarch-rich dough is tender and warms and tears quickly.","s":2},{"t":"Melt the chocolate gently, never above lukewarm, then dunk each sandwich with a fork and tap off the excess. Let them set on parchment, and the cookies soften from the dulce de leche after a day.","s":4}],"rl":"FWSWSSXLLGGFX"},"53321":{"lv":"EREEEEEEEREO","w":{"1":"Rolled around the edges of the filled cookies for texture and sweetness; skip for plain alfajores.","9":"Rounds out the buttery flavor; skip and the cookies taste plainer.","11":"Duplicate line; extra for rolling the edges, skip if you already used the first."},"tips":[{"t":"Mix the dough only until it comes together; cornstarch-heavy dough is tender, and overworking makes it tough. Chill the half you are not rolling so the thick cookies keep their shape.","s":2},{"t":"Pull the cookies at 8 to 10 minutes while they are still very pale. Browning means they have dried out and will not soften with the filling.","s":3},{"t":"Sandwich and let them rest several hours or overnight in a sealed container; the dulce de leche softens the cookies into the classic melt-in-the-mouth texture.","s":3}],"rl":"GGFWSSSSLXGG"},"53322":{"lv":"EEEREEO","w":{"6":"Optional spoonful served over the unmolded flan; skip, since the caramel already sweetens it.","3":"Vanilla flavor; skipped, the flan is plainer."},"sub":{"3":{"x":"1 tsp vanilla extract, stirred into the warm milk off the heat","d":"Less complex aroma than a fresh pod but the same vanilla character.","k":["vanilla extract"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Swirl, never stir, and pull the caramel off the heat at deep amber, just before it smokes: it keeps darkening in the pan and turns bitter fast. Pour it into the molds immediately.","s":0},{"t":"Bake in a water bath at 150C so the custard cooks gently and stays silky; it is done when the edges are set and the center wobbles like gelatin. Strain the custard for a smooth surface.","s":1},{"t":"Chill overnight, run a thin knife around the edge and dip the mold in hot water for 15 seconds before inverting so the caramel releases cleanly.","s":3}],"rl":"WMWXSSG"},"53323":{"lv":"EEOEEEEEERRERR","w":{"2":"A pinch helps stabilize the whipped whites; the effect is tiny, so skip if needed.","9":"Perfumes the whipped cream; skip or use lemon zest.","10":"Sweetens the whipped cream and helps it hold peaks; without it the cream is bland.","12":"Used to moisten the sponge or peaches; skip and use syrup or milk, but the cake is drier.","13":"Thickens the peach juice into a glaze; skip and use the juice unthickened."},"tips":[{"t":"Whip the whites with no trace of yolk in a grease-free bowl. Fold in the flour in three additions with a wide spatula to keep the cake light.","s":1},{"t":"Add the sugar to the meringue a spoonful at a time and keep whipping until stiff and glossy; grittiness means it was not dissolved yet.","s":2},{"t":"Chill the cake before cutting it into layers and soak each layer lightly with peach juice. Assemble at least 4 hours ahead so the layers set and slice cleanly.","s":3}],"rl":"LWZSSSWGFXWVMT"},"53324":{"lv":"EEEREERRRO","w":{"3":"Balances the sweet filling and cocoa dough; skipping makes the dough taste flat.","6":"Loosens the dough or egg wash; a splash of water works too.","7":"Brushed on hot empanadas so the cinnamon sugar sticks.","8":"Forms the crunchy sweet coating; skip and the empanadas are less dessert-like.","9":"Warm aroma in the coating; plain sugar is fine."},"tips":[{"t":"Keep the can fully submerged for the whole 2.5 hours and top up with hot water, because a can that boils dry can burst. Cool it completely before opening.","s":0},{"t":"Rub in cold butter just until pea-sized crumbs form and chill the dough 30 minutes; warm butter makes the cocoa dough sticky and tough.","s":1},{"t":"Use only 2 to 3 teaspoons of filling per circle and press the edge with a fork. Dulce de leche that leaks onto the tray burns and sticks.","s":3}],"rl":"WSXZFSMFGG"},"53331":{"lv":"EEREOOEEOO","w":{"2":"Adds gentle sweetness and browning; skip if you plan a sweet topping.","4":"Sharpens the flavor; skipping is barely noticeable.","5":"Background aroma; skip it.","8":"Topping; honey or maple syrup works.","9":"Fresh acidity against the sweet pancakes; any fruit works.","7":"Fat in the dough or batter; skipped, the result is dry, tough or less tender."},"sub":{"7":{"x":"2 to 3 tbsp vegetable oil, only to grease the pan","d":"150 g is far more than a pan needs; if it was meant for the batter, use 3 tbsp oil.","k":["vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Grind the oats fine in a blender so the batter is smooth; if you use rolled oats, blitz 30 to 40 seconds.","s":0},{"t":"The 10-minute rest lets the oats absorb the milk and thicken. If the batter looks like paste afterward, thin it with a splash of milk.","s":1},{"t":"Oat pancakes are fragile: cook on medium-low and wait for the bubbles to pop and the rim to set before flipping once with a wide spatula.","s":3}],"rl":"SMWLZXBFGG"},"53326":{"lv":"ERORRRERRRERRRROR","w":{"1":"Simmered whole for sweetness in the broth; skip and the broth is less rounded.","2":"Minor seasoning; skip it.","3":"Part of the aromatic base; skipping makes the soup less savory.","4":"Crushed cloves flavor the broth during the long simmer; skip and use extra chopped garlic.","5":"Sweet, oniony body from the dark green tops and light green part; replace with extra green onion.","7":"Mild sweetness and fruity aroma typical of Venezuelan sancocho; bell pepper works.","8":"Fresh onion note in the vegetable stage; skip and use a bit more onion.","9":"Corn adds sweetness and a traditional bite; skip and the soup is less festive.","13":"Breaks down slightly to thicken and sweeten the broth; skip or use extra yam.","14":"Seasons the broth; taste at the end, since boiled root vegetables absorb salt.","15":"Optional spice; skip it.","16":"Fresh herb finish added with the last garlic; skip and it is less bright.","11":"Starchy root; skipped, the stew has less bulk.","12":"Starchy root; skipped, the stew has less bulk."},"sub":{"11":{"x":"0.5 lb (225 g) peeled potato or taro","d":"Milder and less earthy than yautia; add the potato a few minutes earlier to keep it from turning to mush.","k":["potato"]},"12":{"x":"0.5 lb (225 g) sweet potato or potato","d":"Sweet potato is sweeter and softer; use a firm potato for a more traditional starch.","k":["sweet potato","potato"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Skim the foam during the first 10 minutes and keep the pot at a gentle simmer so the broth stays clear. The shank is ready when the meat pulls easily from the bone.","s":0},{"t":"Add roots by hardness: cassava first, then yautia and yam, with the butternut last. Squash cooks fastest and turns the broth cloudy and mushy if added early.","s":3},{"t":"Stir in the raw garlic and cilantro at the end with the heat low for 2 minutes. They keep a fresh, pungent top note that the long simmer would otherwise cook away.","s":4}],"rl":"PXZXXXMVXVBBBVZZG"},"53327":{"lv":"EERRORRRRERRER","w":{"2":"Adds savory depth to the blended sauce; skip and the sauce tastes flatter.","3":"Subtle herbal background while simmering; skip it.","4":"Minor seasoning; skip it.","5":"Gives golden color and earthy flavor; skip and the sauce is paler.","6":"Warm earthy note typical of the sauce; skip for milder taste.","7":"Without salt the coconut sauce tastes flat; taste and adjust at the end.","8":"Balances the acidity of the tomatoes and rounds the coconut; skip if your tomatoes are sweet.","10":"Fresh heat and fragrance in the sauce; skip or use 1/4 tsp ground ginger.","13":"Thins the blended sauce; skip and the sauce is thicker and richer.","11":"Sautes aromatics; skipped, the sauce still works."},"sub":{"11":{"x":"1/3 cup (80 ml) vegetable oil","d":"Neutral rather than fruity; only used for sauteing and does not affect the sauce.","k":["vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Pat the chicken dry and brown it in a single layer; it takes only about 3 minutes. Overcrowding steams it and the sauce loses the toasty fond.","s":5},{"t":"Keep the sauce at a gentle simmer once the coconut milk goes in; a hard boil can split the coconut and leave an oily sauce.","s":6},{"t":"Cook until the chicken reaches 165F (74C) and the sauce is creamy; if it is thin, simmer uncovered for 5 minutes, and always remove the bay leaves before serving.","s":6}],"rl":"PXXXZXXZWVXFFM"},"53328":{"lv":"ERRERRERRRRROR","w":{"1":"Rubbed into the beef for savory depth; skip and add more garlic to the sauce.","2":"Pressure-cooked with the beef, it flavors the stock; skip and the stock is blander.","4":"Sweet, mild vegetal note in the sofrito; skip or use a green bell pepper.","5":"Breaks down into the sauce for body and acidity; use canned diced tomato if needed.","7":"Adds salty umami and tang; use 1 tbsp soy sauce plus 1 tsp vinegar if skipping.","8":"Gives body and color to the sauce; skip and the sauce is thinner and paler.","9":"Warm earthy flavor that gives Venezuelan carne mechada its character.","11":"Seasons the beef and sauce; taste at the end after the sauce reduces.","12":"Minor extra seasoning; skip it.","10":"Sweet-tangy base; skipped, the beef is milder.","13":"Cooks aromatics; skipped, the dish still works."},"sub":{"10":{"x":"2 tbsp tomato paste + 1 tbsp sugar + 1 tsp vinegar + 2 tbsp water","d":"Less sweet and tangy than ketchup; adjust sugar to taste.","k":["tomato paste","sugar","vinegar"]},"13":{"x":"1 tbsp vegetable oil","d":"Neutral; the browned onions and peppers supply the flavor.","k":["vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Cook the skirt steak in the stock until a fork shreds it with almost no resistance. If it still resists, give it 10 more minutes under pressure rather than shredding it tough.","s":3},{"t":"Shred while the meat is still warm and pull with the grain; cold beef fibers tear and turn into mush.","s":6},{"t":"Simmer the shredded beef in the sauce for 5 to 10 minutes uncovered so it absorbs flavor and the sauce clings; loosen with the saved stock a spoonful at a time if it dries.","s":10}],"rl":"PXXXVVMXXXAZZF"},"53329":{"lv":"ERRRROREEEEO","w":{"1":"Sweet aromatic base for the shredded beef; skip and the filling is flatter.","2":"Adds sweetness and fresh flavor to the sofrito; use extra onion if skipping.","3":"Savory aroma in the filling; skip for a milder filling.","4":"Warm earthy backbone of the meat filling; do not skip if you want the classic flavor.","5":"Herbal accent; skip it.","6":"Adds color and mild sweetness; skip and filling is paler.","7":"Liquid the dish simmers or cooks in; skipped, there is not enough liquid.","11":"Tiny amount; skipped, there is little noticeable effect."},"sub":{"7":{"x":"1 l water + 1 beef bouillon cube + 1 tsp salt","d":"Less rich than real beef broth; the long simmer with onion adds flavor.","k":["water","beef bouillon"]},"11":{"x":"1 tbsp vegetable oil","d":"Neutral oil is fine for sauteing the vegetables.","k":["vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"The meat is ready when it shreds with no resistance after about 2 hours at a gentle simmer. Let it cool in the broth so it stays moist before shredding.","s":0},{"t":"Cook the shredded beef with the sofrito until the liquid evaporates; wet filling turns the arepa soggy. The 2.5 cups of water is for the dough.","s":2},{"t":"Use pre-cooked cornmeal (masarepa or harina P.A.N.), not regular cornmeal, which will not form a dough. The dough should feel like soft clay and not stick to your hands.","s":3}],"rl":"PXVXXXXMMZGF"},"53334":{"lv":"EEERERRO","w":{"3":"Fresh acidic salsa that balances the rich meat and beans; skip and add lime juice to the tomato.","5":"Fresh acidity and juiciness in the filling; skip and rely on the pico de gallo.","6":"Beans and meat need salt; taste and adjust.","7":"Optional seasoning for the meat; skip it."},"tips":[{"t":"Fry the ripe plantains over medium heat until deeply caramelized and dark at the edges; pale plantains taste starchy, not sweet.","s":0},{"t":"Griddle the arepas until the crust sounds hollow when tapped, then split with a hinge intact. Put the beans in first so the meat juices do not make the bottom soggy.","s":2},{"t":"2 kg of meat is far more than 2 arepas need; shred the meat after slow cooking in a sofrito of onion, garlic and tomato and use only a portion per arepa.","s":0}],"rl":"BVBXPVZZ"},"53330":{"lv":"EERRROOE","w":{"2":"Fatty, spicy topping that flavors the pizza; swap for bacon or leave it out.","3":"Mild salty topping; use any deli meat or skip.","4":"Sweet pop against the salty toppings; skip.","5":"Briny accent; skip it.","6":"Pepper strips add color and crunch; skip it."},"tips":[{"t":"Casabe is brittle and absorbs moisture fast: spread only a thin layer of sauce and bake just until the cheese melts so the base stays crisp.","s":2},{"t":"If you use raw chorizo or bacon, pre-cook it first. A 7 to 10 minute bake will not cook raw pork sausage safely (pork needs 145F, ground pork 160F).","s":3},{"t":"Slice the casabe thick and pat the toppings dry so they do not release water onto the cassava.","s":4}],"rl":"BXPPVGVF"},"53325":{"lv":"EEER","sub":{"3":{"x":"1 tbsp butter","d":"Browns the crust and tastes richer; use a light coating only so the arepa does not fry.","k":["butter"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Use pre-cooked white cornmeal (masarepa or harina P.A.N.), not regular cornmeal; it forms a smooth dough and cooks through.","s":3},{"t":"The dough should feel like soft clay: if the edges crack when you flatten a ball, knead in water a tablespoon at a time; if it sticks, add a little more cornmeal.","s":5},{"t":"Cook the arepas on the griddle until a crust forms and they release easily, then finish in the oven; they are done when they sound hollow when tapped.","s":14}],"rl":"BMZF","w":{"0":"Must be pre-cooked white cornmeal (masarepa/harina P.A.N.); regular cornmeal will not form a dough.","3":"Light coating for the pan; skipped, a nonstick pan works."}},"53332":{"lv":"EORRRRROREREE","w":{"1":"Minor seasoning; skip it.","2":"Warm earthy note in the beef filling; skip for a milder taste.","4":"Sweet base of the filling; skip and the filling is meatier and less rounded.","5":"Adds sweetness and color; skip or use more onion.","6":"Duplicate pepper line; use half in the filling and half for garnish, or just one.","7":"Savory depth; fresh garlic works too.","8":"Briny capers (alcaparras) are a classic filling element; skip and add a pinch of salt.","10":"A seasoning packet adds color and savory-umami flavor; replace with paprika, garlic and cumin.","3":"Browns the beef; skipped, the beef fat carries it.","12":"Frying medium; skipped, the food cannot be fried."},"sub":{"3":{"x":"1 tbsp olive oil","d":"Neutral enough; only used for browning the beef.","k":["olive oil"]},"12":{"x":"enough sunflower or canola oil for 1/2 inch depth","d":"Any neutral oil works for shallow frying; avoid olive oil.","k":["sunflower oil","canola oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Cook the beef until no pink remains (160F/71C) and the sauce is thick and spoonable. A wet filling steams the dough from inside and bursts the turnover.","s":0},{"t":"Mix the masarepa with warm water and salt; the dough should be moist but not sticky and not cracking at the edges. Rest 5 minutes, then add water by the tablespoon if it cracks.","s":1},{"t":"Fry in 1/2 inch oil on medium-high but not smoking; too hot and the dough browns before it cooks through, too cool and it soaks up oil. Drain on paper towels.","s":2}],"rl":"PZXFXVVXXXXBF"},"53333":{"lv":"EEEEEO","w":{"5":"Garnish only; skip it."},"tips":[{"t":"Sprinkle the gelatin over cold water and wait the full 5 minutes so it blooms; adding it dry to a hot mixture leaves lumps and a weak set.","s":0},{"t":"Use a spotless, grease-free bowl and whip the whites to stiff, glossy peaks. Because the whites are not cooked, use pasteurized egg whites for safety, especially for children, pregnant people or older guests.","s":1},{"t":"Fold the whites in only after the passion fruit base has cooled to room temperature; warm base deflates them and can start to set the gelatin too early. Fold gently in thirds.","s":2}],"rl":"SVWWLG"},"3200":{"lv":"EERRERROEOR","w":{"2":"Garlic deepens the marinade; skip and the chicken tastes less savory.","3":"Shallot adds sweet, mild onion flavor that caramelizes on the chicken.","5":"Sugar helps the chicken caramelize and balances the fish sauce; skip and it browns less.","6":"Adds color and extra salty depth; the fish sauce does most of the work.","7":"Mild bite in the marinade; skip it.","9":"Sprinkled on at the end for heat; skip for a mild dish.","10":"Serving base that soaks up the juices; any noodles or vegetables work.","8":"Only cooking fat; skipped, food sticks and browns poorly, so use an oil or substitute."},"sub":{"8":{"x":"2 tbsp peanut oil or olive oil","d":"Neutral swap for the cooking fat; peanut oil handles the high heat best.","k":["peanut oil","olive oil"],"wn":"Peanut oil contains peanut; avoid with peanut allergy"}},"tips":[{"t":"Use only the tender white lower part of the lemongrass and mince it very fine or pound it to a paste; coarse pieces are woody and fibrous in the finished chicken.","s":0},{"t":"Marinate 30 minutes to a few hours; fish sauce is salty and starts to cure the chicken if left more than about 12 hours.","s":1},{"t":"Cook in batches and do not move the thighs for the first 2 minutes so the sugary marinade browns. Dark meat is tender at 175F/80C, and at least 165F/74C is required.","s":3}],"rl":"PXXXXWXZFXB"},"3202":{"lv":"EERRERRREEERERRO","w":{"2":"Shallot gives sweet onion flavor to the patties and slices; skip and add a little onion.","3":"Garlic deepens the marinade; skip and the pork is less savory.","5":"Caramelizes on the grill for the char that defines bun cha; skip and browning is weaker.","6":"Adds gloss and extra caramelization; replace with 1 tbsp sugar.","7":"Light bite in the marinade; skip it.","11":"Quick-pickled carrot adds crunch and sweetness to the dipping broth; skip or use daikon.","13":"Fresh, cool crunch served on the side; skip if you have other herbs.","14":"Aromatic herb that cools the grilled pork; skip and use extra cilantro.","15":"Adds fresh herbal brightness; skip it, mint and lettuce do the work.","10":"Defining acid for the dipping sauce; skipped, the sauce is flat."},"sub":{"10":{"x":"3 tbsp white vinegar + 1 tbsp water","d":"Sharper than rice vinegar; dissolve it well in the broth so the sugar offsets the sharpness.","k":["white vinegar"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Chill the shaped patties 15 minutes before grilling; they hold together better, and sugar in the marinade means they char quickly over medium-high heat.","s":2},{"t":"Grill the patties and the pork slices over high heat until charred in spots. Ground pork must reach 160F/71C inside, sliced pork 145F/63C.","s":3},{"t":"Taste the dipping broth at room temperature; it should be sweet, sour and salty, as the pork juices and noodles will dilute it in the bowl. Do not serve it boiling hot.","s":1}],"rl":"PPXXXWWZXWAVBGGG"},"3206":{"lv":"ERREERRERROR","w":{"1":"Flavors the poaching broth, which then cooks the rice; skip and the rice is blander.","2":"Adds sweetness to the broth; skip.","5":"Toasted garlic perfumes the rice; skip and it is plainer.","6":"Becomes a fragrant scallion oil in the dipping sauce; skip and use more garlic.","9":"Balances the fish sauce and lime; skip and the sauce is sharper.","10":"Fresh, cool contrast to the warm rice and chicken.","11":"Season the poaching water and rice to taste; the dipping sauce is salty already.","4":"Only cooking fat; skipped, food sticks and browns poorly, so use an oil or substitute.","8":"Adds brightness; skipped, the dish is flatter but still works."},"sub":{"4":{"x":"2 tbsp vegetable oil or chicken fat","d":"Chicken fat skimmed from the broth adds even more flavor to the rice.","k":["vegetable oil"]},"8":{"x":"1 tbsp lemon juice","d":"Slightly less floral than lime but the same sour balance.","k":["lemon juice"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Poach at a bare simmer, not a boil, and skim the foam. Chicken must reach at least 165F/74C; thighs are more tender at 175F/80C as the recipe states.","s":1},{"t":"Toast the rice in the garlic oil until it turns opaque and chalky before adding the strained broth; do not lift the lid for 18 minutes, then let the rice rest off the heat 10 minutes.","s":3},{"t":"Pour the hot oil over the green onion in a heatproof bowl so it sizzles and perfumes the sauce; stand back from the spatter and add the fish sauce after it settles.","s":4}],"rl":"PXXBFXXXAWGZ"},"52828":{"lv":"EERRREERRRRRO","w":{"2":"Crispy egg rolls (cha gio) are the traditional crunchy topping; skip for a lighter bowl.","3":"Shallots sweeten the marinade and char well; replace with finely minced onion.","4":"Garlic gives the marinade its savory punch; skip and flavor is flatter.","7":"Thick (dark) soy adds color and light molasses sweetness; skip and add a pinch of sugar.","8":"Subtle spice; reduce if you like it mild.","10":"Cool, crisp contrast to the grilled pork; skip and add lettuce.","11":"Aromatic herb for freshness; use cilantro or basil.","12":"Crunchy garnish; skip for allergies.","9":"Carries flavor in the marinade or dressing; skipped, the dish is drier but intact."},"sub":{"9":{"x":"3 tbsp vegetable oil","d":"Neutral oil carries the marinade just as well.","k":["vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Partially freeze the pork for 20 minutes and slice it 3 mm thin against the grain so the slices cook through and caramelize in 10 minutes.","s":0},{"t":"Marinate overnight if possible; sugar and fish sauce tenderize and the marinade chars in the oven. Wipe off excess marinade before broiling so it does not burn.","s":2},{"t":"Finish with nuoc cham: about 3 tbsp fish sauce, 3 tbsp sugar, 3 tbsp lime juice and 1/2 cup warm water with chopped garlic and chili. Pork must reach 145F/63C.","s":4}],"rl":"PBGXXWXXZFVGG"},"52997":{"lv":"ERRRRREE","w":{"1":"Softens and sweetens in the pan to season the beef; skip and use more garlic.","2":"Zest goes in the rice and juice in the pickle; skip and add a splash of vinegar.","3":"Savory base for the beef; skip and the beef is plain.","4":"Quick-pickled cucumber gives the bowl its tangy banh mi crunch.","5":"Fresh grated carrot adds sweetness and crunch; skip and double the cucumber."},"tips":[{"t":"Rinse the rice until the water runs clear and let it steam off the heat 10 minutes; fluff with butter and lime zest so it is separate and aromatic.","s":1},{"t":"The additional ingredients are mayonnaise and sriracha: mix about 3 tbsp mayo with 1 to 2 tsp sriracha, a pinch of garlic and a squeeze of lime for the signature drizzle.","s":3},{"t":"Brown the beef in a hot pan without stirring for the first 2 minutes so it caramelizes, break it up, and cook until no pink remains (160F/71C), adding the soy sauce at the end.","s":4}],"rl":"BXAXVVPX"},"53227":{"lv":"ERRERRERREERROO","w":{"1":"Adds sweetness and color to the filling; skip and use more cabbage.","2":"Earthy umami; use button mushrooms or skip.","4":"Aromatic base for the pork; skip for a blander filling.","5":"Fresh heat that lifts the pork; replace with 1/2 tsp ground ginger.","7":"Nutty aroma in the filling; skip or use more soy.","8":"Mild onion flavor in the filling; skip.","11":"Salty base for the dipping sauce.","12":"Sharpens the dipping sauce; use lime juice instead.","13":"Nutty garnish; skip.","14":"Fresh garnish; skip.","10":"Frying medium; skipped, the food cannot be fried."},"sub":{"10":{"x":"75 ml sunflower or canola oil","d":"Any neutral oil for frying; avoid olive oil which smokes and tastes strong.","k":["sunflower oil","canola oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Salt the chopped cabbage and squeeze it hard in a tea towel; leftover water makes the filling soggy and tears the rice paper.","s":0},{"t":"Soak rice paper for only a couple of seconds; it keeps softening on the oiled board. Over-soaked sheets stick and tear.","s":1},{"t":"Fry on medium-high until golden and cook the pork through to 160F/71C; cover briefly if the filling needs more time, and drain on paper towels.","s":2}],"rl":"VVVPXXXXXBFXAGG"},"53231":{"lv":"RERRRRORORREREEROO","w":{"2":"Onions give sweetness to the braising sauce; skip and the sauce is thinner.","3":"Fresh ginger gives the braise heat and aroma; use 1 tsp ground ginger if needed.","4":"Garlic adds savory depth; skip and flavor is flatter.","5":"Chopped chili infuses the braise with heat; skip for mild.","6":"Sliced chili is a fresh garnish; skip.","7":"Balances the fish sauce and deepens the sauce color.","8":"Final sugar to sweeten at the end; skip if sauce is sweet enough.","9":"Star anise gives the warm, pho-like aroma; skip and the braise is more generic.","10":"Citrusy perfume; use lemon zest or skip.","12":"Adds body, color and mild acidity to the sauce; skip and use a splash of ketchup.","15":"Brightens the rich lamb; skip and use lemon or a splash of vinegar.","16":"Fresh herb finish; skip.","17":"Aromatic herb finish; skip.","0":"Browns the lamb; skipped, the shanks still braise."},"sub":{"0":{"x":"2 tbsp vegetable oil","d":"Neutral oil for browning; any oil that handles high heat works.","k":["vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Brown the shanks deeply in two batches, patted dry, without crowding. The fond left behind is the foundation for the sauce.","s":0},{"t":"The braise needs about 2.5 hours in total despite the stated time: add the sweet potatoes for the last hour, and cook until the lamb pulls from the bone.","s":3},{"t":"Season with the fish sauce and lime juice at the very end; heat dulls both. If the sauce is thin, reduce it uncovered for 10 minutes.","s":4}],"rl":"FPXXXXGWWXXMXBXAGG"},"53232":{"lv":"ERREORROREERR","w":{"1":"Adds sweetness and crunch; skip and double the cucumber.","2":"Cool, juicy crunch; replace with extra carrot.","4":"Peppery crunch; skip it.","5":"Sharp bite that is softened by the dressing; skip for a milder salad.","6":"Fresh aromatic herb that is part of the flavor; replace with cilantro.","7":"Nutty garnish; skip for allergies.","8":"Heat in the dressing; skip for mild.","11":"Salt and color in the dressing; extra fish sauce can replace it.","12":"Nutty richness that rounds the sour dressing; replace with a little oil."},"tips":[{"t":"Toss the warm, just-drained noodles in the dressing so they absorb it; cold noodles only coat on the surface and taste bland.","s":0},{"t":"Poach the chicken breasts at a bare simmer until 165F/74C, then cool in the poaching liquid before shredding so they stay juicy.","s":2},{"t":"Salt the peeled cucumber and carrot ribbons for 5 minutes and pat dry; otherwise they release water and thin the dressing.","s":1}],"rl":"BVVPVXGGXAXXF"},"53242":{"lv":"EERERRERRO","w":{"4":"Fresh ginger adds warmth and heat to the filling; skip for a sweeter filling.","5":"Savory depth; skip and the filling is sweeter.","7":"Sweet glaze for the filling; replace with brown sugar.","8":"Gives the filling body, color and tang; skip and the filling is thinner.","9":"Brushed on for a glossy, golden crust; milk works.","2":"Bacon renders fat; skipped, the filling still cooks."},"sub":{"2":{"x":"1 tbsp butter","d":"Bacon renders fat anyway; butter or none at all is fine.","k":["butter"],"wn":"Contains dairy"}},"tips":[{"t":"Mixing the sugar into the bread mix gives soft, lightly sweet buns; knead until the dough is smooth and let it double before shaping.","s":0},{"t":"Cook the filling until thick and jammy, and cool it completely before filling; warm or loose filling leaks and softens the dough.","s":1},{"t":"Pleat and pinch the buns tightly, place them seam-side down, and rest 20 minutes before baking; egg wash twice for a deep, glossy crust.","s":2}],"rl":"WSFPXXXWXG"},"53251":{"lv":"EEOERRRERORRRR","w":{"2":"Only prevents sticking on the tray; flour or semolina works.","4":"Fresh green heat in the topping; skip for a milder flavor.","5":"Adds fresh oniony bite to the meat topping.","6":"Fresh herb brightness in the topping and garnish; skip and add more green onion.","8":"Adds heat and color to the beef topping.","9":"Pinch of warmth; skip it.","10":"Warm sweet spice typical of the beef topping; skip for plainer meat.","11":"Moistens the topping and crisps the edges; skip and it bakes drier.","12":"Squeezed over at the end for brightness; skip and use a splash of vinegar.","13":"Sliced raw onion in the garnish adds sharp, crunchy contrast."},"tips":[{"t":"Preheat the baking tray in the hottest oven you can reach; the thin dough should hit a scorching surface so it bakes fast and stays crisp rather than drying out.","s":2},{"t":"Drain and squeeze the tomatoes and chop the topping fine (a quick pulse helps); wet topping makes soggy flatbread. Spread it paper-thin so the beef cooks in 10 minutes.","s":5},{"t":"Roll the dough very thin, about 3 mm, and bake until the edges darken; serve straight away with parsley, onion and lemon, rolled up around the herbs.","s":7}],"rl":"SLGVXXGPXXXFAG"},"3199":{"lv":"EERRRRRRERO","w":{"2":"Soft noodles add body; skip for a lighter roll.","3":"Soft lettuce adds freshness and helps keep the rice paper from tearing.","4":"Sweet crunch; skip.","5":"Cool crunch; skip.","6":"Fresh aromatic herb, part of the roll's flavor.","7":"Adds fresh brightness; use extra mint.","10":"A little heat in the sauce; skip for mild.","9":"Adds creamy richness to the dip; skipped, the sauce is thinner."},"sub":{"9":{"x":"2 tbsp almond butter (or tahini + 1/2 tsp sugar)","d":"Almond butter is milder; tahini is slightly bitter, so add a pinch of sugar.","k":["almond butter","tahini"],"wn":"Almond butter contains tree nuts; tahini contains sesame"}},"tips":[{"t":"Dip the rice paper only 5 to 10 seconds; it keeps softening on the damp board. Over-soaked sheets are sticky and tear when rolled.","s":1},{"t":"Lay the shrimp pink side down at the edge first so they show through the wrapper, then pile the filling lightly; overfilling makes the rolls burst.","s":2},{"t":"Roll tightly, cover with a damp towel while you work, and serve within 2 hours; the rolls dry out and stick to each other in the fridge.","s":3}],"rl":"BPBVVVXXXFX"},"53228":{"lv":"EERRERROR","w":{"2":"Adds heat to the sauce and garnish; skip for mild.","3":"Fresh ginger adds aroma and warmth against the sweet caramel.","5":"Greens steam in the sauce and balance the sweetness; use any quick-cooking green.","6":"Acid cuts through the sticky sweet sauce; skip and it is cloying.","7":"Fresh herb garnish; skip.","8":"Soaks up the caramel sauce; use noodles or potatoes."},"tips":[{"t":"Cook the sugar to a deep amber (like strong tea), not black, which is bitter. Add the fish sauce and water carefully, since the caramel spits.","s":1},{"t":"Cook the fish skin-side down in the simmering caramel under a lid; it is done when the flesh turns opaque and flakes, about 145F/63C.","s":2},{"t":"Add the lemon off the heat so the acid stays fresh, and spoon the caramel over the fish so it glazes.","s":3}],"rl":"WXXXPVAGB"},"53236":{"lv":"RRREERERROR","w":{"1":"Fresh ginger is a major flavor base; skip and the stew is bland.","2":"Savory aroma; skip and use more ginger.","5":"Balances the soy and sweetens the squash; skip and use a little honey.","7":"Adds fresh crunch and color; use peas or broccoli.","8":"Fresh mild onion finish; skip.","9":"Fresh herb finish; skip.","10":"Soaks up the sauce; use noodles.","0":"Cooks aromatics; skipped, the hotpot still works."},"sub":{"0":{"x":"2 tsp olive oil","d":"Neutral for stir-frying the aromatics; sesame oil adds aroma too.","k":["olive oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Cook the ginger and garlic gently for the full 5 minutes until soft and sweet, not brown; browning makes them bitter.","s":0},{"t":"Cut the squash into even 1.5 cm cubes so it is tender in 10 minutes without collapsing; leave the lid on to steam.","s":1},{"t":"Add the green beans only for the last 3 minutes so they stay bright and crisp, and stir in the green onion off the heat.","s":2}],"rl":"FXXVXWMVGGB"},"53239":{"lv":"EERRRRRE","w":{"4":"Half goes into the sauce, half on top; adds mild onion freshness.","5":"Cool crunch that balances the rich peanut sauce.","6":"Fresh crunch; skip and add more cucumber.","2":"Creamy richness; skipped, the sauce is thinner.","3":"Sweet-spicy dressing; skipped, the salad is plainer."},"sub":{"2":{"x":"4 tbsp whole milk","d":"Sauce is slightly thinner and less coconutty; add the peanut butter first so the sauce still emulsifies.","k":["whole milk"],"wn":"Contains dairy"},"3":{"x":"2 tbsp honey + 1/2 tsp chili flakes + 1 tsp rice vinegar","d":"Less sticky and a little sharper than bottled sauce; adjust honey to taste.","k":["honey","chili flakes","rice vinegar"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Rinse the cooked noodles under cold water and drain very well; leftover starch makes them clump and water dilutes the sauce.","s":0},{"t":"Warm the sauce gently and loosen it with water a teaspoon at a time; peanut butter seizes into a lump over high heat.","s":1},{"t":"If your prawns are raw, cook them in a pan for 2 minutes per side until pink and opaque; if cooked, add them cold on top.","s":3}],"rl":"BFFXGVVP"},"53240":{"lv":"EERORRREERR","w":{"2":"Savory aroma; skip and the dish is blander.","3":"Gentle heat; skip for mild.","4":"Fresh mild onion flavor; skip.","5":"Adds protein and sweet bite; use peas or extra tofu.","6":"Quick-cooking leafy green; use bok choy or spinach.","9":"Salt and depth; hoisin alone is sweet, so skipping makes it cloying.","10":"Crunch and richness; use peanuts or skip.","0":"Only cooking fat; skipped, food sticks and browns poorly, so use an oil or substitute."},"sub":{"0":{"x":"1 tbsp sunflower oil","d":"Neutral oil for stir-frying.","k":["sunflower oil","vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Keep the broccoli moving over high heat and add a splash of water if it catches; the steam helps it become tender-crisp without browning.","s":0},{"t":"Add the garlic last for only 1 minute; it burns quickly and turns bitter.","s":1},{"t":"Add the hoisin off the high heat; its sugars burn fast, and warm the cashews just through so they stay crisp.","s":2}],"rl":"FVXXXVVPXXG"},"53241":{"lv":"ERERRRRRRERRO","w":{"1":"Soft noodle filling adds body; skip for a lighter parcel.","3":"Sweet crunch; use more cucumber.","4":"Cool crunch; skip.","5":"Sweet crunch; skip.","6":"Cool leaf for wrapping each roll in at the table.","7":"Fresh herb that is part of the flavor.","8":"Cooling aromatic herb; use extra coriander.","11":"Brightens the fish-sauce dip; skip and add more vinegar.","12":"Heat for the dip; skip for mild.","10":"Adds brightness; skipped, the dish is flatter but still works."},"sub":{"10":{"x":"2 tbsp white vinegar + 1/2 tsp sugar","d":"Sharper than rice vinegar; the sugar softens it.","k":["white vinegar","sugar"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Soak rice pancakes only 30 seconds; they keep softening as they sit, and over-soaked sheets tear when folded.","s":0},{"t":"Cook the chicken to 165F/74C, cool, and slice it thin so it wraps neatly; do not leave the raw-wrapped parcels out for long.","s":1},{"t":"The fish-sauce dip needs about 3 tbsp water and 1 tbsp sugar to mellow the 4 tbsp of fish sauce, so dissolve the sugar in warm water first.","s":2}],"rl":"BBPVVVGGGXAAX"},"53243":{"lv":"RRRERERRERR","w":{"0":"Brightens and sours the dipping sauce; use more vinegar if skipping.","4":"Fresh chili heat in the sauce; skip for mild.","6":"Fresh herb that is part of the roll flavor; skip and use more coriander.","7":"Fresh herb aroma that complements the prawns.","9":"Sweet crunch; spiralized carrot is part of the filling texture.","10":"Cool crisp noodle-like strands; use cucumber instead.","1":"Adds brightness; skipped, the dish is flatter but still works.","2":"Balances sweetness; skipped, the dish is a bit sharper but still fine."},"sub":{"1":{"x":"2 tbsp white vinegar or extra lime juice","d":"White vinegar is sharper; start with less and taste.","k":["white vinegar","lime juice"]},"2":{"x":"1 tbsp brown sugar","d":"Slightly more molasses flavor than palm sugar, same sweetness.","k":["brown sugar"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Stir the dipping sauce well and let it sit; the sugar needs time to dissolve and the flavor develops. It should be a balance of sweet, sour and salty.","s":0},{"t":"Dip the rice paper in warm water for just a few seconds; it keeps softening on the damp towel, and over-soaking makes it sticky and fragile.","s":1},{"t":"Raw prawns must be cooked until pink and opaque first; place them pink side down for a neat look, and roll tightly with the herbs against the wrapper.","s":2}],"rl":"AAWXXBXXPVV"},"53244":{"lv":"EERRRRROERERRRR","w":{"2":"Sharp crunch that is mellowed by the dressing; skip and use more cucumber.","3":"Heat for the dressing; skip for mild.","4":"Cool crunch to balance the lime and fish sauce.","5":"Fresh herb, part of the salad flavor.","6":"Fresh herb; use extra coriander.","7":"Crunchy garnish; skip for allergies.","9":"Balances the lime and fish sauce; without it the dressing is sharp.","11":"Raw garlic bite in the dressing; skip and use a little ginger.","12":"Crispy shallots add savory crunch and sweetness on top.","14":"Coats the shallots so they crisp; cornstarch works too.","13":"Fries the crisp shallots; skipped, the salad still works."},"sub":{"13":{"x":"enough sunflower or canola oil for 5 cm depth","d":"Any neutral frying oil works.","k":["sunflower oil","canola oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Slice the shallots thin and evenly, toss lightly in flour and fry in small batches; remove while pale gold as they keep darkening off the heat and turn bitter if burnt.","s":1},{"t":"Cover thin rice noodles with just-boiled water for about 2 minutes, then rinse in cold water and shake well; thin noodles go gummy if left to soak.","s":3},{"t":"Dress the salad and add the crispy shallots at the last second so they stay crunchy; if the prawns are raw, cook them until pink first.","s":5}],"rl":"BPVXVGGGAWXXGFT"},"53245":{"lv":"ERRRREOERRRRR","w":{"1":"Sweet crunch from the blanch; use peas or green beans.","2":"Coats the noodles so they do not clump and adds nuttiness; use any oil.","3":"Fresh mild onion; skip.","4":"Fresh herb; skip or use parsley.","6":"Salty crunch serving extra; skip.","11":"Savory sharpness in the dressing; skip and it is sweeter.","12":"Fresh warmth in the dressing; use 1/2 tsp ground ginger.","8":"Balances sweetness; skipped, the dish is a bit sharper but still fine.","9":"Adds brightness; skipped, the dish is flatter but still works.","10":"Dresses the salad; skipped, it is drier."},"sub":{"8":{"x":"1 tbsp maple syrup","d":"Slightly different sweetness but dissolves the same.","k":["maple syrup"]},"9":{"x":"1 tbsp lime juice","d":"Slightly more floral than lemon.","k":["lime juice"]},"10":{"x":"1 tbsp vegetable oil","d":"Neutral; groundnut adds a faint nuttiness only.","k":["vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Soak the thin noodles with the sugar snaps in boiling water so both finish at the same time; the peas stay crisp-tender, not mushy.","s":0},{"t":"Cool under running water and toss with sesame oil right away; the oil keeps the strands from sticking and gives the salad a nutty background.","s":1},{"t":"Dress just before serving and fold in the hot-smoked salmon gently in large flakes; stirring too much breaks it into mush.","s":2}],"rl":"BVFGGPGXWAFXX"},"53246":{"lv":"EERRROERRR","w":{"2":"Sharp bite mellowed by the lime; skip and use more cabbage.","3":"Fresh herb, part of the flavor.","4":"Fresh herb; use extra mint.","5":"Crunchy garnish; skip for allergies.","8":"Heat in the dressing; skip for mild.","9":"Balances the lime; without it the salad is very sharp.","7":"Carries flavor in the marinade or dressing; skipped, the dish is drier but intact."},"sub":{"7":{"x":"1 tbsp vegetable oil","d":"Neutral oil carries the dressing the same.","k":["vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Shred the red cabbage as thinly as you can; thick shreds are chewy. Salt it for 10 minutes and squeeze if you want a softer salad.","s":0},{"t":"Dissolve the sugar fully in the lime juice before adding the oil and chili; undissolved sugar sinks and the dressing tastes sour.","s":1},{"t":"Dress 10 minutes before serving; the lime softens the cabbage and turns it a brighter pink-purple, and add herbs and peanuts at the end so they stay fresh and crunchy.","s":3}],"rl":"VVXGGGAFXW"},"53248":{"lv":"ERRRRRE","w":{"1":"Fresh mild onion in the noodles; skip.","2":"Sweet pop; use frozen peas or skip.","3":"Heat in the flavored butter; skip for mild.","4":"Fresh herb in the butter; use parsley.","5":"Citrus brightens the rich salmon; use lemon."},"tips":[{"t":"Make the parcel with plenty of headroom and crimp the folds tightly; the steam inside cooks the salmon and noodles.","s":3},{"t":"Salmon is done at 145F/63C (opaque and flakes); thicker pieces may need the full 20 minutes. Open the parcels at the table so the aroma stays in.","s":4},{"t":"Soak the noodles for only 2 minutes; they finish in the oven, and fully cooked noodles go soggy in the parcel. Mash the chili, coriander, lime and butter together for the flavored butter.","s":1}],"rl":"BXVXXAP"},"53250":{"lv":"EEREREROO","w":{"2":"Balances the vinegar so the pickle is tangy, not harsh.","4":"Creamy spread that barriers the bread from tempeh juice; vegan mayo works.","6":"Fresh herb that is part of banh mi flavor; use extra mint.","7":"Second herb; skip.","8":"Extra heat; skip for mild."},"sub":{"4":{"x":"100 g (1/3 cup) mayonnaise; vegan mayo to keep it vegan","d":"Milder and creamier than hummus, with no chickpea flavor.","k":["mayonnaise"],"wn":"Regular mayonnaise contains egg; use vegan mayo; hummus contains sesame"}},"tips":[{"t":"Salt the vegetable pickle for 10 minutes and toss it often; it gets better the longer it sits and should stay crunchy, not limp.","s":0},{"t":"Steam or simmer tempeh for 5 minutes before frying to remove bitterness, then brown it in a pan for 3 minutes per side so it is crisp.","s":2},{"t":"Toast the baguette so the crust is crisp and spread the hummus right to the edges; it protects the bread from the pickle and tempeh juices.","s":2}],"rl":"VAWBFPGGG"},"3201":{"lv":"ERERREERRRRER","w":{"1":"Garlic gives the steak savory depth; skip and the beef is plainer.","3":"Savory depth and glossy color; use extra soy sauce.","4":"Helps the beef caramelize; skip and it browns less.","7":"Sweet wedges that soften in the pan; use yellow onion.","8":"Finishes the dish with richness and shine; use a splash of oil.","9":"Cool, crisp bed under the hot beef.","10":"Juicy acidity next to the rich steak; skip and add extra lime.","12":"Seasons the lime-pepper dip; skip and salt to taste.","6":"Only cooking fat; skipped, food sticks and browns poorly, so use an oil or substitute."},"sub":{"6":{"x":"2 tbsp peanut oil","d":"High-heat neutral oil gives the best sear.","k":["peanut oil"],"wn":"Peanut oil contains peanut; avoid with peanut allergy"}},"tips":[{"t":"Get the wok smoking-hot and sear the beef in a single layer in two batches without moving it for the first minute; crowding steams the steak gray.","s":2},{"t":"The salt, pepper and lime dip (muoi tieu chanh) is classic; mix it just before serving, and be generous with the black pepper.","s":1},{"t":"Add the butter last to coat the beef in a glossy sauce; sirloin turns tough if cooked past medium.","s":3}],"rl":"PXXXWZFVFGGAZ"},"3205":{"lv":"EEREREERRRROEEE","w":{"2":"Garlic deepens the marinade; skip and flavor is flatter.","4":"Helps the beef brown and balances the fish sauce.","7":"Cool, crisp contrast to the hot beef.","8":"Sweet crunch; use more cucumber.","9":"Soft crisp base for the bowl; skip and add more cucumber.","10":"Fresh herb that is part of the noodle bowl flavor.","11":"Crunchy topping; skip for allergies.","5":"Only cooking fat; skipped, food sticks and browns poorly, so use an oil or substitute."},"sub":{"5":{"x":"1 tbsp peanut oil","d":"Neutral high-heat oil is best for the sear.","k":["peanut oil","vegetable oil"],"wn":"Peanut oil contains peanut; avoid with peanut allergy"}},"tips":[{"t":"Freeze the flank for 20 minutes and slice it thinly against the grain; with the grain it chews like rope.","s":0},{"t":"Sear the beef over very high heat in two batches for 2 to 3 minutes; overcrowding drops the heat and the thin slices steam and toughen.","s":3},{"t":"Taste the dressing before pouring; it should be sweet, sour and salty in balance, and thin it with a little water if the fish sauce is strong.","s":1}],"rl":"PXXXWFBVVVGGXAW"},"53229":{"lv":"ERERRRRRERORE","w":{"3":"Sweet crunch; use more cabbage.","4":"Crisp, mild cabbage that holds up in the salad.","5":"Fresh mild onion; skip.","6":"Fresh herb; use mint.","7":"Heat; skip for mild.","9":"Balances the lime and fish sauce.","11":"Adds sharp savory depth to the dressing; skip and the dressing is plainer.","1":"Light pan coat; skipped, a nonstick pan works.","10":"Tiny amount; skipped, there is little noticeable effect."},"sub":{"1":{"x":"1 tsp sunflower oil","d":"Neutral oil for the pan.","k":["sunflower oil","vegetable oil"]},"10":{"x":"1 tsp lemon juice","d":"Brighter, slightly sharper than rice vinegar; the lime already supplies acid.","k":["lemon juice"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Pat the steak dry and season well just before the pan goes in; a very hot pan sears the surface rather than steaming it.","s":2},{"t":"Rest the steak 5 minutes before slicing across the grain so the juices stay in the meat; cook to medium-rare (about 130F/54C) for fillet.","s":2},{"t":"Rinse the noodles in cold water until fully cool and drain; warm noodles wilt the herbs and turn the salad gummy.","s":1}],"rl":"BFPVVXGXAWAXX"},"53230":{"lv":"EEOEEEEERE","w":{"2":"Nutty crunch in the batter; skip it.","8":"Heat for the dipping sauce; skip for mild.","3":"Frying medium; skipped, the food cannot be fried.","4":"Liquid of the tempura batter; skipped, there is no batter."},"sub":{"3":{"x":"enough sunflower or canola oil for 5-6 cm depth","d":"Any neutral frying oil works.","k":["sunflower oil","canola oil"]},"4":{"x":"250 ml ice-cold water","d":"Batter is a little denser and less lacy; keep it ice-cold and do not overmix.","k":["water"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Keep the batter ice-cold and lumpy and stop whisking as soon as it is mixed; overmixing builds gluten and gives a heavy, chewy coating.","s":2},{"t":"Fry at about 180C/356F in small batches and keep the oil to a third of the pan; crowding drops the temperature and soaks the broccoli with oil.","s":2},{"t":"Dissolve the sugar in hot water before the lime and fish sauce so the nuoc cham is smooth and balanced, and salt the tempura the moment it comes out.","s":0}],"rl":"TSGFLVXAXW"},"53235":{"lv":"RERRREEOORO","w":{"2":"Sweet, mild onion base for the sauce; replace with regular onion.","3":"Warmth and aroma; use 1 tbsp fresh ginger.","4":"Gentle heat; skip for mild.","7":"Extra heat at the end; skip for mild.","8":"Fresh garnish; skip.","9":"Soaks up the glossy sauce; use any rice or noodles.","10":"Fresh green side that balances the sweet sauce; any greens work.","0":"Stir-fries the pork; skipped, the pork fat carries it."},"sub":{"0":{"x":"1.5 tbsp vegetable oil","d":"Neutral oil for stir-frying.","k":["vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Brown the pork in batches over high heat; crowding the wok steams it and you lose the browned bits that flavor the caramel.","s":0},{"t":"Dissolve the sugar in the water and fish sauce first, then boil; do not stir after it starts to bubble hard or it can crystallize.","s":2},{"t":"Reduce vigorously until the sauce is glossy and coats the pork; the pork must reach 145F/63C, but shoulder gets more tender simmered longer.","s":3}],"rl":"FPXXXWXXGBV"},"53237":{"lv":"RERRREREOERRROROOR","w":{"0":"Dissolved in the lime to balance the sourness of the dressing; skip and it is sharp.","2":"Heat in the dressing; skip for mild.","3":"Fresh herb in the dressing; use mint.","4":"Nutty roundness in the dressing; use a little neutral oil.","6":"Salt and color in the dressing; use extra fish sauce.","10":"Cool, juicy crunch; use more cabbage.","11":"Crisp bulk and freshness; use extra cabbage.","12":"Mild onion freshness; skip.","13":"Extra fresh heat in the salad; skip.","14":"Citrusy aroma in the salad; use lemon zest.","15":"Citrus aroma; skip it.","16":"Extra fresh herb; skip.","17":"Aromatic herb that is part of the salad flavor; use extra coriander.","8":"Tiny amount; skipped, there is little noticeable effect."},"sub":{"8":{"x":"1 tbsp vegetable oil","d":"Only to brush the pork before searing.","k":["vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Make the dressing a day ahead; the chili, coriander and lime meld, and the pork can marinate in some of it if you like.","s":0},{"t":"Sear the tenderloin on all sides, then roast until 145F/63C inside and rest it 10 minutes before slicing so it stays juicy; a pink center is fine at this temperature.","s":1},{"t":"Toss the salad with the dressing just before serving; the cabbage and celery are crunchy, but the lemongrass and herbs wilt if left to sit.","s":2}],"rl":"WAXXFXXPFVVVVXXXGX"},"53249":{"lv":"ERRERRREROER","w":{"1":"Liver pate is the traditional banh mi spread, rich and savory; skip and use a little extra mayonnaise.","2":"Cool, juicy crunch against the rich spread and tangy slaw.","4":"Creamy base for the chili mayo; skip and the sandwich is drier.","5":"Heat in the chili mayo and topping; skip for mild.","6":"Fresh herb that is part of the banh mi flavor.","8":"Sweet crunch and color in the slaw; use more carrot.","9":"A quarter-teaspoon of warmth in the pickle; skip it.","11":"Balances the vinegar so the slaw is tangy, not harsh."},"sub":{"1":{"x":"1 tbsp extra mayonnaise","d":"Loses the rich pate flavor; creamier and milder. Raw liver must be cooked through, so use ready-made pate if you want the original.","k":["mayonnaise"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Use ready-made smooth liver pate; if you start from raw chicken liver, cook it fully (165F/74C) and puree it, never leave it pink.","s":2},{"t":"Salt the carrot and cabbage in the vinegar mix and squeeze it before piling on; the slaw should stay crunchy and not drip into the bread.","s":1},{"t":"Toast the baguette for 3 minutes so the crust is crackly, and spread the pate to the edges so it seals the bread from the slaw juices.","s":2}],"rl":"BFVPFXGVVXAW"},"3203":{"lv":"ERRRRRREREEEEEE","w":{"1":"Adds sweetness and bounce; skip and use more pork.","2":"Earthy savory bulk; use extra carrot.","3":"Sweetness and color; skip.","4":"Lightens the filling and absorbs juices; skip and the rolls are denser.","5":"Sweet onion flavor in the filling; use finely chopped onion.","6":"Savory aroma; skip and it is plainer.","8":"Mild spice; skip.","11":"Frying medium; skipped, the food cannot be fried."},"sub":{"11":{"x":"2 cups sunflower or canola oil","d":"Any neutral frying oil works.","k":["sunflower oil","canola oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Squeeze the mushrooms and soaked noodles dry before mixing; wet filling steams inside the wrapper and splits the roll when it hits the hot oil.","s":0},{"t":"Roll tightly with a slightly damp wrapper and do not let rolls touch in the oil for the first minute or they stick together. Fry at 340F/170C.","s":2},{"t":"Cook until deep golden, 8 to 10 minutes, and check that the pork filling reaches 160F/71C; a low oil temperature gives pale, greasy rolls.","s":3}],"rl":"PPVVBXXXZTBFXWA"},"3473":{"lv":"EEEEEE","sub":{"1":{"x":"1/2 lb (225 g) white radish or peeled kohlrabi, julienned","d":"Slightly sharper and less sweet than daikon; salt and rinse the same way.","k":["radish"]},"4":{"x":"1/2 cup rice vinegar or apple cider vinegar","d":"Rice vinegar is milder; cider adds a fruity note and tints the brine.","k":["rice vinegar","apple cider vinegar"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Massage the vegetables with salt and a little sugar, then rinse and squeeze them well; this draws out water so they stay crunchy in the brine.","s":0},{"t":"Dissolve the sugar fully in the warm water before adding the vinegar, and pack the vegetables tightly so they stay submerged in the brine.","s":2},{"t":"Let the jar sit 30 minutes at room temperature, then refrigerate; it is best after 1 to 2 days and keeps up to 3 weeks if you always use a clean utensil.","s":4}],"rl":"VVKWKM","w":{"1":"Main vegetable of the pickle; skipped, it is not do chua.","4":"Pickling acid; skipped, there is no pickle."}},"3204":{"lv":"EEEEERRERERROORR","w":{"5":"Warm sweet spice; use sparingly, and the broth is a little less complex without it.","6":"Citrusy, nutty spice that rounds out the broth.","8":"Rounds out the fish sauce; skip and the broth is saltier.","10":"Fresh crunch at the table; skip and add extra herbs.","11":"Thai basil gives the anise-like aroma of pho; use cilantro or regular basil.","12":"Fresh herb finish; skip.","13":"Mild fresh onion garnish; skip.","14":"Brightens the broth; use lemon wedges.","15":"Adjust at the end; fish sauce already seasons the broth."},"tips":[{"t":"Char the onion and ginger until blackened in spots but not burnt through; rinse off the loose char so the broth stays clear and sweet.","s":0},{"t":"Skim the foam in the first 10 minutes and keep a gentle simmer; a hard boil clouds the broth. Chicken must reach 165F/74C, and it is tender by 40 minutes.","s":2},{"t":"Season the broth until it tastes slightly too salty and sweet in the pot; the noodles and toppings will dilute it in the bowl. Strain through a fine sieve for a clear broth.","s":4}],"rl":"PXXXXXXXWBGGGGAZ"},"53234":{"lv":"EEERRERROO","w":{"3":"Earthy, meaty umami; use button mushrooms.","4":"Sweet pops of corn; skip.","6":"Brightens the curry broth; skip and it is flatter.","7":"Adds salt and depth; use fish sauce.","8":"Balances the lime and curry paste; skip.","9":"Fresh herb sprinkled on at the end; skip."},"tips":[{"t":"Stir the curry paste into the stock and let it bloom for a minute before adding the noodles; this releases its oils and deepens the broth.","s":0},{"t":"Add the salmon only at the end and keep the broth at a gentle simmer; boiling hard toughens it. It is done at about 145F/63C when it flakes.","s":1},{"t":"Add the lime juice and coriander off the heat so they stay bright; taste and add soy sauce or lime to balance the broth.","s":2}],"rl":"MXBVVPAXWG"},"53238":{"lv":"EEEEERREOEREROOOR","w":{"5":"Citrusy, nutty spice in the broth; skip and the broth is a little flatter.","6":"Warm clove aroma typical of pho; use sparingly.","10":"Adds salt and color; use extra fish sauce.","12":"Fresh mild onion garnish; skip.","13":"Heat at the table; skip for mild.","14":"Aromatic herb for the table; skip.","15":"Fresh herb; skip.","16":"Squeeze over at the end to brighten the broth; use lemon.","8":"Tiny amount; skipped, there is little noticeable effect."},"sub":{"8":{"x":"1 tsp brown sugar","d":"Slightly more molasses flavor, similar sweetness.","k":["brown sugar"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Char the onion and ginger and toast the spices in a dry pan before they go into the stock; this is where the pho flavor comes from. Simmer at least 30 minutes and strain.","s":0},{"t":"Freeze the steak for 15 minutes so it slices paper-thin; the slices cook through in the boiling broth, so ladle it very hot over them.","s":1},{"t":"Season the broth until it tastes slightly too strong; the noodles and beef dilute it. Rinse the cooked noodles so they do not cloud the broth.","s":2}],"rl":"MXXXXXXPWXXBGGGGA"},"53247":{"lv":"EEERRRE","w":{"3":"Crispy sliced garlic gives savory aroma; skip and use a little more ginger.","4":"Fresh heat and color; skip for mild.","5":"Fresh, mild onion that wilts under the hot oil and tops the fish.","1":"Only cooking fat; skipped, food sticks and browns poorly, so use an oil or substitute."},"sub":{"1":{"x":"3 tbsp sunflower or peanut oil","d":"Neutral oil for frying the fish and sizzling the aromatics.","k":["sunflower oil","peanut oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Pat the skin completely dry, slash it, and press each fillet flat for 10 seconds; leave it undisturbed so the skin crisps and releases on its own.","s":2},{"t":"Fry the ginger and chili first and the garlic last for 30 seconds; garlic burns quickly and turns bitter.","s":4},{"t":"Pour the sizzling aromatics and soy sauce over the fish at the last second and serve immediately; the fish is cooked when the flesh is opaque and flakes.","s":5}],"rl":"PFXXXGX"},"53":{"lv":"RRRREEEERRR","w":{"1":"Sweet savory base for the stew; skip and it tastes flatter.","2":"Savory aroma; skip and add a pinch of garlic powder.","3":"Warm, peppery flavor that balances the sweet potato.","8":"Warm earthy note that pairs with the peanut; skip and it is milder.","9":"Adds protein and heft; skip for a lighter stew.","10":"Wilts in at the end for color and iron; use kale or skip.","0":"Sautes aromatics; skipped, the stew still works."},"sub":{"0":{"x":"1 tbsp vegetable oil","d":"Neutral oil for sauteing the aromatics.","k":["vegetable oil"]}},"tips":[{"t":"Cook the onion until soft, then fry the garlic, ginger and cumin for 30 seconds to bloom the spices before adding the liquid.","s":0},{"t":"Whisk the peanut butter with a ladle of hot broth in a bowl first, then stir it in; it blends smoothly and does not clump. Do not boil hard afterward.","s":2},{"t":"Cut the sweet potatoes into 1.5 cm cubes so they are tender in 15 to 20 minutes; mash a few against the pot side to thicken the stew naturally.","s":1}],"rl":"FXXXBVMTXPV"}};
const CHEFFIX={"53407":{"i":["1/2 tsp|salt|"]},"53291":{"i":["1/3 cup|honey|for the glaze"]},"3253":{"i":["1 1/2 cup|water|warm"]},"3255":{"i":["1 1/4 cup|water|warm"]},"3257":{"i":["1 1/4 cup|water|warm"]},"3258":{"i":["1 1/4 cup|water|warm"]},"3627":{"i":["1 1/4 cup|water|warm"]},"125":{"i":["1/4 tsp|salt|"]},"52779":{"i":["3 tbsp|olive oil|for the tomato topping"]},"51":{"i":["|salt|to taste"]},"53457":{"t":240},"52874":{"t":210},"52881":{"repI":{"4":"1 lb|beef|stewing beef, cubed"},"t":150},"52927":{"i":["1 level tsp|Prague Powder #1 (curing salt #1, 6.25% sodium nitrite)|exactly 1 level tsp for 5 lb meat; not Cure #2 or Tender Quick"],"repI":{"0":"5 lb|beef brisket|flat about 1 1/2 to 2 inches thick; weigh it, the cure is measured by weight"},"stEdit":{"0":"To make the cure, weigh the brisket, then mix the salt, the Prague Powder #1 (exactly 1 level tsp for 5 lb of meat), black pepper, coriander, sugar, bay leaf and cloves. Coat the entire brisket with the cure and place in an extra-large resealable bag. Cure in the refrigerator at 32 to 40°F for 4 to 7 days (longer for a thicker brisket), flipping it twice a day."}},"53506":{"i":["2 cup|long-grain rice|rinsed"]},"52955":{"i":["2|eggs|beaten"]},"3318":{"i":["1 1/2 cup|water|warm"]},"3319":{"i":["1 cup|water|warm"]},"3320":{"i":["3/4 cup|water|warm"]},"3015":{"repI":{"7":"1 cup|butter|melted"}},"3017":{"t":80},"3018":{"stEdit":{"1":"If using beef, brown it in 1 tbsp of the oil for 6 minutes.","3":"Toss all the vegetables with the onion, garlic, remaining oil and salt, and spread in a deep baking dish with the beef, if using."}},"3305":{"stEdit":{"3":"Roast on a rimmed sheet pan 40 to 45 minutes, or grill over medium heat 15 to 18 minutes per side with the lid down, until the juices run clear and the thickest part reaches 175 degrees F."}},"3309":{"repI":{"9":"3 cup|rice|cooked, for serving"}},"53028":{"i":["1 tsp|salt|","4 clove|garlic|minced, for the marinade"],"repI":{"3":"1 tsp|cardamom|","0":"1 kg|boneless skinless chicken thighs|"},"c":"wraps"},"53031":{"repI":{"9":"2 tbsp|olive oil|","4":"8 clove|garlic|crushed","8":"1/2 cup|butter|","10":"3|pita bread|"},"stEdit":{"3":"To prepare the rice: Put some butter into a pot, add shareya (fideo noodles) like a handful or so, keep stirring until golden brown, not too dark, but very golden. Then add two cups of rice, stir a little bit until some of the rice turns an opaque white. Add 3 cups of water or meat broth and salt to taste. Bring to a boil, cover and turn down to simmer, and cook until tender and the liquid is absorbed, about 18 to 20 minutes."}},"3012":{"i":["1/2 cup|vegetable oil|for frying the fish"]},"3306":{"stEdit":{"4":"Cook the fish 5 to 7 minutes per side until the skin is blistered and the flesh flakes at the bone."}},"52963":{"i":["|salt|to taste","|black pepper|to taste"],"stEdit":{"1":"Using the back of a large spoon, make 4 dips in the sauce, then crack an egg into each one."}},"53025":{"i":["|salt|to taste","1/2 tsp|chili flakes|optional"],"repI":{"0":"2 cup|dried small fava beans (ful)|soaked overnight","2":"1/4 cup|olive oil|","5":"1 tsp|cumin|"},"repSt":["Soak the dried fava beans overnight in plenty of water, then drain.","Cover with fresh water in a heavy pot, bring to a boil, then simmer gently, partially covered, for 2 to 2 1/2 hours until completely soft. You can cook them ahead and reheat; old beans take longer.","Mash the garlic with the cumin and a little salt, then stir it into the hot beans with the juice of 2 lemons and the olive oil. Taste and adjust.","Serve in bowls sprinkled with chopped parsley, with the remaining lemon quartered, more olive oil, salt, pepper and chili flakes on the side, and warm flatbread.","Optional: top with sliced hard-boiled eggs, a chopped tomato and cucumber salad, scallions or tahini sauce. A handful (1/4 cup) of red lentils added at the start thickens the sauce."]},"53026":{"i":["1 tsp|salt|","1 cup|cilantro|","1/2 cup|dill|","1 tsp|ground coriander|","1/2 tsp|black pepper|"],"repI":{"0":"3 cup|dried peeled split fava beans|soaked overnight","3":"1 cup|parsley|","7":"|sesame seeds|for coating, or flour"},"stEdit":{"2":"Shape the bean mixture into 1-inch balls. Flatten slightly and coat with sesame seeds (or flour)."},"t":70},"53027":{"i":["2 cup|tomato sauce|","4 clove|garlic|minced","1/4 cup|white vinegar|","1 tsp|ground cumin|","2 tbsp|flour|for the onions","|black pepper|to taste","1/4 cup|vermicelli|broken, optional (traditional)"],"st":["Make the tomato sauce: saute 2 minced garlic cloves in 1 tbsp oil for 30 seconds, add the tomato sauce, 1/2 tsp cumin, salt and pepper, and simmer 15 minutes until thick. Stir in 2 tbsp of the vinegar at the end.","Make the daqqa (garlic-vinegar sauce): mix the remaining 2 minced garlic cloves with 2 tbsp vinegar, 1/4 cup water, 1/2 tsp cumin and a pinch of salt.","Assemble: layer the rice and lentils, macaroni and chickpeas in bowls, then top with the tomato sauce, crispy onions and a spoonful of daqqa."],"repI":{"5":"3|onion|thinly sliced","6":"|salt|to taste"},"stEdit":{"1":"Now, for the rice. Rinse and drain the rice. If using vermicelli, brown it in the 1 tbsp cooking oil until golden first. Add the par-cooked lentils and the rice to the saucepan over medium-high heat with the oil (and vermicelli), salt, pepper and coriander. Cook for 3 minutes, stirring regularly. Add warm water to cover the rice and lentil mixture by about 1 1/2 inches (you'll probably use about 3 cups of water here). Bring to a boil; the water should reduce a bit. Now cover and cook until all the liquid has been absorbed and both the rice and lentils are well cooked through (about 20 minutes). Keep covered and undisturbed for 5 minutes or so."}},"3011":{"stEdit":{"3":"Add the garlic, bell pepper, chili, cumin, coriander, paprika, salt and pepper and cook 1 minute.","4":"Squeeze in the lemon juice and toss 30 seconds. Do not overcook or the liver turns tough."}},"53029":{"i":["1 tbsp|ground coriander|fried with the garlic","|lemon|wedges, for serving"],"repI":{"0":"400 g|frozen mulukhiyah|"},"t":90,"c":"lunch"},"3025":{"t":45},"3297":{"stEdit":{"4":"Refrigerate and use within 4 days (fresh garlic in oil), or freeze small portions for up to 3 months."}},"3298":{"i":["1/4 cup|water|warm"]},"3300":{"repI":{"2":"3 cup|chicken broth|"}},"3304":{"i":["3 tbsp|cold water|to thin the tahini sauce"]},"53030":{"repI":{"2":"1 tsp|salt|"},"stEdit":{"2":"Let the dough rest 30 minutes, then divide the dough into 6-8 balls depending on the size you want for your feteer."},"c":"baking"},"3006":{"t":85},"3022":{"repI":{"1":"3 tbsp|cornstarch|"}},"3322":{"i":["3/4 cup|water|for the syrup"]},"3325":{"i":["1 cup|warm water|for the batter","3/4 cup|water|for the syrup"]},"3326":{"i":["3/4 cup|water|for the syrup"]},"53577":{"repI":{"4":"3 tbsp|vegetable oil|for the dressing"}},"53575":{"stEdit":{"2":"Mix together flour, salt and baking soda"}},"53578":{"repI":{"0":"1 1/2 cup|flour|"}},"53068":{"repSt":["Marinate the beef in the soy sauce, a squeeze of the lemon and the black pepper for 30 minutes.","Heat the olive oil in a heavy pot and brown the beef in batches; set it aside.","Saute the onion and garlic in the same pot for 3 minutes until soft.","Return the beef, add the tomato paste, water, bay leaves and the lemon slice. Bring to a boil, cover and simmer on low for 1 1/2 to 2 hours until the beef is tender.","Add the cubed potato for the last 20 minutes and cook until tender and the sauce is thick. Season with salt and serve with rice."]},"53070":{"i":["1 tbsp|liver spread|traditional, optional"],"repI":{"4":"1|red bell pepper|sliced","5":"1|green bell pepper|sliced","10":"1 tsp|chili powder|"}},"53071":{"i":["2|potatoes|cubed","1|carrots|cubed"]},"3272":{"i":["1 1/4 cup|water|cool"]},"3629":{"i":["1 1/2 cup|water|warm"]},"52852":{"i":["4|tomatoes|chopped"]},"53359":{"i":["2 lb|beef|bone-in or boneless, in large chunks"]},"3474":{"i":["2 1/4 cup|water|"]},"52998":{"t":210},"3268":{"i":["1 1/2 cup|water|warm"]},"3269":{"i":["1 cup|water|warm"]},"3630":{"i":["1 3/4 cup|water|room temperature"]},"52849":{"i":["12|cannelloni tubes|about 250 g"]},"52967":{"i":["4 cup|vegetable oil|for deep frying","1/2 tsp|ground cardamom|"]},"53427":{"st":["Food safety: if your naem (fermented pork sausage) is homemade or not a fully cured commercial product, crumble and pan-fry it until it reaches 160°F (71°C) before mixing it in."]},"3040":{"stEdit":{"5":"Refrigerate in a jar and use within 4 days, or freeze in small portions; garlic in oil must never sit at room temperature."}},"3626":{"i":["3/4 cup|water|warm"]},"3495":{"i":["8 cup|water|"]},"3270":{"i":["1 cup|water|warm"]},"53310":{"i":["2 tsp|salt|fine"]},"53082":{"i":["1/4 cup|powdered sugar|"]},"59":{"i":["1 tsp|salt|for the dumplings"]}};
